window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 201, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Walker is lying flat with the bed lowered, gasping slightly, and says the chest pressure is worse than an hour ago. His respiratory rate is 26. The nurse wants to relieve his dyspnea while awaiting the physician. Which position is best?", "options": {"A": "Turn him onto his left side with the head of the bed lowered", "B": "Place him in Trendelenburg to increase blood return to the heart", "C": "Raise the head of the bed to a high Fowler's position", "D": "Keep him flat with a pillow under his knees to rest his heart"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Lowering the head of the bed during dyspnea", "B": "Using Trendelenburg and increasing cardiac workload", "D": "Lying a dyspneic client flat for comfort"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C033", "cluster_text": "Mr. Walker, 68, has coronary artery disease and has just been admitted to a medical ward with chest discomfort and shortness of breath. He smokes, has high cholesterol and hypertension, and takes ramipril. A practical nurse is caring for him with the RN, and an ECG and vital signs every four hours have been ordered. His wife is at the bedside and anxious.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raise the head of the bed to a high Fowler's position): Sitting upright lets the diaphragm drop and reduces venous return, which eases breathing and strain on the heart. Lying flat or lowering the head makes dyspnea worse. Trendelenburg increases blood return and workload, which is harmful in this situation."}, {"id": 202, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Walker's wife asks which of his risk factors can be changed. He is 68, has a father who had a heart attack at 55, smokes daily and has high cholesterol. Which teaching priority best targets his modifiable risk factors?", "options": {"A": "Focus on avoiding all physical activity to protect his damaged heart and prevent further chest discomfort", "B": "Focus teaching on quitting smoking and controlling cholesterol, which he can change", "C": "Focus on reducing stress alone since it outweighs smoking, cholesterol and the other risk factors", "D": "Focus on his age and family history to plan long-term monitoring"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Advising avoidance of activity instead of supervised increase", "C": "Naming one factor as outweighing the others", "D": "Counting age and family history as targets for change"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C033", "cluster_text": "Mr. Walker, 68, has coronary artery disease and has just been admitted to a medical ward with chest discomfort and shortness of breath. He smokes, has high cholesterol and hypertension, and takes ramipril. A practical nurse is caring for him with the RN, and an ECG and vital signs every four hours have been ordered. His wife is at the bedside and anxious.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Focus teaching on quitting smoking and controlling cholesterol, which he can change): Smoking and cholesterol are modifiable, so teaching aims where change can lower his risk. Age and family history cannot be changed. Avoiding all activity weakens the heart over time. Stress matters but does not outweigh smoking and cholesterol."}, {"id": 203, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "One week after his myocardial infarction, Mr. Walker is home. He asks whether he should stay in bed for another month or return to carrying firewood tomorrow. His pulse is 72 and he has had no chest pain. What should the practical nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Gradual activity increase as tolerated, following the cardiac rehabilitation plan and stopping for chest pain", "B": "Limit activity to bed and chair for two weeks before any walking", "C": "Walk daily but resume lifting firewood at week two if pulse stays normal", "D": "Avoid walking until his next clinic visit to prevent strain"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Prolonged restriction after infarction", "C": "Resuming heavy lifting too early", "D": "Avoiding all activity until follow-up"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C033", "cluster_text": "Mr. Walker, 68, has coronary artery disease and has just been admitted to a medical ward with chest discomfort and shortness of breath. He smokes, has high cholesterol and hypertension, and takes ramipril. A practical nurse is caring for him with the RN, and an ECG and vital signs every four hours have been ordered. His wife is at the bedside and anxious.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gradual activity increase as tolerated, following the cardiac rehabilitation plan and stopping for chest pain): Recovery is best supported by gradual, supervised increases in activity under a cardiac rehabilitation plan, stopping for chest pain. Restricting him to bed and chair causes deconditioning and clot risk. A normal pulse does not make heavy lifting safe at two weeks, since the healing heart needs a staged return. Avoiding walking delays recovery."}, {"id": 204, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before discharge, Mr. Walker develops crackles at both lung bases and a cough at night, and he needs three pillows to sleep. His ankles look normal. The practical nurse reports these findings to the RN. Which description matches left-sided heart failure?", "options": {"A": "Hunger, dry skin and increased urine output with weight loss from low cardiac demand", "B": "Bradycardia and low blood pressure with clear lung sounds and warm, well perfused skin", "C": "Pulmonary congestion with crackles, cough and orthopnea, often before leg swelling", "D": "Peripheral edema, weight gain and jugular distension as the main early signs"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Describes signs of reduced metabolic demand, unrelated to pulmonary congestion", "B": "Describes slow pulse and low pressure with clear lungs, not failure", "D": "Expecting peripheral edema as the main sign of left-sided failure"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C033", "cluster_text": "Mr. Walker, 68, has coronary artery disease and has just been admitted to a medical ward with chest discomfort and shortness of breath. He smokes, has high cholesterol and hypertension, and takes ramipril. A practical nurse is caring for him with the RN, and an ECG and vital signs every four hours have been ordered. His wife is at the bedside and anxious.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pulmonary congestion with crackles, cough and orthopnea, often before leg swelling): Left-sided failure backs blood into the lungs, causing crackles, cough and orthopnea, which can appear before leg swelling. Peripheral edema and jugular distension are typical of right-sided failure. The other options describe signs that do not fit pulmonary congestion."}, {"id": 205, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor, 58, has an open venous leg wound with pink granulation tissue and scant serous drainage. His daughter mentions that she once used hydrogen peroxide at home and asks which solution the practical nurse should use to cleanse the wound at each dressing change. Which solution is the best routine choice?", "options": {"A": "Full strength hydrogen peroxide, since the bubbling lifts debris and speeds closure of the wound", "B": "Povidone iodine solution applied each dressing change to reduce bacteria across the wound surface", "C": "Normal saline irrigation, because it cleans without harming healthy granulation tissue", "D": "Soap and tap water scrubbed firmly with gauze to remove surface debris from the wound bed"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believes bubbling peroxide promotes healing, but it harms new tissue", "B": "Uses a cytotoxic antiseptic for routine cleansing of a clean wound", "D": "Scrubs the wound bed, which injures granulation tissue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Normal saline irrigation, because it cleans without harming healthy granulation tissue): Normal saline cleans the wound while leaving the fragile pink granulation tissue intact, which is what Mr. Okafor's wound shows. Hydrogen peroxide bubbles look active but damage new cells and slow healing, and the daughter's past home use does not make it appropriate. Povidone iodine is also cytotoxic to healing tissue and is kept for specific infection situations ordered by the team. Gentle isotonic irrigation is the default for a clean granulating wound."}, {"id": 206, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At 0300 a practical nurse must start an infusion on a pump model she has never used. The only other nurse on the unit is on break, and the client's antibiotic is due now. She feels uneasy about appearing unprepared in front of the night team. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Delay the antibiotic until the next shift so a nurse who knows the pump starts it", "B": "Ask the charge nurse for help and have a colleague verify the pump settings before starting", "C": "Switch to a gravity tubing set and estimate the drip rate by counting drops per minute", "D": "Follow the on-screen prompts and start the infusion, then report any difficulty afterward in the chart"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Postpones a time-sensitive medication instead of getting help", "C": "Uses a different method without competence or an order", "D": "Tries unfamiliar equipment alone to avoid looking incompetent"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask the charge nurse for help and have a colleague verify the pump settings before starting): Asking for help and having settings verified protects the client and keeps the antibiotic on time, and seeking guidance when uncertain is a safety behaviour rather than a weakness. Starting the pump alone risks a programming error, and reporting a problem only afterward does not prevent harm. Delaying the dose for hours leaves the client without timely treatment when a quick consult would solve the problem. Uncertainty with equipment is a cue to ask before acting."}, {"id": 207, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After a resuscitation on the emergency unit, the team leader invites everyone to a ten minute debrief. A newer practical nurse says she did nothing wrong and would rather return to her assignments, because debriefs seem to exist only to review mistakes. How should she respond to the invitation?", "options": {"A": "Attend but stay silent, since commenting on teammates' performance could be seen as criticism", "B": "Request written feedback from the team leader instead, since group talk delays assignments", "C": "Attend and contribute, since debriefs review what worked and what to improve", "D": "Attend only if she took part in the medication or airway steps"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Attends without participating, losing the learning value", "B": "Substituting private feedback for team learning", "D": "Believing debriefs are limited to involved staff"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Attend and contribute, since debriefs review what worked and what to improve): A debrief covers teamwork, communication and equipment as well as what went well, so every member's observations help. Written feedback alone misses the shared discussion. Limiting attendance to staff who handled specific tasks loses the views of others. Staying silent wastes her perspective."}, {"id": 208, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During the foot check, Mr. Rao mentions a painful corn near his small toe and asks whether he should buy a blade to remove it himself. His vision is blurred and his feet feel numb. What advice should the practical nurse give about the corn?", "options": {"A": "Apply a medicated corn pad nightly after soaking the feet in warm water for comfort", "B": "Soak the foot longer each evening to soften the corn until it can be rubbed off", "C": "Use a sterile blade only if the skin is cleaned first with an antiseptic wipe", "D": "Leave it for a podiatrist or foot care nurse, since numbness makes self-trimming risky"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses chemical corn pads that burn skin with reduced sensation", "B": "Soaks the feet, which dries and macerates insensate skin", "C": "Cuts the corn at home despite numbness and poor vision"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C032", "cluster_text": "Mr. Vikram Rao, 63, attends a diabetes clinic for a routine visit with his wife, Anita, 59. He has had type 2 diabetes for 15 years and reduced sensation in both feet. He lives with her, wears slippers indoors, and says he soaks his feet nightly. The practical nurse is completing his foot assessment and education. His HbA1c is 8.4 percent and his skin is dry with thick callus on one great toe.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Leave it for a podiatrist or foot care nurse, since numbness makes self-trimming risky): With reduced sensation and blurred vision, Mr. Rao cannot feel or see an injury that a blade or chemical pad could cause, so a podiatrist or foot care nurse should treat the corn. Medicated pads contain acids that can burn numb skin, and a clean blade does not remove the risk of an unnoticed cut that may become an ulcer. Long soaks dry the skin and increase breakdown. Clients with diabetic neuropathy should never self-treat corns or calluses."}, {"id": 209, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a home visit two weeks later, Mr. Rao shows a one centimetre blister on his heel from new shoes. It is intact with no redness around it, and he feels no pain. He suggests waiting until next week's visit to recheck it. What is the best action by the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Apply an adhesive bandage and teach him to watch for redness before calling the clinic", "B": "Cover it loosely, relieve heel pressure, and report it to the care team today", "C": "Drain the blister with a sterile needle to relieve pressure and prevent it from breaking", "D": "Reassure him that intact blisters heal alone and recheck the heel at next week's visit"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waits for visible infection before reporting", "C": "Drains the blister, breaking the protective barrier", "D": "Reassures and delays follow-up despite neuropathy"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C032", "cluster_text": "Mr. Vikram Rao, 63, attends a diabetes clinic for a routine visit with his wife, Anita, 59. He has had type 2 diabetes for 15 years and reduced sensation in both feet. He lives with her, wears slippers indoors, and says he soaks his feet nightly. The practical nurse is completing his foot assessment and education. His HbA1c is 8.4 percent and his skin is dry with thick callus on one great toe.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cover it loosely, relieve heel pressure, and report it to the care team today): A blister in a client with neuropathy can become an ulcer quickly because he cannot feel it, so the nurse protects the area and reports it today. Waiting a week gives infection time to develop unnoticed, and reassurance misses the added risk of diabetes. Draining the blister removes the protective skin and invites infection. The absence of pain here is a sign of the neuropathy, not of safety."}, {"id": 210, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Rao begins once daily basal insulin with a pen. At his teaching visit the nurse notices small firm lumps on his abdomen where he injected last week. He says that spot hurts least, so he uses it every morning. What is the most important teaching point?", "options": {"A": "Switch to a different body area at each injection, starting with the thigh each time", "B": "Rotate sites within one body area so each spot has time to recover", "C": "Keep using the least painful spot, since consistent absorption matters more than rotating sites", "D": "Massage the lumps firmly after each injection to spread the insulin and soften the tissue"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changes body areas, which alters absorption rate unpredictably", "C": "Reuses one site, causing lipohypertrophy and erratic absorption", "D": "Massages the lumps, which does not treat them and may bruise tissue"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C032", "cluster_text": "Mr. Vikram Rao, 63, attends a diabetes clinic for a routine visit with his wife, Anita, 59. He has had type 2 diabetes for 15 years and reduced sensation in both feet. He lives with her, wears slippers indoors, and says he soaks his feet nightly. The practical nurse is completing his foot assessment and education. His HbA1c is 8.4 percent and his skin is dry with thick callus on one great toe.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rotate sites within one body area so each spot has time to recover): The firm lumps suggest lipohypertrophy from repeated injection at one spot, and rotating within one body area lets tissue recover while keeping absorption predictable. Staying at the least painful spot is the cause of the lumps and makes insulin absorb unreliably. Moving between body areas for each dose changes absorption speed and can make glucose control erratic. Massage does not repair damaged tissue and is not part of correct technique."}, {"id": 211, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Anita Rao, who accompanied her husband, shows the nurse her lab report with a fasting glucose of 7.4 mmol/L and asks whether this means she has diabetes. She is worried and wants an answer before leaving the clinic. Which response by the practical nurse is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Explain that a physician or nurse practitioner diagnoses diabetes, and arrange follow-up", "B": "Order a repeat fasting glucose for her to complete this week and report the result back", "C": "Reassure her that one fasting value in the seven range is normal and needs no action", "D": "Confirm that the result is in the diabetes range and begin teaching her about diet changes"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Orders a test without authority to do so", "C": "Dismisses an abnormal value as normal", "D": "Announces a diagnosis, which is outside nursing scope"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C032", "cluster_text": "Mr. Vikram Rao, 63, attends a diabetes clinic for a routine visit with his wife, Anita, 59. He has had type 2 diabetes for 15 years and reduced sensation in both feet. He lives with her, wears slippers indoors, and says he soaks his feet nightly. The practical nurse is completing his foot assessment and education. His HbA1c is 8.4 percent and his skin is dry with thick callus on one great toe.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that a physician or nurse practitioner diagnoses diabetes, and arrange follow-up): Diagnosing diabetes is done by a physician or nurse practitioner who interprets results, often with repeat testing, so the nurse explains this and arranges follow-up. Telling her she has diabetes goes beyond the practical nurse's scope, and calling the value normal is incorrect because it is above the usual fasting threshold. Ordering a repeat test is also not within her authority without a prescriber. Supporting the client and making sure the right person acts on the result is the best help."}, {"id": 212, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A community clinic physician orders a practical nurse to apply a negative pressure dressing, a technique the nurse was never taught and has not practised. The client, an adult with a deep sacral wound, is waiting. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Perform the dressing carefully following the product leaflet and ask questions if problems arise", "B": "Explain the gap in competence, decline to do it alone, and request a competent colleague", "C": "Delegate the dressing to a nursing student who read about the technique last term", "D": "Carry out the order because the physician accepts responsibility for the outcome of any ordered treatment"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Learns on the client instead of acknowledging limits", "C": "Delegates a task beyond a student's competence", "D": "Believes the physician carries all responsibility"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain the gap in competence, decline to do it alone, and request a competent colleague): Nurses are accountable for working within their own competence even when an order is written, so she explains the limit and seeks a competent colleague or training. Learning on the client risks harm, and the physician's order does not transfer the nurse's accountability for her own actions. Giving the task to a student who has only read about it adds a second unsafe practitioner. Clear communication about competence keeps both client and nurse safe."}, {"id": 213, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On nights, an adult client develops sudden severe pain and the physician gives a telephone order for an analgesic dose. The practical nurse is alone with the charge nurse off the unit, and the order is within her scope. What should she do before giving the medication?", "options": {"A": "Enter the order after giving the dose, and read it back at shift change", "B": "Write it down and give the dose, then ask the physician to confirm later", "C": "Write it down, read it back to the physician, and document it with the time", "D": "Refuse the order, because verbal orders cannot be accepted when a client is in pain"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying documentation and read back", "B": "Skipping the read back before giving", "D": "Rejects all verbal orders despite urgent need"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Write it down, read it back to the physician, and document it with the time): A telephone order within scope is accepted when the nurse writes it down, reads it back to confirm accuracy, and documents it with the time. Reading it back at shift change is too late to catch a dose error. Confirming after giving removes the safeguard. Refusing every verbal order delays pain relief and is not required."}, {"id": 214, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "An acute care nurse notices that a physician's order for an anticoagulant dose looks double the client's usual amount. The physician is brisk and has told staff he dislikes questions. The nurse feels tense and wonders how to raise the concern. Which approach to communicating is best?", "options": {"A": "Calmly state the specific concern about the dose and ask the physician to clarify it", "B": "Tell the physician sharply that the order is wrong and that he must rewrite it now", "C": "Hint that the dose seems high without naming it, hoping he will recheck the order", "D": "Give the dose as ordered and mention the concern to colleagues at shift end"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Uses an aggressive tone that blocks collaboration", "C": "Hints indirectly so the concern is not clear", "D": "Stays silent to avoid conflict"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Calmly state the specific concern about the dose and ask the physician to clarify it): Assertive communication states the specific concern respectfully and asks for clarification, which gives the physician the information needed to verify the order before harm occurs. Giving the dose and venting later puts the client at risk, and an aggressive tone usually makes the other person defensive. Hinting is unclear and easy to ignore. Naming the dose and the concern is direct and professional."}, {"id": 215, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Dana recalls a workshop on emotional intelligence while Marcus waits for her response and the unit remains busy with alarms and requests. She wants to describe the concept accurately to a new graduate nurse later. Which statement best defines emotional intelligence in leadership?", "options": {"A": "Using knowledge of staff feelings to persuade peers to agree with the leader's decisions", "B": "Controlling the emotions of team members so conflict stays low during busy shifts", "C": "Recognizing and managing your own emotions while responding well to others' emotions", "D": "Reading staff feelings after a conflict happens and addressing them at performance review"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confuses it with manipulating others", "B": "Believing leaders manage others' emotions", "D": "Treating emotional awareness as a delayed reaction"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C035", "cluster_text": "On a busy medical unit, a practical nurse named Dana works a tense evening shift after two admissions arrive at once and a call light has gone unanswered for several minutes. A peer, Marcus, snaps at her about a missed task. Dana feels her face heat up and notices her own irritation rising. The charge nurse watches how the team handles the moment.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recognizing and managing your own emotions while responding well to others' emotions): Emotional intelligence combines awareness and management of one's own emotions with empathy and skilled responses to others. A leader cannot control teammates' emotions. Waiting for conflict and addressing feelings at a review is reactive rather than a daily skill. Technical speed is a separate skill."}, {"id": 216, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Later, Dana reflects on why Marcus's tone affected her so strongly. She realizes similar comments from a former preceptor made her defensive, and that her tight voice earlier made Marcus withdraw. How does recognizing this trigger help her function on the team?", "options": {"A": "It allows her to avoid peers who remind her of earlier negative experiences", "B": "It shows that Marcus should be told to change how he gives feedback", "C": "It lets her explain her past experiences to the team so they adjust", "D": "It lets her pause and choose a calm response instead of reacting automatically"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses avoidance instead of managing her reaction", "B": "Shifting responsibility for the reaction to the other person", "C": "Expecting the team to adapt to her history"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C035", "cluster_text": "On a busy medical unit, a practical nurse named Dana works a tense evening shift after two admissions arrive at once and a call light has gone unanswered for several minutes. A peer, Marcus, snaps at her about a missed task. Dana feels her face heat up and notices her own irritation rising. The charge nurse watches how the team handles the moment.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It lets her pause and choose a calm response instead of reacting automatically): Knowing her trigger gives Dana a moment to pause and choose a measured response. It changes her own reaction and does not control what Marcus says. Asking the team to adjust to her history shifts the work onto others. Avoiding peers limits teamwork rather than building skill."}, {"id": 217, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Two days later, Marcus approaches Dana to explain that he was overwhelmed by a client's declining status when he snapped. Dana's reply is already half formed in her head while he talks, and the unit phone is ringing. Which action demonstrates active listening?", "options": {"A": "Answer the phone, then continue listening to Marcus once the call ends", "B": "Give him full attention, let him finish, then paraphrase what she heard", "C": "Share a similar story of her own to show she understands how he felt", "D": "Maintain eye contact, take notes, and offer advice before he finishes speaking"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Splitting attention between a call and the speaker", "C": "Shifts focus to her own experience", "D": "Interrupting with advice before understanding"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C035", "cluster_text": "On a busy medical unit, a practical nurse named Dana works a tense evening shift after two admissions arrive at once and a call light has gone unanswered for several minutes. A peer, Marcus, snaps at her about a missed task. Dana feels her face heat up and notices her own irritation rising. The charge nurse watches how the team handles the moment.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give him full attention, let him finish, then paraphrase what she heard): Active listening means full attention, no interruption, and paraphrasing to check understanding. Answering the phone splits her attention and signals that the call matters more. Offering advice before he finishes cuts him off. Sharing a story of her own moves the focus away from him."}, {"id": 218, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At the next huddle, Dana asks an unregulated care provider to take a stable client's vital signs and report any unusual readings. The provider says he is comfortable doing this, and Dana plans to focus on other clients. What should Dana keep in mind about this delegation?", "options": {"A": "She remains accountable, so she gives clear instructions and checks the results", "B": "She only needs to review the chart at the end of shift for any recorded concerns", "C": "Clear instructions are unnecessary if the provider has done the task many times before", "D": "Accountability transfers to the care provider once he accepts the task, so she can step away"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delays oversight until the end of the shift", "C": "Skips instructions based on assumed experience", "D": "Believes delegation removes the delegator's accountability"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C035", "cluster_text": "On a busy medical unit, a practical nurse named Dana works a tense evening shift after two admissions arrive at once and a call light has gone unanswered for several minutes. A peer, Marcus, snaps at her about a missed task. Dana feels her face heat up and notices her own irritation rising. The charge nurse watches how the team handles the moment.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She remains accountable, so she gives clear instructions and checks the results): The delegating nurse stays accountable for the outcome, so Dana gives clear expectations, such as which readings to report, and follows up on the results. Accountability does not move to the care provider just because he accepts the task, and waiting until the end of shift delays recognition of a change. Even an experienced provider needs client-specific instructions. Delegation requires communication and monitoring."}, {"id": 219, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A practical nurse prepares to irrigate a draining abdominal wound on an adult with a known history of MRSA, and the irrigation may splash. The dressing is saturated, hand hygiene is done, and a contact precaution cart is nearby. Which equipment combination best protects the nurse during the procedure?", "options": {"A": "Gloves and a gown only, since the wound is covered by a saturated dressing", "B": "Gloves and a surgical mask, since the splash risk is mainly to the face", "C": "Double gloves and a plastic apron, because direct contact with the wound is the main risk", "D": "Gown, gloves, a mask, and eye protection or a face shield"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Wears gloves and gown but leaves eyes and mouth exposed", "B": "Protects face but not the clothing from drainage", "C": "Adds gloves but ignores mucous membrane splash risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Gown, gloves, a mask, and eye protection or a face shield): Irrigation may splash body fluids onto clothing, skin, and mucous membranes, so the nurse needs a gown, gloves, a mask, and eye protection or a face shield. Gloves and gown alone leave the eyes and mouth exposed, and gloves with a mask leave clothing unprotected from a drainage splash. Double gloves and an apron address contact only. PPE is chosen for the anticipated exposure, not just the diagnosis."}, {"id": 220, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the ward, a practical nurse finds a 71-year-old man two days after bowel surgery with a heart rate of 124, a blood pressure of 88/50 mmHg, and cool hands. He says he feels a bit tired and his dressing is dry. He is due for vital signs again in four hours. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Recheck his vital signs in thirty minutes and keep him on bed rest", "B": "Report the findings to the RN or physician right away and stay with the client", "C": "Reassess in four hours as scheduled since he has no complaint and the dressing is dry", "D": "Give ordered analgesic, then report the vital signs at the next medical rounds"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Short delay for a worsening trend", "C": "Waits on a routine schedule despite warning signs", "D": "Delaying escalation of unstable vital signs"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the findings to the RN or physician right away and stay with the client): A heart rate of 124, blood pressure of 88/50 and cool hands after surgery suggest bleeding or sepsis, so the nurse reports at once and stays with him. A repeat in thirty minutes still delays medical assessment. Waiting for rounds also delays treatment. A dry dressing does not rule out internal bleeding."}, {"id": 221, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A practical nurse must examine the ear of a 4-year-old who sits quietly on a parent's lap on a pediatric ward and clings to a stuffed rabbit. The nurse is standing, holding the otoscope, and the child's eyes have not left the instrument. What approach best builds the child's trust?", "options": {"A": "Keep the otoscope hidden until the examination begins to avoid frightening the child beforehand", "B": "Ask the parent to hold the child firmly and complete the ear exam quickly", "C": "Crouch to eye level and demonstrate the otoscope on the rabbit before the examination", "D": "Stand beside the bed and explain the otoscope in medical terms so the parent understands"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Hides equipment, which increases fear of the unknown", "B": "Restrains and rushes without preparation", "D": "Stands over the child and uses adult terms"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Crouch to eye level and demonstrate the otoscope on the rabbit before the examination): Lowering to the child's eye level and showing the instrument on the rabbit lets a preschooler see it is safe and gives a sense of control. Standing over the child and using adult terms increases fear, and restraint with a quick exam builds distrust. Hiding the otoscope makes the sudden appearance more frightening. Play and eye level contact fit the preschool stage."}, {"id": 222, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Chen, 65, is receiving intravenous vancomycin every 12 hours on the medical unit. The physician orders a trough level, and the next dose is hung and due at 1000. The practical nurse checks the time and the lab requisition at 0940. When should the trough blood sample be drawn?", "options": {"A": "Two hours after the dose, once the medication has fully distributed in the blood", "B": "At 1000 immediately after the dose is started, so the timing matches the order", "C": "Thirty minutes after the 1000 dose infusion ends to capture the peak concentration", "D": "Before the 1000 dose is started, close to the time the dose is due"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Draws long after the dose, missing the lowest level", "B": "Draws after starting the dose, raising the result", "C": "Confuses trough timing with a peak level"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Before the 1000 dose is started, close to the time the dose is due): A trough is the lowest drug concentration, so it is drawn shortly before the next dose is given, which here means before 1000. Drawing after the infusion would measure a peak or mid-cycle value and could lead to wrong dose changes. Starting the dose first raises the blood level and makes the sample useless as a trough. Timed specimens must be matched to the dosing schedule."}, {"id": 223, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A practical nurse repositions a 78-year-old man in bed on an acute care ward. He has an indwelling urinary catheter, and the drainage bag is hanging on the bed rail at chest level during turning. Where should the drainage bag be placed to promote safe drainage?", "options": {"A": "Level with his chest so urine drains smoothly under the pull of gravity", "B": "Below bladder level, hung from the bed frame and not touching the floor", "C": "Clamp the tubing and hang the bag above bladder level during turning", "D": "Rest the bag on the mattress beside his hip so tubing stays slack"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Hangs the bag above the bladder, allowing backflow", "C": "Raising the bag to prevent pulling", "D": "Placing the bag at bladder level on the bed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Below bladder level, hung from the bed frame and not touching the floor): The bag hangs below bladder level so urine drains by gravity and cannot flow back, and it stays off the floor to prevent contamination. Raising the bag above the bladder, even briefly while clamped, risks backflow and infection. A bag beside his hip on the mattress sits near bladder level and impedes drainage."}, {"id": 224, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "In the post anesthesia recovery unit, a practical nurse sees that an adult client's oxygen saturation has drifted from 96 to 91 percent over twenty minutes, and the colleague monitoring says it is probably just sleepiness. The client is hard to rouse. What should the practical nurse do next?", "options": {"A": "Increase the oxygen flow to the client and document the new setting without notifying the physician", "B": "Reposition the probe, recheck saturation, and report if it falls below 88 percent", "C": "Stimulate the client and record observations every five minutes without calling anyone yet", "D": "Voice the concern, assess and stimulate the client, and call the physician or anesthesiologist now"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adjusts oxygen alone without reporting", "B": "Using a fixed threshold instead of the trend and level of consciousness", "C": "Monitoring without escalating a deterioration"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Voice the concern, assess and stimulate the client, and call the physician or anesthesiologist now): A falling saturation in a client who is hard to rouse suggests airway obstruction or respiratory depression, so the nurse speaks up, assesses and calls for help now. A lower reporting threshold ignores the downward trend. Frequent recording without calling lets hypoxia progress. Raising the oxygen flow without notifying the physician masks a deteriorating airway or respiratory depression."}, {"id": 225, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During lunch in her home, a practical nurse watches an 80-year-old woman with Parkinson disease cough twice after swallowing soup, and her voice sounds wet. The client says she is fine and wants to keep eating. Her caregiver says coughing often happens at meals. How should the nurse act?", "options": {"A": "Offer thinner fluids between bites to clear her throat, then continue the meal", "B": "Reassure the caregiver that coughing is a normal part of Parkinson disease and eating", "C": "Stop the meal, sit her upright, and report possible aspiration for assessment", "D": "Let her finish eating and report the coughing during the end of the visit"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives thin fluids, which increase aspiration risk", "B": "Accepts coughing as normal and ignores the wet voice", "D": "Waits until the meal ends to report"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the meal, sit her upright, and report possible aspiration for assessment): Coughing with a wet voice after swallowing suggests food or fluid entering the airway, so the nurse stops the meal, positions her upright, and reports for a swallowing assessment. Finishing the meal exposes her to more aspiration risk, and thin fluids are the hardest to control with impaired swallowing. Calling the cough normal ignores a common and serious complication of Parkinson disease. Early reporting prevents pneumonia."}, {"id": 226, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At a public health clinic, a practical nurse is asked to give an adult an influenza immunization under a medical directive. The directive lists criteria: age 18 or older, no egg anaphylaxis, and no current fever. The client reports a mild cold with a temperature of 38.6 C. What is the correct approach?", "options": {"A": "Give the vaccine because the directive authorizes the nurse to immunize adult clients at the clinic", "B": "Give half the usual dose since the cold is mild and the directive allows clinical judgment", "C": "Give the vaccine after giving acetaminophen to bring the temperature under the listed cutoff", "D": "Apply the criteria to this client, withhold the vaccine because of the fever, and consult"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats the directive as replacing assessment", "B": "Alters the dose outside the directive", "C": "Masks a fever to meet criteria"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Apply the criteria to this client, withhold the vaccine because of the fever, and consult): A medical directive authorizes the action only when the listed criteria are met, so the nurse assesses the client first and withholds the vaccine because the fever excludes him. Giving it because the directive exists treats the order as a substitute for assessment, and lowering the temperature with a drug only hides the exclusion. Changing the dose is outside the directive. Directives require authorization, assessment, and consultation when criteria are not met."}, {"id": 227, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A practical nurse enters the room of a client on droplet and contact precautions for influenza and MRSA. She has performed hand hygiene and prepares to put on gloves, a mask, a gown, and eye protection from the cart at the door. In which sequence should she don them?", "options": {"A": "Eye protection first, then gloves, gown, and mask, since the eyes are most exposed", "B": "Mask first, then gloves, eye protection, and gown, so the face is covered early", "C": "Gloves first, then gown, mask, and eye protection, so her hands stay protected throughout", "D": "Gown first, then mask, then eye protection, and gloves last"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Starts with eyewear and gloves out of sequence", "B": "Dons the gown last, handling the mask with gloves", "C": "Puts gloves on first, which contaminates them while dressing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Gown first, then mask, then eye protection, and gloves last): The usual order is gown, mask, eye protection, then gloves, because gloves go on last so they cover the gown cuffs and stay clean until contact with the client. Gloving first means the gloves touch other items and may tear while tying the gown. The other sequences either handle the mask with contaminated gloves or leave the gown unsecured. A set order reduces contamination."}, {"id": 228, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A physician on a medical unit asks a practical nurse to perform a new wound assessment technique. The nurse has seen it done but never performed it, and her employer's policy manual does not mention the technique. Which sequence of questions should guide her decision about performing it?", "options": {"A": "Is it in legislation and standards, permitted by policy, and within my competence?", "B": "Is the employer's job description clear, has the nurse done it, and is supervision available?", "C": "Has the physician ordered it, is the client willing, and has a colleague done it before?", "D": "Is the physician present, has the client agreed, and does the procedure have written steps?"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using job description and supervision instead of legislation and competence", "C": "Relies on a physician request and consent alone", "D": "Relying on physician presence and client agreement"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Is it in legislation and standards, permitted by policy, and within my competence?): The decision path checks that the activity is allowed by legislation and standards, permitted by employer policy, and within the nurse's own competence. Here the missing policy and lack of experience point to caution. A job description, supervision, physician presence or client agreement does not establish scope or competence."}, {"id": 229, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a rehabilitation unit, a practical nurse is asked to catheterize a 34-year-old man with a neurogenic bladder using an intermittent catheter. Policy in the hospital is sterile technique for his procedure. He mentions that he uses clean gloves at home. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Use sterile technique, because hospital policy and infection risk require it", "B": "Use clean gloves and a clean catheter, since his bladder is already used to home technique", "C": "Use clean technique, since policy allows it once the client has shown he can self catheterize", "D": "Use sterile gloves but reuse his own home catheter after rinsing it with tap water"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Follows the client's home practice over hospital policy", "C": "Misapplies a policy exception not stated in the stem", "D": "Reuses a non-sterile catheter"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use sterile technique, because hospital policy and infection risk require it): Hospital policy requires sterile technique for catheterization, and acute care settings carry higher infection risks than the home, so the nurse follows policy even though the client uses clean technique at home. His habits do not change the standard of the setting, and a rinsed home catheter is not sterile. Clean technique for self-catheterization is a community practice, not a hospital exception. Follow the policy of the setting."}, {"id": 230, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "An acute care practical nurse is reviewing hand hygiene practice on her unit after a rise in infections. A colleague says the unit has always done it one way and that custom is the best evidence. Which source is best to guide any change in practice?", "options": {"A": "Findings from the manufacturer of a new hand sanitizer, including their product trial", "B": "A recent case study from one hospital that reported a drop in infections", "C": "Current clinical guidelines and peer-reviewed research from recognized health bodies", "D": "The unit's long-standing routine, because experienced nurses have verified it through years of use"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Trusting manufacturer data as independent evidence", "B": "Treating a single case report as strong evidence", "D": "Treats custom as evidence"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Current clinical guidelines and peer-reviewed research from recognized health bodies): Evidence informed practice draws on current guidelines and peer reviewed research from recognized bodies. Manufacturer reports may carry commercial bias. A single hospital case study is limited and may not apply elsewhere. Custom and one senior nurse's advice may carry outdated methods."}, {"id": 231, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A practical nurse removes the indwelling urinary catheter from a 55-year-old woman on the first morning after abdominal surgery. The order states to monitor voiding after removal, and the client asks when someone will check. Which timing and assessment is best?", "options": {"A": "Wait twenty four hours before checking, since the bladder needs time to regain tone", "B": "Check for a first void within six to eight hours, measuring volume and bladder", "C": "Check only when she reports urgency, since the bladder signals when it is full", "D": "Straight catheterize her after four hours if she has not voided, without further assessment"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays monitoring far beyond the expected window", "C": "Relies on sensation, which may be dulled after surgery", "D": "Catheterizes early without assessment or an order"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Check for a first void within six to eight hours, measuring volume and bladder): Most clients should void within six to eight hours of removal, so the nurse monitors for the first void, measures the volume, and checks for bladder distension. Waiting a full day risks retention and discomfort, and relying on urgency can miss a full bladder after surgery or anesthesia. Catheterizing at four hours skips assessment and likely an order. Timely monitoring catches retention early."}, {"id": 232, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a practical nurse reported a client's worsening pain to the RN, who did not act. An hour later the client is still uncomfortable, and the nurse is unsure what to do. Which step comes next in the chain of command?", "options": {"A": "Go to the charge nurse or manager next, describing the concern and actions taken", "B": "Give an extra dose of the prescribed analgesic to settle the client's pain", "C": "Ask the RN to reconsider, then document the concern and monitor the client", "D": "Email the hospital's patient relations office with a description of the RN's response"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Medicates outside the order", "C": "Stopping at the same level of the chain", "D": "Skipping the unit chain for a complaint office"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Go to the charge nurse or manager next, describing the concern and actions taken): The chain of command moves one level up at a time, so after the RN the nurse takes the concern to the charge nurse or manager with clear details. Asking the same RN again does not escalate. A patient relations office is not the next step. Giving an extra dose without an order is a medication error."}, {"id": 233, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Priya asks Sam to keep her visit private because she fears her mother's reaction. Her mother, waiting outside, asks Sam what her daughter came in for. Priya has shown clear understanding of the risks and options discussed. How should Sam respond to the mother?", "options": {"A": "Tell her the reason but ask her to keep it from the rest of the family", "B": "Share the visit reason because parents hold legal authority over a minor's health information", "C": "Ask Priya to leave the room and then decide together with her mother what to disclose", "D": "Explain that Priya's health information is confidential and invite her mother to ask her"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Discloses and then asks for secrecy", "B": "Assumes parents always have access to a minor's information", "C": "Removes the capable client from the decision"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C029", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Sam works a shift that moves between a youth clinic in the morning and a pediatric ward and emergency department in the afternoon. In the clinic, a 16-year-old, Priya, arrives alone seeking contraception advice and appears to understand the choices. Later, Sam cares for children aged 18 months, 2 years, and 3 years, who cannot describe their symptoms reliably.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that Priya's health information is confidential and invite her mother to ask her): A capable adolescent has the same right to confidentiality as an adult, so Sam does not disclose and invites the mother to talk with Priya. Age alone does not give parents access, and disclosing then asking for secrecy still breaches privacy. Asking Priya to leave excludes her from a decision about her own information. Capacity, not age, guides confidentiality."}, {"id": 234, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Later on the ward, Sam assesses an 18-month-old who had an appendectomy. The child cries, pulls at the dressing, and draws up his legs when touched but cannot describe pain. His mother asks how Sam will know if he is hurting. Which assessment tool is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "A zero to ten numeric scale, asking him to point to a number that fits", "B": "A self-report questionnaire completed by the mother about his usual comfort at home", "C": "A faces scale where he points to the cartoon face that matches his hurt", "D": "A behavioural scale such as FLACC, scoring face, legs, activity, cry, consolability"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses a numeric scale a toddler cannot use", "B": "Relies on a home history instead of current observation", "C": "Uses a self-report faces scale beyond his age"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C029", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Sam works a shift that moves between a youth clinic in the morning and a pediatric ward and emergency department in the afternoon. In the clinic, a 16-year-old, Priya, arrives alone seeking contraception advice and appears to understand the choices. Later, Sam cares for children aged 18 months, 2 years, and 3 years, who cannot describe their symptoms reliably.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A behavioural scale such as FLACC, scoring face, legs, activity, cry, consolability): A nonverbal toddler cannot rate pain on a scale, so a behavioural tool such as FLACC scores observable cues like facial expression, leg movement, and cry. A numeric scale and a faces scale both need the child to understand and report, which an 18-month-old cannot do. A mother's questionnaire about home comfort does not measure current postoperative pain. Match the tool to the child's developmental level."}, {"id": 235, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sam assesses a 3-year-old admitted with pneumonia whose oxygen saturation is 95 percent on room air. She is unusually quiet, her respiratory rate has climbed from 28 to 44, and she does not cry when Sam approaches. Her mother says she is just tired. What should Sam do?", "options": {"A": "Report her rising respiratory rate and reduced responsiveness now, despite the saturation", "B": "Continue routine observations because a saturation of 95 percent shows she is stable", "C": "Ask her mother to offer fluids and recheck respiratory rate in one hour", "D": "Increase her oxygen flow and notify the physician if her rate exceeds 50"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relies on saturation alone", "C": "Waiting an hour despite a rising rate", "D": "Adjusting oxygen without an order and setting a late threshold"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C029", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Sam works a shift that moves between a youth clinic in the morning and a pediatric ward and emergency department in the afternoon. In the clinic, a 16-year-old, Priya, arrives alone seeking contraception advice and appears to understand the choices. Later, Sam cares for children aged 18 months, 2 years, and 3 years, who cannot describe their symptoms reliably.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report her rising respiratory rate and reduced responsiveness now, despite the saturation): A rising respiratory rate with reduced responsiveness in a child with pneumonia suggests tiring and early failure, so Sam reports now even though the saturation is acceptable. Saturation is a late sign in children. Waiting an hour or for a higher rate delays care. Changing oxygen without an order exceeds her role."}, {"id": 236, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the emergency department, Sam helps assess a 2-year-old with a barking cough and chest retractions. After oxygen is applied, the child grows quiet and sleepy and stops fighting examination. His mother says he is finally better. What is the nurse's best interpretation and action?", "options": {"A": "Agree with the mother and encourage him to sleep while keeping the oxygen on", "B": "Accept the quiet behaviour as improvement and lower observation frequency to every hour", "C": "Discontinue the oxygen to see whether he stays settled without any support", "D": "Recognize possible worsening from fatigue and alert the physician immediately"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Lets him sleep without reassessing", "B": "Mistakes exhaustion for improvement", "C": "Removes oxygen from a distressed child"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C029", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Sam works a shift that moves between a youth clinic in the morning and a pediatric ward and emergency department in the afternoon. In the clinic, a 16-year-old, Priya, arrives alone seeking contraception advice and appears to understand the choices. Later, Sam cares for children aged 18 months, 2 years, and 3 years, who cannot describe their symptoms reliably.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Recognize possible worsening from fatigue and alert the physician immediately): A child with retractions who becomes quiet and sleepy may be tiring from the effort of breathing, which is a late and dangerous sign, so Sam alerts the physician immediately. Reading calm as improvement and reducing observation could miss respiratory failure. Letting him sleep without reassessment and removing the oxygen both ignore the risk. Always check whether quiet reflects relief or exhaustion."}, {"id": 237, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a community clinic, a practical nurse prepares a 52-year-old man for a fasting lipid panel scheduled at 0800. He arrived at 0745 and mentions having a coffee with cream and a slice of toast at 0700 because he took his morning pills. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Cancel the lipid panel and ask the physician to order a glucose test instead", "B": "Draw the sample as scheduled since coffee with cream does not change lipid results", "C": "Have him wait thirty minutes and then draw, because coffee clears quickly from the blood", "D": "Document the intake and tell the physician or lab, since the test may need rebooking"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changes the test without authority", "B": "Assumes cream and toast do not affect lipids", "C": "Believes a short wait restores the fasting state"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Document the intake and tell the physician or lab, since the test may need rebooking): Food and cream in the morning break the fast ordered for the lipid panel, so the nurse documents the intake and tells the physician or lab who decide whether to proceed or reschedule. A thirty minute wait does not restore a fasting state, and drawing as if nothing happened risks an unreliable result. Changing to a glucose test is not the nurse's decision. The ordering provider decides how to handle a broken fast."}, {"id": 238, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a rehabilitation unit, a 45-year-old man has a calf muscle strain from a fall two hours ago, and the area is swollen and bruised. He asks for a hot pack because it helped his back last year. Which response by the practical nurse is best?", "options": {"A": "Explain that cold reduces early swelling and apply a covered cold pack as ordered", "B": "Alternate hot and cold packs every five minutes to improve circulation to the area", "C": "Apply a heating pad on low setting while he rests with the leg raised", "D": "Apply a hot pack for twenty minutes over a towel to relax the strained muscle"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Alternates heat and cold early, increasing swelling", "C": "Uses low heat despite acute inflammation", "D": "Uses heat on a fresh swollen injury"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that cold reduces early swelling and apply a covered cold pack as ordered): A fresh injury with swelling and bruising needs cold to limit swelling and bleeding in the first day or two, so the nurse explains this and applies a covered cold pack as ordered. Heat dilates vessels and can increase swelling, even at low settings. Alternating hot and cold early gives the same problem. Past benefit of heat for his back does not apply to an acute strain."}, {"id": 239, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 60-year-old woman started ramipril three weeks ago for hypertension. At the clinic she reports a dry, tickly cough that bothers her at night. She has no rash, swelling, or breathing difficulty, and she asks whether she is allergic. How should the practical nurse interpret this?", "options": {"A": "A known side effect, not an allergy; report it to the prescriber for review", "B": "A normal reaction that will fade, so no report is needed to the prescriber", "C": "An early sign of heart failure, so she should go to the emergency department now", "D": "An allergic reaction, so the ramipril should be stopped and an allergy recorded in the chart"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Dismisses a persistent effect without reporting", "C": "Misreads a dry cough as cardiac decompensation", "D": "Labels every adverse effect as an allergy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A known side effect, not an allergy; report it to the prescriber for review): A dry cough is a common side effect of ACE inhibitors and is not an immune allergy, but it can be bothersome, so the nurse reports it so the prescriber can consider a change. Calling it an allergy and stopping the drug without an order is outside scope. Heart failure is unlikely without other signs, and expecting it to fade without reporting may leave her uncomfortable. Distinguish side effect from allergy and report."}, {"id": 240, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During handover, the colleague repeats a stereotype about Mr. Thompson's pain, and a student nurse is present. Lena feels uncomfortable, and the charge nurse is busy at the desk. What is Lena's best action as a nurse committed to anti-racism?", "options": {"A": "Complete the handover quietly and treat Mr. Thompson with extra kindness during her own care", "B": "Stay neutral in front of the student and discuss it privately with the colleague afterward", "C": "Respectfully challenge the stereotype, reassess his pain promptly, and report the concern", "D": "Document the remark in the client's chart so the physician is aware of the attitude"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Compensates personally without addressing the remark", "B": "Stays neutral and lets bias go unchallenged", "D": "Places a colleague's remark in the client's record"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C036", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse named Lena works on a hospital unit caring for Mr. Thompson, an Indigenous adult admitted with chest pain. During handover, a colleague says that people like him exaggerate pain to get medication. Lena notices that Mr. Thompson has waited longer for pain relief than other clients on the unit and appears guarded when staff enter his room.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Respectfully challenge the stereotype, reassess his pain promptly, and report the concern): Anti-racism requires acting: Lena challenges the stereotype respectfully, reassesses Mr. Thompson's pain so his care is not affected by bias, and reports through proper channels. Silence in front of a student models acceptance of bias, and personal kindness does not stop the colleague's attitude from affecting care. Charting the remark places a staff comment in the client's record where it does not belong. Fair care needs both speaking up and checking the care itself."}, {"id": 241, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Later that shift, the same colleague tells Lena, in the staff room, that Mr. Thompson's family will probably cause trouble because of where they come from. Other staff are present and say nothing. Lena wants to keep the team peaceful. How should Lena respond?", "options": {"A": "Report the colleague to the regulatory college immediately without speaking to the manager first", "B": "Quietly change the subject to a work topic and report only if it repeats", "C": "Document the comment in Mr. Thompson's chart so the next shift can prepare", "D": "Calmly name the comment as stereotyping and state that all clients deserve respectful care"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Escalates to the regulator and skips local steps", "B": "Avoiding confrontation until a repeat", "C": "Putting a colleague's stereotype in the client chart"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C036", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse named Lena works on a hospital unit caring for Mr. Thompson, an Indigenous adult admitted with chest pain. During handover, a colleague says that people like him exaggerate pain to get medication. Lena notices that Mr. Thompson has waited longer for pain relief than other clients on the unit and appears guarded when staff enter his room.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Calmly name the comment as stereotyping and state that all clients deserve respectful care): Calmly naming the comment as stereotyping and restating the expectation of respectful care addresses the behaviour directly. Changing the subject and waiting signals that such comments are acceptable. Recording a stereotype in the client chart is inappropriate and could shape his family's care. Going to the regulator first skips local steps."}, {"id": 242, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Thompson tells Lena, in a quiet tone, that nurses did not listen to him earlier and he felt disrespected. He is tense and looks away. Lena knows the care given followed policy. What response best supports the therapeutic relationship?", "options": {"A": "Listen without interrupting, acknowledge his experience, and ask what would help", "B": "Explain that the care followed hospital policy and that staff were busy that night", "C": "Assure him that nobody meant any disrespect and that the matter is now closed", "D": "Tell him she understands, then change the subject to his chest pain plan"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Defends the care instead of listening", "C": "Dismisses his experience by closing the issue", "D": "Claims understanding and deflects"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C036", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse named Lena works on a hospital unit caring for Mr. Thompson, an Indigenous adult admitted with chest pain. During handover, a colleague says that people like him exaggerate pain to get medication. Lena notices that Mr. Thompson has waited longer for pain relief than other clients on the unit and appears guarded when staff enter his room.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Listen without interrupting, acknowledge his experience, and ask what would help): Listening, acknowledging, thanking him, and asking what would help repairs trust and respects his experience, even if the care technically met policy. Explaining and defending puts policy ahead of his feelings, and changing the subject dismisses what he said. Declaring that nobody meant harm invalidates his perception. Responding with openness rather than defence is central to culturally safe care."}, {"id": 243, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Before his transfer to a long-term care home, Mr. Thompson tells Lena that he prefers a family member present during care discussions and wants to smudge each morning. He asks that staff respect this. How should Lena document these preferences in his record for the next team?", "options": {"A": "Record them factually in his own words, as preferences, and share them in the care plan", "B": "Note that he is Indigenous and assume he follows the traditional practices of his nation", "C": "Leave it out of the chart to avoid stereotyping and to protect his privacy", "D": "Summarize the preferences as cultural beliefs that may conflict with routine hospital care"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Records assumptions about the client's culture", "C": "Omits information needed for respectful care", "D": "Frames preferences as problems"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C036", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse named Lena works on a hospital unit caring for Mr. Thompson, an Indigenous adult admitted with chest pain. During handover, a colleague says that people like him exaggerate pain to get medication. Lena notices that Mr. Thompson has waited longer for pain relief than other clients on the unit and appears guarded when staff enter his room.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Record them factually in his own words, as preferences, and share them in the care plan): Documenting preferences factually in his own words makes them visible to the next team and avoids assuming what he believes. Assuming that he follows a nation's practices replaces his voice with a stereotype, and leaving them out means the new home may not honour his wishes. Describing them as a conflict with routine care frames his needs as a problem. Accurate, respectful charting supports culturally safe care."}, {"id": 244, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Jordan is caring for an 82-year-old with advanced dementia and no advance directive, who needs urgent bowel surgery. Her son abroad insists he decides as the oldest child, while a close friend says she would refuse. Who gives consent, and how should Jordan interpret this?", "options": {"A": "Consent comes from the recognized substitute decision maker, identified by the hierarchy", "B": "The oldest child decides, since birth order determines authority when a parent cannot consent", "C": "The nurse decides in the client's best interest because the family disagrees on treatment", "D": "The friend decides, because she knows Mrs. Park's wishes better than the adult children"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believes birth order determines authority", "C": "Believes the nurse takes over when family disagrees", "D": "Believes closeness overrides the legal hierarchy"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C025", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Jordan floats between units during one shift at a Canadian hospital and meets five situations involving consent: an 82-year-old with advanced dementia and no advance directive, a 62-year-old oncology client considering treatment, an unconscious young adult arriving in emergency, a 15-year-old at a pediatric clinic, and a 40-year-old in day surgery. In each, Jordan must apply national consent principles within the standards of her regulatory body.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Consent comes from the recognized substitute decision maker, identified by the hierarchy): When a client lacks capacity, consent comes from the substitute decision maker identified by the legal hierarchy, which the care team confirms, and the SDM follows the client's known wishes. Birth order and personal closeness are not what determines the role. The nurse does not become the decision maker because family members disagree. Jordan reports the conflict so the team can apply the hierarchy."}, {"id": 245, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Alvarez, 62, has been told chemotherapy may extend his life. Mentally capable, he tells Jordan he has decided against it after talking with his family. A colleague says he is making a mistake and suggests holding back his comfort care. What should Jordan do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the physician to reinforce the recommendation and delay comfort care until then", "B": "Arrange discharge from the unit because he is declining the recommended plan of care", "C": "Respect his informed decision, confirm he understands, and continue all other care", "D": "Arrange a family meeting to persuade him, then record his refusal as temporary"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using comfort care as leverage", "B": "Treats refusal as grounds for discharge", "D": "Pressuring a capable client and mislabelling his refusal"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C025", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Jordan floats between units during one shift at a Canadian hospital and meets five situations involving consent: an 82-year-old with advanced dementia and no advance directive, a 62-year-old oncology client considering treatment, an unconscious young adult arriving in emergency, a 15-year-old at a pediatric clinic, and a 40-year-old in day surgery. In each, Jordan must apply national consent principles within the standards of her regulatory body.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Respect his informed decision, confirm he understands, and continue all other care): A capable client has the right to refuse treatment, even life saving treatment, so Jordan respects his informed decision, checks his understanding, and continues all other care. Delaying comfort care punishes refusal. A meeting meant to persuade him pressures his autonomy, and recording the refusal as temporary misstates his decision. Discharging him is not justified."}, {"id": 246, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A 28-year-old man arrives unconscious in the emergency department after a motorcycle crash with heavy bleeding and no identification. No relatives are present, and the physician says he needs an immediate blood transfusion. A colleague asks Jordan whether consent paperwork must be signed first. What principle applies?", "options": {"A": "Treatment must wait until a relative is found and signs, since consent is required first", "B": "Two nurses can sign for him as witnesses so the transfusion can proceed lawfully", "C": "Emergency treatment to save life may proceed without consent when it cannot be obtained", "D": "Only minimal treatment to stabilize him is allowed until he wakes and consents himself"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Insists on consent in a life-threatening emergency", "B": "Uses nurse signatures as a substitute for consent", "D": "Limits care below what the emergency requires"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C025", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Jordan floats between units during one shift at a Canadian hospital and meets five situations involving consent: an 82-year-old with advanced dementia and no advance directive, a 62-year-old oncology client considering treatment, an unconscious young adult arriving in emergency, a 15-year-old at a pediatric clinic, and a 40-year-old in day surgery. In each, Jordan must apply national consent principles within the standards of her regulatory body.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Emergency treatment to save life may proceed without consent when it cannot be obtained): When a person cannot consent and delay would threaten life, necessary emergency treatment may proceed without consent, and the team documents the circumstances. Waiting for a relative would risk his life, and nurses cannot sign as a substitute for his consent. Limiting treatment to minimal measures fails to give the care the emergency requires. The exception applies only while consent cannot be obtained and no known refusal exists."}, {"id": 247, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At a pediatric clinic, a 15-year-old asks Jordan whether she can get care for an ear infection on her own. She understands the proposed antibiotic, its risks, and why it is needed. Her father is not present and says by phone that he must approve everything. What national principle applies?", "options": {"A": "Parental consent is required until age eighteen, so the father must approve the care", "B": "Any adolescent over fourteen may consent to treatment without further assessment", "C": "Capacity to understand the decision, not age alone, decides whether she may consent", "D": "Her consent is valid only if a second health professional also signs the form"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes a fixed age for all consent", "B": "Assumes an age cutoff replaces capacity assessment", "D": "Adds an unnecessary second signature"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C025", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Jordan floats between units during one shift at a Canadian hospital and meets five situations involving consent: an 82-year-old with advanced dementia and no advance directive, a 62-year-old oncology client considering treatment, an unconscious young adult arriving in emergency, a 15-year-old at a pediatric clinic, and a 40-year-old in day surgery. In each, Jordan must apply national consent principles within the standards of her regulatory body.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Capacity to understand the decision, not age alone, decides whether she may consent): Under the mature minor principle, the ability to understand and appreciate the decision matters more than age, and this client shows that understanding. A fixed age of eighteen ignores capacity, and a fixed lower age skips assessment. A second signature is not required to make her consent valid. Provincial details vary, but capacity-based consent is the common principle."}, {"id": 248, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A 40-year-old woman in the day surgery unit has signed a consent form for a hernia repair. While changing into a gown, she tells Jordan she has changed her mind and feels pressured by her husband. What is the correct understanding about her consent?", "options": {"A": "Once the form is signed, only the surgeon can cancel it on her behalf", "B": "She may withdraw only before sedation is given, and must tell the surgeon first", "C": "Withdrawal is valid only if she signs a cancellation form before entering the operating room", "D": "She may withdraw consent at any time before the procedure and must be respected"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believes only the surgeon can withdraw it", "B": "Wrongly limiting when consent can be withdrawn", "C": "Requiring paperwork to withdraw consent"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C025", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Jordan floats between units during one shift at a Canadian hospital and meets five situations involving consent: an 82-year-old with advanced dementia and no advance directive, a 62-year-old oncology client considering treatment, an unconscious young adult arriving in emergency, a 15-year-old at a pediatric clinic, and a 40-year-old in day surgery. In each, Jordan must apply national consent principles within the standards of her regulatory body.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She may withdraw consent at any time before the procedure and must be respected): Consent is an ongoing process and can be withdrawn at any time before or during care, so Jordan respects her decision and informs the surgeon. Sedation timing does not limit her right. No cancellation form is required for her wishes to count. A signed form records an earlier agreement and does not bind her."}, {"id": 249, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a clinic, a practical nurse palpates a 36-year-old woman's red forearm rash before looking closely, and the client winces and says it burns. Small blisters are now visible and have spread under the nurse's fingers. What should the nurse have done differently?", "options": {"A": "Inspected the area first, noting colour, pattern, and lesions before touching it", "B": "Palpated more lightly with the pads of the fingers, then inspected the area afterward", "C": "Percussed the area first to detect fluid under the skin before light palpation", "D": "Washed the area with soap before assessing, to remove surface irritants and bacteria"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Keeps palpation first but lightens touch", "C": "Using percussion on a skin lesion", "D": "Altering the skin before assessment"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Inspected the area first, noting colour, pattern, and lesions before touching it): Inspection comes first because it shows colour, pattern and lesions without touching and can prevent pain or spreading blisters. Percussion does not assess a rash. Washing before assessing alters the lesions and removes useful findings. Palpation follows inspection, using a light touch."}, {"id": 250, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a care home, an 84-year-old woman with mild dementia is newly confused this morning, calling her daughter by her sister's name and refusing breakfast. She was oriented yesterday and has a temperature of 37.9 C. A care aide says it is just her dementia progressing. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Reorient her gently and recheck in the evening before deciding on a report", "B": "Treat the sudden change as possible illness and report it promptly", "C": "Agree that dementia is progressing and record it for the next care conference", "D": "Encourage fluids and ask the aide to watch her through the day"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays reporting by waiting to recheck", "C": "Attributes new confusion to dementia", "D": "Treats it casually without reporting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Treat the sudden change as possible illness and report it promptly): Sudden new confusion is a warning sign of delirium from infection, dehydration, or other illness, and it differs from the slow course of dementia, so the nurse reports promptly. Attributing it to dementia misses treatable causes, and waiting until evening delays assessment. Fluids and watching may help but do not replace reporting. A change from baseline should always be assessed."}, {"id": 251, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In the emergency department, a practical nurse is asked to help with a chest tube drainage system, a procedure her legislation allows nurses of her category to perform. She has never been taught it. The charge nurse says the law covers it, so she can proceed. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Decline all emergency tasks until she completes a certified course in the procedure", "B": "Recognize that legal scope is not personal competence, and seek orientation or supervision", "C": "Proceed slowly while reading the equipment manual beside the client's bed", "D": "Proceed, since legislation permits the activity and the charge nurse takes responsibility"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Overreacts by refusing unrelated tasks", "C": "Learns on the client from a manual", "D": "Equates legal scope with competence"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Recognize that legal scope is not personal competence, and seek orientation or supervision): Legislation shows what a nurse may do, but each nurse must also be competent in the specific skill, so she seeks orientation or supervision before performing the task. Relying on the law or the charge nurse's assurance leaves her accountable for unsafe practice. Reading a manual at the bedside is learning on the client. Declining every emergency task is excessive when she can safely do tasks within her competence."}, {"id": 252, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On the medical unit, a coworker of a client asks Amir whether the client's biopsy results showed cancer, saying he is worried and will keep it quiet. The client was discharged this morning. Amir wants to be kind. What is the best response, knowing privacy continues after discharge?", "options": {"A": "Share the result in general terms since the client has left care", "B": "Decline to share, explaining that the information stays confidential after discharge", "C": "Suggest the coworker check the discharge summary, which is already in the client's chart", "D": "Refer him to the unit manager, who may release results to employers"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming discharge ends confidentiality", "C": "Directs the coworker to the record", "D": "Believing managers can release results to employers"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C026", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Amir spends a week rotating through a hospital medical unit, a surgical unit, and a community clinic. Over that week he meets clients whose care raises ethical questions about privacy, fair access, and informed consent, including a hospital client whose coworker visits, a vulnerable adult at the clinic, and adults facing procedures. Amir uses the practical nurse code of ethics to guide his decisions.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline to share, explaining that the information stays confidential after discharge): Confidentiality continues after care ends, so Amir politely declines and explains that only the client can share the information. A general result is still personal health information. A manager cannot release results to a coworker or employer without consent. A promise of secrecy is not consent."}, {"id": 253, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At the community clinic, Amir sees a vulnerable adult who lives in shared housing, has limited transportation, and has missed two appointments. A colleague suggests treating all clients the same, with the same appointment rules, to be fair. How should Amir apply the value of promoting justice?", "options": {"A": "Apply the same appointment rules to every client, since identical treatment ensures fairness", "B": "Offer this client extra appointments ahead of waiting clients to make up for past barriers", "C": "Refer the client elsewhere after two missed appointments to keep the clinic schedule fair", "D": "Address barriers such as transport and advocate so this client receives equitable care"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confuses justice with identical treatment", "B": "Gives preference that disadvantages other clients", "C": "Removes the client from care because of barriers"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C026", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Amir spends a week rotating through a hospital medical unit, a surgical unit, and a community clinic. Over that week he meets clients whose care raises ethical questions about privacy, fair access, and informed consent, including a hospital client whose coworker visits, a vulnerable adult at the clinic, and adults facing procedures. Amir uses the practical nurse code of ethics to guide his decisions.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Address barriers such as transport and advocate so this client receives equitable care): Justice in nursing means fair and equitable care, which sometimes requires reducing barriers so that this client can reach the same level of care as others. Identical rules can leave vulnerable people behind, and jumping the queue treats other clients unfairly. Referring the client away because of missed appointments punishes the effects of disadvantage. Amir works to remove barriers rather than apply one standard to everyone."}, {"id": 254, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Before a minor procedure at the clinic, an adult client signs a form after a nurse hands it to her and leaves. She tells Amir she did not read it and does not know what the procedure involves. Amir sees the signature on the form. What is the best conclusion?", "options": {"A": "A signature alone is not valid consent, so the provider must explain and confirm understanding", "B": "Consent can be assumed because the procedure is minor and she attended voluntarily", "C": "Consent is valid because a signed form is legal evidence of agreement to treatment", "D": "Consent is valid if a second nurse co-signs after she confirms the signature"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating attendance as consent", "C": "Treats a signature as consent", "D": "Relying on a second signature to confirm understanding"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C026", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Amir spends a week rotating through a hospital medical unit, a surgical unit, and a community clinic. Over that week he meets clients whose care raises ethical questions about privacy, fair access, and informed consent, including a hospital client whose coworker visits, a vulnerable adult at the clinic, and adults facing procedures. Amir uses the practical nurse code of ethics to guide his decisions.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A signature alone is not valid consent, so the provider must explain and confirm understanding): Valid consent requires that the client is capable, informed, voluntary and consenting to the specific treatment, so a signature without understanding is not enough. Attendance does not show she knows what is planned. A co-signature confirms the signing, not her understanding. The provider must explain and confirm."}, {"id": 255, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On the medical unit, an adult client scheduled for a pacemaker tells Amir he does not really want it, but his sons insist and say they will stop visiting if he refuses. He says he will sign to keep the peace. What should Amir recognize about this consent?", "options": {"A": "It is valid once his sons also sign to confirm that they agree", "B": "It is not voluntary because family pressure shapes his decision, so Amir reports it", "C": "It is voluntary, because he agreed in writing and nobody threatened physical harm", "D": "It is valid, since his sons offered emotional support rather than a formal order"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adds family signatures as validation", "C": "Equating coercion with physical force", "D": "Treating family pressure as support"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C026", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Amir spends a week rotating through a hospital medical unit, a surgical unit, and a community clinic. Over that week he meets clients whose care raises ethical questions about privacy, fair access, and informed consent, including a hospital client whose coworker visits, a vulnerable adult at the clinic, and adults facing procedures. Amir uses the practical nurse code of ethics to guide his decisions.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is not voluntary because family pressure shapes his decision, so Amir reports it): Consent must be free of coercion, and threats to stop visiting are pressure that undermines voluntariness even without physical force. Signing to keep the peace is not a free choice. Family support he welcomes differs from pressure he resents. His sons' signatures do not make his consent valid, so Amir reports the concern."}, {"id": 256, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Before elective knee surgery, an adult client on the surgical unit tells Amir the surgeon explained how well recovery usually goes but not the risks of infection and clot formation or the alternative of physiotherapy. She has signed the form. What should Amir do?", "options": {"A": "Tell the surgeon that risks and alternatives were not disclosed, so consent is revisited", "B": "Proceed with preparation because the signed form shows that the information was provided", "C": "Ask the surgeon's office to mail written risk information after the surgery date", "D": "Have her sign a second form acknowledging that she received the discussion"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats the signed form as proof of disclosure", "C": "Delaying disclosure until after surgery", "D": "Using a form to cover incomplete disclosure"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C026", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Amir spends a week rotating through a hospital medical unit, a surgical unit, and a community clinic. Over that week he meets clients whose care raises ethical questions about privacy, fair access, and informed consent, including a hospital client whose coworker visits, a vulnerable adult at the clinic, and adults facing procedures. Amir uses the practical nurse code of ethics to guide his decisions.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tell the surgeon that risks and alternatives were not disclosed, so consent is revisited): Informed consent requires disclosure of material risks, benefits and alternatives before the procedure by the provider performing it, so Amir tells the surgeon the discussion was incomplete. Mailing information afterward is too late. A second form records that a discussion occurred but does not fix its gaps. Giving the risks himself exceeds his role."}, {"id": 257, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Dube asks whether he still needs the raised toilet seat now that his incision pain is well controlled with acetaminophen. He had a posterior approach hip arthroplasty yesterday and is eager to go home this week. Which response best reflects the nurse's teaching about his hip precautions?", "options": {"A": "The raised seat protects the new joint from dislocation until the surgeon lifts restrictions.", "B": "The seat is optional now, since well controlled pain would warn him about any unsafe position.", "C": "The seat is needed, but bending past ninety degrees to dress is acceptable if done slowly.", "D": "The seat is only needed in hospital, so he can use a standard toilet at home."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating pain relief as proof that the joint is safe to flex", "C": "Allowing hip flexion beyond the surgeon's limit during dressing", "D": "Assuming precautions apply only during the hospital stay"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C028", "cluster_text": "You are the practical nurse on an orthopedic unit this morning with four clients. Mr. Dube, 74, is on day 1 after a total hip arthroplasty. Ms. Tran, 62, is on day 1 after a knee arthroplasty. Mr. Okafor, 23, is on day 2 after a femur fracture. Mrs. Lindqvist, 80, is on day 3 after hip surgery. Each client needs your assessment, teaching and reporting during the morning.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The raised seat protects the new joint from dislocation until the surgeon lifts restrictions.): Mr. Dube is on day 1 after a posterior approach, so the joint is vulnerable and the raised seat keeps hip flexion within the surgeon's limit. Acetaminophen masks discomfort and does not show that the joint is stable, so calling the seat optional is unsafe. Precautions also continue at home, and flexing past ninety degrees to dress is the very movement that risks dislocation."}, {"id": 258, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Tran rates her pain 5 out of 10 after medication, and her knee is swollen and warm from surgery yesterday. She asks to stay in bed until the swelling goes down before trying to walk. Her vital signs are stable, and she has a walker at the bedside. What should the practical nurse tell her about mobility today?", "options": {"A": "She should rest the knee today and stand once pain falls below 2 out of 10.", "B": "Early assisted walking is part of the plan and supports circulation and recovery.", "C": "Walking can wait until the swelling resolves, so she should stay in bed until then.", "D": "Sitting in a chair is enough for today, and walking begins on the third day."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a pain score threshold as a rule for starting mobility", "C": "Delaying mobility until swelling resolves", "D": "Postponing walking longer than the usual early mobilization plan"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C028", "cluster_text": "You are the practical nurse on an orthopedic unit this morning with four clients. Mr. Dube, 74, is on day 1 after a total hip arthroplasty. Ms. Tran, 62, is on day 1 after a knee arthroplasty. Mr. Okafor, 23, is on day 2 after a femur fracture. Mrs. Lindqvist, 80, is on day 3 after hip surgery. Each client needs your assessment, teaching and reporting during the morning.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Early assisted walking is part of the plan and supports circulation and recovery.): After knee arthroplasty, early assisted walking is planned for day 1 because movement promotes circulation, lowers clot risk and restores joint range. Waiting for swelling to settle delays recovery, since some swelling is expected for days. Choosing a pain cutoff of 2 out of 10 is also wrong because pain is managed with analgesia so that she can move. Rule to remember: manage pain to allow movement, not the reverse."}, {"id": 259, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor, who is on day 2 after a femur fracture, is suddenly confused and restless. His respiratory rate is 28 breaths per minute, and the nurse sees small pinpoint spots on his chest. He received morphine two hours ago and says he is just anxious. What should the nurse suspect and do first?", "options": {"A": "Suspect fat embolism, then assess oxygen saturation, apply oxygen as ordered and report urgently.", "B": "Suspect opioid sedation, so hold further morphine doses and note the sedation score on his chart.", "C": "Suspect a wound infection, so collect a swab and review the dressing at the next change.", "D": "Suspect pain related anxiety, so reposition him and offer a calm explanation of the care."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Blaming confusion on the opioid despite the fast breathing and rash", "C": "Linking petechiae and confusion to infection instead of embolism", "D": "Treating a possible emergency as anxiety needing reassurance"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C028", "cluster_text": "You are the practical nurse on an orthopedic unit this morning with four clients. Mr. Dube, 74, is on day 1 after a total hip arthroplasty. Ms. Tran, 62, is on day 1 after a knee arthroplasty. Mr. Okafor, 23, is on day 2 after a femur fracture. Mrs. Lindqvist, 80, is on day 3 after hip surgery. Each client needs your assessment, teaching and reporting during the morning.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Suspect fat embolism, then assess oxygen saturation, apply oxygen as ordered and report urgently.): Confusion, rapid breathing and petechiae on day 2 after a long bone fracture point to fat embolism, so the nurse checks oxygenation, applies oxygen as ordered and reports at once. Opioid sedation would slow his breathing, not raise it to 28, and would not cause a petechial rash. Calling it anxiety delays treatment of a condition that can rapidly impair gas exchange. Rule to remember: new confusion with petechiae after a fracture is an emergency until proven otherwise."}, {"id": 260, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lindqvist has a painful, swollen left calf that feels warm and tight. She has walked with the physiotherapist each day, and her pain began this morning. She asks the practical nurse to massage the calf to ease the ache. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Avoid massaging the calf, keep her at rest and report the findings promptly to the RN.", "B": "Apply compression stockings tightly and recheck the calf at the next scheduled assessment.", "C": "Encourage a longer walk with the physiotherapist to improve circulation in the leg.", "D": "Massage the calf gently, then apply a warm compress to ease the ache."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delaying reporting while relying on stockings alone", "C": "Using exercise to treat a suspected clot", "D": "Massaging a possible clot, which could dislodge it"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C028", "cluster_text": "You are the practical nurse on an orthopedic unit this morning with four clients. Mr. Dube, 74, is on day 1 after a total hip arthroplasty. Ms. Tran, 62, is on day 1 after a knee arthroplasty. Mr. Okafor, 23, is on day 2 after a femur fracture. Mrs. Lindqvist, 80, is on day 3 after hip surgery. Each client needs your assessment, teaching and reporting during the morning.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Avoid massaging the calf, keep her at rest and report the findings promptly to the RN.): A painful, warm, swollen calf in a client who is recovering and walking with physiotherapy suggests deep vein thrombosis, so massage could dislodge the clot and travel to the lungs. Resting the leg and reporting promptly allows assessment and treatment. A longer walk adds muscle activity that may also move the clot. Stockings do not treat an existing clot, and waiting until the next assessment delays care."}, {"id": 261, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two hours after twisting his ankle, a 22-year-old soccer player comes to a sports clinic. The ankle is swollen and tender, with strong pulses and normal sensation. He plans to use an ice pack at home tonight and asks how long to keep it on. What teaching should the practical nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Apply a cloth wrapped ice pack for 5 minutes once daily during the first week.", "B": "Use a heating pad for 20 minutes first, since warmth speeds healing in sprains.", "C": "Apply a cloth-wrapped ice pack for about 20 minutes, then check the skin.", "D": "Place ice directly on the skin for an hour so the swelling settles quickly."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying cold too briefly to reduce swelling", "B": "Using heat in the first hours when it increases swelling", "D": "Leaving ice on for an hour without a barrier, risking cold injury"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Apply a cloth-wrapped ice pack for about 20 minutes, then check the skin.): Cold applied for about 20 minutes with a cloth barrier reduces swelling and pain while protecting the skin, and the skin is checked after removal. An hour of direct ice risks frostbite and nerve injury. Heat in the first 48 hours increases blood flow and swelling. Five minutes once daily is too brief to give a useful effect."}, {"id": 262, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a walk in clinic, an adult reports nausea and stomach upset that began after the second dose of a new oral antibiotic. There is no rash, wheeze, lip swelling or itching, and vital signs are normal. The client asks whether this means a drug allergy. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Nausea is expected with antibiotics, so the client can continue and no report is needed.", "B": "Nausea indicates a true allergy, so the antibiotic should be stopped and listed as an allergen.", "C": "Nausea means the dose is too strong, so the client should halve the next dose.", "D": "Nausea is a common intolerance, not an allergy, so document it and tell the prescriber."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Failing to document and report an adverse effect", "B": "Labelling a side effect as a true allergy", "C": "Adjusting a prescribed dose without authority"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Nausea is a common intolerance, not an allergy, so document it and tell the prescriber.): Nausea without rash, wheeze, swelling or itching is a typical gastrointestinal intolerance, not an immune reaction, so it is documented accurately and reported to the prescriber. Recording it as an allergy would remove a useful drug from future care. Halving a dose is outside the nurse's scope and can cause treatment failure. Skipping the report ignores a reaction the prescriber may manage, for example with food."}, {"id": 263, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a surgical unit, the practical nurse notes that an adult after surgery has produced only 120 mL of urine over the last 8 hours. The client is drowsy but arousable, and the shift is nearly over. Which statement best explains why the nurse should report this finding promptly rather than wait until morning?", "options": {"A": "Urine totals matter once the client is fully awake and drinking independently.", "B": "Reporting is the responsibility of the next shift once the totals are complete.", "C": "Low urine output can signal dehydration, bleeding or kidney injury, which worsen without early treatment.", "D": "Output is reported only if it falls below 100 mL over eight hours."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for recovery before treating low output as urgent", "B": "Passing a time sensitive finding to the next shift", "D": "Using a wrong threshold for low urine output"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Low urine output can signal dehydration, bleeding or kidney injury, which worsen without early treatment.): Urine output reflects kidney perfusion, so a low total after surgery can signal dehydration, bleeding or kidney injury that worsens without early treatment. Waiting until the client is awake delays recognition. A threshold of 100 mL over eight hours is too low, since 120 mL in eight hours is already well below the usual target. Handing the finding to the next shift delays care."}, {"id": 264, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Guideline advice and one client's risks conflict at a diabetes clinic. The 55-year-old client has frequent hypoglycemia, lives alone and has reduced vision, while the guideline suggests a general glycemic target. A colleague insists the target must be applied exactly as written. What is the best approach for the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Set a lower target independently, since the practical nurse knows the client best.", "B": "Use the guideline as a base and adapt targets with the team for this client.", "C": "Apply the guideline target exactly, because deviating from it exposes the clinic to liability.", "D": "Ignore the guideline for this client, because it was written for average populations."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing a treatment target without team authority", "C": "Treating guidelines as rigid rules that replace judgment", "D": "Discarding evidence instead of adapting it"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use the guideline as a base and adapt targets with the team for this client.): Guidelines summarize evidence for typical clients, and this client has risks that change the safest target. Using the guideline as a base and adapting the target with the team respects both evidence and judgment. Applying it exactly could increase hypoglycemia in a client living alone with poor vision. Setting a target alone exceeds the nurse's scope, and ignoring the guideline discards useful evidence."}, {"id": 265, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "While teaching a newcomer adult about diabetes foot care, the practical nurse sees the client nod and listen but look down and avoid eye contact for most of the session. A student nurse says the client seems dishonest and uninterested. What is the best interpretation of this behaviour?", "options": {"A": "Looking away suggests the client hides a language problem, so only written handouts should be used.", "B": "Avoiding eye contact shows low engagement, so the teaching should be repeated more slowly.", "C": "Eye contact norms vary by culture, so check understanding with teach-back.", "D": "The client is likely anxious, so the nurse should arrange a mental health referral today."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming a language barrier from eye contact alone", "B": "Reading a cultural norm as disinterest or dishonesty", "D": "Jumping to a mental health explanation for a cultural behaviour"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Eye contact norms vary by culture, so check understanding with teach-back.): In some cultures, looking down shows respect, so avoiding eye contact does not show dishonesty or disinterest. Asking the client to explain the teaching in their own words confirms understanding without assuming anything. Repeating more slowly or referring for anxiety rests on an unproven assumption. Written handouts alone do not confirm that the client understood."}, {"id": 266, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Swallowing is difficult for a long term care resident who takes an extended-release tablet once daily. The practical nurse finds the tablet is large and the resident coughs while trying to swallow it. The nurse considers crushing it into applesauce. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Crush the tablet finely and mix it with applesauce to ease swallowing.", "B": "Do not crush it; ask the pharmacist or prescriber for another form.", "C": "Dissolve it in juice and give the full amount within the hour.", "D": "Cut it in quarters and give each piece with thickened fluid."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Crushing a slow release tablet and releasing the full dose at once", "C": "Dissolving an extended release product", "D": "Splitting an extended release tablet"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Do not crush it; ask the pharmacist or prescriber for another form.): Extended release tablets are built to release the drug slowly, so crushing, splitting or dissolving them can deliver a full day's dose at once and risk toxicity. The pharmacist or prescriber can supply a liquid or other suitable form. Thickened fluid does not change the effect of cutting the tablet."}, {"id": 267, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In a nursing study group, a learner reads a study claiming a new wound product speeds healing. The sample was large, but participants were not randomly assigned and the manufacturer funded the study. A classmate argues that the large sample proves the claim. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Lack of random assignment is acceptable because large samples balance group differences.", "B": "Industry funding is acceptable if the authors disclose it, so accept the claim.", "C": "Sample size alone does not show quality; consider design, bias and funding too.", "D": "Random assignment matters only in drug trials, so the wound study design is adequate."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming a large sample removes selection bias", "B": "Treating disclosure as removing funding bias", "D": "Limiting randomization to drug trials"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sample size alone does not show quality; consider design, bias and funding too.): A large sample improves precision but does not correct bias from poor design, such as missing random assignment, or from industry funding. Disclosure of funding does not remove possible bias. Randomization protects comparisons in many study types, not only drug trials. Appraising design, bias and funding gives a fair view."}, {"id": 268, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Minutes after an injection at a community immunization clinic, a man in his 40s reports throat tightness and dizziness. The nurse hears wheeze and sees hives spreading across his chest, and his blood pressure is falling. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Have him sit upright with a fan on his face and recheck vitals in five minutes.", "B": "Give intramuscular epinephrine per protocol and call emergency services.", "C": "Observe him for ten minutes to see whether the symptoms pass on their own.", "D": "Give an oral antihistamine and recheck his blood pressure in fifteen minutes."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using comfort measures in place of epinephrine", "C": "Waiting to see if anaphylaxis resolves", "D": "Using a slow acting drug for a life threatening reaction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give intramuscular epinephrine per protocol and call emergency services.): Throat tightness, wheeze, hives and falling blood pressure after an injection indicate anaphylaxis, which requires intramuscular epinephrine immediately and a call for emergency help. Watching and waiting can allow airway closure. An oral antihistamine acts too slowly to reverse shock or airway swelling. Positioning and fanning do not treat the reaction."}, {"id": 269, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Right lower quadrant tenderness is the main complaint of an adult on a ward who reports abdominal discomfort. The practical nurse has inspected and auscultated the abdomen and is about to begin palpation. Which approach describes the correct palpation sequence for this client?", "options": {"A": "Begin with light palpation away from the tender area and examine it last.", "B": "Begin with deep palpation across the whole abdomen, then repeat with light touch.", "C": "Palpate quadrants in random order, pressing deeply on each one without a planned pattern.", "D": "Start in the right lower quadrant with deep palpation to locate the source of pain."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using deep pressure before light palpation", "C": "Using random order with deep pressure early", "D": "Starting palpation at the tender area, causing guarding"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Begin with light palpation away from the tender area and examine it last.): Palpating the tender area last prevents early guarding and pain that would limit the rest of the exam, and light palpation precedes deep. Starting at the painful quadrant makes the client tense, which hides true findings. Random order and early deep pressure reduce accuracy and comfort. Rule to remember: light before deep, painful area last."}, {"id": 270, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Aminata is at 30 weeks gestation and has a blood pressure of 150/95 mmHg, compared with 112/70 mmHg at her last visit. She reports a mild headache and asks whether she should wait for a reading above 180 systolic before she worries. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Recheck her pressure at the next visit in two weeks, since 150/95 is only borderline today.", "B": "Report it to the RN or physician now, since it meets hypertension criteria in pregnancy.", "C": "Reassure her that blood pressure rises normally late in pregnancy and give her a leaflet.", "D": "Advise her to rest at home and return if the reading passes 180 systolic."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying follow up of a hypertensive reading", "C": "Treating a new hypertensive reading as normal", "D": "Using a threshold far above diagnostic criteria"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C031", "cluster_text": "You are the practical nurse in a prenatal clinic that also staffs a telephone triage line. During your shift you care for four women: one at 30 weeks with a high blood pressure reading, one at 36 weeks with bleeding, one at 33 weeks after a fall, and one at 32 weeks calling about her baby's movements. Each woman needs your assessment and advice within your scope.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report it to the RN or physician now, since it meets hypertension criteria in pregnancy.): A reading of 150/95 mmHg with a headache at 30 weeks exceeds the 140/90 threshold for hypertension in pregnancy and may signal preeclampsia, so it needs prompt reporting. Waiting two weeks risks seizures or other complications. Reassuring her as normal ignores a rise from her baseline. Waiting for 180 systolic uses a threshold meant for severe hypertension."}, {"id": 271, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sudden painless bright red vaginal bleeding brings a woman at 36 weeks to the emergency department. Her abdomen is soft and the fetal heart rate is 140 beats per minute. A colleague offers to perform a digital vaginal exam to check dilation. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Perform the digital exam gently, since dilation helps decide how quickly to act.", "B": "Perform a speculum exam first and delay notifying the physician until results return.", "C": "Place a pad, monitor the bleeding and complete the exam once labour begins.", "D": "Avoid a vaginal exam, since previa is possible, and report to the physician."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Performing a digital exam that can provoke hemorrhage with previa", "B": "Delaying physician notification during active bleeding", "C": "Delaying assessment until labour starts"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C031", "cluster_text": "You are the practical nurse in a prenatal clinic that also staffs a telephone triage line. During your shift you care for four women: one at 30 weeks with a high blood pressure reading, one at 36 weeks with bleeding, one at 33 weeks after a fall, and one at 32 weeks calling about her baby's movements. Each woman needs your assessment and advice within your scope.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoid a vaginal exam, since previa is possible, and report to the physician.): Painless bright red bleeding in late pregnancy suggests placenta previa, and a digital exam can disturb the placenta and cause heavy hemorrhage. The nurse therefore avoids vaginal examination and reports immediately so ultrasound can confirm the cause. Waiting for labour leaves a bleeding client unassessed. A speculum exam by the nurse is outside scope and also delays notification."}, {"id": 272, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Nowak, 33 weeks pregnant, arrives in the emergency department after falling on ice. She has constant severe abdominal pain and a rigid uterus, with only a small amount of dark vaginal blood. Her pulse is 118 beats per minute. Which interpretation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Dark blood suggests a cervical cause, so a vaginal examination should be completed.", "B": "Abruptio placentae is possible, since blood can stay hidden behind the placenta.", "C": "The small blood loss suggests a minor event, so routine observation is sufficient.", "D": "A rigid uterus indicates normal contractions, so labour is likely beginning."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing a vaginal exam when the placenta is not yet located", "C": "Judging severity by visible blood loss alone", "D": "Mistaking a rigid uterus for ordinary contractions"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C031", "cluster_text": "You are the practical nurse in a prenatal clinic that also staffs a telephone triage line. During your shift you care for four women: one at 30 weeks with a high blood pressure reading, one at 36 weeks with bleeding, one at 33 weeks after a fall, and one at 32 weeks calling about her baby's movements. Each woman needs your assessment and advice within your scope.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Abruptio placentae is possible, since blood can stay hidden behind the placenta.): Constant severe pain, a rigid uterus, a rising pulse and a fall point to placental abruption, and bleeding may stay hidden behind the placenta, so little visible blood does not mean little loss. The tachycardia suggests significant blood loss. A rigid, constantly tense uterus differs from the relaxation between normal contractions. A vaginal exam is unsafe before placenta previa is excluded."}, {"id": 273, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the triage line, a woman at 32 weeks reports that her baby has moved much less than usual for about twelve hours. She feels well and has a prenatal appointment in six days. She asks if she can wait until then. What should the practical nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Monitor movements for the next 24 hours and report only if she feels pain.", "B": "Come in today for assessment; do not wait for the appointment.", "C": "Wait for the appointment in six days because movements vary in late pregnancy.", "D": "Drink cold juice, lie down and count movements; call again only if none occur."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for pain before acting on reduced movements", "C": "Dismissing reduced movements as normal variation", "D": "Relying on home measures after twelve hours of reduced movement"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C031", "cluster_text": "You are the practical nurse in a prenatal clinic that also staffs a telephone triage line. During your shift you care for four women: one at 30 weeks with a high blood pressure reading, one at 36 weeks with bleeding, one at 33 weeks after a fall, and one at 32 weeks calling about her baby's movements. Each woman needs your assessment and advice within your scope.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Come in today for assessment; do not wait for the appointment.): Reduced fetal movements for twelve hours can signal fetal compromise, so she needs same day assessment with monitoring. Juice and lying down may be tried briefly, but a twelve hour change is too long to trial at home. Waiting six days leaves a possible emergency unassessed. Pain is not a required sign of fetal distress."}, {"id": 274, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At an oncology day unit, a man in his 60s tells the practical nurse he is afraid his scan will show that the cancer has spread. His wife looks away and wipes her eyes. The nurse wants to support him and avoid blocking communication. Which response is best?", "options": {"A": "Many people in your position do very well, so there is little reason to worry.", "B": "Worrying will not change the scan, so try to think about something else.", "C": "Your wife seems upset too, so perhaps she should speak with the physician.", "D": "You sound frightened about the results; would you like to say more?"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offering a general statistic in place of listening", "B": "Dismissing his fear with a thought-stopping statement", "C": "Redirecting attention to the wife"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (You sound frightened about the results; would you like to say more?): Reflecting his fear and inviting him to say more opens communication and shows that his feelings matter. Telling him not to worry dismisses the feeling and blocks sharing. Shifting attention to his wife avoids his stated concern. Quoting how well others do or changing the subject also closes the conversation."}, {"id": 275, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Deciding whether to return to work after leave for depression, a woman in her 30s at a mental health clinic asks the practical nurse what the nurse would do in her place. The nurse wants to support the client's own decision making. Which response is most therapeutic?", "options": {"A": "I would wait another month, because returning too early could set you back.", "B": "Most people in your situation return to work, so you should try it.", "C": "I would go back, since a routine often helps people feel better sooner.", "D": "What are your concerns about returning now compared with waiting?"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Advising delay based on the nurse's own view", "B": "Using a majority example to steer the choice", "C": "Giving personal advice that replaces the client's decision"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (What are your concerns about returning now compared with waiting?): Asking her to explore benefits and concerns builds her problem solving and keeps the decision hers. Telling her what the nurse would do, in either direction, replaces her judgment with the nurse's values. Citing what most people do also pressures her toward one choice. Rule to remember: explore options instead of advising."}, {"id": 276, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Light serous drainage and a pink wound bed are seen on a shallow partial thickness wound on an adult's forearm. There are no signs of infection, and the person reports mild itching around the edges. The practical nurse is choosing a dressing. Which choice best supports healing?", "options": {"A": "A wet to dry dressing, which cleans the wound by pulling tissue away.", "B": "An alginate dressing, which absorbs heavy drainage and keeps the bed moist.", "C": "A hydrocolloid dressing, which keeps the wound moist and suits light drainage.", "D": "Dry gauze, which absorbs drainage and keeps the wound surface dry and protected."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing a wet to dry dressing that removes healthy tissue", "B": "Using an alginate for a wound with only light drainage", "D": "Using dry gauze, which sticks to and disrupts new tissue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A hydrocolloid dressing, which keeps the wound moist and suits light drainage.): A moist environment speeds healing, and a hydrocolloid holds moisture and manages light to moderate drainage on a shallow wound. Dry gauze sticks to the bed and damages new tissue when removed. A wet to dry dressing removes healthy cells along with debris. Alginate is designed for heavy exudate and can dry out a lightly draining wound."}, {"id": 277, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Day 2 after bowel surgery finds an adult reporting mild abdominal distension. The practical nurse has inspected the abdomen and will now continue the assessment. The client has a nasogastric tube and an intravenous infusion running. In what order should the nurse proceed?", "options": {"A": "Palpate lightly next, then auscultate, since sounds are not affected by touch.", "B": "Auscultate bowel sounds next, then percuss and palpate, because touching can alter bowel activity.", "C": "Palpate the incision first, then auscultate, since the surgeon needs wound findings.", "D": "Percuss first, then palpate deeply, and skip auscultation after bowel surgery."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Palpating before listening, which can change bowel sounds", "C": "Reordering the exam around the incision", "D": "Omitting auscultation after bowel surgery"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Auscultate bowel sounds next, then percuss and palpate, because touching can alter bowel activity.): For the abdomen the order is inspection, auscultation, percussion and then palpation, because handling the abdomen can change bowel sounds. Palpating first would make the findings unreliable. Skipping auscultation after bowel surgery loses key information about returning bowel function. Starting at the incision alters the sequence without benefit."}, {"id": 278, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Preparing an intermittent tube feeding for an adult in acute care, the practical nurse sees that the nasogastric tube is taped with the external mark unchanged. The nurse listened over the stomach while injecting air and heard a whoosh. What is the best method to confirm placement before feeding?", "options": {"A": "Accept the whoosh as confirmation, since the mark has not moved from the nose.", "B": "Confirm placement by aspirate pH or radiograph, not air auscultation.", "C": "Flush with 30 mL of water and proceed if the client has no coughing.", "D": "Ask the client to hum and proceed if the voice sounds normal."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on air auscultation, an unreliable method", "C": "Using a water flush as a test of placement", "D": "Using voice quality as a sign of position"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Confirm placement by aspirate pH or radiograph, not air auscultation.): Listening for injected air cannot tell a stomach tube from a lung tube, so placement is confirmed by the agency approved method such as aspirate pH or radiograph. An unchanged mark shows the tube has not moved but not that it was ever correctly placed. A water flush with no coughing can still enter the lung quietly. Humming does not rule out a misplaced tube."}, {"id": 279, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a step down unit, an adult is receiving continuous enteral nutrition through a nasogastric tube. After bathing the client, the practical nurse lowers the bed flat to tidy the linen and plans to raise it again later in the shift. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Raise the head only during the first hour of the feeding each shift.", "B": "Lower the bed briefly during care, since short periods flat are low risk.", "C": "Place the client flat on the left side, which prevents reflux well.", "D": "Keep the head of bed raised at least 30 to 45 degrees."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Raising the head for only part of the feeding", "B": "Treating short flat periods as safe during feeding", "C": "Using a flat side position that still allows reflux"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep the head of bed raised at least 30 to 45 degrees.): Continuous feeding raises the volume in the stomach, so keeping the head of bed at 30 to 45 degrees reduces reflux and aspiration. Even short flat periods allow formula to move toward the airway. Elevating only for the first hour leaves the client exposed for the rest of the feeding. A flat left side position still permits reflux."}, {"id": 280, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Due for an oral tablet, a client on a rehabilitation unit after a stroke has a swallowing precaution. The client is lying flat and says water is hard to drink, and the practical nurse has a large glass of water ready. What is the safest way to give the tablet?", "options": {"A": "Keep the client flat to rest and give the tablet with the full glass of water.", "B": "Tilt the chin up slightly and give the tablet with a spoonful of water.", "C": "Sit the client upright, follow the swallowing plan and give small sips.", "D": "Place the tablet under the tongue and give a sip after it dissolves."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving oral medication while lying flat, risking aspiration", "B": "Extending the neck during swallowing", "D": "Changing the route without an order"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sit the client upright, follow the swallowing plan and give small sips.): A client with a swallowing precaution needs an upright position and the textures and sips set by the swallowing plan to lower aspiration risk. Tilting the chin up opens the airway and raises aspiration risk. Changing the route to under the tongue needs an order and does not suit every tablet. Lying flat or large drinks overwhelm a weak swallow."}, {"id": 281, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Minimal drainage and a clean wound bed mark an adult's stage 2 pressure injury on the sacrum. A hydrocolloid dressing was applied two days ago, and its edges are intact with a small gel bubble beneath. The practical nurse is planning how often to change it. What is best?", "options": {"A": "Peel it back each morning to check for gel and reapply the same dressing.", "B": "Remove it daily and replace it with dry gauze to let the skin breathe.", "C": "Leave it in place for three to seven days unless it leaks.", "D": "Change it every shift to inspect the wound bed and clean it with antiseptic."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Lifting and reapplying a dressing that loses its seal", "B": "Replacing a moist dressing with dry gauze", "D": "Changing a hydrocolloid too often and disturbing healing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Leave it in place for three to seven days unless it leaks.): Hydrocolloids are designed to stay in place for several days, forming a gel that keeps the wound moist, so they are changed every three to seven days or when they leak or lift. Frequent changes disturb new tissue, and antiseptic cleaning harms it. Dry gauze removes moisture. Reapplying a lifted dressing breaks the seal and invites contamination."}, {"id": 282, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the post operative unit, an adult has voided only 40 mL twice since returning from surgery eight hours ago. The lower abdomen feels full and the client reports urgency. The practical nurse is considering encouraging oral fluids. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Encourage extra oral fluids first, assuming the low output reflects dehydration from surgery.", "B": "Apply a warm pack to the abdomen and wait another four hours.", "C": "Assess the lower abdomen and use a bladder scanner as ordered, then report.", "D": "Insert a catheter right away without an order and notify the nurse afterward."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adding fluids to a possibly full bladder before assessing", "B": "Delaying assessment while using a comfort measure", "D": "Catheterizing without an order or assessment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess the lower abdomen and use a bladder scanner as ordered, then report.): Small frequent voids with a full lower abdomen and urgency suggest urinary retention with overflow, so the nurse assesses the bladder and scans it as ordered before acting. Extra fluids would worsen a full bladder. A warm pack and a four hour wait delay care. Catheterizing without an order exceeds scope."}, {"id": 283, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After hip fracture repair, a 79-year-old on the surgical unit has become briefly confused at night. The care team wants to lower the risk of delirium. The client's son asks the practical nurse about restraints to keep his father safe. Which non drug measure is best?", "options": {"A": "Limit visitors and mobilization until the confusion clears.", "B": "Promote orientation with glasses, hearing aids, a clock, regular sleep routines and family presence.", "C": "Keep the room dim and avoid conversation so the client can rest.", "D": "Apply soft wrist restraints at night to prevent pulling at lines and falls."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Restricting mobility and family, which removes orienting supports", "C": "Reducing stimulation and contact, which worsens disorientation", "D": "Using restraints, which increase agitation and delirium"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C030", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a gerontology float assignment today. You care for a client on a surgical unit, two residents in long term care and a client receiving home care. Each person has a different need related to confusion, memory loss or communication, and you must respond within your scope and the care plan. Please follow the care plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Promote orientation with glasses, hearing aids, a clock, regular sleep routines and family presence.): Delirium risk falls with orientation aids, sensory devices, normal sleep routines, early mobility and familiar faces. Restraints raise agitation, injury and delirium and are a last resort. Silence and dim light reduce orienting cues. Limiting visitors and mobilization removes two proven supports."}, {"id": 284, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Every evening around 1600, an 86-year-old resident with Alzheimer disease becomes agitated and tries to leave the unit, saying she must collect her children from school. Staff correct her, but she stays distressed for an hour. The practical nurse wants to reduce this sundowning behaviour. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Remind her firmly that her children are adults and school ended years ago.", "B": "Request a standing sedative each evening to prevent the agitation.", "C": "Keep her in her room with the door closed until she settles.", "D": "Offer a calm activity, a snack and a predictable routine."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Arguing with and correcting the confusion", "B": "Using sedation in place of non drug measures", "C": "Isolating the resident, which increases distress"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C030", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a gerontology float assignment today. You care for a client on a surgical unit, two residents in long term care and a client receiving home care. Each person has a different need related to confusion, memory loss or communication, and you must respond within your scope and the care plan. Please follow the care plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Offer a calm activity, a snack and a predictable routine.): Sundowning eases with structure, calm activity, light, food and a quiet environment, so a walk, snack and predictable routine address her unmet needs. Correcting her firmly causes distress because she cannot retain the information. Closing her in her room isolates her and may raise anxiety. Sedation has risks and is not first line."}, {"id": 285, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Repeatedly, an 83-year-old woman in long term care asks the practical nurse when her husband will visit. He died four years ago, and each time she is told this she grieves as if hearing it for the first time. She appears anxious today. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Tell her directly that he has died, since honesty matters most in dementia care.", "B": "Ignore the question and start her routine care without comment.", "C": "Say he is running late and will arrive after supper.", "D": "Acknowledge her feelings, ask about her husband, and gently redirect her."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Repeating a painful truth that she cannot retain", "B": "Dismissing the emotion behind the question", "C": "Telling a false story that may later be exposed"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C030", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a gerontology float assignment today. You care for a client on a surgical unit, two residents in long term care and a client receiving home care. Each person has a different need related to confusion, memory loss or communication, and you must respond within your scope and the care plan. Please follow the care plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acknowledge her feelings, ask about her husband, and gently redirect her.): Her question reflects a need for comfort and connection, so acknowledging feelings, inviting memories and redirecting respond to that need without causing new grief. Repeated blunt reorientation re-traumatizes her each time. A made up story risks distrust and does not address her emotion. Ignoring her leaves the anxiety untreated."}, {"id": 286, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "While being helped to dress at home with her daughter watching, an 80-year-old with moderate dementia becomes upset when given several directions together and begins to refuse. The practical nurse wants her to cooperate and keep her dignity. Which communication strategy is most helpful?", "options": {"A": "Explain the whole sequence once, then leave her to complete it alone.", "B": "Hand her clothes in order and correct each mistake as she dresses.", "C": "Give one simple step at a time in a calm tone.", "D": "Offer all items at once and ask her to choose the order."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving multi step instructions that overload memory", "B": "Correcting errors in a person with dementia", "D": "Giving too many choices at once"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C030", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a gerontology float assignment today. You care for a client on a surgical unit, two residents in long term care and a client receiving home care. Each person has a different need related to confusion, memory loss or communication, and you must respond within your scope and the care plan. Please follow the care plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give one simple step at a time in a calm tone.): Moderate dementia limits working memory, so single step directions given calmly with time to answer match what she can process. Correcting each mistake raises frustration and threatens dignity. Offering everything at once overloads her and demands reasoning she may lack. Loud, fast speech increases anxiety."}, {"id": 287, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Frustration shows in a 74-year-old man after stroke who understands everything said but cannot find the words to answer. He bangs the bed rail when the nurse waits for a reply. The practical nurse wants to improve communication with him. Which strategy is best?", "options": {"A": "Ask yes or no questions, allow extra time, and offer a picture board.", "B": "Finish his sentences for him to reduce his frustration.", "C": "Keep questions open ended so he practises forming full sentences.", "D": "Speak for him with his wife and avoid asking him questions."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Completing sentences, which removes his chance to speak", "C": "Using open questions with expressive aphasia", "D": "Excluding the client from conversation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C034", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a stroke rehabilitation unit. Four clients recovering from stroke need your care this morning. Two have communication problems, one has trouble swallowing and one needs help with safe positioning at mealtime. All of your actions must follow the plan set by the interprofessional team and your scope of practice. Each decision should protect safety and dignity.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask yes or no questions, allow extra time, and offer a picture board.): He has expressive aphasia, so comprehension is intact and the difficulty is producing words. Yes or no questions, extra time and picture tools let him communicate. Open ended questions demand word finding he cannot yet manage. Speaking for him with his wife takes away his role in decisions. Finishing sentences also removes his voice."}, {"id": 288, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Her speech is fluent, yet a 70-year-old woman after stroke cannot follow spoken instructions, and her answers do not match the questions. Her family fears dementia has developed. The practical nurse is planning communication with her. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Explain procedures in full detail so she can choose the right response.", "B": "Use short simple sentences with gestures and demonstration, and report findings for speech language assessment.", "C": "Treat the problem as dementia and use reality orientation at each visit.", "D": "Wait until she answers appropriately before giving any instruction."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Overloading her comprehension with long explanations", "C": "Assuming dementia instead of receptive aphasia", "D": "Withholding instructions until she responds correctly"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C034", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a stroke rehabilitation unit. Four clients recovering from stroke need your care this morning. Two have communication problems, one has trouble swallowing and one needs help with safe positioning at mealtime. All of your actions must follow the plan set by the interprofessional team and your scope of practice. Each decision should protect safety and dignity.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use short simple sentences with gestures and demonstration, and report findings for speech language assessment.): Fluent speech with poor comprehension suggests receptive aphasia from the stroke, so simple sentences with gestures and demonstration support understanding, and reporting allows a speech language assessment. Long explanations exceed what she can process. Labelling the problem dementia leads to the wrong care plan. Waiting for correct answers leaves her without guidance."}, {"id": 289, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Lunch is interrupted when a 68-year-old man after stroke coughs and has a wet voice after sipping water. His oxygen saturation is stable and he is hungry. The practical nurse is considering a trial of thin fluids to test his swallow further. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Offer a trial of thin fluids again in smaller sips to retest.", "B": "Feed pureed food while upright since coughing occurred only with water.", "C": "Switch to thickened fluids on his own and document the change.", "D": "Stop oral intake, keep him upright and report for swallowing assessment."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Repeating thin fluid trials after signs of aspiration", "B": "Continuing oral food after a failed fluid screen", "C": "Changing the diet texture without an order or assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C034", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a stroke rehabilitation unit. Four clients recovering from stroke need your care this morning. Two have communication problems, one has trouble swallowing and one needs help with safe positioning at mealtime. All of your actions must follow the plan set by the interprofessional team and your scope of practice. Each decision should protect safety and dignity.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop oral intake, keep him upright and report for swallowing assessment.): Coughing and a wet voice after water are signs of aspiration risk, so oral intake stops and a formal swallowing assessment is requested. Repeating thin fluids exposes him again to aspiration. Choosing thickened fluids is a diet change that needs an assessment and an order. Giving pureed food after a failed screen continues the risk."}, {"id": 290, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Cleared for a modified diet after stroke, a 75-year-old woman is eating lunch in bed. She is reclined at about 20 degrees and has left sided weakness. The practical nurse notices that she is coughing as she eats. What is the best positioning action?", "options": {"A": "Sit her upright with chin slightly tucked and cue slow eating.", "B": "Raise the bed to forty degrees and encourage larger bites for energy.", "C": "Keep her reclined for comfort and feed her small amounts quickly.", "D": "Pause the meal and give thickened fluids by syringe to clear her airway."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Partial elevation with larger bites", "C": "Feeding in a reclined position", "D": "Using fluids by syringe to treat coughing"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C034", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a stroke rehabilitation unit. Four clients recovering from stroke need your care this morning. Two have communication problems, one has trouble swallowing and one needs help with safe positioning at mealtime. All of your actions must follow the plan set by the interprofessional team and your scope of practice. Each decision should protect safety and dignity.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sit her upright with chin slightly tucked and cue slow eating.): An upright position with the chin slightly tucked closes the airway during swallowing and lowers aspiration risk, and slow eating helps coordination. Forty degrees is still too reclined and larger bites raise the risk. Syringe fluids do not clear the airway and may worsen aspiration. Tilting the head back also invites aspiration."}, {"id": 291, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Fever of 38.9 C and chills affect a 72-year-old on a medical unit. The physician orders blood cultures and intravenous antibiotics. The practical nurse is preparing to collect the samples and the antibiotic is already at the bedside. What is the correct timing and technique?", "options": {"A": "Collect both bottles from one site to reduce the number of punctures.", "B": "Draw cultures from an existing peripheral line and use clean technique.", "C": "Collect from two sites before the first antibiotic dose, using asepsis.", "D": "Give the first antibiotic dose, then collect the cultures within the hour."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using one site, which cannot separate contamination from infection", "B": "Drawing from an existing line, raising contamination risk", "D": "Giving the antibiotic first, which can sterilize the culture"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Collect from two sites before the first antibiotic dose, using asepsis.): Cultures are drawn before antibiotics so that the drug does not suppress growth, and two separate sites help distinguish true bacteremia from skin contamination. Giving the antibiotic first can produce a false negative. A single site cannot identify contamination. Drawing from an existing line raises the contamination rate."}, {"id": 292, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Nil by mouth after midnight applies to an adult on a surgical ward awaiting a morning procedure. The practical nurse notes a scheduled oral cardiac medication due at 0600, and the client asks whether to skip all tablets. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Give the usual tablets with a full glass of water, since they are essential.", "B": "Hold all oral medications until after the procedure and chart the omission.", "C": "Give the cardiac tablet but hold the others based on the nurse's own judgment.", "D": "Clarify essential drugs with the prescriber and give approved ones with sips."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignoring the nil by mouth order for routine medication", "B": "Withholding essential medication without asking", "C": "Deciding which drugs to hold without authority"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Clarify essential drugs with the prescriber and give approved ones with sips.): Some medications, such as cardiac drugs, are often continued with a sip of water, but the prescriber or anesthetist decides, so the nurse clarifies before acting. A full glass of water breaks the nil by mouth order. Holding everything may omit an essential drug. Selecting which drugs to hold without authority exceeds the nurse's scope."}, {"id": 293, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Several medications are due together for an adult with a gastrostomy tube in long term care. The practical nurse has crushed the immediate release tablets and plans to draw them all into one syringe to save time. What is the correct technique?", "options": {"A": "Mix all the crushed medications together, give them at once and flush at the end.", "B": "Give each medication separately and flush with water before, between and after.", "C": "Give each separately and flush only if the tube feels resistant.", "D": "Give each separately with flushes only before the first and after the last."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Mixing medications, which risks incompatibility and clogging", "C": "Flushing only after resistance appears, when a clog may already exist", "D": "Skipping flushes between drugs, which risks interactions and blockage"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give each medication separately and flush with water before, between and after.): Medications are given one at a time with a water flush between each to prevent interactions and tube blockage. Combining crushed drugs can form precipitates or reduce effect. Flushing only at the ends lets drugs mix inside the tube. Waiting for resistance means a clog may already be forming."}, {"id": 294, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Leaving the room of a client on droplet and contact precautions, the practical nurse is still inside near the door, wearing gloves, gown, mask and eye protection, and holds a soiled linen bag. Which sequence is best for removing the protective equipment before exit?", "options": {"A": "Remove eye protection and mask first, then gown and gloves in the hallway.", "B": "Remove the mask first, then gloves, gown and eye protection before cleaning hands.", "C": "Remove the gown, then gloves, then clean hands only after removing the mask.", "D": "Remove gloves and gown, clean hands, then remove eye protection and mask outside."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Taking off face protection before dirty items in the hallway", "B": "Removing the mask while still contaminated in the room", "C": "Delaying hand hygiene until the last step"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Remove gloves and gown, clean hands, then remove eye protection and mask outside.): The most contaminated items, gloves and gown, come off first, followed by hand hygiene, and then eye protection and mask are removed while touching only clean straps. Removing the mask first exposes the face to droplets in the room. Taking off dirty items in the hallway spreads contamination. Delaying hand hygiene lets contaminated hands touch the face."}, {"id": 295, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Nothing will improve and life feels pointless, a 17-year-old tells the practical nurse in a primary care clinic. The nurse worries about suicide but fears that asking directly might plant the idea. The teen is quiet and avoids eye contact. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Ask directly about suicidal thoughts in a calm, caring way and report the answer.", "B": "Ask indirect questions about mood to avoid putting the idea into the teen's mind.", "C": "Provide a hotline number and ask the teen to return if feelings worsen.", "D": "Wait for the teen to bring up suicide so that the conversation stays their own."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Avoiding the word suicide for fear of planting the idea", "C": "Providing a number without assessing risk", "D": "Waiting for the client to raise the topic"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C027", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse in a community mental health and crisis program. Over one day you meet four people at risk for suicide in different settings: a teen in primary care, a man in the community, a young adult in the emergency department and a client on an inpatient unit. Your actions follow current standards of safe care. Each person needs a response that is direct and caring.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask directly about suicidal thoughts in a calm, caring way and report the answer.): Asking directly does not plant the idea and gives the teen permission to talk, and the answer is reported so the team can assess risk. Indirect questions leave the real risk unclear. A hotline number alone is not an assessment. Waiting leaves a hopeless teen to decide alone whether to speak."}, {"id": 296, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two months after losing his job, a 55-year-old man who seemed hopeless and withdrawn is suddenly calm and cheerful. He has given away a few belongings and thanked his family for everything. His wife tells the practical nurse. How should the nurse interpret this?", "options": {"A": "Giving things away is typical of tidying up, so continue monitoring his sleep.", "B": "He is likely relieved after accepting the job loss, so offer reassurance.", "C": "His improved mood shows treatment is working, so routine follow up is enough.", "D": "A sudden calm after distress can signal a plan to die, so assess and report."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating giving away belongings as ordinary behaviour", "B": "Offering reassurance instead of assessing risk", "C": "Reading a sudden calm as recovery"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C027", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse in a community mental health and crisis program. Over one day you meet four people at risk for suicide in different settings: a teen in primary care, a man in the community, a young adult in the emergency department and a client on an inpatient unit. Your actions follow current standards of safe care. Each person needs a response that is direct and caring.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A sudden calm after distress can signal a plan to die, so assess and report.): A sudden calm after despair, giving away belongings and thanking family are warning signs that he may have decided to end his life, so the nurse assesses risk directly and reports at once. Seeing the calm as improvement misses the danger. Reassurance leaves risk unassessed. Monitoring sleep alone does not address suicide risk."}, {"id": 297, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Medically stable after an intentional overdose, a 22-year-old in the emergency department is offered a no suicide contract by a colleague before discharge. She says she feels better, wants to go home and has no plan to try again. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Help her write a safety plan with warning signs, coping steps and contacts.", "B": "Rely on her family to watch her and skip the written plan.", "C": "Have her sign a no suicide contract, since a signature creates commitment to safety.", "D": "Discharge her with a crisis number once she says she feels better."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relying on family supervision in place of a plan", "C": "Using a no suicide contract as a safeguard", "D": "Accepting a statement of feeling better as evidence of safety"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C027", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse in a community mental health and crisis program. Over one day you meet four people at risk for suicide in different settings: a teen in primary care, a man in the community, a young adult in the emergency department and a client on an inpatient unit. Your actions follow current standards of safe care. Each person needs a response that is direct and caring.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Help her write a safety plan with warning signs, coping steps and contacts.): A collaborative safety plan listing warning signs, coping steps, supports and removal of means is evidence based, whereas no suicide contracts do not prevent suicide. Feeling better soon after an attempt does not show that risk has passed. A crisis number alone is not a plan. Family supervision cannot be constant and does not replace her own coping strategies."}, {"id": 298, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "He is calm and cooperative, yet a 38-year-old inpatient states that he plans to hang himself tonight and has identified how. The charge nurse suggests checks every 30 minutes to respect his privacy. What level of observation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Routine checks with a verbal agreement to tell staff if thoughts worsen.", "B": "Checks every 30 minutes, with a room closer to the nursing station.", "C": "Constant one to one observation within arm's reach, according to the unit's suicide protocol.", "D": "Checks every 15 minutes documented on a flow sheet, since he is cooperative."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on a verbal agreement in place of supervision", "B": "Using a closer room as a substitute for observation", "D": "Using intermittent checks despite a specific plan and intent"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C027", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse in a community mental health and crisis program. Over one day you meet four people at risk for suicide in different settings: a teen in primary care, a man in the community, a young adult in the emergency department and a client on an inpatient unit. Your actions follow current standards of safe care. Each person needs a response that is direct and caring.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Constant one to one observation within arm's reach, according to the unit's suicide protocol.): A specific plan with intent signals high risk, so constant observation within arm's reach is required to prevent harm. A calm, cooperative manner does not lower risk and may hide intent. Fifteen or thirty minute checks leave gaps long enough for an attempt. A room location or verbal agreement does not provide supervision."}, {"id": 299, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Moderate to heavy exudate and a moist red wound bed are noted in a deep pressure wound on an adult's heel. The wound has no necrotic tissue, and the client reports mild discomfort. The practical nurse is selecting a primary dressing. Which choice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "An alginate dressing, which absorbs heavy exudate and forms a gel.", "B": "A hydrogel sheet, because it adds moisture to the wound bed and softens tissue.", "C": "Dry gauze packed tightly, because it absorbs drainage and costs less.", "D": "A transparent film, because it keeps the wound clean and visible."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Adding moisture to an already wet wound", "C": "Packing a deep wound with dry gauze that adheres to tissue", "D": "Using film, which cannot absorb heavy exudate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An alginate dressing, which absorbs heavy exudate and forms a gel.): Alginates absorb many times their weight in fluid and turn to a gel that keeps a heavily draining wound moist without macerating the skin. Transparent film has no absorbing ability. Hydrogel adds moisture and suits dry wounds. Tightly packed dry gauze sticks to tissue and injures it."}, {"id": 300, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Pain after a laparotomy keeps a 60-year-old client on day 1 from coughing, and breathing is shallow. The practical nurse plans deep breathing and coughing exercises. The client asks whether coughing can be skipped to avoid pain. What should the nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Cough hard without support to clear secretions quickly and finish sooner.", "B": "Breathe deeply but skip coughing until pain medication is requested by the client.", "C": "Splint the incision with a pillow while coughing, after pain medication.", "D": "Cough hard once each shift and use the incentive spirometer more often."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Coughing without support, which stresses the incision", "B": "Delaying coughing until the client asks for analgesia", "D": "Coughing too infrequently to clear secretions"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Splint the incision with a pillow while coughing, after pain medication.): Splinting with a pillow and giving analgesia first lets him cough and breathe deeply, which prevents atelectasis and pneumonia after abdominal surgery. Waiting until he requests medication delays effective coughing. Coughing once a shift is too infrequent, and spirometer use does not replace coughing. Coughing hard without support strains the incision."}, {"id": 301, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Persistent vomiting for two weeks brings a woman at 9 weeks gestation to a community clinic. She has lost 4 kg, her urine ketones are positive and she feels dizzy on standing. A coworker says vomiting is normal in early pregnancy. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Report weight loss, ketones and dizziness to the physician or NP promptly for assessment.", "B": "Recommend an over the counter antiemetic and extra fluids at home.", "C": "Reassure her that early pregnancy vomiting is normal and suggest small dry snacks.", "D": "Advise her to rest and recheck her weight at the next routine visit."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Recommending medication without prescriber involvement", "C": "Treating hyperemesis gravidarum as normal nausea", "D": "Delaying reassessment despite weight loss and ketosis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report weight loss, ketones and dizziness to the physician or NP promptly for assessment.): Weight loss, ketones and dizziness on standing suggest hyperemesis gravidarum with dehydration, which differs from normal morning sickness and needs prompt assessment. Reassurance would miss a condition that may need intravenous fluids. Waiting for a routine visit risks worsening dehydration. Recommending an antiemetic is outside nursing scope and does not treat dehydration."}, {"id": 302, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Cramping and low back pressure that began this morning bring a woman at 31 weeks to the prenatal clinic. She believes these are Braxton Hicks contractions and wants to go home. The practical nurse has not yet timed the cramps. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Accept her interpretation, since Braxton Hicks contractions are common at this stage.", "B": "Advise her to drink water and rest, and call back if pain worsens.", "C": "Time the contractions, assess for other signs and report before she leaves.", "D": "Perform a vaginal examination to check cervical dilation before notifying anyone."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismissing contractions without timing them", "B": "Relying on home measures despite possible preterm labour", "D": "Performing a vaginal exam outside nursing scope"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Time the contractions, assess for other signs and report before she leaves.): Cramping with low back pressure at 31 weeks may be preterm labour, so the nurse times the contractions, checks for fluid leakage, bleeding and fetal movement, and reports before she leaves. Accepting her self diagnosis could miss preterm labour. Water and rest are tried only after assessment. A vaginal exam by the nurse is outside scope and delays reporting."}, {"id": 303, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "New bilateral ankle swelling and a 2.5 kg weight gain over four days concern a 72-year-old receiving home care. Her neck veins look distended and her abdomen feels full. Her daughter believes the swelling is from sitting too much. What should the practical nurse conclude?", "options": {"A": "These findings suggest right sided heart failure, so report them to the RN.", "B": "Fluid retention from salty meals is likely, so advise reducing sodium intake.", "C": "The swelling is due to reduced mobility, so encourage leg elevation and walking.", "D": "Distended neck veins suggest dehydration, so encourage more oral fluids today."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Attributing heart failure signs to diet alone", "C": "Attributing fluid retention to immobility", "D": "Misreading venous distension as dehydration"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (These findings suggest right sided heart failure, so report them to the RN.): Bilateral ankle edema, rapid weight gain, neck vein distension and abdominal fullness together indicate fluid backing up from a failing right heart, so they are reported promptly. Diet advice alone delays assessment. Distended neck veins signal fluid overload, not dehydration, and more fluids could worsen it. Reduced mobility would not explain these findings."}, {"id": 304, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In a rural clinic, a nurse is shown a new procedure by a physician and asked to begin performing it independently the next day after one demonstration. The nurse believes it may be within scope but has not done it before. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Watch one more demonstration, then begin, since a physician's order makes the skill acceptable.", "B": "Perform it after the demonstration, since the physician has authorized it verbally.", "C": "Confirm it is within scope, then complete education, supervised practice and policy steps first.", "D": "Perform it only when no physician is available, and document the urgent circumstances and outcome."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating observation and an order as enough preparation", "B": "Relying on a verbal authorization instead of competence and policy", "D": "Using the procedure only when unsupervised"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Confirm it is within scope, then complete education, supervised practice and policy steps first.): Taking on a new skill requires that it is within scope, that the nurse has education and supervised practice to be competent, and that employer policy supports it. A single demonstration does not provide competence, and a verbal request does not replace policy. Performing it only when no physician is present increases risk. Watching again and relying on an order still leaves out practice."}, {"id": 305, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After twenty years of practice without incident, an experienced practical nurse declines optional education, saying competence stays the same after licensure. The manager asks about the annual learning plan for the unit. Which statement best reflects professional standards for the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Ongoing self assessment and learning are professional duties because standards and knowledge change.", "B": "Experience maintains competence, so a learning plan is needed only after an error.", "C": "Competence is maintained by employer evaluations alone, so personal learning plans are optional.", "D": "Mandatory continuing education set by the regulator replaces any need for self assessment."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assuming experience alone maintains competence", "C": "Relying on employer evaluations for competence", "D": "Treating mandated courses as a substitute for self assessment"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ongoing self assessment and learning are professional duties because standards and knowledge change.): Nurses are accountable for maintaining competence through continuing self assessment and learning, since evidence, medications and standards change. Employer evaluations support learning but do not replace personal accountability. Regulator requirements do not remove the duty to assess one's own learning needs. Experience alone can preserve outdated habits."}, {"id": 306, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "In a community clinic, a practical nurse assesses an adult client who identifies as Inuit. A colleague says Indigenous clients share one set of traditions, so the same approach can be used with everyone. What is the most accurate and culturally safe response?", "options": {"A": "Review a general guide to Indigenous cultures and follow it for all clients.", "B": "First Nations, Inuit and Métis are distinct peoples, so ask each client about their own preferences.", "C": "Indigenous cultures share core practices, so a standard approach is efficient.", "D": "Ask the client's community leader for permission before beginning the assessment."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying a generic cultural guide", "C": "Assuming all Indigenous peoples share one culture", "D": "Seeking community permission instead of client consent"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (First Nations, Inuit and Métis are distinct peoples, so ask each client about their own preferences.): First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples have distinct histories, languages and practices, and each client holds individual preferences, so asking respectfully is culturally safe. A generic guide stereotypes clients. Consent comes from the client, not a community leader. A standard approach for everyone also ignores individual needs."}, {"id": 307, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Hanna says he felt chest pressure while shovelling snow yesterday and swallowed his nitroglycerin tablet with water, but the pain eased slowly. The nurse sees the bottle was opened eight months ago and sits in a sunny kitchen window. Which teaching point best explains the delayed relief and prevents a repeat?", "options": {"A": "The dose was too low, so take two tablets together when chest pain starts.", "B": "Water improves absorption, so drink a full glass after the tablet dissolves under the tongue.", "C": "Nitroglycerin works best with food, so take it after meals and store it somewhere warm.", "D": "Swallowing bypasses absorption under the tongue, so let it dissolve there and keep the dark bottle."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Escalating the dose instead of correcting the route and storage", "B": "Believing fluids help a sublingual drug absorb better", "C": "Missing that heat and light reduce tablet potency"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C043", "cluster_text": "Amir, a home care nurse, visits the Hanna household. Mr. Delroy Hanna, 74, has stable angina, glaucoma, and bone pain from prostate cancer that has spread. His wife helps with his care, and their two-year-old grandson, Eli, is staying with them while being treated for otitis externa. Amir reviews each person's medications and checks that the family can use them correctly before leaving.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Swallowing bypasses absorption under the tongue, so let it dissolve there and keep the dark bottle.): Nitroglycerin is absorbed through the mucosa under the tongue, and swallowing it sends it through the liver where most of it is destroyed. Mr. Hanna's old bottle in sunlight also lowers potency, so storage needs correcting too. Doubling the dose does not fix route or storage errors and risks a sharp fall in blood pressure. Taking it with food or water delays the very absorption the sublingual route is meant to provide."}, {"id": 308, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Hanna's palliative physician has ordered a buccal opioid tablet for breakthrough bone pain. His wife asks how to give it because he tends to chew tablets when he is nervous, and he has just finished a hot drink. Which instruction about this route does the nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Place it under the tongue, let it dissolve, then rinse his mouth with water immediately.", "B": "Chew the tablet thoroughly so that the medication reaches his bloodstream quickly through the cheek.", "C": "Crush the tablet into a spoonful of applesauce so it is absorbed through the stomach lining.", "D": "Place the tablet between the gum and cheek and let it dissolve without chewing or swallowing."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confusing the buccal route with the sublingual route and rinsing early", "B": "Thinking chewing speeds up mucosal absorption", "C": "Treating a buccal tablet like an oral tablet taken with food"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C043", "cluster_text": "Amir, a home care nurse, visits the Hanna household. Mr. Delroy Hanna, 74, has stable angina, glaucoma, and bone pain from prostate cancer that has spread. His wife helps with his care, and their two-year-old grandson, Eli, is staying with them while being treated for otitis externa. Amir reviews each person's medications and checks that the family can use them correctly before leaving.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Place the tablet between the gum and cheek and let it dissolve without chewing or swallowing.): A buccal tablet is held between the gum and cheek, where it dissolves slowly and is absorbed through the mucosa, so Mr. Hanna needs to leave it alone until it is gone. Chewing or swallowing it sends the drug through the gut and defeats the purpose of the route. Placing it under the tongue is the sublingual route, and rinsing early washes away drug before absorption finishes. Crushing it into food changes the product into an oral dose."}, {"id": 309, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Hanna demonstrates his eye drop technique. He tilts his head back, rests the dropper tip on his lower lid to steady his shaky hand, squeezes in one drop, and blinks hard several times. His second glaucoma drop is due now. What should the nurse teach next?", "options": {"A": "Keep the tip off the eye, blink rapidly, and give the second drop right away.", "B": "Keep the tip off the eye, close the lid gently, and wait five minutes between drops.", "C": "Rest the tip on the lower lid for steadiness, close the eye, and wait one minute.", "D": "Tilt the head forward, squeeze the bottle firmly, and press the drop in with a fingertip."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Blinking forces drops out and no interval is allowed", "C": "Allowing contact with the lid, which contaminates the tip", "D": "Using the wrong head position and touching the eye"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C043", "cluster_text": "Amir, a home care nurse, visits the Hanna household. Mr. Delroy Hanna, 74, has stable angina, glaucoma, and bone pain from prostate cancer that has spread. His wife helps with his care, and their two-year-old grandson, Eli, is staying with them while being treated for otitis externa. Amir reviews each person's medications and checks that the family can use them correctly before leaving.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep the tip off the eye, close the lid gently, and wait five minutes between drops.): Touching the dropper tip to the lid or eye can contaminate the bottle, so Mr. Hanna needs another way to steady his hand, such as bracing his wrist on his cheek. Closing the lid gently keeps the drop in contact with the eye, while hard blinking pushes it out. A five minute gap lets the first drop absorb before the second one washes it away. Waiting only one minute is too short for this purpose."}, {"id": 310, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Eli, 2 years old, has otitis externa and prescribed antibiotic ear drops. His grandmother pulls the earlobe up and back, instills the drops while he sits upright, and he wriggles. Drops run out onto his cheek. Which correction reflects safe technique for a child of this age?", "options": {"A": "Keep him sitting upright, pull the pinna down and back, and rub the canal gently afterward.", "B": "Keep him sitting upright, pull the pinna up and back, and have him tilt his head.", "C": "Lay him on his side, pull the pinna up and back, then let him sit up.", "D": "Lay him on his side, pull the pinna down and back, then keep him still."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Correct pinna direction but wrong position and massage into the canal", "B": "Applying the adult pinna direction to a toddler", "C": "Mixing correct position with the adult pinna direction and releasing early"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C043", "cluster_text": "Amir, a home care nurse, visits the Hanna household. Mr. Delroy Hanna, 74, has stable angina, glaucoma, and bone pain from prostate cancer that has spread. His wife helps with his care, and their two-year-old grandson, Eli, is staying with them while being treated for otitis externa. Amir reviews each person's medications and checks that the family can use them correctly before leaving.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lay him on his side, pull the pinna down and back, then keep him still.): In children under three years the ear canal slopes upward, so the pinna is pulled down and back to straighten it, and he should lie with the affected ear up so the drops stay in. Pulling the pinna up and back is the adult technique and leaves a toddler's canal curved. Letting him sit up immediately lets the drops drain out before they coat the canal. Rubbing inside the canal can hurt an inflamed ear."}, {"id": 311, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "During orientation, practical nurse Dana is asked to read a research summary and decide how much weight its findings deserve. Her preceptor explains that study designs sit on a ladder from weaker to stronger evidence about whether an intervention works. Which source sits highest on that ladder?", "options": {"A": "A systematic review that combines results from several well-designed randomized trials.", "B": "A respected senior clinician's opinion based on many years of experience with similar clients.", "C": "A single case report describing one client's unusual response to the intervention.", "D": "A well-designed cohort study that follows one hospital's clients for several years."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating expert opinion as top evidence", "C": "Treating one detailed case as strong proof", "D": "Placing observational designs above pooled trials"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A systematic review that combines results from several well-designed randomized trials.): A systematic review pools several strong trials, which reduces the chance that one study's bias or luck drives the conclusion, so it ranks highest. A cohort study is useful but observational and cannot control for every difference between groups. A case report describes one person and is weak for judging effectiveness. Expert opinion, however respected, sits at the bottom of the ladder."}, {"id": 312, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sam finds gastric fluid leaking around Ms. Lindqvist's gastrostomy tube, with mild redness on the skin and a thick ointment-saturated gauze packed under the bumper. She reports no fever, pain, or pus at the site. Which action fits the nurse's role and the likely cause of the skin irritation?", "options": {"A": "Clean the site with hydrogen peroxide three times daily and leave it open to air.", "B": "Remove the packed dressing, clean and dry the skin, apply a light dressing, and report it.", "C": "Hold all tube feeds, cover the site with thick gauze, and wait for the morning review.", "D": "Apply more barrier ointment under a thicker gauze pad and tighten the bumper against the skin."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using harsh cleansers that damage skin", "C": "Stopping feeds and covering the site with no report to the team", "D": "Trapping moisture and tightening the bumper, which worsens leakage"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C044", "cluster_text": "Ms. Ingrid Lindqvist, 45, had a bowel resection nine days ago for complications of Crohn's disease. She has a gastrostomy tube for supplemental feeding and receives total parenteral nutrition through a central line. Sam, a practical nurse on the medical unit, cares for her this week and also prepares her for a colonoscopy to review the surgical join. She is alert and cooperative.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Remove the packed dressing, clean and dry the skin, apply a light dressing, and report it.): Thick ointment and packed gauze trap gastric fluid against the skin and worsen irritation, so Ms. Lindqvist's skin needs cleaning, drying, and a light dressing. Leakage also needs reporting because it can mean a tube position or size problem. Tightening the bumper presses on tissue and can cause pressure injury. Peroxide is too harsh for routine site care, and withholding feeds without a plan delays nutrition."}, {"id": 313, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a tube feeding, Ms. Lindqvist's gastric residual measures 280 mL, above the volume the physician set. She says she feels full and mildly nauseated, and her abdomen feels firm. Sam must decide what to do with the syringe contents and the feeding. What action does the nurse take?", "options": {"A": "Slow the feeding rate by half and continue while she stays sitting upright in the chair.", "B": "Give an antiemetic from the as-needed orders and continue the feeding at the usual rate.", "C": "Hold the feeding, follow policy on the residual, and report to the RN.", "D": "Discard the residual, restart the feeding at the same rate, and document the amount measured."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reducing the rate instead of holding the feed", "B": "Treating the nausea and ignoring the high volume", "D": "Discarding the residual and resuming without reporting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C044", "cluster_text": "Ms. Ingrid Lindqvist, 45, had a bowel resection nine days ago for complications of Crohn's disease. She has a gastrostomy tube for supplemental feeding and receives total parenteral nutrition through a central line. Sam, a practical nurse on the medical unit, cares for her this week and also prepares her for a colonoscopy to review the surgical join. She is alert and cooperative.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hold the feeding, follow policy on the residual, and report to the RN.): A residual above the ordered limit with fullness and a firm abdomen suggests slowed emptying and a risk of vomiting and aspiration, so the feeding is held and the finding reported. The nurse follows the facility policy on whether the residual is returned. Restarting at the same rate ignores the warning, and slowing the rate leaves feed entering a full stomach. An antiemetic can mask the sign while the cause goes unassessed."}, {"id": 314, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Lindqvist's total parenteral nutrition bag is nearly empty and the replacement from pharmacy has not arrived. Her capillary glucose is 6.1 mmol/L. Sam knows sudden interruption of the infusion can cause hypoglycemia. The tubing is not due for a change until tomorrow. What does the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Run the remaining solution faster so the bag finishes before the tubing change time.", "B": "Stop the infusion now, clamp the line, and recheck her glucose again after two hours.", "C": "Tell the RN and pharmacy promptly, and start ordered dextrose until the bag arrives.", "D": "Flush the line with normal saline and leave it capped until the new bag arrives."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Speeding the infusion, which risks hyperglycemia", "B": "Abrupt stoppage with no glucose replacement", "D": "Capping the line and leaving her without dextrose"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C044", "cluster_text": "Ms. Ingrid Lindqvist, 45, had a bowel resection nine days ago for complications of Crohn's disease. She has a gastrostomy tube for supplemental feeding and receives total parenteral nutrition through a central line. Sam, a practical nurse on the medical unit, cares for her this week and also prepares her for a colonoscopy to review the surgical join. She is alert and cooperative.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tell the RN and pharmacy promptly, and start ordered dextrose until the bag arrives.): Parenteral nutrition delivers concentrated dextrose, and stopping it abruptly can drop her glucose within an hour. Sam therefore reports the shortage at once and follows the order for an interim dextrose solution while the bag is obtained. Running the bag faster can cause hyperglycemia and fluid overload. Capping the line or clamping it with only a delayed recheck leaves her unprotected during the gap."}, {"id": 315, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before Ms. Lindqvist's colonoscopy, the physician orders a clear liquid diet for 24 hours. She asks which items on her tray she may have. The tray holds red gelatin, strained chicken broth, pulp-free apple juice, and vanilla pudding. She has no allergies. Which interpretation of the order guides the nurse's teaching?", "options": {"A": "Broth and apple juice are suitable, but red gelatin and pudding are not before this test.", "B": "Only the apple juice is suitable because broth contains fat and gelatin contains dye.", "C": "Broth, juice, and red gelatin are suitable, and only the pudding is excluded from the diet.", "D": "All four items are suitable because each one is a liquid or melts at body temperature."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Wrongly excluding broth as fatty", "C": "Missing that red dye can mimic blood on the scope", "D": "Treating anything that melts as clear"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C044", "cluster_text": "Ms. Ingrid Lindqvist, 45, had a bowel resection nine days ago for complications of Crohn's disease. She has a gastrostomy tube for supplemental feeding and receives total parenteral nutrition through a central line. Sam, a practical nurse on the medical unit, cares for her this week and also prepares her for a colonoscopy to review the surgical join. She is alert and cooperative.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Broth and apple juice are suitable, but red gelatin and pudding are not before this test.): A clear liquid diet includes fluids you can see through, such as strained broth and pulp-free juice. Red or purple gelatin is avoided before colonoscopy because the dye can look like blood to the examiner, and pudding is opaque and not clear. Calling every melting food acceptable misses both points. Excluding broth is also wrong, since strained broth is a standard clear liquid."}, {"id": 316, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After a hectic morning, a community care practical nurse gave a client's insulin about forty minutes late, and no harm resulted. Her manager suggests a written reflection and learning plan. She tells a colleague that reflection seems unnecessary because she is experienced. What is the best interpretation of this expectation?", "options": {"A": "Reflection applies when harm occurs, so a near miss without harm needs only a brief note.", "B": "Reflection is a continuing professional duty, and a learning plan turns a near miss into change.", "C": "Reflection is useful only when the nurse chooses it, since regulators do not require it.", "D": "A learning plan becomes necessary when the late dose is reported to the college."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignoring learning from events with no harm", "C": "Believing reflection is voluntary", "D": "Tying learning plans to regulatory reporting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reflection is a continuing professional duty, and a learning plan turns a near miss into change.): Reflective practice is part of continuing competence for every regulated nurse, whatever their experience. Looking at why the dose was late, such as workload or routes, lets her change practice before harm occurs. It is not a voluntary extra, and it is not tied to a report to the college. A brief note alone misses the learning."}, {"id": 317, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Recently discharged with heart failure, a 69-year-old man weighs himself in the evening after dinner, sometimes wearing shoes. His weight rose from 82.0 kg to 84.1 kg over three days. The home care nurse is teaching about daily weights. Which instruction about technique and this change is best?", "options": {"A": "Weigh at any consistent time after meals, since swings of a few kilograms are expected daily.", "B": "Weigh each morning after voiding and before eating, in similar clothing, and report gains.", "C": "Weigh only at clinic visits, because home scales are too unreliable to track fluid changes.", "D": "Weigh after the evening meal wearing shoes, then subtract the shoe weight before reporting."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Allowing post-meal weights and dismissing a rapid gain", "C": "Abandoning home weights altogether", "D": "Keeping an unstandardized time and clothing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Weigh each morning after voiding and before eating, in similar clothing, and report gains.): Daily weights show fluid retention before swelling or breathlessness appear, so they must be done under the same conditions each day: morning, after voiding, before eating, in similar clothing. A gain of about 2 kg in three days is a typical trigger to report to the care team. Evening weights after meals and with shoes add noise that hides true change. Weighing only at clinic visits misses the early warning."}, {"id": 318, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At 0200, an 18-month-old girl is brought to the emergency department with a barking cough, a hoarse cry, and inspiratory stridor that worsens when she cries. She is afebrile, alert, and drinking. Her frightened mother is holding her. Which action by the practical nurse is most appropriate while awaiting the physician?", "options": {"A": "Apply a warm compress to her neck and give oral antibiotics from the unit's standing orders.", "B": "Offer cool sips of juice and collect a throat swab for culture before further assessment.", "C": "Lay her flat on the stretcher and examine the throat with a tongue depressor and penlight.", "D": "Keep her calm on her mother's lap, monitor breathing, and avoid examining the throat."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a viral illness with antibiotics and heat", "B": "Swabbing the throat, which can worsen swelling and distress", "C": "Agitating the child and risking airway spasm"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep her calm on her mother's lap, monitor breathing, and avoid examining the throat.): The picture fits croup, where crying increases airway narrowing and stridor, so keeping her calm with her mother and watching breathing and oxygen saturation is the priority. Probing the throat with an instrument or swab can provoke spasm and is unnecessary for this diagnosis. Lying her flat adds distress. Croup is usually viral, so antibiotics and warm compresses do not treat it."}, {"id": 319, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Admitted with pneumonia, a hospital client tells the practical nurse that he is Métis and prefers that term rather than 'Aboriginal' or 'Native'. A colleague says she writes 'Indigenous' for every client because it is simpler for the chart and the team. Which approach best reflects cultural safety?", "options": {"A": "Use the broad collective term in all documentation and use his preferred term only in conversation.", "B": "Use the term in his health card record, since official records show the most accurate identity.", "C": "Ask his family to choose the term, since families decide how identity is recorded.", "D": "Use the term he prefers in conversation and documentation, and invite him to correct any mistakes."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Prioritizing charting convenience over the client's identity", "B": "Relying on a record instead of asking the client", "C": "Handing the client's self-identification to relatives"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use the term he prefers in conversation and documentation, and invite him to correct any mistakes.): Cultural safety means the client decides how he is described, so the nurse uses Métis as he states it and welcomes correction. A broad term used for convenience erases distinct identities, and family or record entries do not replace the client's own voice. Respecting self-identification builds trust and reduces the risk that he avoids care."}, {"id": 320, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the 1500 snack, a care aide stirs one unmeasured scoop of thickener into tea for an 82-year-old resident with dysphagia after a stroke, who has an order for mildly thick fluids. The resident coughs on the first sip. What is the nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Add a second scoop of thickener by estimation, then resume the drink once it looks thick.", "B": "Offer a smaller sip with her chin tilted back, since the consistency looks acceptable.", "C": "Stop the drink, assess breathing, report the cough, and remake it with the measured thickener.", "D": "Give plain water instead, since thin water is easier to swallow after coughing."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Estimating thickener again instead of measuring", "B": "Tilting the chin back, which increases aspiration risk", "D": "Giving thin fluid to a client with dysphagia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the drink, assess breathing, report the cough, and remake it with the measured thickener.): Thickener must be measured to the label amount for the ordered level, because guessing gives a fluid that may be too thin to be safe. Coughing on a sip signals possible aspiration, so the drink is stopped, breathing is assessed, and the event reported. Tilting her chin back widens the airway opening and lets fluid fall toward it, which raises aspiration risk, and thin water is the riskiest consistency for her. Another estimated scoop repeats the original error."}, {"id": 321, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a hospital unit, a 60-year-old inpatient tells the practical nurse he worries nothing can be made truly safe in hospital. The team is reviewing unit goals at huddle. When explaining what patient safety means, which statement is the most accurate for her to share?", "options": {"A": "Patient safety means keeping clients free from risk, so an incident shows a nurse was careless.", "B": "Patient safety means following each policy to the letter, even when a client's needs clearly differ.", "C": "Patient safety means reducing preventable harm from care through systems, teamwork, and reporting.", "D": "Patient safety means protecting clients from harm caused by their own illness or risky behaviours."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Defining safety as zero risk with blame", "B": "Equating safety with rigid rule following", "D": "Confusing safety with disease prevention"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Patient safety means reducing preventable harm from care through systems, teamwork, and reporting.): Patient safety is about preventing harm that comes from the care itself, and it improves through systems design, teamwork, and reporting rather than individual perfection. Defining it as no risk at all leads to blaming nurses and hiding errors. Harm from illness is a clinical concern, not the definition of safety. Rigid rule following can itself be unsafe when a client's needs change."}, {"id": 322, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Priya, 34, delivered a healthy baby two days ago after a pregnancy complicated by gestational diabetes. Her glucose is normal now and she asks the postpartum nurse whether she can stop worrying about diabetes. She plans to breastfeed. What should the nurse teach about follow-up?", "options": {"A": "Her diabetes risk stays higher, so she needs glucose testing after delivery and periodically.", "B": "Breastfeeding removes her diabetes risk, so follow-up testing is optional if her diet stays healthy.", "C": "The condition ends at delivery, so no testing is needed unless thirst or urination increases.", "D": "She needs glucose testing only with another pregnancy, since the risk is tied to pregnancy itself."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believing breastfeeding replaces screening", "C": "Assuming gestational diabetes resolves fully at delivery", "D": "Limiting risk to future pregnancies only"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Her diabetes risk stays higher, so she needs glucose testing after delivery and periodically.): Gestational diabetes often resolves after birth, but it raises the lifetime risk of type 2 diabetes, so screening is advised after delivery and repeated regularly. A normal glucose today does not remove that risk. Linking testing only to future pregnancies ignores type 2 diabetes. Breastfeeding has benefits for mother and baby but does not replace follow-up testing."}, {"id": 323, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Missing two doses of his antihypertensive has left a medical unit client with a blood pressure of 178/102 mmHg. The practical nurse is about to ask him about it, and he looks tense and avoids eye contact. Which question best supports open communication and finds the reason he missed the doses?", "options": {"A": "Why didn't you take your blood pressure pills when the nurse left them?", "B": "You do understand these pills matter, so what made you skip them?", "C": "Did you forget the doses, or did you decide not to take them?", "D": "What has made it hard to take your blood pressure pills on schedule?"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a why question that sounds blaming", "B": "Leading question that implies fault", "C": "Closed either-or question limiting his answer"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (What has made it hard to take your blood pressure pills on schedule?): An open, non-judgmental question invites him to describe barriers such as side effects, cost, or confusion. Why questions often sound accusing and make clients defensive, and a leading statement pressures him to agree. The either-or question limits his answer to two choices and may hide the real reason. Understanding the cause lets the team solve the problem."}, {"id": 324, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Every afternoon around 1600, an 87-year-old resident with dementia paces the hallway and calls out 'Help'. A staff member says she wants attention and asks whether to request a sedative. The resident last toileted six hours ago and skipped lunch. What is the best nursing approach?", "options": {"A": "Treat the behaviour as communication, check for toileting, hunger, or pain needs, and respond calmly.", "B": "Walk her back to her room, tell her to wait, and check on her later.", "C": "Use a lap belt in her chair to keep her seated until supper.", "D": "Ask the physician for a sedative so she rests during the afternoon hours."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Redirecting without assessing needs", "C": "Using restraint for wandering", "D": "Reaching for sedation before looking for causes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Treat the behaviour as communication, check for toileting, hunger, or pain needs, and respond calmly.): Behaviours in dementia are often attempts to communicate an unmet need, and this resident has a clear candidate in her missed lunch and long gap since toileting. The nurse checks and meets those needs first with a calm approach. Sending her away without assessing leaves the need unmet. A lap belt is a restraint and is not appropriate for wandering. A sedative is not a first response."}, {"id": 325, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Confusion is worse than usual for a 79-year-old long-term care resident whose temperature is 35.5 C. Her pulse is 108 and she has urinary frequency. A new aide says she cannot be infected because she has no fever. Which statement about infection in older adults is correct?", "options": {"A": "A heart rate over 100 is expected in older adults, so it adds little.", "B": "Confusion in older adults is mostly part of aging and rarely signals an infection.", "C": "Older adults with serious infection may have a low or normal temperature, so other changes matter.", "D": "Fever develops first in infection, so a low temperature means the infection is probably not serious."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Normalizing tachycardia in older adults", "B": "Dismissing new confusion as aging", "D": "Expecting fever as the first sign in every age group"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Older adults with serious infection may have a low or normal temperature, so other changes matter.): Older adults often mount a weak fever response, and sepsis can appear as a low temperature with new confusion and a faster pulse. The nurse reports the whole picture instead of waiting for a fever. Treating confusion or tachycardia as normal aging delays recognition of a serious infection."}, {"id": 326, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before lunch, a hospital client with type 2 diabetes who takes insulin has a capillary glucose of 3.6 mmol/L. She is alert, sweaty, and able to swallow. A student nurse says the result is acceptable because her older charts used mg/dL cutoffs. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Wait and recheck in one hour once the meal tray arrives, since 3.6 is near normal", "B": "Offer a glass of milk with lunch when it arrives and record the glucose reading afterward", "C": "Give fast-acting carbohydrate per protocol, recheck in 15 minutes, and report to the RN.", "D": "Hold her insulin and serve lunch with a larger portion of protein to raise glucose"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying treatment for a low glucose", "B": "Using slow-acting food rather than fast-acting carbohydrate", "D": "Skipping fast-acting sugar and relying on protein"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give fast-acting carbohydrate per protocol, recheck in 15 minutes, and report to the RN.): A glucose below 4.0 mmol/L with sweating is hypoglycemia, and mmol/L cutoffs must not be confused with mg/dL numbers. She is alert and can swallow, so fast-acting carbohydrate is given per protocol, rechecked in 15 minutes, and reported. Waiting an hour or serving protein or milk acts too slowly and risks further decline."}, {"id": 327, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Drooling, leaning forward with his chin out, and refusing to speak, a 4-year-old arrives at the emergency department. He has a temperature of 39.4 C and a muffled voice. His father says he has not had routine immunizations. What should the nurse do before the physician arrives?", "options": {"A": "Collect a throat swab specimen for culture while he remains seated upright on the examination bed.", "B": "Take his oral temperature again and give acetaminophen by mouth before assessment.", "C": "Lay him flat and examine the mouth with a tongue depressor before the physician arrives.", "D": "Keep him upright and calm with his father, give oxygen if ordered, and prepare airway equipment."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stimulating the throat and risking airway closure", "B": "Giving oral medication to a child who cannot swallow safely", "C": "Positioning flat and examining the mouth, which can obstruct the airway"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep him upright and calm with his father, give oxygen if ordered, and prepare airway equipment.): Drooling, tripod position, fever, and incomplete immunizations point to epiglottitis, an emergency where distress or throat stimulation can close the airway. The nurse keeps him upright and calm, gives oxygen as ordered, and gets airway equipment ready. A swab or tongue depressor can trigger obstruction, and lying flat worsens it. Oral medication is unsafe when he cannot swallow."}, {"id": 328, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a home visit, the nurse hears a 76-year-old man's daughter say, 'Dad is not right today.' His vital signs match his usual baseline and he says he feels fine, though he answers more slowly. His daughter has cared for him for years. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Treat the daughter's concern as a warning sign, reassess closely, and report to the RN.", "B": "Reassure the daughter because vital signs match his baseline, and review at the next scheduled visit.", "C": "Wait for a fever or low oxygen saturation before involving anyone else in his assessment.", "D": "Explain that families often worry after a stressful week and document the visit as unchanged."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relying on normal vital signs alone", "C": "Waiting for objective signs of deterioration", "D": "Attributing the concern to family stress"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Treat the daughter's concern as a warning sign, reassess closely, and report to the RN.): A family member who knows the client well often notices early change before vital signs shift, and slower responses may signal delirium or infection. The nurse takes the concern seriously, looks for subtle changes, and reports to the RN. Reassuring the family or waiting for fever delays recognition, and documenting the visit as unchanged ignores new information."}, {"id": 329, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After giving a medication from a look-alike vial, a surgical unit nurse noticed the error and reported it. The manager wants to understand why it happened. Staff say storage places two similar vials side by side. Which response best reflects a just culture?", "options": {"A": "Retrain every nurse on the unit and leave the storage arrangement unchanged for now.", "B": "Report the nurse to the college because the error reached the client.", "C": "Support the nurse, review the storage system, and separate the look-alike vials.", "D": "Reassign the nurse from medication tasks while the unit reviews storage."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Retraining staff without fixing the cause", "B": "Treating a reported human error as misconduct", "D": "Penalizing the nurse before fixing the system"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Support the nurse, review the storage system, and separate the look-alike vials.): A slip caused by look alike vials is human error, which a just culture meets with support and system redesign, not punishment. Separating the vials removes the trap for the next nurse. Reporting to the college or removing her from duties punishes an honest reporter and discourages reporting. Retraining alone leaves the hazard."}, {"id": 330, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Nazia is preparing a 50-year-old newcomer for a minor procedure at the community health centre. He speaks little English, and his 15-year-old son offers to translate the consent discussion. A professional interpreter is available by phone. What should Nazia do to obtain valid consent?", "options": {"A": "Let the son interpret because family members understand the client's history best.", "B": "Use the translated form and ask a bilingual housekeeper to explain the risks.", "C": "Have the son translate and confirm his understanding through the phone interpreter afterward.", "D": "Use the professional interpreter so he can understand and ask questions freely."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on a family member as interpreter", "B": "Using an untrained person to interpret consent", "C": "Using a child as the main interpreter"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C037", "cluster_text": "Nazia is a practical nurse in a community health program that covers a clinic, a school, and home visits. During one week she meets a newcomer who needs consent for a procedure, a child with bruising, an older woman at home, a colleague who seems impaired, and a clinic client with a reportable infection. She works within her regulatory body's standards and employer policy.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use the professional interpreter so he can understand and ask questions freely.): Valid consent requires that the client understands the information, so a qualified interpreter is used when language is a barrier. A housekeeper is not trained in medical interpretation and may omit or alter details. A child interpreting may soften details and limits his father's privacy. Checking afterward does not fix a poor first discussion."}, {"id": 331, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At the school clinic, Nazia examines an 8-year-old with several bruises in different stages of healing on his back and upper arms. He says he fell, yet his story changes and he flinches when touched. His teacher says the family is respected in the community. What should Nazia do?", "options": {"A": "Gather proof by questioning the child repeatedly, then report after the family explains the injuries.", "B": "Discuss concerns with the parents first, then report only if their explanation seems doubtful or incomplete.", "C": "Document the findings and report only after consulting the school principal about the family's standing.", "D": "Report a reasonable suspicion to child protection promptly, without proof or parental permission."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Investigating instead of reporting", "B": "Warning the parents before making a report", "C": "Making reporting depend on the school principal"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C037", "cluster_text": "Nazia is a practical nurse in a community health program that covers a clinic, a school, and home visits. During one week she meets a newcomer who needs consent for a procedure, a child with bruising, an older woman at home, a colleague who seems impaired, and a clinic client with a reportable infection. She works within her regulatory body's standards and employer policy.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report a reasonable suspicion to child protection promptly, without proof or parental permission.): Nurses have a duty to report a reasonable suspicion of child abuse and need not prove it, since investigation belongs to child protection and police. Repeated questioning can harm the child and contaminate evidence. Informing parents first may put the child at risk, and the duty is personal and cannot be delegated to a principal."}, {"id": 332, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "During a home visit, Nazia finds an 80-year-old woman who lives with her adult grandson, in soiled clothing with an untreated pressure injury and little food in the cupboards. She looks fearful when he speaks. Nazia suspects neglect. Which action is most appropriate under national professional principles?", "options": {"A": "Confront the grandson about the neglect and report only if he refuses to cooperate with her.", "B": "Ask the physician to make the report, since only a physician may report suspected neglect.", "C": "Check her immediate safety, document facts, and report through legally required channels.", "D": "Treat the pressure injury and monitor her at home for two weeks before deciding to report."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confronting the suspected abuser first", "B": "Believing only physicians can report", "D": "Delaying a report while monitoring"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C037", "cluster_text": "Nazia is a practical nurse in a community health program that covers a clinic, a school, and home visits. During one week she meets a newcomer who needs consent for a procedure, a child with bruising, an older woman at home, a colleague who seems impaired, and a clinic client with a reportable infection. She works within her regulatory body's standards and employer policy.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check her immediate safety, document facts, and report through legally required channels.): Nurses have an ethical and often legal duty to act on suspected abuse or neglect of a vulnerable adult, which starts with her immediate safety and objective documentation, then reporting through the required channel. The duty cannot be passed to a physician, and confronting the grandson may increase risk. Waiting two weeks leaves her exposed to ongoing harm."}, {"id": 333, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Covering a shift on a surgical unit, Nazia notices a colleague who smells of alcohol, has slurred speech, and is about to prepare opioids for two clients. The colleague asks her to say nothing and promises to leave after the shift. What should Nazia do first?", "options": {"A": "Record the signs privately and let the colleague finish preparing the medications.", "B": "Offer the colleague a break and finish the opioid preparation for her.", "C": "Wait until the next shift to see whether the behaviour happens again before reporting.", "D": "Stop the colleague from giving care and inform the charge nurse or manager now."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Allowing an impaired colleague to give care", "B": "Covering for a colleague without reporting", "C": "Delaying a report until a pattern develops"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C037", "cluster_text": "Nazia is a practical nurse in a community health program that covers a clinic, a school, and home visits. During one week she meets a newcomer who needs consent for a procedure, a child with bruising, an older woman at home, a colleague who seems impaired, and a clinic client with a reportable infection. She works within her regulatory body's standards and employer policy.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop the colleague from giving care and inform the charge nurse or manager now.): Clients are at immediate risk, so the duty is to prevent unsafe care and report at once through the chain of command. Letting the colleague continue keeps the risk in place. Finishing the preparation quietly makes Nazia part of concealing the problem. Waiting for another incident is unsafe, and a private promise is not a solution."}, {"id": 334, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At the clinic, a 25-year-old client is diagnosed with a reportable infection. He asks Nazia not to tell anyone because he fears his employer will find out, citing his right to confidentiality. The physician has ordered standard public health notification. What should Nazia explain?", "options": {"A": "She will report to public health and also tell his employer so the workplace can respond.", "B": "Reporting needs his written consent first, so she will ask him to sign the consent form.", "C": "Confidentiality prevents reporting, so she will ask the physician to cancel the notification.", "D": "Reporting is legally required, shares only needed details, and excludes his employer."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Sharing information with his employer", "B": "Requiring consent for a mandatory report", "C": "Believing confidentiality overrides the legal duty"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C037", "cluster_text": "Nazia is a practical nurse in a community health program that covers a clinic, a school, and home visits. During one week she meets a newcomer who needs consent for a procedure, a child with bruising, an older woman at home, a colleague who seems impaired, and a clinic client with a reportable infection. She works within her regulatory body's standards and employer policy.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reporting is legally required, shares only needed details, and excludes his employer.): Reporting reportable diseases to public health is required by law and is an accepted exception to confidentiality, with disclosure limited to what is needed. Public health does not routinely inform employers, and Nazia may not do so either. Consent is not needed for the report, but she can reassure him about how his information is protected."}, {"id": 335, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Kayla, a new graduate, tells Luis that a senior aide rolls her eyes, withholds information, and makes sarcastic remarks about her slow pace in front of residents' families. Luis recognizes this pattern. Which term best names this behaviour among colleagues, and how should it be viewed?", "options": {"A": "Lateral violence, a form of incivility that harms safety and must be addressed.", "B": "Constructive criticism, a normal part of orientation that builds resilience in new staff.", "C": "Hazing, a tradition that helps new nurses adjust and needs no unit response.", "D": "Burnout, a stress reaction in senior staff that the team should tolerate."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Calling hostility constructive feedback", "C": "Normalizing hostile behaviour as tradition", "D": "Mislabelling bullying as burnout"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C047", "cluster_text": "Kayla Torres is a new practical nurse graduate on a 36-bed long-term care unit where Luis, an experienced practical nurse, is team leader today. Kayla has faced eye-rolling and sarcastic remarks from a senior aide. Luis assigns resident care, gives feedback, and mentors Kayla. A first-year practical nursing student is also placed on the unit this week. Residents' families are often present.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lateral violence, a form of incivility that harms safety and must be addressed.): Eye rolling, withholding information and sarcasm are lateral violence, a form of incivility directed at peers that damages morale, communication and resident safety. It is not a harmless tradition, since its aim is to undermine rather than teach. Burnout does not excuse targeted behaviour. It is not feedback or coaching."}, {"id": 336, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Luis gives Kayla feedback after she forgot to document a resident's repositioning during a busy afternoon shift. Kayla's face tightens and she begins explaining that the unit was too busy to chart. Which response from Kayla best shows professional handling of feedback?", "options": {"A": "Explain the busy unit circumstances in detail so Luis understands why the task was missed.", "B": "Listen, ask clarifying questions, and agree on specific steps to improve charting.", "C": "Apologize repeatedly and promise to be perfect from now on.", "D": "Thank him, then ask the manager whether the feedback is fair."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Defending the lapse through excuses about workload", "C": "Over apologizing instead of planning", "D": "Seeking outside validation instead of engaging"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C047", "cluster_text": "Kayla Torres is a new practical nurse graduate on a 36-bed long-term care unit where Luis, an experienced practical nurse, is team leader today. Kayla has faced eye-rolling and sarcastic remarks from a senior aide. Luis assigns resident care, gives feedback, and mentors Kayla. A first-year practical nursing student is also placed on the unit this week. Residents' families are often present.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Listen, ask clarifying questions, and agree on specific steps to improve charting.): Professional feedback handling means listening without defending, asking questions to understand the expectation, and committing to a concrete change. Repeated apology and vague promises do not create a plan. Going to the manager to test the feedback avoids the conversation. Long explanations sound like excuses, and comparing herself to others deflects."}, {"id": 337, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Luis asks Kayla to mentor a first-year practical nursing student who has just joined the unit. The student is eager and asks to give a subcutaneous injection alone because she watched one yesterday. Kayla has not seen her skills checked. Which mentoring approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Match tasks to verified competence and supervise new skills directly.", "B": "Let her give it with a staff nurse watching from the doorway.", "C": "Assign only observation for the entire placement to avoid any risk to residents.", "D": "Allow her to perform it after reading the unit policy once."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Distant observation in place of direct supervision", "C": "Restricting learning to observation", "D": "Substituting policy reading for verified competence"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C047", "cluster_text": "Kayla Torres is a new practical nurse graduate on a 36-bed long-term care unit where Luis, an experienced practical nurse, is team leader today. Kayla has faced eye-rolling and sarcastic remarks from a senior aide. Luis assigns resident care, gives feedback, and mentors Kayla. A first-year practical nursing student is also placed on the unit this week. Residents' families are often present.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Match tasks to verified competence and supervise new skills directly.): A mentor protects residents and the learner by matching tasks to verified competence and supervising new skills directly. Watching from a doorway is not direct supervision. Reading policy once does not show skill. Restricting her to observation prevents learning, and an unsupervised injection exceeds her demonstrated skill."}, {"id": 338, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Luis asks the care aide to take vital signs, which is within her job description, and then to apply a prescribed medicated cream to a resident's rash, which the employer's policy does not list for aides. He tells Kayla both requests are just 'delegating'. How should Kayla interpret these two requests?", "options": {"A": "Both are delegation, so Luis keeps accountability for each task and Kayla has no concerns.", "B": "The first is an assignment within her role; the second needs policy authority, training, and oversight.", "C": "Neither is delegation because the aide is unregulated and does not receive tasks from nurses.", "D": "Both are assignments because the aide works for Luis during this shift and takes his direction."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating both requests as delegation", "C": "Assuming unregulated staff cannot receive any task", "D": "Treating all tasks as simple assignment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C047", "cluster_text": "Kayla Torres is a new practical nurse graduate on a 36-bed long-term care unit where Luis, an experienced practical nurse, is team leader today. Kayla has faced eye-rolling and sarcastic remarks from a senior aide. Luis assigns resident care, gives feedback, and mentors Kayla. A first-year practical nursing student is also placed on the unit this week. Residents' families are often present.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The first is an assignment within her role; the second needs policy authority, training, and oversight.): Assignment gives a worker tasks already within her role and competence, such as vital signs. Delegation transfers a task outside the usual role and needs authority in policy, training, and ongoing oversight, which the cream does not have here. Using the terms interchangeably hides that difference, and Kayla can raise the concern before the task is done."}, {"id": 339, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Today a long-term care resident's blood pressure reads 128/78 mmHg, while her usual is 100/60 mmHg. She is sleepier than usual and ate little breakfast. A colleague says the reading is normal and nothing needs reporting. How should the practical nurse interpret the finding?", "options": {"A": "Accept it as normal because the value falls within the usual adult range.", "B": "Compare it to her baseline, then report the change and her drowsiness.", "C": "Recheck it tomorrow and report only if it reaches 140/90 mmHg or higher.", "D": "Treat it as a good result since higher readings are safer for older adults."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Judging by population norms instead of baseline", "C": "Waiting for a hypertension threshold", "D": "Assuming higher pressure is protective"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Compare it to her baseline, then report the change and her drowsiness.): A reading is interpreted against the individual's baseline, and a rise from 100/60 to 128/78 with new drowsiness may signal pain, infection, or another change. The nurse reports both findings. Using a general normal range misses her personal change, and waiting for a high threshold delays action."}, {"id": 340, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Three hours of sleep in two days, after a family emergency, leave a practical nurse struggling on a night shift. The unit is short staffed and she is assigned eight residents including two receiving insulin. She notices her attention drifting. Which action best reflects knowing and acting within personal limits?", "options": {"A": "Continue the shift and rely on double checks, since the unit needs every available nurse.", "B": "Drink coffee and limit her tasks to those she judges lower risk without informing anyone.", "C": "Tell the charge nurse about her fatigue and ask for a workload or assignment adjustment.", "D": "Complete the insulin rounds first and then tell the supervisor she needs to leave early."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pushing through while impaired", "B": "Hiding fatigue and self-selecting tasks", "D": "Doing high-risk tasks before disclosing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tell the charge nurse about her fatigue and ask for a workload or assignment adjustment.): Fatigue is a safety risk, and nurses are accountable for recognizing limits and speaking up early. Telling the charge nurse allows a fair reassignment before an insulin error occurs. Pushing through, quietly choosing tasks, or giving insulin first all place residents at risk."}, {"id": 341, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a surgical ward, a client's heart rate was 78 at 0800, 96 at 1000, and 112 at 1200. His blood pressure is falling slightly and he looks pale and restless. The next scheduled vital signs are at 1400. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Record the values and report at the 1400 check unless he complains of pain.", "B": "Recognize the worsening trend, stay with him, and report to the RN right away.", "C": "Repeat vital signs in thirty minutes before reporting, since the last reading is only mildly high.", "D": "Encourage fluids and rest, then reassess him when his scheduled vital signs are due."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for symptoms before reporting", "C": "Waiting for another set while the client worsens", "D": "Delaying reassessment until the schedule"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Recognize the worsening trend, stay with him, and report to the RN right away.): A steady rise in heart rate with falling blood pressure, pallor, and restlessness suggests bleeding or shock, so the trend matters more than any single value. The nurse stays with him and reports immediately. Waiting for another set or the next scheduled check delays rescue, and fluids and rest do not address a possible bleed."}, {"id": 342, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Wet to dry gauze dressings are ordered for a client's stage 3 pressure injury with moist granulation tissue, and a colleague says this is the preferred standard because it removes dead tissue. The nurse asks the RN about it. Which statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Wet to dry is the current preferred standard because it gently debrides without harming healthy tissue.", "B": "Wet to dry dressings keep granulation tissue moist and promote healing, so they remain first line.", "C": "Wet to dry suits most wound beds, so the order should be followed unchanged.", "D": "Wet to dry dressings can damage healthy tissue and hurt, so moist dressings are now favoured."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing the outdated practice is current", "B": "Mistaking drying gauze for a moist dressing", "C": "Following an old order without question"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wet to dry dressings can damage healthy tissue and hurt, so moist dressings are now favoured.): Wet to dry gauze dries and sticks, so removing it pulls off new granulation tissue and causes pain, which is why moist wound healing dressings are now preferred. An old order should be questioned and clarified with the RN or prescriber. The dressing does not keep the bed moist, since it dries before it is changed."}, {"id": 343, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a hospital unit, a practical nurse sees a colleague repeatedly skip hand hygiene and reuse a single-use saline flush between clients. She raised it once informally and the practice continued. A client with a central line is at risk. Her manager is on vacation. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Speak to the colleague again and report only if the practice continues for another week.", "B": "Share the concern with other nurses at lunch so peer pressure changes the behaviour.", "C": "Report promptly through the chain of command or the safety reporting system.", "D": "Document her observations privately and wait for a pattern before reporting to leadership."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying until another week passes", "B": "Gossiping instead of reporting", "D": "Waiting to build a pattern"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report promptly through the chain of command or the safety reporting system.): Repeated unsafe practice that puts a client with a central line at risk requires prompt reporting through the proper channel, such as the manager's delegate or the safety reporting system. A single informal conversation has already failed. Waiting, delaying, or sharing the concern with peers leaves the risk in place and bypasses accountability."}, {"id": 344, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Wheezing, a runny nose, and trouble finishing bottles over two days bring a mother and her 5-month-old to the clinic. His respiratory rate is 58 breaths per minute with mild chest retractions and he has no fever. His mother asks whether antibiotics will help. What should the nurse explain about expected management?", "options": {"A": "A course of oral antibiotics is the main treatment and prevents the illness from worsening.", "B": "Regular salbutamol and oral corticosteroids are first-line treatment and shorten the illness.", "C": "Care is mostly supportive, with hydration, suctioning and oxygen; antibiotics do not treat viral infection.", "D": "He should be fed smaller volumes less often and kept lying flat to rest his lungs."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a viral illness with antibiotics", "B": "Expecting bronchodilators and steroids to be routine", "D": "Restricting feeds and positioning flat"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Care is mostly supportive, with hydration, suctioning and oxygen; antibiotics do not treat viral infection.): This picture fits bronchiolitis, a viral illness treated with supportive care: nasal suctioning, small frequent feeds or fluids, and oxygen if saturation drops. Antibiotics do not help a virus, and routine salbutamol or steroids have not been shown to help. Feeding less often and lying flat can worsen dehydration and breathing effort."}, {"id": 345, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Negative pressure wound therapy runs continuously on an adult client's large open abdominal wound. The unit alarms and the dressing loses its seal. Her wound bed is moist and pink, and her vital signs are stable. Which statement describes safe nursing practice for this therapy?", "options": {"A": "Turn the unit off for several hours at night, which rests the wound and speeds healing.", "B": "Replace the foam with dry gauze and restart the unit when the physician visits.", "C": "Report any interruption and follow the policy time limit for therapy being off.", "D": "Leave the unit off until the next dressing change since the seal is already lost."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pausing therapy for convenience", "B": "Substituting dry gauze for the specialized dressing", "D": "Leaving the wound unsupported until the next change"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report any interruption and follow the policy time limit for therapy being off.): Negative pressure therapy must run as ordered, and a lost seal is an interruption that is reported, with the foam replaced or moist dressing applied per policy if the therapy cannot be restored within the time limit. Turning it off for hours or leaving it off until the next change risks infection and slowed healing. Dry gauze does not replace the foam."}, {"id": 346, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During morning care, a 90-year-old resident with advanced dementia, who does not speak, grimaces, moans, guards her left hip, and pulls away when turned. She does not ask for medication. A new aide says she is not in pain because she is not complaining. How should the nurse interpret these behaviours?", "options": {"A": "They suggest agitation, so ask for a sedative rather than an analgesic.", "B": "They are likely signs of pain, so use a behavioural pain tool and report the findings.", "C": "They reflect the dementia itself, so no pain assessment or analgesic is needed.", "D": "They show she dislikes being handled, so slow down and wait for the behaviours to settle."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Seeking sedation over analgesia", "C": "Attributing pain behaviours to dementia", "D": "Interpreting pain as dislike of care"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (They are likely signs of pain, so use a behavioural pain tool and report the findings.): Residents who cannot speak show pain through facial expression, vocal sounds, guarding, and resistance to movement, so these behaviours are treated as pain until proven otherwise. A behavioural pain tool guides assessment and the nurse reports to the RN or physician. Sedation hides the signal without treating the cause, and waiting leaves her in pain."}, {"id": 347, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Jordan is asked to collect blood cultures from a 68-year-old with fever, chills, and low blood pressure before the first antibiotic dose. The physician ordered two sets. Jordan has already drawn one set from the right antecubital vein. Which approach for the second set is best?", "options": {"A": "Draw the second set from the existing peripheral IV catheter, which is quicker.", "B": "Wait until after the first antibiotic dose and draw the second set from the other arm.", "C": "Draw the second set from the same site through the same needle to reduce discomfort.", "D": "Draw the second set from a different venipuncture site with separate skin antisepsis, before antibiotics begin."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Drawing from an existing catheter that may be colonized", "B": "Delaying until antibiotics suppress growth", "C": "Using the same site, which cannot show contamination"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C039", "cluster_text": "Jordan is a practical nurse who works in the emergency department's fever stream. Several febrile clients need specimens collected before antibiotics begin: blood cultures from a client with suspected sepsis, a clean-catch urine from a woman with burning on urination, and a urine specimen from a man with an indwelling catheter. Jordan follows asepsis and labelling policies and reports abnormal results.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Draw the second set from a different venipuncture site with separate skin antisepsis, before antibiotics begin.): Two sets from different sites help the laboratory tell true bloodstream infection from skin contamination, and both should be collected before antibiotics, which can suppress growth. Using one site gives no independent check, and an existing catheter carries a higher contamination risk. Delaying until after antibiotics lowers the chance of finding the organism."}, {"id": 348, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before drawing blood cultures from a febrile 50-year-old on a medical unit, Jordan cleans the skin with chlorhexidine-alcohol and lets it dry. The vein is hard to find, and Jordan wants to feel for it again. What must Jordan do to keep the specimen free of skin contaminants?", "options": {"A": "Avoid touching the cleaned site without sterile gloves, and let the antiseptic dry first.", "B": "Palpate the site with a clean bare finger, then draw the blood without cleaning it again.", "C": "Wipe off the antiseptic after thirty seconds so it does not enter the bottle.", "D": "Palpate with non-sterile gloves since the skin has been cleaned and is considered sterile."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Contaminating the site through bare finger contact", "C": "Removing the antiseptic before contact time", "D": "Assuming non-sterile gloves keep the site sterile"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C039", "cluster_text": "Jordan is a practical nurse who works in the emergency department's fever stream. Several febrile clients need specimens collected before antibiotics begin: blood cultures from a client with suspected sepsis, a clean-catch urine from a woman with burning on urination, and a urine specimen from a man with an indwelling catheter. Jordan follows asepsis and labelling policies and reports abnormal results.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Avoid touching the cleaned site without sterile gloves, and let the antiseptic dry first.): Skin antisepsis only works if the site stays clean and the antiseptic has dried for its full contact time. Touching the area afterward, even with a clean bare finger or non-sterile gloves, reintroduces organisms and can produce a false positive culture. Wiping the antiseptic off early shortens its action, so Jordan uses sterile gloves or a different approach."}, {"id": 349, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Burning on urination brings a 30-year-old woman to the clinic, where she collects a urine specimen. She wiped with a dry tissue and filled the sterile container with the first part of her stream. The label is complete. What does Jordan conclude, and what is the next step?", "options": {"A": "The specimen is likely contaminated, so teach cleansing and recollect midstream urine.", "B": "The first void sample is preferred, so send it with a note.", "C": "The specimen is acceptable because it was voided naturally and labelled, so send it.", "D": "The specimen is valid but needs refrigeration, so store it for four hours."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Confusing first void with midstream collection", "C": "Accepting a first-void sample", "D": "Storing a contaminated specimen"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C039", "cluster_text": "Jordan is a practical nurse who works in the emergency department's fever stream. Several febrile clients need specimens collected before antibiotics begin: blood cultures from a client with suspected sepsis, a clean-catch urine from a woman with burning on urination, and a urine specimen from a man with an indwelling catheter. Jordan follows asepsis and labelling policies and reports abnormal results.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The specimen is likely contaminated, so teach cleansing and recollect midstream urine.): The first part of the stream flushes organisms from the urethra and skin, so a specimen from the start is likely contaminated and can give a misleading result. Jordan teaches cleansing and asks her to pass a little urine, then collect the middle portion. Refrigerating a contaminated specimen does not correct it. Natural voiding and a label do not make it acceptable."}, {"id": 350, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Fever and an indwelling urinary catheter bring a 70-year-old man a urine culture order. His drainage bag is half full and he has not started antibiotics. Jordan has a sterile syringe and an antiseptic swab. Which collection method gives a reliable specimen?", "options": {"A": "Clean the sampling port, then aspirate urine with a sterile syringe.", "B": "Clamp the tubing for one hour, then take a sample from the drainage bag.", "C": "Pour urine from the drainage bag into a sterile container after opening the spout.", "D": "Disconnect the catheter from the tubing and collect the urine that drains."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Clamping too long and sampling from the bag", "C": "Using stagnant bag urine that has grown organisms", "D": "Breaking the closed system and risking infection"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C039", "cluster_text": "Jordan is a practical nurse who works in the emergency department's fever stream. Several febrile clients need specimens collected before antibiotics begin: blood cultures from a client with suspected sepsis, a clean-catch urine from a woman with burning on urination, and a urine specimen from a man with an indwelling catheter. Jordan follows asepsis and labelling policies and reports abnormal results.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Clean the sampling port, then aspirate urine with a sterile syringe.): Fresh urine from the catheter's sampling port, cleaned and aspirated with a sterile syringe, reflects what is in the bladder and keeps the closed system intact. Bag urine has been sitting and grows organisms that do not represent the bladder. Disconnecting the tubing opens a path for infection, and a very long clamp is not needed or safe."}, {"id": 351, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "In palliative home care, a man says quietly to the practical nurse, 'I am afraid of what dying will feel like.' The nurse feels uncomfortable. His wife is in the next room, and a visitor is due soon. Which response best supports him?", "options": {"A": "Suggest he focus on the good days left and move on to the medication review.", "B": "Reassure him that dying is usually peaceful and that he has no reason to be afraid.", "C": "Say that his physician is better placed to discuss this and offer to call the office.", "D": "Acknowledge the fear, sit with him, and invite him to share what worries him."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Redirecting away from his fear", "B": "Offering false reassurance about dying", "C": "Handing off the conversation too quickly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acknowledge the fear, sit with him, and invite him to share what worries him.): Therapeutic communication invites the client to explore the fear, which a calm acknowledgement and presence does without claiming to fix it. Quick reassurance shuts the conversation down, and changing the subject or referring him away tells him the topic is too hard to discuss. The nurse can still involve the team afterward."}, {"id": 352, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Rina is preparing a 7-year-old for a venipuncture at day surgery. His mother says he loves dinosaurs, and he is worried and rubbing his arm. An analgesic cream was ordered and applied 40 minutes ago. Which approach best uses non-drug measures?", "options": {"A": "Use dinosaur distraction or guided imagery during the needle, with the ordered topical anesthetic.", "B": "Explain each step in technical terms and ask him to stay still.", "C": "Use guided imagery and cancel the ordered cream, since non-drug measures replace analgesics for children.", "D": "Ask his mother to hold him firmly during the needle and talk quietly."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using technical explanation for a young child", "C": "Replacing ordered analgesia with distraction", "D": "Using restraint instead of distraction"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C040", "cluster_text": "Rina is a practical nurse in a community comfort program that serves different clients each day. She works with a child before a needle procedure, a palliative client with bone metastases, a pregnant woman with back pain, and an older adult taking several cold remedies. Rina uses non-drug comfort measures alongside prescribed analgesics and reports uncontrolled pain to the RN or physician.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use dinosaur distraction or guided imagery during the needle, with the ordered topical anesthetic.): Distraction and imagery tailored to the child's interests reduce pain and fear, and they work best alongside the ordered topical anesthetic. Technical explanations raise anxiety in a seven year old. Firm holding by his mother does not reduce fear and removes her comforting role. Cancelling the cream leaves him with less relief."}, {"id": 353, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Bone metastases in her spine and hips have brought a 75-year-old woman to a palliative unit. She asks Rina for a back massage for pain. Her skin is intact and she rates her pain 6 out of 10. What is the safest way to offer massage?", "options": {"A": "Use moderate pressure along the spine, avoiding the hips, after checking pain.", "B": "Use light, gentle strokes away from bony metastases after checking with the RN.", "C": "Offer verbal reassurance and music, since touch can cause fractures in her condition.", "D": "Use gentle stroking with a warm pack placed over the painful vertebrae."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying pressure over spinal metastases", "C": "Withholding comforting touch that is safe", "D": "Applying heat over a bone tumour site"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C040", "cluster_text": "Rina is a practical nurse in a community comfort program that serves different clients each day. She works with a child before a needle procedure, a palliative client with bone metastases, a pregnant woman with back pain, and an older adult taking several cold remedies. Rina uses non-drug comfort measures alongside prescribed analgesics and reports uncontrolled pain to the RN or physician.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use light, gentle strokes away from bony metastases after checking with the RN.): Bone metastases weaken the skeleton, so moderate or deep pressure over them risks fracture and pain. Heat over the painful vertebrae can also be unsafe and needs an order. Light, gentle strokes away from the metastases, agreed with the RN, give comfort. Avoiding touch denies her a safe comfort measure she asked for."}, {"id": 354, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At 30 weeks of pregnancy, a 30-year-old woman has back pain and says she feels tense. Rina teaches relaxation breathing. The woman breathes quickly through her mouth with her shoulders raised and feels lightheaded, and her skin is warm. What should Rina do?", "options": {"A": "Have her breathe into a paper bag until the lightheadedness settles.", "B": "Have her hold her breath briefly after each inhale to deepen the muscle relaxation.", "C": "Encourage short panting breaths with quick exhales to ease tension.", "D": "Coach slow, deep breaths with a long exhale and relaxed shoulders."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a rebreathing technique without an order", "B": "Using breath holding that raises tension", "C": "Teaching panting breaths for relaxation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C040", "cluster_text": "Rina is a practical nurse in a community comfort program that serves different clients each day. She works with a child before a needle procedure, a palliative client with bone metastases, a pregnant woman with back pain, and an older adult taking several cold remedies. Rina uses non-drug comfort measures alongside prescribed analgesics and reports uncontrolled pain to the RN or physician.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Coach slow, deep breaths with a long exhale and relaxed shoulders.): Fast shallow breathing causes lightheadedness through hyperventilation and increases tension, so Rina coaches slow, deep breaths with a longer exhale and relaxed shoulders. A paper bag is not a safe nursing response in pregnancy. Panting breaths keep her ventilating too fast. Breath holding and tightening muscles also work against relaxation."}, {"id": 355, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Every six hours, an 80-year-old woman at home takes acetaminophen 650 mg for arthritis. She also uses two cold remedies and a sleep aid that she says have no pain medicine. Rina checks the labels. What is the key point to share?", "options": {"A": "Cold and sleep products rarely contain pain relievers, so she can keep adding them.", "B": "Many combination products contain acetaminophen, so total daily intake must be counted against the maximum.", "C": "Her age lowers the risk of overdose because older adults clear acetaminophen faster.", "D": "The daily maximum applies only to prescription products, so over-the-counter doses are not counted."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Missing hidden acetaminophen in combination products", "C": "Assuming age makes overdose less likely", "D": "Excluding over-the-counter doses from the total"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C040", "cluster_text": "Rina is a practical nurse in a community comfort program that serves different clients each day. She works with a child before a needle procedure, a palliative client with bone metastases, a pregnant woman with back pain, and an older adult taking several cold remedies. Rina uses non-drug comfort measures alongside prescribed analgesics and reports uncontrolled pain to the RN or physician.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Many combination products contain acetaminophen, so total daily intake must be counted against the maximum.): Acetaminophen is a common ingredient in cold, flu, and sleep products, so the total from every source counts toward the daily maximum, and an overdose can injure the liver. The prescription versus over-the-counter distinction does not matter, and older adults are not protected by age. Rina teaches her to read every label."}, {"id": 356, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In clinic, a 66-year-old man with a new diagnosis of heart failure says he will stop drinking fluids and has started a potassium-based salt substitute for flavour. His medications include an ACE inhibitor and spironolactone. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Support stopping all fluids and switching to a potassium salt substitute since sodium is the concern.", "B": "Teach sodium limits, the team's fluid limit, and why potassium salt substitutes are unsafe for him.", "C": "Advise a strict ban on sodium and fluids, and recheck his weight next visit.", "D": "Allow open fluids and a salt substitute, as long as he weighs himself each morning."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Endorsing stopping all fluids and a potassium substitute", "C": "Imposing an unsafe total restriction without guidance", "D": "Allowing unlimited fluids and a potassium substitute"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Teach sodium limits, the team's fluid limit, and why potassium salt substitutes are unsafe for him.): Heart failure care limits sodium and sets an individual fluid limit with the team, but stopping fluids without guidance risks dehydration and kidney injury. Potassium salt substitutes are dangerous with an ACE inhibitor and spironolactone because they can cause high potassium and arrhythmia. The nurse teaches flavour alternatives and reports his plan to the RN or physician."}, {"id": 357, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad tells the practical nurse at the pre-operative clinic that he never heard the surgeon mention a small chance of bowel injury, a rare but serious complication. He asks whether it matters, and the surgeon has already left for the day. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Tell him to raise the question again with the anesthesiologist when he arrives on surgery morning", "B": "Reassure him that surgeons disclose rare complications only when asked directly.", "C": "Tell him the risk is small and have him sign again tomorrow.", "D": "Acknowledge his concern and notify the surgeon so the risk is explained before he consents."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying an unresolved consent concern until surgery morning", "B": "Believing rare serious risks need not be disclosed", "C": "Obtaining a repeat signature without disclosure"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C038", "cluster_text": "Samir Haddad, 62, is booked for elective hernia repair under general anesthesia. He is otherwise well and takes one blood pressure medication. His care covers a pre-operative clinic visit, a short medical unit stay to adjust that medication, and a later emergency department visit after a fall at home. The practical nurse works with him at each stage and follows regulatory standards on consent.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acknowledge his concern and notify the surgeon so the risk is explained before he consents.): Serious risks that a reasonable person would want to know belong in the disclosure, so the surgeon must address Mr. Haddad's concern before he agrees to surgery. A rare risk is still material when it is serious. A repeat signature does not correct missing information. Explaining the risk personally exceeds the nurse's role."}, {"id": 358, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Last week Mr. Haddad signed his surgical consent form, and it sits in his chart on the medical unit. Today he says he is having second thoughts and asks whether the signature means he has to go ahead. How should the nurse answer?", "options": {"A": "Tell him he may withdraw consent at any time, then inform the surgeon.", "B": "Tell him the form stays valid, so the team will proceed unless the surgeon cancels.", "C": "Explain that cancelling now needs the surgeon's written approval before he decides.", "D": "Tell him to withdraw only on the morning of surgery in the pre-operative area."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assuming a form outlasts the client's changed wishes", "C": "Requiring approval to withdraw consent", "D": "Limiting when consent can be withdrawn"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C038", "cluster_text": "Samir Haddad, 62, is booked for elective hernia repair under general anesthesia. He is otherwise well and takes one blood pressure medication. His care covers a pre-operative clinic visit, a short medical unit stay to adjust that medication, and a later emergency department visit after a fall at home. The practical nurse works with him at each stage and follows regulatory standards on consent.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tell him he may withdraw consent at any time, then inform the surgeon.): Consent is an ongoing process, so Mr. Haddad can withdraw it whenever he chooses, and the surgeon needs to know right away. No written approval is needed for his decision to count. Restricting withdrawal to the morning of surgery limits his right and delays planning. The form only records an earlier discussion."}, {"id": 359, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Before leaving the clinic, Mr. Haddad agrees to have a small skin tag on his forearm frozen and removed by the physician. He is alert, has heard the explanation, and says yes clearly, but no consent form is at hand. Which statement about this consent is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Consent is valid only once he signs a written form, so removal must wait.", "B": "Verbal consent is valid when he is capable, informed, and the discussion is documented.", "C": "Verbal agreement counts once a family member has witnessed the discussion and signed a note.", "D": "Written consent is required whenever skin is cut or frozen, even for minor office procedures."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Equating valid consent with a signed document only", "C": "Adding a family witness and signature rule that consent does not require", "D": "Assuming every skin procedure needs written consent"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C038", "cluster_text": "Samir Haddad, 62, is booked for elective hernia repair under general anesthesia. He is otherwise well and takes one blood pressure medication. His care covers a pre-operative clinic visit, a short medical unit stay to adjust that medication, and a later emergency department visit after a fall at home. The practical nurse works with him at each stage and follows regulatory standards on consent.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Verbal consent is valid when he is capable, informed, and the discussion is documented.): Consent depends on capacity, information, and voluntariness, not on a signature, so a clear verbal yes from an informed client is valid for a minor procedure when it is documented. Insisting on a form would delay care without improving his protection. A family witness is not a requirement for a capable adult. Written forms are common for larger procedures, but the law does not demand one for every skin procedure."}, {"id": 360, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On the unit, the nurse holds up the blood pressure cuff and says she is checking his vital signs, and Mr. Haddad rolls up his sleeve and extends his arm. Later she plans to start an intravenous line. Which statement correctly describes consent in these two situations?", "options": {"A": "His gesture covers the intravenous line, since he consented to admission for hernia surgery.", "B": "Both rely on implied consent because he accepted admission and surgery.", "C": "Both need explicit verbal consent, because implied consent is not valid in hospital care.", "D": "His gesture implies consent for vital signs, but the intravenous line needs explicit consent."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stretching implied consent to cover an invasive procedure", "B": "Stretching consent for admission over every later procedure", "C": "Denying that implied consent can cover low-risk routine tasks"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C038", "cluster_text": "Samir Haddad, 62, is booked for elective hernia repair under general anesthesia. He is otherwise well and takes one blood pressure medication. His care covers a pre-operative clinic visit, a short medical unit stay to adjust that medication, and a later emergency department visit after a fall at home. The practical nurse works with him at each stage and follows regulatory standards on consent.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (His gesture implies consent for vital signs, but the intravenous line needs explicit consent.): Offering his arm for the cuff communicates agreement to a low-risk, routine task, which is implied consent. An intravenous line pierces the skin and carries risk, so he needs to be told about it and agree explicitly. Consent for admission does not cover each later procedure, and implied consent is valid for simple, low-risk tasks like a blood pressure check."}, {"id": 361, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Two weeks later Mr. Haddad arrives in the emergency department unconscious after a fall, with a head injury and falling blood pressure. The physician orders urgent treatment. His wife is driving in and expected within forty minutes, and no treatment refusals are on record. What best supports the team's decision to treat?", "options": {"A": "Treatment proceeds on implied consent because he accepted routine care on the unit earlier.", "B": "The emergency exception applies because delay threatens his life.", "C": "Treatment begins only after two staff members sign the hospital's emergency consent form.", "D": "Treatment waits until his wife arrives, since family must authorize every urgent intervention."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using implied consent from earlier unrelated care as the basis", "C": "Requiring paperwork before a time-critical intervention", "D": "Waiting for family when delay endangers life"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C038", "cluster_text": "Samir Haddad, 62, is booked for elective hernia repair under general anesthesia. He is otherwise well and takes one blood pressure medication. His care covers a pre-operative clinic visit, a short medical unit stay to adjust that medication, and a later emergency department visit after a fall at home. The practical nurse works with him at each stage and follows regulatory standards on consent.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The emergency exception applies because delay threatens his life.): When a client cannot consent, delay would risk life or serious harm, and no known refusal exists, care may proceed under the emergency exception. Waiting for his wife could cost him critical time and is not a legal requirement for emergency treatment. Earlier acceptance of routine care does not extend to a new emergency. Paperwork is completed as soon as it is practical, not before lifesaving care."}, {"id": 362, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lucie Gagnon, 74, is recovering from pneumonia. Her respiratory rate is now 28, her oxygen saturation is 89 percent on the same oxygen flow, and she is newly confused. Priya telephones the RN in charge. Which opening best follows SBAR and gets help quickly?", "options": {"A": "State her name and location, report low saturation and new confusion, and ask for assessment.", "B": "Read every set of shift vital signs aloud, then suggest the orders you want.", "C": "Start with her admission history and pneumonia treatment so the RN has the full background first.", "D": "Say you are worried about her and ask the RN to come."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Overloading the report and suggesting orders outside scope", "C": "Burying the urgent finding under background history", "D": "Giving a vague concern with no data or request"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C048", "cluster_text": "Priya Nair, a practical nurse, works a day shift on a medical unit with RN Marcus Tremblay and Alex Boateng, a new graduate practical nurse in his first month. She is briefly sent to the emergency department to help. During the shift she must escalate a worsening client, act on an urgent verbal order, model professional conduct, and mentor Alex.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (State her name and location, report low saturation and new confusion, and ask for assessment.): SBAR leads with who the client is and the situation, then moves quickly to a clear request, which gets Mrs. Gagnon assessed sooner. A long history delays the urgent message, and a worried call without data forces the RN to ask questions first. Reading a full shift of vital signs and proposing orders hides the key change and exceeds scope."}, {"id": 363, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "While Priya helps in the emergency department, a physician calls out a verbal order for an oral dose of a rate-control medication for a client in rapid atrial fibrillation. The room is noisy and Priya's hands are full of equipment. How should she handle the order?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the drug name aloud once, then prepare the dose and document afterward.", "B": "Write down the drug, dose, and route, read it back, and wait for confirmation.", "C": "Nod to show she heard, then prepare the dose and record it later in the chart.", "D": "Accept the order from Alex, who heard it clearly, and prepare the dose."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Repeating aloud without writing or confirmed read-back", "C": "Treating a nod as confirmation of the order", "D": "Relying on a second listener instead of verifying the order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C048", "cluster_text": "Priya Nair, a practical nurse, works a day shift on a medical unit with RN Marcus Tremblay and Alex Boateng, a new graduate practical nurse in his first month. She is briefly sent to the emergency department to help. During the shift she must escalate a worsening client, act on an urgent verbal order, model professional conduct, and mentor Alex.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Write down the drug, dose, and route, read it back, and wait for confirmation.): Closed loop communication means the receiver records the order, reads it back, and the sender confirms it, which prevents errors in a noisy room. Repeating the drug name aloud without writing leaves room for mishearing the dose or route. Relying on Alex's hearing adds a second unverified listener, and a nod or later charting does not confirm the order."}, {"id": 364, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Alex has noticed that some senior staff skip hand hygiene when the unit is busy, even though the policy poster is on every wall. He asks Priya how staff show professional standards. Which of Priya's actions best demonstrates role modelling?", "options": {"A": "Pointing to the hand hygiene poster and praising staff who comply.", "B": "Doing hand hygiene herself at every required moment, even when rushed.", "C": "Reminding the team of the policy each shift while accepting occasional shortcuts.", "D": "Keeping a private log of lapses to share at performance reviews."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using posters and praise instead of modelling the behaviour", "C": "Relying on words while tolerating contrary behaviour", "D": "Monitoring colleagues instead of demonstrating the standard"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C048", "cluster_text": "Priya Nair, a practical nurse, works a day shift on a medical unit with RN Marcus Tremblay and Alex Boateng, a new graduate practical nurse in his first month. She is briefly sent to the emergency department to help. During the shift she must escalate a worsening client, act on an urgent verbal order, model professional conduct, and mentor Alex.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Doing hand hygiene herself at every required moment, even when rushed.): Learners copy what they watch, so Priya's consistent hand hygiene under pressure shows Alex what the standard looks like in real work. Pointing at a poster and praising others keeps the focus on words. Keeping a private log of lapses watches colleagues without demonstrating anything, and reminders alone lose credibility when shortcuts are tolerated."}, {"id": 365, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Late in the shift, Alex confides that he left out a required documentation entry on a medication he gave, and no harm reached the client. He looks embarrassed and worried about being judged. Priya has a quiet moment before the next medication pass. What is her best action?", "options": {"A": "Meet privately, explore what happened without blame, review correct documentation, and agree on follow up.", "B": "Let him work out the documentation pattern alone, since mistakes teach new graduates best during orientation.", "C": "Correct the entry herself without comment, to protect his confidence in his difficult first month.", "D": "Report the omission to the manager at once and request a different preceptor for Alex."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Withholding support under a trial-and-error belief", "C": "Hiding the issue so no learning occurs", "D": "Escalating a minor learning event as punishment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C048", "cluster_text": "Priya Nair, a practical nurse, works a day shift on a medical unit with RN Marcus Tremblay and Alex Boateng, a new graduate practical nurse in his first month. She is briefly sent to the emergency department to help. During the shift she must escalate a worsening client, act on an urgent verbal order, model professional conduct, and mentor Alex.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Meet privately, explore what happened without blame, review correct documentation, and agree on follow up.): A private, blame-free conversation lets Alex learn the reason for the entry, fix the habit, and know support is available, which is the core of mentoring. Quietly correcting the chart removes the learning. Leaving him alone ignores her mentoring role and the safety value of documentation. Immediate escalation and reassignment treats a no-harm omission as misconduct."}, {"id": 366, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, Harpreet says he now drinks a large glass of grapefruit juice each morning for his health while taking atorvastatin. He also reports new aching in both thighs. He feels otherwise well. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Tell him morning juice should be acceptable because the statin is taken in the evening.", "B": "Advise orange juice instead and stopping the statin until the aching has resolved.", "C": "Reassure him that muscle aching is common in aging and probably not related to the juice.", "D": "Explain that grapefruit juice can raise statin levels, and report the aching and juice intake."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming dose timing prevents a food interaction", "B": "Telling the client to stop a drug without prescriber input", "C": "Attributing a possible adverse effect to age alone"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C041", "cluster_text": "A community health practical nurse supports the Dhillon family. Harpreet, 70, takes atorvastatin. His wife Gurpreet, 68, takes warfarin. Their daughter Simran, 29, is 20 weeks pregnant, and their daughter-in-law Navneet, 31, is breastfeeding her newborn. Each person asks the nurse a medication question over several visits, and the nurse works within scope, consulting the pharmacist or prescriber when needed.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that grapefruit juice can raise statin levels, and report the aching and juice intake.): Grapefruit juice slows statin breakdown, raising drug levels and the risk of muscle injury, so the prescriber needs to know about both the juice and the thigh pain. Spacing the doses does not remove the interaction. Stopping the statin on the nurse's advice goes beyond scope. Dismissing the aching as aging could delay recognition of a serious adverse effect."}, {"id": 367, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Gurpreet, who takes warfarin, has started eating much larger portions of spinach and kale after a television nutrition program. She worries about her medication and asks the nurse whether she should now stop eating leafy greens entirely. Which response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Suggest an extra warfarin tablet on days she eats greens to balance the vitamin K.", "B": "Advise her to stop all leafy greens, since vitamin K lowers warfarin's effect.", "C": "Encourage steady, consistent intake of leafy greens and report the change so her INR is monitored.", "D": "Tell her greens are unrelated to warfarin, so she can vary amounts freely."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Self-adjusting a high-alert medication dose", "B": "Eliminating a healthy food instead of keeping intake consistent", "D": "Denying a real vitamin K and warfarin interaction"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C041", "cluster_text": "A community health practical nurse supports the Dhillon family. Harpreet, 70, takes atorvastatin. His wife Gurpreet, 68, takes warfarin. Their daughter Simran, 29, is 20 weeks pregnant, and their daughter-in-law Navneet, 31, is breastfeeding her newborn. Each person asks the nurse a medication question over several visits, and the nurse works within scope, consulting the pharmacist or prescriber when needed.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Encourage steady, consistent intake of leafy greens and report the change so her INR is monitored.): Vitamin K affects warfarin, but the problem is variation, so a consistent intake with INR monitoring keeps her dose effective. Avoiding greens removes a healthy food and is not needed. Saying greens are unrelated is incorrect. Taking extra tablets on her own is a dangerous change to a high-alert drug that only the prescriber may direct."}, {"id": 368, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Simran, 29 and 20 weeks pregnant, attends the prenatal clinic with a headache lasting two days. She has ibuprofen and an herbal sleep tea at home and asks which she may take. Her blood pressure today is 118/72 mmHg. What is the best nursing response?", "options": {"A": "Advise her to take neither until her provider reviews the headache.", "B": "Tell her over-the-counter products are safe because they are sold without a prescription.", "C": "Suggest the natural herbal tea, but advise her to avoid the ibuprofen.", "D": "Recommend the lowest label dose of ibuprofen, since short use is acceptable now."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assuming non-prescription means safe in pregnancy", "C": "Treating herbal products as harmless by default", "D": "Judging a drug safe without provider approval"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C041", "cluster_text": "A community health practical nurse supports the Dhillon family. Harpreet, 70, takes atorvastatin. His wife Gurpreet, 68, takes warfarin. Their daughter Simran, 29, is 20 weeks pregnant, and their daughter-in-law Navneet, 31, is breastfeeding her newborn. Each person asks the nurse a medication question over several visits, and the nurse works within scope, consulting the pharmacist or prescriber when needed.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Advise her to take neither until her provider reviews the headache.): Many over-the-counter and herbal products carry risks to the fetus, and a two-day headache in pregnancy deserves review by her provider, so the nurse should hold both until a safe plan is set. Sale without a prescription does not show safety. Herbal teas are not regulated for pregnancy. Even brief ibuprofen use is a decision for the prescriber, and the nurse should not make it."}, {"id": 369, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Navneet, 31, is on the postpartum unit and has just been prescribed an antibiotic for a wound infection. She says she plans to stop breastfeeding to protect her baby, although she wants to continue if possible. The baby is feeding well. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Advise pumping and discarding milk throughout the course without first checking whether the drug is compatible.", "B": "Reassure her that most antibiotics are compatible, so she can continue feeding.", "C": "Check the drug's breastfeeding safety with the pharmacist, then support feeding if compatible.", "D": "Suggest switching to formula until the course ends, since infant exposure cannot be judged."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Discarding milk without verifying compatibility", "B": "Generalizing about drug safety without checking this antibiotic", "D": "Stopping breastfeeding instead of verifying the drug"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C041", "cluster_text": "A community health practical nurse supports the Dhillon family. Harpreet, 70, takes atorvastatin. His wife Gurpreet, 68, takes warfarin. Their daughter Simran, 29, is 20 weeks pregnant, and their daughter-in-law Navneet, 31, is breastfeeding her newborn. Each person asks the nurse a medication question over several visits, and the nurse works within scope, consulting the pharmacist or prescriber when needed.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check the drug's breastfeeding safety with the pharmacist, then support feeding if compatible.): Drugs differ widely in how much enters milk and in their effect on infants, so verifying this antibiotic with the pharmacist or prescriber lets Navneet make an informed choice and keep feeding when it is safe. Assuming most antibiotics are fine skips the check for this one. Switching to formula removes the benefits of breastfeeding, and pumping and discarding without checking wastes milk."}, {"id": 370, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Joan Whitehorse, 61 and postmenopausal, attends a clinic bone health talk. She is a former smoker, sits most of the day, takes a calcium supplement daily, and asks what else the nurse suggests to lower her fracture risk. Which teaching is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Combine calcium with regular weight-bearing exercise, vitamin D, and continued avoidance of smoking.", "B": "Limit walking to avoid jarring her bones and add more calcium.", "C": "Add vitamin D and calcium, and rest the legs more to avoid fall injury.", "D": "Take a higher calcium dose, since extra calcium offsets low activity and smoking history."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Restricting activity that actually protects bone", "C": "Cutting activity that protects bone density", "D": "Believing a larger calcium dose replaces exercise"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Combine calcium with regular weight-bearing exercise, vitamin D, and continued avoidance of smoking.): Bone density depends on calcium, vitamin D, and mechanical loading from weight-bearing activity, and avoiding smoking protects it further, so combining these gives Mrs. Whitehorse the best prevention plan. More calcium does not offset inactivity or smoking. Resting the legs reduces the loading that bone needs, and sudden high-impact exercise raises fracture risk when she is sedentary."}, {"id": 371, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Edgar Lowe, 78, lives on a memory care unit and paces toward the exit at dusk, saying he must get to work. Staff note he follows the same route daily and becomes agitated when redirected sharply. His pain is controlled. Which plan is the safest?", "options": {"A": "Apply a physical restraint near the exit door, since wandering signals aggression and risk to others.", "B": "Redirect him firmly each time to his room and remind him he has retired.", "C": "Treat it as purposeful wandering, offer supervised walking, and secure the environment.", "D": "Give a prescribed sedative at dusk so he stays calm and stops pacing."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using restraint for a non-aggressive behaviour", "B": "Correcting his confusion instead of meeting his unmet need", "D": "Using a sedative as the first response to wandering"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat it as purposeful wandering, offer supervised walking, and secure the environment.): Wandering often reflects an old routine or unmet need, so supervised walking, meaningful activity, and a secured but open environment protect Mr. Lowe while respecting his dignity. Firm redirection and reality correction increase his agitation. Sedatives carry risk for older adults and are not a first response, and restraint is not justified for non-aggressive pacing."}, {"id": 372, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, the nurse notices throw rugs in the hallway and a dim bathroom at the home of Mrs. Lena Kowalski, 82, who has osteoporosis. She says she fears breaking a hip and wants to stay in bed more. What is the best teaching?", "options": {"A": "Leave the rugs where they are and wear thick padded socks, since they cushion a fall.", "B": "Stay in bed most of the day, because rest protects fragile bones from injury.", "C": "Limit her to a sturdy chair near the kitchen table and stop walking outside that room.", "D": "Remove loose rugs, improve lighting, and keep her walking daily to maintain balance."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaving a tripping hazard in place", "B": "Believing bed rest protects bones, though it weakens them", "C": "Restricting mobility, which raises long-term fall risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Remove loose rugs, improve lighting, and keep her walking daily to maintain balance.): Falls cause most fractures in osteoporosis, so removing hazards, improving lighting, and keeping her active to preserve muscle and balance lowers risk best. Bed rest speeds bone loss and weakness. Loose rugs remain a trip hazard whatever she wears. Restricting her to one room reduces conditioning and increases the chance of a later fall."}, {"id": 373, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Kareem Aziz tells the nurse loudly that the hospital does not care, because his mother has waited four hours for a bed. The nurse knows several emergencies caused the delay and that the family has received no updates. Which response best reflects therapeutic communication?", "options": {"A": "Advise him to submit the complaint in writing and then end the discussion.", "B": "Acknowledge his frustration, apologize for the wait, and explain what is being done next.", "C": "Explain in detail how many emergencies caused the delay to show the wait was unavoidable.", "D": "Tell him the staff are doing their best and ask him to lower his voice."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Deflecting him to a formal process too early", "C": "Arguing facts before acknowledging feelings", "D": "Dismissing the concern while correcting his tone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acknowledge his frustration, apologize for the wait, and explain what is being done next.): People upset about care need to feel heard before explanations help, so recognizing his frustration, apologizing for the wait, and describing the next step builds trust. Defending the delay with facts sounds defensive. Telling him to lower his voice escalates the conflict. Sending him to a written complaint ends the conversation without addressing his worry."}, {"id": 374, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Hugo Alvarez, 67, takes metformin and is two days after a left-sided stroke, leaving a flaccid right arm that hangs at his side when he sits. The nurse is repositioning him in bed. Which action best protects the shoulder and prevents contracture?", "options": {"A": "Keep the arm in a tight sling during every repositioning.", "B": "Position the arm flexed against his chest with a rolled cloth in the hand.", "C": "Support the arm on a pillow and move him by the trunk.", "D": "Lift him by the armpits using a firm underarm grip."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Holding the flaccid arm in a tight sling in bed", "B": "Using a flexed position that encourages contracture", "D": "Lifting through the armpits, which strains the shoulder"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Support the arm on a pillow and move him by the trunk.): A flaccid arm cannot support the shoulder joint, so positioning it supported on a pillow, forward and extended, and moving him by the trunk protects against subluxation and contracture. Holding it flexed with a roll encourages shortening. A tight sling in bed can pull the joint and limit position changes, and lifting under the armpits drags on the shoulder."}, {"id": 375, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A nurse packs a deep cavity wound on an adult's buttock. The wound bed is granulating with moderate drainage. After packing, the client reports pressure pain, and the gauze bulges above skin level at the wound edge. What is the nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Cut the gauze flush with the skin edge and tape over it.", "B": "Leave the packing in place and add a tighter outer dressing for pressure.", "C": "Press more gauze into the cavity until the bulging goes down and no space remains.", "D": "Remove the gauze and repack loosely to the cavity base, leaving a tail visible."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Cutting the tail so the gauze cannot be located", "B": "Applying pressure that compromises granulating tissue", "C": "Overfilling the cavity beyond what it holds"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Remove the gauze and repack loosely to the cavity base, leaving a tail visible.): Packing should fill the cavity gently to contact all surfaces, and bulging with pain means it is too tight, which compromises circulation to healing tissue, so repacking loosely is correct. Adding pressure or forcing in more gauze worsens the problem. Cutting the gauze flush risks losing a piece inside the wound."}, {"id": 376, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Tom Bearskin, 48, an Indigenous client at a primary care clinic, has poorly controlled diabetes and says he missed visits because of distance and earlier negative experiences with care. A colleague says outcomes mainly result from lifestyle choices. Which response best reflects understanding of colonization's ongoing effects?", "options": {"A": "Explain that missed visits reflect personal choices, then book more frequent diabetes teaching sessions.", "B": "Record that he is noncompliant and refer him to an educator without discussing barriers.", "C": "Recognize that colonial policies and systemic barriers still shape his access, and plan care with him.", "D": "Suggest he transfer to a clinic closer to home and close his file here."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating missed visits as personal choice and adding more teaching", "B": "Labelling the client noncompliant without exploring barriers", "D": "Ending care instead of addressing access barriers"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recognize that colonial policies and systemic barriers still shape his access, and plan care with him.): Health gaps for Indigenous people are tied to colonial history, such as residential schools, displacement, and racism in care, which still limit access and trust, so culturally safe care addresses those barriers with the client. Adding teaching while blaming choices repeats the bias. Labelling him noncompliant or closing his file here worsens the barriers."}, {"id": 377, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a clinic, a client with no hypertension history walks in from the parking lot, sits with legs crossed and the arm hanging at his side, and the nurse records 156/94 mmHg. What is the best action before reporting this reading?", "options": {"A": "Report 156/94 but note the arm position and crossed legs for the physician.", "B": "Allow five minutes of seated rest, arm supported at heart level, then repeat.", "C": "Repeat immediately on the other arm while that arm hangs at his side.", "D": "Repeat after two minutes of rest, arm hanging, then average the readings."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reporting a reading known to be taken with poor technique", "C": "Repeating the same technique error on the other arm", "D": "Shortening rest and leaving the arm unsupported"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Allow five minutes of seated rest, arm supported at heart level, then repeat.): Recent walking, crossed legs, and an unsupported arm each raise a reading falsely, so resting five minutes, positioning correctly, and repeating gives a valid measurement. Reporting a flawed reading with a note still passes on a false value. Resting briefly with the arm hanging repeats the mistake, and repeating on the other arm in the same way does not fix the technique."}, {"id": 378, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Chloe finishes perineal care for Mr. Wong after an episode of incontinence and must next change the dressing on his sacral pressure injury. She has worn the same clean gloves since beginning care. What should she do between the two tasks?", "options": {"A": "Apply a second pair of gloves over the soiled first pair and proceed with the dressing.", "B": "Keep the same gloves and wipe them down with an alcohol swab before starting the dressing.", "C": "Remove only the outer glove on each hand, since the inner layer stayed clean during care.", "D": "Remove the soiled gloves, perform hand hygiene, and put on new gloves first."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Layering gloves over contaminated ones", "B": "Cleaning soiled gloves instead of replacing them", "C": "Assuming partial removal keeps hands clean"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C045", "cluster_text": "Chloe Martin, a practical nurse, works on a medical unit caring for Mr. Albert Wong, 66, who has fecal incontinence and a sacral pressure injury dressing, and for Ms. Rhea Patel, 54, admitted with suspected active pulmonary tuberculosis in an airborne isolation room. Over one shift Chloe must choose and use personal protective equipment correctly and follow routine practices consistently.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Remove the soiled gloves, perform hand hygiene, and put on new gloves first.): Moving from a contaminated task to a clean wound without changing gloves transfers organisms to the wound, so removing gloves, cleaning hands, and gloving again is required. Wiping gloves does not make them sterile. A second pair over soiled ones still touches contamination. Wearing only one layer was never the plan."}, {"id": 379, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After finishing routine care for Mr. Wong, Chloe removes her gloves. A nursing student watching her says hand hygiene is unnecessary now because the gloves had no visible tears or holes. Which statement best explains why Chloe still performs hand hygiene?", "options": {"A": "Gloves can have tiny defects and hands are contaminated during removal, so hand hygiene follows.", "B": "Hand hygiene is required after gloves only when body fluids were handled.", "C": "Gloves block pathogens well enough that hand hygiene matters only before putting them on.", "D": "Gloves are tested for defects, so hand hygiene waits until the next client contact."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Limiting hand hygiene to visible fluid exposure", "C": "Believing gloves make later hand hygiene unnecessary", "D": "Trusting glove testing and delaying hand hygiene"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C045", "cluster_text": "Chloe Martin, a practical nurse, works on a medical unit caring for Mr. Albert Wong, 66, who has fecal incontinence and a sacral pressure injury dressing, and for Ms. Rhea Patel, 54, admitted with suspected active pulmonary tuberculosis in an airborne isolation room. Over one shift Chloe must choose and use personal protective equipment correctly and follow routine practices consistently.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gloves can have tiny defects and hands are contaminated during removal, so hand hygiene follows.): Gloves are not a perfect barrier and the hands can pick up organisms as the gloves come off, so hand hygiene afterward is a required step in infection control. Judging by fluid contact or visible damage misses microscopic defects. Waiting until the next client leaves contamination on her hands in between, and hand hygiene before gloving does not cover removal."}, {"id": 380, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Chloe is about to enter Ms. Patel's room. A colleague hands her a surgical mask and says all facial protection is the same and she is running late. Ms. Patel has a cough, night sweats, and an abnormal chest x-ray. Which choice protects Chloe best?", "options": {"A": "Wear the surgical mask and ask Ms. Patel to cover her cough with a tissue.", "B": "Wear the surgical mask plus eye protection, since it blocks droplets from coughing.", "C": "Put on a fit-tested N95 respirator before entering the airborne isolation room.", "D": "Wait outside until the physician confirms the tuberculosis diagnosis, then choose a mask."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on client cough etiquette instead of a respirator", "B": "Using a droplet mask against an airborne pathogen", "D": "Delaying precautions until a diagnosis is confirmed"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C045", "cluster_text": "Chloe Martin, a practical nurse, works on a medical unit caring for Mr. Albert Wong, 66, who has fecal incontinence and a sacral pressure injury dressing, and for Ms. Rhea Patel, 54, admitted with suspected active pulmonary tuberculosis in an airborne isolation room. Over one shift Chloe must choose and use personal protective equipment correctly and follow routine practices consistently.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Put on a fit-tested N95 respirator before entering the airborne isolation room.): Tuberculosis spreads by tiny airborne particles that pass through a surgical mask, so a fit-tested N95 in an airborne isolation room is the right protection while the diagnosis is suspected. Adding eye protection does not stop inhalation. A tissue lowers spread but does not protect Chloe. Precautions begin with the suspicion, not the confirmation."}, {"id": 381, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Chloe puts on her fit-tested N95 and adjusts the straps. When she inhales sharply, air leaks around the nose edge and her glasses fog. She has grown light stubble since her last fit test. What should she do before entering?", "options": {"A": "Switch to a surgical mask and add a face shield before entering the room.", "B": "Readjust the nose clip and straps, recheck the seal, and request refitting if needed.", "C": "Tape the nose edge of the respirator to her skin and enter the room.", "D": "Enter the room, since a small nose leak is common and her fit test was done."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Replacing a respirator with a mask that does not protect from airborne spread", "C": "Taping a leaking respirator instead of refitting it", "D": "Accepting a known leak because of an earlier fit test"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C045", "cluster_text": "Chloe Martin, a practical nurse, works on a medical unit caring for Mr. Albert Wong, 66, who has fecal incontinence and a sacral pressure injury dressing, and for Ms. Rhea Patel, 54, admitted with suspected active pulmonary tuberculosis in an airborne isolation room. Over one shift Chloe must choose and use personal protective equipment correctly and follow routine practices consistently.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Readjust the nose clip and straps, recheck the seal, and request refitting if needed.): A respirator protects only when it seals to the face, and a leak on a user seal check means air bypasses the filter, so Chloe must readjust, recheck, and seek refitting if needed. Facial hair changes can invalidate an old fit test. A surgical mask does not filter airborne particles, and taping a leak does not create a reliable seal."}, {"id": 382, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the long term care home, Mr. Ivan Petrov, 84, has an enlarged prostate and frequent small dribbles of urine. His lower abdomen feels firm and rounded, and he says he cannot empty his bladder. What is the nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Apply absorbent briefs and reassure him that dribbling is normal with aging, then reassess next shift.", "B": "Check for bladder distension with a bladder scan and report for catheterization.", "C": "Restrict his fluids from the afternoon on to reduce how much urine leaks overnight.", "D": "Start a toileting schedule every two hours and record each voided volume in a bladder diary."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Normalizing a sign of urinary retention", "C": "Limiting fluids, which does not relieve retention", "D": "Treating retention-related leakage with scheduling only"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C042", "cluster_text": "Dana Whitfield, a practical nurse, rotates through a long term care home, a postpartum follow-up clinic, a community program, and an emergency department. Over one week she cares for clients with different urinary problems. She must recognize each pattern, teach appropriately, and escalate concerns to the RN, nurse practitioner, or physician within her scope of practice. Each client presents differently.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Check for bladder distension with a bladder scan and report for catheterization.): Frequent small dribbles with a firm lower abdomen and a sense of incomplete emptying point to overflow from retention, so confirming distension and reporting for catheterization is the priority. A schedule alone leaves the bladder full. Fluid restriction does not clear retained urine, and briefs with reassurance miss a condition that can damage the kidneys."}, {"id": 383, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the postpartum clinic, Ms. Aisha Rahman, 29, says that six months after delivery she leaks small amounts of urine when she coughs, laughs, or lifts her toddler. She has no burning or urgency. Which teaching addresses the likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Teach timed voiding every two hours to keep the bladder empty.", "B": "Recommend caffeine reduction and double voiding to lower bladder irritation.", "C": "Recommend that she avoid lifting her toddler for several months and rest until the leaking settles.", "D": "Teach daily pelvic floor muscle exercises to strengthen bladder outlet support."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a bladder retraining approach for stress leakage", "B": "Applying urge incontinence measures to a muscle weakness problem", "C": "Imposing a long activity restriction that ignores muscle weakness"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C042", "cluster_text": "Dana Whitfield, a practical nurse, rotates through a long term care home, a postpartum follow-up clinic, a community program, and an emergency department. Over one week she cares for clients with different urinary problems. She must recognize each pattern, teach appropriately, and escalate concerns to the RN, nurse practitioner, or physician within her scope of practice. Each client presents differently.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Teach daily pelvic floor muscle exercises to strengthen bladder outlet support.): Leaking with coughing, laughing, or lifting without urgency is the pattern of stress incontinence, caused by weak pelvic floor support, which exercises strengthen. Timed voiding and caffeine reduction suit urgency, not this pattern. Restricting lifting her child is impractical and does not treat the cause."}, {"id": 384, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Hilda Brandt, 72, lives at home and reports sudden strong urges to void, then rushes to the bathroom and sometimes leaks on the way. She goes to the toilet at the first urge each time. The nurse plans bladder training. What is the best teaching?", "options": {"A": "Gradually lengthen the time between voids, using relaxation and distraction to delay urges.", "B": "Use scheduled voiding every hour and avoid waiting, since delaying damages the bladder.", "C": "Go to the toilet immediately each time the urge appears so the bladder does not stretch.", "D": "Cut caffeine and keep voiding at the first urge, since training is unneeded."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Scheduling frequent voids and fearing delay harms the bladder", "C": "Reinforcing the urge habit by rushing every time", "D": "Reducing caffeine while keeping the urge habit unchanged"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C042", "cluster_text": "Dana Whitfield, a practical nurse, rotates through a long term care home, a postpartum follow-up clinic, a community program, and an emergency department. Over one week she cares for clients with different urinary problems. She must recognize each pattern, teach appropriately, and escalate concerns to the RN, nurse practitioner, or physician within her scope of practice. Each client presents differently.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gradually lengthen the time between voids, using relaxation and distraction to delay urges.): Bladder training retrains the bladder to hold more by slowly extending intervals with relaxation and distraction. Responding to every urge or voiding hourly keeps the pattern going. Cutting caffeine may help but does not replace training, and the idea that waiting damages the bladder is a myth."}, {"id": 385, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the emergency department, Mr. Leon Fraser, 45, has right flank pain, a temperature of 39.1 C, chills, and nausea after three days of burning urination. His heart rate is 112 beats per minute. Which interpretation of these findings is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Kidney stone passage is likely, so pain control comes before any infection assessment.", "B": "A simple bladder infection is likely and can be managed with fluids and outpatient follow up.", "C": "A kidney infection is likely and needs prompt assessment before sepsis develops.", "D": "Muscle strain from lifting is likely, so he can wait for routine assessment."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Prioritizing analgesia over a fever source", "B": "Treating pyelonephritis like cystitis", "D": "Attributing flank pain to a muscle cause"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C042", "cluster_text": "Dana Whitfield, a practical nurse, rotates through a long term care home, a postpartum follow-up clinic, a community program, and an emergency department. Over one week she cares for clients with different urinary problems. She must recognize each pattern, teach appropriately, and escalate concerns to the RN, nurse practitioner, or physician within her scope of practice. Each client presents differently.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A kidney infection is likely and needs prompt assessment before sepsis develops.): Flank pain with fever, chills, nausea, and tachycardia after urinary symptoms indicates the infection has reached the kidney, which can lead to sepsis, so he needs prompt assessment. A bladder infection alone does not cause systemic signs. Stones can coexist but do not set aside the infection, and muscle strain does not explain fever or urinary burning."}, {"id": 386, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A practical nurse gave a scheduled medication late after being interrupted three times during a twelve hour shift without a break, and no harm reached the client. The manager asks how to analyze the event. Which approach best reflects a safety culture?", "options": {"A": "Hold a brief debrief without documenting, since no harm reached the client.", "B": "Review only the medication record and discipline the nurse if it shows a deviation.", "C": "Examine workload, interruptions, and processes, then improve how the unit works.", "D": "Remind staff to focus during medication passes and post a reminder sign."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Skipping documentation and analysis because no harm occurred", "B": "Limiting the review to the record and punishing", "D": "Relying on reminders instead of changing work conditions"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Examine workload, interruptions, and processes, then improve how the unit works.): Human factors such as interruptions, fatigue, and workload shape most errors, so a safety culture studies the conditions around the event and improves the system. Reminders to focus leave the same interruptions in place. A record-only review with discipline discourages reporting, and skipping documentation loses the chance to learn."}, {"id": 387, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Ms. Rita Cardinal, 41, an Indigenous woman on a mental health unit, becomes distressed and withdrawn after a staff member moves her belongings without asking. She says her grandmother attended residential school and nothing was ever safe. Which interpretation best guides the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Her distress comes from mental illness alone, so redirect her and document noncompliance.", "B": "Her reaction reflects personal distrust, so keep her routine fixed and limit questions.", "C": "Her distress is a side effect of medication, so request a dose review.", "D": "Her reaction may reflect intergenerational trauma, so offer choice-based care and explain each action first."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Attributing the reaction to illness while ignoring history", "B": "Attributing the reaction to personality rather than trauma history", "C": "Assuming a drug cause without assessing the trigger"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Her reaction may reflect intergenerational trauma, so offer choice-based care and explain each action first.): The harms of residential schools continue across generations, so a loss of control over her belongings can trigger fear, and care that offers choice and explains each step respects her safety. Treating it as personal distrust or a drug effect ignores the trigger she described. Labelling her noncompliant does not address it either."}, {"id": 388, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A near miss of a wrong insulin dose occurred on a ward when the pharmacy label was unclear, the order was handwritten, and the double check was skipped for short staffing. The unit lead asks how to prevent recurrence. Which action best applies the Swiss cheese model?", "options": {"A": "Add a second pharmacist check at the label stage, since labelling caused the near miss.", "B": "Strengthen several defences together: clear labelling, legible electronic orders, and protected time for double checks.", "C": "Retrain the nurse who nearly gave the wrong dose, since one person's error started the chain.", "D": "Switch to electronic orders alone, since handwriting was the main hole in the chain."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Fixing one layer after a failure of several", "C": "Locating the cause in one person", "D": "Treating one change as enough to close the chain"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Strengthen several defences together: clear labelling, legible electronic orders, and protected time for double checks.): The Swiss cheese model says harm reaches clients when holes in several layers line up, so closing gaps in labelling, ordering, and checking at once makes alignment unlikely. Fixing only the label or only the order leaves other holes open. Retraining one nurse does not change the system, and removing the double check strips a defence."}, {"id": 389, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The school clinic practical nurse notices several healing cuts on the forearms of Jasmine, 15, who says she cuts to feel less numb. She denies wanting to die, and the marks are shallow and recent. Which response is most therapeutic and safest?", "options": {"A": "Express concern, take the cuts seriously, and ask her to promise she will stop cutting.", "B": "Respond calmly without judgment, ask about suicidal thoughts, treat the wounds, and involve the counsellor.", "C": "Treat the wounds and ask nothing further, since she denied wanting to die.", "D": "Warn her the cuts will scar and tell her the school may discipline her."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Demanding a promise that ignores underlying distress", "C": "Accepting a denial without exploring risk", "D": "Reacting with shock and punishment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Respond calmly without judgment, ask about suicidal thoughts, treat the wounds, and involve the counsellor.): Self-injury often manages painful emotion rather than signaling intent to die, but risk still needs a direct question, so a calm, nonjudgmental response with wound care and counsellor involvement is best. Demanding a promise or threatening discipline damages trust and may drive hiding. Taking the denial at face value skips the risk assessment."}, {"id": 390, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Gus Reyes, 63, has cough and shortness of breath after pneumonia. On the posterior chest the nurse hears resonance over the upper lobes and a dull, flat note over the right lower lobe, with diminished breath sounds there. What is the most likely interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Resonance over upper lobes with dullness below is expected after pneumonia and needs no report.", "B": "Dullness at the right base suggests fluid or consolidation, which should be reported for assessment.", "C": "A dull note over the lower lobe is normal because it overlies the liver.", "D": "Diminished sounds at the base mean shallow breathing, so coach deep breaths and recheck."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Expecting a dull note after pneumonia and skipping a report", "C": "Mistaking an abnormal dull note for normal organ dullness", "D": "Explaining a dull note as shallow breathing alone"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C046", "cluster_text": "Owen Bell, a practical nurse, performs physical assessments on a respiratory ward, in a community visiting program, and in a long term care home. Over one week he completes percussion, skin, perfusion, and edema assessments on four different clients. He must interpret each finding, compare it with expected norms, and report concerns to the RN or physician. Findings are compared with earlier records.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dullness at the right base suggests fluid or consolidation, which should be reported for assessment.): Resonance is normal over air-filled lung, while dullness with reduced breath sounds over one lobe suggests fluid or consolidation replacing air, so it is reported for further assessment. Normal upper lobes do not cancel a lower finding, and a new dull area after pneumonia should not be assumed expected. Coaching breathing alone delays assessment."}, {"id": 391, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit to a four-day-old breastfed infant, the nurse notices yellow skin on the face and chest. The mother says the baby feeds well and has wet diapers. Natural light is poor in the room. What is the best assessment technique?", "options": {"A": "Assess in daylight, gently blanch the skin, check the sclera, and note downward spread.", "B": "Skip the skin assessment, since newborn jaundice is expected and blood tests confirm it.", "C": "Judge colour by the face alone in the current lighting, since it shows most clearly there.", "D": "Look under a lamp and report jaundice only if the palms and soles are yellow."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Withholding assessment because jaundice is common", "C": "Assessing colour under poor lighting", "D": "Using lamp light and waiting for a late sign"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C046", "cluster_text": "Owen Bell, a practical nurse, performs physical assessments on a respiratory ward, in a community visiting program, and in a long term care home. Over one week he completes percussion, skin, perfusion, and edema assessments on four different clients. He must interpret each finding, compare it with expected norms, and report concerns to the RN or physician. Findings are compared with earlier records.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Assess in daylight, gently blanch the skin, check the sclera, and note downward spread.): Skin colour is judged reliably in daylight, and blanching plus checking the sclera and the downward spread shows how significant the jaundice is, so the nurse can report accurate findings. Poor lighting distorts colour. Waiting for yellow palms and soles delays recognition, and expected newborn jaundice still needs assessment."}, {"id": 392, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Wilf Anders, 79, reports a cold left foot. The nurse finds a weak but palpable pedal pulse, capillary refill of five seconds, pale skin, and a foot cooler than the right. His blood pressure is normal. Which conclusion is best?", "options": {"A": "Slow refill reflects dehydration, so encourage fluids and recheck in the morning.", "B": "Cool skin is expected in older adults, so apply a heating pad to restore warmth.", "C": "Perfusion is adequate because the pulse is palpable, so reassess at the next routine check.", "D": "Reduced arterial perfusion is likely despite the palpable pulse, so report promptly."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Attributing a one-sided finding to dehydration", "B": "Applying heat to a limb with poor perfusion", "C": "Relying on pulse alone while ignoring colour, warmth, and refill"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C046", "cluster_text": "Owen Bell, a practical nurse, performs physical assessments on a respiratory ward, in a community visiting program, and in a long term care home. Over one week he completes percussion, skin, perfusion, and edema assessments on four different clients. He must interpret each finding, compare it with expected norms, and report concerns to the RN or physician. Findings are compared with earlier records.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduced arterial perfusion is likely despite the palpable pulse, so report promptly.): A palpable pulse does not rule out poor perfusion when the foot is pale, cool, and slow to refill, and the one-sided pattern suggests an arterial problem that needs prompt reporting. Waiting for routine checks risks tissue loss. Heat can burn insensate, poorly perfused skin, and dehydration would not cool just one foot."}, {"id": 393, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Doris Neufeld, 77, is admitted with heart failure and has gained 1.5 kg in two days. The nurse presses her shin for five seconds and leaves an indentation about 4 mm deep that is slow to refill. How should she document and interpret this finding?", "options": {"A": "Document trace edema and skip depth grading, since weight gain already shows fluid retention.", "B": "Document non-pitting edema, since the indentation took several seconds to return.", "C": "Document pitting edema graded by indentation depth and rebound time, and compare it with weight trends.", "D": "Document mild edema graded by indentation depth alone, without comparing weight trends."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Omitting grading because weight already shows fluid", "B": "Mislabeling slow rebound as non-pitting", "D": "Grading depth but ignoring the daily weight trend"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C046", "cluster_text": "Owen Bell, a practical nurse, performs physical assessments on a respiratory ward, in a community visiting program, and in a long term care home. Over one week he completes percussion, skin, perfusion, and edema assessments on four different clients. He must interpret each finding, compare it with expected norms, and report concerns to the RN or physician. Findings are compared with earlier records.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Document pitting edema graded by indentation depth and rebound time, and compare it with weight trends.): A lasting indentation that rebounds slowly is pitting edema, and grading its depth and rebound time lets the team track fluid retention alongside daily weight in heart failure. Skipping grading loses detail, and grading without weight trends misses the overall pattern. Slow rebound does not make it non-pitting, and eventual rebound does not mean no edema."}, {"id": 394, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Tomas Greene, 70, after a right-sided stroke, eats only from the right side of his tray and does not look to the left. Staff placed his call bell and water on his right. Which nursing approach best addresses his neglect?", "options": {"A": "Cue him to turn his head and scan left, and approach from the left side.", "B": "Leave the behaviour alone, since neglect resolves in days without cueing.", "C": "Place the tray on his left side only so he must reach there to eat.", "D": "Keep all his items on the right so he can reach them easily and avoid frustration."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Expecting spontaneous recovery with no intervention", "C": "Overloading the neglected side without cues or safety", "D": "Reinforcing neglect by keeping everything on one side"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cue him to turn his head and scan left, and approach from the left side.): Unilateral neglect means he does not attend to the left, so cueing him to scan, and approaching from that side gradually retrains attention while keeping items reachable. Keeping everything on the right strengthens the neglect. Moving everything left abruptly with no cues risks missed meals and injury, and waiting does not retrain attention."}, {"id": 395, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Nadia Farouk, 42, visits primary care with fatigue and low mood, poor sleep, loss of interest, and appetite change for over three weeks. Her thyroid test last month was normal. She says it is probably work stress. What is the nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Attribute the fatigue to physical illness and suggest iron tablets and extra rest first.", "B": "Reassure her that work stress passes on its own and suggest a vacation soon.", "C": "Advise her to see a private counsellor and avoid discussing mood today.", "D": "Use a validated depression screening tool, ask about suicide, and report the findings."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Explaining mood symptoms by physical illness alone", "B": "Minimizing symptoms with reassurance and delayed follow up", "C": "Deferring assessment to an outside counsellor"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use a validated depression screening tool, ask about suicide, and report the findings.): A cluster of low mood, loss of interest, and sleep and appetite change lasting weeks fits a major depressive episode, so structured screening, a direct question about suicide, and reporting to the physician or NP is the safe next step. Looking only for physical causes delays care. Reassurance and a long wait leave risk unchecked, and avoiding the topic skips assessment."}, {"id": 396, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the labour unit, Ms. Kayla Brooks, 27, has spontaneous rupture of membranes with clear fluid, and the baby's head is high and not engaged. The fetal heart rate then drops to 90 beats per minute. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Check for a palpable cord, relieve pressure on it, and call for help.", "B": "Apply oxygen and recheck the rate in five minutes, then call for help.", "C": "Do a vaginal exam after the next contraction to check dilation.", "D": "Ask her to walk to help the head engage, then recheck the rate afterward."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delaying help while rechecking the rate", "C": "Prioritizing dilation assessment over cord checking and help", "D": "Using activity that can worsen cord compression"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check for a palpable cord, relieve pressure on it, and call for help.): Rupture with a high presenting part and sudden fetal bradycardia suggests cord prolapse, an emergency, so checking for the cord, relieving pressure on it, and calling for help comes first. Oxygen and rechecking delay the response, and waiting for the next contraction wastes minutes. Walking can press the cord further."}, {"id": 397, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Walter Ng, 80, takes furosemide daily in long term care. Today he has leg weakness, an irregular pulse of 108, and mild leg cramps. His appetite is fair and his blood pressure is 118/70 mmHg. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "High potassium is likely since furosemide retains it, so hold fluids and recheck his pulse.", "B": "Dehydration from reduced appetite is the cause, so offer extra fluids and record intake.", "C": "Low potassium from the diuretic is possible, so report promptly for testing.", "D": "Weakness fits age and low activity, so encourage rest and recheck vital signs next shift."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reversing the drug's effect on potassium", "B": "Settling on dehydration without a laboratory check", "D": "Writing off drug-related signs as aging"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Low potassium from the diuretic is possible, so report promptly for testing.): Furosemide causes potassium loss, and weakness, cramps, and an irregular pulse suggest hypokalemia that can progress to dangerous arrhythmia, so prompt reporting and testing is needed. Attributing signs to age delays care. Furosemide lowers potassium rather than raising it, and fluids with no testing leave an electrolyte problem unconfirmed."}, {"id": 398, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Helen Park, 58, says her hand joints are stiff for over an hour each morning, both wrists are warm and swollen, and she feels tired most days. She asks the nurse why this differs from her mother's arthritis. Which statement best explains the likely pattern of her condition?", "options": {"A": "Bony enlargement at the finger joints points to rheumatoid arthritis rather than osteoarthritis.", "B": "Stiffness lasting more than an hour is the typical pattern of osteoarthritis in the hands.", "C": "Long morning stiffness, symmetrical swollen joints, and fatigue fit inflammatory rheumatoid arthritis.", "D": "Joint pain that worsens late in the day with use is typical of rheumatoid arthritis."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assigning bony nodes to the inflammatory condition", "B": "Treating long stiffness as typical of osteoarthritis", "D": "Reversing the daily pattern of the two conditions"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Long morning stiffness, symmetrical swollen joints, and fatigue fit inflammatory rheumatoid arthritis.): Rheumatoid arthritis is inflammatory, causing long morning stiffness, warm swollen symmetrical joints, and fatigue, while osteoarthritis brings short stiffness that worsens with use. Stiffness over an hour is not typical of osteoarthritis. Late-day worsening describes osteoarthritis, and bony enlargement is a feature of osteoarthritis, not rheumatoid disease."}, {"id": 399, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In rehabilitation, Mr. Anil Chopra, 76, has left-sided weakness after a stroke and is moving from bed to wheelchair with a stand-pivot transfer. The wheelchair brakes are on and the physiotherapist has cleared him for the transfer. The nurse positions the chair. Which placement is correct?", "options": {"A": "Place the chair at his stronger right side so he pivots on that leg.", "B": "Place the chair behind him and use a gait belt to lift straight up.", "C": "Place the chair on his left and guide his weak arm to push.", "D": "Place the chair at his weaker left side so the weak leg bears weight."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using a lift instead of a stand-pivot toward the stronger side", "C": "Relying on the weak arm to support the move", "D": "Transferring toward the weak side"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Place the chair at his stronger right side so he pivots on that leg.): Moving toward the stronger side lets him bear weight and pivot on his strong leg, which is safer for both client and nurse. Transferring toward the weak side asks the weaker limb to support him. A straight lift from behind is not a stand-pivot transfer, and pushing with a weak arm is unreliable."}, {"id": 400, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Dev Menon, 36, started an SSRI five days ago and calls the community nurse, saying nothing has changed, he has mild nausea, and he wants to stop the medication. He denies any thoughts of self-harm. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Suggest he reduce the dose by half for a week and call back.", "B": "Explain that benefit takes weeks, early nausea often settles, and ask him to continue.", "C": "Advise taking the dose at night with food and stopping if nausea continues.", "D": "Agree that it is not working and suggest stopping so another drug can be tried."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing the dose without prescriber direction", "C": "Giving stop criteria that end treatment before onset of action", "D": "Agrees to stop before the expected onset of action"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain that benefit takes weeks, early nausea often settles, and ask him to continue.): SSRIs usually need several weeks to work, and mild early nausea commonly fades, so explaining this and encouraging him to continue while reporting mood changes supports adherence. Halving the dose on his own changes treatment without guidance. Stopping when nausea continues or agreeing it is not working abandons treatment too soon."}, {"id": 401, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a school visit, Liam, 8, is wheezing mildly but speaking in full sentences. He uses his reliever inhaler by breathing in fast and hard through the mouthpiece with no spacer, although his action plan calls for one. What teaching is best?", "options": {"A": "Teach him to inhale slowly without a spacer and hold his breath briefly.", "B": "Have him shake the inhaler, give two puffs back to back, and breathe normally.", "C": "Give several puffs into the spacer at once and have him breathe normally.", "D": "Attach the spacer, give one puff at a time, then have him inhale slowly and hold."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Omitting the spacer despite the action plan", "B": "Releasing two puffs together and breathing normally", "C": "Releasing multiple puffs at once into the spacer"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Attach the spacer, give one puff at a time, then have him inhale slowly and hold.): A spacer holds the medication so a slow breath carries it into the airways instead of the mouth and throat, and one puff at a time gives a reliable dose. Skipping the spacer ignores his action plan. Puffs given together reduce delivery, and breathing normally without a slow inhale and hold deposits less drug in the lungs."}, {"id": 402, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a mental health unit, Mr. Jonas Keller, 30, tells his practical nurse he will harm his former partner tonight and names where she lives. He asks the nurse to keep it secret. He is calm and has a history of anger. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Document the threat in his chart and review it at the next team meeting this week", "B": "Explain the limits of confidentiality, report at once to the RN and physician, and document it.", "C": "Call his former partner directly to warn her and share details of his diagnosis.", "D": "Keep the information confidential, since client trust is the foundation of his ongoing treatment plan."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying action on a serious, imminent threat to an identified person", "C": "Disclosing too much health information outside the team", "D": "Treating confidentiality as unlimited despite a threat to another person"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain the limits of confidentiality, report at once to the RN and physician, and document it.): Confidentiality yields when there is a clear, serious risk to an identifiable person, so the nurse tells him honestly, escalates immediately so the team can warn and protect, and documents the details. Silence leaves someone in danger. Calling the partner herself exposes his diagnosis and bypasses the team. Waiting until a meeting is too late."}, {"id": 403, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a community clinic, Ms. Priya Desai, 31, reports two urinary infections this year. She wipes back to front, delays voiding on busy workdays, and drinks little water at her desk. The nurse plans teaching. Which instruction best lowers her risk of another infection?", "options": {"A": "Wipe front to back, void regularly, and drink fluids steadily through the day.", "B": "Restrict her fluids at work to reduce how often she needs to void.", "C": "Void after the urge becomes strong, and wipe front to back.", "D": "Take a daily cranberry supplement and keep her current voiding and wiping habits."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reducing fluids and urine flow", "C": "Keeping the habit of delaying voids", "D": "Relying on a supplement while leaving risky habits unchanged"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Wipe front to back, void regularly, and drink fluids steadily through the day.): Wiping front to back keeps bowel bacteria away from the urethra, and regular voiding and good fluid intake flush bacteria from the bladder, which targets all three of her habits. Delaying voids lets urine stagnate. A supplement alone leaves her wiping, voiding, and fluid habits unchanged, and limiting fluids lets urine stagnate."}, {"id": 404, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the medical clinic, Mr. Ahmed Sayegh, 65, who has chronic kidney disease, says he takes ibuprofen from the drugstore three times daily for knee pain. He has also noticed occasional black stools lately. Which nursing concern is highest priority?", "options": {"A": "Black stools probably come from dietary iron, so ask about food and recheck.", "B": "NSAIDs can harm the kidneys and cause bleeding, so report his use and the black stools.", "C": "Ask him to stop ibuprofen at once and wait for his next routine visit.", "D": "Advise taking ibuprofen with an antacid, since this protects both stomach and kidneys."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Explaining a bleeding sign by diet without checking", "C": "Stopping the drug but delaying reporting of possible bleeding", "D": "Believing an antacid protects the kidneys from NSAID harm"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C053", "cluster_text": "Anna Costa, a practical nurse, rotates through a medical clinic, a surgical unit, a post-anesthetic unit, and an emergency department. Each client she meets needs safe pain management. She applies medication safety principles, monitors each response, and reports changes to the RN, nurse practitioner, or physician as needed, working within her scope of practice. Pain control is a daily part of her work.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (NSAIDs can harm the kidneys and cause bleeding, so report his use and the black stools.): Regular NSAID use can reduce kidney blood flow and erode the stomach lining, and black stools suggest bleeding, so Mr. Sayegh needs prompt reporting. Stopping the drug without reporting delays assessment of possible bleeding. Attributing black stools to diet delays recognition, and an antacid does not protect the kidneys."}, {"id": 405, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a surgical unit, Mrs. Eleanor Quick, 72, receives hydromorphone for pain after surgery. She was alert at shift start, but four hours later she is drowsy though she wakes to voice, and a dose is due. What should the nurse do before giving it?", "options": {"A": "Give the dose as ordered, since her alertness at the morning assessment showed low sedation risk.", "B": "Give the dose but reassess her sedation and breathing thirty minutes afterward.", "C": "Assess sedation and breathing first, and hold and report if she is hard to rouse.", "D": "Give the dose with a family member watching her, since she wakes to voice."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on an outdated sedation assessment", "B": "Assessing only after the opioid is given", "D": "Handing safety monitoring to a family member"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C053", "cluster_text": "Anna Costa, a practical nurse, rotates through a medical clinic, a surgical unit, a post-anesthetic unit, and an emergency department. Each client she meets needs safe pain management. She applies medication safety principles, monitors each response, and reports changes to the RN, nurse practitioner, or physician as needed, working within her scope of practice. Pain control is a daily part of her work.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess sedation and breathing first, and hold and report if she is hard to rouse.): Increasing drowsiness is an early warning of opioid sedation that can progress to respiratory depression, so sedation and breathing must be assessed immediately before each dose and the dose held and reported if she is hard to rouse. A reading from shift start no longer applies. Assessing after the dose or relying on family leaves her at risk."}, {"id": 406, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the post-anesthetic unit, Mr. Gary Lim, 58, who received intravenous morphine thirty minutes ago, has a respiratory rate of 7, shallow breathing, and oxygen saturation of 88 percent. He responds only to a loud voice. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Stimulate him, support his airway, give oxygen, and call for naloxone immediately.", "B": "Apply oxygen and raise the bed, then reassess in ten minutes before calling.", "C": "Raise the head of the bed and reapply the pulse oximeter probe to another finger.", "D": "Encourage deep breaths and coughing while observing him every fifteen minutes."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delaying a call for reversal while breathing is slow", "C": "Troubleshooting the oximeter instead of treating respiratory depression", "D": "Coaching breathing and observing less often during respiratory depression"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C053", "cluster_text": "Anna Costa, a practical nurse, rotates through a medical clinic, a surgical unit, a post-anesthetic unit, and an emergency department. Each client she meets needs safe pain management. She applies medication safety principles, monitors each response, and reports changes to the RN, nurse practitioner, or physician as needed, working within her scope of practice. Pain control is a daily part of her work.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stimulate him, support his airway, give oxygen, and call for naloxone immediately.): A rate of 7 with shallow breaths, low saturation, and reduced arousal after morphine is respiratory depression, which needs immediate airway support, oxygen, and a call for reversal with naloxone. Oxygen alone with a delayed call lets him worsen. Coaching breaths and slow observation are not enough for a client who barely rouses."}, {"id": 407, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the emergency department, a man of 35 is found unresponsive with pinpoint pupils, a faint pulse, and slow shallow breathing at 6 per minute. A friend says he used something earlier. A physician orders naloxone. What is the safest nursing priority?", "options": {"A": "Place him on his side and give naloxone, supporting breathing only if he stays unresponsive.", "B": "Give oxygen by mask and delay naloxone until toxicology results confirm opioids.", "C": "Open the airway and ventilate with a bag-valve-mask while preparing titrated naloxone doses.", "D": "Give a large, rapid naloxone dose first, then begin ventilation once he wakes."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying ventilation while waiting for a drug effect", "B": "Waiting for lab confirmation in a life-threatening state", "D": "Giving a large rapid dose before oxygenating"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C053", "cluster_text": "Anna Costa, a practical nurse, rotates through a medical clinic, a surgical unit, a post-anesthetic unit, and an emergency department. Each client she meets needs safe pain management. She applies medication safety principles, monitors each response, and reports changes to the RN, nurse practitioner, or physician as needed, working within her scope of practice. Pain control is a daily part of her work.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Open the airway and ventilate with a bag-valve-mask while preparing titrated naloxone doses.): Pinpoint pupils and a rate of 6 point to opioid overdose, and the main danger is lack of oxygen, so ventilating first and giving naloxone in titrated doses reverses breathing without causing severe withdrawal or agitation. A large rapid dose can cause vomiting and aspiration. Waiting for a response before ventilating prolongs hypoxia, and delaying naloxone for lab results is unsafe."}, {"id": 408, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Alvarez, 50, arrives at the emergency department agitated and sweating. His physician added tramadol to his daily sertraline three days ago. Temperature is 38.9 C, his pupils are dilated, and both legs show rhythmic jerking. His wife believes he has influenza. What should the practical nurse suspect and report immediately?", "options": {"A": "Excess serotonin activity from the combined medications, a toxic reaction needing urgent medical assessment", "B": "Influenza with fever and body aches, which usually responds to fluids, rest, and acetaminophen at home", "C": "Alcohol withdrawal developing after a sudden stop in drinking, which requires a sedative protocol", "D": "A panic attack triggered by anxiety, which should settle with reassurance and slow breathing coaching"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Attributing fever to a viral illness despite a new drug combination", "C": "Assuming a substance cause without any supporting history", "D": "Treating jerking and agitation as anxiety alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Excess serotonin activity from the combined medications, a toxic reaction needing urgent medical assessment): Fever, dilated pupils, agitation, and rhythmic leg jerking three days after tramadol was added to sertraline point to serotonin syndrome, which can progress quickly and needs urgent physician assessment. Influenza does not explain the jerking or the pupils, so treating it at home would delay care. Panic does not cause fever or muscle jerking. Report any new serotonergic drug combination with these findings at once."}, {"id": 409, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In clinic, a 46-year-old man with an arm circumference of about 42 cm has a clinic blood pressure of 158/96 mmHg taken with the standard adult cuff. He feels well, jokes about his job stress, and had 130/82 mmHg earlier this year. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Record 158/96 mmHg as his true value and tell the physician he now has hypertension", "B": "Rest him for ten minutes and take the pressure again using the same standard cuff", "C": "Repeat the reading with a large adult cuff sized to his arm, then report it", "D": "Measure the other arm with the standard cuff and average the two readings together"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Trusting a number from a cuff that is too small", "B": "Repeating the same technical error after a delay", "D": "Averaging two readings that share the same cuff error"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat the reading with a large adult cuff sized to his arm, then report it): A cuff that is too small for a 42 cm arm gives falsely high readings, so the best action is to repeat with a correctly sized cuff before reporting. Recording the first value as true could label him hypertensive wrongly. Resting him and repeating with the same cuff, or averaging both arms, keeps the same error. Cuff width should cover about 40 percent of arm circumference."}, {"id": 410, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Lithium is part of the daily regimen of a 44-year-old woman with bipolar disorder. At her clinic visit she reports coarse hand tremor, nausea, and loose stools after two days of vomiting from gastroenteritis. She has been drinking very little and asks whether cutting back on salt would help. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Report her symptoms promptly for a lithium level and advise steady fluid and salt intake", "B": "Tell her to skip tonight's dose and restart tomorrow once her appetite has returned to normal", "C": "Reassure her that mild tremor is a common effect and she should continue the same dose", "D": "Advise her to restrict salt and fluids until the stomach settles, then resume her usual routine"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Changing a dose without consulting the prescriber", "C": "Dismissing early toxicity signs as routine effects", "D": "Restricting sodium, which raises lithium levels"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report her symptoms promptly for a lithium level and advise steady fluid and salt intake): Coarse tremor, nausea, and diarrhea after fluid loss suggest rising lithium levels, so she needs a prompt level and prescriber review. Salt and fluid restriction makes lithium retention worse because the kidneys hold lithium when sodium is low. Calling the tremor routine misses toxicity. Skipping a dose on her own is a decision for the prescriber, not the client or nurse."}, {"id": 411, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Bianchi, 83, lives in long-term care and takes digoxin daily. At 0900 he reports nausea and says he sees yellow halos around lights. His apical pulse is 52 and regular, and his scheduled dose is due now. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Give the dose with toast to settle the nausea, then recheck the pulse at noon", "B": "Give the scheduled dose, because a regular pulse shows the heart rhythm is stable", "C": "Hold the digoxin and report the symptoms and pulse rate to the RN", "D": "Hold the dose and reassess him at the next scheduled vital sign check this afternoon"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating nausea as a food issue and giving the drug", "B": "Relying on rhythm regularity while ignoring rate and symptoms", "D": "Holding the dose without reporting promptly"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hold the digoxin and report the symptoms and pulse rate to the RN): Nausea, visual halos, and a pulse of 52 are classic warning signs of digoxin toxicity in an older adult, so the dose is held and the RN is told promptly. A regular rhythm does not rule out toxicity. Giving the dose with food would add more digoxin to an already toxic level. Waiting until the afternoon delays assessment and any level or ECG that may be needed."}, {"id": 412, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At the meeting, a colleague says careless nurses keep making mistakes and need retraining. Priya notes that the same near miss occurred with different nurses on different shifts. What does this pattern best show about the cause of the repeated near misses?", "options": {"A": "An active failure by each nurse, the true root cause, to be handled individually", "B": "A one-time lapse in staffing that cleared once the unit became less busy", "C": "A latent failure in how the vials are stored, which sets up errors whoever is working", "D": "A knowledge gap about the two drugs that a refresher session can correct"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating every error as an individual's active failure", "B": "Explaining a repeated pattern as a single staffing event", "D": "Attributing a design hazard to a knowledge deficit"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C049", "cluster_text": "On a busy medical unit, three near misses in two months involved nurses almost giving the wrong injectable because two look-alike vials sat side by side in the medication room. Each time the nurse caught the error at the bedside check. The unit manager has called a team meeting, and practical nurse Priya has been asked to take part in the safety review.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A latent failure in how the vials are stored, which sets up errors whoever is working): Different nurses repeating the same near miss on different shifts points to a latent failure, a hidden weakness in the system, here side by side look-alike vials. Active failures by individuals are the visible last step, but they do not explain why many people make the same slip. A refresher leaves the hazard in place, and a one-time staffing lapse does not match three events."}, {"id": 413, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "The manager later orders a formal review of a different event, in which a client received a double dose that reached her. A staff member asks what purpose the root cause analysis serves. Which response does Priya give that best describes its aim?", "options": {"A": "To identify the nurse at fault so that disciplinary action can be applied to her record", "B": "To confirm that each nurse had completed medication competencies", "C": "To find system factors that contributed so changes can prevent a repeat", "D": "To record events so that legal liability can be decided"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using review to assign personal blame", "B": "Treating review as a competency audit of individuals", "D": "Confusing a safety review with a liability finding"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C049", "cluster_text": "On a busy medical unit, three near misses in two months involved nurses almost giving the wrong injectable because two look-alike vials sat side by side in the medication room. Each time the nurse caught the error at the bedside check. The unit manager has called a team meeting, and practical nurse Priya has been asked to take part in the safety review.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (To find system factors that contributed so changes can prevent a repeat): A root cause analysis looks beyond the person closest to the event to the contributing factors in staffing, design, communication, and process so that the system can change. Seeking an individual to discipline or auditing competencies discourages reporting and leaves hazards in place. Determining legal liability is a separate process."}, {"id": 414, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Later, Tom, a practical nurse on the same unit, notices at the bedside scan that the vial he drew up is the wrong drug. He discards it, draws up the correct one, and the client is unharmed. A coworker says there is nothing more to do. What should Tom do?", "options": {"A": "Report the near miss in the incident system with what happened and why", "B": "Document the mix-up in the client's chart and tell the client's family", "C": "Move the look-alike vials to another shelf and tell the manager", "D": "Treat the event as closed because no harm reached the client and mention nothing further"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Putting incident details in the chart and disclosing without the formal process", "C": "Fixing the hazard informally without a formal report", "D": "Ignoring a near miss because there was no harm"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C049", "cluster_text": "On a busy medical unit, three near misses in two months involved nurses almost giving the wrong injectable because two look-alike vials sat side by side in the medication room. Each time the nurse caught the error at the bedside check. The unit manager has called a team meeting, and practical nurse Priya has been asked to take part in the safety review.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the near miss in the incident system with what happened and why): Reporting the near miss through the incident system lets the organization see the pattern and fix the cause while the event costs no one harm. Treating it as closed wastes the chance to learn. Moving vials and telling the manager informally leaves no record for other units. Incident reports are kept apart from the client chart, which records the facts of care only."}, {"id": 415, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Meera, a newly graduated nurse, tells Priya she doubts the heparin dose a senior nurse just asked her to give but is afraid to look incompetent. Several staff are nearby at the nurses' station. What is the best action for Priya to take?", "options": {"A": "Tell her the senior nurse likely checked the order and the dose is probably fine", "B": "Encourage Meera to voice her concern and offer to accompany her to the charge nurse", "C": "Suggest she raise the concern later at the shift huddle once the medication has been given", "D": "Offer to give the dose herself so Meera avoids any conflict with the senior nurse"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Deferring to seniority over verification", "C": "Delaying a safety concern until after the risk", "D": "Removing the task without addressing the concern"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C049", "cluster_text": "On a busy medical unit, three near misses in two months involved nurses almost giving the wrong injectable because two look-alike vials sat side by side in the medication room. Each time the nurse caught the error at the bedside check. The unit manager has called a team meeting, and practical nurse Priya has been asked to take part in the safety review.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Encourage Meera to voice her concern and offer to accompany her to the charge nurse): A psychologically safe unit expects concerns about a high-alert drug to be raised before the dose is given, and Priya can support Meera in doing so. Waiting until after the dose defeats the purpose of speaking up. Assuming the senior nurse is right discourages checking. Taking over the task leaves the possible error and the culture problem untouched."}, {"id": 416, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Priya's team reviews safety practices for a 70-year-old client who is starting a heparin infusion. A staff member argues that all medications carry the same risk, so extra checks are wasteful. Which practice best reflects safe handling of this high-alert medication?", "options": {"A": "Having the pharmacist verify the order once at dispensing, then starting the pump", "B": "Asking the client to confirm the drug name and rate before the infusion is started", "C": "A second nurse initials the record after the infusion has already been started", "D": "An independent double check of dose and pump settings by a second qualified nurse"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on one earlier check with no pump verification", "B": "Relying on the client as the safety check", "C": "Checking after the infusion starts instead of before"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C049", "cluster_text": "On a busy medical unit, three near misses in two months involved nurses almost giving the wrong injectable because two look-alike vials sat side by side in the medication room. Each time the nurse caught the error at the bedside check. The unit manager has called a team meeting, and practical nurse Priya has been asked to take part in the safety review.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An independent double check of dose and pump settings by a second qualified nurse): Heparin is a high-alert medication, meaning errors cause serious harm, so an independent double check of dose and pump settings adds a safeguard worth the time. A check at dispensing does not cover pump programming. A check after starting cannot prevent an error, and clients cannot be relied on to catch rate errors."}, {"id": 417, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Into a community clinic rushes a caregiver holding a 7-month-old who is awake but silent, unable to cry or cough, and working hard to breathe. The caregiver says the baby was mouthing a small toy. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Give five back blows with the infant face down on your forearm, then five chest thrusts", "B": "Start with two rescue breaths and chest compressions since the infant cannot cry or cough", "C": "Perform a blind finger sweep of the mouth to hook the toy out before anything else", "D": "Give firm abdominal thrusts below the ribs while holding the infant upright against your chest"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Starting CPR on a conscious infant", "C": "Blind sweeps that can push the object deeper", "D": "Using abdominal thrusts, which risk liver injury in infants"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give five back blows with the infant face down on your forearm, then five chest thrusts): A conscious infant with a complete airway blockage needs alternating five back blows and five chest thrusts until the object clears or the infant becomes unresponsive. Abdominal thrusts can injure an infant's liver and are not used under one year. Blind finger sweeps may push the object further down. CPR begins only if the infant becomes unresponsive."}, {"id": 418, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor's admission paperwork is nearly complete when the clerk hands the practical nurse his consent form for the hernia repair and asks whether the nurse is responsible for obtaining his informed consent, since she is holding the form. Who holds that responsibility?", "options": {"A": "The charge nurse who co-signs every surgical consent form before the client leaves the unit", "B": "The unit manager who reviews each surgical form for completeness before it enters a client chart", "C": "The surgeon performing the procedure, who must explain risks and alternatives", "D": "The practical nurse who prepares him on the unit and holds the form"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming a nurse's co-signature creates informed consent", "B": "Mistaking administrative review for obtaining consent", "D": "Confusing form handling with the duty to inform"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C050", "cluster_text": "Mr. Okafor, 62, is being admitted to a surgical unit for a hernia repair tomorrow morning. A consent form signed in the surgeon's office is in his chart. His first language is Igbo and he speaks limited English. His adult son usually comes with him. The practical nurse is completing the admission. Surgery is booked for 0800 tomorrow, and the nurse is completing the admission.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The surgeon performing the procedure, who must explain risks and alternatives): The person who performs the procedure is responsible for giving the information and obtaining informed consent. A practical nurse who has the form on the unit does not take on that duty by handling it. A charge nurse's signature or a manager's review checks paperwork, not the client's understanding. If consent is unclear, the nurse tells the surgeon."}, {"id": 419, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "The surgeon returns and asks the practical nurse to witness Mr. Okafor's signature on a second form that updates the operative site. Mr. Okafor signs in front of her without asking questions. What does the nurse's signature as a witness confirm on the form?", "options": {"A": "That the surgeon's explanation covered all required content in a clear and complete way", "B": "That he understood the risks and benefits of the surgery after the surgeon's explanation", "C": "That he signed voluntarily and the signature is his, not that he understood the risks", "D": "That the nurse accepts accountability for the information given to him about the surgery"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming a witness audits the explanation", "B": "Equating a witness with validation of understanding", "D": "Believing a witness takes over the surgeon's accountability"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C050", "cluster_text": "Mr. Okafor, 62, is being admitted to a surgical unit for a hernia repair tomorrow morning. A consent form signed in the surgeon's office is in his chart. His first language is Igbo and he speaks limited English. His adult son usually comes with him. The practical nurse is completing the admission. Surgery is booked for 0800 tomorrow, and the nurse is completing the admission.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (That he signed voluntarily and the signature is his, not that he understood the risks): A witness attests that the client signed the form freely and that the signature is genuine. The witness does not judge how well the client understood, nor audit the surgeon's explanation, nor take on the surgeon's duty to inform. If the nurse doubts understanding, she informs the surgeon before witnessing."}, {"id": 420, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Later, while the nurse takes his history, Mr. Okafor, already signed, says quietly through his son that he is no longer sure he wants the operation. He asks whether anyone will be upset if he waits. What is the nurse's best response?", "options": {"A": "Tell him the surgeon will discuss his concerns after the operation if needed", "B": "Explain that withdrawing consent after signing requires the surgeon's written permission first", "C": "Reassure him that preoperative nerves are very common and continue with the admission checklist", "D": "Pause the preoperative routine, listen to him, and tell the surgeon so consent can be revisited"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Postponing his concerns until after surgery", "B": "Misstating the rules on withdrawing consent", "C": "Normalizing doubt instead of reporting it"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C050", "cluster_text": "Mr. Okafor, 62, is being admitted to a surgical unit for a hernia repair tomorrow morning. A consent form signed in the surgeon's office is in his chart. His first language is Igbo and he speaks limited English. His adult son usually comes with him. The practical nurse is completing the admission. Surgery is booked for 0800 tomorrow, and the nurse is completing the admission.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pause the preoperative routine, listen to him, and tell the surgeon so consent can be revisited): Consent is ongoing and can be withdrawn at any time, so a client who expresses doubt after signing needs his concerns heard and the surgeon told. A signed form does not outweigh his current wishes. Promising a discussion after surgery comes too late, and withdrawal does not need written permission. Reassuring him and continuing ignores a possible withdrawal."}, {"id": 421, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor wishes to ask the surgeon questions about anesthesia. His son offers to interpret and says he can explain everything in Igbo and English. What should the practical nurse do so that Mr. Okafor can ask and receive answers?", "options": {"A": "Provide an Igbo translation of the consent form and let him read it", "B": "Allow his son to interpret since he knows his father's history and speaks both languages well", "C": "Arrange a qualified interpreter so he can ask questions and understand answers", "D": "Use a staff member who speaks some Igbo to explain the anesthesia plan"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Substituting a written translation for two-way communication", "B": "Using a family member as medical interpreter", "D": "Using an untrained bilingual staff member as interpreter"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C050", "cluster_text": "Mr. Okafor, 62, is being admitted to a surgical unit for a hernia repair tomorrow morning. A consent form signed in the surgeon's office is in his chart. His first language is Igbo and he speaks limited English. His adult son usually comes with him. The practical nurse is completing the admission. Surgery is booked for 0800 tomorrow, and the nurse is completing the admission.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Arrange a qualified interpreter so he can ask questions and understand answers): A qualified interpreter gives accurate, neutral translation of medical terms and lets the client speak freely about his fears. A family member or a staff member with some Igbo may shorten or soften information and may not know medical terms. A written form does not let him ask questions, and a nod does not show comprehension."}, {"id": 422, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Months later Mr. Okafor has recovered well and returns to the follow-up clinic. The practical nurse would like to photograph his healed incision for a staff teaching presentation. He agreed to the surgery itself last spring and has voiced no objection. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the surgeon in the hallway if the photograph can be used for teaching", "B": "Rely on his surgical consent, which covers photographs taken as part of his routine care", "C": "Obtain his specific consent for the photograph and its teaching use before taking any picture", "D": "Take the photo without his face or name, since that removes any need for consent"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Seeking the surgeon's permission instead of the client's", "B": "Treating surgical consent as covering teaching images", "D": "Assuming de-identified photos need no consent"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C050", "cluster_text": "Mr. Okafor, 62, is being admitted to a surgical unit for a hernia repair tomorrow morning. A consent form signed in the surgeon's office is in his chart. His first language is Igbo and he speaks limited English. His adult son usually comes with him. The practical nurse is completing the admission. Surgery is booked for 0800 tomorrow, and the nurse is completing the admission.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Obtain his specific consent for the photograph and its teaching use before taking any picture): Photographs for teaching are not part of his care, so they need his own informed consent, covering what will be shown and how it will be used. Surgical consent does not extend to that purpose. A photo without a face may still identify him by a scar or other features. Only the client can give permission for his image."}, {"id": 423, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In long-term care, a practical nurse sees a personal support worker shout at a resident and roughly yank her arm while dressing her. The nurse worries she could be sued if she reports and is wrong. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the personal support worker for her side before deciding whether to report", "B": "Report at once to the manager and the provincial Director; good faith reports are protected", "C": "Document the observation in her own notes and raise it at the next review", "D": "Ask the resident's family for permission before taking the concern to anyone in authority"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Questioning the worker first and delaying the report", "C": "Recording privately and deferring until a later review", "D": "Making a duty to report depend on family permission"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report at once to the manager and the provincial Director; good faith reports are protected): Nurses have a duty to report suspected abuse, and in a long-term care home provincial law, such as Ontario's Long-Term Care Homes Act, also requires immediate reporting to the provincial Director, and legal protection applies to those who report honestly and in good faith, even if the concern is not later confirmed. Questioning the worker first or waiting for a review leaves the resident at risk. Private notes are not a report, and the duty does not depend on family permission."}, {"id": 424, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, a nurse prepares to instill prescribed ear drops for a 52-year-old man with an outer ear canal infection. He sits on the edge of the bed, and the nurse has warmed the bottle in her hands. How should she position him and the ear?", "options": {"A": "Have him sit upright with his head tilted forward, and hold the pinna straight", "B": "Position him side-lying with the affected ear up, and pull the pinna down and back", "C": "Position him side-lying with the affected ear up, and pull the pinna up and back", "D": "Position him on the unaffected side with his head flat, then press cotton into the canal"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using an upright position that lets drops run out", "B": "Applying the child technique to an adult", "D": "Packing cotton firmly, which traps drainage"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Position him side-lying with the affected ear up, and pull the pinna up and back): In an adult, pulling the pinna up and back straightens the ear canal so the drops reach the infected area, and lying with the affected ear up lets them stay there. Pulling down and back is the technique for young children. An upright head lets drops run out. Cotton should be loose, if used at all."}, {"id": 425, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the community, a 38-year-old woman is recovering from a diverticulitis flare with cramping and a low-grade fever. Her provider told her to follow a temporary low-fibre diet until she is reassessed. She asks the nurse what she should eat this week. Which teaching is best?", "options": {"A": "Snack on nuts and seeds, since they add bulk and reduce pressure within the colon", "B": "Choose white rice, eggs, and tender cooked vegetables until told otherwise", "C": "Eat bran cereal and whole grains daily to keep the bowel moving during the flare", "D": "Cut back on fluids so the bowel can rest until the pain and fever have settled"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Repeating the outdated nut and seed warning in reverse", "C": "Giving high-fibre foods during an acute flare", "D": "Restricting fluids, which risks constipation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Choose white rice, eggs, and tender cooked vegetables until told otherwise): During an acute flare a low-residue, low-fibre diet rests the bowel, and fibre is reintroduced gradually when the provider says so. Bran and whole grains add bulk that can worsen inflammation. Nuts and seeds are rough foods that are best avoided in a flare. Restricting fluids can cause hard stool and dehydration."}, {"id": 426, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Suctioning is due for an adult client with a tracheostomy who coughs forcefully each time the catheter is passed. The practical nurse is wearing gloves, a gown, and a surgical mask, and she has washed her hands. What additional protection does she need before starting?", "options": {"A": "A hair cover, because suctioning droplets settle on the head", "B": "A second gown, because secretions soak through the first during suctioning", "C": "None, since a surgical mask alone protects the mouth, nose, and eyes from secretions", "D": "Eye protection or a face shield, because coughing can splash secretions"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing PPE that does not protect mucous membranes", "B": "Adding body protection instead of eye protection", "C": "Believing a mask covers the eyes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Eye protection or a face shield, because coughing can splash secretions): Suctioning and forceful coughing create splashes and sprays that can reach the eyes, so eye protection or a face shield is required in addition to the mask, gloves, and gown. A mask protects the nose and mouth only. A hair cover or a second gown does not shield the eyes."}, {"id": 427, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Fever brings a toddler weighing 11 kg to the clinic. The order reads acetaminophen 15 mg/kg orally. The bottle's age chart lists 240 mg for ages 2 to 3, and the child is 2 years old. How should the practical nurse proceed?", "options": {"A": "Calculate 165 mg from 15 mg/kg and 11 kg, then verify with a second nurse", "B": "Give half the age chart dose of 120 mg and avoid calculating from the order", "C": "Round up to 200 mg so the fever comes down and the child is comfortable", "D": "Give the age chart dose of 240 mg because the child is two years old"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Guessing a fraction instead of calculating", "C": "Rounding up a pediatric dose to be safe", "D": "Using age-band dosing in place of the weight-based order"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Calculate 165 mg from 15 mg/kg and 11 kg, then verify with a second nurse): The order is weight based, so the dose is 15 mg times 11 kg, or 165 mg, and a second nurse verifies it. The label chart assumes an average child and would give more than 20 mg/kg to a light toddler. Rounding up or halving a chart dose are both guesses that ignore the order."}, {"id": 428, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Khalil, 58, lives in long-term care after a spinal cord injury and has no sensation below the waist. He uses a wheelchair most of the day and is turned in bed at night. Which plan best prevents pressure injuries over the coming months?", "options": {"A": "Turn him on a schedule, use a redistributing cushion, and check bony areas", "B": "Rely on him to report discomfort and reposition when he feels pain", "C": "Massage reddened areas firmly over bony prominences to restore their circulation", "D": "Place a donut-shaped ring cushion under his sacrum to lift pressure off the area"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Depending on sensation he does not have", "C": "Massaging damaged tissue over bone", "D": "Using a donut ring that concentrates pressure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Turn him on a schedule, use a redistributing cushion, and check bony areas): Without sensation he cannot sense pressure, so scheduled repositioning, a redistributing cushion, and regular skin checks over bony areas are essential. Donut rings concentrate pressure on surrounding tissue. Firm massage over reddened bone injures tissue further. Waiting for pain fails because he cannot feel it."}, {"id": 429, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a community screening, a 54-year-old man has a blood pressure of 164/102 mmHg, even on repeat measurement. He says he feels perfectly healthy, has no headaches, and sees no reason to worry about it. What should the practical nurse tell him?", "options": {"A": "Having no symptoms suggests low urgency, so he can recheck at next year's screening", "B": "One high reading at a screening is common, so recheck at his next checkup", "C": "Try salt reduction and exercise for three months, then see a provider", "D": "Hypertension often has no symptoms yet damages vessels, so he needs follow-up"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Equating no symptoms with low risk", "B": "Treating a repeated high reading as a one-off", "C": "Delaying provider assessment in favour of self-care"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hypertension often has no symptoms yet damages vessels, so he needs follow-up): Hypertension is called silent because damage to arteries, heart, brain, and kidneys can build for years without symptoms. A repeat reading of 164/102 needs provider follow-up. Waiting a year, waiting for the next checkup, or trying lifestyle change for months first delays diagnosis and treatment."}, {"id": 430, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On a pediatric unit, a 5-year-old's mother, who is Indigenous, stays close to her child, answers staff briefly, and declines to leave during procedures. A colleague says she is non-adherent and wants to call child protection. What approach should the practical nurse use?", "options": {"A": "Offer a quiet room but ask her to wait outside", "B": "Acknowledge past harms, build trust, and include her in decisions", "C": "Ask social work to assess the mother's parenting before continuing", "D": "Avoid raising history so that conversations stay clinical and focused on the child"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offering comfort but removing her from her child", "C": "Referring for assessment instead of building trust", "D": "Ignoring history that shapes trust"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acknowledge past harms, build trust, and include her in decisions): Wariness toward health staff often reflects real history, including the Sixties Scoop, when children were removed from families. A cultural safety approach acknowledges this, communicates respectfully, and shares decisions. Asking her to wait outside or requesting a parenting assessment without cause deepens mistrust. Staying clinical and silent leaves the barrier in place."}, {"id": 431, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a surgical unit, a practical nurse notices a friend and fellow nurse smells of alcohol, walks unsteadily, and is preparing to give medications with six clients assigned to her. The friend says she is just tired and asks her to keep quiet. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Confront her privately and watch her for the shift to see if her condition improves", "B": "Cover her medication tasks quietly this shift and talk with her afterward", "C": "Wait until the shift ends before raising anything, so that she has time to recover", "D": "Inform the charge nurse or manager now, per policy, to protect clients"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Handling an impairment issue informally", "B": "Covering up unsafe practice for a friend", "C": "Delaying a report while clients remain at risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Inform the charge nurse or manager now, per policy, to protect clients): Impaired practice puts clients at immediate risk, so the duty is to report to the charge nurse or manager now, following policy. A private chat with observation leaves her giving medications. Covering for her hides the problem and shifts risk to the nurse. Waiting until the end of the shift allows harm to occur first."}, {"id": 432, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sudden colicky flank pain that comes in waves, nausea, and blood in his urine bring a 35-year-old man to the emergency department. He is pale and restless, and the physician suspects a kidney stone. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Strain each voiding, save any stone, and encourage fluids as ordered", "B": "Withhold oral fluids to lessen ureteral pressure until his pain is gone", "C": "Encourage vigorous walking and measure urine output only once per shift", "D": "Send the first voided specimen to the lab and then stop saving urine"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Restricting fluids during stone passage", "C": "Reducing urine monitoring in an acute stone episode", "D": "Stopping urine collection before the stone is caught"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Strain each voiding, save any stone, and encourage fluids as ordered): Straining urine catches a passing stone so it can be analyzed, and fluids as ordered help move it. Restricting fluids concentrates urine and may worsen pain. Stopping collection after the first specimen could lose the stone. Output should be monitored closely, not once a shift."}, {"id": 433, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two weeks ago a man in his 50s stopped his antihypertensive because his home reading was 124/78 mmHg, he tells the pharmacy follow-up nurse. He feels fine and says the pills were an unnecessary expense for him. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Congratulate him on controlling his pressure and suggest weekly home checks to confirm it", "B": "Explain that normal readings show the drug works, and tell the prescriber he stopped it", "C": "Suggest he take half the usual dose until his next scheduled appointment next month", "D": "Tell him to restart the pill tonight because stopping was a mistake, and say no more"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepting a normal reading as a cure", "C": "Advising a self-chosen dose change", "D": "Instructing a change without informing the prescriber"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain that normal readings show the drug works, and tell the prescriber he stopped it): Normal readings usually mean the medication is working, and stopping it can allow the pressure to rise again. He needs teaching and the prescriber must be informed so the plan can be reviewed. Restarting without telling the prescriber, praising him, or suggesting half doses all skip that review."}, {"id": 434, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a nursing student is supervised by practical nurse Elena, her preceptor. The student administers an oral medication correctly after Elena reviews the order and checks the client's identity with her. Who is accountable for the student's care in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Only the instructor, who is the one responsible for the student's clinical experience", "B": "Elena is accountable for task choice and supervision, while the student answers for her own actions", "C": "Only the student, since she performed the task and is responsible for her own learning", "D": "Only the unit manager, because she signed the student placement agreement with the school"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Placing it entirely with the school instructor", "C": "Assigning all accountability to the learner", "D": "Placing it entirely with the manager"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Elena is accountable for task choice and supervision, while the student answers for her own actions): A preceptor chooses suitable tasks and supervises, so she shares accountability for the care along with the student, who answers for what she does within her level. Putting it on the student alone ignores supervision duties. The instructor and manager have roles but do not replace direct supervision."}, {"id": 435, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Admitted with a manic episode, a 29-year-old man paces the unit, talks rapidly, has slept two hours in three days, and argues with peers in the dayroom. His clothing is disorganized and he interrupts staff often. What is the most appropriate nursing approach?", "options": {"A": "Arrange a group debate to help him channel his energy into discussion", "B": "Encourage frequent visits from friends to keep him engaged and reduce his restlessness", "C": "Let him organize the unit's evening activities to use his high energy", "D": "Reduce stimulation, use short calm statements, and apply consistent limits"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adding stimulation to an overstimulated client", "B": "Increasing social stimulation in an agitated client", "C": "Assigning a leadership task beyond his current judgment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduce stimulation, use short calm statements, and apply consistent limits): Reducing stimulation, using brief calm communication, and setting consistent limits protects a client in mania and supports rest. Debate, many visitors, and organizing activities all add stimulation and increase agitation and the risk of conflict. A calm, predictable environment lowers arousal and protects both him and other clients."}, {"id": 436, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In long-term care, the charge nurse tells a newer nurse she will assign bathing and vital signs to the personal support worker using the usual assignment sheet. The newer nurse asks how assignment differs from delegation and why it matters. Which statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Assignment is done by the nurse, delegation is done by the manager", "B": "Assignment covers tasks in the receiver's role, delegation covers tasks beyond it", "C": "Assignment applies to unregulated staff, delegation applies to regulated staff", "D": "Delegation transfers full accountability to the receiver, while assignment keeps it with the nurse"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confusing who carries out each process", "C": "Assigning the terms to staff categories instead of task scope", "D": "Believing delegation hands over accountability"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assignment covers tasks in the receiver's role, delegation covers tasks beyond it): Assignment distributes tasks that fall within the receiver's usual role and training, while delegation transfers a task outside that role, with the nurse keeping accountability for the decision. The terms do not depend on a manager or on staff category. Delegation never removes the nurse's accountability."}, {"id": 437, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Amara plans to ask Dev to take vital signs on the stable post-operative client. Before doing so she confirms that Dev completed the training and has shown competence in measuring blood pressure and pulse. Which of the five rights of delegation is she applying at this point?", "options": {"A": "Right person, since the task goes to someone trained and shown to be competent", "B": "Right task, since the task must be one that is allowed to be delegated by policy", "C": "Right circumstance, since the client's condition must be stable enough for the task", "D": "Right supervision, since feedback and follow-up must be given after the task is done"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Mixing up person with task suitability", "C": "Mixing up person with client condition", "D": "Mixing up person with follow-up"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C059", "cluster_text": "On an evening medical unit, practical nurse Amara has six clients and one unregulated care provider, Dev, who has three months of experience. Her clients include a stable client on post-operative day 3, a client with new chest pain, a client who needs assessment and teaching, and a client who needs repositioning every two hours. It is 1800 and a call light is ringing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Right person, since the task goes to someone trained and shown to be competent): Checking training and demonstrated competence matches the task to the right person. The right task concerns what may be delegated, the right circumstance concerns the client's condition, and supervision concerns follow-up after the work is done. Matching the person to the task is the first check before any delegation."}, {"id": 438, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Amara reviews her assignment, knowing Dev is competent in basic care but not trained in assessment. One client is stable on post-operative day 3 and needs repositioning every two hours, one has new chest pain, one needs teaching about medications, and one needs a wound check. Which task is most appropriate for her to delegate?", "options": {"A": "Reviewing teaching with the client who has questions about new medications", "B": "Repositioning the stable post-operative client whose turning routine is already established", "C": "Checking the surgical site of the client with a wound for signs of infection or drainage", "D": "Taking vital signs for the client with new chest pain who is being monitored closely"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delegating teaching that needs nursing judgment", "C": "Delegating a nursing assessment", "D": "Delegating care for an unstable client"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C059", "cluster_text": "On an evening medical unit, practical nurse Amara has six clients and one unregulated care provider, Dev, who has three months of experience. Her clients include a stable client on post-operative day 3, a client with new chest pain, a client who needs assessment and teaching, and a client who needs repositioning every two hours. It is 1800 and a call light is ringing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repositioning the stable post-operative client whose turning routine is already established): Repositioning a stable client with a clear routine is a predictable task that falls within an unregulated provider's training. Monitoring a client with new chest pain, assessing a wound, and teaching about medications all need nursing judgment and should stay with the nurse. Delegate predictable tasks for stable clients and keep judgment-based work."}, {"id": 439, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Amara is behind with her charting and considers asking Dev to complete the admission assessment on a newly arrived client and then teach her how to use her inhaler. Dev says he is willing to do both. What should Amara do?", "options": {"A": "Delegate the teaching and complete the assessment herself so the workload is shared", "B": "Let Dev collect findings and teach, while she is nearby to answer his questions", "C": "Delegate both tasks and review Dev's written notes afterward for any missing information", "D": "Keep the assessment and teaching herself, as they need nursing judgment"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delegating only the teaching while ignoring that both need judgment", "B": "Allowing unregulated staff to perform nursing activities with supervision nearby", "C": "Delegating tasks needing clinical judgment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C059", "cluster_text": "On an evening medical unit, practical nurse Amara has six clients and one unregulated care provider, Dev, who has three months of experience. Her clients include a stable client on post-operative day 3, a client with new chest pain, a client who needs assessment and teaching, and a client who needs repositioning every two hours. It is 1800 and a call light is ringing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep the assessment and teaching herself, as they need nursing judgment): Assessment and client teaching require knowledge, judgment, and interpretation, so they cannot be delegated even when the provider is willing. Reviewing notes afterward does not make the delegation appropriate, and being nearby does not change who may perform the task. Willingness of the provider does not widen what can be delegated."}, {"id": 440, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Dev misreads a blood pressure and records 142/90 mmHg when the actual value was 112/70 mmHg. Amara relies on the number without checking and gives an antihypertensive. The client becomes dizzy. Who is accountable for the decision to delegate and for the follow-up?", "options": {"A": "Dev is accountable alone, since responsibility passes to the provider once the task is delegated", "B": "Nobody is accountable, because the error came from a measurement and not a decision", "C": "Amara is accountable for delegating and follow-up; Dev for his own performance", "D": "The charge nurse is accountable alone, because she assigned Dev to the unit that evening"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing accountability transfers fully", "B": "Treating an error as unaccountable", "D": "Placing accountability only on a supervisor"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C059", "cluster_text": "On an evening medical unit, practical nurse Amara has six clients and one unregulated care provider, Dev, who has three months of experience. Her clients include a stable client on post-operative day 3, a client with new chest pain, a client who needs assessment and teaching, and a client who needs repositioning every two hours. It is 1800 and a call light is ringing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Amara is accountable for delegating and follow-up; Dev for his own performance): A nurse who delegates remains accountable for choosing to delegate, giving direction, supervising, and checking results, while the provider is responsible for how he performs the task. Responsibility never passes completely to the provider, and a recording error does not remove accountability for relying on it. Delegation shares the work, never the nurse's accountability for the decision."}, {"id": 441, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Nitroglycerin spray was given at 1420 to a 65-year-old man with chest pain, but the nurse forgot to chart it. At 1600 a colleague says she saw it given and suggests it is fine to leave it, since it counts anyway. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Tell the next nurse verbally that the dose was given and rely on handover", "B": "Leave the record unchanged because a witness saw the dose given and can confirm it", "C": "Enter the dose at 1420 as though it had been recorded at that time", "D": "Chart a late entry with the current time, noting the spray was given at 1420"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Substituting a verbal handover for required documentation", "B": "Relying on a witness memory instead of the record", "C": "Backdating an entry to look as though charted on time"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chart a late entry with the current time, noting the spray was given at 1420): If an action was not charted, it is treated as not done, so the nurse adds a late entry showing the current time and the actual time of administration. Relying on a witness leaves the record incomplete, backdating falsifies it, and a verbal report is not part of the legal record. If it is not documented, it is treated as not done, so late entries must be honest and clearly timed."}, {"id": 442, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Without a leadership title, a practical nurse notices that colleagues often skip hand hygiene between clients. She would like to improve this. At a unit meeting, several coworkers say that only the manager should raise concerns like this. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the manager privately to address it, then wait to see what changes", "B": "Remind each colleague privately at every shift until their habit changes", "C": "Share her observations with the team and propose a small trial of peer prompts", "D": "Complete an incident report naming colleagues who skip hand hygiene"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Handing the concern to the manager and waiting", "B": "Repeating private reminders without a plan for team change", "D": "Using punitive reports instead of collaborative practice change"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Share her observations with the team and propose a small trial of peer prompts): Leadership does not depend on a title, and a nurse can influence practice by sharing observations and proposing a small, practical trial. Waiting for the manager loses time. Naming colleagues in an incident report punishes rather than builds shared practice, and repeated private reminders rarely change a unit's habits."}, {"id": 443, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Dubois cries and pushes the nurse away every morning at 0800 when told it is time for her bath. The unit schedule lists baths at this time for all residents, and she is calm and cooperative after lunch. What approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Move her bath to the evening, when staff have more time available", "B": "Keep to the 0800 schedule and give firm, steady encouragement until she cooperates", "C": "Ask her daughter to bathe her at 0800 so she is calmer", "D": "Offer a warm towel bath when she is most settled, with calm explanations"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing a time that suits staff rather than her calm period", "B": "Rigid schedule over person-centred care", "C": "Keeping the distressing time and shifting the task to family"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C056", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dubois, 84, lives in a long-term care home and has advanced Alzheimer's disease. Over several weeks she has become resistant during morning care, is eating less and coughing at meals, and is restless and agitated in the evenings. She fell last month. Her daughter visits often. The practical nurse is part of her care team. She is usually calmest in the early afternoon.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Offer a warm towel bath when she is most settled, with calm explanations): Resistance to bathing often reflects fear, cold, or loss of control, so person-centred care adjusts timing and method, uses a warm towel bath, and respects her preferences. Choosing a time for staff convenience or keeping the 0800 schedule does not suit her calm period. Holding her increases distress and risk of injury."}, {"id": 444, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Dubois now coughs during meals, pockets food, and takes few bites unless someone cues her. A staff member suggests asking for a feeding tube because it is the standard answer in advanced dementia. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Feed her quickly while she is alert to get enough food before she tires", "B": "Mix thin liquids with her meals to ease swallowing and increase intake", "C": "Request tube feeding, since it is the usual way to ensure adequate intake in advanced dementia", "D": "Offer small, soft portions with her upright and cued, and report the swallowing change for assessment"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Rushing feeding, which raises aspiration risk", "B": "Using thin liquids with a swallowing problem", "C": "Treating a feeding tube as a routine answer"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C056", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dubois, 84, lives in a long-term care home and has advanced Alzheimer's disease. Over several weeks she has become resistant during morning care, is eating less and coughing at meals, and is restless and agitated in the evenings. She fell last month. Her daughter visits often. The practical nurse is part of her care team. She is usually calmest in the early afternoon.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Offer small, soft portions with her upright and cued, and report the swallowing change for assessment): Careful hand feeding, upright positioning, and a swallowing assessment are first steps, and tube feeding has not been shown to improve survival or comfort in advanced dementia. Thin liquids raise aspiration risk, and rushing makes aspiration more likely. Swallowing changes in dementia need assessment and careful feeding support before any tube is considered."}, {"id": 445, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The physician orders an antipsychotic for Mrs. Dubois's evening agitation, and her daughter, who remembers last month's fall and is worried about side effects, asks the nurse about the risks. Which statement about antipsychotics in older adults with dementia is accurate?", "options": {"A": "They improve sleep and appetite, so benefits outweigh risks in dementia", "B": "Higher risk of stroke, falls, and death, so they are used cautiously", "C": "They carry little risk when used at a low dose for short periods", "D": "They are safe at bedtime, since evening agitation responds best"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming symptom benefit outweighs known mortality risk", "C": "Assuming low doses are safe", "D": "Believing timing of the dose removes the risk"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C056", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dubois, 84, lives in a long-term care home and has advanced Alzheimer's disease. Over several weeks she has become resistant during morning care, is eating less and coughing at meals, and is restless and agitated in the evenings. She fell last month. Her daughter visits often. The practical nurse is part of her care team. She is usually calmest in the early afternoon.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Higher risk of stroke, falls, and death, so they are used cautiously): Antipsychotics increase the risk of stroke, falls, and death in older adults with dementia, so non-drug approaches come first and drugs are used cautiously. They do not improve cognition, and bedtime dosing does not remove the risk. Benefits do not usually outweigh harms, and the lowest effective dose is used only when needed."}, {"id": 446, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After a review of Mrs. Dubois's fall last month, a colleague focuses on removing floor clutter from her room. The nurse also notes that Mrs. Dubois takes a sedative at night and an antipsychotic, and wears worn-out slippers. Which risk assessment is most complete?", "options": {"A": "Restrict walking to when staff are available so that her fall risk is reduced", "B": "Use bed rails and an alarm so that she cannot leave bed without staff", "C": "Focus on the environment such as clutter and lighting, since falls are mostly environmental", "D": "Review sedating medications, vision, footwear, and orientation along with the environment"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Restricting walking to prevent falls reduces mobility and strength", "B": "Relies on restraint-type measures instead of finding the causes", "C": "Overlooks sedatives and footwear by blaming the environment alone"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C056", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dubois, 84, lives in a long-term care home and has advanced Alzheimer's disease. Over several weeks she has become resistant during morning care, is eating less and coughing at meals, and is restless and agitated in the evenings. She fell last month. Her daughter visits often. The practical nurse is part of her care team. She is usually calmest in the early afternoon.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Review sedating medications, vision, footwear, and orientation along with the environment): Falls in older adults have several causes, so a complete assessment includes sedating drugs, vision, footwear, and cognition as well as the environment. Rails and restricted walking limit mobility and can increase injury or weakness. Falls have many causes, so the review covers the person as well as the room."}, {"id": 447, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At an addictions clinic, a man in his 40s tells Jordan he used again last weekend after three months of staying clean. He looks down at the floor and says he feels ashamed and that he has let everyone down. Which response is most therapeutic?", "options": {"A": "\"That was a setback, so we will need to restart your goals from the beginning.\"", "B": "\"Have you considered how this affects your recovery plan and your family?\"", "C": "\"It sounds like the weekend was hard. What was happening before you used?\"", "D": "\"Relapse is part of recovery, so let's stay positive about next week.\""}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Judging the lapse as a total personal failure", "B": "Prompting guilt about consequences, which deepens shame", "D": "Redirecting to positivity instead of exploring his feelings"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C060", "cluster_text": "During a full day working beside her preceptor on a community rotation, practical nurse Jordan practises therapeutic communication with several clients. The preceptor asks Jordan to choose the best response to each client's statement, and explains that responses which judge, minimize, approve conditionally, or interrogate block trust and should be avoided. Jordan has completed her communication course and is eager to apply it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (\"It sounds like the weekend was hard. What was happening before you used?\"): The first response reflects his feelings and invites him to explore triggers without judgment. Asking him to consider consequences increases shame, urging positivity minimizes his distress, and declaring a restart treats the lapse as failure. Therapeutic responses invite exploration and keep the client talking without shame."}, {"id": 448, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On the postpartum unit, a new mother tells Jordan that she cries every day, does not feel connected to her three-day-old baby, and worries that something is wrong with her. Her partner is at work and she has slept little. Which response best shows therapeutic communication?", "options": {"A": "\"Try to get sleep and rest today, and I am sure your mood will soon improve.\"", "B": "\"You have a healthy baby and a partner, so you should be happy as a mother.\"", "C": "\"Tell me more about the crying and how you have felt toward your baby.\"", "D": "\"Lots of women feel this way after delivery, and it passes within a few days.\""}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving advice before understanding", "B": "Telling her how to feel", "D": "Minimizing by general reassurance"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C060", "cluster_text": "During a full day working beside her preceptor on a community rotation, practical nurse Jordan practises therapeutic communication with several clients. The preceptor asks Jordan to choose the best response to each client's statement, and explains that responses which judge, minimize, approve conditionally, or interrogate block trust and should be avoided. Jordan has completed her communication course and is eager to apply it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (\"Tell me more about the crying and how you have felt toward your baby.\"): An open invitation to say more helps the nurse understand and screen for postpartum depression. Saying many women feel this way, telling her to be happy, or giving quick advice minimize her concerns and close the conversation. Open questions also allow the nurse to screen for postpartum depression and report concerns."}, {"id": 449, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "An adult in the rehabilitation unit after a stroke struggles to button a shirt during dressing practice, drops it twice, and says, \"I am useless now.\" His right hand is weak and he has refused help so far. Which response by Jordan is best?", "options": {"A": "\"Let's take a short break and try again tomorrow when you feel stronger.\"", "B": "\"Many stroke survivors regain dressing skills, so let's keep practising together.\"", "C": "\"Let me help with the buttons so you do not get so frustrated.\"", "D": "\"This is hard work. I noticed you did two buttons yourself, and that took effort.\""}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Avoiding his feelings by postponing the task", "B": "Generalizing about others instead of acknowledging his effort", "C": "Taking over the task and removing his independence"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C060", "cluster_text": "During a full day working beside her preceptor on a community rotation, practical nurse Jordan practises therapeutic communication with several clients. The preceptor asks Jordan to choose the best response to each client's statement, and explains that responses which judge, minimize, approve conditionally, or interrogate block trust and should be avoided. Jordan has completed her communication course and is eager to apply it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (\"This is hard work. I noticed you did two buttons yourself, and that took effort.\"): Acknowledging the difficulty and describing a specific observed effort supports him without judging or promising a result. Postponing the task avoids his feelings, general statements about other survivors skip his own effort, and taking over removes his independence. Specific, honest feedback on effort supports motivation better than these responses."}, {"id": 450, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "An adult arrives in the emergency department after an assault. She is shaking, avoids eye contact, and has said nothing since arriving with a friend. Her vital signs are stable. Jordan needs to build trust before any examination takes place. Which response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "\"Tell me as much or as little as you want. I am here with you.\"", "B": "\"Shall I call the police now so they can take your statement?\"", "C": "\"Let's get you examined first, then talk afterward.\"", "D": "\"Tell me exactly what happened from the beginning so that we can document it.\""}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Pushing a police report before she has control or trust", "C": "Starting examination before building trust or choice", "D": "Demands a full account before trust or safety is established"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C060", "cluster_text": "During a full day working beside her preceptor on a community rotation, practical nurse Jordan practises therapeutic communication with several clients. The preceptor asks Jordan to choose the best response to each client's statement, and explains that responses which judge, minimize, approve conditionally, or interrogate block trust and should be avoided. Jordan has completed her communication course and is eager to apply it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (\"Tell me as much or as little as you want. I am here with you.\"): Offering control over what to share and staying present builds trust and safety. Raising a police report or starting the examination first takes control away from a person in crisis, and demands for a full account overwhelm her. Giving a survivor control over what she shares supports safety and trust."}, {"id": 451, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An adolescent with allergic rhinitis is prescribed a nasal steroid spray. At the clinic he demonstrates his technique by tilting his head far back, spraying, and sniffing hard to draw the medication in. How should the practical nurse correct his technique?", "options": {"A": "Keep his head level, aim into the throat, and then lie flat for several minutes afterward", "B": "Keep his head forward, aim toward the outer nostril wall, and sniff gently", "C": "Keep his head slightly forward, aim at the septum, and blow his nose right afterward", "D": "Keep his head tilted far back, aim straight up, and sniff hard to draw it in"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Directing spray toward the throat", "C": "Aiming at the septum, which causes irritation", "D": "Tilting back and sniffing hard"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep his head forward, aim toward the outer nostril wall, and sniff gently): A slightly forward head and an aim toward the outer wall keep the spray on the nasal lining and away from the septum, and a gentle sniff stops it going down the throat. Tilting back and sniffing hard sends medication into the throat, aiming at the septum irritates tissue, and blowing the nose removes the dose. Correct technique matters because the medication works on the nasal lining, not the throat."}, {"id": 452, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, a mother tells the nurse that her 4-year-old, who weighs 18 kg, has a fever. She gave a children's acetaminophen dose at 1400, and at 1600 she gave a cold syrup that also lists acetaminophen. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Check both labels for acetaminophen, total the 24-hour amount, and report to the provider", "B": "Give the next dose of acetaminophen now since the child still has a fever", "C": "Tell her to stop all medicines for a day and give a cool bath", "D": "Treat the cold syrup as a separate product and allow the next acetaminophen dose on schedule"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Adding a dose to a possible overdose", "C": "Withholding all care without assessment", "D": "Treating combination products as separate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check both labels for acetaminophen, total the 24-hour amount, and report to the provider): Combination cold products often contain acetaminophen, so the total daily amount must be calculated from every product and the provider informed. Treating the syrup as separate or giving another dose risks overdose. Stopping everything leaves the fever unmanaged without advice."}, {"id": 453, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At a remote nursing station, a family misses a follow-up appointment. Their home has overcrowding and no running water, and the clinic is a long boat ride away. A colleague says the family simply lacks motivation. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Refer the family to social work and close the nursing file afterward", "B": "Focus teaching on hygiene and ignore the housing, since it cannot be changed here", "C": "Recognize how housing, water, and history shape health, and reduce barriers together", "D": "Provide written instructions and a reminder call, since missed visits mean forgetting"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Handing off the family and ending nursing follow-up", "B": "Overlooking housing and water as social determinants of health", "D": "Assuming forgetfulness instead of exploring access barriers"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recognize how housing, water, and history shape health, and reduce barriers together): Social determinants such as housing, safe water, distance, and the legacy of colonization strongly affect health in many Indigenous communities, so the nurse works with the family to reduce barriers. Closing the file after a referral, or assuming forgetfulness, repeats the deficit view and widens the gap. Cultural safety means looking at conditions around the client before judging behaviour."}, {"id": 454, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At a unit meeting, a practical nurse proposes hourly rounding to reduce falls. A senior colleague says changes never happen unless someone with seniority insists on them, and several peers nod in agreement. How should the nurse influence her peers?", "options": {"A": "Ask the manager to order the change so that no discussion is needed", "B": "Start rounding herself and say nothing to the team about her reasons", "C": "Share published evidence and results from a short pilot, and invite their input", "D": "Insist on the change because she has fifteen years of seniority and a charge nurse title."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Avoiding peer engagement by relying on authority", "B": "Acting alone without explaining the reasons", "D": "Using seniority rather than evidence to persuade"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Share published evidence and results from a short pilot, and invite their input): Influence among peers grows from evidence, example, and shared input, and a short pilot shows results. Insisting through seniority creates resistance, acting silently leaves peers uninformed, and asking for an order avoids building commitment. Peers respond best to evidence, example, and invitation rather than rank."}, {"id": 455, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In home care, a practical nurse asks a personal support worker to help an adult client with a bath, then leaves for another visit. A colleague says the nurse has handed over the responsibility. What should she do to stay accountable?", "options": {"A": "Leave a written note and avoid contact so that the worker can work independently", "B": "Review the care only if the client or family makes a complaint later", "C": "Accept that responsibility passes to the support worker once she accepts the task", "D": "Give clear instructions, state what to report, and check back on the outcome"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaves a note with no direct contact or follow up", "B": "Reviews care only after a complaint arrives", "C": "Believes accountability passes to the worker once task accepted"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give clear instructions, state what to report, and check back on the outcome): Accountability stays with the nurse, so she gives clear direction, sets criteria for reporting concerns, and follows up on results. Responsibility does not pass completely to the worker. A note alone and waiting for complaints give no real supervision."}, {"id": 456, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Stung by a wasp at a picnic, a 30-year-old woman arrives by ambulance with hives, wheezing, and swelling of her lips. Her blood pressure is 78/40 mmHg and her skin is flushed and warm. Which type of shock does this describe?", "options": {"A": "Neurogenic shock from spinal injury, with loss of sympathetic tone and slow heart rate", "B": "Distributive shock from anaphylaxis, with widespread vasodilation and leaking vessels", "C": "Cardiogenic shock caused by the heart failing to pump enough blood to the tissues", "D": "Septic shock from infection, with fever, warm skin, and widespread vessel dilation"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing another distributive shock with a spinal cause", "C": "Assumes pump failure despite hives and wheeze", "D": "Choosing septic shock despite an allergic trigger and no infection"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C054", "cluster_text": "During orientation, a practical nurse reviews shock with a nurse educator. The educator presents a series of client situations on a hospital ward and in a clinic, and asks the nurse to identify the type of shock, recognize early signs, and choose the safest immediate action. She has just finished her first week on the unit and wants to practise.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Distributive shock from anaphylaxis, with widespread vasodilation and leaking vessels): Allergic exposure, hives, wheeze, and warm flushed skin with low pressure indicate anaphylactic shock, a form of distributive shock in which vessels widen and leak. Neurogenic and septic shock are also distributive but have different causes, and cardiogenic or obstructive shock do not explain the allergic features. Epinephrine and urgent help are the priority."}, {"id": 457, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On post-operative day 1, a 66-year-old man is restless and has cool, clammy skin, a heart rate of 112, a blood pressure of 118/72 mmHg, and low urine output. His nurse thinks it is only anxiety. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Wait for a drop in blood pressure before reporting, since his pressure is normal", "B": "Give an analgesic for pain and reassess hourly before involving anyone else", "C": "Treat them as normal post-operative anxiety and recheck his vital signs in four hours", "D": "Report these as possible early signs of shock now, before his blood pressure falls"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for hypotension before acting on compensatory signs", "B": "Treating possible shock as pain and delaying the report", "C": "Attributing early shock signs to anxiety and delaying reassessment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C054", "cluster_text": "During orientation, a practical nurse reviews shock with a nurse educator. The educator presents a series of client situations on a hospital ward and in a clinic, and asks the nurse to identify the type of shock, recognize early signs, and choose the safest immediate action. She has just finished her first week on the unit and wants to practise.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report these as possible early signs of shock now, before his blood pressure falls): Restlessness, rapid pulse, cool clammy skin, and low urine output are compensatory signs of early shock that appear before blood pressure falls, so they are reported promptly. Normal pressure does not exclude shock. Waiting, or treating pain alone, delays recognition."}, {"id": 458, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Pale, cool, and anxious, a 52-year-old man with a bleeding stomach ulcer says he feels faint. His blood pressure is 82/48 mmHg. He has no head or spine injury and is awake. Which position is best until the physician arrives?", "options": {"A": "Head-down Trendelenburg position so that blood flows toward the brain", "B": "Lying flat with legs raised about 30 centimetres to improve blood return", "C": "High Fowler's position to ease his breathing and reduce the strain on his heart", "D": "Semi-Fowler's position with knees bent, which is more comfortable for his abdomen"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a harmful head-down position", "C": "Raising the head despite shock", "D": "Choosing comfort over perfusion"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C054", "cluster_text": "During orientation, a practical nurse reviews shock with a nurse educator. The educator presents a series of client situations on a hospital ward and in a clinic, and asks the nurse to identify the type of shock, recognize early signs, and choose the safest immediate action. She has just finished her first week on the unit and wants to practise.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lying flat with legs raised about 30 centimetres to improve blood return): Lying flat with the legs raised improves venous return in hypovolemic shock. High Fowler's lowers return to the heart. The Trendelenburg position is no longer recommended because abdominal organs press on the diaphragm and can worsen breathing and pressure in the head."}, {"id": 459, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a clinic, a client with a deep forearm laceration from broken glass has blood soaking through a gauze dressing and is becoming pale and anxious. The wound is not spurting. What should the practical nurse do first while waiting for the physician?", "options": {"A": "Apply firm direct pressure over the soaked dressing, add layers on top, and raise the arm", "B": "Remove the soaked dressing to see the wound and then apply a clean dressing", "C": "Apply a tight tourniquet above the wound and note the time it was applied", "D": "Apply pressure to the pulse point above the wound only and leave the dressing off"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Disturbing a forming clot", "C": "Using a tourniquet as a first step", "D": "Using pressure away from the wound"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C054", "cluster_text": "During orientation, a practical nurse reviews shock with a nurse educator. The educator presents a series of client situations on a hospital ward and in a clinic, and asks the nurse to identify the type of shock, recognize early signs, and choose the safest immediate action. She has just finished her first week on the unit and wants to practise.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Apply firm direct pressure over the soaked dressing, add layers on top, and raise the arm): Direct firm pressure over the wound controls bleeding, and more layers go on top without removing the soaked dressing, which would disrupt clot formation. Pressing a distant pulse point misses the bleeding site, and a tourniquet is a last resort for bleeding that cannot be controlled. Raise the limb and call for medical help if bleeding continues despite pressure."}, {"id": 460, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the start of your shift, the gauze around Mr. Rahimi's Penrose drain is soaked with pink-tinged fluid, though the skin around the site is intact and he reports no pain. The drain is held by a sterile safety pin at the skin. He asks why the dressing is wet. What is the best nursing action now?", "options": {"A": "Wait until the dressing is fully saturated and leaks through before changing it.", "B": "Reinforce the dressing with extra gauze and check it again next shift.", "C": "Change the dressing aseptically, leave the pin in place, and record drainage amount and colour.", "D": "Change the dressing but remove the pin to let the drain move freely."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for overflow instead of changing a wet dressing", "B": "Layering over a wet dressing instead of changing it", "D": "Removing the pin that stops the drain slipping inward"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C055", "cluster_text": "Mr. Aziz Rahimi, 58, is on postoperative day two after an open bowel resection. He has a Jackson-Pratt drain in the right lower abdomen and a Penrose drain at the lower end of the midline incision, covered by a gauze dressing. He walks short distances with help. You are the practical nurse assigned to his care, working with the RN who coordinates his plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Change the dressing aseptically, leave the pin in place, and record drainage amount and colour.): A Penrose drain is passive, so a wet dressing is expected and needs a clean change with the pin kept in place so the drain cannot slip into the wound. Recording the amount and colour gives the RN and surgeon data about healing. Adding gauze over a wet dressing keeps moisture on the skin, and removing the pin risks losing the drain inside."}, {"id": 461, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Rahimi's Jackson-Pratt bulb is fully round and expanded after you emptied it two hours ago, with only a small amount of drainage inside, while the dressing near the tube exit is damp. He is comfortable and afebrile. Which interpretation and action best fits these findings?", "options": {"A": "The bulb has lost suction, so empty it if needed, compress it, and reseal the plug.", "B": "The expanded bulb shows suction is working well, so only the damp dressing needs changing.", "C": "Drainage has stopped, so leave the bulb expanded and report the drain is ready for removal.", "D": "Little drainage means healing is advanced, so leave the bulb expanded and recheck next shift."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating a lost vacuum as a working one", "C": "Reading low volume as readiness for removal", "D": "Missing that an expanded bulb means no suction"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C055", "cluster_text": "Mr. Aziz Rahimi, 58, is on postoperative day two after an open bowel resection. He has a Jackson-Pratt drain in the right lower abdomen and a Penrose drain at the lower end of the midline incision, covered by a gauze dressing. He walks short distances with help. You are the practical nurse assigned to his care, working with the RN who coordinates his plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The bulb has lost suction, so empty it if needed, compress it, and reseal the plug.): A Jackson-Pratt bulb works by vacuum, so a bulb that stays round after emptying has lost suction and the drain is not pulling fluid, which explains the leaking at the tube exit. Compressing the bulb and closing the plug restores the vacuum. Low volume does not show that the drain can come out, because only the surgeon decides that. An expanded bulb means the opposite of working suction."}, {"id": 462, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the end of your shift Mr. Rahimi's Jackson-Pratt bulb holds drainage, and the day nurse charted 'moderate' amounts yesterday. The care plan asks for intake and output totals, and the oncoming nurse needs to compare shifts. How should you measure and document the drainage in the bulb?", "options": {"A": "Judge the volume by how full the bulb looks and chart that estimate in notes.", "B": "Measure the stained area of the dressing in centimetres and chart that as the drainage total.", "C": "Describe the amount as small, moderate, or large and compare it with the previous shift.", "D": "Pour into a graduated container, read millilitres, and chart the volume with colour and character."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Estimating from the bulb's appearance rather than measuring", "B": "Substituting dressing stain size for drainage volume", "C": "Using vague descriptors instead of measured volume"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C055", "cluster_text": "Mr. Aziz Rahimi, 58, is on postoperative day two after an open bowel resection. He has a Jackson-Pratt drain in the right lower abdomen and a Penrose drain at the lower end of the midline incision, covered by a gauze dressing. He walks short distances with help. You are the practical nurse assigned to his care, working with the RN who coordinates his plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pour into a graduated container, read millilitres, and chart the volume with colour and character.): Drainage must be measured to be useful for the fluid balance record, so emptying into a graduated container and reading the volume in millilitres gives an exact number the next nurse can compare. Colour and character add information about bleeding or infection. Words like moderate cannot be compared between nurses. Judging from the bulb or the dressing stain gives only a rough guess that hides trends."}, {"id": 463, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ten days later, Mr. Rahimi attends the surgical clinic with a midline incision that is dry, with edges closed and no redness. The discharge note states 'staples out at ten days,' and you have verified the order. He asks you to remove them now. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Defer removal and tell him an order cannot be followed without the surgeon present.", "B": "Assess that edges are closed and dry, then remove staples with a sterile remover.", "C": "Remove all staples and apply a dry dressing without examining the incision edges first.", "D": "Remove alternate staples only and leave the remainder until the surgeon reviews the wound next week."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Refusing a valid order the nurse can carry out", "C": "Skipping the wound assessment before removal", "D": "Partial removal not stated in the order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C055", "cluster_text": "Mr. Aziz Rahimi, 58, is on postoperative day two after an open bowel resection. He has a Jackson-Pratt drain in the right lower abdomen and a Penrose drain at the lower end of the midline incision, covered by a gauze dressing. He walks short distances with help. You are the practical nurse assigned to his care, working with the RN who coordinates his plan.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assess that edges are closed and dry, then remove staples with a sterile remover.): With a valid order in hand, the nurse still confirms the wound is healed by checking that the edges are closed, dry, and free of redness or drainage before removing staples with a sterile remover. Removing only alternate staples is a variation the order does not give. Skipping the assessment risks opening an unhealed wound. Refusing to act is unnecessary because removal on order is within practical nurse scope."}, {"id": 464, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "You are walking through a shopping mall when a man of about 35 collapses and begins a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. Bystanders are crowding around him, and a hard tile edge lies next to his head. You are the only health professional present. What should you do to help him?", "options": {"A": "Lift him into a seated position and offer small sips of water once the jerking slows.", "B": "Hold his arms and legs firmly to limit the movement until the jerking ends.", "C": "Place a folded cloth between his teeth and keep his jaw gently open.", "D": "Cushion his head, clear hard objects, time the seizure, then turn him on his side."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Sitting and giving fluids before full recovery", "B": "Restraining a seizing person, which risks injury", "C": "Placing an object in the mouth, which risks airway and tooth injury"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cushion his head, clear hard objects, time the seizure, then turn him on his side.): The goals during a seizure are to prevent injury and protect the airway, so cushioning the head, clearing hazards, timing the event, and side-lying positioning afterward fits this situation. Restraining the limbs can cause fractures and does not stop the seizure. Anything placed in the mouth can break teeth or block the airway, and fluids are unsafe until he is awake."}, {"id": 465, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After a night of severe hypotension from blood loss, a 62-year-old man on an acute care unit has passed only 150 mL of urine in 24 hours. The RN tells you his acute kidney injury is likely prerenal. Which description best defines this kind of cause?", "options": {"A": "Blockage of urine flow below the kidneys, such as from an enlarged prostate or stones.", "B": "Reduced blood flow to the kidneys that lowers filtration before tissue damage occurs.", "C": "Chronic scarring of both kidneys from long-standing diabetes or poorly controlled high blood pressure.", "D": "Direct damage to the kidney tubules from toxins, certain drugs, or infection in the tissue."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Describing a postrenal obstruction instead", "C": "Describing chronic kidney disease rather than acute injury", "D": "Confusing an intrarenal injury with a flow-related cause"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduced blood flow to the kidneys that lowers filtration before tissue damage occurs.): A prerenal cause is a problem of perfusion, so low blood pressure or blood loss reduces flow to otherwise healthy kidneys and filtration falls. Tubular damage from toxins describes an intrarenal cause. Blockage below the kidneys describes a postrenal cause. Chronic scarring from diabetes or hypertension describes chronic kidney disease, which develops over years and not over one night."}, {"id": 466, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In a long-term care home, a care aide asks you to take over bathing an 84-year-old woman whose condition changed overnight. She is newly confused, her blood pressure has dropped, and she looks pale. The RN is on a 20 minute break. Which action best matches the task to provider competence?", "options": {"A": "Report the new findings to the RN now and have the task reassigned.", "B": "Complete the bath yourself first and report the changes when the RN returns.", "C": "Assign the bath to the newest aide since bathing is a routine hygiene task.", "D": "Let the experienced care aide continue bathing because she knows this resident's routine best."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delaying reporting of a significant change", "C": "Treating the task as routine despite instability", "D": "Assigning by familiarity, ignoring the changed status"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the new findings to the RN now and have the task reassigned.): A resident whose status is changing is no longer predictable, so the plan and the provider must be reviewed by the RN before an unregulated provider continues care. Reporting the findings now lets the RN decide who does what. Routine and familiarity do not make bathing safe for an unstable client. Finishing the bath first delays recognition of a possible serious change."}, {"id": 467, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During a hospital orientation, a new graduate practical nurse asks why the Truth and Reconciliation Commission Calls to Action are discussed in nursing sessions. The facilitator notes that several calls address health. What is the most accurate way to describe their relevance to a front line nurse?", "options": {"A": "They direct governments to act on health policy, leaving individual nurses outside their scope.", "B": "They are historical recommendations that were completed once residential school records were released.", "C": "They apply mainly to nurses working in First Nations communities and northern settings.", "D": "They name health gaps and call for culturally safe care that nurses help deliver."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming the calls concern governments alone", "B": "Treating the calls as finished and historical", "C": "Limiting the calls to one practice setting"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (They name health gaps and call for culturally safe care that nurses help deliver.): The health related Calls to Action identify gaps in outcomes and ask for culturally safe, equitable care, and front line nurses carry that out through everyday practice such as respectful communication and advocacy. Governments are named in many calls, but that does not remove nurses from the work. The calls apply across all settings. They remain active commitments, not completed items."}, {"id": 468, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, a 78-year-old woman tells you she feels dizzy and nearly falls each morning when she gets out of bed. Her seated blood pressure is 132/78 mmHg, and her standing blood pressure at three minutes is 104/64 mmHg. Which teaching will best reduce her risk?", "options": {"A": "Sit at the bedside briefly, rise slowly with support, and keep up fluid intake.", "B": "Restrict fluids during the day to reduce the number of trips to the bathroom.", "C": "Wear compression stockings to the knee and stand up briskly after waking.", "D": "Take a long hot bath before bed so circulation improves overnight."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Limiting fluids, which lowers blood volume further", "C": "Pairing a helpful measure with a fast position change", "D": "Using heat that causes vasodilation and worsens the drop"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sit at the bedside briefly, rise slowly with support, and keep up fluid intake.): A fall of more than 20 mmHg systolic on standing points to orthostatic hypotension, so slow position changes, a pause sitting, and adequate fluids give the circulation time to adjust. Standing briskly is the very trigger to avoid. Hot baths widen vessels and can lower pressure further, and restricting fluids lowers blood volume."}, {"id": 469, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "In the emergency department, a 45-year-old Indigenous woman with abdominal pain has waited four hours. She tells you staff dismissed her earlier concerns as drug-seeking, and she is thinking of leaving. Her triage level is moderate. Which nursing action best reflects Joyce's Principle?", "options": {"A": "Offer to move her ahead in triage but avoid discussing the earlier dismissal", "B": "Explain that triage order applies equally to all clients and ask her to wait her turn.", "C": "Ask her to write a complaint to patient relations and return to other duties", "D": "Acknowledge her experience, advocate for her assessment, and report the dismissal concern through the proper channel."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Fixing the wait but ignoring the discrimination concern", "B": "Using equal treatment to dismiss a discrimination concern", "C": "Shifting the burden to the client instead of advocating"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acknowledge her experience, advocate for her assessment, and report the dismissal concern through the proper channel.): Joyce's Principle commits health care to equitable access and care free of discrimination, so listening, advocating for her assessment, and reporting the concern address both her need and the system problem. Citing equal triage rules ignores the harm she describes. Moving her ahead without addressing the dismissal, or asking her to file a complaint herself, leaves the system problem in place."}, {"id": 470, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "You are caring for a 70-year-old man on contact precautions with a draining leg wound. After you dress the wound and take his vital signs, you must help the next client down the hall. You are wearing a gown over your uniform. When should you remove the gown?", "options": {"A": "Remove it before leaving his room, discard it, and perform hand hygiene immediately.", "B": "Hang it on the room hook to reuse during later visits to him that shift.", "C": "Keep it on while moving to the next client, then change it after finishing that visit.", "D": "Remove it outside the room after closing the door so it does not touch the floor."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reusing a contaminated gown", "C": "Carrying contamination to another client", "D": "Taking the gown out into the corridor"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Remove it before leaving his room, discard it, and perform hand hygiene immediately.): A gown protects against contact with the draining wound, so it must come off and be discarded before leaving the room, followed by hand hygiene. Wearing it to another client carries organisms with you and spreads infection. Removing it in the corridor takes contamination outside the room. Hanging a used gown for reuse contaminates it further."}, {"id": 471, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Shouting, pacing, and swaying with slurred speech, a 40-year-old man arrives in the emergency department. He tells you to leave him alone, and no injuries are visible. You are about to write your nursing note. Which entry best follows the principles of objective documentation?", "options": {"A": "Client agitated and likely intoxicated, pacing and shouting; refused help; no injuries seen.", "B": "Pacing and shouting, appears angry and impaired, stating 'Leave me alone'; no injuries.", "C": "Pacing, shouting, slurred speech observed; client stated, 'Leave me alone'; no injuries seen.", "D": "Pacing, shouting, slurred speech observed; client irritable and uncooperative; no injuries seen."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stating a probable cause that was not assessed", "B": "Mixing observations with labels such as angry and impaired", "D": "Adding judgment words like irritable and uncooperative"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pacing, shouting, slurred speech observed; client stated, 'Leave me alone'; no injuries seen.): Objective charting records what you see and hear, including exact client words in quotation marks, without labels or guesses about cause. The entry listing observed behaviours and a direct quote does exactly that and notes that no injuries were seen. Words such as intoxicated, impaired, angry, or uncooperative are conclusions that can be wrong and can bias later readers."}, {"id": 472, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "MRSA is found in the nares of a 52-year-old man admitted to a medical unit, and he has no cough or open wound. A colleague asks which transmission-based precautions apply and what you will wear for routine care. Which response is best?", "options": {"A": "Airborne precautions with a fitted N95 respirator and a negative pressure room.", "B": "Contact precautions with gloves and gown for care, and dedicated equipment kept in the room.", "C": "Droplet precautions with a surgical mask and eye protection during every room entry.", "D": "Standard practices alone, since nasal colonization without symptoms does not spread to other clients."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing airborne precautions for a contact-spread organism", "C": "Choosing droplet precautions for a contact-spread organism", "D": "Dismissing the risk of spread from a colonized client"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contact precautions with gloves and gown for care, and dedicated equipment kept in the room.): MRSA spreads mainly by contact with the client or contaminated surfaces, so contact precautions with gloves, gown, and dedicated equipment fit even when the organism is only colonizing the nose. Airborne precautions are for organisms like tuberculosis. Droplet precautions are for organisms spread by large respiratory droplets. Standard practices alone leave colonized clients as a source of spread."}, {"id": 473, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "'The voices say I should disappear,' a 28-year-old woman on a mental health unit tells you. When asked, she denies having a plan. You are now completing your nursing note, which her psychiatrist will read tomorrow. Which entry best records her statement?", "options": {"A": "Client has auditory hallucinations and thoughts of disappearing, with no plan reported.", "B": "Client stated, 'The voices say I should disappear'; when asked, denied having a plan.", "C": "Client reports hearing voices that tell her to disappear and denies having a plan.", "D": "Client expressed suicidal thoughts driven by voices and denied having a plan."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Substituting clinical labels for her statement", "C": "Paraphrasing instead of quoting her words", "D": "Interpreting intent in words she did not say"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Client stated, 'The voices say I should disappear'; when asked, denied having a plan.): A direct quote preserves her exact words, which can matter to the team assessing risk, and recording her answer about a plan adds the key safety finding. Paraphrases can shift the meaning. Terms such as auditory hallucinations or suicidal thoughts are interpretations she did not state. Quotes keep the record factual and let the psychiatrist draw the clinical conclusion."}, {"id": 474, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 45-year-old woman at a community clinic has hard stools twice a week and has begun eating bran cereal and raw vegetables after reading about fibre. She drinks about two cups of fluid a day. She asks how to relieve her constipation safely. What teaching is best?", "options": {"A": "Eat larger amounts of fibre in a single meal so the bowel responds faster.", "B": "Switch to a low fibre diet and rely on daily stimulant laxatives for relief.", "C": "Increase fibre gradually while raising fluid intake, and add regular physical activity.", "D": "Keep adding bran each day until stools soften, and sip fluids only with meals and snacks."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Loading fibre at once, which causes bloating and cramping", "B": "Using laxatives in place of diet and fluids", "D": "Adding fibre without enough fluid"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Increase fibre gradually while raising fluid intake, and add regular physical activity.): Fibre draws water into stool, so without adequate fluid it can harden stool further, which fits her low intake. Raising fibre slowly and fluid together, with activity, softens stool and limits gas. More bran with little fluid worsens the problem, and a large single dose of fibre causes cramping. Daily stimulant laxatives are not first-line."}, {"id": 475, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On Mrs. Karras's first check, she gives her name and the place correctly, but she answers slowly and falls asleep between questions. She was alert and chatty at admission an hour ago, and her pupils are equal. Which approach to assessing her level of consciousness is best?", "options": {"A": "Record her as alert because she answered correctly for her name and place.", "B": "Wait for the next scheduled check to see whether the drowsiness continues.", "C": "Document how readily she wakes and responds, since drowsiness often precedes disorientation.", "D": "Repeat the questions until she answers quickly, then chart her fastest response with the time."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on orientation alone", "B": "Delaying action on a new drowsiness", "D": "Charting the best response instead of the true one"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C051", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Helen Karras, 76, tripped on a rug at home and struck her head on a kitchen counter. She takes warfarin for atrial fibrillation. She did not lose consciousness but is admitted to a neurological unit for observation. You are the practical nurse completing her neurological checks and reporting changes to the RN, who coordinates her plan of care. Her daughter, Maria, stays at the bedside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Document how readily she wakes and responds, since drowsiness often precedes disorientation.): Level of consciousness is the most sensitive early sign of rising pressure in the head, and decreased alertness often appears before orientation fails. Recording how easily she wakes and how she responds gives an accurate picture, which you then report. Correct answers for name and place do not make her alert. Waiting or charting only her best answer hides a decline in a client on warfarin."}, {"id": 476, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At midnight, Mrs. Karras opens her eyes only when you speak loudly, says a few confused words, and obeys the command to squeeze your hand. You need to give the RN a Glasgow Coma Scale score. Which statement describes how the scale is scored?", "options": {"A": "Add the best eye, verbal, and motor responses to give a total out of ten.", "B": "Add her orientation, pupil size, and limb strength to give a total score.", "C": "Add the best eye, verbal, and motor responses to give a total out of fifteen.", "D": "Add eye, verbal, and motor responses, using her average response in each, for a total."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Wrong maximum score for the scale", "B": "Mixing pupil findings and strength into the total", "D": "Averaging responses instead of using the best one"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C051", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Helen Karras, 76, tripped on a rug at home and struck her head on a kitchen counter. She takes warfarin for atrial fibrillation. She did not lose consciousness but is admitted to a neurological unit for observation. You are the practical nurse completing her neurological checks and reporting changes to the RN, who coordinates her plan of care. Her daughter, Maria, stays at the bedside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Add the best eye, verbal, and motor responses to give a total out of fifteen.): The Glasgow Coma Scale adds three components, eye opening, verbal response, and motor response, using the best response in each, for a total from 3 to 15. A total out of ten uses the wrong range. Averaging responses lowers the score below what she showed. Orientation, pupil size, and strength are not scored components."}, {"id": 477, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the 0200 check, Mrs. Karras's right pupil is 6 mm and sluggish while the left is 3 mm and brisk. She is harder to wake than at midnight. Her daughter says her mother has always had slightly uneven eyes. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Report the change to the RN immediately and continue frequent neurological checks until she is assessed.", "B": "Dim the lights and repeat the pupil check in thirty minutes before reporting anything.", "C": "Ask her daughter for old photographs of her eyes before deciding whether to report.", "D": "Document the uneven pupils as her normal variation and recheck at the next scheduled time."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delaying the report to gather more data", "C": "Seeking confirmation instead of escalating at once", "D": "Dismissing a new finding as a normal variant"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C051", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Helen Karras, 76, tripped on a rug at home and struck her head on a kitchen counter. She takes warfarin for atrial fibrillation. She did not lose consciousness but is admitted to a neurological unit for observation. You are the practical nurse completing her neurological checks and reporting changes to the RN, who coordinates her plan of care. Her daughter, Maria, stays at the bedside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the change to the RN immediately and continue frequent neurological checks until she is assessed.): A new unequal, sluggish pupil together with falling alertness suggests rising pressure from a possible bleed, which is an emergency. Immediate reporting is the priority, with checks continuing. Her daughter's comment may be true but does not explain a new change in reaction and size. Waiting or collecting photographs delays treatment."}, {"id": 478, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On day two, Mrs. Karras is eating breakfast when her left arm drifts downward, the left side of her face droops, and her words are slurred. Symptoms began two minutes ago, and she was normal at the last check. What is the best action now?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the neurological checks in fifteen minutes and report if the signs persist.", "B": "Note the time symptoms began and call the RN immediately for urgent stroke assessment.", "C": "Give her usual morning aspirin for stroke prevention, then report at the end of breakfast.", "D": "Complete her scheduled vital signs first, then report all findings after finishing them."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting and rechecking when a stroke is suspected", "C": "Giving aspirin without an order to a client on warfarin", "D": "Delaying the report for routine tasks"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C051", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Helen Karras, 76, tripped on a rug at home and struck her head on a kitchen counter. She takes warfarin for atrial fibrillation. She did not lose consciousness but is admitted to a neurological unit for observation. You are the practical nurse completing her neurological checks and reporting changes to the RN, who coordinates her plan of care. Her daughter, Maria, stays at the bedside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Note the time symptoms began and call the RN immediately for urgent stroke assessment.): Face droop, arm drift, and slurred speech are positive stroke signs, and treatment depends on knowing exactly when symptoms started, so noting the time and calling the RN at once is correct. Rechecking later wastes the treatment window. Aspirin needs an order and adds bleeding risk with warfarin. Completing routine vital signs first also delays the report."}, {"id": 479, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before giving the morning digoxin to a 71-year-old man on a medical ward, you count his apical pulse for a full minute and find it is 52 beats per minute. He feels well, and yesterday's dose caused no problem. What is the correct nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Give half of the dose and chart the pulse in the medication record.", "B": "Give the dose as ordered and note the slow pulse in the nursing record.", "C": "Hold the dose and report the pulse to the prescriber.", "D": "Delay the dose by two hours and give it if the pulse rises."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing the dose without an order", "B": "Giving the dose first and only charting the pulse", "D": "Delaying without reporting the finding"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hold the dose and report the pulse to the prescriber.): Digoxin slows the heart, so a pulse below the usual hold level, commonly 60 per minute, means the dose is held and the finding reported. Feeling well does not change that rule. Giving the dose and only charting the pulse risks toxicity and further slowing. Giving a half dose or delaying for two hours are changes that only a prescriber can make."}, {"id": 480, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On his first measurement at a community clinic, a 50-year-old man has a blood pressure of 158/96 mmHg. He is anxious, drank two coffees this morning, and reports no symptoms or history of hypertension. He asks whether he now has high blood pressure. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Explain that coffee and anxiety caused the reading, so he needs no further blood pressure checks.", "B": "Repeat the reading after rest with correct technique and arrange further readings on other days.", "C": "Advise him to begin a low sodium diet and return for a recheck in one year.", "D": "Tell him he has hypertension and arrange teaching on medications and salt reduction."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismissing the reading with no follow-up", "C": "Delaying reassessment for a full year", "D": "Labelling hypertension from one reading"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repeat the reading after rest with correct technique and arrange further readings on other days.): Hypertension is confirmed only when readings are elevated on repeated measurements, and anxiety and caffeine can raise a single value. Repeating the reading after rest with proper technique and booking further readings gives a reliable answer. Labelling him now is premature. Blaming coffee with no follow-up could miss true disease, and waiting a year is too long."}, {"id": 481, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Priya tells the maternity line her tightening is irregular, comes every ten to twenty minutes, causes little pain, and eases when she walks around the kitchen. Her membranes are intact and there is no bleeding. What is the best interpretation and advice?", "options": {"A": "These signs suggest early labour, so advise staying active and delaying calls until membranes rupture.", "B": "These tightenings are not significant, so reassure her and end the call without a follow-up plan.", "C": "These signs suggest true labour, so advise her to come to hospital immediately.", "D": "These signs suggest false labour, so advise rest, fluids, and a call back if patterns change."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Setting a wrong trigger for calling", "B": "Closing the call without a safety plan", "C": "Treating irregular tightening as true labour"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C058", "cluster_text": "Priya Nair, 27, is pregnant for the first time and is at 39 weeks. She phones the maternity line from home reporting abdominal tightening. Later that evening she is admitted to the labour and delivery unit, where you work as a practical nurse with the RN and a physician. Her partner, Dev, stays with her throughout and asks many questions about what to expect.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (These signs suggest false labour, so advise rest, fluids, and a call back if patterns change.): Irregular contractions that stay far apart, cause little pain, and ease with walking are typical of false labour, which does not change the cervix. Advising rest, fluids, and a call back if contractions become regular, stronger, or if bleeding or fluid loss starts gives a safe plan. Sending her in at once is unnecessary. Waiting for rupture ignores other warning signs, and ending the call with no plan is unsafe."}, {"id": 482, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Priya is now on the unit. Her cervix is 6 cm dilated, her contractions are regular every three minutes, and she wants to push because she feels pressure. Dev asks which stage of labour she is in. Which statement is the best nursing response?", "options": {"A": "She is in the third stage, which begins once strong regular contractions are established.", "B": "She has entered the second stage because the urge to push has started.", "C": "She is still in the first stage, which lasts until the cervix reaches ten centimetres.", "D": "She has entered the second stage because the cervix is past halfway dilated."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confusing the third stage with established labour", "B": "Using the urge to push instead of full dilation", "D": "Marking the second stage by halfway dilation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C058", "cluster_text": "Priya Nair, 27, is pregnant for the first time and is at 39 weeks. She phones the maternity line from home reporting abdominal tightening. Later that evening she is admitted to the labour and delivery unit, where you work as a practical nurse with the RN and a physician. Her partner, Dev, stays with her throughout and asks many questions about what to expect.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (She is still in the first stage, which lasts until the cervix reaches ten centimetres.): The first stage runs from the onset of regular labour until the cervix is fully dilated at 10 cm, so at 6 cm she is still in it. The second stage begins at full dilation and ends with birth. A premature urge to push does not change the stage. The third stage is the delivery of the placenta, not the establishment of labour."}, {"id": 483, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the monitor, variable decelerations follow several contractions while Priya lies flat on her back. The baseline fetal heart rate is 140. She is uncomfortable but wants to stay still. You are reporting the pattern to the RN. Which nursing action is best while awaiting direction?", "options": {"A": "Encourage her to push with each contraction to speed delivery while monitoring continues.", "B": "Help her change to a side-lying position and continue monitoring the fetal heart pattern.", "C": "Increase the oxytocin infusion to shorten contractions, then continue monitoring the fetal heart pattern.", "D": "Keep her supine so the monitor stays in contact with the fetal heart."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pushing early, which adds stress to the fetus", "C": "Changing a medication rate without an order, worsening cord stress", "D": "Keeping a position that compresses the cord and vessels"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C058", "cluster_text": "Priya Nair, 27, is pregnant for the first time and is at 39 weeks. She phones the maternity line from home reporting abdominal tightening. Later that evening she is admitted to the labour and delivery unit, where you work as a practical nurse with the RN and a physician. Her partner, Dev, stays with her throughout and asks many questions about what to expect.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Help her change to a side-lying position and continue monitoring the fetal heart pattern.): Variable decelerations often reflect cord compression, and a side-lying position can relieve pressure on the cord and on the large maternal vessels. Monitoring continues so the effect can be seen and reported. Lying supine can worsen both compression and maternal blood return. Early pushing and a faster oxytocin rate add stress and are not the nurse's decision."}, {"id": 484, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Later, as Priya rests flat on her back between contractions, she reports dizziness and nausea and looks pale. Her blood pressure is 88/54 mmHg compared with 118/70 earlier. The fetal heart rate is steady at 144. What is the best immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Lower the head of the bed further and give her sips of water.", "B": "Place her on her right side to encourage return of blood to the heart.", "C": "Raise her legs on pillows while she stays on her back and recheck in five minutes.", "D": "Turn her onto her left side and recheck her blood pressure and fetal heart rate."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Lowering the bed head, which keeps the weight on the vena cava", "B": "Choosing the wrong side for decompression", "C": "Raising legs without relieving the vena cava pressure"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C058", "cluster_text": "Priya Nair, 27, is pregnant for the first time and is at 39 weeks. She phones the maternity line from home reporting abdominal tightening. Later that evening she is admitted to the labour and delivery unit, where you work as a practical nurse with the RN and a physician. Her partner, Dev, stays with her throughout and asks many questions about what to expect.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Turn her onto her left side and recheck her blood pressure and fetal heart rate.): A pregnant uterus pressing on the inferior vena cava when she lies flat reduces blood return and causes the drop in blood pressure, so turning her to the left side relieves the pressure immediately. Raising her legs leaves the weight on the vein. Lowering the bed head does the same. The left side, not the right, is the position that frees the vena cava."}, {"id": 485, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A parent says her 6-year-old son has had fever for two days, a wet cough, and fast breathing. At the clinic his oxygen saturation is 94 percent on room air, his respiratory rate is 32 per minute, and he has mild chest indrawing. He is drinking fluids. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Send him home with advice to rest and give acetaminophen for the fever as needed.", "B": "Advise a cough suppressant at bedtime and fluids, and return if fever persists beyond a week.", "C": "Report the findings to the RN or physician, since pneumonia needs prompt assessment.", "D": "Tell the parent the cough is viral and antibiotics are not needed for him."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Sending a child with respiratory distress home", "B": "Treating the cough instead of assessing the cause", "D": "Diagnosing the cause without authority"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the findings to the RN or physician, since pneumonia needs prompt assessment.): Fever, fast breathing, and chest indrawing in a child suggest pneumonia and increased work of breathing, which need assessment by the RN or physician and possible treatment. Suppressing a productive cough does not treat pneumonia. Sending him home with rest ignores the distress signs. Deciding the cause is viral is a diagnosis that is outside practical nurse scope."}, {"id": 486, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 24-year-old woman arrives in the emergency department after taking about thirty extra-strength acetaminophen tablets three hours ago. She has no symptoms and says she feels fine, though she admits the overdose was intentional. The RN is preparing the orders. Which understanding best guides your care?", "options": {"A": "Liver injury is silent early, so she needs blood levels and prompt antidote orders.", "B": "She can be observed at home because the lack of symptoms shows the dose was safe.", "C": "Treatment begins when nausea or jaundice appears because these confirm liver involvement.", "D": "Activated charcoal is the only treatment needed since she arrived within four hours."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Equating no symptoms with safety", "C": "Waiting for symptoms before treating", "D": "Relying on charcoal alone without levels or antidote"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Liver injury is silent early, so she needs blood levels and prompt antidote orders.): Acetaminophen overdose causes liver damage that shows no early symptoms, so treatment is based on timed blood levels and an antidote that works best when started early. Waiting for symptoms means waiting for injury. Being symptom free does not mean the dose was safe. Charcoal may be given, but it does not replace levels and antidote decisions."}, {"id": 487, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 7-year-old girl with asthma at a community program takes two puffs of her salbutamol inhaler through a spacer, then blows out hard into the spacer. Her mother says the wheeze is not improving after the two puffs. What teaching will correct her technique?", "options": {"A": "Fire the inhaler first, then place the spacer in her mouth to inhale.", "B": "Breathe out into the spacer after each puff so the medication spreads evenly.", "C": "Take both puffs into the spacer together before breathing in as deeply as possible.", "D": "Seal lips on the mouthpiece, inhale slowly after each puff, then hold her breath."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Wrong order of actuating and inhaling", "B": "Exhaling into the spacer and blowing out the medication", "C": "Giving two doses at once into the chamber"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Seal lips on the mouthpiece, inhale slowly after each puff, then hold her breath.): A spacer holds the medication so it can be inhaled slowly, so one puff at a time, with a seal around the mouthpiece, a slow breath in, and a brief breath hold, delivers it to the lungs. Exhaling into the spacer pushes the dose out. Two puffs together split the dose and waste it. Firing before placing the spacer in the mouth loses the medication."}, {"id": 488, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Chen, 55, will start a 24 hour urine collection for kidney function at 0700 on Tuesday. He emptied his bladder at 0700 and asks which voids to keep. He will complete the collection at home and return the container. What instruction is best?", "options": {"A": "Store the collected urine at room temperature on the counter until returning it.", "B": "Collect only voids passed while awake and estimate the night volume by memory.", "C": "Keep the first 0700 void in the container and stop at the same time tomorrow.", "D": "Discard the 0700 void, collect every later void, and keep the final 0700 void."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaving the specimen unrefrigerated", "B": "Omitting night voids from the total", "C": "Including the first void in the timed collection"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C052", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse in a community health clinic. The RN has assigned you to collect and handle laboratory specimens this morning for four booked clients. The laboratory courier arrives at noon, and the clinic follows the laboratory's written instructions for each specimen type. Each client has a different test, and you must label, store, and document every specimen correctly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discard the 0700 void, collect every later void, and keep the final 0700 void.): A timed collection starts with an empty bladder, so the first void is discarded and the timer begins; every void after that, including the last one at the end of 24 hours, goes into the container. Keeping the first void adds urine made before the start. Skipping night voids gives an incomplete total. The container is kept cool as instructed, not on a counter."}, {"id": 489, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Alvarez, 25, has flank pain and burning with urination. Her midstream urine sat in an open cup on the clinic counter for three hours before you tested it. The dipstick shows negative nitrites with a high pH. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "The delay may have altered the results, so obtain a fresh specimen and tell the RN.", "B": "The result is reliable because dipstick strips tolerate several hours of standing at room temperature.", "C": "The negative nitrite rules out infection, so reassure her and file the result.", "D": "The high pH confirms a urinary tract infection, so report the result as diagnostic."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assuming a stale sample gives valid values", "C": "Using one negative result to rule out infection", "D": "Treating an altered value as a diagnosis"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C052", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse in a community health clinic. The RN has assigned you to collect and handle laboratory specimens this morning for four booked clients. The laboratory courier arrives at noon, and the clinic follows the laboratory's written instructions for each specimen type. Each client has a different test, and you must label, store, and document every specimen correctly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The delay may have altered the results, so obtain a fresh specimen and tell the RN.): Urine left standing allows bacteria to multiply and break down urea, which raises the pH and can change other values, so the dipstick result cannot be trusted. A fresh specimen should be tested promptly and the RN informed, especially with flank pain. A high pH does not diagnose infection, a negative nitrite does not rule it out, and stale samples are not reliable."}, {"id": 490, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Baby Noah, 8 months, has a fever of 38.9 C. A urine collection bag was applied two hours ago in the clinic, and he is asleep with the bag still attached and no urine yet. A urine culture is ordered. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Cut a small opening in his diaper and squeeze the urine into a clean specimen cup.", "B": "Tell the RN a bag may contaminate a culture and ask about a catheter specimen.", "C": "Cover the bag with a diaper and recheck after four hours before collecting.", "D": "Leave the bag attached until he voids, then send the sample from the bag."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Squeezing urine from a diaper, which is not sterile", "C": "Leaving a bag on for a long period", "D": "Using a bag specimen for a culture"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C052", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse in a community health clinic. The RN has assigned you to collect and handle laboratory specimens this morning for four booked clients. The laboratory courier arrives at noon, and the clinic follows the laboratory's written instructions for each specimen type. Each client has a different test, and you must label, store, and document every specimen correctly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tell the RN a bag may contaminate a culture and ask about a catheter specimen.): Urine in a bag sits against the skin and picks up skin bacteria, so a bag sample can falsely suggest infection on a culture. A clean catch or catheter specimen is preferred in infants, which is a decision for the RN or prescriber. Using the bag or diaper urine gives a contaminated sample, and leaving a bag on for hours adds more contamination."}, {"id": 491, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Dube, 45, on a medical unit, has had watery diarrhea for three days, and a stool culture is ordered. He feels the urge to pass stool again and is about to use the toilet. The toilet bowl contains cleaning solution. What is the best way to collect the specimen?", "options": {"A": "Wipe stool from the tissue paper into the container for the laboratory.", "B": "Use a pan then leave the specimen at room temperature until the next courier run.", "C": "Collect the stool from the toilet water using a sterile spoon and label it.", "D": "Use a clean dry pan, transfer stool to the container, and send promptly."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using tissue as a source of stool", "B": "Delaying transport of a culture specimen", "C": "Collecting from toilet water, which contaminates the sample"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C052", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse in a community health clinic. The RN has assigned you to collect and handle laboratory specimens this morning for four booked clients. The laboratory courier arrives at noon, and the clinic follows the laboratory's written instructions for each specimen type. Each client has a different test, and you must label, store, and document every specimen correctly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use a clean dry pan, transfer stool to the container, and send promptly.): A culture needs stool free of water, urine, and cleaning solution, so a clean dry pan is used, a portion with any mucus or blood goes into the container, and it is sent to the laboratory promptly. Toilet water and tissue contaminate the sample. Leaving it at room temperature for hours lets organisms multiply or die and can change the result."}, {"id": 492, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "You delegated routine vital signs on six stable clients to a care aide. She hands you a written list without comment. One older client's pulse is recorded as 118 beats per minute, up from 76 this morning. What is the best follow-up?", "options": {"A": "Review the results, reassess the client with the changed pulse, and tell the RN.", "B": "Ask the aide to recheck the pulse later and report back if it stays elevated.", "C": "Record the list in the chart since the aide is trained to take vital signs safely.", "D": "Thank the aide and accept the results because these tasks were routine and stable."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Handing the assessment back to the aide", "C": "Skipping review of delegated results", "D": "Assuming stable clients give routine results"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Review the results, reassess the client with the changed pulse, and tell the RN.): Delegating a task does not remove the nurse's accountability, so the results must be reviewed, and a large change in pulse needs your own reassessment and a report to the RN. Charting without review misses the change. Asking the aide to recheck leaves interpretation and escalation to a provider who cannot do either. Routine tasks still need follow-up."}, {"id": 493, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 29-year-old man with epilepsy has been having a generalized tonic-clonic seizure at home for over five minutes. His roommate calls you, a home care nurse, by phone and asks whether to wait. The man usually takes seizure medication. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Wait for five more minutes to see whether the seizure stops by itself without help.", "B": "Call emergency services now, since prolonged seizures risk breathing problems.", "C": "Place a rolled towel between his teeth and stay beside him.", "D": "Give his usual medication by mouth once the shaking eases."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting on a seizure that has already run long", "C": "Putting an object in the mouth during a seizure", "D": "Giving oral medication and delaying emergency help"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Call emergency services now, since prolonged seizures risk breathing problems.): A tonic-clonic seizure lasting more than five minutes is a medical emergency, since breathing is impaired and brain injury can follow, so emergency services should be called right away. Waiting longer wastes time. Objects in the mouth risk injury and airway blockage. Oral medication given to a client who is not fully awake risks choking and does not replace emergency care."}, {"id": 494, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 10-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis, receiving home care, has thick sputum and sweats heavily during a hot week. His mother asks whether to limit salt and fluids to reduce his coughing. His appetite is good and he has no vomiting. What advice is best?", "options": {"A": "Allow salt and fluids when his mother notices dry lips or other signs.", "B": "Encourage normal or extra salt and fluids with airway clearance, since he loses salt.", "C": "Reduce fluids to cut mucus production and give extra cough suppressant at night.", "D": "Limit salt intake to prevent fluid retention and reduce the thickness of his secretions."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for dehydration signs before giving fluids", "C": "Cutting fluids, which thickens mucus", "D": "Restricting salt in a client who loses it in sweat"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Encourage normal or extra salt and fluids with airway clearance, since he loses salt.): Cystic fibrosis causes high sweat salt loss, so in hot weather he needs normal or increased salt and plenty of fluids, which also help thin secretions for airway clearance. Limiting salt risks dehydration and low sodium. Cutting fluids thickens mucus. Waiting for dry lips means dehydration has already started."}, {"id": 495, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 28-year-old woman at a community visit two weeks after birth is tearful most days, cannot sleep even when the baby sleeps, and feels like a failure as a mother. Her own mother called it baby blues. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Advise her to rest, accept help at home, and call the clinic if her feelings worsen.", "B": "Tell her the sadness is a normal adjustment and offer information on infant feeding and sleep.", "C": "Reassure her that baby blues resolve within two weeks and review again at six weeks.", "D": "Assess her mood, sleep, and safety with a screening tool and report findings to the RN."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offering advice without screening", "B": "Normalizing symptoms that last beyond expected time", "C": "Labelling it baby blues without assessment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess her mood, sleep, and safety with a screening tool and report findings to the RN.): Baby blues peak in the first days and ease by about two weeks, but persistent low mood, failure feelings, and poor sleep at this point can signal postpartum depression, so a structured screen, a safety check, and a report to the RN are needed. Waiting six weeks, offering general advice, and calling the sadness normal each delay assessment of a client who may be at risk. Early screening and reporting lets the team act sooner."}, {"id": 496, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 33-year-old man with celiac disease at a community clinic says he follows a gluten free diet but still has bloating. He eats oat porridge, soy sauce on rice, and a small piece of bread weekly. Which teaching is best?", "options": {"A": "Avoid wheat, barley, and rye, and check labels for hidden sources like soy sauce.", "B": "Limit gluten to once a week and watch whether bloating returns afterwards.", "C": "Focus on avoiding wheat only, since barley and rye cause fewer bowel problems.", "D": "Small amounts of bread are acceptable if he takes enzyme supplements with the meal."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using a limited schedule for gluten", "C": "Leaving barley and rye out of the avoidance list", "D": "Allowing small amounts of wheat"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Avoid wheat, barley, and rye, and check labels for hidden sources like soy sauce.): Celiac disease requires strict avoidance of gluten from wheat, barley, and rye, and hidden sources such as soy sauce and cross-contaminated oats can cause ongoing symptoms. Even small amounts of bread damage the intestine. Enzyme supplements do not make gluten safe, and a once a week limit still causes damage. Barley and rye are also harmful."}, {"id": 497, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Dwyer, 64, has rheumatoid arthritis in both hands and lives alone in an apartment. She reports morning stiffness and fatigue by midday, and she asks how to manage cooking, cleaning, and other household tasks safely. Which teaching best addresses joint protection and energy conservation?", "options": {"A": "Push through pain to finish chores in one session so the joints stay limber.", "B": "Alternate activity with rest, use larger joints and assistive tools, and do gentle range of motion.", "C": "Rest the hands whenever they ache and avoid moving the joints until the stiffness has passed.", "D": "Wear rigid splints all day and night to keep the finger joints fixed."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pushing through pain, which damages joints", "C": "Prolonged rest that increases stiffness", "D": "Immobilizing joints, which weakens muscles"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C057", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse in a community rehabilitation program that sees clients with joint and mobility problems. The RN has asked you to teach four clients today, using the program's written teaching materials. Each client has different needs. You use plain language, ask clients to repeat the key steps back, and document the teaching and the client's response. Plan about ten minutes for each client.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Alternate activity with rest, use larger joints and assistive tools, and do gentle range of motion.): Joint protection spreads load across larger joints and uses tools, and pacing activity with rest conserves energy while gentle range of motion keeps joints mobile. Long periods of rest increase stiffness. Pushing through pain risks joint damage and flares. Continuous rigid splinting weakens muscles and limits function."}, {"id": 498, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ellis, 52, has had a hot, swollen, very tender great toe since last night, and he has a history of gout with an elevated uric acid. He works as a mechanic and asks about food. Which dietary teaching is best?", "options": {"A": "Limit red meat, organ meats, shellfish, and alcohol, and drink plenty of water.", "B": "Restrict fluids to reduce toe swelling, and limit alcohol and shellfish.", "C": "Replace red meat with organ meats and sardines, which are lean protein sources.", "D": "Replace water with fruit juices and sports drinks to flush the uric acid out."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Restricting fluids when water helps clear uric acid", "C": "Swapping one purine rich food for others that are also high", "D": "Replacing water with sugary drinks"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C057", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse in a community rehabilitation program that sees clients with joint and mobility problems. The RN has asked you to teach four clients today, using the program's written teaching materials. Each client has different needs. You use plain language, ask clients to repeat the key steps back, and document the teaching and the client's response. Plan about ten minutes for each client.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Limit red meat, organ meats, shellfish, and alcohol, and drink plenty of water.): Gout flares are linked to high uric acid, so limiting purine-rich foods such as red meat, organ meats, shellfish, and alcohol while drinking plenty of water helps lower levels. Restricting fluids makes uric acid more concentrated. Organ meats and sardines are very high in purines. Sugary juices and sports drinks can raise uric acid and do not replace water."}, {"id": 499, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Taylor, 79, is two days after hip surgery and starting to walk with a walker. The physiotherapist has allowed partial weight bearing on the operated leg. You are in the rehabilitation gym and want to demonstrate the walker sequence before he practises. Which sequence is correct?", "options": {"A": "Move the walker forward first, then step the operated leg, then the other.", "B": "Step the operated leg forward, then move the walker, then bring the other leg.", "C": "Advance the walker after the stronger leg steps, then follow with the operated leg.", "D": "Lift the walker and the operated leg together, then step through with the other."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Stepping before the walker supports him", "C": "Moving the stronger leg before the walker", "D": "Moving the walker and leg together, which reduces stability"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C057", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse in a community rehabilitation program that sees clients with joint and mobility problems. The RN has asked you to teach four clients today, using the program's written teaching materials. Each client has different needs. You use plain language, ask clients to repeat the key steps back, and document the teaching and the client's response. Plan about ten minutes for each client.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Move the walker forward first, then step the operated leg, then the other.): With a walker, the device goes first to give support, then the weaker or operated leg steps into the walker's frame, and the stronger leg follows. Stepping first leaves him unsupported. Moving the stronger leg before the walker reverses the order. Lifting the walker and leg together reduces stability."}, {"id": 500, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Harper, 34, has a sprained left ankle and uses crutches with no weight on the left foot. She is at the clinic, where the only exit is a flight of stairs without an elevator. You teach her to go downstairs. What is the correct sequence?", "options": {"A": "Place the crutches and injured leg down first, then bring the stronger leg down.", "B": "Step down with the stronger leg first, then bring the crutches and the injured leg.", "C": "Walk down sideways holding the rail with both hands and the crutches in a bag.", "D": "Hop down each step on the stronger leg with the crutches held under one arm."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reversing the up and down sequence", "C": "Carrying crutches away from use on stairs", "D": "Hopping with crutches held in one arm"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C057", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse in a community rehabilitation program that sees clients with joint and mobility problems. The RN has asked you to teach four clients today, using the program's written teaching materials. Each client has different needs. You use plain language, ask clients to repeat the key steps back, and document the teaching and the client's response. Plan about ten minutes for each client.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Place the crutches and injured leg down first, then bring the stronger leg down.): The rule is up with the good leg and down with the bad, so going down she places the crutches and injured leg on the lower step first and then the stronger leg follows. Leading with the strong leg risks a fall forward. Hopping with crutches under one arm is unstable. Putting the crutches in a bag removes her support."}, {"id": 501, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A woman in labour at 40 weeks has a fetal heart rate baseline of 170 beats per minute for the past fifteen minutes with normal variability. Her temperature is 38.4 C and her membranes have been ruptured for 20 hours. Contractions are every four minutes. What is the best interpretation and action?", "options": {"A": "Reposition her supine for comfort and recheck the baseline heart rate after the next two contractions.", "B": "Report the fetal tachycardia to the RN, assess for maternal infection, and continue monitoring.", "C": "Treat the rate as a normal response to labour stress and recheck it hourly.", "D": "Report the maternal fever to the RN, and recheck the fetal rate in one hour."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a supine position that can worsen the pattern", "C": "Treating tachycardia as a normal labour response", "D": "Reporting only part of the findings and delaying reassessment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the fetal tachycardia to the RN, assess for maternal infection, and continue monitoring.): A baseline above 160 for ten minutes or more is fetal tachycardia, and with maternal fever and a long rupture of membranes it suggests infection such as chorioamnionitis, so the RN must be told of the tachycardia and monitoring continued. Contractions cause temporary changes, not a raised baseline. Waiting an hour delays action, and reporting only the fever leaves out the fetal finding."}, {"id": 502, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Lang, 54, is waiting in the pre-operative area of a day surgery unit for a knee arthroscopy. His hands are shaking, his speech is rapid, and he asks the same question about anesthesia three times. Which communication approach is most helpful?", "options": {"A": "Tell him there is nothing to worry about since arthroscopy is a common routine surgery", "B": "Give a full detailed explanation of the whole procedure and recovery so he feels informed.", "C": "Use short, calm sentences, acknowledge his worry, and give one fact at a time", "D": "Hand him printed anesthesia brochures and leave him alone to read them quietly in his bay"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismissing his feelings with false reassurance", "B": "Overloading an anxious client with information", "D": "Leaving him alone with written material"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C072", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse caring for a range of clients over a busy shift. The RN has asked you to apply therapeutic communication principles with four clients who are anxious, angry, withdrawn, or tearful. You use a calm tone, open body language, and active listening, and you document key client statements and your own observations in each record. Each client is in a different part of the facility.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use short, calm sentences, acknowledge his worry, and give one fact at a time): An anxious client takes in little, so short calm statements, acknowledgment of the worry, and one point at a time lower anxiety and help understanding. A long explanation overwhelms him. Telling him not to worry dismisses his feelings. Brochures and leaving him alone do not meet his need for support."}, {"id": 503, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mrs. Petrov, 45, shouts at you on a medical unit because her discharge has been delayed three hours, and she slams the table. Her husband stands behind her, and other clients can hear. Which response best supports her and keeps everyone safe?", "options": {"A": "Stay calm, use a low tone, acknowledge her frustration, and offer to talk privately.", "B": "Calmly tell her the delay is unavoidable and ask her to lower her voice.", "C": "Apologize and promise she will leave within the hour to settle her quickly.", "D": "Match her volume and firmly explain that the delay is unavoidable on this busy unit today."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Correcting her without acknowledging her feelings or moving privately", "C": "Making a promise the nurse cannot keep", "D": "Matching the client's tone"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C072", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse caring for a range of clients over a busy shift. The RN has asked you to apply therapeutic communication principles with four clients who are anxious, angry, withdrawn, or tearful. You use a calm tone, open body language, and active listening, and you document key client statements and your own observations in each record. Each client is in a different part of the facility.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stay calm, use a low tone, acknowledge her frustration, and offer to talk privately.): A calm low tone and acknowledging the frustration reduce escalation, and moving to a private place protects her dignity and other clients. Matching her volume increases conflict. Asking her to lower her voice without acknowledging her feelings can raise anger. Promising a discharge time the nurse cannot control damages trust if it is missed."}, {"id": 504, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Jordan, 15, sits alone in the dayroom of a mental health unit with his head down and answers your greeting with one word. He has not eaten lunch and has refused group. You have 15 minutes. Which approach is most therapeutic?", "options": {"A": "Ask him direct questions about his feelings until he starts talking about the problem.", "B": "Sit nearby, offer quiet presence, and invite him to talk when he feels ready.", "C": "Invite him to join a quiet activity with peers so that he feels included.", "D": "Check on him every fifteen minutes from the nursing station and document his mood."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Forcing conversation with direct questioning", "C": "Pressing a withdrawn client toward group contact", "D": "Observing from a distance instead of building rapport"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C072", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse caring for a range of clients over a busy shift. The RN has asked you to apply therapeutic communication principles with four clients who are anxious, angry, withdrawn, or tearful. You use a calm tone, open body language, and active listening, and you document key client statements and your own observations in each record. Each client is in a different part of the facility.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sit nearby, offer quiet presence, and invite him to talk when he feels ready.): A withdrawn adolescent often responds to calm, non-demanding presence, so sitting nearby and inviting talk when ready builds trust without pressure. Repeated direct questions can increase withdrawal. Steering him toward peers asks more than he can give now. Watching from the station records his mood but gives up the chance to connect."}, {"id": 505, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Ms. Sobol, 38, begins crying in a clinic exam room after the physician tells her that her biopsy shows cancer. The physician leaves for another patient, and Ms. Sobol, who came alone, says she cannot think. Which nursing response is best?", "options": {"A": "Tell her that feeling overwhelmed is common and that many people recover well after cancer treatment.", "B": "Provide tissues and withdraw for several minutes so she can compose herself without an audience present.", "C": "Hand her tissues, tell her to try to stop crying, and offer a glass of water.", "D": "Stay with her, allow silence, acknowledge her distress, and offer to answer questions later."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offering reassurance that lacks basis", "B": "Leaving a distressed client alone right after bad news", "C": "Urging a client to stop crying"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C072", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse caring for a range of clients over a busy shift. The RN has asked you to apply therapeutic communication principles with four clients who are anxious, angry, withdrawn, or tearful. You use a calm tone, open body language, and active listening, and you document key client statements and your own observations in each record. Each client is in a different part of the facility.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stay with her, allow silence, acknowledge her distress, and offer to answer questions later.): After bad news, staying present, allowing silence, and acknowledging the distress let the client feel supported and ready to take in information later. Telling her to stop crying dismisses her emotions. General reassurance about outcomes is not based on her situation. Leaving her alone can feel like abandonment."}, {"id": 506, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 9-year-old girl with asthma in a clinic has a respiratory rate of 24 per minute, which is within the usual range for her age. She sits forward, uses her neck muscles to breathe, and speaks in two-word phrases. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Report increased work of breathing to the RN at once and stay with her.", "B": "Have her lie flat to rest her breathing muscles and recheck in ten minutes.", "C": "Record the rate as normal and reassess her breathing at the next scheduled check.", "D": "Give her a sip of cold water and encourage slow, deep breaths while she talks."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Placing her flat, which worsens breathing", "C": "Relying on respiratory rate alone", "D": "Encouraging talking during respiratory distress"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report increased work of breathing to the RN at once and stay with her.): A normal rate does not show she is breathing easily, since forward sitting, accessory muscle use, and two-word speech show increased work of breathing and a possible severe attack, which needs immediate reporting and continuous observation. Charting the rate as normal ignores the pattern. Lying flat worsens breathing, and talking during distress tires her."}, {"id": 507, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 60-year-old man at a clinic receives a stool test kit for occult blood as part of colorectal screening. He eats red meat daily and takes high-dose vitamin C and ibuprofen for his knee. You are teaching him how to prepare. What instruction is best?", "options": {"A": "Collect samples as usual because diet and medicines do not change this screening test.", "B": "Stop his regular medicines for a week and eat only vegetables before collecting.", "C": "Review diet and medicines with the RN or kit instructions before collecting, since some affect results.", "D": "Collect one sample from the toilet water and one from the surface of the stool."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignoring factors that cause false results", "B": "Stopping prescribed medicine without an order", "D": "Taking sample from toilet water"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Review diet and medicines with the RN or kit instructions before collecting, since some affect results.): Some occult blood tests are affected by red meat, vitamin C, or drugs that irritate the stomach, so the preparation instructions for his kit must be reviewed and any question taken to the RN. Ignoring these factors can cause false results. Stopping medicines is a prescriber decision, and toilet water contaminates the specimen."}, {"id": 508, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 48-year-old woman at a clinic was diagnosed with hypertension last week with readings of 152/94 mmHg and was prescribed medication. She asks whether changing her habits matters now. She walks rarely, adds salt to meals, and drinks three glasses of wine daily. What teaching is best?", "options": {"A": "She should cut out salt and alcohol but keep activity minimal to avoid cardiac strain.", "B": "Weight loss is the only change that matters, so other habits can stay the same.", "C": "Her medication is enough to control blood pressure, so habit changes are optional.", "D": "Less sodium, regular activity, and less alcohol add to the effect of her medication."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Discouraging physical activity as a risk", "B": "Limiting the teaching to a single change", "C": "Treating medication as the only needed measure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Less sodium, regular activity, and less alcohol add to the effect of her medication.): Blood pressure falls further when medication is paired with less sodium, regular activity, and limited alcohol, all of which apply to this client's habits. Saying medication alone is enough ignores that. Avoiding activity removes a proven benefit. Focusing only on weight skips two habits that are driving her readings."}, {"id": 509, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Bell, 68, at home with COPD, shakes his dry powder inhaler before use, then inhales slowly and gently. His breathing remains labored after doses, and his wife says the capsule looks barely used. You observe his technique during a home visit. What teaching best corrects it?", "options": {"A": "Keep shaking it before each dose and breathe in slowly through the mouthpiece.", "B": "Attach a spacer to the device and breathe in slowly after each puff.", "C": "Exhale into the mouthpiece to load the dose, then inhale gently and swallow.", "D": "Exhale away from the device, then inhale quickly and deeply, and hold the breath briefly."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Shaking a dry powder device and inhaling slowly", "B": "Using a spacer, which does not suit a dry powder device", "C": "Exhaling into the device, which blows out the powder"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Exhale away from the device, then inhale quickly and deeply, and hold the breath briefly.): A dry powder inhaler needs a fast, deep breath to pull the powder into the lungs, so he should exhale away from the device, inhale quickly and deeply, and hold the breath briefly. Shaking is not needed and slow breathing leaves powder behind. Spacers are not used with these devices, and exhaling into the mouthpiece blows out the dose."}, {"id": 510, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Documentation timing is under review on a busy surgical unit. One nurse gave analgesic at 0930, repositioned her 55-year-old client at 1000, and noted his rising temperature at 1100, planning to write everything in one block before leaving. Which approach to documentation best meets professional standards?", "options": {"A": "Chart the temperature rise now and enter earlier care from memory later", "B": "Chart the items together before the break, adding times from memory.", "C": "Chart each item soon after it happens, in time order", "D": "Record each item soon after, ordered by which seems most important."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Splits the record so earlier care is reconstructed late", "B": "Batching entries and reconstructing times from memory", "D": "Charting promptly but out of time order"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Chart each item soon after it happens, in time order): The record must show care as it happened, so entries made soon after each action keep times and findings reliable. Batching entries and adding times from memory risks wrong sequencing, which weakens the legal value of the chart. Ordering entries by importance hides the true sequence of care. Entering the temperature now and the earlier care later splits the record."}, {"id": 511, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Her creatinine was normal this morning, but a 62-year-old woman back on the surgical unit after a contrast study has passed only 20 mL of urine in four hours. She feels well and her family is visiting. What is the most appropriate nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Encourage her to drink more fluids and recheck urine volume once the contrast clears", "B": "Chart the output and recheck urine volume with her next scheduled vital signs.", "C": "Ask the family to watch her intake and call if she feels unwell.", "D": "Report the low output promptly and keep monitoring intake and output"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats a possible injury with self-directed fluids and delay", "B": "Charting without reporting a possible injury", "C": "Relying on family to detect a change she cannot feel"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the low output promptly and keep monitoring intake and output): Urine output falls before creatinine rises after contrast exposure, so a four-hour drop of this size is an early warning that needs prompt reporting. Charting and rechecking later delays recognition. Relying on the family to watch for symptoms misses that she feels well while injury may develop. Encouraging fluids and waiting is not an adequate response, and the nurse reports rather than treats independently."}, {"id": 512, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Pat has asked the team what would help them trust her as charge nurse. Considering that one aide hesitated to report a resident's skin change because she doubted anyone would listen, which action by Pat is most likely to build trust across this long-term care team?", "options": {"A": "Hold regular social events but keep discussion of resident concerns off the agenda.", "B": "Admit when unsure, keep her promises, and invite input from staff of every role", "C": "Resolve staff concerns by deciding quickly herself, then inform the team of the outcome.", "D": "Rely on her rank and experience so staff accept decisions without any discussion at huddles"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Building friendship while avoiding open discussion of concerns", "C": "Deciding alone instead of inviting staff input", "D": "Treats authority as the source of trust"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C071", "cluster_text": "Pat is the new charge nurse on a 40-bed long-term care home. Over the past month, two practical nurses and several care aides have told a colleague they feel unheard. One aide noticed a resident's changing skin condition but waited to mention it because she was unsure anyone would listen. Pat wants to strengthen how the team works together and keep residents safe.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Admit when unsure, keep her promises, and invite input from staff of every role): Trust grows through honesty, reliability and respect for every member of the team, which is what admitting limits and following through demonstrate. Rank can produce compliance but not trust, and the team will not speak up to a leader they do not trust. Social events without open discussion leave concerns unheard, and deciding alone gives staff no voice."}, {"id": 513, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At the morning huddle a care aide mentions she noticed new redness on a resident's heel but had not reported it. Pat thanks her and asks what else the team has seen. What is the most likely effect of Pat's response on how staff report concerns?", "options": {"A": "Staff expect to be thanked each time, so they report only the larger concerns.", "B": "Staff conclude that aides' observations about residents count for less than nurses' observations during huddles", "C": "Staff assume the charge nurse will find resident problems herself during rounds anyway.", "D": "Staff feel safe to raise observations early, so concerns reach the nurse before harm develops"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming thanks changes only which concerns are reported", "B": "Assumes role determines the value of an observation", "C": "Assuming leaders rather than staff are the main source of observations"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C071", "cluster_text": "Pat is the new charge nurse on a 40-bed long-term care home. Over the past month, two practical nurses and several care aides have told a colleague they feel unheard. One aide noticed a resident's changing skin condition but waited to mention it because she was unsure anyone would listen. Pat wants to strengthen how the team works together and keep residents safe.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Staff feel safe to raise observations early, so concerns reach the nurse before harm develops): A thankful, curious response signals that speaking up is welcome, which is the core of psychological safety. Residents benefit because early observations such as heel redness can be assessed before skin breakdown. Pat's response does not teach staff to report only large concerns, and nothing in it suggests the charge nurse will find problems alone or ranks the aide below the nurses."}, {"id": 514, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Rank is on the line in the operating room: the surgeon, a very senior colleague, is about to start while the marked surgical site is not visible to the practical nurse. The team is waiting. How should the nurse proceed?", "options": {"A": "State the concern directly, ask to pause, and use the safety checklist", "B": "Defer to the surgeon, since a more senior person is more likely to know the plan", "C": "Ask a colleague whether the site looks right and proceed if no one objects.", "D": "Document the missing marking in the chart and proceed once the surgeon starts."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Equates seniority with correctness in a safety check", "C": "Relying on silence instead of a structured stop", "D": "Recording a concern instead of acting before harm"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C071", "cluster_text": "Pat is the new charge nurse on a 40-bed long-term care home. Over the past month, two practical nurses and several care aides have told a colleague they feel unheard. One aide noticed a resident's changing skin condition but waited to mention it because she was unsure anyone would listen. Pat wants to strengthen how the team works together and keep residents safe.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (State the concern directly, ask to pause, and use the safety checklist): A potential wrong-site error is a safety concern that every team member is expected to voice before it is too late, regardless of rank, and the checklist gives a structured, accepted way to do so. Deferring because of seniority is the exact pattern that allows errors to proceed. Waiting for no objection or documenting it afterward cannot prevent harm to this client."}, {"id": 515, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Her manager dismissed a practical nurse's report of an unsafe staffing assignment, yet the concern still affects clients on the medical unit. The nurse has already spoken to the manager once. According to the principle of escalation, what should she do next?", "options": {"A": "Raise it with coworkers so they can press the manager together.", "B": "Move up the chain of command and document the concern", "C": "Wait until three further attempts with the same manager have failed before going higher", "D": "Submit the concern anonymously to the regulator before telling anyone at work."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Seeking group pressure instead of the chain of command", "C": "Applies an invented number of required attempts", "D": "Skipping internal escalation steps"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C071", "cluster_text": "Pat is the new charge nurse on a 40-bed long-term care home. Over the past month, two practical nurses and several care aides have told a colleague they feel unheard. One aide noticed a resident's changing skin condition but waited to mention it because she was unsure anyone would listen. Pat wants to strengthen how the team works together and keep residents safe.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Move up the chain of command and document the concern): When a concern remains unresolved and clients may be affected, the nurse moves to the next person in the chain of command and records what was reported and to whom. No fixed number of failed attempts is required before escalating. Gathering coworkers does not follow the reporting line, and going outside before using internal steps skips the expected process."}, {"id": 516, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Right knee weakness after surgery has left Mr. Alvarez, 68, with a new single cane. The practical nurse at a rehabilitation clinic is teaching him to walk on level ground. He rates his pain 3 out of 10. Which instruction about cane use is correct?", "options": {"A": "Hold the cane in the hand opposite the weak knee, moving it with the weaker leg", "B": "Hold the cane on the weaker side so it directly supports the injured knee", "C": "Keep the cane tip well in front of the body to prevent tripping", "D": "Hold the cane in either hand and swing it forward after each step"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Places the cane on the weak side", "C": "Keeps the tip too far forward, reducing stability", "D": "Ignores correct hand placement entirely"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C067", "cluster_text": "Mr. Alvarez, 68, is recovering from knee surgery at a community rehabilitation clinic. His right knee is weak and he has a new cane. The practical nurse works with him on safe walking and transfers, then supports a second client, Mrs. Dube, who has left-sided weakness after a stroke and needs help moving from bed to chair. Both clients have caregivers who are eager to help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold the cane in the hand opposite the weak knee, moving it with the weaker leg): The cane goes on the stronger side so the cane and weaker leg move together and share weight, widening the base of support. Placing it on the weak side shifts the client's weight the wrong way and reduces balance. Using either hand or placing the tip far forward both make the gait less stable."}, {"id": 517, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Left-sided weakness after a stroke makes bed to chair moves difficult for Mrs. Dube. The nurse puts a gait belt around her waist over clothing. She is alert and follows simple one-step directions well. How should the belt be used during the transfer?", "options": {"A": "Hold the belt firmly at her back, keep a wide stance, and guide her movement", "B": "Hold the belt at her front, face her, and pull her forward to stand.", "C": "Hold the belt at her weak side and have her pivot on that leg.", "D": "Grasp her weak arm to lift her while the belt keeps her trunk steady"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Pulling the client forward, which risks a fall", "C": "Pivoting on the weak leg that cannot bear weight", "D": "Lifts by the weak arm, risking shoulder injury"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C067", "cluster_text": "Mr. Alvarez, 68, is recovering from knee surgery at a community rehabilitation clinic. His right knee is weak and he has a new cane. The practical nurse works with him on safe walking and transfers, then supports a second client, Mrs. Dube, who has left-sided weakness after a stroke and needs help moving from bed to chair. Both clients have caregivers who are eager to help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold the belt firmly at her back, keep a wide stance, and guide her movement): The belt gives a secure handhold near the client's centre of gravity, which lets the nurse guide and control a fall without pulling on the weak shoulder. Pulling from the front shifts her balance forward. Pivoting on the weak leg can make it give way. Lifting by the affected arm can also injure the shoulder common after stroke."}, {"id": 518, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Partial weight bearing on the left leg is ordered for an older woman with a fracture, and she has a walker. Her pain is controlled and she is a little anxious. When the nurse prepares to move her from bed to chair, which step is correct?", "options": {"A": "Move her to the chair by lifting her under the arms with no pivot", "B": "Have her stand and take several steps to the chair using only the weak leg", "C": "Place the chair on the affected left side so the injured leg leads the movement", "D": "Set the chair on her stronger right side so she pivots on the unaffected leg"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Lifts instead of using a pivot", "B": "Loads the fractured leg beyond the ordered weight bearing", "C": "Transfers toward the injured side"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C067", "cluster_text": "Mr. Alvarez, 68, is recovering from knee surgery at a community rehabilitation clinic. His right knee is weak and he has a new cane. The practical nurse works with him on safe walking and transfers, then supports a second client, Mrs. Dube, who has left-sided weakness after a stroke and needs help moving from bed to chair. Both clients have caregivers who are eager to help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Set the chair on her stronger right side so she pivots on the unaffected leg): A transfer toward the stronger side lets the client use her unaffected leg as the main support and limits load on the fracture, within the weight-bearing order. Moving toward the injured side forces her to rely on the weak leg. Lifting under the arms injures both client and nurse, and walking on the weak leg exceeds partial weight bearing."}, {"id": 519, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "One practical nurse is on duty in a busy long-term care home, and a large resident who cannot bear weight needs a mechanical lift from bed to chair. The care plan lists him at risk for skin tears. What should the nurse do before using the lift?", "options": {"A": "Ask a care aide untrained on lifts to assist, then check the sling.", "B": "Have a second trained person help, and check the sling only if it looks worn.", "C": "Get a second trained staff member, check the sling and lift for damage, then start", "D": "Operate the lift alone to save time, because the machine does the lifting"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using an untrained helper for a safety critical task", "B": "Making the pre-use inspection depend on appearance", "D": "Assumes the machine removes the need for a second person"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C067", "cluster_text": "Mr. Alvarez, 68, is recovering from knee surgery at a community rehabilitation clinic. His right knee is weak and he has a new cane. The practical nurse works with him on safe walking and transfers, then supports a second client, Mrs. Dube, who has left-sided weakness after a stroke and needs help moving from bed to chair. Both clients have caregivers who are eager to help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Get a second trained staff member, check the sling and lift for damage, then start): Most mechanical lift policies require two trained staff because positioning, steering and watching the client are separate tasks that one person cannot do safely. Checking the sling and lift before each use catches wear that may not be visible. An untrained helper cannot safely assist, and an inspection done only when damage is obvious misses hidden wear."}, {"id": 520, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Hours into a wait for severe flank pain, a 45-year-old Indigenous man in the emergency department is told his pain is probably drug seeking, and staff did not review his history. Which statement best describes the systemic racism in this situation?", "options": {"A": "It results from policies created by outside agencies, so staff have no part in changing it.", "B": "It occurs when an individual staff member deliberately acts out of personal prejudice toward a client", "C": "It exists when hospital policies openly state that groups are to be treated differently.", "D": "Routine policies and assumptions create unequal waits and pain care for some groups without intent"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating systemic harm as outside the control of staff", "B": "Treats racism as requiring individual intent", "C": "Requiring written discriminatory policies for racism to exist"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Routine policies and assumptions create unequal waits and pain care for some groups without intent): Systemic racism is built into policies, assumptions and routines, so unequal waits and undertreated pain can happen without anyone intending harm. Defining it as deliberate individual acts misses how structures produce the gap. It does not need written discriminatory policies, and staff practice can change how routines work."}, {"id": 521, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Temperature 38.9 C, heart rate 112, breathing rate 24, blood pressure 96/58 mmHg, and new confusion: these are the findings for a 79-year-old man with an indwelling urinary catheter in a care home. What is the best action for the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Give acetaminophen for the fever and recheck his temperature in a few hours", "B": "Encourage extra oral fluids and let him rest in a quiet room", "C": "Notify the RN or physician immediately and keep monitoring closely", "D": "Continue routine care and reassess after his scheduled morning medications are given"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats the fever alone and delays escalation", "B": "Uses fluids as the plan for a possible sepsis picture", "D": "Lets a deteriorating client wait for scheduled medication rounds"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Notify the RN or physician immediately and keep monitoring closely): Fever, fast heart and breathing rates, low blood pressure and new confusion in a catheterized client suggest sepsis, which can worsen quickly, so urgent reporting is required. Giving acetaminophen and waiting masks a sign without addressing the cause. Fluids and rest do not respond to a possible infection that needs medical assessment and treatment."}, {"id": 522, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Nausea began about six hours after her first chemotherapy cycle, so Mrs. Chen, 52, now starts a second cycle with a scheduled antiemetic ordered. She also reports mild fatigue. When should the practical nurse give the scheduled antiemetic for best control of her nausea?", "options": {"A": "After the first episode of vomiting, so the dose matches an actual observed symptom", "B": "Once the infusion is finished, at a time chosen by the nurse", "C": "Before the chemotherapy and at ordered intervals afterward, since prevention works better than treatment", "D": "Only when she says her nausea is severe enough to stop her eating"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waits for vomiting before acting", "B": "Ignores the timing in the order", "D": "Waits for severe symptoms instead of following the schedule"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C068", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Chen, 52, attends an oncology day unit for her second cycle of chemotherapy. During the first cycle she had nausea starting about six hours after treatment. Her order includes a scheduled antiemetic. Later, the practical nurse is also asked to help in the emergency department with an adult who has reduced consciousness and is vomiting, and to teach clients with reflux in a community clinic.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Before the chemotherapy and at ordered intervals afterward, since prevention works better than treatment): Antiemetics given on schedule and before chemotherapy prevent nausea, which is easier to control than established vomiting. Waiting for vomiting or severe nausea lets the symptom take hold and may reduce her intake. Giving the dose at a time of the nurse's choosing ignores the order, and the nurse follows the prescribed timing."}, {"id": 523, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Vomiting has just started in a man in his thirties who has reduced consciousness and lies flat on an emergency stretcher, with noisy, gurgling breathing. The nurse has suction set up at the bedside already, and his pulse is 110. Which action should the practical nurse take first?", "options": {"A": "Give an oral antiemetic once he is able to swallow a sip of water", "B": "Keep him flat on his back and raise the head of the stretcher slightly", "C": "Place a pillow behind his head and wait for the RN to arrive", "D": "Turn him to his side, head tilted down, and suction the mouth"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives an oral drug to a client with reduced consciousness", "B": "Leaves airway exposed to aspiration in a supine position", "C": "Delays airway protection while waiting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C068", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Chen, 52, attends an oncology day unit for her second cycle of chemotherapy. During the first cycle she had nausea starting about six hours after treatment. Her order includes a scheduled antiemetic. Later, the practical nurse is also asked to help in the emergency department with an adult who has reduced consciousness and is vomiting, and to teach clients with reflux in a community clinic.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Turn him to his side, head tilted down, and suction the mouth): A client with reduced consciousness cannot protect the airway, so side lying with the head down lets vomit drain and suction clears it. Lying flat risks aspiration. An oral drug is unsafe without a gag and swallow reflex, and waiting for help leaves the airway exposed during active vomiting."}, {"id": 524, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Burning chest discomfort after meals brings a man to the community clinic, and he says he often rests in bed right after eating. He drinks two cups of coffee daily and is a non-smoker. What is the most appropriate reflux teaching advice for the nurse to give?", "options": {"A": "Eat a large meal at night, and take a short walk right before bed.", "B": "Stay upright for two to three hours after eating, and raise the head of the bed", "C": "Lie down after meals to rest, since digestion works better when the body is still", "D": "Drink coffee after meals to speed stomach emptying and stay seated for twenty minutes."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Eating a large late meal that raises reflux", "C": "Encourages lying down, worsening acid reflux", "D": "Using coffee, which relaxes the sphincter and worsens reflux"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C068", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Chen, 52, attends an oncology day unit for her second cycle of chemotherapy. During the first cycle she had nausea starting about six hours after treatment. Her order includes a scheduled antiemetic. Later, the practical nurse is also asked to help in the emergency department with an adult who has reduced consciousness and is vomiting, and to teach clients with reflux in a community clinic.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stay upright for two to three hours after eating, and raise the head of the bed): Gravity helps keep acid in the stomach, so staying upright after meals and raising the head of the bed reduce reflux. Lying down after eating allows acid to move into the oesophagus. Large evening meals raise stomach pressure, and coffee can worsen symptoms. Twenty minutes upright is too short to help."}, {"id": 525, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An older woman with a hiatal hernia eats three large meals daily and enjoys a snack at bedtime. She asks the community nurse how to control her reflux. She lives alone and cooks for herself each day. Which lifestyle advice should the nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Lie flat after the evening snack so her stomach can settle before she falls asleep", "B": "Wear a firm abdominal binder after meals to hold the hernia in place.", "C": "Eat smaller, frequent meals, skip bedtime snacks, and avoid known trigger foods", "D": "Eat three moderate meals with a bedtime snack, and lie on her left side."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reclining after eating promotes reflux", "B": "Raising abdominal pressure with a binder", "D": "Eating late and lying down soon after"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C068", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Chen, 52, attends an oncology day unit for her second cycle of chemotherapy. During the first cycle she had nausea starting about six hours after treatment. Her order includes a scheduled antiemetic. Later, the practical nurse is also asked to help in the emergency department with an adult who has reduced consciousness and is vomiting, and to teach clients with reflux in a community clinic.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Eat smaller, frequent meals, skip bedtime snacks, and avoid known trigger foods): Smaller meals reduce stomach volume and pressure against the weak diaphragm opening, and avoiding late snacks lowers night reflux. Lying down soon after eating encourages acid to rise. A binder raises abdominal pressure, which pushes more of the stomach upward, and a bedtime snack keeps the stomach full overnight."}, {"id": 526, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Most days, a 74-year-old woman takes ibuprofen for knee pain and calls it safe because it needs no prescription. Her last blood work showed mild kidney impairment. She also takes a daily vitamin and has no known allergies. What teaching should the community nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Advise her to take it with food and keep using it at home.", "B": "Reassure her that nonprescription drugs are safe for the kidneys at standard doses", "C": "Explain that NSAIDs harm kidneys and refer her to her provider", "D": "Suggest she take half the usual dose, since that lowers the kidney effect."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing food protects the kidneys from NSAIDs", "B": "Treats non-prescription as risk free", "D": "Assuming a lower self chosen dose is safe for impaired kidneys"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain that NSAIDs harm kidneys and refer her to her provider): NSAIDs reduce blood flow to the kidneys and can worsen existing impairment, so she needs a provider's guidance on alternatives. Over-the-counter status does not guarantee safety. Taking it with food protects the stomach, not the kidneys. A lower self-chosen dose still carries risk and should be decided with her provider."}, {"id": 527, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Each time she stands from her chair, a 76-year-old woman in home care feels dizzy, and her systolic pressure drops 28 mmHg. A neighbour says she should stand up quickly to get her blood moving. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Sit at the edge first, rise slowly, pause to steady herself, and sit if dizzy", "B": "Drink a large glass of water first, then stand and walk right away.", "C": "Stand up quickly and walk, since movement ends the dizziness and improves circulation to her legs", "D": "Wear loose socks and stand quickly so her legs do not pool blood."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using fluids but still standing quickly", "C": "Rapid standing worsens the pressure drop", "D": "Believing loose socks prevent pooling during a fast rise"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sit at the edge first, rise slowly, pause to steady herself, and sit if dizzy): A systolic drop of 28 mmHg on standing is orthostatic hypotension, so gradual position changes give the body time to adjust. Quick standing causes sudden pressure drops and falls, even after drinking water or changing socks. The neighbour's advice contradicts the stated drop."}, {"id": 528, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Because he feels fine, a 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes sees no reason to worry about an eGFR result of 48 at the community clinic. He takes metformin and a statin, and his blood pressure is 128/80 mmHg. How should the nurse respond to his view?", "options": {"A": "Advise him to stop metformin and the statin on his own until his kidney function improves", "B": "Agree that the result is probably laboratory variation and need not be repeated", "C": "Tell him fatigue and leg swelling would appear early, so he can wait for those signs", "D": "Say that kidney disease is often silent, so regular testing and glucose control matter"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stops treatment without a prescriber's order", "B": "Dismisses an abnormal result because he has no symptoms", "C": "Expects early symptoms that usually appear late"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Say that kidney disease is often silent, so regular testing and glucose control matter): Chronic kidney disease is usually silent until it is advanced, so monitoring and control of blood pressure and glucose protect remaining function. Treating the result as a variation ignores an abnormal value. Waiting for symptoms delays care, and stopping medicines is outside the nurse's scope and could cause harm."}, {"id": 529, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Three loose stools in eight hours have followed a recent antibiotic course in an 84-year-old resident of a long-term care home, and the physician orders Clostridioides difficile testing. His temperature is normal, and he is eating and drinking fairly well. Which specimen should the practical nurse collect?", "options": {"A": "A stool sample collected after a laxative is given to produce a loose result.", "B": "A loose stool held at room temperature until the day shift courier arrives.", "C": "A fresh loose stool that takes the shape of the container", "D": "A formed stool sample, since it is easier to handle and transport safely"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving a laxative to obtain a specimen without an order", "B": "Delaying transport so the specimen degrades", "D": "Collects a formed stool that is not appropriate for testing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A fresh loose stool that takes the shape of the container): Testing is done on liquid or loose stool because formed stool does not indicate active toxin-producing infection. A prompt send avoids degradation, and contact precautions should begin while results are pending. A formed sample may give an unreliable result. A laxative induced sample needs an order, and a specimen left at room temperature for hours may give a false result."}, {"id": 530, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Trauma history is part of the chart of a 34-year-old woman on a mental health unit, and she tenses when touched. The practical nurse must take her blood pressure. The unit is quiet, and her mood has been stable. Which approach best reflects trauma informed care?", "options": {"A": "Explain the steps, ask permission, offer choices, and stop if she asks", "B": "Take the reading gently without comment, since a quiet routine procedure needs no explanation", "C": "Tell her you will go quickly and ask her to look away while you work.", "D": "Have a second staff member stand close for safety while you take it."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assumes gentleness replaces explanation and consent", "C": "Prioritizing speed and giving no choice", "D": "Adding an observer, which can feel threatening to a trauma survivor"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain the steps, ask permission, offer choices, and stop if she asks): Trauma informed care rests on safety, choice, trust and collaboration, so explaining, asking and offering control give her back some power. A gentle but silent procedure still takes control away. Rushing and asking her to look away offers no choice. An extra person standing close can feel threatening to a survivor."}, {"id": 531, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Rapid-acting insulin is due for a 5-year-old on the pediatric unit, and the practical nurse has drawn up the dose. She asks a second nurse to verify it. Both nurses are on shift and the child's mother is present. How should the independent double check be done?", "options": {"A": "The second nurse reads the first nurse's calculation and agrees", "B": "The second nurse independently checks the order, dose, drug and child before comparing results", "C": "The second nurse signs after watching the first nurse draw up the insulin dose", "D": "The second nurse checks only the vial label"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Anchors on the first nurse's answer", "C": "Co-signing by observation is not independent", "D": "Checks a single element rather than the whole dose"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The second nurse independently checks the order, dose, drug and child before comparing results): An independent double check means each nurse verifies the order, calculation, drug and client without being influenced by the other, which catches errors in pediatric insulin dosing. Watching and signing does not test the dose. Agreeing with a visible calculation anchors thinking, and a label check alone leaves the calculation unchecked."}, {"id": 532, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Months of stable vital signs describe a resident of a long-term care home. When planning the shift, the practical nurse considers assigning routine vital signs to a trained unregulated care provider (UCP). His family visits every weekend, and no recent falls are recorded. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "The nurse passes on all responsibility, including interpretation, once the task is delegated", "B": "A trained UCP may take them, and the nurse interprets them", "C": "The UCP should decide whether abnormal values need to be reported to the nurse", "D": "Vital signs cannot be delegated to anyone other than a regulated nurse"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believes accountability moves with the task", "C": "Gives assessment judgment to an unregulated provider", "D": "Assumes no delegation of measurement is allowed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A trained UCP may take them, and the nurse interprets them): Measuring vital signs in a stable resident is a task that can be delegated to a competent, trained UCP, but assessment and follow-up stay with the nurse. Accountability does not transfer with the task. Delegation of routine measurement is permitted, and the UCP reports values so the nurse decides what they mean."}, {"id": 533, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Her legs feel heavy fifteen minutes after an epidural was placed for a 29-year-old woman in labour. The practical nurse plans monitoring for the first period afterward. Her contractions are regular, and the fetal heart rate is currently normal. Which plan best protects her and the fetus?", "options": {"A": "Check blood pressure and fetal heart rate often, as ordered, and report any drop", "B": "Stop monitoring once her legs feel numb and she is comfortable", "C": "Check blood pressure every thirty minutes and fetal heart rate once an hour.", "D": "Check blood pressure often, but record the fetal heart rate only if contractions slow."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Ends monitoring while risk of hypotension remains", "C": "Spacing checks too far apart in the high risk period", "D": "Monitoring the fetus only when contractions change"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check blood pressure and fetal heart rate often, as ordered, and report any drop): Epidurals can cause sudden hypotension and affect the fetus, so frequent early blood pressure and fetal heart rate checks are needed and drops are reported. Spacing checks to thirty minutes or an hour misses the period of highest risk. Watching the fetal rate only when contractions slow can miss early changes, and numbness does not mean the risk has ended."}, {"id": 534, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A colleague claims crackles always clear with coughing. The nurse hears crackles over the right lower lobe of a 66-year-old man with pneumonia, asks him to cough, and listens again, and they persist. His temperature is 38.2 C and his oxygen saturation is 93 percent on room air. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Crackles that persist after coughing reflect expected pneumonia changes and can be charted without reporting.", "B": "Crackles that clear after one cough show that the pneumonia has resolved and treatment can stop", "C": "Persisting crackles indicate narrowed airways and should be treated by giving a rescue inhaler.", "D": "Persisting crackles suggest small airway secretions and should be reported with other findings"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating persistent abnormal sounds as routine", "B": "Equates temporary clearing with cure", "C": "Mixing up crackles with wheezes and treating without an order"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Persisting crackles suggest small airway secretions and should be reported with other findings): Crackles arise from fluid, secretions or collapsed airways opening, and when they persist after coughing they point to continuing pathology that should be reported with the rest of the assessment. They are not an expected finding to chart without reporting. Narrowed airways produce wheezes, and an inhaler needs an order. Clearing after a cough does not show that the infection has resolved."}, {"id": 535, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Alert and without visible injury, an 85-year-old resident on warfarin asks to go back to bed after an unwitnessed fall. She weighs 52 kg and her last INR was 2.6, taken yesterday. She remembers sliding off the chair. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Wait until the next shift to complete a fall report and assessment", "B": "Give her acetaminophen and check on her once the next morning", "C": "Return her to bed since there is no visible injury and rest will help", "D": "Assess her, report to the RN, and start neurological checks"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays assessment and reporting", "B": "Treats pain only without monitoring for bleeding", "C": "Relies on lack of visible injury"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess her, report to the RN, and start neurological checks): Warfarin raises the risk of bleeding inside the skull even after a minor or unwitnessed fall, so a thorough assessment, reporting and serial neurological checks are needed. Absence of visible injury does not exclude internal bleeding. Postponing the assessment or giving acetaminophen alone leaves a possible bleed unmonitored."}, {"id": 536, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Dizziness and confusion are new for a 79-year-old community client who takes eight medications, according to her daughter. A prescriber suggests adding another drug for the dizziness. The daughter also mentions that her mother recently moved homes. What principle should the practical nurse raise with the team?", "options": {"A": "Stop the drugs that cause dizziness without waiting for a prescriber's review", "B": "Add the new drug first, since quick treatment of the dizziness prevents falls and hospital stays", "C": "Older adults need more medications as they age, so the number is expected", "D": "A new symptom may be a drug side effect, so review medications before adding another"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stops medications outside scope", "B": "Starts a prescribing cascade", "C": "Normalizes polypharmacy without review"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A new symptom may be a drug side effect, so review medications before adding another): Adding a drug to treat the effect of another drug can start a prescribing cascade, and a medication review looks for a cause first. Polypharmacy increases adverse effects and does not make more drugs expected. Stopping medications without an order is outside the nurse's scope and can be harmful."}, {"id": 537, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Care aides report that a resident's bath was finished by a colleague who has gone on a break, and the chart still shows the bath as pending. The aide mentions that the resident enjoyed the bath and asked for lotion. How should the practical nurse in this long-term care home document it?", "options": {"A": "Chart the bath as done by herself since the care was completed on her shift", "B": "Chart it under the colleague's name, since she gave the bath.", "C": "Do not chart it as her own; have the colleague chart it", "D": "Add a note to the pending entry stating that the bath was probably completed."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Documents care she did not perform", "B": "Entering documentation under another nurse's name", "D": "Recording an uncertain entry"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Do not chart it as her own; have the colleague chart it): A nurse documents only what she personally did or observed, so the colleague should chart the bath or the entry should clearly state the source. Charting someone else's care as her own is a false entry. Signing under the colleague's name is also false. A vague probable entry is not an accurate record, and leaving it blank for days lacks timeliness."}, {"id": 538, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Blood pressure of 152/94 mmHg and a daily habit of canned soup with a table salt substitute describe a 60-year-old man who thinks the substitute is healthy. He works night shifts, lives alone and sees his family doctor every six months. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Keep using the salt substitute freely at the table, as it contains no sodium", "B": "Replace canned soup with canned vegetables, and keep using the substitute freely.", "C": "Choose lower sodium foods and check with his provider before using a salt substitute", "D": "Stop all salt and use only the table substitute in cooking and at the table."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes substitutes are safe in any amount", "B": "Swapping one high sodium food for another and ignoring potassium risk", "D": "Relying entirely on a substitute without provider advice"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Choose lower sodium foods and check with his provider before using a salt substitute): Canned soup is high in sodium, and many salt substitutes use potassium chloride, which can be unsafe with some medications or kidney disease, so provider input is needed. Reading labels helps reduce sodium. Canned vegetables are also high in sodium. Using substitutes freely or as the only seasoning does not address his blood pressure safely."}, {"id": 539, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Daily inhaled corticosteroid has just been started for a 41-year-old woman with asthma, and she asks how to prevent side effects. She is a non-smoker and uses her reliever inhaler twice weekly. Which instruction about mouth care should the practical nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Brush her teeth and gargle with mouthwash before the dose to prevent thrush.", "B": "Rinse the mouth with water after each dose and spit, to lower the risk of thrush", "C": "Drink a full glass of milk after each dose to coat the mouth.", "D": "Skip rinsing, since the medication stays only in the lungs"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Placing mouth care before the dose instead of after", "C": "Using milk, which does not clear the steroid residue", "D": "Believes residue does not remain in the mouth"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rinse the mouth with water after each dose and spit, to lower the risk of thrush): Inhaled steroid deposits in the mouth and throat and can cause oral candidiasis, so rinsing and spitting after each dose removes it. Mouth care before the dose does nothing to remove residue left afterwards. Milk coats the mouth but does not clear the steroid, and skipping the rinse is wrong because much of the dose lands in the mouth and throat, not only the lungs."}, {"id": 540, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Coughing while drinking is new for a resident of a long-term care home with swallowing difficulty. A newly hired care aide with no dysphagia training offers to feed him lunch and save the nurse time. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Thicken his fluids as the aide chooses and let her feed him slowly.", "B": "Feed him lunch with the aide, who watches for coughing and uses a spoon.", "C": "Feed him yourself or assign someone trained in dysphagia feeding who follows the plan", "D": "Delegate the meal to the aide, since feeding is a routine task"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Letting an untrained aide choose the fluid consistency", "B": "Sharing a high risk task with an untrained provider", "D": "Delegates a high risk task to an untrained provider"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Feed him yourself or assign someone trained in dysphagia feeding who follows the plan): Dysphagia increases aspiration risk, so feeding needs a trained person who follows the swallowing plan. An untrained aide cannot recognize warning signs or set the correct consistency. Watching for coughing is not enough without training. Rushing the meal also raises the chance of aspiration and pneumonia."}, {"id": 541, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Potassium of 5.8 mmol/L is her latest result, and a 70-year-old woman with stage 4 chronic kidney disease asks if fruit is a healthy snack. She enjoys bananas and oranges. Her blood pressure is 134/82 mmHg, and she attends dialysis planning classes. What should the practical nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Choose canned fruit, since it holds little potassium.", "B": "Eat more high potassium fruit since fruit is healthy and her kidneys filter well", "C": "Limit high potassium fruits and follow the dietitian's plan", "D": "Skip fruit and season food with a potassium salt substitute."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming canned fruit is low in potassium", "B": "Encourages foods that raise potassium", "D": "Using a potassium salt substitute with a high potassium level"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Limit high potassium fruits and follow the dietitian's plan): A potassium of 5.8 mmol/L is above the normal range, and failing kidneys cannot excrete potassium well, so high potassium fruits should be limited with dietitian guidance. Fruit is healthy for many people but not this client. Canned fruit still contains potassium, and potassium based salt substitutes add more."}, {"id": 542, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Heparin is infusing for a 65-year-old man on a surgical unit, and two practical nurses must verify the pump at the bedside. One says a quick glance from the doorway is enough. The pump alarm is silent, and the room is dim. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "One nurse may check the pump settings while the other nurse checks the client's name band", "B": "Both nurses independently check the order, drug, rate and pump settings at the bedside", "C": "A shared glance from the doorway counts as a double check", "D": "The second nurse may sign after hearing the first nurse read the rate aloud to her"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Splits the check so neither nurse verifies everything", "C": "Treats a doorway glance as verification", "D": "Signs on hearing instead of independent checking"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Both nurses independently check the order, drug, rate and pump settings at the bedside): A high-alert infusion needs a true independent double check at the pump with each nurse verifying order, drug, rate and settings. A glance or a verbal repeat does not meet that standard. Dividing the tasks means that neither nurse has verified the whole infusion."}, {"id": 543, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Each winter a 33-year-old woman feels low, sleeps more and loses interest in activities, and coworkers tease her about being gloomy by nature. She tells the community nurse about this. She works indoors in an office and has no history of other mental health concerns. What is the nurse's best response?", "options": {"A": "Tell her it is a mild case of boredom that goes away with weekend activities.", "B": "Advise her to take herbal supplements bought at a pharmacy and skip seeing her provider.", "C": "Suggest she wait until spring, since it resolves on its own", "D": "Acknowledge that seasonal low mood is real and encourage discussing treatment options with her provider"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Minimizing a recognized pattern of low mood", "B": "Self treating without assessment", "C": "Assumes it will resolve without support"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acknowledge that seasonal low mood is real and encourage discussing treatment options with her provider): Seasonal patterns of low mood are a recognized condition that responds to treatment such as light exposure, activity and sometimes medication, so a provider referral is helpful. Calling it boredom dismisses her experience. Self-chosen supplements skip assessment, and waiting until spring may prolong distress."}, {"id": 544, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Hesitation before a pelvic examination marks a 39-year-old client with a history of abuse at a women's health clinic. The assisting practical nurse wants to apply trauma informed principles. She arrived on time and brought a friend to wait in the lobby. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Tell her to relax, and explain the steps once she is positioned.", "B": "Offer choices, such as who is present and when to pause, and explain each step", "C": "Proceed quickly to reduce her stress and finish the examination in less time than usual", "D": "Ask her friend to join the room without asking the client first."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying explanation until after she is positioned", "C": "Puts speed ahead of choice", "D": "Choosing for the client who should be present"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Offer choices, such as who is present and when to pause, and explain each step): Offering choices and clear explanation gives her control, which supports trust and reduces the risk of retraumatization. Speeding through the exam removes that control. Explaining only after she is positioned reverses the right order. Adding a person without asking takes away her choice about who is present."}, {"id": 545, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "'10 U' of insulin is what the prescriber wrote for a 58-year-old man with diabetes on a medical unit. His blood glucose is 11.2 mmol/L, and the order is clearly signed. Knowing insulin is a high-alert medication, what is the safest action for the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Ask the pharmacist to relabel the vial as 10 units and then give it.", "B": "Give 10 units, since the order is signed and the glucose supports a dose.", "C": "Give 10 units since the abbreviation U is widely understood by experienced nurses and pharmacists.", "D": "Clarify the order with the prescriber, since U can be misread as zero"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing the label instead of clarifying with the prescriber", "B": "Accepting a prohibited abbreviation because the order is signed", "C": "Accepts a prohibited abbreviation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Clarify the order with the prescriber, since U can be misread as zero): The abbreviation U has been mistaken for zero or four, producing tenfold errors, so it is on the do-not-use list and must be clarified with the prescriber before giving. Relabelling the vial does not correct the order. A signed order does not make the abbreviation safe, and a nurse giving a guessed dose risks serious harm."}, {"id": 546, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Nervous at the clinic, Mr. Okoye has a high reading, but his cuff looks small for his large arm and he is wearing a thick sweater. The room is warm and he has just walked in from the parking lot. What should the practical nurse do before repeating the blood pressure measurement?", "options": {"A": "Use a smaller cuff with the arm hanging down at his side", "B": "Use a correctly sized cuff on his bare upper arm, supported at heart level", "C": "Take the reading while he is talking so that he relaxes", "D": "Keep the same cuff and measure over the sweater to save time during the visit"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Errors in arm position raise the reading", "C": "Talking raises the pressure", "D": "Uses an undersized cuff on clothing"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C070", "cluster_text": "Mr. Okoye, 55, is anxious about his blood pressure at a community clinic. The practical nurse measures his pressure, then assists an older resident, Mrs. Singh, who becomes dizzy on standing. Later, Mr. Okoye begins a new antihypertensive and asks about it, and a different client returns with lip swelling after starting a blood pressure drug. Each visit includes teaching and a safety check.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use a correctly sized cuff on his bare upper arm, supported at heart level): An undersized cuff or measurement over clothing gives falsely high values, and the arm should be bare and supported at heart level. Keeping the same cuff repeats the error. An arm hanging down and talking during the reading each raise the measurement."}, {"id": 547, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Faintness on standing is what Mrs. Singh, 79, reports in long-term care. The nurse plans lying and standing blood pressure readings. She uses a walker and has no history of fainting. How should the nurse proceed to keep her safe during the test?", "options": {"A": "Leave her alone at the bedside while she stands", "B": "Help her rise gradually, stay beside her, and stop if she becomes faint", "C": "Have her stand quickly once, then sit, and record both readings together.", "D": "Take the standing reading as soon as she stands, even if she sways."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaving a dizzy client unattended", "C": "Rapid standing that raises the risk of a fall", "D": "Continuing the test despite signs of faintness"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C070", "cluster_text": "Mr. Okoye, 55, is anxious about his blood pressure at a community clinic. The practical nurse measures his pressure, then assists an older resident, Mrs. Singh, who becomes dizzy on standing. Later, Mr. Okoye begins a new antihypertensive and asks about it, and a different client returns with lip swelling after starting a blood pressure drug. Each visit includes teaching and a safety check.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Help her rise gradually, stay beside her, and stop if she becomes faint): A faint-prone older adult needs gradual position changes and a nurse nearby so that a fall can be prevented, and the test stops if symptoms appear. Rising quickly raises the risk of a fall. Continuing while she sways ignores warning signs, and being left alone increases the danger."}, {"id": 548, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At home, a 60-year-old man feels well, his blood pressure is now a normal 118/76 mmHg, and he wants to stop his antihypertensive. He also takes a daily vitamin, and he walks daily. What is the nurse's best response during the teaching visit?", "options": {"A": "Explain that the normal reading shows the drug works, and ask him to call his prescriber", "B": "Agree that he can stop the drug since his reading is now normal", "C": "Advise stopping only if he also has no headaches", "D": "Tell him to cut the dose in half and recheck in a month"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Equates a controlled reading with cure", "C": "Uses symptoms as a stopping rule", "D": "Changes the dose without a prescriber"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C070", "cluster_text": "Mr. Okoye, 55, is anxious about his blood pressure at a community clinic. The practical nurse measures his pressure, then assists an older resident, Mrs. Singh, who becomes dizzy on standing. Later, Mr. Okoye begins a new antihypertensive and asks about it, and a different client returns with lip swelling after starting a blood pressure drug. Each visit includes teaching and a safety check.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that the normal reading shows the drug works, and ask him to call his prescriber): A normal reading usually reflects effective treatment, and stopping suddenly can cause a rebound rise in pressure. Changes in the drug plan are made by the prescriber. Halving the dose or using headaches as a guide are unreliable and unsafe."}, {"id": 549, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Lisinopril was started a month ago for a 57-year-old woman who now reports a dry cough, and the nurse sees swelling of her lips and tongue. She thinks it is minor. She has no previous reaction to medications and feels anxious. What is the priority action?", "options": {"A": "Reassure her that cough and swelling are common minor side effects and can wait", "B": "Give an oral antihistamine and schedule a follow-up visit next week", "C": "Advise her to take the next dose and watch the swelling", "D": "Treat the swelling as an emergency, keep her seated, and call for urgent help"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats angioedema as minor", "B": "Delays emergency care by relying on an oral drug", "C": "Gives another dose despite airway risk"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C070", "cluster_text": "Mr. Okoye, 55, is anxious about his blood pressure at a community clinic. The practical nurse measures his pressure, then assists an older resident, Mrs. Singh, who becomes dizzy on standing. Later, Mr. Okoye begins a new antihypertensive and asks about it, and a different client returns with lip swelling after starting a blood pressure drug. Each visit includes teaching and a safety check.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat the swelling as an emergency, keep her seated, and call for urgent help): Swelling of the lips and tongue with an ACE inhibitor may be angioedema, which can close the airway, so urgent help is needed and the drug is not given. Dry cough alone is a common effect. Waiting, antihistamine alone or taking more medication all put her airway at risk."}, {"id": 550, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Cold hands and nail polish are present on an adult whose finger oximeter reads 84 percent, though he looks comfortable and breathes easily. His pulse is 76 and his lips are pink, and he denies chest pain. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Assume the device is faulty and set the reading aside", "B": "Apply oxygen immediately based on the single low reading", "C": "Record 84 percent in the chart and carry on with routine care", "D": "Warm the hand, remove the polish or use another site, and recheck while observing him"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Discards data without troubleshooting", "B": "Treats an unreliable value as definite", "C": "Documents the value without validating"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Warm the hand, remove the polish or use another site, and recheck while observing him): Cold fingers and nail polish can cause falsely low readings, so the nurse corrects technique, rechecks and assesses the client. Starting oxygen based on one doubtful value is premature. Recording the number without validation, or discarding the device, skips the assessment steps."}, {"id": 551, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sleep trouble is the complaint of Mrs. Ferreira, 82, in long-term care, and a nursing assistant suggests asking for a benzodiazepine because it worked for another resident. Her family visits weekly, and she naps for an hour each afternoon. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Offer a warm drink with caffeine at bedtime to settle her routine.", "B": "Request a benzodiazepine as it is the usual treatment for sleep trouble", "C": "Try non-drug sleep measures first and report so the prescriber can review options", "D": "Give an over the counter antihistamine from the unit stock to help her sleep."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offering a stimulant before sleep", "B": "Overlooks sleep drug risks in older adults", "D": "Giving a drug from stock without an order to an older adult"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C069", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ferreira, 82, lives in a long-term care home and reports trouble sleeping. Her creatinine has recently risen and her medical team is reviewing her medications. In the same unit, Mr. Bell has cirrhosis and a new drug order, and a palliative client is taking morphine. Her daughter visits every weekend and worries about her mother's falls and confusion at night.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Try non-drug sleep measures first and report so the prescriber can review options): Benzodiazepines raise the risk of falls and confusion in older adults and are generally avoided, so non-drug measures and prescriber review are favoured. Caffeine can keep her awake. An antihistamine from stock needs an order and adds confusion risk in older adults. Requesting a benzodiazepine overlooks these risks."}, {"id": 552, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Creatinine has risen from 80 to 150 umol/L in Mrs. Ferreira, and a medication cleared by the kidneys is now due. She is alert, eating well and has no swelling at present, and her last weight was stable. Which action should the practical nurse take?", "options": {"A": "Report the creatinine to the prescriber before giving, since the dose may need adjusting", "B": "Give half the dose based on the nurse's own estimate", "C": "Give the usual dose and recheck her creatinine at her next routine laboratory test visit", "D": "Hold all fluids so that the kidneys can rest"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Adjusts the dose by own estimate without prescriber", "C": "Gives usual dose despite rising creatinine and impaired clearance", "D": "Restricts fluids without an order, risking dehydration"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C069", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ferreira, 82, lives in a long-term care home and reports trouble sleeping. Her creatinine has recently risen and her medical team is reviewing her medications. In the same unit, Mr. Bell has cirrhosis and a new drug order, and a palliative client is taking morphine. Her daughter visits every weekend and worries about her mother's falls and confusion at night.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the creatinine to the prescriber before giving, since the dose may need adjusting): A rising creatinine suggests reduced clearance, so drugs cleared by the kidneys can accumulate and need a prescriber's decision. Giving the usual dose risks toxicity. Fluid restriction needs an order, and the nurse does not independently halve a dose."}, {"id": 553, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Pain medication seems less effective to Mr. Bell, 61, who has cirrhosis, and a coworker says a bigger dose will fix it. His abdomen is slightly distended and his skin is yellowed. What response by the nurse is correct after reviewing his drug record?", "options": {"A": "Shorten the dosing interval without an order so his pain stays controlled through the night", "B": "Withhold the analgesic until his liver function recovers, leaving his pain untreated in the meantime", "C": "Increase the dose on her own so that his pain is controlled more quickly overnight", "D": "Report that reduced liver function can alter drug handling, and ask for a dose review"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Shortens the interval without authority", "B": "Withholds an analgesic without direction", "C": "Raises the dose without an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C069", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ferreira, 82, lives in a long-term care home and reports trouble sleeping. Her creatinine has recently risen and her medical team is reviewing her medications. In the same unit, Mr. Bell has cirrhosis and a new drug order, and a palliative client is taking morphine. Her daughter visits every weekend and worries about her mother's falls and confusion at night.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report that reduced liver function can alter drug handling, and ask for a dose review): In cirrhosis the liver clears many drugs more slowly, so effects and risks can change and a prescriber should review the plan. Increasing the dose or interval without an order is outside scope and risks accumulation. Stopping an analgesic abruptly leaves his pain untreated."}, {"id": 554, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Four days without a bowel movement and a firm abdomen are present in a palliative client on regular morphine, and a colleague says the constipation will pass without treatment. He is comfortable at rest and his appetite is small, with a pain score of 3. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Report it and expect a bowel regimen order, since opioids slow the bowel", "B": "Reduce the morphine dose without an order to ease the abdominal firmness", "C": "Ask him to drink more fluids and watch for a bowel movement over three days.", "D": "Give an enema from the unit supply to clear the stool."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Lowers the opioid dose without an order, undertreating pain", "C": "Waiting on fluids alone for opioid constipation", "D": "Giving a treatment without an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C069", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ferreira, 82, lives in a long-term care home and reports trouble sleeping. Her creatinine has recently risen and her medical team is reviewing her medications. In the same unit, Mr. Bell has cirrhosis and a new drug order, and a palliative client is taking morphine. Her daughter visits every weekend and worries about her mother's falls and confusion at night.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report it and expect a bowel regimen order, since opioids slow the bowel): Opioids slow gut movement, so constipation is expected and a regular bowel regimen is part of care, which the nurse reports and monitors. Waiting on fluids alone allows discomfort and obstruction to develop. An enema needs an order. Reducing the opioid without an order undertreats pain."}, {"id": 555, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Immediately after a birth in the delivery room, the practical nurse assists with the third stage of labour, and the provider asks about signs of placental separation. The newborn is crying and has been placed skin to skin with her mother. Which sign suggests the placenta has separated?", "options": {"A": "A soft uterus that remains low in the abdomen", "B": "A gush of blood, a lengthening cord, and a uterus that rises and becomes firm", "C": "A steady trickle of dark blood with a cord that stays short", "D": "Pain in the mother's back with no change in cord length"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Misreads a soft low uterus as separation", "C": "Misreads steady bleeding as separation", "D": "Misreads pain as a sign"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A gush of blood, a lengthening cord, and a uterus that rises and becomes firm): Separation shows as a gush of blood, a lengthening cord and a firm, rising uterus, and the cord is not pulled before these signs. Steady trickling bleeding without cord change, back pain alone, and a soft low uterus do not indicate separation. The cord is not pulled until separation signs appear."}, {"id": 556, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "New numbness and tingling in the left arm and hand are present in a post-operative client who needs a bed bath, and the nurse thinks about delegating the personal care to a care aide. The surgery was yesterday, and the dressing on the arm is dry and intact. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the aide to bathe the right side and skip the left arm.", "B": "Delegate the full bath to the aide since personal care is a routine task", "C": "Do the assessment after the bath, since warm water may ease the numbness.", "D": "Assess and report the numbness first, then delegate with clear instructions about arm handling"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Handing part of the problem to an unregulated provider", "B": "Delegates care for a client with a new change", "C": "Delaying assessment of a new finding"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess and report the numbness first, then delegate with clear instructions about arm handling): A new sensory change needs nursing assessment and reporting because it may signal nerve or circulatory trouble, and the aide can then be told what to watch for. Delegating without that step treats an unstable change as routine. Skipping the arm leaves the problem unassessed, and warm water may hide the finding."}, {"id": 557, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Heavy lochia and a boggy uterus are found on fundal palpation of a postpartum client whose pulse is 108. A colleague suggests reassessing in 30 minutes. Her blood pressure is 102/64 mmHg, and she is anxious. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Raise her legs and recheck her pulse in thirty minutes before calling anyone.", "B": "Check the pad count and have her empty her bladder, then report at the next hour.", "C": "Massage the fundus until firm, call for help, and report to the RN right away", "D": "Reassess in 30 minutes, since early postpartum bleeding often settles on its own"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying a call for a possible hemorrhage", "B": "Taking partial steps without massaging the fundus or reporting at once", "D": "Delays action for possible hemorrhage"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Massage the fundus until firm, call for help, and report to the RN right away): A boggy uterus with heavy bleeding and a rising pulse suggests uterine atony and postpartum hemorrhage, so fundal massage and urgent notification are needed now. Delay allows blood loss to progress. Emptying the bladder helps but is not enough alone, and waiting thirty minutes or until the next hour is too late."}, {"id": 558, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Warfarin is due this evening for a 72-year-old man with atrial fibrillation on a cardiac unit, and a colleague says lab values need no review before giving it. His pulse is irregular at 82, and he asks about his evening dose. What is the correct action?", "options": {"A": "Check the latest INR and report any abnormal or missing value before giving the dose", "B": "Ask the client for his last INR from memory and give the dose.", "C": "Review the chart for last week's INR and give the dose if it was normal.", "D": "Give the dose without reviewing the INR, since it is a standing daily order for him"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relying on the client's memory for lab data", "C": "Using an outdated INR for a high alert drug", "D": "Skipping lab review before a high alert drug"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check the latest INR and report any abnormal or missing value before giving the dose): Warfarin dosing depends on the INR, so the nurse reviews the latest result and reports a missing or out-of-range value before giving it. Giving without checking risks bleeding or clots. A remembered value or last week's result may no longer be accurate, and how the client feels cannot replace the current INR."}, {"id": 559, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A care aide offers to walk a 50-year-old client on a rehabilitation unit who had hip surgery this morning and is due to get up for the first time. His pain is 4 out of 10 and his blood pressure is stable. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Delay the first walk until the next shift, when the drug effects have worn off.", "B": "Do the first walk herself, checking dizziness and pain, then delegate later walks", "C": "Delegate the first walk to the aide since walking is a routine task", "D": "Have the aide and a family member walk him, with the nurse checking afterward."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Postponing planned mobilization without a reason", "C": "Delegates an unstable first mobilization", "D": "Supervising only after the walk instead of assessing before"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Do the first walk herself, checking dizziness and pain, then delegate later walks): The first ambulation after surgery carries risks of low blood pressure, pain and falls, so a nurse assesses it, and later stable walks can be delegated with instructions. Delegating the first walk hands off assessment. Delaying walking raises complication risks, and checking only afterward misses problems during the walk."}, {"id": 560, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor, 82, lives in long-term care and wears a fentanyl patch changed every 72 hours. At the scheduled change, the practical nurse finds two patches on his upper back, one dated three days ago and one dated six days ago, and he is unusually drowsy with slow breathing. What should she do first?", "options": {"A": "Remove both patches, assess sedation and breathing, and report immediately.", "B": "Apply the new patch to a different site, then document the extra patch and monitor him.", "C": "Leave both patches on and hold his oral analgesic until the physician makes rounds.", "D": "Remove only the older patch, apply the new one, and recheck his sedation next shift."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Adds a third patch and treats the duplicate as a charting matter only", "C": "Right worry about opioid load but delays removing the source and the report", "D": "Removes one source of drug but applies more opioid to a client already oversedated"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Remove both patches, assess sedation and breathing, and report immediately.): Two patches mean he is receiving roughly double the intended dose, and his drowsiness and slow breathing point to opioid toxicity, so the source must come off and the team must be told now. Removing only one patch leaves ongoing absorption, and applying a fresh patch adds more drug. Holding an oral dose without removing the patches does not stop the exposure. Always confirm the old patch is removed before applying a new one."}, {"id": 561, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Caring for a 68-year-old two days after bowel surgery, a practical nurse finds a temperature of 38.9 C, heart rate 118, respirations 26, blood pressure 92/54 and new confusion. She is about to phone the physician. Which approach to the call best communicates her concern?", "options": {"A": "Describe the surgery and his full history first, then give the vital signs if asked.", "B": "Report that he looks a little off this afternoon and ask whether any orders are wanted.", "C": "State the situation, give the vital signs, name possible sepsis, and ask for assessment.", "D": "Send an electronic message with his vital signs and wait for orders before calling again."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Right data given in the wrong order, burying the urgent finding", "B": "Vague wording that hides how unwell he is", "D": "Passive route that delays a time-critical response"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (State the situation, give the vital signs, name possible sepsis, and ask for assessment.): Fever, fast heart and breathing, low blood pressure and new confusion suggest sepsis, so the call should lead with the situation and the numbers and say what is feared. A vague report such as looking off fails to convey urgency. Opening with a long history delays the key message. A structured format such as SBAR keeps the request clear and quick."}, {"id": 562, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a busy medical unit, a practical nurse has twelve scheduled 0900 medications to give. A colleague suggests charting them as given right now to save time later. The nurse wants to follow documentation standards for the round. Which action reflects those standards?", "options": {"A": "Chart the routine medications now and correct any entry later if a dose is held.", "B": "Chart each dose as given in a batch after the full round, using actual times.", "C": "Chart the doses after the round using the medication cart's printed schedule times.", "D": "Give each dose, then chart it right after with actual time and response."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pre-charting before the act, which records events that have not happened", "B": "Batch charting that delays entries and risks missed detail", "C": "Using scheduled times instead of the actual administration times"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give each dose, then chart it right after with actual time and response.): Documentation must record care that has actually been given, at the time it was given, by the person who gave it. Charting ahead leaves a false record if a dose is held or refused. Batch charting after the round risks missed or inaccurate details, and printed schedule times may not match when doses were given."}, {"id": 563, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Kevin lies on a stretcher in the clinic after his seizure has stopped. He is drowsy and confused, his oxygen saturation is 96% on room air, and he asks for a drink because his mouth feels dry. Which action should the practical nurse take now?", "options": {"A": "Let him sleep undisturbed and check on him only once each hour until morning.", "B": "Position him on his side, keep him NPO, and monitor his alertness.", "C": "Sit him upright and offer small sips of water to relieve his dry mouth.", "D": "Give him ice chips slowly and keep him lying flat to reduce another seizure."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaves a postictal client unmonitored, missing airway or recurrent seizure", "C": "Oral intake while swallowing reflexes are still impaired", "D": "Ice chips still risk aspiration, with a position that does not protect the airway"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C065", "cluster_text": "The Mah family visits a community clinic where a practical nurse is working. Kevin, 40, has epilepsy. His daughter Maya, 8, was sent by her teacher because of repeated staring spells at school, and his son Leo, 2, had a brief convulsion with a fever last night. During the visit, Kevin has a two-minute generalized tonic-clonic seizure in the waiting area and is now drowsy but rousable.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Position him on his side, keep him NPO, and monitor his alertness.): In the postictal phase his swallowing and alertness are reduced, so a side-lying position protects the airway and nothing is given by mouth until he is fully alert. Sips of water or ice chips risk aspiration even when the mouth feels dry. Hourly checks are too infrequent for a client who has just seized. Frequent observation of consciousness, breathing, and oxygen saturation guides the next step."}, {"id": 564, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Maya's teacher reports episodes lasting about ten seconds when she stops mid-sentence, stares blankly, then carries on without remembering them. She never falls or shakes, and her father says she is only daydreaming. Which response from the practical nurse is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Suggest the teacher seat her near the front and watch her attention for a month.", "B": "Advise a hearing test first, since inattention at school most often comes from hearing loss.", "C": "Reassure the family that staring spells are common and need review only if she falls.", "D": "Explain these may be seizures and arrange medical assessment, with a written log of episodes."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waits a month on a possible seizure disorder", "B": "Anchors on a common cause and delays seizure assessment", "C": "Dismisses brief seizures because they lack falls or shaking"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C065", "cluster_text": "The Mah family visits a community clinic where a practical nurse is working. Kevin, 40, has epilepsy. His daughter Maya, 8, was sent by her teacher because of repeated staring spells at school, and his son Leo, 2, had a brief convulsion with a fever last night. During the visit, Kevin has a two-minute generalized tonic-clonic seizure in the waiting area and is now drowsy but rousable.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain these may be seizures and arrange medical assessment, with a written log of episodes.): Brief staring spells with abrupt onset, abrupt end, and no memory fit absence seizures, which need medical assessment, and a log of timing and length helps the physician. Daydreaming does not occur mid-sentence with no response, so watching and waiting delays diagnosis. Absence seizures do not involve falling, so that is not the test. Hearing loss is possible, but it does not explain the sudden loss of awareness."}, {"id": 565, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Leo's temperature reached 39.4 C last night and he had a convulsion lasting about one minute, then woke and cried. Today he is alert with a temperature of 38.2 C. His mother wants to give him a cool bath to stop another seizure. What teaching should the practical nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Dress him in several extra blankets to sweat out the fever and prevent another convulsion overnight", "B": "Cooling does not prevent these seizures; give acetaminophen for comfort and seek help if prolonged", "C": "Wake him every hour overnight to check his temperature and give fever medicine whenever it rises", "D": "A tepid bath lowers the temperature quickly, so use one whenever his fever passes 38 C."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Traps heat and raises body temperature further", "C": "Over-treats fever and disturbs sleep, with unsafe repeated dosing", "D": "Believes lowering temperature fast prevents seizures"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C065", "cluster_text": "The Mah family visits a community clinic where a practical nurse is working. Kevin, 40, has epilepsy. His daughter Maya, 8, was sent by her teacher because of repeated staring spells at school, and his son Leo, 2, had a brief convulsion with a fever last night. During the visit, Kevin has a two-minute generalized tonic-clonic seizure in the waiting area and is now drowsy but rousable.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cooling does not prevent these seizures; give acetaminophen for comfort and seek help if prolonged): Febrile seizures relate to how quickly a fever rises, and cooling does not prevent them, so teaching focuses on comfort and when to seek help. A tepid bath may be uncomfortable and does not reduce risk, and extra blankets add heat. Hourly waking and repeated dosing are unnecessary and unsafe. Parents should call for help if a convulsion lasts more than a few minutes."}, {"id": 566, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Kevin admits he skipped his antiepileptic drug for two days because he had been seizure-free for eight months and felt it was no longer needed. The nurse is teaching him about the drug. Which statement best explains the risk of what he did?", "options": {"A": "Months without seizures show the condition has resolved, so he can lower the dose himself.", "B": "Missed doses matter only if he also has fever or sleeps poorly.", "C": "Missed doses lower blood levels and can trigger seizures even after months free.", "D": "Missed doses are safe if he restarts the usual dose at the next scheduled time."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reads seizure freedom as cure and self-adjusts the dose", "B": "Believing only certain triggers make missed doses unsafe", "D": "Assuming restarting at the usual time removes the risk"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C065", "cluster_text": "The Mah family visits a community clinic where a practical nurse is working. Kevin, 40, has epilepsy. His daughter Maya, 8, was sent by her teacher because of repeated staring spells at school, and his son Leo, 2, had a brief convulsion with a fever last night. During the visit, Kevin has a two-minute generalized tonic-clonic seizure in the waiting area and is now drowsy but rousable.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Missed doses lower blood levels and can trigger seizures even after months free.): Seizure control depends on steady blood levels, and stopping or skipping the drug allows levels to drop and seizures to return, even after a long quiet period. Fever and poor sleep can add risk but are not required for breakthrough seizures. Restarting does not undo the period of low levels. Seizure freedom reflects the drug working, not a cure, and any dose change should go through the prescriber."}, {"id": 567, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Forty minutes after morphine 4 mg subcutaneously for pain, a 50-year-old man on the surgical unit has a respiratory rate of 12 and falls asleep mid-sentence but wakes when his name is called. He rates his pain 2 out of 10. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Assess sedation with a scale, monitor breathing closely, report to the RN, and hold the dose.", "B": "Assess his pain again in an hour and give the next dose on time.", "C": "Repeat the respiratory rate in four hours and give the next dose as ordered.", "D": "Give the next dose at half strength, since his respiratory rate remains above the usual minimum."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Giving the next opioid dose without addressing sedation", "C": "Spacing respiratory checks too far apart after sedation", "D": "Adjusts an opioid dose without an order, guided by rate alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Assess sedation with a scale, monitor breathing closely, report to the RN, and hold the dose.): Drowsiness that interrupts speech is an early sign of opioid-induced sedation, and it often appears before the respiratory rate drops, so a sedation scale and close monitoring are needed. Giving the next dose on schedule risks respiratory depression. Checking breathing only after hours misses early decline. Changing the dose without an order is outside practical nurse scope."}, {"id": 568, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lund looks puzzled when a nursing student introduces herself and says she will help the practical nurse with morning hygiene and vital signs today. Mrs. Lund has not been asked about students taking part in her care. Which action best respects her consent about student participation?", "options": {"A": "Explain the student's role in plain words, ask whether she agrees, and accept her answer.", "B": "Ask her daughter to agree on her behalf, because dementia limits her choices.", "C": "Let the student proceed, since the unit's general consent form covers student involvement.", "D": "Introduce the student as a staff member and begin care while she is distracted."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assumes dementia removes her right to decide", "C": "Treats a blanket form as consent for every student task", "D": "Uses deception and distraction in place of real consent"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C061", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dorothy Lund, 79, has dementia and is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia. Her confusion fluctuates through the day and is worse in the evening. A practical nurse is caring for her with a nursing student. The physician has recommended intravenous antibiotics and a swallowing assessment. Mrs. Lund says she does not want a needle, but she also asks the nurse to explain what the treatment involves.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain the student's role in plain words, ask whether she agrees, and accept her answer.): Clients may agree to or decline care from students, and Mrs. Lund can be asked in plain language and her answer respected. A general admission form does not replace asking at the bedside. Dementia alone does not remove her voice or shift the decision to family, and hiding the student's role is deceptive."}, {"id": 569, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lund is calm this morning and answers questions about her illness, yet the physician writes that she lacks capacity because of dementia and asks the nurse to obtain her daughter's consent for antibiotics. How should the practical nurse proceed?", "options": {"A": "Accept the order and ask Mrs. Lund only whether she is comfortable with the plan.", "B": "Obtain the daughter's signature as ordered, since a dementia diagnosis establishes incapacity.", "C": "Raise the concern with the RN, since capacity is presumed and is assessed per decision.", "D": "Ask the physician to reassess once her pneumonia has resolved and she is stronger."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Seeks token agreement while bypassing her right to decide", "B": "Treats a diagnosis as proof of incapacity for every decision", "D": "Defers the assessment past the time the decision is needed"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C061", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dorothy Lund, 79, has dementia and is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia. Her confusion fluctuates through the day and is worse in the evening. A practical nurse is caring for her with a nursing student. The physician has recommended intravenous antibiotics and a swallowing assessment. Mrs. Lund says she does not want a needle, but she also asks the nurse to explain what the treatment involves.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raise the concern with the RN, since capacity is presumed and is assessed per decision.): Adults are presumed capable, and a diagnosis such as dementia does not prove incapacity, so capacity is assessed for the specific decision when it is made. Asking the daughter to consent without that assessment bypasses Mrs. Lund's rights, and seeking only her comfort with the plan is not informed consent. Waiting weeks leaves the present decision unresolved."}, {"id": 570, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "By early evening Mrs. Lund is disoriented and cannot say why she is in hospital, though she was clear at noon when she discussed her treatment. The physician now wants a decision about a swallowing test. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Wait until morning to record anything about her capacity, since it fluctuates daily.", "B": "Treat the noon discussion as final consent and proceed with the test this evening.", "C": "Ask her daughter to sign the form this evening, since she seems confused.", "D": "Report the change to the RN, defer non-urgent decisions, and reassess later."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying documentation and reporting of a change", "B": "Treats one lucid period as consent for later events", "C": "Using a family member as consent giver without a capacity assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C061", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dorothy Lund, 79, has dementia and is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia. Her confusion fluctuates through the day and is worse in the evening. A practical nurse is caring for her with a nursing student. The physician has recommended intravenous antibiotics and a swallowing assessment. Mrs. Lund says she does not want a needle, but she also asks the nurse to explain what the treatment involves.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the change to the RN, defer non-urgent decisions, and reassess later.): Capacity is specific to the decision and to the time, so her evening confusion does not cancel what she decided at noon, but it also means new decisions should wait for a clearer period when safe. Using the noon talk as consent skips the present state. A daughter signing needs a formal capacity finding first, and delaying the report loses timely assessment."}, {"id": 571, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Clear and calm the next morning, Mrs. Lund states she refuses the intravenous line. She can describe her pneumonia, repeat the risk of refusing, explain how it fits her wish to avoid a long stay, and give the same answer twice. How should the practical nurse interpret this?", "options": {"A": "Her refusal shows poor judgment, which indicates incapacity and lets her daughter consent.", "B": "Her capacity cannot be judged without a court ruling, so treatment should go ahead meanwhile.", "C": "Her refusal is valid only if the physician agrees that her choice is reasonable.", "D": "She likely has capacity for this decision, so her refusal stands and the team is informed."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Equates disagreeing with advice with lacking capacity", "B": "Believes only a court can assess capacity", "C": "Makes her choice depend on approval from the physician"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C061", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dorothy Lund, 79, has dementia and is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia. Her confusion fluctuates through the day and is worse in the evening. A practical nurse is caring for her with a nursing student. The physician has recommended intravenous antibiotics and a swallowing assessment. Mrs. Lund says she does not want a needle, but she also asks the nurse to explain what the treatment involves.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She likely has capacity for this decision, so her refusal stands and the team is informed.): She shows understanding, appreciation of her situation, reasoning, and a stable choice, which are the elements of decision-making capacity, so her refusal stands even if the team disagrees. Disagreeing with advice does not prove incapacity. Capacity is assessed by the health care team, not by a court. A valid refusal does not need physician approval."}, {"id": 572, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lund's daughter asks whether the hospital needs a court to declare her mother incompetent before her mother's refusal of the intravenous line can be questioned. The practical nurse wants to answer accurately about the two terms. Which statement is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "A physician's note about capacity is a legal ruling that removes her right to decide.", "B": "Capacity and competence mean the identical thing, and only a judge can formally assess either.", "C": "Capacity is judged once at admission and then applies to every later treatment decision she faces.", "D": "Capacity is judged by the team per decision, while competence is a court's legal finding."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Mistakes a clinical opinion for a legal determination", "B": "Blurs a clinical term with a legal one and gives all assessment to courts", "C": "Treats capacity as fixed instead of decision-specific"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C061", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dorothy Lund, 79, has dementia and is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia. Her confusion fluctuates through the day and is worse in the evening. A practical nurse is caring for her with a nursing student. The physician has recommended intravenous antibiotics and a swallowing assessment. Mrs. Lund says she does not want a needle, but she also asks the nurse to explain what the treatment involves.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Capacity is judged by the team per decision, while competence is a court's legal finding.): Capacity is a clinical judgement made by the team about a specific decision, whereas competence is a legal status decided by a court. The terms are not interchangeable, and capacity is not fixed at admission. A physician's note records an assessment but is not a legal ruling that takes away her right to decide."}, {"id": 573, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Sharma, 70, on a palliative care unit, is starting regular oral morphine for bone pain. He has had no bowel problems so far and eats poorly. The nurse reviews his orders and notes that no laxative has been ordered. What is the most appropriate interpretation and action?", "options": {"A": "Opioids slow the bowel, so request a scheduled laxative with the morphine.", "B": "Give a laxative from the unit supply, and tell the RN afterward.", "C": "Extra fluids alone will prevent opioid constipation, so encourage drinks and skip laxatives.", "D": "Constipation develops in some clients, so ask him to report straining before acting."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Giving a drug without an order", "C": "Relies on fluids alone, which is not enough with opioids", "D": "Waiting for symptoms in a predictable adverse effect"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Opioids slow the bowel, so request a scheduled laxative with the morphine.): Constipation is a common effect of regular opioid use that rarely improves with time, so prevention should start with the drug and needs an order. Waiting for complaints allows discomfort or impaction. A laxative given from supply without an order is outside scope, and fluids help but do not replace a laxative for most clients on regular opioids."}, {"id": 574, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Chest tightness, tingling in her hands, and fear of dying bring a 27-year-old woman to the emergency department. Her ECG and troponin are normal, and her breathing is rapid and shallow. The physician suspects a panic attack. Which nursing approach is best in the moment?", "options": {"A": "Give her a paper bag to rebreathe into and leave her to use it.", "B": "Tell her to calm down, then step away to help others and return in twenty minutes.", "C": "Stay with her, speak calmly, and coach slow breathing with short, simple instructions.", "D": "Explain her test results in detail and ask her to list every trigger."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses an outdated technique unsupervised", "B": "Dismisses distress and leaves her alone", "D": "Overloads her with information while she cannot take it in"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stay with her, speak calmly, and coach slow breathing with short, simple instructions.): During a panic attack the client needs a calm presence and brief, clear coaching, which slows breathing and lowers fear. Telling her to calm down and leaving increases distress. Rebreathing into a bag is not recommended and she should not be left alone. Detailed explanations and trigger lists are better after the symptoms settle."}, {"id": 575, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "New chest pain and sweating are reported by a client in his fifties. The charge nurse suggests the health care aide assess the pain and report back, so the practical nurse can finish her medication round on time. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Ask the aide to stay beside him and call her if the pain gets worse", "B": "Finish the medication round first, then assess his pain, since the round is time-sensitive for others", "C": "Let the aide take vital signs and describe the pain, then decide on the next step.", "D": "Assess him personally and notify the RN, since assessment and judgement cannot be delegated."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses the aide as a monitor in place of a nurse's assessment", "B": "Delays assessment of a possible cardiac event for a routine task", "C": "Hands the judgement part of assessment to an unregulated worker"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C062", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Aisha Rahman works on a busy medical unit with a registered nurse, a health care aide and a nurse manager. During the shift she is asked to take on tasks that raise questions about scope of practice, delegation and professional accountability. Later, a family member lodges a complaint with the regulatory body about a medication error that occurred on the unit the previous week.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess him personally and notify the RN, since assessment and judgement cannot be delegated.): New chest pain with sweating needs a nurse's assessment now, and assessment and clinical judgement are not tasks that can be delegated to an aide. The aide may take measurements, but interpreting them belongs to the nurse. Waiting for the medication round delays care for a possible emergency. Watching for worsening is not assessment."}, {"id": 576, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Aisha is asked by a new graduate why nurses may regulate themselves rather than being supervised directly by government or employers. Aisha wants an explanation she can also use when teaching nursing students on her unit. Which explanation best describes the reason?", "options": {"A": "Self-regulation is a privilege granted by government, on condition that the profession protects the public.", "B": "Self-regulation lets the nurse manager review each nurse's practice, since she knows the unit best.", "C": "Self-regulation is a right of the profession that continues even when public safety is in doubt.", "D": "Self-regulation is granted by the profession's own members, who vote to renew it each year."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Giving the regulatory role to a manager", "C": "Treats the privilege as an unconditional entitlement", "D": "Placing the source of the privilege with the members"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C062", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Aisha Rahman works on a busy medical unit with a registered nurse, a health care aide and a nurse manager. During the shift she is asked to take on tasks that raise questions about scope of practice, delegation and professional accountability. Later, a family member lodges a complaint with the regulatory body about a medication error that occurred on the unit the previous week.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Self-regulation is a privilege granted by government, on condition that the profession protects the public.): Self-regulation is a privilege granted by government, and it carries the duty to act in the public interest, which is why the profession sets standards and handles complaints. Managers review performance for the employer, not for the profession. Members do not vote to renew it, and the privilege can be changed if the public is not protected."}, {"id": 577, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At a unit education session, a nurse says the regulatory body mainly speaks up for better wages and staffing for nurses, and she asks Aisha to correct her. Aisha explains what the regulatory body's primary mandate is. Which statement should she give?", "options": {"A": "It provides continuing education and promotes the nursing profession to the public.", "B": "Its primary mandate is to protect the public through standards and complaint processes.", "C": "It represents members in bargaining and negotiates working conditions with employers.", "D": "It defends nurses named in complaints and protects their licences through the discipline process."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Describes a professional association's role", "C": "Describes a union's role as the regulator's", "D": "Treats the regulator as the nurse's advocate"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C062", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Aisha Rahman works on a busy medical unit with a registered nurse, a health care aide and a nurse manager. During the shift she is asked to take on tasks that raise questions about scope of practice, delegation and professional accountability. Later, a family member lodges a complaint with the regulatory body about a medication error that occurred on the unit the previous week.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Its primary mandate is to protect the public through standards and complaint processes.): The regulatory body exists to protect the public, which it does through standards of practice, licensing, and complaint and discipline processes. Bargaining belongs to the union, while education and promotion of the profession are association roles. The regulator does not defend nurses against complaints."}, {"id": 578, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Documentation standards are just suggestions, a community care nurse tells Aisha, and short notes are fine when visits are busy. Aisha is asked how standards of practice are used if a complaint reviews this nurse's notes. Which description is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Standards are guidelines that a committee may set aside when the unit was short-staffed that day.", "B": "Standards apply only while nurses are on shift and not to off duty conduct.", "C": "Standards are reviewed in complaints only when the nurse has been disciplined before.", "D": "Standards describe expected performance, and a complaint reviews practice against them."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats standards as optional under workload pressure", "B": "Limiting standards to shift hours", "C": "Making review depend on a prior record"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C062", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Aisha Rahman works on a busy medical unit with a registered nurse, a health care aide and a nurse manager. During the shift she is asked to take on tasks that raise questions about scope of practice, delegation and professional accountability. Later, a family member lodges a complaint with the regulatory body about a medication error that occurred on the unit the previous week.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Standards describe expected performance, and a complaint reviews practice against them.): Standards of practice state what is expected of every nurse in the role, and they are the measure used when practice is reviewed. Workload may be considered as context but does not make standards optional. They apply to every complaint review regardless of past discipline."}, {"id": 579, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Once the complaint about the medication error reaches the regulatory body, the nurse involved asks Aisha who will review the matter, assuming her employer alone will decide any discipline in the case. Which description of the review process is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "The regulatory body investigates, and a discipline committee decides outcomes.", "B": "The employer decides any discipline, and the regulatory body only records the decision.", "C": "The nurse manager investigates first, and the regulatory body reviews the report.", "D": "The health authority reviews the complaint and the regulatory body sets any penalty."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Gives the regulator's authority to the employer", "C": "Giving the investigation to the manager", "D": "Splitting review and penalty between an employer body and the regulator"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C062", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Aisha Rahman works on a busy medical unit with a registered nurse, a health care aide and a nurse manager. During the shift she is asked to take on tasks that raise questions about scope of practice, delegation and professional accountability. Later, a family member lodges a complaint with the regulatory body about a medication error that occurred on the unit the previous week.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The regulatory body investigates, and a discipline committee decides outcomes.): The regulatory body receives the complaint, investigates, and may refer it to a committee that decides findings and outcomes independent of the employer. Employers may take their own action, but that is separate. The nurse manager and the health authority do not run the regulatory investigation."}, {"id": 580, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the clinic, a 2-year-old girl is tugging at her left ear, has a temperature of 38.4 C and is fussy, though she ate well this morning. Her father asks whether she must have antibiotics today. What is the best response from the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Give a decongestant syrup from the clinic supply to ease her ear pressure.", "B": "Report findings to the RN or physician, who decides treatment, and give ordered acetaminophen for comfort.", "C": "Say she likely needs antibiotics and explain which one the physician will choose.", "D": "Explain that ear pulling is harmless and fever medication is unneeded until she stops eating."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving a drug without an order", "C": "Predicting treatment on behalf of the prescriber", "D": "Dismisses fever and ear pain in a toddler"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C066", "cluster_text": "Emma Tremblay, a practical nurse, works in a pediatric program that includes a clinic, an emergency department and a ward. During one busy shift she cares for several young children with common illnesses: a toddler with an ear complaint, an infant dehydrated from gastroenteritis, a child vomiting during a home visit, and a child needing intravenous fluids on the ward.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report findings to the RN or physician, who decides treatment, and give ordered acetaminophen for comfort.): Ear pulling with fever suggests otitis media, and the decision on antibiotics belongs to the prescriber, who may choose observation or treatment, so the nurse reports and provides comfort measures. Naming the antibiotic goes beyond scope. Dismissing the findings delays care, and a syrup from supply needs an order."}, {"id": 581, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the emergency department, a 9-month-old with two days of diarrhea has dry mucous membranes, a sunken anterior fontanelle, few wet diapers and a heart rate of 170. His recent weight is not available. Which approach best assesses his hydration?", "options": {"A": "Count the wet diapers over the last hour, since urine output alone reflects his hydration adequately", "B": "Check skin color and temperature only, since these reflect fluid status most reliably.", "C": "Combine fontanelle, mucous membranes, heart rate, perfusion and urine output, not one finding alone", "D": "Estimate his weight loss since the last clinic visit and judge hydration by that figure alone"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses a short urine count that does not reflect overall status", "B": "Chooses a limited set of signs and ignores others", "D": "Relies on one sign that is unavailable here"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C066", "cluster_text": "Emma Tremblay, a practical nurse, works in a pediatric program that includes a clinic, an emergency department and a ward. During one busy shift she cares for several young children with common illnesses: a toddler with an ear complaint, an infant dehydrated from gastroenteritis, a child vomiting during a home visit, and a child needing intravenous fluids on the ward.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Combine fontanelle, mucous membranes, heart rate, perfusion and urine output, not one finding alone): Dehydration in infants is judged from several findings together, including fontanelle, mucous membranes, heart rate, perfusion, and urine output. Weight is helpful but not available and not enough alone. Skin color and temperature are less reliable by themselves, and an hour of diaper counting is too short."}, {"id": 582, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, the practical nurse finds a 2-year-old who has vomited three times today but is alert and drinking small amounts. His mother has apple juice and a sports drink and asks which to give. What should the nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Offer an oral rehydration solution in frequent small sips, such as a teaspoon every few minutes.", "B": "Offer the sports drink in frequent small sips, since its electrolytes replace his losses.", "C": "Hold all fluids for six hours to rest his stomach before restarting.", "D": "Offer diluted apple juice slowly, since its sugar supplies the energy he is losing."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Substitutes a sports drink whose sugar and sodium balance is not suited to children", "C": "Withholds fluids and worsens dehydration risk", "D": "Uses juice, whose sugar can worsen fluid loss"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C066", "cluster_text": "Emma Tremblay, a practical nurse, works in a pediatric program that includes a clinic, an emergency department and a ward. During one busy shift she cares for several young children with common illnesses: a toddler with an ear complaint, an infant dehydrated from gastroenteritis, a child vomiting during a home visit, and a child needing intravenous fluids on the ward.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Offer an oral rehydration solution in frequent small sips, such as a teaspoon every few minutes.): Oral rehydration solution has the right balance of sugar and salts for replacing losses, and small frequent sips are better tolerated during vomiting. Sports drinks and juice contain too much sugar and too little sodium. Holding all fluids for hours increases dehydration risk in a small child."}, {"id": 583, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Maintenance IV fluid at 100 mL/h is ordered for a 20 kg child on the pediatric ward, matching the unit's adult default. The pump is programmed and ready to start. The nurse recalls that children have different fluid requirements. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Start the infusion at 100 mL/h and watch for signs of overload hourly.", "B": "Hold the infusion and clarify the rate with the prescriber first.", "C": "Start the infusion, since a pump limits the rate and prevents fluid overload.", "D": "Calculate a rate from her weight, start it, and document the change."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Starting a possibly unsafe rate and delaying clarification", "C": "Trusts the pump to prevent an unsafe rate", "D": "Setting a new rate without an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C066", "cluster_text": "Emma Tremblay, a practical nurse, works in a pediatric program that includes a clinic, an emergency department and a ward. During one busy shift she cares for several young children with common illnesses: a toddler with an ear complaint, an infant dehydrated from gastroenteritis, a child vomiting during a home visit, and a child needing intravenous fluids on the ward.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold the infusion and clarify the rate with the prescriber first.): Pediatric fluid orders are based on weight, and a rate copied from the adult default may be too high and risk overload, so the nurse clarifies before starting. A pump only delivers the programmed rate. Starting and watching allows fluid to run in the meantime, and calculating a new rate without an order exceeds scope."}, {"id": 584, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Helping a 70-year-old stroke survivor with transfers and walking practice, a practical nurse on a rehabilitation unit writes a note afterward. A coworker says initials are enough, although the unit has no signature legend on file. Which action meets documentation standards for the entry?", "options": {"A": "Sign with the full name only, since the designation is already in the chart header.", "B": "Sign with full name and designation, such as PN, plus the date and time.", "C": "Use initials only, since staff on the unit already recognize them.", "D": "Leave her signature until the end of the shift and sign all notes together."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Omits the designation so the author's role is unclear", "C": "Uses initials that cannot be traced without a legend", "D": "Delays signing so entries are not identified when written"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sign with full name and designation, such as PN, plus the date and time.): Each entry should be identifiable as to who wrote it and in what role, with the date and time, which means a full name and designation unless a signature legend exists. Initials without a legend cannot be traced. Signing later breaks the link to the entry, and a header does not show who wrote each note."}, {"id": 585, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Influenza and a wet cough bring a 6-year-old to the ward. The unit has one single room with negative pressure ventilation and one standard single room. Hospital policy lists droplet precautions for influenza. Which room and protective equipment are best for this child?", "options": {"A": "Standard single room, with a surgical mask and eye protection.", "B": "Negative pressure room, with a fit-tested N95 respirator worn for all contact with the child.", "C": "Standard single room, with gown and gloves only and a mask only if coughing begins.", "D": "Shared room with a curtain, with a surgical mask worn only during suctioning."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Applies airborne precautions and uses a limited room", "C": "Uses contact protection and omits the mask for a droplet infection", "D": "Uses a shared room with protection limited to one task"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C064", "cluster_text": "A hospital practical nurse cares for clients needing additional infection precautions during a busy shift. Beds are limited, single rooms are in short supply, and several clients have respiratory or contact infections. The nurse follows routine practices at all times and chooses additional precautions based on the route of transmission for each infection, using the hospital's infection prevention policy and the unit's available rooms.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Standard single room, with a surgical mask and eye protection.): Influenza spreads mainly by droplets over short distances, so a single room with a mask and eye protection near the child is appropriate. The negative pressure room is kept for airborne diseases, so it adds nothing here. A shared room, partial masking, or contact gear alone does not match the route."}, {"id": 586, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Fever, cough and a spreading rash lead to an adult being admitted with suspected measles. The nurse assigned has never had measles and is unsure of her immunization record. A negative pressure room is open on the unit. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Place him in a standard single room with the door open, and wear a surgical mask.", "B": "Place him in the negative pressure room, and enter with a surgical mask and eye protection.", "C": "Use the negative pressure room, with only staff of proven immunity entering in N95 respirators.", "D": "Place him in a standard single room with the door closed, and wear an N95 respirator."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses neither airborne room nor respirator protection", "B": "Uses droplet protection for an airborne disease", "D": "Uses a room without negative pressure and enters while not immune"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C064", "cluster_text": "A hospital practical nurse cares for clients needing additional infection precautions during a busy shift. Beds are limited, single rooms are in short supply, and several clients have respiratory or contact infections. The nurse follows routine practices at all times and chooses additional precautions based on the route of transmission for each infection, using the hospital's infection prevention policy and the unit's available rooms.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use the negative pressure room, with only staff of proven immunity entering in N95 respirators.): Measles spreads through the air, so he needs a negative pressure room and staff with proven immunity wearing an N95 respirator, which rules out the nurse with an unknown history. A surgical mask does not protect against airborne particles. A standard room with the door open or closed does not contain them."}, {"id": 587, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "No single room is open on a busy ward when a client needing additional precautions arrives. The practical nurse is told to place the client in a shared room with a roommate recovering from surgery. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Place the client in the shared room and close the curtain around the roommate's bed.", "B": "Tell the charge nurse and infection prevention so a risk assessment sets room priority.", "C": "Place the client in the shared room and ask the roommate to wear a mask.", "D": "Place the client in the shared room and assign one dedicated nurse to both beds."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats a curtain as an adequate barrier", "C": "Expects a roommate mask to replace room placement", "D": "Relies on one nurse assignment instead of separating clients"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C064", "cluster_text": "A hospital practical nurse cares for clients needing additional infection precautions during a busy shift. Beds are limited, single rooms are in short supply, and several clients have respiratory or contact infections. The nurse follows routine practices at all times and chooses additional precautions based on the route of transmission for each infection, using the hospital's infection prevention policy and the unit's available rooms.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tell the charge nurse and infection prevention so a risk assessment sets room priority.): A single room is the priority for additional precautions, and when none is open, infection prevention staff assess the risk to decide who is placed where. A curtain is not a barrier for organisms. A mask on a recovering roommate does not replace room placement, and one nurse for both beds does not prevent exposure."}, {"id": 588, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Imaging off the unit is needed for an adult on contact precautions for a resistant organism. The porter is waiting at the door, and the imaging staff have not yet been told about the precautions. Which action is best before the client leaves?", "options": {"A": "Cancel the imaging, since clients on additional precautions cannot leave their rooms.", "B": "Have the porter wear a gown and gloves, and tell imaging staff afterward.", "C": "Notify imaging, cover any wound, and have the client clean their hands.", "D": "Send the client with a note on the chart for imaging staff to read on arrival."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withholds needed care without a safety reason", "B": "Informs the receiving area only after exposure", "D": "Relies on a chart note that may not be read before contact"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C064", "cluster_text": "A hospital practical nurse cares for clients needing additional infection precautions during a busy shift. Beds are limited, single rooms are in short supply, and several clients have respiratory or contact infections. The nurse follows routine practices at all times and chooses additional precautions based on the route of transmission for each infection, using the hospital's infection prevention policy and the unit's available rooms.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Notify imaging, cover any wound, and have the client clean their hands.): Transport is allowed when the receiving department is told in advance and the organism is contained, for example with a covered wound and hand hygiene. A chart note may be read too late, and cancelling needed imaging is not required. Telling staff afterward leaves them unprotected during the visit."}, {"id": 589, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, a 62-year-old woman with a fentanyl patch has a temperature of 39.1 C and is using a heating pad over her sore back near the patch. She is sleepy but rousable. The nurse considers the effect of these on the drug. Which interpretation is correct?", "options": {"A": "Fever lowers drug absorption, so she may need extra analgesia from another source.", "B": "Heat and fever speed absorption, raising the risk of oversedation and slowed breathing.", "C": "Heat narrows the blood vessels in the skin, so the patch will release less medication.", "D": "Heat affects only the skin surface, so her pain control will stay unchanged."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reverses the effect of fever and suggests more analgesia", "C": "Reverses the effect of heat on skin blood flow", "D": "Believes heat has no effect on absorption"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Heat and fever speed absorption, raising the risk of oversedation and slowed breathing.): Heat and fever widen skin blood vessels and speed drug release from the patch, so more fentanyl enters the blood and sedation and slowed breathing become more likely. She therefore needs prompt assessment and reporting. The other statements deny or reverse that effect."}, {"id": 590, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Ten minutes ago, a practical nurse on a medical unit charted a blood pressure reading in the wrong client's record. She notices this while alone at the desk and wants to keep the record tidy. Which action follows documentation standards?", "options": {"A": "Apply correction fluid over the entry, then write the right client's name beside it.", "B": "Draw a single line through the entry, write error, and add initials, date, and time.", "C": "Write the correct reading over the old one and add her initials beside it.", "D": "Draw a single line through the entry and add the correct reading beside it."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses correction fluid, which hides the original entry", "C": "Writing over the original hides the first entry", "D": "Omits the word error, date, and time on the correction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Draw a single line through the entry, write error, and add initials, date, and time.): A correction keeps the original entry legible with a single line, the word error, and initials, date, and time, so the record shows what happened. Correction fluid and writing over the entry hide the original. A line with no error label, date, or time leaves the change untraceable."}, {"id": 591, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a ward, a 72-year-old man leans forward over the bedside table, using his shoulder muscles to breathe, with a respiratory rate of 30. He says he is just nervous about his test results. His oxygen saturation is 88% and he speaks in short phrases. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Stay with him, keep him upright, apply ordered oxygen, and call the RN.", "B": "Lay him flat to rest and recheck his breathing in fifteen minutes.", "C": "Coach calm breathing and reassure him, since nervousness explains the rapid breathing.", "D": "Take a full set of vital signs and review his chart before calling anyone."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Lays him flat, which worsens breathing, and waits", "C": "Attributes distress to anxiety despite low saturation", "D": "Delays urgent help for a full assessment"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stay with him, keep him upright, apply ordered oxygen, and call the RN.): Tripod positioning, accessory muscle use, fast breathing, low oxygen saturation, and short phrases show respiratory distress, so he needs to stay upright with oxygen and urgent help. Calling it anxiety misses the cause. A long assessment before calling delays care, and lying flat usually worsens breathing."}, {"id": 592, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Pain control for a 50-year-old man on the surgical unit comes from a morphine PCA pump. He is drowsy, and his daughter says she will press the button for him because he looks uncomfortable. The pump shows a lockout interval and several recent attempts. What should the practical nurse tell her?", "options": {"A": "She may press it, but only when the nurse confirms his drowsiness is mild.", "B": "Only he should press the button, since his own alertness protects him from oversedation.", "C": "The nurse may press it for him, since the lockout interval prevents overdose.", "D": "She may press it whenever he looks uncomfortable, if she stays within the lockout interval."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Allows proxy dosing with a nurse check", "C": "Lets staff give patient-controlled doses to a sedated client", "D": "Treats the lockout interval as full protection"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Only he should press the button, since his own alertness protects him from oversedation.): PCA is safe because a client who becomes sleepy stops pressing, so only the client should use the button. The lockout interval is a limit, not a guard against proxy dosing. Dosing by a family member or by the nurse removes the safeguard, and drowsiness should be assessed and reported instead."}, {"id": 593, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Visiting at home, the practical nurse finds a woman in her late seventies with heart failure who has gained 2 kg in three days, has increased ankle swelling, and now needs two pillows to sleep. She says she feels fine and wants no fuss. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Advise her to take an extra dose of her diuretic and record the change.", "B": "Review her sodium intake and teach a restricted diet before reporting the weight change.", "C": "Assess her breathing and lung sounds, then report weight gain and symptoms to the RN today.", "D": "Reassure her, encourage rest with her legs raised, and weigh her again at tomorrow's home visit."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Advises a drug change outside scope", "B": "Teaches first and delays reporting a decline", "D": "Waits a day despite signs of worsening failure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess her breathing and lung sounds, then report weight gain and symptoms to the RN today.): A quick weight gain, more swelling, and needing more pillows are early signs of worsening heart failure, so the nurse assesses and reports the same day. Waiting until tomorrow delays treatment. Changing a diuretic dose is not within practical nurse scope without an order, and diet teaching can follow the report."}, {"id": 594, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sputum for culture is needed from a 64-year-old man with a productive cough. He ate breakfast an hour ago and has not brushed his teeth, but he can cough on request and understands instructions. Which instruction should the practical nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Rinse his mouth with water, take a deep breath, and cough deeply into the sterile container.", "B": "Brush his teeth and gargle with antiseptic mouthwash, then spit the saliva into the sterile container.", "C": "Wait until after the next meal, then cough up sputum into a clean container.", "D": "Take shallow breaths and cough lightly so sputum comes up without effort."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Uses antiseptic mouthwash and collects saliva instead of sputum", "C": "Delays collection and uses a non-sterile container", "D": "Uses a shallow cough that brings up saliva, not lower airway sputum"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rinse his mouth with water, take a deep breath, and cough deeply into the sterile container.): A good culture needs sputum from the lower airways, so a water rinse removes food and bacteria, followed by a deep cough into a sterile container. Mouthwash can affect results and saliva is not sputum. A clean container is not sterile, and a light cough does not bring up deep secretions."}, {"id": 595, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 45-year-old man returns after suture removal from a clean abdominal wound, and the nurse applies adhesive skin closure strips over the incision. He asks whether he should peel them off each day to check healing. What should the nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Leave them on and dry; they loosen and fall off in about a week.", "B": "Peel one off daily to inspect the edges, then replace with new strips.", "C": "Remove them after two days, since leaving them longer traps moisture and bacteria.", "D": "Soak them in the shower each day so they release without pulling the skin."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Disturbs wound edges by repeated removal", "C": "Removes support before healing is stable", "D": "Soaks strips and loosens them early"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C063", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse working in a community urgent care clinic sees several clients with wounds during one shift: a man after suture removal, a woman with a bleeding hand laceration, a child with a dog bite, and a man who stepped on a nail while working at home. Each client is stable on arrival and needs first aid or teaching.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Leave them on and dry; they loosen and fall off in about a week.): Skin closure strips support the wound edges and should be left until they loosen on their own, usually within a week or so, while kept dry. Peeling them off daily or after two days removes support and may open the wound. Soaking weakens the adhesive."}, {"id": 596, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Bleeding from a kitchen knife cut on her palm brings a woman to the clinic. Blood has soaked through a dish towel but flows steadily rather than spurting, and she is pale and anxious. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Apply firm direct pressure with a clean dressing and raise the hand above heart level.", "B": "Remove the soaked towel and inspect the wound carefully before applying any pressure again.", "C": "Rinse the cut under running water until the bleeding slows, then cover it.", "D": "Apply a tourniquet above the wrist and note the time of application."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Lifts the clot-forming dressing and restarts bleeding", "C": "Delays pressure and washes while bleeding continues", "D": "Uses a tourniquet for bleeding that direct pressure can control"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C063", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse working in a community urgent care clinic sees several clients with wounds during one shift: a man after suture removal, a woman with a bleeding hand laceration, a child with a dog bite, and a man who stepped on a nail while working at home. Each client is stable on arrival and needs first aid or teaching.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Apply firm direct pressure with a clean dressing and raise the hand above heart level.): Direct pressure with elevation controls most limb bleeding and should come first, adding more dressing on top if needed. A tourniquet is a last resort for bleeding that cannot be controlled. Removing the towel disturbs forming clots, and washing the wound waits until bleeding is controlled."}, {"id": 597, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Deep puncture marks and mild bleeding mark a dog bite on the forearm of a 7-year-old, caused by a neighbour's dog two hours ago. Her mother wants it covered tightly to keep it clean. Which action is most appropriate for the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Soak the arm in warm saline and leave it uncovered so the punctures drain.", "B": "Apply an antiseptic and pressure dressing, then arrange routine follow-up in two days.", "C": "Wash well under running water, cover loosely, and report for rabies, tetanus, and antibiotic assessment.", "D": "Close the edges tightly with adhesive strips to keep bacteria from entering."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Soaks the wound and leaves it open without assessment", "B": "Delays assessment of infection and rabies risk", "D": "Seals bacteria into a deep puncture wound"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C063", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse working in a community urgent care clinic sees several clients with wounds during one shift: a man after suture removal, a woman with a bleeding hand laceration, a child with a dog bite, and a man who stepped on a nail while working at home. Each client is stable on arrival and needs first aid or teaching.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wash well under running water, cover loosely, and report for rabies, tetanus, and antibiotic assessment.): Bite wounds carry a high risk of infection, so thorough washing, a loose covering, and prompt assessment for rabies, tetanus, and antibiotics are needed. Closing the wound tightly traps bacteria. Routine follow-up in two days is too slow, and soaking without assessment does not manage the risks."}, {"id": 598, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 38-year-old man stepped on a nail at home two hours ago. The puncture is small and barely bleeding. He says he is protected because the nail was not rusty. His last tetanus shot was twelve years ago. What should the nurse tell him?", "options": {"A": "Cleaning the wound well removes tetanus risk, so a booster is needed only if symptoms appear.", "B": "Rust is not the cause; deep punctures carry risk, so his booster status needs review today.", "C": "A booster is needed only if the wound becomes red or swollen in the coming days.", "D": "Risk exists only with rusty objects, so his booster status needs no review this time."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Thinks washing replaces immunization", "C": "Waits for local signs that do not predict tetanus", "D": "Believes rust alone causes tetanus"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C063", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse working in a community urgent care clinic sees several clients with wounds during one shift: a man after suture removal, a woman with a bleeding hand laceration, a child with a dog bite, and a man who stepped on a nail while working at home. Each client is stable on arrival and needs first aid or teaching.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rust is not the cause; deep punctures carry risk, so his booster status needs review today.): Tetanus spores live in soil and can enter deep punctures, so rust is not the key factor, and a twelve-year gap since his last shot means a booster should be considered now. Washing helps but does not replace immunization. Redness or swelling is not a reliable sign of tetanus risk."}, {"id": 599, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two hours after receiving an epidural, a 28-year-old in labour reports she cannot lift her left leg off the bed and that it feels heavier than the right. Her pain is well controlled, blood pressure is 108/66, and contractions are regular. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Report the one-sided leg weakness promptly and monitor her motor function.", "B": "Record it as an expected effect of the epidural and reassess at the next routine check.", "C": "Ask her to wiggle her toes and tell the RN only if she cannot move them.", "D": "Reposition her and reassess leg strength in thirty minutes before reporting."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats one-sided weakness as normal", "C": "Uses a narrow test that misses ongoing motor loss", "D": "Delays reporting a possible complication"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the one-sided leg weakness promptly and monitor her motor function.): Heavy, weak legs on one side after an epidural can signal an uneven block or a complication such as a hematoma, so prompt reporting and motor checks are needed. Calling it expected or waiting thirty minutes risks delayed treatment. Toe wiggling alone does not rule out significant weakness."}, {"id": 600, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Constant worry, poor sleep and muscle tension for eight months bring a 38-year-old teacher to the clinic. She asks the practical nurse about her options and says her friend takes lorazepam daily. Which information about management should the nurse share?", "options": {"A": "Medication is required before any other measure, and non-drug strategies work only afterward.", "B": "Benzodiazepines are the best long-term option, so ask the prescriber for a daily dose.", "C": "Therapy, exercise, and relaxation skills are usually tried first, with the prescriber guiding any medication.", "D": "Her worry is best managed by avoiding situations that provoke it and resting more often."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reverses the order and dismisses non-drug care", "B": "Treats long-term benzodiazepines as first choice", "D": "Recommends avoidance, which maintains anxiety"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Therapy, exercise, and relaxation skills are usually tried first, with the prescriber guiding any medication.): For generalized anxiety, non-drug approaches such as therapy, exercise, and relaxation are usually first, and the prescriber decides about medication. Long-term benzodiazepines carry risks of dependence. Medication is not a prerequisite for other measures, and avoidance tends to strengthen anxiety."}, {"id": 601, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A hospitalized 70-year-old man has crackles in both lung bases, orthopnea, and a cough with pink frothy-tinged sputum. His ankles are mildly swollen from long sitting. The nurse considers which type of heart failure explains the main findings. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Place him supine to improve venous return, then listen for added fluid in the lung bases.", "B": "Treat the ankle swelling as the main sign of left-sided failure, and elevate his legs.", "C": "Treat the findings as right-sided failure, and restrict his fluids while checking daily weights.", "D": "Treat the lung findings as left-sided failure, sit him upright, and report promptly to the RN."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Lays him flat, which worsens pulmonary congestion", "B": "Confuses peripheral edema with left-sided failure", "C": "Mislabels pulmonary findings as right-sided"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat the lung findings as left-sided failure, sit him upright, and report promptly to the RN.): Crackles, orthopnea, and frothy sputum reflect fluid backing into the lungs, which is left-sided failure, while ankle swelling is more typical of right-sided failure and here is explained by sitting. Upright positioning eases breathing and the RN needs to know promptly. Lying flat increases lung congestion."}, {"id": 602, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 45-year-old man takes phenytoin for seizures and attends the clinic for a routine visit. The practical nurse notes a wide, unsteady gait and red, swollen, overgrown gums. He says his gait is probably from poor sleep. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Advise him to skip the next dose until the gait improves, then resume.", "B": "Reassure him that gum overgrowth and unsteady gait are common and need no reporting.", "C": "Report the unsteady gait as possible toxicity and teach careful dental hygiene with regular dental visits.", "D": "Attribute the gait to poor sleep and teach gum care with a soft toothbrush."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Advises skipping a dose, risking seizures", "B": "Normalizes signs that need follow-up", "D": "Accepts a convenient explanation for a toxic sign"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the unsteady gait as possible toxicity and teach careful dental hygiene with regular dental visits.): Unsteady gait is a sign of phenytoin toxicity and needs reporting, while gum overgrowth is a known effect managed with careful oral hygiene and dental care. Blaming sleep misses a drug effect, and skipping a dose risks breakthrough seizures. Neither sign should be dismissed."}, {"id": 603, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a pediatric ward during a rotavirus outbreak, a practical nurse removes gloves after changing a toddler with watery diarrhea, uses alcohol hand rub, and goes to the next room. A sink is available beside the door. What should she have done?", "options": {"A": "Rub with alcohol until dry, then wipe hands with a disinfectant wipe.", "B": "Use alcohol rub twice, since repeating the rub gives the same protection as washing with soap.", "C": "Wear a second pair of gloves, since gloves replace hand hygiene between rooms.", "D": "Wash her hands with soap and running water, since alcohol rub is less effective here."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses a wipe on skin instead of washing", "B": "Believes repeating alcohol rub equals washing", "C": "Uses gloves as a substitute for hand hygiene"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wash her hands with soap and running water, since alcohol rub is less effective here.): Rotavirus and similar enteric viruses are better removed by soap and water, so washing is preferred when a sink is available and hands are soiled. Repeating rub does not fix its lower effect on these organisms. Gloves do not replace hand hygiene, and wipes are not meant for skin."}, {"id": 604, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "An adult on an inpatient unit has told her trauma history in full to the admitting nurse, the physician and a student. The team plans to share assessments. A colleague proposes that each member take their own full history from the start. What approach is most consistent with trauma-informed care?", "options": {"A": "Document it once, share it with her consent, and ask only for details needed for care.", "B": "Avoid all questions about her past and rely on the physician's notes for care decisions.", "C": "Have each member obtain a complete history from the start so nothing is missed.", "D": "Ask her to retell the story, but only when she appears calm that day."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Avoids needed questions and ignores her choices", "C": "Repeats the retelling and risks re-traumatization", "D": "Treats calm as consent to retell"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Document it once, share it with her consent, and ask only for details needed for care.): Trauma-informed care avoids asking a survivor to retell the full story repeatedly, so the team records it once, shares it with consent, and asks only what care requires. Repeating histories can cause distress, and retelling when calm does not remove that harm. Avoiding all questions leaves care decisions uninformed."}, {"id": 605, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Wrong doses given repeatedly because of poor drug knowledge, with no intent to harm, are the subject of a complaint about a nurse on Marcus's unit. A coworker says this is professional misconduct with the same outcome as theft from a client. How should Marcus describe the difference?", "options": {"A": "Both mean a failure to meet standards and follow one process.", "B": "Misconduct is a skill gap training fixes; incompetence is deliberate wrongdoing.", "C": "Incompetence is lacking knowledge, skill, or judgement; misconduct is improper conduct.", "D": "Misconduct applies only to intentional harm, while incompetence is reviewed only by the employer."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Merges two separate findings into one process", "B": "Swaps the definitions of incompetence and misconduct", "D": "Limits misconduct to intent and removes the regulator from incompetence"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C074", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse, Marcus Bell, works in a long-term care home. Over several weeks he faces questions about professional conduct, his own health, and where to turn for different problems. A complaint about practice on his unit has been received by the regulatory body, and colleagues are unsure how the regulator, the professional association and the union differ in what they do.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Incompetence is lacking knowledge, skill, or judgement; misconduct is improper conduct.): Incompetence concerns a nurse who lacks the knowledge, skill, or judgement needed, while misconduct concerns improper behaviour, and a regulator can decide different outcomes for each. The terms are not interchangeable and the definitions are not reversed. Both can be reviewed by the regulatory body, not only the employer."}, {"id": 606, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Marcus has a health condition that affects his concentration, and he asks whether it ends his licence automatically. A coworker says a condition always ends a licence. Marcus reviews what fitness to practise means for a nurse. Which statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Practice may continue with conditions or accommodations if safe, as decided by the regulatory body.", "B": "The employer places him on leave, and the regulator reviews the condition only after a complaint.", "C": "A health condition removes the licence until his physician writes a clearance letter.", "D": "He may continue unchanged if he tells his union, which then clears fitness to practise."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Gives the employer control and waits for a complaint", "C": "Treats any condition as automatic loss of licence", "D": "Gives the union the regulator's fitness decision"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C074", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse, Marcus Bell, works in a long-term care home. Over several weeks he faces questions about professional conduct, his own health, and where to turn for different problems. A complaint about practice on his unit has been received by the regulatory body, and colleagues are unsure how the regulator, the professional association and the union differ in what they do.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Practice may continue with conditions or accommodations if safe, as decided by the regulatory body.): Fitness to practise is judged by whether care can be given safely, and the regulatory body can allow practice with conditions or supports instead of ending a licence. A condition does not automatically remove the licence. An employer or union cannot replace the regulator's decision, and the regulator does not wait for a complaint to consider safety."}, {"id": 607, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Marcus was disciplined by his employer for refusing an assignment he believed was unsafe. He wants representation at the meeting with management. A coworker suggests the regulatory body will attend with him. Which source of help best fits his need?", "options": {"A": "The employer's human resources department, since it manages discipline and can speak for him.", "B": "The professional association, since it bargains his discipline outcome directly with management.", "C": "The regulatory body, since it represents nurses in workplace meetings about discipline and safety.", "D": "His union representative, who can support him at the meeting under the collective agreement."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats the employer's own department as his advocate", "B": "Gives the association a bargaining role it lacks", "C": "Expects the regulator to act as the nurse's representative"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C074", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse, Marcus Bell, works in a long-term care home. Over several weeks he faces questions about professional conduct, his own health, and where to turn for different problems. A complaint about practice on his unit has been received by the regulatory body, and colleagues are unsure how the regulator, the professional association and the union differ in what they do.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (His union representative, who can support him at the meeting under the collective agreement.): A union representative supports members in employment matters such as discipline meetings, and Marcus can use that right. The regulatory body protects the public and does not represent nurses. Human resources acts for the employer, and the association supports education and advocacy but does not bargain discipline outcomes."}, {"id": 608, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A practical nurse in Marcus's home wants continuing education in wound care and a voice in public policy on long-term care staffing. A new graduate asks whether the professional association investigates complaints about members and also offers these resources. Which statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "The association handles complaints first and refers serious ones to the regulatory body afterward.", "B": "The association supports education, and the employer investigates complaints before the regulatory body does.", "C": "The association negotiates wages, so education resources come through the union contract.", "D": "The association supports education and advocacy for members, while complaints are investigated by the regulatory body."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives the association a first-stage complaint role", "B": "Places employer investigation ahead of the regulator", "C": "Confuses the association with the union"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C074", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse, Marcus Bell, works in a long-term care home. Over several weeks he faces questions about professional conduct, his own health, and where to turn for different problems. A complaint about practice on his unit has been received by the regulatory body, and colleagues are unsure how the regulator, the professional association and the union differ in what they do.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The association supports education and advocacy for members, while complaints are investigated by the regulatory body.): Professional associations provide continuing education, networking, and advocacy, while complaint investigation and discipline belong to the regulatory body. The association does not handle complaints in a first stage, and the employer does not stand in for the regulator. It also does not bargain wages, which is the role of the union."}, {"id": 609, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Staff in Marcus's home feel shift scheduling and overtime rules are unfair. A new nurse says the regulatory body will negotiate the contract on their behalf. Marcus explains who negotiates scheduling, wages and overtime for unionized nurses. Which is correct?", "options": {"A": "The regulatory body negotiates with the employer, since it protects nurses' working conditions.", "B": "The professional association bargains with the employer, since it speaks for nurses on workplace issues.", "C": "The union bargains collectively with the employer to set these terms in the collective agreement.", "D": "The employer sets them alone, and the union may only comment during annual reviews."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Expects the regulator to bargain for nurses", "B": "Confuses the association with the bargaining union", "D": "Removes the union's collective bargaining role"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C074", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse, Marcus Bell, works in a long-term care home. Over several weeks he faces questions about professional conduct, his own health, and where to turn for different problems. A complaint about practice on his unit has been received by the regulatory body, and colleagues are unsure how the regulator, the professional association and the union differ in what they do.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The union bargains collectively with the employer to set these terms in the collective agreement.): In a unionized workplace, the union bargains collectively with the employer, and the resulting collective agreement sets scheduling, wages, and overtime. The regulatory body protects the public and does not negotiate contracts, and the association does not bargain for workers. The employer does not set these terms alone."}, {"id": 610, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Arriving for hemodialysis, a 58-year-old man has no palpable thrill and no audible bruit over his left forearm fistula, and the arm feels cooler than usual. His blood pressure was already taken on the right arm. He asks to start quickly so he can leave early. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Warm the arm with a heated pad, then reassess before reporting.", "B": "Start cannulation since the access worked at the last treatment.", "C": "Take blood pressure on the left arm to compare it with the right.", "D": "Hold treatment and report the absent thrill and bruit to the RN right away."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying a report while trying a home remedy on a possibly clotted access", "B": "Relying on last visit's patency instead of today's assessment findings", "C": "Using the fistula arm for blood pressure measurement, which can damage the access"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold treatment and report the absent thrill and bruit to the RN right away.): A fistula without a thrill or bruit may be clotted, so the nurse protects it and reports at once; the cool arm adds to the concern. Taking blood pressure on the fistula arm risks damaging the access, and it would not answer the question anyway. Warming the arm delays the report, and past patency says nothing about the access today. Always assess thrill and bruit before every dialysis."}, {"id": 611, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Asked to track fluids for a heart failure client on a fluid restriction, an unregulated care provider measures and records intake and output on a medical unit. At shift end the totals sit on the flow sheet. What must the practical nurse do with this delegated information?", "options": {"A": "File the totals unchanged because measuring intake and output is a basic task needing no review.", "B": "Review the totals against the restriction and weight, then report concerns.", "C": "Repeat each measurement personally before accepting any value the care provider has recorded.", "D": "Co-sign the flow sheet because the care provider remains accountable for the data's accuracy."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Skipping review of delegated data and so missing a change in fluid status", "C": "Duplicating every task, which defeats the purpose of delegation", "D": "Treating the delegate as solely accountable when the nurse retains accountability"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Review the totals against the restriction and weight, then report concerns.): The nurse stays accountable for the outcome of delegated care, so the totals must be interpreted against the restriction and the weight trend. Co-signing without reviewing hands the interpretation to someone outside the nurse's role. Filing the numbers unread misses early fluid overload in heart failure. Repeating every measurement is unnecessary when the provider is competent and the data is reviewed."}, {"id": 612, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Forty-five minutes ago, a 66-year-old on a medical unit received an oral analgesic for incisional pain rated 8 out of 10. The nurse is organizing the coming hour for several clients. Which action reflects correct ongoing reassessment of this client's pain?", "options": {"A": "Return near the drug's expected peak to reassess pain and sedation, then document.", "B": "Ask about pain at the next scheduled vital sign round, record the score, and move on.", "C": "Reassess only if the client rings the call bell to report returning pain.", "D": "Ask the roommate whether the client looks comfortable and record that answer."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reassessing on the routine schedule instead of at the drug's peak effect", "C": "Relying on the client to initiate reassessment, which can leave pain untreated", "D": "Using a third party's impression in place of the client's own report"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Return near the drug's expected peak to reassess pain and sedation, then document.): Pain and sedation are reassessed when the oral drug should be working, about an hour after the dose, so the nurse can judge relief and safety. Waiting for the routine round may leave severe pain untreated for hours. A roommate's opinion is not a valid pain assessment, since pain is what the client says it is. Depending on the call bell shifts responsibility away from the nurse."}, {"id": 613, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a community clinic, a 47-year-old man describes burning epigastric pain that eases after meals. He has taken iron tablets for two weeks and noticed two black, tarry stools yesterday. His blood pressure is 112/70 mmHg. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's response?", "options": {"A": "Melena is expected with ulcers, so suggest antacids and a bland diet only.", "B": "The stool color likely comes from his pain medication, so ask him to record it.", "C": "Black tarry stool may mean upper gastrointestinal bleeding, so report it promptly despite the iron.", "D": "Iron commonly darkens stool, so reassure him and advise review at his next visit."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a warning sign of bleeding as an expected ulcer finding", "B": "Attributing a significant finding to medication and only monitoring it", "D": "Dismissing melena as an iron effect without ruling out bleeding"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Black tarry stool may mean upper gastrointestinal bleeding, so report it promptly despite the iron.): Black tarry stool in a client with ulcer symptoms can mean bleeding from the upper gastrointestinal tract, and iron does not rule that out. Reassurance and a routine follow-up could delay treatment of a bleed. Calling melena expected, or blaming another drug, replaces assessment with assumption. His normal blood pressure does not exclude slow bleeding."}, {"id": 614, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Newly started on digoxin, an 84-year-old man on a medical unit has his apical pulse counted for one full minute before the morning dose. The nurse finds a regular rate of 54 beats per minute. He feels tired but denies nausea. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Recheck at the radial artery for one minute and give the dose if similar.", "B": "Give the dose, since a rate of 54 is acceptable for older adults on digoxin.", "C": "Hold the dose and report the apical rate to the RN or physician.", "D": "Give half of the ordered dose and ask the pharmacist to confirm the amount."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Switching to a radial pulse when digoxin needs an apical count", "B": "Accepting a rate below the usual hold threshold as normal for age", "D": "Altering a dose and consulting pharmacy instead of reporting bradycardia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hold the dose and report the apical rate to the RN or physician.): A slow apical rate in a client starting digoxin is a reason to hold the drug and report, since toxicity can cause bradycardia and fatigue may be an early sign. Age alone does not make a rate of 54 acceptable here. The radial pulse can miss beats, which is why an apical count is required. Changing a dose requires an order and is outside the nurse's scope."}, {"id": 615, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Incapable of consenting to a procedure and with no written wishes, a hospitalized man has his adult daughter, his brother, and a close friend at the bedside, each disagreeing about who should decide. The team asks the practical nurse for help. Which action is correct for the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Ask the ethics committee to name a decision maker.", "B": "Let the family vote and follow the majority choice.", "C": "Choose the family member who knows the client best and record that person as the substitute.", "D": "Follow the legislated substitute hierarchy and involve the team."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Sends a ranked legal question to a committee", "B": "Uses a family vote instead of the legal ranking", "C": "Selecting the substitute personally instead of following the ranked hierarchy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Follow the legislated substitute hierarchy and involve the team.): Substitute decision makers are identified by a ranked order set out in law, so the nurse applies that order rather than judging personal fit. A family vote does not follow the ranking, and an ethics committee does not appoint a substitute when the law already ranks them. The nurse does not choose by who knows him best. Checking the ranking with the team and recording it protects the client's rights."}, {"id": 616, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Boateng struggles to find words, and the nurse needs his consent for a routine blood draw. He understands simple questions but answers in short, halting phrases. A colleague suggests speaking louder and finishing his sentences to save time. Which approach best respects his rights and supports communication?", "options": {"A": "Skip the explanation and proceed since he has tolerated routine blood draws before.", "B": "Ask a nursing aide who knows him to explain the procedure and obtain consent.", "C": "Allow extra time, use short yes or no questions with gestures, and confirm understanding.", "D": "Have him sign a consent form and proceed since his signature shows understanding."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Proceeding without informing him because the task is routine", "B": "Delegates consent to an aide", "D": "Treats a signature as proof of understanding"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C084", "cluster_text": "Mr. Walter Boateng, 82, lives in a long-term care home. Three months ago a stroke left him with expressive aphasia and weakness on the right side. He also has moderate hearing loss and wears hearing aids, has cataracts that limit his vision, and has mild to moderate dementia. He is calm in the mornings but restless in the evenings, and the team is planning how to communicate with him safely and respectfully.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Allow extra time, use short yes or no questions with gestures, and confirm understanding.): Aphasia affects language, not necessarily understanding or capacity, so the nurse gives time, simple questions, and visual cues and checks that he has understood. Delegating consent to an aide, or relying on a signature without checking understanding, leaves out his own informed consent. Skipping the explanation removes informed consent."}, {"id": 617, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At 1900 the nurse finds Mr. Boateng without his hearing aids. He turns away when she speaks from the doorway, then gives an unrelated reply to a question about supper. He seemed alert and conversational at lunch. Which interpretation and response is best?", "options": {"A": "His reply shows sudden decline, so report an acute change and begin cognitive testing today.", "B": "Speak loudly directly into his ear to overcome the hearing loss.", "C": "He may not have heard, so check his hearing aids, face him, and speak slowly.", "D": "Write the instructions on a card and leave it at his bedside."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming dementia progression before ruling out a sensory cause", "B": "Shouting, which distorts speech and raises pitch for hearing-impaired clients", "D": "Relying on writing alone, given his poor vision and aphasia"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C084", "cluster_text": "Mr. Walter Boateng, 82, lives in a long-term care home. Three months ago a stroke left him with expressive aphasia and weakness on the right side. He also has moderate hearing loss and wears hearing aids, has cataracts that limit his vision, and has mild to moderate dementia. He is calm in the mornings but restless in the evenings, and the team is planning how to communicate with him safely and respectfully.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (He may not have heard, so check his hearing aids, face him, and speak slowly.): Missing hearing aids is a likely reason for an unrelated answer, so the nurse restores the aids and improves how she speaks before drawing conclusions. Calling it sudden decline skips a simple cause. Shouting raises pitch and distorts speech for people with hearing loss. A written card leaves him without his hearing aids and does not confirm that he has understood."}, {"id": 618, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Pacing the hallway one evening, Mr. Boateng says he must go to work and pick up his children, and he becomes upset when staff stop him. He has no pain and his vital signs are normal. Which response by the nurse best supports him?", "options": {"A": "Agree that he should leave and walk him to the main entrance to settle him.", "B": "Remind him that he is retired and that his children are grown adults, to orient him.", "C": "Acknowledge his feelings, redirect him gently to a calm activity, and avoid arguing.", "D": "Tell him firmly that he cannot leave and ask him to sit in his room."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Colluding in a way that creates a real elopement risk", "B": "Correcting and arguing with the client's reality, which increases distress", "D": "Using a firm command that escalates agitation and limits his dignity"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C084", "cluster_text": "Mr. Walter Boateng, 82, lives in a long-term care home. Three months ago a stroke left him with expressive aphasia and weakness on the right side. He also has moderate hearing loss and wears hearing aids, has cataracts that limit his vision, and has mild to moderate dementia. He is calm in the mornings but restless in the evenings, and the team is planning how to communicate with him safely and respectfully.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acknowledge his feelings, redirect him gently to a calm activity, and avoid arguing.): Validating the feeling and redirecting lowers distress in moderate dementia without deceiving him or provoking him. Pointing out facts he cannot hold onto often makes him more upset. Firm commands and confinement raise agitation. Walking him to the entrance could lead to an unsafe exit, and the nurse should also tell the team about his evening restlessness."}, {"id": 619, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Cataracts limit Mr. Boateng's vision. He often startles when staff enter his room and has knocked over his supper tray twice this week. The team is planning daily care to protect his safety and independence. Which strategy should the care plan include?", "options": {"A": "Announce yourself by name, explain each action first, and describe items on his tray.", "B": "Keep the room lights dim during the day so glare does not worsen cataracts.", "C": "Give him finger foods on a plain plate and skip describing the meal.", "D": "Enter quietly and touch his shoulder first, so that a spoken greeting does not startle him."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Uses dim light that raises bumping and spill risk", "C": "Skips verbal description of the meal", "D": "Touching first without a verbal cue, which startles a client with low vision"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C084", "cluster_text": "Mr. Walter Boateng, 82, lives in a long-term care home. Three months ago a stroke left him with expressive aphasia and weakness on the right side. He also has moderate hearing loss and wears hearing aids, has cataracts that limit his vision, and has mild to moderate dementia. He is calm in the mornings but restless in the evenings, and the team is planning how to communicate with him safely and respectfully.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Announce yourself by name, explain each action first, and describe items on his tray.): People with low vision need a voice cue before touch and a description of what is happening and where things are. Dim lighting raises the risk of bumping and spills. Describing tray positions, such as a clock face, lets him eat on his own. Skipping descriptions leaves him guessing, and touching first can startle him."}, {"id": 620, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Because his hands shake, a 45-year-old man asks the nurse to apply a prescribed corticosteroid cream to his forearms. The rash is weeping in places, and the nurse has no cuts on her own hands. Which action is best before applying the cream?", "options": {"A": "Apply it with bare hands since her skin is intact and the cream is not absorbed.", "B": "Use sterile gloves because the weeping skin is broken and needs a sterile field.", "C": "Scrub the rash with soap, then rub the cream deeply into the skin.", "D": "Do hand hygiene, put on clean gloves, and apply a thin layer."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Skipping gloves, exposing the nurse to the drug and to the weeping rash", "B": "Overusing sterile technique where clean gloves meet the standard", "C": "Scrubbing and rubbing, which damages skin and increases absorption"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C081", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse is working an afternoon shift at a community health clinic. Four clients are booked for medication care: a 45-year-old man with an itchy rash who has a prescription cream, a 4-year-old with fever and repeated vomiting, a 34-year-old woman with a confirmed yeast infection, and a 59-year-old man with type 2 diabetes who is starting insulin. Orders are on each chart and the nurse is competent in every route.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Do hand hygiene, put on clean gloves, and apply a thin layer.): Gloves protect the nurse from drug absorption and from contact with weeping skin, and clean gloves are enough for a topical cream. Bare hands expose her to the corticosteroid and body fluids. Sterile gloves are not required for topical application. Vigorous scrubbing worsens the rash."}, {"id": 621, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Fever of 39.6 C and two episodes of vomiting mean a 4-year-old cannot keep down an oral antipyretic, so an acetaminophen suppository is ordered. The nurse has positioned the child on the left side with the upper knee flexed. How should she insert the suppository?", "options": {"A": "Insert it halfway and ask the child to bear down so it sits in place.", "B": "Lubricate the tapered end and insert it past the anal sphincter with a gloved finger.", "C": "Insert the flat blunt end first so the tapered end stays accessible for quick removal afterward.", "D": "Insert it only into the first centimetre so the child can easily retain it."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Asking for bearing down, which pushes the suppository back out", "C": "Inserting the blunt end first, which makes retention harder", "D": "Placing it too shallow, so it is expelled before it dissolves"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C081", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse is working an afternoon shift at a community health clinic. Four clients are booked for medication care: a 45-year-old man with an itchy rash who has a prescription cream, a 4-year-old with fever and repeated vomiting, a 34-year-old woman with a confirmed yeast infection, and a 59-year-old man with type 2 diabetes who is starting insulin. Orders are on each chart and the nurse is competent in every route.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lubricate the tapered end and insert it past the anal sphincter with a gloved finger.): The tapered end goes in first, lubricated, and the suppository must pass the internal sphincter so the child can retain it as it melts. Blunt end first makes it harder to retain. A shallow placement is quickly expelled, and bearing down works against retention. Gentle slow breathing helps the child relax."}, {"id": 622, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Prescribed a clotrimazole vaginal tablet to use at bedtime for seven nights for a yeast infection, a 34-year-old woman says she usually inserts things while standing at the bathroom sink. Which teaching point should the nurse give about inserting this medication correctly?", "options": {"A": "Stop treatment once itching resolves, even if some tablets remain unused.", "B": "Insert it while standing with one foot raised on the toilet to reach proper depth.", "C": "Insert it lying on her back with knees bent, and stay lying briefly.", "D": "Insert it in the morning and walk around so the tablet spreads evenly."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stopping early, which risks incomplete treatment of the infection", "B": "Standing, which lets the tablet and medication drain out", "D": "Morning timing with activity, which causes leakage and poor contact time"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C081", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse is working an afternoon shift at a community health clinic. Four clients are booked for medication care: a 45-year-old man with an itchy rash who has a prescription cream, a 4-year-old with fever and repeated vomiting, a 34-year-old woman with a confirmed yeast infection, and a 59-year-old man with type 2 diabetes who is starting insulin. Orders are on each chart and the nurse is competent in every route.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Insert it lying on her back with knees bent, and stay lying briefly.): Lying down at bedtime keeps the medication in contact with the tissue while it dissolves. Standing and walking allow it to leak out. Stopping when itching eases can leave the infection untreated, so she completes all seven nights. The nurse also advises her to use an applicator as directed."}, {"id": 623, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Firm, lumpy areas cover the abdomen of a 59-year-old man where he injects insulin daily. Today he plans to use the same spot again, holding a skin pinch at 90 degrees. Which advice best prevents these skin changes and unpredictable insulin absorption?", "options": {"A": "Warm the lumpy area with a heat pack first, then inject there for better absorption.", "B": "Rotate sites within one area, avoid lumpy tissue, and use a new needle each time.", "C": "Move to a new abdominal site each day but press firmly on the lumps afterward.", "D": "Keep using one comfortable spot for predictable absorption, and reuse the needle until dull."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Injects into lipohypertrophy after warming it", "C": "Presses on lumps instead of avoiding them", "D": "Repeating one site and reusing needles, which worsens tissue changes"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C081", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse is working an afternoon shift at a community health clinic. Four clients are booked for medication care: a 45-year-old man with an itchy rash who has a prescription cream, a 4-year-old with fever and repeated vomiting, a 34-year-old woman with a confirmed yeast infection, and a 59-year-old man with type 2 diabetes who is starting insulin. Orders are on each chart and the nurse is competent in every route.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rotate sites within one area, avoid lumpy tissue, and use a new needle each time.): Rotating sites and using fresh needles prevents lipohypertrophy, which causes erratic absorption and unpredictable glucose levels. Injecting into lumps, even after warming, keeps absorption unreliable. Pressing on the lumps does not remove them. Reusing a site and a needle worsens the damage."}, {"id": 624, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Home care for a 74-year-old man with heart failure includes teaching him to monitor his weight daily. He says he weighs himself after breakfast on whichever scale is closest. Which instruction best helps him detect fluid retention early, before symptoms of worsening heart failure appear?", "options": {"A": "Weigh whenever swelling is noticed, on any available scale, and call if the number seems higher.", "B": "Weigh each evening after the largest meal, since fluid gain is easiest to see then.", "C": "Weigh once weekly on a clinic scale and compare the number with the last visit.", "D": "Weigh daily after voiding, before eating, on one scale in similar clothing."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Weighing only when symptoms appear and using varying scales", "B": "Weighing after meals, which adds variable food weight to the result", "C": "Weighing weekly, which misses rapid daily fluid gain"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Weigh daily after voiding, before eating, on one scale in similar clothing.): A consistent time, scale, and clothing, along with weighing after voiding and before eating, make daily changes reflect fluid and not food or equipment. Evening weights include meals. Weekly weights miss the quick gains that signal worsening heart failure. Weighing only when swollen delays action and varying scales gives unreliable numbers."}, {"id": 625, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "One hour after vaginal birth, a 29-year-old woman has a soft, boggy uterus 2 cm above the umbilicus and moderate lochia rubra. She voided 20 minutes ago and her bladder is not palpable. Which intervention should the nurse perform first?", "options": {"A": "Ask her to walk to the bathroom and void again, then recheck the fundus.", "B": "Place a cold pack on the abdomen and reassess the fundus after her pericare.", "C": "Apply firm sustained pressure with a flat hand until bleeding stops, even once the uterus firms.", "D": "Support the lower uterus and massage the fundus gently until firm, then recheck bleeding."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Repeating voiding when the bladder is already empty", "B": "Delaying treatment of atony with a cold pack and later reassessment", "C": "Continuing vigorous pressure on a firm uterus, which causes pain and fatigue"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Support the lower uterus and massage the fundus gently until firm, then recheck bleeding.): A soft fundus signals uterine atony, and massage, with support of the lower segment, makes it contract; it is stopped when the uterus is firm. Continued hard pressure on a firm uterus is painful and unnecessary. A cold pack with later reassessment delays treatment. She recently voided and her bladder is not palpable, so bladder emptying is not the first step."}, {"id": 626, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Newly prescribed metoprolol, a 65-year-old man tells the nurse he feels dizzy and tired. He checked his pulse for the first time today and found 52 beats per minute. He plans to take his next dose now because otherwise he feels well. What should the nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Hold the dose and call his prescriber about the slow pulse and dizziness.", "B": "Take the dose as ordered, since a pulse of 52 is normal with beta blockers.", "C": "Take half the dose and recheck the pulse in an hour, calling only if low.", "D": "Stop the medication on his own since dizziness shows it is not suitable for him."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Accepting bradycardia with symptoms as an expected drug effect", "C": "Self-adjusting the dose and delaying contact with the prescriber", "D": "Stopping a beta blocker without prescriber direction"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C077", "cluster_text": "During a rotation across a medical unit, a clinic and home care, a practical nurse cares for four adults with cardiovascular problems: a 65-year-old man starting a beta blocker at home, a 61-year-old woman on the ward with a severe headache and very high blood pressure, a 68-year-old man in clinic with calf pain on walking, and a 73-year-old woman receiving home care for a leg ulcer with swelling. The nurse reports to an RN in each setting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold the dose and call his prescriber about the slow pulse and dizziness.): A low pulse with dizziness after starting a beta blocker needs the prescriber's guidance before another dose. Calling the slow rate normal ignores his symptoms. Halving the dose is a self-made change, and stopping the drug suddenly can cause rebound tachycardia or chest pain. Teaching him to check his pulse before each dose is the habit to build."}, {"id": 627, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Severe headache and blurred vision are reported by a 61-year-old woman on the ward, who was admitted yesterday with an infection. Her blood pressure reads 214/124 mmHg. A PRN acetaminophen order has not yet been given. Which action by the nurse should come first?", "options": {"A": "Stay with her, recheck the blood pressure, and report immediately to the RN or physician.", "B": "Reassure her and repeat the blood pressure at the next scheduled check.", "C": "Lower the head of her bed flat to improve blood flow to the brain.", "D": "Give the PRN acetaminophen first, then recheck her blood pressure once the headache has eased."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Waiting for the routine schedule instead of escalating now", "C": "Lying her flat, which can raise intracranial pressure", "D": "Treating a hypertensive emergency sign as a routine headache"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C077", "cluster_text": "During a rotation across a medical unit, a clinic and home care, a practical nurse cares for four adults with cardiovascular problems: a 65-year-old man starting a beta blocker at home, a 61-year-old woman on the ward with a severe headache and very high blood pressure, a 68-year-old man in clinic with calf pain on walking, and a 73-year-old woman receiving home care for a leg ulcer with swelling. The nurse reports to an RN in each setting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stay with her, recheck the blood pressure, and report immediately to the RN or physician.): A very high pressure with headache and visual change suggests possible organ involvement, so verifying the reading and reporting at once is the priority. Giving acetaminophen treats the symptom and delays escalation. Lying flat may worsen intracranial pressure. Waiting for the next scheduled check leaves a dangerous reading unaddressed."}, {"id": 628, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Calf cramping begins after two blocks of walking and settles within minutes of rest, a 68-year-old man tells the clinic nurse. His feet feel cool and pedal pulses are faint. He believes he pulled a muscle. Which nursing response best supports his care?", "options": {"A": "Advise raising both legs well above heart level whenever he sits to ease the cramping.", "B": "Suggest warm heating pads on both calves before each walk to loosen the tight muscles.", "C": "Report the findings, explain circulation may be reduced, and review safe walking.", "D": "Agree it is likely strain and advise rest from walking until pain is gone."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Elevating legs when arterial flow is already limited", "B": "Applying heat to a limb with reduced sensation and flow, risking burns", "D": "Attributing pain with poor perfusion signs to simple strain"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C077", "cluster_text": "During a rotation across a medical unit, a clinic and home care, a practical nurse cares for four adults with cardiovascular problems: a 65-year-old man starting a beta blocker at home, a 61-year-old woman on the ward with a severe headache and very high blood pressure, a 68-year-old man in clinic with calf pain on walking, and a 73-year-old woman receiving home care for a leg ulcer with swelling. The nurse reports to an RN in each setting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the findings, explain circulation may be reduced, and review safe walking.): Pain on walking that eases with rest, along with cool feet and weak pulses, points to peripheral arterial disease, so the nurse reports it and supports a monitored exercise plan. Calling it strain delays diagnosis. Heat can burn a limb with poor circulation. Raising the legs further reduces arterial flow."}, {"id": 629, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Brown staining and mild edema surround a shallow, moist ulcer on the inner ankle of a 73-year-old woman. Her pedal pulses are strong and the foot is warm. A colleague suggests treating it like an arterial ulcer. Which plan fits these findings?", "options": {"A": "Place the leg on a heating pad while resting to improve circulation.", "B": "Elevate the leg at rest and report for compression therapy assessment by the team.", "C": "Keep the leg dependent and avoid compression because poor arterial flow is the concern.", "D": "Apply a firm compression bandage now without an order, since this is venous."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying heat, which risks burns and increases swelling", "C": "Using arterial care for a venous ulcer and withholding elevation", "D": "Starting compression independently without an order or assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C077", "cluster_text": "During a rotation across a medical unit, a clinic and home care, a practical nurse cares for four adults with cardiovascular problems: a 65-year-old man starting a beta blocker at home, a 61-year-old woman on the ward with a severe headache and very high blood pressure, a 68-year-old man in clinic with calf pain on walking, and a 73-year-old woman receiving home care for a leg ulcer with swelling. The nurse reports to an RN in each setting.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Elevate the leg at rest and report for compression therapy assessment by the team.): Strong pulses, brown staining, edema, and a shallow moist ulcer on the inner ankle indicate venous insufficiency, which improves with elevation and compression ordered by the team. Keeping the leg dependent worsens swelling. Compression needs an assessment and order. Heat adds a burn risk without helping healing."}, {"id": 630, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a surgical unit, a practical nurse realizes she recorded an 8 cm wound measurement on the wrong client's paper chart. The page is nearly full and a manager is walking toward her. What is the best way to correct the entry?", "options": {"A": "Strike one line through it, write error with date and initials, and re-enter correctly.", "B": "Draw a line through it, write error, and leave the entry unsigned to avoid blame.", "C": "Write the corrected measurement over the original figure and initial beside it.", "D": "Cover the entry with correction fluid so the chart stays neat, then rewrite it."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Leaves the correction unsigned and untraceable", "C": "Writes over the original so it cannot be read", "D": "Obliterating the original entry with correction fluid"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Strike one line through it, write error with date and initials, and re-enter correctly.): A single line through the mistake keeps the original legible, and the dated, initialed note shows who corrected it and when. Writing over the figure and correction fluid hide the original, which can look like tampering. A correction left unsigned cannot be traced to anyone. The entry should then be recorded on the correct client's chart."}, {"id": 631, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Kim decides to raise her concern about rushed handovers with Jordan. A colleague suggests mentioning it at the afternoon huddle so that everyone benefits from the same message. When and how should Kim give this feedback to Jordan, given that the manager encourages open communication?", "options": {"A": "Post a general reminder about complete handovers on the unit board and wait.", "B": "Ask Jordan for a private moment soon after the shift and discuss it respectfully.", "C": "Raise it at the huddle so the whole team hears the same expectation.", "D": "Tell the manager first and ask the manager to speak with Jordan about the problem."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a vague notice that does not address the person or behaviour", "C": "Giving feedback in front of peers, which embarrasses and invites defensiveness", "D": "Bypassing the peer and handing off a conversation Kim can start"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C083", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, a practical nurse named Kim notices that a peer, Jordan, often gives rushed bedside handovers that leave out changes in a client's condition. The manager encourages open feedback among staff. Kim wants to raise the concern, later receives feedback of her own from the charge nurse, and is also asked by a nursing student placed with her about how to learn from comments.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask Jordan for a private moment soon after the shift and discuss it respectfully.): Private, prompt, respectful feedback lets Jordan listen without an audience and respond. Raising it in front of the team embarrasses him and tends to produce defensiveness. Going straight to the manager skips a direct conversation that is within Kim's reach. A general notice never tells Jordan what he needs to change."}, {"id": 632, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Having asked Jordan for a private conversation, Kim rehearses several statements. She wants her message to be specific, timely, and focused on observable behaviour rather than character. Which of these statements best meets those goals and is most likely to lead to a useful discussion?", "options": {"A": "Yesterday at 1500 your handover omitted the oxygen order, so the evening nurse was unaware.", "B": "Yesterday you rushed handover again, and I think you should be more careful with orders.", "C": "At 1500 yesterday your handover left out the oxygen order, which shows you do not listen.", "D": "Last week's handover on the unit missed an order, and several colleagues have noticed it too."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Uses a vague label and a personal judgement", "C": "Adds a character conclusion to a specific event", "D": "Cites colleagues' views instead of one clear event"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C083", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, a practical nurse named Kim notices that a peer, Jordan, often gives rushed bedside handovers that leave out changes in a client's condition. The manager encourages open feedback among staff. Kim wants to raise the concern, later receives feedback of her own from the charge nurse, and is also asked by a nursing student placed with her about how to learn from comments.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yesterday at 1500 your handover omitted the oxygen order, so the evening nurse was unaware.): Effective feedback names what happened, when it happened, and its effect, without judging the person. Adding remarks about rushing or not listening turns an observation into a character judgement and provokes defence. Citing what other colleagues noticed gives Jordan no clear event to address."}, {"id": 633, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The charge nurse later tells Kim that her documentation was late three times this week. Kim feels her face flush and has an explanation ready about short staffing. Which response best shows that she is receiving this feedback professionally and will use it to improve her practice?", "options": {"A": "Agree to everything without questions, then avoid the charge nurse for the week.", "B": "Accept the comments quietly, then discuss them with coworkers to see whether they were fair.", "C": "Listen fully, thank the charge nurse, ask for examples, and agree on a plan.", "D": "Explain the staffing pressures immediately so the charge nurse understands the full context of delays."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Agreeing without understanding and withdrawing from follow-up", "B": "Venting to peers rather than engaging with the person giving feedback", "D": "Defending at once before listening to the concern"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C083", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, a practical nurse named Kim notices that a peer, Jordan, often gives rushed bedside handovers that leave out changes in a client's condition. The manager encourages open feedback among staff. Kim wants to raise the concern, later receives feedback of her own from the charge nurse, and is also asked by a nursing student placed with her about how to learn from comments.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Listen fully, thank the charge nurse, ask for examples, and agree on a plan.): Listening, thanking, asking for examples, and agreeing on a plan treats feedback as information she can use. Explaining right away signals defensiveness, even when staffing is a real factor she can raise later. Talking it over with coworkers avoids the person who gave it. Passive agreement and avoidance leave the issue unresolved."}, {"id": 634, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A nursing student placed with Kim says she is unsure how her skills are being judged, and her formal evaluation is three weeks away. Kim has noticed that the student rarely speaks up during care. Which advice about feedback should Kim give her?", "options": {"A": "Wait for the formal evaluation, since the comments will be complete and official then.", "B": "Ask the unit manager how others rated her and wait for the formal results.", "C": "Speak up more during care so the preceptor will notice her skills and comment.", "D": "Ask her preceptor for specific comments after each shift so she can improve early."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for a formal evaluation when earlier comments could guide learning", "B": "Seeks others' ratings instead of her own feedback", "C": "Leaves feedback to chance instead of asking"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C083", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, a practical nurse named Kim notices that a peer, Jordan, often gives rushed bedside handovers that leave out changes in a client's condition. The manager encourages open feedback among staff. Kim wants to raise the concern, later receives feedback of her own from the charge nurse, and is also asked by a nursing student placed with her about how to learn from comments.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask her preceptor for specific comments after each shift so she can improve early.): Asking for specific comments regularly turns every shift into a chance to adjust before the formal rating. Waiting three weeks wastes that opportunity. Hoping the preceptor notices and comments leaves feedback to chance, and a manager's report on others does not describe her own skills. Asking is a sign of professional growth."}, {"id": 635, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Preparing to reposition a 120 kg client up in bed, a nurse notes the bed is at knee height, the client can assist a little, and a ceiling lift is available. The nurse's lower back has felt sore all week. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Raise the bed to waist height, lock the wheels, and use the ceiling lift", "B": "Ask a colleague to pull from the bed head while the nurse twists to support", "C": "Leave the bed at knee height and bend at the waist, holding the weight close", "D": "Pull the client up by gripping under the arms while the client pushes."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Twisting the trunk while handling a heavy load", "C": "Working at a low bed height with a bent back", "D": "Manual lifting under the arms instead of using available equipment"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Raise the bed to waist height, lock the wheels, and use the ceiling lift): Working at waist height, with wheels locked, and using a lift or slide sheet with a second person reduces load on the back for a heavy client. Bending at the waist strains the back, especially with prior soreness. Manual under-arm pulling risks injury to both people. Twisting under load is a common cause of back injury."}, {"id": 636, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After a code on the unit, a practical nurse realizes she did not chart a post-event assessment completed at 0215. It is now 0420, and she still has her original notes. How should she document the assessment so that the record stays truthful?", "options": {"A": "Skip charting since the code record already covers it and add nothing now.", "B": "Insert the note in the blank line above the 0300 entry and time it 0215.", "C": "Write a new entry labelled late entry with the current date and time, citing 0215.", "D": "Record it with the time 0215 only, since that is when the assessment actually occurred."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaving a completed assessment undocumented", "B": "Inserting text into earlier entries, which falsifies sequence", "D": "Backdating an entry so it seems written at the event time"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Write a new entry labelled late entry with the current date and time, citing 0215.): A late entry is labelled as such, carries the real date and time of writing, and states when the care happened, so the record stays truthful. Squeezing an entry into earlier space or timing it 0215 hides when it was written. If it was not charted, it is treated as not done, so skipping is not acceptable."}, {"id": 637, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two antibiotics and a proton pump inhibitor were prescribed for 14 days to treat Helicobacter pylori in a 52-year-old man at a community clinic. On day six his pain is gone, and he says he will stop the pills and save the rest. What should the nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Finish the full course, since stopping early can allow recurrence.", "B": "Save the leftover antibiotics in case the pain returns, and restart them then.", "C": "Take the pills only on days when stomach pain is present until the supply is used.", "D": "Stop the antibiotics but continue the acid medication, since the pain was caused by acid."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Keeping leftover antibiotics for self-treatment later", "C": "Taking medication only for symptoms instead of on schedule", "D": "Treating symptom relief as cure and splitting the regimen"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Finish the full course, since stopping early can allow recurrence.): Pain relief does not mean the bacteria are gone, and the full combination course is needed to eradicate them and prevent resistance and relapse. Stopping only the antibiotics leaves the infection. Saving leftovers or taking pills on pain days gives sub-therapeutic dosing and promotes resistance."}, {"id": 638, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A practical nurse in long-term care charted a resident's fall as happening at 1400, but a care aide later reports it occurred at 1330. The original entry is already in the record and the incident report has not been started. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Leave the entry as written and record the correct time in the incident report only.", "B": "Edit the original entry to read 1330 so that the record shows one time.", "C": "Add a dated, signed addendum to clarify the fall time, keeping the original entry.", "D": "Instruct the care aide to document a different fall time so the entries remain separate."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on the incident report to fix errors in the chart", "B": "Altering the original entry rather than adding a clarification", "D": "Splitting records instead of correcting the nurse's own entry"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Add a dated, signed addendum to clarify the fall time, keeping the original entry.): An addendum clarifies the earlier note without changing it, keeping the record legal and transparent. Rewriting the original conceals that a change was made. Having the aide chart a different time leaves two conflicting times. The incident report is not part of the health record and cannot correct it."}, {"id": 639, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A practical nurse in a clinic is deciding which tasks to delegate to a care aide trained in clean-catch urine collection. One client can walk and follow instructions, and another needs a sterile specimen from an indwelling catheter. Which delegation is appropriate for this nurse?", "options": {"A": "Delegate both specimens since the aide has been trained to collect specimens in general.", "B": "Delegate the clean-catch collection with clear instructions, and collect the catheter specimen herself.", "C": "Delegate the catheter specimen because it is quick, and collect the clean-catch herself.", "D": "Delegate neither task, as specimen collection stays with regulated nurses."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming general training covers a sterile procedure", "C": "Reversing the risk by delegating the higher-risk task", "D": "Withholding a task the aide is competent to perform"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Delegate the clean-catch collection with clear instructions, and collect the catheter specimen herself.): A clean-catch specimen is a low-risk task within the aide's training, whereas sterile catheter sampling needs nursing judgment and technique to avoid infection. Delegating both overlooks competence for sterile skills. Delegating the riskier task reverses the logic. Keeping every collection with nurses ignores the aide's validated skill."}, {"id": 640, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Her neighbour has been admitted to another unit in the same hospital, and a practical nurse on a medical unit is curious about how the neighbour is doing. Her login allows access to every record in the system. What is the appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Do not open the record, because access requires a care need or work purpose.", "B": "Open it to read only the room number and diagnosis, which are not private.", "C": "Ask the unit clerk if a neighbour may view the chart.", "D": "Open it only to confirm he arrived safely, then close it."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Considering room and diagnosis harmless despite being health information", "C": "Asks the wrong person for permission to view", "D": "Treats a brief look as a harmless access"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Do not open the record, because access requires a care need or work purpose.): Access to a record is allowed only for clients in the nurse's care or for a legitimate work purpose, whatever the system lets her do. Having a login is not consent. A brief look to confirm arrival is still unauthorised access, and a clerk cannot grant permission for curiosity. Room and diagnosis are personal health information."}, {"id": 641, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Her written advance wishes decline tube feeding, yet the son of an incapable 85-year-old resident on a medical unit, who is her substitute decision maker, wants a tube started because he cannot bear to lose her. The team asks which principle applies. What should the nurse say?", "options": {"A": "The nurse should start feeding until the ethics committee is able to meet.", "B": "The substitute may decide by personal preference because the substitute carries legal authority.", "C": "The physician decides alone whenever family members disagree with the recorded wishes.", "D": "Substitutes must follow the person's known capable prior wishes."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Acting before the dispute is resolved, contradicting the known wishes", "B": "Allowing a substitute to override the person's own wishes", "C": "Giving the physician sole authority over a disagreement"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Substitutes must follow the person's known capable prior wishes.): A substitute decision maker is bound by the person's earlier capable wishes, which apply here and decline tube feeding. His grief does not change that obligation. The physician does not set aside the wishes alone, and starting the feeding in the meantime would act against them. An ethics consult can help the team resolve the conflict."}, {"id": 642, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "While performing passive range of motion for an unconscious 45-year-old adult in hospital, a practical nurse supports the limb above and below each joint and moves it gently. She recalls that the client cannot report pain. How far should she move each joint?", "options": {"A": "Move each joint through half of its range to protect it and limit fatigue.", "B": "Move each joint slowly through its normal range to mild resistance, watching the face.", "C": "Move each joint quickly through its full range several times to improve circulation and tone.", "D": "Move each joint beyond resistance to stretch tight muscles and prevent contracture."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using half range, which allows contractures to develop", "C": "Moving quickly, which may cause injury and spasticity", "D": "Forcing past resistance, which damages tissue and joints"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Move each joint slowly through its normal range to mild resistance, watching the face.): Slow, supported movement to the end of the usual range keeps joints flexible and prevents contractures. Pushing past resistance can tear tissue, and an unconscious client cannot report pain, so facial signs are watched. Half range lets stiffness develop. Fast movements risk injury and trigger spasm."}, {"id": 643, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Recording the Apgar score at one minute in the delivery room, a nurse observes a newborn with a heart rate of 110, a good cry, flexed limbs, a grimace when the nose is suctioned, and blue hands and feet. Which components make up the score?", "options": {"A": "Heart rate, breathing effort, muscle tone, body temperature, and skin color.", "B": "Heart rate, respiratory effort, birth weight, reflex irritability, and skin color.", "C": "Heart rate, respiratory effort, muscle tone, reflex irritability, and skin color.", "D": "Heart rate, blood pressure, muscle tone, reflex irritability, and skin color."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Including temperature, which is monitored but not scored", "B": "Including birth weight, which is not part of the tool", "D": "Including blood pressure, which is not scored in newborns"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Heart rate, respiratory effort, muscle tone, reflex irritability, and skin color.): The Apgar tool scores heart rate, respiratory effort, muscle tone, reflex irritability, and skin color, each from zero to two. Temperature is watched closely after birth but is not scored. Birth weight and blood pressure are not components. This infant would lose a point for the blue hands and feet."}, {"id": 644, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Prolonged bed rest after surgery puts a 70-year-old adult at risk, and the nurse is planning positioning to prevent foot drop and contractures. A colleague has placed a pillow under his knees to prevent stiffness. Which action best prevents these complications?", "options": {"A": "Let the feet rest pointed downward under the blankets to spare the heels.", "B": "Keep feet at right angles with a footboard or splint, and do range of motion regularly.", "C": "Reposition only at shift change so the client can rest without interruption or discomfort.", "D": "Leave a pillow under the knees to keep them flexed and prevent stiffness."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Allowing plantar flexion, which causes foot drop", "C": "Repositioning rarely, which raises pressure injury and contracture risk", "D": "Keeping knees flexed, which promotes flexion contractures"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep feet at right angles with a footboard or splint, and do range of motion regularly.): A footboard or splint keeps the ankle at a right angle, and regular range of motion maintains joint mobility. A pillow under the knees encourages flexion contractures. Feet left pointed down develop foot drop. Turning only at shift change leaves the skin and joints in one position too long."}, {"id": 645, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Seated in a chair on a ward, a 50-year-old with epilepsy begins a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. A family member shouts at the nurse to hold him still. The nurse notes the time and moves toward him. Which action is correct?", "options": {"A": "Move him back to bed during the seizure so the team can monitor him more closely.", "B": "Ease him to the floor, cushion his head, and turn him on his side afterward.", "C": "Place a padded tongue blade between his teeth to prevent tongue biting during the seizure.", "D": "Hold his arms and legs firmly to stop the jerking and prevent injury."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Moving the client during the seizure, which risks injury", "C": "Putting objects in the mouth, which injures teeth and blocks the airway", "D": "Restraining a seizing client, which can cause fractures"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ease him to the floor, cushion his head, and turn him on his side afterward.): Lowering him to the floor, cushioning the head, and side-lying afterward protect him and the airway while the seizure runs its course. Restraint can cause fractures and does not stop a seizure. Objects in the mouth can break teeth or obstruct the airway. Moving a seizing person is unsafe and can wait until the event has ended."}, {"id": 646, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Smiling, a 72-year-old woman receiving community care after hip replacement says she is fine and refuses breakfast, yet she grimaces during transfers. Her culture values quiet endurance. She lives alone and her family visits weekly. The nurse needs to assess her pain. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Accept her calm statement and record no pain, since her smile shows comfort.", "B": "Ask her family to rate her pain and use their score.", "C": "Give analgesic routinely without assessment, since stoic clients tend to under report pain.", "D": "Ask specific questions about discomfort with activity, use a pain scale, and observe her behaviour."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing a calm expression means low pain", "B": "Using a proxy score in place of the client's own report", "C": "Giving medication without assessing the pain"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask specific questions about discomfort with activity, use a pain scale, and observe her behaviour.): A stoic presentation can hide pain, so the nurse asks directly about activity-related discomfort, uses a tool, and observes grimacing and appetite changes. Her smile does not mean she is comfortable. Routine dosing without assessment bypasses clinical judgment. A family rating cannot replace her own report."}, {"id": 647, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Advanced cancer brings a 62-year-old woman to a palliative unit, where she refuses prescribed morphine because she fears becoming addicted. Her pain is 7 out of 10 and she cries when moved. The nurse wants to address her concern. Which response is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Tell her the physician decides and she should not worry about the medication effects.", "B": "Addiction is likely but unimportant at the end of life, so she should accept it.", "C": "Agree to withhold morphine for now and offer only a non-opioid until she is ready.", "D": "Opioids taken for cancer pain with monitoring rarely cause addiction."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismissing her worry rather than answering it", "B": "Confirming a myth and minimizing her concern", "C": "Withholding effective analgesia because of a common myth"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Opioids taken for cancer pain with monitoring rarely cause addiction.): Fear of addiction is a common myth, and when opioids are taken for cancer pain under monitoring, addiction is uncommon while uncontrolled pain reduces comfort and function. Calling addiction likely is inaccurate. Holding back morphine leaves severe pain untreated, and brushing off her fear fails to teach her."}, {"id": 648, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Preparing to take Mr. Rashid's vital signs and listen to his chest, the nurse notices a stethoscope hanging on the supply cart that serves the whole hallway. The cart is used for other clients too. What is the best action for the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Keep a stethoscope and other noncritical equipment in his room for his use only.", "B": "Carry his stethoscope to other rooms but clean it only when it looks soiled.", "C": "Use the cart stethoscope and wear gloves, since gloves prevent contamination of equipment.", "D": "Use the cart stethoscope and clean it with an alcohol wipe at shift end."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Cleaning only when visibly soiled after use on an infected client", "C": "Relying on gloves alone to protect shared equipment", "D": "Sharing equipment and cleaning only at shift end"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C080", "cluster_text": "Mr. Rashid, a 70-year-old man on a medical unit, has diarrhea from Clostridioides difficile infection and is also colonized with vancomycin-resistant enterococci. He is in a private room on contact precautions with a dedicated toilet. The practical nurse is planning his care for the shift, including equipment use, room cleaning, and handling of soiled linen, and works with housekeeping staff.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep a stethoscope and other noncritical equipment in his room for his use only.): Dedicated equipment stays in the room, or shared items are disinfected after each use, to prevent transfer of resistant organisms. Cleaning at shift end leaves the stethoscope contaminated between clients. Gloves do not stop equipment from carrying organisms once removed. Visible soil is not required for contamination."}, {"id": 649, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Only one blood pressure cuff is shared on the unit, and it is needed for another client in the next room. The nurse has just used it for Mr. Rashid, and the unit policy lists the cleaning steps. When should the cuff be disinfected?", "options": {"A": "Disinfect it at the end of the shift with the rest of the unit's equipment.", "B": "Disinfect it only when blood or body fluid is visible on the cuff.", "C": "Clean and disinfect it right after use on him, before anyone else uses it.", "D": "Rinse it with water and let it dry before the next client."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting until shift end, leaving organisms for the next client", "B": "Disinfecting only when visibly soiled", "D": "Rinsing with water, which does not disinfect"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C080", "cluster_text": "Mr. Rashid, a 70-year-old man on a medical unit, has diarrhea from Clostridioides difficile infection and is also colonized with vancomycin-resistant enterococci. He is in a private room on contact precautions with a dedicated toilet. The practical nurse is planning his care for the shift, including equipment use, room cleaning, and handling of soiled linen, and works with housekeeping staff.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Clean and disinfect it right after use on him, before anyone else uses it.): Shared equipment is disinfected between clients, and this is vital after use with a client on contact precautions. Waiting for shift end exposes the next client. Organisms can be on a cuff with no visible soiling, and water alone does not kill them."}, {"id": 650, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Housekeeping asks which product to use for Mr. Rashid's room, where he has stayed for two days. The unit stocks a quaternary ammonium cleaner and a sporicidal bleach-based product, and his diarrhea continues. Which product should be used for cleaning the room, and why?", "options": {"A": "Alcohol-based hand rub sprayed on surfaces, because it kills spores quickly on contact.", "B": "The sporicidal bleach product, because C. difficile spores resist quaternary cleaners.", "C": "Plain soap and water only, because it avoids spreading spores through chemical spray.", "D": "The quaternary ammonium cleaner, because it is the routine disinfectant for contact precaution rooms."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming alcohol kills C. difficile spores", "C": "Cleaning without any sporicidal action", "D": "Using a routine disinfectant that does not kill spores"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C080", "cluster_text": "Mr. Rashid, a 70-year-old man on a medical unit, has diarrhea from Clostridioides difficile infection and is also colonized with vancomycin-resistant enterococci. He is in a private room on contact precautions with a dedicated toilet. The practical nurse is planning his care for the shift, including equipment use, room cleaning, and handling of soiled linen, and works with housekeeping staff.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The sporicidal bleach product, because C. difficile spores resist quaternary cleaners.): C. difficile forms spores that survive routine quaternary ammonium products, so a sporicidal agent is chosen for the room and shared surfaces. Alcohol-based rub does not kill spores either. Soap and water remove some organisms mechanically but do not act as a sporicide for environmental surfaces."}, {"id": 651, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Soiled with loose stool, Mr. Rashid's bed linen needs changing. As the nurse strips the bed, a colleague begins shaking the sheets to loosen debris before bagging them. How should the nurse handle the linen to limit spread of organisms and protect staff?", "options": {"A": "Shake it gently over the hamper so the debris falls out first.", "B": "Rinse the soiled areas in the room's sink before bagging it.", "C": "Roll soiled sides inward, hold the bundle away from the uniform, and bag it there.", "D": "Carry it to the utility room in the arms and bag it there."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Shaking linen, which spreads organisms into the air and onto surfaces", "B": "Rinsing in a sink, creating splashes and a contaminated sink", "D": "Carrying open linen, contaminating the uniform and the hallway"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C080", "cluster_text": "Mr. Rashid, a 70-year-old man on a medical unit, has diarrhea from Clostridioides difficile infection and is also colonized with vancomycin-resistant enterococci. He is in a private room on contact precautions with a dedicated toilet. The practical nurse is planning his care for the shift, including equipment use, room cleaning, and handling of soiled linen, and works with housekeeping staff.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Roll soiled sides inward, hold the bundle away from the uniform, and bag it there.): Rolling the soiled side in and holding it away from the body contains organisms, and bagging at the point of use keeps them in the room. Shaking releases spores into the air. Carrying open linen contaminates clothing and the hallway, and rinsing in a sink spreads contamination to another surface."}, {"id": 652, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Planning a visit to an Indigenous family in which a grandmother is raising two grandchildren on a limited income, a community health nurse wants to use a strengths-based approach. The family has been enrolled in the program for one year and trusts the nurse. Which action reflects that approach?", "options": {"A": "Plan care around the grandmother's income limits and arrange the cheapest programs first.", "B": "Begin by asking what is going well, and build the plan on supports they name.", "C": "Offer programs the agency already runs and ask which one she prefers.", "D": "Ask the grandmother to list her concerns, then set goals the nurse thinks realistic."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Frames the family by income limits", "C": "Offering only agency programs rather than the family's own supports", "D": "Lets the nurse override the family's own goals"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Begin by asking what is going well, and build the plan on supports they name.): A strengths-based approach starts from the family's own resources, goals, and supports and builds the plan with them. Starting with income limits frames them by scarcity, and goals chosen by the nurse override their priorities. A fixed list of agency programs leaves little room for their own supports and respects neither their knowledge nor their culture."}, {"id": 653, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Night sweats, weight loss, and a productive chronic cough bring a 58-year-old man to clinic with suspected tuberculosis, and the nurse is collecting a sputum sample. The client wants to rinse his mouth first with mouthwash. What is the correct instruction for collection?", "options": {"A": "Rinse with mouthwash first to clean the mouth, then spit saliva into the container.", "B": "Collect an early morning deep cough sample after rinsing with water only, avoiding saliva.", "C": "Collect the sample in the afternoon after a meal when cough is most productive.", "D": "Use a cotton swab to collect secretions from the back of the throat."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using mouthwash, which can kill organisms and affect results", "C": "Collecting after meals and at a time of day when sputum is thin", "D": "Swabbing the throat instead of obtaining deep sputum"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C079", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a community clinic and an emergency department, collecting specimens for four clients: a 58-year-old man with a chronic cough and suspected tuberculosis, a 9-year-old with a sore throat, a 70-year-old with a draining leg wound, and a 35-year-old with cough and fever. Specimens go to the laboratory using standard precautions, and correct technique matters to the accuracy of each result.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Collect an early morning deep cough sample after rinsing with water only, avoiding saliva.): An early morning sample after rinsing with water gives the most concentrated deep sputum, and mouthwash could kill organisms and distort the result. Saliva is not a valid specimen. Afternoon collection yields thinner sputum, and a throat swab does not sample the lower airways."}, {"id": 654, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Gagging and spitting, a 9-year-old with a sore throat pulls away as the nurse approaches with a swab, and the swab touches the tongue and cheek before reaching the throat. The nurse suspects contamination of the specimen. What should the nurse do next?", "options": {"A": "Use a new swab and sample the tonsils and posterior pharynx, avoiding the tongue.", "B": "Send the swab as collected and label it, since the posterior pharynx was reached.", "C": "Ask the child to gargle, then use the same swab on the tonsils.", "D": "Rinse the swab with sterile saline, then redo the collection with it."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Sending a contaminated specimen that gives unreliable results", "C": "Reusing a contaminated swab after a gargle", "D": "Reusing a swab after rinsing, which does not make it clean"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C079", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a community clinic and an emergency department, collecting specimens for four clients: a 58-year-old man with a chronic cough and suspected tuberculosis, a 9-year-old with a sore throat, a 70-year-old with a draining leg wound, and a 35-year-old with cough and fever. Specimens go to the laboratory using standard precautions, and correct technique matters to the accuracy of each result.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a new swab and sample the tonsils and posterior pharynx, avoiding the tongue.): A swab that touched the tongue and cheeks is contaminated with normal oral flora, so a fresh swab is needed and the tonsils and posterior pharynx are sampled without touching other tissue. Sending it as is risks a false result. Rinsing or reusing the old swab does not restore it."}, {"id": 655, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Yellow-green pus covers the surface of a 70-year-old's draining leg wound, and a colleague says to swab the visible pus for the best yield. The wound also has exudate and slough, and the client reports mild pain. How should the nurse proceed with the culture?", "options": {"A": "Cleanse with an antiseptic, then swab the wound edge and surrounding intact skin.", "B": "Cleanse with saline, then rotate the swab over healthy tissue in the bed.", "C": "Collect a sample from the soiled dressing, since it holds wound drainage.", "D": "Swab the pus on the surface first, since it contains the most infecting organisms."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using antiseptic, which may kill the organisms to be cultured", "C": "Sampling the dressing instead of the wound tissue", "D": "Sampling surface pus that holds colonizing organisms"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C079", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a community clinic and an emergency department, collecting specimens for four clients: a 58-year-old man with a chronic cough and suspected tuberculosis, a 9-year-old with a sore throat, a 70-year-old with a draining leg wound, and a 35-year-old with cough and fever. Specimens go to the laboratory using standard precautions, and correct technique matters to the accuracy of each result.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cleanse with saline, then rotate the swab over healthy tissue in the bed.): Surface exudate carries colonizing organisms, so the wound is cleansed with saline and the swab is rotated over healthy tissue in the bed where infection is active. Antiseptic can kill the organisms and skew the culture. Dressings and skin edges do not represent the infection."}, {"id": 656, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 35-year-old with cough and fever needs a nasopharyngeal swab in the emergency department. The nurse inserts the swab upward toward the nasal bridge at triage, and the client winces as the swab meets resistance. What should the nurse do next?", "options": {"A": "Switch to the other nostril and again angle the swab upward.", "B": "Push firmly past the resistance, since discomfort is brief.", "C": "Stop and collect a sample from the front of the nostril.", "D": "Withdraw slightly, redirect the swab straight back along the nasal floor, and continue."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Repeating the upward angle that caused the resistance", "B": "Forcing the swab, which risks injury and bleeding", "C": "Collecting an anterior sample that is not nasopharyngeal"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C079", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a community clinic and an emergency department, collecting specimens for four clients: a 58-year-old man with a chronic cough and suspected tuberculosis, a 9-year-old with a sore throat, a 70-year-old with a draining leg wound, and a 35-year-old with cough and fever. Specimens go to the laboratory using standard precautions, and correct technique matters to the accuracy of each result.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withdraw slightly, redirect the swab straight back along the nasal floor, and continue.): The nasopharynx is reached by passing the swab straight back along the floor of the nose, parallel to the palate, and an upward angle meets the turbinates. Forcing it through resistance can injure tissue. Repeating the same upward angle in the other nostril repeats the problem, and an anterior sample is a different specimen."}, {"id": 657, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Glass is embedded in the sole of a 40-year-old man's foot after he stepped on it at home. It is bleeding lightly, and he asks the clinic nurse to pull it out for him. His tetanus status is unknown. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Remove the glass with sterile forceps, then apply pressure and cover the wound.", "B": "Irrigate the wound vigorously to wash the glass out, then apply a dressing.", "C": "Apply a tourniquet above the ankle and remove the glass after bleeding stops.", "D": "Leave the glass in place, pad around it with bulky dressings, and arrange assessment."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Removing an embedded object, which can increase bleeding or damage", "B": "Irrigating forcefully, which can push the object deeper", "C": "Using a tourniquet, which risks tissue damage and is not indicated"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Leave the glass in place, pad around it with bulky dressings, and arrange assessment.): An embedded object may be plugging a vessel, so it is left in place and padded around while a physician assesses it. Removing it can worsen bleeding or injure deeper tissue. Forceful irrigation can drive it deeper, and a tourniquet is not appropriate for light bleeding."}, {"id": 658, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Lying flat while watching television, a 64-year-old hospitalized man with asthma suddenly becomes anxious and has audible wheeze, speaks in three-word phrases, and has a respiratory rate of 28 and oxygen saturation of 89 percent. What is the nurse's first action?", "options": {"A": "Leave to find the RN and return with the emergency cart and chart.", "B": "Keep him lying flat to rest his breathing and recheck saturation in ten minutes.", "C": "Sit him upright, stay with him, and apply ordered oxygen while calling the RN.", "D": "Give him a drink of water to ease the wheeze and cough."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaving a client in distress to collect equipment", "B": "Leaving him flat, which worsens breathing effort", "D": "Offering fluids to a client who is struggling to breathe"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C082", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on a hospital unit and the adjoining long-term care dining room during an evening shift. Over two hours she responds to four clients with breathing problems: an adult with wheeze, a resident with bluish lips, and two residents who begin coughing and gasping while eating. Emergency equipment is on the unit and the RN is nearby.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sit him upright, stay with him, and apply ordered oxygen while calling the RN.): Sitting upright eases breathing, and the nurse stays, applies ordered oxygen, and summons help without leaving him alone. Lying flat increases the work of breathing. Walking away delays care, and giving fluids risks aspiration in a client who is short of breath."}, {"id": 659, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Bluish lips, a respiratory rate of 30, and an oxygen saturation of 86 percent are found in a long-term care resident. A colleague suggests waiting to see whether the blue color spreads to her fingers before calling for help. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Let her rest and recheck saturation at routine rounds.", "B": "Wait for the blue color to spread before escalating to the RN.", "C": "Obtain a full set of vital signs first and report afterward.", "D": "Raise the head of the bed, apply ordered oxygen, and report to the RN now."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying reassessment to routine rounds", "B": "Waiting for a late sign to confirm hypoxia", "C": "Delaying oxygen to complete other measurements"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C082", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on a hospital unit and the adjoining long-term care dining room during an evening shift. Over two hours she responds to four clients with breathing problems: an adult with wheeze, a resident with bluish lips, and two residents who begin coughing and gasping while eating. Emergency equipment is on the unit and the RN is nearby.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Raise the head of the bed, apply ordered oxygen, and report to the RN now.): Cyanosis is a late sign of hypoxia, and a saturation of 86 percent with fast breathing needs immediate oxygen and escalation. Waiting for more color change allows further decline. Routine rounds are too late, and gathering a full set of vital signs should not delay oxygen."}, {"id": 660, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After a bite of meat in the dining room, a 76-year-old resident seated upright coughs forcefully. She is pink, speaks between coughs, and clutches her throat while staff members rush toward her with their hands out. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Sweep a finger into her mouth to remove the meat while she coughs.", "B": "Encourage coughing, stay beside her, and watch for any loss of cough.", "C": "Start abdominal thrusts immediately because any choking with a hand on the throat needs thrusts.", "D": "Give five back blows between the shoulder blades before checking whether she can speak."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Blind finger sweep, which can push the food deeper", "C": "Giving thrusts to a client with an effective cough", "D": "Using back blows without first judging severity"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C082", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on a hospital unit and the adjoining long-term care dining room during an evening shift. Over two hours she responds to four clients with breathing problems: an adult with wheeze, a resident with bluish lips, and two residents who begin coughing and gasping while eating. Emergency equipment is on the unit and the RN is nearby.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Encourage coughing, stay beside her, and watch for any loss of cough.): A forceful cough and the ability to speak signal a mild obstruction, and the best help is to encourage coughing while watching closely. Thrusts and back blows are reserved for ineffective coughing. A blind finger sweep can push the meat further down."}, {"id": 661, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Moments later another resident, aged 80, stands grasping her throat. She cannot speak or cough, and her face is turning dusky. The nurse is directly behind her in the dining room with the RN still across the unit. What action should the nurse take?", "options": {"A": "Give back slaps between the shoulder blades only, repeating until it clears.", "B": "Give abdominal thrusts above the navel with a fist until the object comes out.", "C": "Wait for the RN to arrive before beginning any technique to avoid injuring her.", "D": "Encourage her to cough and sip water to wash the object down."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using back slaps alone when thrusts are the standard", "C": "Delaying treatment of a complete obstruction", "D": "Offering water to a client who is unable to swallow or breathe"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C082", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on a hospital unit and the adjoining long-term care dining room during an evening shift. Over two hours she responds to four clients with breathing problems: an adult with wheeze, a resident with bluish lips, and two residents who begin coughing and gasping while eating. Emergency equipment is on the unit and the RN is nearby.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give abdominal thrusts above the navel with a fist until the object comes out.): A client who cannot speak or cough has a severe obstruction, and abdominal thrusts are started right away. Back slaps alone are not the standard for an adult. Waiting for the RN delays care that cannot wait, and water cannot pass an airway that is blocked."}, {"id": 662, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Hallett, 79, has advanced dementia and cannot understand a proposed feeding tube. He left no written wishes, and his two daughters disagree about what he would want. His son is visiting from out of town this week. Which approach by the nurse best respects the best interests standard?", "options": {"A": "Involve the legal substitute decision maker, who weighs his known values and likely benefit", "B": "Delay every decision until Mr. Hallett regains capacity, even though his intake is falling daily", "C": "Follow the physician's preference, since the disagreement means no one else can support a decision", "D": "Accept the choice of whichever daughter is at the bedside because she speaks for the family"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Waiting for capacity that is not expected to return, which harms the client", "C": "Letting one professional decide when a substitute decision maker should be engaged", "D": "Treating any relative's preference as a valid, ranked substitute decision"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Involve the legal substitute decision maker, who weighs his known values and likely benefit): Best interests means the substitute decision maker weighs the client's known values, beliefs and the likely benefit and burden of treatment. Choosing the daughter at the bedside ignores the ranking of decision makers and the other family members. Leaving the decision to the physician bypasses the substitute decision maker's role, and waiting for capacity that will not return exposes him to harm. Practical nurses support the process and report conflicts to the RN."}, {"id": 663, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two minutes after a vaginal birth, a healthy term newborn is crying and feels slightly cool. The delivery room is warm, the newborn's breathing is regular, and the mother is alert and wants to hold her baby. What is the best nursing action to help the newborn keep warm?", "options": {"A": "Place a hat on the newborn and keep him in the bassinet beside the mother", "B": "Wrap the newborn tightly in a blanket and place him alone under the radiant warmer", "C": "Give the newborn a warm bath right away to raise body temperature before mother holds him", "D": "Dry the newborn, place him bare on the mother's chest, and cover both with a blanket"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Separating the pair and leaving the trunk without direct warmth", "B": "Choosing a warmer over skin contact for a stable newborn", "C": "Bathing too early, which causes heat loss from evaporation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dry the newborn, place him bare on the mother's chest, and cover both with a blanket): Skin to skin contact on the mother's chest is the best way to keep a stable term newborn warm because the mother's body regulates heat and it supports breastfeeding. Wrapping the baby and using the warmer separates the pair without need. An early bath wastes heat through evaporation, and a hat alone in a bassinet leaves the trunk exposed. Dry the newborn first to reduce heat loss."}, {"id": 664, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Okafor, 58, performs peritoneal dialysis at home. During a visit she reports that her last drained fluid looked cloudy and her abdomen feels mildly tender. Her temperature is 36.9 C and she otherwise feels fine. She asks if she can continue her exchanges tonight. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Ask her to complete her usual exchange and show the fluid to the nurse tomorrow", "B": "Reassure her that cloudy fluid is harmless without a fever and that she can continue tonight", "C": "Advise her to hold exchanges and report the cloudy fluid promptly to the RN or physician", "D": "Teach her to lengthen the next dwell time so the cloudiness clears before the following drain"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying reporting by waiting for the next visit", "B": "Dismissing cloudy effluent because there is no fever", "D": "Prolonging dwell time instead of reporting a possible infection"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise her to hold exchanges and report the cloudy fluid promptly to the RN or physician): Cloudy effluent with abdominal tenderness suggests peritonitis, which can occur without fever, so the nurse reports it promptly and holds further exchanges until direction is given. Reassuring her because of the normal temperature misses that fever is not required. Extending dwell time does not treat infection, and waiting until tomorrow delays care. Practical nurses report and document, and they do not decide treatment."}, {"id": 665, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Pain in his left calf has bothered a 66-year-old man since knee surgery yesterday. The nurse finds the left foot pulse faint and the foot cool, and is about to document that pulses are present. What is the best next action?", "options": {"A": "Massage the sore calf to improve blood flow and recheck the pulse in a few hours", "B": "Elevate the left leg and apply a warm pack before reassessing later.", "C": "Compare both feet for pulse and temperature, rate each, and report the difference", "D": "Ask him to flex the ankle and walk, then record pulses if they improve."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Massaging a painful calf, which risks dislodging a clot", "B": "Applies heat to a possibly ischemic limb", "D": "Walks a client with a possible clot"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Compare both feet for pulse and temperature, rate each, and report the difference): Pulses are compared side to side and rated on a consistent scale, because a faint, cool limb after surgery may signal reduced arterial flow or a clot and needs prompt reporting. Heat on a limb with poor flow can worsen injury, and walking him with a possible clot risks dislodging it. Massaging a painful calf may also dislodge a clot."}, {"id": 666, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Pereira, 60, attends the clinic and asks for her lorazepam to be renewed because it calms her nerves. She has taken it daily for eight months and recently noticed forgetfulness and one near fall at home. Which response by the nurse best supports safe care?", "options": {"A": "Share her memory and fall concerns with the prescriber and discuss anxiety alternatives", "B": "Suggest she take an extra tablet at bedtime on nights when she feels most worried", "C": "Advise her to stop lorazepam today because the forgetfulness shows she has become dependent", "D": "Tell her to continue the daily dose for comfort since it has helped her anxiety"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Increasing the dose on her own, which raises sedation and fall risk", "C": "Stopping a benzodiazepine abruptly, risking withdrawal", "D": "Continuing long term use despite adverse effects"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C076", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a community mental health program linked to an inpatient unit. Over one week she cares for several clients who have anxiety-related and trauma-related conditions. She works with an RN lead and a psychiatrist, follows a recovery-focused and nonjudgmental approach, documents her observations in objective terms, and reports changes in client condition to the RN promptly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Share her memory and fall concerns with the prescriber and discuss anxiety alternatives): Long term benzodiazepine use raises risks of memory problems and falls, so the nurse reports these findings and supports a prescriber-led plan, which usually involves gradual tapering and non-drug coping skills. Continuing indefinitely for comfort ignores the harm she has already noticed. Stopping suddenly can cause withdrawal including seizures, and taking extra tablets increases sedation. The nurse does not change doses independently."}, {"id": 667, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Tran, 31, on the inpatient unit washes his hands for about 40 minutes after meals and says he cannot stop or something terrible will happen. His skin is cracked and bleeding. The nurse is assigned his care today. Which nursing approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Tell him firmly that the handwashing is unreasonable and that he must reduce it by tomorrow", "B": "Acknowledge his distress, protect his skin, and support gradual limits within the team's care plan", "C": "Offer a hand cream and ask him to wash only after meals as a simple rule.", "D": "Tell him the rituals will stop once he understands the germs are not harmful."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using criticism and demands that increase anxiety", "C": "Imposes a rule without a team plan", "D": "Relies on reassurance to stop a compulsion"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C076", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a community mental health program linked to an inpatient unit. Over one week she cares for several clients who have anxiety-related and trauma-related conditions. She works with an RN lead and a psychiatrist, follows a recovery-focused and nonjudgmental approach, documents her observations in objective terms, and reports changes in client condition to the RN promptly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acknowledge his distress, protect his skin, and support gradual limits within the team's care plan): Compulsions ease anxiety briefly, so the nurse stays nonjudgmental, treats the skin injury, and follows a team plan that reduces rituals gradually with therapy. A rule set alone, or an explanation about germs, does not address the compulsion. Criticizing him or setting a deadline treats the symptom as a choice."}, {"id": 668, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two weeks after an assault, Ms. Dubois, 40, comes to the clinic reporting nightmares, a startle response to loud noises, and avoidance of the street where it happened. She looks tense but sits quietly. How should the nurse respond to support her?", "options": {"A": "Tell her the nightmares should settle soon and move on to the remaining questions", "B": "Ask what happened in order, then explain that talking soon prevents lasting problems.", "C": "Offer a private space, let her choose how much to share, and explain supports", "D": "Suggest she avoid the street and loud places until the symptoms settle on their own."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Minimizing symptoms and closing the conversation", "B": "Presses for the story with a promise", "D": "Encourages avoidance that feeds fear"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C076", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a community mental health program linked to an inpatient unit. Over one week she cares for several clients who have anxiety-related and trauma-related conditions. She works with an RN lead and a psychiatrist, follows a recovery-focused and nonjudgmental approach, documents her observations in objective terms, and reports changes in client condition to the RN promptly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Offer a private space, let her choose how much to share, and explain supports): Trauma survivors do best when they control the pace of disclosure, so the nurse offers privacy, choice, and information about supports. Pressing for the story soon, even with kind reasons, takes away her control. Suggesting avoidance feeds fear and keeps her from regaining confidence. Promising symptoms will settle dismisses her experience."}, {"id": 669, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Singh, 34, has a history of childhood abuse and needs a routine clinic assessment that involves touching her abdomen. She becomes quiet and still when the nurse approaches with the stethoscope. Her vital signs are stable and she is not in pain today. What action best reflects trauma-informed care?", "options": {"A": "Perform the exam with a chaperone present but without explaining each step to avoid anxiety.", "B": "Explain each step, ask permission before touching, and say she may pause or stop", "C": "Skip the exam for now because of her history of abuse and chart that she declined", "D": "Ask her to describe her abuse history first, then begin touching if she stays calm."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses a chaperone in place of explanation and consent", "C": "Removing needed care instead of adjusting the approach", "D": "Demands disclosure and treats calm as consent"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C076", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a community mental health program linked to an inpatient unit. Over one week she cares for several clients who have anxiety-related and trauma-related conditions. She works with an RN lead and a psychiatrist, follows a recovery-focused and nonjudgmental approach, documents her observations in objective terms, and reports changes in client condition to the RN promptly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain each step, ask permission before touching, and say she may pause or stop): Trauma-informed care emphasizes safety, choice, transparency, and control, so the nurse explains each step, asks before touching, and allows her to pause. Starting without explanation, even with a chaperone, removes her control. Asking for her abuse story first pressures disclosure, and staying calm is not consent. Skipping necessary assessment denies her care when adjustments would work."}, {"id": 670, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a pediatric ward the order reads acetaminophen 15 mg/kg for a child weighing 15 kg. The suspension available is 160 mg per 5 mL, but the pharmacy label lists a 500 mg adult dose. The child is febrile and uncomfortable. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Hold the dose, recalculate 225 mg by weight, and have the pharmacy correct the label.", "B": "Give 225 mg now and request a new pharmacy label only if the child worsens.", "C": "Give 15 mL of suspension, calculated as 15 mg/kg, then fix the label.", "D": "Ask the parent to confirm the usual home dose and give that amount."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Giving the right dose but leaving a labelling error unresolved", "C": "Confuses milligrams with millilitres", "D": "Relies on a parent's recall over the order"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold the dose, recalculate 225 mg by weight, and have the pharmacy correct the label.): Weight based dosing gives 15 mg/kg times 15 kg, which is 225 mg, far below the label's 500 mg, so the nurse stops and clarifies before giving anything. Giving 15 mL confuses milligrams with millilitres and delivers about 480 mg. A parent's usual dose ignores the order and the weight, and giving the correct amount while leaving the label wrong keeps the hazard for the next nurse."}, {"id": 671, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Larsen, 70, is admitted with shortness of breath and ankle swelling, and heart failure is suspected. His weight matches last week's. Sitting him at 45 degrees, the nurse inspects his neck. He has no fever and no known chest pain today. Which finding most supports fluid overload?", "options": {"A": "A dry cough that improves when he lies flat on the bed, with clear lung sounds.", "B": "Visible jugular vein distension above the clavicle with the head of the bed at 45 degrees", "C": "A blood pressure of 128/78 mmHg, which shows the heart is pumping enough.", "D": "A stable weight compared with last week, which shows that fluid has not been retained"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reverses the usual orthopnea pattern", "C": "Reads a normal blood pressure as ruling out overload", "D": "Relying on weight alone to judge fluid status"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Visible jugular vein distension above the clavicle with the head of the bed at 45 degrees): Jugular vein distension at 45 degrees reflects raised right-sided venous pressure and supports fluid overload, particularly with edema and breathlessness. A stable weight does not rule out fluid shifts. A normal blood pressure does not exclude heart failure, and a cough that eases lying flat does not fit congestion, which worsens flat. The nurse reports the finding to the RN."}, {"id": 672, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 55-year-old man on a medical unit received an as-needed analgesic one hour ago and now says he feels better. His pain was 7 out of 10 before the dose and is 3 out of 10 now. Which entry best documents the pain care?", "options": {"A": "Client comfortable after pain medication and resting quietly in bed with his eyes closed", "B": "Pain 7 of 10 before, 3 of 10 after, drug and dose given, client reports relief", "C": "Pain 3 of 10 at 1000 after medication, client resting, nurse will recheck in an hour.", "D": "Analgesic given, pain reduced by half, client satisfied with the result of the treatment."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Charting comfort without a pain score", "C": "Records an after score without baseline or drug", "D": "Uses an estimate instead of scores and drug details"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pain 7 of 10 before, 3 of 10 after, drug and dose given, client reports relief): Good pain documentation includes a before score, the drug and dose, the time, and an after score showing the response. Describing him as comfortable gives no measurable data. An after score alone omits the baseline and the drug, and a phrase such as reduced by half is an estimate with no score or drug named."}, {"id": 673, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Kowalski, 62, has a potassium level of 3.0 mmol/L and an order for replacement. A vial of concentrated potassium chloride sits in the unit medication room beside saline vials because it is easy to reach. What action best prevents harm?", "options": {"A": "Dilute it in a saline bag at the bedside and run it quickly.", "B": "Keep the vial in place and label it clearly so nurses can dilute it quickly", "C": "Remove it from general stock and use premixed pharmacy solution with a double check", "D": "Store it in a locked cabinet in the same room and dilute it when ordered."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dilutes a high-alert drug at the bedside", "B": "Leaving a high-alert drug in general stock", "D": "Keeps concentrate on the unit and mixes it there"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C073", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, practical nurses and registered nurses review medication safety after a near miss involving a similar-sounding drug. The unit uses written and electronic orders, pharmacy supplies stock for several clients, and policy requires independent checks for high-alert drugs. Nurses are expected to speak up about unsafe orders and to follow regulatory standards for medication administration at all times.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Remove it from general stock and use premixed pharmacy solution with a double check): Concentrated potassium can cause fatal arrhythmia if given undiluted, so it is removed from general stock and supplied premixed by pharmacy with an independent double check. Labelling the vial alone leaves it within reach. Bedside dilution depends on one nurse's calculation and stays unsafe, and a locked cabinet on the unit still leaves concentrate available for mixing errors."}, {"id": 674, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "One order for an adult client reads 'Insulin 10 U daily' and another reads 'MS 5 mg q4h'. The unit posts a list of error-prone abbreviations, and the prescriber can be reached by phone. Which action best supports safe medication practice before either drug is given?", "options": {"A": "Give insulin per the posted list and hold MS until pharmacy answers.", "B": "Check the electronic record for the usual dose and give that drug.", "C": "Give insulin as ordered since units are accepted, and assume MS means morphine", "D": "Clarify both orders with the prescriber and request drug names written out"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives a drug ordered with an unsafe abbreviation", "B": "Infers the drug from past records", "C": "Assuming accepted-looking abbreviations are safe"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C073", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, practical nurses and registered nurses review medication safety after a near miss involving a similar-sounding drug. The unit uses written and electronic orders, pharmacy supplies stock for several clients, and policy requires independent checks for high-alert drugs. Nurses are expected to speak up about unsafe orders and to follow regulatory standards for medication administration at all times.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Clarify both orders with the prescriber and request drug names written out): Abbreviations such as MS can mean morphine sulfate or magnesium sulfate, and a handwritten U can be read as zero, so unclear orders are clarified with the prescriber before administration. Giving insulin on an unclear U order is unsafe even while the morphine is held. Relying on the record's usual dose is a guess that can harm the client, and assuming MS means morphine carries the same risk."}, {"id": 675, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "The order set for a 3-year-old lists one drug as 5.0 mg and another as .5 mg. The nurse's unit policy covers how numbers are written. The child is waiting, and the prescriber can be reached. What is the safest response?", "options": {"A": "Give the doses as written because the prescriber has already made the decision about them", "B": "Give the doses after a second nurse agrees which decimal point looks clearer.", "C": "Write the doses again with the same zeros and give them once signed.", "D": "Clarify the orders, since safe practice omits trailing zeros and adds leading zeros"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Following unclear numbers without checking", "B": "Uses a colleague's opinion instead of the prescriber", "C": "Rewrites the order without authority"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C073", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, practical nurses and registered nurses review medication safety after a near miss involving a similar-sounding drug. The unit uses written and electronic orders, pharmacy supplies stock for several clients, and policy requires independent checks for high-alert drugs. Nurses are expected to speak up about unsafe orders and to follow regulatory standards for medication administration at all times.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Clarify the orders, since safe practice omits trailing zeros and adds leading zeros): A trailing zero can be missed and read as a tenfold larger dose, and a bare decimal can be read as 5 mg instead of 0.5 mg, so safe practice drops trailing zeros, uses leading zeros, and clarifies unclear orders with the prescriber. Following unclear numbers as written shifts risk to the child. A second nurse's opinion cannot replace the prescriber, and rewriting the order changes it without authority."}, {"id": 676, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Reaching into the drawer for an adult client's medication, a nurse finds two vials whose names start with the same four letters and look alike. The order is handwritten and slightly unclear. Which action best reduces the risk of a look-alike mix-up?", "options": {"A": "Ask a colleague which vial she usually picks and use the same one.", "B": "Read the full drug name against the order and label, and confirm the indication and dose", "C": "Compare only the strength on the label with the order and give that vial.", "D": "Choose the vial whose first letters match the order because those letters identify the drug"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relies on a colleague's habit", "C": "Matches strength alone, which can be shared by different drugs", "D": "Matching only the first letters of the name"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C073", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, practical nurses and registered nurses review medication safety after a near miss involving a similar-sounding drug. The unit uses written and electronic orders, pharmacy supplies stock for several clients, and policy requires independent checks for high-alert drugs. Nurses are expected to speak up about unsafe orders and to follow regulatory standards for medication administration at all times.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Read the full drug name against the order and label, and confirm the indication and dose): Look-alike and sound-alike names are a leading cause of errors, so the nurse reads the whole name against the order and label and confirms the indication and dose. Using the first letters is the exact error these names invite. A colleague's habit and a matching strength do not confirm identity, because different drugs can share a strength."}, {"id": 677, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "An unconscious 45-year-old man arrives in the emergency department after a collision. His only identifier is a wristband reading Unknown Male 12, and a blood transfusion is ordered. No family has arrived, and the police report lists no name yet. What best meets the requirement for right-client identification?", "options": {"A": "Wait until a family member arrives to state his name before verifying anything in the chart", "B": "Use the unique identifiers on the emergency band and confirm them with a second nurse", "C": "Use his hospital number from a prior admission, matched by age and appearance.", "D": "Use the trauma sheet name and bed label, with one nurse checking the transfusion."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying care when a policy-based identifier exists", "C": "Matches a prior record by appearance", "D": "Accepts a single check and a location label"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C073", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, practical nurses and registered nurses review medication safety after a near miss involving a similar-sounding drug. The unit uses written and electronic orders, pharmacy supplies stock for several clients, and policy requires independent checks for high-alert drugs. Nurses are expected to speak up about unsafe orders and to follow regulatory standards for medication administration at all times.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use the unique identifiers on the emergency band and confirm them with a second nurse): Room and bed numbers can change and are not valid identifiers, so the nurse uses the unique identifiers on the emergency band with an independent check as policy directs. Matching a prior record by age or appearance can link the wrong chart, and a single nurse's check is not an independent check. Waiting for family delays urgent care when an organizational process already exists."}, {"id": 678, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Bellamy, 86, lives in a long-term care home and has advanced dementia. His son shows the nurse a document naming him power of attorney for property and says he can now consent to a feeding tube. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Accept it for finances only and let the physician decide on the feeding tube.", "B": "Ask the son to sign the consent form and file it without further checks", "C": "Explain that it covers finances, and ask the RN to confirm who holds personal care authority", "D": "Ask the son to bring the document to the physician and proceed once it is reviewed."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Lets the physician decide in place of a substitute", "B": "Collecting a signature from someone without verified authority", "D": "Treats a physician's review as converting a property document"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain that it covers finances, and ask the RN to confirm who holds personal care authority): A power of attorney for property gives authority over finances only, while personal care decisions require a separate document or the legal substitute decision maker. Letting the physician decide alone skips the substitute decision making process, and a physician's review cannot turn a property document into health consent. Collecting a signature without verifying authority does not make consent valid. The nurse reports to the RN."}, {"id": 679, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Elder Nancy, 74, visits a community clinic and says her knee pain has worsened since her sister died, and she has stopped attending ceremonies she values. The pain assessment is complete. What care planning approach best reflects a holistic view of health?", "options": {"A": "Include her grief, spiritual connections and community supports in the plan along with knee treatment", "B": "Treat the knee pain with analgesics and suggest she return to ceremonies when ready.", "C": "Plan knee care, and ask her to find cultural support through her community on her own.", "D": "Refer her to a mental health program for grief and leave the knee until next appointment"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats the symptom and leaves grief unaddressed", "C": "Leaves spiritual support to the client alone", "D": "Treating the knee and grief as separate and postponing one"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Include her grief, spiritual connections and community supports in the plan along with knee treatment): Many Indigenous and holistic approaches see health as physical, emotional, mental, and spiritual well-being connected to community, so the nurse plans care that includes her grief and cultural connections along with pain treatment. Addressing only the knee misses contributors to her distress. Leaving cultural support to her own effort keeps it out of the plan, and postponing the knee splits her care unnecessarily."}, {"id": 680, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "In a long-term care home, a resident at high risk for pressure injury needs turning every two hours, and a possible stage 2 area has appeared on the sacrum. A personal support worker offers help. Which delegation is most appropriate for the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Delegate scheduled turning, and keep skin assessment and staging with the nurse", "B": "Delegate repositioning only if the support worker records the exact stage of any redness found", "C": "Delegate both repositioning and the staging of the sacral area to save the nurse's time", "D": "Keep all repositioning with the nurse because the support worker may harm skin during turns"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Making staging part of the delegated task", "C": "Delegating a skin assessment beyond the role", "D": "Refusing to delegate a routine, safe task"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Delegate scheduled turning, and keep skin assessment and staging with the nurse): Turning and repositioning is a routine task suited to delegation with clear instructions, while assessing and staging skin is a nurse responsibility. Delegating staging goes beyond the support worker's role. Asking the worker to record a stage has the same problem, and keeping every turn with the nurse is unnecessary and can leave the schedule unmet when other duties arise."}, {"id": 681, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Repeated seizures have occurred in a 38-year-old hospital client. After a first generalized seizure stopped at one minute, he began seizing again ten minutes later and has not regained awareness between episodes. His airway is open. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Restrain his arms and legs gently to prevent injury until the seizures stop.", "B": "Call for emergency help and the RN, protect his head and airway, and prepare oxygen", "C": "Observe him between episodes and record the length of each separate seizure", "D": "Call the RN after the next episode ends, so that she can report both together."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Restrains a client during a seizure", "C": "Observing a possible status epilepticus", "D": "Delays calling for help until the next episode ends"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Call for emergency help and the RN, protect his head and airway, and prepare oxygen): Repeated seizures without recovery of awareness suggest status epilepticus, a medical emergency, so the nurse summons help at once and protects airway, head, and oxygenation. Observing between episodes delays treatment. Restraining limbs can cause injury and does not stop a seizure, and calling after the next episode wastes time when brain injury risk grows every minute."}, {"id": 682, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit to a 3-year-old with fever, the mother says she gave acetaminophen four hours ago and wants to give another dose now, plus a cold remedy. She is unsure whether the cold product also contains acetaminophen. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Estimate the dose by the child's age on the box and skip weight.", "B": "Give both products together since the cold remedy helps different symptoms", "C": "Give the cold remedy first, then give acetaminophen if the fever has not dropped.", "D": "Check the cold product label for acetaminophen and keep to the daily maximum"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses age rather than weight to dose", "B": "Combining products with hidden acetaminophen", "C": "Skips checking the cold product for acetaminophen"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Check the cold product label for acetaminophen and keep to the daily maximum): Acetaminophen is given at the labelled interval and kept within a daily maximum for the child's weight, and many cold products contain it, so checking labels prevents accidental overdose. Estimating from age alone ignores weight-based dosing. Giving the cold product first without reading ingredients can duplicate acetaminophen. Combining products without reading ingredients also risks overdose, so the parent needs label-reading teaching."}, {"id": 683, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 6-year-old child has a nosebleed from one nostril while sitting in the school office, which has gloves, tissues, a cold pack and a cot. The child is calm, alert and not taking blood thinners. What should the nurse tell the teacher to do?", "options": {"A": "Pack the nostril tightly with tissue and keep the child walking to maintain circulation", "B": "Have the child sit upright leaning forward and pinch the soft nose for ten minutes", "C": "Lay the child flat on the cot and pinch the nose bridge until bleeding eases", "D": "Tilt the child's head back and hold a cold cloth on the forehead until bleeding stops"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Packing tightly and keeping the child active", "C": "Lying flat and pinching the bony bridge", "D": "Tilting the head back so blood is swallowed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Have the child sit upright leaning forward and pinch the soft nose for ten minutes): Leaning forward keeps blood from running down the throat, and pressure on the soft part of the nose over the bleeding vessels for about ten minutes controls most nosebleeds. Tilting the head back allows swallowing of blood and can cause vomiting. Lying flat or pinching the bony bridge does not compress the bleeding site, and tight packing with activity can restart bleeding."}, {"id": 684, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Coffee ground emesis brings an adult client to the emergency department. He is pale, with a heart rate of 112 beats per minute and a blood pressure of 96/60 mmHg. He asks for water to settle his stomach. What is the priority nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Offer ice chips only, and recheck vital signs in one hour.", "B": "Place him flat and ask him to rest quietly until the next scheduled vital signs", "C": "Prepare him for discharge once the bleeding eases and arrange outpatient follow-up.", "D": "Keep him nothing by mouth, monitor vital signs, and report promptly"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives oral intake and delays reassessment", "B": "Resting him without monitoring or reporting", "C": "Plans discharge despite shock signs"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep him nothing by mouth, monitor vital signs, and report promptly): Coffee ground emesis with tachycardia and low blood pressure suggests upper gastrointestinal bleeding with shock risk, so he stays nothing by mouth and the team is told promptly for access and fluids. Oral intake, even ice chips, can provoke vomiting and delay procedures. Resting him flat until the next scheduled vital signs leaves instability unmonitored, and planning discharge ignores the risk of shock."}, {"id": 685, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a telephone follow-up, a 78-year-old woman with heart failure reports a 2 kg weight gain over one week, mild ankle swelling and a new need for an extra pillow. She says she will mention it at her clinic visit next month. What should the nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Report the gain and symptoms to the RN or prescriber today so treatment can be adjusted", "B": "Ask her to call the clinic nurse again next week if the weight keeps rising.", "C": "Tell her to stop eating salty foods and weigh again in a month.", "D": "Wait until the clinic visit next month since weight changes are common and usually not serious"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delays reporting by a week", "C": "Offers diet advice with no escalation", "D": "Waiting for a routine visit"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the gain and symptoms to the RN or prescriber today so treatment can be adjusted): A gain of about 2 kg in a week with swelling and worse breathing at night suggests fluid retention, so it is reported the same day for assessment and possible treatment change. Waiting a week or a month allows decompensation. Diet change helps but does not replace same-day reporting, and medicine changes belong to the prescriber."}, {"id": 686, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Her password has expired, so a registered nurse asks a practical nurse in a clinic to share her computer login to update several charts. The colleague says there is no time to wait for the help desk. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Share the login now and change the password right after the colleague finishes the entries", "B": "Log in for her and stay beside her to watch each entry", "C": "Share the login since the colleague is a trusted RN with a right to the charts", "D": "Decline and direct her to the help desk, since each user is accountable"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Sharing and changing the password afterward", "B": "Logging in for another user's use", "C": "Sharing credentials with a trusted colleague"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline and direct her to the help desk, since each user is accountable): Logins are personal and every chart entry is attributed to the person logged in, so sharing credentials breaks privacy and accountability rules regardless of the colleague's role. Trust does not make sharing acceptable. Logging in for her still records her entries under the wrong name, and changing the password afterward does not undo documentation or privacy exposure."}, {"id": 687, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "An adult client at a community clinic mentions that she follows the medicine wheel to understand her health, and she asks the nurse what the nurse thinks of it. She is calm, speaks easily, and has not mentioned any other health concerns today. Which response best reflects culturally safe care?", "options": {"A": "Assume the wheel means the same for every Indigenous person and apply a standard approach", "B": "Ask her what it means to her and how she wants it reflected in care", "C": "Say you respect it, then proceed with the standard care plan unchanged.", "D": "Share the nurse's own view of how the wheel compares with medical treatments."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming every client shares one meaning", "C": "Voices respect but changes nothing in care", "D": "Centres the nurse's opinion over her meaning"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask her what it means to her and how she wants it reflected in care): The medicine wheel is a framework that represents balance among physical, emotional, mental, and spiritual parts of life, and meanings vary by Nation and person, so the nurse asks and listens. Voicing respect and then ignoring it does not reflect her wishes in care, and sharing the nurse's view centres the nurse. Applying one meaning to everyone is stereotyping and not culturally safe."}, {"id": 688, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a community screening, a healthy 34-year-old woman who runs regularly has a regular resting pulse of 54 beats per minute. She feels well, with no dizziness or chest pain. Her blood pressure is 112/70 mmHg and she drinks plenty of water. How should the nurse interpret this finding?", "options": {"A": "It may be normal for a fit adult, so document it and watch for symptoms", "B": "Advise her to stop exercising until her rate returns to the usual adult range", "C": "Document it as bradycardia and repeat the pulse every hour until she leaves.", "D": "Report the rate to the RN immediately as an abnormal finding needing treatment today."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Restricting activity without symptoms or direction", "C": "Treats a fit adult's rate as a problem to monitor", "D": "Overreacts to an asymptomatic finding"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It may be normal for a fit adult, so document it and watch for symptoms): Resting adult rates are usually 60 to 100 beats per minute, but well-conditioned people can have lower rates without symptoms, so context decides. Hourly checks or urgent reporting treat a fit adult's rate as a problem. Stopping exercise is not supported when she is well and has no warning signs."}, {"id": 689, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Adeyemi, 83, lives at home and takes lorazepam every night for sleep. Her daughter reports that she has become unsteady and drowsy in the mornings. She has no recent illness, but her daughter helps with her pills each evening. Which action by the nurse is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Add a second bedtime check by the daughter and continue the tablet unchanged.", "B": "Report the drowsiness and unsteadiness to the RN or prescriber and ask for a medication review", "C": "Encourage Mrs. Adeyemi to take the tablet earlier in the evening so the morning drowsiness lessens", "D": "Teach the daughter to hold the tablet when Mrs. Adeyemi seems tired in the evening."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Monitors more but never reports the effect", "C": "Adjusting timing without prescriber input", "D": "Has family hold a drug without a prescriber"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C075", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on an acute medical unit and also visits older adults at home through a community program. Many clients are over 80 years old, take several medications, have reduced kidney function and may be confused. The team follows a least-restraint policy, and nurses are expected to report changes to the RN, advocate for safe medication use, and document what they observe in objective terms.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the drowsiness and unsteadiness to the RN or prescriber and ask for a medication review): Benzodiazepines increase confusion, drowsiness, and fall risk in older adults, so the nurse reports these signs and asks for a medication review that may include a slow taper. Reducing, holding, or changing the timing of a dose is a medication change outside nursing scope, and extra watching without reporting leaves the cause untreated. Stopping abruptly can trigger rebound insomnia and withdrawal."}, {"id": 690, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Admission laboratory results for Mr. Ivanov, 87, show a reduced eGFR. The order is for a drug usually given at the standard adult dose, and he is also dehydrated. He is alert, his blood pressure is stable, and he has no known allergies. What should the nurse do before giving it?", "options": {"A": "Give half the dose on the nurse's own judgment and note the change in the record", "B": "Give the dose and increase his fluids at the same time so the kidneys clear it", "C": "Give the standard adult dose since the order is written and the prescriber saw the report", "D": "Question the dose with the prescriber or pharmacist because reduced clearance may require less"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing a dose without an order", "B": "Counting on fluids to fix reduced clearance", "C": "Giving the usual dose despite reduced clearance"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C075", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on an acute medical unit and also visits older adults at home through a community program. Many clients are over 80 years old, take several medications, have reduced kidney function and may be confused. The team follows a least-restraint policy, and nurses are expected to report changes to the RN, advocate for safe medication use, and document what they observe in objective terms.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Question the dose with the prescriber or pharmacist because reduced clearance may require less): Many drugs are cleared by the kidneys, so reduced eGFR in an older adult can cause accumulation and toxicity, and the nurse clarifies the dose before giving it. Giving the standard dose assumes the prescriber factored in the result. Reducing the dose without an order is outside scope, and adding fluids does not restore filtration in a dehydrated older adult."}, {"id": 691, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Fontaine, 80, is confused and keeps pulling at her intravenous line at night. A colleague proposes tying her wrists because the unit is short-staffed. She was admitted two days ago with pneumonia and has been calm in the daytime. A sitter is not available tonight, and the RN is on the floor. Which action should the nurse take?", "options": {"A": "Remove the intravenous line now and let the physician decide later whether to restart it.", "B": "Ask a family member to stay, and use restraints only if none can come.", "C": "Apply wrist restraints as proposed because they prevent line removal and protect her from harm", "D": "Try alternatives such as covering the line and reorienting her, and report to the RN first"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Removes therapy without an order", "B": "Plans restraints because family may not be available", "C": "Using restraints for staffing convenience"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C075", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on an acute medical unit and also visits older adults at home through a community program. Many clients are over 80 years old, take several medications, have reduced kidney function and may be confused. The team follows a least-restraint policy, and nurses are expected to report changes to the RN, advocate for safe medication use, and document what they observe in objective terms.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Try alternatives such as covering the line and reorienting her, and report to the RN first): Restraints are a last resort after less restrictive measures, and staffing is not an acceptable reason, so the nurse uses alternatives such as covering the line, orientation, and closer observation and involves the RN. Removing her line without an order, or planning restraints when family cannot come, skips less restrictive measures and RN involvement. Applying restraints for convenience violates least-restraint practice."}, {"id": 692, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A family member asks the nurse to raise all four bed rails at night for Mr. Gagnon, 84, who lives in long-term care. He can move in bed and sometimes tries to get up alone. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Explain that full rails can be a restraint with entrapment risk, then assess him first", "B": "Raise all four rails because this stops him from falling out and the family asked", "C": "Raise two upper rails only and tell staff to check on him hourly.", "D": "Use a bed alarm and keep all four rails raised so staff are alerted."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using full rails as a routine safety measure", "C": "Uses partial rails that still restrict movement", "D": "Pairs full rails with an alarm instead of assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C075", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on an acute medical unit and also visits older adults at home through a community program. Many clients are over 80 years old, take several medications, have reduced kidney function and may be confused. The team follows a least-restraint policy, and nurses are expected to report changes to the RN, advocate for safe medication use, and document what they observe in objective terms.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that full rails can be a restraint with entrapment risk, then assess him first): Full bed rails can restrict movement, cause entrapment, and lead to worse falls when a person climbs over, so they count as restraints and need assessment and consent. Alternatives include a low bed, floor mats, and regular rounding. Raising rails at family request skips the assessment, and raising upper rails or all rails with an alarm still limits movement and keeps the climbing and entrapment risk."}, {"id": 693, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Thirty minutes after receiving an opioid, a 52-year-old man is drowsy and hard to rouse to speech. His respiratory rate is 9 breaths per minute and his oxygen saturation is 90 percent. His wound dressing is dry and his heart rate is steady. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Stimulate him, apply oxygen, call the RN for help, and hold any further opioid doses", "B": "Raise the head of the bed, give his as-needed antiemetic, and recheck in an hour.", "C": "Let him sleep and recheck the respiratory rate when the routine vital signs are due", "D": "Wake him fully, encourage deep breathing, and give the next opioid dose at half strength."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats nausea and misses respiratory depression", "C": "Delaying reassessment of a falling respiratory rate", "D": "Gives a reduced opioid dose during depression"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C078", "cluster_text": "On a postoperative unit and in community outreach, a practical nurse cares for clients receiving opioid and non-opioid analgesics. She uses sedation scales and respiratory assessments, works from medication orders, reports concerns to the RN or prescriber, and teaches clients about safe medication use. Naloxone kits are available through the community program, and nurses have been trained in their use.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stimulate him, apply oxygen, call the RN for help, and hold any further opioid doses): A rate of 9 with low saturation and decreased arousal signals opioid-induced respiratory depression, so the nurse stimulates him, supports oxygenation, calls for help, and holds further opioids. Giving more opioid, even at half strength, worsens depression. Raising the head and treating nausea misses the respiratory problem, and letting him sleep until routine vital signs delays rescue."}, {"id": 694, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Out in the community, the nurse finds an adult unresponsive with slow, shallow breathing and pinpoint pupils. An empty bottle of an opioid lies nearby and naloxone is available. Bystanders say they do not know how long the person has been there. After calling emergency services, what is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Give one large dose of naloxone and leave once the person begins to breathe more easily", "B": "Wait for emergency crews and give nothing, since only a physician may give naloxone", "C": "Give naloxone as directed, support breathing, and stay to repeat the dose if needed", "D": "Give naloxone and walk outside to meet the ambulance so that the crew is not delayed"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving too much and leaving", "B": "Withholding naloxone when available", "D": "Leaving the person who may relapse"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C078", "cluster_text": "On a postoperative unit and in community outreach, a practical nurse cares for clients receiving opioid and non-opioid analgesics. She uses sedation scales and respiratory assessments, works from medication orders, reports concerns to the RN or prescriber, and teaches clients about safe medication use. Naloxone kits are available through the community program, and nurses have been trained in their use.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give naloxone as directed, support breathing, and stay to repeat the dose if needed): Naloxone reverses opioid effects but wears off sooner than many opioids, so the person is supported with breathing assistance, monitored and given repeat doses if respiratory depression returns. Leaving after one dose risks relapse. Walking away leaves no one to monitor, and withholding it wastes time when naloxone kits are meant for use by trained responders."}, {"id": 695, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Beaulieu, 76, has a stomach ulcer history and takes ibuprofen several times daily for knee pain. He reports black stools and mild dizziness this week, and he says ibuprofen is the safest choice for older adults. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Tell him to switch to acetaminophen from his own cupboard and stop the ibuprofen.", "B": "Suggest taking it with food and recheck the stool color at the next scheduled visit", "C": "Report the black stools and dizziness to the RN or prescriber today for review", "D": "Advise him to take an antacid with the ibuprofen and keep a stool diary."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Switches drugs without review and ignores bleeding signs", "B": "Delaying reporting of possible bleeding", "D": "Masks symptoms and keeps the NSAID"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C078", "cluster_text": "On a postoperative unit and in community outreach, a practical nurse cares for clients receiving opioid and non-opioid analgesics. She uses sedation scales and respiratory assessments, works from medication orders, reports concerns to the RN or prescriber, and teaches clients about safe medication use. Naloxone kits are available through the community program, and nurses have been trained in their use.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the black stools and dizziness to the RN or prescriber today for review): NSAIDs can irritate the stomach lining and cause bleeding, especially in older adults with ulcer history, and black stools suggest upper bleeding, so prompt reporting and medication review are needed. An antacid and a diary delay assessment of possible bleeding. Switching medicines on his own leaves the bleeding unassessed, and taking ibuprofen with food does not remove the risk."}, {"id": 696, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 49-year-old client with mild kidney disease has mild joint pain at a clinic visit and asks which over-the-counter pain reliever is the better choice. He takes no blood thinners and has no stomach complaints. His urine output has been normal, and his latest creatinine was stable at last review. Which agent is generally preferred?", "options": {"A": "Acetaminophen within the daily maximum, since NSAIDs can reduce kidney blood flow", "B": "Naproxen because a twice daily dose is easier to remember than other pain relievers", "C": "Acetylsalicylic acid at a high daily dose because it is anti-inflammatory and widely available", "D": "Ibuprofen because it reduces inflammation and works well for mild joint pain in many adults"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Choosing a longer-acting NSAID for convenience", "C": "Using high dose acetylsalicylic acid despite renal risk", "D": "Choosing an NSAID despite kidney disease"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C078", "cluster_text": "On a postoperative unit and in community outreach, a practical nurse cares for clients receiving opioid and non-opioid analgesics. She uses sedation scales and respiratory assessments, works from medication orders, reports concerns to the RN or prescriber, and teaches clients about safe medication use. Naloxone kits are available through the community program, and nurses have been trained in their use.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acetaminophen within the daily maximum, since NSAIDs can reduce kidney blood flow): NSAIDs reduce prostaglandin-mediated kidney blood flow and can worsen kidney disease, so acetaminophen within safe limits is generally preferred for mild pain in this client. Ibuprofen and naproxen share the kidney risk, and high dose acetylsalicylic acid adds both kidney and bleeding risks. The nurse also encourages the client to confirm choices with a prescriber or pharmacist."}, {"id": 697, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Ms. Thibault, 52, has long-term cognitive impairment after a brain injury and cannot manage her own care. Her brother says that as a close relative he is automatically her legal guardian and may sign any consent. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Ask for documentation of his authority and involve the RN to confirm", "B": "Accept his statement since close relatives are guardians once a person becomes incapable", "C": "Let the physician accept his consent without checking documents.", "D": "Have the social worker sign consent for her."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assuming relatives are automatically guardians", "C": "Skips verification of the brother's authority", "D": "Gives a staff member consent authority"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask for documentation of his authority and involve the RN to confirm): Guardianship is a legal status granted through a formal process, while substitute decision makers follow a ranking set by law, so authority must be confirmed and not assumed from family ties. Accepting his word, even with a physician's agreement, risks invalid consent. A social worker is not a substitute decision maker and cannot sign for her, and a client who lacks capacity cannot give valid consent."}, {"id": 698, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Over two hours after surgery, a 55-year-old man's heart rate climbs from 82 to 118 beats per minute and he becomes restless. His blood pressure is 114/74 mmHg, and the dressing shows a growing stain. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Early shock from bleeding is possible as heart rate rises before pressure falls; report now", "B": "He is likely anxious after surgery and needs reassurance plus a calm, quiet room", "C": "The rising heart rate is an expected pain response after surgery and needs more pain medication", "D": "He is stable because his blood pressure is still in the normal range for adults"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Attributing restlessness to anxiety only", "C": "Treating tachycardia as pain alone", "D": "Relying on a normal blood pressure to rule out shock"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Early shock from bleeding is possible as heart rate rises before pressure falls; report now): In early hemorrhage the body compensates with a faster heart rate and restlessness while blood pressure stays normal until later, so a rising rate with a growing stain needs prompt reporting. A normal pressure does not mean stability. Calling it anxiety or pain delays recognition of bleeding, so the nurse also checks the dressing and positions him safely."}, {"id": 699, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Roy, 84, lives in a long-term care home and has heart failure. She tells the nurse she needs three pillows to sleep because she cannot breathe lying flat, and her ankles are more swollen than last week. What action is most appropriate for her comfort and breathing?", "options": {"A": "Raise the foot of the bed so fluid moves out of her ankles overnight.", "B": "Lay her flat with one pillow since lying flat helps the heart rest overnight", "C": "Help her sit upright with support and report the pillow change to the RN", "D": "Lower the head of the bed slightly and add a second blanket."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Raises the legs and increases venous return", "B": "Lying a client with heart failure flat", "D": "Lowers the head of the bed during orthopnea"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Help her sit upright with support and report the pillow change to the RN): Needing more pillows shows orthopnea, a sign of worsening heart failure, and sitting upright reduces venous return and eases breathing, so the nurse positions her upright and reports the change. Raising the legs or lowering the head increases congestion. Lying flat also worsens breathing and may signal worsening fluid overload."}, {"id": 700, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An adult who receives hemodialysis in the community has gained 3.5 kg between sessions and says small gains of a few hundred grams do not matter. He feels well today and has no breathlessness. What should the nurse teach about weight monitoring?", "options": {"A": "Weigh after dialysis only, since pre-dialysis weights include extra fluid.", "B": "Weigh once a week and compare to the target weight when the next session starts", "C": "Weigh daily after meals and report gains only at the next dialysis.", "D": "Weigh daily at the same time and report large gains or breathlessness"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Misses the gain between sessions", "B": "Weighing too infrequently to catch fluid trends", "C": "Delays reporting until the next session"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Weigh daily at the same time and report large gains or breathlessness): Interdialytic weight gain shows fluid accumulation in a client who cannot excrete it, so daily weights at a consistent time and prompt reporting of large gains or breathlessness protect against overload. Weighing only after dialysis misses the gain between sessions, weekly weights miss the pattern, and holding reports until the next session delays action."}, {"id": 701, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Dhillon, 67, has right-sided weakness after a stroke and sits in a chair with his right arm hanging at his side. He is alert, follows simple commands, and has no pain in the shoulder at present. Which nursing action best prevents complications for the affected arm?", "options": {"A": "Raise the weak arm overhead during transfers to keep the shoulder mobile.", "B": "Tuck the arm tightly against his chest with the fingers curled over a rolled towel", "C": "Place the arm in a sling most of the day with the fingers curled.", "D": "Support the arm on a pillow or lap tray with the shoulder supported and hand open"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Strains the shoulder by overhead lifting", "B": "Positioning in a flexed posture that promotes contracture", "C": "Prolonged sling with curled fingers promotes contracture"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C089", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on a rehabilitation and medical unit caring for clients with mobility problems after stroke, long bed rest, injury and surgery. She works with the physiotherapist and the RN, uses safe handling and positioning techniques, assesses skin and circulation, and teaches clients and families how to prevent complications. Safety and dignity are priorities during every transfer. Skin checks and pain reports are documented at each shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Support the arm on a pillow or lap tray with the shoulder supported and hand open): A weak arm left hanging can cause shoulder subluxation and swelling, so it is supported with the shoulder positioned well and the hand open to reduce contracture. Lifting the arm overhead strains the joint. Prolonged sling use with curled fingers, or tucking the arm tightly, encourages contractures instead of preventing them."}, {"id": 702, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After two weeks of bed rest, Mrs. Wilkins, 78, has a reddened sacrum, shallow breathing, a swollen left calf and constipation. The nurse explains how immobility affects the body. She also eats poorly and has a urinary catheter that was placed last week. Which statement is the most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Shallow breathing mainly needs treatment, while the skin and bowel findings reflect poor nutrition.", "B": "The calf swelling suggests a clot, so assessment focuses on the leg and breathing.", "C": "Immobility affects the muscles, joints and skin, so assessment covers the skin and joints.", "D": "Immobility can affect skin, lungs, circulation and bowels, so assessment covers all systems"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Attributes findings to nutrition alone", "B": "Narrows assessment to the leg and breathing", "C": "Names only some systems affected by immobility"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C089", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on a rehabilitation and medical unit caring for clients with mobility problems after stroke, long bed rest, injury and surgery. She works with the physiotherapist and the RN, uses safe handling and positioning techniques, assesses skin and circulation, and teaches clients and families how to prevent complications. Safety and dignity are priorities during every transfer. Skin checks and pain reports are documented at each shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Immobility can affect skin, lungs, circulation and bowels, so assessment covers all systems): Bed rest slows circulation, bowel movement, and lung expansion, and raises risk of pressure injury, clots, and weakness, so the nurse assesses and reports across systems. Limiting assessment to the skin and joints, or to the leg and breathing, misses linked findings. Poor nutrition contributes but does not explain the clot risk, slowed bowels, and shallow breathing seen with immobility."}, {"id": 703, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 24-year-old athlete twists an ankle during a game. It is swollen and tender, and she can bear partial weight. She asks the nurse whether to use a heating pad right away. What is the best advice for the first 24 hours?", "options": {"A": "Walk on the ankle gently with an elastic wrap to keep it moving.", "B": "Apply cold for an hour without removing it and keep the ankle lowered.", "C": "Rest the ankle, apply cold in short periods, compress gently and elevate it", "D": "Apply heat for twenty minutes at a time so blood flow increases and swelling goes down"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Starts weight bearing too early", "B": "Leaves cold on too long and lowers the swollen limb", "D": "Using heat during the early swelling phase"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C089", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on a rehabilitation and medical unit caring for clients with mobility problems after stroke, long bed rest, injury and surgery. She works with the physiotherapist and the RN, uses safe handling and positioning techniques, assesses skin and circulation, and teaches clients and families how to prevent complications. Safety and dignity are priorities during every transfer. Skin checks and pain reports are documented at each shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rest the ankle, apply cold in short periods, compress gently and elevate it): Early care for a sprain uses rest, cold, compression, and elevation to reduce bleeding and swelling, and heat is avoided in the first day because it increases blood flow and swelling. Walking on the injured ankle risks further injury. Cold left on for an hour can damage skin and tissue, and a lowered ankle increases swelling. She should seek assessment if she cannot bear weight."}, {"id": 704, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two days after a below-knee amputation, Mr. Chen, 58, has his residual limb raised on a pillow and asks to keep it that way for several weeks because it feels better. He is eager to heal and asks many questions about his recovery. What is the best nursing response?", "options": {"A": "Agree that a pillow under the limb is best because it reduces swelling for weeks", "B": "Explain that prolonged elevation can cause hip and knee contractures, and teach limb extension", "C": "Place a pillow under the knee to keep the leg comfortable and supported.", "D": "Wrap the limb tightly and keep it hanging to limit swelling."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Allowing long term elevation on a pillow", "C": "Keeps the knee bent and invites contracture", "D": "Allows dependent hanging that increases swelling"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C089", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on a rehabilitation and medical unit caring for clients with mobility problems after stroke, long bed rest, injury and surgery. She works with the physiotherapist and the RN, uses safe handling and positioning techniques, assesses skin and circulation, and teaches clients and families how to prevent complications. Safety and dignity are priorities during every transfer. Skin checks and pain reports are documented at each shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain that prolonged elevation can cause hip and knee contractures, and teach limb extension): Short term elevation reduces swelling, but keeping the residual limb on a pillow for long periods encourages knee and hip contractures, so teaching focuses on extension and lying flat at times. Agreeing with weeks of elevation ignores that risk. A pillow under the knee keeps it bent and shortens the muscles, and a tightly wrapped hanging limb increases edema and pain."}, {"id": 705, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "An RN asks a practical nurse in acute care to manage a client with complex, unstable needs, including a task the practical nurse has never performed. She feels unsure about the competence needed. The unit is busy, and two other clients need attention at the same time. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Accept the task quietly because the RN has more authority and the client needs prompt care", "B": "Accept the task after asking a coworker to watch her once for a few minutes.", "C": "Tell the RN she lacks the competence and ask for support, supervision or reassignment", "D": "Tell the RN she is willing, then learn the steps during the procedure."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepting a task beyond personal competence", "B": "Uses informal watching in place of planned supervision", "D": "Learns a skill on the client"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tell the RN she lacks the competence and ask for support, supervision or reassignment): Practical nurses are accountable for working within their own competence, so when a task exceeds it, they communicate that and seek support, teaching, or reassignment. Accepting silently puts the client at risk. Informal watching by a coworker, or learning steps during the procedure, is not planned supervision and still places the client at risk."}, {"id": 706, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A healthy adult is receiving an intramuscular vaccine at an immunization clinic. A coworker suggests using the dorsogluteal site as the first choice because the muscle is large. The client is not anticoagulated and has no skin lesions on either arm. Which site selection is best?", "options": {"A": "Use the deltoid but inject into its lowest part near the elbow crease.", "B": "Use the dorsogluteal site because the muscle is large and holds the most medication", "C": "Use the deltoid at a 45 degree angle with a short needle.", "D": "Use the deltoid, found by landmarks below the acromion, as the usual adult vaccine site"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses the wrong landmark near the elbow", "B": "Choosing the dorsogluteal site with nerve risk", "C": "Uses a subcutaneous angle for an intramuscular dose"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use the deltoid, found by landmarks below the acromion, as the usual adult vaccine site): The deltoid is the usual adult vaccine site, found two to three finger widths below the acromion, and it avoids the sciatic nerve injury risk of the dorsogluteal site. Injecting low near the elbow misses the muscle and risks nerve injury, and a 45 degree angle suits subcutaneous injections, not intramuscular ones. Correct landmarks and needle length matter for adults."}, {"id": 707, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ninety minutes after his ordered analgesic, a 48-year-old man on a surgical unit rates his pain 8 out of 10. His next dose is not due for two hours. He asks the nurse to repeat the same dose now. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Give a smaller dose now and document that the pain is improving", "B": "Reassess pain and vital signs, then report uncontrolled pain to the prescriber", "C": "Tell him he must wait two hours because the order cannot be questioned", "D": "Give the same dose now since his pain is severe and he has asked for it"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing the dose without an order and charting inaccurately", "C": "Ignoring uncontrolled pain instead of reporting it", "D": "Giving a dose before the interval"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reassess pain and vital signs, then report uncontrolled pain to the prescriber): When ordered analgesia fails, the nurse reassesses, checks vital signs and reports so the prescriber can change the order, because repeating a dose early exceeds the order and risks oversedation. Telling him to wait without reporting leaves pain unmanaged. Giving a smaller dose is a nurse-made change, and charting improvement when pain is unchanged is inaccurate."}, {"id": 708, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Sinclair, 66, has a known stomach ulcer and says his stools have been black and tarry for two days. He asks whether that means a problem in his lower bowel. He also reports mild dizziness when standing but no chest pain. Which interpretation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Black tarry stool suggests hemorrhoid bleeding, so ask him to recheck stools tomorrow", "B": "Black stool likely reflects iron or diet, so document it and recheck next week", "C": "Tarry stool suggests constipation, so offer fluids and fibre and recheck his bowel pattern", "D": "Black tarry stool suggests digested blood from the upper tract, so report it urgently"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating melena as bleeding from hemorrhoids", "B": "Blaming iron or diet despite an ulcer history", "C": "Reading tarry stool as constipation needing fibre"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C087", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit and in a long-term care home, a practical nurse provides care to clients with gastrointestinal problems. She monitors bowel patterns and stool characteristics, gives bowel care within her scope, follows orders, reports abnormal findings to the RN, and documents objectively. Clients include older adults who take opioids, are on bed rest, or have a history of ulcer disease.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Black tarry stool suggests digested blood from the upper tract, so report it urgently): Melena, which is black and tarry, results from blood digested during passage through the gut and usually points to upper gastrointestinal bleeding, especially with an ulcer, so it needs prompt reporting. Hemorrhoid bleeding is bright red, not tarry. Iron or diet can darken stool without making it tarry, and constipation does not explain the dizziness he reports."}, {"id": 709, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Larocque, 82, in long-term care takes an opioid for pain and has not had a bowel movement for four days, with mild abdominal discomfort. A colleague suggests daily enemas. She is alert, drinks little fluid, and her bowel sounds are present but quiet. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Give a stool softener from the unit stock, since constipation is a minor opioid effect", "B": "Offer more fluids and wait another three days before reporting, since hydration usually resolves it", "C": "Give a daily enema as suggested since opioid constipation requires strong measures to move stool", "D": "Assess the abdomen and intake, encourage activity, and ask the RN about the bowel regimen"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving a medication without an order", "B": "Delaying reporting after four days without a bowel movement", "C": "Using enemas before simple measures"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C087", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit and in a long-term care home, a practical nurse provides care to clients with gastrointestinal problems. She monitors bowel patterns and stool characteristics, gives bowel care within her scope, follows orders, reports abnormal findings to the RN, and documents objectively. Clients include older adults who take opioids, are on bed rest, or have a history of ulcer disease.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess the abdomen and intake, encourage activity, and ask the RN about the bowel regimen): Opioid constipation is managed first with assessment, fluids, activity and the ordered laxative regimen before enemas, which carry risks in frail older adults. Daily enemas skip these steps. Giving a softener from stock needs an order, and waiting several more days to report delays care for a client already four days without a bowel movement."}, {"id": 710, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Petrov, 88, is bedridden in long-term care. Small amounts of liquid stool have leaked all day, and his abdomen is hard and distended with poor appetite. A colleague suggests an antidiarrheal. His vital signs are stable, and he has no vomiting or fever. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Suspect impaction with overflow, withhold the antidiarrheal, and report findings to the RN", "B": "Give an oral laxative from unit stock to clear the stool before reporting", "C": "Give the antidiarrheal because the liquid stool is clearly diarrhea that needs to be stopped", "D": "Offer extra fluids and fibre, record stool amounts, and reassess at the next shift"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Giving a laxative without an order or assessment", "C": "Treating overflow liquid stool as diarrhea", "D": "Using fluids and delayed reporting for a hard distended abdomen"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C087", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit and in a long-term care home, a practical nurse provides care to clients with gastrointestinal problems. She monitors bowel patterns and stool characteristics, gives bowel care within her scope, follows orders, reports abnormal findings to the RN, and documents objectively. Clients include older adults who take opioids, are on bed rest, or have a history of ulcer disease.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Suspect impaction with overflow, withhold the antidiarrheal, and report findings to the RN): Liquid stool around a hard impaction leaks past the blockage and looks like diarrhea, so an antidiarrheal can worsen it and the nurse reports for rectal assessment. Treating it as simple diarrhea misses the cause. A laxative without an order is outside scope and may worsen distension, and fluids with delayed reporting risk obstruction."}, {"id": 711, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Digital removal of stool is ordered for an adult client with impaction in acute care. He has heart disease and a slow pulse earlier this week. He is awake and agreeable, and a second nurse is available to assist. Which precaution is most important for the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Position him flat on his back with legs straight to relax the sphincter", "B": "Apply firm pressure to the abdomen during removal to help the stool descend", "C": "Monitor his pulse and symptoms, because rectal stimulation can slow the heart rate", "D": "Complete it quickly to limit his discomfort, and check his pulse once afterward"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a supine position instead of the left side", "B": "Adding abdominal pressure that does not protect the heart", "D": "Checking pulse only after the procedure ends"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C087", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit and in a long-term care home, a practical nurse provides care to clients with gastrointestinal problems. She monitors bowel patterns and stool characteristics, gives bowel care within her scope, follows orders, reports abnormal findings to the RN, and documents objectively. Clients include older adults who take opioids, are on bed rest, or have a history of ulcer disease.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Monitor his pulse and symptoms, because rectal stimulation can slow the heart rate): Rectal stimulation can cause a vagal response with slowed heart rate and dizziness, particularly in clients with heart disease, so the nurse monitors pulse and symptoms during the procedure and stops if they appear. Checking only afterward misses a falling rate as it happens. Lying flat does not reduce this risk, and abdominal pressure adds discomfort without protecting the heart."}, {"id": 712, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Alvarez, 38, has had three tonic-clonic seizures in 25 minutes on a medical unit and has not regained consciousness between them. His wife mentions he missed his anticonvulsant this week. His oxygen saturation is 91 percent. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Protect his airway, give oxygen, stay at the bedside, and call for emergency help", "B": "Restrain his arms and legs gently to prevent injury until the jerking stops", "C": "Raise the head of the bed fully and suction his mouth while he seizes", "D": "Time each seizure and keep observing him closely until he wakes up on his own"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Restraining limbs during a seizure risks injury", "C": "Upright position and suction do not secure the airway during seizure", "D": "Treating repeated seizures as separate events and only observing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Protect his airway, give oxygen, stay at the bedside, and call for emergency help): Seizures that repeat without recovery of consciousness are status epilepticus, an emergency, and the low saturation shows his airway and oxygenation need immediate support while help arrives. Observing alone delays urgent treatment. Restraining limbs during a seizure can cause injury, and suctioning an actively seizing client with the bed upright does not secure the airway."}, {"id": 713, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mrs. Dube, 67, has lived with advanced dementia for years and can no longer make care decisions. Her adult son, her sister and a neighbour each tell the nurse in a community clinic that they are in charge of her health choices. Which action best respects the legal process for substitute decision making?", "options": {"A": "Choose the sister, since she is the oldest relative and knows the family history", "B": "Ask the family to agree on one person and record that person as decision maker", "C": "Accept the son as decision maker because adult children are usually the closest relatives", "D": "Confirm with the team who is legally appointed as her substitute decision maker"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing by age and family knowledge, not legal ranking", "B": "Letting the family pick instead of checking the legal appointment", "C": "Assuming any relative automatically holds legal authority"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Confirm with the team who is legally appointed as her substitute decision maker): Authority to decide for an incapable adult comes from a legal appointment or a recognized hierarchy set by the jurisdiction, so the nurse confirms who holds it before acting on any instruction. Family closeness does not create that authority, even for a son. Seniority gives the sister no legal standing, and a family agreement has no legal basis. Document the verified decision maker in the chart."}, {"id": 714, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 28-year-old marathon runner attends a workplace screening clinic. Her resting radial pulse is regular at 54 beats per minute, she feels well, and she reports no dizziness or fainting. Her blood pressure is 108/64 mmHg. How should the practical nurse interpret this pulse rate?", "options": {"A": "It is expected for her age group, so no documentation is needed unless she complains", "B": "It may be normal for an athlete, so document it and report only if symptoms appear", "C": "It is abnormal bradycardia, so notify the physician urgently before she leaves the clinic", "D": "It suggests a medication effect, so ask about drugs and report it to the physician"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Skipping documentation of a finding because she feels well", "C": "Labelling a low rate abnormal without considering fitness", "D": "Assuming a drug cause without evidence of any medication"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It may be normal for an athlete, so document it and report only if symptoms appear): Trained athletes often have a resting rate below 60 because of a strong, efficient heart, and she has no symptoms and a normal pressure. Context therefore decides whether the rate is a concern, and the finding should still be documented. Urgent reporting is for a low rate with dizziness, weakness or chest discomfort. Nothing in her history points to a medication effect."}, {"id": 715, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Chen, 81, receives home care and has macular degeneration with very poor vision. The practical nurse arrives for a scheduled visit and finds him seated in the living room. Which approach best supports his safety and dignity as the visit begins?", "options": {"A": "Touch his shoulder gently first to get his attention, then greet him and explain", "B": "Turn on bright lights, stand close in front of him, and begin the visit", "C": "Announce yourself on entering, say what you will do, and tell him before touching him", "D": "Begin the assessment immediately and explain each step afterward during documentation"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Touching a client with low vision before announcing yourself", "B": "Assuming brighter light restores his sight", "D": "Acting first and explaining later, which startles the client"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Announce yourself on entering, say what you will do, and tell him before touching him): A client with low vision cannot rely on seeing the nurse, so naming yourself and describing each action before touching him prevents fear and respects his autonomy. Touching first can startle him. Brighter light and standing close do not restore central vision lost to macular degeneration. Explaining afterward removes his chance to understand and consent as care happens."}, {"id": 716, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Greene, 48, is on her first day after abdominal surgery. Her pain score is 8 out of 10 despite two scheduled doses of the ordered opioid, and the next dose is not due for two hours. She is sweating and guarding her abdomen. What is the practical nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Report the uncontrolled pain to the registered nurse promptly so the order can be reassessed", "B": "Ask her to wait and use distraction such as music until the next dose", "C": "Request a different opioid from the pharmacy and give it once it arrives", "D": "Encourage deep breathing and splinting with a pillow, then wait until the next dose is due"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using distraction alone for severe uncontrolled pain", "C": "Changing the drug without a prescriber order", "D": "Delaying relief by relying only on comfort measures"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the uncontrolled pain to the registered nurse promptly so the order can be reassessed): Pain unrelieved by the ordered regimen needs reassessment and possibly a changed order, which the practical nurse seeks by reporting to the registered nurse or prescriber. Choosing a different drug without an order is outside scope. Distraction and breathing exercises help but should not replace reporting, because severe pain with sweating and guarding may also signal a complication."}, {"id": 717, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the emergency department, a 35-year-old man with a cough and fever needs a nasopharyngeal swab for respiratory viruses. He is coughing often and feels anxious about the test. Which technique should the practical nurse use when collecting the specimen?", "options": {"A": "Wear a mask, tilt his head forward, and pass the swab along the nasal roof", "B": "Insert the swab through the mouth to the back of his throat and rotate", "C": "Tilt his head forward and insert the swab only to the first centimetre", "D": "Wear protective equipment, tilt his head slightly back, and pass the swab along the nasal floor"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Directing the swab along the nasal roof with the head forward", "B": "Collecting an oropharyngeal specimen instead of a nasopharyngeal one", "C": "Too shallow, collecting an inadequate specimen"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wear protective equipment, tilt his head slightly back, and pass the swab along the nasal floor): The swab follows the floor of the nose toward the nasopharynx, parallel to the palate, which collects the right cells with the least injury. Aiming along the roof is painful and can cause bleeding. A throat swab samples the wrong site, and a shallow sample often gives a false negative result. Protective equipment is required because swabbing can provoke coughing and sneezing."}, {"id": 718, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A parent calls the clinic nurse about her 3-year-old daughter, who weighs 14 kg and has a fever of 38.9 C. The mother plans to give acetaminophen every two hours until the fever breaks because the child still feels hot. What should the practical nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Use the age-based dose on the label and repeat every three hours while fever lasts", "B": "Give a double first dose to bring the fever down quickly, then follow the label", "C": "Give a second dose of a different fever medicine each time the child feels hot again", "D": "Give the weight-based dose at no shorter than the labelled interval, within the daily maximum"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Repeating before the labelled interval using an age-based dose", "B": "Doubling the first dose, exceeding the safe amount", "C": "Stacking medicines without checking the daily limits"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give the weight-based dose at no shorter than the labelled interval, within the daily maximum): Acetaminophen is dosed by weight at set intervals with a daily maximum, because liver injury follows repeated excess doses. A child who still feels warm is not a reason to shorten the interval or double a dose. A double first dose risks liver toxicity, and repeating every three hours is shorter than the usual four to six hour interval. Age-based labels can mismatch her weight, and a second fever medicine at each warm spell can duplicate or stack doses."}, {"id": 719, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After a collision, an unconscious 45-year-old man arrives in the emergency department with no identification and no wristband. A nurse is about to give the first ordered medication. The stretcher is in bay 6, and the triage tag lists a temporary trauma name. How should the right-client check be done?", "options": {"A": "Ask a nearby bystander who claims to know the man for his name and use it", "B": "Match the bay number on the stretcher to the number written on the order", "C": "Skip the check because the physician is at the bedside and aware of the order", "D": "Match the temporary name and number on his applied armband to the order"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepting an unverified identity from a bystander", "B": "Using a location as an identifier", "C": "Omitting the check because a physician is present"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Match the temporary name and number on his applied armband to the order): Two reliable identifiers must match the order, and when a client is unidentified the facility's temporary name and number on an applied armband serve that purpose. A bay or room number can change and is not an identifier. A bystander's word is unverified. Physician presence does not replace the nurse's own verification before giving a drug."}, {"id": 720, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Kowal, 34, has told the clinic nurse that she experienced abuse in the past. She now needs a pelvic examination for a screening test and appears tense, with shallow breathing. Which action best reflects trauma-informed care for the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with the usual routine and avoid discussing her history so she is not distressed", "B": "Ask her to describe past abuse in detail first so the examiner understands her triggers", "C": "Explain each step, offer choices, and ask permission before touching her, pausing if she asks", "D": "Reassure her that the examination is routine and ask her to stay still during it"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignoring her history and giving no information", "B": "Pressing her to retell trauma, which can re-traumatize her", "D": "Dismissing her feelings and limiting her control"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain each step, offer choices, and ask permission before touching her, pausing if she asks): Trauma-informed care restores a sense of safety through predictability, choice and control, so the nurse describes steps, seeks permission and stops on request. Skipping her history and explanations leaves her bracing for the unknown. Asking her to retell abuse in detail can re-traumatize her. Telling her it is routine dismisses her experience."}, {"id": 721, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Alone at a community clinic, a 15-year-old girl asks for confidential birth control counselling. She says she has thought carefully about it and can explain the options and risks back to the nurse. Which principle should guide the practical nurse here?", "options": {"A": "A person under 16 cannot consent, so the visit must end until a parent attends", "B": "Parents must be called at once because they hold decision making authority over minors", "C": "Consent is unnecessary because the counselling is only information and does not count as treatment", "D": "Capacity depends on her understanding, so involve the team to assess her as a mature minor"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating age alone as the test of capacity", "B": "Assuming parental authority overrides a mature minor's own consent", "C": "Believing information-giving needs no consent or confidentiality"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Capacity depends on her understanding, so involve the team to assess her as a mature minor): Many jurisdictions recognize that a young person who understands the nature, benefits and risks of a decision can consent, so the focus is capacity rather than a fixed age. The nurse works within agency policy and the regulatory body's standards by involving the team. An absolute age bar ignores that principle. Confidentiality still applies to her health information."}, {"id": 722, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The team at a morning huddle on a medical unit has been working short staffed, and several members completed extra shifts this week. The unit manager asks the practical nurse how best to sustain motivation. Which approach would be most effective?", "options": {"A": "Save praise for exceptional events so it keeps its value over time", "B": "Offer a small gift card to everyone at the end of the month instead of feedback", "C": "Recognize the staff who completed extra shifts and say little to the others", "D": "Give specific, timely recognition, naming what individuals and the team did and why it mattered"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing scarce praise is more meaningful", "B": "Substituting a token reward for meaningful feedback", "C": "Singling out some staff and leaving others unrecognized"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C096", "cluster_text": "A 28-bed medical unit in a Canadian hospital is introducing a new electronic charting system in three weeks. Staff have been working short for two months. The manager asks the unit practical nurses to help lead the change, and some staff have said openly that the old paper charts worked fine. Morale has been low, and several staff members have mentioned feeling unappreciated.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give specific, timely recognition, naming what individuals and the team did and why it mattered): Recognition works best when it is specific, honest and close to the event, because staff then know what to repeat and feel valued. Rare praise leaves most good work unnoticed. A gift card without feedback does not say what was valued, and praising only some staff can divide a tired team. A yearly review is too late to reinforce effort from weeks ago."}, {"id": 723, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Staff on the unit are told the new charting system starts in three weeks. One practical nurse, Dev, says he feels worried and unsure how it will affect his workload. The manager wants to apply the stages people typically move through during change. What is most likely to happen to staff after the announcement?", "options": {"A": "Many pass through denial, resistance and exploration before acceptance and need support", "B": "Staff move through the stages at one pace, so one session is enough", "C": "Staff will resist for a few days, then adopt it without further help", "D": "Resistance signals poor leadership, so the launch should wait until everyone agrees"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assuming all staff move through change at one pace", "C": "Assuming resistance ends alone without support", "D": "Reading normal resistance as a leadership failure needing delay"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C096", "cluster_text": "A 28-bed medical unit in a Canadian hospital is introducing a new electronic charting system in three weeks. Staff have been working short for two months. The manager asks the unit practical nurses to help lead the change, and some staff have said openly that the old paper charts worked fine. Morale has been low, and several staff members have mentioned feeling unappreciated.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Many pass through denial, resistance and exploration before acceptance and need support): People usually move through predictable emotional stages during change, such as denial, resistance, exploration and commitment, and each needs different support like listening, training and time. Staff move at different paces, so one session is not enough. Resistance is a normal response, not proof of poor leadership, and waiting for full agreement can stall the change. Resistance does not fade by itself."}, {"id": 724, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Using Lewin's model, the unit leader plans the switch to electronic charting. Weeks of discussion about why paper charts are slow have taken place, and training is now underway. Which step would belong to the refreezing phase of the change?", "options": {"A": "Update policies, audit use, and reinforce the new routine until it is normal practice", "B": "Give staff a hands-on demonstration of the new screens during the scheduled training sessions", "C": "Hold the first meeting to explain why the old paper charting system must go", "D": "Collect early worries from staff and respond to each concern as it arises during rollout"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Placing training in refreezing instead of the change phase", "C": "Confusing unfreezing with the stabilizing phase", "D": "Treating concern-gathering as the final stage"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C096", "cluster_text": "A 28-bed medical unit in a Canadian hospital is introducing a new electronic charting system in three weeks. Staff have been working short for two months. The manager asks the unit practical nurses to help lead the change, and some staff have said openly that the old paper charts worked fine. Morale has been low, and several staff members have mentioned feeling unappreciated.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Update policies, audit use, and reinforce the new routine until it is normal practice): Refreezing stabilizes the new practice by embedding it in policy, monitoring and reinforcement so staff do not drift back. Explaining the need for change belongs to unfreezing. Hands-on training happens during the moving stage. Early listening to concerns also serves unfreezing and moving, not the final stabilizing work."}, {"id": 725, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During the second week of the launch, an older nurse tells her colleagues the electronic system is a waste of time and asks if they can keep writing paper notes. The practical nurse is asked to respond as a team member. What is the best way to treat this resistance?", "options": {"A": "Remind her of the policy and ask her to try it for two weeks", "B": "Listen to her concerns, learn what drives them, and involve her in the fix", "C": "Allow her to use paper notes for the long term so she feels comfortable", "D": "Ask the manager to move her to another unit so the team stays positive"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Restating policy without exploring her concerns", "C": "Permitting a parallel system that undermines the change", "D": "Removing the critic instead of addressing the issues"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C096", "cluster_text": "A 28-bed medical unit in a Canadian hospital is introducing a new electronic charting system in three weeks. Staff have been working short for two months. The manager asks the unit practical nurses to help lead the change, and some staff have said openly that the old paper charts worked fine. Morale has been low, and several staff members have mentioned feeling unappreciated.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Listen to her concerns, learn what drives them, and involve her in the fix): Resistance often holds useful information about training gaps, workflow problems or fear of losing competence, so listening and involving her turns a critic into a contributor. Restating policy without asking about concerns hardens opposition. Moving her elsewhere removes a useful voice and the problems stay. A permanent paper alternative splits the record and defeats the purpose of the change."}, {"id": 726, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Bear, 62, is admitted with advanced heart failure and is described by his daughter as a man who values balance of body, mind, emotions and spirit. She shows the nurse a medicine wheel. She asks how it fits into his plan. How should the practical nurse best respond?", "options": {"A": "Describe it as whole-person balance, ask how he wants it reflected, and inform the team", "B": "Say it belongs in the community and offer to refer his daughter to the chaplain", "C": "Ask him to choose between the medicine wheel and his heart failure treatments", "D": "Politely note that it is cultural background and not relevant to his heart failure care plan"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Redirecting a cultural request to another setting without asking him", "C": "Presenting cultural practice and medical care as a forced choice", "D": "Dismissing it as irrelevant to care planning"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C095", "cluster_text": "A 62-year-old Indigenous man with advanced heart failure is admitted to a medical unit in a hospital far from his home community. His daughter says the family wants an Elder to join care discussions, and that he prefers an interpreter for complex medical talks. The practical nurse is assigned to his care this shift. The unit has a two-visitor limit at the bedside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Describe it as whole-person balance, ask how he wants it reflected, and inform the team): The medicine wheel is a holistic framework used by many Indigenous peoples to describe balance, and it guides care when the client chooses to use it, so the nurse asks him how to honour it and informs the team. It is not a prescribed treatment. Redirecting the request or forcing a choice between it and his treatments ignores his right to culturally safe care."}, {"id": 727, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The family asks that an Elder from the client's community attend a care meeting, though visitor numbers are limited on the unit. The client's daughter explains that the Elder has known the family for decades and guides them through major decisions. Which statement about Elders is most accurate for the practical nurse to remember?", "options": {"A": "Elders are volunteers whose role is to translate for the client and the care team", "B": "Elders are spiritual advisers who must be called only after a physician agrees", "C": "Elders are respected knowledge keepers and guides, and many communities expect their inclusion", "D": "Elders must be consulted only when the client is too ill to decide"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reducing an Elder to a translator", "B": "Making an Elder's role depend on physician approval", "D": "Believing Elders act only when capacity is lost"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C095", "cluster_text": "A 62-year-old Indigenous man with advanced heart failure is admitted to a medical unit in a hospital far from his home community. His daughter says the family wants an Elder to join care discussions, and that he prefers an interpreter for complex medical talks. The practical nurse is assigned to his care this shift. The unit has a two-visitor limit at the bedside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Elders are respected knowledge keepers and guides, and many communities expect their inclusion): Elders are valued for wisdom, teaching and spiritual guidance, and including them can support the client's health and trust in care when he wants this. They are not translators, and their role does not depend on a physician agreeing. Inclusion depends on the client's wishes, not on whether he is able to decide."}, {"id": 728, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At the bedside, the practical nurse meets the client's daughter for the first time. The unit is busy, and the nurse wants the first interaction to show respect. The daughter has travelled six hours and appears tired and worried about her father. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Greet her by name, introduce yourself and your role, ask how she prefers to be addressed", "B": "Introduce yourself and your role, then use her first name to build a quick connection", "C": "Begin with the assessment questions to use the time efficiently, then introduce yourself later", "D": "Hand her the unit brochure and ask her to wait until the assessment ends"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using her first name without asking her preference", "C": "Putting tasks before the person", "D": "Postponing contact with the family until tasks are done"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C095", "cluster_text": "A 62-year-old Indigenous man with advanced heart failure is admitted to a medical unit in a hospital far from his home community. His daughter says the family wants an Elder to join care discussions, and that he prefers an interpreter for complex medical talks. The practical nurse is assigned to his care this shift. The unit has a two-visitor limit at the bedside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Greet her by name, introduce yourself and your role, ask how she prefers to be addressed): A respectful opening names the person, states who you are, and asks how she prefers to be addressed, which builds trust before questions begin. Starting with the assessment treats the family as a source of data. Using her first name without asking can feel presumptuous, and handing her a brochure and asking her to wait leaves a worried relative without contact."}, {"id": 729, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Because the client speaks mostly Cree, his ten-year-old grandson, who is bilingual, offers to translate the nurse's explanation of new medications. The nurse has a teaching session planned and the grandson is eager to help. A telephone interpreter service is available. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Let the grandson interpret because a family member understands the client best", "B": "Use a qualified interpreter for the teaching and thank the grandson without giving him the role", "C": "Use the telephone interpreter for consent and let the grandson interpret the medication teaching", "D": "Teach the grandson the medication plan and let him explain it to the client"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a child as medical interpreter", "C": "Having a child interpret medical teaching", "D": "Passing teaching to a child instead of teaching the client"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C095", "cluster_text": "A 62-year-old Indigenous man with advanced heart failure is admitted to a medical unit in a hospital far from his home community. His daughter says the family wants an Elder to join care discussions, and that he prefers an interpreter for complex medical talks. The practical nurse is assigned to his care this shift. The unit has a two-visitor limit at the bedside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use a qualified interpreter for the teaching and thank the grandson without giving him the role): Medication teaching needs accurate interpretation of medical terms, which a qualified interpreter provides while protecting confidentiality, and a child should not carry that responsibility or hear distressing details. Using the interpreter for one part and the child for another still exposes the child to medical detail. Teaching the grandson leaves the client without understanding he can use to consent."}, {"id": 730, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 24-year-old man in clinic has a long-standing intense fear of needles and now needs a series of immunizations. He is sweating and says he will faint. The nurse is aware of the idea of graded exposure. Which approach best applies it?", "options": {"A": "Ask him to look away and use distraction, then give the injection straight away", "B": "Give all the immunizations in a single visit to end the fear sooner", "C": "Move stepwise from seeing the equipment to the injection itself, using coping skills", "D": "Give the injection while two staff gently hold him still so that the fear passes sooner"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using distraction alone without gradual exposure", "B": "Flooding him with all injections at once", "D": "Forcing exposure, which can worsen the phobia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Move stepwise from seeing the equipment to the injection itself, using coping skills): Graded exposure reduces a phobia by moving slowly through feared steps while the person uses relaxation or other coping skills, so each success builds confidence. Distraction alone skips the gradual steps. Giving every injection in one visit overwhelms him and may worsen the fear. Phobias often respond well to gradual treatment."}, {"id": 731, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Fourteen years old, a boy asks the clinic nurse for testing for a sexually transmitted infection and begs that his parents not be told. He understands the tests and the follow-up plan. What is the most appropriate action by the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Complete the testing and then post the results in the family chart for access", "B": "Involve the team, assess his understanding, and protect his confidentiality", "C": "Refuse testing until he returns with a parent who can sign the consent", "D": "Call his parents right away, because parents are expected to be told about a minor's care"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Releasing results without his consent", "C": "Refusing care solely because of age", "D": "Assuming parents must be informed of a minor's care"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Involve the team, assess his understanding, and protect his confidentiality): A young person who demonstrates understanding may be able to consent and has a right to confidentiality, subject to limits such as risk of harm, so the nurse brings in the team and applies policy. A blanket duty to inform parents ignores this. Refusing care can cause harm through delay, and placing results where family can see them breaches confidentiality."}, {"id": 732, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On an acute care unit, a registered nurse asks a practical nurse to manage a client with a newly placed central line and unstable blood pressure. The practical nurse has not yet completed the agency's competency for central line care. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Accept the assignment after reading the central line policy before starting any care", "B": "Tell the registered nurse about the competence gap, accept only safe tasks, and ask for support", "C": "Accept the task and ask a colleague to quietly watch over the work", "D": "Decline the central line tasks quietly and leave them for the next shift"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepting work beyond competence after only reading a policy", "C": "Using informal supervision instead of proper assignment", "D": "Leaving unsafe tasks undone without telling the team"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tell the registered nurse about the competence gap, accept only safe tasks, and ask for support): Nurses are accountable for working within their own competence, so the practical nurse communicates the gap, accepts what can be done safely and seeks guidance or orientation. Reading a policy does not replace competency with an unstable client. Quietly declining without telling anyone prevents the team from re-planning. Informal watching does not change that she is responsible for the task performed."}, {"id": 733, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 5-month-old infant in a pediatric clinic has a fever of 38.6 C, and the mother asks if she can give the ibuprofen she has at home. She says it works well for her older child. What should the practical nurse tell her?", "options": {"A": "Give ibuprofen after a feeding to protect the stomach, then monitor his temperature closely", "B": "Use acetaminophen instead at the adult dose and recheck the temperature in one hour", "C": "Do not give ibuprofen under six months, and have the baby assessed by the prescriber", "D": "Alternate ibuprofen with cool baths and call only if the fever lasts two days"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming food makes the drug safe at this age", "B": "Giving an adult dose to an infant", "D": "Waiting two days to seek assessment of a young infant's fever"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Do not give ibuprofen under six months, and have the baby assessed by the prescriber): Ibuprofen is generally not recommended for infants younger than six months because of kidney and bleeding risks, and a fever in a young infant needs a professional assessment. An adult dose of any fever medicine is unsafe for an infant. Waiting two days delays that assessment. Giving ibuprofen with a feeding does not remove the age concern."}, {"id": 734, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mild knee pain from osteoarthritis brings a 66-year-old woman to clinic, and she has chronic kidney disease with an estimated eGFR of 28 mL/min. She also takes a daily blood pressure tablet. She asks to buy an over-the-counter anti-inflammatory like ibuprofen. What should the practical nurse advise her?", "options": {"A": "Take ibuprofen at the lowest dose because mild pain needs only a small amount of it", "B": "Take both ibuprofen and acetaminophen to get better pain control", "C": "Use naproxen instead, since it is gentler on the kidneys than ibuprofen", "D": "Avoid NSAIDs because of her kidneys, and ask the prescriber about acetaminophen"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming a low dose of an NSAID is safe in kidney disease", "B": "Combining analgesics without addressing the kidney risk", "C": "Believing another NSAID is kidney friendly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoid NSAIDs because of her kidneys, and ask the prescriber about acetaminophen): NSAIDs reduce blood flow to the kidneys and can worsen renal function, so they are usually avoided with reduced eGFR, and acetaminophen within the daily limit is the usual alternative. A low dose still carries the risk. Naproxen is also an NSAID. Adding ibuprofen to acetaminophen keeps the kidney risk, so the prescriber should confirm the plan."}, {"id": 735, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a client's home, a practical nurse is changing bedding after the client had a diarrheal illness. The sheets are soiled with stool, and the nurse is wearing gloves and an apron. The household has a washing machine and a laundry basket nearby. How should the soiled linen be handled?", "options": {"A": "Roll it soiled side inward, hold it away from the body, and bag it directly", "B": "Rinse the sheets in the bathroom sink first so that the staining does not set", "C": "Put it in the laundry basket, then bag it after sorting the other linen", "D": "Soak it in a bucket of cold water, then wring it out by hand"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Rinsing in a sink, creating splash and contamination", "C": "Mixing soiled linen with clean laundry before bagging", "D": "Soaking and wringing, which spreads organisms and splashes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Roll it soiled side inward, hold it away from the body, and bag it directly): Soiled linen is handled as little as possible, rolled inward and kept away from the body to limit spread of organisms, then bagged at the point of use. Placing it in a basket with other linen spreads contamination. Soaking and wringing by hand splashes organisms onto the nurse and the room. Rinsing in a household sink contaminates a clean area."}, {"id": 736, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a night shift, the barcode scanner on the medication cart stops working while a practical nurse prepares a 0900 dose of insulin for a client. The charge nurse is busy with an admission. Which action is most appropriate for the nurse to take?", "options": {"A": "Wait to give the insulin until the scanner is repaired later in the day", "B": "Ask the client to confirm the name and give the dose without other checks", "C": "Report the failure, then use the backup process with two independent identifier checks", "D": "Give the insulin as ordered, because the prescriber has already verified the order once today"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying a time-sensitive medication needlessly", "B": "Relying on one verbal identifier from the client", "D": "Skipping verification because the scanner failed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the failure, then use the backup process with two independent identifier checks): A scanner failure is a safety event to report, and the nurse follows the approved backup procedure so right-client and right-drug checks are still completed independently. Giving the drug without checks removes a key safeguard. Insulin is time-sensitive, so a long delay may harm the client. A verbal confirmation alone is not two identifiers."}, {"id": 737, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Park, 19, has anorexia nervosa, has been admitted and is severely underweight. The team plans nutritional rehabilitation. A colleague suggests starting full meals immediately to restore her weight quickly. Which risk should the practical nurse monitor most closely in the first days?", "options": {"A": "Hyperglycemia, which develops because starved cells cannot use the glucose from meals", "B": "Aspiration pneumonia, which develops because meals are started too quickly", "C": "Lactose intolerance, which commonly develops after long periods of poor intake", "D": "Refeeding syndrome, which shifts phosphate and potassium and can cause arrhythmias"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Naming a metabolic effect that is not the key refeeding risk", "B": "Naming a plausible complication unrelated to electrolyte shifts", "C": "Citing a less serious digestive effect"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refeeding syndrome, which shifts phosphate and potassium and can cause arrhythmias): Starting nutrition in a starved client moves phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells and can cause cardiac arrhythmias, so feeding is increased gradually with lab and heart monitoring. Blood glucose is checked but does not explain the sudden life-threatening changes. Aspiration is not the main risk in an alert client, and ulcer or lactose problems are less urgent."}, {"id": 738, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a clinic, a practical nurse is assessing a seated 45-year-old client at rest and has just finished taking a radial pulse. The room is quiet and she has no history of lung disease. The nurse needs an accurate respiratory rate. Which method gives the most reliable reading?", "options": {"A": "Count for 10 seconds while he speaks about his symptoms, then multiply by six", "B": "Tell the client you are about to count breathing, then count for 15 seconds and multiply", "C": "Keep fingers on the wrist as if taking the pulse and count breaths for a minute", "D": "Ask him to lie flat and breathe through his mouth so each breath is clear"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a short count during talking, which distorts the rate", "B": "Alerting the client, who then alters breathing", "D": "Changing his position and breathing pattern before counting"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep fingers on the wrist as if taking the pulse and count breaths for a minute): Clients tend to change their breathing when they know it is being observed, so the nurse continues to look as if taking the pulse and counts for a full minute when rates may be irregular. Announcing the count or asking him to change his breathing alters the rate. A very short count while he talks multiplies a small counting error and can miss irregular patterns."}, {"id": 739, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A seven-year-old child is brought to the emergency department by an ambulance after a fall from a roof with a suspected internal bleed. No parent can be reached, and the physician says the child needs urgent surgery. What is the correct approach to consent?", "options": {"A": "Wait for the hospital's legal office to approve treatment, then begin surgery", "B": "Treat under the emergency exception, and keep trying to reach his parents", "C": "Hold all treatment until a parent is found and consent is given in person", "D": "Ask the surgeon to obtain verbal assent from the child, then begin treatment"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying emergency treatment for legal approval", "C": "Delaying emergency treatment for consent", "D": "Treating a young child's assent as legal consent"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Treat under the emergency exception, and keep trying to reach his parents): When delay would risk death or serious harm and no substitute decision maker can be reached, treatment may proceed under the emergency exception, with documentation of the circumstances and efforts to reach the family. Waiting for a legal office could cost the child's life. A seven-year-old cannot give legal consent, and holding treatment until a parent arrives also delays urgent care."}, {"id": 740, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, a practical nurse is changing a dressing on a client's leg and has used contaminated gauze and a few soiled gloves. The household has regular garbage bins in the kitchen. Where should the nurse place this waste?", "options": {"A": "Seal it in a leak-proof bag and dispose of it under agency biomedical waste policy", "B": "Place it in the household garbage bin because the bag contains no sharp items inside", "C": "Double-bag it in plastic and place it in the client's outdoor recycling bin", "D": "Rinse the gauze and gloves in the sink, then put them in regular garbage"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Putting contaminated waste in regular garbage", "C": "Putting contaminated waste in recycling", "D": "Rinsing contaminated items in a household sink"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Seal it in a leak-proof bag and dispose of it under agency biomedical waste policy): Items soiled with blood or body fluids follow the agency's biomedical waste process, such as sealed bagging and removal, to protect the client's household and waste workers. Regular garbage or recycling exposes others and may breach regulations. Rinsing in a household sink contaminates a clean area and splashes organisms. Leaving waste open in the home risks contact by household members."}, {"id": 741, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Osei, 30, has been newly diagnosed with epilepsy and is starting medication. He feels well and says he plans to keep driving to work tomorrow because he has had no seizure for two weeks. What should the practical nurse tell him?", "options": {"A": "Driving is acceptable after two seizure-free weeks, since the medication is working", "B": "Driving is acceptable if a passenger can take over during a seizure", "C": "Follow licensing rules, which restrict driving until his seizures are controlled", "D": "Driving is allowed if he takes the medication exactly as directed every single morning"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying a short seizure-free period instead of licensing rules", "B": "Believing a passenger makes driving safe", "D": "Assuming adherence alone removes the restriction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Follow licensing rules, which restrict driving until his seizures are controlled): Seizures can occur without warning, so licensing rules and the prescriber's guidance set a seizure-free period before driving is allowed, and the nurse teaches this and refers questions to the prescriber. Two weeks is too short to show control. A passenger cannot take over a moving vehicle in time. Medication adherence is necessary but does not end the restriction."}, {"id": 742, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lavoie, 73, receives home care and has a shallow, moist leg ulcer on the inner ankle with brown skin discoloration and swelling that improves when the leg is raised. Her pedal pulses are strong. Which care is most appropriate for the practical nurse to reinforce?", "options": {"A": "Rub the swollen ankle firmly to push fluid up toward the knee and reduce swelling", "B": "Keep the leg lowered over the edge of the bed to improve blood supply", "C": "Apply firm compression bandages promptly to the leg to reduce swelling and speed healing", "D": "Elevate the leg at rest and apply compression only if the prescriber has ordered it"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Massaging an ulcerated, swollen limb", "B": "Treating venous disease like arterial disease", "C": "Applying compression before an order and pulse check"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Elevate the leg at rest and apply compression only if the prescriber has ordered it): Swelling that eases with elevation, brown staining and strong pulses point to venous insufficiency, so elevation helps and compression is applied under an order after circulation is checked. Dependent positioning suits arterial compromise, not venous. Applying compression without an order is outside safe practice. Massage can damage tissue and dislodge clots."}, {"id": 743, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a 60-year-old man with diabetes is due for a capillary glucose check before his meal. The practical nurse has washed hands, put on gloves and chosen a finger on the side of the fingertip. What should the nurse do next?", "options": {"A": "Confirm the site is dry, puncture it, wipe away the first drop, and test the second", "B": "Puncture a wet site because moisture helps the blood to flow more freely from the finger", "C": "Use the center of the fingertip pad, since it bleeds more and gives a larger drop", "D": "Squeeze the finger hard and use the very first drop so the test is completed quickly"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Testing through moisture which alters the result", "C": "Choosing a more painful central pad", "D": "Squeezing and using the first drop, diluting with tissue fluid"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Confirm the site is dry, puncture it, wipe away the first drop, and test the second): The site must be clean and dry because alcohol, water or residue can alter the reading, and the first drop often contains tissue fluid, so it is wiped away. Hard squeezing dilutes the sample with fluid. A moist site distorts the result. The sides of the fingertip are less painful than the central pad."}, {"id": 744, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 6-year-old boy is at home with his mother after falling from a swing and striking his head. He has vomited twice in two hours and is now unusually sleepy. His mother phones the clinic nurse and asks if she may let him sleep. What should the practical nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Seek urgent assessment now, because these are danger signs after a head injury", "B": "Watch him at home for 24 hours and then decide whether a clinic visit is needed", "C": "Let him sleep and check him briefly every few hours until morning", "D": "Give him a small meal and recheck whether the vomiting settles"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Waiting too long with danger signs", "C": "Allowing sleep without assessment", "D": "Treating vomiting as a stomach complaint"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Seek urgent assessment now, because these are danger signs after a head injury): Repeated vomiting, drowsiness or changes in behaviour after a head injury can indicate raised intracranial pressure or bleeding, which needs urgent assessment rather than home monitoring. Sleeping without evaluation can hide worsening consciousness. A meal does not address the cause. A 24-hour delay risks serious harm."}, {"id": 745, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 3-day-old infant on a pediatric unit needs a capillary blood glucose test. The practical nurse is warming the foot. The infant was born at term and is feeding well, and the test was ordered because of mild jitteriness. The mother is at the bedside. Which site and technique should be used for this heel stick?", "options": {"A": "Puncture the posterior curve of the heel because the skin there is the thinnest and softest", "B": "Puncture the outer side of the heel with a sterile lancet, avoiding the center and back", "C": "Puncture the big toe pad, which is easier to hold still than the heel", "D": "Puncture the center of the heel pad where the most blood is available"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing the posterior heel with a bone risk", "C": "Using a toe, not a recommended infant site", "D": "Choosing the central pad, risking bone injury"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Puncture the outer side of the heel with a sterile lancet, avoiding the center and back): Heel sticks use the lateral or medial plantar surface to avoid the calcaneus and nerves, since a deep or central puncture can injure bone in a small infant. The back of the heel lies close to bone. A toe is not a standard site. Warming the foot first improves blood flow."}, {"id": 746, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ibrahim, 59, is two days after knee replacement surgery. He reports a tight, aching right calf, and the nurse notes it is swollen and warmer than the left. He asks for a calf massage to ease the discomfort. What is the practical nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Massage the calf gently, since this will improve circulation and relieve the ache and swelling", "B": "Apply a warm pack and reassess in the morning before reporting anything", "C": "Do not massage the leg, limit activity, and report promptly for suspected clot", "D": "Ask him to walk the corridor to loosen the muscle and reduce the swelling"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Massaging, which can dislodge a clot", "B": "Delaying reporting while using heat", "D": "Encouraging activity before a clot is excluded"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Do not massage the leg, limit activity, and report promptly for suspected clot): Unilateral calf swelling, warmth and pain after surgery suggest deep vein thrombosis, and massage or vigorous activity could dislodge a clot to the lungs, so the nurse limits movement and reports quickly. Heat and waiting delay diagnosis. The nurse does not diagnose but recognizes and reports the signs."}, {"id": 747, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a neurosurgical unit, a 55-year-old man has a worsening headache and has vomited twice since this morning after a head injury. His level of consciousness is unchanged and his pulse is 84. A colleague says to wait for a slow pulse before calling. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Give an extra dose of analgesic and recheck him at the next routine visit", "B": "Wait for a slow pulse and high blood pressure, since these would confirm the problem", "C": "Report the headache and vomiting now as possible raised pressure and continue checks", "D": "Lay him flat to relieve the headache and note the vomiting in the chart"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a warning sign as simple pain", "B": "Waiting for a late sign", "D": "Lowering the head, which may raise pressure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the headache and vomiting now as possible raised pressure and continue checks): Headache and vomiting can be early signs of rising pressure, while a slow pulse is a late and dangerous change, so waiting for it delays treatment. Pain relief alone hides the trend. Lying flat may increase intracranial pressure, and the head is usually raised unless ordered otherwise. Early reporting allows timely intervention."}, {"id": 748, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Inside a busy medication room, a practical nurse is preparing medications for a 60-year-old client. A colleague asks a quick question about a schedule, and a call bell is ringing nearby. The nurse is also thinking about her lunch break. What is the best way to protect safety?", "options": {"A": "Finish the preparation without interruption, and defer others politely or restart the check", "B": "Answer the colleague briefly, since a quick question rarely affects the preparation of a medication", "C": "Finish quickly to reduce the time that distractions can interfere", "D": "Respond to the call bell first and continue from wherever you stopped"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating interruptions as harmless", "C": "Rushing to beat the distractions", "D": "Resuming mid-task without rechecking"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Finish the preparation without interruption, and defer others politely or restart the check): Interruptions are a known cause of medication errors, so the nurse protects concentration by deferring non-urgent requests and restarts the check if distracted. Even brief questions can break the sequence of checks. Resuming without rechecking can skip a step. Hurrying increases error risk and does not remove the distractions."}, {"id": 749, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Just after an intramuscular injection, a practical nurse is holding a used needle in her hand. The sharps container is on the wall across the room. The client is calm and has no known infections. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Walk to the sharps container and discard the uncapped needle immediately without recapping it", "B": "Recap the needle with two hands before carrying it across the room", "C": "Bend the needle so it cannot be reused and carry it in a pocket", "D": "Place the needle on the tray and discard it with the other supplies later"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Two-handed recapping causes needlesticks", "C": "Bending a needle, risking injury", "D": "Leaving a sharp exposed on a tray"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Walk to the sharps container and discard the uncapped needle immediately without recapping it): The safest practice is to dispose of the uncapped needle at once in an approved container, because recapping, bending or leaving it out causes most needlestick injuries. Carrying it in a pocket is unsafe. Ideally the container is brought to the point of use, and any injury must be reported."}, {"id": 750, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In long-term care, a practical nurse is giving a bed bath to an 82-year-old woman who has fragile skin and a reddened area over her sacrum. She is cooperative but frail. The room is warm and the call bell is within reach. Which technique is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Begin with the perineum and work upward so the dirtiest area is washed before the rest", "B": "Wash from cleanest to dirtiest areas, pat the skin dry, and avoid rubbing red areas", "C": "Use hot water to loosen dirt quickly from the skin during the bath", "D": "Rub the red area firmly to improve circulation and prevent a sore"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reversing the order of cleanest to dirtiest", "C": "Using hot water on fragile skin", "D": "Massaging a pressure-prone area"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Wash from cleanest to dirtiest areas, pat the skin dry, and avoid rubbing red areas): Bathing proceeds from clean to dirty to avoid carrying organisms to cleaner areas, and gentle patting with warm water protects fragile skin. Reddened areas over bony prominences may signal early pressure injury, so rubbing them can cause damage. Hot water increases skin injury risk. Report the reddened area and reposition her."}, {"id": 751, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Her colleague, a registered nurse, asks the practical nurse for her login during a busy clinic afternoon. The workstation is locked, and the colleague says she needs a quick lookup of a lab result and will be careful. How should the practical nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Decline to share it, explain each user needs their own login, and help her sign in", "B": "Give her the password now, then ask the unit to change it after your shift", "C": "Share the login because a registered nurse can be trusted with access to the record", "D": "Log in, then let her use the screen while you stand beside her throughout"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Sharing and relying on a later change", "C": "Sharing credentials based on professional status", "D": "Allowing use under another user's name"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C086", "cluster_text": "At a busy emergency department, a practical nurse is documenting on a shared workstation. She is called to a client in distress, and her session remains open with another client's record on the screen. Later that shift she works with a diabetic client who refuses a bath, and she must also document teaching and a verbal order for potassium. Privacy of client records is a priority.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline to share it, explain each user needs their own login, and help her sign in): Logins identify who accessed and documented information, so sharing them breaches privacy rules and makes records inaccurate, whatever the colleague's role. Letting her use a session logged in under the practical nurse's name records her actions as the nurse's. Changing the password later does not undo the breach."}, {"id": 752, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Urgently called away from the shared workstation, the practical nurse leaves a client's full record on the screen. She expects to return in about ten minutes. The department is crowded, and several visitors and clients are walking past the station. Which action best protects client privacy?", "options": {"A": "Ask a colleague to watch the screen until she returns to the station", "B": "Minimize the window so the record is hidden, then return in a few minutes", "C": "Log off or lock the screen before leaving, even if she will return soon", "D": "Turn the monitor off, since this prevents the record from being seen by passersby"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on a colleague to guard an open session", "B": "Minimizing the window, which leaves the session active", "D": "Turning off the monitor, which leaves the session active"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C086", "cluster_text": "At a busy emergency department, a practical nurse is documenting on a shared workstation. She is called to a client in distress, and her session remains open with another client's record on the screen. Later that shift she works with a diabetic client who refuses a bath, and she must also document teaching and a verbal order for potassium. Privacy of client records is a priority.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Log off or lock the screen before leaving, even if she will return soon): An open session lets anyone view or change a record under her name, so she logs off or locks the screen every time she leaves. Short absences are when unauthorized viewing happens. A colleague watching or a minimized window leaves the session active. Turning off the monitor also leaves it active, and clients cannot protect other clients' information."}, {"id": 753, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On the unit, the nurse documents routine assessments using charting by exception for a client with no changes. The admission assessment was completed on arrival yesterday, and the client has a stable chronic condition. Which statement best describes how charting by exception should be used safely?", "options": {"A": "It records only abnormal findings, so a baseline is optional when the client is stable", "B": "It records findings that differ from defined normal limits, and depends on a baseline", "C": "It uses a checklist of normal findings that the nurse initials once per shift", "D": "It replaces narrative notes for routine care, but not for admission or discharge"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing a baseline is optional for a stable client", "C": "Initialling a checklist of normals without assessing", "D": "Assuming narrative notes are limited to admission and discharge"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C086", "cluster_text": "At a busy emergency department, a practical nurse is documenting on a shared workstation. She is called to a client in distress, and her session remains open with another client's record on the screen. Later that shift she works with a diabetic client who refuses a bath, and she must also document teaching and a verbal order for potassium. Privacy of client records is a priority.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It records findings that differ from defined normal limits, and depends on a baseline): Charting by exception documents deviations from defined norms, so it depends on a clear baseline assessment and agreed standards of what counts as normal. Without a baseline there is nothing to compare, even for a stable client. A signed checklist of normals does not show what was actually assessed. Narrative notes are still needed whenever unusual findings, events or responses occur."}, {"id": 754, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a 58-year-old client tells the practical nurse she does not want her morning bath today. She is alert and says she will wash later. The nurse offers alternatives, and the client still declines. What should the nurse do about documentation?", "options": {"A": "Leave the entry blank, because a refused bath is not an intervention requiring documentation", "B": "Document the refusal, her stated reason, the alternatives offered, and who was informed", "C": "Document only that the bath was refused, without her reason or the alternatives", "D": "Write that the bath was deferred to evening, without noting that she refused"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Not documenting a refusal", "C": "Recording the refusal without reason or alternatives offered", "D": "Using wording that hides the client's refusal"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C086", "cluster_text": "At a busy emergency department, a practical nurse is documenting on a shared workstation. She is called to a client in distress, and her session remains open with another client's record on the screen. Later that shift she works with a diabetic client who refuses a bath, and she must also document teaching and a verbal order for potassium. Privacy of client records is a priority.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Document the refusal, her stated reason, the alternatives offered, and who was informed): A refusal is part of the care record and should show what was offered, the client's stated reason and who was told, which protects the client and the nurse. Leaving it blank implies the care was done or ignored. Recording only that she refused omits detail, and writing deferred hides what happened. Documentation must stay factual and complete."}, {"id": 755, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In a diabetes clinic, a practical nurse teaches a 50-year-old man how to inject insulin. He has type 2 diabetes and has never used insulin before. She finishes the demonstration and wants to document the teaching. Which entry shows the most complete record?", "options": {"A": "Record the pamphlet title and the time the teaching began and ended", "B": "Record that the client appeared to understand the steps and was given a pamphlet to read", "C": "Record what was taught, his return demonstration, his questions, and his understanding", "D": "Record that insulin teaching was given to the client during the visit"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Recording the materials and time, not the response", "B": "Recording a vague impression of understanding", "D": "Documenting only that teaching occurred"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C086", "cluster_text": "At a busy emergency department, a practical nurse is documenting on a shared workstation. She is called to a client in distress, and her session remains open with another client's record on the screen. Later that shift she works with a diabetic client who refuses a bath, and she must also document teaching and a verbal order for potassium. Privacy of client records is a priority.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Record what was taught, his return demonstration, his questions, and his understanding): Teaching documentation should show not only what was taught but how the client responded, such as a correct return demonstration and questions, because that proves learning and guides follow-up. A bare note that teaching was given says nothing about understanding. An impression without evidence is weak. Times and pamphlet names do not show outcomes."}, {"id": 756, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 9-year-old girl in a pediatric clinic has influenza with a fever of 39.0 C, and her father asks the practical nurse whether he may give her the aspirin he keeps at home. Which fact should guide the nurse's reply?", "options": {"A": "Aspirin is preferred for children because it lowers fever faster than acetaminophen does in most cases", "B": "Aspirin is safe for children if given with food to protect the stomach lining", "C": "Aspirin is avoided in children with viral illness because of its link to Reye syndrome", "D": "Aspirin is acceptable if the dose is cut to half of the adult amount"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Thinking speed of fever reduction outweighs harm", "B": "Believing food prevents the serious risk", "D": "Assuming a smaller adult dose removes the risk"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Aspirin is avoided in children with viral illness because of its link to Reye syndrome): Aspirin given during a viral illness such as influenza or varicella has been linked to Reye syndrome, a rare but serious condition causing liver failure and brain swelling, so it is avoided in children. Food only protects the stomach. Dose reduction does not remove the link, and acetaminophen is not shown to be slower in a meaningful way."}, {"id": 757, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Into the upper outer buttock of a 55-year-old man, a practical nurse gave an intramuscular antibiotic. The injection was drawn up correctly and the dose was verified before giving it. Twenty minutes later he reports burning pain down the back of his leg and weakness lifting his foot. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Apply a heat pack to the buttock and recheck the leg in the morning", "B": "Stop injecting at that site, assess the leg, and report a possible nerve injury", "C": "Reassure him that leg pain after an injection is common and will pass in a while", "D": "Massage the injection site firmly to help the medication spread"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying reporting while using heat", "C": "Dismissing a nerve injury as normal discomfort", "D": "Massaging an injured area"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop injecting at that site, assess the leg, and report a possible nerve injury): Burning pain down the leg and foot weakness after a buttock injection suggest the sciatic nerve may have been injured, which needs prompt reporting and assessment, and the ventrogluteal site is the safer choice for future injections. Reassurance without action delays care. Heat and waiting miss the urgency, and massage can worsen the injury."}, {"id": 758, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, the practical nurse is asked by the charge nurse to take over care of a client who needs hourly neurological checks and a drug infusion she has never given and has no orientation for. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Decline all assigned clients until the unit provides full orientation to every task", "B": "Accept the infusion and watch a video on the pump before starting it", "C": "Accept both tasks and ask the client to say if anything seems wrong", "D": "Say which task she cannot do safely, accept the rest, and ask for support"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Refuses the whole assignment instead of negotiating the unsafe task", "B": "Relying on a video instead of competence and orientation", "C": "Relying on the client to detect errors"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Say which task she cannot do safely, accept the rest, and ask for support): Nurses are accountable for recognizing the limits of their competence and for speaking up before accepting work, so she identifies the task she cannot do safely and works with the charge nurse to cover it. Watching a video does not replace orientation for a drug infusion. Relying on the client to spot problems shifts responsibility away from her. Refusing the entire assignment may leave clients without care."}, {"id": 759, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Tran, 66, is four hours after bowel surgery. His pulse has risen from 82 to 112 beats per minute, he is restless, his skin is cool and his blood pressure is still 118/74 mmHg. Which interpretation is most accurate for the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "His blood pressure is within normal range, so nothing needs to be reported just yet", "B": "These are early signs of hemorrhage, since pulse rises before blood pressure falls", "C": "A drop in blood pressure must come first before internal bleeding can be suspected by nurses", "D": "Restlessness and a fast pulse are expected pain responses after major surgery in most clients"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using blood pressure alone to rule out shock", "C": "Waiting for a late sign", "D": "Attributing all changes to pain"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C093", "cluster_text": "A 66-year-old man returns to a surgical unit from the recovery room after bowel surgery. He has an abdominal dressing and a wound drain. The practical nurse is monitoring him over the next several hours, and the registered nurse is caring for other clients on the unit. The client's vital signs are due every thirty minutes tonight, and his wife is waiting outside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (These are early signs of hemorrhage, since pulse rises before blood pressure falls): The body compensates for blood loss with a faster heart rate, restlessness and cool skin, while blood pressure falls late, so these changes after surgery need to be reported at once and the dressing and drains checked. A normal pressure does not exclude bleeding. Pain may raise the pulse but should not be assumed. Waiting for hypotension delays rescue."}, {"id": 760, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two hours later, blood has soaked through the abdominal dressing and the drain shows bright red output. His pulse is 126 and his blood pressure is 96/60 mmHg. The client is pale and anxious. The practical nurse has called for help. Which action should she take while waiting?", "options": {"A": "Remove the soaked dressing to see exactly where the bleeding is coming from, then redress", "B": "Reinforce the dressing with firm pressure, lay him flat, keep him warm, monitor vitals", "C": "Sit him upright and encourage deep breathing to reduce his anxiety", "D": "Give him fluids by mouth to replace the volume he has lost"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Removing the dressing and disturbing clots", "C": "Sitting him up, which can worsen shock", "D": "Giving oral fluids before surgery review"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C093", "cluster_text": "A 66-year-old man returns to a surgical unit from the recovery room after bowel surgery. He has an abdominal dressing and a wound drain. The practical nurse is monitoring him over the next several hours, and the registered nurse is caring for other clients on the unit. The client's vital signs are due every thirty minutes tonight, and his wife is waiting outside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reinforce the dressing with firm pressure, lay him flat, keep him warm, monitor vitals): Reinforcing the dressing keeps clots in place, and a flat position with warmth supports circulation while vital signs track his condition until the surgical team arrives. Removing the dressing can restart bleeding. An upright position can lower blood flow to the brain. Oral fluids are risky if he may return to surgery."}, {"id": 761, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before the first bowel surgery, the practical nurse had taught the client about the procedure. He now says, while holding a pillow against his incision, that he will cough and breathe deeply every hour as taught. Which interpretation of his action is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "He is expressing pain, so request a stronger analgesic before he coughs again", "B": "He is avoiding movement, so tell him to cough only when staff are present", "C": "He is repeating taught words, so retest his recall tomorrow before any reinforcement", "D": "He understood and is splinting correctly, so reinforce it and document his response."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reading splinting as a request for more analgesia", "B": "Limiting coughing to times when staff are present", "C": "Delaying reinforcement to retest recall"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C093", "cluster_text": "A 66-year-old man returns to a surgical unit from the recovery room after bowel surgery. He has an abdominal dressing and a wound drain. The practical nurse is monitoring him over the next several hours, and the registered nurse is caring for other clients on the unit. The client's vital signs are due every thirty minutes tonight, and his wife is waiting outside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (He understood and is splinting correctly, so reinforce it and document his response.): Holding a pillow over the incision while coughing is splinting, a taught technique that reduces pain and protects the wound, and his statement shows learning. Reinforcing it and documenting his response is the best step. Asking for stronger analgesia assumes pain he did not report. Limiting coughing to staff presence reduces deep breathing, and delaying reinforcement wastes a teaching moment."}, {"id": 762, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before surgery, the client had signed a consent form after the surgeon's explanation. During an evening check, he tells the practical nurse that he had not really understood what would be done. Which statement about consent best guides the nurse's action?", "options": {"A": "A signed form proves consent is valid, even when the client voices doubts later", "B": "Consent needs the client's signature and a witness to be legally valid", "C": "Consent must be informed and voluntary, so report his concerns to the surgeon first", "D": "The nurse may explain the full surgical risks and take over the surgeon's own discussion"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating the signature as proof despite later doubts", "B": "Reducing consent to a signature and witness", "D": "Taking over the surgeon's disclosure role"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C093", "cluster_text": "A 66-year-old man returns to a surgical unit from the recovery room after bowel surgery. He has an abdominal dressing and a wound drain. The practical nurse is monitoring him over the next several hours, and the registered nurse is caring for other clients on the unit. The client's vital signs are due every thirty minutes tonight, and his wife is waiting outside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Consent must be informed and voluntary, so report his concerns to the surgeon first): Valid consent depends on the client having understood the nature, risks and alternatives, so a signature given without understanding does not meet that standard, and the nurse reports the concern so the surgeon can re-explain. A form alone is not proof. Explaining surgical risk in detail belongs to the surgeon, and the nurse supports the process."}, {"id": 763, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor, 26, lives in the community. His sister reports that he rarely speaks, stays in his room, and shows almost no facial expression, though he denies hearing voices. He has not bathed for weeks, and she brought old magazines to the visit. Which interpretation of his presentation is most accurate for the nurse to report?", "options": {"A": "Flat expression and withdrawal are positive symptoms that respond within a week of medication.", "B": "Without hallucinations the psychosis is inactive, so the withdrawal reflects poor motivation only.", "C": "These are negative symptoms of schizophrenia, which can persist even without hallucinations.", "D": "The picture fits early mania, because reduced speech and self-care appear at its start."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confusing negative symptoms with positive symptoms and their treatment response", "B": "Believing hallucinations must be present for schizophrenia to be active", "D": "Mixing up depressive or negative features with the elevated mood of mania"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (These are negative symptoms of schizophrenia, which can persist even without hallucinations.): The social withdrawal, flat affect, and neglected hygiene are negative symptoms, meaning a loss of normal function, and they do not depend on hallucinations. Treating the missing voices as proof that the illness is quiet overlooks that negative symptoms often cause the most long-term disability. Positive symptoms such as hallucinations tend to respond first to medication, so calling flat affect positive reverses the idea. Report the pattern to the RN rather than labelling it laziness."}, {"id": 764, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Priya notices the nurse drawing up a needle for Amara's thigh and asks why it is needed when Amara looks perfectly healthy. Her partner wonders whether drops by mouth would do instead. Which explanation of the vitamin K injection should the nurse give the parents now?", "options": {"A": "It helps the liver clear bilirubin faster and lowers the chance of jaundice.", "B": "Newborns have low vitamin K stores, so the injection prevents bleeding.", "C": "It is given only after a newborn shows bruising or bleeding from the cord.", "D": "Oral drops alone give equally reliable protection, so the injection can be declined."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confusing vitamin K with phototherapy or bilirubin management", "C": "Waiting for bleeding before giving prophylaxis instead of preventing it", "D": "Treating oral vitamin K as a full substitute for the injection"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C091", "cluster_text": "Baby Amara is a term newborn, 6 hours old, born vaginally to Priya, a first-time mother, and now on a postnatal unit. Birth weight was 3.4 kg and Apgar scores were 9 and 9. Priya and her partner are nervous and ask the practical nurse to explain routine newborn care before going home on day 2. The nurse practitioner has written orders for standard newborn prophylaxis and routine monitoring.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Newborns have low vitamin K stores, so the injection prevents bleeding.): Newborns are born with low vitamin K stores and a sterile gut, so the injection gives dependable protection against bleeding in the first weeks. Oral drops are absorbed less predictably and are not the standard single prophylaxis in this unit's orders, which is why Amara's partner's idea falls short. Vitamin K has no role in bilirubin clearance, so the jaundice explanation misses the purpose. Prevention is the goal, so the dose is given soon after birth, not after bleeding appears."}, {"id": 765, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Amara is feeding well, and her eyes were treated with erythromycin ointment earlier. Priya now asks how to care for the cord stump at home, since her mother says to dab it with alcohol at every diaper change. What should the nurse teach Priya about cord care?", "options": {"A": "Dab the stump with alcohol at each diaper change to speed drying and prevent infection.", "B": "Cover the stump with an airtight dressing so the cord separates sooner.", "C": "Soak the stump in warm water at bath time to soften the crust.", "D": "Keep the stump clean and dry, fold the diaper below it, and watch for redness."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Following an outdated routine of alcohol application", "B": "Sealing the stump so it stays moist and delays drying", "C": "Wetting the stump when it should be kept dry"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C091", "cluster_text": "Baby Amara is a term newborn, 6 hours old, born vaginally to Priya, a first-time mother, and now on a postnatal unit. Birth weight was 3.4 kg and Apgar scores were 9 and 9. Priya and her partner are nervous and ask the practical nurse to explain routine newborn care before going home on day 2. The nurse practitioner has written orders for standard newborn prophylaxis and routine monitoring.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep the stump clean and dry, fold the diaper below it, and watch for redness.): Dry cord care lets the stump dry and fall off naturally, and folding the diaper below it keeps urine and stool away. Alcohol at each change was once routine, but current practice favours simple dry care, and it can irritate the skin. An airtight dressing or soaking keeps the area moist, which slows separation and invites infection. Teach Priya to report redness spreading onto the abdomen, pus, odour, or fever."}, {"id": 766, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Amara is 6 hours old with a heart rate of 140 beats per minute, a respiratory rate of 50 breaths per minute, and an axillary temperature of 36.8 C. She is pink, feeding, and settled in the bassinet. Which interpretation of these findings is correct?", "options": {"A": "All three values fall within expected newborn ranges, so keep monitoring routinely.", "B": "The respiratory rate of 50 is elevated, so begin oxygen and notify the practitioner.", "C": "A heart rate of 140 shows tachycardia, so hold the next feeding until reassessed.", "D": "A temperature of 36.8 C is low, so use a heat lamp to reach 38 C."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Applying adult respiratory norms to a newborn", "C": "Misreading a normal newborn heart rate as tachycardia", "D": "Treating a normal temperature as hypothermia and overheating the baby"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C091", "cluster_text": "Baby Amara is a term newborn, 6 hours old, born vaginally to Priya, a first-time mother, and now on a postnatal unit. Birth weight was 3.4 kg and Apgar scores were 9 and 9. Priya and her partner are nervous and ask the practical nurse to explain routine newborn care before going home on day 2. The nurse practitioner has written orders for standard newborn prophylaxis and routine monitoring.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (All three values fall within expected newborn ranges, so keep monitoring routinely.): A newborn's expected respiratory rate is about 30 to 60 breaths per minute, heart rate about 110 to 160, and axillary temperature around 36.5 to 37.5 C, so Amara's values are all normal. Applying adult norms makes the rate of 50 look too fast, which would trigger unnecessary oxygen. The heart rate fits the normal newborn range, so holding a feed adds no benefit. A heat lamp to 38 C would risk overheating a baby who is already within the expected range."}, {"id": 767, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a home visit, Amara is 4 days old and weighs 3.23 kg, down from a birth weight of 3.4 kg. She feeds eight times in 24 hours, has six wet diapers, and passes yellow stools. Priya is worried about the weight. What is the best interpretation and response?", "options": {"A": "The loss means dehydration, so arrange same-day transfer to the emergency department.", "B": "A loss near five percent is expected, so reinforce breastfeeding.", "C": "The scale is likely inaccurate, so wait until loss reaches ten percent before acting.", "D": "The loss signals poor intake, so start formula supplementation after every feed today."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Escalating to emergency care without signs of dehydration", "C": "Dismissing the measurement instead of interpreting the whole picture", "D": "Treating a normal early weight loss as a feeding failure"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C091", "cluster_text": "Baby Amara is a term newborn, 6 hours old, born vaginally to Priya, a first-time mother, and now on a postnatal unit. Birth weight was 3.4 kg and Apgar scores were 9 and 9. Priya and her partner are nervous and ask the practical nurse to explain routine newborn care before going home on day 2. The nurse practitioner has written orders for standard newborn prophylaxis and routine monitoring.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A loss near five percent is expected, so reinforce breastfeeding.): Amara has lost about 5 percent of her birth weight, which is within the expected early loss for a breastfed newborn, and her feeding frequency and wet diapers show adequate intake. Starting formula or sending her to hospital would respond to a problem the data do not show. Blaming the scale ignores the supportive findings and delays reassurance. Encourage Priya to continue feeding on cue and arrange the routine weight check."}, {"id": 768, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Jordan hears that a colleague was named in a regulatory complaint about a client fall. The employer already carries liability coverage, so Jordan asks Sam whether buying personal liability protection is worthwhile. Sam has worked in long-term care for eleven years. Which response best reflects a nurse's accountability?", "options": {"A": "Personal liability protection is needed only by nurses in independent practice or home care.", "B": "Employer coverage may not protect the nurse in a complaint, so personal protection is wise.", "C": "The professional association handles complaints automatically, so staff nurses need no separate protection.", "D": "Employer coverage extends to regulatory complaints, so personal protection adds cost without benefit."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Limiting liability risk to independent practice settings", "C": "Assuming membership in an association replaces insurance", "D": "Assuming the employer's policy covers the nurse in regulatory matters"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C085", "cluster_text": "Jordan Lee finished a practical nursing program in June and is waiting for the results of the registration exam. A long-term care home has posted a job for a Licensed Practical Nurse, and a hospital has offered Jordan a casual position. Jordan also expects to move to another province next year. Jordan's mentor, Sam, a practical nurse, answers questions about liability, titles, renewal, and moving between jurisdictions.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Employer coverage may not protect the nurse in a complaint, so personal protection is wise.): Each nurse is personally accountable for practice, and an employer's policy protects the employer first, so it may not cover a regulatory proceeding about the nurse. Believing employer coverage reaches those complaints is the common error in this case. Liability risk applies in hospitals and long-term care too, not only in independent practice. Associations offer support, but coverage terms differ, so a nurse should confirm what protection exists."}, {"id": 769, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "The long-term care home's posting lists the title Licensed Practical Nurse, while a hospital ad asks only for a nurse. Jordan, who recently graduated, asks Sam who may use these titles in Canada. The ad also mentions a signing bonus. Which statement about protected titles is accurate?", "options": {"A": "A graduate may use the title once the college issues the diploma.", "B": "Nursing titles are protected, and only registered people may use them.", "C": "Protected titles apply only to registered nurses, so practical nurse titles are open.", "D": "Any care provider employed in a health setting may use the title nurse."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Equating program completion with registration", "C": "Believing practical nurse titles are unregulated", "D": "Treating protected titles as open to any care worker"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C085", "cluster_text": "Jordan Lee finished a practical nursing program in June and is waiting for the results of the registration exam. A long-term care home has posted a job for a Licensed Practical Nurse, and a hospital has offered Jordan a casual position. Jordan also expects to move to another province next year. Jordan's mentor, Sam, a practical nurse, answers questions about liability, titles, renewal, and moving between jurisdictions.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Nursing titles are protected, and only registered people may use them.): Nursing titles are protected by legislation, so using one means being registered with the regulatory body, not just working in a health role or finishing a program. Allowing any supervised provider to use the title ignores that the public relies on it to signal regulated accountability. A diploma shows education but registration is the legal step. Practical nurse titles are protected as well, so employers cannot define them freely."}, {"id": 770, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "While awaiting exam results, Jordan is asked to sign chart entries as LPN during a casual orientation shift. The manager says the title is only temporary. Jordan has completed the program but is not yet registered, and the unit is short staffed. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Use the protected title if the manager approves it in writing for orientation.", "B": "Sign as LPN with a note that exam results are pending.", "C": "Use only a graduate designation the regulator allows until registered.", "D": "Sign as LPN, because the program is complete and the result is only a formality."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing manager approval can override the protected-title rule", "B": "Using the title with a disclaimer instead of waiting for registration", "D": "Assuming completed education grants the title"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C085", "cluster_text": "Jordan Lee finished a practical nursing program in June and is waiting for the results of the registration exam. A long-term care home has posted a job for a Licensed Practical Nurse, and a hospital has offered Jordan a casual position. Jordan also expects to move to another province next year. Jordan's mentor, Sam, a practical nurse, answers questions about liability, titles, renewal, and moving between jurisdictions.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use only a graduate designation the regulator allows until registered.): A protected title is earned by registration, so Jordan should use only the designation that the regulator allows for graduates until results and registration are confirmed. A manager cannot authorize use of a title that legislation reserves for registrants. A note that results are pending still presents Jordan as holding the title. Misrepresenting credentials can lead to a complaint and could affect later registration."}, {"id": 771, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At annual renewal, Jordan, now registered, tells Sam that renewal is automatic once the fee is paid because nobody checks anything else. Sam has just finished her own renewal and recalls declaring several items online. Which statement about registration renewal is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Renewal depends on the fee plus a signed employer letter confirming each nurse's hours worked.", "B": "Paying the fee keeps registration active, and other requirements apply only if audited.", "C": "Renewal needs the fee plus a new registration exam every five years.", "D": "Renewal needs the fee plus declarations such as practice hours and continuing competence."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Replacing the nurse's own declarations with an employer letter", "B": "Treating audit as the only time requirements count", "C": "Inventing a repeat exam requirement"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C085", "cluster_text": "Jordan Lee finished a practical nursing program in June and is waiting for the results of the registration exam. A long-term care home has posted a job for a Licensed Practical Nurse, and a hospital has offered Jordan a casual position. Jordan also expects to move to another province next year. Jordan's mentor, Sam, a practical nurse, answers questions about liability, titles, renewal, and moving between jurisdictions.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Renewal needs the fee plus declarations such as practice hours and continuing competence.): Regulators require nurses to renew by paying the fee and attesting to meeting requirements such as recent practice hours and continuing competence. The nurse makes these declarations personally, not through an employer letter. A repeat registration exam is not part of routine renewal. Requirements apply whether or not an audit is selected, so declaring them honestly is part of accountability."}, {"id": 772, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Next year Jordan will move from one province to another for work and asks Sam how registration carries over. A friend says a current licence works anywhere in Canada without any paperwork, and that employers handle the details. What should Sam advise Jordan about moving?", "options": {"A": "Apply to the new regulator, which may recognize the current registration.", "B": "Jordan only needs to notify the new employer, who will verify the licence.", "C": "The current registration is valid there, so Jordan may start working on arrival.", "D": "Jordan may work under the home registration while the new application is pending."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Leaving registration to the employer instead of applying personally", "C": "Assuming a licence is valid in any province automatically", "D": "Practising while an application is pending because the employer allows it"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C085", "cluster_text": "Jordan Lee finished a practical nursing program in June and is waiting for the results of the registration exam. A long-term care home has posted a job for a Licensed Practical Nurse, and a hospital has offered Jordan a casual position. Jordan also expects to move to another province next year. Jordan's mentor, Sam, a practical nurse, answers questions about liability, titles, renewal, and moving between jurisdictions.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Apply to the new regulator, which may recognize the current registration.): Each jurisdiction's regulator grants the right to practise within its own borders, so Jordan must apply to the new regulator even though labour mobility agreements may smooth recognition. Starting work on arrival assumes a national licence that does not exist. An employer cannot permit practice before registration is granted. Verification by an employer does not replace the nurse's own application."}, {"id": 773, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A 15-year-old attends a youth clinic with her father for a sports physical. She answers in single words while he speaks for her. The nurse needs a confidential health history that includes mood and sexual health. The clinic is busy this afternoon. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Ask her yes or no questions quickly so her father does not feel excluded.", "B": "Promise that nothing she says will be shared with her father or the team.", "C": "Direct all questions to her father, since she is a minor and he gives consent.", "D": "Offer time alone, explain confidentiality limits, and ask open questions."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using closed questions that limit disclosure", "B": "Promising absolute confidentiality that cannot be kept", "C": "Talking only to the parent and excluding the teen"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Offer time alone, explain confidentiality limits, and ask open questions.): Adolescents give more honest information when part of the visit is private, and explaining the limits of confidentiality, such as safety concerns, keeps trust honest. Speaking only to the father silences the client. Yes or no questions block the detail needed for mood and sexual health. A blanket promise of secrecy is wrong because safety risks must be reported, so the limits must be stated."}, {"id": 774, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Her transfer list shows no furosemide, yet the home medication list and her daughter both say this 85-year-old woman takes it daily. She has just arrived at a long-term care home from hospital, and the hospital orders are signed. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Compare the sources, report the discrepancy to the prescriber, and hold doubtful doses.", "B": "Give the transfer-list medications and correct differences at the pharmacist's next visit.", "C": "Remove home medications missing from the transfer list, since the hospital stopped them.", "D": "Wait to reconcile until discharge, since the admission orders are already signed."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Giving unverified orders and postponing clarification", "C": "Assuming omission means the hospital discontinued the drug", "D": "Delaying reconciliation to discharge when risk is highest at transfer"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Compare the sources, report the discrepancy to the prescriber, and hold doubtful doses.): Transfers are a high-risk point for omissions, so the nurse compares the transfer orders with the best possible medication history and brings any difference to the prescriber. Waiting for discharge ignores that an omitted diuretic can harm her now. Giving orders as listed and fixing them later leaves the gap open for days. Assuming the hospital stopped furosemide is a guess, and only the prescriber can confirm it."}, {"id": 775, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Reyes's Braden scale score on admission is 12, which places her at high risk of pressure injury, and her skin is intact. The care aide asks whether turning her can wait until a red area appears. What should the nurse do now?", "options": {"A": "Reassess monthly with the scale, because risk changes slowly in long-term care.", "B": "Apply barrier cream to intact skin and defer repositioning until the care conference.", "C": "Wait for skin breakdown before turning, since the score predicts but does not direct care.", "D": "Begin repositioning and a support surface now, because the score shows high risk."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Spacing reassessment too widely for a high-risk client", "B": "Substituting a skin product for pressure relief", "C": "Waiting for damage before preventing it"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C094", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dolores Reyes, 88, lives in a long-term care home. She is bedbound after a hip fracture, eats about half of her meals, and is sometimes incontinent of urine. Her daughter is worried about her skin and her weight. The practical nurse works the day shift with a registered nurse and a care aide, and the home follows a skin integrity program.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Begin repositioning and a support surface now, because the score shows high risk.): A Braden score of 12 signals high risk, and the purpose of the tool is to start prevention before damage appears, so turning and a support surface begin now. Waiting for redness means the injury has already started. Monthly reassessment is too infrequent when her condition and mobility can change quickly. Barrier cream protects from moisture but does nothing about pressure, so repositioning cannot be postponed."}, {"id": 776, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two weeks later, Mrs. Reyes has a reddened left heel that stays red when pressed. The skin is intact and slightly warm. Her daughter offers to massage lotion into the area during her afternoon visit and asks whether that will help. What is the best nursing response?", "options": {"A": "Apply a warm pack to the heel to improve blood flow.", "B": "Float the heel on a pillow under the calf, avoid massage, and report to the RN.", "C": "Cover the heel with dry gauze and leave it resting on the mattress.", "D": "Massage the area gently with lotion to increase circulation and fade the redness."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying heat to tissue that needs pressure relief", "C": "Protecting the area without relieving pressure", "D": "Massaging tissue that is already compromised"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C094", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dolores Reyes, 88, lives in a long-term care home. She is bedbound after a hip fracture, eats about half of her meals, and is sometimes incontinent of urine. Her daughter is worried about her skin and her weight. The practical nurse works the day shift with a registered nurse and a care aide, and the home follows a skin integrity program.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Float the heel on a pillow under the calf, avoid massage, and report to the RN.): Non-blanchable redness over intact skin is a stage 1 pressure injury, and the first priority is to remove pressure by floating the heel and reporting it. Massage can damage fragile tissue and deepen the injury. Gauze alone leaves the weight on the heel, so the cause continues. Heat does not relieve pressure and may add to tissue damage."}, {"id": 777, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Reyes needs repositioning. She is on her back with the head of the bed raised for lunch, her sacrum is warm, and her hip fracture was repaired on the right. The nurse plans her next position. Which position best reduces pressure and shear?", "options": {"A": "On her back with a rolled towel under the ankles and the sacrum unchanged.", "B": "A full 90 degree side-lying position with the repaired hip resting on the mattress.", "C": "High head elevation between meals, since sitting up spreads her weight evenly.", "D": "A 30 degree side-lying tilt with pillows and the heels floated free."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offloading only the ankles and leaving the sacral area loaded", "B": "Loading the bony trochanter with a full lateral turn", "C": "Using high head elevation that increases shear at the sacrum"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C094", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dolores Reyes, 88, lives in a long-term care home. She is bedbound after a hip fracture, eats about half of her meals, and is sometimes incontinent of urine. Her daughter is worried about her skin and her weight. The practical nurse works the day shift with a registered nurse and a care aide, and the home follows a skin integrity program.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A 30 degree side-lying tilt with pillows and the heels floated free.): A 30 degree tilt moves weight onto the fleshy buttock rather than the bony hip, and floating the heels removes pressure from another high-risk area. A 90 degree turn places the trochanter on the mattress and adds pressure to a surgical hip. Prolonged high head elevation makes her slide and increases shear over the sacrum. A towel under the ankles helps the heels but keeps the sacrum under pressure."}, {"id": 778, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Reyes's daughter notes that her mother has lost weight and eats only half of each meal. She says this is just part of getting older and that her mother never ate much anyway. The nurse has the weight record in hand. Which statement by the nurse is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Unplanned weight loss with poor intake signals malnutrition risk and needs screening.", "B": "Weight loss at her age is normal aging and needs no further assessment.", "C": "Her low intake is personal preference, so respect it and document the refusal.", "D": "Offer a vitamin supplement and recheck her weight in six months before involving anyone."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Attributing weight loss to normal aging", "C": "Treating poor intake as preference with no assessment", "D": "Delaying team involvement while relying on supplements"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C094", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dolores Reyes, 88, lives in a long-term care home. She is bedbound after a hip fracture, eats about half of her meals, and is sometimes incontinent of urine. Her daughter is worried about her skin and her weight. The practical nurse works the day shift with a registered nurse and a care aide, and the home follows a skin integrity program.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Unplanned weight loss with poor intake signals malnutrition risk and needs screening.): Unplanned weight loss and reduced intake in an older adult are warning signs for malnutrition, which also slows wound healing and raises pressure injury risk. Calling it normal aging closes off assessment. Documenting refusal alone does not look for causes such as swallowing, dentition, or mood. A supplement and a six month wait leave her at risk, so screening and referral should start now."}, {"id": 779, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, the charge nurse prepares shift assignments at 0645. Last week assignments rotated every shift to give everyone variety, and two clients said they never saw the same nurse twice. A new graduate is also joining. How should the practical nurse recommend assigning clients?", "options": {"A": "Rotate assignments each shift so every nurse gains experience with all clients.", "B": "Let nurses choose clients freely, since personal choice improves motivation and satisfaction.", "C": "Keep the same nurse with the same clients across shifts where possible.", "D": "Assign by room proximity, since it saves walking time and speeds task completion."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Rotating assignments at the cost of continuity", "B": "Letting preference decide instead of client needs and competence", "D": "Prioritizing walking distance over client needs"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep the same nurse with the same clients across shifts where possible.): Continuity lets the nurse notice small changes in condition and lets clients build trust, so assignments should stay stable where possible while still matching client needs to nurse competence. Rotating each shift loses that knowledge and was the source of the clients' complaint. Room proximity saves steps but ignores acuity and skill match. Free choice can leave complex clients with a mismatched nurse."}, {"id": 780, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a well-baby clinic, a nurse prepares to give an intramuscular vaccine to an 8-month-old who weighs 8.5 kg and is sitting on his mother's lap. The mother mentions she has just returned from a trip. Which site is most appropriate for the injection?", "options": {"A": "The deltoid, since it is the usual vaccine site for school-aged children and adults.", "B": "The rectus femoris at the front of the thigh, since it is easiest to reach.", "C": "The dorsogluteal muscle, since it offers the largest muscle mass available.", "D": "The vastus lateralis in the anterolateral thigh, where infant muscle is adequate."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying the adult and child site to an infant", "B": "Selecting an easier but less recommended thigh muscle", "C": "Choosing the gluteal site where nerves are at risk in infants"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The vastus lateralis in the anterolateral thigh, where infant muscle is adequate.): In infants the vastus lateralis is the preferred intramuscular site because it is the best developed muscle and is away from major nerves and vessels. The deltoid is too small until a child is older and walking. The dorsogluteal site is poorly developed in infants and lies near the sciatic nerve. The rectus femoris is not the recommended thigh muscle for routine injections."}, {"id": 781, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The written order reads give intramuscularly, left deltoid, for a 4-month-old attending clinic for vaccines. The mother is holding the baby seated and the nurse recognizes this site is unusual for the age. Another nurse says to follow the order as written. What is the best interpretation and action?", "options": {"A": "Give it in the deltoid as written, since orders must be followed exactly.", "B": "Give it in the dorsogluteal site, since the order names the arm only as a suggestion.", "C": "Clarify with the prescriber and use the thigh, since the deltoid is unsuitable.", "D": "Give it subcutaneously in the upper arm, since the deltoid cannot hold the volume."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Following an unsafe order without questioning it", "B": "Deciding on a different site independently and treating the order as optional", "D": "Changing the route without an order"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Clarify with the prescriber and use the thigh, since the deltoid is unsuitable.): The nurse is accountable for questioning an order that does not fit the client, and for an infant the correct intramuscular site is the anterolateral thigh. Giving the injection as written places the vaccine into muscle that is too small. Switching to subcutaneous changes the route without authority and may reduce vaccine effect. Choosing another site alone treats the order as a suggestion instead of clarifying it with the prescriber."}, {"id": 782, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the clinic, a client who is 32 weeks pregnant stands clutching her throat, unable to cough, speak, or breathe. She is conscious, and the nurse stands behind her while a colleague calls for help. Which action follows current basic life support teaching for this situation?", "options": {"A": "Give chest thrusts at the centre of the breastbone until the object clears.", "B": "Give five back blows, then abdominal thrusts with hands above the uterus.", "C": "Help her lie on her left side and call the physician.", "D": "Give abdominal thrusts just below the ribs, as for any conscious adult."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Combining back blows with abdominal thrusts near the uterus", "C": "Delaying treatment of a complete airway obstruction", "D": "Using abdominal thrusts on a pregnant woman"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C088", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse is the first responder during an emergencies training day at a health centre. Four situations arise in sequence: a choking client, a choking infant, a collapse in a dining room, and a ward reaction to medication. The nurse holds current basic life support certification, has an automated external defibrillator nearby, and works with a registered nurse who is on the way to each event.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give chest thrusts at the centre of the breastbone until the object clears.): In late pregnancy the enlarged uterus makes abdominal thrusts unsafe and less effective, so chest thrusts are used in the same position as chest compressions. Standard abdominal thrusts risk injury to the fetus. Placing the hands above the uterus still compresses the abdomen. Lying her down delays relief of a complete obstruction, so thrusts must start immediately."}, {"id": 783, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a daycare, a 6-month-old infant is silent, turning blue, and unable to cry after chewing on a toy. He is conscious, the nurse has him in her arms, and a worker is calling for help. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Give five back blows face down along the forearm, then five chest thrusts face up.", "B": "Hold the infant upside down by the ankles and pat the back.", "C": "Give abdominal thrusts using two fingers below the ribs, since infants cough weakly.", "D": "Sweep the mouth blindly with a finger, then give rescue breaths."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using an unsafe inverted hold in place of the correct sequence", "C": "Using abdominal thrusts, which can injure an infant's organs", "D": "Sweeping the mouth blindly and pushing the object deeper"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C088", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse is the first responder during an emergencies training day at a health centre. Four situations arise in sequence: a choking client, a choking infant, a collapse in a dining room, and a ward reaction to medication. The nurse holds current basic life support certification, has an automated external defibrillator nearby, and works with a registered nurse who is on the way to each event.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give five back blows face down along the forearm, then five chest thrusts face up.): For a conscious choking infant the sequence is five back blows followed by five chest thrusts, repeated until the object clears or he becomes unresponsive. Abdominal thrusts can injure the infant's liver and other organs. A blind sweep can push the object further down. Holding him by the ankles is unsafe and does not follow any recommended sequence."}, {"id": 784, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a dining room, an adult who was choking slumps to the floor and becomes unresponsive with no breathing. The nurse reaches him as other diners crowd in. A tray lies nearby and a colleague runs for the defibrillator. What is the next action?", "options": {"A": "Give two rescue breaths first and wait for the RN before compressing.", "B": "Sweep the mouth blindly to remove the object, then check his pulse for a minute.", "C": "Continue abdominal thrusts on the floor until the airway clears, before any compressions.", "D": "Lower him to the floor, call for help, and start chest compressions."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for a colleague before starting compressions", "B": "Sweeping blindly and delaying life support", "C": "Continuing a technique meant for conscious clients"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C088", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse is the first responder during an emergencies training day at a health centre. Four situations arise in sequence: a choking client, a choking infant, a collapse in a dining room, and a ward reaction to medication. The nurse holds current basic life support certification, has an automated external defibrillator nearby, and works with a registered nurse who is on the way to each event.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lower him to the floor, call for help, and start chest compressions.): Once a choking client becomes unresponsive, the nurse treats him as a cardiac arrest, so help is called and compressions start, which can also dislodge the object. A blind sweep risks pushing it deeper and wastes time. Abdominal thrusts are for a conscious person and delay compressions. Waiting for the registered nurse withholds circulation, so compressions begin immediately."}, {"id": 785, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ten minutes after an intravenous antibiotic started on the ward, a client develops widespread hives, hoarseness, and lip swelling. Her blood pressure is falling and the infusion is still running. The nurse has an order for diphenhydramine and an anaphylaxis protocol. What is the priority action?", "options": {"A": "Stop the infusion, call for help, and give intramuscular epinephrine per protocol.", "B": "Slow the infusion rate and apply cool cloths to the hives.", "C": "Keep the infusion running and recheck oxygen saturation, since hoarseness suggests dryness.", "D": "Give the ordered diphenhydramine first, then reassess before deciding on epinephrine."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Slowing instead of stopping the offending drug", "C": "Mistaking airway swelling for a minor symptom", "D": "Giving an antihistamine before the life-saving drug"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C088", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse is the first responder during an emergencies training day at a health centre. Four situations arise in sequence: a choking client, a choking infant, a collapse in a dining room, and a ward reaction to medication. The nurse holds current basic life support certification, has an automated external defibrillator nearby, and works with a registered nurse who is on the way to each event.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop the infusion, call for help, and give intramuscular epinephrine per protocol.): Hives with hoarseness and falling blood pressure signal anaphylaxis, so the offending drug is stopped and epinephrine given intramuscularly under the protocol without delay. Antihistamines work too slowly and do not treat airway swelling or shock. Slowing the infusion still exposes her to the drug. Hoarseness here suggests laryngeal edema, so waiting to see what happens is dangerous."}, {"id": 786, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ali is due for transport to dialysis in one hour. His blood pressure this morning is 106/60 mmHg, and his antihypertensive is scheduled now. He feels well, ate breakfast, and has no dizziness. What should the nurse do before giving the dose?", "options": {"A": "Give the dose on schedule, since the drug keeps his pressure controlled during treatment.", "B": "Give half the dose now and the rest after dialysis, using personal judgment.", "C": "Skip the dose without reporting, because dialysis removes the drug anyway.", "D": "Recheck his pressure and ask the prescriber about timing, since dialysis may lower it."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving a blood pressure drug before dialysis without checking", "B": "Changing a dose independently, which is outside scope", "C": "Omitting a drug without informing the prescriber"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C092", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hassan Ali, 78, lives in a long-term care home. He has heart failure and chronic kidney disease, receives hemodialysis three mornings a week at a hospital unit, and has pitting edema in both ankles. His morning medications include an antihypertensive. A practical nurse cares for him with the registered nurse and reports changes in his condition promptly to the team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Recheck his pressure and ask the prescriber about timing, since dialysis may lower it.): Dialysis removes fluid and can drop blood pressure, so a dose given before it, with a pressure already at 106/60, may cause hypotension during treatment. The nurse rechecks and asks the prescriber, who decides on holding or timing. Splitting a dose is a prescribing decision outside practical nurse scope. Skipping without reporting leaves the team unaware, and not every antihypertensive is removed by dialysis."}, {"id": 787, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ali's left ankle shows a pit about 4 mm deep after five seconds of pressure, and the skin rebounds in about 15 seconds. His intake record is within the restriction, and his right ankle looks similar. How should the nurse grade and document the edema?", "options": {"A": "Grade 4+ edema, a very deep pit of about 8 mm.", "B": "Grade 2+ pitting edema, noting location, measured depth, and rebound time.", "C": "Grade 3+ edema, a deep pit of about 6 mm lasting over a minute.", "D": "Grade 1+ edema, a barely detectable pit of about 2 mm."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing the most severe grade for a moderate finding", "C": "Overestimating depth and rebound time by one grade", "D": "Underestimating depth and rebound time"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C092", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hassan Ali, 78, lives in a long-term care home. He has heart failure and chronic kidney disease, receives hemodialysis three mornings a week at a hospital unit, and has pitting edema in both ankles. His morning medications include an antihypertensive. A practical nurse cares for him with the registered nurse and reports changes in his condition promptly to the team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Grade 2+ pitting edema, noting location, measured depth, and rebound time.): A pit of about 4 mm that rebounds within roughly 15 seconds matches grade 2+ on the standard pitting scale, and the grade is documented with the site for trend comparison. Grade 1+ has a shallower pit with immediate rebound. Grade 3+ needs a deeper pit and slower return. Grade 4+ is the most severe and lasts several minutes, so neither overstates nor understates is accurate here."}, {"id": 788, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ali's chart shows 72.0 kg at 0700 before breakfast on Monday and 73.5 kg at 1500 after lunch on Tuesday. The care aide used a different scale on Tuesday, and his ankles look unchanged. The registered nurse asks about his fluid status. What is the best action for the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Report an acute gain of 1.5 kg, since weighing conditions rarely change readings.", "B": "Rely on the intake and output record, since it measures retention better than weight.", "C": "Reweigh him at the same time of day on the same scale.", "D": "Record the weights and compare only the weekly trend, since daily values vary."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignoring that scale and timing affect the comparison", "B": "Replacing weight with intake alone", "D": "Postponing action despite a possible fluid gain with heart failure"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C092", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hassan Ali, 78, lives in a long-term care home. He has heart failure and chronic kidney disease, receives hemodialysis three mornings a week at a hospital unit, and has pitting edema in both ankles. His morning medications include an antihypertensive. A practical nurse cares for him with the registered nurse and reports changes in his condition promptly to the team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reweigh him at the same time of day on the same scale.): Consistent timing, scale, and clothing are what make daily weight a reliable fluid indicator, and the Monday and Tuesday readings are not comparable. Reporting a gain based on mismatched conditions may mislead the team. Waiting for a weekly trend could delay care if he is retaining fluid. Intake and output help, but they miss insensible loss, so weight remains the best single indicator."}, {"id": 789, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After two days of vomiting, Mr. Ali has dry mucous membranes, slow skin recoil over the chest, and new confusion. He says he is not thirsty. His fluid restriction order is still active, and his son is visiting. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Document that he denies thirst and reassess at the next shift, since he reports no symptoms.", "B": "Report the dehydration signs and confusion to the RN, since thirst is blunted.", "C": "Attribute the confusion to aging and monitor, since dry mouth is common.", "D": "Encourage free fluids until he is thirsty, since thirst guides safe intake."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting a full shift despite acute change", "C": "Dismissing new confusion as normal aging", "D": "Trusting thirst as a reliable indicator"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C092", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hassan Ali, 78, lives in a long-term care home. He has heart failure and chronic kidney disease, receives hemodialysis three mornings a week at a hospital unit, and has pitting edema in both ankles. His morning medications include an antihypertensive. A practical nurse cares for him with the registered nurse and reports changes in his condition promptly to the team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the dehydration signs and confusion to the RN, since thirst is blunted.): Thirst is blunted in older adults, so dry membranes, poor skin recoil, and new confusion after vomiting point to dehydration that the registered nurse and prescriber must assess, especially with a restriction order in place. Encouraging free fluids ignores the active fluid restriction order. Waiting a shift delays care for a client whose condition is changing. New confusion is never assumed to be normal aging."}, {"id": 790, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "In a hospice, the daughter of a dying man quietly asks the practical nurse whether her father is dying. Her mother is crying nearby, and the physician has already discussed the prognosis with the family. The nurse is caring for him tonight. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Answer honestly, use the word dying gently, and involve the RN for medical detail.", "B": "Tell her to wait for the physician and leave the room to avoid saying anything upsetting.", "C": "Reassure her that her father will be fine and move on to comfort measures.", "D": "Use phrases like passing on or going to sleep so she is spared distress."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Withdrawing instead of staying present within scope", "C": "Giving false reassurance to avoid the topic", "D": "Using euphemisms that confuse and avoid the truth"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Answer honestly, use the word dying gently, and involve the RN for medical detail.): A dying client's family needs honest, gentle communication, and the practical nurse can acknowledge what is happening while directing prognosis detail to the physician or registered nurse. False reassurance damages trust and denies the family the chance to prepare. Leaving the room abandons her at a vulnerable moment. Euphemisms like going to sleep can confuse and frighten, so clear words said with care are better."}, {"id": 791, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a 74-year-old man has a blood pressure of 84/50 mmHg, a heart rate of 112, and new dizziness. He had a hip repair last year, and the unit is busy. The nurse must call the physician now. Which communication approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Leave a message with the unit clerk and ask for a callback.", "B": "Give a detailed history first, including past hospital stays, before stating the concern.", "C": "Identify yourself, report the vital signs and change, give background, and request action.", "D": "Describe the low blood pressure and wait for the physician to ask for more."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a delayed indirect message for an urgent change", "B": "Burying the urgent concern under unneeded background", "D": "Reporting the problem without assessment or recommendation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Identify yourself, report the vital signs and change, give background, and request action.): SBAR gives the physician the situation, background, assessment, and a recommendation in a short, structured order so an unstable client is acted on quickly. Stating only the problem and waiting leaves the physician to pull out the details. Starting with a long history delays the key information, and last year's hip repair is unrelated. A message through a clerk is too indirect for new hypotension with tachycardia."}, {"id": 792, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Care planning is under way for a capable 58-year-old on a medical unit who refuses his intravenous antibiotics because he fears side effects. His family urges him to accept, and the team meets tomorrow to revise the plan. Which nurse action best supports the plan?", "options": {"A": "Respect his refusal, then document it and move on without further discussion.", "B": "Explore his fears, confirm he understands the risks, and document his decision.", "C": "Ask the physician to order the antibiotics by another route without his agreement.", "D": "Ask the family to persuade him, since family support often changes decisions."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepting refusal without exploring his fears", "C": "Bypassing refusal by changing the route of treatment", "D": "Using family pressure to override a capable client"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explore his fears, confirm he understands the risks, and document his decision.): A capable adult has the right to refuse treatment, and the nurse supports that right by exploring fears, confirming understanding of the risks, documenting the decision, and informing the team so alternatives can be planned. Moving on without discussion misses a chance to address his fears. Giving treatment by another route without agreement ignores consent. Pressure from relatives undermines voluntary choice."}, {"id": 793, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Danko's weight is up 1.5 kg overnight and he has crackles at both lung bases. He complains of a dry mouth and asks for a large jug of water, and his daughter has brought juice. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Offer juice instead of water, because the restriction applies only to plain water.", "B": "Allow the extra water to ease his dryness and record it as intake.", "C": "Explain the limit, offer mouth care and measured sips, and report the gain and crackles.", "D": "Lift the restriction today, since his dry mouth suggests dehydration rather than overload."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Misreading the restriction as covering water only", "B": "Giving extra fluid to a client with fluid overload", "D": "Reading thirst as dehydration and ignoring signs of overload"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C090", "cluster_text": "Mr. Peter Danko, 71, has type 2 diabetes, hypertension, heart failure, and COPD with carbon dioxide retention. He is admitted to a medical unit with breathlessness and leg swelling. His orders include a fluid restriction of 1.5 L daily and a low sodium diet. A practical nurse cares for him on the day shift and reports changes to the registered nurse, while his daughter visits each afternoon.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain the limit, offer mouth care and measured sips, and report the gain and crackles.): A 1.5 kg overnight gain with crackles points to fluid retention in heart failure, so the restriction must be kept and the findings reported. Mouth care, ice chips counted in the total, and sips help thirst without exceeding the limit. Extra water adds to the overload. Juice counts as fluid, and removing the restriction is a prescriber's decision that the signs do not support."}, {"id": 794, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before discharge, Mr. Danko says his knee aches and asks whether he can buy ibuprofen at the pharmacy, since it is sold without a prescription. His kidney function is slightly reduced, and his daughter is waiting to drive him home. What should the nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Tell him to use it only on days when his swelling is mild.", "B": "Advise against ibuprofen, since it can harm kidneys and raise blood pressure.", "C": "Approve it taken with food, since protecting the stomach also protects the kidneys.", "D": "Approve the lowest dose, since over-the-counter products are safe with diabetes."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Linking a nephrotoxic drug's use to the level of swelling", "C": "Confusing stomach protection with kidney protection", "D": "Treating nonprescription drugs as safe in kidney disease"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C090", "cluster_text": "Mr. Peter Danko, 71, has type 2 diabetes, hypertension, heart failure, and COPD with carbon dioxide retention. He is admitted to a medical unit with breathlessness and leg swelling. His orders include a fluid restriction of 1.5 L daily and a low sodium diet. A practical nurse cares for him on the day shift and reports changes to the registered nurse, while his daughter visits each afternoon.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise against ibuprofen, since it can harm kidneys and raise blood pressure.): Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs reduce blood flow to the kidneys and can worsen blood pressure and heart failure, so they are best avoided in his case, and the prescriber can suggest a safer pain option. Being sold without a prescription does not make a drug safe for him. Food reduces stomach irritation but not kidney effects. Choosing days by swelling does not lower the risk."}, {"id": 795, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Danko's daughter, who is 45 and healthy, asks what puts her father at risk of chronic kidney disease. She wonders whether it is just old age or whether something can be done. The nurse has his diabetes and blood pressure records available. Which explanation is best?", "options": {"A": "Diabetes and high blood pressure are leading risk factors and can be managed.", "B": "Heart failure and COPD are the main causes, so protection depends on them only.", "C": "Kidney disease results mainly from drinking too little water in earlier years.", "D": "Aging is the main cause of kidney disease, so glucose and pressure matter little."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Overstating lung and heart disease as the cause", "C": "Linking kidney disease to low water intake as a main cause", "D": "Blaming age and dismissing modifiable factors"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C090", "cluster_text": "Mr. Peter Danko, 71, has type 2 diabetes, hypertension, heart failure, and COPD with carbon dioxide retention. He is admitted to a medical unit with breathlessness and leg swelling. His orders include a fluid restriction of 1.5 L daily and a low sodium diet. A practical nurse cares for him on the day shift and reports changes to the registered nurse, while his daughter visits each afternoon.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Diabetes and high blood pressure are leading risk factors and can be managed.): Diabetes and hypertension damage the small vessels of the kidneys and are leading causes of chronic kidney disease, and both can be managed. Age raises risk but is not the main cause, and saying so discourages control. Low water intake in earlier years is not a main cause. Heart and lung disease can affect the kidneys, but they are not the primary drivers, so risk reduction centres on glucose and pressure."}, {"id": 796, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Danko, in his 70s with COPD and carbon dioxide retention, is breathless with an oxygen saturation of 89 percent on 2 L per minute by nasal prongs. He looks anxious and asks for more oxygen. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Compare the reading with his ordered target, usually 88 to 92 percent.", "B": "Remove the oxygen to protect his drive to breathe and recheck in ten minutes.", "C": "Switch to a non-rebreather mask so saturation rises, then lower the flow.", "D": "Increase the flow to 6 L per minute to bring saturation above 95 percent."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Withdrawing oxygen from a hypoxic client", "C": "Choosing high flow oxygen that can worsen carbon dioxide retention", "D": "Raising oxygen without regard to the retainer's target"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C090", "cluster_text": "Mr. Peter Danko, 71, has type 2 diabetes, hypertension, heart failure, and COPD with carbon dioxide retention. He is admitted to a medical unit with breathlessness and leg swelling. His orders include a fluid restriction of 1.5 L daily and a low sodium diet. A practical nurse cares for him on the day shift and reports changes to the registered nurse, while his daughter visits each afternoon.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Compare the reading with his ordered target, usually 88 to 92 percent.): In clients who retain carbon dioxide, oxygen is titrated to a lower target such as 88 to 92 percent, and a reading of 89 percent is already in range, so the nurse reports and reassesses rather than increasing flow. High oxygen can suppress breathing drive and raise carbon dioxide. Removing oxygen would leave him hypoxic. A non-rebreather delivers high concentrations that exceed what he needs."}, {"id": 797, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Starting warfarin today, a 67-year-old man in clinic asks whether he should stop eating green vegetables because a neighbour said they cancel the drug. His diet usually includes a salad on most days, and he takes no supplements. What is the best teaching?", "options": {"A": "Take a daily vitamin K supplement, because it protects against bleeding.", "B": "Avoid all green leafy vegetables, since vitamin K reduces warfarin's effect.", "C": "Keep vitamin K rich food intake steady and report large diet changes.", "D": "Stop eating vegetables for the first month until blood test results stabilize."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using vitamin K supplements without prescriber direction", "B": "Over-restricting a healthy food instead of keeping intake consistent", "D": "Removing vegetables temporarily and then returning unpredictably"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep vitamin K rich food intake steady and report large diet changes.): Warfarin dosing is set against his usual vitamin K intake, so what matters is consistency, not elimination of green vegetables. Avoiding them completely removes healthy foods and a sudden return later would change his INR. A daily vitamin K supplement would work against the drug and is not his to start. Stopping vegetables for a month creates the same swing in intake that the INR monitoring tries to avoid."}, {"id": 798, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A client with a heart rate of 48 beats per minute and a blood pressure of 98/60 mmHg is due for metoprolol on a medical unit. He feels slightly dizzy. The order is written for the usual dose at 0900. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Give the dose and monitor continuously until the heart rate rises.", "B": "Hold the dose, recheck vital signs, and report to the RN or prescriber.", "C": "Give half the dose and document the change.", "D": "Give the dose, since the order stands, and recheck the pulse afterward."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving the drug first and monitoring second", "C": "Altering a dose independently, which is outside scope", "D": "Giving a drug against clear hold parameters"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold the dose, recheck vital signs, and report to the RN or prescriber.): A pulse of 48 with low blood pressure and dizziness is a reason to hold a beta blocker, and the nurse reports before giving it. Giving the dose because the order stands ignores the assessment the nurse is responsible for. Halving the dose is a prescribing decision outside practical nurse scope. Monitoring after giving does not undo the added slowing of the heart."}, {"id": 799, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On ramipril for hypertension, a client reports a dry cough for three weeks and, today, swelling of the lips and tongue. She feels slight tightness in her throat. The nurse is at her bedside in the clinic. What is the best interpretation and action?", "options": {"A": "Advise taking the dose at night so swelling is less noticeable by day.", "B": "Reassure her that cough and mild swelling are common and recheck next visit.", "C": "Advise an antihistamine and continuing ramipril, since swelling is probably a food allergy.", "D": "Treat it as possible angioedema, hold the drug, assess the airway, and call help."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Shifting the dose time instead of stopping a drug that caused airway swelling", "B": "Dismissing a life-threatening reaction as a common effect", "C": "Assuming a food allergy without evidence and continuing the drug"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat it as possible angioedema, hold the drug, assess the airway, and call help.): Lip and tongue swelling with throat tightness on an ACE inhibitor is angioedema until proven otherwise, so the drug is held and the airway and emergency response take priority. The dry cough is a known effect but does not explain the swelling. An antihistamine and continued dosing leave her exposed to the cause. Moving the dose to night does not prevent airway swelling."}, {"id": 800, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Seated with her bare upper arm resting on her lap, a client at a hypertension visit has a reading of 148/90 mmHg. The cuff was the correct size, and she walked in from the parking lot five minutes earlier. How should the nurse interpret the reading?", "options": {"A": "It may be falsely high because the arm was below heart level, so repeat.", "B": "It is falsely low, since gravity moves blood away from the cuff.", "C": "It is accurate, since an arm resting on the lap is standard.", "D": "It confirms uncontrolled hypertension, so notify the prescriber for a medication change."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reversing the effect of arm position on the reading", "C": "Accepting a poor technique as standard", "D": "Acting on a single reading obtained with faulty technique"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It may be falsely high because the arm was below heart level, so repeat.): An arm below heart level adds hydrostatic pressure and gives falsely high readings, and recent walking can raise pressure too, so the nurse repositions the arm at heart level and repeats after rest. Calling the lap position standard repeats the error. Gravity raises, not lowers, the reading here. Changing medication on one flawed measurement is premature."}, {"id": 801, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Six watery stools in 12 hours and abdominal cramping have developed in an older adult being treated with antibiotics for pneumonia. The unit has one private room free, and a roommate shares the current room. Which action best reduces spread of suspected Clostridioides difficile infection?", "options": {"A": "Use contact precautions in a private room with soap-and-water hand washing.", "B": "Use alcohol-based hand rub before and after care, since it kills organisms quickly.", "C": "Use droplet precautions with mask and eye protection, since spread occurs through coughing.", "D": "Keep him in the shared room and wear gloves only."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relying on hand rub that does not kill spores", "C": "Choosing droplet precautions for a spore spread by contact", "D": "Believing gloves alone are enough and keeping a roommate exposed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use contact precautions in a private room with soap-and-water hand washing.): The Clostridioides difficile organism forms spores that alcohol hand rub does not reliably kill, so soap and water, contact precautions, a private room, and sporicidal cleaning are needed. Droplet precautions do not match its route of spread. Gloves reduce but do not remove contamination, and a shared room exposes the roommate. The nurse should also report the diarrhea so testing can be arranged."}, {"id": 802, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After failing her first attempt at the registration exam, a new graduate asks her mentor whether she can keep retaking it any time, since she believes attempts are unlimited. Her employer has offered a graduate position meanwhile. What is the best advice for her mentor to give?", "options": {"A": "Wait one year before the next attempt, since regulators set that interval.", "B": "Contact the regulator to learn retake limits, conditions, and required steps first.", "C": "Work as an unregistered assistant at the same scope as a graduate nurse.", "D": "Ask the education program to approve the next attempt, since the school controls eligibility."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming a fixed one year wait", "C": "Practising a regulated scope without registration", "D": "Misplacing authority for exam eligibility with the school"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contact the regulator to learn retake limits, conditions, and required steps first.): The regulator sets the number of attempts, any waiting periods, and the conditions for retaking, so the graduate should confirm them directly before committing. Assuming a fixed wait may be wrong for her jurisdiction. An unregistered assistant cannot practise at a graduate nurse scope. Eligibility is decided by the regulatory body, not the school, although the school can offer support."}, {"id": 803, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Lying flat with two pillows that keep his neck flexed, a 36-year-old man with a head injury complains of headache but says the position feels comfortable. He is awake and oriented, and his monitor shows stable vital signs. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Raise the head of the bed to 90 degrees to maximize venous drainage from the head.", "B": "Turn his head to the right and keep the bed flat to protect the injury.", "C": "Leave the position as it is, since neck flexion reduces his headache and feels comfortable.", "D": "Raise the head of the bed to about 30 degrees with the neck neutral."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Overshooting elevation and risking reduced cerebral perfusion", "B": "Rotating the neck and lying flat, which impedes venous drainage", "C": "Accepting a position that restricts venous return from the head"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C106", "cluster_text": "During a shift in a hospital with an emergency department and a neurosurgical unit, a practical nurse cares for several clients with head injuries. Each is stable on arrival, has an order for neurological checks, and is assessed by a registered nurse. The nurse applies the unit's head injury routines and reports changes in level of consciousness, vomiting, or drainage immediately.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Raise the head of the bed to about 30 degrees with the neck neutral.): A head-up tilt of about 30 degrees with a neutral neck promotes venous drainage and helps keep intracranial pressure down, and neck flexion or rotation compresses the jugular veins. Leaving him flexed because it feels better ignores the risk. Raising the bed to 90 degrees can lower cerebral perfusion. Turning the head while flat blocks drainage as well, so both options fail."}, {"id": 804, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Straining at stool, a 60-year-old man on a neurosurgical unit says it is the only way he can go. He has a monitored head injury, takes a pain medication that slows the bowel, and has not passed stool for three days. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Withhold fluids after noon to reduce stool volume and lessen the urge.", "B": "Give a stimulant enema from unit stock without an order, since it works quickly.", "C": "Report it, request a stool softener per order, and teach him to exhale.", "D": "Encourage him to bear down firmly so the bowel empties more easily."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Restricting fluids, which hardens stool", "B": "Giving a medication without an order", "D": "Encouraging the Valsalva maneuver in raised pressure"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C106", "cluster_text": "During a shift in a hospital with an emergency department and a neurosurgical unit, a practical nurse cares for several clients with head injuries. Each is stable on arrival, has an order for neurological checks, and is assessed by a registered nurse. The nurse applies the unit's head injury routines and reports changes in level of consciousness, vomiting, or drainage immediately.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report it, request a stool softener per order, and teach him to exhale.): Straining causes the Valsalva maneuver, which raises intracranial pressure, so the nurse seeks an order for a stool softener and teaches breathing out during effort. Encouraging him to bear down is the exact behavior to avoid. Withholding fluids makes stool harder. A stimulant enema without an order is outside scope and could also provoke straining."}, {"id": 805, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After a car crash, a 25-year-old man arrives in the emergency department with clear fluid dripping from his left nostril and a headache. He asks for tissues to wipe his nose. The nurse suspects a skull base fracture. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Avoid packing or suctioning the nose, keep his head up, and report a leak.", "B": "Collect the drainage on tissue and reassure him it is normal discharge.", "C": "Have him blow his nose clear and apply an ice pack.", "D": "Pack the nostril gently with gauze to stop the drainage and prevent infection."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Dismissing clear drainage as ordinary discharge", "C": "Having him blow his nose, which raises pressure", "D": "Packing the nose, which can force infection inward"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C106", "cluster_text": "During a shift in a hospital with an emergency department and a neurosurgical unit, a practical nurse cares for several clients with head injuries. Each is stable on arrival, has an order for neurological checks, and is assessed by a registered nurse. The nurse applies the unit's head injury routines and reports changes in level of consciousness, vomiting, or drainage immediately.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Avoid packing or suctioning the nose, keep his head up, and report a leak.): Clear fluid after head trauma may be cerebrospinal fluid, which signals a skull base fracture, so the nurse avoids packing, suctioning, or nose blowing that could push bacteria into the cranial cavity. Gauze packing blocks drainage and raises infection risk. Blowing the nose increases pressure and can force air or bacteria inward. Calling it normal discharge delays the assessment needed."}, {"id": 806, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two days after a hockey concussion, a 16-year-old boy attends clinic with his mother. His headache has eased and he wants to play tomorrow's game, while she asks what is safe. He passed a short balance test today. What should the nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Advise a gradual return after symptoms resolve, with professional clearance first.", "B": "Allow practice but not games, since practice carries little risk of a second injury.", "C": "Advise strict bed rest in a darkened room for several weeks.", "D": "Allow return to play once the headache lessens and he feels ready."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Underestimating risk during contact practice", "C": "Prescribing prolonged strict rest instead of a graded return", "D": "Letting symptom improvement alone decide return to play"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C106", "cluster_text": "During a shift in a hospital with an emergency department and a neurosurgical unit, a practical nurse cares for several clients with head injuries. Each is stable on arrival, has an order for neurological checks, and is assessed by a registered nurse. The nurse applies the unit's head injury routines and reports changes in level of consciousness, vomiting, or drainage immediately.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Advise a gradual return after symptoms resolve, with professional clearance first.): Recovery after a concussion needs symptoms to settle and a graded return to activity with professional clearance, because a second blow before healing can cause serious harm. A lessened headache alone does not show recovery. Prolonged strict rest is no longer advised and may slow recovery. Contact practice carries similar risk, so staged activity under guidance is safer than a split between practice and games."}, {"id": 807, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "With diabetic neuropathy in both feet, a 60-year-old man is ready for his morning bath on the rehabilitation unit. He is alert and says the water is fine at the temperature he prefers. The basin is already filled. What is the safest way to check the water?", "options": {"A": "Ask whether it feels comfortable and rely on his answer, since he is alert.", "B": "Check it with a thermometer or your wrist, since his feeling of heat is unreliable.", "C": "Use hot water so foot circulation improves and wounds heal faster.", "D": "Fill the basin to his preferred temperature and have him test it with his foot first."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on a client whose sensation is impaired", "C": "Using heat with the belief that it improves healing", "D": "Letting the affected foot test the temperature"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C103", "cluster_text": "On a rehabilitation and medical unit, a practical nurse is assigned morning hygiene care for four clients with different needs. Some have reduced sensation, some have lines in place, and all are working toward more independence. The unit values privacy, dignity, and safe technique, and the nurse reports skin changes to the registered nurse while documenting each client's participation in care that day.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Check it with a thermometer or your wrist, since his feeling of heat is unreliable.): Neuropathy reduces temperature sensation, so he may not feel water that can burn, and the nurse must verify it with a thermometer or the inside of the wrist. Relying on his report, however alert he is, misses the sensory loss. Hot water can scald numb skin and does not speed healing. Testing with the foot puts the numb area at the greatest risk."}, {"id": 808, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Embarrassed and covering his face, a 35-year-old man with a spinal cord injury and limited sensation in his legs is being bathed in a shared room. The nurse is a different sex from the client, and the room is busy this morning. Which action best protects his dignity?", "options": {"A": "Expose only the part being washed, close the curtain, and explain each step.", "B": "Expose his legs and perineum together to finish the bath in one pass", "C": "Close the curtain and drape him, but skip explanations since he is embarrassed", "D": "Wash his upper body and legs while he is uncovered, then drape him"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Exposing large areas together for speed", "C": "Omitting explanation, which removes his control", "D": "Leaving him uncovered during washing"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C103", "cluster_text": "On a rehabilitation and medical unit, a practical nurse is assigned morning hygiene care for four clients with different needs. Some have reduced sensation, some have lines in place, and all are working toward more independence. The unit values privacy, dignity, and safe technique, and the nurse reports skin changes to the registered nurse while documenting each client's participation in care that day.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Expose only the part being washed, close the curtain, and explain each step.): Privacy and dignity are maintained by draping, closing the curtain, and telling the client what will happen, and these steps matter more when he is embarrassed. Exposing large areas together or leaving him uncovered violates dignity even if he cannot feel it. Skipping explanations removes his sense of control while he is already uneasy."}, {"id": 809, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Weakness in his right arm and leg affects a 66-year-old man after a stroke, but he can use his left hand and wants to wash himself. The unit is busy and the nurse has two more baths to finish. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Hand him the cloth but do his face and trunk yourself.", "B": "Complete the whole bath for him to save time and avoid tiring him.", "C": "Wait for the occupational therapist to start bathing practice and do all care until then.", "D": "Set up supplies, let him wash what he can reach, and assist only where needed."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Taking over key steps and limiting participation", "B": "Doing everything for the client to save time", "C": "Postponing independence until another professional acts"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C103", "cluster_text": "On a rehabilitation and medical unit, a practical nurse is assigned morning hygiene care for four clients with different needs. Some have reduced sensation, some have lines in place, and all are working toward more independence. The unit values privacy, dignity, and safe technique, and the nurse reports skin changes to the registered nurse while documenting each client's participation in care that day.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Set up supplies, let him wash what he can reach, and assist only where needed.): Supporting independence builds strength and confidence after a stroke, so the nurse sets up the task, lets him wash what he can reach, and steps in only for the rest. Doing the full bath takes away practice he needs. Doing the face and trunk still removes meaningful parts of the task. Waiting for the therapist delays opportunities that daily care already provides."}, {"id": 810, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "His PICC line in the right upper arm has a transparent dressing that is dry and intact, and the 58-year-old client on the medical unit asks to soak in a basin because it feels soothing. The line is infusing. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Remove the dressing before the bath and apply a new one afterward.", "B": "Cover the dressing with a waterproof barrier, keep the arm out of water, bathe around it.", "C": "Disconnect the infusion tubing during the bath and reconnect when finished.", "D": "Soak the arm in the basin to soften the dressing, then change it."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Removing a dressing without a sterile plan", "C": "Disconnecting a closed system and adding a contamination risk", "D": "Wetting an intact dressing and the line site"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C103", "cluster_text": "On a rehabilitation and medical unit, a practical nurse is assigned morning hygiene care for four clients with different needs. Some have reduced sensation, some have lines in place, and all are working toward more independence. The unit values privacy, dignity, and safe technique, and the nurse reports skin changes to the registered nurse while documenting each client's participation in care that day.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cover the dressing with a waterproof barrier, keep the arm out of water, bathe around it.): A PICC dressing must stay dry and intact to protect against bloodstream infection, so the nurse covers it, keeps the arm out of the water, and washes around it. Soaking the arm contaminates the site. Removing a dressing for convenience opens the line to bacteria. Disconnecting tubing breaks a closed system and adds another entry point, so neither is needed to keep the site dry."}, {"id": 811, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Awaiting biopsy results, a 52-year-old man asks the practical nurse whether it is cancer. The physician has not yet spoken with him, his wife is out getting coffee, and he is tearful and gripping the bed rail. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Say it is probably nothing to reassure him until the physician arrives.", "B": "Tell him the results show cancer, since he asked directly and deserves honesty.", "C": "Acknowledge his worry, avoid stating the diagnosis, and inform the physician.", "D": "Say you cannot discuss results and leave so you do not say anything wrong."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offering false reassurance about the outcome", "B": "Disclosing a diagnosis that belongs to the provider", "D": "Withdrawing and leaving a distressed client alone"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C107", "cluster_text": "Over one afternoon, a practical nurse in an acute care unit and a telehealth clinic communicates with several clients who need sensitive information or language support. The nurse works within scope of practice, follows the organization's policies on interpreters and client identification, and collaborates with the registered nurse, physician, and nurse practitioner when clients ask about results, diagnoses, or next steps.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acknowledge his worry, avoid stating the diagnosis, and inform the physician.): Giving a diagnosis is the responsibility of the physician or nurse practitioner, but the nurse can stay with him, name his fear, and make sure the provider knows he is asking. Stating the result steps outside scope and may not be accurate or sensitively delivered. Guessing that it is probably nothing offers false hope. Leaving abandons him when he needs presence."}, {"id": 812, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "For a consent discussion about a procedure, a woman with limited English is on the acute care unit with her 12-year-old daughter and adult son, who both offer to translate. A phone interpreter service is available, and the procedure is scheduled for this afternoon. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Use a translation app on your phone for the consent discussion.", "B": "Use simple words and gestures and ask her to nod if she agrees.", "C": "Arrange a qualified medical interpreter instead of using her children.", "D": "Ask her adult son to interpret, since he knows her wording and her history."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using an unvetted app for a consent conversation", "B": "Obtaining consent through gestures and nods", "D": "Using a family member as interpreter"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C107", "cluster_text": "Over one afternoon, a practical nurse in an acute care unit and a telehealth clinic communicates with several clients who need sensitive information or language support. The nurse works within scope of practice, follows the organization's policies on interpreters and client identification, and collaborates with the registered nurse, physician, and nurse practitioner when clients ask about results, diagnoses, or next steps.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Arrange a qualified medical interpreter instead of using her children.): Informed consent needs accurate, impartial interpretation, so a qualified interpreter is used, and children in particular should never relay medical information. A relative may soften, omit, or alter content and breaches the client's privacy. Nodding does not prove understanding. A phone app is not a vetted medical interpreter and may mistranslate key risks."}, {"id": 813, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During interpreted teaching about insulin, the nurse notices she is looking at the interpreter and saying, Tell her to check her sugar. The client, who is 70, looks away and seems lost while the interpreter waits quietly. What should the nurse change?", "options": {"A": "Ask the interpreter to explain insulin in detail, then check that the client nods", "B": "Hold the discussion with the interpreter first and summarize for the client later.", "C": "Face the client, speak directly to her in short sentences, and pause for the interpreter.", "D": "Speak to the client but look at the interpreter, and use medical terms for accuracy"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Handing the teaching to the interpreter and accepting nods", "B": "Leaving the client out until a summary", "D": "Looking at the interpreter and using medical terms"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C107", "cluster_text": "Over one afternoon, a practical nurse in an acute care unit and a telehealth clinic communicates with several clients who need sensitive information or language support. The nurse works within scope of practice, follows the organization's policies on interpreters and client identification, and collaborates with the registered nurse, physician, and nurse practitioner when clients ask about results, diagnoses, or next steps.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Face the client, speak directly to her in short sentences, and pause for the interpreter.): The conversation is between the nurse and the client, so the nurse speaks to her, in short phrases, and the interpreter relays them, which keeps her involved and shows respect. Asking the interpreter to teach and accepting nods does not confirm understanding. Looking at the interpreter excludes her, and medical terms are hard to interpret. Summarizing afterward loses detail about insulin safety."}, {"id": 814, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "When a telehealth nurse calls a man in his 60s about his blood pressure follow-up, a woman answers and says he is out but she can take the details. The appointment was booked yesterday, and the line is clear. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Verify identity by confirming only his first name.", "B": "Begin discussing symptoms at once, since he booked the appointment.", "C": "Leave results on voicemail if nobody answers, to save repeat calls.", "D": "Confirm his identity with two identifiers, check privacy, and document before discussing details."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a weak single identifier", "B": "Skipping identity checks because the call was scheduled", "C": "Leaving health information on voicemail"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C107", "cluster_text": "Over one afternoon, a practical nurse in an acute care unit and a telehealth clinic communicates with several clients who need sensitive information or language support. The nurse works within scope of practice, follows the organization's policies on interpreters and client identification, and collaborates with the registered nurse, physician, and nurse practitioner when clients ask about results, diagnoses, or next steps.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Confirm his identity with two identifiers, check privacy, and document before discussing details.): Telephone care carries a privacy risk, so the nurse confirms identity with two identifiers, checks that the client can talk privately, and documents the call. The woman who answered is not authorized unless he has agreed, and a scheduled call does not prove who is on the line. A first name alone is not enough to verify identity. Voicemail messages may be heard by others, so details should not be left."}, {"id": 815, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor, 70, is pale, sweaty and shaky on a medical unit. He skipped lunch after taking his morning diabetes medication, and his daughter is visiting. The glucometer reads 3.1 mmol/L, he is alert, and he can swallow safely. Which action should the practical nurse take first?", "options": {"A": "Keep him resting with nothing by mouth and repeat the glucometer after his medication wears off.", "B": "Give fifteen grams of fast acting carbohydrate such as juice, then recheck glucose in fifteen minutes.", "C": "Offer a full meal tray and reassess his symptoms after he finishes eating the entire meal.", "D": "Give his scheduled rapid acting insulin now, then recheck glucose before the evening meal tray arrives."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays treatment of a symptomatic low reading while waiting", "C": "Gives slow complex food instead of fast-acting sugar", "D": "Treats low glucose like high glucose and gives insulin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give fifteen grams of fast acting carbohydrate such as juice, then recheck glucose in fifteen minutes.): Mr. Okafor has symptoms and a glucose of 3.1 mmol/L while alert and able to swallow, so fast-acting oral carbohydrate corrects it quickest and a recheck in fifteen minutes confirms the response. Giving insulin would lower his glucose further and could cause a seizure. Waiting or serving a full meal delays correction because complex food absorbs slowly. Treat symptomatic hypoglycemia first, then recheck and report."}, {"id": 816, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At shift change Karim must hand over eight clients in twelve minutes. A colleague suggests a quick hallway chat because the team knows the unit well. Last week an unreported allergy update delayed a client's care. Which approach to handover best prevents omitted information like that?", "options": {"A": "Give a brief verbal summary of only the clients whose condition changed during the day shift.", "B": "Share updates informally in the hallway while team members walk toward their assigned clients.", "C": "Rely on the written chart and speak only when the oncoming nurse asks direct questions.", "D": "Use a structured tool such as SBAR, covering each client's status and pending tasks."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Skips stable clients so allergy updates get lost", "B": "Treats familiarity as a substitute for a standard format", "C": "Depends on the receiver to know what to ask"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C097", "cluster_text": "Karim Haddad, a practical nurse, works on a surgical and medical unit and floats to the operating room team for one case. During his shift he gives handover on eight clients, receives an urgent telephone order, cares for a worsening post-operative client, and meets safety concerns that need escalation. Each situation tests structured communication, assertiveness and the chain of command.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use a structured tool such as SBAR, covering each client's status and pending tasks.): A structured tool such as SBAR gives every client the same sequence, so an allergy update or pending task has a place to be reported. Summarizing only changed clients leaves stable but important information unsaid. A hallway chat or waiting for questions depends on memory and on the receiver knowing what to ask. Standard formats reduce errors at transitions of care."}, {"id": 817, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "During a respiratory emergency, the physician calls out an urgent order for a medication dose while Karim is drawing up another drug. He nods and keeps working. The charge nurse reminds him of the standard for urgent verbal orders. What should Karim do to complete the communication loop?", "options": {"A": "Repeat only the drug name aloud and proceed, since the physician is busy managing the airway.", "B": "Repeat the dose only, then confirm the drug name from the label before giving it", "C": "Say the order is understood aloud, then verify it with the charge nurse afterward", "D": "Repeat the order back with drug, dose and route, and wait for the prescriber's confirmation."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reads back part of the order, leaving dose and route unchecked", "B": "Reading back only the dose, leaving drug and route unchecked", "C": "Acknowledging without reading back and verifying late"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C097", "cluster_text": "Karim Haddad, a practical nurse, works on a surgical and medical unit and floats to the operating room team for one case. During his shift he gives handover on eight clients, receives an urgent telephone order, cares for a worsening post-operative client, and meets safety concerns that need escalation. Each situation tests structured communication, assertiveness and the chain of command.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Repeat the order back with drug, dose and route, and wait for the prescriber's confirmation.): Closed-loop communication means the receiver repeats the full order and the sender confirms it, which catches mishearing of drug, dose or route. Repeating only the dose or saying it is understood does not show what Karim heard. Verifying with the charge nurse afterward delays confirmation until after the drug may have been given. Read back every urgent verbal order."}, {"id": 818, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Tremblay, 55, is six hours post-operative and his urine output has fallen to 10 mL in two hours while his heart rate rises. Karim phones the surgeon, who sounds distracted and says it is probably fine. Karim remains worried. Which statement best uses the CUS escalation tool?", "options": {"A": "I am concerned and uncomfortable; this is a safety issue, so please assess him now.", "B": "I will chart the low output and look at his numbers again at end of shift.", "C": "I mentioned the urine output, so I will wait until you can come.", "D": "I wonder if maybe his urine output might be worth a look sometime."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Documents the concern but delays any escalation", "C": "Hands the timing to the person who has already dismissed it", "D": "Uses vague hints that the listener can ignore"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C097", "cluster_text": "Karim Haddad, a practical nurse, works on a surgical and medical unit and floats to the operating room team for one case. During his shift he gives handover on eight clients, receives an urgent telephone order, cares for a worsening post-operative client, and meets safety concerns that need escalation. Each situation tests structured communication, assertiveness and the chain of command.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (I am concerned and uncomfortable; this is a safety issue, so please assess him now.): CUS stands for concerned, uncomfortable, safety issue, and using those words clearly signals that the situation needs attention now. Vague hints are easy for a distracted prescriber to miss. Charting the concern or waiting for the surgeon to choose a time leaves a falling urine output and rising heart rate untreated. State the concern in direct, agreed words."}, {"id": 819, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In the operating room, Karim sees that a sterilization indicator on an opened instrument tray did not change colour. He raises it once and the surgeon does not acknowledge it. The senior circulating nurse says surgeons dislike being questioned twice. According to the two-challenge rule, what should Karim do next?", "options": {"A": "Voice the concern a second time, clearly stating the specific safety risk, before escalating further.", "B": "Ask the surgeon privately after the case why the indicator was not checked", "C": "Move straight to a written incident report without stating the concern a second time.", "D": "Open a replacement tray without telling the surgeon, to avoid delaying the procedure"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Waiting until after the case to raise a safety risk", "C": "Skips the second challenge and substitutes paperwork", "D": "Acting alone without communicating the concern"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C097", "cluster_text": "Karim Haddad, a practical nurse, works on a surgical and medical unit and floats to the operating room team for one case. During his shift he gives handover on eight clients, receives an urgent telephone order, cares for a worsening post-operative client, and meets safety concerns that need escalation. Each situation tests structured communication, assertiveness and the chain of command.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Voice the concern a second time, clearly stating the specific safety risk, before escalating further.): The two-challenge rule says a team member states a concern, and if it is not acknowledged, states it again with clear wording about the risk. Karim has made only one challenge, so a second is the next step. Raising it after the case or swapping the tray silently leaves the surgeon unaware of the risk. An incident report is useful afterward but does not replace speaking up."}, {"id": 820, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A prescriber leaves a plan to give a full anticoagulant dose to a client with active bleeding. Karim's second challenge receives a curt reply, and the order stays. The prescriber is now off the unit. His concern is unresolved. What action best follows the chain of command?", "options": {"A": "Give the dose as ordered, since the prescriber acknowledged the concern and remains responsible.", "B": "Document the disagreement in the chart and move on to his other assigned clients.", "C": "Take the concern to the charge nurse and on-call physician, and hold the dose until resolved.", "D": "Ask the client's family to speak with the physician about the order the next morning."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepts the first response and drops the concern", "B": "Records the disagreement without protecting the client", "D": "Shifts responsibility for safety to the family"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C097", "cluster_text": "Karim Haddad, a practical nurse, works on a surgical and medical unit and floats to the operating room team for one case. During his shift he gives handover on eight clients, receives an urgent telephone order, cares for a worsening post-operative client, and meets safety concerns that need escalation. Each situation tests structured communication, assertiveness and the chain of command.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Take the concern to the charge nurse and on-call physician, and hold the dose until resolved.): When two challenges fail, the nurse takes the concern up the chain of command, here to the charge nurse and the on-call physician, and holds the unsafe dose meanwhile. Giving the dose because the prescriber answered puts the client at risk. Charting alone records the disagreement without stopping harm. Family members cannot be expected to resolve an unsafe order."}, {"id": 821, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Ouellette, 72, has COPD and lives alone. During a home visit the practical nurse finds her sputum has changed from white to thick green, her temperature is 38.4 C, and her breathlessness has increased. She says she will wait a few days to see if it passes. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Encourage rest and fluids at home, then reassess the sputum colour at the next weekly visit.", "B": "Teach her breathing exercises and plan to review the symptoms again after the weekend.", "C": "Report the changes promptly to the primary care provider or RN and keep monitoring her breathing.", "D": "Advise her to turn her home oxygen flow up until the breathlessness improves."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waits on a probable infection in a COPD client", "B": "Substitutes teaching for timely reporting", "D": "Raises oxygen flow without an order, risking suppressed drive"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the changes promptly to the primary care provider or RN and keep monitoring her breathing.): A change in sputum colour with fever and worse breathlessness suggests a possible exacerbation or infection, which needs prompt reporting and ongoing monitoring. Waiting until the weekend or the next visit lets it progress. Raising oxygen without an order is outside practice and risky in COPD. Teaching is helpful but does not replace reporting changes."}, {"id": 822, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 34-year-old at a community clinic develops hives, lip swelling and wheezing ten minutes after a bee sting. Her blood pressure is 88/52 mmHg. A physician orders epinephrine for anaphylaxis, and the practical nurse prepares the dose. Which route and site is correct?", "options": {"A": "Subcutaneous injection into the back of the upper arm, rotating away from any area with hives.", "B": "Intramuscular injection into the deltoid, because that arm is quicker to expose in an emergency.", "C": "Intramuscular injection into the middle outer thigh, the vastus lateralis, using the prescribed epinephrine dose.", "D": "Intravenous push through a peripheral cannula over several minutes while watching her blood pressure."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses the subcutaneous route with slower absorption", "B": "Chooses the deltoid for convenience over absorption", "D": "Gives an intravenous push outside the standard first-line route"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intramuscular injection into the middle outer thigh, the vastus lateralis, using the prescribed epinephrine dose.): Intramuscular epinephrine in the vastus lateralis is absorbed quickly and reliably in anaphylaxis. Subcutaneous injection absorbs more slowly, and the deltoid has less muscle mass and slower uptake than the thigh. Intravenous push is not the usual first-line route for this setting. Use the outer thigh for epinephrine."}, {"id": 823, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In a long-term care home, a physician phones an order for acetaminophen for a 90-year-old resident with a temperature of 38.2 C. The practical nurse writes it down and reads it back. A colleague says no further steps are needed because the physician is well known. What must the nurse document?", "options": {"A": "The date and order wording, leaving the prescriber's name for the pharmacy to add.", "B": "Date, time, prescriber's name, the order, the read back, and a later prescriber countersignature.", "C": "The order and the resident's response, with a countersignature required if the dose is changed.", "D": "Only the order and her own signature, since the read back already confirms the physician's agreement."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaves out the prescriber's identity", "C": "Treats the countersignature as optional", "D": "Omits date, prescriber and read back from the record"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Date, time, prescriber's name, the order, the read back, and a later prescriber countersignature.): A telephone order record needs the date, time, prescriber, the order and the read back, and the prescriber later countersigns it so the order is verified. A familiar voice does not remove those steps. Omitting the prescriber's name or making the signature optional leaves a gap in accountability. Complete documentation protects the resident and the nurse."}, {"id": 824, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Lavoie, 58, is three days after a below-knee amputation. He reports burning, cramping pain in the foot that was removed and rates it 7 out of 10. His roommate jokes that it is all in his head. The surgical site is clean, with no redness or swelling. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Accept the pain as real, assess it fully, give prescribed analgesics, and report it.", "B": "Explain that the pain will pass in a day or two and offer distraction", "C": "Wait to give analgesics until the surgical site shows redness or swelling to confirm a cause.", "D": "Give the opioid dose early and ask the surgeon to review the site tomorrow"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Offering reassurance without assessing or treating the pain", "C": "Demands a visible cause before treating pain", "D": "Giving a dose before the permitted time without an order"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Accept the pain as real, assess it fully, give prescribed analgesics, and report it.): Phantom limb pain is a real neuropathic pain after amputation, so the nurse assesses it, gives ordered analgesics and reports it so the team can adjust treatment. Reassurance alone leaves him in pain. Giving a dose early is a medication error. A clean site does not mean there is no pain, and withholding analgesics out of dependence worries is not supported."}, {"id": 825, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Elena sees Mia, 18 months, in a community clinic after two days of vomiting and watery stools. She has dry lips and fewer wet diapers, and her mother has been offering apple juice and cola. The waiting room is crowded today. Which fluid should Elena recommend for rehydration?", "options": {"A": "Plain water in full cups until the stools return to a normal consistency.", "B": "Cola left to go flat and given in small cups to settle her stomach.", "C": "A commercial oral rehydration solution in small, frequent amounts, such as a teaspoon every few minutes.", "D": "Undiluted apple juice offered between meals, because the sugar provides energy during her recovery from diarrhea."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives plain water that lacks needed electrolytes", "B": "Chooses flat cola, a sugary caffeinated drink", "D": "Uses high-sugar juice that worsens diarrhea"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C099", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Elena Rossi rotates through community and acute care in one day. She sees a toddler with gastroenteritis at a clinic, cares for an adult receiving rapid intravenous fluids, tracks intake and output for another adult, and checks intravenous fluid orders for a postoperative adult. Each situation involves fluid and electrolyte balance.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A commercial oral rehydration solution in small, frequent amounts, such as a teaspoon every few minutes.): Mild to moderate isotonic dehydration is treated with an oral rehydration solution, which has balanced sodium and glucose and is given in small frequent amounts to limit vomiting. Juice and cola are high in sugar and low in electrolytes, which can worsen diarrhea. Plain water lacks the salts she has lost. Use an oral rehydration solution."}, {"id": 826, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Gagnon, 67, is receiving rapid intravenous fluids after surgery. Elena checks him at the start of her shift. His blood pressure is 156/94 mmHg, his pulse is bounding, and his oxygen saturation is 93 percent. Which additional finding best supports fluid volume overload?", "options": {"A": "Bounding pulse with warm flushed skin, a fever, and a raised white cell count", "B": "New crackles in the lung bases, distended neck veins, and weight gain over the past day", "C": "Rapid shallow breathing with chest tightness after a long period of bed rest", "D": "Reduced urine output with concentrated urine, thirst and a drop in blood pressure on standing."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Describes infection findings instead of overload", "C": "Describes a clot related pattern, not fluid excess", "D": "Describes fluid volume deficit with orthostatic change"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C099", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Elena Rossi rotates through community and acute care in one day. She sees a toddler with gastroenteritis at a clinic, cares for an adult receiving rapid intravenous fluids, tracks intake and output for another adult, and checks intravenous fluid orders for a postoperative adult. Each situation involves fluid and electrolyte balance.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (New crackles in the lung bases, distended neck veins, and weight gain over the past day): Crackles, distended neck veins and rapid weight gain together with high blood pressure and a bounding pulse point to excess circulating volume. Warm flushed skin with fever suggests infection. Shallow breathing with chest tightness after bed rest suggests a clot. Concentrated urine with thirst and an orthostatic drop suggests deficit. Combine the data before interpreting."}, {"id": 827, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Elena is recording intake and output for Ms. Bianchi, 49, on an acute care unit. Ms. Bianchi drank from a cup, a soup bowl and a water jug, and a nursing assistant offers to estimate the totals. Which action gives the most accurate record?", "options": {"A": "Count only fluids the client drinks, leaving out soup, ice chips and gelatin.", "B": "Ask Ms. Bianchi to recall what she drank and record the total at day's end.", "C": "Estimate each intake by how full the container looks and record it at shift end.", "D": "Measure each fluid in millilitres using graduated containers or known container volumes, then record promptly."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Excludes fluids that count as intake", "B": "Relies on memory and delayed recording", "C": "Estimates instead of measuring"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C099", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Elena Rossi rotates through community and acute care in one day. She sees a toddler with gastroenteritis at a clinic, cares for an adult receiving rapid intravenous fluids, tracks intake and output for another adult, and checks intravenous fluid orders for a postoperative adult. Each situation involves fluid and electrolyte balance.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Measure each fluid in millilitres using graduated containers or known container volumes, then record promptly.): Accurate records come from measuring each fluid in millilitres and charting it close to the time it was taken. Estimating by sight introduces error. Soup, ice chips and gelatin count toward intake, and relying on memory leads to omissions. Measure and record promptly."}, {"id": 828, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Elena reviews an order for Mr. Dubois, 45, one day after abdominal surgery, who needs fluid to expand his circulating volume. The pharmacy bag is labelled 0.9 percent sodium chloride, and a colleague mentions a 0.45 percent bag on the shelf. Which description of 0.9 percent sodium chloride is correct?", "options": {"A": "It is isotonic, so it stays in the vascular space and expands the circulating volume.", "B": "It is isotonic, so it moves into cells and treats intracellular dehydration first", "C": "It is slightly hypertonic to blood, so it draws a little fluid into vessels", "D": "It is isotonic like the 0.45 percent bag, so the two expand volume equally"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Misplacing where isotonic fluid distributes", "C": "Misclassifying the tonicity as mildly hypertonic", "D": "Assuming the two bags share tonicity"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C099", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Elena Rossi rotates through community and acute care in one day. She sees a toddler with gastroenteritis at a clinic, cares for an adult receiving rapid intravenous fluids, tracks intake and output for another adult, and checks intravenous fluid orders for a postoperative adult. Each situation involves fluid and electrolyte balance.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It is isotonic, so it stays in the vascular space and expands the circulating volume.): A 0.9 percent sodium chloride solution matches the tonicity of blood, so it stays in the vascular space and expands circulating volume. It does not move into cells. It is not hypertonic. The 0.45 percent bag is hypotonic, so the two do not behave alike. Check the tonicity, not the label colour."}, {"id": 829, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Chen, 30, started haloperidol five days ago on a mental health unit. This afternoon he has a stiff jaw, a rigid posture and shuffling steps, and he asks if he is getting worse. His temperature and level of consciousness are normal. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Encourage rest and gentle exercise, and reassess after three more days of the same treatment.", "B": "Report the findings as possible extrapyramidal symptoms so the prescriber can assess and treat him.", "C": "Lower his dose at the next administration time, since the stiffness is probably dose related.", "D": "Document the stiffness as an expected effect and continue the medication without further reporting."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays reporting while waiting several days for the effect to pass", "C": "Changes a dose without an order", "D": "Treats a treatable adverse effect as expected and unreported"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the findings as possible extrapyramidal symptoms so the prescriber can assess and treat him.): Rigidity, a stiff jaw and shuffling gait soon after starting an antipsychotic suggest extrapyramidal symptoms, which are treatable and need prescriber assessment. Reducing the dose is outside practice without an order. Calling the stiffness expected leaves it unreported, and waiting three days prolongs distress. Report promptly."}, {"id": 830, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A client receiving an intravenous antibiotic infusion in hospital develops flushing, throat tightness and wheezing four minutes in. The practical nurse is alone with the client. A colleague suggests turning the rate down to see if the symptoms ease. What is the priority action?", "options": {"A": "Remove the pump and take a full set of vital signs before calling anyone.", "B": "Continue the infusion but pause between doses to give diphenhydramine from the emergency cart.", "C": "Stop the infusion, keep the line open with new tubing and saline, and call for help.", "D": "Slow the infusion to its lowest rate and watch for improvement over the next ten minutes."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays calling for help to collect data", "B": "Continues the antibiotic and gives a drug without an order", "D": "Slows the trigger instead of stopping it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the infusion, keep the line open with new tubing and saline, and call for help.): In a suspected anaphylactic reaction, the first step is to stop the trigger, then keep the vein open with fresh tubing and saline and call for help. Slowing the infusion still delivers the drug. Continuing it while giving an unordered antihistamine is unsafe and out of scope. Taking a full set of vitals before calling help wastes time."}, {"id": 831, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Aarav, 2, has had diarrhea for three days and is seen at a community pediatric clinic. He is active, drinks when offered, and has moist mucous membranes and two wet diapers today. His father asks whether to stop all drinks to rest the bowel. What guidance should the nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Give plain water for the first day and avoid solids until his stools are formed.", "B": "Continue offering fluids, preferably oral rehydration solution, with his usual diet, and watch for dehydration.", "C": "Offer undiluted fruit juice and sports drinks in place of milk and food for several days.", "D": "Hold all fluids and food for twelve hours to rest the bowel, then restart slowly."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Restricts nutrition and electrolytes unnecessarily", "C": "Uses sugary drinks that worsen stool losses", "D": "Withholds fluids from a child with diarrhea"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Continue offering fluids, preferably oral rehydration solution, with his usual diet, and watch for dehydration.): Children with mild diarrhea should keep taking fluids, ideally oral rehydration solution, and continue normal feeding while parents watch for dehydration. Holding fluids risks dehydration. Undiluted juice and sports drinks are high in sugar and can worsen diarrhea, and plain water with food restriction leaves out electrolytes and nutrition. Keep fluids going."}, {"id": 832, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a physician gives a verbal order for a medication that the practical nurse has never administered and that falls outside her practice. The physician is in a hurry and says the client is waiting. What is the correct response?", "options": {"A": "Accept the order to avoid delaying care, then ask the pharmacist to check it afterward.", "B": "Decline the order, explain why, and ask that a nurse within scope administer it.", "C": "Take the order and administer it after reading the drug reference for the usual dose.", "D": "Accept and document the order, then ask a colleague to give it without mentioning scope."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepts an order outside scope to save time", "C": "Relies on a drug reference instead of competence and scope", "D": "Hides the scope problem from the prescriber"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline the order, explain why, and ask that a nurse within scope administer it.): A nurse is accountable for working within her scope and competence and must decline an order outside them, while making sure the client's need is still met by an appropriate nurse. Accepting to save time or reading a reference does not create competence. Passing the order on without explanation hides the problem. Decline and arrange safe care."}, {"id": 833, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A mother brings her 2-month-old to a clinic for routine vaccines. The infant has a mild runny nose and a temperature of 36.9 C, and is feeding well. The mother wonders whether to postpone the visit. What should the practical nurse tell her?", "options": {"A": "A mild cold without fever is not a reason to delay, so vaccinate on schedule", "B": "Wait until the nose has been clear for two weeks, then restart the whole series.", "C": "Give the vaccines but delay the live ones until the cold has fully cleared", "D": "Give only one vaccine today and book the others after the cold clears"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delays vaccines for a mild illness", "C": "Delaying live vaccines for a mild cold", "D": "Splitting the schedule for a mild cold"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A mild cold without fever is not a reason to delay, so vaccinate on schedule): A mild illness such as a runny nose without significant fever is not a contraindication to routine vaccination, and delays leave the infant unprotected. Waiting two weeks, delaying live vaccines or splitting the schedule is not needed. Giving half doses is not accepted practice and may give inadequate protection. Vaccinate on schedule."}, {"id": 834, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Marcus prepares an intramuscular iron injection for Mrs. Aziz, 62, on a medical unit. The drug can stain skin and irritate tissue. He draws up the dose and changes the needle. Which technique should he use to prevent leakage into the subcutaneous tissue?", "options": {"A": "Inject at a 45 degree angle into fatty tissue and apply a warm compress afterward.", "B": "Displace the skin sideways, inject deeply, wait ten seconds, release the skin, and avoid rubbing.", "C": "Inject at 90 degrees into the thigh, withdraw the needle at once, and apply firm pressure", "D": "Displace the skin sideways, inject, withdraw immediately, and massage to disperse the iron"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses subcutaneous angle and site for an IM drug", "C": "Skipping skin displacement and the pause, and pressing the site", "D": "Massaging after Z-track, which pushes iron into tissue"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C102", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Marcus Lee works on a medical unit and in an outpatient clinic, giving injections throughout the day. He gives an intramuscular iron injection, a tuberculin skin test, and an intramuscular antibiotic, and later faces a needlestick injury. Each task tests safe injection technique and the response to an incident.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Displace the skin sideways, inject deeply, wait ten seconds, release the skin, and avoid rubbing.): The Z-track method displaces the skin so that, when released, the needle track seals and the irritating drug stays in muscle. Massage or firm pressure pushes it back along the track, and withdrawing immediately without a pause lets drug leak. A 45 degree angle into fat places an intramuscular drug in the wrong layer. Use Z-track for staining drugs."}, {"id": 835, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At an outpatient clinic, Marcus performs a tuberculin skin test on Mr. Park, 40. He cleans the inner forearm with an alcohol swab and lets it dry, then holds the syringe with the bevel up. At which angle should he insert the needle?", "options": {"A": "About 90 degrees, into the muscle, so the dose is absorbed quickly into the circulation.", "B": "About 45 degrees, into the subcutaneous layer, to deposit the dose in fatty tissue.", "C": "About 30 degrees, advancing fully so the dose sits beneath the dermis.", "D": "About 5 to 15 degrees, just under the skin, so that a small wheal forms."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses the intramuscular angle for an intradermal test", "B": "Uses the subcutaneous angle for an intradermal test", "C": "Inserts too deep, below the dermis"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C102", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Marcus Lee works on a medical unit and in an outpatient clinic, giving injections throughout the day. He gives an intramuscular iron injection, a tuberculin skin test, and an intramuscular antibiotic, and later faces a needlestick injury. Each task tests safe injection technique and the response to an incident.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 5 to 15 degrees, just under the skin, so that a small wheal forms.): An intradermal injection is given at a shallow angle, about 5 to 15 degrees, with the bevel up so the dose enters the dermis and raises a small wheal. A 45 degree angle is for subcutaneous injections, and 90 degrees is for intramuscular ones. A deeper insertion places the antigen below the dermis and makes the test unreliable. Look for the wheal."}, {"id": 836, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Marcus prepares an intramuscular antibiotic for an adult at a clinic and chooses the ventrogluteal site. A coworker tells him to always pull back on the plunger before injecting. His employer follows current evidence-informed guidance. Which action about aspiration fits that guidance?", "options": {"A": "Follow current policy and evidence, which do not support routine aspiration at this site.", "B": "Aspirate for thirty seconds at each site, discarding the dose if blood enters the syringe.", "C": "Aspirate only at the deltoid, because superficial veins sit closer to the skin in that muscle.", "D": "Aspirate only for vaccines, since they carry the highest risk of an intravascular reaction."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Applies an outdated routine aspiration practice", "C": "Believes the deltoid needs aspiration while others do not", "D": "Limits aspiration to vaccines without evidence"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C102", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Marcus Lee works on a medical unit and in an outpatient clinic, giving injections throughout the day. He gives an intramuscular iron injection, a tuberculin skin test, and an intramuscular antibiotic, and later faces a needlestick injury. Each task tests safe injection technique and the response to an incident.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Follow current policy and evidence, which do not support routine aspiration at this site.): Current evidence-informed guidance does not support routine aspiration for most intramuscular sites such as the ventrogluteal, so the nurse follows policy and evidence. Aspirating at every site comes from older practice. Selecting only the deltoid or only vaccines has no evidence base. Know your employer's current policy."}, {"id": 837, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After an injection, Marcus recaps the used needle and the cap slips, causing the needle to puncture his finger. The client has no known infection. He is bleeding slightly and wonders whether he can finish his round first. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Wash the site with soap and water, let it bleed, and report immediately for follow-up.", "B": "Finish his medication round, then report the injury after documenting the client's chart entries.", "C": "Apply an alcohol swab, cover the site, and report it after the afternoon round", "D": "Wash the site, then ask the client to consent to testing before reporting"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delays reporting when time-sensitive follow-up is needed", "C": "Skipping washing and delaying the report", "D": "Seeking testing consent before reporting the exposure"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C102", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Marcus Lee works on a medical unit and in an outpatient clinic, giving injections throughout the day. He gives an intramuscular iron injection, a tuberculin skin test, and an intramuscular antibiotic, and later faces a needlestick injury. Each task tests safe injection technique and the response to an incident.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Wash the site with soap and water, let it bleed, and report immediately for follow-up.): After a needlestick, the nurse washes the site with soap and water, lets it bleed, and reports at once so assessment and any post-exposure treatment can start within the needed window. Delaying until the round is finished wastes that time. An alcohol swab is not a substitute for washing, and seeking the client's consent first delays reporting."}, {"id": 838, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Beaulieu, 34, has a T4 spinal cord injury and reports a pounding headache. His blood pressure is 188/110 mmHg, his skin is flushed above the injury, and his pulse is 52. He is lying flat in bed. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Apply a heating pad to his legs to improve circulation and relax muscle tone.", "B": "Sit him upright, lower his legs, loosen tight clothing, and check bladder and bowel.", "C": "Give a prescribed pain medication for the headache and monitor for any further change.", "D": "Keep him lying flat to rest and offer a warm drink before rechecking his blood pressure."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adds a stimulus below the injury", "C": "Treats the headache but ignores the trigger", "D": "Keeps him flat, which raises blood pressure further"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sit him upright, lower his legs, loosen tight clothing, and check bladder and bowel.): The findings point to autonomic dysreflexia, a medical emergency in injuries at T6 or above, so the nurse raises his head, lowers his legs and removes triggers such as a full bladder or bowel. Lying flat can raise blood pressure further. Analgesia alone leaves the trigger in place, and heat below the injury can be another stimulus. Remove the trigger quickly."}, {"id": 839, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Wells, 55, has taken an antipsychotic for ten years and attends a community clinic. The nurse notices rhythmic lip smacking, tongue movements and repetitive chewing. Mrs. Wells says she has always had odd habits when nervous. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Advise her to stop the medication herself until she can see her prescriber.", "B": "Accept her explanation as a nervous habit and reassure her that no drug effect is involved.", "C": "Teach relaxation techniques and tell her the movements will fade with continued use of the drug.", "D": "Document and report the movements as possible tardive dyskinesia, because early review may limit progression."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Advises stopping medication without an order", "B": "Accepts a habit explanation for a drug effect", "C": "Predicts the movements will fade with the drug"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Document and report the movements as possible tardive dyskinesia, because early review may limit progression.): Rhythmic oral movements after long-term antipsychotic use suggest tardive dyskinesia, so the nurse documents and reports them for prescriber review. Accepting the habit explanation delays detection. Advising a client to stop the drug alone risks relapse and withdrawal effects, and the movements do not typically fade with continued use. Report early."}, {"id": 840, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Jordan graduated from a practical nursing program last month and holds temporary registration while awaiting the licensing exam. The charge nurse asks Jordan to act as the sole nurse in charge of a ward overnight. Which response reflects Jordan's scope?", "options": {"A": "Explain that interim status requires supervision, and ask for a licensed nurse to be assigned.", "B": "Accept the assignment, since interim status carries the same independence as a full licence.", "C": "Accept the role and call a supervisor only if a client's condition worsens during the night.", "D": "Accept the assignment but avoid giving medications until the exam results are released."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats interim status as a full licence", "C": "Seeks supervision only after a problem arises", "D": "Limits only medications and still takes sole charge"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that interim status requires supervision, and ask for a licensed nurse to be assigned.): A graduate with temporary status practises under conditions set by the regulator, usually with supervision, so Jordan should not act as the sole nurse in charge. Holding back medications alone does not address the unsupervised role. Calling a supervisor only after a problem is too late. Know the limits of your registration."}, {"id": 841, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A mother brings her 3-year-old to a clinic for a measles, mumps and rubella vaccine. The child has taken high-dose oral corticosteroids for three weeks for a kidney condition. The mother has no concerns, and the child has no fever. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Give the vaccine as scheduled, since the child has no fever today and appears well.", "B": "Give the vaccine and ask the mother to restart the series in four weeks", "C": "Give the vaccine if the child's steroid dose is lowered on the day", "D": "Hold the vaccine and consult the prescriber or public health nurse, since high-dose steroids suppress immunity."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignores immunosuppression because the child looks well", "B": "Giving a live vaccine and planning a repeat series", "C": "Giving a live vaccine after changing steroids without an order"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the vaccine and consult the prescriber or public health nurse, since high-dose steroids suppress immunity.): High-dose corticosteroids over weeks suppress immunity, and live vaccines such as measles, mumps and rubella may be unsafe, so the nurse holds it and consults. A well appearance and normal temperature do not remove the risk. Giving it and repeating later does not prevent a vaccine-related infection. Adjusting the steroid dose is not a nursing decision."}, {"id": 842, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Silva, 50, comes to a clinic with fatigue, excessive thirst and a fruity odour on his breath. His glucose is 24 mmol/L, his breathing is deep and rapid, and he has vomited twice. He asks to go home and return tomorrow. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Report urgently to the physician or NP, since these findings suggest diabetic ketoacidosis.", "B": "Give oral fluids and a snack, then recheck his glucose in thirty minutes", "C": "Send him to the lab for urine testing and a glucose recheck before reporting", "D": "Document the findings and refer him to his family doctor for an appointment this week"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Giving a snack to a client with very high glucose", "C": "Delaying the report for further testing", "D": "Referring routinely when the findings are urgent"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report urgently to the physician or NP, since these findings suggest diabetic ketoacidosis.): Fruity breath, deep rapid breathing, vomiting and a very high glucose strongly suggest diabetic ketoacidosis, which needs urgent medical assessment and treatment. Giving food raises glucose further. Waiting for lab tests or a routine appointment delays that care. Report urgently."}, {"id": 843, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a surgical unit, a practical nurse reads an order for morphine that is ten times the dose usually given to a post-operative client. The physician is in the operating room, and the client is in moderate pain. A colleague says to give it and monitor closely. What is the safest action?", "options": {"A": "Give the dose and monitor breathing every five minutes, since the physician wrote the order.", "B": "Give half of the ordered dose and document the change in the client's chart.", "C": "Hold the dose and clarify the order with the prescriber or the pharmacist before giving.", "D": "Ask a colleague to confirm the dose matches the written order, then give it."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives a likely overdose and relies on monitoring", "B": "Changes the dose without prescriber approval", "D": "Seeks agreement that the dose matches the order, not that it is safe"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hold the dose and clarify the order with the prescriber or the pharmacist before giving.): A dose that looks ten times too high must be clarified before giving, because the nurse shares accountability for safe administration. Giving it and monitoring accepts a probable overdose. Halving the dose changes an order without authority, and a colleague agreeing the dose is as written does not make it safe. Clarify first."}, {"id": 844, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A hospital unit manager plans to introduce a new bedside report routine next month. A practical nurse on the unit wonders how to build staff commitment, because past changes announced by memo met resistance. Which leadership approach is most likely to gain support for this change?", "options": {"A": "Involve front-line staff early in planning, explain the reasons, and invite feedback throughout.", "B": "Announce the final plan by memo with a start date, and review compliance after one month.", "C": "Ask a small group of managers to design the routine and present it at a meeting.", "D": "Offer a financial reward to staff members who adopt the routine in the first week."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Imposes change from the top without input", "C": "Excludes staff who will use the process", "D": "Relies on rewards rather than participation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Involve front-line staff early in planning, explain the reasons, and invite feedback throughout.): Staff are more likely to support a change when they help plan it, understand why it is needed, and can give feedback. Announcing by memo or having managers design it alone repeats the approaches that met resistance. Rewards may speed use briefly but do not build understanding. Involve staff early."}, {"id": 845, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 12-month-old returns to the clinic the day after vaccines with a 3 cm area of redness and swelling at the thigh injection site. He has no fever, is feeding well and is playful. His mother asks if he should have no more vaccines. What should the nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Apply heat and delay the next vaccine until the swelling has been gone a month.", "B": "Skip all future vaccines because this reaction shows he will have a severe one next time.", "C": "Request an antibiotic from the physician because the swelling probably signals a bacterial infection.", "D": "This expected local reaction needs a cool cloth, the usual schedule, and a report if worse."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applies heat and delays the schedule needlessly", "B": "Treats a mild local reaction as a reason to stop vaccines", "C": "Assumes infection and seeks an antibiotic"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (This expected local reaction needs a cool cloth, the usual schedule, and a report if worse.): Redness and swelling at the injection site are common local reactions and do not predict a severe reaction or contraindicate later doses. A cool cloth eases discomfort and the nurse watches for worsening. Skipping vaccines leaves him unprotected, heat can increase swelling, and antibiotics are not indicated for an afebrile, well child. Reassure and report change."}, {"id": 846, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Tanaka, 72, is tired and pale on a medical unit. Her hemoglobin is 78 g/L and her hematocrit is 0.24. A student says the results indicate an infection. Her white blood cell count is normal. How should the nurse interpret these results?", "options": {"A": "They suggest low iron intake, so offer iron-rich meals and recheck in a month", "B": "They indicate fluid overload diluting the blood, so restrict her fluid intake", "C": "They indicate anemia with reduced oxygen-carrying capacity, which could explain her fatigue, and should be reported.", "D": "They indicate mild anemia expected for age, so document and recheck next week"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating results as a diet problem without reporting", "B": "Assuming dilution without signs of overload", "D": "Calling a hemoglobin of 78 g/L mild and age related"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (They indicate anemia with reduced oxygen-carrying capacity, which could explain her fatigue, and should be reported.): A hemoglobin of 78 g/L with a hematocrit of 0.24 is low and indicates anemia, which lowers oxygen delivery and fits her fatigue and pallor, so it should be reported. Iron-rich meals alone do not address a value this low. Dilution from overload would need signs such as crackles or swelling. A hemoglobin this low is not mild or expected with age."}, {"id": 847, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In an emergency department, a 28-year-old with anaphylaxis received intramuscular epinephrine eight minutes ago. She still has wheezing and a blood pressure of 84/50 mmHg. The practical nurse assisting the physician is asked about timing. When may a second dose be given if there is no improvement?", "options": {"A": "Give no second dose and rely on diphenhydramine and oxygen to treat the continuing symptoms.", "B": "Wait one hour, then give an intravenous dose through a peripheral line.", "C": "Repeat intramuscular epinephrine every five to fifteen minutes as ordered while symptoms persist.", "D": "Wait thirty minutes after the first dose to assess for a delayed response."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Replaces epinephrine with adjunct drugs", "B": "Waits an hour and changes to an unordered route", "D": "Waits too long while symptoms continue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat intramuscular epinephrine every five to fifteen minutes as ordered while symptoms persist.): Persisting wheezing and hypotension after the first dose call for a repeat intramuscular dose, which can be given every five to fifteen minutes as ordered. Waiting thirty minutes or an hour leaves a life-threatening reaction untreated. Antihistamines and oxygen are adjuncts and do not replace epinephrine. Repeat promptly."}, {"id": 848, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Ms. Nguyen, 58, speaks little English and attends a clinic with a professional interpreter present. The practical nurse needs her symptom history and finds herself looking at the interpreter while asking questions. Which approach best supports respectful, culturally safe communication?", "options": {"A": "Let her daughter in the room interpret, using the professional only to check", "B": "Face and speak directly to the client in short sentences while the interpreter relays", "C": "Face the client and speak in long sentences while the interpreter waits", "D": "Direct questions to the interpreter, since they understand both languages and can summarize the history."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a family member as the main interpreter", "C": "Using long sentences that overload interpretation", "D": "Addresses the interpreter instead of the client"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Face and speak directly to the client in short sentences while the interpreter relays): Speaking to the client directly keeps her at the centre of care, while the interpreter conveys each statement. Addressing the interpreter treats the client as absent. A family member is not a neutral substitute and may filter information, and long sentences are hard to interpret accurately. Look at and speak to the client in short phrases."}, {"id": 849, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Fortin, 66, had abdominal surgery last year and comes to the emergency department with colicky abdominal pain, repeated vomiting and no stool or gas for two days. His abdomen is distended with high-pitched bowel sounds. Which action should the practical nurse take?", "options": {"A": "Offer small sips of clear fluid and reassess his distension in two hours.", "B": "Keep him NPO, report the findings promptly, and monitor vital signs and abdomen.", "C": "Give a prescribed laxative and offer clear fluids to help restart his bowel function.", "D": "Record the findings and reassess his abdomen at the next scheduled round."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Allows oral fluids despite vomiting and suspected obstruction", "C": "Gives a laxative in a possible blockage", "D": "Delays reporting a possible obstruction until a routine round"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep him NPO, report the findings promptly, and monitor vital signs and abdomen.): Colicky pain, vomiting, distension and no stool or gas after previous abdominal surgery suggest bowel obstruction, so the nurse keeps him NPO, reports promptly and monitors him. A laxative could increase pressure and risk perforation. Oral fluids add to vomiting and distension. Waiting for a routine round delays treatment of a possible emergency."}, {"id": 850, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Hamel, 60, has a hemoglobin of 68 g/L. He can make decisions and refuses a prescribed blood transfusion because of his beliefs. A colleague suggests stopping his other care because he declined treatment. What is the practical nurse's best response?", "options": {"A": "Document his refusal but leave informing the team to the day shift.", "B": "Respect his decision, report it, and continue all other care and comfort without judgment.", "C": "Press him to reconsider at every visit until he agrees to accept the transfusion.", "D": "Continue care but give lower priority to his needs after he declined treatment."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaves the team uninformed of a treatment refusal", "C": "Coerces a capable client to change his decision", "D": "Lowers care priority as a penalty for refusal"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C098", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Amara Okoye works across a hospital medical unit, a surgical unit, the emergency department and a long-term care home during one rotation. Several adult clients make decisions about their care: refusing a blood product, leaving before treatment is complete, withdrawing consent before a procedure, and stating wishes in advance. Each situation tests ethics and consent.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Respect his decision, report it, and continue all other care and comfort without judgment.): A capable adult has the right to refuse treatment, and the nurse continues to provide all other care respectfully while informing the team promptly. Leaving the report for another shift delays team planning. Giving him lower priority punishes his choice. Repeated pressure undermines voluntary choice."}, {"id": 851, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Ms. Tessier, 45, confirms her refusal of a blood transfusion after the physician explains the risks. She then says she wants to dress and leave quickly. The unit is short-staffed, and a colleague tells the nurse to skip the paperwork. What should be documented?", "options": {"A": "A brief note saying she left, plus the physician's entry, without her stated reasons or witnesses.", "B": "Only that she refused and left, since detailed notes apply when treatment is accepted.", "C": "Her refusal alone, because the physician's note already records the explanation of risks.", "D": "The information given, her stated refusal and reasons, who was present, and the team members notified."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaves out reasons and who was present", "B": "Skips documentation because the client is leaving", "C": "Relies on another professional's note for the nurse's own record"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C098", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Amara Okoye works across a hospital medical unit, a surgical unit, the emergency department and a long-term care home during one rotation. Several adult clients make decisions about their care: refusing a blood product, leaving before treatment is complete, withdrawing consent before a procedure, and stating wishes in advance. Each situation tests ethics and consent.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The information given, her stated refusal and reasons, who was present, and the team members notified.): Documenting an informed refusal records the information given, her decision and reasons, who was present and who was told, which protects both the client and the nurse. A short record or reliance on the physician's note leaves gaps. A busy unit does not remove the duty. Document each refusal fully."}, {"id": 852, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Dufour, 38, a capable adult in the emergency department being assessed for chest pain, says he is leaving now because of work. The physician has explained the risks, but he plans to go. A security guard offers to stand at the door. What is the nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Notify the physician and document, but skip discharge instructions since he left against advice.", "B": "Ask security to block the exit until the physician has convinced him to stay for treatment.", "C": "Respect his right to leave, notify the physician, give discharge instructions, and document his decision", "D": "Let him leave once he signs a form, and give instructions if the physician agrees."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withholds return instructions from a client leaving against advice", "B": "Detains a capable adult against his will", "D": "Makes safety instructions depend on signing a form"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C098", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Amara Okoye works across a hospital medical unit, a surgical unit, the emergency department and a long-term care home during one rotation. Several adult clients make decisions about their care: refusing a blood product, leaving before treatment is complete, withdrawing consent before a procedure, and stating wishes in advance. Each situation tests ethics and consent.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Respect his right to leave, notify the physician, give discharge instructions, and document his decision): A capable adult may leave against medical advice, and the nurse informs the physician, gives instructions for returning and documents what was discussed. Blocking the door is unlawful detention. Withholding instructions or making them conditional on a form removes safety information he needs."}, {"id": 853, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lévesque, 52, signed a consent form this morning for a minor surgical procedure. In the preoperative holding area she says she has changed her mind and does not want it. The porter is waiting, and her husband says she already signed. How should the nurse interpret her statement?", "options": {"A": "As nervousness, so reassure her and proceed because the signed form remains valid.", "B": "As a decision that can be accepted only after the surgeon cancels the signed consent form.", "C": "As a valid withdrawal of consent to respect, so stop preparation and inform the surgeon.", "D": "As a request that must wait until her husband agrees, since he is her support person."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats a signed form as binding", "B": "Requires the surgeon's cancellation first", "D": "Defers to a spouse in a capable client's decision"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C098", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Amara Okoye works across a hospital medical unit, a surgical unit, the emergency department and a long-term care home during one rotation. Several adult clients make decisions about their care: refusing a blood product, leaving before treatment is complete, withdrawing consent before a procedure, and stating wishes in advance. Each situation tests ethics and consent.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (As a valid withdrawal of consent to respect, so stop preparation and inform the surgeon.): Consent can be withdrawn at any time before or during a procedure, so a signed form does not override her words. The nurse stops preparation and informs the surgeon. Treating her statement as nerves, waiting for the form to be cancelled, or seeking her husband's approval overrides a capable adult's choice. Honour withdrawal."}, {"id": 854, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A resident of a long-term care home, Mr. Kowalski, 80, asks the practical nurse whether the advance directive he wrote last year applies only if he is dying. He wants his wishes followed if he cannot speak for himself after a stroke. What is the correct explanation?", "options": {"A": "It guides care whenever he cannot decide for himself, such as after a stroke.", "B": "It is a legal order that prevents any change to his wishes after it is signed.", "C": "It applies only when he is terminally ill, so it would not guide stroke care.", "D": "It becomes active only after the physician reviews it and his family agrees to its content."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believes a directive cannot be revised", "C": "Limits directives to terminal illness", "D": "Requires family approval to activate"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C098", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Amara Okoye works across a hospital medical unit, a surgical unit, the emergency department and a long-term care home during one rotation. Several adult clients make decisions about their care: refusing a blood product, leaving before treatment is complete, withdrawing consent before a procedure, and stating wishes in advance. Each situation tests ethics and consent.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It guides care whenever he cannot decide for himself, such as after a stroke.): An advance directive records a person's wishes for use when capacity is lost, from any cause, so it applies after a stroke as well as in terminal illness. It does not need family agreement to take effect. The resident can also revise it while capable. Explain its purpose clearly."}, {"id": 855, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Costa, 60, is one day after lumbar spinal fusion and needs to turn onto his side for a skin check. A coworker suggests turning his shoulders first, then the hips. He has a drain and a patient-controlled analgesia pump. How should the practical nurse turn him?", "options": {"A": "Sit him up on the edge of the bed, then lower him onto his side.", "B": "Ask him to twist and push off the rail on his own, with a nurse nearby.", "C": "Turn his shoulders first, then his hips, while he bends his knees up toward his chest.", "D": "Logroll him as one unit with a draw sheet, spine aligned, pillow between his knees."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Flexes the spine by sitting up first", "B": "Lets the client twist unaided", "C": "Twists the spine by turning in sections"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Logroll him as one unit with a draw sheet, spine aligned, pillow between his knees.): Logrolling moves the shoulders, trunk and hips together, which keeps the fused spine aligned and avoids twisting or flexing. Turning in sections, letting him twist, or sitting up first all load the new fusion. A pillow between the knees supports alignment. Keep the spine in one line."}, {"id": 856, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a pediatric unit, a practical nurse notices that a 6-year-old's ordered antibiotic dose is above the safe range for the child's weight. The physician insists by phone that the dose is correct. The pharmacist is unavailable. What should the nurse do next?", "options": {"A": "Give a lower dose that she calculates is safe, and tell the physician afterward.", "B": "Give the dose since the physician insisted, and document that the order was confirmed by phone.", "C": "Wait until the next shift and hand the problem to the oncoming nurse.", "D": "Withhold the dose, escalate through the chain of command to the manager, and document it."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changes the dose without authority", "B": "Gives an unsafe dose because the prescriber insisted", "C": "Delays escalation and passes the risk on"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold the dose, escalate through the chain of command to the manager, and document it.): When a prescriber insists on a dose that exceeds the safe range, the nurse withholds it and escalates through the chain of command while documenting. Giving it because the prescriber insists makes the nurse accountable for harm. Adjusting the dose alone is outside scope, and waiting until the next shift leaves the child's treatment delayed and unresolved. Escalate now."}, {"id": 857, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A parent at a public health clinic asks how to position their 3-week-old for sleep. They worry that lying on the back may cause choking and want to add a soft blanket and a bumper in the crib. What guidance should the nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Place the infant on the back with a light blanket tucked firmly around the mattress edge.", "B": "Place the infant on the stomach to prevent choking from spit-up and keep the airway clear", "C": "Place the infant on the side, propped with a rolled towel behind the back for stability.", "D": "Place the infant on the back on a firm, flat surface in an empty crib"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Allows loose bedding in the sleep space", "B": "Chooses prone sleep to avoid choking", "C": "Uses side lying with a prop"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Place the infant on the back on a firm, flat surface in an empty crib): Safe sleep means back sleeping on a firm flat surface with an empty crib, because soft items raise the risk of suffocation and sudden infant death. Healthy infants do not choke more on their backs. Blankets, bumpers and propped side positions all increase risk. Back, flat, bare."}, {"id": 858, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Reilly, 80, reports feeling dizzy when she stands. Her sitting blood pressure is 134/80 mmHg. The practical nurse wants to check for orthostatic hypotension, and a colleague says that a sitting reading is enough. Which method gives the most useful assessment?", "options": {"A": "Measure blood pressure after one minute lying down, then after sitting up, comparing the readings.", "B": "Measure the blood pressure standing first, then lying down after a ten minute rest.", "C": "Measure blood pressure lying down, then after standing for one and three minutes, comparing the readings.", "D": "Measure her blood pressure and pulse sitting, then standing once at five minutes, comparing the readings."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Compares lying with sitting, not standing, after a short rest", "B": "Reverses the order, making comparison unreliable", "D": "Skips a lying baseline and misses the early postural drop"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C104", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Dana Whitehorse spends a shift with several older adults and adults on a medical unit and in the community. She measures orthostatic pressures, chooses temperature routes for an infant, assesses heat exposure, and checks an older adult for hypothermia. Each situation tests careful assessment of vital signs and the response to abnormal findings.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Measure blood pressure lying down, then after standing for one and three minutes, comparing the readings.): Orthostatic hypotension is found by comparing lying pressure with pressure after standing at one and three minutes, so position changes must be measured in that order. Comparing sitting with lying does not stress the circulation. A single standing reading at five minutes misses the early drop and has no lying baseline. Starting while standing reverses the sequence."}, {"id": 859, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A mother brings her 4-month-old to a clinic because the baby feels warm. The nurse takes an axillary temperature and reads 37.6 C, but the infant is flushed and irritable. A colleague says the axillary reading equals core temperature. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Treat the axillary value as an estimate, recheck by a route nearer core temperature, and report.", "B": "Add one degree to the axillary reading and give acetaminophen immediately.", "C": "Accept the reading as the core temperature and reassure the mother that no fever is present.", "D": "Wait ten minutes and repeat the axillary reading, since the first one was probably too soon."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Adds a correction and treats without an order", "C": "Treats an axillary value as core temperature", "D": "Repeats the same less reliable route"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C104", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Dana Whitehorse spends a shift with several older adults and adults on a medical unit and in the community. She measures orthostatic pressures, chooses temperature routes for an infant, assesses heat exposure, and checks an older adult for hypothermia. Each situation tests careful assessment of vital signs and the response to abnormal findings.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Treat the axillary value as an estimate, recheck by a route nearer core temperature, and report.): Axillary readings usually underestimate core temperature, and a flushed irritable infant suggests the true value may be higher, so a route nearer core temperature is needed and the finding reported. Accepting 37.6 C as core risks missing a fever. Adding a degree and giving a drug is not an order or a measurement. Repeating the same route repeats the limitation."}, {"id": 860, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 45-year-old roofer is brought to a clinic after working six hours in 38 C heat. His temperature is 40.5 C, his skin is hot and dry, and he is confused. He took no medication today. His coworker asks the nurse to give acetaminophen. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Move him to a cool area, start active cooling, and call for emergency help.", "B": "Offer a large drink of cold water and let him rest in the shade to recover.", "C": "Move him inside, apply cool cloths, and monitor his temperature before calling for help.", "D": "Move him to shade, cool him with a fan, and transport him privately."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Gives oral fluids to a confused client and delays cooling", "C": "Delays emergency help while using minimal cooling", "D": "Uses private transport instead of calling emergency services"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C104", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Dana Whitehorse spends a shift with several older adults and adults on a medical unit and in the community. She measures orthostatic pressures, chooses temperature routes for an infant, assesses heat exposure, and checks an older adult for hypothermia. Each situation tests careful assessment of vital signs and the response to abnormal findings.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Move him to a cool area, start active cooling, and call for emergency help.): A very high temperature with hot dry skin and confusion after heat exposure suggests heat stroke, which needs rapid active cooling and emergency care. Mild cooling alone delays the help he needs. Private transport removes cooling and monitoring during travel. Oral fluids are unsafe with confusion, and antipyretics do not work in heat illness."}, {"id": 861, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A home care nurse finds Mr. Ellis, 84, in a cold house. He is drowsy, pale and cool to touch, with a rectal temperature of 33.5 C. He is not shivering. His daughter says he would shiver if he were truly cold. What does the lack of shivering suggest?", "options": {"A": "Older adults may not shiver, so the absence of shivering does not rule out significant hypothermia.", "B": "His drowsiness suggests mild fatigue, so warm blankets and a recheck in the morning are enough.", "C": "His body is regulating temperature well, so the low reading is probably a thermometer error.", "D": "Shivering stops when the body conserves heat, so his low reading is a normal adjustment."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats drowsiness with hypothermia as fatigue and delays care", "C": "Assumes shivering must be present", "D": "Misreads absent shivering as normal heat conservation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C104", "cluster_text": "Practical nurse Dana Whitehorse spends a shift with several older adults and adults on a medical unit and in the community. She measures orthostatic pressures, chooses temperature routes for an infant, assesses heat exposure, and checks an older adult for hypothermia. Each situation tests careful assessment of vital signs and the response to abnormal findings.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Older adults may not shiver, so the absence of shivering does not rule out significant hypothermia.): Older adults often have a blunted shivering response, and a temperature of 33.5 C with drowsiness indicates hypothermia that needs gentle rewarming and medical help. Treating drowsiness as tiredness delays care. The missing shiver is not a normal adjustment, and a cold reading is not likely to be a thermometer error. Treat the temperature, not the shiver."}, {"id": 862, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a long-term care home, Mrs. Sinclair, 86, has dry mucous membranes and a furrowed tongue and has been drinking less for two days. The practical nurse pinches the skin on her forearm and it tents slowly. A colleague says to rely on that alone. How should the nurse assess dehydration?", "options": {"A": "Rely on one morning blood pressure reading and ignore changes in urine colour or intake", "B": "Rely on forearm skin turgor, since it is the most dependable sign in older adults.", "C": "Assess several signs together, such as mucous membranes, urine output, weight and mental status.", "D": "Judge from thirst, because older adults reliably feel thirsty as soon as they need fluid."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses one value and ignores other signs", "B": "Over-relies on a sign that is unreliable with age", "D": "Assumes thirst is intact in older adults"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess several signs together, such as mucous membranes, urine output, weight and mental status.): Skin turgor is unreliable in older adults because their skin loses elasticity, so dehydration is judged from several findings such as mucous membranes, urine, weight and mental status. Thirst is often blunted with age. A single blood pressure and ignoring urine colour miss key evidence. Combine the signs."}, {"id": 863, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Larocque, 71, has a nasogastric tube to low intermittent suction for a small bowel obstruction. After six hours he says he feels better and the nausea has stopped. His abdomen is still distended. He asks the nurse to clamp the tube. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Keep the tube connected to suction as ordered, check placement and output, and report the improvement.", "B": "Clamp the tube for an hour and see whether his nausea returns on its own.", "C": "Disconnect the suction and let the tube drain by gravity for the rest of shift.", "D": "Remove the tube since his symptoms have settled and discuss it at the next rounds."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Clamps the tube while the obstruction persists", "C": "Changes drainage method without an order", "D": "Removes the tube based on comfort alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep the tube connected to suction as ordered, check placement and output, and report the improvement.): Feeling better is the result of decompression, and his distended abdomen shows the obstruction remains, so suction continues as ordered with checks of placement and output and a report of the change. Clamping, switching to gravity drainage or removing the tube without an order would allow gastric contents to build up again. Continue until the prescriber changes the plan."}, {"id": 864, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Grant, 35, is scheduled for surgery at 0800, and the surgical team ordered nothing by mouth after midnight. At 0600 she is thirsty and asks whether she can have some tea and a few sips of juice. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Allow tea with milk, since her surgery is only two hours away.", "B": "Allow sips of juice if she swishes and spits the rest into a basin.", "C": "Allow sips of water since fasting rules are only a guideline", "D": "Explain that fasting lowers aspiration risk, offer mouth care, and report her thirst to the team."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Allows milk, which delays gastric emptying", "B": "Allows juice by a loophole", "C": "Treats the fasting order as a loose guideline the nurse may override"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that fasting lowers aspiration risk, offer mouth care, and report her thirst to the team.): The surgical team ordered nothing by mouth after midnight, so the nurse follows the order, explains that fasting lowers aspiration risk, offers mouth care and tells the team about her thirst. Only the surgical or anesthesia team can change the fasting instruction, even where current guidance allows clear fluids closer to surgery. Tea with milk is not a clear fluid, and swishing and spitting juice does not meet the order. Treating the fasting order as a loose guideline overrides the team's plan. Follow the order and meet her comfort needs."}, {"id": 865, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Kovac, 77, started oral morphine for cancer pain nine days ago. At a home visit he reports small hard stools every third day and abdominal bloating. His daughter says he dislikes prune juice and refuses it. The practical nurse finds no bowel regimen on his orders. What is the best interpretation and action?", "options": {"A": "Bloating suggests a bowel obstruction, so hold the morphine and arrange emergency transfer for him.", "B": "Bowel patterns slow with age, so reassess only if five days pass without a bowel movement.", "C": "Opioid constipation is expected, so ask the prescriber for a scheduled laxative regimen now.", "D": "Low fiber intake explains the hard stools, so encourage bran cereal and wait another week."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Jumps to obstruction and withholds analgesia, which exceeds scope and leaves pain untreated", "B": "Waits for several days without stool, missing the preventive purpose of a regimen", "D": "Blames diet alone and delays treatment of an opioid effect"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Opioid constipation is expected, so ask the prescriber for a scheduled laxative regimen now.): Morphine slows bowel motility, and Mr. Kovac already shows hard stools and bloating, so a scheduled regimen is overdue and the prescriber must be asked for it. Diet advice alone fails because fiber will not offset opioid slowing, and his refusal of prunes shows the plan needs more than food. Waiting for several days without a stool lets constipation worsen and hurts comfort. Constipation prevention starts when an opioid does."}, {"id": 866, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Nurse manager Priya is introducing a new bedside handover policy on a surgical unit. Several nurses have said the extra steps feel pointless. As a member of the unit's change team, the practical nurse is asked to brief the night staff. Which approach to the briefing best supports adoption of the policy?", "options": {"A": "Explain why the change is needed and what it improves for clients, then demonstrate the steps.", "B": "Distribute the revised steps in writing and ask staff to sign that they have read them.", "C": "Tell staff the policy is mandatory and describe the organization's consequences for not following it.", "D": "Review the new steps verbally at the start of shift and answer questions about timing."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Gives only the new steps and omits the reason for the change", "C": "Relies on authority and consequences, which builds compliance rather than understanding", "D": "Covers the procedure and timing but not its purpose, leaving resistance in place"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain why the change is needed and what it improves for clients, then demonstrate the steps.): Staff who already see the steps as pointless need to hear the reason for the change and the benefit to clients, and a demonstration makes the steps concrete. A signed written notice proves distribution but does not address the doubt that was voiced. A verbal review of steps and timing answers practical questions yet still skips the purpose. Communicating the why before the how is a core part of leading change."}, {"id": 867, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Fontaine asks why his ankles swelled after he started a new blood pressure pill. His lungs are clear, his weight is stable, and the swelling is symmetrical. The practical nurse considers the timing of the pill and these findings before replying. What is the best interpretation of the swelling?", "options": {"A": "A clot in both legs is likely, so he needs urgent ultrasound before any other step.", "B": "Early heart failure is the probable cause, so he needs urgent chest assessment and diuretic therapy.", "C": "Peripheral edema is a common adverse effect of amlodipine and should be reported to the prescriber.", "D": "Fluid retention from dietary sodium is the cause, so amlodipine can continue unchanged for now."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Suspects deep vein thrombosis, which rarely causes symmetrical bilateral swelling", "B": "Assumes swelling always means heart failure despite clear lungs and stable weight", "D": "Attributes swelling to salt and overlooks the timing after starting the drug"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C100", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dale Fontaine, 68, attends a community clinic with both ankles swollen for two weeks. He has hypertension and started amlodipine five weeks ago. He denies shortness of breath, orthopnea or weight gain. His lungs are clear, blood pressure is 132/78 mmHg, pulse is 74, and the swelling is pitting 1+ and symmetrical. He asks whether he should simply stop the pill.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Peripheral edema is a common adverse effect of amlodipine and should be reported to the prescriber.): The swelling began weeks after amlodipine was started, is symmetrical, and comes with clear lungs and stable weight, which fits a calcium channel blocker adverse effect that the prescriber needs to know about. Heart failure usually brings breathlessness, weight gain or crackles, none of which are present. Blaming sodium would let a drug effect go unreported. Report new swelling after starting a medication rather than stopping it independently."}, {"id": 868, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Fontaine's prescriber added furosemide for the swelling. Two weeks later, on a home visit, he reports leg cramps and weakness, and the nurse notes an irregular pulse. He says he plans to eat more salty foods to replace what he loses. Which interpretation and action are best?", "options": {"A": "Weakness is a normal early response to diuresis, so reassess in one week without reporting.", "B": "Cramps mean the dose is too low, so ask whether he may take an extra tablet.", "C": "Hypokalemia is likely, so report the findings and request a potassium level before any dietary change.", "D": "Dehydration alone explains the cramps, so encourage extra salt and fluids and recheck his pulse tomorrow."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Normalizes weakness and an irregular pulse, delaying a needed report", "B": "Reads cramps as under-dosing and invites self-adjustment of a diuretic", "D": "Treats electrolyte loss as simple dehydration and advises salt, which worsens fluid and blood pressure control"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C100", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dale Fontaine, 68, attends a community clinic with both ankles swollen for two weeks. He has hypertension and started amlodipine five weeks ago. He denies shortness of breath, orthopnea or weight gain. His lungs are clear, blood pressure is 132/78 mmHg, pulse is 74, and the swelling is pitting 1+ and symmetrical. He asks whether he should simply stop the pill.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hypokalemia is likely, so report the findings and request a potassium level before any dietary change.): Furosemide increases potassium loss, and cramps, weakness and an irregular pulse together point to low potassium, so a level and prescriber direction are needed before anything else changes. Extra salt does not replace potassium and may raise his blood pressure. Taking an extra tablet would deepen the loss. Irregular pulse with diuretic use is always a reason to report."}, {"id": 869, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Fontaine's kidney function has since worsened, with an eGFR of 38 mL/min, and the prescriber orders spironolactone alongside ramipril. Four days later he reports muscle weakness and palpitations. He also started a salt substitute last week. Which complication is most likely, and what is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Hyponatremia from fluid retention is likely, so restrict water and recheck vital signs in four hours.", "B": "Hypokalemia from the diuretic is likely, so give an oral potassium supplement from unit stock.", "C": "Anxiety about the new medications is likely, so offer reassurance and reassess weakness after supper.", "D": "Hyperkalemia is likely, so report urgently and seek direction before giving the next doses."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confuses the sodium effect with potassium retention and delays reporting", "B": "Applies the loop diuretic potassium rule to a potassium-sparing drug", "C": "Attributes physical symptoms to anxiety and misses a medication interaction"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C100", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dale Fontaine, 68, attends a community clinic with both ankles swollen for two weeks. He has hypertension and started amlodipine five weeks ago. He denies shortness of breath, orthopnea or weight gain. His lungs are clear, blood pressure is 132/78 mmHg, pulse is 74, and the swelling is pitting 1+ and symmetrical. He asks whether he should simply stop the pill.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hyperkalemia is likely, so report urgently and seek direction before giving the next doses.): Spironolactone and an ACE inhibitor both raise potassium, reduced kidney function slows its removal, and a potassium-based salt substitute adds more, so weakness and palpitations suggest hyperkalemia that needs urgent reporting. Giving potassium would be dangerous. Restricting water addresses sodium, not potassium. Check for hidden potassium sources when these drugs are combined."}, {"id": 870, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Fontaine has now also been prescribed sublingual nitroglycerin for chest tightness on exertion. He plans to move a few tablets into his weekly pill organizer so he can carry them easily. He asks the practical nurse how to use and store them. Which teaching is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Replace the tablets every year and keep the bottle in the bathroom cabinet for convenience.", "B": "Take one tablet while standing, then walk slowly until the chest tightness settles.", "C": "Transfer tablets to a plastic organizer so they stay dry and reachable during daily walks.", "D": "Keep tablets in the original dark glass bottle, close to you, and sit before taking one."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gets the replacement idea partly right but stores it where heat and humidity reduce potency", "B": "Advises standing, which risks dizziness and fainting from the blood pressure drop", "C": "Overlooks that light and moisture degrade nitroglycerin outside its glass bottle"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C100", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dale Fontaine, 68, attends a community clinic with both ankles swollen for two weeks. He has hypertension and started amlodipine five weeks ago. He denies shortness of breath, orthopnea or weight gain. His lungs are clear, blood pressure is 132/78 mmHg, pulse is 74, and the swelling is pitting 1+ and symmetrical. He asks whether he should simply stop the pill.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep tablets in the original dark glass bottle, close to you, and sit before taking one.): Nitroglycerin loses potency in light, heat and moisture, so the original dark glass bottle kept with him is best, and sitting protects him from the blood pressure drop the drug causes. A plastic organizer exposes the tablets to air and light. Standing and walking after a dose raises the risk of fainting. Teach safe storage and positioning together."}, {"id": 871, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "While emptying a drainage bag, Amara, a practical nurse, feels a splash of urine and blood enter her left eye. She wears glasses without side shields. Her eye stings, and she wants to finish drawing up a client's insulin first. What should she do first?", "options": {"A": "Rub the eye gently with a clean tissue to remove blood, then complete the insulin task.", "B": "Flush the eye with running water or saline for several minutes, then report the exposure.", "C": "Apply antibiotic eye ointment from the unit supply, then report the exposure at shift end.", "D": "Cover the eye with a sterile patch and go directly to the emergency department."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Rubs the eye, which spreads contamination and irritates tissue", "C": "Treats with an ointment and delays reporting, missing the time-sensitive follow-up", "D": "Skips immediate irrigation and patches the eye, trapping contaminated fluid"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C101", "cluster_text": "Amara is a practical nurse on a medical unit following routine practices. This shift she is splashed in the eye while emptying a bloody drainage bag, then places an indwelling urinary catheter for Mrs. Lopez, 79, then prepares a sterile dressing change for another client, and later assists in the procedure room. Each task tests her infection prevention practice. The unit also cares for clients with several different infection risks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Flush the eye with running water or saline for several minutes, then report the exposure.): A splash of blood and body fluid to the mucous membranes needs immediate copious flushing, which dilutes and removes the exposure, followed by prompt reporting so post-exposure assessment can start. Rubbing spreads the fluid and may abrade the eye. Ointment and a late report waste the time window. Irrigate first, then report and seek follow-up."}, {"id": 872, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Amara has reported her exposure and returns to insert an indwelling urinary catheter for Mrs. Lopez. After opening the kit she realizes her gloved right hand brushed the outside of the sterile drape while she positioned it. The catheter and lubricant remain untouched. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Move the touched edge away from the perineum and keep working from the clean side.", "B": "Continue with the procedure because the catheter itself was not touched by the glove.", "C": "Treat the kit as contaminated, perform hand hygiene, and open a new sterile kit.", "D": "Wipe the drape edge with an alcohol swab and continue with fresh sterile gloves."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Tries to work around the break, accepting a field that is no longer sterile", "B": "Reasons that only the catheter must be sterile, ignoring the drape and field", "D": "Believes antiseptic can restore sterility to a contaminated sterile field"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C101", "cluster_text": "Amara is a practical nurse on a medical unit following routine practices. This shift she is splashed in the eye while emptying a bloody drainage bag, then places an indwelling urinary catheter for Mrs. Lopez, 79, then prepares a sterile dressing change for another client, and later assists in the procedure room. Each task tests her infection prevention practice. The unit also cares for clients with several different infection risks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat the kit as contaminated, perform hand hygiene, and open a new sterile kit.): Contact between a non-sterile surface and the sterile drape breaks the field, so the kit is treated as contaminated and replaced after hand hygiene. Continuing because the catheter is clean overlooks that the whole field protects the client. Alcohol does not make a drape sterile. When sterility is in doubt, replace the item."}, {"id": 873, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Later Amara prepares a sterile dressing change for another client. She has opened the field and poured saline. A colleague needs forceps from the shelf and reaches across the field to grab them, while the field's lower edge hangs below tabletop level. Which statement about the sterile field applies?", "options": {"A": "Field sterility depends on the gloves worn by the nurse who set it up.", "B": "Reaching over the field is acceptable once the person has performed recent hand hygiene with antiseptic.", "C": "Items below table level and the outer edge are not sterile, and reaching across contaminates it.", "D": "The outer 2.5 cm border and anything near it remain sterile if gloves are worn."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ties field sterility only to gloves and ignores boundaries and passage over the field", "B": "Believes hand hygiene permits reaching over the field", "D": "Treats the border as sterile when it is considered contaminated"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C101", "cluster_text": "Amara is a practical nurse on a medical unit following routine practices. This shift she is splashed in the eye while emptying a bloody drainage bag, then places an indwelling urinary catheter for Mrs. Lopez, 79, then prepares a sterile dressing change for another client, and later assists in the procedure room. Each task tests her infection prevention practice. The unit also cares for clients with several different infection risks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Items below table level and the outer edge are not sterile, and reaching across contaminates it.): A sterile field stays sterile only above waist and table level, inside its border, and without non-sterile items passing over it, so the draped edge and the reach both contaminate it here. The border is treated as non-sterile whatever gloves are worn. Hand hygiene does not make reaching across acceptable. Keep non-sterile objects and arms away from the field."}, {"id": 874, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Amara is asked to scrub in and assist the physician with minor wound closure in the procedure room. Her hands are visibly clean, and she performed routine hand hygiene before breakfast. The surgeon says the procedure is short. Which action best prepares her hands for sterile assisting?", "options": {"A": "Perform routine hand hygiene with soap and water for fifteen seconds and then don sterile gloves.", "B": "Wash for fifteen seconds, then rely on the sterile gloves to provide the protection.", "C": "Apply an alcohol hand rub and put on non-sterile gloves because the procedure is minor.", "D": "Do surgical hand antisepsis with an antiseptic scrub or rub, then don sterile gloves."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats routine hand hygiene as equal to surgical antisepsis", "B": "Depends on the gloves alone and skips the antisepsis that guards against glove punctures", "C": "Uses non-sterile gloves for a sterile procedure because it is minor"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C101", "cluster_text": "Amara is a practical nurse on a medical unit following routine practices. This shift she is splashed in the eye while emptying a bloody drainage bag, then places an indwelling urinary catheter for Mrs. Lopez, 79, then prepares a sterile dressing change for another client, and later assists in the procedure room. Each task tests her infection prevention practice. The unit also cares for clients with several different infection risks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Do surgical hand antisepsis with an antiseptic scrub or rub, then don sterile gloves.): Assisting at a sterile procedure calls for surgical hand antisepsis, which reduces resident and transient organisms for longer than routine hand hygiene, followed by sterile gloves. Routine soap and water leaves higher counts if a glove tears. Non-sterile gloves are inappropriate for a sterile field. Procedure length does not change the standard."}, {"id": 875, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Eight-year-old Mateo has had a fever of 38.9 C for two days and pain over his right shin that worsens when he walks. His mother thinks it is muscle soreness from soccer. The leg is warm and tender at one spot, with no visible injury. Which condition should the practical nurse recognize and report?", "options": {"A": "Growing pains, which cause aching in both legs at night without fever or point tenderness.", "B": "Bone infection such as osteomyelitis, needing prompt assessment, blood work and imaging.", "C": "Muscle strain from sport, which improves with rest, ice and gradual return to activity.", "D": "Simple cellulitis of the skin, which can be managed with a warm compress and observation."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Mislabels growing pains, which are bilateral and without fever", "C": "Accepts the parent's explanation of muscle soreness despite fever and focal tenderness", "D": "Treats a deeper process as superficial skin infection and delays workup"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bone infection such as osteomyelitis, needing prompt assessment, blood work and imaging.): Fever with focal bone pain, warmth and tenderness in a child suggests osteomyelitis, which is urgent because delayed treatment risks bone damage. Muscle strain does not cause fever and tenderness at one spot. Growing pains are bilateral, nocturnal and afebrile. Cellulitis would show skin changes and not point bony tenderness."}, {"id": 876, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the postnatal unit, a 30-hour-old newborn who has fed well for more than 24 hours is due for newborn screening. The mother asks why the heel sample is taken now instead of at birth, and whether it can wait until the first clinic visit. What should the practical nurse explain?", "options": {"A": "Results are more reliable after 24 hours of feeding, and early collection aids treatment.", "B": "Samples before 12 hours of age are preferred, because feeding can alter the results.", "C": "Testing at birth is better, but the sample was missed, so it can wait for clinic.", "D": "The sample must be taken at exactly 48 hours, so collection now is unreliable."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "States a wrong preferred age for collection", "C": "Believes birth testing is preferred and a delay to clinic is harmless", "D": "Gives a wrong fixed hour for valid collection"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Results are more reliable after 24 hours of feeding, and early collection aids treatment.): Several screened conditions need some feeding and metabolism before markers rise, so a sample after about 24 hours of feeding is more reliable, and collecting on schedule lets treatment begin early. Waiting for a clinic visit risks missing the treatment window. Very early samples may miss conditions, and a rigid 48 hour rule is not the standard. Follow the provincial schedule for exact timing."}, {"id": 877, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Chen, 58, is on the surgical list for 0800 tomorrow and has been told to take nothing by mouth after midnight. She takes metformin and a long-acting insulin at bedtime. She asks if she should take her usual doses tonight and tomorrow morning. How should the practical nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Skip all diabetes medications until she is eating again, since fasting prevents hypoglycemia.", "B": "Give the usual insulin dose tonight because skipping insulin causes high glucose and delays healing.", "C": "Take the usual morning dose with a sip of water because medication does not break fasting.", "D": "Check the preoperative orders and confirm any insulin changes with the prescriber first."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Omits all medication without orders, risking hyperglycemia", "B": "Applies an unchanged dose while she is fasting, risking hypoglycemia", "C": "Assumes a morning dose is safe while fasting without confirming orders"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Check the preoperative orders and confirm any insulin changes with the prescriber first.): Dose changes for fasting depend on the preoperative orders and the prescriber, so the nurse checks and confirms before she takes anything. Her usual insulin while fasting risks hypoglycemia. Stopping everything without direction risks high glucose. A morning dose with water may be allowed for some drugs, but only when ordered."}, {"id": 878, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A practical nurse moving from long-term care to a community home care agency asks a colleague whether her legal scope of practice will change because the setting is different. The colleague says each setting has its own legal scope. What guidance best reflects the principle of scope of practice?", "options": {"A": "Scope depends on the client's preference, so the nurse follows what each client agrees to.", "B": "Scope is set by each practice setting, so her legal authority changes when she changes employers.", "C": "Scope is set by the supervising registered nurse on each shift, and changes with each assignment.", "D": "Scope is set by the regulatory body and her competence, and employer policy may add limits."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Lets client preference define legal scope", "B": "Treats the setting as the source of legal scope", "C": "Treats a supervising nurse as the source of scope"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Scope is set by the regulatory body and her competence, and employer policy may add limits.): Legal scope of practice comes from regulation and legislation, and individual competence further defines what she may do, while employers may restrict but not widen it. Settings do not change her legal authority. Client preference and daily assignment do not define scope. Her own judgment works within regulatory limits."}, {"id": 879, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Alvarez, 66, with emphysema comes to clinic reporting that she becomes breathless climbing stairs. She breathes quickly through her mouth during exertion, and her oxygen saturation falls to 88 percent on room air after a short walk. The nurse plans to teach pursed-lip breathing. Which instruction should the practical nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Take rapid deep breaths through the mouth to blow off carbon dioxide during exertion.", "B": "Inhale through pursed lips and exhale forcefully through the mouth to clear secretions.", "C": "Hold each breath for ten seconds before exhaling so oxygen has more time to absorb.", "D": "Breathe in slowly through the nose, then exhale gently through pursed lips twice as long."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Teaches rapid breathing, which traps air and raises breathlessness", "B": "Reverses the pattern, inhaling through pursed lips and forcing out the breath", "C": "Teaches breath holding, which does not help airway trapping and may cause distress"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Breathe in slowly through the nose, then exhale gently through pursed lips twice as long.): Pursed-lip breathing keeps airways open longer on exhalation, reduces air trapping and slows the rate, so inhaling through the nose and exhaling gently twice as long fits her emphysema. Rapid deep breaths increase trapping. Forced exhalation can collapse small airways. Holding the breath adds no benefit and may worsen breathlessness."}, {"id": 880, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor, 72, takes warfarin for atrial fibrillation and is scheduled for hernia repair in five days. His last INR was 2.6. He asks if he should take his dose tonight as usual, and says he does not want to bother the surgeon. What should the practical nurse tell him?", "options": {"A": "Stop warfarin tonight and restart on the morning of surgery to clear it quickly.", "B": "Warfarin is usually adjusted before surgery, so confirm the exact plan with the surgeon today.", "C": "Take an extra aspirin tablet daily this week to protect against clots while off warfarin.", "D": "Continue warfarin unchanged because stopping it raises stroke risk and surgeons manage bleeding during surgery."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Invents a stop and restart schedule without orders", "C": "Adds an antiplatelet drug, increasing bleeding risk", "D": "Continues anticoagulation without a perioperative plan, risking bleeding"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Warfarin is usually adjusted before surgery, so confirm the exact plan with the surgeon today.): Anticoagulants are managed with a specific preoperative plan, which may involve stopping days ahead or bridging, so the nurse confirms the plan with the surgeon rather than guessing. Continuing unchanged risks surgical bleeding. A self-made stop and restart schedule may be wrong for his INR. Aspirin adds bleeding risk and is not a substitute."}, {"id": 881, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Rina plans to assign Dev the morning bath and repositioning for Mrs. Adams, who has a stage 2 pressure injury. She is about to say, 'Please do Mrs. Adams' bath and turns.' Rina hopes this is enough direction. What additional information should she include to give a clear assignment?", "options": {"A": "Name the supplies to use and the time the bath must be finished by.", "B": "State what to observe, how often to turn her, and when to report back.", "C": "Tell him she will be available later and he can ask if something is unclear.", "D": "Remind him of facility policy on documentation and where the chart is kept."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives logistics but omits observation and reporting expectations", "C": "Leaves it to him to seek help, so unknown risks are unreported", "D": "Cites charting policy without the assignment's expected outcomes"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C108", "cluster_text": "Rina, a practical nurse in a 40-bed long-term care home, begins her day shift responsible for eight residents. She works with Dev, an unregulated care provider with four months of experience. Care includes a morning bath for Mr. Hill who is stable, a repositioning schedule for Mrs. Adams who has a stage 2 pressure injury, and vital signs for Mr. Cho who recently started a new antihypertensive.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (State what to observe, how often to turn her, and when to report back.): A clear assignment names the task, what findings to watch for in a skin-compromised resident, the turning interval and when to report back, so Dev knows what matters. Supplies and a deadline cover logistics only. Documentation policy does not explain what to look for. Saying she is available shifts the burden to Dev to recognize what to ask."}, {"id": 882, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Dev tells Rina that Mrs. Adams' sacral area looked a little red after the last turn but he thinks it is fine. Rina is in the middle of a medication pass and had planned to review Dev's work at the end of the shift. How should she apply the principle of following up after delegating?", "options": {"A": "Ask Dev to apply barrier cream and recheck the area himself in two hours.", "B": "Wait until end of shift as planned and review Dev's documentation then.", "C": "Document Dev's observation as fine and reassess at the next scheduled turn.", "D": "Assess the skin herself soon, since a changed finding requires nursing judgment."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delegates reassessment of a changed skin finding back to the unregulated provider", "B": "Delays follow-up until the end of the shift despite a new finding", "C": "Records a finding without assessing it, leaving the change unevaluated"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C108", "cluster_text": "Rina, a practical nurse in a 40-bed long-term care home, begins her day shift responsible for eight residents. She works with Dev, an unregulated care provider with four months of experience. Care includes a morning bath for Mr. Hill who is stable, a repositioning schedule for Mrs. Adams who has a stage 2 pressure injury, and vital signs for Mr. Cho who recently started a new antihypertensive.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess the skin herself soon, since a changed finding requires nursing judgment.): A reported skin change in a client with a pressure injury is an assessment, which the practical nurse keeps, so Rina assesses soon and decides whether to report or adjust the plan. Waiting till the end of shift lets a possible deterioration continue. Delegating the recheck gives away judgment she retains. Charting without assessment hides the change."}, {"id": 883, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At shift end Dev says he charted Mr. Cho's morning blood pressure as 128/76, but Rina does not recall seeing that reading. Dev admits he wrote it from memory after a colleague told him the numbers. Rina is asked to cosign the entry. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Cosign after Dev confirms the value verbally and note it in the chart.", "B": "Verify the reading at its source before signing, and coach Dev on charting.", "C": "Add a late entry with the reading she believes is right, then cosign.", "D": "Leave the entry unsigned and let the registered nurse resolve it with the charge nurse."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepts a verbal confirmation instead of the measured source", "C": "Adds a guessed value and cosigns an unverified entry", "D": "Leaves an unverified entry for the registered nurse, passing on her accountability"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C108", "cluster_text": "Rina, a practical nurse in a 40-bed long-term care home, begins her day shift responsible for eight residents. She works with Dev, an unregulated care provider with four months of experience. Care includes a morning bath for Mr. Hill who is stable, a repositioning schedule for Mrs. Adams who has a stage 2 pressure injury, and vital signs for Mr. Cho who recently started a new antihypertensive.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Verify the reading at its source before signing, and coach Dev on charting.): Cosigning makes Rina accountable for the entry, and a value written from hearsay is unverified, so she confirms it with the real measurement and coaches Dev on documenting only what he measured. A verbal restatement is not a source check. Entering a value she believes is right is another unverified entry. Leaving it unsigned avoids her responsibility."}, {"id": 884, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Near the end of the shift, three things happen together: Mr. Hill says he is thirsty, Mr. Cho rings, feeling dizzy, with a new blood pressure of 90/54 mmHg, and Mrs. Adams' daughter loudly demands an update in the hallway. Which should Rina address first?", "options": {"A": "Ask Dev to check Mr. Cho's blood pressure while she calms the daughter, then review him", "B": "Speak with Mrs. Adams' daughter first, because her distress is disrupting other residents.", "C": "Assess Mr. Cho's dizziness and low blood pressure first, as his circulation is at risk", "D": "Give Mr. Hill a drink first, since the request is quick and meets basic comfort needs"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delegates an unstable resident's assessment to an unregulated provider", "B": "Responds to the loudest concern instead of the physiological risk", "D": "Chooses a quick comfort task before a possible circulation problem"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C108", "cluster_text": "Rina, a practical nurse in a 40-bed long-term care home, begins her day shift responsible for eight residents. She works with Dev, an unregulated care provider with four months of experience. Care includes a morning bath for Mr. Hill who is stable, a repositioning schedule for Mrs. Adams who has a stage 2 pressure injury, and vital signs for Mr. Cho who recently started a new antihypertensive.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess Mr. Cho's dizziness and low blood pressure first, as his circulation is at risk): A new drop in blood pressure with dizziness suggests compromised circulation, which outranks thirst and a distressed family member, so Rina assesses Mr. Cho first and then reports. Calming the daughter matters but is not urgent. Thirst can wait a few minutes. Assessing an unstable resident is not delegated."}, {"id": 885, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Tran, 61, has stable heart failure and is cared for on a medical unit by a practical nurse. His weight, vital signs and lung sounds are unchanged from yesterday. A new nurse believes every assessment and dressing change the practical nurse performs needs RN sign off. What best describes the practical nurse's role?", "options": {"A": "Practical nurses work only under direct RN supervision and need approval before each assessment.", "B": "Practical nurses care for stable clients, but need RN approval for new assessments.", "C": "Practical nurses may assess stable clients, but dressings and reassessments need an RN.", "D": "Practical nurses practise independently within competence, consulting the RN when care is unpredictable."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes constant approval is required", "B": "Adds an approval step for each new assessment", "C": "Restricts dressing changes and reassessments to the RN"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Practical nurses practise independently within competence, consulting the RN when care is unpredictable.): Practical nurses are accountable for their own practice, work independently when the client is stable and the situation is predictable, and consult the RN or another provider as complexity or unpredictability rises. Routine sign off or RN attendance is not required for assessments and dressings. Direct supervision for each task misstates the role."}, {"id": 886, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Two nurses on a medical unit disagree openly in the report room about how to divide evening assignments. A new practical nurse overhears and wants the disagreement hidden from the manager so the team appears united. The charge nurse says tension can be useful. Which view of conflict best guides the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Conflict is normal, and addressing it respectfully can improve processes and team understanding.", "B": "Conflict harms care, so the team should rearrange assignments to reduce contact between the two nurses.", "C": "Conflict signals poor leadership, so the nurse should ask the manager to decide.", "D": "Conflict is best addressed before the whole team so everyone hears both views."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Separates staff to avoid the issue instead of resolving it", "C": "Hands a peer disagreement to a manager to judge", "D": "Airs a disagreement publicly instead of respectfully"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Conflict is normal, and addressing it respectfully can improve processes and team understanding.): Disagreement is a normal part of teamwork, and handling it respectfully and early often reveals problems in processes and builds understanding. Asking a manager to pick a winner skips the nurses' own communication. Airing it before the whole team can embarrass colleagues. Rearranging staff to avoid contact only postpones it."}, {"id": 887, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 33-year-old man with schizophrenia arrives in emergency with a temperature of 39.8 C, muscle rigidity, confusion and heavy sweating. His family says haloperidol was increased four days ago. His heart rate is 124. What should the practical nurse recognize about these findings?", "options": {"A": "Findings fit a viral infection, so oral fluids and acetaminophen can start while awaiting results.", "B": "Findings fit neuroleptic malignant syndrome, an emergency needing urgent report to the physician.", "C": "Findings fit an expected extrapyramidal reaction, so an anticholinergic can be given at the next dose.", "D": "Findings fit worsening psychosis, so a calm environment and a higher antipsychotic dose are suitable."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats fever and rigidity as a simple infection", "C": "Mislabels a life-threatening reaction as a routine movement side effect", "D": "Reads the confusion as psychosis and recommends more of the causative drug"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Findings fit neuroleptic malignant syndrome, an emergency needing urgent report to the physician.): Fever, rigidity, autonomic instability and altered mental state soon after a dose increase of an antipsychotic fit neuroleptic malignant syndrome, which can be fatal and needs urgent medical care. A viral illness would not explain the rigidity. Extrapyramidal effects do not cause high fever. More antipsychotic would worsen it."}, {"id": 888, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Noah's father is anxious and wants to take him home because the cough started with a cold and Noah usually recovers. The team has started oxygen and a reliever. He asks why this visit is different. Which explanation best reflects Noah's current findings and the plan?", "options": {"A": "Speaking only a few words and low oxygen show a severe attack needing monitored treatment.", "B": "Wheeze is expected with asthma and a cold, so brief observation before discharge is enough.", "C": "His breathing rate can be managed at home once he has received another dose of reliever.", "D": "Daily controller use shows good adherence, so oxygen is a precaution and discharge is likely."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats severe findings as usual wheeze needing brief observation", "C": "Assumes home care is safe before response to treatment is known", "D": "Reads daily controller use as proof of safety"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C105", "cluster_text": "Noah, 9, arrives in the emergency department with his father after two days of cough and wheeze. He is leaning forward, using accessory muscles, and can say only two or three words at a time. His oxygen saturation is 90 percent on room air and his respiratory rate is 36. He has asthma and uses a reliever inhaler and a daily controller.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Speaking only a few words and low oxygen show a severe attack needing monitored treatment.): Noah can speak only a few words, uses accessory muscles and has a saturation of 90 percent with a rate of 36, which signal a severe attack requiring monitored treatment and reassessment. Wheeze with these findings is not routine. Daily inhaler use does not show control. Discharge is decided only after he responds to treatment."}, {"id": 889, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Noah has improved. His father says Noah uses his blue reliever inhaler almost every day, including before gym, while the daily controller is often skipped because he feels fine. He wakes coughing twice weekly. How should the practical nurse interpret this pattern?", "options": {"A": "The controller is not needed when symptoms stay mild, so take it only during colds.", "B": "Heavy reliever use and skipped controller doses suggest poor control, so report it.", "C": "Night cough twice a week is an acceptable level of control for most children.", "D": "Frequent reliever use shows good symptom response, so the current routine can continue."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Misunderstands the controller as a symptom-triggered drug", "C": "Accepts night symptoms as normal", "D": "Reads heavy reliever use as success"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C105", "cluster_text": "Noah, 9, arrives in the emergency department with his father after two days of cough and wheeze. He is leaning forward, using accessory muscles, and can say only two or three words at a time. His oxygen saturation is 90 percent on room air and his respiratory rate is 36. He has asthma and uses a reliever inhaler and a daily controller.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Heavy reliever use and skipped controller doses suggest poor control, so report it.): Daily reliever use and regular night waking show inadequately controlled asthma, and the missing controller doses explain it, so the nurse reports the pattern and reinforces daily controller use. Reliever use is a sign of symptoms, not control. Night waking twice weekly is not good control. Controllers work by daily use, not during colds only."}, {"id": 890, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before discharge, the practical nurse watches Noah use his metered dose inhaler. He presses the canister while breathing out into the spacer, then inhales shallowly once, and says the medicine tastes weak. His coordination is poor when he is tired. What is the best correction?", "options": {"A": "Exhale fully into the spacer to mix the medicine, then breathe in quickly twice.", "B": "Remove the spacer for a better dose, and breathe in as the canister is pressed.", "C": "Press the canister several times into the spacer first, then take one deep breath.", "D": "Press once into the spacer, then take five or six slow breaths through it."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Exhales into the device and inhales too fast, wasting the dose", "B": "Drops the spacer, which worsens coordination problems", "C": "Delivers multiple puffs at once, which reduces lung deposition"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C105", "cluster_text": "Noah, 9, arrives in the emergency department with his father after two days of cough and wheeze. He is leaning forward, using accessory muscles, and can say only two or three words at a time. His oxygen saturation is 90 percent on room air and his respiratory rate is 36. He has asthma and uses a reliever inhaler and a daily controller.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Press once into the spacer, then take five or six slow breaths through it.): With poor coordination a spacer lets Noah breathe the medicine in over several slow breaths after a single actuation, so the dose is delivered even if the timing is off. Several puffs together reduce delivery. Removing the spacer requires coordination he lacks. Exhaling into the device pushes the medicine out and fast breaths increase deposition in the mouth."}, {"id": 891, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Later Noah's wheeze disappears but he looks more tired, breathes quietly, and his saturation is 88 percent. His father says the quiet is a good sign. The registered nurse is nearby. Which statement about the absence of wheeze is correct?", "options": {"A": "Wheeze often fades once the reliever works, so reassessing in thirty minutes is suitable.", "B": "A quiet chest with fatigue may mean little air is moving, so he needs urgent reassessment.", "C": "Absence of wheeze means airways are opening, so oxygen can be reduced.", "D": "Quiet breathing means he is falling asleep, so dim the lights and monitor saturation hourly."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes a delayed reassessment is safe", "C": "Equates quiet with improvement despite a low saturation", "D": "Mistakes fatigue and silence for sleep"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C105", "cluster_text": "Noah, 9, arrives in the emergency department with his father after two days of cough and wheeze. He is leaning forward, using accessory muscles, and can say only two or three words at a time. His oxygen saturation is 90 percent on room air and his respiratory rate is 36. He has asthma and uses a reliever inhaler and a daily controller.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A quiet chest with fatigue may mean little air is moving, so he needs urgent reassessment.): In a severe attack, a silent chest with tiredness and a falling saturation suggests very little air movement and impending respiratory failure, so he needs urgent reassessment and team help. Wheeze disappearing with a low saturation is not improvement. Waiting thirty minutes is unsafe. Sleep does not explain a saturation of 88 percent."}, {"id": 892, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In long-term care, Mr. Bruno, 80, with COPD becomes more breathless during an exacerbation while lying flat for a bed bath. His saturation is 89 percent and he is pale. His call bell is within reach and his cough is weak. Which position should the practical nurse help him into?", "options": {"A": "Semi-recumbent at thirty degrees with one pillow, keeping his neck straight.", "B": "Supine with the legs raised to improve circulation and reduce his pallor.", "C": "High Fowler's or leaning forward over a bedside table, supported with pillows.", "D": "Supine with one pillow so the diaphragm can rest and breathing slows."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Chooses a low semi-recumbent angle that limits chest expansion", "B": "Raises the legs for pallor and worsens breathlessness", "D": "Positions flat, which pushes the abdominal contents against the diaphragm"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (High Fowler's or leaning forward over a bedside table, supported with pillows.): Sitting upright or leaning forward lets the diaphragm drop and the chest expand, easing the work of breathing in a COPD exacerbation. A low semi-recumbent angle gives less relief. Lying flat compresses the diaphragm, even with the legs raised. Supine with one pillow also increases breathlessness."}, {"id": 893, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Two hours after hernia repair, Mr. Reyes, 52, has a blood pressure of 84/50 mmHg down from 118/72, a heart rate of 112, and says he feels lightheaded. His dressing is dry. The practical nurse has cared for him competently so far. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Document the readings and continue routine postoperative care because the dressing is dry.", "B": "Give a bolus of the ordered maintenance fluid at a faster rate and then reassess.", "C": "Stay with him, raise his legs, and urgently report to the RN or physician.", "D": "Recheck blood pressure in thirty minutes and report if it has not improved."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reassures herself by the dry dressing and ignores the hemodynamic change", "B": "Changes an infusion rate without an order", "D": "Waits to see if the change resolves"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stay with him, raise his legs, and urgently report to the RN or physician.): A rapid drop in blood pressure with tachycardia and lightheadedness after surgery suggests bleeding or another unpredictable complication, so the nurse stays with him, supports circulation and reports urgently. Waiting loses time. Altering fluid rate needs an order. A dry dressing does not exclude internal bleeding."}, {"id": 894, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Dubois, 83, in long-term care has urge incontinence several times each morning and has started drinking less so she will have fewer accidents. Her urine is dark and she has been confused since yesterday. Which plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Encourage regular fluids and scheduled toileting, and report the dark urine and new confusion.", "B": "Limit fluids after noon and start a timed schedule to lower the number of accidents.", "C": "Restrict caffeine only and review her fluid intake in a week without further assessment.", "D": "Apply an absorbent brief during the day to protect her dignity and the linen."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Follows her fluid restriction trap and risks dehydration", "C": "Delays follow-up of confusion and dark urine", "D": "Manages the leak but ignores the cause and the change in condition"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Encourage regular fluids and scheduled toileting, and report the dark urine and new confusion.): Reduced fluids concentrate urine and irritate the bladder, so encouraging fluids with scheduled toileting helps, and the new confusion with dark urine needs reporting for possible dehydration or infection. Fluid restriction worsens urgency and dehydration. A brief addresses leakage only. Waiting a week delays care."}, {"id": 895, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At a community clinic, a client with low health literacy says he did not understand the instructions for his new blood sugar medication. The practical nurse is tempted to use the correct medical terms so she sounds professional. Which approach best supports his understanding?", "options": {"A": "Give a printed handout with the medical terms defined at the bottom of each page.", "B": "Use everyday words, short sentences and examples, then ask him to explain the plan.", "C": "Ask a family member to explain the instructions simply and skip the teach-back.", "D": "Use simple words once, then assume he understands unless he asks questions."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relies on text for a client who struggles with reading", "C": "Relies on a relative and skips confirming understanding", "D": "Assumes understanding without checking it"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use everyday words, short sentences and examples, then ask him to explain the plan.): Plain language with short sentences and examples fits low health literacy, and asking him to explain the plan confirms what he understood. Written definitions assume reading skill he may not have. Relying on a relative skips checking his understanding. Waiting for questions misses clients who do not know what to ask."}, {"id": 896, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Silva, 55, on a medical unit has a temperature of 38.7 C, a cough with yellow sputum, and a white blood cell count of 16.8 x10^9/L with a left shift. His colleague says raised white cells with fever usually mean a viral infection. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "A raised count with fever means a viral process, so antibiotics are rarely needed.", "B": "A raised count reflects dehydration concentrating the blood, so oral fluids are the main step.", "C": "A rise with immature cells suggests bacterial infection, so report the result promptly.", "D": "A raised count is expected with fever and can be rechecked at the next routine bloodwork."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes high white cells mean viral infection", "B": "Attributes the result to hemoconcentration", "D": "Dismisses an abnormal result and delays reporting"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A rise with immature cells suggests bacterial infection, so report the result promptly.): A white cell count well above the usual range with a left shift of immature neutrophils and productive yellow sputum points to bacterial infection, so reporting promptly allows assessment and treatment. Viral infections often lower or only modestly raise counts. Dehydration does not produce a left shift. Waiting for routine bloodwork delays care."}, {"id": 897, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "During a cardiac arrest on a medical unit, the physician calls out, 'Give epinephrine one milligram IV now.' A practical nurse is assisting while a recorder notes times. The nurse plans to remember all verbal orders and document them from memory after the code ends. What is the best practice?", "options": {"A": "Give the dose after a second nurse checks the written protocol, which delays the drug briefly.", "B": "Read back the order, give it per policy, and have it recorded as soon as possible.", "C": "Have the recorder repeat the order, give the dose, and chart it once the code ends.", "D": "Give the dose immediately without repeating it since time is critical in arrest."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adds a protocol check that delays an emergency drug", "C": "Has the recorder, not the nurse, confirm the order and delays charting", "D": "Skips read-back, increasing error risk"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Read back the order, give it per policy, and have it recorded as soon as possible.): In an emergency, verbal orders are accepted when the nurse reads them back to confirm the drug, dose and route, gives them per agency policy and has them recorded promptly, then authenticated later by the prescriber. A protocol check delays the dose. A read-back by the recorder does not confirm the nurse's understanding. Skipping read-back risks a wrong dose."}, {"id": 898, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the postnatal unit, Anna, a first-time mother on day 2, has sore, cracked nipples and wants to shorten feeds to five minutes. The practical nurse observes the baby's lips tucked in, a clicking sound during sucking, and dimpled cheeks. What is the best interpretation and next step?", "options": {"A": "Latch looks shallow, so reattach the baby with a wide mouth before changing feed length.", "B": "Cracked nipples need rest, so switch to bottle feeds for twenty-four hours.", "C": "Pain is expected on day two, so limit feeds to five minutes and reassess tomorrow morning.", "D": "Clicking indicates good suction, so applying lanolin after feeds will be enough."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Replaces breastfeeding instead of correcting the cause", "C": "Accepts pain as normal and shortens feeds without checking latch", "D": "Misreads clicking as effective suction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Latch looks shallow, so reattach the baby with a wide mouth before changing feed length.): Tucked lips, clicking and dimpled cheeks suggest a shallow latch, which causes nipple damage, so correcting position and attachment treats the cause. Shortening feeds leaves the problem and may reduce milk transfer. Clicking signals lost suction. Bottle feeding for a day risks supply and does not fix the latch."}, {"id": 899, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Before discharge, a client with heart failure nods while the practical nurse reviews daily weights, fluid limits and when to call. The nurse asks whether he has any questions and he says no. His wife seems unsure. Which action best checks his understanding?", "options": {"A": "Review the instructions again slowly while watching for signs of understanding.", "B": "Ask whether the instructions were clear and accept his answer because he is the client.", "C": "Give a second printed copy of the instructions and ask both to read it tonight.", "D": "Ask him to explain in his own words when he will weigh himself and call."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Repeats the teaching and reads body language instead of testing", "B": "Accepts a yes or no reply as proof of understanding", "C": "Adds written material without verifying understanding"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask him to explain in his own words when he will weigh himself and call.): Teach-back has the learner restate the plan in their own words, which shows what was understood and what to reteach. More handouts and repetition do not test understanding. A nod or a no to questions does not confirm it, especially when his wife looks unsure."}, {"id": 900, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During a clinic visit, an Elder pauses for about thirty seconds after the practical nurse asks how she has been managing at home. The nurse feels uneasy and begins listing possible problems to fill the quiet. What is the best response to the silence?", "options": {"A": "Gently ask her to continue after ten seconds, as long silence may be uncomfortable.", "B": "Allow the pause, stay present with relaxed posture, and let her continue when ready.", "C": "Offer a few common concerns so the Elder can choose the one that fits.", "D": "Summarize what she said and ask a closed question to move her along."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Interrupts silence early on an assumption of discomfort", "C": "Fills the silence with leading suggestions", "D": "Steers with a closed question instead of allowing reflection"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Allow the pause, stay present with relaxed posture, and let her continue when ready.): Silence is often respectful reflection in many Indigenous communities, and giving time with calm attention shows respect and invites the Elder to share in her own way. Prompting after ten seconds cuts off thought. A closed question limits her answer. Offering a list can lead her response."}, {"id": 901, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "While Amy sets priorities, Mr. Park asks for help with supper and a call to his family. Mrs. Diaz's oxygen saturation reads 89 percent and she looks tired. Amy considers Maslow's hierarchy of needs. Which action best reflects correct prioritization?", "options": {"A": "Assess Mrs. Diaz's breathing and oxygen first, then help Mr. Park with supper.", "B": "Complete the IV antibiotic first because a late dose delays treatment of infection.", "C": "Help Mr. Park with supper first since nutrition is a basic physiological need.", "D": "Call Mr. Park's family first so his emotional needs are met before care."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Prioritizes a scheduled task over a deteriorating client", "C": "Uses Maslow alone and ignores the airway and breathing priority", "D": "Puts a psychosocial need before a physiological one"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C119", "cluster_text": "Amy, a practical nurse on a busy medical-surgical unit, is assigned five clients at the start of her evening shift. Mr. Park, 74, is two days post knee replacement with stable vitals and needs help with supper. Ms. Bell, 61, has a long-standing mild cough. Mr. Evans, 69, has an IV antibiotic due. Mrs. Diaz, 82, is on oxygen. Handover is complete, and Amy plans her first hour.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Assess Mrs. Diaz's breathing and oxygen first, then help Mr. Park with supper.): Airway and breathing outrank other physiological needs, and Mrs. Diaz shows a falling saturation with fatigue, so she is seen first. Supper is a basic need but stable. The antibiotic can be given soon after. The family call meets a lower priority need."}, {"id": 902, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Ms. Bell's cough is unchanged from her admission note. Meanwhile Mr. Evans, on antibiotics for a leg infection, now reports new shortness of breath and his respiratory rate is 26. Amy has time to see only one of them next. Which interpretation should guide her?", "options": {"A": "Both concerns involve breathing, so she should see whoever called first.", "B": "Mr. Evans is on an antibiotic, so breathlessness is likely a side effect to report later.", "C": "Mr. Evans has a new problem involving breathing, so he comes before the known, stable cough.", "D": "Mr. Evans's respiratory rate of 26 is mild, so he can wait for the cough assessment."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Chooses by order of calls rather than urgency", "B": "Attributes new breathlessness to a drug effect and delays assessment", "D": "Underrates a raised respiratory rate with new symptoms"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C119", "cluster_text": "Amy, a practical nurse on a busy medical-surgical unit, is assigned five clients at the start of her evening shift. Mr. Park, 74, is two days post knee replacement with stable vitals and needs help with supper. Ms. Bell, 61, has a long-standing mild cough. Mr. Evans, 69, has an IV antibiotic due. Mrs. Diaz, 82, is on oxygen. Handover is complete, and Amy plans her first hour.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Mr. Evans has a new problem involving breathing, so he comes before the known, stable cough.): A new onset of breathlessness with a raised respiratory rate is an acute change that may signal a serious complication, while an unchanged cough is stable and known. Call order does not set priority. New breathlessness should not be assumed to be a drug effect, and a rate of 26 with new symptoms should not wait."}, {"id": 903, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Amy completes assessments. Mr. Park's vitals are unchanged and he is eating well. Mrs. Diaz's pulse has risen from 82 to 118 and she is now restless, while Ms. Bell is resting. Seeing Mr. Park would take five minutes. Which client should Amy reassess next, and why?", "options": {"A": "Ms. Bell, because resting clients can be reassessed while they are quiet and calm.", "B": "Mrs. Diaz, because her restlessness and rising pulse suggest she may be deteriorating.", "C": "Mr. Park, because a quick check lets her finish one client and shorten her list.", "D": "Mrs. Diaz after Mr. Park, because quick checks first free up time for longer ones."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Picks the resting client rather than the unstable one", "C": "Chooses the quick, stable client first", "D": "Delays an unstable client to save time"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C119", "cluster_text": "Amy, a practical nurse on a busy medical-surgical unit, is assigned five clients at the start of her evening shift. Mr. Park, 74, is two days post knee replacement with stable vitals and needs help with supper. Ms. Bell, 61, has a long-standing mild cough. Mr. Evans, 69, has an IV antibiotic due. Mrs. Diaz, 82, is on oxygen. Handover is complete, and Amy plans her first hour.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Mrs. Diaz, because her restlessness and rising pulse suggest she may be deteriorating.): Rising pulse and new restlessness in an oxygen-dependent client signal possible hypoxia or another change, so the unstable client is reassessed before stable ones. A quick visit to Mr. Park delays her. Ms. Bell is resting and stable. Seeing the quick client first postpones urgent care."}, {"id": 904, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Amy is giving Mr. Park his evening analgesic when Mr. Evans calls out from his room reporting sudden chest pain with sweating. His pulse is 108 and he looks pale. Amy still has routine charting to finish. What should she do first?", "options": {"A": "Ask another unregulated care provider to check on him and report back after her task.", "B": "Leave the routine task safely, assess Mr. Evans now, and call the RN for help.", "C": "Ask Mr. Evans to wait while she completes the analgesic and then see him.", "D": "Give Mr. Evans his PRN analgesic from stock first, then assess and call the RN."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delegates assessment of possible cardiac pain to unregulated staff", "C": "Delays a possible cardiac event to finish a task", "D": "Gives a drug before assessing and without an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C119", "cluster_text": "Amy, a practical nurse on a busy medical-surgical unit, is assigned five clients at the start of her evening shift. Mr. Park, 74, is two days post knee replacement with stable vitals and needs help with supper. Ms. Bell, 61, has a long-standing mild cough. Mr. Evans, 69, has an IV antibiotic due. Mrs. Diaz, 82, is on oxygen. Handover is complete, and Amy plans her first hour.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Leave the routine task safely, assess Mr. Evans now, and call the RN for help.): New chest pain with sweating and tachycardia could be a heart attack, so it takes priority over routine charting or a finishing task, and Amy secures Mr. Park safely, assesses Mr. Evans and calls for help. Delegating the assessment to an unregulated provider is outside appropriate delegation. Giving a drug before assessment masks symptoms. Waiting loses time that matters."}, {"id": 905, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Jonas must choose the correct level of reprocessing for each reusable item at the clinic. The vaginal speculum touches intact mucous membranes but does not enter sterile tissue or the bloodstream. Which level of reprocessing does this semi-critical item need at minimum?", "options": {"A": "Low-level disinfection with a hospital-grade wipe after visible soil is removed.", "B": "Cleaning with detergent and water and air drying on a clean towel.", "C": "High-level disinfection, or sterilization when the item can tolerate it.", "D": "Intermediate-level disinfection using an alcohol wipe between clients."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applies low-level disinfection to a semi-critical item", "B": "Stops at cleaning, which removes soil but does not disinfect", "D": "Uses a wipe-level process insufficient for mucous membrane contact"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C117", "cluster_text": "A community clinic uses reusable instruments, including a vaginal speculum, stainless steel forceps for suture removal, a reusable blood pressure cuff and a flexible endoscope sheath. The practical nurse, Jonas, reprocesses them at day's end. The clinic has an autoclave, a high-level disinfectant station and low-level wipes. Several pouches are stacked on a shelf, some stored for weeks. Jonas follows the clinic's reprocessing policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (High-level disinfection, or sterilization when the item can tolerate it.): Semi-critical items contact mucous membranes or non-intact skin, so they require at least high-level disinfection, with sterilization preferred when the material allows it. Low and intermediate levels suit non-critical items such as a blood pressure cuff. Cleaning alone is the first step, not the end."}, {"id": 906, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Jonas runs the autoclave. The chemical indicator tape on one pouch has not changed colour, though the pouch looks dry and intact. The cycle printout shows normal time and temperature. A forceps pack is needed for the next client. What should he do with this pouch?", "options": {"A": "Use it because the printout shows the cycle reached the required time and temperature.", "B": "Use it after taping a fresh indicator strip on the outside.", "C": "Use it after checking that the pouch seal and expiry date are intact.", "D": "Treat it as not sterile, reprocess it, and report the indicator failure per policy."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Trusts the machine printout over the pouch indicator", "B": "Adds a new indicator after the cycle instead of reprocessing", "C": "Relies on seal and date checks instead of the failed indicator"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C117", "cluster_text": "A community clinic uses reusable instruments, including a vaginal speculum, stainless steel forceps for suture removal, a reusable blood pressure cuff and a flexible endoscope sheath. The practical nurse, Jonas, reprocesses them at day's end. The clinic has an autoclave, a high-level disinfectant station and low-level wipes. Several pouches are stacked on a shelf, some stored for weeks. Jonas follows the clinic's reprocessing policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat it as not sterile, reprocess it, and report the indicator failure per policy.): An unchanged chemical indicator means that pouch did not meet sterilization conditions, whatever the printout shows, so it is not used, is reprocessed and the failure is reported. Appearance and an intact seal do not prove sterility. A new strip added afterward says nothing about the cycle the pouch received."}, {"id": 907, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Jonas finds a sterile dressing pack with an intact outer wrap and a valid date, but a damp corner and a faint stain near the seal. He needs it immediately for a client's dressing change. What interpretation and action is best?", "options": {"A": "Use it but inspect the contents for dampness before touching the dressing.", "B": "The outer wrap is intact and dated, so it is acceptable if the stain is small.", "C": "Let the pack air dry for ten minutes and use it if the stain disappears.", "D": "Moisture can draw in microbes, so discard it and open a dry, intact package."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Inspects after the barrier has already been compromised", "B": "Accepts date and wrap alone and ignores moisture", "C": "Believes drying restores sterility"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C117", "cluster_text": "A community clinic uses reusable instruments, including a vaginal speculum, stainless steel forceps for suture removal, a reusable blood pressure cuff and a flexible endoscope sheath. The practical nurse, Jonas, reprocesses them at day's end. The clinic has an autoclave, a high-level disinfectant station and low-level wipes. Several pouches are stacked on a shelf, some stored for weeks. Jonas follows the clinic's reprocessing policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Moisture can draw in microbes, so discard it and open a dry, intact package.): Moisture wicks microorganisms through packaging, so a damp or stained pack is considered contaminated even if dated and wrapped, and it is discarded. A small stain still signals a breach. Air drying does not restore sterility. Opening the pack to check defeats its purpose."}, {"id": 908, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a home visit that week, Jonas cares for an older adult with an indwelling urinary catheter. The family asks him to collect a urine sample for the physician. The bag hangs below the bladder and the tubing is free of kinks. What is the best way to obtain the sample?", "options": {"A": "Ask the client to void into a collection hat after clamping the catheter.", "B": "Empty the drainage bag into a specimen container so the sample is fresh.", "C": "Disconnect the catheter from the drainage tube and collect urine in a sterile cup.", "D": "Clean the sampling port with antiseptic and withdraw urine with a sterile syringe."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Misapplies voiding collection to a catheterized client", "B": "Uses stale urine from the bag, giving an unreliable sample", "C": "Breaks the closed system, raising infection risk"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C117", "cluster_text": "A community clinic uses reusable instruments, including a vaginal speculum, stainless steel forceps for suture removal, a reusable blood pressure cuff and a flexible endoscope sheath. The practical nurse, Jonas, reprocesses them at day's end. The clinic has an autoclave, a high-level disinfectant station and low-level wipes. Several pouches are stacked on a shelf, some stored for weeks. Jonas follows the clinic's reprocessing policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Clean the sampling port with antiseptic and withdraw urine with a sterile syringe.): A sample from the port keeps the system closed and gives fresh urine, so cleaning the port and using a sterile syringe is best. Disconnecting opens a path for bacteria. Bag urine has sat and may misrepresent the bladder. A catheterized client cannot void around the catheter."}, {"id": 909, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Schultz, 38, developed lower back pain after lifting boxes yesterday. He has no leg weakness, numbness or bladder changes and a normal temperature. He asks the practical nurse whether he should stay in bed for a week. What teaching is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Stay gently active, avoid long bed rest, and resume normal tasks as pain allows.", "B": "Limit activity to short walks for one week and avoid sitting to protect muscles.", "C": "Rest flat in bed for several days, since movement delays healing of the strained back muscles.", "D": "Stay active but avoid lifting and twisting until a physician clears him."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Restricts activity for a week and risks deconditioning", "C": "Prescribes bed rest, which prolongs recovery", "D": "Delays return to activity until a physician clears it"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stay gently active, avoid long bed rest, and resume normal tasks as pain allows.): For acute non-specific back pain without red flags, staying gently active speeds recovery and prolonged rest causes stiffness and weakness. Bed rest and long restriction delay healing. Waiting for formal clearance is unnecessary. Teach red flags such as leg weakness or bladder changes to report."}, {"id": 910, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lee, 78, in acute care has an IV infusion of normal saline running behind schedule with 600 mL remaining after twelve hours. Her IV site is cool, puffy and tender, and the flow has slowed. A colleague suggests increasing the rate to catch up. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Stop the infusion, remove the cannula, and report the suspected infiltration.", "B": "Flush the line with extra saline to restore flow and resume the original rate.", "C": "Increase the rate within the ordered range to catch up on the volume.", "D": "Apply a warm compress and continue the infusion while watching the site hourly."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Forces fluid into a failing site", "C": "Speeds up an infusion into the tissue", "D": "Continues infusing despite signs of infiltration"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop the infusion, remove the cannula, and report the suspected infiltration.): A cool, puffy, tender site with slowed flow indicates infiltration, so the infusion is stopped, the cannula is removed per policy and the finding is reported. Speeding up pushes more fluid into the tissue. Continuing the infusion worsens damage. Flushing forces fluid into the same failing site."}, {"id": 911, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A client at a clinic looks downward and avoids eye contact while the practical nurse explains his test results. A colleague says avoiding eye contact suggests he is hiding something. The nurse considers cultural safety. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Change to a different topic until he seems more comfortable with her.", "B": "Explain that eye contact is expected and encourage him to practise.", "C": "Recognize that norms vary, and check understanding while respecting his comfort.", "D": "Continue explaining, then ask a family member to confirm his understanding."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Avoids the topic instead of checking understanding", "B": "Imposes the nurse's norm of eye contact on the client", "D": "Uses a relative to check understanding without his consent"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recognize that norms vary, and check understanding while respecting his comfort.): Many cultures regard direct eye contact as disrespectful, so avoiding it is not evidence of dishonesty, and culturally safe care respects his comfort while confirming understanding by other means. Teaching him to make eye contact imposes the nurse's norm. Asking a relative to confirm understanding shares his information without permission. Changing topics leaves the results unexplained."}, {"id": 912, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Two nurses on a hospital unit disagree about a new patient-transfer routine. Both want it done their way, and the matter affects client safety. A third nurse suggests splitting the difference so both feel heard. The practical nurse is asked to help choose a conflict approach. Which approach best fits?", "options": {"A": "Collaborate by exploring both views together and agreeing on the safest routine.", "B": "Accommodate by letting the nurse with more seniority set the routine for the unit.", "C": "Compromise so each nurse gives up a part and both feel fairly treated.", "D": "Avoid the topic until the next staff meeting, when it can be raised again."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Defers to seniority instead of problem solving", "C": "Splits the difference even though safety needs the best solution", "D": "Postpones a safety issue"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Collaborate by exploring both views together and agreeing on the safest routine.): When safety is at stake and both views matter, collaboration finds the best solution rather than a half measure, so the nurses explore the options together and choose the safest routine. Compromise may leave an unsafe middle. Deferring to seniority overlooks evidence. Avoiding leaves the risk in place."}, {"id": 913, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Hanson, 72, with chronic carbon dioxide retention, is admitted with a COPD exacerbation. His oxygen saturation is 84 percent on room air. The practical nurse notes the order to titrate oxygen to a target. A colleague suggests raising flow until he reaches 100 percent. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Increase flow until saturation reaches 100 percent to correct his tissue oxygen.", "B": "Titrate oxygen to the ordered lower range, usually 88 to 92 percent, and monitor.", "C": "Start high-flow oxygen by mask for a few minutes, then reduce it when he is calm.", "D": "Withhold oxygen because it can suppress his drive to breathe and risk arrest."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Aims for full saturation, risking carbon dioxide narcosis", "C": "Gives high flow without titration", "D": "Withholds needed oxygen from a hypoxic client"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Titrate oxygen to the ordered lower range, usually 88 to 92 percent, and monitor.): Clients who retain carbon dioxide are given controlled oxygen titrated to a lower target such as 88 to 92 percent, with close monitoring, because excess oxygen can worsen retention. Aiming for 100 percent or high flow risks that harm. Withholding oxygen leaves hypoxia untreated."}, {"id": 914, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sasha says she waits until the baby cries before offering the breast because she was told that crying means hunger and feeding earlier will spoil a schedule. She also reports the baby often seems sleepy and roots at times. How should the practical nurse interpret this?", "options": {"A": "Crying signals normal hunger, so waiting for it helps establish an effective feeding schedule.", "B": "Sleepiness suggests he is feeding well, so she can wait for crying before offering.", "C": "Rooting is a reflex without meaning, so she should feed on a fixed schedule.", "D": "Crying is a late hunger sign, so teach her to respond to rooting first."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats crying as the first hunger cue", "B": "Reads sleepiness as good intake and keeps waiting for crying", "C": "Dismisses rooting as a meaningless reflex"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C114", "cluster_text": "Sasha, 27, is a first-time mother whose baby boy was born five days ago. A practical nurse visits her at home. The baby weighs 3.3 kg, 4 percent below birth weight. Sasha says he cries a lot, feeds every two to three hours, and she is unsure whether she has enough milk. Wet diapers have been fewer than expected. She has no family nearby.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Crying is a late hunger sign, so teach her to respond to rooting first.): Rooting, hand to mouth and stirring are early hunger cues, while crying comes late and makes latching harder, so responding early helps feeding and weight gain. Sleepiness with low weight and few wet diapers can signal low intake, not good feeding. Rooting is a hunger cue, not a meaningless reflex. Crying alone does not prove low supply."}, {"id": 915, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sasha reports only three wet diapers in the past day and plans to schedule feeds every four hours to avoid overfeeding. The baby is 4 percent below birth weight and has yellow, seedy stools. Which action should the practical nurse take?", "options": {"A": "Reassure her that three wet diapers on day five are normal for a breastfed baby.", "B": "Support her four-hour schedule because it allows breasts to refill and prevents overfeeding.", "C": "Advise feeding at least eight times in twenty-four hours and report the low output.", "D": "Suggest offering water between feeds to raise the number of wet diapers."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reassures despite fewer wet diapers than expected for age", "B": "Endorses a feeding schedule that is too sparse", "D": "Adds water, which displaces milk and is not recommended"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C114", "cluster_text": "Sasha, 27, is a first-time mother whose baby boy was born five days ago. A practical nurse visits her at home. The baby weighs 3.3 kg, 4 percent below birth weight. Sasha says he cries a lot, feeds every two to three hours, and she is unsure whether she has enough milk. Wet diapers have been fewer than expected. She has no family nearby.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise feeding at least eight times in twenty-four hours and report the low output.): By day five a baby should have about six or more wet diapers, and three suggests low intake, so more frequent feeds are needed and the output is reported. A four-hour schedule reduces milk transfer and supply. Water fills the stomach without nutrition. Reassuring her ignores a warning sign."}, {"id": 916, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a clinic follow-up on day nine, Sasha has a red wedge-shaped area on her right breast, a temperature of 38.6 C and aching muscles. She wants to stop nursing on that side because it hurts. The baby is feeding well. What interpretation and action are best?", "options": {"A": "Findings suggest a milk allergy in the baby, so switch to formula until the fever settles.", "B": "Stop feeding from that breast for forty-eight hours to rest the tissue and lower fever.", "C": "Findings suggest mastitis, so keep emptying the breast often and report for treatment.", "D": "Findings fit normal engorgement, so a tight binder and fewer feeds will resolve them."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Blames the baby and stops breastfeeding", "B": "Stops emptying the affected breast, which worsens milk stasis", "D": "Mislabels infection as engorgement and restricts feeds"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C114", "cluster_text": "Sasha, 27, is a first-time mother whose baby boy was born five days ago. A practical nurse visits her at home. The baby weighs 3.3 kg, 4 percent below birth weight. Sasha says he cries a lot, feeds every two to three hours, and she is unsure whether she has enough milk. Wet diapers have been fewer than expected. She has no family nearby.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Findings suggest mastitis, so keep emptying the breast often and report for treatment.): A red wedge-shaped area, fever and flu-like aching suggest mastitis, which improves when the breast is emptied often, and the findings are reported for assessment and treatment. Stopping on that side increases stasis. A binder and fewer feeds worsen it. Milk allergy does not cause maternal fever."}, {"id": 917, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sasha recalls her first days, when her breasts were full, firm and tender on day 3 and she bound them tightly. She asks what would have helped with engorgement, in case it recurs with her next baby. What should the practical nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Pump all milk out after every feed so the breasts remain empty between feeds.", "B": "Bind the breasts firmly and avoid feeding on the full side to reduce milk production.", "C": "Feed often, soften the areola by hand expression first, and use cool compresses after feeds.", "D": "Apply heat for thirty minutes before each feed and limit feeds to three hours apart."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Overpumps, which increases supply and keeps engorgement going", "B": "Binds the breasts and withholds feeds, which worsens engorgement", "D": "Spaces feeds and applies prolonged heat that increases swelling"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C114", "cluster_text": "Sasha, 27, is a first-time mother whose baby boy was born five days ago. A practical nurse visits her at home. The baby weighs 3.3 kg, 4 percent below birth weight. Sasha says he cries a lot, feeds every two to three hours, and she is unsure whether she has enough milk. Wet diapers have been fewer than expected. She has no family nearby.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Feed often, soften the areola by hand expression first, and use cool compresses after feeds.): Frequent feeding removes milk, softening the areola by hand expression helps the baby latch, and cool compresses after feeds relieve swelling. Tight binding and skipping feeds worsen engorgement and risk mastitis. Spacing feeds lets the breasts overfill. Emptying with a pump after every feed boosts supply and prolongs fullness."}, {"id": 918, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 58-year-old man in the emergency department has a new order for an IV push drug for severe pain. The drug reference states to dilute the dose and inject it slowly over several minutes. He is groaning and asking the nurse to hurry, and a coworker says she usually pushes it in seconds. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Dilute the dose per the reference, inject slowly, and watch his breathing and blood pressure throughout.", "B": "Dilute the dose per the reference, then inject it over thirty seconds to relieve his pain.", "C": "Hold the drug until the physician restates the rate, leaving him in pain meanwhile.", "D": "Ask the coworker to push the dose over a minute, since she has more experience."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Dilutes correctly but injects faster than the labelled rate", "C": "Delaying analgesia to clarify a rate that is already printed in the reference", "D": "Defers the rate to a coworker's habit"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Dilute the dose per the reference, inject slowly, and watch his breathing and blood pressure throughout.): Slow injection of the diluted dose follows the drug reference and lets the nurse catch breathing or pressure changes early. Shortening the injection time or following a coworker's habit exposes him to respiratory depression and hypotension. Waiting for the physician to restate a rate already printed in the reference leaves him in pain without need."}, {"id": 919, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Two nurses on a medical unit disagree about who completes a late bed-bath round at shift handover, and their voices are rising near the nurses' station. The practical nurse is one of them. The charge nurse is nearby but busy. Which approach best reflects collaborative conflict resolution?", "options": {"A": "State the facts of the assignment firmly until the other nurse accepts the division of tasks.", "B": "Move somewhere private, listen to the other nurse's view, and agree on a shared plan", "C": "Complete the round alone this once and mention the unfairness to colleagues later in the shift.", "D": "Ask the charge nurse to decide who is right so handover can continue"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating the dispute as a contest one party must win", "C": "Avoiding the conflict and venting to others instead of resolving it", "D": "Handing the problem to authority before trying a direct conversation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Move somewhere private, listen to the other nurse's view, and agree on a shared plan): A private, respectful conversation lets both nurses be heard and builds a shared plan that serves the clients. Passing the dispute to the charge nurse skips the chance to solve it directly and leaves the relationship unresolved. Doing the work silently and complaining later avoids the issue and breeds resentment. Pressing facts until the other gives in makes one person win, which is not collaboration."}, {"id": 920, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "An 84-year-old woman on a medical unit has a valid do-not-resuscitate order that matches her written directive, reviewed with her last week. She suddenly has no pulse and is not breathing. Her daughter, crying, begs the nurse to start chest compressions. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Call a code so the team can decide whether the daughter's request overrides the order.", "B": "Withhold CPR, stay with the daughter, offer support, and notify the RN and physician.", "C": "Ask the daughter to sign a form revoking the order so that compressions can begin.", "D": "Start compressions to calm the daughter, then ask the physician to review the order afterward."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Passing the decision to a code team although the order is clear", "C": "Using the family to revoke an order that belongs to the client", "D": "Letting family distress override a valid client directive"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Withhold CPR, stay with the daughter, offer support, and notify the RN and physician.): A valid order that reflects the client's own wishes guides care even when family members are distressed. Starting compressions to comfort the daughter would override the client's directive. Calling a code or having the daughter revoke the order treats the family's wish as more binding than the client's reviewed decision. Support for the daughter and prompt reporting honour both the client and her family."}, {"id": 921, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Chu, 78, lives in long-term care and uses a walker. He has fallen twice this month and asks for his morning shower. He insists he can manage alone and says staff hover over him. A shower chair and grab bars are available in the room. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Stand him in the shower with the walker beside him and help only on request", "B": "Postpone the shower until family can attend, since his falls make bathing unsafe", "C": "Leave him alone in the shower with the call bell in reach, respecting his privacy", "D": "Seat him on the shower chair, check the water temperature, stay close, and let him wash"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaving a high-risk client standing unsupported on a wet floor", "B": "Withholding hygiene care instead of adapting the method", "C": "Honouring independence at the cost of fall safety"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Seat him on the shower chair, check the water temperature, stay close, and let him wash): A chair, a checked water temperature, and a nurse within reach protect a client who has fallen twice while still supporting his independence. Leaving him alone depends on a call bell he may not be able to reach after a fall. Cancelling hygiene care for a family visit denies a basic need when a safe method exists. Standing unsupported on a wet surface adds fall risk."}, {"id": 922, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 69-year-old woman on a medical unit has dry, flaking skin on her back and legs. She is due for a back rub before sleep. The stock room has an alcohol-based gel, a scented lotion, and a fragrance-free emollient. Which product choice and technique are best?", "options": {"A": "Apply the scented lotion with firm kneading over her bony areas to improve skin circulation.", "B": "Warm a fragrance-free emollient in your hands and apply it with long, smooth strokes.", "C": "Give a dry back rub without product, since added lotions can clog older skin pores.", "D": "Use the alcohol-based gel because it dries quickly and leaves the skin feeling clean."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing a scented product and rubbing bony areas, which can injure fragile skin", "C": "Skipping moisturizer based on a false claim about clogged pores", "D": "Using an alcohol product that dries already dry skin further"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Warm a fragrance-free emollient in your hands and apply it with long, smooth strokes.): A fragrance-free emollient restores moisture without irritating fragile skin, and long smooth strokes are gentle and relaxing. Alcohol-based products draw moisture out of skin that is already dry. Firm kneading over bony areas can damage thin skin and scented products may irritate it. A dry rub adds friction without any moisture benefit."}, {"id": 923, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "While starting a second IV drug through the same peripheral line, a nurse sees cloudy streaks forming in the tubing near the connection. The client, a 66-year-old woman, has no symptoms yet and her vital signs are stable. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Change the infusion bag only and continue giving the drug through the same tubing.", "B": "Stop the infusion, clamp the line without flushing it, and report to the RN and pharmacist.", "C": "Flush the line quickly with saline to clear the cloudiness and then restart the infusion.", "D": "Slow the second drug to a keep-open rate and watch the cloudiness for several minutes."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing the wrong item while the contaminated tubing stays in place", "C": "Flushing precipitate toward the client, risking particles entering the bloodstream", "D": "Continuing an incompatible mixture at a slower rate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop the infusion, clamp the line without flushing it, and report to the RN and pharmacist.): Cloudiness suggests the two drugs are incompatible and have formed particles, so the infusion must stop and the line must not be flushed toward the client. Flushing or slowing the rate still sends precipitate into her circulation. Changing only the bag leaves the contaminated tubing connected. Reporting to the RN and pharmacist brings in the people who can confirm compatibility and replace the line."}, {"id": 924, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 30-year-old man falls from a roof at a worksite and lies on the ground complaining of neck pain and tingling in his hands. He is awake and breathing normally. A coworker wants to help him sit up. As the nurse arriving first, what is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Check his hand grip and ask him to turn his head gently side to side.", "B": "Roll him onto his side with his head turned to protect his airway until help arrives.", "C": "Lift him onto a coworker's vehicle seat to reach the hospital faster for assessment.", "D": "Hold his head and neck neutral, tell him not to move, and call emergency services."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Tests neck movement in a possible spinal injury", "B": "Turning the head and body without need while he is breathing well", "C": "Moves a possible spinal injury into a private vehicle"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold his head and neck neutral, tell him not to move, and call emergency services.): Neck pain and tingling after a fall from height suggest a spinal cord injury, so the head and neck are held in a neutral line and he stays still until help arrives. Asking him to turn his head tests the injury. Moving him into a vehicle can shift an unstable vertebra. His airway is clear, so nothing justifies turning him."}, {"id": 925, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Her wheeze has stopped, her hives are fading, and her blood pressure is back to her baseline twenty minutes after epinephrine. She asks to go home because her ride is waiting. The paramedics are delayed. How should the nurse interpret her improvement and plan her care?", "options": {"A": "Treat the improvement as possibly temporary, observe her, and monitor vital signs until transfer.", "B": "Accept that the reaction is over because the symptoms resolved, and discharge her with allergy teaching.", "C": "Allow her to leave once the nurse practitioner writes a prescription for an epinephrine auto-injector.", "D": "Reduce checks to hourly since her breathing and skin findings have returned to normal."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating symptom relief as proof the reaction has ended", "C": "Equating a prescription with safety although symptoms can return", "D": "Spacing out checks during the window when a second reaction can occur"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C112", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Tanya Boateng, 58, has type 2 diabetes and attends a community clinic where a practical nurse works with a nurse practitioner. Ten minutes after an injection of an antibiotic for a skin infection, she developed hives, wheeze, and throat tightness. Epinephrine was given by intramuscular injection and emergency services were called. Her breathing is now easing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Treat the improvement as possibly temporary, observe her, and monitor vital signs until transfer.): Epinephrine wears off, and a second wave of symptoms can appear hours later, so she needs observation and frequent vital signs until she is transferred. Discharging her because she looks better ignores that risk. A prescription does not replace monitoring, and hourly checks are too far apart in this period. Teaching and an auto-injector come later, after evaluation."}, {"id": 926, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "While waiting for the paramedics, Mrs. Boateng says she feels faint, and her skin is pale and clammy. Her blood pressure has dropped to 82/48 mmHg. Her breathing is easy, and she says her cough feels better when she sits up. What is the best positioning action?", "options": {"A": "Lay her flat with legs raised unless breathing worsens, and report the low pressure", "B": "Keep her sitting upright, since this eases breathing and limits swelling in her airway.", "C": "Help her into a side-lying position with the head of the stretcher raised slightly.", "D": "Place her in high Fowler position with her legs down to reduce pressure on her chest."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Choosing comfort for the cough over circulation in hypotension", "C": "Picking a position meant for vomiting or pregnancy instead of shock", "D": "Using a position that pools blood in the legs while pressure is low"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C112", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Tanya Boateng, 58, has type 2 diabetes and attends a community clinic where a practical nurse works with a nurse practitioner. Ten minutes after an injection of an antibiotic for a skin infection, she developed hives, wheeze, and throat tightness. Epinephrine was given by intramuscular injection and emergency services were called. Her breathing is now easing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lay her flat with legs raised unless breathing worsens, and report the low pressure): Hypotension with faintness and clammy skin calls for lying flat with the legs raised to return blood to the heart, and sitting her up can drop pressure further. Breathing is easy, so there is no reason to prioritize an upright position. High Fowler with legs down pools blood below the heart. A raised head side-lying position does not address the pressure drop."}, {"id": 927, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Thirty minutes later, still at the clinic, Mrs. Boateng says she has pressure in her chest and her pulse is 112. She does not think it is serious and asks for an antacid because she skipped lunch. What is the most appropriate nursing response?", "options": {"A": "Treat it as a possible cardiac emergency, stay with her, and tell the nurse practitioner now.", "B": "Give the antacid and recheck her in thirty minutes to see whether the pressure eases.", "C": "Encourage slow deep breathing and reassure her that anxiety after a reaction commonly tightens the chest.", "D": "Check her blood glucose first and delay reporting until the result shows whether hunger caused it."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Waiting for an antacid effect while a cardiac cause is possible", "C": "Attributing chest pressure to anxiety without ruling out the heart", "D": "Delaying the report to finish a lower-priority check"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C112", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Tanya Boateng, 58, has type 2 diabetes and attends a community clinic where a practical nurse works with a nurse practitioner. Ten minutes after an injection of an antibiotic for a skin infection, she developed hives, wheeze, and throat tightness. Epinephrine was given by intramuscular injection and emergency services were called. Her breathing is now easing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Treat it as a possible cardiac emergency, stay with her, and tell the nurse practitioner now.): Chest pressure with a fast pulse is treated as cardiac until proven otherwise, especially after a reaction that stressed her body, so she is reported at once and kept under watch. Waiting on an antacid delays care for a possible heart event. A glucose check can be done while the report is made, not before it. Reassuring her about anxiety assumes a harmless cause."}, {"id": 928, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "While completing the transfer form, the nurse reads her visit notes from two days earlier. They record unusual tiredness and nausea but no chest pain, and she was told it was probably a stomach bug. Why should these earlier symptoms now raise concern?", "options": {"A": "Tiredness and nausea are common after antibiotic treatment and suggest a delayed drug reaction only.", "B": "These symptoms point to low blood glucose, which can also produce chest pressure in diabetes.", "C": "Symptoms without chest pain rule out heart problems, so the earlier notes carry little weight now.", "D": "Women and people with diabetes often have atypical heart symptoms such as fatigue and nausea."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Blaming the drug when the pattern fits a cardiac presentation", "B": "Assuming hypoglycemia explains both findings without data", "C": "Believing that absence of chest pain excludes a heart problem"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C112", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Tanya Boateng, 58, has type 2 diabetes and attends a community clinic where a practical nurse works with a nurse practitioner. Ten minutes after an injection of an antibiotic for a skin infection, she developed hives, wheeze, and throat tightness. Epinephrine was given by intramuscular injection and emergency services were called. Her breathing is now easing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Women and people with diabetes often have atypical heart symptoms such as fatigue and nausea.): Women and clients with diabetes frequently present with fatigue, nausea, or shortness of breath rather than classic chest pain, so the earlier notes may have been early warning signs. Linking them only to the antibiotic or to low glucose overlooks a cardiac explanation. Believing that no chest pain excludes a heart event is a common reason these presentations are missed. The nurse reports the earlier symptoms with the current ones."}, {"id": 929, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 48-year-old woman on an oncology unit is receiving chemotherapy. Her morning laboratory report shows a platelet count of 18 x 10^9/L, down from 140 before treatment. A physician order is due for an intramuscular antiemetic injection. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Give the injection with a small needle and hold firm pressure for five minutes afterward.", "B": "Give the injection in the thigh instead and apply pressure for ten minutes afterward.", "C": "Give the injection after asking the RN to check that no bruising is visible.", "D": "Hold the intramuscular injection, report the platelet count, and ask for a different route."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Underestimating bleeding risk from muscle injections with a very low platelet count", "B": "Changes the site but still injects into muscle", "C": "Uses visible bruising as the safety check"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the intramuscular injection, report the platelet count, and ask for a different route.): A platelet count of 18 x 10^9/L carries a high risk of bleeding into muscle, so intramuscular injections are avoided and a different route is requested. Changing the site or applying pressure reduces but does not remove the risk. Skin bruising does not show deep bleeding. Waiting leaves the same count and the same risk."}, {"id": 930, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "The health and safety coordinator at a 45-year-old man's workplace phones the clinic nurse and asks for his full record to plan his return to work. He has not given permission. A colleague says anyone helping with his return to work counts as part of his care team. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Release only the summary page, because an employer planning his return belongs to the care team.", "B": "Release nothing without his consent, explain the limits, and refer the request to the privacy officer.", "C": "Fax the full record if the coordinator signs a promise to keep it confidential.", "D": "Describe his condition verbally but withhold the paper record, since spoken details leave no trace."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating an employer as part of the circle of care without consent", "C": "Substituting a confidentiality promise for the client's consent", "D": "Believing verbal disclosure is lower risk than written disclosure"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Release nothing without his consent, explain the limits, and refer the request to the privacy officer.): Privacy principles allow collecting, using, and disclosing only what is needed and authorized, and an employer is outside the circle of care without the client's consent. A partial summary and a promise from the coordinator still disclose without permission. Telling the details aloud is still a disclosure. Referring the request to the privacy officer keeps the nurse within her professional duty."}, {"id": 931, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 66-year-old man with COPD tells the clinic nurse he has smoked for fifty years and sees no point in quitting now because the damage is already done. He coughs through the visit and says he gets breathless climbing one flight of stairs. Which response best reflects current understanding?", "options": {"A": "Agree that most lung damage is permanent, and focus teaching on his inhaler technique instead.", "B": "Explain that quitting at any stage slows lung function decline, and offer counselling and supports.", "C": "Suggest cutting down to ten cigarettes daily, since stopping abruptly at his age strains the heart.", "D": "Tell him quitting matters mainly for people who have smoked for fewer than twenty years."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepting the belief that it is too late to benefit", "C": "Recommending a reduced amount with a false safety claim about stopping", "D": "Applying a false cutoff that excludes long-term smokers"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain that quitting at any stage slows lung function decline, and offer counselling and supports.): Stopping smoking slows the yearly loss of lung function in COPD whatever the age or number of years smoked, so it remains the most effective treatment. Agreeing that the damage is done reinforces his hopelessness. Cutting down gives smaller benefit and the heart-strain claim is not true. No cutoff in years removes the benefit of quitting."}, {"id": 932, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 75-year-old woman with COPD, who lives alone, tells the home care nurse that her sputum has turned yellow-green and her breathlessness has worsened over two days. Her inhaler use has doubled. She says her cough usually settles within a week. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Advise her to increase her inhaler doses further and call back if nothing changes tomorrow.", "B": "Take vital signs and oxygen saturation, recognize a possible exacerbation, and report promptly for assessment.", "C": "Encourage fluids and rest for several days, and recheck her when the cough has settled.", "D": "Teach pursed-lip breathing and arrange a routine follow-up visit within the week."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Advising extra medication without a prescriber, outside scope", "C": "Waiting for the cough to settle on its own during a possible exacerbation", "D": "Offering a coping skill and routine follow-up instead of urgent reporting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Take vital signs and oxygen saturation, recognize a possible exacerbation, and report promptly for assessment.): More sputum, a change in its colour, worse breathlessness, and extra rescue inhaler use are signs of a COPD exacerbation that needs timely treatment. Waiting several days risks respiratory failure in a woman who lives alone. Raising inhaler doses without an order is outside nursing scope. Pursed-lip breathing helps, but it does not replace reporting and assessment."}, {"id": 933, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A practical nurse on a hospital unit is upset that a peer repeatedly leaves charting for the next shift. She has talked with two friends on the unit but not with the peer. Another coworker suggests telling the manager. Which step should the nurse take first?", "options": {"A": "Ask the colleagues she trusts for advice and support before approaching the peer directly.", "B": "Raise the repeated pattern with the unit manager first so a neutral person can address it", "C": "Speak privately with the peer, describe the effect on handover, and ask how to solve it", "D": "Leave a written note on the peer's locker listing the charting missed on each shift."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Discussing the problem with others instead of the person", "B": "Escalating before trying a direct conversation with the person involved", "D": "Using an impersonal note that invites defensiveness"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Speak privately with the peer, describe the effect on handover, and ask how to solve it): Addressing the concern directly with the person involved, privately and with specifics, gives the peer a fair chance to change and keeps trust intact. Going to the manager first skips that step, though the manager remains an option if the talk fails. Collecting advice from friends turns into gossip. A note on a locker feels like an accusation and gives no room to talk."}, {"id": 934, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A home care nurse prepares to hold the hand of a 70-year-old man who is tearful about a new diagnosis. The client sits with his arms crossed and leans back slightly when the nurse steps close. They have met twice. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Offer a brief handhold after explaining it, since comfort matters when distressed.", "B": "Move closer and offer a tissue, then wait for him to uncross his arms.", "C": "Step back, ask permission before any touch, and respect his preferred distance and comfort.", "D": "Keep the usual distance and use a formal tone, avoiding any personal warmth during this visit."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Explains the touch but does not ask permission", "B": "Moves closer despite withdrawing body language", "D": "Withdrawing warmth entirely instead of asking about preferences"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Step back, ask permission before any touch, and respect his preferred distance and comfort.): Preferences for personal space and touch vary by culture, experience, and individual, so asking permission and following his cues shows respect. Explaining touch is not the same as asking permission. Moving closer ignores the signals he is giving. Becoming cold and formal withdraws support when he is distressed."}, {"id": 935, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 2-year-old girl is brought in by her mother for fever and fussiness. While she sits quietly on her mother's lap, the nurse measures a heart rate of 130 and a respiratory rate of 28. The mother's own pulse is 76 and she worries. How should the nurse proceed?", "options": {"A": "Recheck both rates in thirty minutes, since a worried mother raises readings.", "B": "Report tachycardia, since the heart rate exceeds the adult limit of 100 beats per minute.", "C": "Compare with infant ranges, report mild tachycardia, and recheck after she settles.", "D": "Compare with toddler ranges, accept them for age, and continue assessing temperature."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays interpretation by blaming the mother's worry", "B": "Judging a toddler by adult ranges", "C": "Uses infant ranges for a toddler and reports a false tachycardia"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C113", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse is working an evening shift in a small rural emergency department. Within one hour a mother arrives with a toddler, a woman delivers a baby on arrival, a grandfather with COPD comes in with his daughter, and a man with abdominal pain is brought in by ambulance. The nurse must assess each client using appropriate methods.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Compare with toddler ranges, accept them for age, and continue assessing temperature.): Toddler normal ranges are higher than adult values, and a heart rate of 130 with a respiratory rate of 28 at rest is within them. Applying infant or adult limits would create a false alarm. Waiting to recheck delays assessment without need. Age-specific ranges apply from infancy."}, {"id": 936, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The woman who gave birth on arrival hands the nurse her newborn, who is pink, crying, and wrapped in a warm blanket. The nurse prepares to measure respirations during the first hour of life, and the baby settles quietly. Which method is correct for counting this newborn's respirations?", "options": {"A": "Count chest rise for thirty seconds while the infant is crying, and double the number.", "B": "Listen at the baby's mouth with a stethoscope for fifteen seconds and multiply by four.", "C": "Watch abdominal movement for a full minute while the baby is quiet, as newborns breathe diaphragmatically.", "D": "Count only while the baby cries, since crying makes breathing movements easier to see."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Counting during crying, which changes the rate and pattern", "B": "Counting by listening at the mouth and extrapolating a short count", "D": "Believing crying gives a more reliable count"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C113", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse is working an evening shift in a small rural emergency department. Within one hour a mother arrives with a toddler, a woman delivers a baby on arrival, a grandfather with COPD comes in with his daughter, and a man with abdominal pain is brought in by ambulance. The nurse must assess each client using appropriate methods.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Watch abdominal movement for a full minute while the baby is quiet, as newborns breathe diaphragmatically.): Newborns are belly breathers with irregular patterns, so respirations are counted by watching abdominal movement for a full minute while the baby is calm. Short counts multiplied up miss the irregular rhythm. Crying changes the rate, so counts taken then are not representative. Listening only at the mouth does not capture the real breathing pattern."}, {"id": 937, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The grandfather, 72, has COPD and takes home oxygen at 2 L/min. His saturation is 91 percent on that flow and he is breathing comfortably. His daughter says another hospital kept it at 99 percent and asks the nurse to turn up the oxygen. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Explain that chronic lung disease allows a lower target, keep the ordered flow, and report drowsiness", "B": "Stop the oxygen briefly and recheck, because 91 percent shows he no longer needs the supplement.", "C": "Turn the flow up by one litre, recheck in ten minutes, then ask for an order", "D": "Increase the oxygen until his saturation reaches 99 percent to reduce strain on his lungs"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Misreading a target-range value as evidence that oxygen is unnecessary", "C": "Changing the flow without an order", "D": "Chasing the highest possible saturation in chronic lung disease"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C113", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse is working an evening shift in a small rural emergency department. Within one hour a mother arrives with a toddler, a woman delivers a baby on arrival, a grandfather with COPD comes in with his daughter, and a man with abdominal pain is brought in by ambulance. The nurse must assess each client using appropriate methods.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that chronic lung disease allows a lower target, keep the ordered flow, and report drowsiness): Clients with chronic lung disease often have a lower saturation target, and very high oxygen can suppress their drive to breathe, so the flow stays as ordered while he is watched. Raising it to 99 percent risks carbon dioxide retention. Stopping oxygen based on a normal-for-him reading may cause a drop. Adjusting the flow without an order is outside scope."}, {"id": 938, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The man brought by ambulance, aged 44, points to his right lower abdomen and says it has hurt for six hours. He rates the pain 8 out of 10. The nurse is using the PQRST approach and has the number already. What additional step best completes the pain assessment?", "options": {"A": "Ask what eases or worsens it, how it feels, where it spreads, and how long.", "B": "Ask him to repeat the number every fifteen minutes, since score trends show the cause clearly.", "C": "Ask him to describe the pain in his own words and record his first answer.", "D": "Place a heat pack on the area and reassess the pain score after twenty minutes."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relying on a single score instead of a structured history", "C": "Records a single open answer instead of a structured history", "D": "Applies heat to a possible acute abdomen"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C113", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse is working an evening shift in a small rural emergency department. Within one hour a mother arrives with a toddler, a woman delivers a baby on arrival, a grandfather with COPD comes in with his daughter, and a man with abdominal pain is brought in by ambulance. The nurse must assess each client using appropriate methods.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask what eases or worsens it, how it feels, where it spreads, and how long.): PQRST collects provocation, quality, region and radiation, severity, and timing, and a number alone does not describe the pain or point toward a cause. A single open answer leaves the history incomplete. Heat can aggravate a possible acute abdomen. Analgesia may follow, but it should not replace the structured assessment."}, {"id": 939, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An 82-year-old resident of a long-term care home takes a diuretic and has become confused over two days, with a new headache and nausea. His sodium is reported as 120 mmol/L. A care aide suggests encouraging extra water for the confusion. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Give the next diuretic dose as scheduled and recheck the sodium in the morning.", "B": "Encourage water hourly, since confusion in older residents is often caused by dehydration.", "C": "Offer salty snacks and broth freely, then reassess the confusion at the next routine check.", "D": "Withhold extra water, report the result promptly, and monitor neurological status and intake and output"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Continuing the drug that may be lowering sodium", "B": "Giving free water to a client whose sodium is already low", "C": "Adding salt without an order and delaying reporting"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold extra water, report the result promptly, and monitor neurological status and intake and output): A sodium of 120 mmol/L with confusion, headache, and nausea signals hyponatremia, and free water would dilute his sodium further. The diuretic may be contributing, so the result is reported before another dose. Salty food without an order does not treat a sodium of this severity safely. Neurological changes make prompt reporting the priority."}, {"id": 940, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 55-year-old man has had back pain for three days and now reports he has not passed urine since morning and feels numbness between his legs. His pain is 4 out of 10 and he asks for his usual analgesic. What is the nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Help him walk to the washroom to try to void and then recheck for numbness.", "B": "Report urgently as a possible spinal cord compression emergency and keep him still while awaiting assessment.", "C": "Give his usual analgesic and reassure him that back pain with mild numbness usually settles.", "D": "Apply a heat pack to the back and document the urine retention for day shift."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Encouraging movement that could worsen a compressed cord", "C": "Treating red flag signs as routine back pain", "D": "Documenting a serious sign for later instead of acting now"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report urgently as a possible spinal cord compression emergency and keep him still while awaiting assessment.): Bladder retention with numbness in the saddle area are red flags for cauda equina compression, which can cause permanent damage within hours. Analgesia and reassurance treat only the pain. Walking could worsen the injury, and heat with delayed documentation postpones urgent care. The low pain score does not lessen the urgency."}, {"id": 941, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor asks whether he should shave his knee tonight so the surgical team has less work. His skin is intact, and the unit protocol allows clippers on the day of surgery only if hair interferes. What should the nurse explain about skin preparation?", "options": {"A": "Shave the area with a razor tonight so the skin is clear before surgery.", "B": "Avoid razor shaving; clip hair only if needed, and wash with the prescribed antiseptic soap.", "C": "Scrub the knee firmly with a rough brush tonight until the skin is pink.", "D": "Skip washing and bathe only after the operation, since the surgical team cleans the skin."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Shaving the night before, which causes microscopic cuts that raise infection risk", "C": "Scrubbing harshly, which damages skin and increases bacteria", "D": "Omitting preoperative washing because the team cleans later"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C111", "cluster_text": "Mr. Samuel Okafor, 64, is scheduled for a total knee replacement tomorrow morning. A practical nurse on the surgical unit prepares him before surgery, assists with checks in the operating room, and cares for him after he returns. He takes an antihypertensive and has no known allergies.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Avoid razor shaving; clip hair only if needed, and wash with the prescribed antiseptic soap.): Razor shaving creates tiny skin cuts that bacteria can enter, so hair is clipped only if needed and close to surgery. Washing with the prescribed antiseptic soap reduces skin bacteria. Waiting until after surgery to wash misses the benefit of preoperative skin preparation. A rough brush injures the skin and raises infection risk."}, {"id": 942, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two days after surgery, Mr. Okafor's incision is clean, dry, and closed, and the nurse is about to change the dressing. A colleague says that because he is receiving preventive antibiotics, hand hygiene and sterile technique can be relaxed. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Relax technique for the dressing but keep hand hygiene before touching any surgical drain.", "B": "Skip the dressing change, since antibiotics make an undisturbed dressing safer than a changed one.", "C": "Antibiotics do not replace aseptic technique, so perform hand hygiene and use sterile technique each time.", "D": "Use clean gloves without hand hygiene, since the antibiotic protects the wound from most bacteria."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Splitting technique so only some steps are protected", "B": "Avoiding dressing care that the plan requires", "D": "Treating antibiotics as a substitute for aseptic technique"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C111", "cluster_text": "Mr. Samuel Okafor, 64, is scheduled for a total knee replacement tomorrow morning. A practical nurse on the surgical unit prepares him before surgery, assists with checks in the operating room, and cares for him after he returns. He takes an antihypertensive and has no known allergies.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Antibiotics do not replace aseptic technique, so perform hand hygiene and use sterile technique each time.): Preventive antibiotics lower risk but do not make the wound safe from bacteria introduced by hands or equipment, so aseptic technique stays the standard. Relaxing hand hygiene or technique exposes the fresh incision. Skipping the dressing change ignores the plan of care. Consistent technique at every change is the most reliable defence against infection."}, {"id": 943, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the operating room before the incision, the surgeon is delayed and the team is eager to start. The circulating nurse says the surgical safety checklist time out can wait because the schedule is tight. What is the purpose of the time out?", "options": {"A": "A pause before incision when the team confirms identity, procedure, site and allergies to prevent errors.", "B": "A handover for the anesthesia staff completed while the client is moved to the recovery room.", "C": "A review of the surgical count completed by the scrub nurse after the closure.", "D": "A discussion of postoperative pain orders, completed by the surgeon when the client reaches recovery."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Confusing it with a postoperative handover", "C": "Confuses the pre-incision pause with the closing count", "D": "Confuses the time out with postoperative order review"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C111", "cluster_text": "Mr. Samuel Okafor, 64, is scheduled for a total knee replacement tomorrow morning. A practical nurse on the surgical unit prepares him before surgery, assists with checks in the operating room, and cares for him after he returns. He takes an antihypertensive and has no known allergies.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A pause before incision when the team confirms identity, procedure, site and allergies to prevent errors.): The time out is a team-wide pause before incision to confirm identity, procedure, site, and allergies, and it prevents errors that harm clients. The surgical count happens at closure and pain orders are reviewed after surgery. A recovery handover happens at a different time. Schedule pressure is a reason to hold the pause, not to skip it."}, {"id": 944, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In recovery after spinal anesthesia, Mr. Okafor cannot feel or move his legs and asks why. His blood pressure is 96/58 mmHg, down from 134/82 before surgery, and his heart rate is 58. How should the nurse interpret these findings?", "options": {"A": "Leg findings are expected, but low blood pressure and slow pulse need reporting and monitoring.", "B": "Low blood pressure suggests bleeding, so lay his bed flat and begin fluids.", "C": "Leg numbness is the main concern, so keep his legs elevated and apply warm blankets.", "D": "All findings are expected after spinal anesthesia, so routine checks every four hours are enough."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Acting without orders on an assumed cause", "C": "Focusing on the visible deficit and missing the hemodynamic change", "D": "Assuming every finding is normal and reducing monitoring"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C111", "cluster_text": "Mr. Samuel Okafor, 64, is scheduled for a total knee replacement tomorrow morning. A practical nurse on the surgical unit prepares him before surgery, assists with checks in the operating room, and cares for him after he returns. He takes an antihypertensive and has no known allergies.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Leg findings are expected, but low blood pressure and slow pulse need reporting and monitoring.): Numbness and weakness in the legs are expected while the spinal block wears off, but a falling blood pressure and slow heart rate can result from sympathetic blockade and need prompt reporting and close checks. Checking only every four hours misses worsening. Elevating the legs does not address the vital signs. Starting fluids without an order assumes a cause the nurse has not confirmed."}, {"id": 945, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 75-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation takes warfarin. At the clinic her INR is 4.8, with a target of 2.0 to 3.0. She has no bleeding, only a bruise on her forearm, and worries that her blood is too clotty. What does the nurse understand and do?", "options": {"A": "A high INR shows increased clotting risk, so ask the prescriber to raise her warfarin dose.", "B": "The result is within acceptable variation, so continue the usual dose and recheck in a month.", "C": "A high INR means slow clotting, so report it and assess for bleeding before dosing", "D": "The bruise confirms active bleeding, so give vitamin K and send her home."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reading a high INR as too much clotting", "B": "Treating a clearly elevated INR as normal variation", "D": "Giving a reversal drug without an order"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A high INR means slow clotting, so report it and assess for bleeding before dosing): An INR of 4.8 is above her target and means her blood is taking too long to clot, so bleeding risk is raised and the result is reported before another dose. Believing it signals more clotting would lead to a dose increase and harm. Waiting a month ignores a clear risk. Giving vitamin K is a prescriber decision, not a nursing one."}, {"id": 946, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A 79-year-old man on a palliative unit asks the practical nurse whether his treatment is still meant to cure him. His physician is not on the unit until tomorrow, and his family is in the room. Another nurse says only physicians may discuss goals of care. What is the nurse's best response?", "options": {"A": "Listen to his question, share what the plan states within scope, and arrange a physician discussion.", "B": "Explain that nurses do not discuss goals of care and change the subject gently.", "C": "Wait until his family leaves and then give him a full explanation of his prognosis.", "D": "Tell him the treatment will not cure him, since honesty requires a direct prognosis now."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Refusing to engage with a legitimate question", "C": "Giving a full prognosis privately without team involvement", "D": "Giving a prognosis beyond scope without the team"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Listen to his question, share what the plan states within scope, and arrange a physician discussion.): Nurses have a role in goals-of-care conversations: they listen, clarify what he understands, and bring the physician and team together so he receives accurate information. Delivering a prognosis alone goes beyond scope. Changing the subject leaves a client's question unheard. A private full explanation still bypasses the team and may conflict with what the physician plans to say."}, {"id": 947, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 38-year-old woman with asthma has started a fluticasone inhaler twice daily. She uses it each morning and evening before work and wants to keep her routine short. At the clinic she asks whether she must rinse her mouth afterward, since the drug goes straight to the lungs. How should the nurse answer?", "options": {"A": "Skip rinsing, since the dose is small and any mouth residue is swallowed and harmless.", "B": "Rinse once daily at bedtime, since that removes the drug from the whole day.", "C": "Rinse and spit after each dose to remove medication left in the mouth and throat.", "D": "Swallow a sip of water after each dose so the medication reaches the stomach."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes mouth residue is harmless", "B": "Rinses once daily instead of after each dose", "D": "Swallowing water, which carries the drug down instead of removing it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rinse and spit after each dose to remove medication left in the mouth and throat.): A portion of an inhaled corticosteroid lands in the mouth and throat, where it can cause thrush and a hoarse voice, so rinsing and spitting after every dose removes it. Residue is not harmless. Rinsing once a day leaves other doses unprotected. Swallowing water simply sends the drug into the stomach."}, {"id": 948, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lindqvist's incontinence means frequent linen changes overnight, and a care aide suggests asking for an indwelling catheter to save time. She is alert, mobile with help, drinks well, and her urine output is normal with no retention. What is the nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Limit her fluids after supper to reduce voiding at night and the need for care.", "B": "Place the catheter only at night, when linen changes disturb her sleep the most.", "C": "Avoid the catheter, offer scheduled toileting and continence products, and share the plan with the RN.", "D": "Request a catheter for her stay, since it keeps her skin dry and linen clean."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Restricting fluids, which raises dehydration and urinary infection risk", "B": "Using a catheter part-time, which still carries risk", "D": "Using a catheter for staff convenience despite infection risk"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C116", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ingrid Lindqvist, 83, is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia. She has mild urinary incontinence, is hard of hearing, and has been awake at night since admission. A practical nurse is assigned to her care alongside an RN. Her physician has written a new order for a sedating drug.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Avoid the catheter, offer scheduled toileting and continence products, and share the plan with the RN.): An indwelling catheter raises the risk of urinary infection and should not be used for convenience, so scheduled toileting and products are better. Part-time use still exposes her to the same risk. Limiting fluids risks dehydration and concentrated urine. Sharing the plan with the RN keeps care coordinated."}, {"id": 949, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lindqvist remains awake at night and is restless and calling out. Her oxygen saturation is stable and her pain is controlled. The night nurse is considering the new sedating drug that is ordered as needed. Which is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Try non-drug measures first, such as dimming lights and grouping care, and report ongoing disturbance.", "B": "Give the sedating drug now, since sleep supports recovery and the order is already written.", "C": "Keep her sitting in a chair in bright light overnight to reset her sleep rhythm.", "D": "Remind her firmly that it is night time and ask her to stay in bed quietly."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Choosing a sedative-hypnotic before non-drug approaches", "C": "Using a method that increases disorientation and falls risk", "D": "Correcting her behaviour instead of addressing causes"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C116", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ingrid Lindqvist, 83, is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia. She has mild urinary incontinence, is hard of hearing, and has been awake at night since admission. A practical nurse is assigned to her care alongside an RN. Her physician has written a new order for a sedating drug.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Try non-drug measures first, such as dimming lights and grouping care, and report ongoing disturbance.): Older adults are especially sensitive to sedatives, so quiet, dim surroundings, comfort measures, and clustered care are tried first, with ongoing problems reported. Giving the drug first adds risk of confusion and falls. Bright light all night worsens sleep disruption. Firm reminders ignore causes and may increase distress."}, {"id": 950, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During morning care, Mrs. Lindqvist does not respond when the nurse speaks from the doorway and looks confused by the instructions. Her hearing aids are in the drawer, and a television is playing loudly in the room. What is the best communication approach?", "options": {"A": "Write each instruction on paper and stand beside the bed while she reads quietly.", "B": "Speak close to her ear in simple words and leave the television on for company.", "C": "Raise your voice and repeat the instruction until she responds from across the room.", "D": "Reduce noise, help her insert her hearing aids, face her at eye level, and speak slowly."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying only on writing without addressing hearing", "B": "Speaking into the ear while background noise stays", "C": "Shouting instead of reducing noise and improving access to hearing aids"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C116", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ingrid Lindqvist, 83, is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia. She has mild urinary incontinence, is hard of hearing, and has been awake at night since admission. A practical nurse is assigned to her care alongside an RN. Her physician has written a new order for a sedating drug.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduce noise, help her insert her hearing aids, face her at eye level, and speak slowly.): Reducing noise, using her hearing aids, and facing her at eye level with slow, clear speech gives her the best chance to understand. Shouting distorts speech and adds distress. Speaking into her ear with the television on keeps the competing noise. Writing alone assumes vision is intact and ignores her hearing aids."}, {"id": 951, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The physician's order is for a sedating sleep drug at the standard adult dose. Mrs. Lindqvist is 83, weighs 48 kg, has reduced kidney function, and has been awake at night since her admission. The nurse reviews the order before giving it. What is the main concern?", "options": {"A": "Older adults may need the standard dose, but timed earlier in the evening.", "B": "Older adults absorb drugs faster, so the dose is probably too weak and may need raising.", "C": "Age-related slower clearance may cause excess sedation and falls, so the dose should be queried.", "D": "Kidney function matters in antibiotic dosing, so the dose is appropriate for her weight."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changes timing but ignores reduced clearance", "B": "Believing older adults need higher doses", "D": "Limits kidney concerns to antibiotics"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C116", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ingrid Lindqvist, 83, is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia. She has mild urinary incontinence, is hard of hearing, and has been awake at night since admission. A practical nurse is assigned to her care alongside an RN. Her physician has written a new order for a sedating drug.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Age-related slower clearance may cause excess sedation and falls, so the dose should be queried.): Older adults, especially those with low weight and reduced kidney function, clear drugs more slowly, so a standard adult dose can accumulate and cause oversedation, confusion, and falls. Changing the timing does not change clearance. Many sedatives and their metabolites rely on the kidneys, so function matters, and the nurse queries the order."}, {"id": 952, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 15-year-old girl in the emergency department has had periumbilical pain that moved to the right lower abdomen, with nausea and a low fever. Her mother asks the nurse to give her a heating pad for comfort. Which response is best?", "options": {"A": "Offer sips of fluid and a light meal so she stays hydrated before any procedure.", "B": "Withhold heat and food, report the findings promptly, and monitor her pain and vital signs", "C": "Apply the heating pad on low, since warmth relaxes the abdominal muscles and eases cramping.", "D": "Give an oral laxative to relieve the cramping, then record her stool pattern."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving food and fluid before assessment for possible surgery", "C": "Applying heat, which may increase inflammation and risk of rupture", "D": "Giving a laxative that can worsen a possible appendicitis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Withhold heat and food, report the findings promptly, and monitor her pain and vital signs): Pain that moves to the right lower abdomen with nausea and fever suggests appendicitis, so heat, laxatives, and food are avoided and the findings are reported at once. Heat may speed inflammation and raise rupture risk. Laxatives increase bowel activity and can worsen the condition. Eating or drinking before assessment complicates possible surgery."}, {"id": 953, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 79-year-old man is admitted from home to a medical unit after a fall. He is thin, incontinent, and spent most of the day in a chair. His skin is intact on admission. When and how should the nurse assess his pressure injury risk?", "options": {"A": "Assess his skin daily and use the Braden scale only if a red area appears.", "B": "Complete a Braden scale assessment on admission, repeat it regularly, and target prevention to the subscores.", "C": "Estimate risk from his weight and age and document that he is high risk.", "D": "Delay the scale until he is on bed rest, since only bedridden clients are at risk."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for skin breakdown before scoring risk", "C": "Estimating risk without a validated tool", "D": "Believing only bed-bound clients need a risk scale"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Complete a Braden scale assessment on admission, repeat it regularly, and target prevention to the subscores.): Risk scoring on admission, with regular repeats, identifies clients such as this man who are at risk before the skin breaks down, and the subscores guide prevention such as moisture care and repositioning. Using the scale only after redness appears is too late. Chair-bound clients are also at risk. A guess based on age and weight does not replace a validated tool."}, {"id": 954, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An 88-year-old resident of a long-term care home takes digoxin daily. This morning she reports nausea and says lights look yellow-green with halos. Her apical pulse is 54 beats per minute. The resident thinks she needs her eyes checked. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Offer the as-needed antiemetic for the nausea and continue the usual digoxin schedule.", "B": "Hold the dose, recount the apical pulse for a minute, and report possible digoxin toxicity.", "C": "Give the dose as ordered and book an eye appointment for the end of next week.", "D": "Give the dose with food to lessen the nausea, and recheck the pulse tomorrow."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating the symptom without recognizing toxicity", "C": "Attributing visual change to the eyes while the drug is the likely cause", "D": "Masking nausea and continuing the drug"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold the dose, recount the apical pulse for a minute, and report possible digoxin toxicity.): Nausea, yellow-green visual halos, and a slow pulse are classic signs of digoxin toxicity, so the dose is held and the findings are reported before further administration. Booking an eye visit misses the drug cause. Giving the dose with food or an antiemetic treats symptoms while the toxicity continues. An older adult with reduced clearance is at particular risk."}, {"id": 955, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "During shift handover on an acute care unit, a nurse from another unit who is not assigned to a 63-year-old man asks the practical nurse for details about him because she knows his family. She has no task involving him today. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Share details only with those directly providing his care and politely decline her request.", "B": "Share only his diagnosis but not the treatment plan, since that is lower risk.", "C": "Share a brief update, since all nurses in the hospital belong to the same care team.", "D": "Give the details after she promises not to tell the family."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Sharing a partial record without a need to know", "C": "Treating every employee as part of the circle of care", "D": "Accepting a promise instead of a care role"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Share details only with those directly providing his care and politely decline her request.): Implied consent covers sharing among those directly involved in his care, and a nurse not assigned to him is outside that circle. Working in the same hospital does not create access. A promise of silence does not make disclosure appropriate. Sharing only the diagnosis is still a disclosure without a need to know."}, {"id": 956, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 60-year-old man started a statin one month ago for high cholesterol. At the clinic visit he reports new aching in both thighs and dark urine since last week, and wonders whether he should ignore it because he feels otherwise well. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Suggest he drink more fluids and take the statin in the morning instead of the evening.", "B": "Report the pain and dark urine promptly, since they may signal muscle damage needing drug review", "C": "Advise him to keep taking the statin and stretch, since muscle aches commonly fade over time.", "D": "Tell him muscle pain is expected and needs attention if it lasts beyond three months"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing timing and fluids without a prescriber", "C": "Encouraging continued use despite warning symptoms", "D": "Setting a false time threshold for reporting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the pain and dark urine promptly, since they may signal muscle damage needing drug review): Muscle pain with dark urine after starting a statin may indicate muscle breakdown, so it is reported at once for the prescriber to decide about the drug. Telling him to carry on risks kidney damage. Changing the timing or fluids does not address the cause. Waiting three months delays care for a possible serious reaction."}, {"id": 957, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 70-year-old woman on a telemetry unit has been started on IV amiodarone for atrial fibrillation. She is alert and reports no chest pain. Her heart rate is 92 and her blood pressure is 118/70. The practical nurse is planning monitoring. Which assessment plan is best?", "options": {"A": "Check vital signs once a shift, since the drug mainly affects thyroid function over time.", "B": "Monitor heart rate and blood pressure, but skip the ECG since rhythm is unaffected.", "C": "Monitor closely for the first dose, then check vital signs every four hours.", "D": "Monitor ECG rhythm, heart rate, and blood pressure, and report bradycardia, long QT, or hypotension."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Underestimating acute cardiac effects of an IV antiarrhythmic", "B": "Omits ECG monitoring for a drug that changes the tracing", "C": "Reduces monitoring too soon for an IV antiarrhythmic"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Monitor ECG rhythm, heart rate, and blood pressure, and report bradycardia, long QT, or hypotension.): IV amiodarone can cause slow heart rate, hypotension, and a prolonged QT interval, so rhythm, heart rate, and blood pressure are watched and changes are reported. The drug does change the ECG, so skipping it is unsafe. Monitoring closely only for the first dose, then every four hours, misses early bradycardia or hypotension later in the infusion. Checking once a shift leaves the infusion effects unwatched."}, {"id": 958, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 72-year-old woman with COPD attends a community clinic for her annual checkup. She says vaccines are for children and she has had none since retirement. She lives alone, walks daily, and has had no chest infection this year. The nurse reviews her record. What is the best teaching point?", "options": {"A": "Recommend yearly influenza vaccine plus the recommended pneumococcal vaccine, because lung disease raises serious infection risk", "B": "Suggest the influenza vaccine only if she has had a chest infection this year.", "C": "Immunization after age sixty-five adds little protection, so treat infections once they develop.", "D": "Advise the pneumococcal vaccine yearly each autumn along with the influenza vaccine."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Limiting the vaccine to those with recent infection", "C": "Believing vaccines lose value in older adults", "D": "Misstating the pneumococcal schedule as yearly"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Recommend yearly influenza vaccine plus the recommended pneumococcal vaccine, because lung disease raises serious infection risk): Clients with COPD are at high risk of serious respiratory infection, so annual influenza vaccine and the recommended pneumococcal and other vaccines are advised. Older adults still benefit from vaccines. Linking the influenza vaccine to a recent infection misses prevention. Pneumococcal vaccine is not given every year, so that advice is inaccurate."}, {"id": 959, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "An 80-year-old man in intensive care has multiple organ failure. His wife, as substitute decision-maker, has agreed with the team to stop the ventilator. A new nurse says stopping treatment differs from withholding it and may be unlawful. What principle should the practical nurse apply?", "options": {"A": "Withdrawal is acceptable only after the wife also asks the physician to continue it.", "B": "Withdrawal is a harmful act that only a physician may carry out, so nurses must abstain.", "C": "Stopping a treatment is ethically weaker than not starting it, so more approvals are needed.", "D": "Stopping and withholding treatment are ethically equivalent when consent supports them, so follow the agreed plan."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Requiring contradictory family requests", "B": "Misreading withdrawal as an act of harm", "C": "Treating withdrawal as ethically worse than withholding"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stopping and withholding treatment are ethically equivalent when consent supports them, so follow the agreed plan.): Ethically and legally, stopping a treatment that no longer serves the client's goals is equivalent to not starting it when consent and the team plan support it. Requiring extra approvals treats withdrawal as worse without basis. Asking the wife to request continuation contradicts her decision. Nurses take part in the agreed plan within policy and may raise concerns through the team."}, {"id": 960, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 3-year-old arrives in her mother's arms, still jerking all four limbs for about two minutes after a fever began. The mother wants to put a spoon in her mouth so she does not bite her tongue. What first aid should the nurse direct?", "options": {"A": "Sit her upright on the mother's lap and cool her with a cold bath immediately.", "B": "Lay her on her side, loosen clothing, time the seizure, and put nothing in her mouth.", "C": "Hold her limbs firmly to stop the movements and place a soft object between her teeth.", "D": "Keep her flat on her back and give acetaminophen by mouth to lower the fever."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Cold bathing and upright seating during a seizure", "C": "Restraining and placing an object in the mouth, risking injury and airway blockage", "D": "Giving oral medication to a child who is convulsing"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C115", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works an evening shift in a pediatric urgent care clinic. Over three hours she sees a toddler who convulsed at home, a young infant with fever brought by a worried father, a school-aged child with neck stiffness and a rash who arrives with a teacher, and a preschooler with a long fever and red eyes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lay her on her side, loosen clothing, time the seizure, and put nothing in her mouth.): Side-lying keeps the airway clear, loosening clothing and timing the seizure supports safe care, and nothing goes into the mouth. Restraining limbs and placing an object in the mouth can injure her and block her airway. A cold bath is not advised and seated holding risks falls. Oral medication is unsafe while she is convulsing."}, {"id": 961, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 6-week-old boy is brought in by his father with a rectal temperature of 38.2 C, feeding slightly less, and no rash. He is alert and cries normally. His father suggests waiting at home for 24 hours and giving acetaminophen. What is the nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Report immediately for urgent medical assessment, since fever under three months old may signal serious infection.", "B": "Give acetaminophen and have him return if the fever persists beyond twenty-four hours.", "C": "Undress him and recheck the temperature in an hour before deciding whether a doctor sees him.", "D": "Advise a lukewarm bath and extra feeds, and reassess him at tomorrow's routine visit."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Waiting at home for a fever in a very young infant", "C": "Delaying escalation while rechecking a fever that is already confirmed", "D": "Home cooling measures instead of urgent assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C115", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works an evening shift in a pediatric urgent care clinic. Over three hours she sees a toddler who convulsed at home, a young infant with fever brought by a worried father, a school-aged child with neck stiffness and a rash who arrives with a teacher, and a preschooler with a long fever and red eyes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report immediately for urgent medical assessment, since fever under three months old may signal serious infection.): A temperature of 38.0 C or higher in an infant under three months is treated as an emergency because serious infection can progress quickly even when he looks well. Giving acetaminophen and waiting hides the fever and delays care. Rechecking the temperature does not change the need for assessment. Bathing and a routine visit leave him unassessed for too long."}, {"id": 962, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 5-year-old girl arrives with her teacher, with neck stiffness, headache, and vomiting. The nurse sees small purple spots on her legs that do not fade when pressed. She is sleepy but responds, and her temperature is 38.0 C. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Give acetaminophen and watch the rash for an hour to see whether it fades.", "B": "Place her on droplet precautions and report immediately as an emergency, suspecting meningitis.", "C": "Offer fluids and encourage rest in the waiting room until a doctor is free.", "D": "Wait for a higher fever before reporting, since meningitis usually presents with a very high temperature."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating the fever and observing a non-blanching rash", "C": "Leaving a child with warning signs in a waiting room", "D": "Waiting for a high fever to confirm"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C115", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works an evening shift in a pediatric urgent care clinic. Over three hours she sees a toddler who convulsed at home, a young infant with fever brought by a worried father, a school-aged child with neck stiffness and a rash who arrives with a teacher, and a preschooler with a long fever and red eyes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Place her on droplet precautions and report immediately as an emergency, suspecting meningitis.): Neck stiffness, vomiting, drowsiness, and a non-blanching rash point to possible bacterial meningitis, which needs droplet precautions and emergency care without delay. Waiting for a higher fever is unsafe, since the temperature may be only moderate. Giving acetaminophen and watching the rash delays treatment. Leaving her in the waiting room exposes others and postpones care."}, {"id": 963, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 3-year-old boy has had fever for five days despite acetaminophen. The nurse notes red eyes without discharge, cracked red lips, a swollen hand, and an enlarged neck gland. His mother says another clinic called it a virus. How should the nurse interpret these findings?", "options": {"A": "They suggest a bacterial throat infection, so ask for a culture swab and penicillin.", "B": "They suggest an allergic reaction, so ask about new foods and offer an antihistamine.", "C": "These fit a viral illness, so advise fluids and rest and review if fever persists", "D": "This pattern suggests Kawasaki disease, so report it promptly for urgent assessment"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Naming a throat infection and antibiotic without the typical signs", "B": "Assuming allergy without a trigger", "C": "Treating a recognizable pattern as a routine viral illness"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C115", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works an evening shift in a pediatric urgent care clinic. Over three hours she sees a toddler who convulsed at home, a young infant with fever brought by a worried father, a school-aged child with neck stiffness and a rash who arrives with a teacher, and a preschooler with a long fever and red eyes.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (This pattern suggests Kawasaki disease, so report it promptly for urgent assessment): Prolonged fever with red eyes, cracked lips, hand swelling, and a swollen gland is the pattern of Kawasaki disease, which needs prompt treatment to protect the coronary arteries. Calling it viral delays care. A throat infection would not explain the hand and eye findings. Allergy does not cause a fever of five days with this pattern."}, {"id": 964, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A 60-year-old man on a medical unit is alert and capable. His wife, at the bedside, asks the nurse for his test results and diagnosis. He has told staff he wants his diagnosis kept private for now, and she says spouses have a right to know. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Respect his wishes, explain that sharing needs his consent, and offer to ask him about it.", "B": "Share only the diagnosis and not the treatment details, since this limits what is disclosed.", "C": "Share the results since she is his closest family member and wants to support his care.", "D": "Ask the physician to tell her while the nurse stays out of the conversation."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Partial disclosure without consent", "C": "Assuming a spouse has an automatic right to information", "D": "Passing disclosure to another professional without consent"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Respect his wishes, explain that sharing needs his consent, and offer to ask him about it.): A capable client controls who receives his information, and a spouse has no automatic right to it, so the nurse respects his request and offers to ask him. Sharing results because she is family overrides his decision. Sharing only the diagnosis is still disclosure without permission. The physician is bound by the same duty, so passing the task on does not solve it."}, {"id": 965, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A caller tells a practical nurse on a medical unit that she is the sister of a 70-year-old man admitted overnight. She sounds distressed and asks for his condition and room number. The nurse does not recognize her voice, and the client has not named any contacts. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Release no details, explain privacy policy, and offer to pass her name to the client.", "B": "Ask for his birth date and address, then share his general condition if correct.", "C": "Say he is stable, since this single word reveals very little about his health.", "D": "Transfer her directly to his room phone so that he can speak with her."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using identifying details as proof of authorization", "C": "Giving a one-word status update as if harmless", "D": "Connecting the call without checking the client's wishes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Release no details, explain privacy policy, and offer to pass her name to the client.): Callers cannot be verified by phone, and the client decides who learns of his admission, so the nurse shares nothing and offers to relay the message. Answering identity questions correctly does not prove permission. Even a one-word status confirms he is a client. Transferring the call without asking him may breach his privacy."}, {"id": 966, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad, one day after hernia repair, rates his incision pain 8 out of 10 and winces when he moves. The RN asked the nurse to start discharge teaching about wound care now, since he goes home tomorrow. He appears distracted and tense. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Give him printed handouts to read while pain control is arranged, so no time is lost.", "B": "Treat pain first with the ordered analgesic and comfort measures, then teach once he can focus.", "C": "Ask a family member to attend and take notes while his pain is still unresolved.", "D": "Begin teaching now in brief sessions, so enough time remains before tomorrow's discharge."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Substituting written material for teaching during distress", "C": "Relying on a family member instead of treating the pain", "D": "Teaching a client who cannot concentrate because of pain"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C120", "cluster_text": "Mr. Karim Haddad, 58, is on day 1 after an open hernia repair on a surgical unit and expects discharge tomorrow. A practical nurse is helping the RN prepare him for going home with a new prescription for an analgesic and a stool softener. He has limited reading skills but has not told the staff.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Treat pain first with the ordered analgesic and comfort measures, then teach once he can focus.): Pain competes for attention and memory, so relief comes first and teaching follows when he can focus. Starting now wastes the session because little will be retained. Handouts depend on reading skills and attention he cannot give. Having family take notes avoids the problem rather than solving it."}, {"id": 967, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "While reviewing his new prescription, Mr. Haddad nods and says he understands, but holds the label at arm's length and asks the nurse to read the drug name. He mentions he forgot his glasses. How should the nurse interpret these cues?", "options": {"A": "They may signal limited health literacy, so use plain language, teach-back, and tell the RN.", "B": "He probably just needs his glasses, so teaching can continue unchanged once they arrive.", "C": "He is probably avoiding teaching because of pain, so the session should be postponed.", "D": "His nodding shows understanding, so a short summary is enough and extra reinforcement is not needed."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Explaining the cue only as an eyesight problem", "C": "Assuming pain explains the avoidance", "D": "Treating a nod as proof of understanding"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C120", "cluster_text": "Mr. Karim Haddad, 58, is on day 1 after an open hernia repair on a surgical unit and expects discharge tomorrow. A practical nurse is helping the RN prepare him for going home with a new prescription for an analgesic and a stool softener. He has limited reading skills but has not told the staff.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (They may signal limited health literacy, so use plain language, teach-back, and tell the RN.): Asking someone else to read, nodding without asking questions, and offering an excuse such as forgotten glasses are common cues of limited health literacy, so plain language and teach-back are used and the RN is told. Nodding does not confirm understanding. Glasses may help but do not explain the pattern. Postponing teaching ignores the real barrier."}, {"id": 968, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad is on day 1 after an open hernia repair and expects to go home tomorrow. The nurse is writing a handout on caring for his incision at home. He reads slowly and prefers short instructions. Which writing approach is best for this handout?", "options": {"A": "Use the correct medical terms, each followed by a definition in brackets for clarity.", "B": "Write short paragraphs in small print with every step included, to keep the handout complete.", "C": "Use short sentences, everyday words, large print and simple pictures, with the key action first.", "D": "Use a medical illustration from a textbook with technical labels and a short caption."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adding definitions after terms, which still burdens a slow reader", "B": "Keeps small dense text that a slow reader cannot use", "D": "Uses technical labels that need medical vocabulary"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C120", "cluster_text": "Mr. Karim Haddad, 58, is on day 1 after an open hernia repair on a surgical unit and expects discharge tomorrow. A practical nurse is helping the RN prepare him for going home with a new prescription for an analgesic and a stool softener. He has limited reading skills but has not told the staff.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use short sentences, everyday words, large print and simple pictures, with the key action first.): Plain language with short sentences, large print, pictures, and the key action first suits a slow reader and helps him remember what matters. Medical terms with definitions still force him to decode jargon. Small dense text hides the key steps. Technical illustrations need vocabulary he may lack."}, {"id": 969, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At discharge the nurse reviews the prescription instructions with Mr. Haddad, who nods but asks no questions. The label reads 1 tab PO BID PRN pain, and the stool softener is marked daily while on analgesic. Which way of explaining the instructions is best?", "options": {"A": "Explain the instructions in the pharmacy's wording, and have him sign that he understood.", "B": "Say take one tablet twice daily as needed, and give him a printed list of terms.", "C": "Read the label aloud, using terms like PO and BID, and ask for questions.", "D": "Say take one tablet by mouth twice daily if needed for pain, then ask for teach-back."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a signature as proof of understanding", "B": "Relying on terms without confirming understanding", "C": "Using abbreviations a client may not know"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C120", "cluster_text": "Mr. Karim Haddad, 58, is on day 1 after an open hernia repair on a surgical unit and expects discharge tomorrow. A practical nurse is helping the RN prepare him for going home with a new prescription for an analgesic and a stool softener. He has limited reading skills but has not told the staff.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Say take one tablet by mouth twice daily if needed for pain, then ask for teach-back.): Everyday words that state when and how to take the drug, followed by teach-back, show whether he can act on the instructions. Reading abbreviations aloud leaves the meaning hidden. A list of terms adds jargon rather than removing it. A signature records that he was given information, not that he understood it."}, {"id": 970, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Oduya, 55, is on a medical unit receiving intravenous antibiotics. He is alert but eats very little and says brushing his teeth feels pointless when his mouth is nearly empty. His lips are dry and his tongue has a pale coating. What is the best nursing response about his mouth care?", "options": {"A": "Delay mouth care until the pharmacist reviews whether the antibiotic is causing the coating", "B": "Support twice daily brushing of teeth and tongue with a soft brush, even with minimal intake", "C": "Rinse his mouth with water once a shift and apply lip balm only", "D": "Offer oral care only after meals, since little eating leaves little debris in his mouth"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for a drug review when a routine nursing measure is already needed", "C": "Under-treating the mouth with a single rinse that does not remove plaque", "D": "Linking mouth care to the amount eaten instead of to plaque and dryness"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Support twice daily brushing of teeth and tongue with a soft brush, even with minimal intake): Mr. Oduya has a dry mouth and a coated tongue, which show that plaque and bacteria build up even when he eats little, so regular brushing of teeth and tongue is needed. Cleaning only after meals fails because debris and bacteria collect without intake, and a once-a-shift rinse does not remove plaque. Delaying care for a pharmacy review postpones a basic nursing action that does not need an order. Oral care is scheduled by need, not by how much the client eats."}, {"id": 971, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 70-year-old woman on a medical unit takes furosemide daily for heart failure. Morning laboratory results show potassium 3.1 mmol/L. She feels fine, her pulse is 78 and regular, and she asks whether the result matters. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Document the value and recheck it with the next routine blood work tomorrow", "B": "Report the value to the RN promptly and monitor her heart rhythm and muscle strength", "C": "Hold the next furosemide dose on her own judgment and tell the RN afterward", "D": "Give a potassium supplement from the unit stock without waiting for an order"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a low potassium without symptoms as safe to ignore", "C": "Changing a medication without an order, outside practical nurse scope", "D": "Giving a drug without an order, which is a scope and safety error"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the value to the RN promptly and monitor her heart rhythm and muscle strength): A potassium of 3.1 mmol/L is below the usual range and raises the risk of dangerous heart rhythms, even when the client feels well. Reporting it and monitoring the pulse and muscle strength lets the RN and prescriber decide on treatment. Waiting a day to recheck ignores that risk, and holding furosemide or giving potassium without an order is outside practical nurse scope. Abnormal laboratory values are reported promptly whether or not symptoms are present."}, {"id": 972, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Two nurses ride a crowded hospital elevator with visitors present. One says quietly to the other, 'The man in room 12 with the new cancer diagnosis refused treatment again, but I only use first names, so it is fine.' You overhear. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Report the colleague to the manager right away without speaking to the colleague first", "B": "Tell the colleague later that first names are fine in elevators but not in hallways.", "C": "Quietly suggest stopping, then remind the colleague later that client details belong only in private settings", "D": "Wait until the elevator empties, then document the conversation in the client's chart."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Skipping direct, respectful feedback in favour of immediate formal reporting", "B": "Wrongly treats first names in public as acceptable in some places", "D": "Records a hallway remark in the chart and does not address it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Quietly suggest stopping, then remind the colleague later that client details belong only in private settings): Details such as a room, a diagnosis, and a treatment refusal can identify a client even without a full name, and an elevator is a public space. Stopping the conversation calmly and following up privately protects the client and supports the colleague. Saying first names are acceptable keeps the breach going. A chart note does not correct it. Going straight to a formal report skips professional feedback, though a repeated breach would need to be reported under policy."}, {"id": 973, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad, 70, has emphysema and attends a clinic. He becomes breathless climbing the two stairs to his apartment and takes quick deep breaths at the top. During the visit, the nurse teaches him a breathing technique to use during daily activity. Which instruction is best?", "options": {"A": "Breathe out forcefully through wide open lips to clear trapped air quickly", "B": "Hold each breath for ten seconds at the top of the stairs before continuing", "C": "Inhale slowly through the nose, then breathe out through pursed lips for twice as long", "D": "Take fast deep breaths through the mouth before each step to build oxygen reserve"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Forceful exhalation collapses weak airways, trapping more air", "B": "Breath holding raises carbon dioxide and does not relieve breathlessness", "D": "Fast deep breathing increases air trapping and breathlessness in emphysema"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Inhale slowly through the nose, then breathe out through pursed lips for twice as long): In emphysema the small airways collapse during exhalation, and pursed lips create back pressure that keeps them open so trapped air can leave. Breathing in slowly and out for twice as long reduces breathlessness during activity for Mr. Haddad. Fast deep breaths and forceful exhalation increase air trapping, and breath holding does not help gas exchange. Slow, controlled breathing paced with effort is the key teaching point."}, {"id": 974, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 40-year-old man with a T4 spinal cord injury sits in his wheelchair and reports a pounding headache. His face is flushed and sweaty, his skin below the chest is pale and cool, and his blood pressure is 196/112. He ate lunch an hour ago. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Give his scheduled analgesic and recheck his blood pressure after thirty minutes once the headache eases", "B": "Start passive range of motion to reduce muscle tone and increase venous return to the heart", "C": "Keep him upright, loosen tight clothing, and check bladder and bowel while alerting the RN", "D": "Lay him flat with his legs raised to improve circulation and reduce the headache"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating the headache alone and delaying removal of the trigger", "B": "Choosing an activity that may add stimulation instead of removing the cause", "D": "Lying flat raises blood pressure further in autonomic dysreflexia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep him upright, loosen tight clothing, and check bladder and bowel while alerting the RN): A very high pressure with a pounding headache and flushing above a T4 injury suggests autonomic dysreflexia, which is an emergency triggered by a stimulus such as a full bladder or bowel. Sitting him upright lowers pressure by gravity, and loosening clothing and checking for the trigger removes the cause while the RN is alerted. Lying flat raises pressure, analgesia alone leaves the trigger in place, and exercise adds stimulation. Remove the trigger and keep the client upright."}, {"id": 975, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 68-year-old woman visiting a community clinic reports three days of steady left lower abdominal pain, a temperature of 37.9 C, and reduced appetite. She has chronic constipation and asks for a laxative to feel better. Her abdomen is tender on the left. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Apply a heating pad to the left lower abdomen and reassess in two days", "B": "Advise a high-fibre diet with bran cereal to soften stools and settle the pain", "C": "Suggest a mild stimulant laxative to relieve the constipation, then recheck pain next week", "D": "Advise against a laxative for now and report the findings promptly to the NP or physician"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying assessment by treating the pain at home", "B": "Applying chronic constipation advice to an acute inflammatory picture", "C": "Using a laxative during a likely inflamed, infected bowel segment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Advise against a laxative for now and report the findings promptly to the NP or physician): Left lower quadrant pain, a low-grade fever, and tenderness suggest diverticulitis, in which the bowel is inflamed and may perforate. Findings like these need prompt assessment by the NP or physician, and laxatives can worsen inflammation. Bran and a heating pad treat constipation or cramping, not infection, and waiting several days risks complications. The nurse recognizes the pattern and reports it rather than treating the symptom."}, {"id": 976, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An 82-year-old man on a medical unit became confused overnight and is calling out for his late wife. Morning laboratory results show sodium 121 mmol/L. His daughter says he has just gotten old. His blood pressure is 128/76. What should the nurse do next?", "options": {"A": "Encourage him to drink extra water to correct the sodium result before the next draw", "B": "Report the low sodium and new confusion to the RN promptly and monitor him closely.", "C": "Document that the confusion is expected with age and repeat the sodium next week", "D": "Offer salty snacks from the family and chart the amount he eats this morning"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adding free water, which dilutes sodium further", "C": "Attributing a sudden change to age instead of a treatable cause", "D": "Self-treating electrolyte imbalance without an order"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the low sodium and new confusion to the RN promptly and monitor him closely.): A sodium of 121 mmol/L is markedly low and can cause sudden confusion and seizures, so the change is not simply due to age. Reporting it promptly and monitoring him closely allows the team to find the cause and treat it. Extra water would dilute the sodium further, and giving salt without an order is outside practical nurse scope. New confusion calls for a search for a treatable cause."}, {"id": 977, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At an earlier clinic visit, Dev says he skipped four of seven tablets this week because the drowsiness makes work hard. His mood is flat, and he denies hearing voices today. The RN and prescriber discuss options. Which interpretation of his situation best guides the nurse's next step?", "options": {"A": "He lacks motivation, so his family should count his tablets every evening", "B": "Poor insight is the main cause, so the nurse should warn him about relapse.", "C": "His flat mood shows the medication is working, so the dose needs no discussion", "D": "Side effects are driving missed doses, so a long-acting injectable is worth raising with the team"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using family monitoring in place of a collaborative treatment change", "B": "Blaming poor insight instead of exploring side effect barriers", "C": "Misreading flat mood as proof of effective treatment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C118", "cluster_text": "Dev Malhotra, 29, has schizophrenia and lives alone. He often misses his daily oral antipsychotic tablets, saying he forgets or dislikes the drowsiness. Over several weeks he becomes more suspicious and withdrawn. His family brings him to the emergency department, and he is later admitted to an inpatient mental health unit where the practical nurse helps care for him.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Side effects are driving missed doses, so a long-acting injectable is worth raising with the team): Dev links missed doses to drowsiness, which is a treatable barrier rather than a lack of motivation, so a long-acting injectable is a reasonable option to discuss. Warning him about relapse or assigning family tablet counts blames him or removes his role in the plan. A flat mood does not show that the drug is working and may reflect illness or sedation. Understanding why a client misses doses guides a respectful plan."}, {"id": 978, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the emergency department, Dev becomes convinced the tablets are poison and presses his lips shut when offered the prescribed dose. He is quiet and not threatening, and his vital signs are stable. The nurse wants to respond safely. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Acknowledge his fear calmly, offer to review the medication, and report the refusal to the RN", "B": "Crush the tablet into his juice so he receives the dose without a confrontation", "C": "Say the physician will order an injection if he refuses again, and offer one more try.", "D": "Document the refusal and tell him it is acceptable, without informing the RN of his fear."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Covert medication violates consent and trust", "C": "Uses the threat of an injection to pressure him", "D": "Records the refusal but leaves out reporting to the RN"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C118", "cluster_text": "Dev Malhotra, 29, has schizophrenia and lives alone. He often misses his daily oral antipsychotic tablets, saying he forgets or dislikes the drowsiness. Over several weeks he becomes more suspicious and withdrawn. His family brings him to the emergency department, and he is later admitted to an inpatient mental health unit where the practical nurse helps care for him.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acknowledge his fear calmly, offer to review the medication, and report the refusal to the RN): Dev has the right to refuse medication, and a calm acknowledgement of his fear with an offer to review the drug builds trust. The refusal is reported to the RN so the team can adjust the plan. Hiding the tablet in juice is covert administration without consent, and threatening an injection is coercive. Not telling the RN leaves the team unaware."}, {"id": 979, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "While still in the emergency department, Dev is pacing, shouting, and clenching his fists in the hallway. He is breathing fast and says staff are watching him. No one has been struck. The nurse is with a colleague. Which approach is most likely to reduce his agitation?", "options": {"A": "Ask several detailed questions about his delusions to understand what frightens him", "B": "Move toward him with colleagues positioned around him to show staff are in control", "C": "Stand close and use a firm voice to tell him which behaviours will not be tolerated", "D": "Stay at a safe distance with open posture, speak slowly, and offer a quieter space"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Probing delusions overloads an agitated client and feeds suspicion", "B": "A show of force can be perceived as an attack by a paranoid client", "C": "Crowding and confronting raises threat and agitation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C118", "cluster_text": "Dev Malhotra, 29, has schizophrenia and lives alone. He often misses his daily oral antipsychotic tablets, saying he forgets or dislikes the drowsiness. Over several weeks he becomes more suspicious and withdrawn. His family brings him to the emergency department, and he is later admitted to an inpatient mental health unit where the practical nurse helps care for him.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stay at a safe distance with open posture, speak slowly, and offer a quieter space): A calm approach with personal space, a slow quiet voice, and a quieter setting reduces stimulation and helps a frightened, paranoid client feel less threatened. Standing close, surrounding him, or questioning his beliefs increases pressure and the risk of escalation. The nurse keeps an exit route and a colleague nearby. De-escalation starts with lowering the threat."}, {"id": 980, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the inpatient unit the next afternoon, Dev is escalating again, raising his voice and slamming a door. He has not harmed anyone, and staff have already spoken with him calmly twice today. The team wants to avoid seclusion or restraint. What principle applies before any restriction is considered?", "options": {"A": "Ask the RN to approve restraint if talking fails twice, then record the reason afterward.", "B": "Apply the unit's standard seclusion protocol when a client raises his voice or slams doors.", "C": "Use the least restrictive measure that keeps everyone safe, trying verbal and environmental options first", "D": "Apply physical restraint early to prevent injury, then try verbal strategies afterward"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Sets a fixed number of attempts before restraint", "B": "Applies a uniform seclusion rule to any raised voice", "D": "Skipping less restrictive options and moving straight to restraint"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C118", "cluster_text": "Dev Malhotra, 29, has schizophrenia and lives alone. He often misses his daily oral antipsychotic tablets, saying he forgets or dislikes the drowsiness. Over several weeks he becomes more suspicious and withdrawn. His family brings him to the emergency department, and he is later admitted to an inpatient mental health unit where the practical nurse helps care for him.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use the least restrictive measure that keeps everyone safe, trying verbal and environmental options first): The least restrictive principle requires trying verbal de-escalation, reduced stimulation, and offered choices before seclusion or restraint, which are last resorts. A set count of failed attempts or a uniform rule ignores Dev's individual needs, and early restraint skips less restrictive steps. Any restriction is also reported and documented under policy."}, {"id": 981, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 62-year-old woman on a step-down unit has reduced consciousness after a stroke and cannot protect her airway reliably. She is lying flat while the nurse prepares mouth care with a cup of water. Her gag reflex is weak. What is the safest way to give oral care?", "options": {"A": "Postpone oral care until she is fully awake, since her mouth is closed and inactive", "B": "Use a firm toothbrush with a full cup of mouthwash to clean quickly", "C": "Keep her flat and pour small amounts of water to rinse her mouth after brushing", "D": "Turn her to her side and use a damp swab with little fluid, suctioning if needed"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Omitting care allows plaque and bacteria to build up in the mouth", "B": "Using a large fluid volume and a firm brush adds aspiration and gum trauma risk", "C": "Fluid pooling in a flat, unprotected airway risks aspiration"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Turn her to her side and use a damp swab with little fluid, suctioning if needed): With a weak gag reflex, fluid can run into the airway and cause aspiration, so she is turned to her side and cleaned with a damp swab or small brush and little liquid. Suction is available if secretions pool. Skipping care allows bacteria to grow and raises the risk of pneumonia, and large volumes while lying flat are unsafe. Position and fluid control come first."}, {"id": 982, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A nurse tells a student that privacy and confidentiality mean the same thing in a hospital. A 58-year-old client later asks why staff close the curtain during an exam and why her results are shared only with her care team. Which statement correctly separates the two terms?", "options": {"A": "Privacy is a right to control access, while confidentiality is the duty to protect shared information", "B": "Privacy applies only to physical examinations, while confidentiality applies only to written records and charts", "C": "Privacy and confidentiality both protect the record, but privacy ends at discharge.", "D": "Confidentiality covers anything said aloud, while privacy covers only written or electronic records."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Limiting each term to a single setting or format", "C": "Limits privacy to the period of admission", "D": "Splits the terms by spoken versus written information"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Privacy is a right to control access, while confidentiality is the duty to protect shared information): Privacy is the client's right to control who has access to her body, space, and information, and confidentiality is the professional duty to protect information that has been shared in the care relationship. The curtain illustrates privacy, and limiting results to the care team illustrates confidentiality. The other statements restrict each term to one format or time. Knowing the difference helps nurses explain their actions."}, {"id": 983, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 22-year-old woman tells the clinic nurse she uses her blue salbutamol inhaler most mornings and twice each night for cough and wheeze. She takes her brown inhaled corticosteroid only when she feels tight. She woke twice this week at night. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "She needs a higher reliever dose, so the controller can safely be reduced to monthly use", "B": "Her asthma is well managed because the reliever works quickly each time it is used", "C": "Her asthma is poorly controlled, so the controller must be taken daily as prescribed", "D": "Night wakings are normal for young adults, so no change to her inhaler routine is needed"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Increasing the reliever while reducing the preventive drug", "B": "Judging control by quick reliever effect rather than frequency of use", "D": "Normalizing night symptoms that signal poor control"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Her asthma is poorly controlled, so the controller must be taken daily as prescribed): Frequent reliever use and night wakings are signs of poor control, and the controller works only when taken daily to reduce airway inflammation. Using it only when tight misses its purpose. Reliever effectiveness does not show good control, and cutting the controller would worsen inflammation. The nurse reports the pattern so the team can review the plan and technique."}, {"id": 984, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A unit assistant asks a practical nurse whether privacy laws apply at the clinic where they work, since it is a small community clinic and not a hospital. The assistant also wonders if paper charts count. Which statement about health information privacy in Canada is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Privacy law covers hospitals only, so small clinics set their own rules about health records", "B": "Privacy law applies only to physicians, so nurses follow it only when directed", "C": "Privacy law protects health information in many settings, so she follows it and the clinic policies", "D": "Privacy law protects only electronic records, so paper charts need no particular safeguards"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Limiting privacy duties to hospital settings", "B": "Believing privacy duties belong only to physicians", "D": "Assuming paper records are outside legal protection"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Privacy law protects health information in many settings, so she follows it and the clinic policies): Health privacy legislation applies to health information held by many types of care providers and organizations, including community clinics, and nurses are accountable for following it alongside employer policy. Legislation differs in detail between provinces, so the nurse follows the rules and standards of the regulatory body. The other statements wrongly limit coverage by setting, record format, or profession. Privacy duties follow the information, not the building."}, {"id": 985, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Tariq is alone with the unit phone when the physician gives the heparin order. The dose is about double what Tariq has seen for similar clients, and the physician sounds rushed. The physician has twenty years of experience. What should Tariq do before anyone acts on the order?", "options": {"A": "Transcribe the order and ask the charge nurse to decide without contacting the physician again", "B": "Accept the order given the physician's experience, then monitor the client closely after the dose", "C": "Hold the order silently and wait for the pharmacist to call with a correction", "D": "Voice the concern respectfully, read the order back, and ask the physician to confirm the dose"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Passing the decision on without clarifying with the prescriber", "B": "Deferring to seniority instead of speaking up about a safety concern", "C": "Taking no action and relying on another person to find the error"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C109", "cluster_text": "Tariq, a practical nurse, works nights on an acute care medical unit and cares for Mr. Okafor, 65, who has atrial fibrillation. A physician telephones an order for a heparin dose that looks much higher than the usual protocol. Tariq is unsure, and the unit is busy. The dose is given by another nurse, and Mr. Okafor later develops heavy bleeding and becomes weak.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Voice the concern respectfully, read the order back, and ask the physician to confirm the dose): A dose that looks twice the usual amount is a safety concern, and the nurse must clarify it with the prescriber before it is given. Reading the order back and asking for confirmation is a respectful, structured way to speak up. Accepting it because of seniority puts the client at risk, and monitoring afterward does not prevent harm. Waiting silently for a pharmacist leaves the responsibility unmet."}, {"id": 986, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After the telephoned heparin dose, Mr. Okafor develops heavy bleeding, becomes weak, and needs transfer to intensive care. A student nurse on the unit asks whether this counts as a critical incident. Which description best defines a critical incident in health care?", "options": {"A": "An event that is reported to a regulator or leads to a legal claim.", "B": "Any event, however minor, that the nurse believes could have been done differently", "C": "An event that results in a disciplinary hearing for one of the nurses involved.", "D": "An unexpected event that results in serious harm or significant risk of it, requiring review"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Defines it by external reporting or legal action", "B": "Treating every minor event as critical regardless of harm", "C": "Defines it by discipline of an individual nurse"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C109", "cluster_text": "Tariq, a practical nurse, works nights on an acute care medical unit and cares for Mr. Okafor, 65, who has atrial fibrillation. A physician telephones an order for a heparin dose that looks much higher than the usual protocol. Tariq is unsure, and the unit is busy. The dose is given by another nurse, and Mr. Okafor later develops heavy bleeding and becomes weak.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An unexpected event that results in serious harm or significant risk of it, requiring review): A critical incident is an unexpected event that causes serious harm or carries a significant risk of it, and it calls for review and learning. Mr. Okafor's heavy bleeding meets that definition. It does not depend on a legal claim or a disciplinary hearing, and harm short of death still counts. Minor events are reported but are not critical."}, {"id": 987, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor is bleeding, drowsy, and his blood pressure is 88/50. Tariq realizes the order was probably wrong just as an incident form is available at the desk. The charge nurse is on another call. What is Tariq's first priority?", "options": {"A": "Contact the manager to decide who is responsible before calling for help", "B": "Stay with Mr. Okafor, assess him, and get urgent help for his condition before anything else", "C": "Complete the incident report first so that details are accurate while they are fresh", "D": "Finish the medication round and then report the error to the pharmacy department"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Seeking blame assignment before emergency care", "C": "Putting paperwork ahead of an unstable client", "D": "Delaying the response to finish routine tasks"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C109", "cluster_text": "Tariq, a practical nurse, works nights on an acute care medical unit and cares for Mr. Okafor, 65, who has atrial fibrillation. A physician telephones an order for a heparin dose that looks much higher than the usual protocol. Tariq is unsure, and the unit is busy. The dose is given by another nurse, and Mr. Okafor later develops heavy bleeding and becomes weak.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stay with Mr. Okafor, assess him, and get urgent help for his condition before anything else): A bleeding client with low blood pressure needs immediate assessment and help, so client safety comes before documentation or reporting. The incident report is completed afterward, as soon as the client is stable. Finishing the round or deciding responsibility delays care. After an incident, protect the client first, then report."}, {"id": 988, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor is stabilized in intensive care and his adult daughter arrives asking only about his bleeding and recovery. The team agrees the dose was an error that harmed him. The attending physician is on the way. What is the practical nurse's best action regarding disclosure?", "options": {"A": "Leave disclosure to the hospital's lawyers until the review has finished", "B": "Explain the error fully to her now, since she is waiting and anxious", "C": "Wait to see whether the daughter asks about the cause, then answer her honestly", "D": "Tell the RN that family disclosure is due, and help the team arrange it"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying disclosure until legal processes conclude", "B": "Disclosing alone without the team and facts being confirmed", "C": "Disclosing only if asked, delaying a duty to inform"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C109", "cluster_text": "Tariq, a practical nurse, works nights on an acute care medical unit and cares for Mr. Okafor, 65, who has atrial fibrillation. A physician telephones an order for a heparin dose that looks much higher than the usual protocol. Tariq is unsure, and the unit is busy. The dose is given by another nurse, and Mr. Okafor later develops heavy bleeding and becomes weak.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tell the RN that family disclosure is due, and help the team arrange it): Clients and families are entitled to timely disclosure of harm, and it is not left until someone asks. The practical nurse alerts the RN and supports the team in arranging a planned conversation led by the appropriate person. Giving a full explanation alone risks inaccurate or incomplete information, and waiting for legal review postpones the duty. Disclosure is prompt, honest, and coordinated."}, {"id": 989, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "The attending physician and charge nurse meet Mr. Okafor and his daughter to disclose the heparin dosing error that caused his bleeding. Tariq attends the meeting. The physician begins by describing the facts of what happened and the harm that followed. Which element should the conversation also include to meet disclosure standards?", "options": {"A": "A statement that the physician cannot discuss the cause until the review has finished.", "B": "A summary of the unit's staffing levels on the night so the family understands.", "C": "Avoiding the word sorry so the statement cannot be read as admitting fault", "D": "An expression of regret, what is being done for his care, and steps to prevent recurrence"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays disclosure until after a review", "B": "Offers staffing detail in place of an expression of regret", "C": "Withholding an apology because of fear of legal consequences"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C109", "cluster_text": "Tariq, a practical nurse, works nights on an acute care medical unit and cares for Mr. Okafor, 65, who has atrial fibrillation. A physician telephones an order for a heparin dose that looks much higher than the usual protocol. Tariq is unsure, and the unit is busy. The dose is given by another nurse, and Mr. Okafor later develops heavy bleeding and becomes weak.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An expression of regret, what is being done for his care, and steps to prevent recurrence): A good disclosure includes the facts, an expression of regret, the current care plan, and what will be done to prevent a repeat. Many provinces protect apologies from being used as proof of fault, so avoiding the word sorry is not required. Delaying disclosure until a review ends withholds information, and staffing detail does not replace regret. Clear, empathetic, and forward-looking communication builds trust."}, {"id": 990, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 24-year-old attends a community clinic with six weeks of bloody diarrhea, urgency, and cramping in the lower left abdomen. Stools are frequent but small. The nurse reviews features that separate ulcerative colitis from Crohn disease. Which feature supports ulcerative colitis?", "options": {"A": "Continuous inflammation starting in the rectum, often with bloody stools and urgency", "B": "Deep inflammation through all bowel layers causing right lower quadrant pain and weight loss", "C": "Patchy inflammation with healthy bowel between affected areas, often with fistulas", "D": "Disease most often in the terminal ileum with thickened bowel wall and strictures"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Describing transmural inflammation, typical of Crohn disease", "C": "Describing skip lesions, which belong to Crohn disease", "D": "Describing ileal disease and strictures, typical of Crohn disease"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continuous inflammation starting in the rectum, often with bloody stools and urgency): Ulcerative colitis begins in the rectum and spreads continuously upward, producing bloody stools, urgency, and small frequent stools like this client's. Skip lesions, fistulas, ileal disease, strictures, and full-thickness inflammation describe Crohn disease. The pattern and location of disease are the key distinguishing features. The nurse reports the findings for assessment and diagnosis."}, {"id": 991, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 35-year-old man in rehabilitation has a T10 spinal cord injury and no sensation of rectal fullness. He has had no stool for two days and says he feels fine, so he wants to skip tonight's scheduled bowel program. What should the nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Replace the program with an extra laxative dose taken only when symptoms appear", "B": "Skip tonight and resume once he feels fullness or abdominal discomfort", "C": "Continue the program on schedule, because he cannot rely on sensation to signal fullness", "D": "Double the fluids tomorrow and delay the program until the third day"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Substituting symptom-based laxative use for a routine", "B": "Waiting for sensation that the injury has removed", "D": "Delaying the program and relying on fluids alone"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue the program on schedule, because he cannot rely on sensation to signal fullness): After a spinal cord injury he may not feel fullness, so a consistent timed bowel program prevents impaction and constipation complications. Waiting for symptoms or using laxatives only when symptoms appear delays emptying. Extra fluids support bowel function but do not replace the routine. Regular timing is the key to success."}, {"id": 992, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mrs. Brandt, 88, lives in a long-term care home and has early cognitive changes. A nurse enters for the morning bath, pulls back the blanket, and begins washing her arm without speaking. Mrs. Brandt pulls away and looks frightened. What should the nurse do next?", "options": {"A": "Continue the bath but speak softly and explain each step.", "B": "Stop, greet her, explain the care, and ask permission before touching her", "C": "Ask her daughter by phone to approve the bath before any further contact", "D": "Stop, wait until her daughter arrives, and delay care until consent."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Explains steps but never asks permission to touch", "C": "Replacing the client's own consent with a family call", "D": "Delays care for family consent instead of seeking hers"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop, greet her, explain the care, and ask permission before touching her): Touch without warning can frighten a person, especially someone with cognitive changes, and consent is sought each time through greeting, explanation, and asking. Stopping and explaining respects her dignity and cultural safety. Explaining while continuing ignores her signals. Waiting for her daughter does not replace her own response and leaves care undone."}, {"id": 993, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During a busy shift, a colleague with ten years on the unit raises her voice at a practical nurse about a missed handover and points a finger. Other staff turn to watch. The nurse feels her face flush and knows two clients nearby can hear the exchange. Which response best de-escalates the situation?", "options": {"A": "Stay calm, speak quietly, listen, and suggest discussing the concern privately once the unit is settled", "B": "Walk away silently and file a formal complaint before any conversation takes place", "C": "Ask her to lower her voice immediately in front of the clients and staff.", "D": "Apologize for the missed handover, then report the colleague to the manager the same day."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Avoiding conversation and moving directly to formal action", "C": "Corrects the colleague publicly", "D": "Apologizes and reports without speaking to her first"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stay calm, speak quietly, listen, and suggest discussing the concern privately once the unit is settled): A calm, quiet voice, active listening, and an offer to talk privately lower tension and keep the conflict out of public view. Correcting her in front of others escalates it. Reporting the same day skips the chance to resolve the concern, though serious or repeated behaviour is escalated under policy. Respectful tone usually settles disagreements fastest."}, {"id": 994, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 60-year-old man in acute care is due for intravenous antibiotics. The nurse finds his cannula site swollen, cool, and tender, though the infusion pump shows no alarms. He says his arm feels tight. What is the nurse's best next action?", "options": {"A": "Flush the cannula with saline first and give the dose if it flushes easily", "B": "Elevate the arm and apply a warm cloth, then give the dose at the scheduled time", "C": "Give the dose slowly, since the pump has detected no resistance or alarm", "D": "Withhold the dose, stop the infusion, assess the site, and report to the RN promptly"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a flush to test a site that already shows infiltration signs", "B": "Treating the site and still giving a drug into it", "C": "Relying on the pump instead of the client's site assessment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold the dose, stop the infusion, assess the site, and report to the RN promptly): A swollen, cool, tender site with a tight arm suggests infiltration, and giving an antibiotic into it can injure tissue and fail to reach the bloodstream. Pumps often do not alarm for early infiltration, so the nurse trusts the assessment. The infusion is stopped, the site is assessed, and the RN is told so a new access can be arranged. Check the site every time before giving a drug."}, {"id": 995, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Greco returned from abdominal surgery this morning. Leah's assessment shows his heart rate at 118, blood pressure 92/54, and a firm tender abdomen. The RN is busy. Leah's unit uses SBAR to communicate concerns. Which message best fits the format when Leah reaches the RN?", "options": {"A": "Mr. Greco's pulse is 118; I will recheck him in thirty minutes.", "B": "Mr. Greco had surgery two days ago and is drowsy; the physician is away.", "C": "He seems a bit different tonight; I will chart the findings and recheck in four hours", "D": "He is deteriorating after surgery, pulse 118, pressure 92/54, abdomen firm; please assess him now"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives data but defers assessment instead of requesting it", "B": "Gives background only and no findings or request", "C": "Understating a deteriorating client and delaying reassessment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C110", "cluster_text": "Leah, a practical nurse, works nights on a surgical unit and cares for Mr. Greco, 58, two days after bowel surgery. He is usually alert, but tonight he seems drowsy, with a rising heart rate and a falling blood pressure. His abdomen is firmer than at the start of the shift. The RN is helping in another room, and the physician is off site.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (He is deteriorating after surgery, pulse 118, pressure 92/54, abdomen firm; please assess him now): SBAR presents the situation, background, assessment, and a clear request, and a deteriorating client needs an immediate request for assessment. Leah's message gives the current findings and the action needed. Reporting data while planning a later recheck delays escalation. Background alone has no findings or request."}, {"id": 996, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Leah reports to the RN, who says she will check soon but does not come for twenty minutes. Mr. Greco's pressure is now 86/50 and he is more drowsy. The unit policy describes a chain of command. What should Leah do next?", "options": {"A": "Escalate through the chain of command to the charge nurse, stating the concern is still unresolved", "B": "Accept the RN's reassurance and repeat vital signs at the next scheduled interval", "C": "Call the physician off site directly instead of using the unit chain of command.", "D": "Page the RN again, reposition him flat, and recheck his pressure in fifteen minutes."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Dropping the concern despite continued deterioration", "C": "Bypasses the chain of command set by unit policy", "D": "Repeats the same page and delays escalation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C110", "cluster_text": "Leah, a practical nurse, works nights on a surgical unit and cares for Mr. Greco, 58, two days after bowel surgery. He is usually alert, but tonight he seems drowsy, with a rising heart rate and a falling blood pressure. His abdomen is firmer than at the start of the shift. The RN is helping in another room, and the physician is off site.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Escalate through the chain of command to the charge nurse, stating the concern is still unresolved): When a serious concern is not addressed, the nurse moves up the chain of command so someone with authority responds. Leah states the findings and that the RN has not yet assessed him. Calling the physician directly bypasses the policy path, and paging the same RN again delays help. Accepting the delay allows him to worsen."}, {"id": 997, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "The charge nurse arrives and begins care. Mr. Greco, now clearer, says he is unsure he understood the plan for a return to surgery, and that he is frightened. Leah is asked why she should raise this with the team. Which statement best describes the nurse's advocacy role?", "options": {"A": "Advocacy is shared with the physician, who speaks for the client in all care decisions.", "B": "Advocacy applies only when a client has no family present to speak for them", "C": "Advocacy means speaking up for the client's needs and wishes, and it is every nurse's duty", "D": "Advocacy means telling the client what the team has decided and encouraging him to agree."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assigns advocacy to the physician", "B": "Limiting advocacy to clients without family", "D": "Treats advocacy as persuading the client to accept the plan"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C110", "cluster_text": "Leah, a practical nurse, works nights on a surgical unit and cares for Mr. Greco, 58, two days after bowel surgery. He is usually alert, but tonight he seems drowsy, with a rising heart rate and a falling blood pressure. His abdomen is firmer than at the start of the shift. The RN is helping in another room, and the physician is off site.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advocacy means speaking up for the client's needs and wishes, and it is every nurse's duty): Advocacy means supporting the client's right to understand and decide by speaking up about unmet needs and concerns, and every nurse has this duty. Mr. Greco's uncertainty about the surgical plan should be raised so the team can clarify consent. Leaving it to the physician or persuading him removes his voice, and family presence does not end the nurse's duty."}, {"id": 998, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After stabilization, Mr. Greco's wound shows spreading redness at one drain site. Leah is unsure what the finding means and asks the RN to review it. Later the team requests a wound specialist to take over wound care. Which pair of terms correctly names these steps?", "options": {"A": "Asking the RN is referral, and a specialist taking over the wound care is consultation", "B": "Both are consultation, since only physicians can accept responsibility for a transfer", "C": "Advice from the RN is consultation, and handing wound care to a specialist is referral", "D": "Both steps are referrals, since each involves another professional in the care plan"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reversing the meaning of the two terms", "B": "Believing responsibility transfer is consultation only", "D": "Treating consultation and referral as the same step"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C110", "cluster_text": "Leah, a practical nurse, works nights on a surgical unit and cares for Mr. Greco, 58, two days after bowel surgery. He is usually alert, but tonight he seems drowsy, with a rising heart rate and a falling blood pressure. His abdomen is firmer than at the start of the shift. The RN is helping in another room, and the physician is off site.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advice from the RN is consultation, and handing wound care to a specialist is referral): Consultation is seeking advice or input while keeping responsibility for the care, and referral is handing over responsibility for part of care to another provider. Leah's request to the RN is a consultation, and the specialist taking over wound care is a referral. The other options merge or reverse the terms. Knowing the difference clarifies who is accountable."}, {"id": 999, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Leah gave the prescribed analgesic and monitored Mr. Greco's vital signs hourly before his transfer. At the end of the shift she is busy and considers leaving her notes for the next morning because the care was clearly delivered. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Document the next morning, after the full details have been recalled together with colleagues", "B": "Document the assessments, medication, reports to the RN, and his response before leaving", "C": "Leave the notes for the day nurse, who will read the handover and can summarize it", "D": "Write only that he was stable, since the details are already known to the whole team"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Documenting from shared memory instead of at the time of care", "C": "Delaying documentation and assuming others will complete it", "D": "Recording a vague summary that omits assessments and actions"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C110", "cluster_text": "Leah, a practical nurse, works nights on a surgical unit and cares for Mr. Greco, 58, two days after bowel surgery. He is usually alert, but tonight he seems drowsy, with a rising heart rate and a falling blood pressure. His abdomen is firmer than at the start of the shift. The RN is helping in another room, and the physician is off site.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Document the assessments, medication, reports to the RN, and his response before leaving): Documentation is the legal proof of care, and an undocumented action may be viewed as not done. Leah records her assessments, the analgesic, her report to the RN, and his response before leaving. Asking someone else to summarize or writing a vague note loses accurate detail, and waiting until the next day weakens reliability. Chart your own care, promptly and accurately."}, {"id": 1000, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An 86-year-old woman in long-term care has had a fever of 38.6 C for two days and is eating little. Today she is drowsy, her mouth is dry, and her skin tents when pinched. Laboratory results show sodium 154 mmol/L. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Offer salty broth and snacks to improve her appetite and chart the amounts", "B": "Wait for the fever to resolve, since sodium will correct itself without a change", "C": "Report to the RN and offer oral fluids as ordered while monitoring intake and mental status", "D": "Restrict her fluids further and recheck the sodium level at the next routine blood work"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving more sodium when the sodium level is already high", "B": "Waiting passively without reporting an abnormal value", "D": "Restricting water when the client is already dehydrated"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report to the RN and offer oral fluids as ordered while monitoring intake and mental status): A high sodium with dry mucous membranes and skin tenting points to water loss from fever and poor intake, so she needs fluid support and the RN needs to know. Restricting fluids or adding salty foods raises sodium further, and waiting delays treatment. The nurse monitors intake, output, and level of consciousness. Older adults with fever dehydrate quickly."}, {"id": 1001, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 65-year-old man with hypertension attends a community clinic for a routine follow-up visit. Results show urea 7.2 mmol/L, creatinine 160 umol/L, and an eGFR of 38 mL/min. He asks whether urea alone shows that his kidneys are fine. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Urea is the best marker of kidney function, so his kidneys are working well", "B": "The eGFR of 38 is normal for his age, so no follow-up is needed", "C": "The creatinine rise reflects muscle use only, so repeat testing is not needed", "D": "Creatinine and eGFR indicate reduced kidney function that needs follow-up by the NP or physician"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on urea, which is affected by diet and hydration", "B": "Accepting a low eGFR as normal ageing", "C": "Dismissing a raised creatinine as muscle effect alone"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Creatinine and eGFR indicate reduced kidney function that needs follow-up by the NP or physician): A creatinine of 160 umol/L with an eGFR of 38 mL/min shows moderately reduced filtration, and eGFR is a better guide than urea, which varies with diet and hydration. The result is reported for assessment by the NP or physician, particularly with hypertension. Age lowers eGFR somewhat but not to this degree, and muscle use does not explain this pattern. Use creatinine and eGFR together."}, {"id": 1002, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A school nurse is told that a 9-year-old girl with asthma used her salbutamol inhaler almost every day this week, usually after recess and once during class. She is smiling and playing now. Her parent says that is just how asthma is. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Move her to quiet activities every recess until her next scheduled appointment", "B": "Limit her to two reliever uses weekly so the inhaler lasts longer", "C": "Tell her parent and provider that frequent reliever use signals poor control and needs review", "D": "Record the use and take no further action, since she recovers quickly each time"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Restricting activity instead of improving control", "B": "Restricting a rescue medicine, which is unsafe", "D": "Normalizing frequent reliever use as expected asthma"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tell her parent and provider that frequent reliever use signals poor control and needs review): Needing a reliever almost daily shows that her asthma is not well controlled, and her care plan or controller medicine may need adjustment. The nurse communicates the pattern to the parent and provider rather than accepting it as normal. Limiting reliever access is unsafe, and restricting activity does not treat the cause. Good control means little need for the reliever."}, {"id": 1003, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A nurse on a cardiac unit teaches a 74-year-old woman with heart failure to weigh herself daily and call if she gains 2 kg in two days. The client nods and says she understands. Which approach best checks that the teaching worked?", "options": {"A": "Ask her to describe in her own words when she will weigh herself and call", "B": "Repeat the instructions more slowly and ask her to nod again if clear", "C": "Give her the printed handout and note that education was completed", "D": "Ask whether she has any questions about the teaching before leaving the room"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Repeating information without checking recall", "C": "Treating a handout as proof of learning", "D": "Using a yes or no question that hides gaps in understanding"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask her to describe in her own words when she will weigh herself and call): Teach-back asks the client to explain the plan in her own words, which shows whether the teaching was understood and lets the nurse fix gaps. A nod or a general question about questions does not show understanding, and a handout alone does not either. Documenting that she described the plan accurately is stronger evidence. Check understanding by asking for explanation."}, {"id": 1004, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Alvarez, 84, lives in long-term care and has full upper and lower dentures. At bedtime he says he usually rinses them and leaves them on the bedside table. The nurse is helping with bedtime care. What is the best way to care for his dentures?", "options": {"A": "Wrap them in a dry tissue to keep them from drying and cracking", "B": "Keep them in his mouth overnight to prevent them being lost or damaged", "C": "Soak them in hot water overnight with bleach to remove stains", "D": "Brush them clean after meals and store them overnight in a labelled container of cool water"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dry storage warps the dentures and loses them in waste", "B": "Overnight wear promotes gum irritation and infection", "C": "Hot water and bleach damage the denture material"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Brush them clean after meals and store them overnight in a labelled container of cool water): Dentures are brushed after meals and kept in cool water overnight so they hold their shape, while a labelled container prevents loss. Dry storage can distort them, hot water or bleach damages them, and wearing them overnight irritates the gums and promotes fungal infection. The nurse also examines his mouth and gums each day. Proper storage protects fit and comfort."}, {"id": 1005, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 59-year-old man on a medical ward grips his chest, saying crushing pain spreads to his left arm. He is pale and sweating, and his pulse is 112. The nurse is the only staff member nearby, and oxygen supplies are in another room. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Leave to collect oxygen and a pulse oximeter, then return to assess his chest pain", "B": "Walk him back to his bed so he can lie flat while vital signs are checked", "C": "Stay with him, keep him calm and resting, and call for the RN and emergency help", "D": "Give the acetaminophen ordered earlier for headaches, then recheck pain in fifteen minutes"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaving a symptomatic client alone to find supplies", "B": "Making a possible cardiac client exert himself", "D": "Giving an unrelated drug that does not treat cardiac pain"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stay with him, keep him calm and resting, and call for the RN and emergency help): Crushing chest pain spreading to the arm with pallor and sweating suggests a heart attack, so the nurse stays, keeps him resting, and summons help immediately using the call system. Leaving him, walking him, or giving an unrelated analgesic delays treatment or increases cardiac workload. Others bring equipment while the nurse monitors him. Stay with the client and call for help."}, {"id": 1006, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After a minor medication delay, a practical nurse wants to say sorry to a 70-year-old client but worries it will count as an admission of legal fault. The manager notes that apology legislation exists in many provinces. Which principle should guide the nurse?", "options": {"A": "An apology counts as a legal admission of fault, so the nurse avoids saying sorry", "B": "Many provinces limit using an apology as proof of fault, and organizational policy still guides", "C": "Apology legislation removes reporting duties, so a brief apology replaces the incident report", "D": "Apology legislation applies only to physicians, so nurses use a neutral statement of facts alone"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming every apology is a legal admission", "C": "Thinking an apology replaces the required incident report", "D": "Believing the protection covers only physicians"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Many provinces limit using an apology as proof of fault, and organizational policy still guides): Apology legislation in many provinces protects an expression of regret from being used as proof of fault, so the nurse can respond with honesty and empathy. Provinces differ in detail, so organizational policy and regulatory standards still guide what is said. The protection does not remove reporting duties, and it is not limited to one profession. Apologize sincerely, and still report."}, {"id": 1007, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A practical nurse finishes a hard night shift and posts on a personal social media page about a 6-year-old client's rare condition, leaving out the child's name and hospital. Several coworkers react with approval. A colleague asks whether this is acceptable. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "It is acceptable because it was written off duty as emotional support after a hard shift", "B": "Remove the post, since details can identify the child, and report the disclosure", "C": "It is acceptable, since no name, hospital or photo was included in the post", "D": "It is acceptable if the page is private and limited to personal friends"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Thinking off-duty timing removes professional duties", "C": "Believing removing the name removes the duty of confidentiality", "D": "Treating privacy settings as protection for client information"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Remove the post, since details can identify the child, and report the disclosure): A rare condition and details of an age and a shift can allow others to identify the child, so posting breaches confidentiality even without a name. A private page can still be copied or shared, and professional duties apply off duty. The nurse removes the post and reports the disclosure under policy. Never share client information on social media in any form."}, {"id": 1008, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A 72-year-old man has just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. A nurse has twenty minutes in clinic and many topics to cover: diet, glucose testing, foot care, medications, and exercise. He looks tired and keeps rubbing his eyes. What is the best way to plan the teaching?", "options": {"A": "Teach two or three key messages now, check understanding, and schedule follow-up for the rest", "B": "Teach the most complex topics first, since he will concentrate best at the start", "C": "Cover diet and medications today, with a handout on testing, foot care, and exercise.", "D": "Skip teaching today and mail written material, since he looks too tired to learn."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Starting with the hardest content when the client is already tired", "C": "Splits topics but overloads the visit and relies on a handout", "D": "Skips teaching and relies on mailed material"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Teach two or three key messages now, check understanding, and schedule follow-up for the rest): New learners retain limited information, especially when tired, so the nurse limits teaching to a few priority messages and confirms understanding. Follow-up visits cover the rest. Covering many topics with a handout overloads him, and mailing material skips the teaching and the check of understanding. Starting with the hardest topic also reduces learning."}, {"id": 1009, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A wound clinic nurse wants a photograph of a 70-year-old man's leg ulcer to track healing. The nurse's personal phone is in her pocket and the clinic tablet is at the other desk. The man says he is comfortable with photos. Which action best protects his privacy?", "options": {"A": "Confirm consent, use the clinic device, and save the image in his health record", "B": "Take the photo on the personal phone since he has agreed verbally to the photograph", "C": "Use the personal phone, then delete the photo after copying it into the chart note", "D": "Photograph quickly without his face and keep it on the phone for later comparison"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating verbal agreement as enough while using an unsecured device", "C": "Using a personal device that may back up images elsewhere", "D": "Believing removing the face removes identifiability and storage risk"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Confirm consent, use the clinic device, and save the image in his health record): Wound photos are part of the health record, so the nurse confirms consent, uses an approved clinic device, and stores the image only in the record. Personal phones may back up photos to private accounts, so deleting later does not remove the risk. Leaving out the face does not remove other identifying features. Use policy-approved tools every time."}, {"id": 1010, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 62-year-old man on a medical ward reports chest pressure. A sublingual nitroglycerin order is available. He mentions he took a tablet for erectile dysfunction this morning. His blood pressure is 118/72. What should the nurse do before giving the nitroglycerin?", "options": {"A": "Give half the dose and recheck his blood pressure after five minutes", "B": "Give the dose and place him upright to reduce a drop in pressure", "C": "Hold the dose and report the erectile dysfunction drug use to the RN or physician urgently", "D": "Give the dose, since his blood pressure is currently within a safe range"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reducing the dose instead of reporting the interaction", "B": "Relying on positioning to manage a severe hypotension risk", "D": "Using a current normal reading to ignore a dangerous interaction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hold the dose and report the erectile dysfunction drug use to the RN or physician urgently): Nitroglycerin combined with an erectile dysfunction drug can cause a severe drop in blood pressure, even if the pressure is normal now. The nurse holds the dose and reports so the prescriber can choose a safe plan. Halving the dose or relying on positioning does not remove the risk. Always ask about recent medication use before giving nitrates."}, {"id": 1011, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 50-year-old man takes acetaminophen daily for arthritis and drinks wine on weekends. Clinic results show ALT 210 U/L and AST 160 U/L, both above normal. He has no muscle pain. What does this pattern most likely suggest, and what should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Possible liver injury, so report the results and review his acetaminophen and alcohol use", "B": "Muscle injury from exercise, so advise rest and repeat the test in one year", "C": "Kidney injury, so restrict protein and check his urine output at home", "D": "Normal variation for his age, so provide reassurance without further review"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Attributing raised liver enzymes to muscle injury without symptoms", "C": "Confusing liver enzymes with kidney markers", "D": "Dismissing abnormal results as normal variation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Possible liver injury, so report the results and review his acetaminophen and alcohol use): ALT is more specific to the liver, and a rise with daily acetaminophen and regular alcohol suggests possible liver injury. The nurse reports the values and reviews his drug and alcohol use so the NP or physician can act. He has no muscle pain, so muscle injury is unlikely, and these enzymes do not measure kidney function. Raised enzymes are reported, not reassured away."}, {"id": 1012, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 50-year-old woman receiving chemotherapy has a sore mouth with red, raw gums, and her platelet count is low. Her husband brought a commercial mouthwash from the pharmacy. She can still swallow and eat soft food. Which mouth care instruction is best?", "options": {"A": "Brush firmly with a regular toothbrush to remove coating from the gums", "B": "Rinse often with a salt and baking soda solution, and brush gently with an extra-soft brush", "C": "Avoid brushing until the sores heal and use lemon glycerin swabs", "D": "Use the commercial mouthwash after meals to reduce bacteria and soreness"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using firm brushing that traumatizes fragile gums", "C": "Stopping brushing and using drying swabs", "D": "Using an irritating mouthwash on inflamed tissue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rinse often with a salt and baking soda solution, and brush gently with an extra-soft brush): Mucositis makes tissue fragile and painful, so gentle cleansing with a mild salt and baking soda rinse and an extra-soft brush reduces bacteria without irritation. Commercial mouthwashes can sting and dry the mouth, and lemon glycerin swabs also dry it. Firm brushing injures tissue and raises infection risk. Frequent gentle care is the priority."}, {"id": 1013, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An 88-year-old woman with dementia is two days after a hip fracture repair. She does not complain, but she grimaces during turns, guards her leg, and moans. Staff say she is quiet and has no pain. Which approach best assesses her pain?", "options": {"A": "Use a behavioural pain scale and observe her face, sounds, and movements, then report findings", "B": "Ask her family to rate pain from visits and use only that rating", "C": "Assume no pain as she does not complain, and reassess if she cries out", "D": "Use a numeric 0 to 10 scale and accept her answer if she says no"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relying on visiting family instead of direct observation", "C": "Interpreting silence as absence of pain", "D": "Using a self-report tool that she may not be able to use"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a behavioural pain scale and observe her face, sounds, and movements, then report findings): People with dementia often cannot report pain, so behavioural tools that score facial expression, vocal sounds, and body movement are used. Her grimacing, guarding, and moaning suggest pain despite her silence. Numeric scales rely on self-report, and waiting for crying delays relief. Family input helps but does not replace observation. Not complaining does not mean no pain."}, {"id": 1014, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 52-year-old man is two days after lumbar spinal surgery and needs repositioning to protect the incision and spine. He is lying on his back in bed, two nurses are at the bedside, and a pillow for between his knees is ready. What is the correct log rolling technique?", "options": {"A": "Turn his shoulders first and then his hips, using the draw sheet for support", "B": "Have him bend his knees and twist his trunk while the nurses guide", "C": "Lift the head while one nurse rotates the legs and the other holds the arms", "D": "Roll him as one unit, keeping the spine aligned, on a count given by one nurse"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Twisting the spine by turning segments separately", "B": "Allowing trunk rotation after spinal surgery", "C": "Moving body parts independently, which misaligns the spine"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Roll him as one unit, keeping the spine aligned, on a count given by one nurse): Log rolling keeps the head, shoulders, hips, and legs moving together so the spine does not twist, and one nurse gives the count so both move at the same time. Turning shoulders and hips separately or having him twist strains the surgical site. A pillow between the knees keeps the hips aligned. Move the spine as one unit."}, {"id": 1015, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During a shift on a medical unit, a practical nurse has several tasks: a call bell from a client with mild nausea, a scheduled dressing change, a client teaching session, and a family question about discharge. A client's IV pump begins alarming. Which plan best balances urgent and important work?", "options": {"A": "Answer the call bell and family question first because they will be quickest", "B": "Respond to the alarm first, then schedule the dressing and teaching so neither is lost", "C": "Delay all tasks until the RN assigns an order for each of them", "D": "Complete the dressing change first since it was scheduled and is time-sensitive"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing by speed instead of urgency", "C": "Waiting for direction instead of using own prioritization", "D": "Prioritizing a scheduled task over a possible safety alarm"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Respond to the alarm first, then schedule the dressing and teaching so neither is lost): An IV alarm may signal a safety problem, so it is dealt with first, but important tasks such as teaching and dressings must still be planned or they never happen. Choosing the quickest or the scheduled task ignores urgency, and waiting for an assigned order wastes time. Prioritize by safety, then plan the important work. Urgent tasks come first, but they should not be the only tasks done."}, {"id": 1016, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 34-year-old man is in the recovery unit after general anesthesia for knee surgery. His temperature has risen from 37.2 C to 39.4 C, his jaw and arms feel rigid, and his heart rate is 138. He is sweating. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Apply a cool cloth and give an acetaminophen dose as ordered for the fever", "B": "Call for the RN and anesthesia team immediately, because this pattern needs urgent emergency treatment", "C": "Remove blankets, encourage fluids, and recheck his temperature in fifteen minutes before calling the RN.", "D": "Give the ordered antiemetic and apply ice packs before reporting the rigidity to the RN."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating as ordinary fever with an antipyretic", "C": "Delays calling for help while rechecking", "D": "Gives an unrelated drug and delays reporting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Call for the RN and anesthesia team immediately, because this pattern needs urgent emergency treatment): A sudden high temperature with muscle rigidity and a fast pulse after anesthesia is the pattern of malignant hyperthermia, a life-threatening emergency that needs immediate team response and specific treatment. Delaying the call to recheck or treat other symptoms loses time. Antipyretics and blankets delay care or worsen the condition. Call for help first."}, {"id": 1017, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A long-term care nurse finds that a resident's morning medication was not given, and the resident has not been harmed. A colleague says incident reports are used only for complaints and discipline, so none is needed here. What is the best action for the nurse to take?", "options": {"A": "Mention the omission at the next team huddle without completing an incident report.", "B": "Inform the colleague only, since a report is needed only when harm occurs", "C": "Report the omission to the resident's family so they can decide whether to formalise it.", "D": "Report the omission through the incident reporting system so the team can learn and prevent repeats"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Raises it verbally and skips the official report", "B": "Limiting reports to events with harm", "C": "Leaves reporting to the family"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the omission through the incident reporting system so the team can learn and prevent repeats): Incident reports record errors and near misses, including omissions without harm, so the organization can find causes and improve systems. A verbal mention is not a record. Leaving reporting to the family misplaces the nurse's duty. Reporting only after harm or a complaint misses chances to prevent problems. Report all errors and near misses."}, {"id": 1018, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 16-year-old arrives in the emergency department sitting forward, using accessory muscles, and speaking two words at a time. Oxygen saturation is 88 percent. His wheeze has become faint, and his mother says he is quieter now. What does this indicate, and what is the priority?", "options": {"A": "Mild airway narrowing, so a repeat reliever dose in thirty minutes is enough", "B": "Severe airflow limitation with danger of respiratory failure, so call the RN or physician urgently", "C": "Improving asthma with fewer wheezes, so continue observing and repeat saturation at the next routine check", "D": "Anxiety settling after treatment, so encourage him to relax and breathe slowly"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Underestimating severity despite low saturation", "C": "Misreading a quiet chest as improvement", "D": "Attributing distress to anxiety"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Severe airflow limitation with danger of respiratory failure, so call the RN or physician urgently): A faint wheeze with severe distress and low oxygen saturation means very little air is moving, which is a sign of impending respiratory failure rather than improvement. The nurse calls for urgent help and stays with him. Observing, encouraging relaxation, or waiting for another reliever dose delays life-saving treatment. A quiet chest in severe asthma is a warning sign."}, {"id": 1019, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On evening rounds, Sam, a practical nurse, hears a unit clerk ask whether any staff member may read Ms. Reyes's chart because everyone works on the same floor. Ms. Reyes is recovering from surgery and has not discussed sharing her information with anyone. Which statement best describes the circle of care?", "options": {"A": "Her relatives and friends who visit often and help with her daily tasks", "B": "Anyone employed by the hospital who has signed a confidentiality agreement", "C": "Those directly providing her care who need the information to do so", "D": "All staff on the unit, since they work in the same physical area"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Including visitors who are not providing care", "B": "Defining it by employment and an agreement rather than need to know", "D": "Defining the circle by location instead of care role"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C121", "cluster_text": "Ms. Reyes, 46, is a client on a medical unit recovering from pneumonia. She has capacity and has said she wants her health information shared only with those caring for her. Her sister visits daily, and Sam, a practical nurse, cares for her on most shifts. Over several days staff, family, and callers ask about her care, and Ms. Reyes asks for a copy of her chart.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Those directly providing her care who need the information to do so): The circle of care is made up of people directly involved in providing care to the client who need the information to do so, and access is based on need to know. Working on the same floor or signing an agreement does not by itself create a care role. Visiting family are not part of the circle unless the client agrees to share information. Need to know, not location, sets access."}, {"id": 1020, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Another nurse on the floor says she recognizes Ms. Reyes as a neighbour and wants to look at her chart only to see how she is doing, without telling anyone. She is not assigned to Ms. Reyes. How should Sam interpret this request?", "options": {"A": "It is unauthorized access, since the nurse has no care role, even if nothing is shared", "B": "It is acceptable, since looking at the chart harms no one when no information is shared", "C": "It is acceptable if the nurse documents the chart access and her personal reason for looking", "D": "It is acceptable because they are colleagues and share the same professional duty"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believing viewing without sharing is harmless", "C": "Thinking documentation makes the access legitimate", "D": "Treating a shared profession as permission"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C121", "cluster_text": "Ms. Reyes, 46, is a client on a medical unit recovering from pneumonia. She has capacity and has said she wants her health information shared only with those caring for her. Her sister visits daily, and Sam, a practical nurse, cares for her on most shifts. Over several days staff, family, and callers ask about her care, and Ms. Reyes asks for a copy of her chart.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It is unauthorized access, since the nurse has no care role, even if nothing is shared): Accessing a record without a care purpose is a privacy breach, even when nothing is shared, because the client controls who sees her information. Curiosity or a personal connection does not create a care role. Documenting the access does not make it authorized. Sam kindly declines and reminds the colleague that records are for care only."}, {"id": 1021, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Ms. Reyes asks Sam for a copy of her chart so she can show it to her family doctor. Sam is unsure and does not want to refuse. The unit follows policy for record requests. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Say she may view her chart only in the presence of the physician.", "B": "Explain her right to access her records and direct the request through the unit process", "C": "Tell her copies are released only to another physician, so her doctor must request it.", "D": "Hand her the chart at the desk for review whenever she likes"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adds a physician presence condition to access", "C": "Limits release of copies to physician requests", "D": "Releasing the original chart outside the formal process"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C121", "cluster_text": "Ms. Reyes, 46, is a client on a medical unit recovering from pneumonia. She has capacity and has said she wants her health information shared only with those caring for her. Her sister visits daily, and Sam, a practical nurse, cares for her on most shifts. Over several days staff, family, and callers ask about her care, and Ms. Reyes asks for a copy of her chart.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain her right to access her records and direct the request through the unit process): Clients generally have a right to access their own health information, though the request goes through the facility's process so it is handled securely and correctly. Sam supports her and directs the request. Requiring a physician present or limiting copies to physician requests adds unjustified conditions. Handing over the working chart bypasses safeguards."}, {"id": 1022, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Ms. Reyes's sister, Marta, asks Sam in the hallway about the pneumonia result and the antibiotics. Marta says she is the closest family and has always handled the family's health matters. Ms. Reyes is awake and able to decide. What should Sam do?", "options": {"A": "Share only the antibiotic names and avoid the test result", "B": "Ask Marta to wait while Sam checks whether other family members agree", "C": "Share the general results, since close relatives are entitled to know about care", "D": "Say information is shared with Ms. Reyes's permission, then ask Ms. Reyes what she wishes shared"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Sharing part of the information without the client's consent", "B": "Seeking other relatives' agreement instead of the client's", "C": "Assuming family are always entitled to information"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C121", "cluster_text": "Ms. Reyes, 46, is a client on a medical unit recovering from pneumonia. She has capacity and has said she wants her health information shared only with those caring for her. Her sister visits daily, and Sam, a practical nurse, cares for her on most shifts. Over several days staff, family, and callers ask about her care, and Ms. Reyes asks for a copy of her chart.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Say information is shared with Ms. Reyes's permission, then ask Ms. Reyes what she wishes shared): A client with capacity decides who receives her information, and a close relationship does not override that. Sam asks Ms. Reyes what she wants shared, and then follows her wishes. Sharing even part of the details without her permission is a breach, and other relatives' views do not matter. Consent comes from the client."}, {"id": 1023, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "That evening a caller says she is Ms. Reyes's cousin and gives accurate details, including the birth date. She asks if Ms. Reyes is improving. Ms. Reyes has not named the cousin as someone to receive information. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Confirm only that she is on the unit and share her room number", "B": "Share a general update, since the caller knows accurate details about her", "C": "Ask the caller to describe her illness first, then confirm if correct", "D": "Decline to share details, and offer to pass a message to Ms. Reyes"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confirming presence and room, which is itself identifying", "B": "Treating accurate personal details as proof of authority", "C": "Using confirmation questioning that leaks information"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C121", "cluster_text": "Ms. Reyes, 46, is a client on a medical unit recovering from pneumonia. She has capacity and has said she wants her health information shared only with those caring for her. Her sister visits daily, and Sam, a practical nurse, cares for her on most shifts. Over several days staff, family, and callers ask about her care, and Ms. Reyes asks for a copy of her chart.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline to share details, and offer to pass a message to Ms. Reyes): The caller's identity and entitlement cannot be verified by phone, and Ms. Reyes has not agreed to share information with this person. Accurate details can be found by others, so they do not prove authority. Even confirming that she is on the unit or her room can breach privacy. Offering to pass on a message respects the client's wishes."}, {"id": 1024, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, a nurse learns that 7-year-old Mateo has asthma that worsens around the family cat, which sleeps on his bed. His parents love the pet and ask whether avoiding triggers matters when he takes his controller inhaler daily. What should the nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Rely on the controller inhaler and keep the cat, since medication makes avoiding triggers unnecessary", "B": "Give an extra reliever dose each evening before Mateo goes near the cat on the bed", "C": "Bathe the cat daily with gentle shampoo so that its dander no longer affects the home", "D": "Keep the cat out of his bedroom and wash his bedding weekly in hot water"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing daily medication replaces trigger control", "B": "Using a reliever as a preventive habit instead of removing exposure", "C": "Choosing a partial measure that leaves allergen in the sleeping area"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep the cat out of his bedroom and wash his bedding weekly in hot water): Reducing exposure to a known trigger lowers airway inflammation, so keeping the cat out of the bedroom and washing bedding in hot water supports the controller therapy. Relying on medication alone leaves Mateo exposed to the allergen that keeps provoking symptoms. Extra reliever doses treat symptoms rather than the cause and can hide poor control. Trigger control and medication work together."}, {"id": 1025, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the anticoagulant clinic, Mr. Dubois reports no bleeding, but his INR today is 5.2 and his target range is 2.0 to 3.0. He mentions a recent course of antibiotics and a week of poor appetite. What is the practical nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Instruct him to stop warfarin for one week and return afterward for another INR", "B": "Give oral vitamin K from the clinic supply to bring the INR down today", "C": "Report the result to the prescriber promptly and hold the next warfarin dose until advised", "D": "Reassure him that antibiotics explain the value and tell him to continue the usual dose"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stopping a drug for a set period without prescriber advice", "B": "Giving a reversal agent without an order, a scope error", "D": "Dismissing a supratherapeutic value as expected"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C128", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dubois, 76, has atrial fibrillation and a coronary stent placed last year. Over several months he receives different antithrombotic therapies managed through an anticoagulant clinic, a community pharmacy and a medical unit. A practical nurse works with the care team at each stage. He lives with his wife and takes a diuretic and a statin as well.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the result to the prescriber promptly and hold the next warfarin dose until advised): An INR of 5.2 is well above range, and antibiotics plus poor intake commonly raise it, so the prescriber must decide on dosing while the next dose is held. A week off warfarin without advice risks clotting and is not the nurse's decision. Vitamin K requires an order and may be unnecessary without bleeding. Report abnormal results and let the prescriber direct changes."}, {"id": 1026, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Dubois has been changed to apixaban. At a pharmacy visit he says his dentist booked a tooth extraction next week, and he plans to skip apixaban from tomorrow so he will not bleed. He asks the nurse whether that is wise. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Advise him to ask the prescriber how to time apixaban around the extraction, not skip doses", "B": "Tell him to cancel the extraction until his course of apixaban therapy has been completed", "C": "Agree that stopping three days ahead is safe because the drug leaves the body within hours", "D": "Suggest he take a double dose the evening before to protect against clots after the extraction"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delaying needed dental care instead of planning with the prescriber", "C": "Assuming a self-chosen stopping time is safe", "D": "Doubling a dose, which raises bleeding risk"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C128", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dubois, 76, has atrial fibrillation and a coronary stent placed last year. Over several months he receives different antithrombotic therapies managed through an anticoagulant clinic, a community pharmacy and a medical unit. A practical nurse works with the care team at each stage. He lives with his wife and takes a diuretic and a statin as well.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Advise him to ask the prescriber how to time apixaban around the extraction, not skip doses): Apixaban is stopped before procedures only on the prescriber's plan, which weighs his stroke risk against bleeding risk at the extraction site. Stopping early on his own could leave him unprotected from stroke. Doubling the dose increases bleeding without benefit. His stent and atrial fibrillation also make cancelling care unhelpful, so coordination between the dentist and prescriber is the safe path."}, {"id": 1027, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During an admission for pneumonia, Mr. Dubois receives a continuous heparin infusion. This morning he has two small nosebleeds, and his aPTT is above the target range. His blood pressure is stable and he feels well. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Apply direct pressure, then report the bleeding and the elevated aPTT to the RN immediately", "B": "Lay him flat with his head tilted back so blood does not drip from his nose", "C": "Apply direct pressure and chart the episode, then continue the infusion at the ordered rate", "D": "Give protamine sulfate from the medication room to reverse the heparin before telling anyone"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using a position that lets blood run down the throat", "C": "Treating bleeding locally without reporting the lab result", "D": "Giving a reversal drug without an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C128", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dubois, 76, has atrial fibrillation and a coronary stent placed last year. Over several months he receives different antithrombotic therapies managed through an anticoagulant clinic, a community pharmacy and a medical unit. A practical nurse works with the care team at each stage. He lives with his wife and takes a diuretic and a statin as well.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Apply direct pressure, then report the bleeding and the elevated aPTT to the RN immediately): Bleeding with an aPTT above range signals that the heparin may be too strong, so the RN must be told promptly while local pressure controls the nosebleed. Continuing the infusion unchanged ignores that signal. Protamine requires an order and is not a practical nurse decision. Tilting the head back sends blood into the throat and risks aspiration, so he should sit upright leaning slightly forward."}, {"id": 1028, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After discharge, Mr. Dubois takes ASA and clopidogrel for his stent. At a clinic visit he shows large bruises on both forearms and asks whether to stop both drugs until they fade. He has no black stools and no blood in his urine. What should the nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Stop clopidogrel but keep the ASA until the bruises fade, since one antiplatelet drug is enough", "B": "Stop both for several days and restart them once the bruises have faded", "C": "Continue both drugs unless the prescriber changes them, and report growing bruises, black stools or bleeding", "D": "Add daily ibuprofen for the soreness since it will also protect his stent"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stopping one stent drug on his own", "B": "Stopping both antiplatelets, risking stent thrombosis", "D": "Adding an NSAID that increases bleeding risk"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C128", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dubois, 76, has atrial fibrillation and a coronary stent placed last year. Over several months he receives different antithrombotic therapies managed through an anticoagulant clinic, a community pharmacy and a medical unit. A practical nurse works with the care team at each stage. He lives with his wife and takes a diuretic and a statin as well.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue both drugs unless the prescriber changes them, and report growing bruises, black stools or bleeding): Dual antiplatelet therapy protects a coronary stent from clotting, so he should not stop either drug unless the prescriber decides. Stopping one or both on his own raises the risk of stent thrombosis. Ibuprofen adds bleeding risk and does not protect the stent. Teaching him which signs of serious bleeding to report keeps him safe while he stays on therapy."}, {"id": 1029, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Priya's husband asks how to prepare powdered formula for the night feeds. Their supply is running low, so he wonders about adding extra water to each bottle to make the tin last longer. The baby is two days old and slightly below birth weight. What should the nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Mix powder and water exactly as the label states, since dilution can lower her baby's sodium", "B": "Boil the water for one minute, then mix the powder and keep it warm for hours.", "C": "Adding a little extra water is acceptable for newborns because their kidneys handle fluid well", "D": "Prepare a day of bottles and refrigerate them, warming each bottle in the microwave."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Keeps prepared formula warm for hours, allowing bacterial growth", "C": "Stretching supply by diluting, risking water intoxication", "D": "Warms bottles in a microwave, causing hot spots"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C126", "cluster_text": "Priya, 29, delivered a healthy term daughter vaginally two days ago and is cared for on a postpartum unit. She plans to breastfeed and to use powdered formula for some feeds once she returns to work. A practical nurse assesses and teaches her on the unit and again during a home visit later in the first week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mix powder and water exactly as the label states, since dilution can lower her baby's sodium): Formula must be mixed in the exact ratio on the label because extra water lowers sodium and nutrients and can cause seizures and poor weight gain in a newborn. Newborn kidneys cannot handle extra free water. Formula kept warm for hours allows bacterial growth, and microwaving creates hot spots that can burn. If supply is short, the family should seek help from the clinic or community resources."}, {"id": 1030, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On day 2 after her vaginal birth, Priya has a perineal repair and uterine cramping. She asks whether taking acetaminophen means she must stop breastfeeding, because a friend told her medications pass into milk and harm babies. What should the nurse say?", "options": {"A": "Acetaminophen at recommended doses is compatible with breastfeeding; check other medications with a pharmacist.", "B": "She may take over-the-counter products freely, since they do not reach the milk in meaningful amounts", "C": "She should pump and discard her milk for 24 hours after each acetaminophen dose", "D": "Medications require stopping breastfeeding until the baby is weaned, so ice packs are her only choice"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assuming all over-the-counter products are safe", "C": "Believing routine drugs require discarding milk", "D": "Overgeneralizing that all drugs stop breastfeeding"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C126", "cluster_text": "Priya, 29, delivered a healthy term daughter vaginally two days ago and is cared for on a postpartum unit. She plans to breastfeed and to use powdered formula for some feeds once she returns to work. A practical nurse assesses and teaches her on the unit and again during a home visit later in the first week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acetaminophen at recommended doses is compatible with breastfeeding; check other medications with a pharmacist.): Most common analgesics, including acetaminophen at recommended doses, pass into milk in very small amounts and are compatible with breastfeeding. Pumping and discarding milk wastes it and disrupts supply without benefit. Telling her to stop breastfeeding for any drug is inaccurate, yet not every product is safe, so other medications should be checked with a pharmacist or prescriber."}, {"id": 1031, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the postpartum unit, the nurse assesses Priya on day 2. Her temperature is 37.4 C, pulse 78, and her fundus is firm at the umbilicus. Her pad holds a moderate amount of dark red lochia without clots. Which interpretation is correct?", "options": {"A": "The lochia should already be white and scant, so healing is delayed and needs reporting", "B": "Lochia should stay bright red for two weeks, so the darker color signals bleeding", "C": "The lochia is too dark and heavy, so she should be reported for suspected retained placenta", "D": "These findings are expected, and the lochia should lighten to pinkish brown over the coming days"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Expecting lochia alba on day 2", "B": "Expecting bright red lochia to persist", "C": "Mistaking normal lochia rubra for retained tissue"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C126", "cluster_text": "Priya, 29, delivered a healthy term daughter vaginally two days ago and is cared for on a postpartum unit. She plans to breastfeed and to use powdered formula for some feeds once she returns to work. A practical nurse assesses and teaches her on the unit and again during a home visit later in the first week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (These findings are expected, and the lochia should lighten to pinkish brown over the coming days): Lochia rubra is dark red for the first few days, and a moderate flow with a firm fundus, normal vital signs and no clots is normal at day 2. It then turns pinkish brown (serosa) and later yellowish white. A firm fundus argues against retained tissue or atony. Nurses should still report large clots, a soft fundus or foul odor."}, {"id": 1032, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A nurse visits Priya at home on day 5. She reports chills, and the nurse measures a temperature of 38.6 C and finds a tender uterus with foul-smelling lochia. Priya believes her milk coming in explains the fever. Which condition do these findings most suggest?", "options": {"A": "Normal breast engorgement with a mild temperature rise that settles without treatment", "B": "Subinvolution of the uterus that is corrected by massaging the fundus", "C": "Postpartum uterine infection, which needs prompt reporting to the care provider", "D": "Mastitis from a blocked duct that needs only more frequent feeding"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Attributing fever to milk coming in", "B": "Confusing infection with subinvolution", "D": "Naming a breast condition when uterine findings point elsewhere"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C126", "cluster_text": "Priya, 29, delivered a healthy term daughter vaginally two days ago and is cared for on a postpartum unit. She plans to breastfeed and to use powdered formula for some feeds once she returns to work. A practical nurse assesses and teaches her on the unit and again during a home visit later in the first week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Postpartum uterine infection, which needs prompt reporting to the care provider): Fever above 38 C with chills, a tender uterus and foul lochia point to endometritis, which needs urgent assessment and antibiotics. Engorgement can cause a low-grade rise but not foul lochia or uterine tenderness. Subinvolution gives a soft, large uterus rather than fever and odor. Mastitis presents with breast redness and pain, so the nurse should report and not wait."}, {"id": 1033, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Hendricks is admitted with leg swelling after taking furosemide and digoxin at home. His potassium is 3.0 mmol/L, he reports palpitations, and his apical pulse is irregular at 108. His daughter says he has seemed tired for days. What is the nurse's best interpretation and action?", "options": {"A": "This is a mild variation common with diuretics, so chart it and recheck next bloodwork", "B": "Low potassium raises digoxin toxicity risk, so report it and the palpitations to the RN", "C": "Low potassium weakens digoxin action, so ask whether the digoxin dose should be increased", "D": "Digoxin has lowered the potassium, so hold the furosemide and take his pulse every hour"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating hypokalemia as a minor lab abnormality", "C": "Reversing the true potassium and digoxin relationship", "D": "Blaming the wrong drug and changing orders on her own"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C125", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hendricks, 68, has heart failure and chronic kidney disease treated with furosemide, digoxin and hemodialysis. Over several months he is admitted to a medical unit and later to a surgical unit with different electrolyte problems. The practical nurse assesses him, gives medications safely, teaches him, and reports changes to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Low potassium raises digoxin toxicity risk, so report it and the palpitations to the RN): Hypokalemia makes the heart more sensitive to digoxin, so toxicity and dangerous rhythms can occur even at usual doses. Palpitations and an irregular rapid pulse add urgency, so the RN must hear about it promptly. Waiting for a routine recheck delays treatment. Furosemide, not digoxin, is the cause of the potassium loss, and an increase in digoxin would worsen the risk."}, {"id": 1034, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Months later, Mr. Hendricks comes to the dialysis unit after missing his last two sessions. He has muscle weakness, and the monitor shows tall peaked T waves with a widened QRS. His potassium is 6.8 mmol/L. He asks for a salt substitute for his meals. What is the practical nurse's priority?", "options": {"A": "Teach him to use a potassium-containing salt substitute to cut sodium until his next session", "B": "Ask him to restrict fluids and recheck his potassium at his next routine session.", "C": "Give his scheduled digoxin dose and ask the dietitian to review his menu next week.", "D": "Report the ECG changes and potassium to the RN immediately and prepare for urgent treatment"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offering a potassium-rich substitute to a hyperkalemic client", "B": "Delays treatment of dangerous hyperkalemia and ECG changes", "C": "Continues routine drug and diet review instead of reporting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C125", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hendricks, 68, has heart failure and chronic kidney disease treated with furosemide, digoxin and hemodialysis. Over several months he is admitted to a medical unit and later to a surgical unit with different electrolyte problems. The practical nurse assesses him, gives medications safely, teaches him, and reports changes to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the ECG changes and potassium to the RN immediately and prepare for urgent treatment): Peaked T waves, a widened QRS and a potassium of 6.8 mmol/L signal life-threatening hyperkalemia that can cause cardiac arrest, so immediate reporting and urgent treatment come first. Salt substitutes are high in potassium and would worsen the problem. Waiting for a routine session or a later dietitian review leaves him at risk of a fatal arrhythmia."}, {"id": 1035, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During another admission, Mr. Hendricks has a potassium of 2.9 mmol/L and an order for intravenous potassium chloride. The pharmacy sends a concentrated vial, and a colleague is rushing to finish before shift change. What is the safe nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Run it by gravity over thirty minutes and watch the drip rate", "B": "Confirm dilution, infuse by pump at the ordered rate, and monitor his cardiac rhythm", "C": "Give it slowly by direct push over five minutes since the level is dangerously low", "D": "Add the concentrated vial to the existing bag at the bedside without mixing it"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on gravity instead of a pump", "C": "Using a direct push for concentrated potassium", "D": "Adding concentrate to a bag without mixing, creating a bolus"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C125", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hendricks, 68, has heart failure and chronic kidney disease treated with furosemide, digoxin and hemodialysis. Over several months he is admitted to a medical unit and later to a surgical unit with different electrolyte problems. The practical nurse assesses him, gives medications safely, teaches him, and reports changes to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Confirm dilution, infuse by pump at the ordered rate, and monitor his cardiac rhythm): Concentrated potassium given quickly can cause cardiac arrest, so it must be diluted, infused through a pump at the ordered rate, and monitored with cardiac monitoring. A slow push still delivers undiluted drug to the heart. Adding concentrate to a bag without mixing can pool in the bottom and deliver a bolus. Gravity infusion cannot control the rate safely."}, {"id": 1036, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After thyroid surgery for a goiter, Mr. Hendricks tells the nurse on the surgical unit that his lips and fingertips tingle. He says it is probably nerves about going home. The nurse notes a positive Chvostek sign. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Reassure him that anxiety often causes finger tingling and encourage slow breathing before discharge", "B": "Remove the dressing to check for a hematoma and apply a cold pack to his neck", "C": "Report possible hypocalcemia to the RN promptly, since tingling can progress to tetany and laryngospasm", "D": "Offer a warm drink and recheck his symptoms in four hours as routine postoperative care"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Mistaking tingling for anxiety", "B": "Checking a different complication and missing hypocalcemia", "D": "Delaying reassessment of a possible electrolyte emergency"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C125", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hendricks, 68, has heart failure and chronic kidney disease treated with furosemide, digoxin and hemodialysis. Over several months he is admitted to a medical unit and later to a surgical unit with different electrolyte problems. The practical nurse assesses him, gives medications safely, teaches him, and reports changes to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report possible hypocalcemia to the RN promptly, since tingling can progress to tetany and laryngospasm): Thyroid surgery can injure or remove parathyroid tissue, which lowers calcium. Perioral tingling and a positive Chvostek sign are early signs and can progress to tetany and laryngospasm, so prompt reporting is needed. Attributing symptoms to anxiety or waiting several hours risks airway compromise. A hematoma is a real concern after neck surgery but does not explain tingling with a Chvostek sign."}, {"id": 1037, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A school nurse examines 8-year-old Layla, who has scratched her scalp for a week. The nurse sees tiny white specks firmly attached to hair shafts close to the scalp and a red area behind her ears. Which action is most appropriate for the school setting?", "options": {"A": "Check every classmate using one shared comb through each child's hair to see if lice spread", "B": "Exclude Layla for two weeks and tell the family lice mainly affect children with poor hygiene", "C": "Confirm the finding, teach the family treatment and cleaning steps, and advise against sharing combs", "D": "Tell the family the specks are dandruff and suggest medicated shampoo for one week"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a shared comb for screening", "B": "Stigmatizing hygiene and applying a long exclusion", "D": "Mistaking nits for dandruff"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Confirm the finding, teach the family treatment and cleaning steps, and advise against sharing combs): Nits attached close to the scalp with itching suggest head lice, so confirming the finding and teaching treatment and prevention suits the school setting. A shared comb would spread lice between children. Lice are not related to hygiene, and long exclusion is unnecessary and stigmatizing. Dandruff flakes brush off easily, while nits stay firmly attached to the hair shaft."}, {"id": 1038, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On an inpatient unit, a 28-year-old man in acute psychosis was placed in physical restraints after attempting to strike staff. He is now calm in bed with limbs secured. The nurse in charge asks the practical nurse to monitor him. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Loosen all restraints once he stops shouting so he can rest comfortably", "B": "Leave a call bell within reach and check when he rings it", "C": "Check on him at the start and end of the shift because he is calm", "D": "Observe him at ordered intervals, check limb circulation and skin, offer fluids and toileting, and document"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Releasing restraints without an order or assessment", "B": "Relying on a call bell from a client who may not use it", "C": "Leaving a restrained client unobserved"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Observe him at ordered intervals, check limb circulation and skin, offer fluids and toileting, and document): A restrained client is at risk of injury, impaired circulation, aspiration and distress, so frequent observation, limb checks, fluids, toileting and documentation are required. Checking only twice a shift leaves him unsafe. A call bell cannot replace direct observation. Restraints are released only through the team's assessment and order, and as soon as it is safe."}, {"id": 1039, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 34-year-old woman tells the community nurse she has had cramping, bloating and alternating constipation and diarrhea for months. A recent colonoscopy was normal, and she worries her bowel is being damaged. Which teaching about irritable bowel syndrome is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "It leads to ulcers and gradual bowel damage, so she needs frequent repeat colonoscopies", "B": "It resolves once dairy and gluten are removed, so no other changes are needed", "C": "It is a form of inflammatory bowel disease that needs long-term steroid treatment", "D": "It is a functional disorder without bowel damage, and trigger foods and stress can be managed"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming the condition causes bowel damage", "B": "Promising a single dietary cure", "C": "Confusing irritable bowel syndrome with inflammatory bowel disease"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C124", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works a shift in a community health clinic during a local hepatitis A outbreak linked to a restaurant. Clients arrive with different gastrointestinal and liver concerns, and the nurse also faces a workplace exposure. Each situation calls for safe assessment, accurate teaching and appropriate reporting within practical nurse scope.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is a functional disorder without bowel damage, and trigger foods and stress can be managed): Irritable bowel syndrome is a functional disorder, so symptoms are real but the bowel is not structurally damaged, and a normal colonoscopy supports this. Management includes identifying trigger foods, managing stress, and following the care plan. Inflammatory bowel disease causes inflammation and ulcers and is a different condition. Diet changes may help but are rarely a complete cure."}, {"id": 1040, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 41-year-old man who ate at the implicated restaurant has jaundice, dark urine and nausea. Hepatitis A is suspected, and public health has linked other cases to the same meal. His wife asks what precautions the household needs while he recovers at home. Which instruction best matches how the virus spreads?", "options": {"A": "Use airborne precautions and wear a mask whenever anyone enters his bedroom", "B": "Avoid sharing razors and toothbrushes and use condoms, since the virus spreads mainly through blood", "C": "Wash hands after toileting and before preparing food, and avoid sharing food or utensils", "D": "Boil all his dishes and clothing together, after which no other precautions are needed"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using airborne precautions for a fecal-oral illness", "B": "Applying bloodborne precautions to hepatitis A", "D": "Relying on one measure and dropping hand hygiene"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C124", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works a shift in a community health clinic during a local hepatitis A outbreak linked to a restaurant. Clients arrive with different gastrointestinal and liver concerns, and the nurse also faces a workplace exposure. Each situation calls for safe assessment, accurate teaching and appropriate reporting within practical nurse scope.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wash hands after toileting and before preparing food, and avoid sharing food or utensils): Hepatitis A spreads by the fecal-oral route, so hand hygiene, safe food handling and not sharing food or utensils are the key household precautions. Airborne precautions do not apply. Razor and sexual precautions fit hepatitis B and C, not hepatitis A. Boiling dishes alone does not replace hand hygiene, which is the main barrier to spread."}, {"id": 1041, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "While drawing blood from a client of unknown infection status, the practical nurse sustains a needlestick. She washes the site and feels well, and she wonders whether she needs to report since she has no symptoms. What should she do next?", "options": {"A": "Squeeze the puncture site and soak it in disinfectant for ten minutes before continuing her shift", "B": "Record the injury in her own notes and ask the client to consent to testing later", "C": "Wait until symptoms appear, then request blood tests to confirm infection", "D": "Report the exposure now to her supervisor and occupational health so testing and prophylaxis can start."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using an outdated first aid measure", "B": "Delaying testing and handling the report informally", "C": "Waiting for symptoms before reporting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C124", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works a shift in a community health clinic during a local hepatitis A outbreak linked to a restaurant. Clients arrive with different gastrointestinal and liver concerns, and the nurse also faces a workplace exposure. Each situation calls for safe assessment, accurate teaching and appropriate reporting within practical nurse scope.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the exposure now to her supervisor and occupational health so testing and prophylaxis can start.): Bloodborne viruses such as hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV can be transmitted before any symptoms, and post-exposure prophylaxis works best when started quickly. Immediate reporting starts source and baseline testing and follow-up. Waiting for symptoms or a delayed informal approach loses that window. Squeezing the site and soaking in disinfectant is not recommended; wash with soap and water."}, {"id": 1042, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 60-year-old man with cirrhosis on the medical unit has jaundice and intense itching. His skin is dry with scratch marks on the arms. He reports the itching keeps him awake most nights. He asks for the lotion his wife brought, which contains alcohol. Which intervention is best?", "options": {"A": "Cover his arms with long sleeves and leave the dry skin untreated to avoid more products", "B": "Keep his nails short, use lukewarm baths with mild soap, and apply a fragrance-free, alcohol-free moisturizer", "C": "Apply his wife's lotion after bathing to keep the skin soft", "D": "Use hot baths with a scrubbing cloth to remove irritating bile salts from the skin"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaving dry skin untreated", "C": "Using an alcohol-based lotion that dries skin", "D": "Using heat and friction that worsen itching"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C124", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works a shift in a community health clinic during a local hepatitis A outbreak linked to a restaurant. Clients arrive with different gastrointestinal and liver concerns, and the nurse also faces a workplace exposure. Each situation calls for safe assessment, accurate teaching and appropriate reporting within practical nurse scope.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep his nails short, use lukewarm baths with mild soap, and apply a fragrance-free, alcohol-free moisturizer): Itching in liver disease is linked to bile salts in the skin, and dry skin worsens it, so lukewarm baths, mild soap, short nails and a gentle moisturizer reduce scratching and skin breakdown. Alcohol-based lotion dries the skin further. Hot water and scrubbing increase itching and irritation. Leaving the skin untreated allows scratching to continue and breaks the skin."}, {"id": 1043, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 35-year-old client on a mental health unit was restrained for 40 minutes after becoming aggressive and is now calm and oriented. The team plans follow-up for the next morning. The client says he felt frightened and does not understand why it happened. Which action by the nurse is best?", "options": {"A": "Join a debrief with the client and team to review triggers, his experience and prevention", "B": "Ask him to write a reflection about the incident and hand it to the manager", "C": "Reassure him it was necessary and routine, then resume his usual schedule without discussion", "D": "Hold a team-only review and leave the client out so the discussion stays objective"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using paperwork instead of a conversation with the client", "C": "Dismissing his fear by calling the restraint routine", "D": "Excluding the client from the review"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Join a debrief with the client and team to review triggers, his experience and prevention): Debriefing with the client helps him process a frightening event, identify triggers and plan alternatives, and it helps the team improve practice and reduce future restraint. A written reflection places the work on him without dialogue. Reassuring him and moving on dismisses his fear and confusion. A team-only review misses his perspective."}, {"id": 1044, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 58-year-old man arrives at the emergency department 90 minutes after the start of chest pressure radiating to his jaw. His first troponin is within normal limits and the ECG shows no ST changes. The nurse prepares for the next step. What should the nurse expect?", "options": {"A": "Troponin will be repeated over several hours, since an early value can be normal in injury.", "B": "The normal value rules out heart injury, so he can be discharged without further testing", "C": "A second troponin is needed only if his pain recurs after a normal first result", "D": "A repeat troponin is drawn within fifteen minutes, since results turn positive almost immediately"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Ruling out injury on one early value", "C": "Repeating testing only after symptoms return", "D": "Wrong timing for the troponin rise"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Troponin will be repeated over several hours, since an early value can be normal in injury.): Troponin begins to rise a few hours after myocardial injury, so a normal value at 90 minutes does not exclude it, and serial testing with repeat ECGs is standard. Repeating only if pain returns could miss ongoing injury. Troponin does not turn positive within minutes, so a quick repeat is not informative. Discharging him on one early value is unsafe."}, {"id": 1045, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On postoperative day 2, a 66-year-old woman who had abdominal surgery still has an indwelling urinary catheter. She is walking, her urine output is adequate, and no urologic procedure was performed. The nurse is reviewing her plan of care. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Keep the catheter until her diet is fully advanced, since bladder function returns afterwards", "B": "Change the catheter every 24 hours on a fixed schedule to lower infection risk", "C": "Ask the RN or prescriber about removal, since it is not needed and raises infection risk", "D": "Clamp the catheter for several hours daily to train the bladder, then keep it in"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using diet progression as the removal criterion", "B": "Believing routine catheter changes prevent infection", "D": "Clamping, which is not evidence-based and raises infection risk"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask the RN or prescriber about removal, since it is not needed and raises infection risk): Each day a catheter stays in increases the risk of urinary tract infection, so its need should be reviewed daily and removal requested when no indication remains. Diet progression is not a reason to keep it. Routine exchanges do not prevent infection and add trauma. Clamping to train the bladder is not recommended and also leaves the catheter in place."}, {"id": 1046, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At 0800, a practical nurse has four medications due: rapid-acting insulin for a client whose breakfast tray arrives at 0830, a scheduled stool softener, a daily vitamin, and an intravenous antibiotic that was due at 0730 for a client with sepsis. Which should she give first?", "options": {"A": "The vitamin first because it is the simplest and takes under a minute", "B": "The stool softener first because it appears earliest in the medication list", "C": "The overdue antibiotic for the client with sepsis, then the insulin shortly before the tray arrives", "D": "The insulin first because it is rapid-acting, then the antibiotic after the tray has arrived"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing the quickest task over the most urgent one", "B": "Following medication list order instead of clinical urgency", "D": "Giving a time-critical drug late for a meal-timed one"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The overdue antibiotic for the client with sepsis, then the insulin shortly before the tray arrives): Antibiotics for sepsis are time-critical and this dose is already late, so it comes first, and the insulin can still be given just before the tray arrives at 0830. Giving insulin first delays sepsis treatment further. List order and simplicity do not reflect urgency. Prioritize by the effect of delay on each client."}, {"id": 1047, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 3-year-old boy is on the surgical unit after a tonsillectomy. He is whimpering, holding his throat and refusing to drink. His mother asks the nurse how she will know how much pain he feels. Which assessment approach is most suitable?", "options": {"A": "His parents' rating alone, because only a parent can truly judge a young child's pain", "B": "Vital signs alone, since heart rate and blood pressure reliably show pain in young children", "C": "A numeric rating scale from zero to ten, since he can say how bad it is", "D": "A behavioural observation tool such as FLACC, plus a faces scale if he can point"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying only on parents' judgment", "B": "Relying on vital signs, which are unreliable", "C": "Using a numeric scale with a toddler"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A behavioural observation tool such as FLACC, plus a faces scale if he can point): Children of three cannot use a numeric scale, so behavioural tools such as FLACC, combined with a faces scale if he can point, fit his age. Parents add useful information but are not the only source. Vital signs can change with pain but are not reliable alone because fear, fever and other causes alter them. Observe expression, crying, legs, activity and consolability."}, {"id": 1048, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A practical nurse on a surgical unit cared for a client who died unexpectedly after a postoperative complication. Over the next week she sleeps poorly, replays the events, and says she is thinking of resigning. The charge nurse notices her distress. Which response best reflects a safety culture?", "options": {"A": "Offer her extra shifts to keep her busy and suggest she stop discussing the event", "B": "Ask her to write a formal statement and avoid contact until the investigation ends", "C": "Refer her to the manager for a performance review because the event may signal unsafe practice", "D": "Offer a private talk, peer support or the employee assistance program, and a no-blame event review"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Distraction and silence instead of support", "B": "Treating her as a suspect and withdrawing support", "C": "Treating a second victim reaction as a performance problem"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Offer a private talk, peer support or the employee assistance program, and a no-blame event review): Nurses affected by a patient event, sometimes called second victims, benefit from timely, confidential support such as peer programs or an employee assistance program, along with a no-blame review of the event. Keeping her busy and silent leaves her distress unaddressed. A performance review is punitive and undermines a safety culture. A formal statement with no contact isolates her."}, {"id": 1049, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During morning care, the practical nurse notices a 3 cm area of intact skin over Mr. Alvarez's sacrum that is red and does not turn white when she presses on it. The skin feels warm and slightly firm. Which classification and action are correct?", "options": {"A": "A stage 1 pressure injury; relieve pressure by repositioning and report it to the RN", "B": "Normal reactive redness; recheck the skin at the next routine bath", "C": "A stage 2 pressure injury that needs a hydrocolloid dressing and a wound culture", "D": "A stage 1 pressure injury; massage the area gently to restore circulation and chart it"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Mistaking non-blanchable redness for normal redness", "C": "Overstaging intact skin and applying a dressing", "D": "Massaging a reddened pressure area"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C123", "cluster_text": "Mr. Alvarez, 82, lives in a long-term care home. He has limited mobility after a stroke and spends most of the day in a wheelchair or in bed. He has diabetes, is incontinent of urine at night, and eats about half of his meals. Over several weeks the practical nurse watches his skin and reports changes to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A stage 1 pressure injury; relieve pressure by repositioning and report it to the RN): Intact skin with non-blanchable redness is a stage 1 pressure injury, and the priority is to remove pressure through repositioning and to report it so the plan can be adjusted. Massage can damage fragile tissue. Redness that does not blanch is not normal reactive redness. Stage 2 involves partial-thickness skin loss, which is not present here."}, {"id": 1050, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two weeks later, Mr. Alvarez has a shallow open area on his left heel with a pink wound bed and an intact fluid-filled blister beside it. There is no slough and no visible fat. His pulses are palpable. Which stage and response fit?", "options": {"A": "Unstageable; cover with a dressing and wait for debridement before assigning a stage", "B": "Stage 2; protect it with a suitable dressing, float the heels, and report to the RN", "C": "Stage 1; apply a transparent film dressing and reassess the heel at the next weekly review", "D": "Stage 3 with full thickness loss; pack the wound with moist gauze and await review"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Calling a visible pink wound bed unstageable", "C": "Treating an open area and blister as intact skin", "D": "Overstaging a shallow wound as full thickness"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C123", "cluster_text": "Mr. Alvarez, 82, lives in a long-term care home. He has limited mobility after a stroke and spends most of the day in a wheelchair or in bed. He has diabetes, is incontinent of urine at night, and eats about half of his meals. Over several weeks the practical nurse watches his skin and reports changes to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stage 2; protect it with a suitable dressing, float the heels, and report to the RN): A shallow open area with a pink wound bed and an intact or ruptured blister with no slough or fat is a stage 2 pressure injury. Care includes pressure relief, floating the heels and an appropriate dressing, with a report to the RN. A wound is unstageable only when slough or eschar hides the base. Stage 1 means intact skin, so an open area does not fit. Stage 3 would show visible fat."}, {"id": 1051, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A month later, the sacral wound is deep, with exposed tendon in the base, undermined edges and a foul odor. He has a temperature of 38.1 C and new confusion, and his intake has dropped. Which is the nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Record stage 3 because bone is not visible and continue the current dressing plan", "B": "Apply a hydrogel dressing, increase his protein intake and reassess the wound in 48 hours", "C": "Report the wound changes and possible infection to the RN promptly, since fever may signal spread", "D": "Swab the wound for culture and apply topical antibiotic ointment from unit stock"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Understaging a wound with exposed tendon", "B": "Delaying reporting while treating the wound alone", "D": "Acting beyond scope without an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C123", "cluster_text": "Mr. Alvarez, 82, lives in a long-term care home. He has limited mobility after a stroke and spends most of the day in a wheelchair or in bed. He has diabetes, is incontinent of urine at night, and eats about half of his meals. Over several weeks the practical nurse watches his skin and reports changes to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the wound changes and possible infection to the RN promptly, since fever may signal spread): Exposed tendon makes this a stage 4 injury, and fever with new confusion and odor suggests infection that could spread to bone or blood, so prompt reporting is needed. A dressing change and later reassessment delays care for possible sepsis. Culturing and applying antibiotic ointment need an order and RN direction. Calling it stage 3 understates the depth."}, {"id": 1052, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Later that week, Mr. Alvarez's right heel has an intact area of dark purple discoloration that feels boggy and cooler than the surrounding skin. He wears pressure-relieving boots. The wound nurse is not on until tomorrow. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Offload and protect the heel, monitor it, and report suspected deep tissue injury to the RN", "B": "Cover the area with a dressing left unchanged until the wound nurse arrives tomorrow", "C": "Apply a warm compress to the heel to improve circulation to the discoloured area", "D": "Apply a hydrocolloid dressing and document it as a stage 1 pressure injury"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Waiting without monitoring or reporting", "C": "Using heat on deep tissue damage", "D": "Misclassifying deep tissue injury as stage 1"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C123", "cluster_text": "Mr. Alvarez, 82, lives in a long-term care home. He has limited mobility after a stroke and spends most of the day in a wheelchair or in bed. He has diabetes, is incontinent of urine at night, and eats about half of his meals. Over several weeks the practical nurse watches his skin and reports changes to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Offload and protect the heel, monitor it, and report suspected deep tissue injury to the RN): Dark purple, boggy intact skin suggests deep tissue injury, where damage lies beneath the surface and may evolve quickly. The nurse offloads the heel, protects it, monitors for change and reports to the RN without waiting. Heat can worsen damaged tissue. Labelling it stage 1 understates the risk. Leaving a dressing alone until tomorrow delays assessment."}, {"id": 1053, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 4-year-old girl arrives at the emergency department with a temperature of 37.6 C. She is lethargic, her skin is mottled with a capillary refill of 5 seconds, and her heart rate is 168. Her mother says she played normally yesterday. Which interpretation should the nurse act on?", "options": {"A": "The tachycardia suggests dehydration, so offer oral fluids and observe her over time", "B": "The temperature is nearly normal, so she can wait in the waiting room for a recheck", "C": "Mottling and a fast pulse reflect fear in children, so comfort her and observe", "D": "These findings suggest possible sepsis despite the near-normal temperature, so alert the RN or physician immediately"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming dehydration and delaying escalation", "B": "Relying on temperature alone", "C": "Attributing signs of poor perfusion to fear"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (These findings suggest possible sepsis despite the near-normal temperature, so alert the RN or physician immediately): Lethargy, mottling, delayed capillary refill and marked tachycardia indicate poor perfusion and possible septic shock, and children may not have a fever. Temperature alone should never decide urgency. Fear and dehydration do not explain this combination, and waiting delays life-saving treatment, so immediate escalation is required."}, {"id": 1054, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 68-year-old man attends a clinic with a tremor of the right hand that is worst when he sits quietly and lessens when he lifts a cup. His wife notes slower walking and smaller handwriting. What should the nurse do next?", "options": {"A": "Test for intention tremor by having him touch his nose and then her finger", "B": "Report the resting tremor, slowness and small handwriting to the physician and assess his fall risk", "C": "Ask whether he has had recent head trauma, since tremor usually follows an old injury", "D": "Advise him to use a weighted cup and handle utensils, and review the tremor next year"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Looking for the wrong type of tremor", "C": "Attributing a resting tremor to old trauma", "D": "Managing symptoms and delaying evaluation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the resting tremor, slowness and small handwriting to the physician and assess his fall risk): A resting tremor that eases with movement, slowed movement and micrographia are cardinal features of Parkinson disease and need medical evaluation, along with a fall-risk assessment. Intention tremor testing looks for a different problem. Old head trauma is not the usual cause. A weighted cup helps function but a year of delay is unsafe."}, {"id": 1055, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 52-year-old man received oral hydromorphone for severe incisional pain 20 minutes ago. He rated his pain 8 out of 10 and now lies quietly with his eyes closed. The nurse is deciding when to reassess. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Reassess within five minutes, since oral hydromorphone reaches peak effect almost immediately", "B": "Reassess after two hours, once the dose has finished acting and sedation has worn off", "C": "Wait until the next scheduled vital signs several hours later to avoid disturbing his rest", "D": "Reassess pain and sedation at peak effect, about 30 to 60 minutes after the oral dose"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Wrong onset time for an oral opioid", "B": "Reassessing after the peak and duration have passed", "C": "Reassesses hours later, missing peak effect and oversedation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reassess pain and sedation at peak effect, about 30 to 60 minutes after the oral dose): Reassessment should happen when the drug reaches peak effect, roughly 30 to 60 minutes after an oral opioid, and should include pain relief, sedation and breathing. Five minutes is too early for oral dosing. Waiting two hours misses early sedation or inadequate relief. Waiting for the next scheduled vital signs several hours later is unsafe."}, {"id": 1056, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On an inpatient unit, a 45-year-old man with a history of paranoia shouts threats and clenches his fists in a small closed office. He stands between the nurse and the only door. Other staff are in the hallway. Which action is most important for safety?", "options": {"A": "Move toward the exit, keep a path clear, and call for help in a calm voice.", "B": "Step back, speak firmly and tell him his behaviour will be reported to security", "C": "Sit down at his level, lower your voice and ask him to explain his fears", "D": "Stay in the office and speak firmly until he calms down on his own"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Threatening a client instead of securing an exit", "C": "Staying trapped in the room to de-escalate", "D": "Staying alone with an aggressive client in a closed room"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Move toward the exit, keep a path clear, and call for help in a calm voice.): Personal safety comes first with escalating aggression, so the nurse keeps a clear exit, uses a calm voice and gets help. Threats can raise agitation. Sitting down and staying in a closed room puts the nurse at risk even if talking is useful. Staying and speaking firmly alone, or blocking his way, also traps the nurse or provokes him."}, {"id": 1057, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A 59-year-old man newly prescribed insulin pens receives home care. His wife says he listened to the explanation yesterday but could not recall the steps. His reading level is Grade 6, and he is eager to learn. How should the nurse teach pen use?", "options": {"A": "Give his wife the teaching sheets and let her explain each step at home", "B": "Demonstrate each step with a pen, give simple illustrated instructions, and watch him return the demonstration", "C": "Show a short video, then ask him to describe each step in his own words", "D": "Repeat the verbal explanation more slowly and leave a long pamphlet written in medical terms"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delegating teaching to a family member", "C": "Using verbal recall instead of a return demonstration", "D": "Using verbal teaching alone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Demonstrate each step with a pen, give simple illustrated instructions, and watch him return the demonstration): Adults learn skills best by seeing, doing and receiving feedback, so demonstration, plain illustrated materials and return demonstration confirm understanding at a Grade 6 reading level. Handing teaching to his wife does not build his skill. Describing steps from a video does not show he can handle the pen. Repeating verbal teaching with a dense pamphlet has already failed."}, {"id": 1058, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A 45-year-old woman was told this morning that she has type 2 diabetes. She is tearful, repeatedly says she cannot take anything in, and shakes her head when the community nurse offers her a booklet. Which approach to teaching is best?", "options": {"A": "Acknowledge her feelings, share only essential information now, and plan fuller teaching when she is ready", "B": "Review the booklet's key sections with her now and ask her to repeat them back", "C": "Reassure her that diabetes is manageable, then postpone all teaching until her next clinic visit", "D": "Ask her husband to learn the material and skip teaching her until her next clinic"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Teaching despite lack of readiness", "C": "Delaying all teaching, including essential safety information", "D": "Bypassing the client by teaching only her husband"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acknowledge her feelings, share only essential information now, and plan fuller teaching when she is ready): Learning depends on readiness, and strong emotion blocks it, so the nurse acknowledges feelings, gives only essential safety information and schedules teaching when she is ready. Reviewing booklet sections and expecting recall overloads her. Postponing everything leaves her without essential information. Leaving a booklet to read alone or skipping her in favour of her husband ignores her needs."}, {"id": 1059, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At 1630, Jordan realizes that a wound dressing change done at 1500 was never charted before the day shift ended. Jordan clearly remembers the care and how the wound looked. The facility uses an electronic record. What should Jordan do?", "options": {"A": "Backdate the entry to 1500 so the record matches the care time", "B": "Skip charting because the dressing change was reported verbally to the oncoming nurse", "C": "Add a late entry with today's date and time, refer to the 1500 care, and sign", "D": "Delete the nearby entry and rewrite the note so it includes the dressing change"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Backdating, which falsifies the record", "B": "Treating verbal report as documentation", "D": "Altering an existing entry to hide the omission"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C122", "cluster_text": "Jordan, a practical nurse, is in the first month of employment on a medical unit in a community hospital. During orientation and the first shifts, Jordan faces questions about documentation, policy use, professional boundaries and incident reporting. Each situation calls for accountability to the regulatory body, accurate records and safe practice within scope.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Add a late entry with today's date and time, refer to the 1500 care, and sign): A late entry is acceptable when it is clearly identified as late, carries the current date and time, refers to the earlier event and is signed. Backdating or altering existing entries falsifies the record. A verbal report does not replace documentation, which is the legal record of care. Accuracy and transparency protect both the client and the nurse."}, {"id": 1060, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Jordan cares for a long-term care resident on a unit that uses charting by exception. The resident has shown a gradual decline in appetite over three days, though no single event occurred. Jordan is unsure what to record. Which statement about charting by exception is correct?", "options": {"A": "A verbal report to the RN replaces the written entry for any change in condition", "B": "Use a verbal handover each shift and record the decline only if appetite falls abruptly", "C": "Deviations from defined normal limits must be documented, so the gradual appetite decline is reported.", "D": "Document appetite only when a meal is refused entirely, since partial intake meets normal limits"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Substituting a verbal report for documentation", "B": "Limiting documentation to sudden changes", "D": "Misdefining the normal limits for intake"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C122", "cluster_text": "Jordan, a practical nurse, is in the first month of employment on a medical unit in a community hospital. During orientation and the first shifts, Jordan faces questions about documentation, policy use, professional boundaries and incident reporting. Each situation calls for accountability to the regulatory body, accurate records and safe practice within scope.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Deviations from defined normal limits must be documented, so the gradual appetite decline is reported.): Charting by exception records findings that differ from the defined norms, and a gradual decline in appetite is exactly such a deviation and must be documented and reported. Verbal handover does not replace a written entry. Gradual change still counts as a deviation, so waiting for an abrupt drop is wrong. Reduced intake is outside normal limits even when meals are not refused."}, {"id": 1061, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "During orientation, Jordan reads the facility policy manual and finds a policy that lets practical nurses perform a task Jordan was not educated to do, if a manager approves. Jordan wonders how the policy relates to professional standards. Which statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Policy cannot override legislation or regulatory standards, so Jordan must stay within scope and competence", "B": "Policy applies only to managers, and staff nurses follow regulatory standards without reading it", "C": "Policy replaces regulatory standards once the employer approves it, so Jordan may perform the task", "D": "Policy can expand scope if the physician on the unit agrees verbally to the task"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believing employer policy applies only to managers", "C": "Thinking employer policy overrides legislation and standards", "D": "Treating a verbal physician agreement as scope expansion"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C122", "cluster_text": "Jordan, a practical nurse, is in the first month of employment on a medical unit in a community hospital. During orientation and the first shifts, Jordan faces questions about documentation, policy use, professional boundaries and incident reporting. Each situation calls for accountability to the regulatory body, accurate records and safe practice within scope.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Policy cannot override legislation or regulatory standards, so Jordan must stay within scope and competence): Employer policy must work within legislation and the regulatory body's standards, so a policy cannot give a nurse authority beyond education, competence and scope. Nurses remain accountable for their own practice and should clarify with the manager or regulatory body. Policies apply to all staff. A verbal physician agreement does not alter scope."}, {"id": 1062, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A client on the unit, Marcus, belongs to the same neighbourhood sports club as Jordan. He asks for Jordan's personal phone number and offers to become friends on social media during his stay, saying it would help him feel comfortable. How should Jordan respond?", "options": {"A": "Accept his request but message him only about non-clinical topics outside of work", "B": "Politely decline, explain that care stays professional, and consult the RN or manager.", "C": "Give the unit's phone number so he can reach Jordan whenever he needs help", "D": "Tell him they can be friends after discharge and keep caring for him"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepting a dual relationship with limits", "C": "Offering contact outside professional channels", "D": "Setting up a future personal relationship while still his nurse"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C122", "cluster_text": "Jordan, a practical nurse, is in the first month of employment on a medical unit in a community hospital. During orientation and the first shifts, Jordan faces questions about documentation, policy use, professional boundaries and incident reporting. Each situation calls for accountability to the regulatory body, accurate records and safe practice within scope.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Politely decline, explain that care stays professional, and consult the RN or manager.): A nurse-client relationship has a power imbalance, so personal contact and social media friendships blur professional boundaries. Jordan declines kindly, explains why, and consults the RN or manager. Giving a unit number for personal help or promising friendship still crosses the boundary. Accepting for non-clinical talk is still a personal relationship. Quietly handing off his care avoids the issue."}, {"id": 1063, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Jordan finds a 78-year-old client sitting on the floor beside the bed with no visible injury. He says he slipped on the way to the bathroom, and the bed alarm was off. He says he is fine and asks Jordan not to make a fuss. What should Jordan do?", "options": {"A": "Assess him, notify the RN, and complete an incident report even though no harm occurred", "B": "Complete the report if the client's family later asks about the event", "C": "Write a brief nursing note since no injury means an incident report is unnecessary", "D": "Turn the alarm back on and mention it at report, leaving documentation to the RN"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Waiting for a complaint before reporting", "C": "Reporting only events that cause harm", "D": "Delegating incident documentation to another nurse"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C122", "cluster_text": "Jordan, a practical nurse, is in the first month of employment on a medical unit in a community hospital. During orientation and the first shifts, Jordan faces questions about documentation, policy use, professional boundaries and incident reporting. Each situation calls for accountability to the regulatory body, accurate records and safe practice within scope.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Assess him, notify the RN, and complete an incident report even though no harm occurred): Falls are reportable incidents whether or not injury occurs, since reports reveal system problems such as an alarm that was off. Jordan first assesses and notifies the RN, then completes the report. A note alone hides the event from quality review. Leaving documentation to someone else and waiting for family inquiry both fail accountability."}, {"id": 1064, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A practical nurse prepares to change the dressing on a central venous catheter for a 63-year-old man. The old dressing is damp at the edges, although the site looks clean. The nurse has non-sterile gloves and a sterile dressing kit. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Reinforce the damp dressing with extra tape and change it at the next scheduled day", "B": "Perform hand hygiene, use sterile gloves and aseptic technique, and cleanse with approved antiseptic.", "C": "Perform hand hygiene, wear clean gloves and cleanse the site with sterile saline only", "D": "Use clean non-sterile gloves because the site looks clean and the change is routine"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaving a compromised dressing in place", "C": "Skipping sterile gloves and an approved antiseptic", "D": "Using non-sterile gloves near a central line"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Perform hand hygiene, use sterile gloves and aseptic technique, and cleanse with approved antiseptic.): Central lines give microbes direct access to the bloodstream, so dressing changes need hand hygiene, sterile gloves and an approved antiseptic. A damp dressing is a contamination risk and is changed promptly. Taping over it leaves moisture against the site. Saline alone with clean gloves does not provide adequate skin antisepsis. Non-sterile gloves because the site looks clean is also unsafe."}, {"id": 1065, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A 32-year-old woman in labour on the maternity unit tells the nurse she would prefer only female staff because of her beliefs. The nurse assigned to her is male, and a female nurse is available on the unit. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Ask her to wait until her labour is further along before raising her concern again", "B": "Acknowledge her preference, consult the charge nurse, and try to arrange a female caregiver safely.", "C": "Tell her a female nurse is possible only after delivery, once the baby is born", "D": "Ask her partner to approve the male nurse, since he can speak for her wishes"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Postpones a reasonable request during a vulnerable labour", "C": "Postponing a preference until it is moot", "D": "Asking a family member to override her own preference"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C131", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on a busy hospital unit serving a diverse community, including clients who bring their cultural, religious and Indigenous practices into care. The nurse is expected to provide culturally safe care, work within facility policy, and advocate for the client when a request cannot be met right away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acknowledge her preference, consult the charge nurse, and try to arrange a female caregiver safely.): Culturally safe care respects a client's request for a caregiver of a certain gender when it is reasonably possible, and the nurse works with the charge nurse to adjust assignments safely. Deferring the request until after delivery ignores it when it matters. Her partner cannot decide for her. Asking her to wait and raise it later, or stating that assignments cannot change, is not client-centred."}, {"id": 1066, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A woman wearing a religious head covering attends a clinic for an ear infection. The nurse needs to examine her ears and scalp, and she looks uncomfortable when the nurse approaches her head. A male physician will perform the exam. What approach is most culturally safe?", "options": {"A": "Skip the ear exam and note that she declined because of her religious dress", "B": "Ask her husband for permission before the exam so that she feels more supported", "C": "Remove the covering briefly with the door open, then replace it without discussion", "D": "Explain why the exam is needed, ask her preferences, and offer privacy and a female examiner"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withholding assessment instead of adapting", "B": "Seeking permission from a family member instead of the client", "C": "Removing a head covering without consent"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C131", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on a busy hospital unit serving a diverse community, including clients who bring their cultural, religious and Indigenous practices into care. The nurse is expected to provide culturally safe care, work within facility policy, and advocate for the client when a request cannot be met right away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain why the exam is needed, ask her preferences, and offer privacy and a female examiner): Cultural safety means explaining the purpose, asking about preferences, and adapting the setting, such as offering privacy and a same-gender examiner, while still completing the needed assessment. Removing the covering without her consent violates her dignity. Skipping the exam harms care. She is an adult who gives her own consent, not her husband."}, {"id": 1067, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A 79-year-old Indigenous resident in a long-term care home asks if he can hold a smudging ceremony in his room for his spiritual health. The home has a smoke-free policy and smoke detectors. The nurse does not know the process. Which response best supports culturally safe care?", "options": {"A": "Affirm the request and work with the manager and family to arrange smudging safely within policy.", "B": "Ask him to postpone smudging until the family visits, since staff cannot supervise ceremony", "C": "Tell him smudging is prohibited because of the smoke-free policy and offer a prayer book instead", "D": "Direct him to the spiritual care department and leave it to them without follow-up"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assuming the home cannot support ceremony", "C": "Refusing because of fire policy without exploring options", "D": "Referring on without advocating or following up"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C131", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on a busy hospital unit serving a diverse community, including clients who bring their cultural, religious and Indigenous practices into care. The nurse is expected to provide culturally safe care, work within facility policy, and advocate for the client when a request cannot be met right away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Affirm the request and work with the manager and family to arrange smudging safely within policy.): Smudging is an important spiritual practice, so the nurse affirms the request and works with the manager, the family and any Indigenous liaison to find a safe way within the home's policy. Postponing it until visitors arrive dismisses his need now. A referral without follow-up leaves the request unresolved. Banning it outright or disabling the smoke detector is also wrong."}, {"id": 1068, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On a hospital unit, a 44-year-old Indigenous client asks to smudge. Hospital policy requires approval and a designated space because of fire safety and nearby oxygen use. The unit manager is off-site, and an Indigenous health service is available. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Tell him to wait until the manager returns in a few days and then ask again", "B": "Contact the Indigenous health service and charge nurse to arrange a policy-compliant space now", "C": "Let him smudge at the bedside quickly and document it afterward", "D": "Refuse the request until the policy is changed, since the nurse must uphold current rules"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying because the manager is away", "C": "Bypassing fire safety rules", "D": "Refusing until policy changes"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C131", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works on a busy hospital unit serving a diverse community, including clients who bring their cultural, religious and Indigenous practices into care. The nurse is expected to provide culturally safe care, work within facility policy, and advocate for the client when a request cannot be met right away.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Contact the Indigenous health service and charge nurse to arrange a policy-compliant space now): The nurse advocates for the client while respecting safety, so she uses the available Indigenous health service and charge nurse to find an approved space promptly. Refusing until policy changes ignores the need and the available resources. Smudging at the bedside near oxygen is a fire risk. Waiting days when others can authorize arrangements is an avoidable delay."}, {"id": 1069, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 55-year-old man arrives in the PACU after abdominal surgery under general anesthesia. He is drowsy, has snoring respirations, and reports pain of 8 out of 10. His oxygen saturation is 90 percent. Which action should the nurse take first?", "options": {"A": "Raise the head of the bed fully and ask him to take deep breaths later", "B": "Check his dressing and drains for bleeding before addressing his breathing", "C": "Give the prescribed IV opioid for pain so that his breathing becomes more comfortable", "D": "Open the airway with a head tilt and chin lift or jaw thrust, and apply oxygen"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying airway care by postponing deep breathing until later", "B": "Prioritizing bleeding checks over an obstructed airway", "C": "Treating pain first, risking deeper sedation and obstruction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Open the airway with a head tilt and chin lift or jaw thrust, and apply oxygen): Snoring respirations and a saturation of 90 percent suggest a partly obstructed airway from the tongue or residual anesthesia, so airway, breathing and oxygen come first. An opioid could deepen sedation and worsen obstruction. Breathing exercises later do not fix obstruction now. Bleeding matters but does not take priority over airway."}, {"id": 1070, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 6-year-old boy fell from playground equipment onto his head 2 hours ago. He cried right away, is now alert and playing, and vomited once. His parents ask if they may go home with no more checks. What should the nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Allow him to sleep and check only his breathing once when the parents wake him", "B": "He needs a skull x-ray before discharge, and parents need only watch for headache", "C": "He needs neurological observation, and the parents must learn warning signs such as repeated vomiting", "D": "He can go home because he is alert, and observation is needed if he cries again"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Too infrequent neurological checks", "B": "Assuming imaging replaces observation and narrowing warning signs", "D": "Dismissing a head injury because the child is alert now"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (He needs neurological observation, and the parents must learn warning signs such as repeated vomiting): Even an alert child with vomiting after a head injury needs neurological observation, since signs of bleeding or swelling can appear hours later. The parents need clear teaching about warning signs such as repeated vomiting, drowsiness or worsening headache. A single breathing check is inadequate. Imaging alone does not replace observation, and headache is only one sign. Being alert now does not remove the risk."}, {"id": 1071, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Taylor, a new graduate, is repeatedly ignored by two senior nurses when asking questions. They roll their eyes, give Taylor the heaviest assignments, and say all new nurses have to earn their place. Taylor is losing sleep and dreads shifts. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Ask the senior nurses privately what Taylor is doing wrong and adjust accordingly", "B": "Recognize lateral violence, document specific incidents, use workplace policy, and seek manager support", "C": "Confront the nurses publicly at the next team huddle to expose their behaviour", "D": "Discuss it with other new graduates and wait to see if the behaviour continues"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepting blame for hostile behaviour", "C": "Public confrontation escalating conflict", "D": "Waiting and relying on peers instead of reporting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C132", "cluster_text": "Taylor is a new graduate practical nurse on a busy medical unit. During the first months Taylor meets hostile behaviour from colleagues, a harassment concern, a disagreement with a physician about a client's plan, and an incident that tests the unit's safety culture. Each situation calls for professional communication and reporting through the proper channels.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Recognize lateral violence, document specific incidents, use workplace policy, and seek manager support): Repeated exclusion, unfair assignments and belittling are forms of lateral violence that harm nurses and client safety, so Taylor documents incidents, uses the policy and seeks manager support. Asking what Taylor is doing wrong accepts blame for the behaviour. Venting to peers and waiting allows harm to continue. Public confrontation or accepting it as a rite of passage is also unhelpful."}, {"id": 1072, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A colleague repeatedly makes sexual comments to Taylor during shifts, and Taylor feels uncomfortable working alone with him. Taylor has told him to stop twice. The behaviour continues, and a manager is on duty today. Which step is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Tell him in writing to stop, and report only if the comments continue after a month", "B": "Report it to the manager using the harassment policy and keep dated notes of each incident", "C": "Handle it privately with the colleague and avoid records to keep the team relationship intact", "D": "Request a schedule change so Taylor avoids him, and report if comments increase later"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying reporting of continued harassment", "C": "Handling harassment quietly without a record", "D": "Avoiding the colleague instead of using the policy"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C132", "cluster_text": "Taylor is a new graduate practical nurse on a busy medical unit. During the first months Taylor meets hostile behaviour from colleagues, a harassment concern, a disagreement with a physician about a client's plan, and an incident that tests the unit's safety culture. Each situation calls for professional communication and reporting through the proper channels.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report it to the manager using the harassment policy and keep dated notes of each incident): Harassment that continues after a clear request to stop should be reported through the formal policy, with dated notes to support the process and protect Taylor. Waiting another month prolongs exposure. Avoidance through scheduling leaves the behaviour unchallenged. Quiet handling without records, or waiting for an appraisal, also fails to use the policy."}, {"id": 1073, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Taylor questions a physician's order for a drug to which the client has a listed allergy. The physician says the order stands and walks away. The client has not yet received the drug. How should Taylor handle the interprofessional disagreement?", "options": {"A": "Hold the drug, state the allergy concern calmly, and escalate through the chain of command", "B": "Document that the physician refused to listen and move on to the next task", "C": "Give the drug but monitor closely for a reaction and keep emergency medications nearby", "D": "Ask the pharmacist to dispense it, then give the drug if the pharmacist agrees"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Documenting without protecting the client", "C": "Giving a known allergen with only monitoring", "D": "Using the pharmacist to override a prescriber without escalation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C132", "cluster_text": "Taylor is a new graduate practical nurse on a busy medical unit. During the first months Taylor meets hostile behaviour from colleagues, a harassment concern, a disagreement with a physician about a client's plan, and an incident that tests the unit's safety culture. Each situation calls for professional communication and reporting through the proper channels.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold the drug, state the allergy concern calmly, and escalate through the chain of command): A nurse is accountable for client safety and must not give a drug that appears unsafe, so Taylor holds it, communicates the allergy clearly and escalates through the chain of command. Giving it with monitoring exposes the client to a known allergen. Pharmacist agreement does not resolve the unsafe order or replace escalation. Documenting and moving on, or letting the family choose, also fails the client."}, {"id": 1074, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "After a medication error that reached a client without harm, the nurse manager asks Taylor how the unit should respond. Staff are worried that the nurse who made the error will be disciplined. Which approach best supports a patient safety culture?", "options": {"A": "Identify the person who erred, issue a warning and post the lesson learned for all", "B": "Complete an incident report only if the client shows signs of harm in the next week", "C": "Review contributing system factors, share learning with the team, and support the nurse while reporting", "D": "Talk privately with the nurse and handle follow-up within the unit without a formal report"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Blaming one nurse and publicizing the punishment", "B": "Reporting only when harm appears", "D": "Keeping an error informal and unreported"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C132", "cluster_text": "Taylor is a new graduate practical nurse on a busy medical unit. During the first months Taylor meets hostile behaviour from colleagues, a harassment concern, a disagreement with a physician about a client's plan, and an incident that tests the unit's safety culture. Each situation calls for professional communication and reporting through the proper channels.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Review contributing system factors, share learning with the team, and support the nurse while reporting): A safety culture treats errors as chances to find system weaknesses, so the unit reviews contributing factors, shares learning and supports the nurse while reporting appropriately. Reporting only after harm misses near misses and no-harm events. An informal talk with no report prevents system learning. Blaming and posting the person, or waiting for a complaint, undermines trust."}, {"id": 1075, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On morning rounds Mr. Peters is lying flat with the bed lowered, gripping the sheet. His respirations are 32 with audible wheezing, his SpO2 is 88%, and he says he cannot get air. His breakfast tray sits untouched beside him. What is the best first action?", "options": {"A": "Turn him onto his side and recheck his oxygen saturation after five minutes.", "B": "Start his as-needed bronchodilator while he lies flat, then reposition him later.", "C": "Raise his oxygen flow to ten litres per minute by mask before changing his position.", "D": "Sit him upright, leaning forward over the overbed table with pillows, then reassess."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Side lying that does not ease breathing", "B": "Treating before correcting position", "C": "High oxygen flow risks suppressing the respiratory drive in COPD"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C129", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dale Peters, 67, is admitted to a medical ward with a COPD exacerbation and left lower lobe pneumonia. A mild stroke last year left slight right facial weakness. He receives oxygen at 2 L/min by nasal prongs, has a wet productive cough, and tires easily when speaking. Admission vital signs: respirations 28, SpO2 90%, heart rate 98, temperature 37.8 C. You are the practical nurse assigned to him and you report to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sit him upright, leaning forward over the overbed table with pillows, then reassess.): Leaning forward in an upright tripod position lets the diaphragm and accessory muscles expand the chest, which eases the work of breathing for a client with wheezing and an SpO2 of 88%. Side lying does not relieve the work of breathing and delays help. Giving medication while flat misses the simplest first step. Raising oxygen flow alone does not ease the work of breathing, so he is positioned upright while prescribed oxygen is applied, not afterward, and coaching breathing in a lowered bed does not help."}, {"id": 1076, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The next morning Mr. Peters is newly confused and restless. His temperature is 37.2 C, his cough is weak, and his respirations are 26. His daughter says he has never been like this before. Which statement best explains these findings in an older adult with pneumonia?", "options": {"A": "A normal temperature shows the pneumonia has cleared, so the confusion must have an unrelated cause.", "B": "Confusion in older adults with pneumonia usually reflects dehydration and improves after fluids alone.", "C": "Older adults with pneumonia often show new confusion and fast breathing without a high fever.", "D": "New confusion suggests a stroke recurrence, so pneumonia can be set aside as the cause."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Equating a normal temperature with resolved infection", "B": "Attributing confusion only to dehydration", "D": "Anchoring on his stroke history and ignoring infection"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C129", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dale Peters, 67, is admitted to a medical ward with a COPD exacerbation and left lower lobe pneumonia. A mild stroke last year left slight right facial weakness. He receives oxygen at 2 L/min by nasal prongs, has a wet productive cough, and tires easily when speaking. Admission vital signs: respirations 28, SpO2 90%, heart rate 98, temperature 37.8 C. You are the practical nurse assigned to him and you report to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Older adults with pneumonia often show new confusion and fast breathing without a high fever.): Pneumonia in older adults often presents atypically, with new confusion, tachypnea and little or no fever, so Mr. Peters' findings fit his infection. Dehydration may contribute but fluids alone will not treat hypoxia or sepsis. His past stroke does not explain why pneumonia is irrelevant, and new confusion needs prompt reporting. A normal temperature does not mean the pneumonia has cleared."}, {"id": 1077, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At breakfast Mr. Peters coughs, and his voice sounds wet after each sip of water from his cup. His stroke left slight right facial weakness. He asks for more juice because he feels dry. Which action is best for the nurse to take?", "options": {"A": "Encourage him to lean back slightly while swallowing so the fluids pass down quickly.", "B": "Give thin fluids by straw in smaller sips so that he can drink more safely.", "C": "Thicken his juice with the unit thickener and let him keep drinking it slowly.", "D": "Hold oral fluids, keep him upright, and report the coughing so swallowing can be assessed."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaning back widens aspiration risk", "B": "Straw use increases speed of fluid entering the airway", "C": "Choosing a fluid consistency without a swallowing assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C129", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dale Peters, 67, is admitted to a medical ward with a COPD exacerbation and left lower lobe pneumonia. A mild stroke last year left slight right facial weakness. He receives oxygen at 2 L/min by nasal prongs, has a wet productive cough, and tires easily when speaking. Admission vital signs: respirations 28, SpO2 90%, heart rate 98, temperature 37.8 C. You are the practical nurse assigned to him and you report to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold oral fluids, keep him upright, and report the coughing so swallowing can be assessed.): Coughing and a wet voice after drinking, with a prior stroke, signal aspiration risk, so oral intake should pause until a swallowing assessment is done. Thickening fluids is a treatment decision that depends on that assessment. Straws and reclining both make aspiration more likely. Report to the RN and offer mouth care while he waits."}, {"id": 1078, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before discharge planning, the RN asks you to coach Mr. Peters with his incentive spirometer. He takes short quick sniffs, the indicator barely rises, and he tires and becomes breathless. His SpO2 is 93% and his heart rate is 96. What coaching is best?", "options": {"A": "Have him take ten rapid breaths in a row, then rest for an hour.", "B": "Sit him upright and coach slow deep inhalation with a short hold, resting between repetitions.", "C": "Stop the exercise and wait for the physiotherapist to teach it before he tries again.", "D": "Lower the target marker so the indicator rises easily, then continue the same technique."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Rapid breathing instead of slow deep inhalation", "C": "Withholding a routine nursing teaching task unnecessarily", "D": "Changing the goal instead of correcting technique"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C129", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dale Peters, 67, is admitted to a medical ward with a COPD exacerbation and left lower lobe pneumonia. A mild stroke last year left slight right facial weakness. He receives oxygen at 2 L/min by nasal prongs, has a wet productive cough, and tires easily when speaking. Admission vital signs: respirations 28, SpO2 90%, heart rate 98, temperature 37.8 C. You are the practical nurse assigned to him and you report to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sit him upright and coach slow deep inhalation with a short hold, resting between repetitions.): A slow deep inhalation with a brief hold expands the alveoli, and rests between breaths prevent the fatigue that is limiting Mr. Peters. Rapid breaths only move the indicator briefly and tire him. Lowering the target hides poor technique. Long continuous sessions cause breathlessness. Stopping until a specialist arrives delays a task within the nurse's role."}, {"id": 1079, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Vance, 58, is drowsy in the post-anesthesia care unit after abdominal surgery. He snores loudly with each breath and his SpO2 has fallen to 91%. His wife says he always snores at home, and his color looks pale. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Rub his chest firmly to wake him, then recheck his oxygen saturation in fifteen minutes.", "B": "Turn him onto his back with the head flat and reassess when his snoring settles.", "C": "Leave him undisturbed because snoring in a sleeping client is normal and reassuring after surgery.", "D": "Open his airway with a chin lift, stimulate him, give oxygen as ordered, call the RN."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stimulating without opening the airway or giving oxygen", "B": "Positioning that worsens airway obstruction", "C": "Accepting snoring as normal after anesthesia"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Open his airway with a chin lift, stimulate him, give oxygen as ordered, call the RN.): Loud snoring with falling SpO2 after anesthesia signals partial airway obstruction from the tongue or relaxed tissues. Opening the airway, stimulating the client, giving oxygen and calling the RN is the priority. Rubbing his chest and waiting fifteen minutes delays airway care. A flat head position can worsen obstruction. Dimming lights or calling snoring normal ignores the desaturation."}, {"id": 1080, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After a child dies in the emergency department, the charge nurse schedules a team debriefing for the end of the shift. One nurse says she would rather not attend because she feels overwhelmed. What should the practical nurse understand about debriefing?", "options": {"A": "Debriefing is a formal investigation, and staff who attend give statements for the file.", "B": "Debriefing is offered to support staff and attendance is voluntary, with other supports available.", "C": "Debriefing should wait one month, since early discussion increases distress after trauma.", "D": "Attendance is required for all staff who were present, since the debrief protects the whole unit."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confusing debriefing with an investigation", "C": "Believing early debriefing worsens distress", "D": "Believing participation can be forced"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Debriefing is offered to support staff and attendance is voluntary, with other supports available.): Critical incident debriefing offers a supportive group review but it is voluntary, and forcing attendance can cause further distress. Colleagues who decline can still use an employee assistance program or peer support. It is not an investigation. Delaying it a month is not standard practice. It does not replace individual support, and staff are not penalized for declining."}, {"id": 1081, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Faxing from your clinic, you sent a client's laboratory report to a wrong number, and you notice only an hour later. The agency policy requires every privacy breach to go to the privacy officer. No harm has been reported. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Leave it unreported because the number belongs to a business and no harm appears likely.", "B": "Tell your manager and the privacy officer right away, and document the facts.", "C": "Telephone the recipient to ask for the fax to be destroyed and then close the matter.", "D": "Wait until the client asks about the report before deciding whether to disclose."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignoring a breach because harm seems unlikely", "C": "Handling the breach alone without reporting", "D": "Delaying disclosure until the client asks"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tell your manager and the privacy officer right away, and document the facts.): A misdirected fax is a privacy breach even when no harm has occurred, and the policy requires the privacy officer to guide containment and notification. The nurse reports promptly and documents objective facts. Calling the recipient may be part of the response, but only as directed by the privacy officer. Silence or waiting leaves the client and agency unprotected."}, {"id": 1082, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In the emergency department, a 35-year-old man shouts at staff, clenches his fists and steps toward you, saying you are wasting his time. You are alone in the triage room and the door is half closed. What is the best first action?", "options": {"A": "Keep a clear exit, speak calmly, step back, and call security for help.", "B": "Tell him he will be removed from the department if he does not stop shouting.", "C": "Block the doorway and speak firmly until he lowers his voice and sits down again.", "D": "Handle the situation alone to show colleagues that you can manage difficult clients."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Issuing threats that raise tension", "C": "Blocking the exit and escalating the confrontation", "D": "Handling aggression alone to prove competence"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep a clear exit, speak calmly, step back, and call security for help.): Escalating anger with a step toward staff is a sign of possible violence, and the nurse keeps an exit, stays calm and calls for help early under the agency's violence policy. Blocking the doorway traps the nurse and raises tension. Handling it alone puts staff at risk. Threats tend to inflame rather than settle a client who is already agitated."}, {"id": 1083, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the ward, a 66-year-old man reports chest pressure. His chart shows a stomach ulcer bleed two months ago and he is not on a blood thinner. The RN is nearby. Which action is the best for the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Give ASA with milk to protect his stomach and then tell the RN.", "B": "Give ASA from the unit stock immediately because it is standard for chest pain.", "C": "Stay with him, report the chest pain to the RN, and mention the bleeding history.", "D": "Ask him to rest and report the pain after you finish the medication pass."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming milk makes ASA safe after a bleed", "B": "Giving ASA without checking the bleeding history", "D": "Delaying reporting of chest pain"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stay with him, report the chest pain to the RN, and mention the bleeding history.): Chest pressure needs immediate reporting, and ASA must be given only on order or directive after checking contraindications such as a recent gastrointestinal bleed. Staying with the client and informing the RN lets the team decide on ASA safely. Giving it at once could worsen bleeding. Waiting to report loses time in a possible cardiac event."}, {"id": 1084, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "You are running behind on the morning medication pass in acute care. At 0900 you have a pre-meal insulin dose, a scheduled IV antibiotic, a vitamin and a stool softener due for four different clients. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Give all medications in room order so no client waits longer than any other client.", "B": "Ask the RN to take over the insulin while you finish everything else in room order.", "C": "Give the insulin with the meal and the IV antibiotic on time first, then the others.", "D": "Give the vitamin and stool softener first since they are the quickest to prepare and pass."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating all medications as equally flexible", "B": "Delegating the time-critical drug unnecessarily", "D": "Prioritizing speed over time sensitivity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give the insulin with the meal and the IV antibiotic on time first, then the others.): Insulin tied to a meal and an antibiotic with a set interval are time-critical, so they are given within their windows first. A vitamin and stool softener can be given a little later without harm. Passing medications in room order ignores the difference in urgency. The nurse asks for help if overwhelmed but remains accountable for prioritizing."}, {"id": 1085, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, the nurse finds a 2-year-old with a fresh scald on his forearm from spilled tea. His mother has already spread butter on it. The skin is red and blistering, and he is crying. What should the nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Apply a tight occlusive dressing over the burn and monitor the skin carefully for twenty-four hours.", "B": "Remove the butter, cool the burn under running water for twenty minutes, and arrange assessment.", "C": "Apply a thin layer of petroleum jelly over the burn and cover it loosely.", "D": "Cool the burn in a cold water bath for two minutes, then apply a dressing."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying assessment of a blistering burn in a toddler", "C": "Using an occlusive ointment on a fresh burn", "D": "Cooling for far too short a time"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Remove the butter, cool the burn under running water for twenty minutes, and arrange assessment.): Cool running water for about twenty minutes limits burn depth, and a blistering scald in a toddler needs medical assessment. Butter traps heat and raises infection risk, so it is removed. Petroleum jelly also traps heat. Two minutes of cooling is too short to limit depth. Tight occlusive dressings, ice and draining blisters can all cause harm."}, {"id": 1086, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Hassan, 74, lives in long-term care and is due for levodopa at lunch, and his tray holds a large chicken meal. He has no nausea today. The order states to give the dose before meals. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Give levodopa with the chicken meal so that you are sure he finishes eating his tray.", "B": "Postpone the medication until lunch has finished, then administer it and document the delayed dose.", "C": "Give levodopa thirty minutes before the meal, since protein competes with its absorption.", "D": "Administer it halfway through the meal as a compromise between the order and his appetite."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving levodopa with protein reducing absorption", "B": "Delaying a time-sensitive medication", "D": "Compromising the timing against the order"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give levodopa thirty minutes before the meal, since protein competes with its absorption.): Protein competes with levodopa for absorption in the gut, so a dose before meals gives more reliable control of symptoms, and he has no nausea that would justify giving it with food. Giving it with the meal or midway reduces the effect. Holding it until later lets symptoms such as rigidity return. Following the order protects both timing and function."}, {"id": 1087, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, Mrs. Helen Brandt, 79, wears short sleeves and you see grip-shaped bruises on both upper arms. Her husband says she bruises easily because of her blood thinner. Mrs. Brandt looks at him before saying nothing. What should the nurse do next?", "options": {"A": "Complete a skin assessment, document objective findings, and report your concerns to the RN.", "B": "Ask the husband directly whether he has been gripping his wife's arms too hard.", "C": "Ask Mrs. Brandt privately about the bruises at the next visit before reporting anything.", "D": "Tell the husband the bruises look suspicious and ask him to see her physician."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Confronting a suspected abuser directly", "C": "Delaying a report while gathering more information", "D": "Alerting the possible abuser and bypassing the RN"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C130", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Helen Brandt, 79, receives home care visits after a hip fracture repair. She lives with her husband, 81, who helps with her care, and her adult son manages her banking. She takes a blood thinner for atrial fibrillation and uses a walker. You are the practical nurse making the visits, and you report to the RN case manager who coordinates her services.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Complete a skin assessment, document objective findings, and report your concerns to the RN.): Grip-pattern bruises on both upper arms and a fearful glance are red flags that a blood thinner does not fully explain. Objective documentation and reporting to the RN keeps the response safe and within scope. Waiting until the next visit delays protection. Confronting the husband risks her safety and bypasses the team. Accepting the blood thinner explanation or questioning him directly is also unsafe."}, {"id": 1088, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a later visit Mrs. Brandt says she skipped buying her pills because her account is empty. She did not agree to the withdrawals and her son will not show her the statements. Which interpretation of these findings is best?", "options": {"A": "This may be financial abuse, so document her words and report it to the RN.", "B": "This is likely a bank error, so suggest she phone the bank to dispute it.", "C": "This reflects memory loss, so ask her to review her bank statements herself.", "D": "This is her son's responsibility, so refer her to a lawyer and note nothing further."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating possible abuse as a banking mistake", "C": "Attributing the report to memory loss without evidence", "D": "Referring out without documenting or reporting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C130", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Helen Brandt, 79, receives home care visits after a hip fracture repair. She lives with her husband, 81, who helps with her care, and her adult son manages her banking. She takes a blood thinner for atrial fibrillation and uses a walker. You are the practical nurse making the visits, and you report to the RN case manager who coordinates her services.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (This may be financial abuse, so document her words and report it to the RN.): Unexplained withdrawals she did not agree to, a refusal to share statements, and missed medication because of lack of funds are indicators of financial abuse. Nurses document the client's own words and report to the RN. A bank dispute does not address possible abuse. A referral without documentation leaves her at risk. Calling it normal or private ignores the nurse's duty."}, {"id": 1089, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Brandt, 81, tells you he is exhausted after months of lifting his wife at night. Mrs. Brandt has an unwashed odour and a reddened sacrum today. He tears up and asks whether someone could please help them both. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Arrange a nursing home placement for Mrs. Brandt without consulting the RN or the family.", "B": "Report the neglect alone, then step back from the household while the investigation runs its course.", "C": "Teach him safe lifting techniques and advise him to ask his son for help overnight.", "D": "Report the skin findings and his exhaustion to the RN so respite can be arranged."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Making a placement decision beyond the nurse's role", "B": "Reporting alone and withdrawing support", "C": "Teaching only, without reporting or arranging support"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C130", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Helen Brandt, 79, receives home care visits after a hip fracture repair. She lives with her husband, 81, who helps with her care, and her adult son manages her banking. She takes a blood thinner for atrial fibrillation and uses a walker. You are the practical nurse making the visits, and you report to the RN case manager who coordinates her services.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the skin findings and his exhaustion to the RN so respite can be arranged.): This picture fits neglect arising from caregiver burnout, because he asked for help and the care gaps followed his exhaustion. The nurse reports both findings so the team can arrange respite, and does not act alone. Arranging placement alone oversteps the nurse's role. Lifting teaching and relying on the son leaves the skin and exhaustion unreported. Taking over care alone or only reassuring him is also inadequate."}, {"id": 1090, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Alone with you at the clinic, Mrs. Brandt says in a low voice, my son gets angry when I ask about money. She changes the subject when you ask more. You suspect abuse but her disclosure is vague. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Press her gently to name who is hurting her before deciding whether to report.", "B": "Wait until she gives clearer details before deciding whether to share anything with the team.", "C": "Tell her you will keep it between you, unless she describes physical harm later.", "D": "Listen without leading, record her exact words, and report the disclosure to the RN today."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pushing for details before sharing a concern", "B": "Delaying reporting until the disclosure is clear", "C": "Offering confidentiality that has a condition attached"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C130", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Helen Brandt, 79, receives home care visits after a hip fracture repair. She lives with her husband, 81, who helps with her care, and her adult son manages her banking. She takes a blood thinner for atrial fibrillation and uses a walker. You are the practical nurse making the visits, and you report to the RN case manager who coordinates her services.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Listen without leading, record her exact words, and report the disclosure to the RN today.): A vague disclosure still needs to be shared, because safety concerns override a request for secrecy. Recording her exact words without leading questions preserves accuracy for the RN. Pressing her to name someone is leading. Promising confidentiality, even conditionally, cannot be kept. Waiting for clearer details or questioning her son directly delays safety."}, {"id": 1091, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In hospital, a 70-year-old client with Parkinson disease is two hours late for his scheduled dose after a delay on the unit. He now has marked rigidity, slow speech and a tremor. His next dose is due in two hours. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Hold the late dose until the physician reviews the order at morning rounds.", "B": "Give a sedative as ordered for the tremor and recheck rigidity after an hour.", "C": "Report the delay to the RN, give the dose as ordered, and assess swallowing.", "D": "Treat it as a minor delay and give the next dose on schedule."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withholding a time-critical medication", "B": "Treating the symptom instead of the delayed dose", "D": "Treating a missed dose as minor"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the delay to the RN, give the dose as ordered, and assess swallowing.): Delayed Parkinson medication can bring rigidity, trouble swallowing and falls within hours, so it is time-critical. The nurse reports to the RN, gives the late dose as ordered, and checks swallowing. Holding it for rounds prolongs the symptoms. A sedative does not treat the cause and may worsen swallowing. Skipping the dose or treating it as minor leaves him at risk."}, {"id": 1092, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Phoning the clinic, a parent says her toddler swallowed some drain cleaner from under the sink. The child is awake, crying and has a red mouth. The parent offers to give him milk first. He is two years old and weighs 12 kg. What advice is best?", "options": {"A": "Tell her to give activated charcoal from her cabinet and call if he worsens.", "B": "Tell her to give small sips of milk first and call only if he worsens.", "C": "Have her rinse his mouth with water, then monitor him at home.", "D": "Have her call the poison control centre now, with the container in hand."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using charcoal, which does not help with caustics", "B": "Giving dilution fluids first without poison guidance", "C": "Home monitoring after a caustic ingestion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Have her call the poison control centre now, with the container in hand.): Caustic ingestion needs immediate expert direction, so the parent calls the poison control centre with the container. Charcoal does not bind caustics and delays proper advice. Rinsing and watching at home may miss internal burns. Inducing vomiting must also be avoided, because it re-exposes the esophagus to a caustic product. Giving milk first and calling only if he worsens also delays expert advice."}, {"id": 1093, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Albert Tran, 61, has had a peripheral IV for four days. The site has a red streak 3 cm long, is warm, and he winces when the antibiotic infuses. The IV is still flowing. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Slow the infusion rate and apply a warm compress while keeping the same site.", "B": "Lower the arm below heart level and continue the infusion with more frequent checks.", "C": "Stop the infusion, remove the cannula, report to the RN, and restart at a new site.", "D": "Outline the redness with a pen and recheck it at the end of shift."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Continuing an infusion through an inflamed site", "B": "Trying a positional measure instead of removing the cannula", "D": "Observing a phlebitis streak without removing the IV"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the infusion, remove the cannula, report to the RN, and restart at a new site.): A warm red streak with pain on infusion suggests phlebitis, so the infusion is stopped and the cannula removed. The RN is informed and a new site is chosen under the order. Slowing the rate or lowering the arm keeps irritating medication flowing into an inflamed vein. Flushing to check patency adds more fluid, and outlining the redness for later delays action."}, {"id": 1094, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Gabriel Roy complains of burning pain at his IV site, and you see swelling above the cannula while a chemotherapy drug is infusing. Blood return is poor and the RN is in another room. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Remove the cannula first, then restart the infusion in the opposite arm.", "B": "Stop the infusion at once, leave the cannula in place, and call the RN immediately.", "C": "Slow the infusion rate and apply a cold pack to the site until the drug finishes.", "D": "Flush the cannula briskly with saline to see whether the pain eases."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Removing the cannula before drawing back drug", "C": "Continuing the vesicant infusion while applying cold", "D": "Flushing pushes more drug into the tissue"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C127", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gabriel Roy, 62, is a client on a rural hospital medical unit who receives chemotherapy through a peripheral IV, a heparin infusion for a deep vein thrombosis, and oral medications. One infusion pump on the unit is out of service this week. You are the practical nurse working with the RN, and you follow the facility policy on medication checks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop the infusion at once, leave the cannula in place, and call the RN immediately.): Burning pain, swelling and poor blood return during a vesicant infusion suggest extravasation, which damages tissue every minute the drug runs. The infusion is stopped and the cannula is left in so the team can aspirate drug and follow the policy for treatment. Continuing the infusion or flushing forces more drug into the tissue. Restarting elsewhere without the RN skips the assessment."}, {"id": 1095, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A new heparin bag is hung for Mr. Roy and the pump is being programmed. The pump library lists the drug name and a default concentration. The facility policy requires an independent check for high-alert drugs. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Rely on the pump's dose-error reduction alert, since it flags unsafe concentrations automatically", "B": "Check the programmed settings yourself twice, then start the infusion once both checks agree", "C": "Have a second nurse independently verify the drug, concentration and rate against the order", "D": "Have the nurse who prepared the bag confirm the label while you program the pump"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Trusting technology instead of an independent check", "B": "Substituting a self-check for an independent check", "D": "Using a non-independent verifier"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C127", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gabriel Roy, 62, is a client on a rural hospital medical unit who receives chemotherapy through a peripheral IV, a heparin infusion for a deep vein thrombosis, and oral medications. One infusion pump on the unit is out of service this week. You are the practical nurse working with the RN, and you follow the facility policy on medication checks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Have a second nurse independently verify the drug, concentration and rate against the order): Heparin is a high-alert drug, and an independent second check catches errors in concentration, rate or programming that one nurse can miss. A pump library is a helpful tool but does not replace the check, and an alert may not cover every error. The nurse who prepared the bag is not independent. Checking twice yourself or asking the client is not a valid check."}, {"id": 1096, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The pump for Mr. Roy is out of service, so the RN asks you to run 1000 mL of normal saline over 8 hours by gravity. The tubing drop factor is 15 gtt/mL, and the bag is hanging. What flow rate should you set?", "options": {"A": "Set 31 drops per minute, using volume times drop factor divided by minutes.", "B": "Set 125 drops per minute, taken directly from the hourly volume rate.", "C": "Set 8 drops per minute, found by dividing the hourly volume by the drop factor.", "D": "Set 63 drops per minute, found by using only four hours as the infusion time."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using millilitres per hour as drops per minute", "C": "Dividing instead of multiplying by the drop factor", "D": "Using an incorrect infusion time"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C127", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gabriel Roy, 62, is a client on a rural hospital medical unit who receives chemotherapy through a peripheral IV, a heparin infusion for a deep vein thrombosis, and oral medications. One infusion pump on the unit is out of service this week. You are the practical nurse working with the RN, and you follow the facility policy on medication checks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Set 31 drops per minute, using volume times drop factor divided by minutes.): The gravity formula is volume times drop factor divided by time in minutes: 1000 times 15 divided by 480 equals about 31 drops per minute. The 125 figure is the hourly volume, not a drop rate. Dividing the hourly rate by the drop factor gives about 8, which is wrong. Using four hours instead of eight doubles the rate."}, {"id": 1097, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Roy has an order for furosemide 60 mg by mouth. The pharmacy supplied 40 mg scored tablets. You are tired after a long shift and the medication room is noisy. Which result is correct using the desired over have formula?", "options": {"A": "Give 2 tablets, since rounding up to whole tablets avoids splitting the pill and saves time.", "B": "Give 1.5 tablets, splitting the scored tablet at the score after checking the calculation.", "C": "Give 1 tablet and ask the physician whether the ordered dose can be reduced.", "D": "Give 0.67 of a tablet, found by dividing the dose on hand by the dose desired."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Rounding to whole tablets and overdosing", "C": "Changing the dose without an order", "D": "Inverting desired over have"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C127", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gabriel Roy, 62, is a client on a rural hospital medical unit who receives chemotherapy through a peripheral IV, a heparin infusion for a deep vein thrombosis, and oral medications. One infusion pump on the unit is out of service this week. You are the practical nurse working with the RN, and you follow the facility policy on medication checks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give 1.5 tablets, splitting the scored tablet at the score after checking the calculation.): Desired over have gives 60 divided by 40, which equals 1.5 tablets, and scored tablets can be split safely at the line. Two tablets would deliver 80 mg, which exceeds the order by a third. Inverting the formula gives 0.67, which would underdose. Giving a different dose requires an order, so you check the math and give the ordered amount."}, {"id": 1098, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A 55-year-old man in hospital asks you for a copy of his own chart so that he can see his test results. You are the practical nurse assigned to him, and the unit is quiet. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Tell him that chart copies are only released to his physician or his family.", "B": "Hand him the chart right now because it contains his own personal information.", "C": "Offer to photocopy the nursing notes and withhold the physician and laboratory sections.", "D": "Explain his right of access and direct his request through the health records process."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Wrongly limiting who may receive records", "B": "Releasing the chart without following the facility process", "C": "Releasing only part of the chart informally"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain his right of access and direct his request through the health records process.): Clients have a right of access to their own health information, and the facility process makes sure it is released correctly. The nurse explains this and tells the RN about the request. Releasing only to a physician or family wrongly restricts access. Selective informal copying bypasses the process. Handing over the chart immediately or saying records belong only to the hospital is also wrong."}, {"id": 1099, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Cleaning a clinic storage room, you find boxes of old paper client records in the recycling bin, with names and diagnoses visible. A coworker says the notes are far too old to matter. The clinic closes for the weekend tomorrow. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Secure the records, tell your manager or the privacy officer, and follow the secure destruction policy.", "B": "Remove the visible names with a marker and leave the boxes in recycling.", "C": "Put the boxes in the locked storage room and follow up after the weekend.", "D": "Tear the pages in half and put them into the regular garbage."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believing redaction with a marker is adequate", "C": "Delaying reporting and secure destruction", "D": "Partial destruction still exposes identifiers"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Secure the records, tell your manager or the privacy officer, and follow the secure destruction policy.): Paper records with names and diagnoses must be destroyed securely, by shredding or a certified service under the retention schedule, never in recycling. Securing them and telling the manager or privacy officer protects privacy. A marker may not hide the information. Storing them and waiting leaves a possible breach unreported. Tearing pages in half, or leaving them in the bin, is also inadequate."}, {"id": 1100, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ben Walters, 63, returns to the ward two hours after a bowel resection. His blood pressure is 100/60 and he is drowsy but arousable. His wife asks how often you will check his vital signs. What is the best plan?", "options": {"A": "Check them every thirty minutes for twenty-four hours, then stop monitoring.", "B": "Check them every two hours for the first day, then once daily.", "C": "Check frequently at first as ordered, spacing them out only as he remains stable.", "D": "Check them every four hours starting from his arrival on the ward and continuing overnight."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stopping monitoring abruptly after a fixed period", "B": "Fixed interval too long for the early postoperative period", "D": "Using routine intervals too early after surgery"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check frequently at first as ordered, spacing them out only as he remains stable.): The first hours after major surgery carry the highest risk of bleeding and shock, so vital signs are taken often at first and spaced as the client stays stable. A fixed thirty minute schedule that stops at twenty-four hours ignores his changing condition. Two hourly checks are too infrequent for this stage. A four hour interval from arrival or one check per shift is unsafe."}, {"id": 1101, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Kyle Reddick, 28, is brought to the emergency department by his sister. He has a history of schizophrenia, says strangers are tracking him, and refuses blood tests. He can explain what the tests are for and what happens if he declines. What should the nurse understand?", "options": {"A": "A diagnosis alone does not remove capacity, which depends on understanding the decision and its consequences.", "B": "Refusing tests shows he lacks capacity, so staff can proceed without discussion.", "C": "Capacity is decided only by his sister since she knows his usual behaviour.", "D": "His diagnosis means he lacks capacity, so his sister can consent for him now."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating refusal as proof of incapacity", "C": "Giving a relative the capacity decision", "D": "Assuming diagnosis equals incapacity"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C139", "cluster_text": "Kyle Reddick, 28, is brought to a hospital emergency department by his sister, who reports he has not slept for three days. He has a history of schizophrenia and takes olanzapine inconsistently. He is anxious and guarded, and he speaks quietly. You are the practical nurse working with the RN and the emergency physician, and you follow provincial mental health legislation.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A diagnosis alone does not remove capacity, which depends on understanding the decision and its consequences.): Capacity is decision-specific and rests on whether the client understands the information and can weigh consequences. Mr. Reddick can explain the tests and the effect of declining, so a diagnosis of schizophrenia alone does not remove his capacity. Refusal itself is not evidence of incapacity. The capacity assessment is made by the treating team, not a relative."}, {"id": 1102, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Later in the emergency department, Mr. Reddick says he will end his life tonight and tries to leave. He appears to be hearing voices. His sister has gone to move her car. He is pacing near the exit and sweating. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "His refusal to stay should be accepted because a competent adult may leave.", "B": "He may meet general criteria for involuntary assessment, so alert the RN and physician now.", "C": "He should be moved to a quiet room, and his sister asked to supervise him.", "D": "He should be allowed to leave if he agrees to return for assessment tomorrow."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating refusal as final despite risk", "C": "Relying on a family member for safety", "D": "Accepting a promise to return despite imminent risk"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C139", "cluster_text": "Kyle Reddick, 28, is brought to a hospital emergency department by his sister, who reports he has not slept for three days. He has a history of schizophrenia and takes olanzapine inconsistently. He is anxious and guarded, and he speaks quietly. You are the practical nurse working with the RN and the emergency physician, and you follow provincial mental health legislation.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (He may meet general criteria for involuntary assessment, so alert the RN and physician now.): A stated plan to end his life with signs of psychosis suggests a risk of serious harm linked to a mental disorder, which is the core of involuntary assessment criteria. The nurse keeps him safe and alerts the RN and physician now. A sister cannot provide supervision for this risk. A promise to return does not manage tonight's danger. Accepting his departure or calling it anxiety is unsafe."}, {"id": 1103, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the unit, Mr. Reddick declines his olanzapine tablet. His sister, visiting, insists you hide it in his pudding. He is calm and says he dislikes the weight gain it causes. It is the evening medication round. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Respect his refusal, explain the risks, and inform the RN so the team can respond.", "B": "Offer the tablet again in thirty minutes and mention the physician's ordered dose.", "C": "Chart the refusal and give the missed dose before bedtime without informing anyone.", "D": "Tell him the physician ordered the drug so he must take it."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Pressuring a client with capacity after refusal", "C": "Giving a refused medication and not informing the team", "D": "Coercing a client with capacity"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C139", "cluster_text": "Kyle Reddick, 28, is brought to a hospital emergency department by his sister, who reports he has not slept for three days. He has a history of schizophrenia and takes olanzapine inconsistently. He is anxious and guarded, and he speaks quietly. You are the practical nurse working with the RN and the emergency physician, and you follow provincial mental health legislation.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Respect his refusal, explain the risks, and inform the RN so the team can respond.): A client with capacity has the right to refuse treatment, and a relative's request does not override it. The nurse explains the risks, listens to his concern about weight gain, and reports to the RN so the team can respond. Repeated pressure or stating the order does not respect refusal. Giving the dose later without informing anyone is covert. Hiding it in pudding is also wrong."}, {"id": 1104, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a private talk, Mr. Reddick tells you he plans to hurt the neighbour he believes is tracking him. He asks you to promise that you will tell no one at all. He is sitting alone in the quiet room. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Ask him whether he truly intends to act, and report only if he says yes.", "B": "Keep it private until you see whether he repeats the statement again tomorrow.", "C": "Report it to the neighbour's family and the police yourself after your shift ends.", "D": "Explain that confidentiality has limits when someone may be harmed, and report to the team."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Making reporting depend on his answer", "B": "Delaying reporting of a threat", "C": "Acting alone outside the team and the proper process"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C139", "cluster_text": "Kyle Reddick, 28, is brought to a hospital emergency department by his sister, who reports he has not slept for three days. He has a history of schizophrenia and takes olanzapine inconsistently. He is anxious and guarded, and he speaks quietly. You are the practical nurse working with the RN and the emergency physician, and you follow provincial mental health legislation.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that confidentiality has limits when someone may be harmed, and report to the team.): Confidentiality is limited when there is a risk of serious harm to others, so the practical nurse shares the threat with the RN and team right away. Reporting only if he confirms intent delays action. Contacting others yourself bypasses the team and the proper process. Promising secrecy or waiting to see if he repeats the statement is dishonest and unsafe."}, {"id": 1105, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An 81-year-old woman in hospital has bruises on her back. Her daughter stays at the bedside and answers every question for her mother. You need to ask screening questions about safety at home. Visiting hours are nearly over. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Ask the daughter about the bruises first and accept her explanation before moving on.", "B": "Wait until the daughter leaves for the day and ask only if the client seems upset.", "C": "Find a routine reason for the daughter to step out, then ask open questions privately.", "D": "Ask the screening questions in front of the daughter, using gentle and indirect wording."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on the suspected person's account", "B": "Delaying screening until an opportunity appears by chance", "D": "Asking in front of a possible abuser"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Find a routine reason for the daughter to step out, then ask open questions privately.): Older clients may not disclose abuse in front of a family member, so the nurse creates a routine private moment such as an examination. Open questions in private let her speak freely. Asking in front of the daughter may silence her. Waiting passively or relying only on the daughter's account leaves a possible risk unassessed."}, {"id": 1106, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 48-year-old man rates his incisional pain 8 out of 10 three hours after surgery. Acetaminophen given an hour ago did not help. He declines the ordered opioid because he fears becoming addicted. His respirations are 16 and he is alert. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Offer a non-drug measure such as repositioning and ice, and offer the opioid later.", "B": "Respect his refusal, document it, and plan to offer the opioid again next shift.", "C": "Give a second acetaminophen dose now, one hour after the first, to avoid using the opioid.", "D": "Explain that short-term opioid use after surgery carries a low risk, offer the dose, reassess."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying opioid for severe pain with only comfort measures", "B": "Accepting refusal without addressing his fear", "C": "Duplicating a drug that has already failed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that short-term opioid use after surgery carries a low risk, offer the dose, reassess.): Untreated severe pain slows recovery and breathing, and short-term opioid use under supervision carries a low addiction risk. The nurse listens to his fear, explains, offers the dose and reassesses. Comfort measures alone are not enough for pain of 8 out of 10. Deferring to next shift leaves him in pain. A second early acetaminophen dose is unsafe."}, {"id": 1107, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Omar Yusuf, 52, has cirrhosis with a swollen abdomen, and the RN asks you to monitor his ascites. He is drinking well and his urine output seems normal this week. His daily weight sheet is blank today. Which method gives the best tracking?", "options": {"A": "Weigh him daily at the same time and measure girth at the same abdominal landmark.", "B": "Weigh him daily on any available scale and estimate girth by eye from the bedside.", "C": "Rely on intake and output records, since they show the same trend as his weight.", "D": "Weigh him every second day and measure girth wherever the tape sits comfortably."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using inconsistent scales and estimating instead of measuring", "C": "Using intake and output alone", "D": "Using inconsistent timing and measurement sites"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Weigh him daily at the same time and measure girth at the same abdominal landmark.): Daily weights at the same time on the same scale and a girth measurement at a marked point detect fluid changes that intake and output can miss, since fluid collects in the abdomen. Different scales and visual estimates are unreliable. Weighing every second day and varying the tape site makes the trend inconsistent. Relying on intake and output alone misses fluid retained in the abdomen."}, {"id": 1108, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Ines Duarte, 78, is bedbound and has a reddened area over her sacrum that stays red after you relieve the pressure. She was last turned two hours ago and now lies on her back. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Place her on her back with a pillow under the sacrum to lift the area.", "B": "Apply a thick gauze pad and leave her position unchanged for four hours.", "C": "Turn her onto her back again and rub the reddened area to improve circulation.", "D": "Turn her onto her side at a thirty-degree tilt, off the red area, regularly."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Positioning that keeps pressure on the sacrum", "B": "Padding the area but not relieving pressure", "C": "Massaging an injured area"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Turn her onto her side at a thirty-degree tilt, off the red area, regularly.): Persistent redness after pressure is relieved is an early sign of pressure injury, so weight must come off the area and repositioning continues on a regular schedule. A thirty-degree side-lying position reduces pressure on the sacrum. A pillow under the sacrum increases pressure. Padding without turning leaves pressure in place. Rubbing the area damages tissue."}, {"id": 1109, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Tomas Reyes, 49, has a thoracic incision, breathes shallowly and refuses to cough because it hurts. His SpO2 is 92%, and the PRN analgesic is due, as the last dose was four hours ago. What is the best sequence?", "options": {"A": "Have him cough hard immediately to clear secretions before any medication.", "B": "Give the ordered analgesic, let it work, then coach splinting with a pillow and deep breaths.", "C": "Withhold the analgesic so he stays alert enough to cough on his own.", "D": "Coach splinting and deep breathing first, and give the analgesic only if he asks again."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Forcing a painful cough", "C": "Withholding analgesia for alertness", "D": "Coaching before pain relief"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give the ordered analgesic, let it work, then coach splinting with a pillow and deep breaths.): Pain controls his shallow breathing, so relieving it first allows effective splinting, deep breathing and coughing that prevent atelectasis. Coaching without analgesia is unlikely to work. Withholding analgesia keeps him in pain and hypoventilating. Forcing a hard cough against a fresh incision increases pain and distress."}, {"id": 1110, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Teaching is planned by a community nurse for Mr. Lee, 59, who was recently diagnosed with hypertension and lives alone. He has not yet been seen by the dietitian. Before planning any teaching sessions for him, what should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Ask what he already knows about hypertension and what he wants to learn.", "B": "Give him a pamphlet on diet and exercise and review it together at the first visit.", "C": "Start with a detailed explanation of how blood pressure medications work in the body.", "D": "Plan teaching around the topics that the nurse considers to be the most important."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Teaching content before assessing learning needs", "C": "Beginning with unrequested detail", "D": "Imposing the nurse's priorities"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask what he already knows about hypertension and what he wants to learn.): Teaching begins with assessing the client's learning needs, current knowledge and readiness so that content fits his goals. A pamphlet or detailed drug lecture assumes needs that have not been checked. Teaching only what the nurse considers important can miss what Mr. Lee actually wants and is ready to learn."}, {"id": 1111, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Dana Cole, 28, is on postpartum day 1 with a second-degree perineal tear and asks how to clean the area at home. She says she wants to use soap and scrub the stitches gently. Her baby is feeding well. What should you teach?", "options": {"A": "Wipe from back to front with dry tissue after each void to keep the area dry.", "B": "Rinse with warm water from a peri bottle, then pat dry front to back.", "C": "Rinse with warm water, then rub the area briskly with a towel to dry.", "D": "Take a hot sitz bath with scented salts after each void to ease healing."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Wiping from back to front spreading bacteria", "C": "Friction on a perineal repair", "D": "Using irritating products and excess heat"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rinse with warm water from a peri bottle, then pat dry front to back.): A peri bottle with warm water cleans gently, and patting dry from front to back keeps stool bacteria away from the stitches. Brisk rubbing irritates healing tissue. Scented salts and very hot water can irritate the wound. Scrubbing with soap and wiping from back to front also risk irritation and infection."}, {"id": 1112, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mrs. Ola Reid, a resident in long-term care, is found on the floor after a fall. You assess her, report to the RN, and now begin charting. Facility policy also requires a separate incident report. What is the best documentation approach?", "options": {"A": "Skip the chart entry because the separate incident report already covers the fall.", "B": "Chart your opinion on why the fall happened and which staff member was at fault.", "C": "Write in the chart that an incident report has been completed.", "D": "Chart objective facts and care given, and file the incident report separately."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Omitting clinical documentation of the event", "B": "Recording opinions and blame", "C": "Referencing the incident report in the clinical record"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chart objective facts and care given, and file the incident report separately.): The clinical record documents facts and care, while the incident report is a separate quality tool kept out of the client's chart. Mentioning it in the chart can make the report discoverable and mixes purposes. Skipping the chart entry leaves care undocumented. Opinions and blame do not belong in either record."}, {"id": 1113, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A surgical client was given a double dose of an anticoagulant by mistake, and you were the nurse who noticed. The client is stable. Another nurse suggests staying quiet until the physician decides what to say. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Wait until discharge so that his recovery is not disturbed by the news.", "B": "Tell the client yourself and promise that the error will not appear in his record.", "C": "Report promptly to the RN, support assessment of the client, and join disclosure through agency process.", "D": "Say nothing to the client until the physician decides whether it is worth telling him."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying disclosure until discharge", "B": "Promising to omit the error from the record", "D": "Withholding disclosure until later"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report promptly to the RN, support assessment of the client, and join disclosure through agency process.): Medication errors are reported immediately so the client can be assessed and treated, and honest disclosure follows the agency process with the team. Silence breaks the duty of honesty. Promising to leave the error out of the record is dishonest and unsafe. Waiting until discharge delays care that he might need now."}, {"id": 1114, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a community health session, the parents of a 3-year-old tell the nurse they will let her wear arm floaties at the backyard pool while they chat nearby on the patio with friends. The pool was just filled for summer. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Suggest enrolling her in swimming lessons this summer so supervision can be relaxed at the pool.", "B": "Teach that floaties cannot replace adult supervision within arm's reach, plus a pool fence.", "C": "Agree that floaties make close supervision unnecessary for a child of her age at home.", "D": "Advise that an older sibling can watch her closely while the parents relax nearby."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating lessons as a substitute for supervision", "C": "Believing flotation devices replace supervision", "D": "Delegating supervision to a child"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Teach that floaties cannot replace adult supervision within arm's reach, plus a pool fence.): Young children can drown quickly and silently, so flotation devices never replace a focused adult within reach, and fencing adds a layer of protection. Siblings are not reliable supervisors. Lessons build skills but do not remove the need for watching a preschooler near water."}, {"id": 1115, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Visiting the home of a 2-month-old boy, a public health nurse learns his parents plan to use soft bumper pads in his crib and place him on his side to sleep. Both parents work during the day. What should the nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Place him on his back but use a soft mattress topper for comfort.", "B": "Place him on his back on a firm flat surface in his own empty crib.", "C": "Bumper pads are fine to use once he starts to wiggle and lift his head.", "D": "Use a firm crib in the parents' room with a loose blanket pulled to his chest."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Soft surface that raises suffocation risk", "C": "Believing bumpers protect infants", "D": "Loose bedding in the sleep space"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Place him on his back on a firm flat surface in his own empty crib.): Back sleeping on a firm flat surface in an empty crib lowers the risk of sudden infant death. A soft topper and loose blankets increase suffocation risk, even when the position is right. Side lying and rolled blankets can lead to a roll onto the stomach. Bumper pads add suffocation risk. Sharing an adult bed is also unsafe."}, {"id": 1116, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mrs. Joan Weir, 69, has COPD and tells the clinic nurse that her main goal is walking her dog to the corner again. The nurse had planned goals about inhaler technique and quitting smoking. How should the goals be set?", "options": {"A": "Set inhaler goals first and add her walking goal later.", "B": "Write the goals into the plan and tell her what she should aim for at home", "C": "Set measurable goals with her, tied to her priority of walking again.", "D": "Pick two goals from the nurse's list and leave walking out."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying the goal that matters most to her", "B": "Telling the client what to aim for", "D": "Narrowing the plan to the nurse's priorities"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Set measurable goals with her, tied to her priority of walking again.): Goals are most effective when set together, measurable, and tied to what matters to the client, such as walking her dog. Inhaler technique and smoking cessation support that aim and can be included as steps. Postponing her goal or omitting it ignores her priority. Writing goals without her input does not engage her."}, {"id": 1117, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An adult with bone cancer who lives at home reports nausea, constipation, thirst and passing large volumes of urine. Today she seems confused. Her daughter wants to give extra calcium tablets for her bones. Her pain is controlled with her usual medication. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Treat the nausea with her as-needed antiemetic and review the confusion at the next visit.", "B": "Recognize signs of high calcium, hold the calcium, and report urgently to the RN or physician.", "C": "Treat the constipation with a laxative and review the confusion tomorrow.", "D": "Give extra calcium tablets, but at half the dose the daughter suggested."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating one symptom and delaying review of confusion", "C": "Treating one symptom and delaying review", "D": "Reducing a harmful dose instead of holding it"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Recognize signs of high calcium, hold the calcium, and report urgently to the RN or physician.): Nausea, constipation, polyuria, thirst and confusion in a client with cancer fit hypercalcemia, which can become life threatening. Extra calcium would worsen it, so the nurse holds it and reports urgently to the RN or physician. Treating the nausea and waiting delays care. Any calcium supplement is harmful here. A laxative with later review or rest at home also delays treatment."}, {"id": 1118, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A client with cirrhosis becomes newly confused and sleepy and has a flapping tremor of the hands. His daughter asks whether he should now eat no protein at all, to protect his liver. His last bowel movement was yesterday. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Report the change to the RN promptly, monitor his level of consciousness, and keep him safe.", "B": "Cut protein from his diet to keep his ammonia level low, starting today.", "C": "Encourage fluids and rest and reassess him at the next scheduled round of vital signs.", "D": "Give him a sedative to settle the confusion and help him sleep through the night."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Cutting protein out instead of adjusting the amount", "C": "Delaying reassessment of altered consciousness", "D": "Sedating a client with hepatic encephalopathy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the change to the RN promptly, monitor his level of consciousness, and keep him safe.): New confusion and a flapping tremor in cirrhosis suggest hepatic encephalopathy, which needs prompt medical treatment, often with lactulose. The nurse reports, monitors consciousness and keeps him safe. Protein is usually adjusted, not cut out. Sedatives can worsen encephalopathy and waiting delays treatment."}, {"id": 1119, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "You are an off-duty nurse at a community picnic when a man collapses and is not breathing normally. A bystander says you are not responsible because you are not at work today. It is a warm afternoon and about fifty people are nearby. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Wait for another bystander to start care before you decide whether to get involved.", "B": "Give care only after you have gathered the man's history and consent from bystanders.", "C": "Call emergency services and give care within your knowledge and skills until help arrives.", "D": "Call emergency services and wait beside him without touching him until responders arrive."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for someone else to act", "B": "Delaying life-saving care for history and consent", "D": "Avoiding care out of liability fear"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Call emergency services and give care within your knowledge and skills until help arrives.): Nurses are expected to act as a reasonable person with their training would, calling for help and giving care within their competence, and Good Samaritan principles support those who help in good faith. Gathering history first delays care for a person who is not breathing. Waiting without acting fails to use the nurse's skills. Waiting for others, unsafe treatment or walking away is wrong."}, {"id": 1120, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "You are the practical nurse on a surgical unit, and the RN asks you to plan your day-of-surgery clients before the noon medication pass, since four clients need attention at the same time. The unit has two nurses and one aide today. Which client should you see first?", "options": {"A": "The client with the highest reported pain score of 9 out of 10 after surgery yesterday.", "B": "The client requesting help with a shower and a clean gown before breakfast.", "C": "The client two hours after surgery with new shortness of breath and an SpO2 of 89%.", "D": "The client due for a routine dressing change on the second postoperative day."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Prioritizing pain score over airway", "B": "Prioritizing comfort tasks over instability", "D": "Prioritizing routine care over urgent assessment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The client two hours after surgery with new shortness of breath and an SpO2 of 89%.): Airway and breathing come first, and new shortness of breath with a low SpO2 soon after surgery may signal a serious complication. Severe pain matters but is not as time critical. A routine dressing change and hygiene can wait. Report the findings to the RN immediately."}, {"id": 1121, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Perrin, 77, is on the medical unit with dyspnea, leg swelling and orthopnea. Her BNP result is 850 ng/L, and the normal value is under 100. The RN is on the phone. Her vital signs are otherwise stable. How should you interpret this result?", "options": {"A": "It is mildly raised for her age, so recheck it at her next routine visit.", "B": "It suggests kidney impairment, so ask the RN to order a urinalysis before anything else.", "C": "It suggests a chest infection, so encourage deep breathing and wait for a sputum culture.", "D": "It supports heart failure as the cause of her symptoms, and you report it."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a markedly raised value as a minor age variation", "B": "Linking a raised BNP to kidney disease instead of the heart", "C": "Reading BNP as a marker of lung infection"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It supports heart failure as the cause of her symptoms, and you report it.): A markedly raised BNP with dyspnea, edema and orthopnea supports heart failure, and the practical nurse reports the result with her findings. A value of 850 ng/L is far above normal and should not wait for a routine visit. Kidney function is judged with creatinine and urinalysis, and BNP is not an infection marker."}, {"id": 1122, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A woman using a patient-controlled analgesia pump after surgery is asleep. Her son presses the button repeatedly because she looks uncomfortable to him. Her respirations are 10 per minute. The call bell has been working normally. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Let him continue, because the pump lockout interval limits how much she can receive.", "B": "Ask him to press it only when she moans or grimaces during her sleep.", "C": "Stop the pump, assess her breathing and sedation, report to the RN, and explain the rule.", "D": "Tell him you will teach him how to program the pump safely at home."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Trusting the pump alone to prevent overdose", "B": "Permitting family to dose the client", "D": "Teaching a family member to use the pump"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the pump, assess her breathing and sedation, report to the RN, and explain the rule.): PCA safety depends on the client being the only one to press the button, because sedation then stops further doses. Respirations of 10 and a sleeping client signal possible opioid effect, so the nurse stops the pump and assesses. Lockout limits do not stop a family member from stacking doses. Programming is a nursing role."}, {"id": 1123, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A clinic nurse must teach a 60-year-old man how to use a new inhaler. He tells you he learns best by doing things with his own hands rather than reading. He has no prior experience with inhalers. Which teaching approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Show a short video on lung anatomy, then answer his questions at the end.", "B": "Demonstrate the steps, then ask him to do a return demonstration while you coach.", "C": "Explain the steps verbally, check that he understands, and let him try it later.", "D": "Demonstrate the steps once and then give him the inhaler to practise at home."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a knowledge approach for a hands-on skill", "C": "Teaching verbally without a return demonstration", "D": "Demonstrating without observing his practice or giving feedback"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Demonstrate the steps, then ask him to do a return demonstration while you coach.): Using an inhaler is a psychomotor skill, so learning is strongest when the nurse demonstrates and the client practises with feedback through return demonstration. A video builds knowledge but not technique. Verbal explanation with later practice delays correction of errors. Practising alone at home after one demonstration gives no coaching."}, {"id": 1124, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Six hours after an epidural birth, a postpartum client has passed small amounts of urine, about 30 mL, five times, and feels pressure low in her abdomen. Her fundus is firm and high on the right. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Give her a laxative ordered earlier to relieve the lower abdominal pressure she describes.", "B": "Suspect a full bladder, assess her lower abdomen per policy, and report to the RN.", "C": "Massage her fundus firmly to reduce the pressure that she feels in her lower abdomen.", "D": "Accept the small frequent voids as normal and encourage her to drink more fluids."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a laxative for urinary retention", "C": "Massaging for the wrong problem", "D": "Accepting overflow voiding as normal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suspect a full bladder, assess her lower abdomen per policy, and report to the RN.): Small frequent voids with a high, displaced fundus and suprapubic pressure after an epidural suggest urinary retention with overflow. The nurse assesses the bladder per policy and reports so catheterization can be considered. Small voids are not a sign of a normal bladder. A distended bladder prevents the uterus from contracting."}, {"id": 1125, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor, 48, tells the clinic nurse that his father died of a heart attack at 55. His fasting lipid panel shows high LDL cholesterol, high HDL cholesterol and normal triglycerides. He asks which result he should work hardest to lower. Which teaching should the practical nurse give?", "options": {"A": "The high HDL is the main concern, so he should limit foods that raise it.", "B": "Both LDL and HDL need to fall together to reduce his heart disease and stroke risk.", "C": "Triglycerides are the key risk value, so the other results can be set aside.", "D": "The raised LDL is the main target, since LDL carries cholesterol into artery walls."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating HDL as the harmful cholesterol", "B": "Assuming every lipid fraction should be lowered", "C": "Focusing on the normal triglyceride value and dismissing the LDL result"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The raised LDL is the main target, since LDL carries cholesterol into artery walls.): Mr. Okafor's high LDL is the fraction that deposits cholesterol in artery walls, and his family history raises the importance of lowering it. HDL helps carry cholesterol away from vessels, so a high HDL is not something to reduce. Lowering all fractions together misunderstands their opposite roles, and normal triglycerides do not cancel an abnormal LDL. Remember that LDL is the one to lower and HDL is the one to protect."}, {"id": 1126, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit on postpartum day 8, Aisha, 29, reports a tender, swollen, warm left calf that began yesterday. She plans to massage it to ease the ache. Her bleeding is light and her temperature is 37.1 C. Which condition do these findings most strongly suggest?", "options": {"A": "Muscle strain from carrying the newborn and lifting during the first week.", "B": "Sciatic nerve irritation from pushing during labour, causing one-sided leg discomfort.", "C": "Deep vein thrombosis, linked to the raised clotting tendency after birth.", "D": "Leg cramps from low calcium after delivery, which usually settle with stretching."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Attributing calf findings to overuse from baby care", "B": "Confusing a nerve problem with a vascular one", "D": "Mistaking a clot for an electrolyte cramp"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Deep vein thrombosis, linked to the raised clotting tendency after birth.): Pregnancy and the early weeks after birth raise clotting tendency, and a single swollen, warm, tender calf is the typical picture of a deep vein thrombosis. Muscle strain would not produce warmth and swelling in one calf. Cramps tend to come and go with stretching relief, while sciatic irritation causes shooting pain without swelling. This is why massage is avoided and the RN or physician is told promptly."}, {"id": 1127, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a short-staffed adult medical unit, the charge nurse gives Priya, a practical nurse, an unstable post-operative client who needs frequent assessments. Priya has not cared for this type of client and already has a heavy workload. She believes the assignment is unsafe. What should Priya do first?", "options": {"A": "Decline the assignment and leave the unit so she is not held responsible.", "B": "Ask a colleague to take the client informally without informing the charge nurse.", "C": "Accept the assignment and quietly manage, since shortages are a normal part of nursing.", "D": "Tell the charge nurse about her competence gap and ask for support or a revised assignment."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Abandoning the unit instead of communicating", "B": "Shifting care informally without the manager's knowledge", "C": "Accepting an unsafe assignment out of loyalty to the team"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tell the charge nurse about her competence gap and ask for support or a revised assignment.): Priya's duty to provide care and her duty to practise within her competence are both met when she raises the concern at once and asks for help or a change. Walking away risks abandonment of clients already under her care. Quietly accepting leaves the client exposed to avoidable harm, and an informal swap leaves accountability unclear. Safe practice means communicating early and staying with assigned clients until the assignment is resolved."}, {"id": 1128, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Dubois has just been repositioned and his bed is now flat. His oxygen saturation is 96 percent and his abdominal incision is intact. The nurse plans care to lower his risk of ventilator-associated pneumonia. Which intervention should be done now?", "options": {"A": "Suction the endotracheal tube on a fixed hourly schedule to keep the airway clear.", "B": "Change the ventilator circuit tubing every shift to reduce contamination.", "C": "Raise the head of the bed to 30 to 45 degrees and keep it there.", "D": "Keep the bed flat to protect the abdominal incision during the night."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Suctioning by the clock rather than by need", "B": "Changing circuits too often, which adds contamination risk", "D": "Prioritizing the incision over aspiration prevention"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C142", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dubois, 63, is in intensive care after a bowel resection with a midline abdominal incision. He was intubated in the operating room and remains on a ventilator overnight. His temperature is 37.4 C and his dressing is dry and intact. His wife visits each day and asks many questions. The unit follows infection prevention bundles, and the practical nurse is caring for him on a night shift under the direction of the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raise the head of the bed to 30 to 45 degrees and keep it there.): A semi-upright position of 30 to 45 degrees lowers the chance that stomach contents and mouth secretions reach the lungs, which is the main route to ventilator-associated pneumonia. Keeping him flat protects nothing here and raises aspiration risk. Suctioning is done when secretions are present, not on a timer, and frequent circuit changes add handling and contamination. Head-of-bed elevation is a core bundle step."}, {"id": 1129, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Overnight, the nurse notes dried secretions on Mr. Dubois's lips and a coated tongue, while the endotracheal tube is secured at 22 cm. His wife asks whether mouth care is risky while he is intubated. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Lip moisturizer is enough, since the mouth is not used for eating or drinking.", "B": "Oral care is withheld while intubated because it could dislodge the tube and cause aspiration.", "C": "Regular oral care lowers mouth bacteria that could be aspirated, so it follows unit protocol.", "D": "Oral care is done once daily by the dentist rather than by nursing staff."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating lip care as a substitute for mouth cleaning", "B": "Withholding oral care out of fear of tube dislodgement", "D": "Assigning routine oral care to another profession"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C142", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dubois, 63, is in intensive care after a bowel resection with a midline abdominal incision. He was intubated in the operating room and remains on a ventilator overnight. His temperature is 37.4 C and his dressing is dry and intact. His wife visits each day and asks many questions. The unit follows infection prevention bundles, and the practical nurse is caring for him on a night shift under the direction of the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Regular oral care lowers mouth bacteria that could be aspirated, so it follows unit protocol.): Bacteria build up quickly in the mouth of an intubated client, and aspirated secretions are a main cause of ventilator-associated pneumonia, so scheduled oral care per protocol is part of prevention. Tube security can be maintained during careful oral care, so the concern about dislodgement does not justify skipping it. Moisturizer does not clean the mouth, and oral care is a routine nursing responsibility rather than a dental visit."}, {"id": 1130, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The pre-operative checklist shows that hair around Mr. Dubois's incision site was removed the night before surgery with a razor, and the skin showed small nicks. The RN asks the nurse what this means for his infection risk. What interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "Night-before shaving allows nicks to heal before surgery, so routine incision checks are enough.", "B": "Razor nicks can let bacteria in, so he needs close incision monitoring and early redness reporting.", "C": "Nicks are a theatre concern that ends once the incision is closed, so routine checks suffice.", "D": "The ventilator is his main infection risk, so the shaving method needs no special follow up."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing nicks heal before surgery and do not raise risk", "C": "Thinking skin damage matters only during the operation", "D": "Focusing on the ventilator and ignoring the skin breaks"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C142", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dubois, 63, is in intensive care after a bowel resection with a midline abdominal incision. He was intubated in the operating room and remains on a ventilator overnight. His temperature is 37.4 C and his dressing is dry and intact. His wife visits each day and asks many questions. The unit follows infection prevention bundles, and the practical nurse is caring for him on a night shift under the direction of the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Razor nicks can let bacteria in, so he needs close incision monitoring and early redness reporting.): Small nicks from a razor create entry points where skin bacteria multiply, and doing this the night before gives them time to colonize, so the nurse watches the incision closely and reports early redness, warmth or drainage. Nicks do not heal safely before surgery, and the risk continues after closure. The ventilator adds a separate risk but does not cancel this one, and clipping just before surgery is the safer method."}, {"id": 1131, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On day 4, Mr. Dubois's dressing is saturated with serosanguineous drainage and the RN asks the nurse to change it. The incision edges are approximated with no redness. After hand hygiene and gloving, how should the nurse cleanse the incision?", "options": {"A": "Scrub back and forth along the incision with a single saline-soaked gauze to loosen drainage.", "B": "Wipe from the incision outward with a fresh swab for each single stroke.", "C": "Wipe from the incision outward, then return to the incision with the same swab.", "D": "Wipe from the surrounding skin toward the incision with one swab for the whole area."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Scrubbing a healing incision back and forth with one gauze", "C": "Reusing a swab and returning to the cleanest area", "D": "Moving contamination from dirty skin toward the clean wound"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C142", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dubois, 63, is in intensive care after a bowel resection with a midline abdominal incision. He was intubated in the operating room and remains on a ventilator overnight. His temperature is 37.4 C and his dressing is dry and intact. His wife visits each day and asks many questions. The unit follows infection prevention bundles, and the practical nurse is caring for him on a night shift under the direction of the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Wipe from the incision outward with a fresh swab for each single stroke.): The incision is the cleanest area, so cleansing moves from it outward, and each swab is used once so organisms are not carried back. Starting at the outer skin and moving inward drags contamination toward the wound. Scrubbing with one gauze disrupts healing tissue, and returning to the incision with a used swab redeposits bacteria. Clean to less clean with a new swab each stroke is the rule to remember."}, {"id": 1132, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Tran, 56, has type 2 diabetes and took her usual breakfast two hours ago. Her fingerstick glucose this morning is 5.8 mmol/L, but her HbA1c result is 9.2 percent. She says the numbers contradict each other and asks which one is wrong. Which explanation is best?", "options": {"A": "The laboratory result is probably wrong, because HbA1c should match the fingerstick reading taken today.", "B": "HbA1c measures the glucose spike after her last meal, which was unusually large.", "C": "HbA1c reflects average glucose over about three months, so control has been poor despite today's value.", "D": "HbA1c shows glucose from the past 24 hours, so her overnight values were likely high."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Expecting a long-term test to match a single reading", "B": "Believing HbA1c captures only the most recent meal", "D": "Confusing HbA1c with a one-day average"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (HbA1c reflects average glucose over about three months, so control has been poor despite today's value.): HbA1c reflects the average glucose bound to red blood cells over roughly three months, so a single normal fingerstick does not cancel a high value. Mrs. Tran's control over the longer term has been poor even if this morning was acceptable. The test does not track one day or one meal. The result is useful because it shows the pattern that individual readings can miss."}, {"id": 1133, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Dana, 42, visits the clinic with weight gain, fatigue and feeling cold all the time. Her TSH is 9.8 mIU/L, above the reference range of 0.4 to 4.0. She asks what the high result means. What should the nurse explain?", "options": {"A": "A high TSH means a pituitary tumour is likely, so she needs a brain scan.", "B": "Extra TSH is released to push an underactive thyroid, so thyroid hormone is likely low.", "C": "A slightly high TSH is normal in midlife, so her symptoms likely come from stress.", "D": "Her thyroid is overactive and using up hormone, so TSH rises and she gains weight."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming a high TSH signals a pituitary tumour", "C": "Calling a clearly raised TSH a normal midlife variation", "D": "Reversing the TSH and thyroid hormone relationship"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Extra TSH is released to push an underactive thyroid, so thyroid hormone is likely low.): The pituitary releases more TSH when circulating thyroid hormone is low, so a high TSH with fatigue, cold intolerance and weight gain points toward an underactive thyroid. An overactive thyroid produces a suppressed TSH instead. A value of 9.8 is well above the range and not a normal variation. A pituitary tumour is a rare cause and is not the usual explanation. The provider makes the diagnosis, and the nurse explains what the pattern suggests."}, {"id": 1134, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At a community clinic, Mr. Singh, 45, asks the practical nurse to fax a summary of his hypertension visit to his employer because his supervisor requested it. He has not signed any release form and is waiting for his next appointment. Which principle should guide the nurse's response?", "options": {"A": "Send only the diagnosis and medication list since these are the basic details employers need.", "B": "Send the summary because employers are entitled to know about conditions affecting attendance.", "C": "Phone the supervisor to confirm the request is genuine, then release the summary.", "D": "Share nothing until Mr. Singh gives informed consent for the specific information and recipient."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing partial disclosure avoids a breach", "B": "Assuming employers have a right to health information", "C": "Treating verification of the requester as consent"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Share nothing until Mr. Singh gives informed consent for the specific information and recipient.): Health information belongs to the client, and an employer has no automatic right to it, so release needs Mr. Singh's informed consent naming what is shared and with whom. Sending a partial summary is still a disclosure without consent. Confirming the supervisor's identity does not replace the client's permission. The nurse can offer to help him decide what he wishes to share."}, {"id": 1135, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Nadia, 31, is on postpartum day 5 after a cesarean birth. She suddenly reports sharp chest pain on breathing and shortness of breath. Heart rate is 118, respiratory rate 28 and oxygen saturation 90 percent on room air. She says she feels panicky. What should the nurse suspect?", "options": {"A": "Anxiety, so the nurse coaches slow breathing and offers a quiet room with dimmed lights.", "B": "Postpartum fluid overload, so the nurse restricts oral fluids and weighs her daily.", "C": "Pulmonary embolism, so the nurse stays with her, applies oxygen and alerts the RN immediately.", "D": "Incisional pain, so the nurse gives analgesia and monitors her comfort."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming anxiety explains abnormal vital signs and coaching alone", "B": "Choosing a fluid explanation that does not fit the sudden onset", "D": "Attributing chest pain on breathing to the surgical wound"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pulmonary embolism, so the nurse stays with her, applies oxygen and alerts the RN immediately.): Sudden pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, fast breathing and low saturation after a cesarean birth fit a pulmonary embolism, which is an emergency. Panic can accompany it but is not the cause here, so coaching alone is unsafe. Incisional pain does not drop oxygen saturation, and fluid overload develops more gradually. Staying with the client, giving oxygen and calling the RN are the priorities."}, {"id": 1136, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Marcus, 22, tall and thin, arrives in the emergency department with sudden sharp right chest pain and breathlessness after coughing. Breath sounds are diminished on the right and his saturation is 92 percent. He mentions that he vapes daily. What is the nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Raise the head of the bed, apply oxygen and notify the physician immediately for imaging.", "B": "Encourage deep coughing exercises to re-expand the lung while waiting for the physician.", "C": "Lay him flat for comfort and give analgesia before reporting the findings.", "D": "Apply a pillow to the chest for comfort and recheck breath sounds after analgesia."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Coughing hard, which can worsen an air leak", "C": "Delaying the report to treat pain first", "D": "Waiting and monitoring a probable pneumothorax"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Raise the head of the bed, apply oxygen and notify the physician immediately for imaging.): Sudden one-sided pleuritic pain with diminished breath sounds in a tall, thin young adult suggests a pneumothorax, so he needs oxygen, a position that eases breathing and prompt physician notification for imaging. A pillow with a delayed recheck leaves a possible collapsing lung unaddressed. Forceful coughing can worsen the leak, and lying flat while pain is treated delays the report. His vaping is background detail."}, {"id": 1137, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Hale, 61, has cirrhosis with known esophageal varices. At lunch he reports a hot feeling in his chest, then vomits about 200 mL of bright red blood. His blood pressure is 92/58 and heart rate 124. His wife says he ate a bagel with seeds. Which action is the nurse's priority?", "options": {"A": "Stay with him, position him to protect the airway and call the RN immediately.", "B": "Document the amount of vomit and recheck his vital signs again in about thirty minutes.", "C": "Lay him supine with his legs raised to support his falling blood pressure until help arrives.", "D": "Offer ice chips and sips of water to soothe his throat and settle the vomiting."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delaying escalation to chart and recheck later", "C": "Choosing a position that raises aspiration risk", "D": "Giving oral intake during active bleeding"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stay with him, position him to protect the airway and call the RN immediately.): Bright red vomit with low blood pressure and a fast heart rate signals bleeding varices, a life-threatening emergency. The nurse protects the airway, stays with him and calls for help at once. Oral intake can provoke more bleeding, and delaying escalation to chart is unsafe. Lying supine with vomiting risks aspiration. The bagel may have scraped the varices, but the priority is the airway and rapid help."}, {"id": 1138, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lavoie, 74, has advanced heart failure and still takes diuretics and sees her cardiologist. Her daughter asks the community nurse whether palliative care means treatment is being stopped and that death is only days away. How should the nurse describe palliative care?", "options": {"A": "It is a service for cancer clients, so heart failure is managed separately.", "B": "It replaces her cardiology care with a team that manages comfort alone.", "C": "It begins once active treatment has stopped and life expectancy is a few days.", "D": "It supports comfort and quality of life alongside ongoing treatment from early in serious illness."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Restricting palliative care to cancer diagnoses", "B": "Believing it replaces disease-directed treatment", "C": "Limiting palliative care to the final days"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It supports comfort and quality of life alongside ongoing treatment from early in serious illness.): Palliative care aims to relieve symptoms and support quality of life for anyone with a serious illness, and it can run alongside treatments such as diuretics from the time of diagnosis. It does not wait for the last days, and it does not replace her cardiology care. Heart failure is a common reason for referral. Explaining this removes the fear that palliative care means giving up."}, {"id": 1139, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Before meeting staff, the manager explains to the practical nurse on the unit committee that just culture sorts events by the nurse's behaviour, not only by the outcome. One event caused a mild reaction and another caused no harm. Which classification of the three events is correct?", "options": {"A": "A is at risk behaviour, B is human error, and C is reckless behaviour.", "B": "A is human error, B is reckless behaviour, and C is at risk behaviour.", "C": "A is human error, B is at risk behaviour, and C is reckless behaviour.", "D": "All three are at risk behaviour because each involved a medication safety step."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Swapping human error and at risk behaviour", "B": "Calling an unintended habit reckless and a deliberate violation at risk", "D": "Grouping every event by the shared setting rather than the choice made"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C143", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, the nurse manager reviews three recent events using a just culture approach. Event A: a nurse gave 5 mg instead of 0.5 mg of a drug after two similar vials looked alike. Event B: a nurse often skipped the second client identifier check on busy shifts because colleagues did too and nothing had gone wrong. Event C: a nurse knowingly gave a drug without a required independent double check after being reminded of the rule, saying it was a waste of time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A is human error, B is at risk behaviour, and C is reckless behaviour.): Event A was an unintended slip in a setting with look-alike vials, which is human error. Event B was a drifted habit where the nurse did not see the risk, which is at risk behaviour. Event C was a conscious disregard of a known substantial risk, which is reckless behaviour. Classification follows the choice the person made, not the harm that resulted."}, {"id": 1140, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The manager now plans the response to Event A. The nurse is distressed, reported her own error immediately, and the client was monitored with no harm. Look-alike vials are stored side by side in the same bin. Which response best fits a just culture?", "options": {"A": "Console and support the nurse, and separate the look-alike vials to fix the system.", "B": "Retrain the whole unit on vial labels without changing how the vials are stored today.", "C": "Issue a written warning to the nurse to discourage careless dosing by staff in future.", "D": "Remove the nurse from medication duties for three months until her competency is formally rechecked."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Fixing only staff knowledge and leaving the hazard", "C": "Punishing an honest slip", "D": "Removing duties that the error does not justify"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C143", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, the nurse manager reviews three recent events using a just culture approach. Event A: a nurse gave 5 mg instead of 0.5 mg of a drug after two similar vials looked alike. Event B: a nurse often skipped the second client identifier check on busy shifts because colleagues did too and nothing had gone wrong. Event C: a nurse knowingly gave a drug without a required independent double check after being reminded of the rule, saying it was a waste of time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Console and support the nurse, and separate the look-alike vials to fix the system.): Human error is managed by consoling the individual and fixing the system conditions that made the slip likely, here the side-by-side storage of similar vials. A warning punishes an honest mistake and discourages reporting. Retraining without changing storage leaves the same trap in place. Removal from duties is out of proportion to an unintended error that she reported herself."}, {"id": 1141, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The manager meets the nurse from Event B. She says skipping the second identifier check saved time and that she did not think it was risky. Her unit peers do the same on busy shifts, and no client has been harmed. What should the manager do?", "options": {"A": "Treat it as human error, console her, and take no further action on the unit workflow", "B": "Give a formal disciplinary letter, since she broke a rule that protects clients and colleagues.", "C": "Remind the whole unit by email only and not speak with her directly about the risk.", "D": "Coach her on the risk, address the unit workflow, and monitor that the check is done."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Under-responding by treating a habit as a slip", "B": "Punishing a drifted habit as if it were reckless", "C": "Avoiding direct coaching with the individual"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C143", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, the nurse manager reviews three recent events using a just culture approach. Event A: a nurse gave 5 mg instead of 0.5 mg of a drug after two similar vials looked alike. Event B: a nurse often skipped the second client identifier check on busy shifts because colleagues did too and nothing had gone wrong. Event C: a nurse knowingly gave a drug without a required independent double check after being reminded of the rule, saying it was a waste of time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Coach her on the risk, address the unit workflow, and monitor that the check is done.): At risk behaviour is a choice made without recognizing the danger, so the manager coaches the nurse, looks at why the shortcut became common on this unit and checks that the practice changes. Console-only handling suits unintended error. Discipline is reserved for reckless conduct, and an email alone gives her no chance to understand the risk."}, {"id": 1142, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The manager turns to Event C. The nurse had been reminded of the double check rule twice, understood the risk to clients, and chose to skip it, calling it a waste of time. The client given the drug had no reaction. What action fits a just culture?", "options": {"A": "Document a verbal reminder only, because no client harm resulted from the omitted independent double check.", "B": "Apply proportionate disciplinary action because the nurse knowingly disregarded a substantial risk.", "C": "Coach the nurse on the risk and return her to full duties with a written reminder.", "D": "Console the nurse and redesign the double check process to prevent repeat events."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Judging the event by the outcome rather than the behaviour", "C": "Coaching a deliberate violation as if it were a habit", "D": "Treating a conscious choice as a system fault"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C143", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, the nurse manager reviews three recent events using a just culture approach. Event A: a nurse gave 5 mg instead of 0.5 mg of a drug after two similar vials looked alike. Event B: a nurse often skipped the second client identifier check on busy shifts because colleagues did too and nothing had gone wrong. Event C: a nurse knowingly gave a drug without a required independent double check after being reminded of the rule, saying it was a waste of time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Apply proportionate disciplinary action because the nurse knowingly disregarded a substantial risk.): Knowingly ignoring a rule after reminders, with awareness of the risk, is reckless behaviour, and just culture calls for proportionate disciplinary action. Coaching suits behaviour that the person did not see as risky, and consoling and redesigning suit unintended error. Having no harm does not lower the seriousness, because the response is based on the choice made."}, {"id": 1143, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a mental health unit, Mr. Brandt, 38, tells the practical nurse he plans to hurt his coworker, Dale, after discharge tomorrow and asks her to keep it secret. He has described how he would do it. What is the nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Contact Dale directly to warn him before notifying the treatment team.", "B": "Ask him to promise not to act, then document the promise and review it at discharge.", "C": "Tell him she must share it, then let him decide whether to inform the team.", "D": "Report the threat to the RN and physician right away so protection steps can follow."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Warning a third party personally outside the team process", "B": "Accepting a promise in place of reporting a clear threat", "C": "Letting the client control whether a duty to warn is met"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the threat to the RN and physician right away so protection steps can follow.): Confidentiality has limits when there is a clear risk of serious harm to an identifiable person, and a described plan with a target and timeline meets that threshold. The nurse reports at once so the team can assess and decide on protective steps. A promise does not remove the risk, and the decision to report is not the client's to make. Contacting Dale personally bypasses the team and the proper process."}, {"id": 1144, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ivanov, 59, received rapid-acting insulin before lunch, but his tray is delayed by an hour. Now he is sweaty, shaky and says his vision is blurred. He is awake and can swallow. His capillary glucose is 3.1 mmol/L. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Give 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate such as juice and recheck glucose in 15 minutes.", "B": "Give a glucagon injection and call the physician before offering anything by mouth.", "C": "Hold the lunch insulin and wait for the tray before treating the low glucose.", "D": "Give a high-protein, high-fat snack such as cheese and peanut butter crackers."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using glucagon in a client who can swallow", "C": "Waiting for the meal while symptomatic", "D": "Choosing a slow-acting snack for acute hypoglycemia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate such as juice and recheck glucose in 15 minutes.): Mr. Ivanov has symptomatic hypoglycemia from insulin without food, and because he is alert and can swallow, 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate is the first treatment, with a recheck in 15 minutes. Waiting for the tray leaves him low. Glucagon is for a client who cannot safely swallow, and fat and protein digest too slowly to raise glucose quickly. The insulin has already been given, so holding it is irrelevant."}, {"id": 1145, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On the pediatric ward at shift start, the nurse reviews four children: a toddler crying loudly with a cast, a 6-year-old whose scheduled medication is due, a 4-year-old with bronchiolitis who is now quiet, drowsy and breathing 62 per minute, and an infant awaiting discharge teaching. Which child should be assessed first?", "options": {"A": "The quiet, drowsy 4-year-old with bronchiolitis and a respiratory rate of 62.", "B": "The toddler crying loudly with a cast, because distress suggests pain or limb compromise.", "C": "The 6-year-old whose scheduled medication is due, since timing affects therapeutic effect.", "D": "The infant awaiting discharge teaching, since discharge planning delays bed availability."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Equating loud crying with the greatest urgency", "C": "Prioritizing a routine task by the clock", "D": "Placing discharge flow ahead of an unstable client"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The quiet, drowsy 4-year-old with bronchiolitis and a respiratory rate of 62.): A child with bronchiolitis who has become quiet, drowsy and tachypneic is showing signs of tiring and impending respiratory failure, so airway and breathing take priority. A loudly crying toddler is breathing well and is likely uncomfortable but stable. A due medication can be given within its window, and discharge teaching is not urgent. The quiet child is the one most at risk."}, {"id": 1146, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At the community clinic, Mrs. Whitehorse says she drinks a traditional plant tea each morning along with her prescribed medications. She lowers her voice and says she was once told such remedies are nonsense. How should the practical nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Accept the practice and record it, but do not raise interactions with the physician.", "B": "Advise her to stop the tea so it cannot interfere with her prescribed medications.", "C": "Thank her for sharing, show respect for the practice, and ask about the tea's ingredients.", "D": "Record the tea in her chart and explain that herbal remedies lack safety evidence."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Respecting the practice but skipping the interaction check with warfarin", "B": "Telling her to abandon a valued practice", "D": "Dismissing a cultural remedy as unsafe and lacking evidence"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C144", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Eleanor Whitehorse, 79, belongs to a First Nation and lives in a rural community. She has atrial fibrillation treated with warfarin and metastatic colon cancer with worsening pain. She sees a nurse at the community clinic, is later admitted to a hospital medical unit, and may move to a palliative care unit. She values her traditional practices and has felt dismissed by health staff before. A practical nurse is part of her care team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Thank her for sharing, show respect for the practice, and ask about the tea's ingredients.): Culturally safe care starts with respect and curiosity, so the nurse thanks her for sharing and asks what is in the tea, which also lets the team check for interactions with warfarin. Telling her to stop repeats the dismissal she has experienced. Accepting the tea without raising it misses the safety review respect should include. Lecturing about missing evidence is dismissive and discourages her from sharing again."}, {"id": 1147, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "After admission to the medical unit, Mrs. Whitehorse's INR is 3.9, above her usual range of 2.0 to 3.0, and she has bruising on both forearms. She has no new prescribed medications. The nurse has not yet asked about herbal products. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Ask her respectfully about all herbal and traditional products and report the INR and bruising.", "B": "Hold the next warfarin dose independently and wait for the physician to arrive.", "C": "Ask her to stop any herbal tea during admission and note it on the chart.", "D": "Assume the tea is harmless and recheck the INR with the next scheduled blood draw."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Changing a medication without an order", "C": "Directing the client to stop a practice without assessment", "D": "Presuming herbal products are harmless"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C144", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Eleanor Whitehorse, 79, belongs to a First Nation and lives in a rural community. She has atrial fibrillation treated with warfarin and metastatic colon cancer with worsening pain. She sees a nurse at the community clinic, is later admitted to a hospital medical unit, and may move to a palliative care unit. She values her traditional practices and has felt dismissed by health staff before. A practical nurse is part of her care team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask her respectfully about all herbal and traditional products and report the INR and bruising.): A raised INR with new bruising and no new prescribed drugs suggests an interaction, and herbal products can change how warfarin works. The nurse asks respectfully about everything she uses and reports the INR and bruising so the prescriber can decide. Assuming harmlessness delays action, holding a dose without an order is outside scope, and telling her to stop the tea before understanding it is neither respectful nor informed."}, {"id": 1148, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mrs. Whitehorse asks for an Elder from her community to visit her on the unit and to join discussions about her pain plan. A colleague says only licensed staff can be on the care team. What is the best nursing response?", "options": {"A": "Ask the unit manager to decide on the request before involving the care team.", "B": "Explain that Elders may visit socially but cannot be included in her formal care planning meetings.", "C": "Support the request, tell the RN, and arrange Elder involvement through Indigenous health services.", "D": "Allow the visit during regular hours but keep pain plan meetings to licensed staff."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying a client's request by handing it to a manager", "B": "Excluding Elders from planning because they are unlicensed", "D": "Allowing visits but excluding the Elder from care planning"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C144", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Eleanor Whitehorse, 79, belongs to a First Nation and lives in a rural community. She has atrial fibrillation treated with warfarin and metastatic colon cancer with worsening pain. She sees a nurse at the community clinic, is later admitted to a hospital medical unit, and may move to a palliative care unit. She values her traditional practices and has felt dismissed by health staff before. A practical nurse is part of her care team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Support the request, tell the RN, and arrange Elder involvement through Indigenous health services.): Elders and traditional healers hold a recognized role in many Indigenous clients' wellbeing, and including them is part of culturally safe, client-centred care. The nurse supports the request, informs the RN and uses hospital Indigenous health services to arrange it. Referring it upward only delays her choice. Allowing visits but excluding the Elder from planning limits the role she asked for. A license is not a condition for an Elder's role."}, {"id": 1149, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mrs. Whitehorse is transferred to the palliative care unit. Her family asks to burn sweetgrass and sing at her bedside, and her daughter wants to stay overnight. The nurse is unsure which rituals are suitable. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Allow the singing but ask the family to skip any smoke or burning items.", "B": "Ask the family what matters, then plan the practices with the team within safety policy.", "C": "Ask the chaplain to choose appropriate rituals and inform the family afterward.", "D": "Allow the singing and sweetgrass, but limit visitors and overnight stays to hospital routine."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Removing the key ritual without exploring safe alternatives", "C": "Handing the decision to another professional without the family", "D": "Applying routine limits that override the family's wishes"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C144", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Eleanor Whitehorse, 79, belongs to a First Nation and lives in a rural community. She has atrial fibrillation treated with warfarin and metastatic colon cancer with worsening pain. She sees a nurse at the community clinic, is later admitted to a hospital medical unit, and may move to a palliative care unit. She values her traditional practices and has felt dismissed by health staff before. A practical nurse is part of her care team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask the family what matters, then plan the practices with the team within safety policy.): Practices around dying belong to the client and family, so the nurse asks what matters to them and works with the team to meet those wishes safely, for example by exploring smoke alternatives or a private room. Dropping the smoke without discussion removes the part that matters most. Applying routine visiting limits ignores her daughter's request. The chaplain may help, but the family decides what is meaningful."}, {"id": 1150, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A mother tells the nurse at a well-child clinic that her 3-year-old son, 14 kg and 95 cm tall, complains that his harness is uncomfortable and wants to sit like his older brother. She asks about moving him to a booster seat. Which advice is best?", "options": {"A": "Move him to a booster seat with a lap belt once he complains about the harness.", "B": "Keep him in his harnessed seat until he reaches its weight or height limit.", "C": "Seat him in the back with only the vehicle seat belt, which suits his height.", "D": "A booster seat is suitable now because his age is over two years."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Responding to comfort complaints rather than size limits", "C": "Using an adult belt on a child who is too small", "D": "Using age alone to move to a booster"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep him in his harnessed seat until he reaches its weight or height limit.): A 3-year-old who is 14 kg has not outgrown a harnessed seat, and the harness gives better protection than a booster until the seat's weight or height limits are reached. Comfort complaints and age alone are not reasons to move. An adult belt does not fit a small child and can injure the abdomen. Suggest ways to improve comfort within the harnessed seat."}, {"id": 1151, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, Ms. Grant, 64, who takes insulin, feels shaky and sweaty but is alert and able to swallow. Her glucose meter reads 3.2 mmol/L. A chocolate bar sits on the table and her next meal is two hours away. What should the nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Plain water and rest for one hour, then recheck her glucose before treating.", "B": "A cheese sandwich, since protein and fat keep glucose stable until the meal.", "C": "A chocolate bar from the table, since its sugar will raise glucose quickly.", "D": "Fifteen grams of fast-acting sugar, such as juice or glucose tablets, then recheck in 15 minutes."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying treatment of symptomatic hypoglycemia", "B": "Using protein and fat to treat an acute low", "C": "Choosing a fat-rich food that absorbs slowly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fifteen grams of fast-acting sugar, such as juice or glucose tablets, then recheck in 15 minutes.): A conscious client with a glucose of 3.2 mmol/L needs 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, which raises glucose quickly, followed by a recheck in 15 minutes. Chocolate contains fat that slows absorption, so it works too slowly. A sandwich is useful later for stability but not for the acute low. Waiting an hour risks further deterioration."}, {"id": 1152, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit before her admission, Mrs. Alvarez asks how to trim her toenails. Her skin is dry, she has a thin callus, and she cannot feel light touch on her soles. Her last foot check was two weeks ago. Which instruction best protects her feet?", "options": {"A": "Cut away the callus with a small blade to prevent painful pressure points from forming.", "B": "Cut nails straight across, file the edges smooth and do not trim the cuticles.", "C": "Soak her feet in hot water first to soften the nails and the thin callus.", "D": "Cut the nails in a curve to match the toe shape and trim the cuticles."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Self-trimming callus, which risks cuts", "C": "Using hot water on feet with reduced sensation", "D": "Curving the cut, which promotes ingrown nails"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C137", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Rosa Alvarez, 67, has type 2 diabetes with reduced sensation in both feet. She received home care for foot checks before developing a urinary tract infection and sepsis. She is now sedated and ventilated in a critical care unit with an indwelling urinary catheter. The practical nurse provides her hygiene and comfort care across these settings, with direction from the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cut nails straight across, file the edges smooth and do not trim the cuticles.): With reduced foot sensation, small injuries go unnoticed, so nails are cut straight across, filed smooth and the cuticles are left alone to avoid ingrown nails and infection. Curved cuts and cuticle trimming raise the risk of injury. Hot water can burn feet that cannot feel temperature, and cutting callus with a blade invites wounds. She should have callus managed by a foot care professional."}, {"id": 1153, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In critical care, Mrs. Alvarez is sedated and does not blink. Her eyelids are partly open, and the conjunctivae look dry and red. Her vital signs are stable and her ventilator settings are unchanged. The RN asks the nurse to protect her corneas. Which intervention is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Leave her eyes uncovered, since the sedation reduces the need for extra routine eye care.", "B": "Apply prescribed lubricant drops and gently close her eyelids using clean technique.", "C": "Tape the eyelids shut with firm adhesive tape placed across the lashes for the entire shift.", "D": "Irrigate both eyes with cool tap water each hour to flush away the dryness and debris."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignoring dry eyes in a sedated client", "C": "Using firm tape that can injure lashes and skin", "D": "Irrigating with non-sterile water"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C137", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Rosa Alvarez, 67, has type 2 diabetes with reduced sensation in both feet. She received home care for foot checks before developing a urinary tract infection and sepsis. She is now sedated and ventilated in a critical care unit with an indwelling urinary catheter. The practical nurse provides her hygiene and comfort care across these settings, with direction from the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Apply prescribed lubricant drops and gently close her eyelids using clean technique.): Sedated clients lose the blink reflex, so corneas dry and can ulcerate, and lubrication with gentle lid closure under clean technique protects them. Leaving the eyes open exposes the cornea. Firm tape across the lashes can tear skin and abrade the cornea, and tap water is not sterile and does not lubricate. Eye care is done per the order and policy."}, {"id": 1154, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Alvarez has stool on her perineum and an indwelling catheter in place. The nurse prepares soap, water and a clean washcloth for perineal care, and the bed is raised to a comfortable working height. Which principle should guide the direction of cleansing?", "options": {"A": "Cleanse in circles starting at the anus and moving toward the catheter.", "B": "Cleanse from front to back, using a clean part of the cloth for each stroke.", "C": "Cleanse back and forth along one side, reusing the same cloth area for speed.", "D": "Cleanse from back to front, so stool is removed before the urethral area."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Starting at the dirtiest area and working inward", "C": "Reusing a soiled cloth surface", "D": "Moving bacteria from the anus toward the urethra"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C137", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Rosa Alvarez, 67, has type 2 diabetes with reduced sensation in both feet. She received home care for foot checks before developing a urinary tract infection and sepsis. She is now sedated and ventilated in a critical care unit with an indwelling urinary catheter. The practical nurse provides her hygiene and comfort care across these settings, with direction from the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cleanse from front to back, using a clean part of the cloth for each stroke.): Cleansing from the cleanest area, the urethra, toward the dirtiest, the anus, keeps fecal bacteria away from the catheter and urinary tract, and each stroke uses a clean part of the cloth. Back to front and circles toward the catheter carry organisms to the urethra. Reusing a soiled cloth area spreads them. Front to back with a fresh surface lowers catheter-associated infection risk."}, {"id": 1155, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Alvarez's catheter is secured to her thigh, and crusted secretions surround it at the urethral opening. Her temperature is 37.2 C and the urine in the drainage bag is clear yellow. How should the nurse safely clean beneath the crusting?", "options": {"A": "Pull the catheter gently outward to lift crusting and clean beneath the tubing.", "B": "Rotate the catheter back and forth inside the urethra to loosen the dried crusting around it", "C": "Disconnect the catheter from the drainage bag so the crusting can be reached more easily", "D": "Hold the catheter steady near the meatus and cleanse the crusting from the meatus outward."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pulling the catheter, which traumatizes the urethra", "B": "Moving the catheter in and out, which introduces bacteria", "C": "Breaking the closed drainage system"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C137", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Rosa Alvarez, 67, has type 2 diabetes with reduced sensation in both feet. She received home care for foot checks before developing a urinary tract infection and sepsis. She is now sedated and ventilated in a critical care unit with an indwelling urinary catheter. The practical nurse provides her hygiene and comfort care across these settings, with direction from the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the catheter steady near the meatus and cleanse the crusting from the meatus outward.): Stabilizing the catheter at the meatus prevents traction and urethral trauma, and cleansing from the meatus outward moves away from the opening. Pulling the catheter injures tissue and moves it in the urethra. Disconnecting opens a closed system to bacteria, and rotating the catheter pushes organisms inward. Securement and a closed system are the key protections."}, {"id": 1156, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ng, 58, has a chest tube on suction after lung surgery. The nurse sees continuous bubbling in the water seal chamber, not only when he coughs. His oxygen saturation is 95 percent and he reports mild incisional pain. What action is best?", "options": {"A": "Document the bubbling as expected drainage and reassess at the next scheduled routine nursing check.", "B": "Clamp the tube close to his chest to stop the bubbling, then recheck the chamber.", "C": "Strip the tubing downward to clear the bubbles and document that drainage is functioning normally.", "D": "Check connections for an air leak and notify the RN, leaving the tube unclamped."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a continuous leak as normal", "B": "Clamping a tube that may trap air", "C": "Stripping tubing, which creates high pressure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Check connections for an air leak and notify the RN, leaving the tube unclamped.): Continuous bubbling in the water seal chamber signals an air leak, which may be from loose connections or the lung, so the nurse checks the system and informs the RN while keeping the tube open. Clamping can cause a tension pneumothorax. Stripping the tubing creates damaging negative pressure, and calling it normal misses a leak that needs assessment. Only intermittent bubbling with coughing is expected."}, {"id": 1157, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At the youth clinic, Sam, 16, attends alone for a sexual health visit and asks Jordan not to tell his parents. His mother phones and asks what the visit was about. Sam understands the information and his choices. How should Jordan respond?", "options": {"A": "Share the visit summary, because parents are entitled to the records of children under 18.", "B": "Confirm only that Sam attended and the topic, since this is not a diagnosis.", "C": "Tell her to ask the physician and share the visit purpose in the meantime.", "D": "Decline to share details, explaining that Sam controls his information as a capable minor."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming parents have automatic access to a minor's records", "B": "Treating a partial disclosure as harmless", "C": "Sharing purpose while redirecting the question"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C134", "cluster_text": "Jordan Reyes is a practical nurse who works at a youth clinic on Tuesdays, in a long-term care home on most weekdays, and on occasional float shifts to other units. Over one week, Jordan faces several legal and accountability decisions about confidentiality, delegation to unregulated care providers, and the limits of competence. Jordan's employer expects all work to follow the regulatory body's standards of practice and the agency's policies.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline to share details, explaining that Sam controls his information as a capable minor.): A capable minor who understands the information and choices controls his own health information, so Jordan does not disclose details to his mother without Sam's consent. Age under 18 alone does not give parents access. Even the topic of the visit is protected information. Jordan can encourage Sam to involve his parents and support him in doing so."}, {"id": 1158, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In the long-term care home, Jordan asks an unregulated care provider (UCP) to take a resident's routine vital signs, a task the UCP is trained for and policy permits. A colleague says responsibility for the outcome now rests with the UCP. Which statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Jordan remains accountable for the decision to delegate, the supervision and follow up.", "B": "Accountability is spread among all staff on the unit who observed the task being done.", "C": "Accountability passes to the charge nurse because the overall unit assignment came from her that morning.", "D": "The UCP becomes accountable for the result of the task once she has accepted it."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Diffusing responsibility across bystanders", "C": "Crediting the unit assignment rather than the delegation decision", "D": "Believing delegation transfers accountability"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C134", "cluster_text": "Jordan Reyes is a practical nurse who works at a youth clinic on Tuesdays, in a long-term care home on most weekdays, and on occasional float shifts to other units. Over one week, Jordan faces several legal and accountability decisions about confidentiality, delegation to unregulated care providers, and the limits of competence. Jordan's employer expects all work to follow the regulatory body's standards of practice and the agency's policies.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Jordan remains accountable for the decision to delegate, the supervision and follow up.): The nurse who delegates stays accountable for deciding the task is appropriate, for the supervision given and for acting on the results. The UCP is responsible for performing the task competently, but accountability for the delegation decision does not move. The charge nurse's unit assignment does not transfer Jordan's delegation accountability, and observers carry none."}, {"id": 1159, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Jordan plans care for Mr. Beck, 86, who needs a bed bath. The UCP says she can do anything on the list because she has done it many times. Mr. Beck has stable vital signs and intact skin. What should Jordan consider before assigning the bath?", "options": {"A": "The resident's stability, the task's complexity and the provider's verified training and competence.", "B": "Whether the task appears on the care plan template used by the unit.", "C": "The provider's own statement of confidence, since she has performed bathing many times before on shift.", "D": "The number of years the provider has worked in long-term care."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relying on a task list rather than an individual assessment", "C": "Relying on self-reported ability alone", "D": "Treating years of experience as proof of competence"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C134", "cluster_text": "Jordan Reyes is a practical nurse who works at a youth clinic on Tuesdays, in a long-term care home on most weekdays, and on occasional float shifts to other units. Over one week, Jordan faces several legal and accountability decisions about confidentiality, delegation to unregulated care providers, and the limits of competence. Jordan's employer expects all work to follow the regulatory body's standards of practice and the agency's policies.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The resident's stability, the task's complexity and the provider's verified training and competence.): Before assigning, the nurse weighs the client's condition, how complex and predictable the task is, and whether the provider has the verified training and competence to do it. A provider's confidence is not verification. Years of experience do not prove current competence, and a task being on a template does not show it is safe to assign. Mr. Beck is stable, so a bath is reasonable to assign."}, {"id": 1160, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, Mrs. Dolan, 72, has a new cough and her oxygen saturation has dropped from 95 to 91 percent. The UCP offers to check her over and report back to save time. Jordan is busy with a medication pass. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Complete the assessment personally, since interpreting changes in condition cannot be assigned to a UCP.", "B": "Assign the assessment to the UCP with a checklist since the task is routine.", "C": "Let the UCP assess her and report back, then review the findings after medications.", "D": "Ask the UCP to measure vitals and decide whether the RN needs to be called."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating a checklist as a substitute for clinical judgement", "C": "Delegating an assessment that requires judgement", "D": "Letting the UCP decide on escalation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C134", "cluster_text": "Jordan Reyes is a practical nurse who works at a youth clinic on Tuesdays, in a long-term care home on most weekdays, and on occasional float shifts to other units. Over one week, Jordan faces several legal and accountability decisions about confidentiality, delegation to unregulated care providers, and the limits of competence. Jordan's employer expects all work to follow the regulatory body's standards of practice and the agency's policies.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Complete the assessment personally, since interpreting changes in condition cannot be assigned to a UCP.): Assessing a client with a new cough and falling saturation requires nursing judgement, and interpretation of changes in condition cannot be assigned to a UCP. Jordan completes it and reports to the RN. The UCP may measure vital signs when directed, but deciding what they mean and whether to escalate is not hers. A checklist does not replace the nurse's clinical judgement."}, {"id": 1161, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Jordan is floated to an unfamiliar surgical unit and assigned two clients with epidurals and a client with a central line, all unfamiliar procedures. No orientation is available, and the manager says Jordan can learn as the shift goes. What is Jordan's best action?", "options": {"A": "Accept the clients and plan to report any mistakes to the charge nurse after the shift.", "B": "Accept the full assignment and learn each unfamiliar procedure while caring for the clients.", "C": "Refuse to work the shift and leave without speaking to the manager or the charge nurse.", "D": "Tell the manager which tasks exceed current competence and negotiate a safe assignment."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepting risk and reporting only after harm", "B": "Practising beyond competence and learning on the client", "C": "Abandoning the shift without discussion"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C134", "cluster_text": "Jordan Reyes is a practical nurse who works at a youth clinic on Tuesdays, in a long-term care home on most weekdays, and on occasional float shifts to other units. Over one week, Jordan faces several legal and accountability decisions about confidentiality, delegation to unregulated care providers, and the limits of competence. Jordan's employer expects all work to follow the regulatory body's standards of practice and the agency's policies.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tell the manager which tasks exceed current competence and negotiate a safe assignment.): Nurses practise within their own competence, so Jordan names the procedures that are outside it and works with the manager on a safe assignment, for example pairing or reassigning tasks. Learning on clients with unfamiliar epidurals and central lines endangers them. Reporting errors afterward is too late, and leaving without discussion abandons the unit and the clients."}, {"id": 1162, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At a community clinic, Mr. Alves, 46, tells the practical nurse he will stop his ex-partner from leaving town and describes bringing a firearm to her workplace tomorrow. He appears calm and asks the nurse to tell no one. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Ask him to sign a safety agreement and review the plan at his next visit.", "B": "Tell the RN or physician now so the clinic's duty to warn process begins.", "C": "Document the statement only, since confidentiality covers what a client says in the clinic.", "D": "Call the ex-partner herself to warn her before telling the RN or physician."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a safety agreement in place of a duty to warn report", "C": "Believing confidentiality has no exceptions", "D": "Warning the target personally before involving the team"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tell the RN or physician now so the clinic's duty to warn process begins.): A specific plan with a named person, a weapon and a near time is a clear risk of serious harm, so the duty to warn and protect overrides confidentiality. The nurse reports at once so the team can act. A safety agreement does not manage this risk, and documenting alone takes no protective step. Contacting the ex-partner personally bypasses the team and the proper process."}, {"id": 1163, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Cho, 34, is two hours after laparoscopic cholecystectomy under general anesthesia and reports nausea. She has not vomited, her abdomen is soft, and she asks for her sandwich. Her pain is 3 out of 10. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Give extra analgesic doses, since nausea after surgery is caused by unrelieved pain.", "B": "Give her the sandwich now since food settles an empty stomach after anesthesia.", "C": "Offer clear sips once she is alert, give prescribed antiemetic if needed and reassess nausea.", "D": "Lay her flat and hold fluids until the surgeon rounds in the morning."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming all postoperative nausea comes from pain", "B": "Offering solid food too early", "D": "Withholding fluids and lying flat, which raises aspiration risk"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Offer clear sips once she is alert, give prescribed antiemetic if needed and reassess nausea.): Nausea after general anesthesia is common, and the safe approach is to advance slowly from clear sips when she is alert, use a prescribed antiemetic if needed and reassess. A heavy meal risks vomiting. Lying flat and withholding fluids for hours increases aspiration risk and dehydration, and her pain is mild, so extra analgesia would not address the cause."}, {"id": 1164, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a home visit when Mr. Petrov is 72, he walks with a shuffling gait and short steps, and a loose rug lies at the doorway. He says he hurries to avoid his legs locking. He fell last month. Which advice best lowers his fall risk?", "options": {"A": "Walk slowly with large, deliberate steps, heel first, and remove loose rugs.", "B": "Limit walking to once daily and use a wheelchair for any other trips.", "C": "Wear soft slippers indoors and hold furniture while walking between rooms at home.", "D": "Walk quickly in short steps to keep his momentum and prevent freezing episodes."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Restricting mobility, which causes deconditioning", "C": "Using unsupportive footwear and unstable handholds", "D": "Hurrying, which worsens festination"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C140", "cluster_text": "Mr. Walter Petrov has had Parkinson disease for several years, first living at home and later in a long-term care home. His daughter, Elena, has relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis. Both are followed by the same community neurology program, where a practical nurse provides home visits, follow up in the care home and clinic teaching for Elena. The nurse follows both clients over several years.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Walk slowly with large, deliberate steps, heel first, and remove loose rugs.): Parkinson gait tends toward short, fast, shuffling steps that increase falls, so teaching slow, large, heel-first steps and removing tripping hazards addresses the cause. Hurrying worsens festination. Soft slippers and furniture are unstable supports, and limiting walking causes weakness that raises long-term fall risk."}, {"id": 1165, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Now 77 and living in long-term care, Mr. Petrov suddenly stops in a hallway, his feet appearing stuck to the floor, while a staff member calls to him from ahead. The nurse is beside him. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Hold his elbow and guide him quickly forward while telling him to take bigger steps.", "B": "Stay calm and cue him to rock side to side or step over a floor line.", "C": "Help him sit in a wheelchair at once and take him back to his room.", "D": "Tell him to stop, take a deep breath and wait until the freezing passes."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Forcing movement instead of using a cue", "C": "Ending activity instead of using a cue to restart movement", "D": "Waiting passively when a cue could restart the steps"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C140", "cluster_text": "Mr. Walter Petrov has had Parkinson disease for several years, first living at home and later in a long-term care home. His daughter, Elena, has relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis. Both are followed by the same community neurology program, where a practical nurse provides home visits, follow up in the care home and clinic teaching for Elena. The nurse follows both clients over several years.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stay calm and cue him to rock side to side or step over a floor line.): Freezing is a temporary inability to start a step, and calm cues such as shifting weight side to side, stepping over a line or counting can restart movement. Guiding him quickly forward adds pressure and a fall risk. Moving him to a wheelchair removes his mobility without trying a cue. Waiting for it to pass can leave him stuck, and the staff member calling from ahead may add pressure."}, {"id": 1166, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Elena, 36, attends the clinic and reports her legs feel weak and her vision blurs after hot baths, which she takes to relax and ease fatigue. Symptoms ease within an hour of cooling off. She works as a teacher and has no new medications. What should the nurse recommend?", "options": {"A": "Continue the hot baths, since heat relaxes muscles and treats her fatigue.", "B": "Use lukewarm or cool water, pace activities and rest, since heat can temporarily worsen MS symptoms.", "C": "Alternate very hot and cold water to build tolerance to temperature changes.", "D": "Take hot baths only before bed, which prevents symptoms from returning later."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Recommending heat for a heat-sensitive condition", "C": "Using temperature extremes to build tolerance", "D": "Delaying hot exposure rather than avoiding it"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C140", "cluster_text": "Mr. Walter Petrov has had Parkinson disease for several years, first living at home and later in a long-term care home. His daughter, Elena, has relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis. Both are followed by the same community neurology program, where a practical nurse provides home visits, follow up in the care home and clinic teaching for Elena. The nurse follows both clients over several years.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use lukewarm or cool water, pace activities and rest, since heat can temporarily worsen MS symptoms.): Raised body temperature slows nerve conduction in demyelinated nerves, so heat can temporarily worsen MS symptoms and fatigue, which fits her pattern of relief after cooling. Cooler water, activity pacing and rest are appropriate. Hot baths, even at bedtime, repeat the trigger, and alternating extremes does not build tolerance."}, {"id": 1167, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Elena, 36, phones the community nurse. She woke with new blurred vision in her left eye three days ago, and it hurts when she moves the eye. She has not been hot or ill. Which statement best describes what this may be?", "options": {"A": "A routine tiredness symptom that can be monitored at home until her next appointment.", "B": "Ordinary eye strain that will settle if she rests her eyes for a few days.", "C": "A temporary heat effect that will settle once she rests in a cool room.", "D": "A possible MS relapse that needs prompt assessment and a call to her neurologist."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying assessment of a probable relapse", "B": "Dismissing a new neurological symptom as strain", "C": "Blaming heat when no heat exposure occurred"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C140", "cluster_text": "Mr. Walter Petrov has had Parkinson disease for several years, first living at home and later in a long-term care home. His daughter, Elena, has relapsing remitting multiple sclerosis. Both are followed by the same community neurology program, where a practical nurse provides home visits, follow up in the care home and clinic teaching for Elena. The nurse follows both clients over several years.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A possible MS relapse that needs prompt assessment and a call to her neurologist.): New visual loss with pain on eye movement lasting days is a typical optic neuritis presentation and may be a multiple sclerosis relapse, which needs prompt assessment because treatment can shorten it. Heat effects resolve quickly with cooling and she has had no heat exposure. Strain and routine tiredness do not explain pain on movement, and waiting for her next appointment delays care."}, {"id": 1168, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At a pediatric clinic, Lily, 7, has bruises on her upper arms in the shape of fingers and tells the practical nurse her stepfather grips her when angry. Her mother waits outside and says Lily bruises easily. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the mother for permission to report before contacting any outside authority.", "B": "Document the bruises on a body diagram and discuss them with the mother first.", "C": "Ask Lily to repeat what happened in front of her mother before any report.", "D": "Report suspicion of abuse to the RN and child protection authority without waiting for parental consent."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Seeking parental consent for a mandatory report", "B": "Discussing findings with a possible source of harm before reporting", "C": "Questioning the child in front of a caregiver before reporting"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report suspicion of abuse to the RN and child protection authority without waiting for parental consent.): Everyone who suspects a child is being abused has a legal duty to report to child protection, and this duty overrides confidentiality and does not need parental permission. Patterned bruises and the child's statement are reasonable grounds. Asking the mother for permission or discussing it with her first could endanger Lily. Making Lily repeat her account in front of her mother risks her safety and the later investigation."}, {"id": 1169, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Reyes, 48, who has type 1 diabetes, phones the community nurse. He has vomited twice today, cannot keep food down, and his glucose is 16.4 mmol/L. He asks whether to skip his insulin because he is not eating. What advice is best?", "options": {"A": "Rest and avoid any fluids for several hours to let the vomiting settle down first.", "B": "Double his rapid-acting insulin to bring the high glucose down quickly before it rises further.", "C": "Hold his insulin until he can eat normally, since low food intake raises his hypoglycemia risk.", "D": "Continue basal insulin, check glucose and ketones often, sip fluids and contact his provider."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withholding fluids and risking dehydration", "B": "Doubling a dose without direction", "C": "Stopping insulin in type 1 diabetes during illness"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue basal insulin, check glucose and ketones often, sip fluids and contact his provider.): A person with type 1 diabetes needs basal insulin even when not eating, because stopping it risks diabetic ketoacidosis, and illness raises glucose. Sick-day care includes frequent glucose and ketone checks, small sips of fluid and contacting the provider about dose changes. Doubling a dose without direction risks hypoglycemia, and withholding fluids worsens dehydration."}, {"id": 1170, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, Mr. Hollis, 70, with COPD, is breathing 30 per minute, using accessory muscles and looking flushed. His saturation is 82 percent on room air. His care plan sets an oxygen saturation target for COPD. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Complete his scheduled medication pass first, then reassess his breathing and saturation.", "B": "Sit him upright, start ordered oxygen toward 88 to 92 percent and inform the RN.", "C": "Lay him flat to conserve energy and avoid stimulating his breathing.", "D": "Apply oxygen at the highest flow to reach 100 percent saturation as quickly as possible."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying care for a routine task", "C": "Lying flat during respiratory distress", "D": "Aiming for a normal saturation in COPD, risking carbon dioxide retention"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sit him upright, start ordered oxygen toward 88 to 92 percent and inform the RN.): Mr. Hollis is in respiratory distress, so the first steps are to sit him upright, start oxygen as ordered toward the COPD target of 88 to 92 percent and call the RN. A saturation of 100 percent can suppress his drive to breathe and cause carbon dioxide retention. Lying flat worsens breathlessness, and a medication pass can wait while he is unstable."}, {"id": 1171, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Park, 81, is bedbound with a stage 1 pressure injury over the sacrum and slides down the bed when raised. Her family suggests sitting her at 90 degrees so she stays up. Which positioning best prevents further sacral pressure?", "options": {"A": "Position her supine with a donut cushion under the sacrum to relieve pressure.", "B": "Sit her upright at 90 degrees in bed with knees bent to stop her sliding down.", "C": "Turn her to a 30 degree side-lying position supported with pillows to offload the sacrum.", "D": "Turn her to a full 90 degree side-lying position resting on the hip."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a donut device that concentrates pressure around the injury", "B": "Sitting upright, which adds shear on the sacrum", "D": "Using a 90 degree turn that loads the trochanter"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Turn her to a 30 degree side-lying position supported with pillows to offload the sacrum.): A 30 degree lateral tilt shifts weight to the fleshy buttock and away from the sacrum and trochanter, and pillows hold the position. Sitting at 90 degrees increases sliding and shear over the sacrum. A full side-lying position places pressure on the hip bone, and a donut cushion creates a ring of pressure around the injured tissue."}, {"id": 1172, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Osei, 66, takes metformin for type 2 diabetes and is booked for a contrast-enhanced CT scan tomorrow. His eGFR is 38 mL/min/1.73 m2, lower than last year. He asks whether to take tomorrow's dose. What should the nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Double his dose tonight to keep glucose stable while he is fasting for the scan.", "B": "Take his usual dose, since a normal diet protects the kidneys during the scan.", "C": "Hold the metformin around the scan as the prescriber directs, given his reduced kidney function.", "D": "Take metformin as usual, since contrast dye does not affect kidney function or glucose."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Doubling a dose in a client with reduced kidney function", "B": "Assuming diet protects the kidneys", "D": "Ignoring contrast and kidney interaction risk"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hold the metformin around the scan as the prescriber directs, given his reduced kidney function.): Contrast can injure the kidneys, and metformin build-up in reduced kidney function raises the risk of lactic acidosis, so the prescriber's plan to hold it around the scan should be followed. Taking it as usual ignores the interaction. Diet does not protect the kidneys from contrast, and doubling a dose would add risk. The nurse confirms the plan with the prescriber."}, {"id": 1173, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Fraser, 55, has alcohol use disorder and is admitted with poor intake. His potassium is 3.1 mmol/L, calcium 1.95 mmol/L and magnesium 0.5 mmol/L, and the monitor shows frequent premature beats. The RN asks what the nurse should anticipate. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Low potassium explains the premature beats, so potassium replacement is the only step needed.", "B": "Low calcium explains the premature beats, so calcium replacement is the priority.", "C": "Low magnesium can drive arrhythmias and keep potassium low, so replacement is anticipated.", "D": "The premature beats reflect alcohol withdrawal anxiety and need no electrolyte follow up."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating only potassium while the magnesium deficit persists", "B": "Treating only calcium and missing the magnesium deficit", "D": "Blaming electrolyte-driven arrhythmia on withdrawal anxiety"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Low magnesium can drive arrhythmias and keep potassium low, so replacement is anticipated.): Low magnesium is common in alcohol use disorder, increases the risk of arrhythmias and prevents potassium from correcting until magnesium is replaced. Treating only potassium or only calcium leaves the underlying deficit. Attributing the beats to anxiety ignores clear electrolyte abnormalities, so the nurse anticipates orders for magnesium along with the other electrolytes."}, {"id": 1174, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Marie, a practical nurse on a gynecology unit, has a moral objection to assisting with a scheduled procedure for a client. The client is stable and the procedure is in two days. Another qualified nurse is available. What should Marie do to meet her professional obligations?", "options": {"A": "Tell her manager early about the objection so another nurse can provide care.", "B": "Stay silent and assist, setting her personal beliefs aside to avoid conflict.", "C": "Ask a colleague to swap assignments without telling the manager about her objection.", "D": "Assist with the procedure but tell the client afterward that she disagreed with it."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Suppressing a conscientious objection", "C": "Arranging a swap informally without notifying the employer", "D": "Disclosing a personal objection to the client after taking part"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tell her manager early about the objection so another nurse can provide care.): A nurse may decline to participate for moral reasons but must notify the employer early enough that care continues without interruption for the client. Marie's early notice and the available colleague allow that. An informal swap leaves the manager unaware and the plan unsecured. Staying silent ignores her conscience, and telling the client afterward imposes her beliefs and may distress her."}, {"id": 1175, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Lindqvist, 68, receives chemotherapy for colon cancer at the cancer clinic. He refuses a palliative care referral, saying it means his treatment will stop. His nausea and fatigue are poorly controlled, and his daughter drove him in today. Which response by the practical nurse best addresses his concern?", "options": {"A": "Explain palliative care starts when curative treatment stops, so he is right to decline for now", "B": "Agree the referral can wait until chemotherapy ends, since symptoms usually settle after treatment finishes", "C": "Explain palliative care can accompany chemotherapy to relieve symptoms, and report his concern to the team", "D": "Adjust his antiemetic dose today and leave the referral discussion to his family doctor"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing palliative care replaces disease treatment, the very misconception the client holds", "B": "Delaying palliative input until treatment ends instead of offering it alongside treatment", "D": "Changing medication outside practical nurse scope and avoiding the referral question"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain palliative care can accompany chemotherapy to relieve symptoms, and report his concern to the team): Mr. Lindqvist fears that a referral ends his chemotherapy, so correcting that belief and passing his concern to the team addresses the real barrier. Waiting until chemotherapy ends leaves his nausea and fatigue untreated while he is still on treatment. Agreeing that palliative care begins when curative treatment stops repeats the same misunderstanding. Palliative care works beside disease treatment from diagnosis onward."}, {"id": 1176, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During Mrs. Beaulieu's admission assessment, she rates her back pain 5 out of 10 and smiles when her daughter visits. The nurse wants to understand how long-term pain affects her daily life. Which assessment focus best reflects care of long-term pain?", "options": {"A": "Ask which daily activities pain limits and how she copes with it", "B": "Check vital signs for tachycardia and sweating, since these confirm whether long-term pain is present", "C": "Rely on her facial expression and smiling to judge whether the pain is currently controlled", "D": "Record the numeric rating alone each shift and report it only when it exceeds seven"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Expecting acute stress signs that fade in long-term pain", "C": "Judging pain from facial expression, which is unreliable in long-term pain", "D": "Using a score without function or coping, ignoring impact on daily life"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C141", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Agnes Beaulieu, 78, was admitted four days ago to a long-term care home after moving from another facility. She has long-term low back pain, has lost weight over three months and spends most of the day in bed or a recliner. Her daughter visits often. The practical nurse is completing her assessments and sharing findings with the RN and care team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask which daily activities pain limits and how she copes with it): Long-term pain is best understood by what it prevents her from doing and how she copes, so function-focused questions fit Mrs. Beaulieu. Facial expression is unreliable because people adapt to persistent pain and may smile despite discomfort. A number alone misses the effect on sleep, walking and mood. Vital sign changes are typical of acute pain and are often absent in long-term pain."}, {"id": 1177, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Beaulieu weighs 6 kg less than she did three months ago, and her room overlooks the garden. Her daughter says she eats little because her dentures hurt. The nurse is completing the nutritional screen. Which action should the nurse take next?", "options": {"A": "Review weight change, intake, denture pain and medications, then report for a dietitian", "B": "Ask her daughter to bring favourite foods, since appetite loss is an expected part of aging", "C": "Reweigh her weekly and begin a dietitian referral if she loses a further 2 kg", "D": "Start high-calorie supplements at every meal and document how much of each she accepts"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Accepting appetite loss as normal aging instead of screening", "C": "Using weight alone as the trigger and delaying referral", "D": "Treating the symptom with supplements before finding the cause"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C141", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Agnes Beaulieu, 78, was admitted four days ago to a long-term care home after moving from another facility. She has long-term low back pain, has lost weight over three months and spends most of the day in bed or a recliner. Her daughter visits often. The practical nurse is completing her assessments and sharing findings with the RN and care team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Review weight change, intake, denture pain and medications, then report for a dietitian): A 6 kg loss in three months is significant, and the screen must include intake, the painful dentures and her medications so the cause is found. Waiting for another 2 kg delays help while she keeps losing weight. Supplements may help later but do not address why she eats little. Appetite loss is not a normal part of aging, so it needs reporting and review."}, {"id": 1178, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the Braden scale, Mrs. Beaulieu scores 12, which indicates high risk. Her sacrum is pink and blanches when pressed. She sits in her recliner most afternoons, and her dressing gown is warm. What should the nurse do next to protect her skin?", "options": {"A": "Record the Braden score and recheck her sacrum and heels at the next weekly assessment", "B": "Inspect heels, hips and other pressure points, reposition her, and report the sacral change", "C": "Massage the reddened sacral area firmly each shift and check other sites only if pain develops", "D": "Apply a donut cushion under her sacrum in the recliner and recheck the area tomorrow morning"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Documenting a risk score and delaying reassessment for a week", "C": "Massaging at-risk skin, which can damage tissue, and checking only one site", "D": "Using a ring device that concentrates pressure around the area"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C141", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Agnes Beaulieu, 78, was admitted four days ago to a long-term care home after moving from another facility. She has long-term low back pain, has lost weight over three months and spends most of the day in bed or a recliner. Her daughter visits often. The practical nurse is completing her assessments and sharing findings with the RN and care team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Inspect heels, hips and other pressure points, reposition her, and report the sacral change): A high Braden score means every pressure point needs inspection, not just the sacrum, and repositioning relieves the pressure behind the early change. Firm massage of reddened skin can injure fragile tissue. A donut cushion concentrates pressure at its ring and reduces blood flow. Recording a score without a plan lets the change progress for a week."}, {"id": 1179, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Although Mrs. Beaulieu insists she does not fall, her daughter reports two near falls at the previous home. She walks with a shuffle, takes a sedative at night and needs help rising. A fall risk tool places her at high risk. Which interpretation should guide the plan?", "options": {"A": "Her own report of no falls is dependable, so routine precautions are enough until she falls", "B": "The sedative is given only at night, so risk is limited to bedtime hours", "C": "The near falls happened at another home, so they have little bearing on her risk here", "D": "Her history, gait and sedative outweigh her statement, so begin precautions and tell the RN"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on the client's self-report despite contrary evidence", "B": "Limiting medication risk to the time of administration", "C": "Dismissing fall history because it came from a prior setting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C141", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Agnes Beaulieu, 78, was admitted four days ago to a long-term care home after moving from another facility. She has long-term low back pain, has lost weight over three months and spends most of the day in bed or a recliner. Her daughter visits often. The practical nurse is completing her assessments and sharing findings with the RN and care team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Her history, gait and sedative outweigh her statement, so begin precautions and tell the RN): Her near falls, shuffling gait, need for help rising and sedative together put her at high risk, whatever she believes about herself. Self-report is often unreliable in older adults who underestimate their risk. Prior near falls are among the strongest predictors of a future fall. Sedative effects and weakness persist into the day, so risk is not limited to bedtime."}, {"id": 1180, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At a public health clinic, a 29-year-old man tests positive for syphilis and asks the practical nurse not to tell anyone, saying he never agreed to be reported. He is calm and has no symptoms. Which action best reflects the nurse's legal and ethical duty?", "options": {"A": "Keep the result confidential because he refused consent, and encourage him to tell his partners himself", "B": "Report the case as legislation requires, and explain to him what is shared and why", "C": "Obtain his written consent before reporting, and delay the report until he agrees", "D": "Report only if he develops symptoms, since silent infections fall outside the reporting requirement"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating confidentiality as absolute when a legal duty applies", "C": "Making a mandatory report depend on consent", "D": "Believing only symptomatic infections are reportable"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the case as legislation requires, and explain to him what is shared and why): Syphilis is a reportable communicable disease, so the law requires the report and client consent is not needed. Explaining what is shared and why respects him and keeps trust. Keeping the result private because he refused breaches the legal duty. Waiting for consent or for symptoms delays public health follow-up that the law requires."}, {"id": 1181, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor's order reads levothyroxine 100 micrograms daily, but the pharmacy label on his supply states 0.1 mg per tablet. The tablets are small and white, and he takes one each morning before breakfast. He asks whether the two doses match. Which teaching statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "100 micrograms equals 0.1 mg, so one tablet matches his order", "B": "100 micrograms equals 1 mg, so he should take one tenth of a tablet", "C": "100 micrograms equals 0.01 mg, so he needs ten tablets to match the order", "D": "The units cannot be compared, so he should hold the tablet until the pharmacist relabels it"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Moving the decimal the wrong way and overstating the dose tenfold", "C": "Moving the decimal the wrong way and understating the dose tenfold", "D": "Not knowing that micrograms and milligrams convert directly"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C136", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse makes a home care visit to the Okafor family. Emeka, 58, takes levothyroxine; his daughter Ada, 4, has an ear infection and weighs 33 pounds on the home scale; her infant brother, 9 months, has a fever. The nurse works from written orders and a drug reference, verifies every dose in Canadian units and reports concerns to the supervising nurse practitioner.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (100 micrograms equals 0.1 mg, so one tablet matches his order): One milligram equals 1000 micrograms, so 100 micrograms is 0.1 mg and one tablet matches the order. Reading it as 1 mg would suggest cutting the tablet and would underdose him. Reading it as 0.01 mg would suggest ten tablets and risk a tenfold overdose. The units convert directly, so holding the dose is unnecessary."}, {"id": 1182, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ada weighs 33 pounds on the family's home scale. The prescriber's order is written in milligrams per kilogram, and the nurse is about to calculate the antibiotic dose. A colleague suggests using 33 in the formula because it is the number shown. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Estimate her weight as 20 kilograms from her age instead of using the scale reading.", "B": "Multiply 33 pounds by 2.2 to get 72.6 kilograms, then calculate the dose from that weight", "C": "Use 33 in the formula, since the child's weight in pounds is acceptable for dosing", "D": "Divide 33 pounds by 2.2 to get 15 kilograms, then calculate the dose from that weight"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Estimating weight instead of using the measured value", "B": "Multiplying instead of dividing when converting pounds to kilograms", "C": "Using pounds in a milligram per kilogram formula"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C136", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse makes a home care visit to the Okafor family. Emeka, 58, takes levothyroxine; his daughter Ada, 4, has an ear infection and weighs 33 pounds on the home scale; her infant brother, 9 months, has a fever. The nurse works from written orders and a drug reference, verifies every dose in Canadian units and reports concerns to the supervising nurse practitioner.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Divide 33 pounds by 2.2 to get 15 kilograms, then calculate the dose from that weight): The order is per kilogram, so the weight must be converted: 33 divided by 2.2 gives 15 kg. Multiplying by 2.2 would give an impossible 72.6 kg and cause a large overdose. Using 33 as if it were kilograms would more than double the dose. An age-based estimate ignores the measured weight available."}, {"id": 1183, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ada weighs 15 kilograms. The order is amoxicillin 40 mg per kilogram per day, divided into two equal doses, and the pink suspension is labelled 250 mg per 5 mL. Her mother asks how much medication each dose will contain. How much amoxicillin is in each dose?", "options": {"A": "150 mg per dose, found by halving the total twice after multiplying 40 by 15", "B": "600 mg per dose, found by multiplying 40 by 15 for the whole day", "C": "300 mg per dose, found by multiplying 40 by 15 and halving the total", "D": "250 mg per dose, matching the strength printed on the suspension label"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dividing the daily total too many times", "B": "Forgetting to divide the daily total into two doses", "D": "Using the label strength instead of a weight-based calculation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C136", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse makes a home care visit to the Okafor family. Emeka, 58, takes levothyroxine; his daughter Ada, 4, has an ear infection and weighs 33 pounds on the home scale; her infant brother, 9 months, has a fever. The nurse works from written orders and a drug reference, verifies every dose in Canadian units and reports concerns to the supervising nurse practitioner.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (300 mg per dose, found by multiplying 40 by 15 and halving the total): The daily dose is 40 mg times 15 kg, or 600 mg, and splitting it into two equal doses gives 300 mg each. Giving 600 mg per dose would double the intended daily amount. Halving twice gives 150 mg and underdoses her. The 250 mg on the label describes concentration per 5 mL, not her dose."}, {"id": 1184, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The Okafor infant weighs 9 kilograms and has a fever. The prescriber ordered acetaminophen 200 mg every 4 hours. The drug reference lists 10 to 15 mg per kilogram per dose, and the pharmacy label matches the order. How should the nurse interpret the order?", "options": {"A": "The dose is lower than the range, so give it and ask whether more is needed", "B": "200 mg exceeds the 135 mg maximum for his weight, so hold it and clarify", "C": "The dose is acceptable because the pharmacy label matches the order, so give it as written", "D": "The dose falls between 90 and 135 mg, so it is within range"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Miscomparing the ordered dose with the weight-based range", "C": "Trusting the pharmacy label without checking against a safe range", "D": "Misreading the calculated range as containing the ordered dose"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C136", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse makes a home care visit to the Okafor family. Emeka, 58, takes levothyroxine; his daughter Ada, 4, has an ear infection and weighs 33 pounds on the home scale; her infant brother, 9 months, has a fever. The nurse works from written orders and a drug reference, verifies every dose in Canadian units and reports concerns to the supervising nurse practitioner.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (200 mg exceeds the 135 mg maximum for his weight, so hold it and clarify): At 9 kg the safe range is 90 to 135 mg per dose, so 200 mg is well above the maximum and must be clarified before giving. A matching label only shows the pharmacy copied the order, not that the dose is safe. The range is 90 to 135 mg, which does not include 200 mg. The dose is higher than the range, not lower, so giving it risks overdose."}, {"id": 1185, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On the mental health unit, a 40-year-old client paces, clenches his fists and shouts about his medications while other clients stand in the hallway. The nurse is nearby and a team member is coming. Which first step best applies de-escalation principles?", "options": {"A": "Stay at a safe distance, speak calmly and briefly, and ask what he needs", "B": "Step close and rest a hand on his arm to calm him while explaining the plan", "C": "Match his volume and give firm commands so that he knows the nurse is in control", "D": "Give a sedative as needed from the medication cabinet to settle him before talking"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Entering personal space and touching an agitated client", "C": "Raising the voice, which escalates agitation", "D": "Using medication before verbal de-escalation and outside the order process"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C133", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works for a regional health organization and covers shifts on a mental health unit, in a long-term care home, on a rehabilitation unit, in a community clinic and on home care visits. The organization has a safety culture, a no-lift policy and written violence prevention procedures, and the nurse follows them at every site, reporting hazards promptly.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stay at a safe distance, speak calmly and briefly, and ask what he needs): Calm, brief speech from a safe distance lowers arousal and gives him a chance to say what is bothering him while help arrives. Matching his volume with commands escalates the confrontation. Moving close or touching him invades personal space and can provoke an attack. Medication is considered after verbal approaches fail and under the proper order."}, {"id": 1186, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In the long-term care home, a 90-year-old resident with weak legs needs to move from bed to wheelchair. She is cooperative and can bear a little weight. The home follows a no-lift policy and a mechanical lift is down the hall. Which action best fits safe patient handling principles?", "options": {"A": "Assess her mobility, then use a mechanical or transfer aid with a second staff member", "B": "Use proper body mechanics and lift her manually, since she can bear some weight", "C": "Have two colleagues lift her together with a gait belt, which spreads the effort evenly", "D": "Ask her to grip the nurse's neck and pivot while the nurse bends her knees"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relying on body mechanics alone to justify manual lifting", "C": "Believing two staff lifting manually is safe", "D": "Using a hold that risks injury to both resident and nurse"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C133", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works for a regional health organization and covers shifts on a mental health unit, in a long-term care home, on a rehabilitation unit, in a community clinic and on home care visits. The organization has a safety culture, a no-lift policy and written violence prevention procedures, and the nurse follows them at every site, reporting hazards promptly.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Assess her mobility, then use a mechanical or transfer aid with a second staff member): Safe handling begins with assessing how much she can do and then using the lift or transfer aid, with help, as the no-lift policy requires. Good body mechanics do not remove the injury risk of lifting a person. Letting a resident hang from the nurse's neck risks a fall and strain. Two staff lifting manually, even with a belt, still carries injury risk that equipment avoids."}, {"id": 1187, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In the rehabilitation unit, an 85 kg client recovering from hip replacement needs to move from bed to chair. Two staff members offer to lift him manually because they are strong. A ceiling lift is available with a 200 kg limit. Which interpretation of the situation is correct?", "options": {"A": "Strength does not make manual lifting safe, and he is within the lift limit", "B": "Using a gait belt with two staff turns a manual lift into a safe assisted transfer", "C": "Two staff are enough, since 85 kg is below the weight at which lifts are required", "D": "The lift is for clients over 100 kg, so a manual transfer is acceptable for him"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believing a gait belt makes a manual lift safe", "C": "Assuming a weight threshold exists for lifting equipment", "D": "Inventing a weight cut-off that excludes him from lift use"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C133", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works for a regional health organization and covers shifts on a mental health unit, in a long-term care home, on a rehabilitation unit, in a community clinic and on home care visits. The organization has a safety culture, a no-lift policy and written violence prevention procedures, and the nurse follows them at every site, reporting hazards promptly.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Strength does not make manual lifting safe, and he is within the lift limit): No-lift practice rests on injury risk, and staff strength does not remove it, so the ceiling lift suits an 85 kg client within its 200 kg limit. There is no weight below which manual lifting becomes acceptable. A gait belt helps guide a client but does not make a manual lift safe. The lift is not restricted to clients over 100 kg."}, {"id": 1188, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "During a home visit, the nurse repositions a client in a low bed that cannot be raised. She already feels a mild ache in her lower back from earlier visits. The client can assist slightly. Which action best protects the nurse's back?", "options": {"A": "Pull the client toward her with one arm while leaning over the bed rail.", "B": "Keep the knees locked, bend at the hips and slide the client in one pull.", "C": "Wear a back support belt and lift the client directly with the back straight.", "D": "Spread the feet, bend the knees, keep the load close and push with the legs"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using one arm and leaning, which loads the spine", "B": "Bending at the hips with locked knees", "C": "Relying on a belt and lifting instead of using safe technique"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C133", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works for a regional health organization and covers shifts on a mental health unit, in a long-term care home, on a rehabilitation unit, in a community clinic and on home care visits. The organization has a safety culture, a no-lift policy and written violence prevention procedures, and the nurse follows them at every site, reporting hazards promptly.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Spread the feet, bend the knees, keep the load close and push with the legs): A wide stance, bent knees, a close load and leg power keep the back in line and use the strongest muscles, which protects an already sore back. Pulling with one arm while leaning adds reach and rotation strain. Bending at the hips with locked knees concentrates force on the lumbar spine. A support belt does not make a direct lift safe, and a no-lift policy applies."}, {"id": 1189, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At the clinic, the nurse pricks her finger with a used needle after giving an injection. The client has no known infections. Her shift ends in twenty minutes and the waiting room is busy. Which action should she take first?", "options": {"A": "Cover the site with a bandage and wait for the client's test results before reporting", "B": "Squeeze the wound firmly to push blood out, then finish the shift and report later", "C": "Soak the finger in bleach for several minutes to disinfect the needle puncture wound", "D": "Wash the site with soap and running water, then report the injury and be assessed."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for client results before seeking assessment and prophylaxis", "B": "Squeezing the wound and delaying reporting", "C": "Using bleach, which damages tissue and does not help"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C133", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works for a regional health organization and covers shifts on a mental health unit, in a long-term care home, on a rehabilitation unit, in a community clinic and on home care visits. The organization has a safety culture, a no-lift policy and written violence prevention procedures, and the nurse follows them at every site, reporting hazards promptly.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wash the site with soap and running water, then report the injury and be assessed.): Immediate washing with soap and water reduces contamination, and prompt reporting starts follow-up and any prophylaxis that works best when given early. Squeezing the wound does not remove infection and delay loses time. Bleach injures tissue without lowering risk. Waiting for test results postpones assessment when timing matters."}, {"id": 1190, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Alvarez does not respond when the nurse shakes his shoulder and calls his name. His chest moves only with an occasional gasp, and no one else is in the room. The call bell is within reach. Which action should the nurse take first?", "options": {"A": "Give two rescue breaths with a pocket mask and then call for the code team", "B": "Place him on oxygen at high flow and wait to see whether breathing improves", "C": "Call for help and the AED, check pulse up to 10 seconds, then compress", "D": "Check the carotid pulse for a full minute to confirm arrest before calling anyone"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Starting breaths before activating the response", "B": "Waiting and treating occasional gasps as adequate breathing", "D": "Taking a long pulse check and delaying help"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C138", "cluster_text": "At 0210 on a medical ward, the practical nurse finds Mr. Alvarez, 67, unresponsive in bed. He was admitted yesterday with pneumonia. His skin is pale and he gasps only occasionally. The ward has a call bell, a crash cart, an automated external defibrillator and a code team. The nurse holds current basic life support certification and follows hospital policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Call for help and the AED, check pulse up to 10 seconds, then compress): An unresponsive adult who is only gasping is in cardiac arrest, so the nurse activates the team, gets the AED, limits the pulse check to ten seconds and starts compressions. A full minute pulse check wastes time that the brain cannot spare. Starting with breaths delays compressions and help. Gasping is not normal breathing, so waiting on oxygen is unsafe."}, {"id": 1191, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Compressions are under way. A colleague taking over presses slowly and lightly, about 70 per minute and 3 cm deep, and says she is tired. The patient is an adult lying on a firm surface. Which feedback to the colleague is best?", "options": {"A": "Slow to 60 per minute and press 4 cm deep to last longer.", "B": "Push harder and faster, at 100 to 120 per minute and 5 to 6 cm deep", "C": "Keep 100 to 120 per minute but limit depth to 3 to 4 cm.", "D": "Suggest she tap the chest quickly at 150 per minute to reduce arm strain."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a rate and depth below the recommended range", "C": "Keeping the correct rate but using inadequate depth", "D": "Using a rapid tapping rate that does not move blood"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C138", "cluster_text": "At 0210 on a medical ward, the practical nurse finds Mr. Alvarez, 67, unresponsive in bed. He was admitted yesterday with pneumonia. His skin is pale and he gasps only occasionally. The ward has a call bell, a crash cart, an automated external defibrillator and a code team. The nurse holds current basic life support certification and follows hospital policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Push harder and faster, at 100 to 120 per minute and 5 to 6 cm deep): Effective CPR needs a rate of 100 to 120 per minute and a depth of 5 to 6 cm, so her slow, shallow compressions need correcting right away. Slowing further or keeping a depth of 3 to 4 cm does not generate adequate blood flow. Tapping at 150 per minute is too fast and too shallow. Rib injury is a possible result of good CPR but is accepted over ineffective compressions."}, {"id": 1192, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The AED pads are attached and the device announces, analyzing rhythm, do not touch the patient. A colleague is still holding the patient's head to keep the airway open, and the bed rails are down. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Tell everyone, including the colleague holding the head, to stand clear while the AED analyzes", "B": "Continue compressions during analysis to avoid any interruption to blood flow", "C": "Switch the AED off and resume compressions until the code team arrives", "D": "Let the colleague keep steadying the head, since light contact will not affect analysis"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Compressing during analysis, which causes artifact", "C": "Turning off the AED and abandoning defibrillation", "D": "Believing light contact does not disturb rhythm analysis"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C138", "cluster_text": "At 0210 on a medical ward, the practical nurse finds Mr. Alvarez, 67, unresponsive in bed. He was admitted yesterday with pneumonia. His skin is pale and he gasps only occasionally. The ward has a call bell, a crash cart, an automated external defibrillator and a code team. The nurse holds current basic life support certification and follows hospital policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tell everyone, including the colleague holding the head, to stand clear while the AED analyzes): Movement or touch during analysis can create artifact and lead the AED to misread the rhythm, so everyone including the person at the head must stand clear. Light contact still transmits motion. Continuing compressions during analysis interferes with the reading. Turning the device off removes the chance of a shock for a shockable rhythm."}, {"id": 1193, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The AED has delivered one shock to Mr. Alvarez, whose rhythm was shockable. His chest is still, he does not respond, and the monitor from the code team has not arrived. The nurse wonders when to restart. What should the nurse do immediately after the shock?", "options": {"A": "Resume chest compressions at once and continue for about two minutes before the next analysis", "B": "Check for a pulse for thirty seconds to see whether the shock worked", "C": "Wait for the AED to prompt a second analysis before touching him", "D": "Give rescue breaths alone for two minutes, then restart compressions with the second analysis"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Pausing for a prolonged pulse check after the shock", "C": "Delaying compressions while waiting for the device", "D": "Giving breaths without compressions after the shock"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C138", "cluster_text": "At 0210 on a medical ward, the practical nurse finds Mr. Alvarez, 67, unresponsive in bed. He was admitted yesterday with pneumonia. His skin is pale and he gasps only occasionally. The ward has a call bell, a crash cart, an automated external defibrillator and a code team. The nurse holds current basic life support certification and follows hospital policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Resume chest compressions at once and continue for about two minutes before the next analysis): Compressions restart immediately after a shock because the heart often needs perfusion before it can maintain a rhythm, and the AED reanalyzes after about two minutes. A thirty second pulse check interrupts circulation. Waiting for the device leaves the brain without blood flow. Breaths alone provide no circulation."}, {"id": 1194, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Fruity breath and deep rapid breathing at 28 per minute are noted in a 24-year-old with type 1 diabetes in the emergency department, with glucose of 31 mmol/L and low serum bicarbonate. A student suggests coaching her to slow her breathing so she relaxes. What should the nurse explain to the team?", "options": {"A": "The breathing signals respiratory failure, so she needs sedation to lower her work of breathing", "B": "The breathing is caused by low oxygen, so oxygen alone will settle the acid problem", "C": "The fast deep breathing compensates for acid buildup, so treat with insulin and fluids", "D": "The breathing reflects anxiety, so coaching her to slow it should come before other care"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Misreading compensation as failure and proposing harmful sedation", "B": "Treating the breathing as hypoxia instead of acidosis", "D": "Mistaking Kussmaul breathing for anxiety and suppressing compensation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The fast deep breathing compensates for acid buildup, so treat with insulin and fluids): Deep rapid respirations in diabetic ketoacidosis blow off carbon dioxide to offset metabolic acidosis, so the cause is treated with insulin, fluids and close monitoring. Coaching her to slow down would remove her compensation and worsen acidosis. Sedation could cause respiratory depression. Oxygen does not correct ketone-driven acid buildup."}, {"id": 1195, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 58-year-old man with diabetes is on bed rest after surgery and has reduced sensation in both feet. His heels are intact, but he lies on his back most of the day. The nurse plans prevention. Which method best offloads his heels?", "options": {"A": "Wrap each heel in a thick layer of gauze and cotton padding", "B": "Position a donut ring cushion under each heel to spread the pressure around it", "C": "Place a pillow lengthwise under both calves so the heels float off the mattress", "D": "Rub each heel firmly with lotion twice daily to improve circulation to the skin"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Padding the heel while leaving it under load", "B": "Using a ring device that concentrates pressure around the heel", "D": "Massaging at-risk skin instead of removing pressure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Place a pillow lengthwise under both calves so the heels float off the mattress): Floating the heels by supporting the calves removes pressure from the heel completely, which matters with reduced sensation and diabetes. A donut ring creates a pressure edge around the heel. Padding leaves the heel bearing weight against the mattress. Firm rubbing can damage fragile skin and does not remove pressure."}, {"id": 1196, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the asthma clinic, a 6-year-old girl sprays her salbutamol inhaler straight into her mouth without a spacer and coughs. Her father says the spacer is in the car because it is awkward. She is wheezing mildly. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Give one puff now and teach spacer use at the next visit.", "B": "Tell him spacers suit infants, so a school-age child can manage without one.", "C": "Explain a spacer improves delivery, demonstrate it, and watch her practise using one", "D": "Allow direct use today since she is only mildly wheezy, and mention the spacer at review"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Postponing teaching and allowing poor delivery now", "B": "Believing spacers are needed only for infants", "D": "Postponing correct technique because symptoms are mild"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C135", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works at a community health centre on a busy Thursday. The morning includes a children's asthma clinic. At midday a health fair runs in the lobby for adults and teens, and the afternoon includes an adult screening clinic and a cardiac rehabilitation group. The nurse provides teaching and care at each of these services, supervised by an RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain a spacer improves delivery, demonstrate it, and watch her practise using one): Young children cannot coordinate pressing and breathing, so a spacer improves delivery to the lungs, and a demonstration with return practice confirms the skill. Calling the spacer unnecessary for her age ignores her poor technique. Delaying teaching to a later visit, or allowing direct use today, leaves her with poor delivery during wheezing. Changing the dose pattern needs an order."}, {"id": 1197, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the health fair, the nurse has time for one booth that best represents primary prevention for adults and youth. Visitors are mostly healthy, though some have family histories of diabetes and heart disease. Several services are available that day. Which service is the best example of primary prevention?", "options": {"A": "Conducting a foot-care clinic for clients with established diabetic ulcers", "B": "Offering blood pressure screening at the booth to find high readings early in adults", "C": "Running a support group for people recovering from a stroke", "D": "Teaching healthy eating and physical activity to prevent chronic disease before it develops"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing management of existing complications, also tertiary prevention", "B": "Choosing early detection screening, which is secondary prevention", "C": "Choosing recovery support, which is tertiary prevention"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C135", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works at a community health centre on a busy Thursday. The morning includes a children's asthma clinic. At midday a health fair runs in the lobby for adults and teens, and the afternoon includes an adult screening clinic and a cardiac rehabilitation group. The nurse provides teaching and care at each of these services, supervised by an RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Teaching healthy eating and physical activity to prevent chronic disease before it develops): Primary prevention stops disease before it starts, so teaching healthy eating and activity to mostly healthy visitors fits. Blood pressure screening finds disease early, which is secondary prevention. A stroke recovery group limits disability after disease and is tertiary. A foot-care clinic for established ulcers manages existing complications."}, {"id": 1198, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the afternoon screening visit, a 52-year-old woman has a blood pressure of 158/96 mmHg and no symptoms. Her father had a stroke. She works full-time, drives herself in, and has no diabetes and normal bloodwork from last year. Which activity represents secondary prevention for her?", "options": {"A": "Referring her to a rehabilitation program to restore daily function and reduce disability", "B": "Arranging a home visit to help her manage complications of established heart failure", "C": "Teaching her to exercise and limit salt so that hypertension does not develop", "D": "Repeating her readings and referring her for diagnosis and early treatment"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing rehabilitation, which is tertiary prevention", "B": "Choosing management of established disease, which is tertiary prevention", "C": "Choosing health teaching before disease, which is primary prevention"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C135", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works at a community health centre on a busy Thursday. The morning includes a children's asthma clinic. At midday a health fair runs in the lobby for adults and teens, and the afternoon includes an adult screening clinic and a cardiac rehabilitation group. The nurse provides teaching and care at each of these services, supervised by an RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Repeating her readings and referring her for diagnosis and early treatment): Secondary prevention finds disease early and treats it before symptoms or complications, which matches confirming her high readings and referring for diagnosis. Rehabilitation restores function after illness and is tertiary. Teaching to prevent hypertension is primary, but her readings are already high. Managing established heart failure is also tertiary."}, {"id": 1199, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Four weeks after a myocardial infarction, a 61-year-old man attends supervised exercise sessions in the cardiac rehabilitation group. He feels well and asks why he must keep attending the weekly program. Which statement best describes tertiary prevention in his care?", "options": {"A": "Supervised exercise and teaching limit disability and reduce repeat cardiac events", "B": "Cholesterol screening finds early disease before symptoms appear in people who feel well", "C": "Annual influenza immunization protects him and others from infection before disease develops", "D": "Nutrition education for the general public lowers the number of new cases of heart disease"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Choosing screening, which is secondary prevention", "C": "Choosing immunization, which is primary prevention", "D": "Choosing population education, which is primary prevention"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C135", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works at a community health centre on a busy Thursday. The morning includes a children's asthma clinic. At midday a health fair runs in the lobby for adults and teens, and the afternoon includes an adult screening clinic and a cardiac rehabilitation group. The nurse provides teaching and care at each of these services, supervised by an RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Supervised exercise and teaching limit disability and reduce repeat cardiac events): Tertiary prevention reduces the effects and recurrence of established disease, which is what supervised exercise and teaching do after a heart attack. Immunization prevents infection and is primary. Cholesterol screening detects early disease in well people and is secondary. Public nutrition education aims at preventing new cases and is primary."}, {"id": 1200, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Two clients wait at triage in the emergency department: a 55-year-old man with crushing chest pain spreading to his left arm, sweating and nausea, and a woman with a sprained ankle who has waited an hour. Which client should the team attend first?", "options": {"A": "The man with chest pain, after a full history and allergy list are completed first", "B": "The woman, since she has waited the longest already.", "C": "The man with chest pain, who needs an ECG immediately", "D": "Both together, with one nurse for each client at once."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying the ECG for a lengthy history in a possible heart attack", "B": "Prioritizing by waiting time instead of acuity", "D": "Splitting attention instead of prioritizing the higher acuity client"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The man with chest pain, who needs an ECG immediately): Crushing chest pain with sweating and arm radiation suggests a heart attack, so an immediate ECG and physician assessment come first. A full history before the ECG delays time-sensitive treatment. Waiting time does not outrank a possible life threat. Treating both together without a priority order splits resources when the man needs immediate attention."}, {"id": 1201, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a surgical ward, a practical nurse is caring for a 60-year-old man one day after bowel surgery. His heart rate has risen to 118, his blood pressure has dropped to 88/50 mmHg and he is confused. How should the practical nurse interpret her role?", "options": {"A": "Start a fluid bolus from the standing orders and report after the next scheduled rounds", "B": "Recheck vital signs in fifteen minutes before reporting.", "C": "Recognize the deterioration, stay with him, and report promptly to the RN", "D": "Position him flat, apply oxygen and call the physician directly."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating before reporting and delaying communication", "B": "Delaying a report when the client is deteriorating", "D": "Bypassing the RN and acting beyond PN role"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recognize the deterioration, stay with him, and report promptly to the RN): A rising heart rate, falling pressure and confusion signal an unstable client, so the practical nurse stays with him and reports at once so the RN or physician can direct care. Unstable and unpredictable situations need RN or physician involvement, not independent PN management. Rechecking first delays rescue. Calling the physician directly bypasses the RN, and giving fluids and reporting later puts treatment ahead of communication."}, {"id": 1202, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 64-year-old man on a surgical ward has a chest tube to water-seal drainage. While he walks to the bathroom, the tube separates from the drainage unit tubing. He is breathing comfortably and the nurse is beside him. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Cover the end with sterile gauze and leave it open to air until help arrives", "B": "Help him to bed and wait for the drainage unit to be replaced before reconnecting", "C": "Clamp the chest tube close to his chest and walk him back to bed", "D": "Reconnect the tube promptly, then assess his breathing and notify the RN"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaving the tube open to air, risking pneumothorax", "B": "Delaying reconnection while the system stays open", "C": "Clamping a chest tube without direction, risking tension pneumothorax"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reconnect the tube promptly, then assess his breathing and notify the RN): Prompt reconnection restores the closed drainage system, and assessing his breathing and notifying the RN follows. Clamping can trap air and cause a tension pneumothorax. Leaving the tube open to air lets air enter the pleural space. Waiting for a new unit leaves the system open longer."}, {"id": 1203, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a practical nurse finds a vial of insulin of the wrong concentration in a client's drawer. She catches the mix-up before giving anything and no harm occurs. A colleague says no report is needed. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Report only if the same error happens again on another shift", "B": "Note it in the shift report and leave the formal report to the manager", "C": "Report the near miss in the incident system so the cause can be found", "D": "Tell the pharmacist verbally and skip reporting, since no client was affected"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for repetition before reporting", "B": "Passing the responsibility for formal reporting to someone else", "D": "Treating a no-harm event as unreportable"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the near miss in the incident system so the cause can be found): Near misses reveal system weaknesses before harm occurs, so reporting lets the team find the cause and fix it. A verbal mention without a report leaves no record to analyze. Waiting for a repeat accepts avoidable risk. A shift report note does not replace the incident report that the nurse is responsible for completing."}, {"id": 1204, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Newly diagnosed with type 2 diabetes, a 54-year-old accountant is being taught meal management by a practical nurse. He is motivated but dislikes lectures and wants practical use. He mentions his work lunches are rushed and the clinic gave him a binder. Which teaching approach best applies adult learning principles?", "options": {"A": "Give him a printed meal plan and ask him to follow it without changes", "B": "Use his own work lunches as examples, set goals together, and let him practise choices", "C": "Teach basic nutrition facts first, then discuss his lunches only if time remains afterwards", "D": "Deliver a structured lecture covering all diabetes topics, then give him the binder to read"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Prescribing a plan without involving the learner", "C": "Delaying relevance to the learner's situation", "D": "Treating the adult as a passive recipient of lecture"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use his own work lunches as examples, set goals together, and let him practise choices): Adults learn best when content is relevant to their lives, they take part in setting goals and they can practise, so using his work lunches fits. A lecture treats him as a passive learner. A fixed plan leaves out his input. Leaving his real situation until the end delays the relevance that motivates adult learners."}, {"id": 1205, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Dana, a practical nurse in a hospital, receives a subpoena to testify in court about a former client's care. The client has not given consent for her to speak. She is worried about breaching confidentiality, and the hearing is in three weeks. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Ask the former client for verbal consent and then testify about what she recalls", "B": "Share the complete chart with the lawyer who served the subpoena before the hearing date.", "C": "Refuse to attend, since the client has not consented to any disclosure of care details", "D": "Seek guidance from the privacy officer and disclose only what the court order requires."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Making testimony depend on consent and disclosing broadly", "B": "Releasing the whole chart beyond the order", "C": "Refusing a legal summons because of confidentiality"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Seek guidance from the privacy officer and disclose only what the court order requires.): A subpoena is a legal requirement that overrides confidentiality to the extent the court requires, so the nurse seeks guidance and discloses only what is ordered. Refusing to attend defies the court. Handing the full chart to a lawyer exceeds what the order requires. Disclosure under a court order does not depend on client consent."}, {"id": 1206, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad's condition is declining, and about ten relatives want to stay at his bedside through the night, which they say is important in their tradition. The unit policy lists two visitors at a time. He is calm and the room is private. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Explain that policy allows two visitors at a time and ask the others to wait outside", "B": "Ask the RN to approve flexible family presence near death, and arrange privacy", "C": "Let all relatives stay and chart it later, since the room is private", "D": "Offer a waiting lounge and rotate relatives every thirty minutes to keep policy numbers"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying a visiting limit rigidly at end of life", "C": "Bypassing policy without consulting the team", "D": "Imposing rotation that disrupts family presence"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C155", "cluster_text": "Mr. Samir Haddad, 74, is dying on a palliative care unit. He also has type 2 diabetes treated with insulin. His large family, including his wife, four adult children and several grandchildren, is devout and wishes to follow their faith practices. The practical nurse is assigned to his care and works with the RN, the unit chaplain and the dietary department.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask the RN to approve flexible family presence near death, and arrange privacy): Family presence near death is a priority in culturally safe palliative care, so the nurse works with the RN to adapt the visiting policy and supports the family with seating and privacy. Strict application of a two-person limit causes distress. Allowing everyone without discussion bypasses accountability. Rotating relatives disrupts the vigil."}, {"id": 1207, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad has died peacefully. His family asks to wash the body themselves, keep it covered and delay transport while they pray. His wife is composed but asks the nurse for time. The morgue transport is waiting in the hallway. What does cultural safety require of the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Complete postmortem care first, then invite the family to view afterward.", "B": "Let the family pray in the hallway while staff wash the body.", "C": "Honour the requests within policy and involve the RN and chaplain", "D": "Allow ten minutes with the body, then remove him to free the room"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Placing routine tasks before the family's rites", "B": "Separating the family from the rites they asked to perform", "D": "Setting an arbitrary time limit that cuts short a ritual"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C155", "cluster_text": "Mr. Samir Haddad, 74, is dying on a palliative care unit. He also has type 2 diabetes treated with insulin. His large family, including his wife, four adult children and several grandchildren, is devout and wishes to follow their faith practices. The practical nurse is assigned to his care and works with the RN, the unit chaplain and the dietary department.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Honour the requests within policy and involve the RN and chaplain): Cultural safety means supporting the family's rites after death within policy, with the RN and chaplain helping and transport waiting. Completing postmortem care first treats routine tasks above the family's needs. Washing the body without them denies the ritual they asked to perform. A ten minute limit disregards the time the rites need."}, {"id": 1208, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Earlier in the admission, Mr. Haddad's meal tray arrives with a pork-based sausage while his chart shows a halal dietary request. His wife is worried he will refuse to eat. He is on insulin. Which action best respects culture and safety?", "options": {"A": "Ask the family to bring food from home and skip the hospital meal", "B": "Remove the sausage and serve the remaining items, since he can eat other foods", "C": "Replace the tray with a halal meal and update his dietary order", "D": "Explain that dietary requests are preferences and the meal will be served as planned"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Skipping the meal, which risks hypoglycemia on insulin", "B": "Serving food that may still be cross-contaminated", "D": "Treating a religious dietary requirement as a preference"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C155", "cluster_text": "Mr. Samir Haddad, 74, is dying on a palliative care unit. He also has type 2 diabetes treated with insulin. His large family, including his wife, four adult children and several grandchildren, is devout and wishes to follow their faith practices. The practical nurse is assigned to his care and works with the RN, the unit chaplain and the dietary department.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Replace the tray with a halal meal and update his dietary order): A halal request is a religious requirement, so the tray is replaced and the order corrected to prevent repeat errors, while his insulin still needs a meal. Removing the sausage leaves other items that may have touched pork. Calling it a preference disrespects his faith. Skipping the hospital meal risks low glucose after insulin."}, {"id": 1209, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad, who is alert, asks to fast during a religious observance. He takes basal and mealtime insulin, and his morning glucose was 6.2 mmol/L. His wife supports him and he has a swollen ankle the team is monitoring. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Hold all insulin during the fast and recheck his glucose only at meal times", "B": "Let him fast and give his usual insulin doses at the usual times", "C": "Tell him fasting is not allowed while he takes insulin and offer a snack instead", "D": "Respect his wish, inform the team, and request a safe insulin and glucose monitoring plan"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withholding insulin on her own, risking hyperglycemia", "B": "Giving usual insulin during a fast, risking hypoglycemia", "C": "Denying the request instead of supporting a safe plan"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C155", "cluster_text": "Mr. Samir Haddad, 74, is dying on a palliative care unit. He also has type 2 diabetes treated with insulin. His large family, including his wife, four adult children and several grandchildren, is devout and wishes to follow their faith practices. The practical nurse is assigned to his care and works with the RN, the unit chaplain and the dietary department.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Respect his wish, inform the team, and request a safe insulin and glucose monitoring plan): Respecting his religious wish while the team adjusts insulin and monitors glucose balances autonomy and safety. Refusing the request disrespects his beliefs. Usual insulin doses during a fast risk hypoglycemia. Holding all insulin risks severe hyperglycemia and ketoacidosis, and a practical nurse cannot change doses without orders."}, {"id": 1210, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a practical nurse realizes she gave a 70-year-old woman her antibiotic two hours late because the order was missed on the schedule. The woman is not harmed. The manager is off site until tomorrow. Which action best follows disclosure principles?", "options": {"A": "Tell the client what happened, report to the RN, and complete an incident report", "B": "Tell the client at her next dose, since delays under three hours are minor.", "C": "Complete an incident report but skip telling the client, as no harm occurred.", "D": "Wait for the manager to return tomorrow and decide whether the client should be told"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating a short delay as too minor to disclose now", "C": "Reporting internally but withholding disclosure because no harm resulted", "D": "Delaying disclosure until a manager decides"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tell the client what happened, report to the RN, and complete an incident report): Clients have a right to know about errors that affect their care, so honest disclosure, notifying the RN and completing the incident report follow accountability principles. Waiting for the manager delays what is the nurse's own duty. A delay does not become minor just because the dose was a few hours late. An incident report does not replace telling the client, even when no harm resulted."}, {"id": 1211, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a community clinic, a 32-year-old parent mentions that her 5-year-old is often left alone overnight and goes without food for a day at a time. The parent asks the practical nurse to keep this private and says there is no proof. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Gather evidence of neglect through a home visit before deciding whether to make a report", "B": "Report the concern to child protection as the law requires, and explain this to the parent", "C": "Keep the information private and offer parenting resources, since there is no proof of neglect", "D": "Ask the parent for consent to report and only report if she agrees to it"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for proof when reasonable suspicion is enough", "C": "Keeping confidentiality despite a legal duty to report", "D": "Making a mandatory report depend on parent consent"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the concern to child protection as the law requires, and explain this to the parent): Reasonable concern that a child is at risk triggers a legal duty to report to child protection, and the nurse can explain this openly. Proof is not required, because investigation is the agency's role. Keeping it private ignores the duty. The duty does not depend on the parent's consent."}, {"id": 1212, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Three weeks after her husband died, a 46-year-old woman attends a clinic for a routine blood pressure check. She is tearful briefly, sleeps poorly and answers questions quietly, but says she is fine. What should the practical nurse do about her mood?", "options": {"A": "Wait for her to bring up sadness herself, since she told the nurse she is fine", "B": "Document her blood pressure and defer mood questions to her family doctor, who knows her history", "C": "Reassure her that grief is normal and recommend she return if symptoms clearly worsen later", "D": "Ask directly about her mood, sleep, appetite and safety with a screening tool, then report"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for the client to raise mood concerns", "B": "Deferring assessment to another provider", "C": "Reassuring without assessing and delaying follow-up"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask directly about her mood, sleep, appetite and safety with a screening tool, then report): Tearfulness, poor sleep and quiet answers after a loss call for a direct, structured mood and safety screen, since clients often understate distress. Waiting for her to raise it misses the chance. Reassurance alone skips assessment. Deferring to another provider leaves possible depression or risk unexamined."}, {"id": 1213, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An adult 66-year-old in acute care has a physician's order for oxygen at 4 L per minute. He is alert and has no nasal obstruction. The unit has nasal prongs, a simple face mask and a non-rebreather mask. Which device is appropriate for delivering this flow rate?", "options": {"A": "A simple face mask, which works well at 4 to 8 L per minute.", "B": "A non-rebreather mask with a deflated bag at 4 L per minute.", "C": "Nasal prongs, which deliver flows from 1 to 6 L per minute", "D": "Any of the three devices, since the flowmeter can be set at 4 L per minute"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using the wrong minimum flow range for a simple mask", "B": "Running a non-rebreather below its required flow", "D": "Believing the device does not matter for flow"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Nasal prongs, which deliver flows from 1 to 6 L per minute): Nasal prongs suit flows of 1 to 6 L per minute, so 4 L fits. A simple mask needs at least 5 L per minute to flush out exhaled carbon dioxide, so 4 L would cause rebreathing. A non-rebreather needs 10 to 15 L to keep its reservoir inflated, so a low flow defeats its purpose. Each device has a flow range, so the choice matters."}, {"id": 1214, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a community site, a 35-year-old man who injects drugs asks for sterile needles and says he is not ready to stop. He has a swollen red area on his forearm and has shared equipment before. How should the nurse interpret and respond to his request?", "options": {"A": "Give needles only after he completes a counselling session about quitting", "B": "Treat the forearm swelling but decline supplies, since they encourage ongoing drug use", "C": "Provide sterile supplies, assess the swollen area, and offer treatment and support whenever he is ready", "D": "Explain that supplies and care are available once he commits to stopping drug use"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adding a barrier before harm reduction supplies", "B": "Withholding supplies on a mistaken belief they encourage use", "D": "Making care conditional on abstinence"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Provide sterile supplies, assess the swollen area, and offer treatment and support whenever he is ready): Harm reduction meets people where they are, so providing sterile supplies, assessing the possible infection and offering ongoing support protects him now. Requiring abstinence first denies care to people who are not ready. A counselling requirement creates a barrier. Evidence shows supply programs do not increase drug use and reduce infection."}, {"id": 1215, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Beside the bed on a medical unit, a practical nurse finds an 80-year-old resident on the floor. The resident denies injury and has no visible bruising. The nurse completes an assessment, notifies the RN and begins the incident report. Which content belongs in the report?", "options": {"A": "Objective facts: what she saw, the client's words, the assessment findings, and who was notified", "B": "A summary of corrective actions the unit should take to prevent another fall.", "C": "The likely contributing factors, such as footwear or lighting, as she judged them.", "D": "A note in the client's chart stating that an incident report was completed"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Adding recommendations that do not belong in the report", "C": "Recording her impressions of cause rather than facts", "D": "Referring to the incident report in the clinical record"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Objective facts: what she saw, the client's words, the assessment findings, and who was notified): An incident report records objective facts: what was seen, what the client said, the findings and who was told. Recommendations and blame do not belong because the report is for system learning. Mentioning the report in the chart is not recommended. Speculation about contributing factors is not a fact and can mislead later review."}, {"id": 1216, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the medical unit, a 72-year-old man with COPD has a pH of 7.31, a PaCO2 of 62 mmHg and normal bicarbonate. He is drowsy and receiving oxygen at 6 L per minute through a mask. The nurse recognizes which acid-base disturbance?", "options": {"A": "Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis from poor ventilation plus lactate.", "B": "Compensated respiratory acidosis, with the kidneys normalizing the pH.", "C": "Metabolic acidosis, caused by buildup of acids such as lactate from poor perfusion", "D": "Respiratory acidosis, caused by carbon dioxide retention from poor ventilation"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming a metabolic component despite normal bicarbonate", "B": "Calling a clearly low pH compensated with normal bicarbonate", "C": "Confusing a high carbon dioxide level with an acid load"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C151", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse covers a medical unit and the adjoining emergency area during a busy shift. She cares for several clients with acid-base and endocrine problems, uses their blood gas results and histories to guide her assessments, and reports each change in condition to the RN or physician right away. Every chart is complete and orders are current. Equipment is working.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Respiratory acidosis, caused by carbon dioxide retention from poor ventilation): A low pH with a high PaCO2 and normal bicarbonate indicates uncompensated respiratory acidosis from retained carbon dioxide, and his drowsiness fits. A mixed disturbance would need a low bicarbonate. Compensation would show a raised bicarbonate and a pH moving toward normal. Metabolic acidosis and respiratory alkalosis do not fit these values."}, {"id": 1217, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After two days of repeated vomiting from gastroenteritis, a 35-year-old man has a pH of 7.52, bicarbonate 34 mmol/L and PaCO2 of 46 mmHg. He feels light-headed, his tongue is dry, and he denies diarrhea. Which cause best explains these gas results?", "options": {"A": "Loss of bicarbonate-rich intestinal fluid through diarrhea, which lowers the blood pH and bicarbonate", "B": "Overbreathing from nausea and anxiety, which lowers carbon dioxide in the blood", "C": "Buildup of ketone acids from poor intake after two days of vomiting", "D": "Loss of stomach acid through vomiting, which leaves excess bicarbonate in the blood"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confusing vomiting with diarrhea fluid loss", "B": "Naming a respiratory cause for a metabolic picture", "C": "Choosing an acidosis cause for an alkalosis result"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C151", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse covers a medical unit and the adjoining emergency area during a busy shift. She cares for several clients with acid-base and endocrine problems, uses their blood gas results and histories to guide her assessments, and reports each change in condition to the RN or physician right away. Every chart is complete and orders are current. Equipment is working.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Loss of stomach acid through vomiting, which leaves excess bicarbonate in the blood): Vomiting removes stomach acid, so bicarbonate rises, pH climbs and the result is metabolic alkalosis. Loss of intestinal fluid, as in diarrhea, would lower bicarbonate. Overbreathing would lower carbon dioxide, but his is slightly raised from compensation. Ketones would cause acidosis, not a high pH."}, {"id": 1218, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 22-year-old woman in the emergency department is breathing 32 times a minute, reports tingling around her mouth and has a pH of 7.51, PaCO2 of 28 mmHg and SpO2 of 99 percent. She is frightened after an argument. What is the best interpretation and response?", "options": {"A": "Metabolic acidosis, so prepare to give intravenous bicarbonate as soon as possible", "B": "Hypoxia from low oxygen, so apply high flow oxygen by mask right away", "C": "Respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation, so coach slow breathing, calm her and report to the RN", "D": "Respiratory acidosis from retained carbon dioxide, so encourage coughing and deep breathing"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Misreading a high pH as acidosis", "B": "Treating hyperventilation with oxygen despite a normal saturation", "D": "Encouraging deep breathing in a client already overbreathing"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C151", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse covers a medical unit and the adjoining emergency area during a busy shift. She cares for several clients with acid-base and endocrine problems, uses their blood gas results and histories to guide her assessments, and reports each change in condition to the RN or physician right away. Every chart is complete and orders are current. Equipment is working.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation, so coach slow breathing, calm her and report to the RN): A high pH with a low carbon dioxide and a normal saturation points to respiratory alkalosis from anxiety-driven hyperventilation, so calming and coached breathing treat the cause. Oxygen does not help when the saturation is already 99 percent. Bicarbonate would worsen alkalosis. Deep breathing would blow off even more carbon dioxide."}, {"id": 1219, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 14-year-old newly diagnosed with type 1 diabetes tells the nurse she feels well after a week of insulin and wants to switch to pills like her uncle takes. Her glucose is 6.8 mmol/L before breakfast. Which response is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Feeling well shows her pancreas is recovering, so pills may replace insulin after review", "B": "Her pancreas makes little or no insulin, so she needs insulin, and pills cannot replace it", "C": "Her uncle's pills suit her too, because type 1 and type 2 have the same cause", "D": "Pills work well in young people, so she could switch once her glucose stays normal"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing feeling well means insulin is no longer needed", "C": "Equating type 1 with type 2 diabetes", "D": "Assuming normal glucose allows a switch to oral agents"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C151", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse covers a medical unit and the adjoining emergency area during a busy shift. She cares for several clients with acid-base and endocrine problems, uses their blood gas results and histories to guide her assessments, and reports each change in condition to the RN or physician right away. Every chart is complete and orders are current. Equipment is working.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Her pancreas makes little or no insulin, so she needs insulin, and pills cannot replace it): Type 1 diabetes results from loss of insulin production, so insulin is lifelong and her normal glucose reflects the treatment working. Feeling well is not recovery. Oral agents cannot replace insulin when the body makes almost none. Type 2 and type 1 have different causes, so her uncle's pills do not apply."}, {"id": 1220, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "For an 84-year-old resident's stage 3 sacral pressure injury, the practical nurse prepares a dressing change. Clean gloves are within reach, but the order specifies sterile saline and a sterile dressing. The unit is busy. Which action best prevents infection?", "options": {"A": "Perform hand hygiene, set up a sterile field, and use sterile supplies as ordered", "B": "Use sterile supplies, but reuse yesterday's opened saline if it was capped tightly.", "C": "Use clean gloves and clean tap water to save time, since the wound is chronic", "D": "Clean hands before removing the old dressing and use clean gloves for the new one."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reusing an opened solution that may be contaminated", "C": "Using nonsterile technique for convenience against the order", "D": "Skipping hand hygiene and glove change after removing the soiled dressing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Perform hand hygiene, set up a sterile field, and use sterile supplies as ordered): A stage 3 wound is open to deep tissue, so following the order with hand hygiene and a sterile field protects her from infection. Clean technique saves time but breaks the order. Hand hygiene and fresh gloves are needed after handling soiled dressings. An opened saline bottle can be contaminated even when capped and should not be reused."}, {"id": 1221, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A 75-year-old woman on a medical unit has become newly confused over the last two hours. Her vital signs are within her usual range and her glucose is normal. The practical nurse is unsure of the cause. Which action best reflects her accountability?", "options": {"A": "Record the confusion and recheck her orientation at the next scheduled vital signs.", "B": "Perform a focused assessment, then consult the RN now about the change in her condition", "C": "Contact the family doctor directly to request new orders for her confusion", "D": "Ask a nurse colleague on the unit for advice before involving the RN."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Monitoring only when new confusion needs prompt escalation", "C": "Bypassing the RN to seek orders", "D": "Consulting a peer instead of the responsible RN"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Perform a focused assessment, then consult the RN now about the change in her condition): New confusion is a change in condition with many possible causes, so a focused assessment and prompt RN consultation match the nurse's accountability. Recording and rechecking later delays recognition of a serious problem. Asking a colleague does not replace reporting to the RN. Going directly to a physician bypasses the care team and role structure."}, {"id": 1222, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a clinic note and handover, a colleague refers to a 41-year-old client as an alcoholic who is noncompliant. The client has alcohol use disorder and missed one appointment because of a shift change. Which revision best reflects person-first language?", "options": {"A": "A noncompliant client with a drinking problem who did not attend his visit", "B": "A client with alcohol use disorder who missed an appointment because of a work schedule change", "C": "A person who abuses alcohol and was not committed to attending his appointment", "D": "An alcoholic client who has difficulty following the plan and missed an appointment"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Keeping the label noncompliant, which blames the client", "C": "Using stigmatizing terms such as abuses and not committed", "D": "Keeping the label alcoholic as an identity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A client with alcohol use disorder who missed an appointment because of a work schedule change): Person-first language names the person before the condition and describes behaviour with the real reason, as in the correct wording. Alcoholic labels the whole person by the disorder. Noncompliant suggests blame and ignores the shift change. Abuses and not committed carry moral judgment that stigma research links to poorer care."}, {"id": 1223, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Amoxicillin 200 mg by mouth is ordered for a toddler. The home bottle is labelled 400 mg per 5 mL. The child's mother has a medicine cup and an oral syringe, and the nurse prepares the dose for her. What volume should be given?", "options": {"A": "10 mL, found by dividing 400 by 200 and multiplying by 5", "B": "0.5 mL, found by dividing 200 by 400 and reading the answer as millilitres", "C": "5 mL, the full strength volume shown on the bottle label", "D": "2.5 mL, calculated from 200 divided by 400 multiplied by 5"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Inverting the fraction and doubling the volume", "B": "Omitting the 5 mL multiplier", "C": "Giving the label volume instead of calculating the dose"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (2.5 mL, calculated from 200 divided by 400 multiplied by 5): The dose needed is half of the 400 mg in 5 mL, so 200 divided by 400 times 5 gives 2.5 mL. Giving the full 5 mL would double the dose. Inverting the fraction gives 10 mL, four times too much volume for the dose. Stopping at 0.5 forgets that the concentration is per 5 mL."}, {"id": 1224, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An 82-year-old resident in long-term care uses continuous oxygen by nasal prongs. She is alert, eats well and wears the prongs all day. The nurse notices redness behind both ears and dry nostrils. The resident says the tubing pulls and stings. Which action best addresses this complication?", "options": {"A": "Switch the tubing to the other side of the face and leave the redness uncovered", "B": "Stop the oxygen for several hours to let the skin recover, then restart it", "C": "Cushion the tubing over the ears, apply water-based gel to the nostrils, and recheck the skin", "D": "Apply petroleum jelly inside the nostrils and tighten the tubing to stop it from slipping"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Moves the pressure to new skin without padding", "B": "Interrupts prescribed oxygen without an order", "D": "Uses petroleum jelly with oxygen, a fire risk, and tightens tubing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cushion the tubing over the ears, apply water-based gel to the nostrils, and recheck the skin): Padding relieves pressure behind the ears and a water-based gel moistens dry nostrils without fire risk, and rechecking the skin tracks healing. Petroleum products are flammable near oxygen, and tighter tubing adds pressure. Stopping oxygen is unsafe without an order. Moving the tubing only shifts the pressure."}, {"id": 1225, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 66-year-old man admitted with a COPD exacerbation has an arterial blood gas showing pH 7.31, PaCO2 8.3 kPa and HCO3 27 mmol/L. He is drowsy but arousable on oxygen by nasal prongs. Which interpretation should the practical nurse report to the RN?", "options": {"A": "Respiratory acidosis from carbon dioxide retention, only partly compensated by the kidneys", "B": "Chronic respiratory acidosis, with kidneys fully compensated and pH close to normal.", "C": "Metabolic acidosis from bicarbonate loss, with the lungs compensating by blowing off carbon dioxide", "D": "Respiratory acidosis caused by oxygen therapy overcorrecting, so oxygen should be stopped now."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Calling a pH of 7.31 compensated", "C": "Reads the low pH without checking which value moves the same way", "D": "Blaming oxygen and stopping it without an order"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Respiratory acidosis from carbon dioxide retention, only partly compensated by the kidneys): The low pH with a high PaCO2 of 8.3 kPa and a mildly raised bicarbonate points to a respiratory cause with only partial renal compensation, as expected in a COPD exacerbation. Full compensation would show a clearly raised bicarbonate and a pH near normal. Stopping oxygen without an order is unsafe, and the cause is his COPD exacerbation. A metabolic acidosis would show a low bicarbonate, and a respiratory alkalosis would raise the pH. Read the pH with the PaCO2 and HCO3 together."}, {"id": 1226, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor, 70, on a medical unit has cold, pale fingers and a pulse oximeter reading of 84 percent that keeps fluctuating. He is calm, breathing at 16 per minute, and his lips are pink. Before reporting a low oxygen level, what should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Warm the hand or try an earlobe probe, then recheck the reading with his pulse", "B": "Start oxygen at a high flow rate immediately because the displayed saturation is below ninety percent", "C": "Document the reading as accurate and recheck it at the next scheduled vital sign set", "D": "Place the probe on the same cold finger again and wait for the number to stabilize"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Trusts a number without checking perfusion", "C": "Delays follow-up on a reading that may be real", "D": "Repeats the same poor-perfusion site and expects a different result"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Warm the hand or try an earlobe probe, then recheck the reading with his pulse): Cold fingers and a fluctuating signal suggest poor peripheral perfusion is distorting the oximeter, and his calm breathing and pink lips do not match a true saturation of 84 percent. Starting high-flow oxygen on an unverified number treats the device rather than the client. Waiting for the next vital sign set leaves a possible real problem unchecked. Verify the reading by improving perfusion and comparing it with the client's clinical signs."}, {"id": 1227, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Ferreira, 84, in long-term care has eaten only a few bites of breakfast and is due for her morning glyburide. She is a little shaky and sweaty, and her glucose has not been checked yet. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Check her capillary glucose, hold the dose, and report to the RN or prescriber", "B": "Give the glyburide as ordered, since the dose is timed to the clock", "C": "Wait until lunch, then give the glyburide with the meal and recheck her later", "D": "Offer a sweet drink, then give the glyburide with it so both are absorbed together"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Follows the order without regard to poor intake", "C": "Delays action while a symptomatic client's glucose is unknown", "D": "Treats symptoms but still gives a drug that lowers glucose"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check her capillary glucose, hold the dose, and report to the RN or prescriber): Shakiness and sweating with poor intake in an older adult on a sulfonylurea suggest possible hypoglycemia, so the glucose must be checked and the dose held until the prescriber is informed. Giving the drug as ordered adds a glucose-lowering agent to a client who has not eaten. Offering sugar and then giving the dose treats the symptom while still risking a further drop. Sulfonylureas are linked to hypoglycemia in older adults with variable intake."}, {"id": 1228, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the emergency department, a 60-year-old woman has a blood pressure of 78/44 mmHg, cool mottled skin and a heart rate of 128. Her lactate returns at 5.2 mmol/L. How should the practical nurse interpret this result when reporting to the physician?", "options": {"A": "Heart muscle damage is releasing enzymes, so cardiac injury is likely.", "B": "Her kidneys are failing to filter waste, which is the usual reason lactate rises", "C": "Tissues lack oxygen, so anaerobic metabolism is producing lactic acid", "D": "Shivering is raising it, so it will settle with warming."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confusing lactate with cardiac enzymes", "B": "Treats lactate as a kidney function test", "D": "Attributing lactate to shivering and ignoring the shock picture"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tissues lack oxygen, so anaerobic metabolism is producing lactic acid): A raised lactate together with hypotension, tachycardia and mottled skin points to poor tissue perfusion, where cells switch to anaerobic metabolism and release lactic acid. Cardiac injury is judged with troponin, not lactate. Shivering does not explain this value, and warming would not correct hypoperfusion. Kidney function is judged by creatinine and urea."}, {"id": 1229, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A practical nurse with five years of adult medical experience is told at the start of her shift to float to the pediatric unit because of a staff shortage. She has never cared for children. Which response best reflects her professional accountability?", "options": {"A": "Accept the full assignment quietly, since the employer decides where nurses are placed", "B": "Decline the assignment outright and return to the medical unit without speaking to the charge nurse", "C": "Accept it and rely on the pediatric staff to correct any errors she makes", "D": "Discuss her limits, ask for orientation and a resource nurse, and work within her competence"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignores personal competence limits", "B": "Refuses without communicating or negotiating", "C": "Shifts responsibility for her practice onto coworkers"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discuss her limits, ask for orientation and a resource nurse, and work within her competence): A practical nurse is accountable for practising within her own competence, so she should state her limits and ask for orientation and support rather than refuse or stay silent. Walking away without discussion abandons the team and leaves the unit short. Accepting everything quietly risks unsafe care. Relying on others to catch errors does not transfer her own accountability."}, {"id": 1230, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Through an infusion pump, a practical nurse must give an antibiotic in 100 mL of normal saline over 30 minutes. The order and the label agree. She must program the pump in millilitres per hour. What rate should she enter?", "options": {"A": "200 mL per hour, found by dividing 100 mL by 0.5 hour", "B": "3.3 mL per hour, found by dividing 100 mL by 30 minutes", "C": "0.3 mL per hour, found by dividing 30 minutes by 100 mL", "D": "50 mL per hour, found by dividing 100 mL by 2 hours"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Calculates millilitres per minute and labels it per hour", "C": "Divides time by volume instead of volume by time", "D": "Uses a time that does not match thirty minutes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (200 mL per hour, found by dividing 100 mL by 0.5 hour): Thirty minutes is half an hour, so 100 mL divided by 0.5 hour gives 200 mL per hour. Dividing time by volume inverts the formula and produces a meaningless tiny number. Dividing 100 mL by 30 minutes yields about 3.3 mL per minute, which must be multiplied by 60 to convert to hours. Always check that the units on the answer match the units the pump requires."}, {"id": 1231, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A 24-year-old man arrives in the emergency department with a gunshot wound to the thigh. He is alert, asks the practical nurse not to tell anyone how he was hurt, and says he will leave if police are called. How should the nurse proceed?", "options": {"A": "Call the police herself before telling the RN, since reporting is each nurse's individual task", "B": "Ask him to sign a release first, and report only if he gives written consent", "C": "Honour his request and record only the wound care, because confidentiality covers how he was injured", "D": "Continue care and tell the RN, since a legal duty to report gunshot wounds applies"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Bypasses the facility process for mandatory reporting", "B": "Makes a legal duty depend on client consent", "C": "Treats confidentiality as covering legally reportable injuries"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue care and tell the RN, since a legal duty to report gunshot wounds applies): Most Canadian jurisdictions place a legal duty on health facilities to report gunshot wounds, and that duty does not depend on the client's consent. The nurse continues to give care and informs the RN so the facility can follow its reporting process. Confidentiality has legal limits, and this is one of them. Calling police alone skips the team, and waiting for consent would make the duty optional."}, {"id": 1232, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Two practical nurses stand in a crowded hospital elevator after rounds. One begins describing a client's diagnosis and room number, and a visitor is standing beside them. The other nurse recognizes a privacy concern and feels uneasy about speaking up. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Say nothing, because staff conversations inside the hospital building fall within the circle of care", "B": "Suggest they use the client's initials instead of the name while she listens.", "C": "Wait until the visitor steps off, then remind them about privacy afterward.", "D": "Quietly suggest they stop and continue the discussion later in a private area"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes the circle of care covers any location in the building", "B": "Believing initials make details unidentifiable", "C": "Delaying the correction until after the disclosure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Quietly suggest they stop and continue the discussion later in a private area): Discussing a client where others can hear risks a privacy breach, even without a name, because a diagnosis and room number can identify the person. The circle of care applies to those directly involved in care, not to anyone who happens to be in the same building. Using initials still exposes information. Reminding them afterward is too late. Moving the conversation to a private space respects confidentiality."}, {"id": 1233, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Rotating on a medical unit, a 22-year-old nursing student gives a scheduled medication to the wrong client. The practical nurse who assigned the task was in another room. The client shows no apparent effects and the error was found at the next check. Who holds accountability for this event?", "options": {"A": "Both share accountability, and the event must be reported and documented", "B": "The student's school and the supervising nurse, but not the student herself.", "C": "The student alone, because she personally gave the medication and signed the record", "D": "The unit manager alone, since she assigned the student to the unit."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Excluding the student from accountability for her own action", "C": "Places all blame on the person who performed the task", "D": "Assigning all accountability to the manager"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Both share accountability, and the event must be reported and documented): The student is accountable for her own actions, and the supervising nurse is accountable for the decision to assign and for the level of supervision provided. Both must report the event and document it as the facility requires, even when the client is unharmed. Excluding the student ignores her own role, and placing everything on the manager ignores how supervision works. Reporting also allows the team to review the cause of the error."}, {"id": 1234, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Newly diagnosed with type 1 diabetes, a 9-year-old boy will learn to check his own glucose. His mother is present and eager to answer every question. The practical nurse is planning the teaching session. Which approach fits his developmental stage best?", "options": {"A": "Show a short video alone and test him later, since school-age children learn best independently", "B": "Use detailed physiology with technical terms, as he can reason about abstract concepts", "C": "Speak directly to him with simple explanations and let him handle the meter with supervision", "D": "Direct all explanations to his mother, who will perform every glucose check for him"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaves a child to learn a new skill without hands-on guidance", "B": "Pitches teaching above his concrete thinking stage", "D": "Teaches only the parent and excludes the child"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Speak directly to him with simple explanations and let him handle the meter with supervision): School-age children think concretely and are eager to master skills, so teaching should speak to the child, use simple language and let him practise with supervision while the parent supports. Excluding him builds dependence rather than skill. Abstract physiology is beyond his stage. Learning a procedure alone from a video lacks the guided practice he needs."}, {"id": 1235, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a clinic, a 16-year-old girl says she has felt hopeless since her best friend moved away and cannot sleep. She shrugs when asked how things are going. The practical nurse is worried and wonders how to raise the topic. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Ask general questions about mood and avoid naming suicide, so as not to plant the idea", "B": "Reassure her that teens feel this way often and suggest talking to friends", "C": "Ask her directly whether she has thought about suicide, then report the answer to the RN", "D": "Wait for her to bring up suicide on her own before raising the subject"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Avoids the question out of fear of suggesting it", "B": "Minimizes her distress with reassurance", "D": "Leaves the topic unexplored until the client volunteers it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask her directly whether she has thought about suicide, then report the answer to the RN): Asking directly about suicide does not plant the idea and is the recommended way to assess risk when hopelessness and sleep loss are present. Vague questions let a hesitant teen avoid the subject. Reassurance dismisses her feelings. Waiting delays assessment, so she should ask plainly and report the answer promptly."}, {"id": 1236, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a teaching session, a practical nurse mentor shows a new graduate how to calculate a dose when the order is in milligrams and the stock is in micrograms per millilitre. The graduate wants a method that catches setup errors. Which method should the mentor recommend?", "options": {"A": "The ratio method alone, then confirming the answer by estimating whether it seems reasonable", "B": "Using a calculator on the numbers in the order, without writing units", "C": "Dimensional analysis, setting up fractions so unwanted units cancel", "D": "Memorizing the usual dose for the drug and adjusting by eye for the client"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Skips the built-in unit check", "B": "Drops units, which hides setup errors", "D": "Relies on memory instead of calculation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Dimensional analysis, setting up fractions so unwanted units cancel): Dimensional analysis carries the units through each step, so a wrong setup leaves the wrong units and signals the error immediately. The ratio method can give a number without revealing a unit mismatch. Memory and visual adjustment are unsafe for dosing. Dropping units removes the main safeguard against conversion mistakes."}, {"id": 1237, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a diabetes clinic, a practical nurse teaches a man who has had type 2 diabetes for twelve years to inspect his feet daily and wear protective footwear. He has reduced foot sensation but no ulcers yet. Which level of prevention does this teaching represent?", "options": {"A": "Health promotion only, because teaching is not aimed at a specific disease", "B": "Tertiary prevention, because it aims to prevent complications and disability in established disease", "C": "Primary prevention, because the foot has not yet developed an ulcer or infection", "D": "Secondary prevention, because it screens for undiagnosed diabetes in people without symptoms"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignores that the teaching targets a known disease", "C": "Calls it primary because no ulcer exists yet", "D": "Mixes up screening for new disease with managing known disease"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tertiary prevention, because it aims to prevent complications and disability in established disease): The disease is already established, and the teaching aims to prevent complications such as ulcers and amputation, which is tertiary prevention. Primary prevention applies before disease develops. Secondary prevention detects disease early, and his diabetes is already diagnosed. The teaching is clearly aimed at diabetic foot complications, not general health promotion."}, {"id": 1238, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Reviewing wheelchair equipment, the nurse hears a coworker offer a ring-shaped foam donut cushion from the supply room, saying it will take pressure off the sacrum. Mr. Sharma weighs 49 kilograms and sits up about six hours daily. Which cushion choice is best?", "options": {"A": "A pressure-redistributing cushion that spreads weight widely, chosen after team assessment", "B": "A folded blanket on the seat, which he can use for as long as he likes", "C": "Stacked pillows under the buttocks, because thicker padding lowers pressure in proportion to height", "D": "The donut cushion, since the hole keeps the reddened area from touching the seat"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats improvised padding as adequate support", "C": "Assumes more thickness always means less pressure", "D": "Believes ring cushions relieve pressure at the centre"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C147", "cluster_text": "Mr. Arvind Sharma, 79, lives in long-term care and uses a wheelchair for most of the day after a stroke. He is underweight, has urinary incontinence, and slides down in bed whenever the head is raised. Today the practical nurse notices non-blanchable redness over his sacrum. The RN has asked her to carry out his care plan and report any change.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A pressure-redistributing cushion that spreads weight widely, chosen after team assessment): A cushion that redistributes pressure over a larger surface lowers pressure on the sacrum and other bony areas, and the team should choose it for his size and posture. Donut cushions concentrate pressure on the surrounding tissue and can reduce blood flow. Blankets and stacked pillows do not spread load in a controlled way. Equipment should be matched to the person, not improvised."}, {"id": 1239, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Sharma slides toward the foot of the bed whenever the head is raised for meals. The practical nurse wants to reduce shear over his sacrum. The head of bed is currently at 60 degrees for most of the day. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Massage the reddened sacral skin firmly at each position change to improve circulation", "B": "Keep the head of bed near 30 degrees outside meals and lift him instead of dragging", "C": "Raise the head of bed higher between meals so he stays upright and alert", "D": "Place a pillow behind his back and leave the bed at 60 degrees"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applies friction to compromised skin", "C": "Increases the angle that causes sliding", "D": "Props him without addressing the head of bed angle"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C147", "cluster_text": "Mr. Arvind Sharma, 79, lives in long-term care and uses a wheelchair for most of the day after a stroke. He is underweight, has urinary incontinence, and slides down in bed whenever the head is raised. Today the practical nurse notices non-blanchable redness over his sacrum. The RN has asked her to carry out his care plan and report any change.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep the head of bed near 30 degrees outside meals and lift him instead of dragging): A raised head of bed makes the body slide and drags the skin over the sacrum, so keeping the angle near 30 degrees except during meals reduces shear. Lifting rather than dragging also limits friction. A higher angle or a pillow at 60 degrees leaves the sliding in place. Massaging reddened skin over a bony prominence damages tissue."}, {"id": 1240, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Sharma's incontinence has caused redness and stinging on his buttocks and perineum, separate from the sacral area. He cries out when the area is touched. The practical nurse is choosing a cleansing routine for after each episode. Which approach best protects the skin?", "options": {"A": "Apply thick zinc paste and leave it in place until the next day.", "B": "Gently cleanse with a pH-balanced no-rinse cleanser, pat dry, and apply a barrier", "C": "Cleanse with antibacterial soap and rub the skin dry with a towel.", "D": "Wash with soap and warm water and scrub firmly until all residue is gone"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Layering product over soiled skin without cleansing", "C": "Using harsh soap and friction on irritated skin", "D": "Uses harsh soap and friction on damaged skin"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C147", "cluster_text": "Mr. Arvind Sharma, 79, lives in long-term care and uses a wheelchair for most of the day after a stroke. He is underweight, has urinary incontinence, and slides down in bed whenever the head is raised. Today the practical nurse notices non-blanchable redness over his sacrum. The RN has asked her to carry out his care plan and report any change.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gently cleanse with a pH-balanced no-rinse cleanser, pat dry, and apply a barrier): Incontinence-associated dermatitis needs gentle, pH-balanced cleansing, patting dry and a barrier to protect against moisture. Scrubbing with soap strips protective oils and adds friction. Antibacterial soap and rubbing irritate the skin further. Paste applied over soiled skin traps moisture and bacteria, and a plastic-backed pad traps heat."}, {"id": 1241, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Sharma, underweight with a stage 2 sacral pressure injury, eats about a third of his meals. A family member suggests a low-protein diet to lessen the strain on healing tissue. The practical nurse reports to the RN and dietitian. What nutrition goal best supports healing?", "options": {"A": "Fluids restricted to limit urine output and so reduce skin moisture exposure", "B": "Adequate protein and calories, with supplements planned by the dietitian", "C": "High-sugar snacks between meals to raise calories, with no change to protein intake", "D": "Reduced protein to limit waste products, with calories kept near his current intake"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Restricts fluids to manage incontinence at the cost of hydration", "C": "Raises calories but ignores protein needs", "D": "Believes less protein lowers the healing workload"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C147", "cluster_text": "Mr. Arvind Sharma, 79, lives in long-term care and uses a wheelchair for most of the day after a stroke. He is underweight, has urinary incontinence, and slides down in bed whenever the head is raised. Today the practical nurse notices non-blanchable redness over his sacrum. The RN has asked her to carry out his care plan and report any change.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Adequate protein and calories, with supplements planned by the dietitian): Wound healing raises the need for protein and energy, and he is already underweight, so the goal is adequate intake with dietitian-planned supplements and monitoring. Restricting protein slows repair. Restricting fluids risks dehydration and does not treat the cause of skin breakdown. Sugary snacks without protein do not meet healing needs."}, {"id": 1242, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a visit, Mr. Leclerc rates his pain 7 out of 10 and says he lies awake fearing he will leave his wife with debts. His tumour has not changed on recent assessments. How should the practical nurse understand his pain?", "options": {"A": "As total pain with physical, emotional, social and spiritual parts that the team should all address", "B": "As cancer progression pain that needs a higher dose, since his tumour may be growing.", "C": "As physical pain only, because worry about debts is a separate matter for the social worker", "D": "As an expected psychological response that the family doctor should address after the visit."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Attributing all pain to the tumour despite stable assessments", "C": "Treats pain as only a physical symptom", "D": "Treating distress as separate from pain and deferring it"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C153", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gordon Leclerc, 72, has lung cancer and COPD and receives hospice care at home. He takes long-acting morphine twice daily and short-acting morphine for breakthrough pain. His wife is his main caregiver and says he has been restless, worried and uncomfortable despite his medication. A practical nurse visits under the direction of the palliative care RN. He has been in the program for two weeks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (As total pain with physical, emotional, social and spiritual parts that the team should all address): Total pain recognizes that physical, emotional, social and spiritual distress all shape how pain is felt, so the nurse reports all of these concerns to the team. Assuming tumour growth ignores the stable assessments. Treating only the physical part leaves the fears and financial worry unaddressed. A practical nurse does not change opioid doses, and deferring his distress delays help he needs."}, {"id": 1243, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Leclerc used his short-acting morphine five times yesterday for pain. His wife is afraid he will become addicted and wants to give fewer doses. The practical nurse has the order and the palliative RN's plan. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Explain that relief is the goal, and report the dose frequency to the RN", "B": "Suggest switching to an over-the-counter analgesic, since he has used many doses of morphine", "C": "Agree to give fewer doses and watch whether his pain settles over the next day", "D": "Add an extra dose of the long-acting tablet herself to reduce the number of breakthrough doses"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Substitutes a weaker drug for cancer pain", "C": "Withholds analgesia out of fear of addiction", "D": "Adjusts a prescribed dose outside scope"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C153", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gordon Leclerc, 72, has lung cancer and COPD and receives hospice care at home. He takes long-acting morphine twice daily and short-acting morphine for breakthrough pain. His wife is his main caregiver and says he has been restless, worried and uncomfortable despite his medication. A practical nurse visits under the direction of the palliative care RN. He has been in the program for two weeks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that relief is the goal, and report the dose frequency to the RN): In palliative cancer care the aim is comfort, and addiction is not a reason to withhold opioids. Frequent breakthrough use signals that the regular dose may need review, so the nurse explains this and reports the pattern to the RN. Giving fewer doses leaves him in pain. Changing the long-acting dose or substituting an over-the-counter drug is not within her role."}, {"id": 1244, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Leclerc's morphine was increased this week. His wife reports no bowel movement for three days and he feels bloated, with no vomiting. The practical nurse recalls that his bowel plan was never started and no laxative is on the list. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Suggest holding the morphine until a bowel movement happens", "B": "Wait a few more days because tolerance to opioid constipation develops quickly with ongoing doses", "C": "Report the constipation to the RN and ask about a scheduled laxative with the opioid", "D": "Encourage extra fluids and bran cereal alone, since diet usually corrects constipation from opioids"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withholds pain control to fix a side effect", "B": "Expects tolerance to constipation to develop", "D": "Relies on diet alone for opioid-induced constipation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C153", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gordon Leclerc, 72, has lung cancer and COPD and receives hospice care at home. He takes long-acting morphine twice daily and short-acting morphine for breakthrough pain. His wife is his main caregiver and says he has been restless, worried and uncomfortable despite his medication. A practical nurse visits under the direction of the palliative care RN. He has been in the program for two weeks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the constipation to the RN and ask about a scheduled laxative with the opioid): Opioids slow the bowel and tolerance to constipation does not develop, so a scheduled laxative is planned from the start of therapy. Reporting the problem lets the RN arrange it. Waiting allows impaction to progress. Diet changes alone are rarely enough, and holding morphine leaves him in pain."}, {"id": 1245, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Leclerc becomes short of breath when walking to the bathroom, yet his oxygen saturation reads 94 percent at rest. He looks frightened. His wife asks whether oxygen is the only thing that will help. What should the practical nurse recommend within the palliative plan?", "options": {"A": "Apply oxygen at a high flow rate, because breathlessness means his saturation must be falling", "B": "Tell his wife that breathlessness near the end of life cannot be eased", "C": "Use a fan, upright positioning and calm coaching, and report the distress to the RN", "D": "Encourage him to rest flat in bed with the door closed until the feeling passes"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Links breathlessness only to low saturation", "B": "Assumes nothing can be done for the symptom", "D": "Chooses a position that worsens breathing"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C153", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gordon Leclerc, 72, has lung cancer and COPD and receives hospice care at home. He takes long-acting morphine twice daily and short-acting morphine for breakthrough pain. His wife is his main caregiver and says he has been restless, worried and uncomfortable despite his medication. A practical nurse visits under the direction of the palliative care RN. He has been in the program for two weeks.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use a fan, upright positioning and calm coaching, and report the distress to the RN): Dyspnea is a subjective experience, and a normal saturation does not rule it out. A fan on the face, upright positioning and calm coaching can reduce the sensation, and the RN can consider an opioid. High-flow oxygen is not supported by a normal reading. Lying flat worsens breathing, and telling his wife nothing can be done is incorrect."}, {"id": 1246, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Four weeks after a heart attack, a 58-year-old man joins an outpatient program offering supervised exercise, medication review and diet counselling. The practical nurse is asked to classify the program for a report. Which level of prevention best describes it?", "options": {"A": "Health protection, because it controls hazards in the environment that affect the population", "B": "Primary prevention, because it teaches healthy lifestyle habits that anyone could follow", "C": "Tertiary prevention, because it restores function and lowers the risk of another event", "D": "Secondary prevention, because it detects heart disease early through screening and testing"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confuses clinical rehabilitation with population hazard control", "B": "Ignores that the disease has already occurred", "D": "Treats rehabilitation as early detection"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tertiary prevention, because it restores function and lowers the risk of another event): After a heart attack the disease is established, and rehabilitation aims to limit disability and prevent recurrence, which is tertiary prevention. Secondary prevention centres on early detection through screening. Primary prevention targets people without disease. Health protection deals with environmental or population-wide hazards, not an individual's recovery."}, {"id": 1247, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a primary care clinic, a 48-year-old man has elevated liver enzymes and says he drinks a normal amount. The practical nurse is asked to screen his alcohol use before the physician visit later today. Which approach best supports accurate screening?", "options": {"A": "Use a validated tool such as AUDIT-C, asking about frequency and quantity", "B": "Compare his liver enzymes with a standard chart to rate his drinking level.", "C": "Chat informally about his weekly drinks and judge his risk level herself.", "D": "Ask him whether he thinks he drinks too much and record his yes or no answer"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Estimating intake from laboratory values", "C": "Using an unstructured chat instead of a validated tool", "D": "Relies on a single subjective question"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a validated tool such as AUDIT-C, asking about frequency and quantity): A validated tool gives structured, comparable results and asking about quantity and frequency without judgment encourages honest answers. Enzymes are not a reliable measure of intake. An informal chat with a personal judgment of risk is not standardized. Asking whether he drinks too much depends on his own definition, and asking his spouse first bypasses the client and his consent."}, {"id": 1248, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Working on an oncology unit, a practical nurse develops a mild dry cough and sore throat at the start of her shift. She feels well enough to work and the unit is short staffed and her manager has not yet been told. Which action best protects clients?", "options": {"A": "Take an over-the-counter cough suppressant and work, since it controls symptom spread", "B": "Work as scheduled and cover her coughs with her sleeve, since symptoms are mild", "C": "Report her symptoms and follow facility policy, which may mean staying home", "D": "Work the shift wearing a mask and avoid the most vulnerable oncology clients."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats a symptom without reducing transmission", "B": "Underrates mild symptoms in a high-risk setting", "D": "Relies on a mask alone to protect immunocompromised clients"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report her symptoms and follow facility policy, which may mean staying home): Clients with cancer are often immunosuppressed, so even mild respiratory symptoms in staff carry risk. The nurse reports and follows policy, which may require leaving work. A mask or sleeve covering reduces but does not remove transmission. A cough suppressant hides the symptom without stopping spread."}, {"id": 1249, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Diagnosed with asthma, a 35-year-old woman has both a salbutamol inhaler and a corticosteroid inhaler. She reports a sore, white-patched mouth and says she uses the corticosteroid first for quick relief of chest tightness. What teaching should the practical nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Use salbutamol first for relief, the steroid after it, and rinse her mouth afterward", "B": "Keep using the corticosteroid first, since it opens the airways faster than salbutamol", "C": "Skip the mouth rinse, as thrush comes from the salbutamol rather than the corticosteroid", "D": "Use the two inhalers together in one breath so the doses reach the lungs at once"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reverses the reliever and controller roles", "C": "Misattributes thrush to the bronchodilator", "D": "Combines two devices in a way they are not designed for"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use salbutamol first for relief, the steroid after it, and rinse her mouth afterward): Salbutamol is the fast-acting reliever that opens the airways, and the corticosteroid is a controller that works over time, so the bronchodilator goes first and the steroid after it. Rinsing the mouth after the steroid reduces oral thrush, which is the likely cause of her symptoms. Inhalers are not taken together in one breath. The corticosteroid is not a quick-relief drug."}, {"id": 1250, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Nguyen's perineal skin is red and slightly weeping after several episodes of incontinence overnight. The night nurse used bar soap and a rough cloth. The practical nurse reviews the skin care plan with the RN. Which change best protects her skin?", "options": {"A": "Use a gentle no-rinse cleanser, pat dry, and apply a moisture barrier after each episode", "B": "Use a thick absorbent brief and check her only at scheduled rounds", "C": "Apply talcum powder to the weeping area to absorb moisture between cleanings", "D": "Continue soap and a firm cloth, but cleanse less often to avoid drying the skin"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Leaving moisture against skin between scheduled checks", "C": "Applying powder that cakes on weeping skin", "D": "Keeping harsh products while reducing cleansing frequency"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C152", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dorothy Nguyen, 86, lives in long-term care. She had a hip fracture repaired three weeks ago, is now mostly bedbound, and has urinary incontinence. She is overweight, with deep skin folds under her breasts and abdomen. The practical nurse is assigned her skin care and reports to the RN each shift. Her family wants her comfortable and clean. She is alert and oriented.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a gentle no-rinse cleanser, pat dry, and apply a moisture barrier after each episode): Incontinence skin needs prompt, gentle cleansing with a pH-balanced product, careful drying and a barrier to guard against moisture. Soap and rough cloths strip the skin and add friction, and cleaning less often prolongs urine contact. Powder cakes on weeping skin. A thick brief checked only on schedule holds moisture against the skin."}, {"id": 1251, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Under Mrs. Nguyen's breasts the skin is red, macerated and has a musty odour. The practical nurse is planning care for the intertrigo. The aide suggests dusting the folds with cornstarch after washing, as was done for another resident last month. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Apply a thick petroleum ointment into the folds to keep the skin soft and protected", "B": "Leave the folds open to air and avoid washing them until the redness fades", "C": "Wash, then sprinkle cornstarch over damp skin so the powder soaks up the leftover moisture", "D": "Wash gently, dry the folds thoroughly, and keep surfaces apart with wicking fabric"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Seals more moisture into the fold", "B": "Skips cleansing on skin that is already infected", "C": "Uses powder on damp skin, which feeds yeast"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C152", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dorothy Nguyen, 86, lives in long-term care. She had a hip fracture repaired three weeks ago, is now mostly bedbound, and has urinary incontinence. She is overweight, with deep skin folds under her breasts and abdomen. The practical nurse is assigned her skin care and reports to the RN each shift. Her family wants her comfortable and clean. She is alert and oriented.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wash gently, dry the folds thoroughly, and keep surfaces apart with wicking fabric): Skin folds need gentle cleansing, thorough drying and a wicking layer to keep surfaces apart and reduce moisture. Cornstarch on damp skin can encourage yeast growth. Petroleum seals in moisture and heat. Skipping washing leaves sweat and organisms in the fold."}, {"id": 1252, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Nguyen, 86, immobile since her hip fracture, scored 12 on the Braden scale at admission and her skin is currently intact. The practical nurse is deciding when to reassess her risk, since the score was recorded three weeks ago. What is the best timing?", "options": {"A": "Wait for the dietitian to complete the nutrition review before using the scale again", "B": "Reassess only if redness or open skin appears, since the score applies once skin is damaged", "C": "Reassess on a set schedule and with any change in condition, and begin prevention now", "D": "Reassess once on admission only, because the score does not change during a stay"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays reassessment for another discipline", "B": "Treats the scale as a tool after injury", "D": "Assumes risk stays fixed"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C152", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dorothy Nguyen, 86, lives in long-term care. She had a hip fracture repaired three weeks ago, is now mostly bedbound, and has urinary incontinence. She is overweight, with deep skin folds under her breasts and abdomen. The practical nurse is assigned her skin care and reports to the RN each shift. Her family wants her comfortable and clean. She is alert and oriented.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reassess on a set schedule and with any change in condition, and begin prevention now): A Braden score of 12 signals high risk, and prevention should start now with reassessment at regular intervals and whenever her condition changes. The scale is designed to predict risk before skin breaks down. Risk changes with mobility, moisture and nutrition. Waiting for the dietitian delays a nursing assessment that is already due."}, {"id": 1253, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Nguyen is bedbound with early redness over her left trochanter from lying on her side. The aide proposes turning her onto the reddened side because the other sites look fine. The practical nurse reviews the repositioning plan. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Massage the red trochanter firmly with each turn to restore blood flow to the area", "B": "Turn her on a schedule keeping weight off the reddened area, using 30-degree tilts", "C": "Place her on the reddened side to rest the other sites, then recheck in four hours", "D": "Turn her only when she complains of pain, since sleep is important for healing too"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Massages a bony prominence", "C": "Loads tissue that is already injured", "D": "Waits for discomfort before repositioning"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C152", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Dorothy Nguyen, 86, lives in long-term care. She had a hip fracture repaired three weeks ago, is now mostly bedbound, and has urinary incontinence. She is overweight, with deep skin folds under her breasts and abdomen. The practical nurse is assigned her skin care and reports to the RN each shift. Her family wants her comfortable and clean. She is alert and oriented.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Turn her on a schedule keeping weight off the reddened area, using 30-degree tilts): Early redness means the tissue is already under stress, so turns on a schedule should keep pressure off it, and a 30-degree tilt lowers pressure over the trochanter. Placing her on the red area risks deeper injury. Massage damages tissue at bony points. Repositioning should follow a plan, not wait for complaints."}, {"id": 1254, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Living with myasthenia gravis, a 45-year-old man takes pyridostigmine and reports weakness in his swallowing and legs by evening. He takes his tablets at random times, sometimes after eating. The practical nurse is teaching him about his medication schedule today. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Take double doses in the evening, when his weakness is worst, to catch up", "B": "Take it with food at any time, since timing relative to meals does not matter", "C": "Skip evening doses because fatigue is normal and the drug may disturb his sleep", "D": "Take it on a fixed schedule, about 30 to 60 minutes before meals"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Doubles a dose without an order", "B": "Ignores the link between timing and chewing strength", "C": "Omits doses when symptoms are worst"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Take it on a fixed schedule, about 30 to 60 minutes before meals): Pyridostigmine works best on a regular schedule, and taking it before meals gives peak muscle strength for chewing and swallowing. Random timing causes swings in strength. Doubling a dose risks cholinergic toxicity. Skipping evening doses lets weakness worsen when he is already at his weakest."}, {"id": 1255, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At home, a practical nurse teaches an 82-year-old woman to use a new pill organizer. She takes time to answer and asks the nurse to repeat steps. Her daughter says she is losing her memory. How should the nurse proceed?", "options": {"A": "Slow the pace, use large print, teach in short steps and have her demonstrate back", "B": "Use a written handout in small print and rely on the daughter to reinforce.", "C": "Teach the daughter to fill the organizer and keep her mother's medications secured.", "D": "Give a long demonstration, then leave the organizer for her to practise alone."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relying on small print and a family member instead of adapting teaching", "C": "Assuming slow responses mean she cannot learn", "D": "Overloading one session and skipping return demonstration"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Slow the pace, use large print, teach in short steps and have her demonstrate back): Older adults can learn well when teaching is paced to them, with large print, short steps and return demonstration. Slow responses reflect processing speed, not necessarily cognitive decline. Small print and reliance on her daughter do not adapt the teaching. Giving all the instruction to the daughter excludes her. A long session without return demonstration overwhelms and does not confirm learning."}, {"id": 1256, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An 81-year-old man with mild dementia became suddenly confused and agitated two days after hip surgery. His daughter says he was alert yesterday. The practical nurse notes fluctuating attention and a temperature of 38.2 C. What should she conclude and do?", "options": {"A": "Suspect a pain-medication reaction only and ask the physician to stop the drug", "B": "Expect progression of his dementia and document the confusion for the next review", "C": "Treat the agitation as a behaviour problem and reorient him until it settles", "D": "Suspect delirium with a possible physical cause and report the change to the RN"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Settles on one cause without assessment", "B": "Labels sudden confusion as dementia progression", "C": "Treats a medical change as behaviour"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Suspect delirium with a possible physical cause and report the change to the RN): Sudden onset, fluctuating attention and fever point to delirium, which signals an acute physical problem such as infection or medication effect. Dementia progresses slowly, so a rapid change needs prompt reporting. Reorientation alone does not address the cause. Naming one cause without assessment may miss infection or pain."}, {"id": 1257, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the day 12 visit, Priya cries easily, feels overwhelmed and sleeps little even when the baby sleeps. The symptoms began on day 3 and are getting worse instead of easing. She denies thoughts of harming herself or the baby. How should the practical nurse interpret this?", "options": {"A": "Normal fatigue from breastfeeding, so she advises Priya to nap and drink more fluids", "B": "A thyroid disorder that she can confirm by asking about weight changes at home", "C": "Worsening low mood and poor sleep suggest more than baby blues, so she reports it", "D": "Typical baby blues that resolve without help, so she reassures and plans a routine visit"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Attributes mood symptoms only to fatigue", "B": "Makes a medical diagnosis outside scope", "D": "Reassures despite worsening symptoms"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C154", "cluster_text": "Priya Desai, 29, is two weeks postpartum after the vaginal birth of her first baby and is breastfeeding. A practical nurse in a community program visits her home, working with the public health nurse. Priya is tearful and sleeping poorly, and her mother-in-law is staying to help. The newborn is feeding well and gaining weight. Her partner works long shifts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Worsening low mood and poor sleep suggest more than baby blues, so she reports it): Baby blues peak in the first week and ease by about two weeks, so symptoms that are worsening at day 12 suggest possible postpartum depression and need screening by the team. Reassurance alone delays help. Fatigue does not explain persistent low mood. A practical nurse reports findings and does not diagnose thyroid disease."}, {"id": 1258, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two weeks later Priya's husband phones the community line. She has not slept for two nights, says strangers are watching the baby, and hears voices telling her to protect him. He sounds frightened and says he is alone with the baby. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Arrange an appointment for tomorrow, leaving the baby in her care so breastfeeding continues", "B": "Advise him to keep her calm and call back if symptoms worsen.", "C": "Treat it as an emergency, keep the baby safe, and arrange immediate assessment", "D": "Call the public health nurse in the morning to plan a home visit."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaves the infant with a psychotic mother while waiting", "B": "Waiting for worsening when psychosis is already an emergency", "D": "Deferring urgent assessment to the next day"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C154", "cluster_text": "Priya Desai, 29, is two weeks postpartum after the vaginal birth of her first baby and is breastfeeding. A practical nurse in a community program visits her home, working with the public health nurse. Priya is tearful and sleeping poorly, and her mother-in-law is staying to help. The newborn is feeding well and gaining weight. Her partner works long shifts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat it as an emergency, keep the baby safe, and arrange immediate assessment): Delusions and voices about the baby suggest postpartum psychosis, which is a psychiatric emergency with a risk to mother and infant. The infant must be kept safe and urgent assessment arranged through emergency services or the crisis team. Waiting for worsening or planning a visit in the morning leaves the baby at risk. Voices are not normal after birth, and the situation cannot wait for tomorrow."}, {"id": 1259, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At six weeks postpartum, Priya asks if breastfeeding alone will prevent another pregnancy. She feeds the baby every four hours, sometimes with formula. Her periods have not returned. The practical nurse answers within her role. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Tell her that giving formula at times does not change her pregnancy risk", "B": "Advise a combined estrogen pill and start it today without further assessment", "C": "Tell her breastfeeding gives full protection until her periods return, so no method is needed", "D": "Explain that breastfeeding protects only when exclusive and frequent, and offer counselling"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignores that supplementing lowers protection", "B": "Recommends a prescription method without assessment", "C": "Overstates breastfeeding as reliable contraception"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C154", "cluster_text": "Priya Desai, 29, is two weeks postpartum after the vaginal birth of her first baby and is breastfeeding. A practical nurse in a community program visits her home, working with the public health nurse. Priya is tearful and sleeping poorly, and her mother-in-law is staying to help. The newborn is feeding well and gaining weight. Her partner works long shifts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that breastfeeding protects only when exclusive and frequent, and offer counselling): Lactational amenorrhea is reliable only with exclusive, frequent feeding, usually day and night, and Priya feeds every four hours and supplements. She should be offered counselling about suitable methods. Telling her she is fully protected is incorrect. Starting an estrogen pill is a prescribing decision, and estrogen may affect milk supply."}, {"id": 1260, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Priya's prenatal record shows she is not immune to rubella. Her doctor has ordered a live rubella vaccine before she leaves the program. She mentions she hopes to conceive again within the next few months. What should the practical nurse include in teaching?", "options": {"A": "Receive the vaccine only if she becomes pregnant again, since protection against rubella is needed then", "B": "Delay the vaccine until she has fully stopped breastfeeding because the virus passes into breast milk", "C": "Take the vaccine now and begin trying for pregnancy, since the virus is weakened", "D": "Avoid pregnancy for one month after the live vaccine, and tell her she may continue breastfeeding"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives a live vaccine during a later pregnancy", "B": "Believes the vaccine requires stopping breastfeeding", "C": "Ignores the waiting period after a live vaccine"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C154", "cluster_text": "Priya Desai, 29, is two weeks postpartum after the vaginal birth of her first baby and is breastfeeding. A practical nurse in a community program visits her home, working with the public health nurse. Priya is tearful and sleeping poorly, and her mother-in-law is staying to help. The newborn is feeding well and gaining weight. Her partner works long shifts.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoid pregnancy for one month after the live vaccine, and tell her she may continue breastfeeding): A nonimmune woman can receive the rubella vaccine after birth, and breastfeeding is not a contraindication. Because it is a live vaccine, pregnancy should be avoided for one month afterward. Live vaccines are not given during pregnancy. Starting to conceive straight away ignores the waiting period."}, {"id": 1261, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Marcus asks whether eating a very fatty meal might help the gallbladder empty and clear the pain. He is hungry, and the ultrasound is still pending. The practical nurse recognizes the presentation and knows the physician has not yet seen him. What should she tell him?", "options": {"A": "A fatty meal will flush the stones, so it is reasonable once the nausea settles", "B": "He may drink freely and eat dry toast, since bland food does not stimulate the gallbladder", "C": "Eating can worsen the pain, so he should stay NPO until the physician decides", "D": "A small low-fat snack is safe, since only large meals trigger gallbladder spasm"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believes fat flushes gallstones", "B": "Assumes bland food is harmless during an attack", "D": "Thinks only large meals cause spasm"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C149", "cluster_text": "Marcus Bell, 52, comes to the emergency department with right upper abdominal pain that began an hour after a fatty supper. He has nausea and has vomited once, and he has had milder episodes before. His temperature is 37.9 C and the area under his right ribs is tender. The practical nurse assists the RN and the physician. He weighs 90 kilograms.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Eating can worsen the pain, so he should stay NPO until the physician decides): Food, especially fat, stimulates gallbladder contraction against a blocked duct and worsens pain, so he stays NPO until assessment and the physician's plan. A fatty meal does not flush stones. Even small or bland intake can provoke contraction. Fasting also prepares him for possible surgery."}, {"id": 1262, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Marcus has had a laparoscopic cholecystectomy as day surgery. Six hours later he reports sharp pain in his right shoulder and mild abdominal bloating. His vital signs are stable and the incisions are dry. What should the practical nurse teach him?", "options": {"A": "Shoulder pain suggests a wound infection, so he should return for antibiotics.", "B": "Shoulder pain is common from residual gas, and walking and a warm pack may ease it", "C": "Shoulder pain suggests a pulled muscle from positioning, so he should rest in bed.", "D": "Shoulder pain is expected, so he should lie flat and avoid walking until it ends."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reading referred gas pain as infection", "C": "Attributing referred pain to a muscle strain and advising bed rest", "D": "Advising immobility, which prolongs gas pain"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C149", "cluster_text": "Marcus Bell, 52, comes to the emergency department with right upper abdominal pain that began an hour after a fatty supper. He has nausea and has vomited once, and he has had milder episodes before. His temperature is 37.9 C and the area under his right ribs is tender. The practical nurse assists the RN and the physician. He weighs 90 kilograms.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Shoulder pain is common from residual gas, and walking and a warm pack may ease it): Carbon dioxide used to inflate the abdomen can irritate the diaphragm and refer pain to the shoulder, and it usually settles with walking and heat. Infection would bring fever or wound changes. A pulled muscle does not fit the timing, and lying flat prolongs the gas discomfort. His stable vital signs and dry incisions do not suggest a complication. Report worsening pain, fever or vomiting."}, {"id": 1263, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Three weeks later Marcus is admitted with severe epigastric pain radiating to his back after gallstones passed. His lipase is high. He lies flat and refuses to move. The practical nurse is helping with comfort and positioning. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Position him side-lying with knees flexed, give ordered analgesia, and keep him NPO", "B": "Sit him upright at ninety degrees and offer a small meal to settle the pain", "C": "Withhold analgesia until the cause is confirmed so that the exam findings are not masked", "D": "Keep him flat and encourage sips of clear fluid early to rest the pancreas"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Positions upright and feeds him", "C": "Withholds pain relief in severe pain", "D": "Chooses a flat position and early oral intake"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C149", "cluster_text": "Marcus Bell, 52, comes to the emergency department with right upper abdominal pain that began an hour after a fatty supper. He has nausea and has vomited once, and he has had milder episodes before. His temperature is 37.9 C and the area under his right ribs is tender. The practical nurse assists the RN and the physician. He weighs 90 kilograms.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Position him side-lying with knees flexed, give ordered analgesia, and keep him NPO): Acute pancreatitis pain eases in a side-lying or knee-flexed position, and analgesia and bowel rest are standard until the pancreas settles. Lying flat and early feeding stimulate the pancreas and worsen pain. Feeding at ninety degrees does the same. Pain relief should not be withheld, since it does not hide the diagnosis."}, {"id": 1264, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Months later Marcus attends a community clinic with oily, foul-smelling stools and weight loss. He has chronic pancreatitis and a prescription for pancreatic enzyme capsules. He takes them at bedtime on an empty stomach. How should the practical nurse correct this?", "options": {"A": "Take the capsules an hour after meals so stomach acid does not destroy them", "B": "Take the capsules once daily on an empty stomach, since the enzymes digest food later", "C": "Crush the capsules and mix them with hot soup to help them dissolve", "D": "Take the capsules with the first bites of each meal and snack, swallowing them whole"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Mistimes the dose after food has passed", "B": "Misses that enzymes must meet food", "C": "Crushes protected capsules and heats them"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C149", "cluster_text": "Marcus Bell, 52, comes to the emergency department with right upper abdominal pain that began an hour after a fatty supper. He has nausea and has vomited once, and he has had milder episodes before. His temperature is 37.9 C and the area under his right ribs is tender. The practical nurse assists the RN and the physician. He weighs 90 kilograms.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Take the capsules with the first bites of each meal and snack, swallowing them whole): Pancreatic enzymes must mix with food in the stomach and duodenum, so they are taken with the first bites of meals and snacks. Taking them on an empty stomach gives them nothing to digest. Capsules are often coated and should not be crushed or heated. Taking them an hour after eating is too late to help digestion."}, {"id": 1265, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 42-year-old woman admitted with Guillain-Barre syndrome has ascending weakness that now reaches her chest. She is alert and her oxygen saturation is 96 percent. The practical nurse plans monitoring for the shift and is told she is stable. What should be monitored most closely?", "options": {"A": "Bowel sounds and appetite, since gut paralysis is the usual first life threat", "B": "Limb strength and mobility, because walking recovery signals how the illness is progressing", "C": "Respiratory effort and vital capacity, since ventilation can fail before saturation drops", "D": "Skin color and temperature, because fever is the main sign of worsening disease"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Picks gut function over breathing", "B": "Focuses only on mobility", "D": "Treats fever as the key sign"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Respiratory effort and vital capacity, since ventilation can fail before saturation drops): Ascending weakness can reach the respiratory muscles, and ventilation can fail before saturation falls, so breathing effort and vital capacity are tracked closely and changes reported at once. Mobility matters but is not the immediate danger. Gut slowing is less urgent than respiratory failure. Fever is not the main sign of progression."}, {"id": 1266, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A practical nurse spent forty minutes teaching a 54-year-old man how to use a metered-dose inhaler with a spacer. He nodded throughout and said he understood. The nurse wants to judge whether the teaching worked. Which method best evaluates it?", "options": {"A": "Ask him to describe the steps in his own words, then end the session.", "B": "Ask whether he understands and accept his yes as evidence he learned it", "C": "Phone him a week later and ask whether his symptoms have improved.", "D": "Ask him to demonstrate the technique and explain when to use it, then correct errors"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Checking verbal recall instead of skill performance", "B": "Accepts self-report without verification", "C": "Using symptom change as a measure of learning"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask him to demonstrate the technique and explain when to use it, then correct errors): Teaching is judged by what the learner can do, so return demonstration and explanation show whether he learned and let errors be fixed on the spot. Describing the steps in words does not show skill. Symptom change is a delayed and indirect measure. Nodding and saying yes do not confirm skill, and a pamphlet leaves the check to chance."}, {"id": 1267, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In long-term care a resident is found unresponsive and not breathing. The practical nurse sees that the code status section of the chart is blank and cannot recall any order. The resident was admitted last week. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Call the family to ask their wishes before starting any chest compressions", "B": "Wait for the RN to arrive and decide, keeping the resident positioned flat", "C": "Search the chart and family notes until a code status is found before beginning", "D": "Start CPR and call for help, since no valid order exists to withhold it"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays care to phone relatives", "B": "Waits for another nurse's decision", "C": "Delays compressions while searching records"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Start CPR and call for help, since no valid order exists to withhold it): Without a valid order to withhold resuscitation, the default is to start CPR and summon help, since delay reduces the chance of survival. Searching records or phoning family costs minutes that the resident does not have. Waiting for the RN also delays care. Code status gaps are resolved after the emergency."}, {"id": 1268, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ali refuses to cough and breathe deeply, holding his abdomen and saying coughing will split the incision. His pain is rated 6 out of 10. The practical nurse wants to reduce his risk of lung complications. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Delay breathing exercises until tomorrow morning when the anesthetic has worn off and his pain eases", "B": "Give ordered analgesia, then coach him to cough and breathe deeply with a splinting pillow", "C": "Let him skip coughing until the staples are removed, and encourage shallow breaths instead", "D": "Have him cough only when he feels secretions, and avoid pillows that press on the abdomen"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Postpones exercises during the highest-risk period", "C": "Allows avoidance of coughing to protect the incision", "D": "Waits for secretions before coughing"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C150", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hassan Ali, 63, had open upper abdominal surgery this morning and is on a surgical unit. He has a midline incision with staples, a patient-controlled analgesia pump, and a thirty-five year smoking history. The practical nurse is assigned his postoperative care and reports to the RN throughout the shift. He is alert, with stable vital signs and a urinary catheter in place.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give ordered analgesia, then coach him to cough and breathe deeply with a splinting pillow): Pain causes shallow breathing and avoidance of coughing, so analgesia comes first, then coached breathing and coughing with a pillow splint to support the incision. Skipping coughing leaves secretions in the lungs. Waiting for secretions is too late. The first hours after surgery are the key time to start exercises."}, {"id": 1269, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ali has an incentive spirometer at the bedside, but the nurse finds it unused. He says he will use it at night when he cannot sleep. The practical nurse teaches proper use at the bedside this afternoon. What instruction is correct?", "options": {"A": "Use it about ten times every hour while awake, inhaling slowly and holding the breath briefly", "B": "Use it when he feels short of breath, since that is when it helps.", "C": "Inhale quickly and deeply through the mouthpiece, then rest an hour between uses.", "D": "Use it about once per shift, taking five slow breaths each time."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using it as a rescue device rather than regularly", "C": "Inhaling fast and spacing use too far apart", "D": "Using it too rarely to keep the lung bases open"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C150", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hassan Ali, 63, had open upper abdominal surgery this morning and is on a surgical unit. He has a midline incision with staples, a patient-controlled analgesia pump, and a thirty-five year smoking history. The practical nurse is assigned his postoperative care and reports to the RN throughout the shift. He is alert, with stable vital signs and a urinary catheter in place.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use it about ten times every hour while awake, inhaling slowly and holding the breath briefly): Incentive spirometry works through frequent, slow, deep inhalations with a short hold to open the lung bases, so it is used regularly while awake. Using it only when short of breath is too late. A fast breath reduces effective volume and an hour gap is too long. Once per shift is too infrequent to prevent atelectasis."}, {"id": 1270, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On day 2 Mr. Ali has a temperature of 38.1 C, a respiratory rate of 22, and diminished breath sounds at the bases. The incision is clean and dry. His wife says fever always means infection. The practical nurse interprets the findings. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Explain that fever on day 2 means wound infection and expect antibiotics to begin", "B": "Increase his pain medication so he can lie flatter and sleep through the afternoon", "C": "Document the temperature as an expected response to surgery and recheck in eight hours", "D": "Report the findings to the RN as possible atelectasis, and keep up breathing exercises"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes any fever is infection", "B": "Sedates a client who needs lung expansion", "C": "Dismisses abnormal findings as expected"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C150", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hassan Ali, 63, had open upper abdominal surgery this morning and is on a surgical unit. He has a midline incision with staples, a patient-controlled analgesia pump, and a thirty-five year smoking history. The practical nurse is assigned his postoperative care and reports to the RN throughout the shift. He is alert, with stable vital signs and a urinary catheter in place.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the findings to the RN as possible atelectasis, and keep up breathing exercises): Early postoperative fever with reduced bibasilar breath sounds and a faster respiratory rate suggests atelectasis, and the RN needs to know so the plan can be adjusted while breathing exercises continue. The wound looks clean, so a wound infection is unlikely. Fever with respiratory findings should be reported, not simply charted. More sedation and lying flat reduce lung expansion."}, {"id": 1271, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On postoperative day 1 Mr. Ali's vital signs are stable and his pain is controlled. He asks to stay in bed to avoid bleeding from the incision. The practical nurse reviews the plan to ambulate him. What should she explain?", "options": {"A": "Walking is for recovery at home, so it is delayed until the day of discharge", "B": "Walking lowers the risk of clots, pneumonia and constipation, and she will assist", "C": "Turning in bed every hour gives the same benefit as walking, so walking is optional", "D": "Bed rest lets the incision seal, so walking waits until the staples are out"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays mobility until discharge", "C": "Equates turning with walking", "D": "Believes rest prevents bleeding"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C150", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hassan Ali, 63, had open upper abdominal surgery this morning and is on a surgical unit. He has a midline incision with staples, a patient-controlled analgesia pump, and a thirty-five year smoking history. The practical nurse is assigned his postoperative care and reports to the RN throughout the shift. He is alert, with stable vital signs and a urinary catheter in place.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Walking lowers the risk of clots, pneumonia and constipation, and she will assist): Early walking improves circulation, lung expansion and bowel function, and reduces the risk of clots and pneumonia. Rest does not seal an incision faster. Turning helps but does not replace walking. Waiting until discharge removes the benefit during the highest-risk days."}, {"id": 1272, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A hospitalized 80-year-old woman collapses and has no pulse. The practical nurse finds a valid, signed do-not-resuscitate order on her chart that matches her wristband. Her daughter is crying and pleads with her to begin compressions. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Start CPR until the physician confirms the order is valid.", "B": "Respect the order, comfort the daughter, and notify the RN", "C": "Call the RN first and begin compressions while waiting.", "D": "Check with the daughter whether she agrees with the order."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Starting CPR despite a valid order", "C": "Beginning compressions before confirming with the RN", "D": "Seeking family agreement to a valid order"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Respect the order, comfort the daughter, and notify the RN): A valid DNR reflects the client's own decision, and the nurse respects it while offering comfort and informing the RN. Family distress does not change the order, and CPR contrary to it is unethical. The order is already valid and matches her wristband, so waiting for confirmation or the daughter's agreement is not needed. Compassion for the family can be shown without resuscitating."}, {"id": 1273, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Strike notice has been posted for next month. A colleague tells Riley she should simply walk off the unit at the start of job action with no handover to protect her own position. Several staff members are listening to this advice. What principle should guide Riley?", "options": {"A": "Remain until the union steward tells her when to leave the unit.", "B": "Hand over to the new graduate, then leave when job action starts.", "C": "Leave once job action begins, because the union's decision replaces her duty to clients", "D": "Keep meeting professional obligations through safe handover and continuity of care"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Letting the union direct professional duties", "B": "Handing over to an inexperienced nurse and then leaving", "C": "Believes a union decision ends her duty to clients"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C145", "cluster_text": "Riley Tran is a practical nurse and preceptor on a busy medical unit. This week she is orienting a new graduate named Sam and supervising a 22-year-old nursing student. The union has issued strike notice for next month, and staff are discussing it. Riley is aware of professional standards covering practice, social media and personal devices. The unit is often short staffed.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep meeting professional obligations through safe handover and continuity of care): Nurses keep their professional accountability during job action, which includes handing over client information and ensuring continuity of care. Walking off without handover could harm clients. Her duty does not depend on a steward's instruction. Handover goes to a competent nurse or manager, and a new graduate cannot take responsibility alone."}, {"id": 1274, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Sam posts a photo of the unit coffee area with a caption about a draining shift and a rude family member, with no client names. Colleagues comment, and one adds the room number of the family's loved one. Riley is concerned. What is the best guidance?", "options": {"A": "Details can still identify a client, so ask Sam to remove it and review the standards", "B": "The post is acceptable because no client names appear, and the comment should stay up", "C": "Only the comment is a problem, so Riley should delete it herself and leave the post", "D": "The post is acceptable if the account is private so only friends can see it"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assumes no name means no identification", "C": "Treats the post as separate from the comment", "D": "Relies on privacy settings for protection"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C145", "cluster_text": "Riley Tran is a practical nurse and preceptor on a busy medical unit. This week she is orienting a new graduate named Sam and supervising a 22-year-old nursing student. The union has issued strike notice for next month, and staff are discussing it. Riley is aware of professional standards covering practice, social media and personal devices. The unit is often short staffed.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Details can still identify a client, so ask Sam to remove it and review the standards): A caption about a family member plus a room number can identify the client and the family, so the post breaches confidentiality even without a name. Private settings do not guarantee that content stays private. The post and comment together are the concern. Riley guides Sam to remove it and review professional standards and facility policy."}, {"id": 1275, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Riley finds Sam preparing to photograph a client's sacral wound on his personal phone to show the wound nurse, because the unit camera is across the hall. The client is asleep and has not been asked about photographs, and the wound nurse is waiting. What should Riley do?", "options": {"A": "Allow it if the photo is sent immediately and deleted from the phone afterward", "B": "Stop it and follow policy on approved devices, with consent and secure storage", "C": "Allow it if the client's face is not visible, since the wound alone is not identifying", "D": "Take the photograph herself on her own phone so that Sam is not responsible"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepts convenience and later deletion as safe", "C": "Assumes a wound image is not identifying", "D": "Moves the same breach to another personal device"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C145", "cluster_text": "Riley Tran is a practical nurse and preceptor on a busy medical unit. This week she is orienting a new graduate named Sam and supervising a 22-year-old nursing student. The union has issued strike notice for next month, and staff are discussing it. Riley is aware of professional standards covering practice, social media and personal devices. The unit is often short staffed.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop it and follow policy on approved devices, with consent and secure storage): Client images should be taken only with consent and on approved devices with secure storage, and a sleeping client has not consented. Sending and deleting does not control copies in messaging apps. A wound image can identify a client through context or marks. Using Riley's own phone repeats the breach."}, {"id": 1276, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "The nursing student, 22, tells Riley that she practised giving subcutaneous injections in the lab and asks to give one now to a client alone. The instructor is off the unit. Riley must decide quickly because a client is waiting for the injection. What is correct?", "options": {"A": "Students need supervision, so Riley observes or waits for the instructor", "B": "Allow it if another student watches and signs the record.", "C": "Allow it if the client agrees to be injected by a student.", "D": "Allow it, because a skill practised in the lab is within the student's scope"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating peer observation as supervision", "C": "Treating client consent as removing the supervision requirement", "D": "Equates lab practice with independent competence"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C145", "cluster_text": "Riley Tran is a practical nurse and preceptor on a busy medical unit. This week she is orienting a new graduate named Sam and supervising a 22-year-old nursing student. The union has issued strike notice for next month, and staff are discussing it. Riley is aware of professional standards covering practice, social media and personal devices. The unit is often short staffed.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Students need supervision, so Riley observes or waits for the instructor): Students may perform skills only under supervision and within the limits set by their school and the facility, so the injection needs Riley's direct observation or the instructor. Lab practice alone does not authorize independent practice. Another student cannot supervise, and client consent does not remove the supervision requirement."}, {"id": 1277, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Sam, a new graduate on orientation, gives a medication after a preceptor-approved check but later realizes the dose was wrong and tells Riley immediately. Sam asks whether Riley, as preceptor, holds all the accountability. Which statement about their professional accountability is correct?", "options": {"A": "The orientee alone is accountable, because the preceptor only observes the shifts", "B": "Neither is accountable, as the facility orientation program assumes the risk", "C": "Both are accountable for their own practice, so the error is reported and documented by each", "D": "The preceptor holds all accountability, since the orientee is practising under her supervision"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Removes the preceptor's role in the check", "B": "Removes accountability from both", "D": "Places all accountability on the preceptor"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C145", "cluster_text": "Riley Tran is a practical nurse and preceptor on a busy medical unit. This week she is orienting a new graduate named Sam and supervising a 22-year-old nursing student. The union has issued strike notice for next month, and staff are discussing it. Riley is aware of professional standards covering practice, social media and personal devices. The unit is often short staffed.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Both are accountable for their own practice, so the error is reported and documented by each): Each nurse remains accountable for her own decisions and actions, and the preceptor is also accountable for the guidance and approval she gave. Both report and document the error through the facility process. The preceptor does not take on the orientee's accountability. The facility program does not remove either nurse's responsibility."}, {"id": 1278, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Marcus, 52, has type 2 diabetes and tells the clinic PN he checks his glucose only twice weekly because strips cost $60 monthly and his drug plan excludes them. His readings vary widely. He says the teaching he received was clear and he understands it. Which action best addresses the cause of his inconsistent monitoring?", "options": {"A": "Document that he is non-adherent and report the concern to the primary care provider", "B": "Advise him to test only when he feels symptoms of high or low glucose", "C": "Repeat the glucose monitoring teaching with a written handout and a return demonstration today", "D": "Refer him to the social worker or pharmacist to explore strip funding programs"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Labels a social barrier as non-adherence", "B": "Reduces testing further, which risks missed low or high readings", "C": "Treats a cost barrier as a knowledge gap"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refer him to the social worker or pharmacist to explore strip funding programs): Marcus already understands the teaching, so the barrier is the monthly cost of strips, and a referral to the social worker or pharmacist targets that determinant of health. Repeating teaching would not change his access to supplies. Labelling him non-adherent blames the client for a financial problem and leaves the cause unaddressed. Always look for social determinants before assuming a client lacks knowledge."}, {"id": 1279, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "While walking her dog, Elena, a PN who is off duty, meets a neighbour aged 66 whose left face is drooping and whose words sound thick. The neighbour says she feels fine and wants to go home and lie down. Her left grip is weak. What should Elena do first?", "options": {"A": "Give her water to swallow and check whether her speech improves.", "B": "Call 911 right away and note the time her symptoms first started", "C": "Stay with her, call her family, and reassess her speech later.", "D": "Drive her to the nearest clinic in her own car and park near the entrance"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving oral intake before a swallow screen", "C": "Waiting and reassessing when stroke needs emergency care", "D": "Uses private transport instead of activating emergency services"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Call 911 right away and note the time her symptoms first started): Facial droop, slurred speech and arm weakness are positive FAST signs, so the neighbour needs emergency stroke care and the onset time. Giving fluids risks aspiration. Waiting to reassess delays treatment that depends on time. Driving her to a clinic skips the stroke-capable emergency pathway, and medication should wait until a prescriber has ruled out bleeding."}, {"id": 1280, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a PN wants advice from the physician about a client's worsening rash and thinks of texting a photo and the client's name from her personal phone. The hospital provides a secure messaging application on unit devices. Following privacy standards, how should the PN proceed?", "options": {"A": "Text the photo from her personal phone after cropping out the client's face", "B": "Use the approved secure messaging system and share only what the care needs", "C": "Email the photo to the physician's personal account and delete it after a reply", "D": "Send the photo with initials only, because initials alone cannot identify a client"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believes cropping the face removes identifiability", "C": "Uses unsecured personal channels that leave copies behind", "D": "Believes initials are anonymous when the context identifies the client"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use the approved secure messaging system and share only what the care needs): Approved secure messaging protects client information and creates a record under the organization's controls, and sharing only what is needed respects the minimum-necessary principle. Cropping the face still leaves a body image, rash and timing that can identify a client. Initials by text remain identifying in context and travel over unsecured channels. Use only systems the employer has authorized for client information."}, {"id": 1281, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In long-term care, a PN finds a resident, 84, with new right arm weakness and slurred speech at 0630. The night aide says he was talking normally when she turned him at 0200, and he ate supper at 1800. The PN calls the RN. Which time should be reported as last known well?", "options": {"A": "0645, when the RN arrived and confirmed the stroke signs on assessment", "B": "0200, when staff last saw him without symptoms", "C": "0630, when the weakness and slurred speech were first found by the PN", "D": "1800, when he last ate a full meal without any trouble at supper"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses the time of confirmation, not onset or last normal", "C": "Records the time found instead of the last time he was normal", "D": "Uses an earlier time that skips a later normal observation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (0200, when staff last saw him without symptoms): Last known well is the most recent time the resident was seen free of stroke symptoms, and the aide's 0200 turn is that observation. The time the signs were found only marks discovery, not onset. The supper at 1800 is earlier than a documented normal contact. Treatment windows are calculated from last known well, so the accurate time must be given to the team."}, {"id": 1282, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Ferreira, 70, was oriented at supper but now cannot say his name and has slurred speech. Another client's call light is ringing, a visitor asks Dana about visiting hours, and the desk phone is also ringing. Which action should Dana take first?", "options": {"A": "Finish answering the visitor's question about visiting hours before leaving the room to assess him", "B": "Document the new confusion and slurred speech, then reassess his orientation after the visitor leaves", "C": "Stay with him, check vital signs and glucose, and tell Imran about the change at once", "D": "Answer the ringing call light first, since it may be a quick request"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Lets a non-urgent social request delay urgent assessment", "B": "Waits to see whether confusion resolves", "D": "Prioritizes a routine request over an acute neurological change"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C156", "cluster_text": "Dana, a PN, begins an evening shift on a small mixed hospital unit and works with the team lead, RN Imran Sheikh. Her assignment includes a medical client, a postpartum client, a client with diabetes and an older adult with a urinary catheter. During the first hour each of these four clients has a change in condition, and Dana must decide what to do first and what to report.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stay with him, check vital signs and glucose, and tell Imran about the change at once): A sudden change in speech and orientation may be a stroke or low glucose, so Dana stays, assesses and reports to the RN immediately. The call light can be covered by a colleague or answered by asking another team member. Documenting first and reassessing later loses time that matters in stroke care. Acute neurological change outranks routine requests."}, {"id": 1283, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Ms. Alvarez, three hours after a vaginal birth, has soaked two pads in forty minutes and her fundus feels soft above the umbilicus. She looks pale. Dana has told Imran. Which action fits Dana's role as the team plans care?", "options": {"A": "Give oxytocin from the unit stock without an order, since bleeding must stop quickly", "B": "Massage the fundus as taught, monitor bleeding, and report so treatment is ordered", "C": "Wait for the physician to arrive before touching the abdomen or counting any pads", "D": "Recheck the pad count in one hour after the visitors have left the room"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Administers a drug without an order, outside scope", "C": "Delays basic first-line care until a physician is present", "D": "Delays reassessment of a possible hemorrhage"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C156", "cluster_text": "Dana, a PN, begins an evening shift on a small mixed hospital unit and works with the team lead, RN Imran Sheikh. Her assignment includes a medical client, a postpartum client, a client with diabetes and an older adult with a urinary catheter. During the first hour each of these four clients has a change in condition, and Dana must decide what to do first and what to report.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Massage the fundus as taught, monitor bleeding, and report so treatment is ordered): Heavy bleeding with a boggy fundus suggests uterine atony, and fundal massage with close monitoring is a nursing action that the team expects while orders are obtained. Giving a drug without an order is outside the PN's authority. Waiting for the physician delays first-line care. Rechecking in an hour risks significant blood loss. Act within scope and report promptly."}, {"id": 1284, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Chen, 60, treated with insulin for type 2 diabetes, is sweaty and shaky, and his glucose is 2.8 mmol/L. He is alert and can swallow. His supper tray has just arrived. Dana is planning care with Imran. Which sequence is best?", "options": {"A": "Give 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, recheck in 15 minutes, then give the meal", "B": "Hold all carbohydrate until Imran confirms a physician's order for treatment of the low glucose", "C": "Give the supper tray first, since the starch and protein will raise his glucose safely", "D": "Give 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate and recheck his glucose in an hour with the meal"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delays treatment of hypoglycemia waiting for an order", "C": "Treats low glucose with a mixed meal that acts too slowly", "D": "Rechecks too late to confirm the glucose has risen"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C156", "cluster_text": "Dana, a PN, begins an evening shift on a small mixed hospital unit and works with the team lead, RN Imran Sheikh. Her assignment includes a medical client, a postpartum client, a client with diabetes and an older adult with a urinary catheter. During the first hour each of these four clients has a change in condition, and Dana must decide what to do first and what to report.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, recheck in 15 minutes, then give the meal): A glucose of 2.8 mmol/L with symptoms needs fast-acting sugar immediately, followed by a recheck in 15 minutes to confirm recovery. A meal alone acts too slowly to correct the low. An hour is too long to recheck a client who may fall again. Hypoglycemia protocols allow treatment before an individual order, with prompt reporting to the RN."}, {"id": 1285, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mrs. Dubois, 80, has an indwelling urinary catheter. Dana finds a temperature of 38.9 C, heart rate 118, respiratory rate 24, blood pressure 88/50 mmHg and new confusion. Imran is at another bedside, and Dana must reach him quickly. Which communication is best?", "options": {"A": "Use SBAR to report all abnormal values and state concern about sepsis", "B": "Report that she looks unwell and wait for culture results before asking for a review", "C": "Ask a colleague to pass the vital signs along when Imran is free.", "D": "Report the temperature and the low blood pressure, and recheck the rest later."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Waits for cultures before escalating", "C": "Relaying critical data through a colleague with a delay", "D": "Giving a partial report of an unstable client's findings"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C156", "cluster_text": "Dana, a PN, begins an evening shift on a small mixed hospital unit and works with the team lead, RN Imran Sheikh. Her assignment includes a medical client, a postpartum client, a client with diabetes and an older adult with a urinary catheter. During the first hour each of these four clients has a change in condition, and Dana must decide what to do first and what to report.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use SBAR to report all abnormal values and state concern about sepsis): These findings point to sepsis with hypotension and confusion, so Dana gives a structured SBAR with every abnormal value and a clear request for immediate assessment. Waiting for cultures delays treatment, which is time-critical. Passing data through a colleague later leaves the client unreviewed. A partial report hides the pattern that signals the danger. Escalate early and completely."}, {"id": 1286, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Tomas, a PN in community care, is asked by an agency to give a newly ordered subcutaneous injectable he has never administered. He completed a general injection course. The agency policy does not mention this drug. Before giving it, what is the best guideline-based step?", "options": {"A": "Give it after reading the package insert once while a coworker watches him", "B": "Confirm the drug is within PN scope and employer policy, and that he is competent", "C": "Ask the client to sign a form accepting the risk of a new administrator", "D": "Give it, because subcutaneous injections are already part of his usual nursing practice"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Substitutes a brief read and observer for confirmed competence", "C": "Shifts accountability onto the client through a waiver", "D": "Assumes any injectable is within scope"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Confirm the drug is within PN scope and employer policy, and that he is competent): Practical nurses are accountable for working within regulatory standards, employer policy and their own competence, so Tomas confirms authority and competence before giving a new drug. Routine injection skill does not cover every injectable. A quick read and observer are not verified competence. A client waiver does not transfer the nurse's accountability."}, {"id": 1287, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After a hard shift, a PN posts online about a frequent visitor to the unit with the worst wound she has seen, naming no one. A coworker points out that a follower from the same town could identify the client. The PN removes the post. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Apologize to the client directly and tell the manager if he is upset.", "B": "Check her followers' list and block anyone from the client's town.", "C": "Take no further action, since the post was deleted within minutes of going online", "D": "Report the breach to the manager and follow the privacy breach process"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Apologizing informally and skipping the breach process", "B": "Managing the audience instead of reporting", "C": "Believes quick deletion cancels a breach"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the breach to the manager and follow the privacy breach process): A possible identification means client information may have been disclosed, so the organization's breach process must be started through the manager and documented. Deleting the post does not undo what was seen or copied. Blocking followers cannot control what has spread. An apology without a report skips the required step that lets the organization assess and notify properly."}, {"id": 1288, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Waiting for a clinic visit, an adult coughs into bare hands and then handles the magazines on a shared table. No mask is worn, and the client sits close to a pregnant woman. The PN sees this from the desk. Which action should she take first?", "options": {"A": "Offer a mask and tissues, prompt hand hygiene, and seat the client apart", "B": "Hand the pregnant woman a mask and ask her to sit nearer the desk.", "C": "Ask the client to leave the waiting room and return when the cough is gone", "D": "Wipe down the table now and ask him to use sanitizer when called in."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Protecting the exposed person without controlling the source", "C": "Excludes a client from care instead of applying source control", "D": "Cleaning a surface and delaying source control"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Offer a mask and tissues, prompt hand hygiene, and seat the client apart): Source control at the person coughing is the priority, which means a mask, tissues, hand hygiene and distance from others. Sending the client away denies care that can be given safely with precautions. Wiping the table treats a surface but not the source, and sanitizer later delays control. Masking the pregnant woman alone does not stop spread to others in the room."}, {"id": 1289, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Lucia, 62, receives home care for amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and now reports morning headaches, shallow breaths and daytime sleepiness. Her family asks how the disease will progress. Which statement by the PN best explains why early respiratory planning discussions are needed?", "options": {"A": "Breathing muscles weaken over time, so ventilation wishes need discussion before a crisis", "B": "Morning headaches show carbon dioxide is building up, so oxygen should be started now.", "C": "Breathing problems usually arrive suddenly, so planning is best left until they occur.", "D": "Planning belongs to the family doctor, so the nurse should refer without discussing it."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Starting oxygen in place of planning discussion", "C": "Waiting for a crisis before planning", "D": "Deferring all goals of care discussion to the physician"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Breathing muscles weaken over time, so ventilation wishes need discussion before a crisis): Progressive weakness of the respiratory muscles produces hypoventilation signs such as morning headache and sleepiness, so her goals about ventilation support need a calm discussion early. Oxygen alone does not address the planning need and can mask carbon dioxide retention. Waiting for a crisis removes the chance to decide with time. Nurses support and initiate goals of care conversations and involve the team."}, {"id": 1290, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At snack time in the day care, toddlers aged 18 to 30 months are served whole grapes, raw carrot sticks, soft banana slices and plain crackers. The staff ask Shreya which food carries the highest choking risk and what to change. What should she advise?", "options": {"A": "Remove the crackers because dry foods dissolve slowly and can block a toddler's airway", "B": "Cut grapes in half across the middle and keep serving raw carrot sticks as they are", "C": "Cut grapes lengthwise into quarters and cook or grate the carrot first", "D": "Keep every snack whole but require toddlers to stay seated while an adult watches them eat"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Misidentifies the highest risk foods", "B": "Halving round foods crosswise still leaves a plug-shaped piece", "D": "Relies on seating and supervision without changing food shape or texture"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C148", "cluster_text": "Shreya, a PN, works in community health. This week she gives health advice to a licensed day care, helps in an emergency department on one shift, visits a school, and makes home visits to referred families. In each place she meets a child safety concern, and she needs to act within her role and the legal duty to protect children.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cut grapes lengthwise into quarters and cook or grate the carrot first): Whole grapes and hard raw carrot are classic choking hazards for toddlers, so the shape and texture must change by quartering lengthwise and cooking or grating. Halving a grape crosswise leaves a piece that can still plug the airway. Crackers soften in the mouth and are lower risk. Supervision helps but cannot replace safer food preparation."}, {"id": 1291, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Bruising on the cheek and torso brings an 8-month-old to the emergency department. His mother says he fell off the couch while she answered the door, and the bruises are two days old. Which finding most strongly suggests physical abuse?", "options": {"A": "A fall story that does not match his abilities or the pattern of bruises", "B": "A small bruise on the forehead that matches a crawling bump.", "C": "The infant cries when handled and settles when held by his mother.", "D": "The father is not present and the mother answers all questions alone."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Accepting a common minor injury as suspicious", "C": "Reading normal infant behaviour as a sign of abuse", "D": "Treating family circumstances as evidence of abuse"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C148", "cluster_text": "Shreya, a PN, works in community health. This week she gives health advice to a licensed day care, helps in an emergency department on one shift, visits a school, and makes home visits to referred families. In each place she meets a child safety concern, and she needs to act within her role and the legal duty to protect children.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A fall story that does not match his abilities or the pattern of bruises): Abuse is suspected when the history does not fit the injury, the child's developmental stage or the bruise pattern, such as torso and cheek bruising in an infant who cannot move far. A small forehead bump fits crawling. Crying when handled and settling with a parent are normal. A parent being alone is not evidence of abuse. Report concerns to the team and protection services."}, {"id": 1292, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a school health visit, a 10-year-old tells the PN that her stepfather hits her when he is angry, then asks the PN to tell no one. There is no visible injury and no photograph. What legal duty applies to the PN?", "options": {"A": "Report after informing the mother, so the family can take part.", "B": "Wait until the child repeats the disclosure to another adult.", "C": "Discuss it with the principal and let the principal decide on reporting.", "D": "Report promptly to child protection services; proof is not required first"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Informing a caregiver before a report", "B": "Requiring a repeat disclosure before reporting", "C": "Passing the legal duty to another person"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C148", "cluster_text": "Shreya, a PN, works in community health. This week she gives health advice to a licensed day care, helps in an emergency department on one shift, visits a school, and makes home visits to referred families. In each place she meets a child safety concern, and she needs to act within her role and the legal duty to protect children.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report promptly to child protection services; proof is not required first): The duty to report is triggered by reasonable grounds to suspect harm, and a child's disclosure meets that threshold without proof. Informing the mother first may endanger her and compromise an investigation. Waiting for a repeated disclosure leaves the child unprotected. The duty is personal and cannot be handed to a principal."}, {"id": 1293, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, the PN finds a 5-month-old in a soiled diaper with a rash and dry lips, weighing 600 g less than at the last visit. The mother says she cannot afford formula and sleeps most of the day. What should the PN do next?", "options": {"A": "Give her food bank information and arrange a repeat weighing next week.", "B": "Ask the mother to bring the infant in for a weight check.", "C": "Teach the mother hygiene and formula preparation and plan to revisit in two weeks", "D": "Report neglect to child protection and arrange an urgent infant assessment"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offering resources and delaying reporting of neglect", "B": "Arranging routine follow up for an infant at risk", "C": "Treats neglect as a teaching need and delays action"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C148", "cluster_text": "Shreya, a PN, works in community health. This week she gives health advice to a licensed day care, helps in an emergency department on one shift, visits a school, and makes home visits to referred families. In each place she meets a child safety concern, and she needs to act within her role and the legal duty to protect children.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report neglect to child protection and arrange an urgent infant assessment): Weight loss, dehydration and poor hygiene in an infant raise reasonable suspicion of neglect, so the PN reports to child protection and gets the infant assessed urgently. Resources and a repeat weighing next week leave a fragile infant at risk. A clinic weight check relies on the mother and delays assessment. Teaching alone does not meet the legal duty."}, {"id": 1294, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "During a code, a needle punctures Priya's glove and finger as she handles a used syringe from a client with unknown infection status. She washes the area and feels well. She plans to finish her shift first. What is the best next action?", "options": {"A": "Report to her supervisor now, but delay occupational health until results are back.", "B": "Report to occupational health during her break and return to her duties after.", "C": "Report to her supervisor and occupational health now, as prophylaxis is time-sensitive", "D": "Finish the shift, then report the exposure at the start of her next shift"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying prophylaxis assessment until results are available", "B": "Postponing the report until a break", "D": "Delays reporting past the effective prophylaxis window"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C146", "cluster_text": "Priya, a PN, works on an acute medical unit and is floated to emergency for one shift. During a busy day she is exposed to blood, cares for a client on contact precautions, helps with a client who is receiving chemotherapy, and finds an unlabeled cleaning product. Each event tests her knowledge of workplace safety, infection control and her duty to report.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report to her supervisor and occupational health now, as prophylaxis is time-sensitive): Post-exposure prophylaxis is most effective when started promptly, so immediate reporting lets occupational health assess the risk and begin treatment. Delaying occupational health or waiting for a break wastes the window. Finishing the shift also wastes the window. Symptoms may not appear for weeks, so watching for illness is not a safe plan."}, {"id": 1295, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Priya finishes taking a blood pressure on one client while wearing gloves, then walks straight to the next client's bedside. The gloves look clean and are not torn, and both clients are on routine precautions. Which statement about hand hygiene at this point is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Wearing gloves replaces hand hygiene, because they work as a barrier against microbes", "B": "Gloves come off, and hand hygiene is done after the first client and before the next", "C": "Hand hygiene is needed only after contact with visible soil or body fluids", "D": "Clean gloves can be worn between clients if no body fluids were touched"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats gloves as a substitute for hand hygiene", "C": "Limits hand hygiene to visible soil", "D": "Reuses gloves between clients"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C146", "cluster_text": "Priya, a PN, works on an acute medical unit and is floated to emergency for one shift. During a busy day she is exposed to blood, cares for a client on contact precautions, helps with a client who is receiving chemotherapy, and finds an unlabeled cleaning product. Each event tests her knowledge of workplace safety, infection control and her duty to report.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gloves come off, and hand hygiene is done after the first client and before the next): Hand hygiene is required between clients, after glove removal and before the next contact, because hands can be contaminated even when gloves look clean. Reusing gloves spreads organisms. Hands can carry microbes with no visible soil. Gloves can have tiny defects and are not a replacement for cleaning hands."}, {"id": 1296, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Priya finishes caring for a client with C. difficile on contact precautions. Her gloves are soiled, her gown is tied at the neck, and the door is two steps away. A coworker waits in the hall holding a clean cart. Which approach to removing protective equipment is best?", "options": {"A": "Remove the gown, keep gloves on to open the door, then remove them outside", "B": "Remove gloves and gown inside the room, then do hand hygiene before touching the door", "C": "Remove gloves inside the room but keep the gown on until she reaches the hallway", "D": "Leave the room wearing gloves and gown and remove both at the nurses' station"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Touches the door with contaminated gloves, spreading contamination", "C": "Carries a contaminated gown into the hallway", "D": "Spreads organisms through corridors on contaminated equipment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C146", "cluster_text": "Priya, a PN, works on an acute medical unit and is floated to emergency for one shift. During a busy day she is exposed to blood, cares for a client on contact precautions, helps with a client who is receiving chemotherapy, and finds an unlabeled cleaning product. Each event tests her knowledge of workplace safety, infection control and her duty to report.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Remove gloves and gown inside the room, then do hand hygiene before touching the door): Contaminated gloves and gown are removed inside the room so that organisms stay there, and hand hygiene follows before touching clean surfaces. A gown worn into the hallway carries contamination. Leaving the room fully dressed spreads it. Using soiled gloves on the door handle contaminates a surface that others will touch."}, {"id": 1297, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On the oncology unit, Priya is asked to empty a urine bag for a client who received chemotherapy 24 hours ago. The unit stocks standard exam gloves, and a hazardous drug cart holds chemotherapy-tested gloves and gowns. Which choice is best?", "options": {"A": "Wear one pair of standard exam gloves, since the drug has already been given", "B": "Wear chemotherapy-tested double gloves and a gown, since fluids may contain drug", "C": "Wear two pairs of standard exam gloves, which give the same protection as tested ones", "D": "Wear a gown only and use bare hands briefly, then wash them well afterward"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes drug clearance is complete after a day", "C": "Assumes thickness of two standard gloves equals rated protection", "D": "Allows direct skin contact with hazardous drug residue"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C146", "cluster_text": "Priya, a PN, works on an acute medical unit and is floated to emergency for one shift. During a busy day she is exposed to blood, cares for a client on contact precautions, helps with a client who is receiving chemotherapy, and finds an unlabeled cleaning product. Each event tests her knowledge of workplace safety, infection control and her duty to report.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Wear chemotherapy-tested double gloves and a gown, since fluids may contain drug): Chemotherapy drugs and metabolites are excreted in body fluids for several days, so handling urine needs the same hazardous drug precautions as handling the drug. Standard exam gloves are not tested for permeation, even doubled. Bare-handed contact exposes the skin to a hazardous agent that washing afterward cannot undo."}, {"id": 1298, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Priya finds an unlabeled spray bottle by the sink that a housekeeper left, and a coworker says it is just disinfectant and tells her to wipe the table with it. A nearby supplier-labelled concentrate carries a corrosive pictogram. What should Priya do?", "options": {"A": "Not use the bottle, and report it so the contents are identified and labelled", "B": "Ask the coworker to confirm the contents and use it if they are sure.", "C": "Check the corrosive concentrate's label for the dilution ratio and relabel the bottle accordingly.", "D": "Put the bottle in the supply closet and use the labelled concentrate instead."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Accepting a verbal identification of an unlabelled product", "C": "Relabelling an unknown product from another container's label", "D": "Storing an unidentified product without reporting it"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C146", "cluster_text": "Priya, a PN, works on an acute medical unit and is floated to emergency for one shift. During a busy day she is exposed to blood, cares for a client on contact precautions, helps with a client who is receiving chemotherapy, and finds an unlabeled cleaning product. Each event tests her knowledge of workplace safety, infection control and her duty to report.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Not use the bottle, and report it so the contents are identified and labelled): Workplace hazardous materials rules require every container to be labelled, and an unidentified product could be corrosive or incompatible with other agents, so Priya should avoid it and report it. A coworker's word does not identify the contents. Copying a label from another container creates a false safety record. Storing it away leaves the hazard for others."}, {"id": 1299, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a public health talk, the PN describes a neighbourhood walking club, a petition for a bike lane and a vaccination clinic. An attendee asks whether these are all the same. Which response best distinguishes health promotion from disease prevention?", "options": {"A": "Prevention addresses whole communities, while promotion is aimed only at individual clients' choices", "B": "Promotion builds conditions and skills for health, while prevention targets specific diseases", "C": "Promotion treats existing illness while prevention is aimed at slowing complications of disease", "D": "They are identical, since both aim to lower a person's chance of becoming ill"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reverses the usual scope of each concept", "C": "Mixes promotion with treatment and tertiary prevention", "D": "Treats two related ideas as the same"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Promotion builds conditions and skills for health, while prevention targets specific diseases): Health promotion enables people and communities to improve their overall health through skills, supportive environments and policy, such as walking clubs and bike lanes. Prevention aims at specific diseases, as vaccination does. They overlap but are not identical. Promotion is not treatment, and both can target individuals or communities."}, {"id": 1300, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Hamid, 34, has asthma and takes two puffs of salbutamol before a run. He phones the clinic saying his hands tremble and his heart feels fast for about twenty minutes, and he fears an allergy. He has no rash, swelling or wheeze. What should the PN explain?", "options": {"A": "He should switch to a daily long-acting inhaler for better symptom control", "B": "These signs suggest an allergy, so he should stop all inhaler use immediately", "C": "Tremor means the dose is too low, so he should take two more puffs", "D": "Tremor and a fast pulse are common salbutamol effects; report chest pain or lasting palpitations"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changes therapy without a prescriber's assessment", "B": "Mistakes an expected effect for an allergy", "C": "Treats a side effect as under-dosing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tremor and a fast pulse are common salbutamol effects; report chest pain or lasting palpitations): Beta-agonist stimulation of skeletal muscle and the heart causes tremor and a fast pulse, effects that are common, brief and not allergic, especially without rash, swelling or wheeze. Calling it an allergy could lead him to avoid his reliever. Extra puffs would worsen the effect. Changing his inhaler plan is a prescriber decision."}, {"id": 1301, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sam, 64, receives palliative home care for a bowel obstruction from advanced cancer and vomits several times daily. The nurse practitioner orders an antiemetic, and the family asks why a friend with cancer took a different pill. Which principle best explains the choice?", "options": {"A": "One standard antiemetic suits cancer clients, with the dose raised if vomiting continues", "B": "Gut-motility drugs are preferred first, since they relieve nausea from most causes", "C": "Antiemetics act in much the same way, so the cheapest or most familiar can be given", "D": "Nausea has different pathways, so the drug is matched to the cause and obstruction"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes one drug suits every cancer client", "B": "Uses a prokinetic that can be harmful in a blocked bowel", "C": "Overlooks that antiemetics target different receptors and pathways"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Nausea has different pathways, so the drug is matched to the cause and obstruction): Antiemetics work on different receptors, and in bowel obstruction some drugs can be unsafe, so the choice follows the probable cause and the client's situation. Treating all drugs as interchangeable risks a poor result or harm. A single standard drug ignores the cause of the vomiting. Motility drugs can be unsafe in a blocked bowel."}, {"id": 1302, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a hospital unit with two nurses absent and a high-acuity load, a PN is told to take a full assignment of eight clients, including an insulin infusion she has not been trained to manage. She feels unsafe. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Tell the charge nurse, state what she can safely do, and file a workload report", "B": "Accept the assignment but quietly ask a coworker to manage the insulin infusion", "C": "Accept the assignment and record her concern in a personal note for later", "D": "Refuse the whole assignment immediately and leave the unit to protect her licence"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delegates informally without notifying the charge nurse", "C": "Records the concern privately without escalating it", "D": "Abandons assignment without communication"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tell the charge nurse, state what she can safely do, and file a workload report): Nurses must communicate unsafe conditions, set limits on tasks beyond their competence, and report workload concerns through the proper channel. Leaving the unit may amount to abandonment. Quietly passing the infusion to a coworker skips the charge nurse and leaves accountability unclear. A private note does not reach anyone who can change the assignment."}, {"id": 1303, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ng, 79, comes to the clinic with his daughter after months of forgetting recent conversations and misplacing keys. He scores 23 of 30 on a cognitive screening tool completed in a noisy room after a poor night's sleep. He walked in and is well dressed. How should the PN interpret the result?", "options": {"A": "A normal result, because he is well dressed and walks in independently", "B": "Invalid, so no further assessment is needed until he repeats it next year", "C": "Proof of dementia, so the family should be told the diagnosis is confirmed", "D": "A screening result needing follow-up assessment and review of other factors, not a diagnosis"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses appearance and mobility to dismiss cognitive concern", "B": "Dismisses a worrying score and delays assessment", "C": "Treats a screening score as diagnostic"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A screening result needing follow-up assessment and review of other factors, not a diagnosis): A screening score suggests possible impairment but cannot diagnose dementia, and noise and poor sleep may lower it, so the result is reported for further assessment. Calling it proof overreaches the tool. Good grooming and walking say little about cognition. Waiting a year ignores months of progressive symptoms."}, {"id": 1304, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Kofi writes in the resident's chart, \"incident report completed and sent to risk management.\" The charge nurse reads the entry during rounds and advises him to change it before the shift ends. Kofi asks why. Which rationale best supports the change?", "options": {"A": "The incident report is confidential, so mentioning it would breach the manager's privacy rights", "B": "A chart reference to forms is acceptable only if the family has been told", "C": "It is a separate quality tool, while the chart holds only facts", "D": "The chart should refer to the report so reviewers can find the full account"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confuses confidentiality of a report with chart content", "B": "Links acceptable entries to family notification", "D": "Believes the chart should cross-reference the report"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C167", "cluster_text": "Kofi, a PN on a surgical unit, was caring for a resident who fell while walking alone to the bathroom on an evening shift. The resident had a bed alarm, but its battery was dead, and the unit was short-staffed. The manager has called a review of the fall, and Kofi must document, explain safety concepts and help the team learn.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It is a separate quality tool, while the chart holds only facts): The chart records objective facts about the client's condition, assessments and care, while the incident report supports quality improvement and stays separate from the record. Mentioning it in the chart blurs that separation. The reason is not manager privacy or family notice, and cross-referencing makes the report look like part of the clinical record."}, {"id": 1305, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At the review, a senior leader proposes finding the nurse who left the bed alarm off so she can be disciplined. Kofi recalls that the battery was dead and the unit was short-staffed. Which approach best reflects a root cause analysis?", "options": {"A": "Examine system factors such as checks and staffing, not one person's blame", "B": "Review the resident's care plan and fall risk score, then add a stronger bed alarm", "C": "Focus on individual actions first and review the system only if discipline fails", "D": "Meet the family to explain the fall, then close the review"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Fixes the equipment but overlooks staffing and checks", "C": "Starts with blame, which discourages reporting", "D": "Treats disclosure as the review and skips analysis of causes"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C167", "cluster_text": "Kofi, a PN on a surgical unit, was caring for a resident who fell while walking alone to the bathroom on an evening shift. The resident had a bed alarm, but its battery was dead, and the unit was short-staffed. The manager has called a review of the fall, and Kofi must document, explain safety concepts and help the team learn.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Examine system factors such as checks and staffing, not one person's blame): Root cause analysis seeks the system conditions that allowed the event, such as no battery checks and short staffing, so that changes can prevent recurrence. Starting with individual blame discourages reporting and hides causes. Adding a stronger alarm treats one symptom and leaves checks and staffing unexamined. Telling the family is part of disclosure but does not explain why the event happened."}, {"id": 1306, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Using a diagram of cheese slices with holes that line up, the manager explains the fall. A new graduate says one alarm failure caused it. Kofi is asked to explain the Swiss cheese model to her. What should he say?", "options": {"A": "The model applies only to medication errors, since falls are random events", "B": "Harm occurs when weaknesses in layers of defence line up", "C": "One large hole in a single defence is enough, so only that layer matters", "D": "Each hole stands for one careless worker, so removing those workers closes the holes"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Limits the model to medication errors", "C": "Ignores that multiple failures align", "D": "Turns a system model into individual blame"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C167", "cluster_text": "Kofi, a PN on a surgical unit, was caring for a resident who fell while walking alone to the bathroom on an evening shift. The resident had a bed alarm, but its battery was dead, and the unit was short-staffed. The manager has called a review of the fall, and Kofi must document, explain safety concepts and help the team learn.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Harm occurs when weaknesses in layers of defence line up): The Swiss cheese model shows that accidents result from several imperfect defences failing at once, so the alarm, staffing, checks and rounding all matter and each can be strengthened. It does not assign holes to individuals. A single failure alone would be caught by other layers. The model applies to any adverse event, including falls."}, {"id": 1307, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Reviewers list a nurse who answered the alarm late, a resident who stood without help, and a battery check that was never part of any routine. Which is the best example of a latent condition that the unit should correct first?", "options": {"A": "The resident's decision to stand up without calling for help from staff", "B": "The lack of any routine check of alarm batteries before the event", "C": "The charge nurse's choice to assign her to the far end of the hall", "D": "The nurse who answered the alarm late while she was helping another resident"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Blames the client's action instead of a system weakness", "C": "Selects a visible decision rather than a hidden condition", "D": "Mistakes an active failure for a hidden system flaw"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C167", "cluster_text": "Kofi, a PN on a surgical unit, was caring for a resident who fell while walking alone to the bathroom on an evening shift. The resident had a bed alarm, but its battery was dead, and the unit was short-staffed. The manager has called a review of the fall, and Kofi must document, explain safety concepts and help the team learn.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The lack of any routine check of alarm batteries before the event): Latent conditions are hidden weaknesses in systems that lie dormant until combined with an active failure, and an unchecked battery process is a clear example the unit can fix. The late answer and the resident's action are active events at the time of harm. The assignment choice is also an active decision. Correct system gaps first."}, {"id": 1308, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mrs. Wanjiru, 58, had abdominal surgery this morning. She lies quietly, avoids eye contact and says \"I am fine\" when asked about pain. Her heart rate is 108 and she guards her abdomen when turning. The call light is within reach. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Ask about pain directly with a pain scale and observe her behaviours respectfully", "B": "Assume her quiet manner is cultural and reassess her pain at the next shift change", "C": "Offer a distraction such as music and check again if she asks for help", "D": "Check her incision and dressing, then record pain as controlled if no drainage is seen"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Applies a cultural assumption instead of assessing", "C": "Relies on comfort measures and waits for the client to request help", "D": "Judges pain from wound appearance and ignores guarding and heart rate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask about pain directly with a pain scale and observe her behaviours respectfully): A raised heart rate and guarding suggest pain even though she denies it, so the PN assesses directly with a scale and observation while respecting how she may express discomfort. Offering distraction and waiting leaves her pain unassessed. Judging pain from the dressing ignores the objective signs. Assuming a cultural trait replaces assessment with a stereotype."}, {"id": 1309, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Near a client's room, a hospital worker finds a wet floor with an exposed live wire. Her supervisor says to keep pushing wheelchairs through that hallway because transport is behind schedule. Which principle describes the worker's right to refuse unsafe work?", "options": {"A": "Workers may refuse work they find unsafe, but only after finishing the current shift", "B": "Workers may refuse work they believe is an immediate danger, and must report it", "C": "Workers may refuse only when a union representative has approved the refusal in writing", "D": "Workers may refuse only if the employer agrees that the hazard really exists"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays refusal until shift end despite immediate danger", "C": "Requires union approval that the right does not need", "D": "Makes the right depend on employer agreement"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Workers may refuse work they believe is an immediate danger, and must report it): Workers have a right to refuse work they reasonably believe is an immediate danger, and they must report the hazard so that it can be investigated and corrected. The right does not depend on the employer's agreement. It does not wait until the shift ends or require union approval. Client involvement does not remove it, though the worker must follow the reporting process."}, {"id": 1310, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "After a fall, a man in his mid-forties rates his pain 9 out of 10, yet he laughs with his brother and his vital signs are normal. A colleague at triage says people like him tend to exaggerate. How should the PN interpret his report?", "options": {"A": "Ask him to rate the pain again after his brother leaves and use the lower number", "B": "Assume clients from his background exaggerate and discount the score he gave", "C": "Lower his priority, since calm behaviour and normal vital signs mean his pain is mild", "D": "Accept his report as the main measure and assess further without assuming exaggeration"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pressures the client and favours a lower rating", "B": "Applies a group stereotype to the pain score", "C": "Uses behaviour and normal vitals to override a self-report"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Accept his report as the main measure and assess further without assuming exaggeration): Pain is whatever the client says it is, and normal vital signs and laughter do not rule it out, so the PN believes the report and assesses the injury. Using behaviour to downgrade the score or applying a group stereotype causes biased and unsafe care. Asking him to re-rate in private and keeping the lower score pressures him instead of assessing."}, {"id": 1311, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At a clinic, a physician asks a PN to perform a procedure that involves a controlled act, which the PN did in a previous job. The clinic has no policy about it, and the regulatory body's standards seem unclear. What should the PN do first?", "options": {"A": "Perform it, since the physician delegated it and takes responsibility for the outcome", "B": "Check regulatory standards and legal authority, then obtain employer authorization", "C": "Perform it, because she did it before and so has the competence required", "D": "Perform it with another nurse watching, who then shares the accountability for it"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relies on delegation without confirming authority", "C": "Equates past experience with legal authority", "D": "Assumes an observer takes joint accountability"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Check regulatory standards and legal authority, then obtain employer authorization): Controlled acts are authorized only for those with legal authority under legislation, regulatory standards and employer support, and each nurse stays accountable for her own practice. A physician's request does not create authority. Past competence alone is not authorization. An observer does not transfer accountability. Confirm authority before acting."}, {"id": 1312, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Vomiting and watery stools have affected a 2-year-old for two days. His mother says he is drinking, but he cries without tears. The PN notes dry lips, sunken eyes, capillary refill of four seconds and a heart rate of 150. Which approach best judges his hydration?", "options": {"A": "Rely on weight change since his last clinic visit, the best measure", "B": "Rely on skin turgor, since it is the earliest and most precise sign of fluid loss", "C": "Rely on heart rate, since a fast pulse alone confirms how severe dehydration is", "D": "Combine mucous membranes, tears, perfusion, heart rate and urine output"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses an old weight that may not reflect current fluid status", "B": "Treats one sign as the definitive guide", "C": "Treats one vital sign as the definitive guide"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Combine mucous membranes, tears, perfusion, heart rate and urine output): No single sign is reliable, so the PN combines dry mucous membranes, absent tears, delayed capillary refill, a fast heart rate and urine output, which together point to moderate to significant dehydration and the need to report. An old weight may not match his present state. Heart rate alone has many causes. Turgor alone can mislead."}, {"id": 1313, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Before discharge, a PN teaches a 66-year-old about insulin self-injection, using a demonstration and a handout. He repeats the steps correctly but hesitates when measuring the dose. The PN now wants to chart the teaching for the next shift. Which entry is best?", "options": {"A": "Insulin technique taught by demonstration; steps returned; dose measurement needs reinforcement; follow-up planned", "B": "Discharge teaching completed using handout and verbal instructions according to unit policy", "C": "Teaching done, client understood, no further needs identified at this time", "D": "Client taught insulin injection and handout given to the client and the family"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Records method but not the client's response", "C": "Records only that teaching occurred", "D": "Records resources but omits evaluation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Insulin technique taught by demonstration; steps returned; dose measurement needs reinforcement; follow-up planned): Good documentation of teaching records what was taught, how, the client's response and any follow-up need, and here the hesitation at dose measurement matters. An entry saying only teaching done tells the next nurse nothing and claims understanding that did not occur. Entries that omit the response or the gap cannot guide further teaching."}, {"id": 1314, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Over the sacrum of a 75-year-old resident with intact skin, a PN finds a red area that stays red when pressed with a finger. The resident reports mild tenderness and is incontinent of urine. This finding is consistent with which classification?", "options": {"A": "Stage 3 pressure injury, with full-thickness loss and visible fat in the wound bed", "B": "Stage 1 pressure injury, with intact skin and redness that does not blanch", "C": "Stage 2 pressure injury, with partial-thickness loss and a shallow open ulcer", "D": "Moisture-associated skin damage, with redness that fades once pressure is removed"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Overstates depth when skin is intact", "C": "Confuses intact redness with broken skin", "D": "Confuses non-blanchable redness with blanching redness"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stage 1 pressure injury, with intact skin and redness that does not blanch): Intact skin with redness that does not blanch defines a stage 1 pressure injury. Stage 2 involves loss of the dermis, and stage 3 involves full-thickness loss, neither of which is present. Redness that blanches and fades suggests transient hyperemia or moisture damage rather than a pressure injury. Early recognition lets the team relieve pressure before skin breaks down."}, {"id": 1315, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, a PN finds a 70-year-old with heart failure who has gained 2 kg in two days, has new leg swelling and more breathlessness on stairs. Her oxygen saturation is 93 percent, down from 96. Which action fits the PN's role?", "options": {"A": "Manage independently by raising her diuretic dose according to the weight gain", "B": "Teach fluid restriction and leave a diary, consulting only if saturation drops below 90", "C": "Recognize an unstable change and report to the physician or NP today", "D": "Document the findings and review again at the next scheduled visit next week"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changes a prescribed drug dose outside scope", "B": "Sets her own consult threshold instead of reporting", "D": "Delays reporting a deteriorating client"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recognize an unstable change and report to the physician or NP today): Rapid weight gain, edema and falling saturation suggest worsening heart failure, a changing and unstable situation that the PN reports promptly for orders. Adjusting the diuretic is prescribing, which is outside PN scope. Waiting until the next visit allows decline to continue. Teaching helps, but it should accompany the report, not replace it."}, {"id": 1316, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a ward, a client, 72, has slurred speech and drooling, and she is thirsty. She asks to take her morning pills with water. Her speech cleared slightly an hour ago, and a coworker says one sip is fine. What should the PN do?", "options": {"A": "Keep her NPO, including pills, until a swallow screen is done, and tell the RN", "B": "Give the pills crushed in applesauce, since a thick food is easier to swallow safely", "C": "Let her take her pills with a small sip while sitting fully upright", "D": "Allow thickened water for the pills, since it is safer than thin fluids for her swallow"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assumes thick food is safe without a swallow screen", "C": "Relies on upright positioning instead of a swallow screen", "D": "Chooses a modified fluid before any swallowing screen"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep her NPO, including pills, until a swallow screen is done, and tell the RN): A possible stroke can impair swallowing, so nothing goes by mouth, including pills, until a formal swallowing screen is done. A small improvement in speech does not mean the swallow is safe. Crushed pills in a soft food, upright sips and thickened fluids all expose her airway to risk. Report the request so the team can screen and arrange alternatives."}, {"id": 1317, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Duarte, 72, lies in bed on the second day after hip surgery. His heels are reddened, he sleeps most of the afternoon, and he is on a standard hospital mattress. The PN wants to protect his heels from pressure. Which positioning is best?", "options": {"A": "Use sheepskin on the heels while leaving them resting on the mattress surface", "B": "Place a donut-shaped ring cushion under each heel to relieve the pressure there", "C": "Rub the heels with lotion each shift to improve blood flow to the area", "D": "Float the heels off the mattress with a pillow lengthwise under the calves"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Cushions the heel without removing pressure", "B": "Uses a ring cushion that concentrates pressure", "C": "Massages reddened tissue, which can cause injury"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Float the heels off the mattress with a pillow lengthwise under the calves): Floating the heels with a pillow under the calves removes pressure from the heel completely. Ring cushions create a pressure ring and reduce circulation to the area. Massaging reddened skin can damage fragile tissue. Sheepskin softens contact but does not relieve pressure when the heel still rests on the surface."}, {"id": 1318, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a ward, a client, 68, develops sudden right leg weakness and trouble speaking. A colleague reaches for aspirin from the unit stock, saying it helps strokes. The type of stroke is unknown. Why should the PN not give it?", "options": {"A": "Bleeding cannot be excluded without imaging, so a prescriber must decide", "B": "Unit stock aspirin is too low a dose for stroke, so a larger one is needed", "C": "Aspirin would mask the neurological signs that the team must assess after the event", "D": "Aspirin is for heart attacks, so a stroke needs a different drug"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Focuses on dose strength instead of the bleeding risk", "C": "Claims an unsupported masking effect", "D": "Confuses stroke care with heart attack treatment"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bleeding cannot be excluded without imaging, so a prescriber must decide): Ischemic and hemorrhagic strokes look alike at the bedside, and aspirin given in bleeding stroke can increase harm, so no antiplatelet is given until imaging and a prescriber's order. The issue is not the dose strength. Aspirin is used in some strokes, so the drug is not the problem. Aspirin does not hide neurological signs. Call for help and note the time."}, {"id": 1319, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a home visit, a PN sees a day-2 infant whose skin and eyes look yellow to the knees. He sleeps through feeds and has fewer wet diapers. The mother plans to put the baby by a sunny window. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Report urgently for bilirubin testing and encourage frequent feeds, since yellow legs suggest a high level", "B": "Advise the mother to watch the colour and call if it reaches the feet", "C": "Place the infant in indirect sunlight for twenty minutes daily and recheck in two days", "D": "Encourage longer sleep between feeds so the liver has time to process the pigment"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Waits for jaundice to spread further before acting", "C": "Treats jaundice with sunlight, which is unsafe and ineffective", "D": "Reduces feeding, which slows bilirubin removal in stool"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report urgently for bilirubin testing and encourage frequent feeds, since yellow legs suggest a high level): Jaundice within the first days that extends to the legs, with sleepiness and fewer wet diapers, suggests a high bilirubin and needs prompt testing and treatment. Sunlight is not a safe treatment. Waiting for further spread delays care. Longer gaps between feeds reduce milk intake and weaken bilirubin removal in stool."}, {"id": 1320, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Taking furosemide, a client, 78, has an intake of 1,200 mL and a urine output of 2,400 mL over 24 hours. His weight dropped 1.8 kg, and he feels dizzy when standing. The PN reviews the flow sheet. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "Mild fluid deficit that needs no report, since the output is within the expected diuretic range", "B": "Normal balance, since diuretics are meant to increase the amount of urine passed", "C": "Fluid volume excess, since the large urine output means the kidneys are overloaded", "D": "Output above intake with weight loss and dizziness suggests fluid deficit, so report to the RN"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Recognizes the deficit but fails to report it", "B": "Excuses abnormal findings because the drug causes diuresis", "C": "Misreads high output as kidney overload"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Output above intake with weight loss and dizziness suggests fluid deficit, so report to the RN): A negative balance of 1.2 L, a weight loss of 1.8 kg and orthostatic dizziness are consistent with volume depletion from over-diuresis, so the PN reports to the RN. The drug effect explains the output but not why the findings are acceptable. Excess would show weight gain and edema. Large output does not mean overload."}, {"id": 1321, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At school, a 9-year-old tells the PN that her uncle touches her in ways that make her uncomfortable. She begs the PN to keep it secret, and she is calm, with no visible injuries. Her teacher is waiting outside. What should the PN say and do?", "options": {"A": "Explain she cannot keep it secret, listen without leading questions, and report", "B": "Tell her she did the right thing, then wait to see if she discloses again", "C": "Ask the teacher to speak with the uncle so the family can resolve it privately", "D": "Ask detailed questions about each incident so the report includes the full facts"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delays reporting while waiting for more disclosure", "C": "Involves the suspected abuser and risks the child's safety", "D": "Interviews the child, which belongs to trained investigators"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain she cannot keep it secret, listen without leading questions, and report): The PN must be honest that a report is required, receive the disclosure calmly, avoid leading questions, and report right away. Waiting for a second disclosure delays protection. Detailed questioning can taint later interviews. Asking the teacher to approach the uncle alerts the suspect and risks the child's safety."}, {"id": 1322, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Aaron, 39, says at a primary care visit that he drinks six beers most nights and has been late to work. He says it helps him relax and he is not ready to quit. Which PN response best uses motivational interviewing?", "options": {"A": "List the consequences of drinking and ask him to agree to quit this week", "B": "Tell him directly that he is an alcoholic and needs to enter treatment now", "C": "Accept his decision and move on without raising the topic again at later visits", "D": "Ask what he likes and dislikes about drinking, reflect his answers, and ask permission to inform"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pressures commitment before readiness", "B": "Confronts the client, which increases resistance", "C": "Drops the topic and misses a brief intervention"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask what he likes and dislikes about drinking, reflect his answers, and ask permission to inform): Motivational interviewing explores the client's own ambivalence with open questions, reflection and permission, which helps him weigh change at his own pace. Labelling him and demanding treatment raises resistance. Listing consequences and asking for a quit date pushes him past his readiness. Avoiding the topic abandons an opportunity for brief intervention."}, {"id": 1323, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a visit, the shins of Mr. Boateng show flaking and cracked skin. He says he takes a hot bath twice daily to soften it, and he applies a scented lotion only occasionally. Which care plan best addresses his skin condition?", "options": {"A": "Limit all moisturizer, since it can trap moisture and cause the skin to crack", "B": "Use an alcohol-based rub on the shins to reduce skin bacteria on the surface", "C": "Advise short lukewarm baths, patting dry, and fragrance-free moisturizer afterward", "D": "Continue hot baths twice daily and apply a heavy scented lotion before drying off"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withholds moisturizer, worsening dryness", "B": "Uses an alcohol product that dries the skin", "D": "Keeps hot water that strips skin oils"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C166", "cluster_text": "Mr. Kwame Boateng, 78, receives home care visits twice weekly from a PN. He has dry, flaking skin, type 2 diabetes with reduced sensation in his feet, and takes apixaban, an anticoagulant. He has dark brown skin and lives alone. The PN assesses his skin and teaches him how to protect it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise short lukewarm baths, patting dry, and fragrance-free moisturizer afterward): Hot water and fragrance strip natural oils and irritate dry skin, so short lukewarm baths and a fragrance-free moisturizer applied to damp skin help trap moisture. Continuing hot baths keeps the problem going. Alcohol dries and irritates. Moisturizer is the main treatment for xerosis, not a cause of cracks."}, {"id": 1324, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Boateng's forearm has a flap of thin skin lifted by a tape strip, and the area oozes blood because he takes apixaban. The edges are fragile and the flap is still attached. The PN prepares to dress the tear. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Soak the arm in warm water daily to loosen the dressing before each change", "B": "Remove the old adhesive dressing quickly each day to keep the wound clean", "C": "Apply gentle pressure, realign the flap, and cover with a non-adherent dressing marked with removal direction", "D": "Trim away the skin flap and apply a strong adhesive film for firm cover"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Soaks the wound, which can macerate the area", "B": "Strips fragile skin with fast adhesive removal", "D": "Removes viable tissue and uses adhesive on fragile skin"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C166", "cluster_text": "Mr. Kwame Boateng, 78, receives home care visits twice weekly from a PN. He has dry, flaking skin, type 2 diabetes with reduced sensation in his feet, and takes apixaban, an anticoagulant. He has dark brown skin and lives alone. The PN assesses his skin and teaches him how to protect it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Apply gentle pressure, realign the flap, and cover with a non-adherent dressing marked with removal direction): Gentle pressure controls bleeding, and keeping the flap in place gives the best chance of healing, while a non-adherent dressing avoids pulling new skin. Quick adhesive removal and strong adhesives cause more tears. Trimming the flap discards living tissue. Soaking can soften and damage fragile skin and does not help dressing removal as much as using silicone or non-stick materials."}, {"id": 1325, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Over the coccyx, Mr. Boateng shows an area that feels warmer and firmer than surrounding skin, with no obvious redness. He says it is tender when touched. The PN wonders whether pressure damage is beginning, and the visit has just started. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Conclude the skin is intact because no redness shows, and document it as unremarkable.", "B": "Wait for a blister to appear, since that is the first reliable sign in darker skin", "C": "Rely on a photograph taken in room light to decide whether damage exists", "D": "Assess by touch for warmth, firmness and tenderness, comparing it with nearby skin"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Depends on redness, which is hard to see in dark skin", "B": "Waits for late signs before acting", "C": "Uses photography instead of a hands-on assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C166", "cluster_text": "Mr. Kwame Boateng, 78, receives home care visits twice weekly from a PN. He has dry, flaking skin, type 2 diabetes with reduced sensation in his feet, and takes apixaban, an anticoagulant. He has dark brown skin and lives alone. The PN assesses his skin and teaches him how to protect it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess by touch for warmth, firmness and tenderness, comparing it with nearby skin): In darker skin, early pressure injury often appears as temperature change, firmness, swelling, pain or a shift in skin tone rather than visible redness, so touch and comparison with nearby skin are essential. Absence of redness does not mean intact skin. A blister is a late sign. A photograph under room light can miss the same changes."}, {"id": 1326, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Boateng has reduced sensation in both feet and tells the PN he soaks them in hot water each night because they feel cold. His toenails are thick and he lives alone. The PN teaches foot care. Which teaching point is most important?", "options": {"A": "Soak the feet in warm water for twenty minutes, since warmth improves circulation", "B": "Apply moisturizing cream between the toes daily to keep the skin soft", "C": "Cut the thick toenails short and round the corners at home each week", "D": "Check the feet daily, test any water with a thermometer, and avoid long hot soaks"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses soaks without testing the water temperature", "B": "Moisture between the toes promotes fungal infection", "C": "Encourages home nail cutting, which risks injury with neuropathy"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C166", "cluster_text": "Mr. Kwame Boateng, 78, receives home care visits twice weekly from a PN. He has dry, flaking skin, type 2 diabetes with reduced sensation in his feet, and takes apixaban, an anticoagulant. He has dark brown skin and lives alone. The PN assesses his skin and teaches him how to protect it.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Check the feet daily, test any water with a thermometer, and avoid long hot soaks): With neuropathy he cannot feel burns, so daily inspection and thermometer-tested water protect him from injuries he would not notice. Long soaks without testing raise the risk of scald injury. Cream between the toes keeps skin moist and invites infection. Thick nails are better managed by a foot care professional than by home cutting."}, {"id": 1327, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lindqvist uses oxygen at 2 L/min from a concentrator in the living room. Her husband smokes on the back porch and says he will keep the door closed and keep his cigarettes away from the machine. What should the PN advise?", "options": {"A": "Smoke on the porch, but wait ten minutes after finishing before entering the home", "B": "Smoke in the living room only when her oxygen flow is lowered to 1 L/min", "C": "Smoke in another room with the door closed if the oxygen is shut off first", "D": "Smoke well away from the home, change clothing after, and post no-smoking signs"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes a brief delay removes the residual smoke hazard", "B": "Believes a lower flow makes smoking near oxygen safe", "C": "Assumes a closed door and shut-off make smoking safe"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C159", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ingrid Lindqvist, 68, receives home care for a tracheostomy and uses home oxygen. Her husband smokes, and the PN visits regularly and also sees her at a monthly clinic visit. The PN must keep her airway safe, interpret her oxygen readings and respond if equipment fails.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Smoke well away from the home, change clothing after, and post no-smoking signs): Oxygen supports burning, so smoking must be well away from the home and the oxygen equipment, and smoke on clothing and hands can also be a hazard, which is why clothing is changed and signs are posted. A short wait does not remove residue on clothing. Lowering the flow does not stop oxygen from feeding a fire. A closed door does not stop oxygen from leaking into a room."}, {"id": 1328, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At her monthly clinic visit, Mrs. Lindqvist's finger oximeter reads 86 percent. Her fingers are cold and she wears dark nail polish. She speaks in full sentences, is calm and has pink lips, and reports no new symptoms. What should the PN do first?", "options": {"A": "Increase her oxygen flow by 2 L/min and assume the low reading reflects true hypoxemia.", "B": "Accept the reading as accurate and report severe hypoxemia to the physician right away", "C": "Warm the hand, remove the polish or change the site, and recheck", "D": "Ignore the reading since she looks well and move on to the other vital signs"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changes oxygen flow without an order based on a doubtful reading", "B": "Trusts a reading with known interference", "D": "Dismisses the reading without checking causes"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C159", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ingrid Lindqvist, 68, receives home care for a tracheostomy and uses home oxygen. Her husband smokes, and the PN visits regularly and also sees her at a monthly clinic visit. The PN must keep her airway safe, interpret her oxygen readings and respond if equipment fails.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Warm the hand, remove the polish or change the site, and recheck): Cold fingers and dark polish can give a falsely low oximetry reading, and her calm speech and pink colour do not fit severe hypoxemia, so the PN corrects the cause and rechecks while assessing her breathing. Raising oxygen without an order is outside scope. Accepting the number as true can trigger needless alarm. Ignoring it skips verification."}, {"id": 1329, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lindqvist has wet, noisy breathing through her tracheostomy. Her care plan lists suctioning every four hours, though her cough is weak. Secretions are visible at the tube opening, and the last suctioning was ninety minutes ago. When should the PN suction?", "options": {"A": "Suction now, since secretions are present, using sterile technique and brief passes", "B": "Suction only at fixed four-hour intervals, as her care plan states in writing", "C": "Suction after instilling saline, since saline loosens secretions and prevents blockage", "D": "Suction using clean technique with a reused catheter rinsed in water between passes"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Follows a fixed schedule over assessed need", "C": "Uses routine saline instillation, which is not supported", "D": "Reuses a catheter, which breaks aseptic technique"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C159", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ingrid Lindqvist, 68, receives home care for a tracheostomy and uses home oxygen. Her husband smokes, and the PN visits regularly and also sees her at a monthly clinic visit. The PN must keep her airway safe, interpret her oxygen readings and respond if equipment fails.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Suction now, since secretions are present, using sterile technique and brief passes): Tracheostomy suctioning is done when the client needs it, such as noisy breathing or visible secretions, and not only on a clock. Using aseptic technique with hand hygiene and brief passes limits infection and hypoxia. A fixed interval can leave secretions blocking the airway. Routine saline is not recommended, and a reused catheter carries bacteria into the airway."}, {"id": 1330, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Visiting Mrs. Lindqvist, the PN finds her sitting up in distress with her tracheostomy tube partly out of the neck and a high-pitched sound with each breath. Her daughter offers to push the tube back in. Saturation is falling. What should the PN do first?", "options": {"A": "Remove the tube fully and lay her flat to open her airway", "B": "Push the tube back in firmly while her daughter holds her head still", "C": "Reassure her and wait for the saturation to settle before calling for help", "D": "Call 911, stay with her, and give oxygen at the stoma and mouth"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Removes the tube and lays her flat, worsening distress", "B": "Forces a displaced tube, risking a false passage", "C": "Delays emergency help while hypoxia worsens"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C159", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ingrid Lindqvist, 68, receives home care for a tracheostomy and uses home oxygen. Her husband smokes, and the PN visits regularly and also sees her at a monthly clinic visit. The PN must keep her airway safe, interpret her oxygen readings and respond if equipment fails.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Call 911, stay with her, and give oxygen at the stoma and mouth): A displaced tube with falling saturation is an airway emergency, so the PN activates emergency services, stays with the client and gives oxygen at the stoma and mouth while preparing supplies. Forcing a tube in can create a false passage. Laying her flat can worsen breathing. Waiting allows hypoxia to progress."}, {"id": 1331, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Maya, 13, has stopped eating lunch with friends, avoids gym class, and has bruises she cannot explain. Her teacher calls it a normal teenage phase, and her grades have not changed. Maya tells the practical nurse she is fine. What is the best action for the practical nurse to take?", "options": {"A": "Ask her teacher to keep watching and report back if more bruises appear.", "B": "Talk privately with Maya about her safety, and tell the supervising nurse.", "C": "Wait for her grades to fall before acting, since steady marks show she is coping well.", "D": "Tell Maya's parents about the concern first, before speaking with Maya herself."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delegates observation and delays direct assessment", "C": "Using grades as the only marker of mental health", "D": "Bypasses the client and the supervising nurse"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Talk privately with Maya about her safety, and tell the supervising nurse.): Maya's withdrawal, avoided activities, and unexplained bruises together suggest possible bullying or harm, so a private conversation plus reporting to the supervising nurse and school team is best. Asking the teacher to keep watching delays help. Informing her parents first bypasses Maya and the nurse's own assessment. Stable grades do not rule out distress, so waiting delays help."}, {"id": 1332, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ostrowski, 80, is actively dying in hospice. His breathing is loud and gurgling, yet his face looks relaxed and his respiratory rate is 14. His daughter asks whether he is choking. How should the practical nurse interpret this and respond?", "options": {"A": "Raise his oxygen flow to the maximum rate to quiet the sounds and prevent low oxygen.", "B": "Perform deep suctioning right away to clear his airway and ease his daughter's distress.", "C": "Tell his daughter he is choking and call the physician to arrange urgent intubation.", "D": "Explain the noise is pooled secretions, turn him on his side, give ordered medication."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using oxygen to treat a comfort problem", "B": "Deep suctioning that distresses a dying client", "C": "Misreading secretions as choking and escalating care against goals"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain the noise is pooled secretions, turn him on his side, give ordered medication.): Gurgling in a relaxed, actively dying client with a normal rate points to pooled secretions that he cannot clear, not choking. Side-lying position and the ordered anticholinergic reduce the sound, and a calm explanation eases his daughter's fear. Repeated deep suctioning is uncomfortable and rarely helps. Intubation and high oxygen do not fit comfort-focused care."}, {"id": 1333, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Jordan, 15, visits a youth clinic and says nothing matters and everyone would be better off without them. Jordan has slept little for weeks. The practical nurse feels uneasy raising the subject. Which approach best reflects sound knowledge about screening adolescents for suicide risk?", "options": {"A": "Avoid the word suicide and ask whether Jordan has been feeling a little down lately.", "B": "Ask Jordan directly and calmly about suicidal thoughts, since asking does not plant the idea.", "C": "Screen only if Jordan states a plan, since thoughts without a plan carry little risk.", "D": "Use a written mood questionnaire alone and avoid raising the subject in conversation."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing direct questions increase suicide risk", "C": "Waits for a plan before screening", "D": "Substitutes a form for a direct conversation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask Jordan directly and calmly about suicidal thoughts, since asking does not plant the idea.): Jordan's hopeless statement and poor sleep call for a direct, calm question about suicide, because evidence shows asking does not create the thought and opens the way to safety planning. Vague wording lets Jordan sidestep the question. Waiting for a plan delays help. A form alone does not replace a conversation."}, {"id": 1334, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Chaudhry, 82, receives home care and has an order for half of a 25 mg tablet each morning. The tablet has no score line, and arthritis limits her grip. She asks the practical nurse to simply snap it with her fingers. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Snap the tablet by hand along its edge, since any tablet can be halved safely.", "B": "Cut the tablet with a kitchen knife on a clean surface and check the halves.", "C": "Ask the pharmacist for a pre-split tablet or another strength, or a splitter.", "D": "Dissolve the tablet in water and give half of the liquid, since this is easier."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Splitting an unscored tablet inaccurately", "B": "Estimates halves with an improvised cutter", "D": "Splits a dose by dissolving a tablet, which may not mix evenly"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask the pharmacist for a pre-split tablet or another strength, or a splitter.): An unscored tablet may not break evenly, and arthritic hands make accurate splitting unlikely, so the pharmacist can supply a pre-split product or advise on a splitter. Snapping it by hand risks an uneven dose. A knife cut is also uneven, and dissolving an unscored tablet may give an uneven dose in each half."}, {"id": 1335, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Raymond coughs and sounds wet right after an aide gives him thin water from the bedside jug. The aide says nobody explained the thickened-fluid order to her. His oxygen saturation is 94% and he is alert. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Document the cough and explain the thickened-fluid order to the aide before the next meal arrives.", "B": "Ask the speech-language pathologist to reassess his swallow at the next scheduled visit this week.", "C": "Stop all fluids, sit him upright, assess breathing and saturation, and report to the RN.", "D": "Encourage Raymond to cough forcefully and then offer thickened fluid to rinse his mouth afterwards."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Teaching first while the client may still be at risk", "B": "Delaying reporting until a routine visit", "D": "Giving more fluid after a suspected aspiration"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C158", "cluster_text": "Raymond Lefebvre, 67, is on a rehabilitation unit eleven days after a right-sided stroke, with left weakness and mild difficulty swallowing. You are the practical nurse assigned to him, working with an RN partner. His team includes a physiotherapist, an occupational therapist, a speech-language pathologist, and a social worker. Today staff give him differing instructions about transfers and fluids, and his family is confused about who to ask.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop all fluids, sit him upright, assess breathing and saturation, and report to the RN.): Raymond may have aspirated, so the first step is to stop fluids, position him upright, assess breathing and saturation, and report to the RN. Educating the aide matters but follows client safety. Offering more fluid risks further aspiration. Waiting for a routine swallowing reassessment delays recognition of a possible complication."}, {"id": 1336, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Raymond's wife asks the practical nurse to approve a change from thickened fluids to regular fluids because Raymond says he feels fine. His chart lists swallowing recommendations from one team member. Whose assessment determines when his fluid consistency can safely change?", "options": {"A": "The occupational therapist, because feeding skills during meals show that he is ready for regular fluids.", "B": "The physician, by ordering regular fluids after hearing that Raymond feels fine.", "C": "The speech-language pathologist, through a formal swallowing reassessment shared with the whole team.", "D": "The practical nurse, based on bedside observation of several uneventful drinks taken by Raymond today."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confuses feeding function with swallowing safety", "B": "Accepts the client's report in place of a formal swallow reassessment", "D": "Replacing a specialist assessment with nursing observation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C158", "cluster_text": "Raymond Lefebvre, 67, is on a rehabilitation unit eleven days after a right-sided stroke, with left weakness and mild difficulty swallowing. You are the practical nurse assigned to him, working with an RN partner. His team includes a physiotherapist, an occupational therapist, a speech-language pathologist, and a social worker. Today staff give him differing instructions about transfers and fluids, and his family is confused about who to ask.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The speech-language pathologist, through a formal swallowing reassessment shared with the whole team.): Swallowing safety is assessed by the speech-language pathologist, whose recommendations guide the whole team and the thickened-fluid order. Nursing observations of drinks are useful to report but do not replace a formal reassessment. Feeding skills and swallowing safety are separate functions. A fluid order based only on how Raymond feels skips the reassessment."}, {"id": 1337, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At huddle the occupational therapist says she assumed nursing handled bathing transfers, while the physiotherapist assumed she did. Raymond's morning care has been delayed twice this week. The practical nurse wants to prevent further confusion. What is the best step?", "options": {"A": "Propose the team agree on and record who does each transfer and task.", "B": "Ask the occupational therapist to take over bathing transfers.", "C": "Write a reminder note for the bedside and ask staff to follow it.", "D": "Wait for the manager to assign tasks, since discussions about roles belong to supervisors only."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reassigns the task to one person without team agreement", "C": "Posts an informal note instead of a recorded team agreement", "D": "Passing responsibility for role clarity to management"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C158", "cluster_text": "Raymond Lefebvre, 67, is on a rehabilitation unit eleven days after a right-sided stroke, with left weakness and mild difficulty swallowing. You are the practical nurse assigned to him, working with an RN partner. His team includes a physiotherapist, an occupational therapist, a speech-language pathologist, and a social worker. Today staff give him differing instructions about transfers and fluids, and his family is confused about who to ask.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Propose the team agree on and record who does each transfer and task.): Delays arose from unclear roles, so the team should agree on and record who performs each task in the shared care plan. Handing the task to one therapist without agreement repeats the confusion. A bedside note is not a team agreement. Waiting for the manager delays a solution the team can build together."}, {"id": 1338, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Raymond's wife is tired of repeating his daily routine to six different staff members. A goals meeting is planned for Thursday. She asks whether this repetition is necessary. How can the practical nurse best use the team approach to respond to her concern?", "options": {"A": "Tell her the repeated questions help staff check that her answers stay consistent.", "B": "Advise her to write her answers down and give a copy to each staff member.", "C": "Acknowledge her frustration, pass her input to the team, and invite her to the meeting.", "D": "Suggest she answer only the first staff member each day and refer the others to them."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Justifies duplicate history taking as a consistency check", "B": "Shifting a team coordination task onto the family", "D": "Limits the family's role in sharing information with the team"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C158", "cluster_text": "Raymond Lefebvre, 67, is on a rehabilitation unit eleven days after a right-sided stroke, with left weakness and mild difficulty swallowing. You are the practical nurse assigned to him, working with an RN partner. His team includes a physiotherapist, an occupational therapist, a speech-language pathologist, and a social worker. Today staff give him differing instructions about transfers and fluids, and his family is confused about who to ask.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acknowledge her frustration, pass her input to the team, and invite her to the meeting.): A shared plan reduces repeated questions, so acknowledging her frustration, relaying her input, and inviting her to the goals meeting uses the team approach and values her role. Calling repeats a consistency check ignores the shared record. Asking her to refer others to one person leaves the team uncoordinated. Asking her to copy answers for everyone adds burden."}, {"id": 1339, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "The occupational therapist's note reads: stand-pivot transfer, one-person assist, gait belt, guard the weak left side. Tonight, near the end of a long day, Raymond reports being unusually tired and a bit unsteady on his feet. How should the practical nurse apply this note?", "options": {"A": "Switch to a mechanical lift on your own judgement because fatigue makes the written plan unsafe.", "B": "Ask Raymond to choose his preferred transfer method, since the therapist's note is only a suggestion.", "C": "Assess his strength and alertness first, use the documented method if safe, and tell the RN.", "D": "Use the documented method without assessing him, since the therapist already judged the method safe."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing a plan without consulting the team", "B": "Letting the client override a professional plan", "D": "Following the plan without reassessing current condition"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C158", "cluster_text": "Raymond Lefebvre, 67, is on a rehabilitation unit eleven days after a right-sided stroke, with left weakness and mild difficulty swallowing. You are the practical nurse assigned to him, working with an RN partner. His team includes a physiotherapist, an occupational therapist, a speech-language pathologist, and a social worker. Today staff give him differing instructions about transfers and fluids, and his family is confused about who to ask.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess his strength and alertness first, use the documented method if safe, and tell the RN.): A care plan reflects past assessment, so tonight's tiredness needs a quick check of strength and alertness before choosing to follow the plan, with a report to the RN if he seems worse. Changing to a lift alone bypasses the team. Letting him choose ignores professional input. Using the method without assessing him ignores his changed condition."}, {"id": 1340, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Priya, 38, attends a community clinic for new diabetes teaching. She rubs her temples, says anxiety kept her awake, and asks the practical nurse to write everything down. She missed the last two sessions. What is the best way to plan her teaching?", "options": {"A": "Give her the program booklet to read at home and book a visit next month.", "B": "Give her the complete printed program and review every topic in one long visit this week.", "C": "Explore what makes learning hard, then give short sessions with written summaries and follow-up.", "D": "Refer her to a mental health program first and pause diabetes teaching until it ends."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Defers teaching and relies on written material alone", "B": "Overloading a distracted, tired learner with all topics at once", "D": "Pauses essential diabetes teaching pending separate treatment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explore what makes learning hard, then give short sessions with written summaries and follow-up.): Priya's fatigue and anxiety are barriers to learning, so exploring them and adapting teaching with short sessions, written summaries, and planned follow-up fits her needs. A booklet and a long wait leave her without support. Pausing teaching leaves her without needed diabetes knowledge. A long full-program visit overwhelms a tired, anxious learner."}, {"id": 1341, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Dalia, a practical nurse on her third consecutive night shift, notices at 0430 that she misread a medication label twice and caught it each time. She has had two coffees and feels alert. The unit is short-staffed. How should she interpret this and act?", "options": {"A": "Recognize the near misses as fatigue risk, tell the charge nurse, and ask for a check.", "B": "Switch to double checking only the labels for insulin and opioids, then finish the shift.", "C": "Ask a coworker to double check her labels informally, without telling the charge nurse.", "D": "Request a nap at 0500 and then resume the full medication round on her own afterwards."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Narrows safety checks and does not report the fatigue risk", "C": "Uses an informal check without reporting the fatigue risk", "D": "Handling fatigue alone without informing the team"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Recognize the near misses as fatigue risk, tell the charge nurse, and ask for a check.): Two misread labels at the end of a run of night shifts show fatigue is affecting her accuracy, so telling the charge nurse and arranging a safety check protects clients. Checking only some drugs leaves other errors possible. An informal check without reporting leaves the risk unmanaged. Caffeine does not reverse impaired judgement."}, {"id": 1342, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Marcus has vomited twice and had loose stools since 0300. At 0630 he feels much better, and his unit is short-staffed during a gastroenteritis outbreak among residents. Which statement best reflects infection prevention principles for his return to resident care?", "options": {"A": "Return after eating a meal without vomiting, since that proves he is no longer infectious.", "B": "Work once symptoms ease, wearing gloves and washing hands carefully before each resident contact today.", "C": "Work if assigned only to residents who already have gastroenteritis, since they are already infected.", "D": "Stay home until symptom-free for the period in policy, usually 48 hours after symptoms stop."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating one meal as proof of recovery", "B": "Believing gloves and hand hygiene cancel infectivity", "C": "Cohorting an ill worker with ill residents"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C160", "cluster_text": "Marcus Reid is a practical nurse on a 40-bed long-term care unit, where several residents are ill with gastroenteritis. He started work three weeks ago. During the week he faces a series of occupational health events, each described in the questions that follow. The unit has an infection prevention and control nurse and an occupational health service, and Marcus wants to protect residents and himself.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stay home until symptom-free for the period in policy, usually 48 hours after symptoms stop.): Staff with vomiting and diarrhea can shed organisms after symptoms ease, so they stay off work until symptom-free for the facility period, usually 48 hours. Gloves and hand hygiene reduce spread but do not remove the risk. Working only with ill residents still exposes them to new organisms. One meal does not prove he is no longer infectious."}, {"id": 1343, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At hire, Marcus's occupational health record shows incomplete hepatitis B doses, no recent influenza vaccine, and unknown measles immunity. He says he will get vaccinated only after an exposure happens. How should this plan be interpreted from an infection prevention view?", "options": {"A": "Start the hepatitis B series now and defer other vaccines until after an exposure.", "B": "Accept his report of childhood illness as proof of immunity to measles and hepatitis B.", "C": "Complete vaccines and confirm immunity before exposure, since protection must exist before infectious contact.", "D": "Delay vaccines until the outbreak ends, because immunization during active illness reduces how well they work."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Partly immunizes and delays other protection", "B": "Accepts self-report instead of records or confirmed immunity", "D": "Postponing vaccines for an incorrect reason"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C160", "cluster_text": "Marcus Reid is a practical nurse on a 40-bed long-term care unit, where several residents are ill with gastroenteritis. He started work three weeks ago. During the week he faces a series of occupational health events, each described in the questions that follow. The unit has an infection prevention and control nurse and an occupational health service, and Marcus wants to protect residents and himself.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Complete vaccines and confirm immunity before exposure, since protection must exist before infectious contact.): Immunization works by preparing the body in advance, so Marcus needs completed vaccines and confirmed immunity before he has contact with infectious clients. Deferring some vaccines until after exposure leaves a gap in protection. A personal report of past illness does not confirm immunity. Active illness among residents is not a reason to delay his own vaccines."}, {"id": 1344, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Marcus gets a needlestick from a resident known to be hepatitis B surface antigen positive. He believes he is immune from vaccine doses years ago but never had antibody testing. He washed the site with soap and water. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Ask the resident's physician to order prophylaxis for him without informing occupational health.", "B": "Report to occupational health at once for blood testing of his immune status and prophylaxis.", "C": "Assume the earlier vaccine doses protect him and note the event in the incident file.", "D": "Wait six months and then request a hepatitis B antibody test at his annual review."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Bypassing the occupational health process", "C": "Assuming immunity without verifying it", "D": "Delaying testing past the time-sensitive window"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C160", "cluster_text": "Marcus Reid is a practical nurse on a 40-bed long-term care unit, where several residents are ill with gastroenteritis. He started work three weeks ago. During the week he faces a series of occupational health events, each described in the questions that follow. The unit has an infection prevention and control nurse and an occupational health service, and Marcus wants to protect residents and himself.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report to occupational health at once for blood testing of his immune status and prophylaxis.): Hepatitis B prophylaxis is time-sensitive and depends on his immune status, which is unknown, so immediate reporting to occupational health allows testing and a decision on treatment. Assuming past vaccination protects him skips verification. Waiting six months loses the treatment window. The resident's physician cannot manage the nurse's follow-up outside occupational health."}, {"id": 1345, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two weeks ago Marcus cared for a resident later confirmed to have active pulmonary tuberculosis, and no respirator was used. Marcus has no cough or fever today. Occupational health asks him to attend screening. How should Marcus interpret his lack of symptoms?", "options": {"A": "It shows he was not infected, so screening can be skipped unless he later develops symptoms.", "B": "It confirms latent infection, so he should start preventive medication at once without any testing.", "C": "It means screening can wait until he develops a cough that lasts for several weeks.", "D": "It does not rule out infection, so he should complete the screening and follow-up as scheduled."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating no symptoms as proof of no infection", "B": "Starting treatment without testing", "C": "Waiting for symptoms before screening"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C160", "cluster_text": "Marcus Reid is a practical nurse on a 40-bed long-term care unit, where several residents are ill with gastroenteritis. He started work three weeks ago. During the week he faces a series of occupational health events, each described in the questions that follow. The unit has an infection prevention and control nurse and an occupational health service, and Marcus wants to protect residents and himself.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It does not rule out infection, so he should complete the screening and follow-up as scheduled.): Infection after tuberculosis exposure can be present without symptoms, so screening and follow-up are needed even when he feels well. Skipping screening because he feels well misses silent infection. Waiting for a cough delays detection. Preventive medication is started only after testing and assessment, not because symptoms are absent."}, {"id": 1346, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Kai, 3, weighs 14 kg and is ordered an oral antibiotic at 15 mg/kg. The calculation gives 4.2 mL, and the oral syringe measures in 0.1 mL steps. A colleague says to round up to 5 mL to be safe. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Draw up 4.5 mL, halfway between the two values, to balance underdosing against overdosing risks.", "B": "Measure 4.2 mL, which the syringe can deliver, and have a second nurse verify the calculation.", "C": "Round down to 4 mL because smaller doses are safer for a young child.", "D": "Round up to 5 mL so the child is sure to receive a full therapeutic dose."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing an arbitrary midpoint volume", "C": "Rounding down and underdosing a child", "D": "Rounding up beyond what the measuring device requires"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Measure 4.2 mL, which the syringe can deliver, and have a second nurse verify the calculation.): The syringe measures to 0.1 mL, so the exact calculated volume of 4.2 mL can be given and rounding adds error, especially in a small child. Independent verification supports safety. Rounding to 5 mL overdoses by almost a fifth. Rounding to 4 mL underdoses, and a midpoint of 4.5 mL matches no calculated dose."}, {"id": 1347, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sana, 29, has cystic fibrosis and reports greasy, foul stools and weight loss despite taking her pancreatic enzyme capsules. She opens them and stirs the beads into hot oatmeal, then eats slowly over an hour. What should the practical nurse correct?", "options": {"A": "Hot food inactivates the beads, so mix them into cool soft food at meal start.", "B": "She should take the capsules intact and chew them well so the beads mix with food.", "C": "She should add a second capsule after the meal if her stools stay greasy for long.", "D": "The beads should be mixed into fruit juice and sipped slowly before the meal begins."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Chewing the beads damages their protective coating", "C": "Adjusting the enzyme dose without a prescriber order", "D": "Takes the enzymes before the meal instead of with food"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hot food inactivates the beads, so mix them into cool soft food at meal start.): Pancreatic enzyme beads are heat sensitive and must act with food, so mixing them with a cool soft food at the start of the meal restores digestion of fat. Chewing damages the coating that protects the enzymes. Taking them before the meal leaves no food to digest. Adding capsules on her own is outside the prescribed dose and needs prescriber input."}, {"id": 1348, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At a public health clinic, a practical nurse immunizes clients under a medical directive. A walk-in adult, 52, wants a vaccine the directive lists, but reports fever today and a severe reaction to a previous dose. What must the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Check him against the directive's criteria, then consult the authorizing practitioner first.", "B": "Vaccinate him but observe him for thirty minutes and record the fever as a precaution.", "C": "Vaccinate with a different site and a smaller needle to reduce the reaction risk.", "D": "Give the vaccine, since the directive authorizes immunizing any walk-in adult who asks for it."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Vaccinates despite a contraindication, using observation as the safeguard", "C": "Alters technique instead of addressing the contraindication", "D": "Applying a directive without client assessment"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check him against the directive's criteria, then consult the authorizing practitioner first.): A directive authorizes immunization only when the client meets its criteria, and fever with a prior severe reaction raises a contraindication, so the nurse assesses and consults the authorizing practitioner. Vaccinating anyone ignores the criteria. Observation after the dose does not remove the contraindication. Changing site or needle does not address it either."}, {"id": 1349, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Hanna, 79, had his urinary catheter removed this morning. He has voided 40 mL three times in two hours and reports lower abdominal fullness. He is eating well and walking in the hall. Which finding best suggests urinary retention with overflow?", "options": {"A": "Frequent small voids with a distended bladder, or a high residual on bladder scan.", "B": "Rising temperature with flank pain and cloudy urine, suggesting a kidney infection.", "C": "Hesitancy with a normal stream and a post-void residual under 50 mL.", "D": "Burning on urination and cloudy urine without any abdominal fullness or lower belly discomfort."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Mistakes kidney infection signs for retention", "C": "Misses that a low residual shows adequate emptying", "D": "Mistaking infection signs for retention"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Frequent small voids with a distended bladder, or a high residual on bladder scan.): Retention with overflow shows as frequent small voids because the bladder stays full, with a palpable distended bladder or a high post-void residual on scanning. A low residual shows the bladder empties. Burning and cloudy urine suggest infection. Fever with flank pain points to a kidney infection, not retained urine."}, {"id": 1350, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Eli Navarro, 19, arrives in the emergency department with fever, severe headache, a stiff neck, and a rash that does not blanch when pressed. He is seated among other clients while tests are arranged. Which action reflects what the practical nurse recalls about suspected bacterial meningitis?", "options": {"A": "Have him wear a mask while seated, with isolation started after the culture results return.", "B": "Start droplet precautions at once and notify the RN before diagnosis is confirmed.", "C": "Keep him in the waiting area until the lumbar puncture confirms the diagnosis of meningitis.", "D": "Apply contact precautions only, since the rash suggests that spread occurs through skin touch."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying isolation until laboratory results", "C": "Waiting for a diagnosis before isolating", "D": "Choosing the wrong precaution type"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C161", "cluster_text": "You are the practical nurse on a busy neurological and medical unit that also receives overflow clients from the emergency department and covers an attached long-term care wing. During this shift you assist an RN partner with clients who have neurological presentations, including infection, confusion, and safety concerns. You follow infection control policy and each client's care plan. Each question describes a different client or event.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Start droplet precautions at once and notify the RN before diagnosis is confirmed.): Fever, stiff neck, and a non-blanching rash suggest meningococcal meningitis, which spreads by droplets, so droplet precautions begin at once and the RN is told. Waiting for the lumbar puncture exposes others. Contact precautions alone do not match droplet spread. A mask on a seated client without isolation still delays proper protection."}, {"id": 1351, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Wu, 84, was admitted with a urinary tract infection and has been confused since last night. This morning she is drowsy, misnames her daughter, and her attention fluctuates. Her daughter reports mild memory loss for years. What should the practical nurse conclude and do?", "options": {"A": "Document progression of her dementia and plan reorientation as the main nursing intervention for her.", "B": "Suspect delirium as a sudden change from baseline, and report promptly to the RN.", "C": "Suspect depression and arrange a mood review before looking for any physical causes of confusion.", "D": "Record normal aging changes and recheck her confusion at the next scheduled assessment time."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming a sudden change is dementia", "C": "Attributing acute confusion to depression first", "D": "Dismissing acute confusion as aging"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C161", "cluster_text": "You are the practical nurse on a busy neurological and medical unit that also receives overflow clients from the emergency department and covers an attached long-term care wing. During this shift you assist an RN partner with clients who have neurological presentations, including infection, confusion, and safety concerns. You follow infection control policy and each client's care plan. Each question describes a different client or event.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suspect delirium as a sudden change from baseline, and report promptly to the RN.): Sudden onset, fluctuating attention, and drowsiness in a client with an infection point to delirium superimposed on baseline memory loss, which is a medical urgency requiring a prompt report to the RN. Dementia progresses slowly over months or years. Aging does not cause abrupt confusion. Depression does not explain fluctuating attention and drowsiness."}, {"id": 1352, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Fabian, 80, lives in long-term care and repeatedly says he must collect his children from school. He becomes loud when told they are grown, but calms when staff speak softly. The practical nurse wants to reduce his agitation. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Ask him several questions about the date and place to orient him to his surroundings.", "B": "Tell him that someone else is collecting the children, then leave him alone in his room.", "C": "Acknowledge his worry, speak calmly, and redirect him to a pleasant activity.", "D": "Remind him each time that his children are adults so that he comes to accept reality."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using reality testing that increases agitation", "B": "Deceiving and then abandoning the client", "D": "Correcting repeatedly and escalating distress"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C161", "cluster_text": "You are the practical nurse on a busy neurological and medical unit that also receives overflow clients from the emergency department and covers an attached long-term care wing. During this shift you assist an RN partner with clients who have neurological presentations, including infection, confusion, and safety concerns. You follow infection control policy and each client's care plan. Each question describes a different client or event.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acknowledge his worry, speak calmly, and redirect him to a pleasant activity.): Mr. Fabian becomes distressed when corrected and settles with a calm voice, so acknowledging his feelings and redirecting him respects his reality and lowers agitation. Repeated correction renews the distress. Orientation questions can feel like a test. Telling him a made-up reassurance and leaving does not address the underlying worry."}, {"id": 1353, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Kowalczuk, 78, has dementia and was found twice at dusk at the unit exit, pulling the handle and saying she is late for work. A coworker suggests a lap belt on her chair to keep her safe. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Apply the lap belt with a physician's verbal agreement and release it once per shift.", "B": "Use least restrictive measures such as an alarm and activities, and tell the RN", "C": "Move her bed near the exit so that staff can observe her much more easily.", "D": "Lock her in her room during dusk hours until she settles for the night each evening."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using physical restraint for wandering", "C": "Increasing exit-seeking cues by moving closer", "D": "Using seclusion instead of safer alternatives"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C161", "cluster_text": "You are the practical nurse on a busy neurological and medical unit that also receives overflow clients from the emergency department and covers an attached long-term care wing. During this shift you assist an RN partner with clients who have neurological presentations, including infection, confusion, and safety concerns. You follow infection control policy and each client's care plan. Each question describes a different client or event.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use least restrictive measures such as an alarm and activities, and tell the RN): Exit-seeking at dusk calls for least restrictive measures such as an alarm and meaningful evening activity, with the pattern reported to the RN for care planning. A lap belt is a restraint, which needs strict criteria and is not a first response. Locking her in is seclusion. Placing her near the exit invites more attempts to leave."}, {"id": 1354, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Dubois, 77, is dying in hospice, cannot swallow, and has dry cracked lips with a coated tongue. She coughs when moved. Her daughter asks whether she may give sips of water for her mother's thirst. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Offer small sips of water by spoon, since comfort outweighs the small risk of aspiration here.", "B": "Give frequent gentle mouth care with moist swabs, and explain why sips are unsafe.", "C": "Hold mouth care so that she is not disturbed while resting peacefully at end of life.", "D": "Place ice chips in her mouth regularly while she lies flat to rest in her bed."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving oral fluids to a client who cannot swallow", "C": "Withholding mouth care that provides comfort at end of life", "D": "Offering ice chips that increase aspiration risk"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give frequent gentle mouth care with moist swabs, and explain why sips are unsafe.): Mrs. Dubois cannot swallow, so fluids or ice could be aspirated, while frequent gentle mouth care relieves dryness and gives her daughter a safe way to help. Spoon sips carry a real aspiration risk. Ice chips lying flat carry the same risk. Skipping mouth care leaves her mouth dry."}, {"id": 1355, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Greg, 44, tells the community clinic nurse he knows drinking harms his health but is not ready to stop, and lists reasons on both sides. He declines an action plan. At which stage is he, and which nursing action fits?", "options": {"A": "Precontemplation; give him information on harms and urge him to see a counsellor right now.", "B": "Preparation; help him set a quit date and write a detailed step-by-step plan together today.", "C": "Contemplation; explore his mixed feelings and support his own reasons without pushing a plan.", "D": "Action; praise his progress so far and review strategies to prevent a return to drinking."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pushing a client who already sees the problem", "B": "Giving an action plan to a client not ready", "D": "Treating no change as already underway"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Contemplation; explore his mixed feelings and support his own reasons without pushing a plan.): Greg recognizes the problem and weighs both sides without committing, which is contemplation, so the nurse explores his ambivalence and strengthens his own reasons. A quit date and written plan suit preparation. Praising progress fits action, which he has not begun. Urging a counsellor now ignores that he already sees the harm and is ambivalent."}, {"id": 1356, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Arlene's urinary catheter was removed after surgery. Eight hours later she has not voided, reports lower abdominal discomfort, and a bladder scan shows 650 mL. A colleague suggests waiting until tomorrow morning. Which understanding best guides the practical nurse's action?", "options": {"A": "Offer a warm sitz bath and fluids for an hour, then rescan before reporting.", "B": "Retention needs prompt reporting, so notify the RN and obtain orders for catheterization.", "C": "Retention after anesthesia usually resolves overnight, so recheck her the next morning before acting further.", "D": "Insert a catheter using sterile technique to drain the bladder and tell the RN afterwards."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays reporting while trying comfort measures", "C": "Waiting too long with a full bladder", "D": "Catheterizes without an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C162", "cluster_text": "Arlene Beaulieu, 58, had an open abdominal hysterectomy yesterday morning and is on a surgical unit. She has a patient-controlled analgesia pump, an intravenous infusion, and a nasogastric tube to low intermittent suction. You are the practical nurse caring for her over the next two days, working closely with the RN partner on your unit and following the surgeon's postoperative orders.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Retention needs prompt reporting, so notify the RN and obtain orders for catheterization.): A scan of 650 mL eight hours after surgery with discomfort confirms retention that needs prompt reporting and an order for catheterization. Waiting overnight risks bladder overdistension. Comfort measures first delay reporting. Catheterizing before an order is outside the plan of care."}, {"id": 1357, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On day 2 Arlene's abdomen is distended and firm, bowel sounds are absent in all quadrants, and she feels nauseated. She asks for her breakfast tray because she is hungry. Her nasogastric tube is on low suction and her temperature is 37.4 C. What is the best action for the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Encourage hot tea and walking in the hall while reporting the findings at morning rounds.", "B": "Offer a light solid meal to stimulate bowel movement and relieve the nausea she describes.", "C": "Insert a rectal suppository to start peristalsis, then reassess her abdomen again at noon.", "D": "Keep her on nothing by mouth, tell the RN, and monitor distension, pain, and tube output."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying report of a significant finding", "B": "Feeding a client with possible ileus", "C": "Giving a treatment without an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C162", "cluster_text": "Arlene Beaulieu, 58, had an open abdominal hysterectomy yesterday morning and is on a surgical unit. She has a patient-controlled analgesia pump, an intravenous infusion, and a nasogastric tube to low intermittent suction. You are the practical nurse caring for her over the next two days, working closely with the RN partner on your unit and following the surgeon's postoperative orders.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep her on nothing by mouth, tell the RN, and monitor distension, pain, and tube output.): Distension, absent bowel sounds, and nausea on day 2 suggest postoperative ileus, so she stays nothing by mouth and the RN is told while the nurse monitors her abdomen and tube output. Solid food can worsen vomiting and distension. Waiting until rounds delays care. A suppository needs an order and does not treat ileus."}, {"id": 1358, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Arlene's nasogastric tube to low intermittent suction drained 40 mL in four hours, down from 150 mL, and she is now more nauseated with a firm abdomen. The pump is running. The practical nurse suspects a blockage. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Irrigate with sterile water so that electrolytes are not added to the contents of the stomach.", "B": "Irrigate with tap water, since saline is meant only for flushing wounds and surgical incisions.", "C": "Remove the tube and insert a new one without telling the RN about the blockage.", "D": "Check for kinks and suction, then irrigate with the ordered amount of normal saline if blocked."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing sterile water over saline", "B": "Using water that washes out electrolytes", "C": "Removing a tube without orders or reporting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C162", "cluster_text": "Arlene Beaulieu, 58, had an open abdominal hysterectomy yesterday morning and is on a surgical unit. She has a patient-controlled analgesia pump, an intravenous infusion, and a nasogastric tube to low intermittent suction. You are the practical nurse caring for her over the next two days, working closely with the RN partner on your unit and following the surgeon's postoperative orders.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Check for kinks and suction, then irrigate with the ordered amount of normal saline if blocked.): Reduced drainage with worsening nausea suggests the tube is blocked, so the nurse checks for kinks and suction, then irrigates with the ordered normal saline, which avoids electrolyte loss that water causes. Tap water is not sterile and removes electrolytes. Sterile water has the same electrolyte problem. Removing the tube without reporting is outside scope and unsafe."}, {"id": 1359, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Arlene walks short distances twice a day after pelvic surgery and says compression stockings are unnecessary because she is already up and about. Her calves are soft and not swollen. Which knowledge best supports the practical nurse's response to her?", "options": {"A": "Walking twice daily removes her clot risk, so compression stockings only add discomfort after her operation", "B": "Clot risk is confined to the first six postoperative hours, so later preventive measures add little", "C": "Stockings, leg exercises, and frequent walking together lower stasis and clot risk after surgery.", "D": "Stockings are required only for clients who are confined to bed for several days after surgery."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing brief walking replaces other measures", "B": "Underestimating the duration of clot risk", "D": "Limiting prevention to bedbound clients"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C162", "cluster_text": "Arlene Beaulieu, 58, had an open abdominal hysterectomy yesterday morning and is on a surgical unit. She has a patient-controlled analgesia pump, an intravenous infusion, and a nasogastric tube to low intermittent suction. You are the practical nurse caring for her over the next two days, working closely with the RN partner on your unit and following the surgeon's postoperative orders.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stockings, leg exercises, and frequent walking together lower stasis and clot risk after surgery.): Clot risk after pelvic surgery lasts days, and short walks alone do not remove venous stasis, so stockings, leg exercises, and frequent activity work together. Two short walks do not eliminate risk. Prevention is not limited to bedbound clients. Risk extends well beyond the first hours after surgery."}, {"id": 1360, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Brian, a practical nurse on rotating shifts, finishes a night shift at 0730 and returns for an evening shift tomorrow. He plans to stay awake all day and sleep that night, since a colleague says it helps adjustment. Which sleep strategy should he choose?", "options": {"A": "Take extra caffeine near the end of the shift to prevent drowsiness while he drives home.", "B": "Sleep soon after the shift in a dark, quiet room, and avoid late caffeine.", "C": "Use a sedating over-the-counter sleep aid before each shift to speed up his adjustment.", "D": "Stay awake until evening, then sleep that night to match the usual daytime schedules of others."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using caffeine to mask fatigue", "C": "Relying on sedatives for scheduling", "D": "Believing sleep deprivation aids adjustment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sleep soon after the shift in a dark, quiet room, and avoid late caffeine.): Sleeping soon after the night shift in a dark, quiet room protects total sleep time, and avoiding late caffeine keeps sleep possible. Staying awake builds sleep debt that raises error risk. Extra caffeine hides fatigue without reversing it. Sedating aids risk next-shift drowsiness and do not fix scheduling."}, {"id": 1361, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Baby Tremblay, 2 hours old, was born at 39 weeks to a mother with insulin-treated diabetes and weighs 4.1 kg. He is sleepy and fed poorly, but he is not jittery. The practical nurse reviews the glucose monitoring plan. How should she apply it?", "options": {"A": "Offer sterile water feeds and recheck his glucose later at the discharge teaching session.", "B": "Delay testing until he shows jitteriness or a weak cry, which are the first reliable signs.", "C": "Check blood glucose at the planned early times, since his risk is high without jitteriness.", "D": "Test only if his temperature falls, since glucose problems follow cooling of the newborn infant."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using sterile water feeds instead of monitoring", "B": "Waiting for symptoms before testing", "D": "Linking testing only to temperature changes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check blood glucose at the planned early times, since his risk is high without jitteriness.): Infants of diabetic mothers are at high risk of hypoglycemia, and signs such as sleepiness and poor feeding can be subtle, so glucose is checked on schedule without waiting for jitteriness. Waiting for symptoms delays detection. Temperature is not the only trigger for testing. Sterile water provides no calories and does not replace monitoring."}, {"id": 1362, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Tom, 45, smokes a pack a day and tells the clinic nurse his cough bothers him but quitting stresses him. When risks are listed, he shrugs and says everyone dies of something. Which statement from the practical nurse reflects motivational interviewing?", "options": {"A": "You say the cough bothers you and quitting feels stressful; what would make change worth it?", "B": "Many people with a cough like yours develop lung disease, so quitting is your decision today.", "C": "I understand it is stressful, but the health risks are too serious to ignore.", "D": "Let me give you a quit date chart and nicotine patch information to start with."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Pressuring a decision through fear", "C": "Offers empathy and then uses persuasion", "D": "Moves to action planning before he is ready"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (You say the cough bothers you and quitting feels stressful; what would make change worth it?): Motivational interviewing uses reflection of the client's own concerns and an open question that draws out his reasons, which fits Tom's mixed feelings. Warnings about worsening cough confront him and invite resistance. Pressing the risks after empathy still persuades. Handing over a quit plan moves to action before he is ready."}, {"id": 1363, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Greco, 52, needs a cleansing enema before a planned procedure in acute care. He is alert and able to turn without help. The practical nurse has the lubricated tube and solution ready at the bedside. Which positioning and technique is best?", "options": {"A": "Right side-lying with the left knee flexed, tube inserted about 15 cm, solution given quickly.", "B": "Prone with hips raised, tube inserted about 15 cm, solution given at a steady fast pace.", "C": "Supine with knees straight, tube inserted about 4 cm, solution given in one quick push.", "D": "Left side-lying with right knee flexed, tube inserted gently about 7 to 10 cm, slow flow."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using the wrong side for colon anatomy", "B": "Inserting too deep and giving too fast", "C": "Inserting too short and using a poor position"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Left side-lying with right knee flexed, tube inserted gently about 7 to 10 cm, slow flow.): Left side-lying follows the sigmoid colon anatomy so fluid flows by gravity, a lubricated tube inserted about 7 to 10 cm is safe for an adult, and slow instillation limits cramping. Right side-lying works against the colon's curve. A short insertion supine is ineffective and uncomfortable. Deep insertion and fast flow raise the risk of injury."}, {"id": 1364, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lund, 76, is ordered 14 units of rapid-acting insulin with a 100-unit syringe whose barrel lines mark every 2 units. The practical nurse has the syringe at eye level and a second nurse waits to verify. Where should the dose be read?", "options": {"A": "Line up the 14-unit mark with the first ring of the black stopper nearest the needle.", "B": "Line up the 14-unit mark with the back ring of the stopper nearest the barrel flange.", "C": "Line up the 14-unit mark with the middle of the stopper after tapping out air bubbles.", "D": "Line up the 14-unit mark with the tip of the cone-shaped end of the plunger stopper."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reading from the wrong ring", "C": "Reading from the stopper middle", "D": "Reading from the cone tip"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Line up the 14-unit mark with the first ring of the black stopper nearest the needle.): The accepted reading point on an insulin syringe is the first ring of the black stopper nearest the needle, read at eye level, and the independent check confirms the dose. The cone-shaped tip lies past the ring and overstates volume. The back ring reads too little. The middle of the stopper is not a calibration mark."}, {"id": 1365, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Bernard, 54, newly diagnosed with type 2 diabetes, is angry about changing his habits and believes medication is the only real treatment. His A1C is 7.8% and he works a desk job. What is the best teaching priority for the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Delay teaching about lifestyle until his A1C has stayed high for six months on treatment.", "B": "Explain that lifestyle changes add little to medication, so he should focus on taking tablets daily.", "C": "Explain that diet should control his glucose well enough that medication is not needed later.", "D": "Explain that nutrition changes, activity, and weight loss can lower glucose and may cut medication needs."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Postponing teaching without reason", "B": "Dismissing lifestyle as ineffective", "C": "Promising lifestyle will replace medication"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C163", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse floating between a community diabetes clinic and an emergency department. You work with an RN and follow the written protocols for hypoglycemia and hyperglycemia at both sites. Each question describes a different client you meet during the day, and you must decide how to act within practical nurse scope using only the information given in that question.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that nutrition changes, activity, and weight loss can lower glucose and may cut medication needs.): Lifestyle changes lower glucose and body weight, which can reduce medication needs, and Bernard's sedentary job gives clear targets to work on. Telling him lifestyle adds little reinforces his belief. Promising diet will remove the need for medication overstates what it can do. Delaying teaching wastes the most useful time to build habits."}, {"id": 1366, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Kelsey, 33, uses insulin and is sweating, shaky, and irritable at the clinic. She is alert and swallows well. Her meter reads 3.1 mmol/L, and she asks for a large lunch right now. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Give 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate like juice, recheck in 15 minutes, then give a snack.", "B": "Give 15 g of carbohydrate and recheck her glucose in an hour before acting any further.", "C": "Give a chocolate bar, since its fat slows absorption and prevents a later drop in glucose.", "D": "Give a full meal with protein and fat now to sustain her glucose over the afternoon."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Rechecking too late after treatment", "C": "Choosing a fatty food that delays correction", "D": "Starting with a slow-absorbing meal"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C163", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse floating between a community diabetes clinic and an emergency department. You work with an RN and follow the written protocols for hypoglycemia and hyperglycemia at both sites. Each question describes a different client you meet during the day, and you must decide how to act within practical nurse scope using only the information given in that question.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate like juice, recheck in 15 minutes, then give a snack.): Kelsey has symptomatic hypoglycemia and can swallow, so 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate raises glucose quickly, with a recheck in 15 minutes and a snack once it recovers. A full meal absorbs too slowly for the first step. Chocolate's fat delays absorption. Waiting an hour to recheck leaves her at risk if the treatment failed."}, {"id": 1367, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the emergency department, Mr. Reyes, 61, who takes insulin, is brought in unresponsive with a glucose of 1.8 mmol/L. His wife says he skipped lunch and had a long walk. Which understanding guides the practical nurse in assisting care?", "options": {"A": "Give a small amount of sweetened fluid by syringe into his cheek, then reassess.", "B": "He cannot swallow safely, so intravenous dextrose or intramuscular glucagon is given as ordered.", "C": "Treatment should wait until the physician confirms his glucose with a laboratory sample from a vein.", "D": "Start intravenous saline and recheck the glucose after one litre has infused."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives oral sugar to an unresponsive client", "C": "Delaying treatment of severe hypoglycemia", "D": "Uses saline instead of dextrose for severe hypoglycemia"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C163", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse floating between a community diabetes clinic and an emergency department. You work with an RN and follow the written protocols for hypoglycemia and hyperglycemia at both sites. Each question describes a different client you meet during the day, and you must decide how to act within practical nurse scope using only the information given in that question.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (He cannot swallow safely, so intravenous dextrose or intramuscular glucagon is given as ordered.): An unresponsive client cannot protect his airway, so parenteral treatment with dextrose or glucagon is ordered and the airway is watched. Oral fluids risk aspiration. Saline does not raise glucose. Waiting for a laboratory sample delays rescue from a glucose that threatens life."}, {"id": 1368, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ana, 22, has type 1 diabetes and arrives with deep rapid breathing, fruity breath, abdominal pain, and a glucose of 28 mmol/L. She stopped her insulin yesterday because she was vomiting and could not eat. What does the practical nurse interpret and anticipate?", "options": {"A": "Probable diabetic ketoacidosis; expect insulin and intravenous fluids, since insulin is needed even when not eating.", "B": "Hypoglycemia from vomiting; expect glucose gel and withholding of insulin until she is eating again.", "C": "Simple dehydration; expect oral fluids and a delay in insulin until her intake improves again.", "D": "Anxiety hyperventilation; expect reassurance and breathing coaching with no changes made to her insulin."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Mistaking hyperglycemia for hypoglycemia", "C": "Underestimating ketoacidosis as dehydration", "D": "Attributing Kussmaul breathing to anxiety"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C163", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse floating between a community diabetes clinic and an emergency department. You work with an RN and follow the written protocols for hypoglycemia and hyperglycemia at both sites. Each question describes a different client you meet during the day, and you must decide how to act within practical nurse scope using only the information given in that question.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Probable diabetic ketoacidosis; expect insulin and intravenous fluids, since insulin is needed even when not eating.): Deep rapid breathing, fruity breath, abdominal pain, and high glucose after stopping insulin indicate ketoacidosis, which needs insulin and fluids even when she is not eating. Her glucose is high, so treating hypoglycemia is wrong. Dehydration alone does not explain the ketone breath. Anxiety does not cause this breathing pattern."}, {"id": 1369, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Sutherland, 70, is ordered 6 units of regular insulin and 18 units of NPH insulin in the same syringe. The practical nurse has cleaned both vials and injected air into each. Which drawing sequence is correct for this order?", "options": {"A": "Draw up the regular insulin first, then the NPH, to avoid contaminating the regular vial.", "B": "Draw up each insulin in a separate syringe, since mixing different types is not permitted.", "C": "Draw up the larger NPH dose first, then add regular insulin to reach the total volume.", "D": "Draw up the NPH first, then the regular, so that the cloudy insulin is not diluted."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believing mixing is never allowed", "C": "Ordering by dose size", "D": "Reversing the clear-to-cloudy order"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Draw up the regular insulin first, then the NPH, to avoid contaminating the regular vial.): The rule is clear before cloudy, so regular insulin is drawn first and NPH second, preventing NPH from contaminating the regular vial. Drawing NPH first risks carrying it into the clear vial. Ordering by dose size has no basis. Regular and NPH can be mixed in one syringe when ordered, so separate injections are not required."}, {"id": 1370, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "The clinic's fax machine is down, and the physician asks you to send a client's lab results today by regular email to a personal address. The results contain the client's name and diagnosis. The physician says it is urgent. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Use an approved secure method or telephone the information, following the privacy policy.", "B": "Photograph the page with your personal phone and message the picture to the physician.", "C": "Send the results by regular email after the physician confirms that the address is correct.", "D": "Send them by regular email but leave the diagnosis out of the body of the message."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using a personal device for client records", "C": "Using unsecured email for health information", "D": "Believing partial removal makes email secure"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C157", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a busy surgical unit in a hospital. During this shift several privacy and safety events occur involving client information and a medication incident. You follow the hospital privacy policy and your regulatory body's standards. Each question describes a separate event or a later development, and you decide what a practical nurse should do, considering accountability, confidentiality, and honest communication.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use an approved secure method or telephone the information, following the privacy policy.): Health information must travel by an approved secure method or be given by telephone, so urgency does not justify unsecured channels. Confirming the address does not make regular email secure. Leaving out the diagnosis still sends identifiable data. Personal phone photographs store client information on an unapproved device."}, {"id": 1371, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At shift end, handover slips with client names and room numbers are left on the desk. A colleague drops them into the regular recycling bin, saying they are small and contain no diagnoses. Which statement reflects the practical nurse's responsibility?", "options": {"A": "Cross out the names with a pen and recycle the slips, leaving room numbers.", "B": "Regular recycling is acceptable when slips contain no diagnoses or treatment details about any client.", "C": "Place them in the confidential shredding bin, since names and room numbers still identify clients.", "D": "Leave them locked in the desk drawer so the next shift can shred them."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Partial redaction leaves identifiers", "B": "Thinking identifiers without diagnoses are not private", "D": "Delays secure disposal and leaves information accessible"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C157", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a busy surgical unit in a hospital. During this shift several privacy and safety events occur involving client information and a medication incident. You follow the hospital privacy policy and your regulatory body's standards. Each question describes a separate event or a later development, and you decide what a practical nurse should do, considering accountability, confidentiality, and honest communication.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Place them in the confidential shredding bin, since names and room numbers still identify clients.): A name with a room number identifies a client, so any paper holding it goes into confidential shredding. The absence of a diagnosis does not make the slip safe to recycle. A pen mark leaves room numbers and can be read through. Leaving slips in a drawer delays secure disposal."}, {"id": 1372, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A fax holding a client's results was sent to the wrong clinic number. The receiving clinic phoned the unit to report it. The client has not complained, and a coworker says nothing more is needed unless the client asks. Which knowledge of breach response applies?", "options": {"A": "No action is needed when the recipient of the fax is another health care provider.", "B": "Wait to act until the client notices or complains about the misdirected fax.", "C": "Ask the receiving clinic to shred the fax and note the call in the chart.", "D": "Report it promptly to the manager or privacy officer for containment and notification."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a breach to a provider as harmless", "B": "Waiting for client complaint", "C": "Handling a breach informally without reporting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C157", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a busy surgical unit in a hospital. During this shift several privacy and safety events occur involving client information and a medication incident. You follow the hospital privacy policy and your regulatory body's standards. Each question describes a separate event or a later development, and you decide what a practical nurse should do, considering accountability, confidentiality, and honest communication.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report it promptly to the manager or privacy officer for containment and notification.): A misdirected fax is a privacy breach, so it is reported at once to the manager or privacy officer who direct containment, retrieval, and notification of the client. Being a health care provider does not exempt the recipient from privacy duties. Waiting for a complaint delays response. Informal shredding alone skips required reporting and notification."}, {"id": 1373, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A practical nurse gave Mr. Tran, 63, his morning medication twice because the first dose was not charted. He is stable. The RN says to wait for the physician to decide whether to tell him. What is the correct principle?", "options": {"A": "Disclosure waits until the incident review is finished and the cause of the error is known.", "B": "Disclosure is needed only when the error caused harm that staff members can actually see.", "C": "The client has the right to be told about the error promptly, with team support.", "D": "Disclosure is the physician's decision, so nurses should avoid telling clients about errors that occur."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying disclosure until review ends", "B": "Disclosing only when harm is obvious", "D": "Leaving disclosure entirely to the physician"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C157", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a busy surgical unit in a hospital. During this shift several privacy and safety events occur involving client information and a medication incident. You follow the hospital privacy policy and your regulatory body's standards. Each question describes a separate event or a later development, and you decide what a practical nurse should do, considering accountability, confidentiality, and honest communication.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The client has the right to be told about the error promptly, with team support.): Clients are entitled to prompt, honest disclosure of errors that affect their care, even without harm, and the team coordinates how it is done. Avoiding the conversation breaches that right. Limiting disclosure to visible harm hides minor errors. Waiting for the review denies Mr. Tran timely information."}, {"id": 1374, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After the double dose, a colleague suggests charting that the day nurse failed to document the first dose. The practical nurse had also given the second dose without checking the medication record. How should the practical nurse document the event?", "options": {"A": "Write a late entry stating the first dose was probably given, to match the second.", "B": "Chart that the day nurse omitted documentation, since this explains why the dose was repeated twice.", "C": "Report the event verbally to the manager and leave the charting to her.", "D": "Chart objective facts, the client's condition, and who was notified, without blame or speculation."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Documents an assumption as fact", "B": "Assigning blame in the record", "C": "Delegates documentation of her own event to another"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C157", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a busy surgical unit in a hospital. During this shift several privacy and safety events occur involving client information and a medication incident. You follow the hospital privacy policy and your regulatory body's standards. Each question describes a separate event or a later development, and you decide what a practical nurse should do, considering accountability, confidentiality, and honest communication.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chart objective facts, the client's condition, and who was notified, without blame or speculation.): Documentation should state facts, the client's condition, and who was informed, without blaming others, because the record is a legal document. Charting blame is subjective and ignores her own part. A late entry built on an assumption falsifies the record. Leaving charting to the manager makes the record incomplete."}, {"id": 1375, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A newborn girl, 30 minutes old, in the nursery has a respiratory rate of 72, nasal flaring, and grunting with each exhalation. She is pink but has mild sternal retractions. A colleague says grunting just clears mucus. How should the practical nurse interpret this?", "options": {"A": "Mucus clearing; lay her flat in the crib and recheck her breathing after her next feed.", "B": "Signs of respiratory distress; tell the RN promptly and keep monitoring saturation.", "C": "Normal transition breathing; wipe her mouth and recheck her rate again in about an hour.", "D": "Early hunger cues; offer a feed and watch her breathing while she sucks on the nipple."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Interpreting grunting as mucus clearing", "C": "Interpreting distress as normal transition", "D": "Mistaking respiratory distress for hunger"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Signs of respiratory distress; tell the RN promptly and keep monitoring saturation.): A rate of 72 with flaring, grunting, and retractions are signs of respiratory distress in a newborn, so the RN is notified promptly and saturation is monitored. Normal transition does not include these signs together. Mucus does not explain retractions and grunting. Feeding a baby in distress increases the risk of aspiration."}, {"id": 1376, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Kerr, 71, has COPD and an order for oxygen at 1 to 2 L/min with a target saturation of 88 to 92%. He is drowsy, and his saturation reads 98% after a visitor turned the flow to 6 L/min. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Turn the oxygen off and recheck his saturation on room air after an hour has passed.", "B": "Leave the flow as it is, since higher saturation is safer in lung disease.", "C": "Wake him and encourage deep breathing while keeping the higher oxygen flow going for now.", "D": "Reduce the flow to the ordered range, assess his breathing, and tell the RN."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withdrawing oxygen contrary to the order", "B": "Believing more oxygen is always safer", "C": "Continuing excess oxygen while hypoventilation risk persists"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C165", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse rotating between a medical unit and a community health office. You give and monitor medications under orders and report concerns to the RN partner on duty. Each question describes a separate client and a medication issue, and you must decide what to do within practical nurse scope, using only the facts given in that question.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduce the flow to the ordered range, assess his breathing, and tell the RN.): Oxygen is a drug, and excess flow can suppress breathing in a client with COPD, so the flow returns to the ordered range while breathing and consciousness are assessed and the RN is told. Leaving high flow risks respiratory depression. Turning it off ignores the order. Encouraging deep breaths at high flow does not remove the risk."}, {"id": 1377, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Odum, 34, began a 10-day course of amoxicillin for a sinus infection. On day 3 she feels well and wants to stop and keep the remaining tablets for later. Which explanation should the practical nurse give her about this plan today?", "options": {"A": "Finish the full course, since stopping early can let surviving bacteria cause relapse or resistance.", "B": "Switch to one tablet daily instead of the prescribed schedule until symptoms clear.", "C": "Stop now and restart the course only if her fever returns within the next week.", "D": "Stop at day 7, since most sinus infections clear within a week of treatment."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reduces the dose without an order", "C": "Stopping and restarting on a symptom basis", "D": "Stops early using a general rule instead of the prescribed course"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C165", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse rotating between a medical unit and a community health office. You give and monitor medications under orders and report concerns to the RN partner on duty. Each question describes a separate client and a medication issue, and you must decide what to do within practical nurse scope, using only the facts given in that question.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Finish the full course, since stopping early can let surviving bacteria cause relapse or resistance.): Feeling better on day 3 does not mean all the bacteria are gone, so finishing the course prevents relapse and limits resistance. Changing the schedule without an order gives low doses. Stopping at a general time point leaves the course incomplete. Restarting after a fever repeats the same risk."}, {"id": 1378, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Paul, 45, reports a rash with penicillin years ago, and the physician orders a cephalosporin for his cellulitis. The practical nurse reviews his allergy record before giving the first dose on the ward. Which action fits best in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Refuse all antibiotics and mark the order as cancelled until his allergy is formally tested.", "B": "Give it, since cephalosporins and penicillins are unrelated drug classes with no shared structure.", "C": "Clarify the reaction with him, report the details to the prescriber, and watch for any reaction.", "D": "Give it at a lower dose than ordered to reduce the chance of a rash."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Cancelling the antibiotic order instead of clarifying the reaction", "B": "Ignoring possible cross-reactivity between the two drug classes", "D": "Reducing a dose without a prescriber's order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C165", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse rotating between a medical unit and a community health office. You give and monitor medications under orders and report concerns to the RN partner on duty. Each question describes a separate client and a medication issue, and you must decide what to do within practical nurse scope, using only the facts given in that question.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Clarify the reaction with him, report the details to the prescriber, and watch for any reaction.): Penicillins and cephalosporins share a related structure, so cross-reaction is possible, and clarifying his reaction, informing the prescriber, and watching him protect him while treating the infection. Calling the classes unrelated ignores that risk. Cancelling all antibiotics leaves cellulitis untreated. Reducing the dose without an order is outside scope."}, {"id": 1379, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Allard, 68, receives intravenous gentamicin every 8 hours, and a trough level is ordered before the 1400 dose. At 1350 the lab says the sample will be taken at 1420. How should the practical nurse proceed with the level and the dose?", "options": {"A": "Skip the level since the 1400 dose cannot wait for the lab to collect the sample.", "B": "Give the dose on time, then send the level whenever the lab arrives.", "C": "Draw the trough just before the 1400 dose, then give the dose and tell the RN.", "D": "Give the dose and then draw the sample 30 minutes after the infusion has finished running."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Omitting an ordered level", "B": "Sampling after dosing instead of before", "D": "Drawing a peak rather than trough level"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C165", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse rotating between a medical unit and a community health office. You give and monitor medications under orders and report concerns to the RN partner on duty. Each question describes a separate client and a medication issue, and you must decide what to do within practical nurse scope, using only the facts given in that question.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Draw the trough just before the 1400 dose, then give the dose and tell the RN.): A trough level reflects the lowest concentration, so it is drawn just before the next dose, then the dose is given and the RN informed of the lab delay. Drawing after the dose gives a falsely high result. A level 30 minutes after infusion is a peak. Skipping the level removes the check that guards against kidney and hearing toxicity."}, {"id": 1380, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Brandt, 85, lives in long-term care and has constipation, with an order for a rectal glycerin suppository. She is alert and can turn to her left side. The practical nurse prepares to give it. Which technique is best for this client?", "options": {"A": "Insert it only to the first centimetre so it melts near the anal opening.", "B": "Insert it past the sphincter and press it against the rectal wall, not into stool.", "C": "Insert it with her lying on her back with both legs extended straight out in front.", "D": "Push it deep into the stool mass so it dissolves where the stool is lodged."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Inserts only to the first centimetre so it is expelled", "C": "Positions her supine instead of left side-lying for insertion", "D": "Places the suppository inside stool so it cannot absorb"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Insert it past the sphincter and press it against the rectal wall, not into stool.): A suppository must contact the rectal wall to be absorbed and stimulate the bowel, so it is inserted past the sphincter against the wall with her in a left side-lying position. Embedding it in stool prevents absorption. Shallow placement allows it to be expelled. Lying supine with legs extended makes insertion harder and uncomfortable."}, {"id": 1381, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The laboratory phones the unit at 1030 with Mr. Rahimi's potassium of 6.8 mmol/L. He is lying quietly, reports mild muscle weakness, and his apical pulse is irregular at 54 beats per minute. His breakfast tray is half eaten. What should you do first?", "options": {"A": "Encourage extra oral fluids to dilute the potassium and recheck the level tomorrow.", "B": "Hold his morning potassium supplement and recheck his vital signs after the physician rounds.", "C": "Document the value and tell the physician when rounds begin later this morning.", "D": "Report the result and irregular pulse to the RN now, then keep monitoring him closely."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Tries to treat the potassium independently without an order", "B": "Holds the supplement, a sound step, but delays reporting a dangerous value", "C": "Saves a critical value for scheduled rounds instead of reporting at once"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C164", "cluster_text": "Hamid Rahimi, 55, is admitted to a medical unit with weakness and a recent fall. He has heart failure and chronic kidney disease and takes furosemide, ramipril, and a potassium supplement. You are the practical nurse assigned to him on a day shift. The charge nurse is in a meeting, and the physician is not expected to round for about three hours.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the result and irregular pulse to the RN now, then keep monitoring him closely.): A potassium of 6.8 mmol/L with weakness and a slow, irregular pulse signals risk of a dangerous heart rhythm, so the RN or physician must hear it now. Waiting for rounds leaves him unmonitored for hours while the level may climb. Holding the supplement is sensible, but it cannot replace prompt reporting, and no order lets you treat the level alone. Critical values are reported at once."}, {"id": 1382, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "By afternoon, Mr. Rahimi's potassium has fallen to 5.2 mmol/L after treatment. His hemoglobin on three consecutive days was 124, 112, and 103 g/L, and today's value is still above the unit's critical limit. He denies any bleeding. How should you use these hemoglobin results when planning his care?", "options": {"A": "Take no action because the latest value remains above the critical reporting limit.", "B": "Report the downward trend to the RN and ask about blood loss.", "C": "Repeat the test right away because three different results suggest a laboratory error.", "D": "Document the trend in the chart and wait for the next scheduled blood work."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Looks only at the latest value and ignores the trend", "C": "Assumes a falling trend is a laboratory error", "D": "Records the trend but fails to report it for follow-up"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C164", "cluster_text": "Hamid Rahimi, 55, is admitted to a medical unit with weakness and a recent fall. He has heart failure and chronic kidney disease and takes furosemide, ramipril, and a potassium supplement. You are the practical nurse assigned to him on a day shift. The charge nurse is in a meeting, and the physician is not expected to round for about three hours.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the downward trend to the RN and ask about blood loss.): A fall of about 20 g/L over three days is a trend that needs assessment for hidden blood loss, even though no single value is critical yet. Judging only today's number misses the pattern the team needs to see. Repeating the test without reporting delays the search for a cause. Report trends as well as critical values."}, {"id": 1383, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "That evening Mr. Rahimi is alert and chatting, with warm dry skin and no thirst or confusion. The unit meter reads a glucose of 21.4 mmol/L, but he peeled an orange a few minutes ago and did not wash his hands. Which action best reflects interpreting results alongside how the client looks?", "options": {"A": "Give a correction dose of rapid-acting insulin using the sliding scale for 21.4 mmol/L", "B": "Assume the meter is faulty, document the result as an error, and skip any repeat testing", "C": "Wash his hands, repeat the fingertip test, and compare the new result with how he looks.", "D": "Treat the number as accurate and report a glucose of 21.4 mmol/L as an emergency."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives insulin without an order based on a doubtful value", "B": "Blames the meter without repeating the test", "D": "Treats the number only without checking the client's condition"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C164", "cluster_text": "Hamid Rahimi, 55, is admitted to a medical unit with weakness and a recent fall. He has heart failure and chronic kidney disease and takes furosemide, ramipril, and a potassium supplement. You are the practical nurse assigned to him on a day shift. The charge nurse is in a meeting, and the physician is not expected to round for about three hours.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wash his hands, repeat the fingertip test, and compare the new result with how he looks.): Sugar from the orange on his fingertip can falsely raise a fingertip reading, and his appearance does not match a glucose of 21.4 mmol/L. Clean hands and a repeat test settle the question before anyone acts. Reporting an emergency or giving insulin treats an unverified number, and insulin needs an order. Compare every result with the client."}, {"id": 1384, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The next morning Mr. Rahimi has a new cough and a chest x-ray is ordered. He is 55, walks independently, and wears a necklace and a hospital gown with metal snaps. His oxygen saturation is 95 percent. Which preparation is best before he goes to radiology?", "options": {"A": "Remove the necklace and chest metal, and follow radiology staff direction on shielding.", "B": "Leave the cardiac monitor leads on, since they will not show on a chest film.", "C": "Keep him fasting from midnight because chest imaging needs an empty stomach.", "D": "Remove only the necklace and leave the gown snaps, since snaps are too small to matter."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Overlooks that monitor leads create artifacts", "C": "Applies abdominal imaging fasting rules to a chest film", "D": "Removes some metal but leaves snaps that create artifacts"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C164", "cluster_text": "Hamid Rahimi, 55, is admitted to a medical unit with weakness and a recent fall. He has heart failure and chronic kidney disease and takes furosemide, ramipril, and a potassium supplement. You are the practical nurse assigned to him on a day shift. The charge nurse is in a meeting, and the physician is not expected to round for about three hours.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Remove the necklace and chest metal, and follow radiology staff direction on shielding.): Metal in the beam area can hide lung detail, so the necklace and snaps come off, and radiology staff decide how to shield since a shield can sometimes block the area being imaged. Monitor leads and snaps both show on the film. Fasting is not needed for a chest film. Prepare for the specific test."}, {"id": 1385, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Gordon Pike, 52, was admitted for pneumonia, and his last drink was two days ago. Tonight he has a tremor, sweating, a heart rate of 112, and mild anxiety, but he is oriented and has no seizure history. His wife says he seemed fine yesterday. What do these findings most likely indicate?", "options": {"A": "Anxiety from hospitalization, which reassurance and a quiet room can manage.", "B": "Early alcohol withdrawal that can still progress, so report it and monitor him closely.", "C": "Expected fever response to pneumonia, since withdrawal symptoms appear within the first twenty-four hours.", "D": "Delirium tremens, a late emergency needing rapid response team activation right now."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Attributes physical signs to situational anxiety", "C": "Expects withdrawal symptoms only on the first day", "D": "Jumps to the most severe stage without assessment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Early alcohol withdrawal that can still progress, so report it and monitor him closely.): Tremor, sweating, and a fast heart rate two days after the last drink fit early withdrawal, which can progress to seizures or delirium over the next day or two. Withdrawal does not end after the first twenty-four hours, so pneumonia and anxiety do not explain the whole picture. He is oriented, so delirium tremens is not present yet. Report and watch for progression."}, {"id": 1386, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Maria, 65, on a surgical ward, received a double dose of her morning antibiotic. You assessed her and told the RN, and she is stable. A colleague suggests writing the incident report in her chart so the physician will see it. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Record in the chart that an incident report was completed so the team knows.", "B": "File the report through the facility system and chart only objective facts of the event.", "C": "Skip the report because she is stable and the RN already knows.", "D": "Write the report and give a copy to Maria to keep her informed."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believes the report belongs in the client record", "C": "Thinks no harm means no report is needed", "D": "Gives an internal quality document to the client"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (File the report through the facility system and chart only objective facts of the event.): The incident report is an internal quality tool, so it goes through the facility system and stays out of the client record, while the chart holds objective facts, her assessment, and who was notified. Skipping the report loses the chance to prevent a repeat. A copy for Maria confuses disclosure with the report. Keep the two documents separate."}, {"id": 1387, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "You are visiting Lena, 71, who has type 2 diabetes and lives alone. Her daughter says Lena's speech became slurred 20 minutes ago and her right arm feels weak. Lena is awake but drowsy. You have a glucometer in your bag. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Skip glucose testing to avoid delay and give her juice by mouth.", "B": "Lay her flat and drive her to the hospital yourself since it is close.", "C": "Call 911, check glucose while waiting, note symptom onset time.", "D": "Check her glucose and, if normal, wait an hour to see whether her speech improves."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives oral intake to a drowsy client with possible stroke", "B": "Transports the client personally instead of calling emergency services", "D": "Delays emergency help after a normal glucose result"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Call 911, check glucose while waiting, note symptom onset time.): Sudden slurred speech and arm weakness suggest stroke, and time of onset guides treatment, so emergency help is called first. A quick glucose check is still useful because hypoglycemia can look the same, and it can be done while waiting. Waiting an hour wastes treatment time, and fluids by mouth risk aspiration in a drowsy client."}, {"id": 1388, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Gerald, 78, lives in long-term care and has heart failure. His daughter is upset about swelling in his legs and asks you to give an extra furosemide tablet today, saying his physician once did so. His chart shows only a scheduled dose. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Hold the scheduled dose until the swelling goes down to protect his kidneys.", "B": "Tell the daughter nothing can be done until his next routine appointment.", "C": "Assess his edema, weight, and breathing, then report to the RN or physician for an order.", "D": "Give one extra tablet because his physician once approved doing so and the swelling is obvious."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withholds a scheduled medication without an order", "B": "Dismisses a change in condition and delays reporting", "D": "Adjusts treatment without a current order"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess his edema, weight, and breathing, then report to the RN or physician for an order.): Increased leg swelling in heart failure needs assessment and a report so a prescriber can decide on the dose. A practical nurse cannot change a dose on a past practice or a family request. Holding the scheduled dose is also a change without an order. Work within scope and bring the finding to the team."}, {"id": 1389, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ewa, 80, keeps sliding down in bed on a medical unit, and her sacrum and heels look reddened but intact. The head of her bed has been raised high since lunch so she can watch television. Which factor most directly explains these skin changes?", "options": {"A": "Moisture from perspiration, which softens tissue and breaks down the skin barrier.", "B": "Shear and friction as she slides with the bed head high.", "C": "Direct pressure from the mattress, which a regular turning schedule would resolve.", "D": "Poor circulation from the foot of the bed being lowered too far."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Blames moisture when the stem points to sliding", "C": "Treats pressure as the sole cause and overlooks shear", "D": "Invents a circulation cause tied to bed position"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Shear and friction as she slides with the bed head high.): Sliding down with the head of the bed raised drags the skin over the sheet and pulls deeper tissue, producing shear and friction over the sacrum and heels. Moisture and pressure can add to the risk, but neither explains the sliding. Limiting head elevation and lifting rather than dragging reduces the injury."}, {"id": 1390, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Alvarez, 66, signed the consent form for a procedure this morning. Later he tells you he is unsure what it involves and says his daughter told him to sign. The physician has left the unit. How should you proceed as the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Ask his daughter to explain the procedure and confirm he agrees with it.", "B": "Report his uncertainty to the RN and physician before the procedure, since consent requires understanding.", "C": "Reassure him that the signed form shows consent and proceed as scheduled.", "D": "Explain the procedure and its risks yourself so that he is ready to proceed."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relies on a family member instead of the client's own understanding", "C": "Treats a signature as proof of consent", "D": "Takes over the physician's duty to explain risks"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report his uncertainty to the RN and physician before the procedure, since consent requires understanding.): Consent must be informed and voluntary, and Mr. Alvarez shows neither understanding nor freedom from pressure, so the signed form is not enough. The physician must explain the procedure and risks, so you report the concern to the RN and physician. Explaining risks yourself goes beyond your role, and his daughter cannot stand in for his understanding."}, {"id": 1391, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Tomas, 77, fell beside his bed on a medical unit and has a bruised hip. After assessing him and notifying the RN, you complete the incident report. You are now writing your nursing note for the day shift. Which entry is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Note that the fall happened because staff did not answer his call bell promptly.", "B": "Describe the fall, findings, actions, and notifications, leaving out the report.", "C": "Write that the incident report holds further details about how the fall occurred.", "D": "Add that an incident report was completed and submitted to the manager."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Records blame and an assumed cause", "C": "Points readers to the incident report as a source of detail", "D": "Cross-references the incident report in the chart"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Describe the fall, findings, actions, and notifications, leaving out the report.): The chart should hold facts: what you found, what you did, and whom you told. Mentioning the incident report in the chart can expose an internal quality document and confuse the clinical record. Blame or assumed causes do not belong either. The report carries the analysis, and the chart carries the clinical story."}, {"id": 1392, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Nia, 48, is on postoperative day two after a bowel resection. Her new stoma is pink and moist and drains about 600 mL of loose green-brown liquid over 12 hours. She asks whether this is normal and says the stoma is on her right lower abdomen. How should you interpret her output?", "options": {"A": "Liquid output is expected from an ileostomy, so continue to monitor volume and hydration.", "B": "Abnormal diarrhea suggesting infection, so obtain a stool specimen and notify the physician.", "C": "A sign of obstruction because stool should be formed by now, so hold oral intake.", "D": "Expected from a colostomy, so formed stool should appear within another day."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats normal ileostomy output as infection", "C": "Expects formed stool from the small bowel", "D": "Confuses an ileostomy with a colostomy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Liquid output is expected from an ileostomy, so continue to monitor volume and hydration.): A right lower stoma with loose, green-brown output points to an ileostomy, which drains liquid stool because little water is absorbed beyond the small bowel. Monitoring the volume matters because dehydration is a real risk. Formed stool belongs to a colostomy lower in the colon. Do not treat expected output as an infection or obstruction."}, {"id": 1393, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During his first rounds, Ravi finds Mrs. Okafor, 84, on the floor beside her bed, awake and holding her wrist. Down the hall, another client's IV pump is alarming. Her call light was not on. What should Ravi do first?", "options": {"A": "Stay with her, assess for injury and consciousness, and call for help without moving her.", "B": "Call for help, then leave her to check the alarming pump while help is coming.", "C": "Silence the alarming pump first, since it may mean an interrupted infusion for another client.", "D": "Cover her with a blanket and ask her to rate her pain, then call the RN."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Leaves a fallen client unattended while help comes", "C": "Prioritizes an equipment alarm over an injured client", "D": "Relies on self-report before checking for injury"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C168", "cluster_text": "Ravi, a practical nurse, begins a day shift on a 28-bed medical unit with six assigned clients in different rooms. An RN is the team lead, and one care aide is available. Shift report has just ended. The unit also has a pending admission and a planned discharge, and the emergency department two floors below may call for extra help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stay with her, assess for injury and consciousness, and call for help without moving her.): A client on the floor may have a fracture or head injury, so you stay, assess, and call for help before anything is moved. Leaving her alone risks missed deterioration. Asking about pain before assessing misses injury she may not report. The alarm matters, but a colleague can respond to it while you stay with the person at risk of harm."}, {"id": 1394, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mid-morning, the hospital declares an external disaster after a bus collision and asks the unit to prepare. Ravi's team learns that arriving casualties will be sorted into triage categories. Which principle should guide how the emergency team assigns first priority?", "options": {"A": "Treat children and older adults first since they are more vulnerable to serious harm.", "B": "Treat clients in order of arrival so everyone receives equal consideration and fairness.", "C": "Prioritize life-threatening injuries that have the best chance of survival with prompt care", "D": "Treat the most severely injured clients first, even when their chance of survival is very low."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses age rather than injury severity to set priority", "B": "Uses arrival order instead of clinical need", "D": "Treats the most severely injured regardless of survival chance"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C168", "cluster_text": "Ravi, a practical nurse, begins a day shift on a 28-bed medical unit with six assigned clients in different rooms. An RN is the team lead, and one care aide is available. Shift report has just ended. The unit also has a pending admission and a planned discharge, and the emergency department two floors below may call for extra help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Prioritize life-threatening injuries that have the best chance of survival with prompt care): Disaster triage aims to save the most lives, so the first priority goes to people whose life-threatening injuries can be treated quickly with a good chance of survival. Treating the most severely injured regardless of outlook uses scarce resources on those least likely to survive. Arrival order and age ignore clinical need."}, {"id": 1395, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "By noon, the unit has a planned discharge, a new admission arriving from the emergency department, and a client whose blood pressure has fallen since morning. Ravi has time for only one task right now. Which should he do first?", "options": {"A": "Give the new admission's due medications first, since orders have arrived.", "B": "Take the blood pressure again in ten minutes before reporting, then start the admission.", "C": "Help transport the new admission since the emergency department needs the stretcher.", "D": "Reassess the client with falling blood pressure and report the findings to the RN."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Prioritizes timed medications of a stable client", "B": "Delays reporting an unstable finding to start other work", "C": "Prioritizes a transport request over a client at risk"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C168", "cluster_text": "Ravi, a practical nurse, begins a day shift on a 28-bed medical unit with six assigned clients in different rooms. An RN is the team lead, and one care aide is available. Shift report has just ended. The unit also has a pending admission and a planned discharge, and the emergency department two floors below may call for extra help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reassess the client with falling blood pressure and report the findings to the RN.): A falling blood pressure may signal bleeding, sepsis, or another change, so the unstable client is assessed and reported first. Medications for a new admission and delayed reporting both postpone care for the client at risk. Discharges and admissions are planned tasks that can wait a short time. The RN can reassign tasks when needed."}, {"id": 1396, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Ravi's report notes list several timed tasks across his six clients, including medications, dressing changes, and glucose checks. He is tempted to rely on memory instead of writing them down. Which practice best supports safe organization for the rest of his shift?", "options": {"A": "Write only the medication times on the whiteboard.", "B": "Memorize the schedule since writing it down takes time away from client care.", "C": "Photograph the assignment sheet at report and check it later.", "D": "Record each task and time on a worksheet and update it."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Records only part of the tasks and leaves the rest to memory", "B": "Relies on memory instead of recording tasks", "C": "Uses an assignment copy that does not record task timing"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C168", "cluster_text": "Ravi, a practical nurse, begins a day shift on a 28-bed medical unit with six assigned clients in different rooms. An RN is the team lead, and one care aide is available. Shift report has just ended. The unit also has a pending admission and a planned discharge, and the emergency department two floors below may call for extra help.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Record each task and time on a worksheet and update it.): A worksheet holds the timed tasks, priorities, and changes in one place, which reduces missed or late care when interruptions come. Memory fails under pressure, and recording only some tasks leaves others unprotected. An assignment photo does not track times or changes. Relying on memory alone is unsafe."}, {"id": 1397, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Hana, an experienced practical nurse on a palliative unit, has felt numb after several client deaths this month. She sleeps poorly, dreads her shifts, and tells you she feels like a weak nurse. Peer support is available on the unit. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Suggest she take extra shifts to regain a sense of purpose and confidence.", "B": "Tell her numbness is expected in palliative care and will fade on its own.", "C": "Advise her to take a leave of absence and avoid talking about the deaths.", "D": "Recognize these as common signs of compassion fatigue and encourage her to use peer support."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats the problem as a lack of purpose that more work will fix", "B": "Normalizes the symptoms and offers no support", "C": "Avoids processing grief instead of using support"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Recognize these as common signs of compassion fatigue and encourage her to use peer support.): Numbness, poor sleep, and dread after repeated losses are typical of compassion fatigue, which affects caring, committed nurses and is not a weakness. Support from peers, counselling, and rest helps. Waiting for it to fade offers no help. Extra shifts add to the strain, and avoiding talk about the deaths blocks processing."}, {"id": 1398, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Edith, 68, attends a clinic six weeks after a hip fracture repair. She says walking her dog around the block again matters more to her than anything else. The practical nurse is helping update her plan of care. Which approach to goal setting is best?", "options": {"A": "Build goals with Edith around walking her dog, using measurable steps she agrees to.", "B": "Set goals from what the nurse considers a safe, realistic recovery for someone her age.", "C": "Set goals with Edith, but limit them to physiotherapy exercises the team selected.", "D": "Ask Edith's family to choose goals that suit her energy level and recovery stage."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Substitutes the nurse's goals for the client's", "C": "Involves the client but restricts goals to clinical priorities", "D": "Delegates goal setting to the family instead of the client"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Build goals with Edith around walking her dog, using measurable steps she agrees to.): Goals work best when they reflect what matters to the client, so walking her dog becomes the target and is broken into measurable steps she agrees to. Nurse-chosen goals reduce motivation. Limiting goals to team exercises ignores what matters to her. Family-chosen goals replace her own voice."}, {"id": 1399, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Agnes, 90, was found on the floor in long-term care. After assessing her and notifying the RN, you write your note. A staff member says the fall happened because the evening aide left her alone. Which entry belongs in the chart?", "options": {"A": "A note that the evening aide left her, as a staff member reported.", "B": "Objective findings, actions taken, and who was notified.", "C": "An opinion that she probably stood up without help because she was confused.", "D": "A summary of why the fall was preventable and needed staffing changes."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Records hearsay about another staff member", "C": "Charts an assumed cause as fact", "D": "Charts analysis that belongs in the incident review"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Objective findings, actions taken, and who was notified.): Charting records what you saw and did, such as findings, her position, interventions, and notifications, in objective terms. Hearsay about another worker, guesses about cause, and conclusions about staffing do not belong in the record and may harm both the client and the nurse. Analysis of causes belongs in the incident review."}, {"id": 1400, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Samir, 45, attends a community clinic and has a BMI of 36. When you mention a walking program, he says he is not interested and sees no problem with his weight. A colleague wants to hand him a detailed meal and exercise plan. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Set firm weight targets and weekly weigh-ins to create accountability right away.", "B": "Give the plan now, since early detail will motivate him to start changing.", "C": "Recognize precontemplation, respect his view, offer information, and invite him to revisit the topic later.", "D": "Document noncompliance and discharge him from the program for lack of readiness."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Imposes targets before he sees a problem", "B": "Gives an action plan to a client in precontemplation", "D": "Labels lack of readiness as noncompliance and discharges him"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recognize precontemplation, respect his view, offer information, and invite him to revisit the topic later.): Samir sees no problem, which marks precontemplation, so the nurse's task is to respect his view and offer information without pressure. Action plans suit clients who are preparing to change. Discharge and firm targets damage trust and are unlikely to help him move toward readiness."}, {"id": 1401, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Suction passes are under way for Mr. Lau, 65, who has a tracheostomy and wet-sounding secretions. During suctioning, his oxygen saturation drops from 95 to 88 percent and he looks anxious. The catheter has been in for about 20 seconds. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Ask him to cough hard with the catheter in place while saturation recovers.", "B": "Pull the catheter out and place him flat to see whether saturation recovers.", "C": "Continue the pass to finish clearing the secretions, then give oxygen once the airway is clear.", "D": "Withdraw the catheter, give oxygen, let him recover, and reassess before suctioning again."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Keeps the catheter in while oxygen falls", "B": "Removes the catheter but delays giving oxygen", "C": "Continues suctioning while oxygen falls"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withdraw the catheter, give oxygen, let him recover, and reassess before suctioning again.): Suctioning removes oxygen along with secretions, so a drop to 88 percent means stop, give oxygen, and let him recover. Passes should be short, usually under 10 to 15 seconds. Leaving the catheter in blocks airflow, and waiting without oxygen delays recovery. Continuing the pass keeps removing oxygen."}, {"id": 1402, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Dube, 82, lives in a care home and has type 2 diabetes. For three days she has had extreme thirst, dry mucous membranes, and drowsiness, and her glucose is 38 mmol/L. There is no abdominal pain or fruity breath. Which interpretation fits best?", "options": {"A": "Normal aging changes with mild dehydration that oral fluids should correct.", "B": "Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state, needing urgent report because dehydration and confusion may worsen.", "C": "Diabetic ketoacidosis, the usual cause of very high glucose with drowsiness in older adults with diabetes.", "D": "Hypoglycemia, which explains the drowsiness and calls for fast-acting sugar by mouth right away."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Attributes serious findings to aging", "C": "Expecting ketoacidosis for very high glucose and drowsiness", "D": "Mistakes very high glucose for low glucose"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state, needing urgent report because dehydration and confusion may worsen.): Extreme thirst, drowsiness, and a glucose of 38 mmol/L without ketone signs suggest hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state, common in older adults with type 2 diabetes, and it needs urgent report. Ketoacidosis usually brings fruity breath and abdominal pain. Sugar would worsen a high glucose, and aging does not explain these findings."}, {"id": 1403, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad shows you his new anticoagulant bottle and says the hospital told him only the drug name. He asks what to do if he cuts himself shaving, and he often takes ibuprofen for sore knees. Which teaching is most important now?", "options": {"A": "Advise a diet high in leafy greens to keep his clotting steady.", "B": "Review the drug name, dose, and schedule, and stress taking it at the same time.", "C": "Teach bleeding precautions, ibuprofen avoidance, and when to seek urgent help.", "D": "Tell him to stop the anticoagulant for a few days if he notices bruising."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applies a warfarin diet rule to a different anticoagulant", "B": "Gives drug name and timing but skips safety teaching", "D": "Advises stopping the drug without a prescriber"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C179", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse making a home visit to the Haddad family. Grandfather Yusuf, 74, was discharged two days ago on a new anticoagulant for atrial fibrillation. His grandson Omar, 9, has asthma. His wife Salma, 70, was recently diagnosed with type 2 diabetes and has started insulin. All three live in the same house, and you will teach each of them.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Teach bleeding precautions, ibuprofen avoidance, and when to seek urgent help.): His questions about cuts and his ibuprofen use point to bleeding risk, so precautions and interacting drugs come first. Name, dose, and timing matter but do not address his risk. A leafy green rule belongs to warfarin, and stopping an anticoagulant without direction could lead to a stroke."}, {"id": 1404, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Omar has been using his inhaler without a spacer and coughs after each puff. His mother says he just presses and breathes in quickly. You have a spare spacer and a practice inhaler with you. What teaching approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Give a written guide and ask Omar to teach the steps back to his mother.", "B": "Show a brief video on inhaler use, then leave the pamphlet with his mother.", "C": "Demonstrate with the spacer, then have Omar and his mother practice while you coach.", "D": "Explain each step aloud and ask Omar's mother to demonstrate at the next visit."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relies on written guidance and child teach-back without observation", "B": "Relies on passive viewing without a return demonstration", "D": "Delays observing technique until a later visit"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C179", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse making a home visit to the Haddad family. Grandfather Yusuf, 74, was discharged two days ago on a new anticoagulant for atrial fibrillation. His grandson Omar, 9, has asthma. His wife Salma, 70, was recently diagnosed with type 2 diabetes and has started insulin. All three live in the same house, and you will teach each of them.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Demonstrate with the spacer, then have Omar and his mother practice while you coach.): Inhaler technique is a skill, so showing it with the spacer and watching the family practice shows whether they can do it. A written guide or spoken explanation does not show whether the technique is right. Waiting for the next visit leaves the coughing and poor delivery in place. Skipping the spacer sends more medication to his throat."}, {"id": 1405, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Salma has been injecting insulin into her abdomen beside the navel every evening because the spot is easy to reach, and the skin there looks slightly lumpy. She asks whether she should keep using it. What should you teach her about injection sites?", "options": {"A": "Keep using the one comfortable spot, because staying in one consistent location gives even absorption.", "B": "Rotate injection sites within an area and avoid lumpy tissue to keep absorption predictable.", "C": "Use one thigh spot for the next several weeks while the abdomen heals.", "D": "Inject at the edge of the lumpy area, since tissue there absorbs insulin faster."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believes using one site is better for absorption", "C": "Uses a single alternate site, still without rotation", "D": "Injects near damaged tissue believing absorption is faster"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C179", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse making a home visit to the Haddad family. Grandfather Yusuf, 74, was discharged two days ago on a new anticoagulant for atrial fibrillation. His grandson Omar, 9, has asthma. His wife Salma, 70, was recently diagnosed with type 2 diabetes and has started insulin. All three live in the same house, and you will teach each of them.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rotate injection sites within an area and avoid lumpy tissue to keep absorption predictable.): Repeated injections in the same spot cause lumpy tissue, and insulin absorbs unpredictably from it, so sites should be rotated within an area and lumps avoided. Using the same spot is the habit that created the problem, and a new single spot repeats it. Injecting beside a lump still uses damaged tissue."}, {"id": 1406, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Salma checks her glucose before meals and at bedtime. Before supper her meter reads 3.4 mmol/L and she feels shaky, sweaty, and a little confused. She asks what to do and when to call the clinic. Which teaching about glucose monitoring is best?", "options": {"A": "Skip supper insulin if the reading is low and eat a larger bedtime snack.", "B": "Retest only when she has symptoms, since numbers matter less than feelings.", "C": "Record every reading in the log and bring the log to her next appointment.", "D": "Treat lows below 4.0 mmol/L as taught, recheck, and call if symptoms persist"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Advises skipping insulin without direction from a prescriber", "B": "Teaches symptom-only testing and ignores the readings", "C": "Teaches logging but not when to call for help"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C179", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse making a home visit to the Haddad family. Grandfather Yusuf, 74, was discharged two days ago on a new anticoagulant for atrial fibrillation. His grandson Omar, 9, has asthma. His wife Salma, 70, was recently diagnosed with type 2 diabetes and has started insulin. All three live in the same house, and you will teach each of them.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat lows below 4.0 mmol/L as taught, recheck, and call if symptoms persist): A reading of 3.4 mmol/L is below the Diabetes Canada low glucose threshold of 4.0 mmol/L, and with shakiness she needs a plan to treat it, recheck, and call when symptoms or low readings continue. A log is useful but does not tell her when to act. Changing doses on her own or testing only with symptoms is unsafe."}, {"id": 1407, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On postoperative day one after abdominal surgery, Mr. Costa, 59, has IV fluids at 100 mL per hour and a surgical drain. His urine output has been 25 mL per hour for four hours, and the drain output is higher than yesterday. How should you use his intake and output record?", "options": {"A": "Reduce the drain output record to a shift total since hourly values add little detail.", "B": "Report the low urine output with the rising drain output and keep recording totals.", "C": "Continue current care because urine under 30 mL per hour is normal after surgery.", "D": "Increase the IV rate yourself to bring his urine output back up."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reduces monitoring when output is changing", "C": "Treats an abnormal output as normal", "D": "Adjusts the IV rate without an order"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the low urine output with the rising drain output and keep recording totals.): Falling urine output with rising drain losses suggests he may be losing volume, so the pattern needs to be reported, and accurate totals help the team decide. Less detailed recording hides changes. Changing the IV rate needs an order, and an hourly output under about 30 mL is a warning sign, not a normal finding."}, {"id": 1408, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Brennan, 81, in long-term care, finished a course of antibiotics five days ago and now has several new watery stools with abdominal cramping. His roommate asks if the cause is contagious. Loperamide is on the medication cart. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Offer extra oral fluids and report only if he develops a fever.", "B": "Use alcohol hand rub after care, since it destroys the spores quickly.", "C": "Give loperamide to slow his bowel so he does not become dehydrated.", "D": "Start contact precautions, report the diarrhea to the RN, and withhold antidiarrheals unless ordered."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays reporting until a fever appears", "B": "Relies on alcohol rub, which does not kill spores", "C": "Gives an antidiarrheal that can worsen possible C. difficile"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Start contact precautions, report the diarrhea to the RN, and withhold antidiarrheals unless ordered.): New watery diarrhea after antibiotics raises concern for Clostridioides difficile, so isolation, soap-and-water hand washing, and a prompt report come first. Antidiarrheals can trap the toxin in the bowel. Alcohol rub does not kill the spores, and waiting for a fever delays testing and treatment."}, {"id": 1409, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Priya, a practical nurse on a medical unit, scanned a client's wristband and the barcode alerted her that the medication was ordered for the client in the next bed. She stopped and prepared the correct one, and no harm occurred. A coworker says nothing needs reporting. What should Priya do?", "options": {"A": "Report it to the pharmacy only, since the scanner is theirs.", "B": "Skip reporting because no harm occurred and the scanner worked as designed.", "C": "Report the near miss so the team can fix the cause.", "D": "Note in the client's chart that the wrong drug was scanned."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses the wrong reporting channel and leaves the unit process unchanged", "B": "Believes only events that cause harm need reporting", "D": "Records a near miss in the client chart instead of an incident report"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the near miss so the team can fix the cause.): Near misses reveal weaknesses, such as a labeling or storage problem, that could harm someone next time, so they are reported through the incident system without blame. A working scanner does not remove the cause. A pharmacy-only report leaves the unit unaware, and a chart comment is not a safety report."}, {"id": 1410, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A medication dose reached Mr. Fernandez, 72, half an hour late because an order was missed. He is stable and asks you what happened. A colleague warns you not to apologize because it might create legal liability. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Explain that an error occurred and name the nurse who was responsible.", "B": "Avoid apologizing and state that the medication has now been given.", "C": "Apologize in detail and promise that this will not happen again to any client.", "D": "Express sincere regret and tell the RN so disclosure can follow."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assigns blame to an individual in disclosure", "B": "Treats an apology as a liability to avoid", "C": "Makes promises that cannot be kept"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Express sincere regret and tell the RN so disclosure can follow.): An honest, sincere apology supports trust and is part of ethical practice, while the RN and facility handle formal disclosure. Silence leaves him with unanswered questions. Naming individuals shifts focus to blame, and promises about the future are not yours to make."}, {"id": 1411, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before lunch, a colleague suggests drawing up three clients' rapid-acting insulin doses now and giving each one at its own meal time to save time. Rapid-acting insulin starts to work in about 15 minutes, and the unit is short staffed today. What is the safest response?", "options": {"A": "Agree, but draw up only the doses for clients whose meals are expected within the hour.", "B": "Decline and give all three doses together at the first meal so the timing matches.", "C": "Prepare and verify each dose near that client's meal, with all required bedside checks.", "D": "Agree, as long as each syringe is labeled with the client's name, dose, and meal time."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepts pre-drawing with a time limit but still skips checks", "B": "Gives insulin to clients before their own meals", "D": "Accepts pre-drawn doses with only a label as a safeguard"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Prepare and verify each dose near that client's meal, with all required bedside checks.): Rapid-acting insulin works quickly and is a high-alert drug, so each dose is prepared and verified close to that client's meal using all the checks. Labeled pre-drawn syringes can still be given to the wrong client or at the wrong time. Giving all doses at the first meal leaves other clients at risk of low glucose."}, {"id": 1412, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mrs. Wu, 68, received a medication that was ordered for another client. She has mild dizziness but stable vital signs. Her daughter, visiting, asks why the nurse looks upset. The risk manager is on call. As the practical nurse involved, what should you do?", "options": {"A": "Tell Mrs. Wu what happened after her dizziness is checked again and settles.", "B": "Give Mrs. Wu a general explanation that a medication was changed and monitor her closely.", "C": "Wait until the risk manager arrives before telling Mrs. Wu or her daughter anything.", "D": "Report to the RN and physician at once and follow the facility process to inform her."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays disclosure until symptoms resolve", "B": "Gives a vague explanation that misstates what occurred", "C": "Delays disclosure until the risk manager is involved"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report to the RN and physician at once and follow the facility process to inform her.): Mrs. Wu is entitled to honest information about harm or risk, and the team must be told immediately so she can be assessed. Disclosure follows the facility process but cannot be postponed until symptoms settle. A vague or inaccurate explanation misleads her. Waiting for the risk manager delays disclosure."}, {"id": 1413, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Daniel, 62, takes rapid-acting insulin with meals on a medical unit. His supper tray is delayed, and the kitchen says it should arrive within 30 minutes. His glucose is 6.1 mmol/L and the insulin is due now. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Hold the insulin, tell the RN, and give it with the tray.", "B": "Offer a snack from the unit fridge, then give the full dose.", "C": "Give the insulin on schedule and let the tray arrive when it does.", "D": "Give half the dose now to avoid a high glucose and half later."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Adds a snack and gives the insulin without checking with the RN", "C": "Gives rapid-acting insulin well before food is present", "D": "Splits the dose without an order"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold the insulin, tell the RN, and give it with the tray.): Rapid-acting insulin peaks within an hour or two, and without food his glucose could fall too low, so the dose waits until the tray is close, and the RN is told about the delay. Giving it now or splitting the dose changes the plan without an order. A snack changes carbohydrate intake and needs a plan."}, {"id": 1414, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Hamza, 37, attends a community clinic for follow-up of depression. He enjoys chatting with the practical nurse and says she feels like a friend. She wants the relationship to stay therapeutic. Which description best defines a therapeutic nurse-client relationship in this setting?", "options": {"A": "A warm friendship in which the nurse shares personal stories to build rapport.", "B": "A relationship of mutual obligation in which both parties share decisions equally.", "C": "A purposeful, professional relationship centred on client needs with firm boundaries.", "D": "A neutral relationship in which the nurse limits contact to tasks and avoids emotions."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Equates friendliness with a therapeutic relationship", "B": "Describes a social relationship with shared obligations", "D": "Confuses professionalism with emotional detachment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A purposeful, professional relationship centred on client needs with firm boundaries.): A therapeutic relationship is purposeful and centred on the client's needs, with trust and professional boundaries. Friendliness helps but friendship blurs boundaries. Therapeutic does not mean cold, since caring and empathy are part of it, and it is not a relationship of mutual obligation."}, {"id": 1415, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Ingram, 44, uses a corticosteroid inhaler daily for asthma. Today she has white patches on her tongue and cheeks that scrape off, plus mild soreness when swallowing. She asks whether she should stop her inhaler. You are the practical nurse in a community clinic. What is your best response?", "options": {"A": "Report findings for assessment and reinforce rinsing after each dose.", "B": "Advise her to stop the inhaler today since it caused the infection.", "C": "Reassure her that the patches are harmless and will clear without treatment.", "D": "Suggest she use her rescue inhaler for daily asthma control instead."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Stops a controller medication without a prescriber", "C": "Dismisses a probable oral infection", "D": "Swaps a controller for a rescue medication"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report findings for assessment and reinforce rinsing after each dose.): White patches that scrape off in a client on an inhaled steroid suggest oral thrush, which needs assessment and an antifungal order. Rinsing after each dose reduces the risk. Stopping the controller can bring on asthma symptoms, and a rescue inhaler does not prevent them."}, {"id": 1416, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Ng tells you he values his family's guidance and would like his son and daughter involved in discussions about his care. His daughter says, \"Dad doesn't need the details, we will decide.\" How should the team approach decision making?", "options": {"A": "Speak only with the family since he has asked them to guide him.", "B": "Let the daughter choose which information he receives to protect him from distress.", "C": "Meet with him alone each time because individual autonomy takes priority over family wishes.", "D": "Honour his wish for family involvement while confirming that he makes the final decisions."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Bypasses the client entirely in favour of the family", "B": "Lets a relative control the information he receives", "C": "Overrides his stated wish for family involvement"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C180", "cluster_text": "Mr. Ng, 82, attends an oncology clinic for the results of a biopsy. His son and daughter came with him. He speaks English well and values his family's guidance in important choices. The practical nurse is helping the team plan communication and care. Cultural safety, consent, and the client's own stated wishes guide the whole plan of care for him.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Honour his wish for family involvement while confirming that he makes the final decisions.): Mr. Ng wants his family involved, so the team includes them, but he remains the decision maker and receives the information he wants. Excluding him ignores his autonomy, and excluding his family ignores his values. The daughter cannot decide what he hears."}, {"id": 1417, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Before the results meeting, Mr. Ng quietly asks that his children hear the diagnosis first and then decide what he is told. He shows no cognitive concerns. The physician asks you what he said. How should this request be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "As a wish the team should follow, without discussing it with him or the family.", "B": "As a request he must make in writing before the team can change who is told.", "C": "As his informed preference about how information is shared, which the team should clarify and respect.", "D": "As pressure from his family that should be set aside until he is alone."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepts the request without clarifying it with the client", "B": "Imposes a written requirement not needed for a preference", "D": "Assumes family pressure without evidence"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C180", "cluster_text": "Mr. Ng, 82, attends an oncology clinic for the results of a biopsy. His son and daughter came with him. He speaks English well and values his family's guidance in important choices. The practical nurse is helping the team plan communication and care. Cultural safety, consent, and the client's own stated wishes guide the whole plan of care for him.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (As his informed preference about how information is shared, which the team should clarify and respect.): A capable client may choose how and when information is shared, so Mr. Ng's request is a preference to be clarified with him and respected, while he keeps the right to ask for the information at any time. It should be clarified, not simply followed. A written form is not required. It does not show a lack of capacity or family pressure."}, {"id": 1418, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At admission planning, Mr. Ng's eldest son begins signing forms and speaking for the family, and staff assume he is the decision maker. Mr. Ng has not named anyone to speak for him. What is the best way to clarify who is involved?", "options": {"A": "Ask Mr. Ng whom he wants involved and in what role.", "B": "Ask the family to choose one person to speak for Mr. Ng.", "C": "Accept the eldest son as decision maker because he is taking the lead.", "D": "Check the chart for next of kin and treat that person as decision maker."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Lets the family choose the client's representative", "C": "Assumes the eldest male decides", "D": "Uses the next of kin listing as a substitute for the client's choice"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C180", "cluster_text": "Mr. Ng, 82, attends an oncology clinic for the results of a biopsy. His son and daughter came with him. He speaks English well and values his family's guidance in important choices. The practical nurse is helping the team plan communication and care. Cultural safety, consent, and the client's own stated wishes guide the whole plan of care for him.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask Mr. Ng whom he wants involved and in what role.): The person who decides who is involved is the client, so you ask Mr. Ng. Assumptions based on age, sex, or chart listings ignore his wishes and can lead to consent given by someone without the right to give it."}, {"id": 1419, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A week later, Mr. Ng, who has capacity, declines a recommended treatment and says he will rely on traditional healing practices instead. His son is upset and asks you to persuade him. The team believes the refusal is unwise. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Discharge him with a signed refusal form and no further discussion.", "B": "Give the treatment with his son's consent because the family wants it.", "C": "Respect his decision, confirm he understands the risks, and notify the RN.", "D": "Encourage him again to accept treatment, since the refusal does not seem rational."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ends discussion once a form is signed", "B": "Accepts consent from a family member over a capable client", "D": "Treats a refusal as irrational and pressures him"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C180", "cluster_text": "Mr. Ng, 82, attends an oncology clinic for the results of a biopsy. His son and daughter came with him. He speaks English well and values his family's guidance in important choices. The practical nurse is helping the team plan communication and care. Cultural safety, consent, and the client's own stated wishes guide the whole plan of care for him.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Respect his decision, confirm he understands the risks, and notify the RN.): A capable client may refuse treatment for reasons the team does not share, so you respect the decision and confirm he understands the risks. You then tell the RN and physician so that options and support can continue. Pressure and substitute consent override his autonomy. A signed form alone does not complete the process."}, {"id": 1420, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Karim, a practical nurse, checks a morphine vial from the pharmacy and notices the strength is ten times higher than the order for his 70-year-old client. He returns the vial and receives the correct one, so the client gets the right dose. What should Karim do afterward?", "options": {"A": "Report it through the incident system and alert pharmacy.", "B": "Report it to the RN only and note it in the client's chart.", "C": "Tell the pharmacy by phone but avoid a formal report since no harm occurred.", "D": "Document only that the correct dose was given and move on."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reports informally and documents the event in the chart", "C": "Informs verbally but skips the formal report", "D": "Treats a caught error as not worth reporting"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report it through the incident system and alert pharmacy.): A tenfold strength error that was caught is a serious near miss, and reporting it lets the pharmacy and unit fix the cause before someone else is harmed. A phone call or chart note does not enter the incident system. Avoiding reporting because no harm occurred wastes the warning."}, {"id": 1421, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Jon, a practical nurse on a surgical unit, tearfully tells you he gave an antibiotic dose late and was told he may face discipline. The charge nurse says discipline will stop future mistakes. Unit policy supports a just culture. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Support Jon, encourage him to report the event, and look at the system factors involved.", "B": "Advise him to report the event but leave out the lateness to avoid discipline.", "C": "Agree that discipline is the best way to teach him to avoid errors.", "D": "Suggest he ask the charge nurse to record the event as a minor delay instead."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Gives a partial report that hides contributing facts", "C": "Believes punishment prevents future errors", "D": "Understates the event to avoid discipline"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Support Jon, encourage him to report the event, and look at the system factors involved.): A just culture looks at system causes and supports the person who made the error, because fear of punishment leads to hidden errors. Leaving out facts or understating the event hides the cause. Agreeing that discipline teaches does not fix the process. Support, honest reporting, and a review of the process prevent repeats."}, {"id": 1422, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Bell, 45, is booked for an abdominal ultrasound for right upper abdominal pain at an outpatient imaging clinic. Last night she ate a large fatty dinner, and this morning she had coffee with milk and toast. The appointment is in one hour. What should you advise?", "options": {"A": "Advise her to take a laxative to clear her bowel before the exam.", "B": "Tell imaging staff about her breakfast and ask whether to reschedule.", "C": "Give her extra water to drink so the ultrasound has a clearer view.", "D": "Tell her to proceed since the earlier meal will not affect the images."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Suggests a laxative that was not ordered", "C": "Applies a full bladder rule to an upper abdominal scan", "D": "Assumes a large meal does not affect the study"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tell imaging staff about her breakfast and ask whether to reschedule.): Food and gas can hide the gallbladder and bile ducts, so fasting for several hours is usually requested before a right upper abdominal scan. Imaging staff then decide whether to proceed or rebook. Proceeding as if the meal does not matter risks a poor study. Extra water does not help, and a laxative needs an order."}, {"id": 1423, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A heparin infusion is running for Mr. Osei, 63, on a medical unit. A new rate was just ordered after his blood work. You are about to change the pump and your colleague is busy. What is the best way to handle this high-alert medication?", "options": {"A": "Have a second nurse independently verify the order, concentration, and pump settings before starting.", "B": "Verify the settings yourself carefully, since you checked the bag at the start.", "C": "Ask Mr. Osei to confirm the rate on the pump display.", "D": "Change the rate and ask the RN to confirm it at the end of shift."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relies on a single check for a high-alert drug", "C": "Uses the client as the safety check", "D": "Delays verification until after the change"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Have a second nurse independently verify the order, concentration, and pump settings before starting.): Heparin is a high-alert drug, so an independent second check of the order, concentration, and pump settings is done before the rate changes. A single check can miss an error you have already made once. A late confirmation lets the error reach him first. The client cannot serve as the safety check."}, {"id": 1424, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Silva, 78, is on postoperative day two with IV fluids at 125 mL per hour. She now has crackles at the lung bases, shortness of breath, tiredness, and a 2 kg weight gain. Her urine output is adequate. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Report possible fluid overload and ask for the IV rate to be reviewed.", "B": "Increase the IV rate because her tiredness suggests she is dehydrated.", "C": "Encourage more oral fluids to thin the secretions in her lungs.", "D": "Lay her flat to help her breathing and raise the foot of the bed."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats fluid overload as hypovolemia", "C": "Adds fluid to a client who may be overloaded", "D": "Positions her to worsen breathlessness"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report possible fluid overload and ask for the IV rate to be reviewed.): Crackles, breathlessness, and rapid weight gain on IV fluids point to fluid overload, especially in an older adult, so the findings are reported and the rate reviewed. Extra fluid worsens it, and lying flat makes breathing harder. Tiredness alone does not show dehydration here."}, {"id": 1425, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On rounds in a long-term care home, you see that Mr. Park, 86, needs a dressing change, repositioning, mouth care, and a blood pressure check in the next hour. The care aide notes each task would need its own trip. What is the best way to organize this work?", "options": {"A": "Do the blood pressure check now and the other tasks at next rounds.", "B": "Delegate the dressing change to the care aide and do the other tasks.", "C": "Complete the quickest task for every client first and return later for the longer ones.", "D": "Cluster the tasks into one visit, prioritizing time-sensitive care."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Does one task and delays time-sensitive care", "B": "Delegates a task beyond the care aide's role", "C": "Organizes by task length instead of need"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cluster the tasks into one visit, prioritizing time-sensitive care.): Clustering tasks lowers interruptions and tiredness for him and saves time, and gathering supplies first prevents repeat trips. Time-sensitive items go first. Doing one task and postponing the rest delays care. A dressing change is not routinely delegated to a care aide. Ordering by length ignores priority."}, {"id": 1426, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mrs. Kowalski, 84, lives in long-term care and depends on the practical nurse for bathing and medications. A new nurse believes that treating residents as equals means no power difference exists between them. How should the nurse understand this relationship?", "options": {"A": "Power is shared evenly in long-term care because residents live there as at home.", "B": "The nurse holds power only over tasks, and not over information about the resident.", "C": "Power is equal once both parties agree to the care plan and sign the consent form.", "D": "The nurse holds more power through knowledge and access, so must use it carefully."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confuses the home setting with equal power", "B": "Underestimates the power that comes from access to information", "C": "Equates agreement with equal power"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The nurse holds more power through knowledge and access, so must use it carefully.): The nurse has information, access to the chart, and control over care, while a resident depends on her, so the relationship is unequal even when respectful. Awareness of this imbalance guides honest, boundary-aware care. A home-like setting or courtesy does not remove it, and the nurse's power covers information too. Agreement does not equalize power."}, {"id": 1427, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Hughes, 75, is in hospice and has become agitated over six hours, picking at his sheets and trying to climb out of bed. He is actively dying and has not had a bowel movement in three days. What should the practical nurse consider first?", "options": {"A": "Opioid overdose, so he should be given a reversal agent at once.", "B": "Physical causes like pain or constipation, with a report to the RN.", "C": "Expected dying behaviour that needs no assessment or report.", "D": "Emotional distress, which family presence and reassurance will resolve."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Jumps to overdose and reversal without an order or assessment", "C": "Dismisses restlessness as normal without assessment", "D": "Assumes distress is only emotional"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Physical causes like pain or constipation, with a report to the RN.): Terminal restlessness can have treatable causes such as pain, a full bladder, or constipation, so you assess and report to the RN, who contacts the prescriber. Comfort and family presence help but do not replace assessment. Reversal agents need an order and can cause severe pain."}, {"id": 1428, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Yilmaz, 69, is on postoperative day one after a colostomy. His stoma is red-pink, moist, and slightly swollen, with a small amount of blood-tinged mucus on the pouch. His vital signs are stable. How should you interpret this finding?", "options": {"A": "Early ischemia, so notify the physician and remove the pouch at once.", "B": "A healthy stoma, so keep assessing its colour and the pouch.", "C": "Infection of the stoma, so request antibiotics and send a wound swab.", "D": "Bleeding from damage, so hold firm pressure until swelling subsides."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats a normal pink, moist stoma as abnormal", "C": "Mistakes normal swelling for infection", "D": "Treats expected mucus as bleeding"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A healthy stoma, so keep assessing its colour and the pouch.): A pink to red, moist, slightly swollen stoma is expected early after surgery, and a little blood-tinged mucus is common. Dusky or dark colour would signal poor blood supply. Continue assessing colour, swelling, and output and report any change."}, {"id": 1429, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Dunn, 72, has diabetes with decreased sensation in both feet and lives alone at home. He says he walks barefoot indoors to strengthen his feet. A clinic practical nurse is teaching him foot care. Which teaching is most appropriate for him?", "options": {"A": "Wear well-fitting shoes or slippers indoors and inspect both feet daily for injuries.", "B": "Have a nurse inspect his feet weekly, as daily checks are not needed.", "C": "Soak his feet in warm water daily to improve circulation and help restore their sensation.", "D": "Walk barefoot on carpet, since soft surfaces protect against injury."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reduces inspection frequency despite loss of sensation", "C": "Advises soaking, which can burn insensitive skin", "D": "Believes barefoot walking builds strength and is safe on carpet"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Wear well-fitting shoes or slippers indoors and inspect both feet daily for injuries.): With reduced sensation, a small cut or blister can go unnoticed, so footwear indoors and daily inspection are central. Carpet still allows injury, and warm soaks can burn feet that cannot feel heat. Weekly checks miss early damage."}, {"id": 1430, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Reid, 58, is on postoperative day one after a colostomy. At 1400 her stoma, pink this morning, looks dusky purple and feels cool. The RN is on a break until 1500, and a surgeon is in the building. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Wait for the RN to return from break and then discuss the change.", "B": "Document the colour change and compare the stoma with the surgeon's postoperative description.", "C": "Report the dusky stoma to the RN or surgeon now, since blood supply may be compromised.", "D": "Apply a warm cloth over the stoma to improve circulation, then reassess."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays reporting until a break ends", "B": "Records and compares the change without reporting it", "D": "Treats a possible ischemia with an unordered warm application"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the dusky stoma to the RN or surgeon now, since blood supply may be compromised.): A dusky, cool stoma suggests poor blood supply, which can lead to tissue death and needs urgent assessment, so you find someone now rather than waiting. Charting alone or waiting an hour delays treatment. Heat can damage tissue and is not an ordered intervention."}, {"id": 1431, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Priya, a practical nurse on a long-term care hallway, receives requests from three residents between 0900 and 1100: one wants water, one wants a blanket, and one reports hip pain. Each request has so far sent her back to the supply room separately. Which approach to organizing her rounds best reduces wasted trips and interruptions?", "options": {"A": "Assess all three needs first, gather supplies, then complete every task in one planned round.", "B": "Ask the care aide to fetch the blanket and judge how serious the hip pain is.", "C": "Answer each call light the moment it rings so no resident waits for attention.", "D": "Refill water for the entire hallway first, then come back later for blankets and pain checks."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Passing a pain assessment to unregulated staff when it needs nursing judgement.", "C": "Reacting to each call separately, which multiplies trips and breaks up other work.", "D": "Clustering by a single task, which still leaves return trips for the other requests."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Assess all three needs first, gather supplies, then complete every task in one planned round.): Assessing all three residents first lets Priya collect everything in one trip and build a single round, which cuts walking and interruptions. Answering each call as it rings feels responsive but creates the repeated trips she is trying to avoid. Grouping by water alone leaves the blanket and the pain check undone, and the pain check cannot be handed to an aide because it requires nursing assessment."}, {"id": 1432, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Rahul, a registered nurse, is about to start a heparin infusion for a 68-year-old with a new pulmonary embolus. He asks Wen, a practical nurse, to help before the pump starts. The order is written and the bag has arrived from pharmacy. Which action best meets the requirement for a high-alert medication check?", "options": {"A": "Sign the checking record after Rahul reads out the order, concentration and pump settings.", "B": "Confirm the pharmacy label matches the order, since pharmacy has already prepared and checked the bag.", "C": "Independently check order, bag, pump and client identity, then compare with Rahul.", "D": "Watch Rahul program the pump, then agree with the infusion rate he states aloud before starting."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Signing on the first nurse's spoken report, which turns the second check into a formality.", "B": "Treating the pharmacy label as proof and skipping the pump and client identity checks.", "D": "Observing and agreeing instead of forming an independent answer, so one error passes twice."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Independently check order, bag, pump and client identity, then compare with Rahul.): Wen should verify each element on her own, because an independent check only catches an error when the second nurse does not simply adopt the first nurse's numbers. A matching pharmacy label supports the check but does not replace bedside verification of the pump and the client. Agreeing with a spoken rate or signing after listening gives the look of a double check without its safety value."}, {"id": 1433, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Six hours after catheter removal, Mr. Beaumont has voided only 40 mL twice and looks uncomfortable. His lower abdomen feels slightly firm just above the pubic bone, and he rates his discomfort 4 out of 10. Which action best reflects safe assessment of these findings?", "options": {"A": "Palpate for bladder distension, ask about fullness, and report for a bladder scan.", "B": "Reinsert a urinary catheter to relieve pressure, since small voids mean the bladder is full.", "C": "Record the two voids as normal output because he is passing urine at regular intervals today.", "D": "Encourage extra oral fluids and compare the voided volumes again after the next two voids"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Acting beyond scope by reinserting a catheter without an order or completed assessment.", "C": "Accepting small frequent voids as normal without checking whether the bladder empties.", "D": "Delaying assessment for hours despite a firm lower abdomen and visible discomfort."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C175", "cluster_text": "Harold Beaumont, 79, is on a surgical ward on day 2 after hip hemiarthroplasty. His urinary catheter was removed this morning, he takes oxycodone every four hours for pain, and he says he is tired. Elena, the practical nurse caring for him, completes a focused assessment during the evening shift and reports her findings to the registered nurse in charge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Palpate for bladder distension, ask about fullness, and report for a bladder scan.): Small voids with a firm suprapubic area and discomfort suggest retention with overflow, so the nurse assesses the bladder and reports so a scan can confirm it. Calling the voids normal ignores the distension, and waiting for hours risks bladder overfilling. Catheter insertion needs an order and a completed assessment, so it is not the first step."}, {"id": 1434, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On his third day without a bowel movement, Mr. Beaumont tells Elena he passed a small amount of liquid stool yesterday and now feels bloated. His abdomen is mildly distended with active bowel sounds. What is the best interpretation of this finding?", "options": {"A": "Liquid stool may leak around hard retained stool, so he needs further assessment.", "B": "His opioid dose is too high, so the dose should be held until stools resume again.", "C": "Bowel function is returning, because he has now passed stool within the past day.", "D": "The bloating comes from swallowed air during meals, so no change in his care is needed."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Deciding alone to hold a prescribed analgesic, which is outside practical nurse scope.", "C": "Counting any stool as normal elimination and ignoring its volume and consistency.", "D": "Dismissing distension and discomfort without linking them to a constipating medication."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C175", "cluster_text": "Harold Beaumont, 79, is on a surgical ward on day 2 after hip hemiarthroplasty. His urinary catheter was removed this morning, he takes oxycodone every four hours for pain, and he says he is tired. Elena, the practical nurse caring for him, completes a focused assessment during the evening shift and reports her findings to the registered nurse in charge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Liquid stool may leak around hard retained stool, so he needs further assessment.): Opioids slow the gut, and a small liquid stool in a bloated client can be leakage around hard retained stool. Elena gathers more detail on his pattern and reports it. Treating the stool as recovery or the bloating as harmless misses this risk, and holding a prescribed opioid is not a decision she makes alone."}, {"id": 1435, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Beaumont says, \"I am wiped out, and I cannot sleep through the night.\" Elena reviews the night chart: pain rated 6 out of 10 at 0200, two washroom trips, and hallway noise. He also napped for two hours after lunch yesterday. Which approach best explores his fatigue?", "options": {"A": "Ask the provider for a sleep aid order, since he wakes repeatedly and reports feeling exhausted.", "B": "Advise him to nap less during the day and mention it again at next week's assessment.", "C": "Explore pain, night voiding, medications, noise and mood together as possible contributors.", "D": "Offer a warm drink at bedtime and teach sleep habits, since fatigue results from poor sleep."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Jumping to a medication solution before the contributing causes have been assessed.", "B": "Offering general advice and deferring reassessment without addressing specific findings.", "D": "Treating fatigue as a pure sleep problem and not examining pain or elimination."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C175", "cluster_text": "Harold Beaumont, 79, is on a surgical ward on day 2 after hip hemiarthroplasty. His urinary catheter was removed this morning, he takes oxycodone every four hours for pain, and he says he is tired. Elena, the practical nurse caring for him, completes a focused assessment during the evening shift and reports her findings to the registered nurse in charge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explore pain, night voiding, medications, noise and mood together as possible contributors.): His chart shows pain, voiding and noise interrupting rest, so fatigue after surgery has several contributors that Elena should assess together. Teaching sleep habits alone addresses only one of them. A sleep aid in an older adult already taking opioids adds risk and should not come before finding the causes."}, {"id": 1436, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During the admission history, Mr. Beaumont, who appears alert and well groomed, says he has \"a drink or two\" most evenings. His daughter is sitting beside the bed, and the unit is busy this evening. Elena needs to complete substance use screening. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Skip detailed questions because his appearance and age make a drinking problem unlikely.", "B": "Ask his daughter about his drinking, since families usually know the true amount.", "C": "Ask only about prescription medications, since alcohol falls outside surgical admission questions.", "D": "Ask the screening questions privately and without judgment, and report concerning answers to the RN."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Letting appearance decide whether to screen, which misses hidden alcohol use.", "B": "Gathering sensitive information in front of family without his consent.", "C": "Narrowing screening to medications, which omits alcohol that affects withdrawal and healing."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C175", "cluster_text": "Harold Beaumont, 79, is on a surgical ward on day 2 after hip hemiarthroplasty. His urinary catheter was removed this morning, he takes oxycodone every four hours for pain, and he says he is tired. Elena, the practical nurse caring for him, completes a focused assessment during the evening shift and reports her findings to the registered nurse in charge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask the screening questions privately and without judgment, and report concerning answers to the RN.): Screening is routine and does not depend on how a client looks, and it is done privately so he can answer honestly. Alcohol history matters here because withdrawal and drug interactions can affect surgical recovery, so concerning answers go to the RN. Asking a relative or limiting the questions to prescriptions fails on confidentiality and completeness."}, {"id": 1437, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Dana, 45, arrives at outpatient imaging for an abdominal ultrasound to investigate right upper quadrant pain after meals. She ate a large breakfast an hour ago and drank coffee with cream. Which instruction from the practical nurse is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Proceed but ask the technologist to scan the liver rather than the gallbladder.", "B": "Explain that fasting is needed, tell the technologist, and ask about rescheduling.", "C": "Have her walk for thirty minutes to move food along before the scan starts.", "D": "Ask her to drink a large amount of water so the stomach is flushed before scanning."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Alters the study to suit her diet instead of rescheduling", "C": "Believes walking empties stomach contents quickly enough", "D": "Applying bladder-filling preparation to an upper abdominal study."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain that fasting is needed, tell the technologist, and ask about rescheduling.): Fasting keeps the gallbladder filled and reduces bowel gas, so a recent large meal can make the images unreadable. The nurse informs the technologist and lets the department decide on rescheduling. Changing the target organ does not answer the question asked. Walking does not clear a fatty meal, and water does not undo it."}, {"id": 1438, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Sofia, a practical nurse on a surgical unit, tearfully tells her colleague Jamal that she gave a medication 90 minutes late because of an overloaded assignment. An incident report is being completed. Which response by Jamal best supports a safety culture on the unit?", "options": {"A": "Suggest she write the report briefly and leave out the workload as an excuse.", "B": "Tell her she should have asked for help earlier, and that the delay is her responsibility.", "C": "Listen with empathy, encourage honest reporting, and support a review of the workload behind the delay.", "D": "Reassure her that one late dose is minor and does not need to be written up."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Hides a contributing system factor", "B": "Places sole responsibility on the individual", "D": "Minimizing the event and skipping the report, which hides the system contributor."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Listen with empathy, encourage honest reporting, and support a review of the workload behind the delay.): A just culture treats the error as a chance to learn, so Jamal supports honest reporting and a look at the assignment that contributed to the delay. Leaving out the workload hides the system cause. Placing full responsibility on her blames the person and discourages disclosure. Calling the delay minor skips the report."}, {"id": 1439, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Wei, a practical nurse, is about to suction a 65-year-old with a tracheostomy who has audible secretions and an oxygen saturation of 93 percent on room air. He has the catheter and sterile gloves ready. Which technique best protects the client's oxygenation during suctioning?", "options": {"A": "Choose the largest catheter that fits so each pass clears more secretions.", "B": "Wait to suction until saturation falls below 90 percent so secretions are clearly present.", "C": "Apply suction while advancing the catheter to loosen secretions, continuing for up to thirty seconds.", "D": "Pre-oxygenate as ordered, suction up to about ten seconds while withdrawing, then reassess saturation."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Choosing an oversized catheter, which can obstruct the airway and injure tissue.", "B": "Waiting for desaturation, which delays removal until oxygenation is already compromised.", "C": "Suctioning too long and during insertion, which removes oxygen along with secretions."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pre-oxygenate as ordered, suction up to about ten seconds while withdrawing, then reassess saturation.): Brief passes with suction applied only on withdrawal, plus pre-oxygenation, limit hypoxia and mucosal injury. Long passes with suction on insertion remove oxygen faster than the client can replace it. Waiting for a drop in saturation or choosing a bigger catheter each adds avoidable harm."}, {"id": 1440, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Alvarez, 58, visits a community clinic with white patches on her tongue and a sore mouth. She uses a corticosteroid inhaler twice daily for asthma. The practical nurse suspects oral thrush and reviews her routine. Which teaching point is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Rinse and spit after each dose, and report the patches so treatment can be ordered.", "B": "Use antiseptic mouthwash after each dose and delay reporting until patches spread.", "C": "Rinse before each dose rather than after, and recheck the patches next week.", "D": "Stop the steroid inhaler right away, since it caused the fungal infection in her mouth."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relying on mouthwash and delaying the report that treatment needs", "C": "Rinsing at the wrong time so residue stays in the mouth", "D": "Stopping a controller medication without an order, risking an asthma flare."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rinse and spit after each dose, and report the patches so treatment can be ordered.): Rinsing after each dose removes steroid residue that encourages yeast growth, and reporting lets the provider prescribe an antifungal while the controller continues. Rinsing only before a dose leaves residue behind, and mouthwash with delay leaves the infection untreated. Stopping the inhaler risks an asthma flare."}, {"id": 1441, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Kowalski keeps sliding down in bed after each repositioning, and her head of bed is raised to 60 degrees most of the day because she says it helps her breathing. She weighs 52 kg. Which action best addresses the shear and friction threatening her sacral skin?", "options": {"A": "Keep the head of bed at 30 degrees or lower between meals, using a draw sheet.", "B": "Pull her up the bed by the arms each time she slides down.", "C": "Keep the head of bed at 60 degrees all day so her breathing stays comfortable.", "D": "Fold an extra pad under her buttocks to hold her position in bed."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Dragging across the sheet, which creates friction and shear on fragile skin.", "C": "Keeping the head raised for long periods, which causes the sliding and shear.", "D": "Adding layers that trap moisture and raise pressure instead of relieving it."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C173", "cluster_text": "Irene Kowalski, 80, is on a medical unit with infectious diarrhea and generalized weakness. She cannot leave bed without two staff. Over the past day she has slid toward the foot of the bed, her sacral skin has become reddened, and her linen is repeatedly soiled. Tomasz, a practical nurse, plans her care for the shift with a care aide.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep the head of bed at 30 degrees or lower between meals, using a draw sheet.): A high head of bed pushes the body downward and drags skin against the sheet, so lowering the angle between meals and lifting with a draw sheet reduces shear. Dragging her up adds friction. Extra padding raises pressure and moisture, which worsens the risk at the sacrum."}, {"id": 1442, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Tomasz now plans how to protect Mrs. Kowalski's reddened sacral skin from the combined effects of pressure and moisture while her diarrhea continues. Her family has brought a sheepskin pad from home and asked whether it can replace other measures. Which combination of measures is best?", "options": {"A": "Reposition regularly, cleanse and dry after each soiling, and use a pressure-redistributing surface.", "B": "Wash the area once each shift using hot water and soap.", "C": "Apply a thick layer of petroleum ointment over the red area after each cleansing.", "D": "Place her on a specialty mattress and leave position unchanged between assessments to avoid disturbing rest."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using hot water and harsh soap, which dries and damages skin already at risk.", "C": "Sealing damp skin under heavy ointment, which keeps moisture against the sacrum.", "D": "Relying on the mattress alone without turning, so pressure stays on one area."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C173", "cluster_text": "Irene Kowalski, 80, is on a medical unit with infectious diarrhea and generalized weakness. She cannot leave bed without two staff. Over the past day she has slid toward the foot of the bed, her sacral skin has become reddened, and her linen is repeatedly soiled. Tomasz, a practical nurse, plans her care for the shift with a care aide.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reposition regularly, cleanse and dry after each soiling, and use a pressure-redistributing surface.): Pressure and moisture act together, so turning, prompt cleansing and drying, and a pressure-redistributing surface are all needed. A mattress cannot replace turning. Heavy ointment or hot soapy washing each damage skin that is already reddened."}, {"id": 1443, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Kowalski cannot leave bed, and her bottom sheet is damp and creased under her sacrum. Tomasz has a helper and clean linen at the bedside, and her room is warm. Which technique is best for changing the linen while she remains in bed?", "options": {"A": "Pull the soiled sheet from under her in one motion and slide the new one in.", "B": "Slide a clean sheet over the damp one and tuck the edges.", "C": "Roll her toward you without raising the rail, then change the linen quickly.", "D": "Roll her side to side with the rail up, replacing linen smooth and tight."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pulling linen across skin, which causes friction and ignores the need for a smooth fit.", "B": "Layering clean linen over a damp sheet traps moisture and adds bulk", "C": "Rolling a weak client without the rail up risks a fall"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C173", "cluster_text": "Irene Kowalski, 80, is on a medical unit with infectious diarrhea and generalized weakness. She cannot leave bed without two staff. Over the past day she has slid toward the foot of the bed, her sacral skin has become reddened, and her linen is repeatedly soiled. Tomasz, a practical nurse, plans her care for the shift with a care aide.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Roll her side to side with the rail up, replacing linen smooth and tight.): A tight, wrinkle-free bottom sheet spreads pressure, and rolling her side to side with the rail up keeps her safe and protects skin from friction. Layering clean linen over a damp sheet traps moisture, and rolling her without the rail risks a fall. Pulling the sheet out in one motion drags across her skin."}, {"id": 1444, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Tomasz removes a heavily soiled sheet from Mrs. Kowalski's bed while she has infectious diarrhea. The hallway linen cart is about ten metres away and another resident is walking past. Which method of handling the linen best limits spread of organisms?", "options": {"A": "Shake the sheet gently over the bed to remove stool before it is bagged.", "B": "Carry the bundle along the hallway to the linen cart and bag it there with others.", "C": "Roll soiled surfaces inward, hold them away from his body, and bag at the bedside.", "D": "Hold the linen close to his body so nothing drips on the floor."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Shaking the linen, which sends organisms into the air and across the room.", "B": "Carrying open soiled linen through the corridor, which spreads contamination.", "D": "Hugging linen against the uniform, which contaminates his clothing."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C173", "cluster_text": "Irene Kowalski, 80, is on a medical unit with infectious diarrhea and generalized weakness. She cannot leave bed without two staff. Over the past day she has slid toward the foot of the bed, her sacral skin has become reddened, and her linen is repeatedly soiled. Tomasz, a practical nurse, plans her care for the shift with a care aide.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Roll soiled surfaces inward, hold them away from his body, and bag at the bedside.): Rolling soiled surfaces inward, holding the bundle away from the body and bagging at the point of use contains organisms. Shaking spreads them through the air. Carrying open linen or hugging it to the uniform contaminates the hallway and his clothing."}, {"id": 1445, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Kevin, 45, has come to a clinic for routine vital signs. After counting his radial pulse for 30 seconds, the practical nurse keeps her fingers on his wrist. He is chatting about his weekend plans. Which action best gives an accurate respiratory rate?", "options": {"A": "Count his breaths for 15 seconds and multiply by four to save time during the visit.", "B": "Tell him his breathing is about to be counted so he stays calm and breathes normally.", "C": "Ask him to breathe in deeply so that each breath is easy to see.", "D": "Keep fingers on his wrist as if taking pulse, then count chest rises for one minute."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a short count, which magnifies small errors in a rate that varies.", "B": "Announcing the count, which makes clients change their breathing pattern.", "C": "Coaching deep breaths, which gives an unnatural rate."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep fingers on his wrist as if taking pulse, then count chest rises for one minute.): People change their breathing when they know it is observed, so the nurse appears to be still taking the pulse and counts naturally. A full minute is the safest count when the pattern is unknown. A shortened count magnifies error, and coaching breaths alters the rate."}, {"id": 1446, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor, an 86-year-old resident of long-term care, tells the practical nurse he is grateful and wants her to join his family for supper this weekend. He depends on her for bathing and medications most days. How should the nurse understand this relationship?", "options": {"A": "The relationship is equal when he feels comfortable, so accepting the invitation causes no harm.", "B": "A professional relationship carries a power imbalance, so the nurse stays responsible for keeping clear boundaries.", "C": "The imbalance applies only to residents with dementia or other memory problems.", "D": "The imbalance ends if the resident chooses the relationship, so his invitation settles it."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing comfort means equality, which ignores his dependence on care.", "C": "Limiting power imbalance to residents with cognitive loss", "D": "Treating the client's wish as removing the nurse's duty"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A professional relationship carries a power imbalance, so the nurse stays responsible for keeping clear boundaries.): The nurse holds knowledge, access and influence over a dependent resident, so responsibility for boundaries rests with her however warm the relationship feels. The imbalance is not limited to residents with memory loss, and his invitation does not remove her duty. She can thank him warmly while declining the social invitation."}, {"id": 1447, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Duval, 78, has moderate dementia and has tried to leave through the front door three times this shift, saying she must collect her children from school. The practical nurse reviews options with the team. Which intervention should be tried first?", "options": {"A": "Tell her firmly that the door is locked and that she must remain seated here.", "B": "Increase her scheduled activities this afternoon without checking for pain, hunger or toileting needs.", "C": "Offer a calm walk, redirect to an activity she values, and check for unmet needs.", "D": "Explain that her children are grown and reorient her until she accepts it."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confronting and correcting her, which raises agitation.", "B": "Adding activity without assessing for unmet needs behind the exit seeking", "D": "Using repeated reality correction that raises distress in dementia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Offer a calm walk, redirect to an activity she values, and check for unmet needs.): Exit-seeking often reflects an unmet need or a sense of purpose, so walking with her, redirecting and checking for pain or toileting needs is the least restrictive first step. Adding activity without assessing misses the cause, and repeated correction about her children tends to increase distress."}, {"id": 1448, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On day two after bowel surgery, Mr. Lindgren, 74, has crackles at both lung bases, a respiratory rate of 26, and a saturation of 91 percent. His IV is infusing at 150 mL per hour and his ankles are swollen. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Lay him flat to improve circulation and raise his legs on pillows.", "B": "Sit him upright, apply oxygen per protocol, and report promptly to the RN.", "C": "Encourage coughing and deep breathing, then continue the same IV rate.", "D": "Increase the IV rate to protect against the low blood pressure he might develop."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Positioning flat, which worsens breathing difficulty from pulmonary congestion.", "C": "Treating only the lungs and delaying a report about an infusion that may be too fast.", "D": "Giving more fluid to a client whose findings suggest overload."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sit him upright, apply oxygen per protocol, and report promptly to the RN.): Crackles, fast breathing, low saturation and edema after IV fluids point toward overload, so sitting him upright, giving oxygen and reporting quickly are the priorities. Raising the rate adds to the load, and lying flat worsens breathing. Coughing exercises will not help if the cause is the infusion."}, {"id": 1449, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Nasrin learns the resident she cared for has active pulmonary tuberculosis and that she wore no respirator during close contact on two shifts. She has no symptoms and feels well, and her next shift starts in two hours. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Take a preventive antibiotic from a colleague and continue her usual shift.", "B": "Report the exposure to occupational health and infection control now, and follow the screening they direct.", "C": "Wait to see whether cough or fever develops, then report the exposure to her supervisor.", "D": "Continue working as usual, since one unprotected exposure rarely causes disease."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Self-treating with another person's medication, which is unsafe and not prescribed.", "C": "Waiting for symptoms, which delays screening and risks further transmission.", "D": "Minimizing the exposure and ignoring the duty to report and be screened."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C176", "cluster_text": "Maple Grove is a 60-bed long-term care home. Within 24 hours, four residents on the second floor develop vomiting and watery diarrhea. During the same week, a resident with a three-week productive cough and night sweats is confirmed to have active tuberculosis. Nasrin, a practical nurse on the unit, cared for that resident without a respirator on two shifts. The unit manager is off site.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the exposure to occupational health and infection control now, and follow the screening they direct.): Latent infection produces no symptoms, so Nasrin reports the exposure immediately and follows the screening plan set by occupational health. Waiting for symptoms delays detection. Taking a colleague's medication is unsafe, and dismissing the exposure ignores her duty to residents and coworkers."}, {"id": 1450, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Four residents on the second floor have developed vomiting and watery diarrhea within 24 hours. The first stool sample is still at the laboratory, and the day charge nurse says the unit is probably just having a rough week. Which observation best identifies this as a possible outbreak?", "options": {"A": "Each resident with symptoms has a different cause confirmed by separate laboratory tests.", "B": "More residents than usual in one area share similar symptoms within a short time.", "C": "Only one resident has vomited so far, so laboratory results are needed before any conclusion.", "D": "Symptoms resolve within a day for most residents, so no review is needed."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Requiring separate laboratory proof before seeing a shared pattern", "C": "Waiting for laboratory results before recognizing a pattern across residents.", "D": "Dismissing a cluster because individual illnesses seem brief"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C176", "cluster_text": "Maple Grove is a 60-bed long-term care home. Within 24 hours, four residents on the second floor develop vomiting and watery diarrhea. During the same week, a resident with a three-week productive cough and night sweats is confirmed to have active tuberculosis. Nasrin, a practical nurse on the unit, cared for that resident without a respirator on two shifts. The unit manager is off site.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (More residents than usual in one area share similar symptoms within a short time.): An outbreak is recognized by a cluster of similar illness in a place and time that exceeds the usual, and it does not require laboratory confirmation first. Acting on the pattern protects other residents. Waiting for separate test results, or treating brief illness as no concern, delays that recognition."}, {"id": 1451, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The second floor now has six residents with vomiting and diarrhea, and group dining is scheduled for noon. Staff have gloves, gowns and alcohol hand rub available, and some residents are asking to eat together. Which measure best controls spread of the illness?", "options": {"A": "Move the ill residents into the main lounge so staff can watch them closely.", "B": "Continue group dining with extra alcohol hand rub stations placed on the tables.", "C": "Restrict only residents who are vomiting and let those with diarrhea attend.", "D": "Use contact precautions with soap-and-water hand hygiene, cohort ill residents, and hold group dining and activities."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gathering ill residents in a shared common area, which spreads illness.", "B": "Continuing group gatherings and relying on rub, which is weak against some gastroenteric organisms.", "C": "Splitting restrictions by symptom, leaving infectious residents among well residents."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C176", "cluster_text": "Maple Grove is a 60-bed long-term care home. Within 24 hours, four residents on the second floor develop vomiting and watery diarrhea. During the same week, a resident with a three-week productive cough and night sweats is confirmed to have active tuberculosis. Nasrin, a practical nurse on the unit, cared for that resident without a respirator on two shifts. The unit manager is off site.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use contact precautions with soap-and-water hand hygiene, cohort ill residents, and hold group dining and activities.): Some causes of gastroenteritis resist alcohol, so soap and water, contact precautions, cohorting and cancelling group events reduce spread. Keeping dining open with rub stations, or gathering ill residents in the lounge, creates exposure. Partial restriction leaves infectious residents in contact with others."}, {"id": 1452, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Nasrin notices a fifth resident has started vomiting and believes the number of cases is larger than the manager knows. A coworker suggests staying quiet to avoid alarming families and visitors. Nasrin is worried about being blamed for raising the alarm. What should Nasrin do?", "options": {"A": "Report the cluster promptly to the charge nurse and infection prevention staff.", "B": "Wait until a physician confirms a diagnosis before telling anyone about the cluster.", "C": "Share the concern with staff at the break so everyone watches carefully.", "D": "Tell the families directly first so they can decide whether to visit."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delaying a report until diagnosis, which allows continued spread.", "C": "Sharing informally with staff, which bypasses people who can act.", "D": "Skipping the reporting chain and communicating unverified information first."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C176", "cluster_text": "Maple Grove is a 60-bed long-term care home. Within 24 hours, four residents on the second floor develop vomiting and watery diarrhea. During the same week, a resident with a three-week productive cough and night sweats is confirmed to have active tuberculosis. Nasrin, a practical nurse on the unit, cared for that resident without a respirator on two shifts. The unit manager is off site.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the cluster promptly to the charge nurse and infection prevention staff.): Prompt reporting to the charge nurse and infection prevention starts an investigation and notification of public health, which is expected when an outbreak is suspected. Waiting for a diagnosis or chatting with coworkers delays the response. Informing families before the proper channels bypasses the team."}, {"id": 1453, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ferreira's slurred speech and facial droop could suggest a stroke. Gabriel calls his name and finds him drowsy but still responding to questions. His glucose meter is in the bag and the nearest ambulance is about twelve minutes away. What should Gabriel do first?", "options": {"A": "Focus on the facial droop as a stroke and skip the glucose check.", "B": "Check blood glucose right away while arranging emergency help, since low glucose can mimic stroke signs.", "C": "Wait for the speech to clear before calling anyone, since low glucose may be the cause.", "D": "Give orange juice by mouth right away to raise his glucose before anything else."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming the cause from the droop alone and missing a reversible one.", "C": "Delaying emergency help for a possible stroke while waiting to see if it passes.", "D": "Giving oral fluids to a drowsy client with facial droop, risking aspiration."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C174", "cluster_text": "Tomas Ferreira, 66, has type 2 diabetes and takes insulin. Gabriel, a practical nurse, makes a scheduled home visit at midday. Mr. Ferreira is sitting in a chair with slurred speech and a slightly drooping left side of his face. His wife says he skipped lunch because he felt unwell, and the house is quiet and warm. His glucose meter and insulin pen are on the table.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Check blood glucose right away while arranging emergency help, since low glucose can mimic stroke signs.): Hypoglycemia can mimic stroke, and both are emergencies, so Gabriel checks glucose quickly while summoning help. Waiting wastes time if this is a stroke. Oral juice in a drowsy client with facial weakness risks aspiration, and ignoring glucose could miss an easily corrected cause."}, {"id": 1454, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The glucose reading is 2.1 mmol/L. As Gabriel reaches for the glucagon kit, Mr. Ferreira stiffens, slides sideways from the chair, and begins rhythmic jerking of all limbs while his wife shouts. His head is close to a coffee table. Which action should Gabriel take first?", "options": {"A": "Lay him flat on his back and tilt his chin up.", "B": "Lower him to the floor, clear hazards, cushion his head, and note the time.", "C": "Move him to the couch quickly and elevate his legs to improve circulation.", "D": "Squeeze glucose gel into his mouth right away to reverse the low sugar quickly."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Positioning flat during seizure risks aspiration and ignores nearby hazards", "C": "Moving a convulsing client onto furniture risks a fall and injury", "D": "Giving oral glucose during a seizure, which risks choking."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C174", "cluster_text": "Tomas Ferreira, 66, has type 2 diabetes and takes insulin. Gabriel, a practical nurse, makes a scheduled home visit at midday. Mr. Ferreira is sitting in a chair with slurred speech and a slightly drooping left side of his face. His wife says he skipped lunch because he felt unwell, and the house is quiet and warm. His glucose meter and insulin pen are on the table.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lower him to the floor, clear hazards, cushion his head, and note the time.): During a tonic-clonic seizure the nurse protects the client from injury, cushions the head and records the start time. Lying him flat or moving him onto the couch adds risk of aspiration or a fall. Oral glucose is unsafe when airway protection is lost, and glucagon or IV treatment follows once the seizure allows."}, {"id": 1455, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The jerking stops after about 90 seconds. Mr. Ferreira is breathing noisily, saliva is at the corner of his mouth, and he does not respond to his name. His wife is asking Gabriel to help him sit up. What is the best next position?", "options": {"A": "Prop him upright in a chair so that his breathing can recover more easily.", "B": "Keep him lying flat on his back so his spine stays straight and supported.", "C": "Place him face down with a pillow under his chest so the saliva can drain.", "D": "Turn him onto his side, keep the airway clear, and monitor his breathing."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Sitting up an unresponsive client, who may slump and cannot protect the airway.", "B": "Keeping him supine, which lets saliva and the tongue block the airway.", "C": "Placing him prone, which restricts chest movement after a seizure."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C174", "cluster_text": "Tomas Ferreira, 66, has type 2 diabetes and takes insulin. Gabriel, a practical nurse, makes a scheduled home visit at midday. Mr. Ferreira is sitting in a chair with slurred speech and a slightly drooping left side of his face. His wife says he skipped lunch because he felt unwell, and the house is quiet and warm. His glucose meter and insulin pen are on the table.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Turn him onto his side, keep the airway clear, and monitor his breathing.): After a seizure the client may be unresponsive with secretions, so the side-lying recovery position lets fluid drain and keeps the airway open. Supine or upright positions risk obstruction or slumping. Face-down positioning restricts breathing."}, {"id": 1456, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Eight minutes later, Mr. Ferreira begins jerking again without regaining consciousness between episodes. Emergency services have already been called, and his wife asks whether this is expected after a low glucose reading. She is frightened. Which response shows correct understanding?", "options": {"A": "Repeated episodes suggest low glucose persists, so Gabriel gives juice by mouth.", "B": "Repeated seizures without recovery are an emergency, so Gabriel stays with him.", "C": "Gabriel should recheck the glucose and call again only if it stays low.", "D": "The seizure will probably stop alone, so waiting a few more minutes is reasonable."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving oral fluid to a seizing client, which risks choking", "C": "Delaying the emergency response while repeating a routine check", "D": "Waiting out a prolonged event, which risks brain injury."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C174", "cluster_text": "Tomas Ferreira, 66, has type 2 diabetes and takes insulin. Gabriel, a practical nurse, makes a scheduled home visit at midday. Mr. Ferreira is sitting in a chair with slurred speech and a slightly drooping left side of his face. His wife says he skipped lunch because he felt unwell, and the house is quiet and warm. His glucose meter and insulin pen are on the table.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repeated seizures without recovery are an emergency, so Gabriel stays with him.): Seizures that recur without recovery of consciousness, or last beyond five minutes, need emergency treatment. Gabriel stays, protects the airway and gives responders the timing details. Giving juice by mouth risks choking, and rechecking glucose first delays the care the client needs."}, {"id": 1457, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Amira, a practical nurse, checks the stoma of a client on the first day after colostomy surgery. It is now dusky purple, cool and dry, although two hours earlier it was pink and moist. The surgeon is due to round in the morning. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Report the colour change to the RN at once and document findings and time.", "B": "Document the colour and recheck the stoma after the pouch is changed.", "C": "Loosen the pouch faceplate and keep watching until the surgeon arrives for rounds.", "D": "Place a warm moist cloth over the stoma and watch carefully for improvement."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delaying the report of a finding that suggests lost blood supply.", "C": "Adjusting the appliance and holding the report until the scheduled surgeon visit.", "D": "Applying treatment without an order and delaying escalation of a possible emergency."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the colour change to the RN at once and document findings and time.): A dusky, cool, dry stoma suggests poor blood supply and tissue death, which is time-critical, so the nurse reports at once and records the change. Waiting until the pouch is changed or the morning round delays treatment. A warm cloth does not reverse the problem and is not an ordered treatment."}, {"id": 1458, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Marta, a practical nurse, is preparing three morning medications when a visitor asks about parking and a colleague asks her to cover a break. She is halfway through verifying the second medication against the record. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Hand the half-prepared medication to the colleague so she can help the visitor with parking.", "B": "Politely say she is preparing medications, restart that check, then answer the requests.", "C": "Answer both requests quickly and carry on from where she left off in the record.", "D": "Cover the break first and finish the medications after the colleague's break ends."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Passing a half-prepared medication without a proper handoff.", "C": "Splitting attention mid-check, which is when omissions and wrong-drug errors occur.", "D": "Taking on extra work that delays time-sensitive medications."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Politely say she is preparing medications, restart that check, then answer the requests.): Interruptions during medication preparation raise error risk, so Marta protects the task, redirects the requests and restarts the check if she loses her place. Answering mid-task or handing off a half-prepared drug invites mistakes. Covering a break first delays time-sensitive medicines."}, {"id": 1459, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Peter, 50, with no history of migraine, arrives at a clinic holding his head. He says it is the worst headache of his life and began suddenly while he lifted a box. He is nauseated and his neck feels stiff. What is the practical nurse's best response?", "options": {"A": "Treat as an emergency, stay with him, report now, and monitor neurological signs.", "B": "Have him complete the intake forms first and see how the pain progresses meanwhile.", "C": "Offer acetaminophen and a dark quiet room, since the pain is most likely a migraine.", "D": "Apply a cold cloth and let him rest for an hour before reassessing his pain."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delaying care for paperwork when warning signs are present.", "C": "Assuming migraine despite sudden worst-ever pain and neck stiffness.", "D": "Waiting an hour with a presentation that could be bleeding."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Treat as an emergency, stay with him, report now, and monitor neurological signs.): A sudden severe headache with stiff neck and nausea is a red flag for bleeding or infection around the brain, so the nurse stays, reports immediately and monitors neurological changes. Comfort measures and waiting do not address the danger. His lack of migraine history also argues against a migraine."}, {"id": 1460, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Jin, a registered nurse, asks Carmen, a practical nurse, to double check a pediatric opioid dose and says, \"I got 2.4 mL, please confirm it.\" The order and vial concentration are on the label, and the shift is busy. How should Carmen proceed?", "options": {"A": "Agree with 2.4 mL after confirming that Jin's multiplication looks reasonable.", "B": "Ask Jin to explain each calculation step before beginning her own.", "C": "Sign the record, trusting that Jin is experienced and rarely makes mistakes.", "D": "Calculate the dose on her own from the order and label, then compare answers."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reviewing the first answer instead of finding an independent one, which carries over the same error.", "B": "Following the first nurse's reasoning, which biases the check.", "C": "Signing on trust, which removes the safeguard completely."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Calculate the dose on her own from the order and label, then compare answers.): An independent check means Carmen reaches her own result from the order and label before seeing Jin's number, so the two calculations can expose a mistake. Reviewing Jin's answer or steps anchors her to the same error. Signing on trust defeats the purpose of the check."}, {"id": 1461, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Boateng, 62, with chronic kidney disease, is scheduled for a contrast CT scan. The practical nurse in radiology reviews his chart, and he mentions he developed a rash after a previous scan. The department is running behind schedule. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Tell the RN and radiologist about his kidneys and past reaction, and check creatinine.", "B": "Hold the contrast and send him home to return when the department is quieter.", "C": "Ask him to drink extra water and begin the scan without notifying the radiologist.", "D": "Check creatinine from last year's chart and proceed if the value looks acceptable."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Cancelling the procedure on her own instead of reporting to the team", "C": "Managing hydration alone without sharing the history with the team.", "D": "Relying on outdated results and deciding safety without the radiologist"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tell the RN and radiologist about his kidneys and past reaction, and check creatinine.): Contrast can harm weak kidneys and trigger allergic reactions, so the nurse reports his history and recent kidney results so the radiologist can decide on safety measures. Sending him home or relying on old results bypasses that decision. Being behind schedule does not justify skipping this screening."}, {"id": 1462, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A client in acute care is due for rapid-acting insulin before lunch. The meal tray has not left the kitchen and is expected in about 40 minutes, and the client's glucose before lunch is 7.2 mmol/L. Which action by the practical nurse is best?", "options": {"A": "Skip the dose and tell the client to ask for it at dinner.", "B": "Hold the dose until the tray is arriving, then give it as ordered.", "C": "Give the dose now so the insulin is already working when the meal arrives.", "D": "Give half the dose now and the remainder when the tray arrives."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Omitting an ordered dose without consulting the RN or provider.", "C": "Giving rapid-acting insulin far ahead of food, which risks hypoglycemia.", "D": "Splitting the dose independently, which is not what was ordered."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold the dose until the tray is arriving, then give it as ordered.): Rapid-acting insulin starts within minutes, so it is timed with food to prevent low glucose. The nurse waits until the tray is near and gives it as ordered. Giving it early risks hypoglycemia, and omitting or splitting a dose requires direction from the provider."}, {"id": 1463, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mei, 15, tells Sunita, \"Nothing is ever going to get better.\" She has stopped seeing friends and sleeps poorly. Sunita feels uneasy about raising the topic of suicide with a teenager and wonders whether to wait for her mother to leave the room. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Avoid mentioning suicide, since asking about it may put the idea into her head.", "B": "Promise to keep whatever she says private so that she feels safe opening up to you.", "C": "Ask directly and calmly about thoughts of ending her life, and report the answer.", "D": "Ask whether she has been a little sad lately, and avoid asking about suicide."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Avoiding the topic, based on a myth that asking increases risk.", "B": "Promising secrecy, which cannot be kept when safety is at risk.", "D": "Using vague wording that avoids asking about suicide directly."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C178", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Chen brings her three children to a community clinic. Mei, 15, has lost weight and says she feels hopeless. Owen, 2, rarely makes eye contact and has few words. Daniel, 8, takes a stimulant for attention problems. Sunita, a practical nurse, sees each child in turn during the visit and reports to the nurse practitioner afterward. The clinic is busy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask directly and calmly about thoughts of ending her life, and report the answer.): Direct questions about suicide do not plant the idea and allow risk to be identified, so Sunita asks plainly and reports the answer. Vague wording or delayed follow-up leaves a hopeless teen unassessed. She cannot promise secrecy because safety limits confidentiality."}, {"id": 1464, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Chen adds that Mei skips meals, exercises each morning before school, and insists she is fine despite a weight well below normal for her height. Mei's menstrual periods have also stopped. Which finding best supports concern for an eating disorder?", "options": {"A": "Intentional weight loss through team sport training with increased appetite and regular periods", "B": "Strict calorie counting and frequent weighing, but steady weight and regular periods", "C": "Dieting talk among friends while Mei eats regular meals and keeps stable weight", "D": "Food restriction with compulsive exercise and denial of weight loss in a teenager"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Mistaking athletic training with normal intake for disordered eating", "B": "Treating food tracking alone as proof without low weight or amenorrhea", "C": "Treating peer dieting talk as a sign when her own intake is normal"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C178", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Chen brings her three children to a community clinic. Mei, 15, has lost weight and says she feels hopeless. Owen, 2, rarely makes eye contact and has few words. Daniel, 8, takes a stimulant for attention problems. Sunita, a practical nurse, sees each child in turn during the visit and reports to the nurse practitioner afterward. The clinic is busy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Food restriction with compulsive exercise and denial of weight loss in a teenager): Restriction, compulsive exercise, denial of weight loss and low weight together form the pattern linked to eating disorders. Athletic training with a good appetite, calorie counting with steady weight and regular periods, and friends' dieting talk lack the low weight and stopped periods Mei shows. Sunita reports the pattern so the team can assess Mei fully."}, {"id": 1465, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Owen, 2, does not respond to his name, rarely looks at faces, and says only a few words. His mother is worried, but a relative told her to wait until school age. He walks well and enjoys stacking blocks. What should Sunita advise?", "options": {"A": "Share the concerns with the provider so developmental screening can begin now.", "B": "Teach him with flash cards at home and recheck his language at age five.", "C": "Reassure her that limited eye contact is normal at this age.", "D": "Wait until school age, since children develop at very different speeds and catch up."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Deferring assessment and substituting home practice for screening.", "C": "Dismissing the signs, which are not typical at age two.", "D": "Waiting for school, which delays services during a sensitive period."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C178", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Chen brings her three children to a community clinic. Mei, 15, has lost weight and says she feels hopeless. Owen, 2, rarely makes eye contact and has few words. Daniel, 8, takes a stimulant for attention problems. Sunita, a practical nurse, sees each child in turn during the visit and reports to the nurse practitioner afterward. The clinic is busy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Share the concerns with the provider so developmental screening can begin now.): Limited eye contact, few words and reduced response to his name are warning signs, and early screening and intervention give the best developmental benefit. Sunita raises it with the provider now. Waiting or offering reassurance loses valuable time, and good walking does not rule out a language delay."}, {"id": 1466, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Daniel, 8, takes a stimulant for ADHD. His mother says his focus in class has improved, but he eats little at lunch and has not gained weight in six months. He is also taking longer to fall asleep at night. Which nursing action is best?", "options": {"A": "Measure height and weight, ask about appetite and sleep, and report to the prescriber.", "B": "Advise stopping the medication on weekends so that his appetite can recover on those days.", "C": "Advise giving the stimulant later in the evening so appetite returns at dinner.", "D": "Explain that children outgrow the effect, so recheck his weight at the next annual visit."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Advising dose changes outside the prescriber's direction.", "C": "Changing dose timing without the prescriber, which can worsen his sleep", "D": "Delaying follow-up of a growth concern for months"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C178", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Chen brings her three children to a community clinic. Mei, 15, has lost weight and says she feels hopeless. Owen, 2, rarely makes eye contact and has few words. Daniel, 8, takes a stimulant for attention problems. Sunita, a practical nurse, sees each child in turn during the visit and reports to the nurse practitioner afterward. The clinic is busy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Measure height and weight, ask about appetite and sleep, and report to the prescriber.): Stimulants can suppress appetite, delay sleep and slow growth. Measuring height and weight, asking about appetite and sleep, and reporting lets the prescriber adjust care. Changing dose timing herself or waiting a year to recheck leaves the problem unmanaged."}, {"id": 1467, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad, 75, is on a hospice unit and has become increasingly restless over the evening, picking at his blankets and calling out. His son suggests he is simply anxious about dying. His last bowel movement was five days ago. Which response by the practical nurse is best?", "options": {"A": "Ask the son to leave so the room is quieter for his father.", "B": "Agree that it is emotional distress and stay with him until it passes.", "C": "Assess for treatable causes such as pain, retention, constipation or medications, and report to the RN.", "D": "Increase the dose of his opioid on her own to calm him."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Removing family without assessing cause, which may increase distress.", "B": "Assuming emotional causes, which misses physical triggers.", "D": "Changing a prescribed dose without an order."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess for treatable causes such as pain, retention, constipation or medications, and report to the RN.): Restlessness near death often has reversible physical causes, so the nurse assesses pain, retention, constipation and medicines first and reports to the RN. Presuming emotional distress skips that assessment. Increasing an opioid alone is outside her scope and could worsen agitation if constipation or retention is the cause."}, {"id": 1468, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Roy, 69, receives home palliative care and can no longer swallow. Her oral pain medication is still ordered, and her husband asks the practical nurse to crush the tablet into applesauce. Her pain is rated 7 out of 10. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Contact the RN or provider to request an order for a subcutaneous route.", "B": "Place the tablets under her tongue in case they slowly dissolve in saliva.", "C": "Crush the tablets into applesauce and watch her closely as she tries to swallow.", "D": "Hold the pain medication until she is again able to swallow without coughing."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using a route that was not ordered, with unreliable absorption.", "C": "Insisting on oral doses when swallowing is unsafe, risking choking.", "D": "Withholding analgesia, leaving pain uncontrolled."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Contact the RN or provider to request an order for a subcutaneous route.): When swallowing fails, comfort depends on another route, and the practical nurse advocates by contacting the RN or provider for an order. Oral doses risk aspiration, and holding medication leaves her in pain. Placing tablets under the tongue is not an ordered route and gives unreliable absorption."}, {"id": 1469, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Singh, 71, has reduced foot sensation from diabetes. He tells the practical nurse that he walks barefoot at home to strengthen his feet and checks them only if they hurt. His left heel has a small dry crack. Which teaching is best?", "options": {"A": "Apply moisturizer between his toes each day to heal the dry heel crack.", "B": "Inspect both feet daily, including between toes, and wear well-fitted closed footwear indoors.", "C": "Check his feet only when he notices pain or a visible change in them.", "D": "Walk barefoot only on soft carpet indoors, and check feet weekly for changes."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Moisture between toes encourages fungal infection and misses the heel", "C": "Relying on symptoms that reduced sensation hides.", "D": "Allowing barefoot walking and infrequent checks despite lost sensation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Inspect both feet daily, including between toes, and wear well-fitted closed footwear indoors.): With reduced sensation, injuries go unnoticed, so daily inspection and protective footwear prevent ulcers. Barefoot walking, even on carpet, and weekly checks leave a cut or crack unseen, and moisturizer between the toes invites fungal growth. The cracked heel is an early warning to report."}, {"id": 1470, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During an error review on a medical unit, the team learns a nurse mislabeled a specimen after being called away. The labeling station is far from the bedside, and two similar labels were printed. What best describes these two findings?", "options": {"A": "Unavoidable human lapses that training and reminders to be careful will fix", "B": "Latent conditions in the system that set up the error and need correction", "C": "Violations that happened because the nurse chose to ignore the labelling policy", "D": "Active failures by the nurse at the bedside that call for personal discipline"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a system problem as a personal lapse fixed by reminders", "C": "Calling an interrupted slip a deliberate violation of policy", "D": "Treating the person as the only cause and ignoring system design."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Latent conditions in the system that set up the error and need correction): Poor station placement and similar labels are latent conditions that make an error more likely, while the mislabel itself was the active failure. A safety culture fixes the system as well as supporting the nurse. Calling it a deliberate violation or a lapse that reminders will fix prevents learning."}, {"id": 1471, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Pereira, 55, with a cardiac pacemaker, is booked for a brain MRI. He has removed his watch and jewellery. The practical nurse in radiology reviews his screening form, and the technologist is waiting to start. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Hold the scan and report the pacemaker for the radiologist to confirm compatibility.", "B": "Ask him to tell the technologist only if he feels warmth during the scan.", "C": "Proceed, since modern pacemakers are built to tolerate magnetic scanners at normal settings.", "D": "Ask the cardiology clinic to turn the pacemaker off, then proceed with the scan."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relying on symptoms during the scan rather than screening beforehand.", "C": "Assuming every pacemaker is MRI conditional", "D": "Arranging device changes without the radiologist confirming compatibility"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold the scan and report the pacemaker for the radiologist to confirm compatibility.): A pacemaker may malfunction or heat in an MRI unless it is MRI-conditional, so the scan is held until the radiologist decides. Assuming modern devices are safe skips that check, and arranging the device to be switched off bypasses the radiologist. Waiting for symptoms comes too late."}, {"id": 1472, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Baby Amara, 3 hours old, has a respiratory rate of 72, grunts with each breath, and shows flaring nostrils. Her mother says the grunting is just mucus being cleared, and the baby's colour is pink. How should Rosa interpret these findings?", "options": {"A": "Normal adjustment on the first day, needing only reassurance for the worried mother", "B": "Signs of respiratory distress needing prompt reporting, not normal mucus clearing", "C": "Transient fast breathing that settles, so recheck her rate next shift.", "D": "Early distress, but saturation must fall below 90 percent before reporting."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reading grunting as normal when it signals distress.", "C": "Dismissing a sustained high rate with grunting as transient", "D": "Waiting for a set saturation value before reporting distress"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C177", "cluster_text": "Rosa, a practical nurse on a postnatal unit, is assigned four newborns during a night shift. Baby Amara is 3 hours old, Baby Lucas is 36 hours old, and Baby Noor was born to a mother using opioids during pregnancy. Baby Ethan is a 2-week-old seen at a well baby follow-up clinic the same week. The unit is fully staffed tonight.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Signs of respiratory distress needing prompt reporting, not normal mucus clearing): Grunting, nasal flaring and a rate above 60 are classic signs of newborn respiratory distress and need prompt reporting and oxygen monitoring. Treating them as mucus or transient breathing delays care. Waiting for a low saturation risks harm because colour changes are late signs."}, {"id": 1473, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Baby Lucas, 36 hours old, feeds poorly and is sleepy. His temperature has swung between 35.8 and 37.6 C over the last hours, and his mother worries he just needs rest. His heart rate is 168. What should Rosa do?", "options": {"A": "Wrap him in extra blankets and recheck feeding after the next nap.", "B": "Recognize poor feeding and unstable temperature as possible early sepsis, and report to the provider promptly.", "C": "Wait for a fever above 38 C before notifying the provider.", "D": "Offer formula and observe him until the morning assessment."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating temperature swings with warmth alone and delaying the report.", "C": "Waiting for fever, which newborns with sepsis often lack.", "D": "Observing until morning despite concerning early signs."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C177", "cluster_text": "Rosa, a practical nurse on a postnatal unit, is assigned four newborns during a night shift. Baby Amara is 3 hours old, Baby Lucas is 36 hours old, and Baby Noor was born to a mother using opioids during pregnancy. Baby Ethan is a 2-week-old seen at a well baby follow-up clinic the same week. The unit is fully staffed tonight.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Recognize poor feeding and unstable temperature as possible early sepsis, and report to the provider promptly.): Newborns with sepsis can present with poor feeding, lethargy, a fast heart rate and an unstable temperature without any fever, so Rosa reports early. Waiting for a high temperature delays antibiotics. Observing until morning leaves a possibly septic newborn untreated for hours."}, {"id": 1474, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Baby Noor was exposed to opioids before birth. At 30 hours she is irritable, has a high-pitched cry, tremors and poor feeding, and her symptom score is rising. Her mother is anxious and wants to help. Which care measure is best?", "options": {"A": "Offer small amounts of water between feeds to settle her stomach.", "B": "Feed her each time she cries and delay symptom scoring until she settles.", "C": "Hold her upright and rock briskly with music to settle the tremors.", "D": "Swaddle her, keep the room dim, cluster care, and report symptom scores."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offering water, which gives no nutrition and may cause harm.", "B": "Delaying symptom scoring that guides withdrawal care", "C": "Using brisk movement and sound, which increases stimulation and irritability"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C177", "cluster_text": "Rosa, a practical nurse on a postnatal unit, is assigned four newborns during a night shift. Baby Amara is 3 hours old, Baby Lucas is 36 hours old, and Baby Noor was born to a mother using opioids during pregnancy. Baby Ethan is a 2-week-old seen at a well baby follow-up clinic the same week. The unit is fully staffed tonight.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Swaddle her, keep the room dim, cluster care, and report symptom scores.): Infants with neonatal abstinence syndrome do best with low stimulation, swaddling and clustered care, and symptom scores guide the team. Brisk rocking with music adds stimulation. Delaying scoring hides worsening withdrawal, and water offers no nutrition."}, {"id": 1475, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the 2-week visit, Baby Ethan's skin folds look even, but Rosa feels a clunk when she gently abducts his hips. His mother says everything seems normal and that he was born head first after a full-term pregnancy. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Reassure the mother that even skin folds indicate that the hips are normal.", "B": "Report the hip clunk to the provider, since skin folds cannot rule out dysplasia.", "C": "Document the clunk carefully and recheck at the next well baby visit in two months.", "D": "Press the hips firmly several more times to be sure of the clunk."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on skin folds, which are not a reliable test.", "C": "Postponing assessment for months instead of reporting.", "D": "Repeating forceful manoeuvres, which can injure the joint."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C177", "cluster_text": "Rosa, a practical nurse on a postnatal unit, is assigned four newborns during a night shift. Baby Amara is 3 hours old, Baby Lucas is 36 hours old, and Baby Noor was born to a mother using opioids during pregnancy. Baby Ethan is a 2-week-old seen at a well baby follow-up clinic the same week. The unit is fully staffed tonight.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the hip clunk to the provider, since skin folds cannot rule out dysplasia.): A clunk on hip movement suggests instability, and even skin folds do not exclude dysplasia, so Rosa reports for prompt assessment. Repeated forceful testing can harm the joint. Delay for months risks long-term disability."}, {"id": 1476, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Marcus tells Elena he feels emotionally numb and less caring toward clients he once cared for deeply, and he wonders whether he is weak. He still completes every task on time and has never missed a shift. How should Elena interpret this?", "options": {"A": "A personal weakness that stronger nurses overcome through willpower and a positive attitude", "B": "Depression that needs treatment first before any workplace response is considered", "C": "A recognized response to repeated exposure to suffering, called compassion fatigue", "D": "Poor professional boundaries with clients that need a formal performance review"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Labelling it a weakness, which causes shame and silence.", "B": "Jumping to a diagnosis of depression instead of recognizing the pattern", "D": "Treating a stress response as misconduct needing discipline"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C169", "cluster_text": "Marcus, a practical nurse with eight years in palliative care, has begun to dread his shifts, feels numb with dying clients, and snaps at coworkers. He picked up extra shifts last month. Coworkers tease that he is soft, and he has not told anyone how he feels. Elena, his charge nurse, notices the change and meets with him privately. The unit has been short staffed.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A recognized response to repeated exposure to suffering, called compassion fatigue): Numbness, reduced empathy and dread after long exposure to suffering describe compassion fatigue, which is common and responds to support. Labelling it depression or a boundary problem skips that recognition, and calling it weakness increases shame."}, {"id": 1477, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Marcus now has trouble sleeping, frequent headaches and dread before every shift, and he has made two small documentation errors this month. He plans to pick up more shifts to catch up on rest and pay his bills. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Use caffeine on shift days and keep his schedule to stay alert.", "B": "Tell coworkers about his symptoms so they can cover his documentation work.", "C": "Recognize burnout signs, lower his workload, and talk with his manager about support.", "D": "Take the extra shifts and plan to rest on his scheduled days off to recover."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Masking exhaustion with caffeine without addressing the cause", "B": "Sharing his health concerns with peers before seeking proper support", "D": "Adding work, which deepens exhaustion."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C169", "cluster_text": "Marcus, a practical nurse with eight years in palliative care, has begun to dread his shifts, feels numb with dying clients, and snaps at coworkers. He picked up extra shifts last month. Coworkers tease that he is soft, and he has not told anyone how he feels. Elena, his charge nurse, notices the change and meets with him privately. The unit has been short staffed.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recognize burnout signs, lower his workload, and talk with his manager about support.): Exhaustion, headaches, dread and new errors are burnout signs, and recovery needs a lower workload and support. Caffeine only masks the exhaustion, and telling coworkers before seeking support does not reduce his workload. More shifts worsen the risk."}, {"id": 1478, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Marcus is also a parent and says he feels guilty each time he is away from his children. His unit schedules him for three weekends in a row. Which statement best describes work-life balance for a nurse in his situation?", "options": {"A": "Balance means strictly splitting hours equally between work and home each week.", "B": "Balance is a purely personal matter that the employer cannot influence.", "C": "Balance takes personal strategies such as rest and boundaries, plus workplace support like reasonable scheduling.", "D": "Balance comes from the employer scheduling alone, so personal habits play no part."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Setting a rigid hours rule that ignores real work demands", "B": "Treating balance as only personal and ignoring the workplace role.", "D": "Placing all responsibility on the employer and none on the nurse"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C169", "cluster_text": "Marcus, a practical nurse with eight years in palliative care, has begun to dread his shifts, feels numb with dying clients, and snaps at coworkers. He picked up extra shifts last month. Coworkers tease that he is soft, and he has not told anyone how he feels. Elena, his charge nurse, notices the change and meets with him privately. The unit has been short staffed.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Balance takes personal strategies such as rest and boundaries, plus workplace support like reasonable scheduling.): Both personal habits such as boundaries and rest, and employer supports such as scheduling, shape balance. Treating it as only personal ignores the unit's role, and leaving it all to the employer ignores the nurse's own habits. A rigid hours split does not fit nursing work."}, {"id": 1479, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Marcus says he would like counselling but worries that the employee assistance program will tell the hospital about his visits. Elena has the program's brochure in her office and offers to share it. What accurate information should Elena give him about how it works?", "options": {"A": "Counselling is free only after his manager submits a referral form for him.", "B": "Only nurses who have taken sick leave for stress may attend sessions.", "C": "The program reports his visits to the employer to protect client safety.", "D": "The program is confidential, and the employer is not told who uses it."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing the employer controls access to a confidential service", "B": "Assuming a leave record is needed to use the program", "C": "Believing visits are reported to the employer."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C169", "cluster_text": "Marcus, a practical nurse with eight years in palliative care, has begun to dread his shifts, feels numb with dying clients, and snaps at coworkers. He picked up extra shifts last month. Coworkers tease that he is soft, and he has not told anyone how he feels. Elena, his charge nurse, notices the change and meets with him privately. The unit has been short staffed.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The program is confidential, and the employer is not told who uses it.): Employee assistance programs are confidential and voluntary, so Marcus can contact them directly. The employer is not told who uses the service. No referral by the manager, leave record or documented error is needed."}, {"id": 1480, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At the nursing station, a coworker laughs and says, \"Marcus is too soft for palliative care.\" Others stay silent, and Marcus is not present. Elena hears it and knows the coworker has made similar remarks before. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Tell Marcus to toughen up and ignore teasing, since the job requires thick skin.", "B": "Ignore it, since commenting could create more conflict on an already tense team.", "C": "Wait to see whether the coworker repeats the comment before taking any action.", "D": "Address the comment promptly and privately, state expectations, and follow policy."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Putting the burden on the target, which is unfair.", "B": "Ignoring disrespect, which allows it to continue.", "C": "Waiting for repetition, which signals tolerance."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C169", "cluster_text": "Marcus, a practical nurse with eight years in palliative care, has begun to dread his shifts, feels numb with dying clients, and snaps at coworkers. He picked up extra shifts last month. Coworkers tease that he is soft, and he has not told anyone how he feels. Elena, his charge nurse, notices the change and meets with him privately. The unit has been short staffed.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Address the comment promptly and privately, state expectations, and follow policy.): Disrespectful comments harm morale and safety, so Elena addresses the behaviour and uses workplace policy. Ignoring it lets the pattern continue. Telling the target to cope or waiting for repetition signals tolerance, and the earlier remarks make prompt action more important."}, {"id": 1481, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Above his inner ankle, Mr. Okafor, 78, has a shallow, irregular ulcer with brown skin staining, a swollen lower leg, and aching that eases when he elevates it. His pedal pulses are strong and his foot is warm. His daughter visits on Sundays. The RN confirms a venous ulcer. Which nursing action best supports healing?", "options": {"A": "Cover the ulcer with dry gauze and avoid wrapping the leg to prevent pressure", "B": "Rub a warming lotion into the swollen calf and ankle to improve circulation.", "C": "Apply the ordered compression therapy and encourage leg elevation during rest periods", "D": "Encourage long periods of standing to pump blood through the damaged leg veins."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Avoiding compression removes the main treatment for venous return", "B": "Rubbing a swollen leg risks dislodging clots and does not treat venous return", "D": "Prolonged standing increases venous pooling and swelling"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Apply the ordered compression therapy and encourage leg elevation during rest periods): Brown staining, edema, and strong pulses point to venous disease, so compression with elevation improves venous return and healing. Long standing increases pooling and swelling, and rubbing the leg risks harm without helping return. A dry dressing without wrapping ignores the venous cause. Venous ulcers need compression once arterial supply is confirmed adequate."}, {"id": 1482, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Over three weeks, Priya, a practical nurse on a medical unit, has seen staffing fall so low that two clients missed scheduled medications. She raised the problem with coworkers, who agreed but took no action. Her manager dismissed the concern at the last meeting. Which action best fulfils her professional duty to protect clients?", "options": {"A": "Continue discussing the problem with trusted coworkers until someone with authority notices the pattern", "B": "Wait for the next scheduled staff meeting and raise the concern again with her manager", "C": "Post a description of the missed medications on a personal social media account", "D": "Document the events factually and report the risk through formal safety reporting channels"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Venting to peers never reaches decision makers", "B": "Repeating the same route after it failed delays protection", "C": "Public disclosure breaches client confidentiality and employer channels"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Document the events factually and report the risk through formal safety reporting channels): Factual documentation and a formal report move the risk to people with authority to fix staffing, which protects clients. Talking only with coworkers feels supportive but leaves the system problem unaddressed. Raising it again with the same manager repeats a route that has already failed and delays action. Practical nurses report unsafe conditions through established channels and protect confidentiality."}, {"id": 1483, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Jon, 28, comes to a clinic with a throbbing one-sided headache that started after shimmering visual spots. Light and noise worsen his nausea. He drank two coffees this morning and works as a graphic designer. His vital signs are stable. Which nursing measure should the practical nurse offer first while waiting for the nurse practitioner?", "options": {"A": "Help him to a quiet, dim room and apply a cool cloth to his forehead", "B": "Have him sit near a window and sip a cold energy drink", "C": "Apply a heating pad to his neck and encourage gentle stretching exercises", "D": "Give a pain reliever from clinic stock before the practitioner assesses him"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Bright light and stimulants aggravate migraine symptoms", "C": "Heat and movement do not ease migraine and can worsen nausea", "D": "Giving medication without an order, which is outside nursing scope"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Help him to a quiet, dim room and apply a cool cloth to his forehead): Aura with photophobia and phonophobia suggests migraine, and a quiet, dim room with a cool cloth reduces sensory triggers while he waits. A bright window and a stimulating drink worsen symptoms, heat and stretching do not help, and medication is given only as ordered."}, {"id": 1484, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Dubois, 71, has advanced heart failure and receives home care. She says washing, dressing, and cooking leave her too exhausted to eat. Her resting oxygen saturation is 93 percent. Her niece suggests a daily walking program to rebuild strength. Which teaching best helps Mrs. Dubois cope with her fatigue?", "options": {"A": "Stay in bed during the day to store energy for evening family time", "B": "Follow a structured daily walking schedule to gradually rebuild her stamina and strength", "C": "Plan rest between tasks and schedule key activities for her best energy time", "D": "Complete all hygiene and meal preparation in one morning block to finish early"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Prolonged bed rest worsens weakness and deconditioning", "B": "Exercise targets for healthy adults overtax advanced illness", "D": "Grouping tasks together increases exhaustion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Plan rest between tasks and schedule key activities for her best energy time): Energy conservation spreads tasks across the day with rests and puts priority activities when she has the most energy, which fits advanced heart failure. A fixed walking program asks for stamina she does not have and adds to exhaustion. Doing everything in one block drains her before she can eat. Prolonged bed rest weakens muscles and does not restore energy."}, {"id": 1485, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Weiss asks why he feels shaky and sweaty when he has not had a drink since Tuesday. It is Thursday evening and his heart rate is 104. He worries the pneumonia is worsening. The practical nurse explains alcohol withdrawal. Which statement about the usual timeline best informs her teaching and monitoring?", "options": {"A": "Shakiness means the withdrawal has passed and his body is recovering normally now", "B": "Symptoms begin only after five days, so his shakiness is probably anxiety.", "C": "Symptoms start within hours but stop by the second day if he drinks fluids.", "D": "Symptoms can begin within hours and peak between two and three days after the last drink"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Misreads tremor as recovery rather than withdrawal", "B": "Delaying the expected onset and mislabelling withdrawal as anxiety", "C": "Assuming withdrawal ends by day two with fluids"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C171", "cluster_text": "Mr. Harold Weiss, 52, is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia. His wife reports he drinks about a dozen beers daily and had his last drink on Tuesday. He appears underweight, and the admission nurse notes a mild hand tremor. The care plan includes symptom-triggered withdrawal monitoring using the CIWA-Ar scale. The practical nurse is assigned to monitor him and report changes to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Symptoms can begin within hours and peak between two and three days after the last drink): Withdrawal often starts within hours and peaks around two to three days, so Thursday symptoms fit and require close monitoring. Believing it ends by day two would lead staff to stop monitoring too early, and calling it anxiety hides a risk of seizures and delirium. Tremor and a fast heart rate in this window are warning signs, not recovery."}, {"id": 1486, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On Friday morning, Mr. Weiss has visible tremor, drenching sweats, and marked anxiety, and his CIWA-Ar score is 18. He is oriented and has no hallucinations. The order set allows symptom-triggered lorazepam for scores of 10 or higher. His wife is at the bedside. What should the practical nurse do next?", "options": {"A": "Report the score to the RN and prepare the ordered lorazepam within scope", "B": "Withhold medication and reassess in four hours unless a seizure occurs first", "C": "Offer fluids and a back rub, then recheck the CIWA-Ar score at the end of shift", "D": "Record the score in the chart and wait for a physician to write a new order"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Waiting for a seizure delays preventive treatment", "C": "Comfort measures alone leave a high score untreated", "D": "Ignores an existing symptom-triggered order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C171", "cluster_text": "Mr. Harold Weiss, 52, is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia. His wife reports he drinks about a dozen beers daily and had his last drink on Tuesday. He appears underweight, and the admission nurse notes a mild hand tremor. The care plan includes symptom-triggered withdrawal monitoring using the CIWA-Ar scale. The practical nurse is assigned to monitor him and report changes to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the score to the RN and prepare the ordered lorazepam within scope): A score of 18 meets the order threshold, so reporting and giving the ordered benzodiazepine treats withdrawal before seizures or delirium develop. Waiting for a seizure allows avoidable harm. Comfort measures help but do not treat a score this high. The order already exists, so no new order is needed."}, {"id": 1487, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Early Saturday, Mr. Weiss is febrile at 38.9 C, pulling at his IV, and says spiders are crawling on the wall. His heart rate is 128 and he does not know the date. His wife says he often has trouble sleeping. What is the practical nurse's priority action?", "options": {"A": "Stay with him, keep him safe, and report to the RN immediately", "B": "Reassure him the spiders are not real and encourage him to rest", "C": "Give an extra dose of his usual lorazepam from the unit stock now.", "D": "Move him to a quiet room alone and recheck him each hour."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reassurance alone does not address a medical emergency", "C": "Giving a sedative without an order to a client in crisis", "D": "Leaving a delirious client unattended for long periods"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C171", "cluster_text": "Mr. Harold Weiss, 52, is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia. His wife reports he drinks about a dozen beers daily and had his last drink on Tuesday. He appears underweight, and the admission nurse notes a mild hand tremor. The care plan includes symptom-triggered withdrawal monitoring using the CIWA-Ar scale. The practical nurse is assigned to monitor him and report changes to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stay with him, keep him safe, and report to the RN immediately): Fever, hallucinations, disorientation, and a rapid heart rate suggest delirium tremens, a medical emergency needing immediate reporting and constant safety observation. Giving medication without an order or leaving him alone raises risk. Reassurance alone cannot correct a physiological crisis."}, {"id": 1488, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "With a glucose of 3.1 mmol/L, Mr. Weiss is confused and malnourished. The prescriber orders both intravenous thiamine and dextrose, and the RN asks the practical nurse to assist. It is Sunday afternoon. In what order does the practical nurse expect these to be given, and why?", "options": {"A": "Dextrose first, then thiamine later if his confusion has not cleared after the glucose", "B": "Both together in one syringe so the vitamin reaches tissues with the glucose", "C": "Thiamine first, since glucose can trigger Wernicke encephalopathy when thiamine is low", "D": "Dextrose first, because severe low glucose is the more immediately life-threatening problem"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays thiamine until after harm may have occurred", "B": "Mixes medications without compatibility or orders", "D": "Prioritizes glucose without considering vitamin depletion"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C171", "cluster_text": "Mr. Harold Weiss, 52, is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia. His wife reports he drinks about a dozen beers daily and had his last drink on Tuesday. He appears underweight, and the admission nurse notes a mild hand tremor. The care plan includes symptom-triggered withdrawal monitoring using the CIWA-Ar scale. The practical nurse is assigned to monitor him and report changes to the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Thiamine first, since glucose can trigger Wernicke encephalopathy when thiamine is low): Giving thiamine before or with glucose protects a malnourished drinker from Wernicke encephalopathy, because glucose metabolism uses up remaining thiamine. Dextrose first treats the low glucose but risks brain injury. Combining drugs in one syringe is not an ordered or safe practice. Waiting for confusion to clear defeats the purpose of thiamine."}, {"id": 1489, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Park says, I just want to walk Biscuit around the block again, though my doctor wants my weight and sugar numbers lower. Her A1C is 8.2 percent and her sister urges her to join a gym. Which response best supports client-centred care at this visit?", "options": {"A": "Explain that her knee condition makes walking unrealistic and suggest chair exercises only", "B": "Enroll her in the gym program her sister recommends since it offers supervised exercise", "C": "Set weight loss and glucose targets first, then add her walking goal later", "D": "Build the care plan around her walking goal and link her glucose and weight to it"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismisses her goal without assessing safe options", "B": "Adopts a family member's goal instead of hers", "C": "Puts clinician goals ahead of the client's own goal"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C172", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ellen Park, 66, attends a community clinic after learning she has type 2 diabetes and mild knee osteoarthritis. She lives alone with her small dog, Biscuit, takes metformin, and has been prescribed insulin glargine. Her goal is to walk Biscuit around the block again. She reads at a grade-six level and wears glasses. The nurse practitioner asks the practical nurse to provide teaching and support.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Build the care plan around her walking goal and link her glucose and weight to it): Client-centred care starts from what matters to her, and the walking goal connects naturally to better glucose and weight. Setting targets first replaces her priorities with ours and lowers motivation. The gym is her sister's idea, not her stated goal. Ruling out walking without assessment ignores that mild osteoarthritis often allows graded walking."}, {"id": 1490, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Park agrees to start walking and says she will exercise more this month. The practical nurse wants to help her turn this into a goal she can follow and measure. Her knee aches after about ten minutes and she already walks to the mailbox daily. Which goal statement is best?", "options": {"A": "Lose five kilograms over the next two months through diet and daily activity", "B": "Walk Biscuit for ten minutes on Monday, Wednesday, and Friday mornings", "C": "Walk thirty minutes daily starting tomorrow and increase the pace each week", "D": "Exercise more and try to be more active every day this month"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Outcome goal without specific actions", "C": "Target exceeds her current knee tolerance", "D": "Vague wording cannot be measured"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C172", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ellen Park, 66, attends a community clinic after learning she has type 2 diabetes and mild knee osteoarthritis. She lives alone with her small dog, Biscuit, takes metformin, and has been prescribed insulin glargine. Her goal is to walk Biscuit around the block again. She reads at a grade-six level and wears glasses. The nurse practitioner asks the practical nurse to provide teaching and support.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Walk Biscuit for ten minutes on Monday, Wednesday, and Friday mornings): A specific goal states what, how long, and when, and ten minutes matches her knee tolerance, so she can tell whether she succeeded. Exercise more is vague and cannot be checked. Thirty minutes daily ignores the knee ache she reports at ten minutes. A weight target is an outcome with no actions attached."}, {"id": 1491, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After teaching Mrs. Park to inject insulin glargine, the practical nurse wants to confirm her understanding. Mrs. Park nods and says she has it. Her hands shake slightly and she paused twice during the demonstration. Her glasses are in her bag. Which approach best confirms that she understood?", "options": {"A": "Hand her a printed instruction sheet and have her sign a note confirming she read it", "B": "Ask whether she has any questions and accept a simple yes or no answer as confirmation", "C": "Ask her to demonstrate the injection steps and explain them in her own words", "D": "Repeat the entire demonstration a second time at the same pace and then end the session"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "A signature shows receipt, not skill", "B": "Closed questions invite polite agreement", "D": "Repeating teaching does not test comprehension"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C172", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ellen Park, 66, attends a community clinic after learning she has type 2 diabetes and mild knee osteoarthritis. She lives alone with her small dog, Biscuit, takes metformin, and has been prescribed insulin glargine. Her goal is to walk Biscuit around the block again. She reads at a grade-six level and wears glasses. The nurse practitioner asks the practical nurse to provide teaching and support.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask her to demonstrate the injection steps and explain them in her own words): Teach-back asks the learner to show and tell, which exposes gaps such as the pauses she showed. A yes or no answer invites agreement without proof. A signed sheet confirms reading, which is a risk given her grade-six reading level. Repeating the demonstration only repeats teaching and does not check what she learned."}, {"id": 1492, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Park goes home tonight with insulin, a glucose meter, diet changes, foot care instructions, and a follow-up schedule. She looks overwhelmed and tearful. Her daughter will visit tomorrow. The practical nurse has twenty minutes. Which teaching plan best fits this situation?", "options": {"A": "Cover all topics briefly now so nothing is left out before discharge", "B": "Wait until her daughter arrives tomorrow and teach every topic to the daughter alone", "C": "Teach injection and low glucose signs now, and schedule other topics for later", "D": "Give her a large binder covering every topic and review it at the follow-up"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Overloads a learner who is already overwhelmed", "B": "Leaves unsafe insulin use until after discharge", "D": "Written volume replaces teaching and she reads at grade six"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C172", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Ellen Park, 66, attends a community clinic after learning she has type 2 diabetes and mild knee osteoarthritis. She lives alone with her small dog, Biscuit, takes metformin, and has been prescribed insulin glargine. Her goal is to walk Biscuit around the block again. She reads at a grade-six level and wears glasses. The nurse practitioner asks the practical nurse to provide teaching and support.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Teach injection and low glucose signs now, and schedule other topics for later): Limiting teaching to the one or two safety-critical skills lets an overwhelmed learner retain them, with the rest planned for later. Covering everything quickly overloads her and little will be remembered. A big binder suits neither her reading level nor her distress. Delaying insulin teaching means she may inject unsafely tonight."}, {"id": 1493, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Marcus, a practical nurse, is preparing insulin for a client when two call bells ring and a coworker asks him about a transfer. He has drawn up the dose but has not checked it against the order. He has worked three twelve-hour shifts in a row. What should Marcus do?", "options": {"A": "Leave the drawn dose on the counter and answer the bells first", "B": "Give the dose immediately and check the order afterward when things are calmer", "C": "Ignore the interruptions silently and keep going to save the unit time", "D": "Finish the check first, then respond, asking a coworker to cover the bells"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaving a prepared high-alert drug unattended", "B": "Skips verification before administration", "C": "Silent tolerance of interruptions raises error risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Finish the check first, then respond, asking a coworker to cover the bells): Finishing the check without being pulled away and delegating the bells protects the client from an insulin error that interruptions and fatigue make more likely. An unattended drawn dose can be mixed up or taken by someone else. Working on without help leaves him vulnerable to slips. Giving insulin before verifying it removes the main safeguard."}, {"id": 1494, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Nadia, a practical nurse on a medical unit, arrives to find one nurse short. She is assigned eleven clients, including two newly postoperative and one on a heparin infusion, which exceeds the usual assignment. Her charge nurse is busy at a rapid response. She has worked here for six years. What is her best action?", "options": {"A": "Refuse all care until the manager comes to the unit and fixes staffing", "B": "Complete a staffing complaint form after the shift and take the assignment now", "C": "Accept the assignment quietly to avoid conflict and rush through her tasks", "D": "Tell the charge nurse the specific risks and ask for reassignment or support"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Abandoning clients is a professional breach", "B": "Reporting after the shift does not protect clients tonight", "C": "Silent acceptance increases client risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tell the charge nurse the specific risks and ask for reassignment or support): Naming specific risks to the charge nurse and asking for support escalates the problem while care continues. Silent acceptance trades client safety for peace. Refusing all care amounts to abandonment. A form filed later records the problem but leaves tonight's clients unprotected."}, {"id": 1495, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During the surgical safety time out in the operating room, the surgeon says the team is behind and suggests skipping the checklist for this routine case. The practical nurse circulating notes the site marking is not visible. The client is already draped. How should the practical nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Allow the surgeon to proceed this time and raise the missing marking afterward.", "B": "Ask the team to pause and complete the checklist and site verification before incision", "C": "Draw the site mark on the draped client and continue the case.", "D": "Complete the checklist paperwork after surgery to record the missing items and site details."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Deferring a safety concern until after incision", "C": "Marking the site without verification by the surgeon and client", "D": "Late checklist completion misses its prevention purpose"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask the team to pause and complete the checklist and site verification before incision): A checklist works only if done before incision, and a missing mark is a real warning, so pausing is the safest response. Raising it afterward is too late, and marking the site herself skips verification by the team. Completing paperwork afterward cannot prevent wrong-site surgery."}, {"id": 1496, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Ana cares for an adult with type 2 diabetes whose clinical practice guideline recommends a glucose target. The nurse practitioner has written a different order because the client keeps having hypoglycemia. Ana wonders whether to follow the guideline or the order. Which principle applies to the use of guidelines?", "options": {"A": "Guidelines are optional suggestions, so she may ignore them when she disagrees.", "B": "Guidelines inform decisions, while orders and client circumstances guide her care", "C": "Guidelines override orders when the two differ, so she should follow the guideline", "D": "Follow the guideline target on nights and the order during day shifts."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismissing guidelines as optional rather than as informing practice", "C": "Treats a guideline as binding over an individualized order", "D": "Switching sources by shift instead of using individualized care"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C170", "cluster_text": "Ana Silva, a practical nurse, works nights on a thirty-bed medical unit caring for adults with diabetes and heart disease. This week a client received a double dose of a prescribed anticoagulant. The client was monitored and recovered. The unit manager has called a team meeting to review the event, and Ana is asked to participate. Staffing on the unit has been short for two weeks.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Guidelines inform decisions, while orders and client circumstances guide her care): Guidelines summarize evidence for typical clients, while an individualized order reflects this client's recurrent hypoglycemia, so the order guides her care. Guidelines still inform practice, so they are not optional, and alternating sources by shift is inconsistent. Letting the guideline override an order bypasses the prescriber."}, {"id": 1497, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At the meeting, the manager asks how the unit can reduce anticoagulant errors. Several staff suggest naming the nurse responsible, while Ana proposes reviewing how the event happened, including workload and labelling. The client suffered no lasting harm. Which approach best reflects a safety culture that improves care?", "options": {"A": "Review the event privately with the nurse and keep the findings off record.", "B": "Add a double check for her unit only and skip a wider workload review.", "C": "Analyze the system factors, such as workload and labelling, and share learning openly", "D": "Identify the individual nurse and require repeat medication training for her alone"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Keeping findings private, which prevents shared learning", "B": "Making a narrow fix that ignores workload and labelling factors", "D": "Focuses on one person instead of the system"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C170", "cluster_text": "Ana Silva, a practical nurse, works nights on a thirty-bed medical unit caring for adults with diabetes and heart disease. This week a client received a double dose of a prescribed anticoagulant. The client was monitored and recovered. The unit manager has called a team meeting to review the event, and Ana is asked to participate. Staffing on the unit has been short for two weeks.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Analyze the system factors, such as workload and labelling, and share learning openly): Examining system factors and sharing the lessons removes the conditions that allow errors, which is the aim of a safety culture. Private review and a narrow fix without a workload review leave the causes in place. Singling out one nurse treats a system problem as personal failure."}, {"id": 1498, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "The review finds the nurse followed her usual process, but a look-alike label and a short-staffed shift contributed to the double dose. She reported the error herself within minutes. The manager asks what a just culture response involves. Which is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Remove the nurse from medication administration for several months as a protective measure", "B": "Discipline the nurse because a medication error is a breach of professional duty", "C": "Take little action because the client recovered and no lasting harm remained", "D": "Examine the contributing system factors, support the nurse, and reserve discipline for reckless behaviour"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Overreacts to an honest, self-reported error", "B": "Blames individuals for system failures", "C": "Ignores learning when no harm occurs"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C170", "cluster_text": "Ana Silva, a practical nurse, works nights on a thirty-bed medical unit caring for adults with diabetes and heart disease. This week a client received a double dose of a prescribed anticoagulant. The client was monitored and recovered. The unit manager has called a team meeting to review the event, and Ana is asked to participate. Staffing on the unit has been short for two weeks.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Examine the contributing system factors, support the nurse, and reserve discipline for reckless behaviour): A just culture separates human error and at-risk behaviour from recklessness, so this self-reported error with system causes calls for support and fixes. Disciplining every error makes staff hide mistakes. Taking little action wastes a chance to correct the labelling and staffing. Removal from medication duty is out of proportion."}, {"id": 1499, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At 0300, Ana learns two nurses have called in sick and the unit has four fewer staff than planned. Her assignment now includes a client on a heparin infusion and a newly confused client at fall risk. A colleague tells her to be a team player and accept. Which action best fulfills her responsibility?", "options": {"A": "Accept it and document that she objects only after the shift ends", "B": "Tell the charge nurse the specific risks and request reassignment or help", "C": "Decline the heparin client and leave her colleagues to manage the remaining care", "D": "Accept the assignment and prioritize speed to finish before the day shift arrives"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Documenting afterward does not reduce current risk", "C": "Abandons a client without arranging coverage", "D": "Equates silence with teamwork"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C170", "cluster_text": "Ana Silva, a practical nurse, works nights on a thirty-bed medical unit caring for adults with diabetes and heart disease. This week a client received a double dose of a prescribed anticoagulant. The client was monitored and recovered. The unit manager has called a team meeting to review the event, and Ana is asked to participate. Staffing on the unit has been short for two weeks.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tell the charge nurse the specific risks and request reassignment or help): Naming specific risks and requesting help lets the charge nurse adjust assignments while clients stay covered. Speeding up through an unsafe load increases error risk. A late objection records disagreement but does not protect tonight's clients. Leaving one client to others without a plan shifts risk rather than solving it."}, {"id": 1500, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At shift change, Ana finishes her report to the oncoming nurse. Before leaving she realizes she forgot to mention that a client's IV site was reddened. Her coat is on and the nurse is already at the next room. What does transfer of accountability require?", "options": {"A": "Accountability transfers at the start of report, so the omission is the next nurse's issue", "B": "Accountability transfers when the shift ends, so she phones the nurse tomorrow.", "C": "Accountability transfers when the oncoming nurse accepts the report, so she tells her now", "D": "Accountability transfers once the nurse signs the assignment sheet, so no action is needed."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats a report omission as the receiver's fault", "B": "Tying accountability to shift end and delaying a safety report", "D": "Treating a signature as acceptance of an incomplete report"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C170", "cluster_text": "Ana Silva, a practical nurse, works nights on a thirty-bed medical unit caring for adults with diabetes and heart disease. This week a client received a double dose of a prescribed anticoagulant. The client was monitored and recovered. The unit manager has called a team meeting to review the event, and Ana is asked to participate. Staffing on the unit has been short for two weeks.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Accountability transfers when the oncoming nurse accepts the report, so she tells her now): Accountability moves when the receiving nurse accepts the report, so Ana remains responsible for completing it and should tell the nurse now. The end of shift and a signed sheet are not the transfer point. Waiting until tomorrow leaves a reddened IV site unreported."}, {"id": 1501, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a community clinic, Mrs. Tran, newly arrived and speaking little English, needs teaching about her blood pressure medication. Her ten-year-old son offers to translate while the practical nurse explains side effects. A professional interpreter is available by telephone. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Accept the son's help since family members understand the client's language best", "B": "Use the professional interpreter by telephone and invite the son to stay as support", "C": "Ask the son to interpret only the medication names and handle the rest herself", "D": "Speak slowly in plain English with gestures and give an English pamphlet to take home"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses a child for medical interpretation", "C": "Partial child interpretation still risks errors", "D": "Relies on English the client cannot understand"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use the professional interpreter by telephone and invite the son to stay as support): A trained interpreter gives accurate, confidential translation, and the son can remain as comfort. A child may mistranslate or be burdened with sensitive information. Slow English and an English pamphlet do not reach a client with limited English. Using the child for even part of the message keeps the same risk."}, {"id": 1502, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Fortier shows how he mixes his insulins in one syringe each morning. He injects air into both vials, then draws up the NPH first and adds the regular insulin. His glucose runs high after breakfast. The practical nurse reviews the technique. What should she teach?", "options": {"A": "Continue drawing up NPH first, since the longer acting insulin should enter the syringe before regular", "B": "Draw up regular insulin first, then NPH, so intermediate insulin does not contaminate the regular vial", "C": "Shake both vials vigorously so the insulins blend evenly before drawing up either dose", "D": "Use separate syringes for each insulin and inject both doses into the same site"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reverses the standard draw-up order", "C": "Shaking causes bubbles and inaccurate dosing", "D": "Changes the method without a reason or order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C184", "cluster_text": "Mr. Leon Fortier, 59, has type 2 diabetes and mixes regular insulin and NPH insulin in one syringe each morning at home. He also takes metformin. A community nurse practitioner asks the practical nurse to review his technique during a home visit. He has just recovered from influenza, his eGFR is 52, and a CT scan with intravenous contrast is booked for next week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Draw up regular insulin first, then NPH, so intermediate insulin does not contaminate the regular vial): Regular insulin goes in first so cloudy NPH never contaminates the clear vial and the dose stays accurate. Drawing NPH first, as he does, risks contaminating the regular insulin. Vigorous shaking creates bubbles and dosing error. Separate syringes at one site do not address the draw-up order."}, {"id": 1503, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the home visit, Mr. Fortier says he injects insulin into the same spot on his abdomen because absorption is consistent there. The practical nurse notices a firm, raised area. He keeps unopened vials in the refrigerator. Which teaching is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Rotate injection sites within a region, because repeated use causes lumps that alter absorption", "B": "Continue using the same spot, since consistent absorption keeps his glucose readings predictable", "C": "Switch to a different region each day, including the abdomen, thigh and arm.", "D": "Warm the firm area with a heating pad before each injection to speed absorption."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Keeps one site and risks lipohypertrophy", "C": "Moving between regions daily, which makes absorption vary", "D": "Using heat on a lump instead of rotating sites"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C184", "cluster_text": "Mr. Leon Fortier, 59, has type 2 diabetes and mixes regular insulin and NPH insulin in one syringe each morning at home. He also takes metformin. A community nurse practitioner asks the practical nurse to review his technique during a home visit. He has just recovered from influenza, his eGFR is 52, and a CT scan with intravenous contrast is booked for next week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rotate injection sites within a region, because repeated use causes lumps that alter absorption): Rotating within a region prevents lipohypertrophy, which slows and varies absorption and may explain his high readings. Staying in the same spot creates the lump he already has. Changing regions daily makes absorption unpredictable, and heat does not remove a lump."}, {"id": 1504, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Calling the clinic, Mr. Fortier reports that he has influenza, is vomiting, and has eaten little for a day. His glucose is 14.2 mmol/L. He plans to skip his insulin because he is not eating. The practical nurse reviews the sick day rules with him. What should she advise?", "options": {"A": "Stop checking glucose, since illness makes the readings unreliable", "B": "Take double insulin to bring the high glucose down quickly while he is ill", "C": "Continue insulin, check glucose often, sip fluids, and call the care team", "D": "Skip insulin until he is eating normally to avoid a low glucose"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stops monitoring when it matters most", "B": "Doubles insulin without an order", "D": "Stops insulin during illness and risks ketoacidosis"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C184", "cluster_text": "Mr. Leon Fortier, 59, has type 2 diabetes and mixes regular insulin and NPH insulin in one syringe each morning at home. He also takes metformin. A community nurse practitioner asks the practical nurse to review his technique during a home visit. He has just recovered from influenza, his eGFR is 52, and a CT scan with intravenous contrast is booked for next week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue insulin, check glucose often, sip fluids, and call the care team): Illness raises glucose, so insulin continues with frequent checks, fluids, and contact with the team about dose adjustments. Skipping insulin risks diabetic ketoacidosis. Doubling it without an order risks hypoglycemia. Monitoring matters most during illness, so stopping it is unsafe."}, {"id": 1505, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Fortier arrives at the radiology day unit for a CT scan with intravenous contrast. His eGFR is 52 and he took metformin this morning. The radiology nurse asks the practical nurse to review his medication list. He is anxious about the scan. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with the scan and tell him to hold metformin for a week.", "B": "Proceed with the scan and resume metformin that evening as part of routine", "C": "Give an oral antihistamine and steroid before contrast as routine kidney protection.", "D": "Report the metformin and eGFR to the RN or radiologist before contrast"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Making a medication decision herself without consulting the radiologist", "B": "Ignores a lactic acidosis risk with contrast and reduced kidney function", "C": "Giving unordered premedication that does not protect the kidneys"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C184", "cluster_text": "Mr. Leon Fortier, 59, has type 2 diabetes and mixes regular insulin and NPH insulin in one syringe each morning at home. He also takes metformin. A community nurse practitioner asks the practical nurse to review his technique during a home visit. He has just recovered from influenza, his eGFR is 52, and a CT scan with intravenous contrast is booked for next week.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the metformin and eGFR to the RN or radiologist before contrast): Contrast with reduced kidney function raises the risk of metformin-associated lactic acidosis, so the prescriber or radiologist must decide on holding it. Proceeding and advising a hold herself skips that review. Premedication without an order does not protect the kidneys."}, {"id": 1506, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Tanya, 34, visits a clinic for the third time in four months with bruising on her upper arms and a sprained wrist. Her partner stays in the room and answers for her. She avoids eye contact. The practical nurse suspects intimate partner violence. What is the best approach for screening?", "options": {"A": "Speak with her alone and ask direct, kind questions about her safety", "B": "Hand her a pamphlet and leave screening to the next visit.", "C": "Tell her partner that the clinic suspects abuse and will call the police", "D": "Give her a checklist to complete beside her partner."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Postponing screening despite clear warning signs", "C": "Confronting the partner increases risk", "D": "Screening in the partner's presence, which endangers her"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Speak with her alone and ask direct, kind questions about her safety): Private, direct questions protect her safety and give her a chance to disclose. A checklist beside her partner can lead to retaliation, and a pamphlet with delayed screening misses this chance. Telling him about suspicion removes her control and raises risk."}, {"id": 1507, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Gill, 80, a long-term care resident, has a seizure at 1415 in the dining room. His arms stiffen and then jerk for about two minutes, his lips turn dusky, and he is drowsy afterward. The practical nurse stayed with him throughout. Which charting entry is best?", "options": {"A": "1415 all limbs stiff then jerking for two minutes, lips dusky, drowsy after", "B": "1415 resident seemed to have a seizure lasting a couple of minutes, now sleepy", "C": "1415 witnessed tonic clonic seizure, airway maintained, physician notified, resident resting", "D": "1415 seizure due to epilepsy, resident calm and returned to his room afterward"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Vague duration with no movements or colour recorded", "C": "Using interpretation and actions in place of observed details", "D": "Records an unconfirmed cause as fact"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (1415 all limbs stiff then jerking for two minutes, lips dusky, drowsy after): Good documentation records objective details: time, duration, body movements, colour, and the post-seizure state. A vague duration or a summary label without observations cannot be interpreted by the team. Naming epilepsy as the cause is a diagnosis outside nursing scope and may be wrong."}, {"id": 1508, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Lucia, a practical nurse at a clinic, has cared for Mr. Reyes for months. Last week she drove him to the bus stop because it was raining. Today he asked to meet her for coffee on the weekend, and a coworker says her ride was already a violation. How should Lucia interpret these events?", "options": {"A": "Treat the ride as a boundary crossing to review, and decline the social meeting", "B": "Treat both as acceptable because he is not currently acutely ill", "C": "Treat both as violations that require her to end his care immediately", "D": "Treat the ride as a violation requiring a report, and accept coffee as harmless"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Dismisses the power imbalance", "C": "Overreacts and abandons the client", "D": "Labels a minor crossing as a violation while allowing a risky one"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Treat the ride as a boundary crossing to review, and decline the social meeting): A single well-meant ride is a boundary crossing to reflect on and discuss with a supervisor, while a social outing would move toward a violation. Calling the ride a violation but accepting coffee reverses the risk. Illness status does not remove the power imbalance. Ending his care immediately is out of proportion."}, {"id": 1509, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Lund, 74, has a painful, round ulcer on his left toe with a pale base and a punched-out edge. His foot is cool, hairless, and shiny, and pain wakes him at night, easing when the leg hangs down. Pulses are faint. Which nursing action fits these findings?", "options": {"A": "Massage the calf and foot firmly to bring more blood to the painful toe", "B": "Report promptly and hold compression and elevation until the prescriber reviews", "C": "Apply a firm compression wrap and elevate the leg above heart level to reduce swelling", "D": "Soak the foot in warm water to improve circulation and ease the pain"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Massage does not fix inadequate arterial flow", "C": "Compression and elevation worsen arterial ischemia", "D": "Warm soaks can burn insensate ischemic skin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report promptly and hold compression and elevation until the prescriber reviews): Pale punched-out ulcers, cool shiny skin, faint pulses, and rest pain relieved by dependency point to arterial disease, which needs prompt reporting and no compression or elevation. Compression and elevation reduce already poor arterial flow. Warm soaks risk burns and increase tissue demand. Massage cannot restore blood supply."}, {"id": 1510, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Starting insulin at 62, Mr. Alvarez will check his glucose at home. The practical nurse teaches fingerstick technique and meter use. He asks what to do if his reading is very low and he feels shaky. His wife is out of town this week. What must the teaching include?", "options": {"A": "How to adjust his insulin dose himself, then tell the care team at his next visit", "B": "Fingerstick technique and meter use, since his results will be reviewed at his appointment", "C": "When to treat a low reading, what to take, and when to call for help", "D": "The target ranges for his readings, since low readings are uncommon once he adjusts to insulin"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Encourages self-adjustment of insulin beyond his prescribed plan", "B": "Teaching technique alone omits the safety actions for lows", "D": "Treats hypoglycemia as unlikely during early insulin use"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (When to treat a low reading, what to take, and when to call for help): Because he lives alone this week and insulin can cause hypoglycemia, teaching must cover treating lows and when to seek help. Technique alone leaves him unprepared. Hypoglycemia is common with insulin, so calling it rare is wrong. Changing doses independently is outside his plan and unsafe."}, {"id": 1511, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mrs. Thibault, a long-term care resident, gives practical nurse Sam a handmade card and a gold ring that belonged to her late husband, saying she wants him to have it. Sam is fond of her and she is alert. A coworker says to accept it quietly. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Decline both gifts, including the card, and avoid discussing the matter with anyone", "B": "Accept the ring but keep it hidden from her family", "C": "Accept both gifts quietly since she is capable and clearly wishes to give them", "D": "Thank her for the card, decline the ring kindly, and report the offer per policy"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Rejects a harmless token and handles the matter secretly", "B": "Accepts and conceals a valuable gift", "C": "Accepts a valuable gift and blurs the professional boundary"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Thank her for the card, decline the ring kindly, and report the offer per policy): A small token such as a card can be accepted graciously, but a valuable ring creates a conflict of interest and should be declined and reported. Quiet acceptance risks exploitation concerns even with a capable client. Refusing the card hurts her without reason, and silence prevents a policy review. Hiding a ring from family adds secrecy."}, {"id": 1512, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Hall, 72, comes to emergency with sudden severe pain in her right eye, headache, nausea, and halos around lights. The eye is red, the pupil is mid-dilated and fixed, and the cornea looks cloudy. She had just watched a movie in a dim room. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Give acetaminophen for the headache and ask her to rest until the pain settles", "B": "Apply a cold compress and have her lie flat in a dim room", "C": "Report urgently to the RN or physician as a possible acute angle-closure emergency", "D": "Check her vision with a wall chart and report only if it has dropped"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats the headache but not the eye", "B": "Using comfort measures that delay urgent reporting", "D": "Waiting for a vision change before reporting an emergency"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report urgently to the RN or physician as a possible acute angle-closure emergency): Severe eye pain, halos, a fixed mid-dilated pupil, and a cloudy cornea suggest acute angle-closure glaucoma, which can cause permanent vision loss within hours. Comfort measures and waiting for a vision change delay pressure-lowering treatment. Acetaminophen treats only the headache."}, {"id": 1513, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, Mr. Bell cuts his forearm deeply on broken glass, and blood soaks through a thin cloth. His wife asks whether she should remove the cloth to check the wound. He is pale and anxious. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Lift the cloth every minute or so to check whether the bleeding has slowed", "B": "Rinse the wound under running water to clean it before pressure", "C": "Apply a tourniquet above the elbow immediately and note the time", "D": "Apply firm direct pressure over the cloth and call emergency services"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Lifting the dressing disrupts clotting", "B": "Delays pressure to clean the wound", "C": "Uses a tourniquet before direct pressure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Apply firm direct pressure over the cloth and call emergency services): Direct pressure with added dressings, left in place, controls most bleeding while help is called. Lifting the cloth tears away clots and restarts bleeding. A tourniquet is a later option if pressure fails. Rinsing first wastes time while he continues to bleed."}, {"id": 1514, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During recess, Maya, 11, who has type 1 diabetes, becomes shaky, sweaty, and pale. Her glucometer reads 3.1 mmol/L. She is awake and able to swallow. The practical nurse at the school has her insulin pen available. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Give her a slice of whole grain bread and a small piece of cheese", "B": "Give a sugar-free electrolyte drink and recheck her glucose in thirty minutes", "C": "Give a glucose tablet then let her return to play and recheck at lunch", "D": "Give juice or other fast-acting sugar, then recheck her glucose in fifteen minutes"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Slow-digesting food acts too slowly", "B": "Offering a drink with no carbohydrate and delaying the recheck", "C": "Skipping the fifteen minute recheck and returning her to activity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give juice or other fast-acting sugar, then recheck her glucose in fifteen minutes): A reading of 3.1 with symptoms is hypoglycemia, and fast-acting sugar raises it quickly, with a recheck in fifteen minutes. A sugar-free drink gives no carbohydrate, and returning to play without a recheck risks another drop. Bread and cheese absorb too slowly to correct a low right now."}, {"id": 1515, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Chu, 68, with heart failure, tells the practical nurse he removed the salt shaker from his table, so his diet is now low in sodium. He eats canned soup and deli meat daily and his ankles are swollen. He asks whether sea salt is better. What teaching about sodium is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Removing the salt shaker is enough, since table salt is the main source", "B": "Potassium based salt substitutes are safe for heart failure, so he can use them freely.", "C": "Most sodium comes from processed foods, so he should read labels and limit them", "D": "Draining the liquid from canned soup removes most of the sodium it contains."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believes table salt is the main source", "B": "Assuming salt substitutes are harmless despite potassium risks", "D": "Believing sodium sits only in the soup liquid"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Most sodium comes from processed foods, so he should read labels and limit them): Most sodium is hidden in processed and prepared foods such as canned soup and deli meat, so label reading helps his heart failure. Dropping the shaker leaves most intake untouched, and draining soup does not remove the sodium. Sea salt has about the same sodium as table salt."}, {"id": 1516, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Priya, a practical nurse at a clinic, sees a feverish 6-year-old with a blotchy rash, cough, and red eyes. His mother says he has not had his immunizations. A coworker thinks measles is possible. The clinic is busy. What should she do first?", "options": {"A": "Report the case to public health only after laboratory confirmation returns", "B": "Wait for the family to ask for health authority notification before acting", "C": "Isolate him in a private room with airborne precautions and notify the RN", "D": "Seat him in the waiting room with a surgical mask and call the family doctor"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays reporting a suspected reportable disease", "B": "Waits for the client to request notification", "D": "Leaves an airborne risk in a shared space"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Isolate him in a private room with airborne precautions and notify the RN): Suspected measles is airborne and reportable, so isolation and RN notification protect others and start public health follow-up. The family does not trigger reporting duties. A mask in the waiting room still exposes others. Waiting for laboratory confirmation delays contact tracing."}, {"id": 1517, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Theo latches poorly on a standard bottle nipple, and milk leaks from the cleft side. He coughs but his colour stays pink. His birth weight is 3.4 kg and Chloe is discouraged. The practical nurse reviews his feeding. Which action best supports his feeding?", "options": {"A": "Feed him lying flat on his back so milk stays away from the cleft", "B": "Press the cleft lip closed with a finger and use the standard nipple", "C": "Stop oral feeds and request a feeding tube because cleft lip prevents suckling", "D": "Use a specialized soft nipple or squeeze bottle, hold him upright, and burp him often"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Flat positioning raises the risk of choking and aspiration", "B": "Using unadapted equipment and a hands on workaround", "C": "Overestimates the feeding problem of an isolated cleft lip"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C188", "cluster_text": "Chloe Martin, 27, has just given birth vaginally to her first baby, a term boy named Theo, on a postnatal unit. Theo has a unilateral cleft lip with an intact palate. Chloe and her partner Sam are anxious and eager to learn. The practical nurse provides teaching, monitors mother and baby, and reports changes to the RN. Discharge is planned in 24 hours.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use a specialized soft nipple or squeeze bottle, hold him upright, and burp him often): A cleft lip with intact palate often feeds well with adapted equipment, upright positioning, and frequent burping to reduce air and coughing. Feeding him flat increases choking risk, and pressing the lip with a standard nipple does not fix the poor latch. A feeding tube is not needed when oral feeding can be adapted."}, {"id": 1518, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "That evening Theo cries for forty minutes. Sam says he is frustrated and asks whether gentle shaking might calm him. Chloe is exhausted. The practical nurse is giving crying and coping teaching before discharge. Which statement best prevents abusive head trauma?", "options": {"A": "Put on loud music and rock him quickly in your arms until he settles", "B": "Give him a pacifier and a bottle and keep trying until he quiets", "C": "Leave Theo crying in the crib until morning so he learns to self-settle", "D": "Place him safely in his crib, step away, and calm yourself before returning to him"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Vigorous rocking is rough handling that can harm an infant", "B": "Persisting while frustrated instead of taking a break", "C": "Leaving a distressed newborn without checks for hours"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C188", "cluster_text": "Chloe Martin, 27, has just given birth vaginally to her first baby, a term boy named Theo, on a postnatal unit. Theo has a unilateral cleft lip with an intact palate. Chloe and her partner Sam are anxious and eager to learn. The practical nurse provides teaching, monitors mother and baby, and reports changes to the RN. Discharge is planned in 24 hours.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Place him safely in his crib, step away, and calm yourself before returning to him): Putting the baby down safely and stepping away gives a frustrated caregiver time to calm, which is the key prevention message. Fast rocking is rough handling, and persisting while frustrated increases risk. Leaving a newborn unchecked for hours is unsafe, because the baby still needs regular checks."}, {"id": 1519, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On discharge day, Sam brings a car seat and plans to install it facing forward in the back seat so Theo can watch the road. The practical nurse notices the harness straps sit above Theo's shoulders and that he slumps. Which teaching is correct?", "options": {"A": "Place the seat in the front passenger seat so he stays in view", "B": "Forward-facing is acceptable once he can hold his head up unassisted", "C": "Use a rear-facing seat with straps at or below his shoulders", "D": "Rear-facing is needed only for the first month, then he can face forward"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Moves the infant to a risky seat position", "B": "Uses head control as the turning point", "D": "Limits rear-facing to a month"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C188", "cluster_text": "Chloe Martin, 27, has just given birth vaginally to her first baby, a term boy named Theo, on a postnatal unit. Theo has a unilateral cleft lip with an intact palate. Chloe and her partner Sam are anxious and eager to learn. The practical nurse provides teaching, monitors mother and baby, and reports changes to the RN. Discharge is planned in 24 hours.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use a rear-facing seat with straps at or below his shoulders): Rear-facing seats support the head and spine in a crash, and straps at or below the shoulders keep a newborn from sliding up. Head control is not the criterion for turning forward. One month is far too short. The front seat exposes him to airbag injury."}, {"id": 1520, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "While waiting for discharge, Chloe asks what staff would have done if the umbilical cord had appeared after her waters broke, as happened to her sister, whose baby was fine. The practical nurse explains the emergency. Which first action is correct?", "options": {"A": "Call for help and position her knees-to-chest or hips raised", "B": "Cover the cord with a dry cloth and wait for contractions to progress", "C": "Lie her on her left side and give oxygen by mask", "D": "Feel the cord for a pulse, then lay her flat"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Waiting leaves the cord compressed", "C": "Side lying does not relieve cord pressure", "D": "Handling the cord causes spasm and lying flat adds compression"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C188", "cluster_text": "Chloe Martin, 27, has just given birth vaginally to her first baby, a term boy named Theo, on a postnatal unit. Theo has a unilateral cleft lip with an intact palate. Chloe and her partner Sam are anxious and eager to learn. The practical nurse provides teaching, monitors mother and baby, and reports changes to the RN. Discharge is planned in 24 hours.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Call for help and position her knees-to-chest or hips raised): Cord prolapse is an emergency, and calling for help while relieving pressure with a knees-to-chest or head-down position keeps blood flowing to the baby. Side lying does not lift the presenting part, and handling the cord then lying flat adds compression. A dry cloth and waiting fail to relieve pressure."}, {"id": 1521, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a community event, a cyclist has a deep thigh wound with bright blood spurting despite two minutes of firm direct pressure through a thick dressing. The practical nurse has a commercial tourniquet and emergency services are on the way. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Tighten the tourniquet directly over the wound to compress the bleeding vessel", "B": "Release the pressure and wrap the thigh loosely to let it clot", "C": "Keep applying direct pressure until paramedics arrive, and avoid using the tourniquet", "D": "Apply the tourniquet two to three finger widths above the wound, tighten, and record the time"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Places the tourniquet over the wound", "B": "Removes pressure from life-threatening bleeding", "C": "Fails to escalate when pressure has failed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Apply the tourniquet two to three finger widths above the wound, tighten, and record the time): When direct pressure fails to stop life-threatening limb bleeding, a tourniquet above the wound with the time recorded is indicated. Releasing pressure allows more blood loss. Placing it over the wound is ineffective and painful. Continuing pressure alone ignores that it is not working."}, {"id": 1522, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At 1500, Mr. Boyle becomes restless, picks at his sheets, and says the room is spinning. He was oriented at noon. A colleague mentions that an as-needed sedative order is available. His wife is at the bedside. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Check his airway, breathing, and oxygen saturation, then report to the RN", "B": "Increase his oxygen flow to ten litres per minute without waiting for an order", "C": "Give the as-needed sedative now and reassess his agitation once he settles", "D": "Reorient him gently to the date and place, then recheck him at the end of shift"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Changes oxygen without an order", "C": "Sedates before finding the cause", "D": "Treats a change as simple confusion"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C186", "cluster_text": "Mr. Arthur Boyle, 77, is on a surgical ward on day two after a hip replacement. He has COPD and hypertension. The practical nurse is assigned his care this shift and reports changes to the RN. His admission respiratory rate was 16 and oxygen saturation 95 percent on room air. He was pleasant and oriented until this afternoon. His wife is at the bedside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check his airway, breathing, and oxygen saturation, then report to the RN): New restlessness and confusion are early signs of hypoxia or other deterioration, so a rapid assessment and report come before any treatment. Sedation could mask and worsen hypoxia. Reorientation alone delays recognition of a physiological cause. Changing oxygen without an order exceeds scope."}, {"id": 1523, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Over two shifts, Mr. Boyle's early warning scores rose from 1 to 3 to 5. His respiratory rate is now 24, heart rate 108, and temperature 37.9 C. His blood pressure is 118/70 and he denies pain. A colleague says to wait for a single alarming value. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Increase his oxygen from the wall supply and recheck in an hour", "B": "Recheck his vital signs hourly through the night and report only if scores reach nine", "C": "Ask his wife whether he looks different before deciding if she should report", "D": "Report the rising trend to the RN now, using the escalation pathway for his score"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing oxygen without an order and delaying escalation", "B": "Sets a personal threshold above the pathway", "C": "Relying on family impression instead of the measured scores"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C186", "cluster_text": "Mr. Arthur Boyle, 77, is on a surgical ward on day two after a hip replacement. He has COPD and hypertension. The practical nurse is assigned his care this shift and reports changes to the RN. His admission respiratory rate was 16 and oxygen saturation 95 percent on room air. He was pleasant and oriented until this afternoon. His wife is at the bedside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the rising trend to the RN now, using the escalation pathway for his score): Early warning scores exist to catch gradual decline, and a rising trend to 5 triggers escalation before any single value looks extreme. Adjusting oxygen on her own or waiting for his wife's view delays the response. Waiting for a red value loses time."}, {"id": 1524, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The practical nurse phones the RN about Mr. Boyle, who is now confused, with a respiratory rate of 28, oxygen saturation of 89 percent on room air, and heart rate 118. The RN is on another unit and the practical nurse has about one minute. Which report best follows SBAR communication?", "options": {"A": "Describe his full hospital course from admission and wait for the RN to ask questions", "B": "Give vital signs and oxygen saturation, voice concern, and request urgent assessment", "C": "Give vital signs and history, and suggest the RN consider giving more oxygen", "D": "Report only that he looks unwell and ask the RN to check later"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Long narrative buries the key problem", "C": "Omits a clear request for assessment", "D": "Vague concern without data or urgency"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C186", "cluster_text": "Mr. Arthur Boyle, 77, is on a surgical ward on day two after a hip replacement. He has COPD and hypertension. The practical nurse is assigned his care this shift and reports changes to the RN. His admission respiratory rate was 16 and oxygen saturation 95 percent on room air. He was pleasant and oriented until this afternoon. His wife is at the bedside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give vital signs and oxygen saturation, voice concern, and request urgent assessment): SBAR gives the situation, relevant background, assessment, and a recommendation, and here that means data, concern, and a request for urgent review. A long history wastes limited time. Looks unwell lacks specifics. Suggesting oxygen without an explicit request for assessment leaves out the recommendation the RN needs."}, {"id": 1525, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Boyle's wife says he cannot be seriously ill because his lips are not blue. His respiratory rate is 30, oxygen saturation is 88 percent, and he is using accessory muscles to breathe. How should the practical nurse interpret her comment?", "options": {"A": "Cyanosis is a late sign, so he is hypoxic and needs urgent escalation", "B": "Pink lips mean oxygenation is adequate, so he can be monitored for now", "C": "Cyanosis shows only when anemia is present, so his oxygen level is adequate", "D": "Accessory muscle use comes from pain after surgery, so pain relief should come first"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats absence of cyanosis as reassurance", "C": "Misattributing cyanosis to anemia and ignoring the saturation", "D": "Explaining laboured breathing as pain and delaying escalation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C186", "cluster_text": "Mr. Arthur Boyle, 77, is on a surgical ward on day two after a hip replacement. He has COPD and hypertension. The practical nurse is assigned his care this shift and reports changes to the RN. His admission respiratory rate was 16 and oxygen saturation 95 percent on room air. He was pleasant and oriented until this afternoon. His wife is at the bedside.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cyanosis is a late sign, so he is hypoxic and needs urgent escalation): Cyanosis appears late, after significant hypoxia, so rate, effort, and a saturation of 88 percent outweigh normal lip colour. Pink lips do not prove adequate oxygen, and accessory muscle use signals respiratory distress, not pain. Low saturation with laboured breathing needs action now."}, {"id": 1526, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mrs. Osei, 64, is going home after abdominal surgery with a dressing over her incision. The practical nurse demonstrates how to change the dressing and wash her hands. She says she feels ready. Her daughter will help her. Which addition makes the discharge teaching complete?", "options": {"A": "Explain the surgical procedure again, in detail, so she understands why healing takes time", "B": "Advise her to remove the dressing daily to inspect and air the incision", "C": "Provide a written dressing supply list and stress keeping the wound covered between changes", "D": "Review signs of infection, such as spreading redness, warmth, drainage, and fever, and when to call"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Repeats background instead of safety information", "B": "Opening the wound daily raises infection risk", "C": "Gives steps without warning signs"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Review signs of infection, such as spreading redness, warmth, drainage, and fever, and when to call): Teaching dressing steps alone is incomplete, and she must also recognize infection and know when to call for help. A supply list adds convenience but not recognition of problems. Repeating the procedure explanation does not help at home. Removing the dressing daily exposes the incision."}, {"id": 1527, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "With low reading skills, Mr. Evans, 58, has just started antihypertensive medication. He says he left his glasses at home and reads slowly. The practical nurse has a dense four-page pamphlet and a one-page picture sheet. His son is waiting in the car. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Ask his son to read it to him and explain the contents", "B": "Read the pamphlet aloud word for word and then ask whether he has questions", "C": "Use plain language, the picture sheet, and teach-back on key points", "D": "Give the complete pamphlet and ask him to read it before his next visit"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Hands teaching to a family member", "B": "Reading aloud still overloads and invites a polite yes", "D": "Gives dense text to a low-literacy reader"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use plain language, the picture sheet, and teach-back on key points): Plain language, pictures, and teach-back on the essentials match his literacy and confirm learning. A dense pamphlet he cannot read will not be used. Reading it aloud overloads him and the closing question invites polite agreement. Relying on his son leaves the nurse's teaching responsibility unmet."}, {"id": 1528, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Dunn, 68, has dysphagia after a stroke and is scheduled for a barium swallow. The practical nurse notes he coughed and had a wet voice when sipping water this morning. The technologist is ready to start, and he is hungry after fasting. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Give him a thickened barium mixture instead to lower the risk of aspiration", "B": "Report the cough and wet voice to the RN before giving barium and await direction", "C": "Proceed as ordered, since radiology staff will monitor swallowing during the procedure", "D": "Offer a small drink of water to test his swallow, then proceed with barium"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing the ordered contrast without authority", "C": "Assumes someone else will manage the risk", "D": "Testing swallowing at the bedside without an order or assessment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the cough and wet voice to the RN before giving barium and await direction): Coughing and a wet voice suggest aspiration risk, so the team must reassess before barium, which can be harmful if inhaled. Changing the barium mix or testing with water starts the exposure without direction. Assuming radiology will manage it ignores her duty to report what she observed."}, {"id": 1529, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At 1000 on a surgical unit, practical nurse Dana planned to start her round with a dressing change. Before she begins, one client's pain increases, another reports new chest tightness, and a third needs help to the toilet. Which statement best reflects how Dana should prioritize?", "options": {"A": "Reassess, see the client with chest tightness first, and report to the RN", "B": "Treat the client in pain first since pain is the fifth vital sign", "C": "Keep her original plan and complete the dressing change before other tasks", "D": "Help the client to the toilet first because the need is clear and simple"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Ranks pain above a possible cardiac problem", "C": "Keeps a plan that no longer fits", "D": "Chooses the easy task over urgency"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reassess, see the client with chest tightness first, and report to the RN): Priorities change with the clients' condition, and new chest tightness is a possible emergency that must be assessed and reported first. Sticking to the original plan ignores new information. The toileting need can wait briefly. Pain matters, but chest tightness carries higher immediate risk."}, {"id": 1530, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Hassan declines the transfusion, saying his faith forbids it. The physician calls the refusal irrational and urges the nurses to persuade him, since he is quite ill. His oxygen saturation is 92 percent. How should the practical nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Respect his informed decision, confirm he understands the risks, and report to the RN", "B": "Ask a hospital chaplain to persuade him, because the transfusion could save his life", "C": "Contact the hospital ethics service to override his refusal before the next dose", "D": "Document that he is confused and ask the physician to assess capacity"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using indirect pressure on a capable adult", "C": "Seeking to override a capable client's informed refusal", "D": "Labels a capable client as confused"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C192", "cluster_text": "Mr. Abdi Hassan, 61, is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia and a hemoglobin of 68 g/L. He is a practising Jehovah's Witness, alert and fully capable of decisions. His wife, Amina, sits at the bedside, and their adolescent daughter visits daily. The physician recommends a blood transfusion. The practical nurse works with the RN to support the client and record decisions.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Respect his informed decision, confirm he understands the risks, and report to the RN): A capable adult may refuse treatment, and the nurse ensures the decision is informed and reports it. Persuasion through others undermines autonomy, and ethics review cannot override a capable refusal. Substitute consent applies only when the client lacks capacity."}, {"id": 1531, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Overnight Mr. Hassan becomes drowsy and hard to rouse, and his hemoglobin falls to 52 g/L. His wife says he would refuse blood, but no written directive is on the chart. The physician asks whether transfusion may proceed. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Look for an advance directive in the chart or wallet and tell the RN", "B": "Proceed with the transfusion because he can no longer refuse treatment", "C": "Wait for his daughter to arrive and let her make the decision about blood", "D": "Assume his wife's statement is binding and cancel all further treatment"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Overrides previously stated wishes", "C": "Gives a minor the decision", "D": "Treats a spouse's word as a directive"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C192", "cluster_text": "Mr. Abdi Hassan, 61, is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia and a hemoglobin of 68 g/L. He is a practising Jehovah's Witness, alert and fully capable of decisions. His wife, Amina, sits at the bedside, and their adolescent daughter visits daily. The physician recommends a blood transfusion. The practical nurse works with the RN to support the client and record decisions.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Look for an advance directive in the chart or wallet and tell the RN): His earlier refusal still stands when he loses capacity, so the nurse looks for a directive such as a wallet card and involves the RN for urgent decisions. Proceeding ignores the wishes he stated while capable. His wife's statement is important evidence but must be verified. A daughter cannot decide for him."}, {"id": 1532, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Leila, 15, Mr. Hassan's daughter, asks the practical nurse to arrange confidential counselling for anxiety. Her parents expect to decide all her health matters. She seems mature and understands the options and the nurse is concerned about her wellbeing. How should the practical nurse proceed?", "options": {"A": "Cultural expectations require her parents to consent, so no assessment of capacity is needed", "B": "Share her request with both parents at once, since they are responsible for her", "C": "Assess her capacity, since a mature adolescent may consent, and involve the RN", "D": "Parental wishes decide all adolescent health care, so refer her back to her parents"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Replaces capacity assessment with culture", "B": "Breaches her confidentiality without assessment", "D": "Assumes parents always override the adolescent"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C192", "cluster_text": "Mr. Abdi Hassan, 61, is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia and a hemoglobin of 68 g/L. He is a practising Jehovah's Witness, alert and fully capable of decisions. His wife, Amina, sits at the bedside, and their adolescent daughter visits daily. The physician recommends a blood transfusion. The practical nurse works with the RN to support the client and record decisions.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess her capacity, since a mature adolescent may consent, and involve the RN): Consent depends on understanding and maturity, not on age alone, so assessing her capacity with the RN respects both her rights and her safety. Automatic parental override ignores capacity. Culture should be respected but does not replace an assessment. Telling her parents without assessment breaches confidentiality."}, {"id": 1533, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "While visiting, Mr. Hassan's younger son Omar, 5, has linear bruises on his back that his father says come from a traditional healing practice. Omar flinches when touched. The practical nurse feels uneasy about appearing disrespectful. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Confront the father and demand an explanation for each separate mark before reporting the injuries", "B": "Wait until several more visits show a clear pattern of injury before deciding", "C": "Accept the explanation as cultural practice and document the bruises without reporting", "D": "Report the injuries to the RN and child protection services, without accusing the father"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confronts the parent before reporting, risking escalation", "B": "Delays reporting a child at possible risk of harm", "C": "Lets culture explain a possible injury"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C192", "cluster_text": "Mr. Abdi Hassan, 61, is admitted to a medical unit with pneumonia and a hemoglobin of 68 g/L. He is a practising Jehovah's Witness, alert and fully capable of decisions. His wife, Amina, sits at the bedside, and their adolescent daughter visits daily. The physician recommends a blood transfusion. The practical nurse works with the RN to support the client and record decisions.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the injuries to the RN and child protection services, without accusing the father): A reasonable concern for a child's safety must be reported, and cultural respect shows in a calm, non-accusing manner. Accepting the explanation without reporting may leave a child at risk. Confronting the father can escalate danger and tamper with the investigation. Waiting for a pattern delays protection."}, {"id": 1534, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Dube, 52, lives next door to you and arrives at the only rural clinic within 80 kilometres with a sprained wrist. You are the sole nurse on duty, and no colleague can attend for hours. He jokes that he still plans to borrow your truck this weekend. What is the best nursing approach?", "options": {"A": "Treat the wrist but leave out charting details, so his visit stays private from town gossip.", "B": "Treat him, name the dual relationship, keep boundaries professional, and tell your manager.", "C": "Send him home to wait for another nurse, because neighbours cannot receive care from you.", "D": "Treat him like any other client and say nothing about the friendship or the truck request."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confuses privacy with omitting required documentation", "C": "Over-applies the rule and leaves a client without needed care in a remote setting", "D": "Ignores the conflict of interest and skips safeguards"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Treat him, name the dual relationship, keep boundaries professional, and tell your manager.): Care cannot be withheld when you are the only nurse available, so the safest path is to treat him, name the overlap in roles, and keep the relationship strictly professional during the visit. Telling your manager gives the employer a chance to arrange support next time. Treating him as usual with no precautions leaves the dual relationship unmanaged, and sending him away abandons a client who needs care. Leaving out documentation breaches your accountability, because records protect both of you."}, {"id": 1535, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At today's visit Marcus smiles and says he is a consenting adult, recovering well, and would like to take you for coffee after his next appointment. He adds that no one in town would need to know. You feel flattered. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Decline kindly, restate your professional role, document it, and consult your supervisor.", "B": "Say nothing about the invitation and avoid being alone with him until his discharge date arrives.", "C": "Accept a coffee once his treatment ends, since he is an adult who has given consent.", "D": "Tell him you are flattered but must wait until he is discharged, then you can meet."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Avoids the issue without addressing or reporting it", "C": "Believes client consent removes the professional boundary", "D": "Treats discharge as an end to the power imbalance"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C182", "cluster_text": "Marcus Bell, 30, receives outpatient mental health follow-up in a small town after a hospital stay for depression. You are his practical nurse and also know his family from the local hockey league. Over several weeks he has praised your care, has begun texting you personally, and left a gift card at the reception desk for you.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline kindly, restate your professional role, document it, and consult your supervisor.): The nurse holds more power and knowledge than a client with depression, so personal or romantic involvement breaches professional boundaries even when the client seems willing. A clear, kind refusal with documentation and supervisor input protects Marcus and you. Accepting after discharge keeps the same problem alive, and silence leaves the boundary unclear. Consent from the client never converts a therapeutic relationship into a personal one."}, {"id": 1536, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Marcus's gift card is worth $100 and came with a note thanking you for saving his life. The card was left at reception, and he says he will be hurt if you refuse. Your employer has a gift policy. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Keep the card this once, because refusing might damage his recovery and his trust in you.", "B": "Hold the card in a drawer until discharge, then decide with him how to use it.", "C": "Accept the card and share it with unit staff so nobody benefits in a personal way.", "D": "Return the card politely, explain that policy prohibits it, and tell your manager."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Puts client feelings ahead of the gift policy", "B": "Delays the decision without informing the employer", "C": "Disguises acceptance by redistributing the gift"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C182", "cluster_text": "Marcus Bell, 30, receives outpatient mental health follow-up in a small town after a hospital stay for depression. You are his practical nurse and also know his family from the local hockey league. Over several weeks he has praised your care, has begun texting you personally, and left a gift card at the reception desk for you.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Return the card politely, explain that policy prohibits it, and tell your manager.): Gifts of significant value can blur boundaries and create an expectation of special treatment, so the employer's policy and your regulatory standards direct you to decline. Returning it kindly with an explanation preserves the relationship, and telling your manager keeps the situation transparent. Sharing it with colleagues still means accepting it, and storing it delays a clear answer. Insisting by the client does not change the standard."}, {"id": 1537, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At a staff education session in a small community, a colleague who has just moved to town asks you to explain the term dual relationship, because she realized she now treats her own hairdresser and her child's coach. Which description best fits the term?", "options": {"A": "A situation in which a nurse treats two members of one family together.", "B": "A working relationship in which the nurse reports to both a manager and a clinical supervisor.", "C": "A boundary violation in which the nurse accepts gifts, texts or favours from a client.", "D": "A personal, social or business tie with a client existing beside the nurse and client relationship."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confusing it with caring for several family members", "B": "Confuses it with dual reporting lines at work", "C": "Confusing a boundary violation with the existence of a second relationship"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C182", "cluster_text": "Marcus Bell, 30, receives outpatient mental health follow-up in a small town after a hospital stay for depression. You are his practical nurse and also know his family from the local hockey league. Over several weeks he has praised your care, has begun texting you personally, and left a gift card at the reception desk for you.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A personal, social or business tie with a client existing beside the nurse and client relationship.): A dual relationship exists when a nurse has another connection with a client, such as friendship, family tie, business or neighbour contact, in addition to the nurse and client role. Small communities make this common, so nurses must recognize and manage it rather than ignore it. Treating family members is a different matter, a boundary violation is a behaviour rather than the extra tie, and reporting to two supervisors concerns employment structure."}, {"id": 1538, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Marcus now phones you after hours several times a week, says you are the only one who understands him, and has stopped speaking in group sessions. A new graduate says this closeness is fine since he is an adult who agrees to it. Which principle should guide your reply?", "options": {"A": "Close contact is acceptable when it improves a client's mood and attendance at appointments.", "B": "Boundary limits can be loosened when the client consents in writing to closer contact.", "C": "The nurse holds the greater power, so keeping boundaries is the nurse's duty.", "D": "Responsibility depends on how long the nurse has known the client personally."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Judges closeness by client benefit and not professional standards", "B": "Treating client consent as removing the nurse's duty", "D": "Linking responsibility to length of acquaintance rather than role"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C182", "cluster_text": "Marcus Bell, 30, receives outpatient mental health follow-up in a small town after a hospital stay for depression. You are his practical nurse and also know his family from the local hockey league. Over several weeks he has praised your care, has begun texting you personally, and left a gift card at the reception desk for you.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The nurse holds the greater power, so keeping boundaries is the nurse's duty.): The therapeutic relationship is unequal because the nurse has knowledge, access and influence, and a client in distress is vulnerable and may depend on that nurse. For that reason the nurse is accountable for setting and keeping boundaries, whatever the client says or signs. Length of acquaintance does not change this. Good outcomes do not justify crossing the line."}, {"id": 1539, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "During handover you overhear a coworker say she would rather not be assigned to Marcus because he mentioned his partner is a man, and her values conflict with that. She asks the charge nurse to swap her quietly. What is your best response?", "options": {"A": "Say nothing, since she has not refused care in front of Marcus or other clients.", "B": "Suggest she tell Marcus about her views so he can choose a nurse who suits him.", "C": "Support the quiet swap, because a nurse whose values conflict should not provide that care.", "D": "Remind her that standards and human rights duties require non-discriminatory care, and tell the manager."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Overlooks discriminatory attitudes because no care was withheld yet", "B": "Shifts the burden of her bias onto the client", "C": "Treats personal values as a valid reason to refuse care"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C182", "cluster_text": "Marcus Bell, 30, receives outpatient mental health follow-up in a small town after a hospital stay for depression. You are his practical nurse and also know his family from the local hockey league. Over several weeks he has praised your care, has begun texting you personally, and left a gift card at the reception desk for you.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Remind her that standards and human rights duties require non-discriminatory care, and tell the manager.): Nurses must provide care without discrimination on grounds protected by human rights law, and personal values do not justify refusing a client. Reminding her of that duty and informing the manager addresses the behaviour while protecting Marcus. A quiet swap normalizes the bias, and silence lets it pass. Telling Marcus about her views would harm him and place the burden on him."}, {"id": 1540, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A rural caller, 58, phones the telehealth line with two days of burning urination and asks you to call in an antibiotic because the nearest clinic is far away. You are a practical nurse covering the line. Which action fits your scope of practice?", "options": {"A": "Gather assessment details, advise on urgency per protocol, and report to the RN or physician.", "B": "Phone in a common antibiotic that the physician usually orders, since distance limits his access.", "C": "Diagnose a urinary tract infection from his symptoms and recommend an over-the-counter remedy.", "D": "Explain that telehealth scope is narrower, so advise fluids and have him call back later."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believes remote practice allows prescribing", "C": "Makes a medical diagnosis outside practical nurse scope", "D": "Assumes scope shrinks with distance and withholds assessment"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gather assessment details, advise on urgency per protocol, and report to the RN or physician.): Scope of practice stays the same whether care is delivered in person or by phone, so you assess, apply the protocol and refer to the RN or physician who can diagnose and prescribe. Calling in an antibiotic is prescribing, which is outside your scope. Diagnosing the infection is a medical act. Offering only fluids ignores the duty to assess and escalate."}, {"id": 1541, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After an upper endoscopy with throat spray and sedation, Mr. Alvarez, 50, lies awake in the recovery area, says he is thirsty, and asks for a sandwich and coffee. Which check must the nurse complete before offering any sips of water?", "options": {"A": "Palpate the abdomen for rigidity and rebound tenderness that might signal perforation.", "B": "Confirm the gag reflex has returned by watching him swallow or touching his throat.", "C": "Check oxygen saturation on room air to confirm he breathes adequately unassisted.", "D": "Auscultate bowel sounds in all four quadrants to confirm peristalsis has resumed."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Looks for a complication but delays oral intake check", "C": "Monitors oxygenation but skips swallowing safety", "D": "Checks gut motility instead of airway protection"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Confirm the gag reflex has returned by watching him swallow or touching his throat.): The throat spray and sedation blunt the gag reflex, so giving fluids before it returns risks aspiration. Confirming the reflex, usually by watching a small sip or testing the back of the throat, comes first. Bowel sounds do not show airway protection. Oxygen saturation and abdominal palpation are useful aftercare checks but do not tell you whether he can swallow safely."}, {"id": 1542, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Okafor, 67, eight hours after hip surgery, has an order for hydromorphone 1 mg subcutaneously every three hours as needed. She rates pain 7 out of 10 but is drowsy and rouses to voice. Respirations are 9 per minute and SpO2 is 93 percent. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Give the full dose, then recheck her sedation and respirations about thirty minutes afterward.", "B": "Withhold the dose, stimulate her, keep monitoring, and report to the RN or physician.", "C": "Give half the ordered dose, since her pain is severe and breathing is only mildly slow.", "D": "Hold the dose, document that she often naps, and reassess her pain at the next round."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives an opioid despite sedation and slow breathing", "C": "Alters an ordered dose without authority and ignores respiratory depression", "D": "Attributes sedation to napping and delays reporting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Withhold the dose, stimulate her, keep monitoring, and report to the RN or physician.): Drowsiness with a respiratory rate of 9 and falling saturation signals opioid-induced respiratory depression, so the dose must be withheld and she needs stimulation and close monitoring. Reporting lets the team adjust analgesia safely. Giving half a dose is changing an order and still adds opioid. Giving the full dose is unsafe, and waiting until the next round leaves worsening sedation unreported."}, {"id": 1543, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lavoie, 72, has a venous leg ulcer and a new order for multilayer compression bandaging. Her pulses were not documented at the last visit, so before applying compression at this dressing change you assess the foot and lower leg carefully. Which finding would require you to hold the compression and report?", "options": {"A": "Brown staining at the ankle with mild swelling", "B": "Shallow ulcer with moderate clear weeping drainage today", "C": "Dry itchy flaking skin around the wound edges", "D": "Absent pedal pulses with a cool, pale foot."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Mistaking venous staining for a contraindication", "B": "Mistaking usual venous ulcer drainage for arterial compromise", "C": "Mistaking stasis dermatitis for arterial compromise"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Absent pedal pulses with a cool, pale foot.): Compression on a limb with poor arterial supply can cause tissue damage, so absent pulses, a cool pale foot and slow capillary refill mean you should hold it and report for vascular assessment. Staining, swelling, clear drainage and dry itchy skin are common with venous disease and do not prohibit compression. Checking circulation before each application protects her."}, {"id": 1544, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A community clinic chart labels Mr. Whitebear, 61, a First Nations client, as non-compliant after two missed wound-care visits. He tells you staff once dismissed his request to include a healer in his care. Which interpretation of the missed visits reflects cultural safety?", "options": {"A": "The missed visits show low motivation, so a stricter appointment contract with reminders should be set.", "B": "Missed visits may reflect past disrespect, so ask him what would make care feel safe.", "C": "The missed visits probably come from transport difficulty alone, so taxi vouchers should be arranged.", "D": "Culture matters mainly for language, so an interpreter should be booked before his next visit."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Blames the client and uses a punitive approach", "C": "Reduces a complex barrier to a practical issue", "D": "Equates cultural safety with language support"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Missed visits may reflect past disrespect, so ask him what would make care feel safe.): Cultural safety asks the nurse to look at power imbalances and past experiences of the health system, and to let the client define what feels safe. His story suggests a trust barrier, so asking him is the most respectful next step. A contract punishes him, vouchers address only one possible barrier, and an interpreter assumes the issue is language, which he never raised."}, {"id": 1545, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Late in a shift you hold eight clients on a medical unit, and two have just become unwell, one needing frequent vital signs. You realize you cannot safely finish scheduled dressing changes and medications on time. Which action best applies safe practice principles in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Ask the unregulated care provider to complete the dressing changes and give the medications.", "B": "Tell the charge nurse, prioritize by client need, and ask for help.", "C": "Finish the quick tasks first and quietly skip the dressing changes without reporting it.", "D": "Skip your break and finish every task slowly while keeping the problem to yourself."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delegates tasks outside the provider's role", "C": "Omits care silently and hides unsafe workload", "D": "Tries to cope alone and risks errors from fatigue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tell the charge nurse, prioritize by client need, and ask for help.): Safe practice means speaking up early when workload threatens care, so the charge nurse can redistribute work and you can prioritize the unwell clients. Skipping care silently leaves tasks undone and unreported. Working through a break while hiding the problem raises the risk of error. Unregulated care providers cannot give medications or perform wound care."}, {"id": 1546, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ana, 40, arrives to visit her father, who is on droplet precautions for influenza, and she has a mild cough herself. She asks whether she may sit with him all afternoon because she does not want him to be alone. Which nursing action best reflects droplet precaution principles?", "options": {"A": "Explain that visits are forbidden during any isolation and offer a phone call instead.", "B": "Allow the visit if she sits beyond two metres, since droplets cannot travel farther.", "C": "Teach hand hygiene and mask use, and ask her to postpone the visit while symptomatic.", "D": "Provide a gown and gloves only, since droplet precautions focus on contact with body fluids."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applies a blanket ban that the precaution does not require", "B": "Misjudges droplet travel distance and omits a mask", "D": "Confuses droplet precautions with contact precautions"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Teach hand hygiene and mask use, and ask her to postpone the visit while symptomatic.): Droplet precautions rely on a mask within about two metres, hand hygiene and limiting spread, and Ana's cough means she could pass infection to others or be a risk to him. Teaching the measures and asking her to wait while symptomatic balances safety with family needs. Distance alone is not protective, gowns and gloves do not replace a mask, and isolation does not forbid every visit."}, {"id": 1547, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Elder Margaret, 78, hesitates when you ask her to remove her ceremonial pouch for a chest exam at a community clinic. She quietly says the hospital once took things from her family. Which understanding of truth and reconciliation commitments should guide your next action?", "options": {"A": "Explain that the pouch can stay only if a supervisor has approved it first.", "B": "Apologize on behalf of the hospital, then ask her to remove it for the exam.", "C": "Acknowledge the history, ask her preferences, and adapt the exam so the pouch stays with her.", "D": "Ask her to describe residential school history so that you can understand her reaction."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adding an approval barrier to a respectful request", "B": "Acknowledging harm in words but not adapting the exam", "D": "Burdens the client with educating the nurse"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acknowledge the history, ask her preferences, and adapt the exam so the pouch stays with her.): Reconciliation commitments in nursing include recognizing historical and ongoing harm, respecting Indigenous practices and sharing decisions with the client. Adapting the exam and asking Margaret what she needs shows that respect. An apology without adapting, or a supervisor hurdle, does not honour her preference, and asking her to explain painful history makes her do the educating."}, {"id": 1548, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In an emergency department, Liam, 3, arrives with bruises on both ears and his trunk, said by his father to be from falling off a couch. The bruises are different colours and Liam flinches when touched. The father is calm and cooperative. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Report to the charge nurse or physician, document objectively, and follow the duty to report.", "B": "Ask the father to bring Liam back for a recheck in a week before reporting", "C": "Wait for imaging and bloodwork to confirm abuse before involving anyone beyond the treating team.", "D": "Report only after the physician agrees abuse is likely, and note it in his chart"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delaying the duty to report while waiting for another visit", "C": "Waits for diagnostic confirmation before reporting", "D": "Waiting for a physician's opinion before meeting a personal duty to report"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report to the charge nurse or physician, document objectively, and follow the duty to report.): The duty to report is triggered by reasonable grounds to suspect abuse, not by proof, and the pattern of bruises in uncommon places with different ages is concerning. Objective documentation and prompt reporting through the team and child protection protects Liam. Waiting a week or for a physician's agreement delays protection, and waiting for tests does the same."}, {"id": 1549, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "You are partway through preparing a scheduled oral medication when a call bell sounds from room 12 and the client's daughter shouts from the hallway that her mother is suddenly short of breath. The medication is due within its window. Which response correctly applies prioritization?", "options": {"A": "Call the manager for direction on which task to complete first.", "B": "Tell the daughter to bring her mother to the nursing station for assessment.", "C": "Set the medication aside safely, then assess the client in distress immediately.", "D": "Finish giving the scheduled medication first, because its time window is closing soon."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying the response to ask for direction in an emergency", "B": "Moving a breathless client instead of assessing her where she is", "D": "Prioritizes a scheduled task over an acute change"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Set the medication aside safely, then assess the client in distress immediately.): Sudden shortness of breath is a potential airway or breathing emergency, so it outranks a routine medication that remains within its administration window. Securing the medication prevents errors, and going at once allows assessment and escalation. Calling for direction, moving the client, or finishing the dose first all delay a qualified assessment."}, {"id": 1550, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Priya, 28, is 30 weeks pregnant. At a clinic visit she says her ankles swell by evening and ease overnight, with more backache and tiredness. Her blood pressure is 156/98 mmHg, up from 112/70, and she reports a new headache. Which finding should the nurse report first to the RN or physician?", "options": {"A": "The increasing backache and tiredness, signs of preterm labour needing urgent review.", "B": "The evening ankle swelling that resolves overnight, a likely sign of cardiac failure.", "C": "The pairing of ankle swelling and fatigue, which suggests a thyroid problem needing bloodwork.", "D": "The rise in blood pressure with the new headache, a possible sign of preeclampsia."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Over-reads common pregnancy discomfort as labour", "B": "Treats expected dependent edema as the main danger", "C": "Misattributes common symptoms to thyroid disease"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The rise in blood pressure with the new headache, a possible sign of preeclampsia.): A marked blood pressure rise with a new headache after 20 weeks suggests preeclampsia, which can progress quickly to seizures, so it takes priority. Dependent edema that eases overnight, backache and fatigue are common in late pregnancy and are less urgent on their own. Reporting the pattern allows prompt medical assessment."}, {"id": 1551, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "You drew up 2 mg of hydromorphone but the order is for 1 mg, so half remains in the syringe. The narcotic count is logged, and the unit is busy. A colleague is nearby on the phone. What is the correct action for the remaining drug?", "options": {"A": "Waste the 1 mg with a second nurse witnessing, and both sign the record.", "B": "Keep the syringe in the locked drawer and adjust the count at the shift change.", "C": "Return the extra 1 mg to the original vial and record the amount returned.", "D": "Waste it with the RN's verbal approval and record it later on the sheet."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Retains drug without proper disposal and defers the count", "C": "Returning drawn drug to stock, which contaminates the supply", "D": "Wasting without a witness present to sign at the time"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Waste the 1 mg with a second nurse witnessing, and both sign the record.): Controlled substances require two licensed witnesses for waste, with both signing the record so the count stays accurate and diversion is deterred. Verbal approval without a signing witness breaks that safeguard. Returning drawn drug to a vial contaminates stock. Saving a drawn-up dose for another client is unsafe and prohibited."}, {"id": 1552, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At school, Maya, 8, who uses insulin, is trembling, sweaty and dizzy. She is alert and swallowing normally, and her glucometer reads 3.0 mmol/L. Lunch is scheduled in forty minutes, and her teacher says she skipped her morning snack today. What should the school nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Give 15 grams of fast-acting carbohydrate such as juice, then recheck glucose in fifteen minutes.", "B": "Give a sugar-free snack with protein to hold her until lunch is served.", "C": "Give a glucagon injection at once, since her glucose is below 4.0 mmol/L.", "D": "Give 15 grams of fast-acting carbohydrate and skip rechecking until lunch is served."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Offering food with no quick sugar for a documented low", "C": "Uses glucagon for a child who can still swallow", "D": "Omitting the fifteen minute recheck after treatment"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give 15 grams of fast-acting carbohydrate such as juice, then recheck glucose in fifteen minutes.): A child who is alert and able to swallow with a glucose of 3.0 mmol/L needs fast-acting carbohydrate immediately, followed by a recheck to confirm recovery. Skipping the recheck leaves the result unconfirmed, and a protein snack lacks quick sugar. Glucagon is reserved for those who cannot swallow safely or are unconscious."}, {"id": 1553, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sam tells you he has noticed no change in his mood after six days on sertraline and plans to stop it because it clearly is not working. He wonders why it was added. What is the most appropriate nursing response?", "options": {"A": "Advise him to double the dose for a week to speed up the effect.", "B": "Suggest stopping sertraline and relying on lithium alone since it works faster.", "C": "Tell him effects normally appear within hours, so little change means the dose should be raised.", "D": "Explain it usually takes several weeks, so keep taking it, and report his concerns."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing a dose without the prescriber to hasten onset", "B": "Stopping a medication based on a wrong view of timing", "C": "Expects an immediate antidepressant effect"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C183", "cluster_text": "Sam Whitford, 44, attends a mental health clinic for bipolar disorder with a recent depressive episode. His regimen includes lithium, sertraline started last week, risperidone at bedtime, and lorazepam 1 mg nightly for sleep, taken for several months. You are the practical nurse completing his medication review under the direction of the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain it usually takes several weeks, so keep taking it, and report his concerns.): Antidepressants of this class usually need two to six weeks of regular use before mood improves, so stopping at six days would lose the chance of benefit. Encouraging him to continue and reporting his doubts lets the prescriber review the plan. Doubling the dose or dropping sertraline are decisions for the prescriber."}, {"id": 1554, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A week later Sam tells you his neck feels stiff and twisted, his jaw is tight, and he is drooling slightly. He says it started the evening after he took risperidone, and he is worried. Which action is most appropriate for the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Report the stiffness promptly to the RN or prescriber, since it may be a side effect.", "B": "Reassure him it will pass and suggest stretching and relaxation exercises at home.", "C": "Document that stiffness is voluntary and encourage him to relax his muscles when nervous.", "D": "Tell him to skip tonight's risperidone and resume when the stiffness has gone."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Minimizes a possible acute dystonic reaction", "C": "Treats an involuntary muscle effect as voluntary", "D": "Advises changing the regimen without authority"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C183", "cluster_text": "Sam Whitford, 44, attends a mental health clinic for bipolar disorder with a recent depressive episode. His regimen includes lithium, sertraline started last week, risperidone at bedtime, and lorazepam 1 mg nightly for sleep, taken for several months. You are the practical nurse completing his medication review under the direction of the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the stiffness promptly to the RN or prescriber, since it may be a side effect.): Twisting neck posture with jaw tightness soon after starting an antipsychotic suggests acute dystonia, an involuntary reaction that can affect swallowing. It needs prompt reporting and treatment. Home stretches delay care. Advising him to skip doses is outside your scope, and calling the movements voluntary misreads the cause."}, {"id": 1555, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sam phones the clinic with two days of vomiting and diarrhea, a fine hand tremor and unsteady walking. He says he has also cut back on salt to eat healthier. What is the nurse's best understanding of this situation, and what should happen next?", "options": {"A": "A fine tremor is expected, so he should rehydrate with plain water and monitor himself.", "B": "Low salt intake protects the kidneys, so he should keep restricting salt and sip fluids.", "C": "Fluid and sodium loss can raise lithium levels, so report promptly and expect a blood level.", "D": "Vomiting means he lost his dose, so he should repeat the lithium after eating."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismissing a sign of toxicity as a routine effect", "B": "Believes sodium restriction protects against lithium toxicity", "D": "Repeating a dose after vomiting instead of reporting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C183", "cluster_text": "Sam Whitford, 44, attends a mental health clinic for bipolar disorder with a recent depressive episode. His regimen includes lithium, sertraline started last week, risperidone at bedtime, and lorazepam 1 mg nightly for sleep, taken for several months. You are the practical nurse completing his medication review under the direction of the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fluid and sodium loss can raise lithium levels, so report promptly and expect a blood level.): Lithium is cleared by the kidneys in the same way as sodium, so dehydration and a low-salt diet cause the body to retain it and levels can climb to toxic range. Vomiting, diarrhea, tremor and unsteady gait are warning signs that need urgent reporting and a level check. Repeating a dose after vomiting or restricting salt would worsen toxicity."}, {"id": 1556, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sam says he is worried about dependence on lorazepam and plans to stop taking it tonight after using it nightly for months. He asks the nurse whether that is a good idea, and he wants an answer today. Which response is best?", "options": {"A": "Suggest an over-the-counter sleep aid while he stops, with no prescriber involvement.", "B": "Support stopping tonight, since he has taken it under a year and dependence risk is small.", "C": "Advise him not to stop suddenly, and arrange a prescriber-supervised taper because withdrawal can cause seizures.", "D": "Suggest halving the tablets for a week, then stopping without contacting anyone."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Substitutes another sedative without medical review", "B": "Underestimates dependence and withdrawal risk after months of use", "D": "Plans a taper without prescriber oversight"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C183", "cluster_text": "Sam Whitford, 44, attends a mental health clinic for bipolar disorder with a recent depressive episode. His regimen includes lithium, sertraline started last week, risperidone at bedtime, and lorazepam 1 mg nightly for sleep, taken for several months. You are the practical nurse completing his medication review under the direction of the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise him not to stop suddenly, and arrange a prescriber-supervised taper because withdrawal can cause seizures.): After months of nightly use the brain adapts, and abrupt cessation can cause rebound anxiety, insomnia and seizures. His concern is valid, so the safest path is a gradual taper supervised by the prescriber. Stopping at once is risky, self-directed tapering lacks monitoring, and substituting an over-the-counter sedative adds unsupervised drug effects."}, {"id": 1557, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Alicia confides in you that she feels belittled daily and wonders if she should toughen up and say nothing. She asks what you would advise as a colleague who supports a respectful workplace and wants her to stay in nursing. What is the best guidance you can offer?", "options": {"A": "Request transfer to another unit without telling anyone why the move is being sought.", "B": "Name the behaviour, keep dated notes, and report it through policy.", "C": "Toughen up and avoid conflict, since new graduates earn acceptance with time and effort.", "D": "Speak privately to each coworker first, and report only if the behaviour continues for months."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Avoids the problem rather than addressing it", "C": "Normalizes lateral violence as part of being new", "D": "Delays reporting while the harm continues"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C181", "cluster_text": "Alicia Moreau, a new graduate practical nurse, joined a long-term care home six weeks ago. Her preceptor rolls her eyes at questions, and two coworkers exclude her from breaks. The home has had three falls this month, including Mr. Tran, 88, and the team is starting a quality improvement project with Alicia on the committee.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Name the behaviour, keep dated notes, and report it through policy.): Lateral violence harms the nurse and weakens safety, and a respectful workplace requires that it be named and reported through the organization's process, with documentation and support. Enduring it normalizes the behaviour. A long delay prolongs harm. Quietly transferring leaves the cause unaddressed for the next new nurse."}, {"id": 1558, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "The committee designs an hourly rounding routine to reduce falls, and the manager wants it in all four units next week. Alicia has just learned the Plan Do Study Act cycle. According to that model, how should the team proceed?", "options": {"A": "Implement it on all four units at once to avoid inconsistent care between the teams.", "B": "Test the routine on one unit briefly, collect data, then decide whether to expand.", "C": "Start on one unit, skip data collection, and expand once staff say it feels helpful.", "D": "Finalize the routine on paper, then roll it out once the committee approves it."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Skips testing and spreads an unproven change", "C": "Replacing measurement with staff impressions", "D": "Treating approval as a substitute for testing"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C181", "cluster_text": "Alicia Moreau, a new graduate practical nurse, joined a long-term care home six weeks ago. Her preceptor rolls her eyes at questions, and two coworkers exclude her from breaks. The home has had three falls this month, including Mr. Tran, 88, and the team is starting a quality improvement project with Alicia on the committee.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Test the routine on one unit briefly, collect data, then decide whether to expand.): The Plan Do Study Act cycle uses small, short, transparent tests so the team can see whether a change works before committing resources to it. Starting on one unit gathers real data with limited risk. Rolling out everywhere first defeats the purpose, and judging success by staff impressions or committee approval replaces measurement with opinion."}, {"id": 1559, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After the one-unit trial, a nurse says residents seem steadier on their feet. The committee wants to know whether hourly rounding truly reduced falls on the unit, and the manager asks for a fair comparison that other units could repeat. Which measure best answers the question?", "options": {"A": "Survey family members once about how safe their relatives feel during visiting hours.", "B": "Compare the number of falls per 1,000 resident days before and after the trial.", "C": "Count only the falls that caused injury and leave out minor or near-miss events.", "D": "Rely on staff impressions that residents appear steadier, since staff know residents well."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses a single subjective sample as the outcome", "C": "Narrows data and hides near misses", "D": "Substitutes anecdote for measurement"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C181", "cluster_text": "Alicia Moreau, a new graduate practical nurse, joined a long-term care home six weeks ago. Her preceptor rolls her eyes at questions, and two coworkers exclude her from breaks. The home has had three falls this month, including Mr. Tran, 88, and the team is starting a quality improvement project with Alicia on the committee.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Compare the number of falls per 1,000 resident days before and after the trial.): A rate such as falls per 1,000 resident days adjusts for the number of residents and days, so before and after periods can be compared fairly. Impressions are subjective and cannot show a change. Counting injuries alone hides near misses that signal risk. One family survey measures feelings rather than outcomes, and it cannot be repeated by other units."}, {"id": 1560, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Tran, 88, fell in his room last night while walking to the bathroom, and now uses a walker. He takes a sedative at bedtime and often does not call for help. Which approach to preventing another fall is best?", "options": {"A": "Apply a lap restraint at night, which stops him from leaving the bed unsupervised overnight.", "B": "Use a bed alarm alone, since it alerts staff to each attempt he makes to rise.", "C": "Combine a toileting routine, footwear check, medication review and call bell within reach.", "D": "Raise both bed rails, the most effective single measure to keep him safe during the night."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses restraint, which is unsafe and not least restrictive", "B": "Depends on one device and ignores causes", "D": "Relies on rails that raise injury risk"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C181", "cluster_text": "Alicia Moreau, a new graduate practical nurse, joined a long-term care home six weeks ago. Her preceptor rolls her eyes at questions, and two coworkers exclude her from breaks. The home has had three falls this month, including Mr. Tran, 88, and the team is starting a quality improvement project with Alicia on the committee.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Combine a toileting routine, footwear check, medication review and call bell within reach.): Falls come from several causes, so a bundle addressing toileting needs, footwear, sedating medications and access to the call bell works better than one tool. Bed rails can cause injury and entrapment. A bed alarm only alerts staff after he has started to move. Restraints raise harm and are not the least restrictive option."}, {"id": 1561, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Tran is now on bed rest after his fall, and the unit routine is to turn residents every four hours. During your skin check you find a patch of non-blanching redness over his sacrum. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Massage the reddened area firmly to improve blood flow before turning him over again.", "B": "Place a donut cushion under the sacrum and reassess the area in the morning.", "C": "Reposition him on an individualized schedule, offload the sacrum, and reassess his skin each turn.", "D": "Turn him every four hours as the policy states, since that interval suits every resident."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Massages damaged tissue and increases harm", "B": "Uses a device that concentrates pressure and delays reassessment", "D": "Applies a fixed interval despite early skin damage"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C181", "cluster_text": "Alicia Moreau, a new graduate practical nurse, joined a long-term care home six weeks ago. Her preceptor rolls her eyes at questions, and two coworkers exclude her from breaks. The home has had three falls this month, including Mr. Tran, 88, and the team is starting a quality improvement project with Alicia on the committee.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reposition him on an individualized schedule, offload the sacrum, and reassess his skin each turn.): Non-blanching redness is early pressure injury, so repositioning should match his skin and risk, usually more often than every four hours, with pressure kept off the sacrum. Fixed intervals ignore the damage already present. Massage damages fragile tissue. Donut cushions concentrate pressure at their edges and delay reassessment."}, {"id": 1562, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A screening colonoscopy is booked for 0800 tomorrow for Mr. Chen, 58. He phones the clinic and says he plans a normal breakfast and lunch today, with the bowel preparation at supper, because the leaflet looked complicated and he lives alone. What should the nurse advise him to do?", "options": {"A": "Switch to clear fluids as instructed, and drink the full preparation on schedule.", "B": "Take half of the preparation only, since a full dose may cause cramping and dehydration.", "C": "Follow the clear fluid diet today, but drink the preparation only if stools are firm.", "D": "Have a low-fibre breakfast and lunch, then drink the preparation at supper as planned."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Under-doses the preparation and leaves residue", "C": "Making the preparation conditional on stool appearance", "D": "Confusing low-fibre food with a clear fluid diet"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Switch to clear fluids as instructed, and drink the full preparation on schedule.): A clear view of the bowel lining depends on following the diet restriction and finishing the preparation exactly as directed, which usually means clear fluids only the day before. Low-fibre food still leaves residue. Taking half the dose cleans poorly. Drinking it only when stools look firm risks an incomplete examination and a repeat procedure."}, {"id": 1563, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Nurse Chloe, 36, discharged Mr. Ortiz, 42, from her hospital unit after a six-week stay for depression. He now messages asking her out, noting he is no longer her client. She asks you what standards say. What is your best response?", "options": {"A": "A relationship is acceptable if she informs her manager and keeps it outside work hours.", "B": "Standards say discharge does not end the power imbalance, so decline and consult her regulator.", "C": "Discharge ends the nurse and client relationship, so dating is acceptable once he has left care.", "D": "A relationship is acceptable after one month if he agrees and nobody at work objects."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believes disclosure to a manager makes it acceptable", "C": "Treats discharge as ending the professional obligation", "D": "Uses an arbitrary waiting period"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Standards say discharge does not end the power imbalance, so decline and consult her regulator.): Regulatory standards recognize that knowledge of a client's vulnerabilities and the earlier power difference persist after discharge, especially after mental health care. Chloe should decline and seek guidance from her regulatory body. Time limits, agreement and manager notification do not remove the risk of exploiting a former client."}, {"id": 1564, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Dalton, 45, last drank alcohol 36 hours ago. He is sweating and anxious with a hand tremor, heart rate 118 and blood pressure 164/96 mmHg, and a protocol order for lorazepam is on his chart. His wife says he had a seizure once before. What is the best nursing approach to his care?", "options": {"A": "Give the ordered benzodiazepine, monitor sedation, and report any seizure activity at once.", "B": "Give thiamine and fluids first, and delay the benzodiazepine until his tremor worsens.", "C": "Give the benzodiazepine, then reassess his vital signs in four hours and report if worse.", "D": "Withhold medication until a breath test reads zero, then reassess his tremor and heart rate."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delaying the protocol sedative until symptoms worsen", "C": "Reassessment interval too long for withdrawal", "D": "Delays treatment until alcohol is cleared"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C190", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on an evening shift in a community hospital emergency department, working with RN Cole and Dr. Singh. The department has an alcohol withdrawal protocol, a naloxone kit at the nurses' station, and a policy of giving thiamine for suspected alcohol-related confusion. Four clients with substance use concerns present during your shift, each needing prompt assessment and reporting within your scope.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give the ordered benzodiazepine, monitor sedation, and report any seizure activity at once.): His timing, tremor, tachycardia and hypertension indicate alcohol withdrawal, and benzodiazepines treat it and prevent seizures. Ongoing monitoring of sedation and reporting of seizures keeps him safe. Delaying the sedative lets withdrawal progress, a four-hour gap is too long for close monitoring, and waiting for a zero breath test postpones treatment."}, {"id": 1565, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Hale, 54, is brought to the emergency department confused, with a wide unsteady gait and abnormal eye movements. His breath smells of alcohol, his glucose is 5.6 mmol/L, and his friend says he has barely eaten for weeks and has lost weight. Which nursing priority fits these findings?", "options": {"A": "Report urgently as possible Wernicke encephalopathy, and prepare thiamine before glucose-containing fluids.", "B": "Prepare intravenous dextrose first, since confusion usually signals a low blood sugar level.", "C": "Treat the confusion as alcohol withdrawal and request a higher dose of benzodiazepine.", "D": "Document intoxication and let him sleep it off in a quiet room, rechecking hourly."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Gives glucose first, which can worsen thiamine deficiency", "C": "Misreads confusion with ataxia as withdrawal alone", "D": "Dismisses neurological signs as simple intoxication"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C190", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on an evening shift in a community hospital emergency department, working with RN Cole and Dr. Singh. The department has an alcohol withdrawal protocol, a naloxone kit at the nurses' station, and a policy of giving thiamine for suspected alcohol-related confusion. Four clients with substance use concerns present during your shift, each needing prompt assessment and reporting within your scope.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report urgently as possible Wernicke encephalopathy, and prepare thiamine before glucose-containing fluids.): Confusion, ataxia and abnormal eye movements in a person with heavy alcohol use form the classic picture of Wernicke encephalopathy, which needs prompt thiamine because glucose can precipitate worsening injury. His normal glucose also rules out hypoglycemia. Calling it intoxication risks permanent brain damage, and benzodiazepines do not treat the thiamine deficiency."}, {"id": 1566, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Paramedics bring in Kai, 28, found unresponsive in a park with a used syringe nearby. His respirations are 6 per minute and shallow, his pupils are pinpoint, and his lips are blue. No pulse problem is reported. What is the most appropriate nursing action at this moment?", "options": {"A": "Start chest compressions immediately, since an unresponsive client with a syringe has likely arrested.", "B": "Give oxygen by nasal cannula at 2 L per minute and monitor his saturation closely.", "C": "Wait for him to wake while monitoring, and give naloxone only if his breathing stops.", "D": "Recognize a likely opioid overdose, open the airway, ventilate with a bag-mask, and prepare naloxone."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Starting compressions in a client who still has a pulse", "B": "Low-flow oxygen cannot correct a respiratory rate of 6", "C": "Delays reversal and ventilation in respiratory depression"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C190", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on an evening shift in a community hospital emergency department, working with RN Cole and Dr. Singh. The department has an alcohol withdrawal protocol, a naloxone kit at the nurses' station, and a policy of giving thiamine for suspected alcohol-related confusion. Four clients with substance use concerns present during your shift, each needing prompt assessment and reporting within your scope.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Recognize a likely opioid overdose, open the airway, ventilate with a bag-mask, and prepare naloxone.): Slow shallow breathing, pinpoint pupils and cyanosis point to opioid toxicity, and the immediate priority is oxygenation through airway positioning and bag-mask ventilation while naloxone is made ready. Compressions are for a client without a pulse. Low-flow oxygen cannot support a respiratory rate of 6. Waiting delays reversal."}, {"id": 1567, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Twenty minutes after naloxone 0.4 mg intravenously, Kai is awake, sitting up and asks to leave because he feels fine. His respirations are 14 per minute and his oxygen saturation is 97 percent. His friend is willing to take him home but has no phone. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Give a second naloxone dose now to prevent his symptoms from returning later on.", "B": "Keep observing him, since naloxone may wear off before the opioid does.", "C": "Allow him to leave with the friend, who should call 911 if he becomes drowsy.", "D": "Discharge him, since being awake and talking shows the overdose has fully resolved."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives an unneeded dose and risks withdrawal", "C": "Releases him before the observation period ends", "D": "Equates alertness with resolution of the overdose"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C190", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on an evening shift in a community hospital emergency department, working with RN Cole and Dr. Singh. The department has an alcohol withdrawal protocol, a naloxone kit at the nurses' station, and a policy of giving thiamine for suspected alcohol-related confusion. Four clients with substance use concerns present during your shift, each needing prompt assessment and reporting within your scope.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep observing him, since naloxone may wear off before the opioid does.): Naloxone acts for a shorter time than many opioids, so respiratory depression can return and he needs continued monitoring according to the unit protocol. Good vital signs right now do not guarantee safety later. Discharging early or letting him leave puts him at risk, and giving more naloxone without a clinical reason can cause acute withdrawal."}, {"id": 1568, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Peters, 66, has open-angle glaucoma. He tells the clinic nurse that his drops sting and his vision seems unchanged, so he stopped them two weeks ago. His last eye pressure reading was borderline, and he is due for review next month at the eye clinic. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Accept his decision since vision is stable, and schedule his next routine checkup in six months.", "B": "Reassure him that pressure was borderline, so a break is fine until his review.", "C": "Explain that drops protect vision silently, explore the stinging, and notify the prescriber.", "D": "Advise him to use the drops every second day to reduce the stinging."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats lack of symptoms as lack of disease", "B": "Treating a borderline pressure as permission to pause drops", "D": "Reducing prescribed frequency without a prescriber order"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain that drops protect vision silently, explore the stinging, and notify the prescriber.): Open-angle glaucoma damages the optic nerve silently, so unchanged vision does not mean the disease is controlled. Reinforcing why the drops matter, finding out why they sting and informing the prescriber lets a better option be found. Treating borderline pressure as permission to pause, or changing the schedule without an order, risks permanent vision loss."}, {"id": 1569, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sofia, 15, visits a community clinic because she falls asleep in class. She goes to bed at 0030 for an 0800 start and sleeps about six and a half hours. Her mother says everyone needs eight hours and Sofia is fine. What should the nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Teens need about eight to ten hours, so an earlier consistent bedtime and fewer screens help.", "B": "Eight hours is enough, so her mother is right and screen use needs no change.", "C": "Teens need about eight to ten hours, so a long afternoon nap will fix the debt.", "D": "Teens need more sleep on weekdays than weekends, so long weekend sleeping repairs the deficit."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Applying an adult sleep need to an adolescent", "C": "Using a long nap to replace night sleep", "D": "Believes weekend catch-up sleep resolves chronic deficit"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Teens need about eight to ten hours, so an earlier consistent bedtime and fewer screens help.): Sleep needs vary by age, and adolescents typically need eight to ten hours, so about six and a half hours explains her daytime sleepiness. A consistent earlier bedtime and limiting screens address the cause. An adult sleep rule, a long afternoon nap and weekend catch-up sleep all miss how chronic shortage builds."}, {"id": 1570, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Rowan, 29, uses they and them pronouns and attends a clinic for a wound check. The intake form offers only male or female. A coworker says that treating everyone neutrally means identity should not be mentioned. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Ask Rowan to explain their identity in detail so that it can be recorded correctly.", "B": "Ask Rowan how they wish to be addressed, record it, and report the form's limits.", "C": "Leave identity out and record clinical details only, since neutrality gives equal treatment to everyone.", "D": "Mark the form according to appearance and use only the name on the health card."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Places an intrusive burden on the client", "C": "Confuses neutrality with erasing identity", "D": "Assumes identity from appearance"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask Rowan how they wish to be addressed, record it, and report the form's limits.): Inclusive practice means asking clients how they wish to be addressed and respecting their answer, which supports trust and safety. Raising the form with the manager helps change a barrier for others. Guessing from appearance disrespects Rowan, erasing identity is not neutral, and demanding detailed explanations is intrusive and unrelated to the wound."}, {"id": 1571, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Grant, 35, phones the clinic after a hot-water scald to his forearm. The skin is red and painful with small intact blisters, about the size of his palm. A neighbour offered a home remedy. What does the nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Cool the burn under running water for two minutes, then apply petroleum jelly and a dressing.", "B": "Cool the burn under running water for twenty minutes, then loosely cover with non-stick pad", "C": "Apply ice directly to the burn for ten minutes, then cover it with a light bandage.", "D": "Cool the burn briefly with cold water, then pop the large blisters and cover loosely."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Cooling time far too short and greasy ointment applied", "C": "Uses ice that can deepen tissue injury", "D": "Breaking blisters, which raises infection risk"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cool the burn under running water for twenty minutes, then loosely cover with non-stick pad): Running cool water for about twenty minutes limits tissue damage and relieves pain, and a loose non-stick dressing protects the area. Direct ice can injure tissue. Popping blisters removes a natural barrier and risks infection. Two minutes of cooling is too short, and petroleum jelly traps heat."}, {"id": 1572, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Brandt now refuses food and takes only a few sips of water. Nina cries and asks you to arrange tube feeding because she believes her mother will starve. Mrs. Brandt's goals are comfort only. What is the most appropriate nursing response to Nina?", "options": {"A": "Suggest a feeding tube trial so Mrs. Brandt does not starve in her final days.", "B": "Encourage Nina to coax each meal, since eating protects against rapid decline.", "C": "Tell Nina to ask the physician for intravenous nutrition and wait before any other care.", "D": "Explain that reduced appetite is expected, and offer mouth care and sips."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Equates declining intake with starvation and suffering", "B": "Believes forced eating prevents decline", "C": "Delays comfort care while waiting for nutrition"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C185", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Elsie Brandt, 83, has advanced heart failure and has entered her last days in a hospice, with a goal of comfort only and a do-not-resuscitate order on her chart. Her daughter Nina stays at the bedside and is distressed by changes she sees. You are the practical nurse caring for Mrs. Brandt, working with the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that reduced appetite is expected, and offer mouth care and sips.): Loss of appetite is a normal part of dying, as the body no longer needs or can use food, and forcing nutrition can cause discomfort. Compassionate explanation, mouth care and offered sips meet comfort goals. Tube feeding and intravenous nutrition do not improve comfort in the dying phase, and coaxing meals adds distress."}, {"id": 1573, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Hours later Mrs. Brandt's knees and feet are mottled purple and feel cool, and her temperature is 36.0 degrees C. Nina asks whether her mother has developed an infection and wants someone to act. What is the best nursing interpretation and action?", "options": {"A": "Report mottling as poor circulation and request an urgent order for oxygen therapy.", "B": "Report a probable new infection and request blood cultures and antibiotics for her.", "C": "Document mottling as a pressure injury and reposition her every hour to relieve it.", "D": "Recognize mottling and cool extremities as expected dying changes, offering light blankets."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating an expected dying change as a reversible problem", "B": "Misreads mottling as new infection in a comfort-only plan", "C": "Mistaking dying mottling for a pressure injury"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C185", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Elsie Brandt, 83, has advanced heart failure and has entered her last days in a hospice, with a goal of comfort only and a do-not-resuscitate order on her chart. Her daughter Nina stays at the bedside and is distressed by changes she sees. You are the practical nurse caring for Mrs. Brandt, working with the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Recognize mottling and cool extremities as expected dying changes, offering light blankets.): As circulation slows near death, blood is diverted from the limbs, producing mottling and coolness, and normal temperature argues against infection. Light blankets provide comfort. Heating pads can burn fragile skin, and testing and antibiotics conflict with the comfort goal. Mottling at death is not a pressure injury or a reversible circulation problem."}, {"id": 1574, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Overnight Mrs. Brandt's breathing becomes irregular, with pauses of about twenty seconds followed by a series of deep breaths. She appears peaceful and her color is unchanged. Nina is frightened and asks if her mother is choking or suffering. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Call a code and begin CPR, since pauses in breathing signal a respiratory arrest.", "B": "Apply oxygen by mask to treat the pauses, and reassure Nina the colour will improve.", "C": "Reposition her flat on her back to even out the breathing, and reassure Nina.", "D": "Identify Cheyne-Stokes breathing as common near death, reassure Nina, and position her comfortably."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Starts CPR despite a do-not-resuscitate order", "B": "Treating expected Cheyne-Stokes breathing as a problem needing oxygen", "C": "Lying flat worsens secretions and breathlessness near death"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C185", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Elsie Brandt, 83, has advanced heart failure and has entered her last days in a hospice, with a goal of comfort only and a do-not-resuscitate order on her chart. Her daughter Nina stays at the bedside and is distressed by changes she sees. You are the practical nurse caring for Mrs. Brandt, working with the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Identify Cheyne-Stokes breathing as common near death, reassure Nina, and position her comfortably.): Cheyne-Stokes breathing, with cycles of deep breaths and pauses, is a common sign in the last days and is often not distressing to the client. Explaining this to Nina and positioning for comfort fits the goals of care. CPR contradicts the do-not-resuscitate order, oxygen does not correct this pattern, and lying flat increases breathlessness."}, {"id": 1575, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Brandt is now unresponsive. A staff member standing at the bedside discusses her prognosis with a colleague, and Nina, who has barely left the room, asks whether her mother can hear anything at all. Which response from the nurse is best?", "options": {"A": "Explain that hearing is lost first, so talking now matters mainly for the family.", "B": "Tell Nina that unresponsive clients cannot hear, so she may rest and talk freely nearby.", "C": "Say hearing may persist, speak to her gently, and encourage Nina to talk and touch her.", "D": "Advise Nina that hearing persists but speech should cover essential care information only."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reversing the order in which senses fade", "B": "Assumes unresponsive means unaware", "D": "Limiting bedside talk to clinical information"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C185", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Elsie Brandt, 83, has advanced heart failure and has entered her last days in a hospice, with a goal of comfort only and a do-not-resuscitate order on her chart. Her daughter Nina stays at the bedside and is distressed by changes she sees. You are the practical nurse caring for Mrs. Brandt, working with the RN.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Say hearing may persist, speak to her gently, and encourage Nina to talk and touch her.): Hearing is thought to be among the last senses to fade, so conversations at the bedside should be respectful and kind. Speaking gently, explaining care and inviting Nina to talk and touch her comforts both. Assuming she cannot hear permits harmful talk, limiting speech to care information loses connection, and saying hearing goes first is inaccurate."}, {"id": 1576, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At the bedside, Mr. Lee shares a room with Mr. Leigh, whose name is similar. Both men are drowsy, and the medication record lists Mr. Lee in bed 2. Which identification method is correct before you give the evening medication to him?", "options": {"A": "Check the room and bed number against the medication record, then give it as ordered.", "B": "Have him state his name and date of birth, then match both to wristband and record.", "C": "Compare the name on the bedside whiteboard with the record, then give the medication.", "D": "Ask whether he is Mr. Lee and give the dose when he nods in agreement."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses location as an identifier", "C": "Relies on a whiteboard that may be wrong", "D": "Uses a leading question that a drowsy client may accept"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C191", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a 30-bed medical unit. After two near-miss medication events involving wrong-patient mix-ups, the manager forms a team including you, RN Okoye and a pharmacist to improve medication safety and the shift handover tool. The team meets weekly and wants approaches that frontline staff can use.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Have him state his name and date of birth, then match both to wristband and record.): Two independent client identifiers, such as full name and date of birth, checked against the wristband and record are required, and the client should state them when able. Room numbers can lead to error when clients share similar names. Whiteboards may be outdated. Yes-no questions let a drowsy client agree to the wrong name."}, {"id": 1577, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At the team meeting, the unit manager says quality improvement is something managers complete once a year for accreditation. Several frontline nurses say they are too busy to take part, and the pharmacist looks to you. What should you say?", "options": {"A": "Suggest quality improvement be limited to units with poor scores on the annual audit.", "B": "Explain that quality improvement is continuous and team based, using frontline observations.", "C": "Propose that the pharmacist alone lead it, since medication safety belongs to pharmacy.", "D": "Agree and let managers set the annual project while staff focus on daily tasks."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waits for poor audit scores before acting", "C": "Assigns improvement to one profession alone", "D": "Treats improvement as an annual management task"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C191", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a 30-bed medical unit. After two near-miss medication events involving wrong-patient mix-ups, the manager forms a team including you, RN Okoye and a pharmacist to improve medication safety and the shift handover tool. The team meets weekly and wants approaches that frontline staff can use.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain that quality improvement is continuous and team based, using frontline observations.): Quality improvement is an ongoing practice, and the people closest to care often notice problems first, so frontline staff must take part. Annual projects limit learning to one point in the year. Audit-triggered action waits for harm to show up. Single-profession ownership excludes the nurses who give medications every day."}, {"id": 1578, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The team has drafted a two-identifier checklist and predicted that near misses will fall by half. The planning step of the Plan Do Study Act cycle is complete, and the manager asks what comes next for the team on the unit. Which step comes next?", "options": {"A": "Hold a team meeting to refine the checklist again before any staff try it.", "B": "Carry out the small trial, collect data, and study results before wider use.", "C": "Adopt the checklist on the unit permanently once the pharmacist approves the plan.", "D": "Run the trial but delay measuring results until the end of the year."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Staying in the planning step instead of testing", "C": "Adopting a change before studying trial data", "D": "Postpones data collection until it is too late to learn"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C191", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a 30-bed medical unit. After two near-miss medication events involving wrong-patient mix-ups, the manager forms a team including you, RN Okoye and a pharmacist to improve medication safety and the shift handover tool. The team meets weekly and wants approaches that frontline staff can use.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Carry out the small trial, collect data, and study results before wider use.): After planning comes doing, meaning a small test with data collection, followed by studying what happened and acting on it. Staying in planning delays learning. Adopting the checklist permanently before the trial skips the study step. Waiting a year to measure defeats the rapid learning the cycle provides."}, {"id": 1579, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The team wants to try a new written handover tool on the medical unit, and several staff worry it will slow their work. The pharmacist asks how the first test should be arranged so that learning is quick and risk stays low. Which approach best reflects a small test of change?", "options": {"A": "Try it with one pair of nurses on one shift, gather feedback, and adjust.", "B": "Wait until managers approve a finished tool before any staff try it.", "C": "Launch it on every unit after one meeting so all staff learn it together.", "D": "Roll it out across the program for three months before collecting any staff comments."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Withholds testing until the design is final", "C": "Scales up before any testing", "D": "Collects feedback only after a long delay"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C191", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse on a 30-bed medical unit. After two near-miss medication events involving wrong-patient mix-ups, the manager forms a team including you, RN Okoye and a pharmacist to improve medication safety and the shift handover tool. The team meets weekly and wants approaches that frontline staff can use.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Try it with one pair of nurses on one shift, gather feedback, and adjust.): Small tests of change use one small group over a short time so problems surface quickly and the tool can be refined cheaply. Large launches spread flaws widely. Late feedback comes after the damage is done. Waiting for a finished tool prevents the learning that shapes it."}, {"id": 1580, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During your home visit, Mr. Rahimi's pouch has leaked twice this week and the skin around the stoma is red. You are preparing a new barrier and will cut the opening yourself. What is the correct size for the opening in the barrier?", "options": {"A": "Cut the opening exactly the stoma size so that no skin is exposed.", "B": "Cut the opening about 1 centimetre larger than the stoma so that it does not rub.", "C": "Apply the barrier with a larger opening and cover gaps with skin cream beneath it.", "D": "Cut it only 2 to 3 millimetres larger than the stoma."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Cuts too tight and risks injuring the stoma", "B": "Makes the opening too wide and exposes skin to output", "C": "Uses cream that stops the barrier from sticking"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C189", "cluster_text": "Mr. Rahimi, 68, has an ileostomy created three weeks ago after surgery for Crohn disease. He lives alone and receives home care visits from you, a practical nurse supervised by an RN. His stoma is moist and pink, output is loose, and he is learning to manage his pouching system between visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cut it only 2 to 3 millimetres larger than the stoma.): An opening just slightly larger than the stoma protects the surrounding skin from stool and enzymes while avoiding pressure on the stoma. An opening that is too big exposes skin and causes leaks. One cut exactly to size can injure the stoma as it swells. Cream under a barrier prevents adhesion."}, {"id": 1581, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Rahimi empties his pouch only at bedtime and in the morning, and it has leaked overnight twice this week. His output is higher after meals, and he asks how you would plan his emptying routine at home. When should he empty the pouch?", "options": {"A": "Empty it before each meal and at bedtime, since output rises after eating.", "B": "Empty it when one third to one half full, since weight and gas break the seal.", "C": "Empty it at the same two times each day, whatever its fullness at the time.", "D": "Empty it when two thirds full, since a larger volume gives a longer wear time."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using fixed meal times instead of pouch fullness", "C": "Follows a fixed schedule instead of volume", "D": "Waiting until the pouch is too heavy for the seal"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C189", "cluster_text": "Mr. Rahimi, 68, has an ileostomy created three weeks ago after surgery for Crohn disease. He lives alone and receives home care visits from you, a practical nurse supervised by an RN. His stoma is moist and pink, output is loose, and he is learning to manage his pouching system between visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Empty it when one third to one half full, since weight and gas break the seal.): A pouch that is one third to one half full stays secure, because a heavier pouch pulls on the seal and gas expands. Waiting until it is two thirds full or full invites leaks. Fixed times, whether twice daily or before meals, ignore how much output has collected."}, {"id": 1582, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Rahimi asks which foods are safe now. A friend told him to cut out fibre for good, and he is bothered by gas and odour after meals. He eats small amounts and wants clear teaching he can follow alone. What teaching is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Try raw vegetables and nuts in small portions daily, and swallow them quickly.", "B": "Remove fibre from his diet for good, since fibre causes the gas, odour and blockage.", "C": "Drink carbonated beverages with meals to settle gas, and skip fibre until the stoma heals.", "D": "Add fibrous foods slowly, chew thoroughly, and keep a food record of gas or blockage."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Swallowing fibrous foods without chewing raises blockage risk", "B": "Eliminates fibre and reduces nutrition", "C": "Carbonation adds gas rather than relieving it"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C189", "cluster_text": "Mr. Rahimi, 68, has an ileostomy created three weeks ago after surgery for Crohn disease. He lives alone and receives home care visits from you, a practical nurse supervised by an RN. His stoma is moist and pink, output is loose, and he is learning to manage his pouching system between visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Add fibrous foods slowly, chew thoroughly, and keep a food record of gas or blockage.): People with an ileostomy can eat most foods if they introduce new ones gradually, chew well and learn their own responses, which a food record helps show. Cutting fibre for good limits nutrition. Fizzy drinks add gas. Swallowing raw vegetables and nuts quickly raises the risk of blockage."}, {"id": 1583, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two weeks later Mr. Rahimi has about 1.8 litres of watery output daily, a dry mouth, dizziness on standing, and dark scant urine. He says he has been drinking mostly plain water and feels worse than last week. Which nursing action is best?", "options": {"A": "Tell him dizziness is expected with an ileostomy and encourage him to rest.", "B": "Advise him to cut back fluids at night to reduce output from the stoma.", "C": "Report the dehydration signs and encourage oral rehydration fluids with sodium and glucose.", "D": "Encourage only plain water, which will replace his losses and restore fluid balance quickly."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats a warning sign as normal and delays reporting", "B": "Restricts fluids and worsens dehydration", "D": "Replaces salt losses with plain water only"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C189", "cluster_text": "Mr. Rahimi, 68, has an ileostomy created three weeks ago after surgery for Crohn disease. He lives alone and receives home care visits from you, a practical nurse supervised by an RN. His stoma is moist and pink, output is loose, and he is learning to manage his pouching system between visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the dehydration signs and encourage oral rehydration fluids with sodium and glucose.): High ileostomy output loses water and sodium, and dizziness with dark scant urine shows dehydration that needs reporting and electrolyte-containing fluids. Plain water does not replace the salt he is losing. Restricting fluid worsens the deficit. Dismissing the dizziness as expected delays treatment of a worsening problem."}, {"id": 1584, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Emergency triage: Mateo, 9, has had three days of thirst and frequent urination, and now has deep rapid breathing, abdominal pain and fruity breath. His mother says he refused lunch because of nausea. His capillary glucose reads 29 mmol/L. Which interpretation should guide the practical nurse's immediate report to the RN?", "options": {"A": "Findings suggest ketoacidosis, so report urgently to the physician and prepare for intravenous fluids", "B": "Findings suggest a gastrointestinal infection, so offer small sips of sugary fluid and monitor the nausea", "C": "Findings suggest simple dehydration from poor intake, so encourage oral water and weigh him.", "D": "Findings suggest an asthma exacerbation, so give the child's salbutamol and reassess breathing after ten minutes"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating nausea as the cause and giving sugar to a child with very high glucose", "C": "Ignoring the acidotic breathing pattern and treating only with oral fluids", "D": "Attributing deep rapid breathing to asthma and overlooking the glucose reading"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Findings suggest ketoacidosis, so report urgently to the physician and prepare for intravenous fluids): Thirst, polyuria, fruity breath, abdominal pain and deep rapid breathing with a glucose of 29 mmol/L point to diabetic ketoacidosis, which needs urgent medical treatment with fluids and insulin. Sugary fluids would raise glucose further and worsen the acidosis. Oral water alone cannot correct the deficit or the ketones, and bronchodilators do not treat this breathing pattern, which is compensation for acidosis. Fruity breath plus very high glucose should always trigger an immediate report."}, {"id": 1585, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Priya, a practical nurse in a small community, meets a former home care client at a farmers market. The client, whose care ended eight months ago, invites her for coffee and later asks to connect on social media. Priya is unsure how to respond. Which approach best protects professional boundaries?", "options": {"A": "Accept the coffee but decline social media, since only online contact leaves a reviewable record", "B": "Accept both invitations, because the care has ended and no professional duty remains", "C": "Stay courteous, and weigh whether the past power imbalance could still affect the client", "D": "Decline and report the invitation to the employer, since regulation prohibits contact with former clients"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating only online contact as a boundary risk", "B": "Believing duties end the day care ends", "D": "Overstating regulation as a total ban and escalating needlessly"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stay courteous, and weigh whether the past power imbalance could still affect the client): A therapeutic relationship carries a power imbalance and knowledge of private health details that can outlast the care episode, so Priya should be courteous and consider whether contact could still harm or influence the client. Assuming duties end with discharge ignores that lasting imbalance. Accepting the coffee while refusing only online contact misses that in-person contact carries the same risk. Regulatory standards ask nurses to use judgment, not to treat every contact as prohibited."}, {"id": 1586, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, Mr. Faisal, 66, has a potassium of 2.8 mmol/L and mild muscle weakness. The order reads potassium chloride IV. Pharmacy sent a vial of concentrated potassium chloride to the unit instead of a premixed bag, and the RN is in another room. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Add the concentrate to a 100 mL minibag himself, then infuse it over one hour.", "B": "Add the concentrate to a small saline flush syringe and give it over five minutes", "C": "Infuse the concentrate undiluted by pump over thirty minutes after a second nurse verifies.", "D": "Hold the vial, since concentrate needs dilution first, and request a premixed bag from pharmacy"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Compounding a high-alert concentrate on the unit outside scope", "B": "Believing a small flush syringe makes concentrate safe", "C": "Giving undiluted potassium chloride despite a second check"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the vial, since concentrate needs dilution first, and request a premixed bag from pharmacy): Concentrated potassium chloride can cause fatal cardiac arrhythmias if given undiluted or too fast, so it must be diluted and given by a controlled infusion. Holding the vial and obtaining a premixed bag from pharmacy follows safe practice. Symptoms from low potassium do not justify push administration, and a second check does not make an undiluted infusion safe. Preparing high-alert concentrates on the unit is outside safe practice."}, {"id": 1587, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Home care visit: Mr. Dubois, 78, was discharged after a Clostridioides difficile infection. His daughter says he uses an alcohol-based hand rub after toileting and before meals because it is quicker. He has a mild hand tremor and kept the hospital pamphlet. Which finding should guide the nurse's teaching?", "options": {"A": "The pamphlet is enough, so ask him to read it aloud and sign that he understands", "B": "Alcohol rub does not remove spores, so teach soap and running water with a return demonstration", "C": "His tremor is the main risk, so the daughter should wash his hands for him", "D": "Alcohol rub is acceptable if applied for thirty seconds, so reinforce his technique and the pamphlet"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on a handout without confirming skill", "C": "Substituting caregiver action for client teaching without checking ability", "D": "Assuming alcohol rub kills spore-forming organisms with longer contact"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Alcohol rub does not remove spores, so teach soap and running water with a return demonstration): Alcohol-based rubs do not reliably destroy C. difficile spores, so washing with soap and running water is needed after toileting and before meals. A return demonstration confirms he can manage the technique despite his tremor. Longer rub time does not solve the spore problem. A handout alone does not show that he can perform or understands the skill."}, {"id": 1588, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "School setting: 8-year-old Kayla, who has epilepsy, falls to the floor at 1030 with generalized shaking of her arms and legs. A classmate yells that someone should hold her still. The practical nurse arrives, and the teacher notes the clock. What is the best action while the seizure continues?", "options": {"A": "Cushion her head, loosen tight clothing, note the time, then turn her on her side", "B": "Move her away from classmates to the nurse's office and stay beside her there.", "C": "Hold her head steady and keep her flat on her back until it ends.", "D": "Place a rolled cloth between her teeth to protect her tongue and keep the airway open"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Moving a seizing child instead of protecting her where she is", "C": "Keeping a seizing child supine risks aspiration", "D": "Putting an object in the mouth, risking choking and tooth damage"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cushion her head, loosen tight clothing, note the time, then turn her on her side): During a generalized seizure the aim is to prevent injury and maintain the airway, so the nurse cushions her head, loosens tight items, times the seizure and turns her to the side once the shaking stops. Moving her can cause injury, and lying flat on her back risks aspiration. Restraint can cause fractures. Anything placed in the mouth can break teeth."}, {"id": 1589, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Primary care: Diane, 45, reports trouble falling asleep for two months. She takes a two-hour nap after work, drinks coffee at 1600, and scrolls on her phone in bed until midnight. She asks what to change first. Which teaching best matches sleep hygiene principles?", "options": {"A": "Stay in bed until sleep comes, and keep the phone nearby for reading to relax", "B": "Try a glass of wine near bedtime to relax, since alcohol helps maintain deep sleep", "C": "Move bedtime one hour earlier and add a short evening nap to build more sleep pressure", "D": "Keep a fixed wake time, limit caffeine to mornings, and use the bed only for sleep"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Keeping screens and wakefulness in bed, weakening the bed-sleep link", "B": "Believing alcohol improves sleep quality", "C": "Adding naps and early bedtime that reduce sleep drive"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep a fixed wake time, limit caffeine to mornings, and use the bed only for sleep): Sleep hygiene relies on a consistent wake time, limiting caffeine to the morning, and reserving the bed for sleep so the brain links it with rest. Her late nap, afternoon coffee and screen use are the likely causes. Extra naps and an earlier bedtime lower sleep drive. Alcohol fragments sleep, and lying awake with a phone strengthens the habit of wakefulness in bed."}, {"id": 1590, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A hospital client, Mr. Nguyen, 59, speaks limited English and needs a procedure consent explained. His 12-year-old granddaughter is at the bedside and offers to translate. The unit has access to a telephone interpreter service, and the procedure is scheduled this afternoon. Which plan best supports safe, accurate communication?", "options": {"A": "Use simple English words and gestures, then ask him to nod if he understands the form", "B": "Use the qualified interpreter service for consent, and let the granddaughter stay", "C": "Let the granddaughter translate the consent, then confirm his signature after she has finished", "D": "Ask the granddaughter to translate and have a bilingual housekeeper check the main parts"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on a nod as proof of informed consent", "C": "Using a child as interpreter for medical information", "D": "Substituting an untrained bilingual staff member for a qualified interpreter"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use the qualified interpreter service for consent, and let the granddaughter stay): Informed consent requires that the client understands the risks and options, which needs a qualified interpreter rather than a child or untrained helper. A child may lack the vocabulary and should not carry emotional burdens of medical news. A nod does not prove understanding, and an informal check by non-clinical staff does not meet accuracy standards. The family can stay for support."}, {"id": 1591, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Hale, 70, is at home using prescribed timolol eye drops but has a hand tremor. The nurse observes him touch the bottle tip to his eyelid twice and spill drops on his cheek. He says the bottle empties early. Which teaching is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Tilt his head back, hold the bottle near the eye, and rest the tip on it", "B": "Lie flat, squeeze several drops into the eye, and blink hard to spread the medication", "C": "Brace his hand on his cheek, pull the lower lid down, and keep the tip clear", "D": "Ask his daughter to give all doses while he keeps the bottle capped"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Resting the dropper tip on the lid, contaminating the bottle", "B": "Using extra drops and hard blinking, which wastes medication and washes it out", "D": "Replacing client teaching with dependence when he can learn adaptive technique"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Brace his hand on his cheek, pull the lower lid down, and keep the tip clear): Bracing the hand steadies the tremor, pulling the lower lid down creates a pocket for one drop, and keeping the tip clear prevents contamination and infection. Resting the tip on the lid contaminates the bottle. Extra drops and hard blinking waste the medication and flush it away. Removing his role without trying adaptive technique reduces independence."}, {"id": 1592, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 35-year-old woman has blistered, very painful burns over her entire front chest and abdomen and her whole right arm. The nurse estimates the area using the rule of nines. She says the pain shows the burns must be minor. Which statement best supports care planning?", "options": {"A": "About 9 percent is burned, and since pain is present, no further depth assessment is needed", "B": "About 27 percent is burned, and pain fits partial thickness, so report for fluid orders", "C": "About 36 percent is burned, and blisters show only a superficial burn, so encourage oral fluids", "D": "About 18 percent is burned, and pain shows the burns are superficial, so apply cool dressings"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Counting only the arm and assuming pain removes the need for assessment", "C": "Misreading blisters as a sign of superficial burns", "D": "Underestimating burn area by omitting the arm"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 27 percent is burned, and pain fits partial thickness, so report for fluid orders): The anterior trunk counts as 18 percent and one whole arm as 9 percent, giving about 27 percent of body surface. Blisters and severe pain fit partial thickness burns, and an area this large needs fluid resuscitation orders. Superficial burns are not blistered, and pain does not mean a burn is minor. Underestimating the area delays treatment."}, {"id": 1593, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Ito, 76, is at home after right cataract surgery. She tells the home care nurse the eye feels gritty and that she rubbed it during the night. She sleeps on her right side and has no shield on. Which teaching is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Avoid rubbing, wear the protective shield at night, and sleep on the back or opposite side", "B": "Hold a cool compress firmly over the eye for relief and sleep on the back.", "C": "Sleep on the operated side so drainage leaves the eye, and skip the shield", "D": "Rinse the eye with sterile saline several times daily and leave the shield off."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Pressing on a freshly operated eye", "C": "Lying on the operated eye, causing pressure and injury", "D": "Omitting the protective shield overnight"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Avoid rubbing, wear the protective shield at night, and sleep on the back or opposite side): Rubbing or pressure on a freshly operated eye can disrupt the incision and raise the risk of infection or lens displacement. A shield at night and sleeping on the back or the other side protect it. Pressing a compress on the eye adds pressure, and sleeping on the operated side does the same. Omitting the shield removes protection while she sleeps."}, {"id": 1594, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "In long-term care, Nina, a practical nurse, finds at 1400 that Mr. Kowal, who is to be turned every two hours, has not been repositioned since 0900 because the unit was short-staffed. His sacral skin is red but blanches. A coworker suggests charting the turns as completed. What should Nina do?", "options": {"A": "Chart the delay but wait for the next scheduled turn so his routine stays consistent", "B": "Reposition him and chart the turns on schedule, since the delay caused no skin breakdown", "C": "Reposition him now, chart the true times, and report the missed care to the supervisor", "D": "Reposition him and tell only the coworker, so the team can solve staffing informally"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying needed repositioning to keep a schedule", "B": "Falsifying records to hide missed care", "D": "Keeping the staffing and safety problem within the team without reporting"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reposition him now, chart the true times, and report the missed care to the supervisor): Missed repositioning raises pressure injury risk, so he should be turned now, and documentation must reflect what actually happened. Charting turns that did not occur is falsification. Waiting for the next scheduled turn prolongs pressure on reddened skin. Reporting to the supervisor lets the organization address the staffing gap, which an informal chat with a coworker does not."}, {"id": 1595, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During the 1000 dressing check, you see a 3 cm area along the incision that is warm, firm and red, with a small amount of cloudy drainage. Mr. Osei says it is only slightly tender and does not want to bother anyone. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Wait for a fever above 38 C before reporting, since redness alone is expected healing", "B": "Reinforce the dressing and recheck the site at the end of shift if tenderness increases", "C": "Document the measurements and report the findings promptly to the RN or surgeon", "D": "Clean the area with a topical antiseptic and continue routine assessment each shift"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for fever, a late sign, before escalating", "B": "Reporting only when the client complains", "D": "Treating possible infection with self-chosen topical care"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C187", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gerald Osei, 62, is on postoperative day 3 after an open sigmoid colon resection on a surgical unit. He has type 2 diabetes managed with metformin at home. His midline incision is closed with staples and covered by a transparent dressing. At 0800 his temperature is 37.4 C, heart rate is 88, and he rates incisional pain 4 of 10. He walks in the hall twice daily and eats a soft diet.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Document the measurements and report the findings promptly to the RN or surgeon): Warmth, firmness, redness and cloudy drainage on day 3 are early signs of a possible surgical site infection, so measured documentation and prompt reporting are needed. His reluctance to complain does not lower the importance of the finding. Reinforcing the dressing and waiting leaves a possible infection unassessed. Fever often appears late, so waiting for it delays treatment."}, {"id": 1596, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At 1400 the dressing over the upper incision is saturated with new bright red drainage about 4 cm across. Blood pressure is 124/76, and he reports no new pain. What does this most likely indicate, and what should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Active bleeding: reinforce the dressing without removing it, mark the edge, and report to the RN", "B": "Dehiscence beginning: remove the dressing, inspect the staples, and cover the wound with saline-moistened gauze", "C": "Adhesive allergy: remove the dressing, wash the skin, and switch to a different tape", "D": "Expected serous leakage: change the dressing now to keep the incision dry and recheck later"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Applying an evisceration response to a bleeding finding", "C": "Blaming the tape and ignoring the drainage colour", "D": "Calling bright red blood normal and disturbing the clot"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C187", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gerald Osei, 62, is on postoperative day 3 after an open sigmoid colon resection on a surgical unit. He has type 2 diabetes managed with metformin at home. His midline incision is closed with staples and covered by a transparent dressing. At 0800 his temperature is 37.4 C, heart rate is 88, and he rates incisional pain 4 of 10. He walks in the hall twice daily and eats a soft diet.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Active bleeding: reinforce the dressing without removing it, mark the edge, and report to the RN): New bright red saturation points to bleeding, not serous leakage. Reinforcing without removing preserves any clot, marking the edge shows how fast it spreads, and the RN must be told promptly even though his vital signs are stable. Changing the dressing disturbs the clot. Treating this as dehiscence or an adhesive reaction misreads the colour and amount of drainage."}, {"id": 1597, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On day 5, Mr. Osei's temperature is 38.6 C and his pulse is 102. He reports increasing incisional pain and chills. The incision edges look slightly separated with thick yellow drainage. He asks whether a fever is just his body healing. Which response is best for the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Offer acetaminophen and reassess the temperature after an hour before deciding whether to report anything", "B": "Agree mild fever is part of healing, encourage fluids and rest, and recheck in four hours", "C": "Explain that fever with worsening pain and drainage may signal infection, then report it", "D": "Explain that early fever usually comes from the lungs, so focus on deep breathing exercises"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Masking fever with an antipyretic and delaying escalation", "B": "Calling infection signs normal healing", "D": "Assuming a pulmonary cause despite wound signs"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C187", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gerald Osei, 62, is on postoperative day 3 after an open sigmoid colon resection on a surgical unit. He has type 2 diabetes managed with metformin at home. His midline incision is closed with staples and covered by a transparent dressing. At 0800 his temperature is 37.4 C, heart rate is 88, and he rates incisional pain 4 of 10. He walks in the hall twice daily and eats a soft diet.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain that fever with worsening pain and drainage may signal infection, then report it): Fever on day 5 with chills, rising pain, wound separation and purulent drainage fits a surgical site infection, not routine healing. Reporting now allows culture, orders and treatment. Waiting four hours or giving acetaminophen first delays assessment and can hide the fever pattern. Atelectasis causes early fever in the first two days, and the wound findings here point elsewhere."}, {"id": 1598, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The day 5 morning glucose is 14.2 mmol/L, and blood work shows a high white cell count. Mr. Osei's metformin is on hold, and sliding scale insulin is ordered. He feels fine and skipped breakfast. Which action best supports wound healing and safety?", "options": {"A": "Withhold insulin and offer juice to prevent hypoglycemia because he skipped breakfast", "B": "Hold all insulin until the glucose passes 20 mmol/L, since moderate elevation does not affect healing", "C": "Give the full scale insulin dose immediately and encourage a large lunch to match the insulin", "D": "Confirm intake plans and the insulin dose with the RN, then recheck glucose within two hours"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving juice to a client who is already hyperglycemic", "B": "Believing mild hyperglycemia does not slow healing", "C": "Giving insulin without planning intake, risking hypoglycemia"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C187", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gerald Osei, 62, is on postoperative day 3 after an open sigmoid colon resection on a surgical unit. He has type 2 diabetes managed with metformin at home. His midline incision is closed with staples and covered by a transparent dressing. At 0800 his temperature is 37.4 C, heart rate is 88, and he rates incisional pain 4 of 10. He walks in the hall twice daily and eats a soft diet.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Confirm intake plans and the insulin dose with the RN, then recheck glucose within two hours): Sustained hyperglycemia slows healing and raises infection risk, so glucose must be treated, but insulin must fit his actual intake. Confirming the plan with the RN and rechecking glucose handles both. A high threshold for treating leaves glucose uncontrolled. A full dose with no meal planned can cause hypoglycemia, and juice raises glucose further."}, {"id": 1599, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Marcus, 48, comes to a clinic. He smokes a pack a day and says he has no plans to quit. He came only because his wife insisted. The nurse is about to describe a nicotine patch schedule. Which approach best fits his stage of change?", "options": {"A": "Review a detailed quit date schedule and patch dosing in case he changes his mind", "B": "Give quit-line cards and book weekly follow-up visits to hold him accountable for stopping", "C": "Ask permission to share information, explore his views, and offer to talk again later", "D": "Explain lung cancer risks firmly and ask him to commit to a quit date"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving an action plan to someone not yet ready", "B": "Imposing accountability on a client who has not chosen to change", "D": "Using fear and pressure to force commitment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask permission to share information, explore his views, and offer to talk again later): A client who is not considering change is in the precontemplation stage, where the best approach is respectful, nonjudgmental exploration and an open door for later. A detailed quit plan assumes readiness he does not have. Pressure and fear tactics tend to increase resistance. Weekly accountability visits suit someone already preparing to act."}, {"id": 1600, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a medical unit at 0200, Mr. Pratt, 68, is awakened for routine vital signs although he is stable, afebrile and has no new orders requiring night monitoring. He says he has slept about three hours in two nights. His daughter visits at 1500 daily. Which action best supports his sleep?", "options": {"A": "Offer a late afternoon nap and a coffee to compensate for the lost night sleep", "B": "Ask the RN whether stable vital signs can be spaced to allow longer uninterrupted sleep", "C": "Check vital signs quietly at 0200 with a dimmed light and leave scheduled times unchanged.", "D": "Cluster his care at 0200 with a bath and linen change so daytime stays quiet."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Recommending late naps and caffeine that disrupt night sleep", "C": "Reducing disturbance without removing the unnecessary night check", "D": "Scheduling routine care during the night"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask the RN whether stable vital signs can be spaced to allow longer uninterrupted sleep): Hospital routines are a common cause of broken sleep, so the nurse should ask the RN whether checks can be spaced for a stable client, protecting uninterrupted rest that supports recovery. Dimming the light at 0200 still wakes him. Care scheduled at night breaks sleep further. Late naps and coffee delay night sleep."}, {"id": 1601, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Four-year-old Liam arrives at a community clinic with his father. He has finger-shaped bruises on his upper arm and a story of a fall off a swing that does not fit the pattern. Liam is quiet and avoids eye contact. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the father to bring a doctor's note confirming the cause before deciding on a report", "B": "Confront the father privately and report only if his explanation changes", "C": "Report promptly to child protection and tell the RN, without confronting the father", "D": "Wait until another visit shows a pattern, then report if the injuries recur"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Requiring a medical letter to delay reporting", "B": "Making reporting depend on the parent's story", "D": "Waiting for proof before meeting the duty to report"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report promptly to child protection and tell the RN, without confronting the father): Reasonable grounds to suspect abuse trigger a duty to report promptly, and proof is not needed. Finger-shaped bruises and an inconsistent explanation are reasonable grounds. Waiting for recurrence exposes the child to risk, and asking for a note delays the report. Confronting the parent may endanger the child and is not the nurse's role."}, {"id": 1602, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In long-term care, Mrs. Okafor, 90, who had a do-not-resuscitate order, died peacefully at 0330, and the RN has confirmed the death. Her daughter arrives and asks to sit with her. The practical nurse has the death-care checklist. What is the best next action?", "options": {"A": "Begin removing equipment and bathing the body at once, then invite the family in", "B": "Ask the family to wait in the lounge until body care is complete", "C": "Welcome the family to the bedside, allow quiet time, then follow facility policy", "D": "Call the funeral home first and ask the daughter to sign release forms at the bedside"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Prioritizing tasks over family presence", "B": "Rushing the family out of the room", "D": "Starting paperwork before supporting the grieving daughter"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Welcome the family to the bedside, allow quiet time, then follow facility policy): Offering family time at the bedside supports grieving and fits person-centred end-of-life care, and body care follows once they are ready. Asking the family to wait treats the task as more important than their presence. Completing tasks first and then inviting them in delays what matters most to them. Paperwork should not be the first act when a daughter arrives."}, {"id": 1603, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The night nurse notes that Mrs. Brandt was woken four separate times by vital signs, a medication, a glucose check and repositioning. She is irritable and says she cannot rest. Orders allow some flexibility in timing. Which action best protects her sleep while keeping care safe?", "options": {"A": "Offer an ordered sleeping pill after the third interruption so she can fall back asleep", "B": "Keep each task at its fixed time so nothing is missed, and allow daytime naps", "C": "Cluster non-urgent tasks into fewer visits and ask the RN about fewer overnight checks", "D": "Skip the overnight repositioning and glucose check because sleep takes priority over scheduled care"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a sedative before addressing interruptions", "B": "Following fixed times without individualizing care", "D": "Omitting ordered care entirely to protect sleep"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C197", "cluster_text": "Helen Brandt, 71, is on day 4 of admission for community-acquired pneumonia on a medical unit. She takes prednisone 40 mg once daily and has osteoarthritis of both knees. Her husband says she snores loudly and sometimes stops breathing briefly during sleep at home. Overnight, the unit checks vital signs at 2200, 0000, 0200 and 0400, and she reports sleeping about three hours.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cluster non-urgent tasks into fewer visits and ask the RN about fewer overnight checks): Clustering non-urgent care into fewer visits reduces interruptions while keeping ordered care in place, and asking the RN about the vital sign schedule addresses the largest source of waking. Rigid timing ignores the harm of fragmented sleep. Skipping ordered care is unsafe. Offering a sedative first treats the symptom without fixing the cause, and sedatives carry risks in older adults."}, {"id": 1604, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Brandt asks for the lorazepam ordered as needed for sleep. She walked unsteadily to the bathroom twice tonight and fell at home last month. Her oxygen saturation is 93% on room air. How should the practical nurse interpret this request before acting?", "options": {"A": "Her falls, unsteady gait and low saturation raise the sedative's risk, so consult the RN", "B": "The order is valid and as needed, so give the lorazepam and check sedation hourly", "C": "Her request shows anxiety only, so give half the dose to lower the risk of sedation", "D": "Her sleeplessness comes from the steroid alone, so decline and suggest waiting for the physician"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Giving a benzodiazepine by order alone without risk review", "C": "Changing a dose without authority", "D": "Refusing without assessing causes or consulting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C197", "cluster_text": "Helen Brandt, 71, is on day 4 of admission for community-acquired pneumonia on a medical unit. She takes prednisone 40 mg once daily and has osteoarthritis of both knees. Her husband says she snores loudly and sometimes stops breathing briefly during sleep at home. Overnight, the unit checks vital signs at 2200, 0000, 0200 and 0400, and she reports sleeping about three hours.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Her falls, unsteady gait and low saturation raise the sedative's risk, so consult the RN): Benzodiazepines increase confusion, falls and respiratory depression in older adults, and Mrs. Brandt already has a recent fall, unsteady gait and borderline oxygenation. Consulting the RN allows review of the order and non-drug options. Giving the drug because it is ordered ignores the risk. Halving a dose is outside the nurse's scope, and a flat refusal leaves her problem unaddressed."}, {"id": 1605, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Review of the record shows Mrs. Brandt's prednisone has been given at 2000 each evening since admission, and her knee pain is 6 of 10 at bedtime with no analgesia. She cannot fall asleep until after 0200. Which finding most plausibly explains her insomnia and should be reported?", "options": {"A": "Evening corticosteroid dosing and untreated knee pain are both likely delaying her sleep onset", "B": "Hospital noise accounts for the delay, so earplugs are the only change worth requesting", "C": "Hospital stress explains the delay, so asking for a bedtime sedative is the best request.", "D": "Her suspected sleep apnea explains the delay, so the prednisone timing needs no review."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Blaming noise alone and overlooking pain", "C": "Seeking a sedative before addressing steroid timing and pain", "D": "Ignoring evening steroid dosing as a contributor"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C197", "cluster_text": "Helen Brandt, 71, is on day 4 of admission for community-acquired pneumonia on a medical unit. She takes prednisone 40 mg once daily and has osteoarthritis of both knees. Her husband says she snores loudly and sometimes stops breathing briefly during sleep at home. Overnight, the unit checks vital signs at 2200, 0000, 0200 and 0400, and she reports sleeping about three hours.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Evening corticosteroid dosing and untreated knee pain are both likely delaying her sleep onset): Corticosteroids are stimulating and are usually given in the morning, and uncontrolled pain also prevents sleep, so both findings belong in the report. Blaming noise, cough, stress, apnea or inactivity ignores these two clear causes. Extra naps would shorten night sleep further. Reporting lets the RN and prescriber move the dose time and address pain."}, {"id": 1606, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Overnight, the nurse sees Mrs. Brandt snoring loudly with pauses of about fifteen seconds followed by a snort, and her saturation dips to 86% during the pauses. Her husband describes the same pattern at home. What is the most appropriate nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Document the snoring and raise the head of bed, but wait to report until morning.", "B": "Wake her gently, give a sedative from the chart, and recheck oxygen in thirty minutes.", "C": "Report the pauses and desaturations to the RN, position her side-lying, ask about apnea", "D": "Apply a nasal cannula at a high flow rate without an order to correct the dips"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying a report of witnessed apnea and desaturation", "B": "Sedatives worsen obstructive sleep apnea", "D": "Giving oxygen at an unordered rate"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C197", "cluster_text": "Helen Brandt, 71, is on day 4 of admission for community-acquired pneumonia on a medical unit. She takes prednisone 40 mg once daily and has osteoarthritis of both knees. Her husband says she snores loudly and sometimes stops breathing briefly during sleep at home. Overnight, the unit checks vital signs at 2200, 0000, 0200 and 0400, and she reports sleeping about three hours.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the pauses and desaturations to the RN, position her side-lying, ask about apnea): Loud snoring with witnessed pauses and desaturation are warning signs of obstructive sleep apnea, so the RN needs to know and further assessment is appropriate. Side-lying reduces airway collapse. Waiting until morning delays care, and sedatives worsen apnea. Waking her repeatedly adds sleep deprivation, and starting oxygen at a high rate without an order exceeds the practical nurse's scope."}, {"id": 1607, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ellis, 79, lives alone and takes glyburide. He has eaten little this week. At 1500 he reports sweating, shakiness and confusion, and his meter reads 3.0 mmol/L. His neighbour says the drug cannot cause lows. What should the practical nurse conclude and do first?", "options": {"A": "Sulfonylureas do not lower glucose below normal, so recheck the meter and look for another cause", "B": "Hypoglycemia is unlikely with oral agents, so give a high-protein snack and let him rest", "C": "Give 15 g of carbohydrate, then give the next glyburide dose to prevent a rebound high", "D": "Sulfonylureas can cause hypoglycemia, so give 15 g of fast carbohydrate and recheck in 15 minutes"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing oral agents never cause lows", "B": "Using protein instead of fast carbohydrate", "C": "Giving glyburide while hypoglycemic"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sulfonylureas can cause hypoglycemia, so give 15 g of fast carbohydrate and recheck in 15 minutes): Sulfonylureas stimulate insulin release regardless of intake, so low intake in an older adult can cause hypoglycemia. A reading of 3.0 mmol/L with symptoms calls for 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate and a recheck in 15 minutes. Doubting the meter delays treatment, and protein acts too slowly. Giving more glyburide would deepen the hypoglycemia."}, {"id": 1608, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Rosa, 73, has early Alzheimer disease. She has mild memory loss and can still explain her wishes. Her daughter says they should wait until Rosa's memory worsens before discussing future care, but her physician is open to talking now. Which action by the practical nurse best supports planning?", "options": {"A": "Complete the advance care plan form yourself from the daughter's account of her mother's wishes", "B": "Suggest a one-time conversation about resuscitation only, and delay other treatment decisions", "C": "Support talking about her wishes now while she has capacity, and encourage naming a substitute", "D": "Postpone planning talks until loss of capacity is clear, then involve the substitute decision maker"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Filling in the plan for the client", "B": "Limiting planning to a single narrow topic", "D": "Waiting until capacity is lost, when her voice is gone"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Support talking about her wishes now while she has capacity, and encourage naming a substitute): Advance care planning is most valuable while the client can still express her values, and early dementia does not remove capacity. Encouraging Rosa to name a substitute decision maker and share her wishes keeps her at the centre. Waiting risks losing her voice. The nurse should not complete the plan from a relative's account or narrow the conversation to one topic."}, {"id": 1609, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "In rehabilitation, Mr. Tran, 58, is two days after a total knee replacement, with weight bearing as tolerated ordered. The nurse sees him lifting the walker with both hands and hopping on his operated leg to cross the room. His pain is 3 of 10. Which teaching best corrects his technique?", "options": {"A": "Advance the walker, step the operated leg into it, then bring the other leg forward", "B": "Lift the walker and move both legs together, then rest before the next step", "C": "Move the stronger leg first, then the walker, then the operated leg last to protect it", "D": "Slide the walker well ahead of his feet to stretch the leg and improve motion"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Lifting the walker and hopping, which unbalances him", "C": "Reversing the stepping sequence", "D": "Placing the walker too far ahead, causing instability"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Advance the walker, step the operated leg into it, then bring the other leg forward): The walker moves first, then the operated leg steps into its frame, then the stronger leg follows, allowing weight to be shared across the walker and legs. Hopping and lifting reduce stability and stress the new joint. Leading with the stronger leg and leaving the operated leg last inverts the sequence, and placing the walker too far forward causes loss of balance."}, {"id": 1610, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a mental health unit, Jonah, 35, with depression finds it hard to talk. Alicia, a practical nurse, considers telling him about her own divorce so he will trust her. He has just asked if she has ever felt low. What best fits therapeutic boundaries?", "options": {"A": "Share a brief, relevant fact only if it serves his needs, then refocus on him", "B": "Share her divorce in detail so he sees that she understands, and let him ask questions", "C": "Tell him she cannot answer, then add that she cries at times too", "D": "Decline to answer anything personal and change the subject to avoid any attachment"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using the client's time to share personal problems", "C": "Contradicting herself while still disclosing", "D": "Rigid avoidance that may feel dismissive"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Share a brief, relevant fact only if it serves his needs, then refocus on him): Self-disclosure is acceptable when brief, rare and used for the client's benefit, with the focus returning to him. Detailed sharing shifts attention to the nurse and risks role reversal. Refusing and changing the subject can feel cold and damage trust. Saying she cannot answer and then disclosing sends a mixed message about boundaries."}, {"id": 1611, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Reyes has had three episodes of leakage over two weeks. His daily suppository has been given at a different hour each week depending on staffing, and bowel sounds are present. His abdomen is soft, and his intake is steady. Which decision best improves his bowel retraining?", "options": {"A": "Set one consistent time, ideally after a meal, and keep it daily", "B": "Add a second daily suppository so that one dose falls near a bowel movement", "C": "Use a daily enema at varied times so the bowel empties out each day", "D": "Stop the suppository and rely on abdominal massage and fluids until the leakage ends"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Doubling doses instead of fixing timing", "C": "Using enemas at inconsistent times", "D": "Dropping the program and relying on weaker measures"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C196", "cluster_text": "Tom Reyes, 34, is in a rehabilitation unit eight weeks after a T6 spinal cord injury. He has a neurogenic bowel with episodes of constipation and occasional fecal leakage. He takes oxycodone for neuropathic pain, uses a wheelchair, and eats a low-fibre diet. His care plan includes bowel retraining with a daily suppository. Staffing on the unit varies from day to day, and he has no sensation below the injury level.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Set one consistent time, ideally after a meal, and keep it daily): Bowel retraining depends on a regular schedule, ideally after meals when the gastrocolic reflex is active, so the bowel learns a predictable emptying time. A varied hour defeats this training. Doubling the suppository adds irritation without fixing timing. Dropping the program leaves him without structure, and daily enemas at irregular times are not a retraining method."}, {"id": 1612, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After a liquid stool, the skin over Mr. Reyes's buttocks and sacrum is pink and blanches with pressure, but he cannot feel the area. Staff usually cleanse with soap and a washcloth. What care should the practical nurse provide after each episode?", "options": {"A": "Wash only with warm water, then massage the reddened area firmly to improve circulation", "B": "Apply a thick layer of petroleum-based ointment to damp skin to trap moisture in", "C": "Scrub with a washcloth and soap until no residue remains, then leave the skin bare", "D": "Cleanse gently with a pH-balanced cleanser, pat dry, and apply a moisture barrier"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Massaging reddened skin, which injures deeper tissue", "B": "Sealing moisture under ointment", "C": "Scrubbing, which damages already fragile skin"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C196", "cluster_text": "Tom Reyes, 34, is in a rehabilitation unit eight weeks after a T6 spinal cord injury. He has a neurogenic bowel with episodes of constipation and occasional fecal leakage. He takes oxycodone for neuropathic pain, uses a wheelchair, and eats a low-fibre diet. His care plan includes bowel retraining with a daily suppository. Staffing on the unit varies from day to day, and he has no sensation below the injury level.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cleanse gently with a pH-balanced cleanser, pat dry, and apply a moisture barrier): Stool irritates skin, and a client without sensation cannot sense early damage, so gentle cleansing with a pH-balanced product, patting dry and applying a barrier protects the skin. Scrubbing causes friction injury. Ointment over damp skin traps moisture and promotes breakdown. Massaging reddened areas damages tissue and is no longer recommended."}, {"id": 1613, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The dietitian recommends adding psyllium, a bulk-forming laxative, to Mr. Reyes's daily plan. His partner wants to know when to expect results and what Mr. Reyes should do alongside the product. Which statement about onset and about proper use is correct?", "options": {"A": "They act within thirty minutes by drawing water into the bowel from surrounding tissues", "B": "Bulk-forming agents usually take twelve to seventy-two hours and need plenty of fluid", "C": "They work within two to four hours by stimulating nerves in the bowel wall", "D": "They usually produce a bowel movement within one hour of the dose, similar to a suppository"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confusing the mechanism with that of osmotic agents", "C": "Confusing the mechanism with that of stimulant laxatives", "D": "Expecting suppository-like speed from a bulk agent"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C196", "cluster_text": "Tom Reyes, 34, is in a rehabilitation unit eight weeks after a T6 spinal cord injury. He has a neurogenic bowel with episodes of constipation and occasional fecal leakage. He takes oxycodone for neuropathic pain, uses a wheelchair, and eats a low-fibre diet. His care plan includes bowel retraining with a daily suppository. Staffing on the unit varies from day to day, and he has no sensation below the injury level.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bulk-forming agents usually take twelve to seventy-two hours and need plenty of fluid): Bulk-forming agents absorb water to enlarge and soften stool, which takes twelve to seventy-two hours, and they require adequate fluid to avoid obstruction. Expecting results within an hour mistakes them for suppositories. Nerve stimulation describes stimulant laxatives, and rapid fluid shifts describe osmotic agents, so neither fits psyllium."}, {"id": 1614, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Because Mr. Reyes takes oxycodone, he has harder stools and straining despite psyllium. The NP orders docusate daily and senna at bedtime. His partner asks why two products are needed. Which explanation by the practical nurse is the most accurate and appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Docusate is bulk-forming and senna is a lubricant, so together they coat and expand the stool", "B": "Docusate softens stool, but senna stimulates movement, which opioid slowing usually needs", "C": "Senna softens the stool and docusate stimulates the bowel, so each protects the other", "D": "Both products are stimulants, and using two simply speeds the effect when one is slow"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assigning bulk-forming and lubricant roles incorrectly", "C": "Reversing the actions of the two drugs", "D": "Mislabeling both agents as stimulants"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C196", "cluster_text": "Tom Reyes, 34, is in a rehabilitation unit eight weeks after a T6 spinal cord injury. He has a neurogenic bowel with episodes of constipation and occasional fecal leakage. He takes oxycodone for neuropathic pain, uses a wheelchair, and eats a low-fibre diet. His care plan includes bowel retraining with a daily suppository. Staffing on the unit varies from day to day, and he has no sensation below the injury level.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Docusate softens stool, but senna stimulates movement, which opioid slowing usually needs): Opioids slow bowel motility, so a softener alone often fails and a stimulant such as senna is added to restore movement. Docusate draws water into stool and softens it. Calling both stimulants, swapping their actions, or labeling them bulk-forming and lubricant gives the family a wrong picture of how the regimen works."}, {"id": 1615, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In home care, Ahmed, 7, has spastic cerebral palsy and uses a walker. His teacher says he struggles to keep up. His mother says people assume he cannot learn, but his reading is at grade level and he enjoys puzzles. Which approach should the nurse support?", "options": {"A": "Lower his learning goals to reduce stress and focus the plan on mobility alone", "B": "Replace the walker with a wheelchair at school to prevent fatigue and falls", "C": "Treat movement and thinking as separate, support mobility, and ask for school accommodations", "D": "Request cognitive testing before any therapy since movement difficulty suggests impaired learning"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming motor disability means cognitive limits", "B": "Reducing independence for convenience", "D": "Linking mobility treatment to cognitive testing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat movement and thinking as separate, support mobility, and ask for school accommodations): Cerebral palsy affects movement and posture, but intelligence varies widely and his grade-level reading supports typical learning. The nurse can advocate for accommodations such as extra time to move between classes. Lowering learning goals or testing first rests on a false assumption. Swapping to a wheelchair removes his independence without evidence it is needed."}, {"id": 1616, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 42-year-old hiker arrives at a clinic with white, waxy, numb fingers on both hands after hours in minus 20 C. A friend rubbed them with snow. His temperature is 36.0 C, and his gloves are wet. What is the best action by the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Remove wet clothing, soak the fingers in warm water near 38 C, then report", "B": "Soak the fingers in hot water near 45 C, then loosely wrap them.", "C": "Keep the fingers elevated and rewarm slowly beside a hot air heater vent", "D": "Rewarm the fingers by exercising them in a warm room, then report the findings."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using water hot enough to burn numb tissue", "C": "Using uncontrolled heat that cannot be regulated", "D": "Slow dry rewarming instead of warm water immersion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Remove wet clothing, soak the fingers in warm water near 38 C, then report): Frostbitten tissue needs rapid rewarming in warm water around 37 to 39 C after wet clothing is removed, with medical follow-up. Water near 45 C can burn numb skin, and slow dry rewarming prolongs freezing. Rubbing, including with snow, damages ice-crystal injured tissue. A vent offers uncontrolled temperature."}, {"id": 1617, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In palliative care, Mr. Baptiste, 80, is restless and anxious near the end of life. He reaches for the nurse's hand, and his family has gone home to rest. A coworker says touch should be avoided so boundaries stay intact. Which interpretation fits professional limits?", "options": {"A": "Touch is acceptable if it replaces talking, since words may increase his anxiety", "B": "Touch is appropriate only when the family is present to witness it", "C": "Consented touch such as holding a hand is comforting and fits professional care", "D": "Touch should be avoided with clients since it blurs boundaries and may be misread"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using touch as a substitute for communication", "B": "Requiring family presence as a condition for comfort", "D": "Treating all touch as a boundary violation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Consented touch such as holding a hand is comforting and fits professional care): Therapeutic touch that is invited and for the client's comfort, such as holding a hand, is appropriate and often valued in palliative care. A blanket ban confuses caring touch with boundary violations. Touch should supplement, not replace, communication. A witness is not required when touch is welcomed and appropriate."}, {"id": 1618, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At 0930 Mr. Pike again makes sexual comments during his bath and asks Dana to stay longer. He is alert, oriented and able to understand her requests. Dana has already told him once that the comments are unwelcome. What is the best next action?", "options": {"A": "Continue care while a colleague stays nearby, and avoid raising it since he is a client", "B": "Refuse to provide further care for him and hand his care to a colleague without explanation", "C": "State that the behaviour must stop, document the incidents factually, and report to the charge nurse", "D": "Tell him in front of other clients that he will be discharged if the comments continue"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Tolerating harassment as part of the job", "B": "Abandoning care without a plan", "D": "Humiliating or threatening the client in public"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C193", "cluster_text": "Dana Whitfield, a practical nurse, is working a 12-hour day shift on a surgical unit with eight assigned clients. Mr. Lorne Pike, 40, has made sexual comments toward her during care. Near the end of shift, 70-year-old Mrs. Alvarez becomes unstable while the relief nurse is late. At handover, the hydromorphone count is off by one tablet, and the charge nurse is off the unit.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (State that the behaviour must stop, document the incidents factually, and report to the charge nurse): Nurses are not required to tolerate sexual harassment from a capable client, and the response is to set a clear limit, document factually and report to the charge nurse so care can be planned safely. Quietly accepting it leaves her unprotected. Refusing care without arrangement risks abandonment. Public threats damage dignity and are unprofessional."}, {"id": 1619, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At 1850 Mrs. Alvarez, 70, has a blood pressure of 88/50 and new confusion. Dana's shift ends at 1900, and the relief nurse has not arrived. Dana feels she has done her hours. Which statement best reflects her professional duty?", "options": {"A": "She must stay until the physician arrives and writes new orders, and cannot hand over care", "B": "She remains responsible until a qualified nurse accepts the client, and reports the change", "C": "Her duty ends at the scheduled time, so she may leave after telling an aide", "D": "She may leave once the charge nurse is told, even if no nurse accepts the client"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing she must stay until a physician acts", "C": "Assuming duty ends with the clock", "D": "Leaving after informing someone without a handover"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C193", "cluster_text": "Dana Whitfield, a practical nurse, is working a 12-hour day shift on a surgical unit with eight assigned clients. Mr. Lorne Pike, 40, has made sexual comments toward her during care. Near the end of shift, 70-year-old Mrs. Alvarez becomes unstable while the relief nurse is late. At handover, the hydromorphone count is off by one tablet, and the charge nurse is off the unit.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (She remains responsible until a qualified nurse accepts the client, and reports the change): Abandonment occurs when a nurse leaves a client who needs care without transferring responsibility to a competent person. Dana must report the change and stay until a qualified nurse accepts the client. Leaving after telling an aide or after a verbal notice to the charge nurse does not transfer care. Staying until a physician arrives is not required once a nurse accepts the handover."}, {"id": 1620, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Dana also must pass on a time-sensitive 2000 blood test for Mrs. Alvarez and a pending antibiotic dose. The relief nurse is on a break, and Dana considers leaving a handwritten note on the desk and going home. What should Dana do instead?", "options": {"A": "Chart the pending tasks in the record and trust the nurse to read them during rounds", "B": "Give a verbal and written handover directly to the accepting nurse, who confirms the pending tasks", "C": "Tell the health care aide about the pending tasks and ask the aide to relay them", "D": "Leave the note in a visible spot and text the nurse the tasks after leaving"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming charting replaces communication", "C": "Delegating a nursing handover to an aide", "D": "Relying on a note without direct handover"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C193", "cluster_text": "Dana Whitfield, a practical nurse, is working a 12-hour day shift on a surgical unit with eight assigned clients. Mr. Lorne Pike, 40, has made sexual comments toward her during care. Near the end of shift, 70-year-old Mrs. Alvarez becomes unstable while the relief nurse is late. At handover, the hydromorphone count is off by one tablet, and the charge nurse is off the unit.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give a verbal and written handover directly to the accepting nurse, who confirms the pending tasks): Safe handover is a direct exchange in which the accepting nurse hears, asks questions and confirms the pending tasks. A note on the desk or a text after leaving does not guarantee transfer of responsibility. An aide cannot accept nursing tasks, and charting alone does not alert the nurse to time-sensitive items."}, {"id": 1621, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Dana prepares hydromorphone 1 mg from a 2 mg ampoule for a post-operative client and gives half. Policy requires a witness for any wasted narcotic. The only available nurse is busy for ten minutes. What is the correct approach to the unused portion?", "options": {"A": "Waste it now and ask the witness to cosign the entry when the witness is free", "B": "Keep the remainder secured, then waste it with a witnessing nurse, both signing then", "C": "Return the remainder to the narcotic cupboard in the syringe, labelled for the next dose", "D": "Waste it now and record the volume, since the charge nurse can cosign tomorrow"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Wasting without a witness and cosigning later", "C": "Storing an opened narcotic for reuse", "D": "Delaying cosignature to another day"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C193", "cluster_text": "Dana Whitfield, a practical nurse, is working a 12-hour day shift on a surgical unit with eight assigned clients. Mr. Lorne Pike, 40, has made sexual comments toward her during care. Near the end of shift, 70-year-old Mrs. Alvarez becomes unstable while the relief nurse is late. At handover, the hydromorphone count is off by one tablet, and the charge nurse is off the unit.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep the remainder secured, then waste it with a witnessing nurse, both signing then): A narcotic waste must be witnessed at the time it is wasted, so the nurse secures the remainder and waits for a witness. Cosigning later or by someone who did not see the waste defeats the control and is a documentation violation. Returning an opened syringe to stock risks contamination and diversion."}, {"id": 1622, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Count at shift change shows one fewer hydromorphone 2 mg tablet than the record. Dana remembers a busy morning and thinks she may have forgotten to sign one entry. The oncoming nurse is waiting at the desk to take report. What should Dana do?", "options": {"A": "Tell the oncoming nurse verbally and leave the follow-up to pharmacy", "B": "Recount with the oncoming nurse and record the corrected number in the narcotic book.", "C": "Report the discrepancy to the charge nurse now and file an incident report", "D": "Write a late entry for the missing dose and tell the charge nurse next shift."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Informally mentioning the issue without formal reporting", "B": "Correcting the record without reporting the discrepancy", "D": "Charting a dose she is unsure she gave"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C193", "cluster_text": "Dana Whitfield, a practical nurse, is working a 12-hour day shift on a surgical unit with eight assigned clients. Mr. Lorne Pike, 40, has made sexual comments toward her during care. Near the end of shift, 70-year-old Mrs. Alvarez becomes unstable while the relief nurse is late. At handover, the hydromorphone count is off by one tablet, and the charge nurse is off the unit.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the discrepancy to the charge nurse now and file an incident report): A count discrepancy must be reported at once and investigated, with an incident report describing what is known. Correcting the book without reporting hides the problem and is falsification. Entering a late dose from memory is unreliable and unsafe, and reporting next shift is too late. A verbal mention to a colleague does not meet reporting requirements."}, {"id": 1623, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a hospital, Mr. Lund, 84, has advanced dementia and lacks capacity. His son, the substitute decision maker, says he personally wants everything done, but Mr. Lund once told his family he would not want a feeding tube. What should guide the decision?", "options": {"A": "The physician decides alone because capacity is lacking and earlier wishes cannot bind", "B": "The substitute decides by Mr. Lund's own known wishes, not personal preferences", "C": "The son's own views should lead since he is closest and will live with the outcome", "D": "The care team should vote on what a reasonable person would choose in these conditions"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Excluding the substitute decision maker entirely", "C": "Substituting the decider's views for the client's", "D": "Replacing individual wishes with a group vote"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The substitute decides by Mr. Lund's own known wishes, not personal preferences): A substitute decision maker is expected to apply substituted judgment, meaning what the client would have chosen based on earlier wishes and values. The son's personal preference does not replace those. The physician and team advise, but the decision is made by the substitute using the client's own wishes. A vote ignores the individual."}, {"id": 1624, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In long-term care, the practical nurse notices that resident Mrs. Ferrer, 86, has unexplained bruises on both upper arms and flinches when a care aide approaches. She says nothing. The RN is off site, and a physician is on call. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Keep notes for a week to see if the pattern continues before involving anyone", "B": "Wait for the physician to examine her and decide whether a report is needed", "C": "Question the aide privately and report only if the aide admits to rough handling", "D": "Report through the required facility process now and document the facts"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying while gathering more evidence", "B": "Believing only a physician can report", "C": "Investigating personally before reporting"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report through the required facility process now and document the facts): Nurses who have reasonable grounds to suspect abuse of a vulnerable adult must report through the required process, and they do not need proof or permission from a physician. Bruises with fear around a staff member are reasonable grounds. Confronting the aide may compromise safety and the investigation, and waiting a week leaves the resident at risk."}, {"id": 1625, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Marc, 30, attends a clinic and has taken buprenorphine-naloxone for four months, feels stable and works full time. A coworker said he is trading one addiction for another, and Marc asks whether he should taper right away. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Suggest switching to abstinence-only counselling, since medication blocks real recovery", "B": "Agree it is a temporary substitute and encourage a quick taper to remove dependence", "C": "Advise stopping suddenly once stable since gradual tapering prolongs dependence", "D": "Call it proven treatment that cuts relapse risk, and plan any taper with his prescriber"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Discouraging an effective, evidence-based medication", "B": "Accepting the stigma of replacing one addiction with another", "C": "Advising abrupt discontinuation, raising relapse and overdose risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Call it proven treatment that cuts relapse risk, and plan any taper with his prescriber): Opioid agonist therapy is an evidence-based treatment that reduces cravings, withdrawal, illicit use and overdose, and decisions about tapering belong with the client and prescriber over time. Describing it as a substitute reinforces stigma. Abrupt cessation raises relapse risk, and abstinence-only advice discourages an effective treatment."}, {"id": 1626, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The quality team at a long-term care home wants to reduce pressure injuries. A draft aim reads, improve skin care on the unit. The team lead asks the practical nurse to help refine it. Which aim is best for a quality improvement project?", "options": {"A": "Increase staff awareness of pressure injuries through posters and an annual education session", "B": "Turn every resident more often and chart carefully starting next month", "C": "Reduce new facility-acquired pressure injuries by 25 percent on the unit within six months", "D": "Improve skin care across the unit as staff learn more about prevention"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stating an activity rather than an outcome", "B": "Listing an action without a target", "D": "Writing an aim with no measure or deadline"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduce new facility-acquired pressure injuries by 25 percent on the unit within six months): A strong improvement aim states what will change, by how much, in which group and by when, so progress can be measured. The refined version names the outcome, target and time. Statements about improving care, raising awareness or turning residents more often describe activities without measurable results."}, {"id": 1627, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the emergency department, Lena, 3, has a high fever, muffled voice and drooling, and sits leaning forward with her chin out. She looks anxious and breathes with effort. A student asks to inspect her throat with a tongue depressor. What is the nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Give acetaminophen by mouth for the fever, and examine the throat after she cools.", "B": "Inspect the throat quickly with a tongue depressor and penlight to confirm swelling", "C": "Keep her upright and calm, avoid examining the throat, and call for airway help", "D": "Lay her flat for a quick chest assessment and start oral fluids for dehydration."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Oral medication and delayed airway recognition", "B": "Examining the throat, which can trigger complete airway obstruction", "D": "Lying flat can obstruct the airway in epiglottitis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep her upright and calm, avoid examining the throat, and call for airway help): Drooling, muffled voice, fever and a tripod posture suggest epiglottitis, a threat to the airway. Throat examination or agitation can cause total obstruction, so the child is kept calm and upright while urgent airway help is called. Lying flat worsens obstruction. Oral medication and delayed examination lose time in an emergency."}, {"id": 1628, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a labour unit, Keisha, 28, with severe preeclampsia receives a magnesium sulfate infusion. Her urine output is 20 mL over the last hour, her respiratory rate is 11, and her patellar reflexes are absent. Which action should come first?", "options": {"A": "Recheck blood pressure and report only if the respiratory rate falls below eight", "B": "Stop the infusion per protocol and call the RN, as findings suggest magnesium toxicity", "C": "Continue the infusion and give oxygen, since low urine output is expected in preeclampsia", "D": "Reduce the rate by half and keep monitoring her reflexes and respiratory rate."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for respiratory failure before reporting", "C": "Attributing signs to preeclampsia and continuing", "D": "Reducing the rate instead of stopping at toxicity signs"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop the infusion per protocol and call the RN, as findings suggest magnesium toxicity): Absent reflexes, a slowed respiratory rate and low urine output indicate magnesium toxicity, which is excreted by the kidneys and can cause respiratory arrest. The infusion is stopped per protocol and the RN is called at once. Slowing it and waiting continue the exposure. Waiting for a respiratory rate under eight misses the earlier warning signs already present."}, {"id": 1629, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Barton, 72, attends a community clinic and has type 2 diabetes and reduced foot sensation. He says he soaks his feet in hot water each night for comfort and walks barefoot indoors. His glucose is steady. Which teaching best reduces his risk of foot injury?", "options": {"A": "Inspect feet daily, test water with the elbow, dry between toes, wear shoes", "B": "Keep soaking in warm water for comfort, but shorten the time to ten minutes", "C": "Apply moisturizing lotion between the toes at night and wear socks to bed", "D": "Trim calluses at home with a sharp razor after soaking to reduce pressure"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Continuing hot soaks with reduced sensation", "C": "Applying lotion between toes, trapping moisture", "D": "Cutting calluses at home with a blade"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Inspect feet daily, test water with the elbow, dry between toes, wear shoes): Reduced sensation means burns and cuts may go unnoticed, so daily inspection, water tested with the elbow, drying between the toes and protective footwear lower the risk. Shorter hot soaks still risk burns. Moisture between toes promotes fungal infection. Cutting calluses with a razor can cause wounds that heal poorly in diabetes."}, {"id": 1630, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sophie, 26, is being induced with oxytocin on a labour unit. Her contractions come every 90 seconds and last 100 seconds, and the fetal heart rate shows late decelerations. The nurse's checklist lists increase the rate if contractions are less than every two minutes. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Decrease the oxytocin by one increment and reassess the contraction pattern in thirty minutes.", "B": "Maintain the current rate and reassess contractions after thirty minutes of monitoring", "C": "Stop the oxytocin per protocol, turn her left side, and notify the RN immediately", "D": "Give a fluid bolus, turn her to the left side, and continue oxytocin unchanged."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reducing instead of stopping oxytocin during tachysystole", "B": "Continuing an infusion that is harming the fetus", "D": "Continuing oxytocin despite late decelerations"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the oxytocin per protocol, turn her left side, and notify the RN immediately): Contractions more frequent than five in ten minutes with late decelerations indicate uterine tachysystole and fetal compromise, so oxytocin is stopped, she is turned on her left side to improve placental perfusion, and the RN is called. Reducing slightly or maintaining the rate prolongs fetal hypoxia. Continuing oxytocin after repositioning leaves the cause untreated."}, {"id": 1631, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Weiss, 58, tells a clinic nurse that he has a sudden shower of floaters and a curtain-like shadow over the upper left part of his vision since this morning. He has no pain, wears bifocals, and has a routine eye appointment in three weeks. What should the nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Patch the eye and arrange an eye appointment early tomorrow morning.", "B": "Keep the routine appointment and record any change in vision on a daily chart", "C": "Treat it as an emergency and arrange urgent eye assessment today", "D": "Arrange an urgent optometrist visit within three days and avoid heavy lifting meanwhile."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying urgent assessment of suspected retinal detachment", "B": "Waiting for a routine visit with signs of detachment", "D": "Timeline too slow for retinal detachment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat it as an emergency and arrange urgent eye assessment today): Sudden floaters with a curtain over vision suggest retinal detachment, which needs assessment within hours to protect sight. Retinal detachment is painless, so the lack of pain is not reassuring. Waiting until tomorrow, three days or three weeks delays surgery. Lubricating drops do not treat it."}, {"id": 1632, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Rahim, 55, visiting a clinic, has had type 2 diabetes for eight years. She has good vision and says she will see an eye specialist only if her vision changes. Her A1C is 8.1 percent. Which response by the practical nurse is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Explain that retinopathy can progress silently, so regular eye examinations are advised", "B": "Suggest eye exams only after her A1C stays above 10 percent for a year", "C": "Advise a specialist exam every five years, since her A1C is near target.", "D": "Explain that eye exams are needed once vision becomes blurred or floaters begin."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Setting an arbitrary glucose threshold for screening", "C": "Screening interval too long for diabetes of eight years", "D": "Waiting for symptoms of a silent disease"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that retinopathy can progress silently, so regular eye examinations are advised): Diabetic retinopathy is often silent until damage is advanced, so scheduled dilated eye examinations are recommended regardless of symptoms. Waiting for vision changes means screening happens too late. Five years is too long for eight years of diabetes, an A1C threshold is not a screening rule, and a home chart cannot detect retinal changes."}, {"id": 1633, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Zoe, 5, is brought to a clinic with an itchy rash for three days. Her mother says it is red and that Zoe recently changed laundry soap and had a cold. The nurse is preparing to document the rash. Which documentation best describes the lesions?", "options": {"A": "Record the colour and write rash on skin, since naming lesions is the physician's role", "B": "Record only size and location since other features need a physician's examination", "C": "Record the suspected diagnosis such as eczema and the treatment the mother tried", "D": "Record location, distribution, size, shape, colour, texture, and onset"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reporting only the colour of the rash and nothing else", "B": "Omitting observable features that a physician needs", "C": "Documenting a diagnosis instead of observations"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Record location, distribution, size, shape, colour, texture, and onset): Objective, detailed description lets the team judge the cause and track change, and includes location, distribution, size, shape, colour, texture, blisters or crusting, plus onset and itching. Colour alone is inadequate. Nurses document observations rather than diagnoses, and describing visible features is within the nursing assessment, not limited to physicians."}, {"id": 1634, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Six hours ago Mr. Pelletier received intravenous chemotherapy. The oncology practical nurse must empty his urinal and change soiled bed linen while a porter waits for a transport request and his daughter asks about lunch. Which approach to handling his urine and linen best protects the nurse over the next two days?", "options": {"A": "Wear a surgical mask and ordinary gloves, then place soiled linen in the regular unit hamper", "B": "Wear one pair of ordinary examination gloves and pour the urine into the regular hopper", "C": "Wear a chemotherapy-rated gown and double gloves, and bag linen in hazardous containers", "D": "Wear a paper gown with ordinary gloves, then rinse soiled linen in the utility room sink"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using respiratory protection for a skin and splash exposure risk", "B": "Treating body fluids as routine waste though cytotoxic drugs are excreted for days", "D": "Rinsing hazardous linen, which splashes and spreads the drug residue"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C198", "cluster_text": "Harold Pelletier, 78, is admitted to the oncology unit for a cycle of intravenous chemotherapy. He takes eight home medications, including a bedtime sedative and a diuretic, and his daughter brought the pill bottles. He has had two falls this winter. After this admission he will transfer to a long-term care home for respite, and the practical nurse is part of his care team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wear a chemotherapy-rated gown and double gloves, and bag linen in hazardous containers): Cytotoxic drugs and their metabolites stay in urine and other body fluids for about two days, so a rated gown, double gloves and hazardous waste containers protect the nurse. Ordinary gloves with routine disposal leave skin and splash exposure unaddressed. A mask protects the airway, not the skin, and rinsing linen in a sink spreads residue. Treat all fluids after chemotherapy as hazardous."}, {"id": 1635, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On admission, Mr. Pelletier's daughter hands the nurse eight pill bottles, yet the physician's admission orders list only five medications. He says he takes a sedative every night and a water pill each morning, and neither is ordered. What is the best next step in reconciling his medications?", "options": {"A": "Compare the home list with the admission orders and report each omission to the prescriber", "B": "Give the home medications from the daughter's bottles so his routine is not interrupted", "C": "Remove the sedative and the diuretic from the home list because both raise his fall risk", "D": "Leave the unordered medications alone because the physician has already written the full admission orders"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Giving drugs without a current order, bypassing the prescriber", "C": "Discontinuing medications, a decision outside practical nurse scope", "D": "Assuming the written orders are complete without a source comparison"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C198", "cluster_text": "Harold Pelletier, 78, is admitted to the oncology unit for a cycle of intravenous chemotherapy. He takes eight home medications, including a bedtime sedative and a diuretic, and his daughter brought the pill bottles. He has had two falls this winter. After this admission he will transfer to a long-term care home for respite, and the practical nurse is part of his care team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Compare the home list with the admission orders and report each omission to the prescriber): Reconciliation means comparing every home medication with the admission orders and sending discrepancies to the prescriber, who decides to continue, change or stop each one. Giving drugs from home bottles lacks an order and risks duplication. Ignoring the omissions leaves possible withdrawal or therapy gaps unrecognized. Removing drugs is a prescriber decision, so the nurse reports rather than decides."}, {"id": 1636, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Pelletier is ready to transfer to a long-term care home. The hospital's discharge list differs from his old home list in three places: one dose is lower and two drugs are absent. A care aide suggests restarting the old list because the resident knows it well. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the daughter which list she prefers and follow her choice for each drug", "B": "Give the hospital list as written and file the old list without comparing the two lists", "C": "Restart the old home list because the resident has taken those medications safely for many years", "D": "Clarify every difference with the prescriber before giving any dose from either list"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Letting family preference replace a prescriber's order", "B": "Accepting a list without checking for unexplained changes", "C": "Resuming a list that the hospital has revised"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C198", "cluster_text": "Harold Pelletier, 78, is admitted to the oncology unit for a cycle of intravenous chemotherapy. He takes eight home medications, including a bedtime sedative and a diuretic, and his daughter brought the pill bottles. He has had two falls this winter. After this admission he will transfer to a long-term care home for respite, and the practical nurse is part of his care team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Clarify every difference with the prescriber before giving any dose from either list): At transfer the nurse compares the old and new lists and clarifies each difference with the prescriber, because the hospital may have changed doses deliberately. The old list may now cause harm, and following the new list blindly can carry forward an error. A family member's preference cannot replace an order. Reconciliation is repeated at every transition of care."}, {"id": 1637, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After respite, a home care nurse reviews medications for Mr. Pelletier. He reports dizziness on standing and fell once last week. His list has eight drugs, including a sedative, a diuretic and an antihypertensive. His daughter asks the nurse to request a new dizziness medication. Which action best addresses this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Report the dizziness and falls and request a prescriber review of each medication's ongoing need", "B": "Advise him to take the diuretic and antihypertensive together in the evening to reduce daytime dizziness", "C": "Suggest he skip the sedative on nights when he feels well rested and relaxed", "D": "Ask the prescriber for a new medication to treat the dizziness at the next scheduled appointment"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Changing administration times without an order", "C": "Altering doses without prescriber direction", "D": "Adding a drug to treat an adverse effect, which creates a prescribing cascade"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C198", "cluster_text": "Harold Pelletier, 78, is admitted to the oncology unit for a cycle of intravenous chemotherapy. He takes eight home medications, including a bedtime sedative and a diuretic, and his daughter brought the pill bottles. He has had two falls this winter. After this admission he will transfer to a long-term care home for respite, and the practical nurse is part of his care team.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the dizziness and falls and request a prescriber review of each medication's ongoing need): Dizziness and falls in an older adult on many drugs signal possible adverse effects, so the nurse reports and requests a review of each drug's ongoing need. A new drug for dizziness risks a prescribing cascade. Rescheduling or skipping doses changes therapy without an order. Deprescribing review is the safest response to polypharmacy."}, {"id": 1638, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Raj, 46, had an outpatient hernia repair and received intravenous sedation two hours ago. He feels alert and wide awake. His sister is delayed in traffic, so he plans to drive himself home, and the unit is busy with discharge teaching overdue. What advice should the practical nurse give about transport and activity?", "options": {"A": "Allow him to take a taxi home alone as long as he keeps a phone nearby", "B": "Allow him to drive himself after one more hour of rest in the waiting area", "C": "Allow him to drive home once he feels alert, since the sedative has now worn off", "D": "Arrange an adult to take him home and avoid driving for a day"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating transport alone as safe despite lingering impairment", "B": "Setting a short rest time that ignores the effects of sedation on judgment", "C": "Using feeling alert as proof that sedation has cleared"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Arrange an adult to take him home and avoid driving for a day): Sedation impairs judgment and reaction time for hours after a person feels alert, so a responsible adult should escort him and stay overnight. Feeling alert does not show the drug has cleared. A taxi alone leaves him without help if he becomes drowsy or faint. A short rest period does not match the sedative's duration. No driving or major decisions for twenty-four hours is the standard teaching."}, {"id": 1639, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Dube, 58, is going home with abdominal staples after bowel surgery. She asks whether a long hot bath would ease her stiffness, and she wants to remove a dry dressing herself. She is anxious about infection and her husband will help at home. Which teaching about incision care is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply an antibiotic ointment over the staples daily to prevent infection from developing", "B": "Soak in a warm bath each evening to soften the skin and loosen the staples", "C": "Keep the incision clean and dry, pat it dry after showering, and avoid soaking", "D": "Scrub the incision with soap and a cloth to remove any crusts around the staples"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying products without an order", "B": "Soaking a closed wound, which softens tissue and invites bacteria", "D": "Scrubbing, which irritates the wound edges and delays healing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep the incision clean and dry, pat it dry after showering, and avoid soaking): A healing incision should stay clean and dry, so showering is acceptable when permitted but soaking is not until the surgeon allows it. Baths soften the closure and can introduce bacteria. Ointment without an order can trap moisture. Scrubbing damages new tissue. Gentle cleansing and patting dry protect the wound, and she should report redness, drainage or fever."}, {"id": 1640, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mei, 29, gave birth vaginally four hours ago. Her vital signs are stable and she says she feels fine and wants to rest. The nurse notes that her perineal pad soaked through in fifteen minutes and her partner is asking about visiting hours. What should the practical nurse assess next?", "options": {"A": "Perineal swelling and bruising, then offer an ice pack and extra oral fluids for comfort", "B": "Respirations and pulse readings, then check them again in four hours if they remain stable", "C": "Fundal firmness and position, then lochia and clots, reporting a boggy uterus at once", "D": "Breast filling and infant latch, then plan feeding teaching and a quiet room."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Focusing on comfort measures while heavy bleeding goes unevaluated", "B": "Relying on vital signs, which change late in hemorrhage", "D": "Prioritizing lactation teaching over active bleeding"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fundal firmness and position, then lochia and clots, reporting a boggy uterus at once): A saturated pad in fifteen minutes suggests heavy bleeding, and the most common cause is uterine atony, so the nurse checks fundal firmness, position and lochia and reports a boggy uterus. Normal vital signs can persist until blood loss is large. Perineal and breast care matter but do not address the bleeding. Fundal massage may be required under direction."}, {"id": 1641, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Alvarez, 63, went home two days after a cholecystectomy. His wife phones the clinic to say he has pain and nausea, which she thinks are expected. She adds that he has a temperature of 38.6 C and his eyes look yellow, and he ate a small lunch. What should the nurse tell her?", "options": {"A": "Keep him on clear fluids and call only if the abdominal pain becomes severe", "B": "Arrange assessment today, because fever with jaundice and pain after surgery needs medical review", "C": "Wait for his follow-up visit next week and mention the symptoms to the surgeon then", "D": "Give acetaminophen and watch him through the weekend before calling the surgeon about his symptoms"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a combination of new signs as ordinary recovery", "C": "Postponing review until a scheduled visit", "D": "Treating fever with a medication and delaying assessment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Arrange assessment today, because fever with jaundice and pain after surgery needs medical review): Fever with jaundice and persistent pain suggests infection or a bile duct problem, so he needs assessment the same day. Acetaminophen can mask a fever without treating the cause. Clear fluids and waiting both delay care. Mild pain and nausea alone may be expected, but the added signs make them reportable."}, {"id": 1642, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a well-child visit, Jamal's mother says her 19-month-old son does not walk yet, although he crawls quickly and babbles. His grandmother insists that boys are late walkers, and the clinic is running behind schedule today. What is the best action for the practical nurse to take?", "options": {"A": "Advise the family to buy a baby walker so he can practise standing and walking daily", "B": "Reassure the mother that he will likely walk within the next few months on his own", "C": "Schedule a repeat visit in six months to see whether he has caught up in walking", "D": "Report to the NP and support a developmental screen and early intervention referral"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Recommending equipment that does not address the cause", "B": "Reassuring without a screen, repeating a family belief", "C": "Delaying referral while the window for early intervention narrows"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report to the NP and support a developmental screen and early intervention referral): Most children walk by eighteen months, and a child not yet walking at nineteen months warrants screening because early intervention improves outcomes. Reassurance and waiting may postpone help. A walker does not treat delay and may pose safety risks. The nurse reports and supports screening rather than diagnosing."}, {"id": 1643, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a wellness visit, Mrs. Fontaine, 81, reports new confusion over the past three days, a poor appetite and a mild cough. Her daughter says it is just old age. She also mentions that her grandson visited last weekend. Which response best reflects sound assessment of an older adult?", "options": {"A": "Check for a urinary infection at the next scheduled visit and watch her appetite.", "B": "Reassure her that memory slips are common after the age of eighty and move on", "C": "Treat the sudden confusion as a change needing assessment and report it", "D": "Suggest a memory aid and book a cognitive screen in three months."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Postponing assessment of acute confusion", "B": "Reassuring away an acute change", "D": "Treating acute confusion as slow dementia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat the sudden confusion as a change needing assessment and report it): Sudden confusion over days is a change from baseline and may signal infection, dehydration or medication effects, so it needs prompt assessment and reporting. Normal aging causes slow, mild changes, not rapid confusion. Waiting a month or three months delays treatable causes. Any new symptom in an older adult deserves a look beyond age."}, {"id": 1644, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "During a visit, home care client Mr. Gagnon, 72, says he is short of money before his pension arrives. He asks the nurse to lend him twenty dollars for groceries, promising to repay it on her next visit. He adds that nobody needs to know. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Lend the small amount once since he is trustworthy and has promised to repay it soon", "B": "Accept an item of his as security and return it when he repays the loan", "C": "Lend the money and record the loan in the care notes so everyone can see it", "D": "Decline kindly, explain the professional boundary, and offer a social work referral"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a small loan as harmless", "B": "Creating a financial arrangement through collateral", "C": "Believing documentation makes a financial exchange acceptable"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline kindly, explain the professional boundary, and offer a social work referral): Financial dealings with clients blur the professional relationship and can be exploitation, even when small and well meant, so the nurse declines and links him to resources. Documenting a loan does not make it acceptable. Taking collateral deepens the entanglement. Referral addresses the real need while keeping the boundary."}, {"id": 1645, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Practical nurse Priya Nair finishes changing a leg ulcer dressing for Mr. Okafor in his cramped kitchen. Soap is available at the sink, but the only towel in sight is a household cloth hanging by the stove. How should she complete hand hygiene before leaving the home?", "options": {"A": "Wash with soap and water, then dry with a clean paper towel from her bag", "B": "Wash with soap and water, then dry with her uniform sleeve to avoid contact.", "C": "Wash with soap and water and dry with the household towel hanging beside the stove", "D": "Wash with soap and water, then dry with the cloth after wiping it with disinfectant."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Drying with a contaminated sleeve", "C": "Contaminating clean hands with a shared cloth", "D": "Wiping a household cloth does not make it clean"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C201", "cluster_text": "Priya Nair is a practical nurse who does home visits in a small apartment. She will change a dressing for Mr. Okafor, 66, who has a leg ulcer, and later she will run a one-day vaccine clinic at a community centre. Space in the home is limited and the kitchen sink is the only water source. Several clients will wait in a long line for injections there.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Wash with soap and water, then dry with a clean paper towel from her bag): Hands must be dried on a single-use clean towel so washing is not undone. A shared household towel carries organisms from other people, and a sleeve or an unwashed cloth recontaminates the hands. Skipping washing because gloves were worn is wrong since gloves can have small defects. Carrying paper towels is a standard home care practice."}, {"id": 1646, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the community vaccine clinic, Priya has drawn up a dose for a client who then changes his mind and leaves. A colleague suggests giving the filled syringe to the next person because the needle is still capped. The line of clients is long. What should Priya do?", "options": {"A": "Hold the syringe at room temperature and use it on a client arriving within the hour", "B": "Discard the prepared syringe in the sharps container and draw fresh vaccine", "C": "Use the prepared syringe for the next client after wiping the needle with an alcohol swab", "D": "Return the unused dose to the vial so that the vaccine is not wasted"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Keeping a prepared dose without labelling or storage control", "C": "Reusing equipment between clients", "D": "Returning drawn product to a vial, contaminating it"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C201", "cluster_text": "Priya Nair is a practical nurse who does home visits in a small apartment. She will change a dressing for Mr. Okafor, 66, who has a leg ulcer, and later she will run a one-day vaccine clinic at a community centre. Space in the home is limited and the kitchen sink is the only water source. Several clients will wait in a long line for injections there.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Discard the prepared syringe in the sharps container and draw fresh vaccine): A syringe or needle used or opened for one person is never shared, and a dose drawn up for one client is discarded if not given. Wiping a needle does not sterilize it. Returning medication to a vial contaminates the contents. Waiting beside the clinic table risks mix-ups and temperature breaks. Single-use equipment is a core infection control rule."}, {"id": 1647, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "While drawing medication from a multi-dose vial, Priya's needle brushes the edge of the clinic countertop. The vial still holds many doses and the line of clients keeps growing. A volunteer says the counter looks clean. What action is correct?", "options": {"A": "Continue drawing, since the needle touched only a surface that looks clean and wiped", "B": "Wipe the needle with an alcohol swab and continue drawing from the same vial", "C": "Discard the needle and syringe and use a new sterile set while protecting the vial stopper", "D": "Change only the syringe and keep using the needle that touched the counter"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Judging contamination by appearance", "B": "Believing alcohol disinfects a contaminated needle", "D": "Replacing part of the equipment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C201", "cluster_text": "Priya Nair is a practical nurse who does home visits in a small apartment. She will change a dressing for Mr. Okafor, 66, who has a leg ulcer, and later she will run a one-day vaccine clinic at a community centre. Space in the home is limited and the kitchen sink is the only water source. Several clients will wait in a long line for injections there.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Discard the needle and syringe and use a new sterile set while protecting the vial stopper): A needle that touches any non-sterile surface is contaminated, so the nurse replaces the whole needle and syringe and protects the stopper. Alcohol swabs do not sterilize a needle. Appearance does not show whether a surface is free of organisms. Replacing only the syringe leaves the contaminated needle in use."}, {"id": 1648, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before giving an intramuscular injection to an adult at the clinic, Priya cleans the skin with an alcohol swab. The line is long and she wants to save time, so she is tempted to speed up drying. What technique best supports safe skin antisepsis?", "options": {"A": "Wipe the site with alcohol for fifteen seconds, then insert the needle immediately.", "B": "Allow the alcohol to air dry fully on the skin before inserting the needle", "C": "Wipe the site and insert the needle right away to keep the line moving", "D": "Wipe once, then blot the site with dry gauze before inserting the needle."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Inserting before the alcohol has dried", "C": "Skipping contact time needed to kill organisms", "D": "Blotting removes alcohol before it can act"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C201", "cluster_text": "Priya Nair is a practical nurse who does home visits in a small apartment. She will change a dressing for Mr. Okafor, 66, who has a leg ulcer, and later she will run a one-day vaccine clinic at a community centre. Space in the home is limited and the kitchen sink is the only water source. Several clients will wait in a long line for injections there.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Allow the alcohol to air dry fully on the skin before inserting the needle): Alcohol needs time to act and evaporate, so letting it air dry gives antisepsis and reduces stinging. Inserting the needle before it dries carries alcohol into tissue without full effect, and blotting removes it early. Fanning or vigorous rubbing does not replace drying time. Patience at this step is part of safe injection practice."}, {"id": 1649, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sam tells the nurse, \"I know I should be exercising, but I just can't see myself doing it every day.\" He sighs and looks at the floor, and his blood pressure was normal at triage. The nurse recognizes ambivalence. Which response best uses a motivational interviewing approach?", "options": {"A": "Offer a printed exercise plan and ask him to follow it faithfully for one month", "B": "Explain how walking lowers blood glucose and blood pressure and describe the risks of inactivity", "C": "Tell him he must walk every day or his diabetes will steadily get worse", "D": "Ask what he would like about walking more and what worries him, then reflect his answers"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Handing over a plan without exploring his own reasons", "B": "Lecturing on risks, which raises resistance", "C": "Using a directive warning that removes his choice"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C195", "cluster_text": "Sam Whitehorse, 54, attends a community clinic for follow-up of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure. He started a daily walking plan two months ago but stopped three weeks ago after a cold. He also has COPD, has lost his appetite, and says he feels flat. The practical nurse is meeting him with the NP's plan. He arrives alone today and seems tired.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask what he would like about walking more and what worries him, then reflect his answers): Motivational interviewing draws out the person's own reasons through open questions and reflection, which strengthens commitment. Lecturing and warnings often increase resistance when someone is ambivalent. A printed plan alone ignores his stated doubt. The nurse supports his autonomy and lets him weigh pros and cons."}, {"id": 1650, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a follow-up visit, Sam admits he stopped walking three weeks ago after a cold and now feels discouraged about starting again. A colleague at the desk calls him noncompliant. Which understanding should guide the nurse's response to Sam during this visit?", "options": {"A": "Recommend he restart the full daily plan at once to rebuild the habit quickly.", "B": "Lapses are common, so explore the barrier and restart with a small goal", "C": "Defer any exercise discussion until his next annual review with the physician", "D": "Refer him to the physician for exercise clearance first and pause the plan meanwhile."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignoring the barrier and demanding a full restart", "C": "Dropping support at a time he needs it", "D": "Adding an unnecessary barrier to restarting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C195", "cluster_text": "Sam Whitehorse, 54, attends a community clinic for follow-up of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure. He started a daily walking plan two months ago but stopped three weeks ago after a cold. He also has COPD, has lost his appetite, and says he feels flat. The practical nurse is meeting him with the NP's plan. He arrives alone today and seems tired.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lapses are common, so explore the barrier and restart with a small goal): Relapse to old habits is expected during change, and judging it as noncompliance damages trust. The nurse explores what got in the way and sets a smaller, realistic goal. A full restart can feel overwhelming. Pausing the plan for clearance adds a barrier and removes support when it is most needed."}, {"id": 1651, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A home care nurse visits Mrs. Beaulieu, 77, who has diabetes and often misses insulin doses. Her hands are swollen from arthritis and she squints at the numbers on the pen. Her son calls her lazy. What is the best next step for the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Explain that missed doses can cause serious problems and ask her to try harder", "B": "Remind her each day to take the insulin and record every missed dose in her chart", "C": "Assess her dexterity and vision, then ask the NP about adaptive devices", "D": "Ask her son to give every injection for her from now on to prevent missed doses"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using persuasion where a device problem exists", "B": "Treating a physical barrier as lack of effort", "D": "Removing her independence before exploring adaptations"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C195", "cluster_text": "Sam Whitehorse, 54, attends a community clinic for follow-up of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure. He started a daily walking plan two months ago but stopped three weeks ago after a cold. He also has COPD, has lost his appetite, and says he feels flat. The practical nurse is meeting him with the NP's plan. He arrives alone today and seems tired.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess her dexterity and vision, then ask the NP about adaptive devices): Missed doses may come from limited grip and poor vision, not laziness, so the nurse assesses barriers and seeks adaptive aids. Reminders do not fix dexterity. Transferring all injections to her son removes her independence without trying supports. Urging her to try harder ignores the physical cause."}, {"id": 1652, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Sam's COPD is stable and his breathing score has not changed, yet he has eaten poorly for a month, sleeps badly and says nothing interests him. He still walks to the clinic from the bus stop. Which interpretation of these findings is most appropriate for the nurse to act on?", "options": {"A": "His low appetite reflects poor food choices and needs a written meal plan only", "B": "His symptoms come from COPD, so reassure him and continue the current care plan", "C": "His low interest is caused by cold weather and will lift when spring arrives", "D": "These may be signs of depression, so report them to the NP for assessment"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming a nutritional cause alone", "B": "Blaming mood changes on physical illness", "C": "Dismissing symptoms as seasonal"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C195", "cluster_text": "Sam Whitehorse, 54, attends a community clinic for follow-up of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure. He started a daily walking plan two months ago but stopped three weeks ago after a cold. He also has COPD, has lost his appetite, and says he feels flat. The practical nurse is meeting him with the NP's plan. He arrives alone today and seems tired.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (These may be signs of depression, so report them to the NP for assessment): Poor appetite, sleep disturbance and loss of interest with stable physical status suggest depression, which is common in chronic illness and treatable. Attributing the symptoms to COPD delays care. Nutrition support alone misses the mood cause, and seasonal explanations are speculation. The nurse reports observations for assessment rather than naming a diagnosis."}, {"id": 1653, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mrs. Tran, 35, visits her sick son on a pediatric ward and tells the nurse she keeps gloves on in his room all day so she does not need to wash her hands. The nurse is teaching her about infection control. Which teaching is best?", "options": {"A": "Gloves protect her well enough, so wash her hands only when they look visibly soiled", "B": "Washing is needed once before leaving the unit each day and not more often", "C": "Gloves do not replace handwashing, so wash before and after contact and glove use", "D": "Use sanitizer before putting gloves on and skip washing after she removes them"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating gloves as a substitute for hand hygiene", "B": "Washing only when hands look dirty", "D": "Skipping hygiene after glove removal"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Gloves do not replace handwashing, so wash before and after contact and glove use): Gloves can have tiny holes and hands become contaminated during removal, so hand hygiene is still needed before and after contact and after glove removal. Washing only when hands look soiled misses invisible organisms. A single daily wash is far too infrequent. Teach the family the moments of hand hygiene in plain language."}, {"id": 1654, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A home care nurse visits four-year-old Lucas, who has a gastrostomy tube. His father says he flushes the tube with whatever drink is handy, including juice, and the tube sometimes clogs. Lucas is gaining weight well. What teaching about flushing is best?", "options": {"A": "Flush with the ordered amount of water before and after each feed or medication", "B": "Flush with warm juice because the sugar helps loosen the formula inside the tube", "C": "Flush with cooled boiled water after feeds, since water before feeds dilutes the formula.", "D": "Flush with the ordered amount of water once daily, and use juice for clogs."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using juice, which can clog and grow bacteria", "C": "Flushing only after feeds", "D": "Too few flushes and juice used to clear clogs"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Flush with the ordered amount of water before and after each feed or medication): Water is the standard flush because it does not coagulate, ferment or cling to the tube, and flushing before and after feeds helps prevent clogs. Juice and milk can form deposits and support bacterial growth. Flushing only after feeds or once daily leaves formula in the tube. Volume follows the order."}, {"id": 1655, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Duval, 84, in long-term care, has become quiet, avoids the dining room and no longer joins bingo. Staff think he is depressed. He does not respond when the nurse speaks from behind him, and his glasses have a cracked lens. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Document his withdrawal as normal aging and monitor it once each week", "B": "Request an antidepressant review before checking for any sensory problems that could explain withdrawal", "C": "Encourage him to attend group activities despite his reluctance to leave his room", "D": "Check his hearing and vision for correctable problems and report them with his mood changes"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepting withdrawal as normal", "B": "Assuming a mood problem without ruling out sensory loss", "C": "Pushing participation without addressing the barrier"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Check his hearing and vision for correctable problems and report them with his mood changes): Sensory loss often looks like withdrawal because people cannot take part in what they cannot hear or see. Checking hearing aids, wax and glasses is simple and correctable. Seeking medication first skips basic assessment. Pushing group activity ignores the cause. Withdrawal is not a normal aging change and needs follow-up if it persists."}, {"id": 1656, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A man in his thirties comes into the walk-in clinic with a deep forearm laceration, and blood drips onto the floor. The nurse is alone and the gloves are in a drawer across the room. The client is pale but talking. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Apply direct pressure with bare hands to stop the bleeding sooner and wash afterward", "B": "Apply firm pressure with clean gauze held by the client and fetch gloves.", "C": "Apply pressure through a thick towel without gloves, then wash her hands afterward.", "D": "Put on gloves and eye protection if available, then apply firm pressure"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Skipping gloves because it is urgent", "B": "Leaving the nurse unprotected while blood is exposed", "C": "A towel does not replace gloves"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C199", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a walk-in clinic. A man in his thirties arrives with a deep forearm laceration from a broken window. Later that day, a resident of a nearby long-term care home, 83, who takes warfarin, is sent for review after a fall. The nurse must manage bleeding, safety and documentation. The clinic is short staffed today and the waiting room is full.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Put on gloves and eye protection if available, then apply firm pressure): Standard precautions apply even in emergencies, and gloves take seconds to put on, so the nurse protects herself while controlling bleeding with firm pressure. Bare hands expose her to blood. Leaving the client to rely on bare hands while she searches delays control. A towel is not a barrier against blood."}, {"id": 1657, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A resident at the care home, Mrs. Lindqvist, 83, is found on the floor after a fall and complains of neck pain. She asks to be helped up, and an aide steps forward to lift her. Her lunch tray is still on the bed. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Stop the aide, keep her still with head supported, and call for help", "B": "Log-roll her flat with two aides and check her neck for tenderness first.", "C": "Apply a cervical collar from the supply cabinet, then help her up to a chair.", "D": "Help her to sit up slowly with support to see whether the neck pain eases"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Moving a client with suspected spinal injury", "C": "Collar applied but client still moved", "D": "Sitting up a client with neck pain after a fall"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C199", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a walk-in clinic. A man in his thirties arrives with a deep forearm laceration from a broken window. Later that day, a resident of a nearby long-term care home, 83, who takes warfarin, is sent for review after a fall. The nurse must manage bleeding, safety and documentation. The clinic is short staffed today and the waiting room is full.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop the aide, keep her still with head supported, and call for help): Neck pain after a fall raises concern for cervical injury, so the client is kept still with the spine supported until emergency help assesses. Sitting her up or lifting her can worsen a spinal injury. Log-rolling or a collar followed by moving her can twist the neck. Calling for help early speeds assessment."}, {"id": 1658, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Rossi, 79, takes warfarin and fell at the care home, striking his head on a door frame. He has no symptoms and wants to leave observation after one hour so he can attend a card game. What is the best response by the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Continue neurological observations as ordered and report headache, vomiting or drowsiness", "B": "Discharge him from observation since he feels well and has no visible head injury", "C": "Let him sleep through the night without neurological checks so he can recover from the fall", "D": "Stop observations after the first hour if his vital signs are normal and he is alert"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relying on how he feels", "C": "Skipping checks despite delayed bleeding risk", "D": "Stopping observation too soon"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C199", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a walk-in clinic. A man in his thirties arrives with a deep forearm laceration from a broken window. Later that day, a resident of a nearby long-term care home, 83, who takes warfarin, is sent for review after a fall. The nurse must manage bleeding, safety and documentation. The clinic is short staffed today and the waiting room is full.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continue neurological observations as ordered and report headache, vomiting or drowsiness): Anticoagulants raise the risk of intracranial bleeding, which can appear hours later, so neurological observations continue and new symptoms are reported urgently. Feeling well and normal vital signs early do not exclude delayed bleeding. Skipping checks during sleep could miss a change in consciousness."}, {"id": 1659, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A client on a hospital ward slid from her chair while the nurse watched, then stood up unharmed and says she is fine. A colleague suggests skipping a report because no injury occurred and the shift is already busy. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Assess the client, notify the charge nurse, and file an incident report", "B": "Skip the report because no harm came to the client or anyone else during the event", "C": "Record the event in her chart and leave the hospital incident reporting system unused", "D": "Write a report only if the client later develops pain, bruising or a complaint"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reporting only when harm occurs", "C": "Charting without using the safety system", "D": "Waiting for injury before reporting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C199", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a walk-in clinic. A man in his thirties arrives with a deep forearm laceration from a broken window. Later that day, a resident of a nearby long-term care home, 83, who takes warfarin, is sent for review after a fall. The nurse must manage bleeding, safety and documentation. The clinic is short staffed today and the waiting room is full.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Assess the client, notify the charge nurse, and file an incident report): Incident reports capture near misses and events regardless of injury, so patterns can be found and prevention improved. The nurse assesses the client, notifies the charge nurse and documents facts. Reporting only after harm loses learning. Charting alone does not trigger review. Reports are for system improvement, not blame."}, {"id": 1660, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Alex, 35, in hospital for an opioid use disorder, has abdominal cramps, diarrhea, yawning and muscle aches. His vital signs are stable and he says he feels awful. A colleague fears seizures like those seen in alcohol withdrawal. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Give fluids and an antidiarrheal from the chart without asking the NP.", "B": "Prepare seizure precautions since opioid withdrawal is life-threatening in the same way as alcohol withdrawal", "C": "Reassure him the symptoms will pass and check his vitals once each shift.", "D": "Provide comfort measures and hydration, monitor him, and report symptoms so the NP can order treatment"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving medication outside the practical nurse scope without orders", "B": "Equating opioid withdrawal with alcohol withdrawal complications", "C": "Inadequate monitoring and no comfort measures"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Provide comfort measures and hydration, monitor him, and report symptoms so the NP can order treatment): Opioid withdrawal is very uncomfortable but rarely life-threatening, so care centres on comfort, fluids, monitoring for dehydration and reporting for medication support. Seizure risk is typical of alcohol or sedative withdrawal. Giving medication without an order is outside the practical nurse's scope. Offering nothing neglects distress and raises the chance he leaves treatment."}, {"id": 1661, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A refugee family of five attends the community clinic for a first visit three weeks after arriving in Canada. A clinic assistant suggests isolating them and testing everyone for tuberculosis because they are newcomers. The father looks worried. What approach should the nurse support?", "options": {"A": "Delay screening for six months to let the family settle into their new community", "B": "Test only the children, since they attend school with many other children in the area", "C": "Isolate the whole family in a separate room until every test result has returned", "D": "Screen each person by individual risk and explain each step through an interpreter"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying screening without clinical reason", "B": "Applying a narrow screen without assessing risk", "C": "Assuming newcomers are infectious"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C203", "cluster_text": "A community health clinic sees a family of five who arrived in Canada from a conflict zone three weeks ago. The parents, in their thirties, speak limited English, and three children are in school. The practical nurse works with an interpreter, an NP and a settlement worker to plan screening, care and teaching. The family has been staying with relatives since arrival.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Screen each person by individual risk and explain each step through an interpreter): Screening follows individual risk, exposure and guidelines, not a blanket assumption that newcomers carry disease. Isolating a family stigmatizes them and is not justified. Testing children alone ignores adults. Postponing for months delays care. An interpreter helps the family understand and consent to each test."}, {"id": 1662, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During a physician's explanation of surgery in English, Mrs. Haddad, 41, a newcomer, nods and says yes. She appears anxious and speaks little English, and her husband is translating. The consent form is ready to sign. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Arrange a qualified interpreter and have her explain the plan back before signing", "B": "Give her the English consent form to read at home with her family and sign tomorrow", "C": "Accept her nods and polite smiles as agreement and have her sign the form right now", "D": "Let her husband keep translating since he knows her best and is present"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Sending written material alone to a client who may not read English", "C": "Treating a nod as understanding", "D": "Using a family member as interpreter"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C203", "cluster_text": "A community health clinic sees a family of five who arrived in Canada from a conflict zone three weeks ago. The parents, in their thirties, speak limited English, and three children are in school. The practical nurse works with an interpreter, an NP and a settlement worker to plan screening, care and teaching. The family has been staying with relatives since arrival.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Arrange a qualified interpreter and have her explain the plan back before signing): Informed consent requires understanding, and a nod or smile may show politeness, not comprehension. A qualified interpreter protects accuracy and confidentiality, whereas family translation may filter or alter information. A form in English does not resolve the language gap. Asking her to explain it back confirms understanding."}, {"id": 1663, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At discharge, Mr. Haddad, 45, a newcomer, is given a translated pamphlet about his new insulin. He cannot yet read well in either language. A coworker says the pamphlet is enough teaching. What is the best action for the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Ask his teenage child to translate the instructions for him at home", "B": "Teach with an interpreter, demonstrate injection, and have him return the demonstration", "C": "Give the translated pamphlet and ask him to read it carefully before his next clinic visit", "D": "Explain the steps quickly in English and then hand over the pamphlet"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on a child to interpret health information", "C": "Using written material as the only teaching", "D": "Teaching without confirming understanding"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C203", "cluster_text": "A community health clinic sees a family of five who arrived in Canada from a conflict zone three weeks ago. The parents, in their thirties, speak limited English, and three children are in school. The practical nurse works with an interpreter, an NP and a settlement worker to plan screening, care and teaching. The family has been staying with relatives since arrival.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Teach with an interpreter, demonstrate injection, and have him return the demonstration): Translated materials support teaching but do not replace it, especially when literacy is limited, so demonstration and return demonstration confirm skill. A pamphlet alone misses nonreaders. Quick English explanations may not be understood. A child should not carry the burden of interpreting medical information."}, {"id": 1664, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A community health nurse reviews why some recent newcomers delay seeking care even though they were healthy on arrival. A colleague says newcomers face no unique obstacles. Which statement best reflects current understanding of newcomer health and access to care?", "options": {"A": "Newcomers arrive with worse health than other Canadians, so they avoid screening.", "B": "Newcomers need care mainly for infectious disease soon after they arrive in Canada", "C": "Newcomers often arrive healthy but face barriers such as language, cost and unfamiliarity with services", "D": "Newcomers face barriers that fade once they receive provincial health coverage."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reversing the healthy immigrant effect", "B": "Stereotyping newcomers as infection risks", "D": "Assuming coverage removes language and cultural barriers"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C203", "cluster_text": "A community health clinic sees a family of five who arrived in Canada from a conflict zone three weeks ago. The parents, in their thirties, speak limited English, and three children are in school. The practical nurse works with an interpreter, an NP and a settlement worker to plan screening, care and teaching. The family has been staying with relatives since arrival.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Newcomers often arrive healthy but face barriers such as language, cost and unfamiliarity with services): The healthy immigrant effect means newcomers often arrive healthy, but language, costs, transport, unfamiliarity and fear of consequences delay care and health can decline over time. Claiming they arrive unwell reverses the evidence, and coverage alone does not remove barriers. Focusing on infection reflects stereotype, not evidence."}, {"id": 1665, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A home care nurse visits Mrs. Ouellet, 80, who tripped on a loose rug last week and now wants to stay in bed to avoid another fall. Her daughter agrees with this plan. What teaching about fall prevention is best?", "options": {"A": "Rest in bed for most of the day until she feels much steadier on her feet", "B": "Move around only with her daughter's help and avoid walking alone in the home", "C": "Avoid the stairs and the bathroom and use a bedside commode from now on", "D": "Stay active, remove loose rugs, improve lighting and keep aids close"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Advising bed rest, which weakens muscles", "B": "Creating dependence rather than safety", "C": "Restricting activity without addressing hazards"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stay active, remove loose rugs, improve lighting and keep aids close): Bed rest leads to weakness, which raises fall risk, so prevention combines activity with a safer environment: no loose rugs, good light, proper footwear and aids within reach. Constant assistance and permanent restriction lower independence and conditioning. Teach changes the home, not the person's freedom."}, {"id": 1666, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Taylor, 58, is one day after abdominal surgery. His dressing is dry, but the skin around the incision is slightly red and warm, and he reports increasing pain and has a temperature of 38.3 C. What should the nurse report to the RN?", "options": {"A": "The fever and pain, but not the redness, since redness is normal healing.", "B": "Only the redness, since the other findings are quite common in the first day after surgery", "C": "Redness, warmth, increasing pain and fever together, since they may indicate infection", "D": "The pain and fever now, and chart the redness for review next shift."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismissing redness as expected healing", "B": "Reporting a single finding", "D": "Leaving warmth and redness unreported"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Redness, warmth, increasing pain and fever together, since they may indicate infection): Wound assessment looks at several signs together: redness, warmth, swelling, drainage, pain and fever, and the cluster suggests infection. Reporting one or two signs loses the pattern. Treating redness as normal healing or postponing the report delays care. A dry dressing does not exclude infection beneath it."}, {"id": 1667, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Baby Noah, 5 months, is in the pediatric unit with a cold for three days and now has nasal flaring, a respiratory rate of 62 and poor feeding. His mother asks whether feeds should stop completely. Bronchiolitis is suspected. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Withhold all feeds for twelve hours to prevent choking and rest his lungs", "B": "Suction his nose, then give decongestant drops from home to ease his feeding.", "C": "Offer larger feeds less often so he rests between them and tires less.", "D": "Assess his breathing effort and hydration, report findings, and support small frequent feeds"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withholding feeds without assessment risks dehydration", "B": "Giving over-the-counter drops to an infant", "C": "Large feeds worsen fatigue and risk aspiration"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess his breathing effort and hydration, report findings, and support small frequent feeds): Infants with bronchiolitis tire during feeding, so the nurse assesses breathing effort and hydration, clears the nose and offers small frequent feeds while reporting a rising rate or effort. Withholding all feeds risks dehydration. Large feeds increase strain and aspiration risk. Decongestant drops and cough syrup are not recommended in infants."}, {"id": 1668, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Dana, 14, has cystic fibrosis and does daily airway clearance. At the clinic she says she skips therapy on days she feels well and wonders if that matters. Her mother nods in agreement. Dana plays soccer on weekends. What should the nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "It is only needed during chest infections, which her care team will announce to her", "B": "It can be skipped on well days as long as she does it when she coughs", "C": "It can be done every other day once her lungs sound clear", "D": "Airway clearance is daily, even when she feels well, to prevent mucus buildup"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Limiting therapy to illness", "B": "Treating therapy as optional when symptomatic relief is absent", "C": "Reducing frequency based on feeling well"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Airway clearance is daily, even when she feels well, to prevent mucus buildup): Thick mucus builds up silently in cystic fibrosis, so daily airway clearance prevents infection and lung damage even when she feels well. Skipping well days or reducing to alternate days allows buildup. Waiting for infection is too late. Linking therapy to routines helps her keep it up."}, {"id": 1669, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Reyes, 48, is admitted with night sweats, weight loss and a productive cough, and tuberculosis is suspected. His wife is waiting in the lobby. The nurse must choose a room and precautions. What measures best protect staff and others?", "options": {"A": "Use airborne isolation, fitted N95 respirators for staff and a mask on him", "B": "Use droplet isolation with surgical masks for staff and a private room.", "C": "Use gloves and gowns for all contact and a surgical mask for his family visitors", "D": "Place him in a private room with the door open and N95 for visitors."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Confusing droplet with airborne precautions", "C": "Choosing contact precautions for an airborne disease", "D": "Open door defeats containment and staff go unprotected"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use airborne isolation, fitted N95 respirators for staff and a mask on him): Tuberculosis spreads through airborne particles, so he needs a negative pressure airborne isolation room, staff in fitted N95 respirators and a surgical mask on him when he leaves the room. Droplet precautions with a surgical mask do not filter small particles. An open door lets particles escape. Gloves and gowns address contact, not airborne spread."}, {"id": 1670, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Chaudhry, 62, newly diagnosed with diabetes, is learning to use a glucose meter at a community clinic. She squeezes her fingertip hard and uses the first drop of blood, and her readings seem higher than expected. What technique should the nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Wash hands, prick the fingertip pad centre, and press firmly to form a drop", "B": "Wash hands, prick the side of the fingertip, and use a gently formed drop without squeezing", "C": "Wipe the finger with alcohol, prick the side, and squeeze firmly for a full drop.", "D": "Use an alcohol wipe and test as soon as the blood appears on the fingertip surface"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pricks the painful centre pad and squeezes the finger hard", "C": "Squeezing dilutes the sample with tissue fluid", "D": "Testing before alcohol has dried"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Wash hands, prick the side of the fingertip, and use a gently formed drop without squeezing): Washing with warm water, pricking the side of the fingertip, and using a gently formed drop gives an accurate reading and less pain. Squeezing hard mixes tissue fluid into the sample and alters the result, and the centre pad is more painful. Testing while alcohol is still wet can also alter the reading. Teach her to follow the meter's instructions and to record results."}, {"id": 1671, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a hospital unit, Ms. Kim, 68, refuses a blood transfusion for personal reasons, while her family begs the team to give it. A student asks the nurse what makes this an ethical dilemma. Which statement best defines an ethical dilemma?", "options": {"A": "Two morally supportable actions conflict, so choosing one means giving up the other", "B": "One person disagrees with a policy that the unit has adopted", "C": "A colleague has broken a rule and the nurse must decide whether to report it", "D": "The correct action is clear but hard to carry out because of limited time and resources"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Confusing a disagreement with a dilemma", "C": "Confusing misconduct with a dilemma", "D": "Confusing moral distress with a dilemma"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Two morally supportable actions conflict, so choosing one means giving up the other): An ethical dilemma exists when valid values conflict, such as respect for autonomy and the wish to preserve life, so no choice satisfies both. When the right action is clear but hard, the problem is moral distress. A policy disagreement or a broken rule is not a dilemma. The nurse supports a structured ethical process and the care team."}, {"id": 1672, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, the falls programme tracks the number of falls per 1,000 patient days. The manager asks the nurse which kind of measure this is and whether other measures are needed. Which response best reflects quality improvement practice?", "options": {"A": "Falls are an outcome measure, so also track process and balancing measures such as restraint use", "B": "Falls are a process measure, so also track outcomes such as staff survey scores.", "C": "Falls are an outcome measure, so process measures are enough without balancing ones.", "D": "Falls are a balancing measure, so also track rounding completion as the outcome."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Mislabelling falls as a process measure", "C": "Omitting balancing measures for unintended effects", "D": "Confusing balancing and outcome measures"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Falls are an outcome measure, so also track process and balancing measures such as restraint use): Outcome measures show results, process measures show whether the work is being done, and balancing measures show unintended effects, so a program needs all three. Falls are an outcome, not a process or balancing measure. Tracking only outcomes cannot show which steps worked, and satisfaction alone does not describe safety."}, {"id": 1673, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 40-year-old worker at a plant splashes a cleaning chemical into one eye and cries out in pain. The nurse on site brings him to the eyewash station, and a coworker suggests neutralizing the chemical with a solution from the shelf. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Flush the eye with water or saline for fifteen minutes and call for emergency help", "B": "Flush for five minutes with water, then cover the eye with a sterile dressing.", "C": "Flush with water for fifteen minutes but delay calling help until symptoms worsen.", "D": "Apply a neutralizing solution first and then flush with water after about five minutes"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Irrigation time far too short", "C": "Delaying emergency help after chemical exposure", "D": "Adds a second chemical, which can worsen the injury"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Flush the eye with water or saline for fifteen minutes and call for emergency help): Immediate, prolonged irrigation dilutes and removes the chemical and limits damage, so the nurse flushes at least fifteen minutes with water or saline and seeks urgent help. Neutralizing agents can cause heat or a second injury. Five minutes is too brief, and delaying help risks vision loss."}, {"id": 1674, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Ouellette, 64, has palpitations and is scheduled for an electrocardiogram at the clinic. She has large breasts and is a little embarrassed. The nurse prepares to apply the chest leads. Where should the V3 to V6 leads be placed on her?", "options": {"A": "On the upper chest above the breast tissue line.", "B": "On the chest wall beneath the lifted breast tissue", "C": "On top of the breast tissue in a straight line across her chest", "D": "Under the breast on the abdominal wall below the ribs."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Placing leads above the standard landmarks", "C": "Placing leads on tissue, which distorts the tracing", "D": "Placing leads below the correct intercostal spaces"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C200", "cluster_text": "An outpatient cardiology and imaging clinic sees several adults on the same day. A practical nurse prepares each client for tests ordered by the physician and gives instructions beforehand. The clients include a woman with palpitations, a man with chest pain on exertion, a postmenopausal woman for bone density, and a woman attending routine breast screening. The clinic is quiet this afternoon.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (On the chest wall beneath the lifted breast tissue): Leads must sit on the chest wall at standard landmarks, so breast tissue is lifted and leads placed beneath it, which gives an accurate tracing. Leads on tissue change the signal. Positions above the breast or on the abdomen do not follow the landmarks. Clean dry skin improves contact."}, {"id": 1675, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Chen, 57, has exertional chest pain and is scheduled for an outpatient stress test and echocardiogram tomorrow. He has no allergies. He drinks coffee all morning and usually walks to the clinic. What preparation teaching should the nurse give him?", "options": {"A": "Avoid caffeine for one hour and take all heart medications as usual without asking.", "B": "Stop his heart medications the day before, assuming the test works better without them", "C": "Eat a light meal two hours before, wear dress shoes, and keep his routine caffeine.", "D": "Avoid caffeine as the clinic specifies, wear comfortable clothing and shoes, and ask about heart medications"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Not asking whether heart medications should be held", "B": "Stops medications without clinic direction", "C": "Continuing caffeine and wearing unsuitable footwear"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C200", "cluster_text": "An outpatient cardiology and imaging clinic sees several adults on the same day. A practical nurse prepares each client for tests ordered by the physician and gives instructions beforehand. The clients include a woman with palpitations, a man with chest pain on exertion, a postmenopausal woman for bone density, and a woman attending routine breast screening. The clinic is quiet this afternoon.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoid caffeine as the clinic specifies, wear comfortable clothing and shoes, and ask about heart medications): Caffeine can affect heart rate and test results, so clients avoid it as instructed, wear comfortable clothes and shoes, and follow directions about medications. Continuing caffeine or dress shoes interferes with the test. Taking or stopping medications without asking may be unsafe or invalidate the test. Clinics supply written preparation."}, {"id": 1676, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Verma, 70, postmenopausal, is booked for a bone density scan next week. She plans to take her usual calcium tablet on the morning of the scan, as she does every day. She has no pain. What should the nurse tell her?", "options": {"A": "Hold calcium supplements on the day of the scan, since tablets can alter the result", "B": "Hold calcium for one week before the scan, and restart afterward.", "C": "Take the tablet as usual because it supports her bones before the scan is done today", "D": "Take a laxative the night before so bowel gas does not blur the scan."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Holding calcium far longer than needed", "C": "Ignoring interference from supplements", "D": "Confusing bone density preparation with abdominal imaging"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C200", "cluster_text": "An outpatient cardiology and imaging clinic sees several adults on the same day. A practical nurse prepares each client for tests ordered by the physician and gives instructions beforehand. The clients include a woman with palpitations, a man with chest pain on exertion, a postmenopausal woman for bone density, and a woman attending routine breast screening. The clinic is quiet this afternoon.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold calcium supplements on the day of the scan, since tablets can alter the result): Calcium tablets in the gut can show on the scan and falsely alter the reading, so clients usually avoid them for twenty-four hours as instructed by the facility. Holding them for a week is longer than needed. Laxatives and fasting are not required for a bone density scan."}, {"id": 1677, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Fraser, 52, arrives for a screening mammogram wearing deodorant and body powder. She is nervous because her sister had breast cancer. The technologist says she may need to wipe it off. What preparation teaching should the nurse have given her beforehand?", "options": {"A": "Apply a thin layer of unscented lotion to the underarms to prevent skin irritation.", "B": "Skip deodorant, powder and lotion on the chest and underarms that day", "C": "Schedule the test in the week after her period, and wear deodorant for comfort.", "D": "Take acetaminophen only if the mammogram becomes painful during compression"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying lotion that can mimic calcifications", "C": "Right timing but deodorant still affects the image", "D": "Treating pain relief as the main preparation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C200", "cluster_text": "An outpatient cardiology and imaging clinic sees several adults on the same day. A practical nurse prepares each client for tests ordered by the physician and gives instructions beforehand. The clients include a woman with palpitations, a man with chest pain on exertion, a postmenopausal woman for bone density, and a woman attending routine breast screening. The clinic is quiet this afternoon.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Skip deodorant, powder and lotion on the chest and underarms that day): Deodorants and powders can contain particles that look like calcifications on the image, so clients skip them that day. Lotion and deodorant carry the same risk. Scheduling after menstruation may reduce tenderness. Powder or lotion interferes with the image."}, {"id": 1678, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On a hospital unit, falls were 8 last month and 6 this month. A manager says the rate has clearly improved, and the nurse has a run chart available. The unit has also changed its staffing pattern recently. What is the best use of the run chart?", "options": {"A": "Use only the highest month to judge the level of fall risk", "B": "Wait for one more month and then decide using three points", "C": "Compare the two monthly values since they show the change directly and clearly on paper", "D": "Review many data points over time to see trends or shifts, not two single points"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using an extreme value", "B": "Treating three points as a trend", "C": "Reading change from two points, which may be random variation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Review many data points over time to see trends or shifts, not two single points): A run chart displays data over time so patterns, trends and shifts can be separated from normal variation. Comparing two points may reflect random change. Using only the highest month ignores the pattern. Three points do not establish a trend. More points give a more reliable picture."}, {"id": 1679, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A 28-year-old man arrives at the busy emergency department with a gunshot wound and tells the nurse not to tell the police. A colleague says confidentiality means nothing can be reported without his consent. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Wait until he is discharged and then decide whether to report", "B": "Follow facility policy and the legal duty to report, sharing only required information", "C": "Keep every detail private because he refused consent to any report", "D": "Call the police immediately and share his complete medical history and personal details"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying a required report", "C": "Treating confidentiality as unbreakable", "D": "Disclosing more than required"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Follow facility policy and the legal duty to report, sharing only required information): Confidentiality has legal limits, and reporting of gunshot wounds is required in many circumstances, so the nurse follows policy and law and discloses only the minimum necessary. Refusing to report ignores the duty. Sharing the full history exceeds what is required. Waiting delays reporting. Check regulatory body standards and policy."}, {"id": 1680, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Bell, 33, comes to the clinic after working under a car and feels grit in his right eye. He is rubbing the eye and says it feels scratchy, and his vision is clear. What should the nurse advise before assessment?", "options": {"A": "Apply an antibiotic ointment from home to the eye before the exam", "B": "Cover the eye with a firm pad and keep it closed until seen.", "C": "Use eye drops from home for the scratchiness and wait to be seen.", "D": "Stop rubbing, blink gently, and let the nurse irrigate and assess"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using ointment without assessment", "B": "Pressing on a possibly scratched cornea", "C": "Using unprescribed drops before assessment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop rubbing, blink gently, and let the nurse irrigate and assess): Rubbing can scratch the cornea and push the particle deeper, so he should stop and let the nurse irrigate and assess. Pressing on the eye risks injury. Drops from home applied before assessment can hide findings. Report embedded objects, vision change or pain to the physician or NP."}, {"id": 1681, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A public health clinic nurse learns that an adult client has active tuberculosis. The client asks the nurse not to tell anyone and says he has not agreed to any report. The client seems frightened. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Explain that reporting active tuberculosis to public health is required by law without his consent", "B": "Obtain his written consent before any report is made to public health", "C": "Report only after confirming the diagnosis with a second test and informing his family.", "D": "Ask him to report it himself within a week, since the duty belongs to him."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating reporting as consent-dependent", "C": "Delaying a mandatory report and breaching confidentiality", "D": "Shifting a legal nursing duty onto the client"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that reporting active tuberculosis to public health is required by law without his consent): Communicable diseases like tuberculosis are legally reportable to public health, and the duty overrides the need for consent to protect the community. Waiting for a second test or shifting the duty to the client leaves contacts unprotected. The nurse explains the duty respectfully and shares only required information."}, {"id": 1682, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Silva, 45, takes phenytoin for seizures and visits the neurology clinic. He arrives alone by bus. He asks whether his blood level tests can stop since he feels well. He also has gum swelling. What should the nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Keep scheduled drug level checks, take doses consistently, and practise oral hygiene", "B": "Skip levels while seizure free, but keep taking doses and brush gums gently.", "C": "Double the next dose if one is missed to keep his level steady", "D": "Check levels yearly, and expect gum swelling to settle on its own."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Skipping levels for a narrow range drug", "C": "Doubling a dose after a miss", "D": "Spacing monitoring too widely and ignoring gum overgrowth"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C202", "cluster_text": "Mr. Silva, 45, attends a neurology clinic for follow-up. He takes phenytoin for seizures. Later the same clinic sees Mrs. Mercier, 60, who is on long-term prednisone, Mr. Kowalski, 55, with hypothyroidism, and Leah, 22, who takes an oral contraceptive. The practical nurse gives medication teaching within scope and reports concerns to the NP. The clinic is running on time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep scheduled drug level checks, take doses consistently, and practise oral hygiene): Phenytoin has a narrow therapeutic range, so levels are checked to prevent toxicity or breakthrough seizures even when he feels well. Gum overgrowth is a known effect, so oral hygiene and dental care are taught. Skipping or spacing levels widely is unsafe, as is doubling doses. Missed doses are managed as directed."}, {"id": 1683, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Mercier, 60, has taken prednisone for several months for an autoimmune illness. She feels better and plans to stop it today. She has a rounded face and bruises easily. She is anxious about gaining weight. What should the nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Stop it now, since the rounded face and bruising show the dose is too high.", "B": "Do not stop suddenly, since abrupt withdrawal can cause adrenal crisis, and ask about tapering", "C": "Switch to taking it every other day on her own to limit side effects.", "D": "Cut the dose in half on her own and keep it for two weeks"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stopping a long-term steroid abruptly", "C": "Changing a steroid schedule without the prescriber", "D": "Self-adjusting a dose without a prescriber"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C202", "cluster_text": "Mr. Silva, 45, attends a neurology clinic for follow-up. He takes phenytoin for seizures. Later the same clinic sees Mrs. Mercier, 60, who is on long-term prednisone, Mr. Kowalski, 55, with hypothyroidism, and Leah, 22, who takes an oral contraceptive. The practical nurse gives medication teaching within scope and reports concerns to the NP. The clinic is running on time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Do not stop suddenly, since abrupt withdrawal can cause adrenal crisis, and ask about tapering): Long-term steroids suppress the body's own cortisol, so sudden stopping can cause adrenal crisis, and the prescriber decides on a slow taper. Moon face and easy bruising are known effects to monitor. Self-adjusting the schedule or dose is unsafe. The nurse reports the intention and her side effects."}, {"id": 1684, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Kowalski, 55, has hypothyroidism and takes levothyroxine with breakfast and a calcium supplement. His fatigue persists after three months. He works night shifts at a warehouse. He asks the nurse how he should take the medication. What teaching is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Take it with breakfast and calcium so he remembers both medications together", "B": "Take it with water on an empty stomach before breakfast and separate it from calcium", "C": "Take it with breakfast, and take the calcium supplement two hours before it.", "D": "Take it with a glass of milk before his night shift for steady absorption."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Combining with food and calcium reduces absorption", "C": "Taking levothyroxine with food and calcium before it", "D": "Milk calcium reduces levothyroxine absorption"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C202", "cluster_text": "Mr. Silva, 45, attends a neurology clinic for follow-up. He takes phenytoin for seizures. Later the same clinic sees Mrs. Mercier, 60, who is on long-term prednisone, Mr. Kowalski, 55, with hypothyroidism, and Leah, 22, who takes an oral contraceptive. The practical nurse gives medication teaching within scope and reports concerns to the NP. The clinic is running on time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Take it with water on an empty stomach before breakfast and separate it from calcium): Levothyroxine absorbs best on an empty stomach, and calcium, iron, milk and coffee reduce absorption, so he takes it with water well before breakfast and keeps supplements hours apart. Taking it with food or calcium may explain his ongoing fatigue. The nurse reports persistent symptoms for review."}, {"id": 1685, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Leah, 22, takes a combined oral contraceptive. She tells the nurse at the clinic she missed a pill yesterday and thinks one pill makes no difference. She is sexually active and does not want to become pregnant. What should the nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Take two pills at bedtime tomorrow and stop using any backup", "B": "Take the missed pill and finish the pack, using backup if she feels unwell", "C": "Take the missed pill later today, then start a new pack early to reset", "D": "Take the missed pill when remembered, finish the pack, and use backup as instructed"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Doubling without backup advice", "B": "Tying backup use to symptoms instead of instructions", "C": "Resetting the cycle early instead of continuing the pack"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C202", "cluster_text": "Mr. Silva, 45, attends a neurology clinic for follow-up. He takes phenytoin for seizures. Later the same clinic sees Mrs. Mercier, 60, who is on long-term prednisone, Mr. Kowalski, 55, with hypothyroidism, and Leah, 22, who takes an oral contraceptive. The practical nurse gives medication teaching within scope and reports concerns to the NP. The clinic is running on time.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Take the missed pill when remembered, finish the pack, and use backup as instructed): A missed pill reduces protection, so she takes it as soon as she remembers, continues the pack and follows the product's instructions on backup. Linking backup to symptoms ignores the risk of ovulation, and starting a new pack early disrupts the cycle. Doubling pills tomorrow without backup also leaves gaps. Direct her to the pharmacist or NP."}, {"id": 1686, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Before discharge, a postpartum nurse reviews infant sleep with new parents who plan to share their bed because the baby settles better beside them. The parents ask what arrangement the nurse recommends for the first months at home. Which teaching best reflects current safe sleep guidance for a healthy full-term newborn?", "options": {"A": "Place the baby on the side with a rolled blanket behind the back for support", "B": "Use a soft padded mattress with a pillow to cushion the baby's head during sleep", "C": "Share the adult bed if the baby is swaddled loosely with a light quilt", "D": "Place the baby on the back on a firm flat surface in a bassinet nearby"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Side positioning with a prop that allows rolling prone", "B": "Soft bedding that raises suffocation risk", "C": "Bed sharing with loose covers adding entrapment hazard"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Place the baby on the back on a firm flat surface in a bassinet nearby): Back sleeping on a firm, flat surface in a separate bassinet lowers the risk of sudden unexpected infant death, and keeping it near the parents' bed still allows easy night feeding. Side positioning is unstable because the baby can roll onto the stomach, even with a prop. Soft padding and shared adult beds add suffocation and entrapment hazards, even when the parents are attentive. Teach back sleeping, a firm surface, and a separate sleep space."}, {"id": 1687, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Priya, 31, is at 36 weeks gestation at a prenatal clinic. Her rectovaginal swab came back positive for group B streptococcus, and she feels well with no fever or unusual discharge. She asks whether she needs anything because she has no symptoms. What should the practical nurse explain about care during labour?", "options": {"A": "Treatment is started only if fever or unusual discharge develops before labour begins", "B": "IV antibiotics during labour lower the newborn's chance of infection", "C": "Colonization is harmless to the newborn, so a plan is needed only when she is febrile", "D": "A course of oral antibiotics now will clear the bacteria before the baby arrives"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for symptoms when carriers are usually symptom-free", "C": "Confusing colonization with harmlessness to the baby", "D": "Oral course expected to eradicate vaginal colonization permanently"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (IV antibiotics during labour lower the newborn's chance of infection): A positive swab means Priya carries the bacteria without symptoms, and IV antibiotics during labour protect the newborn from early-onset infection. Waiting for fever fails because carriers are usually well and the baby is exposed during birth. An oral course now does not reliably clear colonization, since the bacteria return. Remember that screening at 35 to 37 weeks guides intrapartum prophylaxis, not treatment of symptoms."}, {"id": 1688, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a 72-year-old client with advanced heart failure declines a recommended procedure. His daughter asks the nurse to persuade him, and two colleagues disagree about what to do. The nurse wants a structured way forward before acting. Which step should the practical nurse take first when using an ethical decision-making process?", "options": {"A": "Consult the hospital's lawyer first to learn which choice limits liability", "B": "Follow the daughter's request because family input carries the most weight in values conflicts", "C": "List possible options and weigh their outcomes before gathering the facts", "D": "Clarify the facts and who is affected before weighing any options"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Putting liability advice ahead of the ethical process", "B": "Letting family override a capable client's decision", "C": "Weighing options before gathering the facts"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Clarify the facts and who is affected before weighing any options): Ethical decision making begins with gathering the facts and naming the issue and who is affected, so later options rest on accurate information. Weighing options before the facts are clear builds the analysis on guesses. Liability advice does not address the client's values. The daughter's request cannot override a capable client's choice, which is the central value in this case. Remember the sequence: clarify, identify options, decide, act, evaluate."}, {"id": 1689, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At 0700 three call bells ring in a long-term care wing while the care provider uses the only mechanical lift for another resident. A nurse from the next wing asks to borrow the lift for her own resident right away. Mr. Osei's light is among the three. What should the practical nurse do about the lift?", "options": {"A": "Reserve the lift for residents of this wing and decline sharing with other wings", "B": "Give the lift to the visiting nurse because her request arrived first in time", "C": "Weigh each resident's urgency and coordinate lift use with the other nurse", "D": "Tell the care provider to keep the lift until every task on this wing is finished"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Territorial refusal that ignores shared resources", "B": "Allocating by order of request instead of urgency", "D": "Holding equipment regardless of competing urgent needs"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C204", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse (PN) on a 30-resident long-term care wing at 0700 with one unregulated care provider and a registered nurse (RN) at the far end of the hall. Mr. Osei, 84, is quiet and breathing noisily. Mrs. Lavoie, 79, is demanding her routine bath. Mr. Tran, 90, is on a comfort care plan. One mechanical lift is shared with another wing, and three call bells are ringing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Weigh each resident's urgency and coordinate lift use with the other nurse): Shared equipment is allocated by urgency of need and through communication, so the nurse weighs both residents' needs and agrees on timing with the colleague. Holding the lift until all wing tasks are done delays an urgent need elsewhere. Giving it away because the request came first ignores that first-come does not equal most urgent. Remember to share resources fairly and escalate to the RN when needs conflict."}, {"id": 1690, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The care provider reports that Mr. Osei, 84, has been quiet and breathing noisily since breakfast, while Mrs. Lavoie, 79, is loudly demanding her bath. The provider believes the bath is more urgent because she is upset. The PN decides how to use this report. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Document the provider's report and finish the bath before assessing either resident this morning", "B": "Assess Mr. Osei's breathing and consciousness first, since respiratory change signals risk", "C": "Ask the provider to recheck Mr. Osei in an hour and report any change", "D": "Start Mrs. Lavoie's bath first because the provider judged her complaint the more urgent matter"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Documenting instead of assessing a changing client", "C": "Delaying assessment of a possible respiratory change", "D": "Accepting the provider's perception of urgency alone"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C204", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse (PN) on a 30-resident long-term care wing at 0700 with one unregulated care provider and a registered nurse (RN) at the far end of the hall. Mr. Osei, 84, is quiet and breathing noisily. Mrs. Lavoie, 79, is demanding her routine bath. Mr. Tran, 90, is on a comfort care plan. One mechanical lift is shared with another wing, and three call bells are ringing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assess Mr. Osei's breathing and consciousness first, since respiratory change signals risk): New noisy breathing and quietness in an older adult may signal a change in condition, so the nurse assesses the client herself rather than relying on a perception of urgency. Starting the bath first lets a possibly serious problem go unassessed. Asking for a recheck in an hour hands assessment to someone outside her role and delays response. Remember that the nurse is accountable for assessing and prioritizing, not the delegate."}, {"id": 1691, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Osei's respiratory rate is now 28 with an oxygen saturation of 88 percent on room air. Mrs. Lavoie is still waiting for her bath, and Mr. Tran's family has just arrived. The RN is giving medications at the far end of the wing. What should the practical nurse do next?", "options": {"A": "Raise his head, apply oxygen as directed by protocol, and notify the RN immediately", "B": "Finish the medication pass first because all residents depend on scheduled drugs being given on time", "C": "Ask the care provider to stay with him while the PN completes Mrs. Lavoie's bath", "D": "Give an extra dose of his as-needed salbutamol and recheck the saturation after the medication pass"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Prioritizing routine tasks over an unstable client", "C": "Delegating an unstable client and continuing a routine task", "D": "Medicating and delaying reassessment and reporting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C204", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse (PN) on a 30-resident long-term care wing at 0700 with one unregulated care provider and a registered nurse (RN) at the far end of the hall. Mr. Osei, 84, is quiet and breathing noisily. Mrs. Lavoie, 79, is demanding her routine bath. Mr. Tran, 90, is on a comfort care plan. One mechanical lift is shared with another wing, and three call bells are ringing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Raise his head, apply oxygen as directed by protocol, and notify the RN immediately): A low saturation and fast breathing mean an unstable client, so the nurse positions him, starts oxygen within protocol, and calls the RN for help now. Finishing routine medications first delays care for the client at greatest risk. Delegating his observation while bathing another resident leaves an unstable client without nursing assessment. Remember that unstable clients are never delegated."}, {"id": 1692, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Tran, 90, is on a comfort care plan and is now restless, grimacing, and has mottled knees. The care provider is waiting to turn him as scheduled, and the weekly weights round is due this hour. What should the practical nurse prioritize for Mr. Tran?", "options": {"A": "Hold the analgesic until the physician reviews him because his breathing pattern is now changing", "B": "Assess his comfort, give prescribed analgesia, and adjust repositioning to his tolerance", "C": "Turn him on schedule with the care provider and reassess his comfort after the round", "D": "Complete the weekly weights round first so that all other routine tasks stay on schedule"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withholding comfort medication for an assumed risk", "C": "Following the schedule rather than the client's distress", "D": "Putting routine tasks ahead of comfort"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C204", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse (PN) on a 30-resident long-term care wing at 0700 with one unregulated care provider and a registered nurse (RN) at the far end of the hall. Mr. Osei, 84, is quiet and breathing noisily. Mrs. Lavoie, 79, is demanding her routine bath. Mr. Tran, 90, is on a comfort care plan. One mechanical lift is shared with another wing, and three call bells are ringing.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assess his comfort, give prescribed analgesia, and adjust repositioning to his tolerance): Comfort is the goal of his plan, so the nurse assesses his distress, gives the prescribed as-needed analgesic, and adapts turning to what he tolerates. Weights add nothing to comfort and can wait. Withholding analgesia while waiting for the physician prolongs suffering and ignores the existing order. Turning on schedule before treating pain can worsen distress. Remember that at end of life, comfort outranks routine tasks."}, {"id": 1693, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Alvarez, 68, lives alone and receives home care visits from a practical nurse. The agency is a two-hour drive from the nearest hospital, and the nurse works without colleagues on site. A new nurse says that because the visit is isolated, the standard of care can be relaxed. Which statement about practising in isolation is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Standards stay the same, but the nurse may skip consultation when no one is available", "B": "Accountability transfers to the agency while the nurse works alone in the home", "C": "The standard of care and accountability stay the same, and the nurse consults when needed", "D": "The agency's policies replace the nurse's college standards when working alone in homes"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Skipping consultation because help is distant", "B": "Assuming employer holds accountability for individual practice", "D": "Treating employer policy as replacing professional standards"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The standard of care and accountability stay the same, and the nurse consults when needed): The standard of care is the same wherever the nurse practises, and the nurse remains accountable for her own decisions, including consulting when a situation exceeds her competence. Skipping consultation because no one is nearby raises risk. Agency policy supports practice but does not replace college standards. Accountability does not transfer to the employer. Remember that isolation increases the need for consultation."}, {"id": 1694, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a community health centre, a woman, 45, whose son uses opioids asks for a take-home naloxone kit. A colleague worries that giving it will encourage drug use. The woman says her son overdosed last month. What should the practical nurse do about the kit and teaching?", "options": {"A": "Refer her to a physician for a prescription before the kit is released to the family", "B": "Provide the kit and teach overdose signs, calling 911, giving naloxone, and staying with him", "C": "Give the kit after she signs a form agreeing to report his drug use", "D": "Give the kit and teach her to give naloxone, then leave him to rest"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Requiring a prescription that is not needed for kits", "C": "Adding an unnecessary condition before releasing the kit", "D": "Leaving the person alone after giving naloxone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Provide the kit and teach overdose signs, calling 911, giving naloxone, and staying with him): Naloxone kits save lives and evidence shows they do not increase drug use, so the nurse provides the kit with practical teaching on recognizing overdose, calling 911, giving the dose, and staying. Requiring a signed agreement or a prescription is an unnecessary barrier in programs that distribute kits. Leaving him to rest after a dose ignores that overdose can return when naloxone wears off."}, {"id": 1695, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Two catheter-associated urinary infections in one month have prompted the ward to review care. On rounds, Dana finds a client's drainage bag resting on the floor and the catheter tubing loosely secured. The unit lead asks what the PN should do first to address this infection prevention gap?", "options": {"A": "Irrigate the bladder with saline each shift to flush bacteria from the catheter", "B": "Clamp the catheter between voids to keep the bladder trained for normal filling and emptying cycles", "C": "Disconnect the tubing and rinse the drainage bag with water before reattaching it", "D": "Raise the bag off the floor and below bladder level, and secure the catheter"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Breaking the closed system with routine irrigation", "B": "Clamping that causes bladder overfilling and infection", "C": "Opening the closed drainage system to rinse the bag"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C194", "cluster_text": "A surgical program at a regional hospital reviewed its safety culture after two catheter-associated urinary tract infections in one month. The team uses a surgical safety checklist, and its post-operative ward and outpatient follow-up clinic both serve Indigenous clients and newly arrived families. You are the practical nurse, Dana, working across the ward and the clinic and supporting clients to speak up about their care.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Raise the bag off the floor and below bladder level, and secure the catheter): A bag on the floor and loose tubing raise contamination and traction risks, so the nurse fixes them by keeping the bag below the bladder and off the floor and anchoring the tubing. Routine irrigation and disconnecting the tubing break the closed system and invite infection. Clamping can cause urine stasis and distention. Remember that infection rates act as safety indicators for basic care."}, {"id": 1696, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In the operating room, the surgeon wants to skip the surgical pause because the list is running late. The checklist confirms client identity, procedure, site, and allergies before incision. Dana, circulating, is concerned that a key detail could be missed. Which response best supports client safety?", "options": {"A": "Complete the pause after the incision is made so that the schedule is not delayed further", "B": "Explain that the pause confirms key details and ask to complete it before incision", "C": "Record the pause as done and proceed with the incision to avoid conflict with the surgeon", "D": "Skip it for this client since the whole team already knows the case and procedure well"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying the pause until after incision", "C": "Falsifying documentation to avoid conflict", "D": "Assuming familiarity replaces verification"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C194", "cluster_text": "A surgical program at a regional hospital reviewed its safety culture after two catheter-associated urinary tract infections in one month. The team uses a surgical safety checklist, and its post-operative ward and outpatient follow-up clinic both serve Indigenous clients and newly arrived families. You are the practical nurse, Dana, working across the ward and the clinic and supporting clients to speak up about their care.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain that the pause confirms key details and ask to complete it before incision): The pause is designed to confirm identity, procedure, site, and allergies before any incision, when errors can still be prevented, so Dana speaks up and asks that it be completed. Doing it after incision defeats its purpose. Familiarity with the case does not remove the risk of wrong-site or wrong-client errors. Recording a pause that did not happen is falsification. Remember that checklists protect clients when the team feels pressured."}, {"id": 1697, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At the follow-up clinic, Mr. Bear, 55, who is Indigenous, avoids eye contact and says little during wound care teaching. A colleague says everyone should be treated the same. Dana wants to practise cultural safety and humility during this visit. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Use the standard teaching script for every client so nobody gets special treatment", "B": "Ask how he prefers to learn, acknowledge his experience, and adapt teaching", "C": "Ask which cultural group he belongs to and teach from general beliefs about that group", "D": "Assume silence means he understands and skip questions to save him discomfort"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Equating equal treatment with identical treatment", "C": "Stereotyping from group beliefs", "D": "Reading silence as understanding"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C194", "cluster_text": "A surgical program at a regional hospital reviewed its safety culture after two catheter-associated urinary tract infections in one month. The team uses a surgical safety checklist, and its post-operative ward and outpatient follow-up clinic both serve Indigenous clients and newly arrived families. You are the practical nurse, Dana, working across the ward and the clinic and supporting clients to speak up about their care.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask how he prefers to learn, acknowledge his experience, and adapt teaching): Cultural safety and humility mean the client defines whether care feels safe, so Dana asks how he wants to learn and adapts her teaching. Identical treatment ignores differences in history and need. Silence does not confirm understanding and may reflect discomfort or past negative experiences. Teaching from general beliefs about a group assumes sameness within it."}, {"id": 1698, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mrs. Hadiza, 34, recently arrived in Canada and speaks limited English. She attends the clinic for wound checks, and her husband interprets, but she seems reluctant to speak. The visit is short and another client is waiting. What should Dana do?", "options": {"A": "Arrange a qualified interpreter and speak with her privately to confirm understanding", "B": "Continue using her husband as interpreter since he knows her history and her wishes best", "C": "Use simple written handouts in English and ask her to read them at home", "D": "Shorten the teaching to fit the schedule and cover only the dressing change steps."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relying on a family member to interpret", "C": "Giving English handouts to a client with limited English", "D": "Reducing teaching because of time pressure"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C194", "cluster_text": "A surgical program at a regional hospital reviewed its safety culture after two catheter-associated urinary tract infections in one month. The team uses a surgical safety checklist, and its post-operative ward and outpatient follow-up clinic both serve Indigenous clients and newly arrived families. You are the practical nurse, Dana, working across the ward and the clinic and supporting clients to speak up about their care.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Arrange a qualified interpreter and speak with her privately to confirm understanding): Equitable care means the client can understand and take part, so Dana uses a qualified interpreter and speaks with Mrs. Hadiza privately, which also lets her voice concerns freely. A husband may filter information and limits her privacy. English handouts do not help if she cannot read them. Shortening teaching to protect the schedule leaves an important wound care gap."}, {"id": 1699, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Dubois, 60, looks at the pill Dana hands him on the ward and says it does not look like his usual one. A colleague says clients should not question nurses about their medications. Dana pauses before giving it. Why is client involvement valued in safety?", "options": {"A": "Clients know their medications best, so the nurse may defer to their dose judgment", "B": "Clients notice discrepancies, and their questions can catch errors before a drug is given", "C": "It shifts legal responsibility for medication errors from the nurse to the informed client afterward", "D": "It satisfies the facility's requirement for a client signature before each medication dose"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Deferring to client judgment on doses", "C": "Believing client awareness removes nurse accountability", "D": "Confusing client questions with a signature requirement"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C194", "cluster_text": "A surgical program at a regional hospital reviewed its safety culture after two catheter-associated urinary tract infections in one month. The team uses a surgical safety checklist, and its post-operative ward and outpatient follow-up clinic both serve Indigenous clients and newly arrived families. You are the practical nurse, Dana, working across the ward and the clinic and supporting clients to speak up about their care.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Clients notice discrepancies, and their questions can catch errors before a drug is given): Clients know their routine and often notice differences in appearance, dose, or timing, so encouraging questions adds a layer of protection against errors. A client signature is not a safety check on medications. Client judgment supplements the nurse's checks and does not replace them. Responsibility stays with the nurse even when a client is informed. Remember to welcome and act on client questions."}, {"id": 1700, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After a school nurse notifies the clinic of a scabies outbreak, a 7-year-old girl presents with an intensely itchy rash between her fingers that worsens at night. Her parents ask who in the family should be treated. Which instruction should the practical nurse give based on how scabies spreads?", "options": {"A": "Treat the child now and treat household members only if they develop itching or a rash", "B": "Treat the whole classroom and teachers but exclude household members since classroom contact is more prolonged", "C": "Treat the child and all household members and close contacts at the same time", "D": "Treat only the child, because the rash is visible only on her and nobody else itches"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for symptoms in contacts who may be infested", "B": "Misjudging which contacts are most at risk", "D": "Treating only the symptomatic person"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat the child and all household members and close contacts at the same time): Scabies spreads through prolonged skin contact, and contacts can carry mites before symptoms appear, so the child, household members, and close contacts are treated together to prevent reinfestation. Treating only the child leaves mites in the home. Waiting for itching delays treatment by weeks. Household members have closer contact than classmates and are at higher risk. Remember to wash linen and clothing in hot water."}, {"id": 1701, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a practical nurse finds that a 66-year-old client received double his ordered antihypertensive dose because two nurses each gave it. His blood pressure is 88/52 mmHg and he feels dizzy. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Hold the next dose and report the error to the manager after shift", "B": "Assess the client, ensure his safety, and notify the RN and prescriber immediately", "C": "Give his other scheduled medications on time and recheck pressure in two hours", "D": "Document the extra dose and recheck blood pressure at the next scheduled vital signs"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying the report of a medication error", "C": "Continuing scheduled medications without reassessing the hypotension", "D": "Documenting without acting on the client's change"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assess the client, ensure his safety, and notify the RN and prescriber immediately): The first priority after an error is the client's safety, so the nurse assesses, positions him to prevent a fall, and reports at once to the RN and prescriber so treatment can be ordered. Waiting until the end of the shift or the next vital signs leaves a hypotensive client unmonitored. Continuing other scheduled medications could worsen his blood pressure. Report errors and complete an incident report afterward."}, {"id": 1702, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Priya is tearful because her newborn's latch is painful during feeds. Her mother-in-law says to give formula to stop the pain, and Priya looks to the nurse for direction. The practical nurse wants to respect Priya's choices while promoting safe feeding. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Advise supplementing with formula now to reduce nipple pain and her distress", "B": "Tell her to feed on a strict schedule so her nipples can rest", "C": "Reassure her that pain is normal and will ease by itself within days", "D": "Observe a feed, then teach positioning and latch, respecting her choice"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Supplementing without assessing latch", "B": "Imposing a rigid schedule that reduces milk supply", "C": "Dismissing painful feeding as normal"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C216", "cluster_text": "Priya, 29, is two days postpartum on a maternal unit after the birth of her first baby. She is tired and tearful. Her husband, 33, smokes and is visiting, and her mother-in-law has arrived and is directing the care. You are the practical nurse caring for Priya and her baby, and you are preparing her for discharge teaching later today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Observe a feed, then teach positioning and latch, respecting her choice): Observing a feed identifies the cause of pain, usually positioning or latch, and lets the nurse teach and support Priya's own choice. Supplementing without assessment does not address the problem and may reduce milk supply. Persistent pain is not normal and needs correction rather than reassurance. A strict schedule is not baby-led and can lower supply. Remember to assess first, teach, and then support the client's decision."}, {"id": 1703, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Priya's husband says he has tried to quit smoking twice and failed, and he asks the nurse if any help exists. Priya is breastfeeding, and the baby sleeps in the room. The practical nurse is discussing support with him. What should the nurse offer?", "options": {"A": "Tell him to smoke outside only and see if that satisfies his cravings", "B": "Give a pamphlet and end the discussion since he already failed twice", "C": "Advise him to stop abruptly with willpower and avoid medications or other aids", "D": "Offer quit lines, counselling, cessation aids, and arranged follow-up support"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offering harm reduction only with no cessation plan", "B": "Giving up because of previous relapses", "C": "Recommending unaided quitting despite past failure"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C216", "cluster_text": "Priya, 29, is two days postpartum on a maternal unit after the birth of her first baby. She is tired and tearful. Her husband, 33, smokes and is visiting, and her mother-in-law has arrived and is directing the care. You are the practical nurse caring for Priya and her baby, and you are preparing her for discharge teaching later today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Offer quit lines, counselling, cessation aids, and arranged follow-up support): Relapses are common, and combining counselling, quit lines, and cessation aids improves success, so the nurse offers resources and follow-up. Quitting abruptly without supports repeats what has not worked. Smoking outside reduces exposure but is not a plan to quit. A pamphlet and no follow-up ignores his readiness. Remember that each attempt builds toward success and that offering supports is a nursing teaching role."}, {"id": 1704, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Before discharge, the practical nurse teaches Priya which signs after birth need urgent care. Priya asks how to tell the difference between normal changes and emergencies at home, since she will be alone with the baby during the day. Which sign should the nurse teach her to call 911 for?", "options": {"A": "Heavy bleeding that soaks a pad in an hour, with dizziness or fainting", "B": "A temperature of 38.5 C with foul-smelling lochia and a tender uterus", "C": "Bleeding that soaks a pad in two hours with a few small clots", "D": "Sore cracked nipples with a low-grade temperature that settles after feeding"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Sending an infection sign to 911 instead of the provider", "C": "Treating slower bleeding with small clots as a 911 emergency", "D": "Treating early mastitis signs as an emergency call"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C216", "cluster_text": "Priya, 29, is two days postpartum on a maternal unit after the birth of her first baby. She is tired and tearful. Her husband, 33, smokes and is visiting, and her mother-in-law has arrived and is directing the care. You are the practical nurse caring for Priya and her baby, and you are preparing her for discharge teaching later today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Heavy bleeding that soaks a pad in an hour, with dizziness or fainting): Bleeding that soaks a pad in an hour with dizziness suggests postpartum hemorrhage, a life-threatening emergency that needs emergency services. Fever with foul lochia, slower bleeding with small clots, and nipple soreness with a low-grade temperature call for a prompt call to the care provider but not 911. Teach when to call 911 and when to call the care provider so Priya can act without delay."}, {"id": 1705, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Priya's mother-in-law stops the nurse at the station and asks for the baby's weight and Priya's laboratory results, saying she is paying for the stay. Priya is asleep and has not named anyone to receive information. How should the practical nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Share only the baby's weight because it is not sensitive medical information", "B": "Refuse to speak and walk away without explaining the reason or alternatives", "C": "Explain that information requires Priya's consent and offer to ask her when she wakes", "D": "Share results with any family member who is financially supporting the client during her stay"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a newborn's weight as non-confidential", "B": "Refusing without explanation or offering a path", "D": "Letting payment confer a right to information"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C216", "cluster_text": "Priya, 29, is two days postpartum on a maternal unit after the birth of her first baby. She is tired and tearful. Her husband, 33, smokes and is visiting, and her mother-in-law has arrived and is directing the care. You are the practical nurse caring for Priya and her baby, and you are preparing her for discharge teaching later today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain that information requires Priya's consent and offer to ask her when she wakes): Health information belongs to the client, and sharing with family needs her consent, so the nurse explains this politely and offers to ask Priya. The baby's weight is still part of the health record under the mother's authority. Paying for care does not create access to information. Walking away without explanation is unprofessional and offers no alternative. Remember that confidentiality is protected through consent."}, {"id": 1706, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, the practical nurse finds a 3-year-old who spilled hot tea on his forearm ten minutes ago. The skin is red and blistered over an area about the size of his hand. His mother has covered the burn with butter and is upset. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Remove the butter gently, cool the burn with running water, and cover loosely with clean cloth", "B": "Wipe off the butter, hold the burn under cool water for two minutes, then cover", "C": "Apply ice directly to the burn for fifteen minutes to stop the damage", "D": "Cool the burn with running water, leave the butter on, and cover with gauze"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Cooling for far too short a time", "C": "Using ice that deepens tissue injury", "D": "Leaving butter on the burn under a dressing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Remove the butter gently, cool the burn with running water, and cover loosely with clean cloth): Cool running water for about twenty minutes limits tissue injury, and butter must be removed because it traps heat and raises infection risk; a loose clean covering protects the burn. Cooling for two minutes stops too early to limit the damage. Leaving butter in place under gauze keeps heat and bacteria against the skin. Ice can cause further tissue damage. A blistered burn this size in a young child also needs assessment by the RN or physician."}, {"id": 1707, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Repeatedly pulling at his IV line, a 78-year-old client on a medical unit is agitated. A coworker suggests restraining his wrists based on her own judgment because family is absent. The practical nurse wants to protect the client lawfully. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Request a sedative order at once, since sedation is gentler than restraining his wrists", "B": "Remove the IV on her own judgment and chart that the client was agitated", "C": "Try least restrictive alternatives first and follow policy and legal requirements before any restraint", "D": "Obtain verbal consent from his absent daughter by phone and then restrain him for the night"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using sedation as a milder form of restraint", "B": "Removing treatment without an order to avoid restraint", "D": "Relying on phone consent instead of required process"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Try least restrictive alternatives first and follow policy and legal requirements before any restraint): Restraints are a last resort that must follow policy, legal requirements, and an assessment of cause, so the nurse first tries alternatives such as reorientation, covering the IV site, and checking for pain or retention. Sedating a client to manage behaviour is a chemical restraint with its own requirements. Removing the IV without an order or assessment ignores the reason for the line. A phone consent does not replace the required process."}, {"id": 1708, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Since starting a regular opioid for cancer pain, a 68-year-old on a palliative unit reports nausea and hard stools, with no bowel movement for four days. A colleague suggests adding an antiemetic only. What should the practical nurse recognize about this client's symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Opioid constipation needs a regular laxative and may add to the nausea", "B": "Constipation will resolve on its own as the body adjusts to the opioid", "C": "Nausea comes from the opioid alone, so an antiemetic is enough to treat it", "D": "A bulk-forming laxative without extra fluids is best for opioid-related constipation"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Expecting tolerance to constipation that rarely develops", "C": "Treating nausea alone and ignoring the constipation", "D": "Choosing a bulk agent that worsens impaction without fluids"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Opioid constipation needs a regular laxative and may add to the nausea): Opioids slow gut movement, and constipation does not resolve with time, so a regular stimulant or osmotic laxative should be started with the opioid, and constipation can add to nausea. An antiemetic alone leaves the cause untreated. Bulk-forming agents without ample fluids can worsen impaction in a palliative client. Tolerance to constipation seldom develops. Report the findings and expect an order for a bowel regimen."}, {"id": 1709, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Clear nasal drainage, sore throat, and a low-grade fever for two days lead a 35-year-old woman to call a clinic and ask for antibiotics to prevent complications. She says a friend got them quickly last winter. What should the practical nurse explain?", "options": {"A": "Antibiotics shorten a cold when started within the first three days", "B": "Antibiotics should be started early to prevent a bacterial infection later", "C": "Colds are viral, antibiotics do not help, and misuse promotes resistance", "D": "A sore throat with fever means a bacterial cause needs antibiotics"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing early antibiotics shorten a viral cold", "B": "Using antibiotics preventively for a viral cold", "D": "Treating sore throat with fever as automatically bacterial"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Colds are viral, antibiotics do not help, and misuse promotes resistance): Colds are caused by viruses, which antibiotics do not treat, and unnecessary use fosters resistant bacteria and side effects, so the nurse teaches symptom care and when to seek care. Starting antibiotics early does not prevent bacterial complications. Antibiotics do not shorten a viral cold, even when started early. A sore throat with fever and clear drainage is usually viral, not an automatic sign of bacterial infection. Remember that stewardship protects every client."}, {"id": 1710, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Back in day surgery after a percutaneous liver biopsy, a 55-year-old man feels fine and wants to sit up and walk to the washroom soon, though the procedure was thirty minutes ago and his vital signs are stable. Which positioning and monitoring should the practical nurse follow?", "options": {"A": "Lay him flat on his back and check vital signs every hour", "B": "Keep him on his right side and check vital signs when pain begins", "C": "Keep him on his right side and monitor vital signs and bleeding often", "D": "Position him on his left side with his arms above his head"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using back-lying and infrequent checks after biopsy", "B": "Waiting for pain before checking for bleeding", "D": "Wrong-side positioning that reduces pressure on the site"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep him on his right side and monitor vital signs and bleeding often): Lying on the right side applies pressure to the biopsy site and lowers the risk of bleeding, and frequent vital signs and site checks detect early hemorrhage. Lying flat on his back gives no pressure over the site, and hourly checks are too far apart soon after a biopsy. Waiting for pain before checking misses early bleeding. The left side removes the helpful pressure. Report falling blood pressure, rising pulse, or increasing pain promptly."}, {"id": 1711, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Visiting a 4-year-old with a gastrostomy tube at home, the practical nurse finds the skin around the site red, with thick drainage and a loose dressing. His parent says she checks the skin only once a week. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Clean the site and start his leftover antibiotic ointment, then recheck in a week", "B": "Apply thick ointment and cover to keep drainage from reaching clothing", "C": "Assess the site, clean it as taught, and report the redness and drainage to the RN", "D": "Teach the parent daily checks and review the redness at the next scheduled visit"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using leftover medication without an order or report", "B": "Trapping moisture with ointment and occlusion", "D": "Delaying a report of possible infection"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess the site, clean it as taught, and report the redness and drainage to the RN): Redness with thick drainage suggests possible infection, so the nurse assesses, cleans the site, and reports to the RN, and teaches daily site checks to the parent. Using leftover ointment without an order treats a possible infection without assessment. Waiting for the next visit delays care for redness and drainage that are not normal. Thick ointment under a cover traps moisture and irritates skin."}, {"id": 1712, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Watery bloody diarrhea for two days, a temperature of 38.9 C, and abdominal cramping bring a 30-year-old to a community clinic, where he reports feeling weak. He asks the practical nurse about taking loperamide from his medicine cabinet. What should the nurse tell him?", "options": {"A": "Take loperamide at bedtime to rest, and drink fluids through the day", "B": "Take loperamide after each loose stool and stop once the fever has resolved.", "C": "Take loperamide with an oral rehydration solution, and see the physician if fever persists", "D": "Avoid loperamide because slowing the bowel can worsen invasive infection, and see the physician."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Limiting antimotility dosing to bedtime but still using it", "B": "Dosing loperamide despite risk of toxin retention", "C": "Adding rehydration to loperamide despite invasive infection"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoid loperamide because slowing the bowel can worsen invasive infection, and see the physician.): Bloody diarrhea with fever suggests invasive infection, and loperamide slows the bowel, which can keep toxins and organisms in and increase risk of complications, so the nurse advises against it and arranges assessment. Limiting it to bedtime or pairing it with rehydration still slows motility. Taking it after each stool slows the bowel as well. Remember to teach hydration and when to seek care."}, {"id": 1713, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Newly arrived in town, a family of four visits a community clinic and says they do not know how to find a family doctor, book appointments, or access health coverage. The father says they will figure it out on their own. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Give a list of clinic phone numbers and tell them to call whenever care is needed", "B": "Refer them only to the nearest emergency department for any health concern, including routine checkups", "C": "Explain local services and help with coverage registration and settlement supports", "D": "Let them explore the local options themselves so they learn how the system works"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offering a list without explanation or support", "B": "Directing routine care to emergency services", "D": "Leaving newcomers to navigate unaided"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain local services and help with coverage registration and settlement supports): Newcomers often face language, cost, and system barriers, so the nurse explains how services work, helps with coverage registration, and links them with settlement agencies. Leaving them to figure it out delays care. A bare phone list lacks guidance. Sending routine concerns to emergency departments strains services and does not build a continuing care relationship. Remember that navigation support is part of equitable care."}, {"id": 1714, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Returning to a medical unit after a diagnostic lumbar puncture, a 40-year-old reports a mild headache when upright and asks to go to the washroom independently. The practical nurse reviews the post-procedure instructions with him. Which instruction should be given about positioning?", "options": {"A": "Keep the head of the bed at forty-five degrees and limit fluids for the shift", "B": "Lie flat for one hour, then walk freely to the washroom", "C": "Lie flat for the ordered period and increase fluids to lower headache risk", "D": "Raise the bed head slightly and drink fluids to ease back pressure"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Restricting fluids when hydration helps", "B": "Ending flat rest too early after lumbar puncture", "D": "Raising the head of the bed after the procedure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lie flat for the ordered period and increase fluids to lower headache risk): Lying flat reduces leakage of cerebrospinal fluid through the puncture site, and fluids help prevent headache, so the nurse reinforces both for the ordered period. Getting up after one hour ends the flat rest too soon and can increase leakage. Raising the head, even slightly, can worsen a headache. Limiting fluids also works against headache prevention. Report severe headache, fever, or weakness."}, {"id": 1715, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Booked for a 75 g oral glucose tolerance test at 26 weeks gestation, a 28-year-old arrives at a prenatal clinic. She ate a muffin and juice an hour ago because she was hungry, and asks whether the test can still go ahead. What should the practical nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Reschedule the test and teach her to fast for eight hours before the next one", "B": "Test her capillary glucose now and proceed with the test if it is under 7 mmol/L", "C": "Proceed now because the meal can be corrected for in the interpretation", "D": "Give the glucose drink and draw fasting bloods afterwards instead"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using a capillary result to replace fasting", "C": "Ignoring the fasting requirement", "D": "Reversing the order of blood draws"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reschedule the test and teach her to fast for eight hours before the next one): A recent meal invalidates the fasting sample, so the test must be rescheduled with clear teaching about an eight-hour fast. Interpretation cannot correct for a meal. Drawing fasting blood after the drink gives meaningless values. A capillary glucose does not replace a proper fasting plasma glucose. Remember to give written fasting instructions and to confirm the test date."}, {"id": 1716, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In long-term care, an 82-year-old resident with dementia wanders at night and has twice left the building, though she is otherwise healthy and pleasant by day. Staff suggest using a lap belt on her chair. Which approach reflects the least restraint principle?", "options": {"A": "Try scheduled toileting, activity, and a safe path before using restraints", "B": "Use a bed alarm and sitter, then add a lap belt", "C": "Move her to a locked unit first so wandering is stopped", "D": "Use a lap belt first because it quickly stops the wandering"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Adding a physical restraint to monitoring measures", "C": "Choosing a locked setting before trying alternatives", "D": "Choosing a restraint before alternatives"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Try scheduled toileting, activity, and a safe path before using restraints): Least restraint means trying less restrictive measures first and identifying the cause of wandering, so the nurse uses activity, toileting, supervision, and a safe space. A lap belt is a physical restraint and should not be first-line, even when paired with alarms. Moving her to a locked unit restricts her movement without trying alternatives. Remember to document alternatives tried and review the plan with the team."}, {"id": 1717, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Brought in agitated and sweating, a 25-year-old has a heart rate of 132 and a temperature of 38.6 C after reported cocaine use. He is pacing and yelling. Staff debate avoiding sedation. What should the practical nurse anticipate and prepare for in planning care with the team?", "options": {"A": "Prepare for a prescribed sedative and monitor vital signs and temperature", "B": "Withhold sedation so signs of toxicity remain visible to the team", "C": "Apply cooling and wait to see whether agitation settles alone", "D": "Place him in a quiet room alone and check hourly"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Withholding treatment of severe agitation", "C": "Cooling without treating the agitation", "D": "Checking too infrequently on an unstable client"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C207", "cluster_text": "It is a busy Friday evening in a hospital emergency department, and you are the practical nurse working with an RN and a physician. Several clients have arrived with problems related to substance use. The department has a take-home naloxone program and a mental health nurse available for consultation, and the physician is asking the team to plan care for each client.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Prepare for a prescribed sedative and monitor vital signs and temperature): Severe agitation with fever and tachycardia from a stimulant risks heart and brain complications, so the team plans a prescribed sedative, continuous monitoring, and cooling, which the nurse prepares for. Cooling alone does not control the agitation driving the heat. Isolation with hourly checks is too infrequent for a client with a dangerous heart rate and temperature. Withholding sedation delays treatment of a dangerous state. Remember to keep the environment calm."}, {"id": 1718, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Neighbours are watching him through the walls and speaking about him, a 30-year-old reports. He used methamphetamine two days ago, is alert, and is not threatening anyone. The practical nurse is assigned to stay with him. What response should the nurse use?", "options": {"A": "Reassure him he is safe and explain that meth causes paranoid thoughts", "B": "Speak calmly, acknowledge his fear without agreeing, and keep the area quiet", "C": "Show him that no one is outside the building to disprove the belief directly right away", "D": "Ask for details of what the neighbours say to understand it"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Explaining the cause and dismissing his fear", "C": "Arguing against the delusion", "D": "Probing delusional content and strengthening it"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C207", "cluster_text": "It is a busy Friday evening in a hospital emergency department, and you are the practical nurse working with an RN and a physician. Several clients have arrived with problems related to substance use. The department has a take-home naloxone program and a mental health nurse available for consultation, and the physician is asking the team to plan care for each client.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Speak calmly, acknowledge his fear without agreeing, and keep the area quiet): Acknowledging that the fear is real to him without confirming the belief builds trust and lowers distress, and a quiet, safe space supports him. Arguing with delusions, or proving them false right away, usually increases agitation. Explaining that drug use causes his thoughts is likely to feel dismissive. Probing the details of the content can deepen the delusion. Remember to keep checking his safety and to report any threats to the RN."}, {"id": 1719, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Worsening anxiety brings a 19-year-old student to the department, and he says he uses cannabis daily for a year. He believes it helps him relax and has no health effects. The practical nurse is completing the assessment. What should the nurse say to him?", "options": {"A": "Explain that regular heavy cannabis use can worsen anxiety and invite a discussion of reducing use", "B": "Warn that he will develop psychosis within a year if he continues", "C": "Explain that cannabis may help short-term anxiety and suggest he cut back later", "D": "Advise him that cannabis anxiety fades in days so he can keep using"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Overstating risk with a frightening claim", "C": "Accepting short-term relief as a benefit of cannabis", "D": "Expecting anxiety to fade despite continued use"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C207", "cluster_text": "It is a busy Friday evening in a hospital emergency department, and you are the practical nurse working with an RN and a physician. Several clients have arrived with problems related to substance use. The department has a take-home naloxone program and a mental health nurse available for consultation, and the physician is asking the team to plan care for each client.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that regular heavy cannabis use can worsen anxiety and invite a discussion of reducing use): Regular cannabis use can worsen anxiety and other mental health symptoms, so the nurse gives accurate information without judgment and opens a conversation about change. Describing cannabis as helpful for anxiety overlooks that heavy use often worsens it. Saying the anxiety will fade while he keeps using ignores a likely contributor. Predicting psychosis in a year is an exaggeration that damages trust. Remember to use motivational interviewing and report findings to the team."}, {"id": 1720, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Repeated vomiting episodes for three days with abdominal pain bring a 27-year-old to the department, and she gets relief only from long hot showers. She has used cannabis daily for years, and staff are treating this as gastroenteritis. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Report the history and relief from hot showers to the RN, as cannabis hyperemesis is possible", "B": "Ask the RN about possible food poisoning and request stool cultures before other tests", "C": "Treat it as routine gastroenteritis and discharge after one antiemetic", "D": "Suggest she stop hot showers because heat may be triggering the vomiting"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Chasing food poisoning and ignoring the cannabis pattern", "C": "Anchoring on gastroenteritis and missing the cannabis pattern", "D": "Removing the relief measure instead of finding the cause"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C207", "cluster_text": "It is a busy Friday evening in a hospital emergency department, and you are the practical nurse working with an RN and a physician. Several clients have arrived with problems related to substance use. The department has a take-home naloxone program and a mental health nurse available for consultation, and the physician is asking the team to plan care for each client.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the history and relief from hot showers to the RN, as cannabis hyperemesis is possible): Cyclic vomiting with relief from hot water in a long-term daily cannabis user fits cannabis hyperemesis syndrome, so the nurse reports these specific findings to the RN and physician. Routine gastroenteritis care misses the pattern. Pursuing food poisoning ignores the cannabis history and the hot-shower clue. Stopping hot showers removes her relief without treating the cause. Remember that stopping cannabis is the definitive treatment."}, {"id": 1721, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Twenty minutes after abdominal surgery under general anesthesia, an adult returns to the surgical ward drowsy but rousable, with stable vital signs reported by the recovery staff. Her dressing looks dry and she asks to be repositioned for comfort. Which assessment should the practical nurse complete first?", "options": {"A": "Assess her pain using a scale and give the prescribed analgesic", "B": "Measure urine output and review the intake record first", "C": "Inspect the dressing and the surgical site for bleeding before completing any other checks", "D": "Check airway, breathing, and circulation with vital signs and level of consciousness"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating pain before confirming stability", "B": "Prioritizing intake and output over basic physiology", "C": "Starting with the wound instead of the airway"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Check airway, breathing, and circulation with vital signs and level of consciousness): After general anesthesia, airway, breathing, and circulation come first, along with consciousness, because these threaten life soonest. The dressing check is important but follows. Urine output and intake review are later data. Analgesia is given after she is stable and may worsen respiratory depression if given first. Remember the ABC priority in any new post-operative client."}, {"id": 1722, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Itching from dry skin brings a 78-year-old to a clinic, and she asks about an over-the-counter allergy pill for her dry, flaky shins. She fell once last month and takes a prescribed sleep medication. Which advice about antihistamines should the practical nurse give her?", "options": {"A": "Take half a sedating tablet at bedtime to lower side effects", "B": "Use a sedating antihistamine, because it will also help her sleep through the itch", "C": "Use a non-sedating one, since sedating antihistamines raise fall and confusion risk", "D": "Use an antihistamine cream on cracked skin several times daily"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reducing the dose but keeping a sedating drug", "B": "Choosing sedation in an older adult with falls", "D": "Applying antihistamine cream to broken skin"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use a non-sedating one, since sedating antihistamines raise fall and confusion risk): Older adults are sensitive to sedating antihistamines, which increase confusion and falls, particularly alongside a sleep medication, so a non-sedating option is the safer teaching. Halving a sedating tablet still adds sedation to her sleep medication. Antihistamine creams on cracked skin can irritate or sensitize it and do not treat dry-skin itch. Using sedation as a sleep aid adds risk. Remember to refer for dry skin care."}, {"id": 1723, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On a hospital unit, a practical nurse notices a water spill in the corridor near a client who is walking with a walker. Another client's call bell is ringing, and a third needs help to the washroom. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Protect the walking client, keep others from the spill, and call for help", "B": "Leave the area to find a mop and let the client keep walking with the walker", "C": "Assume housekeeping already noticed the spill and attend to the client needing the washroom", "D": "Answer the ringing call bell first and return to the spill once that client is settled"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Abandoning a client at risk", "C": "Assuming someone else would act", "D": "Leaving a hazard unattended"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Protect the walking client, keep others from the spill, and call for help): A hazard that already places a client at risk is addressed first, so the nurse protects the walking client, warns others, and calls for assistance. Answering a bell first leaves the hazard in place. Leaving to find a mop abandons a client in danger. Assuming housekeeping noticed it relies on guesses. Remember to remain with the hazard until it is dealt with."}, {"id": 1724, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Awake at night, confused, and picking at his bedding, an 86-year-old on a medical unit slept well the night before. A colleague says he is simply a poor sleeper and should be left alone. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Assess for causes of delirium such as pain, infection, or retention and report to the RN", "B": "Offer a sedative sleep aid and note it as a sleep problem", "C": "Move him to a bed near the station and monitor him until morning", "D": "Give his usual evening medications on time, then reassess his sleep at breakfast"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Misreading delirium as insomnia and sedating", "C": "Observing closely without assessing causes", "D": "Delaying assessment of acute confusion until morning"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C214", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse who rotates through a medical unit, a pediatric ward, and a community health centre during one week. Sleep concerns come up with clients of different ages in each setting, including an older adult, a young child, an infant, and an adult who works rotating shifts. You are asked to assess each concern and teach the family or client.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Assess for causes of delirium such as pain, infection, or retention and report to the RN): Sudden confusion and restlessness at night in an older adult is delirium until proved otherwise, so the nurse looks for pain, infection, retention, or medication causes and reports to the RN. Sedatives can worsen delirium. Closer observation alone does not find the cause. Waiting until breakfast delays treatment of a medical cause. Remember that an acute change in mental status needs prompt reporting."}, {"id": 1725, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the pediatric ward, a 2-year-old cries at bedtime and asks for his bottle of juice. His mother has brought his stuffed bear and says juice at night is their routine. The nurse wants to promote sleep and protect his teeth. What is best?", "options": {"A": "Follow a calm routine with his comfort toy and offer water instead of juice", "B": "Stay with him until he sleeps and give a bottle of milk in bed", "C": "Allow the juice bottle in bed so he settles quickly", "D": "Remove his bear and bottle to stop dependence on bedtime comfort habits"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Giving milk in bed, which promotes tooth decay", "C": "Allowing sugary drinks in bed and risking caries", "D": "Removing comfort objects to end dependence"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C214", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse who rotates through a medical unit, a pediatric ward, and a community health centre during one week. Sleep concerns come up with clients of different ages in each setting, including an older adult, a young child, an infant, and an adult who works rotating shifts. You are asked to assess each concern and teach the family or client.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Follow a calm routine with his comfort toy and offer water instead of juice): A predictable calm routine with a comfort object eases bedtime fears, and water instead of juice protects against tooth decay from sugary liquids pooling in the mouth. Juice or milk in bed causes caries. Removing the comfort toy and bottle together takes away his security at a hard time. Remember to explain the reasons to the parent."}, {"id": 1726, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a community visit, a mother says her 2-month-old sleeps longer on his stomach on a soft mattress, so she plans to continue. The practical nurse notices a pillow beside him in the crib. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Recommend tummy sleeping only while she is nearby watching him", "B": "Accept her choice since the baby is old enough to lift his head and turn it", "C": "Suggest side lying with a rolled blanket to prevent choking", "D": "Explain the risk of sudden infant death and teach back sleeping on a firm surface"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Allowing prone sleep with supervision", "B": "Believing the baby's age removes risk", "C": "Recommending side lying that is unstable"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C214", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse who rotates through a medical unit, a pediatric ward, and a community health centre during one week. Sleep concerns come up with clients of different ages in each setting, including an older adult, a young child, an infant, and an adult who works rotating shifts. You are asked to assess each concern and teach the family or client.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain the risk of sudden infant death and teach back sleeping on a firm surface): A baby under six months who sleeps prone on a soft surface is at higher risk of sudden infant death and suffocation, so the nurse teaches back sleeping on a firm, flat surface without pillows. Two months is still a high-risk period. Side lying lets the baby roll prone. Prone sleep with supervision does not lower risk. Remember to respect her concerns and to use teach-back."}, {"id": 1727, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Working rotating shifts, a 38-year-old woman at an occupational health clinic says she cannot sleep after night shifts. She watches a screen in bed and drinks coffee at the end of her shift. Which advice should the practical nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Take short naps all day and night to match her shifts", "B": "Take a long nap before each night shift and skip morning sleep to reset her clock", "C": "Darken the bedroom, avoid late caffeine, and keep a regular sleep schedule", "D": "Take an over-the-counter sleep aid nightly to help her fall asleep"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Fragmenting sleep into short naps", "B": "Skipping daytime sleep and adding to sleep debt", "D": "Relying on nightly sleep aids"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C214", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse who rotates through a medical unit, a pediatric ward, and a community health centre during one week. Sleep concerns come up with clients of different ages in each setting, including an older adult, a young child, an infant, and an adult who works rotating shifts. You are asked to assess each concern and teach the family or client.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Darken the bedroom, avoid late caffeine, and keep a regular sleep schedule): Darkness, avoiding caffeine near sleep time, and a regular schedule support sleep after night work. Scattered naps break sleep into short pieces and add to sleep debt. Nightly sleep aids do not fix the schedule problem and can cause daytime drowsiness. Skipping morning sleep adds a sleep debt. Remember to teach screen avoidance and a cool quiet room."}, {"id": 1728, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At a rural clinic, a 58-year-old man arrives with chest pain radiating to his arm, sweating, and nausea. The only physician is two hours away by phone. The practical nurse considers acting entirely alone. What best reflects the duty to consult?", "options": {"A": "Treat independently using her best judgment and document afterwards", "B": "Start emergency care within her competence and contact the physician or EMS immediately", "C": "Take his vital signs and wait for the physician before calling EMS", "D": "Have him rest and recheck vital signs in thirty minutes"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Managing independently without consulting", "C": "Delaying EMS until the physician responds", "D": "Delaying treatment and monitoring of chest pain"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C205", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse working in a small rural hospital where the physician is on site only part of the day and an RN is available by phone at night. You care for adults on a medical-surgical unit and in a small clinic, and you often make decisions about what you can do alone, when to consult, and what falls inside your scope of practice under your regulatory standards.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Start emergency care within her competence and contact the physician or EMS immediately): A possible heart attack is an emergency, so the nurse acts within her competence while calling for physician direction and emergency services at once. Treating alone without consultation exceeds her role. Waiting for the physician before calling EMS delays life-saving care. Resting him and rechecking later leaves an unstable client without treatment. Remember that working alone increases the need to consult early."}, {"id": 1729, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "With an order for oral morphine for his severe pain, a 66-year-old is on the unit when a new practical nurse says controlled drugs never fall within practical nurse practice. Which statement is accurate about scope for giving this drug?", "options": {"A": "Practical nurses may give ordered controlled drugs when standards, policy, and competence allow", "B": "Practical nurses may count controlled drugs but not administer them under standards", "C": "Practical nurses cannot give controlled drugs, which belong to RN practice alone", "D": "Practical nurses may give any controlled drug in an emergency without an order from the physician"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Confusing counting with administering", "C": "Believing controlled drugs are excluded from PN scope", "D": "Giving drugs without an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C205", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse working in a small rural hospital where the physician is on site only part of the day and an RN is available by phone at night. You care for adults on a medical-surgical unit and in a small clinic, and you often make decisions about what you can do alone, when to consult, and what falls inside your scope of practice under your regulatory standards.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Practical nurses may give ordered controlled drugs when standards, policy, and competence allow): Scope depends on regulatory standards, employer policy, and individual competence, not on whether a drug is controlled, so the nurse may give an ordered controlled drug when all three support it. A blanket exclusion misstates the standards. Giving drugs without an order is outside scope. Counting but not giving has no basis in standards. Remember to check the standard for your jurisdiction."}, {"id": 1730, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Needing IV antibiotics started, a 70-year-old client has a peripheral IV infusion in the rural hospital. The practical nurse has no IV certification and the employer requires it. A colleague says the physician's order makes it within her scope. What is best?", "options": {"A": "Start it while the RN watches, since supervision removes competency requirements", "B": "Start it if she has watched colleagues do it many times", "C": "Start it because the physician's order makes it within her scope", "D": "Decline until she holds the required education and competency per regulator and employer policy"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing supervision replaces certification", "B": "Substituting observation for competence", "C": "Treating an order as authority"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C205", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse working in a small rural hospital where the physician is on site only part of the day and an RN is available by phone at night. You care for adults on a medical-surgical unit and in a small clinic, and you often make decisions about what you can do alone, when to consult, and what falls inside your scope of practice under your regulatory standards.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline until she holds the required education and competency per regulator and employer policy): Performing IV therapy requires education, competency, and employer authorization, so she declines and arranges for an authorized colleague or certification. An order does not create scope. Supervision does not replace required competency. Watching others is not education. Remember that scope depends on regulation, policy, and competence together."}, {"id": 1731, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Insertion of a nasogastric tube is needed for a surgical client on the unit tonight, and the physician tells the practical nurse to do it. She has never inserted one, and the employer has no policy for it. What best describes her authority?", "options": {"A": "The physician's order alone gives the practical nurse full legal authority to insert the tube", "B": "An order alone is not enough; the activity needs regulatory, employer, and competence support", "C": "The RN may delegate it without regard for her education", "D": "A client's consent alone makes the activity acceptable for her to perform"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating an order as sole authority", "C": "Believing delegation overrides competence", "D": "Confusing consent with scope"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C205", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse working in a small rural hospital where the physician is on site only part of the day and an RN is available by phone at night. You care for adults on a medical-surgical unit and in a small clinic, and you often make decisions about what you can do alone, when to consult, and what falls inside your scope of practice under your regulatory standards.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An order alone is not enough; the activity needs regulatory, employer, and competence support): Initiating a new activity needs support from the regulatory standards, employer policy, and the nurse's own education and competence, so an order alone is insufficient. Delegation does not remove competence requirements. Consent protects the client's rights but does not extend scope. Remember to ask for a policy and training before taking on a new skill."}, {"id": 1732, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At the clinic, a 62-year-old verbally agrees to wound debridement by the practical nurse. She has attended weekly visits for a heel pressure injury. Policy requires documentation of consent for the activity, and the nurse is unsure whether verbal agreement is valid. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Verbal agreement is not valid here, so she should stop until written consent is signed", "B": "Verbal consent can be valid; document the discussion, agreement and understanding", "C": "Consent is implied by the client's attendance at weekly visits, so no note is needed", "D": "Obtain a family member's signature in place of the client's own signature for the procedure"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating all verbal consent as invalid", "C": "Treating attendance as consent", "D": "Substituting family consent for a capable client"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C205", "cluster_text": "You are a practical nurse working in a small rural hospital where the physician is on site only part of the day and an RN is available by phone at night. You care for adults on a medical-surgical unit and in a small clinic, and you often make decisions about what you can do alone, when to consult, and what falls inside your scope of practice under your regulatory standards.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Verbal consent can be valid; document the discussion, agreement and understanding): Consent can be verbal, written, or implied when valid, and documenting the information given, the client's agreement, and understanding provides evidence. Calling verbal consent invalid is incorrect. Presence alone is not informed consent. A capable client gives consent herself, not a family member. If policy specifically requires a signed form, that form must be followed."}, {"id": 1733, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The unit lead wants to understand why medication errors occurred. The team lists staffing, interruptions, labelling, and communication under category headings on a large diagram. A colleague asks the practical nurse what the tool shows and what it does not. Which description is accurate?", "options": {"A": "It groups possible causes under categories to explore why problems happen", "B": "It shows every step of the process in order with decision points", "C": "It tracks the number of errors over time on a line graph", "D": "It ranks the causes by frequency so that the most common one is addressed first"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Confusing it with a process map", "C": "Confusing it with a run chart", "D": "Confusing a fishbone diagram with a Pareto chart"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C215", "cluster_text": "The medical unit's quality committee is reviewing three issues: repeated medication errors, rising incident reports across categories, and slow discharges. The unit also compares its catheter infection rate to a provincial benchmark. You are the practical nurse on the committee, asked to help choose tools to understand the problems, to contribute from your bedside experience, and to help the unit reduce harm to clients.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It groups possible causes under categories to explore why problems happen): A cause-and-effect or fishbone diagram groups potential causes under categories to explore why a problem occurs, which matches the team's headings. A ranking by frequency describes a Pareto chart. A line graph over time is a run chart. A step-by-step flow with decisions is a process map. Remember that the fishbone does not measure frequency."}, {"id": 1734, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The committee has incident data of 40 falls, 25 medication errors, and 12 pressure injuries over six months. They want to focus effort on the few categories producing most of the harm. Which tool should the practical nurse suggest to the group?", "options": {"A": "A run chart showing how incident counts change from week to week over time", "B": "A Pareto chart that ranks categories by frequency to target the biggest few", "C": "A flowchart showing each step of the discharge process from admission onward", "D": "A fishbone diagram listing possible causes by category without any frequency counts"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a trend tool for ranking", "C": "Using a process map for frequency data", "D": "Using a cause tool without counts"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C215", "cluster_text": "The medical unit's quality committee is reviewing three issues: repeated medication errors, rising incident reports across categories, and slow discharges. The unit also compares its catheter infection rate to a provincial benchmark. You are the practical nurse on the committee, asked to help choose tools to understand the problems, to contribute from your bedside experience, and to help the unit reduce harm to clients.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A Pareto chart that ranks categories by frequency to target the biggest few): A Pareto chart ranks categories by frequency, showing which few account for most incidents so effort is focused there. A run chart shows change over time rather than ranking. A flowchart describes steps, not counts. A fishbone lists causes but does not rank them by frequency. Remember to choose the tool that matches the question being asked."}, {"id": 1735, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The team maps the discharge process and finds a four-hour wait between the physician's order and pharmacy release, a step no one owns. The lead says process maps are only for managers. What should the practical nurse conclude from this finding?", "options": {"A": "The delay is pharmacy's issue, so nursing should leave the map to that department", "B": "The map reveals a gap, and frontline staff contribute to mapping and improving the process", "C": "Add a second order check to the process, since extra steps reduce waits", "D": "The gap is a staffing problem to be solved by hiring more nurses"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assigning an unowned step to one department", "C": "Adding steps to fix a delay", "D": "Assuming a staffing cause without evidence"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C215", "cluster_text": "The medical unit's quality committee is reviewing three issues: repeated medication errors, rising incident reports across categories, and slow discharges. The unit also compares its catheter infection rate to a provincial benchmark. You are the practical nurse on the committee, asked to help choose tools to understand the problems, to contribute from your bedside experience, and to help the unit reduce harm to clients.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The map reveals a gap, and frontline staff contribute to mapping and improving the process): Process maps expose gaps such as a step without an owner, and frontline staff know how the work actually happens, so their input is essential. Assigning the delay to one department leaves the unowned step unaddressed. Adding checks lengthens a process that already waits too long. Assuming the problem is staffing skips analysis. Remember that improvement is a team responsibility."}, {"id": 1736, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The unit's catheter infection rate is 3.5 per 1000 catheter days, and the provincial benchmark is 2.0. A colleague wants to compare with another unit with different clients and conclude the unit is fine. What is the best action for the practical nurse to support?", "options": {"A": "Compare only with last quarter's rate and continue current practice if it improved", "B": "Compare with a unit that has a higher rate and conclude the unit is performing well", "C": "Compare with the standard and similar units, then analyze causes and plan action", "D": "Compare with the provincial benchmark and report it, leaving action planning to managers"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Comparing only with own past rate", "B": "Selective comparison to excuse performance", "D": "Reporting the gap without analysing causes"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C215", "cluster_text": "The medical unit's quality committee is reviewing three issues: repeated medication errors, rising incident reports across categories, and slow discharges. The unit also compares its catheter infection rate to a provincial benchmark. You are the practical nurse on the committee, asked to help choose tools to understand the problems, to contribute from your bedside experience, and to help the unit reduce harm to clients.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Compare with the standard and similar units, then analyze causes and plan action): Benchmarking compares performance with a recognized standard and comparable units, and a rate above the benchmark calls for cause analysis and an action plan. Choosing a worse-performing unit hides the gap. Comparing only with the unit's own past rate ignores the external standard. Reporting the number without planning action leaves harm unaddressed, and frontline staff should help analyze causes. Remember that benchmarks guide improvement, not blame."}, {"id": 1737, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Clients at a clinic are handed a survey about waiting times and how clearly staff communicate. A new practical nurse asks whether collecting this feedback is part of her role, because her manager says quality belongs to leaders and committees. Which view of quality improvement best reflects a safety culture?", "options": {"A": "She documents care accurately and leaves any improvement suggestions to senior staff or committees", "B": "Client feedback matters for serious incidents, so routine comments about waiting are filed unreviewed", "C": "Quality belongs to the committee, so she passes survey results to managers without comment", "D": "Everyone contributes, including clients, so she invites feedback and shares her observations"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confusing documentation with participation in improvement", "B": "Valuing only reports of serious harm", "C": "Treating quality as a leadership task only"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Everyone contributes, including clients, so she invites feedback and shares her observations): A safety culture treats quality as shared work, so clients and every staff member have a part, and the nurse's observations are useful data. Passing results along silently fails because it leaves frontline insight out of the improvement cycle. Reviewing only serious incidents misses the small patterns that predict larger problems. Remember that quality improvement depends on informed participation from everyone involved in care."}, {"id": 1738, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The day before discharge, Nadia asks how she should take the daily levothyroxine she will need after surgery. She usually eats a large breakfast at 0800 and takes her calcium supplement with that meal every day. Which teaching about taking this medication is best?", "options": {"A": "Take it with breakfast and her calcium supplement so she remembers both daily routines together", "B": "Take it with water at breakfast, then take calcium an hour afterward", "C": "Take it with water before breakfast and her calcium one hour later", "D": "Take it with water before breakfast and separate it from calcium by several hours"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving the drug with food and calcium that bind it", "B": "Taking levothyroxine with food", "C": "Spacing calcium too close to the dose"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C209", "cluster_text": "Nadia Petrov, 44, has Graves disease with palpitations and weight loss and is admitted to a surgical unit for a total thyroidectomy. She also has autoimmune adrenal insufficiency, treated with daily hydrocortisone. The practical nurse cares for her from admission through discharge teaching, working with the registered nurse and the surgeon, and reports changes in her condition promptly. Her surgeon expects lifelong medication after surgery, so she receives teaching at every stage.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Take it with water before breakfast and separate it from calcium by several hours): Levothyroxine is absorbed best on an empty stomach, and calcium binds it in the gut, so spacing the two by several hours protects the dose she needs. Taking it with breakfast, even when calcium comes later, reduces absorption. Taking calcium one hour after the tablet still allows binding. Taking it with breakfast and calcium together can leave her underdosed. Teach consistent timing, water only, and spacing from calcium and iron."}, {"id": 1739, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Four hours after surgery Nadia has a temperature of 39.4 C, a heart rate of 142, and is restless and sweating. Her neck dressing is dry. A colleague suggests giving acetylsalicylic acid for the fever. Which action should the nurse take first?", "options": {"A": "Notify the registered nurse about suspected thyroid storm and begin external cooling", "B": "Give the prescribed acetylsalicylic acid for the fever and recheck her temperature once it acts", "C": "Reassure her that fever and a fast heart rate are expected after anesthesia", "D": "Encourage oral fluids and a warm blanket, then report if the temperature keeps rising"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using a drug that can raise free thyroid hormone", "C": "Attributing a crisis to ordinary anesthesia effects", "D": "Warming a client who is overheated and delaying the report"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C209", "cluster_text": "Nadia Petrov, 44, has Graves disease with palpitations and weight loss and is admitted to a surgical unit for a total thyroidectomy. She also has autoimmune adrenal insufficiency, treated with daily hydrocortisone. The practical nurse cares for her from admission through discharge teaching, working with the registered nurse and the surgeon, and reports changes in her condition promptly. Her surgeon expects lifelong medication after surgery, so she receives teaching at every stage.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Notify the registered nurse about suspected thyroid storm and begin external cooling): Fever, severe tachycardia and restlessness soon after thyroid surgery fit thyroid storm, which needs urgent reporting and cooling. Acetylsalicylic acid is avoided because it can raise free thyroid hormone levels and worsen the crisis. Calling these signs normal delays treatment of a life threatening complication. Remember that a hypermetabolic picture after thyroidectomy is an emergency, not an expected recovery finding."}, {"id": 1740, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Six hours after Nadia's thyroidectomy, the front of her neck dressing is dry and clean. She reports tingling around her mouth and fingertips, and her voice is hoarse and weak. Her calcium level has not yet been checked today. Which nursing action is most appropriate now?", "options": {"A": "Report possible low calcium to the registered nurse and keep airway equipment at the bedside", "B": "Document the tingling as a normal anesthesia effect and reassess at the next routine check", "C": "Check beneath her neck and shoulders for pooled blood, since the dry dressing excludes other problems", "D": "Encourage her to rest her voice and offer a warm drink for her throat"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Dismissing an early sign of calcium loss", "C": "Checking only for bleeding and ignoring neuromuscular signs", "D": "Treating hoarseness alone as minor irritation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C209", "cluster_text": "Nadia Petrov, 44, has Graves disease with palpitations and weight loss and is admitted to a surgical unit for a total thyroidectomy. She also has autoimmune adrenal insufficiency, treated with daily hydrocortisone. The practical nurse cares for her from admission through discharge teaching, working with the registered nurse and the surgeon, and reports changes in her condition promptly. Her surgeon expects lifelong medication after surgery, so she receives teaching at every stage.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report possible low calcium to the registered nurse and keep airway equipment at the bedside): Tingling around the mouth and fingers after thyroidectomy suggests hypocalcemia from parathyroid injury, which can progress to spasm of the larynx, so she needs prompt reporting and airway readiness. Calling it an anesthesia effect risks missing seizures or laryngospasm. Checking behind the neck for blood is a good habit but does not explain tingling or address calcium. Assess for bleeding and calcium signs together."}, {"id": 1741, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On postoperative day one Nadia is drowsy, with a blood pressure of 82/48 mmHg, a heart rate of 118, and repeated vomiting. She says she could not keep her hydrocortisone down yesterday. How should the nurse interpret this and respond?", "options": {"A": "Suspect fluid overload, restrict oral fluids and salt, and weigh her on the bed scale.", "B": "Suspect pain related anxiety, offer a prescribed analgesic, and encourage slow deep breathing exercises", "C": "Suspect adrenal crisis, report urgently to the nurse, and expect fluids with steroid replacement", "D": "Suspect thyroid storm, apply cooling measures, and hold all medications until the surgeon examines her"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Restricting fluid and salt in adrenal insufficiency", "B": "Assuming anxiety explains low blood pressure", "D": "Mislabelling the picture as thyroid storm without fever"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C209", "cluster_text": "Nadia Petrov, 44, has Graves disease with palpitations and weight loss and is admitted to a surgical unit for a total thyroidectomy. She also has autoimmune adrenal insufficiency, treated with daily hydrocortisone. The practical nurse cares for her from admission through discharge teaching, working with the registered nurse and the surgeon, and reports changes in her condition promptly. Her surgeon expects lifelong medication after surgery, so she receives teaching at every stage.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Suspect adrenal crisis, report urgently to the nurse, and expect fluids with steroid replacement): Low blood pressure, vomiting and missed steroid doses in a client with adrenal insufficiency point to adrenal crisis, which needs fluids and corticosteroid replacement quickly. Restricting fluid and salt would worsen the hypotension. Thyroid storm presents with high temperature and agitation rather than low pressure and drowsiness. Remember that surgical stress raises steroid needs in these clients."}, {"id": 1742, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Parents ask to stay with their five-year-old daughter during an intravenous insertion, saying she is calmer with them. A colleague says parents should wait in the lounge because they upset children and slow the procedure. Which principle of family centred care should guide the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Procedures belong to the health care team, so parents wait outside until the site is secure", "B": "The child decides who attends, so the nurse asks the young child to choose first", "C": "Parents are partners in care, so offer a supported bedside role and prepare them first", "D": "Parents may stay when the unit is quiet, otherwise the usual routine applies"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Excluding family from procedures", "B": "Giving a young child sole decision making power", "D": "Making family presence depend on unit convenience"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Parents are partners in care, so offer a supported bedside role and prepare them first): Family centred care recognizes parents as partners who comfort the child and know her best, so a prepared and supported parent at the bedside is the usual approach. Sending them out treats family as visitors rather than partners. Making presence depend on how busy the unit is ignores the child's needs. A five-year-old cannot be expected to make that decision alone."}, {"id": 1743, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lau, 84, is being treated for a urinary tract infection in long term care. At 1400 she is newly confused, her temperature is 36.1 C, her respiratory rate is 24, and her blood pressure is lower than usual. Which finding should lead the nurse to suspect sepsis and report urgently?", "options": {"A": "Burning on urination with frequent voiding, which are the earliest signs of a spread of infection", "B": "New confusion with fast breathing and falling blood pressure, even though she has no fever", "C": "Cloudy and strong smelling urine, which confirms the infection has already reached her bloodstream", "D": "A temperature above 38 C, since sepsis does not occur without fever in older adults"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Mistaking local urinary symptoms for systemic spread", "C": "Treating urine appearance as proof of bloodstream infection", "D": "Waiting for fever in a client who may not mount one"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (New confusion with fast breathing and falling blood pressure, even though she has no fever): Older adults often show sepsis through new confusion, rapid breathing and low blood pressure rather than fever, so those changes call for urgent reporting. Insisting on a fever delays recognition in a client who may not mount one. Burning and frequency describe the bladder infection, not its spread. Urine appearance cannot confirm a bloodstream infection."}, {"id": 1744, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Alder, 81, is on day two after hip fracture repair. This morning his daughter says he is suddenly confused and picking at the sheets, though he was clear yesterday. His vital signs are stable. How should the nurse interpret this change?", "options": {"A": "As progression of dementia, so document his baseline memory and plan teaching about memory loss", "B": "As anxiety about discharge, so offer information about rehabilitation services and encourage him to rest", "C": "As expected aging after anesthesia that settles alone, so only reorient him at mealtimes", "D": "Possible acute delirium needing assessment for pain, infection or drug causes, with a prompt report"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming dementia without a baseline", "B": "Attributing a sudden change to worry about discharge", "C": "Treating delirium as normal aging"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C210", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse on a short stay surgical unit cares for three clients today. Mr. Alder, 81, is two days after hip fracture repair. Emma, 7, is scheduled for a tonsillectomy this afternoon, and her father is with her. Mr. Tran, 38, with a documented latex allergy, is waiting for a hernia repair. The nurse works with the registered nurse, the anesthesia team and the families.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Possible acute delirium needing assessment for pain, infection or drug causes, with a prompt report): A sudden change in attention and thinking is delirium until proven otherwise, and it signals a treatable cause such as pain, infection, dehydration or drug effects. Calling it normal aging leaves the cause unaddressed and raises risks of falls and longer stays. Dementia develops gradually, so an overnight change does not fit. Anxiety about discharge does not explain picking at sheets and fluctuating attention."}, {"id": 1745, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Emma, 7, is scheduled for tonsillectomy this afternoon and clutches her toy rabbit. Her father suggests telling her she is only going for a nap so she will not worry. She asks what the mask is for. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Wait for the anesthesia team, since they alone decide what a child hears about induction", "B": "Follow her father's suggestion, because a sleep story prevents fear until she wakes", "C": "Show her a picture book of surgical instruments so she sees every step of the operation", "D": "Explain honestly in simple words what she will feel and let her practise with the mask"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Deferring all preparation to another team", "B": "Using deception that destroys trust", "C": "Giving too much frightening detail for her age"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C210", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse on a short stay surgical unit cares for three clients today. Mr. Alder, 81, is two days after hip fracture repair. Emma, 7, is scheduled for a tonsillectomy this afternoon, and her father is with her. Mr. Tran, 38, with a documented latex allergy, is waiting for a hernia repair. The nurse works with the registered nurse, the anesthesia team and the families.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain honestly in simple words what she will feel and let her practise with the mask): Children cope best when they get truthful, age appropriate preparation and a chance to rehearse, and a toy lets her practise the mask without pressure. A nap story breaks trust if she wakes in pain. Waiting for another team delays the preparation she needs now. Detailed pictures of instruments overwhelm a seven year old and add fear."}, {"id": 1746, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Emma returned from surgery eight hours ago. Her mother says Emma is swallowing every few minutes and asks whether she is thirsty. Emma looks pale, and her pulse has risen from 96 to 128. Her mother is worried and asks what to do. What is the best plan?", "options": {"A": "Record frequent swallowing as normal healing and tell the family it settles alone", "B": "Give acetaminophen for throat discomfort and reassess her pulse at the next scheduled check", "C": "Report suspected bleeding to the registered nurse now and keep her fasting", "D": "Offer cool sips of water and ice chips, then recheck her swallowing after she drinks"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Calling a warning sign normal healing", "B": "Treating pain while ignoring a rising pulse", "D": "Reading a bleeding sign as thirst"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C210", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse on a short stay surgical unit cares for three clients today. Mr. Alder, 81, is two days after hip fracture repair. Emma, 7, is scheduled for a tonsillectomy this afternoon, and her father is with her. Mr. Tran, 38, with a documented latex allergy, is waiting for a hernia repair. The nurse works with the registered nurse, the anesthesia team and the families.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report suspected bleeding to the registered nurse now and keep her fasting): Frequent swallowing with pallor and a rising pulse after tonsillectomy suggests bleeding swallowed from the surgical site, which needs urgent reporting and keeping her fasting in case of return to the operating room. Offering fluids delays that response. Calling it normal healing misses the pattern of sudden change. Acetaminophen does not address blood loss, and waiting for scheduled vital signs wastes time."}, {"id": 1747, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Tran, 38, has a documented latex allergy and arrives for hernia repair. A colleague proposes using powder free latex gloves for his intravenous start, saying the powder carries the allergen. The unit stocks both glove types. Which statement shows correct understanding of this allergy?", "options": {"A": "The allergy covers balloons and gloves, so tourniquets and tubing in his room need no check", "B": "Only latex touching mucous membranes causes reactions, so skin contact with latex gloves is acceptable", "C": "Latex protein is the allergen, so powder free latex items still risk a reaction", "D": "Removing the powder removes the allergen, so powder free latex gloves are acceptable"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Overlooking hidden latex in equipment", "B": "Limiting reactions to mucous membrane contact", "D": "Believing powder is the only source of allergen"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C210", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse on a short stay surgical unit cares for three clients today. Mr. Alder, 81, is two days after hip fracture repair. Emma, 7, is scheduled for a tonsillectomy this afternoon, and her father is with her. Mr. Tran, 38, with a documented latex allergy, is waiting for a hernia repair. The nurse works with the registered nurse, the anesthesia team and the families.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Latex protein is the allergen, so powder free latex items still risk a reaction): The allergen is latex protein, which remains in powder free products, so his care needs latex free gloves and equipment including tourniquets and tubing. Removing powder lowers airborne exposure but does not remove the protein. Skin contact can trigger reactions too. Latex hides in many medical supplies, so the whole environment is checked."}, {"id": 1748, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a nurse sees a drug interaction alert on the electronic system almost every time she gives a common blood pressure drug, and colleagues routinely click past them. Tonight a different alert flags a new combination for a client with kidney impairment. How should she respond?", "options": {"A": "Override it as usual because most alerts here are low value and slow medication rounds", "B": "Wait until the end of the shift, then ask the pharmacist about the alerts together", "C": "Give the drug now and chart that the alert was acknowledged on the medication screen", "D": "Read this alert against the client's data and check with the pharmacist before giving"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating every alert as noise", "B": "Delaying verification until after the dose", "C": "Acknowledging an alert without acting on it"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Read this alert against the client's data and check with the pharmacist before giving): Alert fatigue makes real warnings easy to miss, so she reads this one against the client's kidney function and checks with the pharmacist or prescriber before giving the drug. Routine overriding is how serious interactions reach clients. Waiting until shift end allows the dose to be given first. Acknowledging the alert in the record does not make the dose safe. Reporting the nuisance alerts helps the organization tune the system."}, {"id": 1749, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Bauer, 52, has smoked a pack a day for thirty years and visits the clinic for a cough. When asked about quitting, he says he is not ready and has tried before without success. Which response by the nurse is best?", "options": {"A": "Acknowledge his feelings, share quitting benefits and supports, and offer to revisit.", "B": "Tell him his cough cannot improve while he smokes and suggest returning when he is ready", "C": "Explain the lung damage he already has, document his refusal, and end the discussion today", "D": "Set a quit date together today and start nicotine patches before he leaves the clinic"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Withholding care until he agrees to quit", "C": "Using fear and ending the conversation", "D": "Pressuring a client who is not ready"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acknowledge his feelings, share quitting benefits and supports, and offer to revisit.): A client who is not ready responds best to a respectful approach that keeps the door open, so the nurse shares information and asks permission to revisit it. Pressing for a quit date ignores his readiness and can damage trust. Fear based talk followed by closing the topic ends the relationship around quitting. Linking cough care to quitting is coercive and unhelpful."}, {"id": 1750, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Hassan's oxygen saturation falls to 86 percent with severe breathlessness, and he rates his chest discomfort 8 out of 10. A colleague says the do not resuscitate order means the team should stop active treatment and observe. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Hold oxygen and opioids until the physician clarifies which treatments the order allows", "B": "Call a code blue because the breathing emergency may lead to an arrest", "C": "Raise the head of the bed, apply prescribed oxygen, and give prescribed comfort measures", "D": "Stay with him and give emotional support only, since treatment conflicts with his wishes"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withholding comfort measures because of the order", "B": "Activating resuscitation against the order", "D": "Confusing the order with stopping all treatment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C212", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hassan, 78, has end stage heart failure and was admitted to a medical unit with worsening breathlessness. He has a signed do not resuscitate order and is mentally clear. His wife and three adult children visit daily. The practical nurse cares for him alongside the registered nurse and the palliative care team over three days, and reports changes in his condition and any questions he raises.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raise the head of the bed, apply prescribed oxygen, and give prescribed comfort measures): A do not resuscitate order limits cardiopulmonary resuscitation only, so symptom relief, oxygen and positioning continue and distress is reported. Withholding comfort measures while seeking clarification leaves him suffering. Calling a code contradicts his written wishes. Emotional support alone ignores his need for active symptom care."}, {"id": 1751, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The next morning Mr. Hassan quietly asks the nurse whether he could request medical assistance in dying, because he is tired of struggling to breathe. He is mentally clear, and his wife is not in the room. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Explain the legal criteria in detail and help him complete the written request form", "B": "Listen without judgment, explain that authorized clinicians assess eligibility, and report the request to the team", "C": "Say this is not something she can discuss, and turn the talk to his comfort", "D": "Suggest he discuss it with his wife first before the team hears about it"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Acting beyond her role in the legal process", "C": "Shutting down the discussion", "D": "Redirecting a private request to family first"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C212", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hassan, 78, has end stage heart failure and was admitted to a medical unit with worsening breathlessness. He has a signed do not resuscitate order and is mentally clear. His wife and three adult children visit daily. The practical nurse cares for him alongside the registered nurse and the palliative care team over three days, and reports changes in his condition and any questions he raises.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Listen without judgment, explain that authorized clinicians assess eligibility, and report the request to the team): A client who raises assisted dying needs a respectful, open response, and the nurse's role is to listen, give accurate basic information and bring the request to the team so formal assessment can occur. Completing request forms exceeds her role and the legal process. Asking him to talk to his wife first delays and redirects a request he made privately. Refusing to discuss it leaves him alone with his distress."}, {"id": 1752, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Another nurse on the unit tells the charge nurse she cannot take part in care related to assisted dying because of her personal beliefs. Mr. Hassan has now made a formal request. What action best balances her objection with his access to care?", "options": {"A": "She tells the family the unit does not support the request, protecting her integrity", "B": "She quietly withdraws from his care without telling anyone, since her objection is personal", "C": "She stays on his assignment and takes part, because refusing is not permitted", "D": "She tells her employer early and helps arrange a handover of that part of his care"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pushing personal belief onto the client's family", "B": "Abandoning the client without notice to the employer", "C": "Ignoring recognized conscience protections for nurses"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C212", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hassan, 78, has end stage heart failure and was admitted to a medical unit with worsening breathlessness. He has a signed do not resuscitate order and is mentally clear. His wife and three adult children visit daily. The practical nurse cares for him alongside the registered nurse and the palliative care team over three days, and reports changes in his condition and any questions he raises.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She tells her employer early and helps arrange a handover of that part of his care): Conscientious objection is respected when the nurse tells the employer early and helps arrange a handover so the client's access is not delayed, and she keeps providing other care. Quietly withdrawing leaves him without planned care. Telling the family the unit opposes the request is a personal imposition. Forcing participation ignores recognized conscience protections."}, {"id": 1753, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "As Mr. Hassan's condition declines, his family asks to recite prayers, face his bed in a certain direction and stay overnight. A student suggests using the same bedside routine for all dying clients to be fair. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the family what matters to them and adapt care within safety limits", "B": "Assume the practices usual for the family's faith and arrange them without asking", "C": "Limit the practices to visiting hours, because overnight stays interfere with unit routines", "D": "Apply the unit's standard routine to every dying client, since equal treatment means fairness"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Making assumptions based on a stereotype", "C": "Putting unit convenience above cultural needs", "D": "Confusing fairness with sameness"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C212", "cluster_text": "Mr. Hassan, 78, has end stage heart failure and was admitted to a medical unit with worsening breathlessness. He has a signed do not resuscitate order and is mentally clear. His wife and three adult children visit daily. The practical nurse cares for him alongside the registered nurse and the palliative care team over three days, and reports changes in his condition and any questions he raises.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask the family what matters to them and adapt care within safety limits): Respecting cultural practice at end of life means asking what matters to this family and adapting care safely, rather than assuming or imposing a script. A single routine treats everyone the same instead of fairly. Assuming practices from a faith group risks error. Restricting practices for convenience dismisses their needs when adaptation is possible."}, {"id": 1754, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a nurse starts a heparin infusion. The smart pump alerts that the programmed rate exceeds the drug library's upper limit. The latest blood test suggests more anticoagulation is needed, and the nurse is behind on tasks. What is the safest response?", "options": {"A": "Start at the ordered rate and request a second check next shift", "B": "Stop and verify the order with a second nurse or pharmacist before programming", "C": "Bypass the library limit in manual mode, since the prescriber's order outweighs the pump", "D": "Reduce the rate to the limit without telling anyone and document the lower dose"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying verification of a high risk drug", "C": "Overriding a safety limit to save time", "D": "Changing a dose without authority or reporting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop and verify the order with a second nurse or pharmacist before programming): Heparin is a high alert drug, and a limit alert is a prompt to recheck the order and calculation with another professional before proceeding. Overriding the library removes a safeguard built to catch errors. Quietly lowering the rate is an unauthorized change. Starting now and checking later puts the client at risk during the delay."}, {"id": 1755, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Greg's lower leg is warm, red and tender, and the redness has passed the line drawn at its edge yesterday. A coworker suggests applying a hot pack and massaging the area to improve circulation. His temperature is slightly raised. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Elevate the leg and watch whether the redness fades over two days", "B": "Mark the border again, report the spreading redness to the nurse practitioner, and avoid heat", "C": "Massage the area gently to move fluid out before applying a compression bandage", "D": "Apply a warm pack to bring blood to the area, then recheck in the morning"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting while an infection advances", "C": "Massaging an infected limb", "D": "Using heat and spreading infection"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C211", "cluster_text": "Greg Morrison, 49, works outdoors as a roofer and attends a community clinic. He has a hot, swollen, red area on his lower leg that has spread over two days, a changing dark mole on his back that his wife noticed, and itchy eczema on both forearms. The practical nurse assists the nurse practitioner with assessment, teaching and treatments. He lives with his wife and has no other health problems.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Mark the border again, report the spreading redness to the nurse practitioner, and avoid heat): Spreading redness past a marked border suggests cellulitis that is advancing, so it needs prompt assessment and likely antibiotics. Heat and massage can spread infection and increase pain. Elevation helps swelling, but waiting two days allows the infection to progress. The nurse documents and reports the change within her scope."}, {"id": 1756, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Greg's wife describes a brown black mole on his back that has grown larger over three months, has uneven edges, and shows two shades. It does not hurt or itch. Greg says painless moles are harmless. Which interpretation of these findings is best?", "options": {"A": "Sun exposure often darkens moles harmlessly, so he should use sunscreen and watch it", "B": "Irregular borders, mixed colour and growth are warning signs needing prompt assessment, even without pain", "C": "Itching or bleeding must appear before a mole is suspicious, so he should wait", "D": "Lack of pain suggests a benign mole, so checking at his next annual visit is adequate"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Attributing changes to harmless darkening", "C": "Waiting for late symptoms", "D": "Treating absence of pain as reassurance"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C211", "cluster_text": "Greg Morrison, 49, works outdoors as a roofer and attends a community clinic. He has a hot, swollen, red area on his lower leg that has spread over two days, a changing dark mole on his back that his wife noticed, and itchy eczema on both forearms. The practical nurse assists the nurse practitioner with assessment, teaching and treatments. He lives with his wife and has no other health problems.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Irregular borders, mixed colour and growth are warning signs needing prompt assessment, even without pain): Growth, an irregular border and more than one colour are recognized warning features of possible melanoma, and pain is not part of the pattern. Using pain as reassurance delays a diagnosis that depends on early removal. Sun darkening does not explain changing shape. Waiting for itching or bleeding means waiting for later signs."}, {"id": 1757, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Greg says he uses sunscreen only on hot summer days and wears a cap on the roof. He works outdoors from March to November, often for eight hours, and sweats heavily. He asks how he should protect his skin. Which teaching about sun protection is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Ultraviolet rays reach him in cool, cloudy weather too, so protection is needed daily with reapplication", "B": "One morning application of high number sunscreen covers the whole working day", "C": "Ultraviolet exposure peaks at midday in summer, so cooler months need no protection", "D": "A cap protects the face adequately, so sunscreen is needed only on the arms"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believing one application lasts all day", "C": "Linking risk to heat instead of ultraviolet rays", "D": "Relying on a cap alone"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C211", "cluster_text": "Greg Morrison, 49, works outdoors as a roofer and attends a community clinic. He has a hot, swollen, red area on his lower leg that has spread over two days, a changing dark mole on his back that his wife noticed, and itchy eczema on both forearms. The practical nurse assists the nurse practitioner with assessment, teaching and treatments. He lives with his wife and has no other health problems.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ultraviolet rays reach him in cool, cloudy weather too, so protection is needed daily with reapplication): Ultraviolet exposure depends on sun angle and duration, not temperature, so a roofer working eight months a year needs daily protection with reapplication and covering clothing. One application does not last through sweating and long hours. A cap leaves the neck, ears and arms exposed. Cool weather does not remove the risk."}, {"id": 1758, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The nurse prepares to apply a prescribed corticosteroid cream to Greg's forearm eczema. The skin is dry and cracked but not weeping, and he has not used this cream before. She plans to teach him self application as she works. Which technique is best?", "options": {"A": "Wear gloves and apply a thin layer with gentle strokes along hair growth", "B": "Apply a thick layer under a tight dressing so it stays in contact overnight", "C": "Apply with bare hands and rub firmly until the cream disappears into the skin", "D": "Rub it in circles into damp skin, then cover with plastic wrap for several hours"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Over applying and occluding", "C": "Skipping gloves and rubbing hard", "D": "Occluding the skin and increasing absorption"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C211", "cluster_text": "Greg Morrison, 49, works outdoors as a roofer and attends a community clinic. He has a hot, swollen, red area on his lower leg that has spread over two days, a changing dark mole on his back that his wife noticed, and itchy eczema on both forearms. The practical nurse assists the nurse practitioner with assessment, teaching and treatments. He lives with his wife and has no other health problems.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Wear gloves and apply a thin layer with gentle strokes along hair growth): Gloves protect the nurse from absorbing the drug, and a thin layer with gentle strokes in the direction of hair growth limits irritation of the follicles and cracked skin. Firm rubbing damages fragile skin. Thick layers or plastic wrap increase absorption and side effects. Teach Greg the same technique and the prescribed frequency."}, {"id": 1759, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mrs. Cortez, 80, on a palliative unit, has a written living will declining intubation. Tonight she is drowsy and cannot speak. Her son arrives upset and insists the team do everything, including intubation, if she worsens. How should the nurse proceed?", "options": {"A": "Ask the son to sign a new form replacing her written directive for the unit", "B": "Follow the son's request because family holds final authority once she cannot speak", "C": "Review the directive with the team, support the son, and follow her documented wishes", "D": "Delay decisions until the son and physician agree, continuing full treatment meanwhile"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Replacing the client's wishes with a family signature", "B": "Letting family override a written directive", "D": "Defaulting to full treatment during disagreement"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Review the directive with the team, support the son, and follow her documented wishes): A capable adult's written wishes continue to guide care when she cannot speak, so the team reviews the directive and supports the son while honouring it. Giving the son final authority overrides her choice. Continuing full treatment during the disagreement defaults against her wishes. A new form signed by the son cannot replace her own directive."}, {"id": 1760, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A 70 year old client on a palliative unit asks a practical nurse about medical assistance in dying. The nurse is newly graduated and unsure what her role allows. Which statement best describes the nurse's role at a principle level in Canada?", "options": {"A": "She must decline all discussion, because any talk about assisted dying is outside her scope", "B": "She may take part if the physician delegates it, since delegation sets her role", "C": "She may give information but must report the request to the regulator first", "D": "She may give information and support within her college standards and the law, with conscience protections"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Refusing all discussion of assisted dying", "B": "Treating physician delegation as legal authority", "C": "Inventing a duty to report requests to the regulator"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She may give information and support within her college standards and the law, with conscience protections): The role of the nurse is defined by legislation and her regulatory body's standards, so she can provide information, support and referral and participate only as the law and her college allow, with conscience protections. Delegation by a physician does not create legal authority. Reporting the request to the regulator is not required. Declining all conversation abandons the client. Eligibility is assessed by designated practitioners, not decided by nurses."}, {"id": 1761, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 15 year old comes to the clinic alone requesting treatment for a suspected sexually transmitted infection and asks the nurse not to tell her parents. She seems calm and describes her symptoms clearly. How should the nurse interpret her ability to consent?", "options": {"A": "Minors under sixteen need parental consent, so a parent must be contacted first", "B": "Asking for privacy shows maturity, so she is treated as an adult for all decisions", "C": "She may consent to testing but not treatment, even if she understands both", "D": "Capacity depends on understanding the treatment and its consequences, not on age"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying a fixed age rule", "B": "Equating confidentiality requests with maturity", "C": "Splitting consent without a basis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Capacity depends on understanding the treatment and its consequences, not on age): Under the mature minor principle, ability to consent rests on understanding the nature, benefits and risks of the treatment, so the team assesses capacity for this decision and follows regulatory and agency policy. A fixed age rule ignores that principle. Asking for privacy is not proof of capacity. Splitting testing from treatment has no basis when she understands both."}, {"id": 1762, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ten minutes after spilling boiling water on her forearm, a client arrives at the clinic. The skin is red and blistered over an area about the size of her palm. A coworker fills a basin with ice water. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Open the larger blisters and apply antibiotic cream before covering with gauze", "B": "Cool the burn under cool running water for twenty minutes and remove nearby rings", "C": "Apply butter or petroleum jelly to the blisters to soothe pain and protect the skin", "D": "Immerse the arm in ice water for ten minutes, then cover it with a dry dressing"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Breaking blisters and risking infection", "C": "Applying a greasy substance that traps heat", "D": "Using ice that deepens tissue injury"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cool the burn under cool running water for twenty minutes and remove nearby rings): Cool running water for about twenty minutes limits tissue damage and relieves pain, and removing jewelry prevents constriction as swelling develops. Ice water can cause further tissue injury. Butter and petroleum products trap heat and raise infection risk. Opening blisters removes the protective barrier."}, {"id": 1763, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, a mother says her two year old swallowed several of her iron tablets about fifteen minutes ago. The child is alert and playing with no symptoms yet. The mother wants to induce vomiting with salt water. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Induce vomiting with salt water as the mother suggests, then observe for two hours", "B": "Give the child milk and bread to absorb the tablets, then call if symptoms begin", "C": "Contact poison control or emergency services now for direction, keeping the child and pill container together", "D": "Watch the child closely, since poison control advises after symptoms begin"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Inducing vomiting with a harmful method", "B": "Using food in place of expert advice", "D": "Waiting for symptoms before calling"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Contact poison control or emergency services now for direction, keeping the child and pill container together): Iron overdose can cause serious harm hours after a symptom free period, so expert direction is sought immediately with the container and amount at hand. Salt water can cause dangerous electrolyte problems. Waiting for symptoms wastes the early treatment window. Milk and bread do not neutralize iron."}, {"id": 1764, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Clinic staff see that a new adult client has a registration form listing a name and sex that do not match how the client presents. The nurse prepares to greet the client and document. Which approach best supports cultural safety and accurate care planning?", "options": {"A": "Use the name on the form and pronouns suggested by appearance until corrected", "B": "Ask privately which name and pronouns the client uses, and record them as stated", "C": "Avoid pronouns and refer to the client as the patient, even after a stated preference", "D": "Ask the client to explain gender identity in detail so the team understands"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Guessing identity from appearance", "C": "Ignoring a stated preference", "D": "Asking intrusive unnecessary questions"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask privately which name and pronouns the client uses, and record them as stated): Asking privately and recording what the client says respects identity and gives the team accurate information. Guessing from appearance risks misgendering. Demanding a detailed explanation is intrusive when only name and pronouns are needed. Avoiding pronouns after a stated preference ignores the client's request."}, {"id": 1765, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A home care nurse visits a 68 year old man who says he swallowed a mouthful of a household cleaner while thirsty at night. He talks normally and has mild throat burning. His wife thinks they should watch for symptoms. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Induce vomiting to remove the cleaner, then recheck his throat in about an hour", "B": "Record the event and ask him to report any new symptoms at the next scheduled visit", "C": "Offer water to dilute it and observe at home, calling if the burning increases", "D": "Call poison control now and give the product name and amount"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Inducing vomiting after a corrosive", "B": "Delaying action until later", "C": "Diluting a caustic without advice"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Call poison control now and give the product name and amount): Corrosive ingestion can injure the throat and esophagus before symptoms are obvious, so poison control directs treatment immediately with the product details. Dilution without advice and induced vomiting can worsen injury. Waiting for the next visit leaves a possible serious injury unassessed."}, {"id": 1766, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In long term care, Mr. Ito, 79, receives levothyroxine, ciprofloxacin and an iron supplement, and has an order for a calcium carbonate antacid at 0900. To save time, the aide suggests giving all morning medications together. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Skip the antacid for the day and give the other drugs on their usual schedule", "B": "Give everything together, since the stomach absorbs each drug independently of others", "C": "Check spacing with the pharmacist and give the antacid apart from the other drugs", "D": "Give the antacid first to protect the stomach, then the other drugs minutes later"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Omits an ordered drug without prescriber direction", "B": "Ignores binding interactions between antacid and other drugs", "D": "Spaces the antacid only minutes from other drugs"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check spacing with the pharmacist and give the antacid apart from the other drugs): Antacids containing calcium bind levothyroxine and ciprofloxacin and reduce their absorption, so the pharmacist's spacing guidance is applied and doses are separated. Giving all drugs together defeats absorption. A few minutes is not enough separation. Skipping an ordered drug without direction is outside scope."}, {"id": 1767, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Iyer, 78, has chronic obstructive lung disease and declines the influenza vaccine in clinic. She believes it gave her the flu last year, when she felt achy and feverish for two days afterward. Her daughter asks the nurse to explain. Which teaching is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Mild illness after the vaccine means the virus was in it, so she should skip it", "B": "Pneumococcal vaccine replaces yearly influenza vaccination, so one dose covers both illnesses", "C": "One influenza vaccine protects against chest infections for life, so repeats are unneeded", "D": "The injected vaccine has no live virus and lowers her risk of serious complications"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing the vaccine causes influenza", "B": "Confusing two different vaccines", "C": "Thinking one dose gives lasting protection"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The injected vaccine has no live virus and lowers her risk of serious complications): The injectable vaccine cannot cause influenza, and mild aches reflect the immune response, while the benefit is fewer severe complications in a client with lung disease. Mistaking symptoms for infection is a common myth. Pneumococcal vaccine protects against a different organism. Influenza strains change yearly, so annual dosing is advised."}, {"id": 1768, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two days after starting a new antibiotic, a hospital client develops a raised itchy rash on the trunk with no breathing difficulty. The next dose is due in thirty minutes. A coworker suggests charting the rash and giving the dose. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Give the dose with an as needed antihistamine and recheck the skin later", "B": "Hold the dose, assess the client, report to the nurse and prescriber, and document", "C": "Ask the pharmacist about the rash after giving the dose", "D": "Give the dose and chart the rash, since itching without breathing problems is not allergy"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Masks an early reaction with an antihistamine", "C": "Delays review until after the dose is given", "D": "Continues a drug that may be causing an allergy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold the dose, assess the client, report to the nurse and prescriber, and document): A new rash after a drug may be an early allergic reaction that can progress, so the dose is held pending review and the reaction is reported and documented through the organization's system. Giving the dose risks a severe reaction. Masking the rash with an antihistamine hides evidence. Checking with the pharmacist after the dose is too late."}, {"id": 1769, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "In the emergency department, an agitated man shouts, paces and clenches his fists while the nurse is alone with him, standing between the stretcher and a closed door. She has not yet taken his vital signs, and no security staff are in the room. What should she do first?", "options": {"A": "Block the doorway so he cannot leave and wait for security", "B": "Remain beside him and speak firmly until he follows instructions to sit down", "C": "Finish the vital signs quickly to complete her assessment before leaving", "D": "Stay calm, keep an exit path, speak softly, step out and call for help"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Restraining movement by blocking the exit", "B": "Staying alone with an escalating client", "C": "Putting a task ahead of safety"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stay calm, keep an exit path, speak softly, step out and call for help): Early warning signs of violence call for a calm approach, a safe exit and a call for help so the team can respond together. Staying alone and giving commands may escalate him. Completing vital signs puts a task before safety. Blocking the doorway restricts him and traps the nurse."}, {"id": 1770, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "During a night shift, an infusion pump on a client's intravenous antibiotic stops with an error, restarts, then alarms again. A colleague says it has done this before and suggests using it with a note on the chart. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Run the infusion by gravity and leave the pump at the bedside for the day team", "B": "Replace the pump with a working one and tag the faulty pump for removal", "C": "Restart the pump and watch it closely, reporting only if it fails a third time", "D": "Keep using it and add a chart note, since the antibiotic is nearly finished"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Bypassing the pump without direction", "C": "Waiting for repeated failure", "D": "Using faulty equipment with a note"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Replace the pump with a working one and tag the faulty pump for removal): Equipment that malfunctions can deliver the wrong dose, so it is removed from service, tagged and reported so engineering can repair it, and a working pump is used. A chart note does not make a faulty device safe. Gravity infusion removes rate control. Waiting for a third failure leaves the client exposed."}, {"id": 1771, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In long term care, a nurse preparing a resident's insulin notices the product on the label is different from the one ordered, and she corrects it before giving anything. The resident is not harmed. A coworker says no report is needed. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "It is reported only if the same mistake occurs again within the same month", "B": "It is a near miss and should be reported to help the team fix weaknesses", "C": "It is mentioned to the coworker only, since written reports are for events with injury", "D": "No report is needed because the nurse corrected it, and reports are for harmed residents"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting for repeated events before reporting", "C": "Keeping reports informal and verbal only", "D": "Reporting only events that cause harm"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is a near miss and should be reported to help the team fix weaknesses): A near miss reveals a weakness in the process, and reporting it lets the team fix the cause before harm occurs. Believing a corrected error needs no report loses learning. Verbal comments to a coworker do not reach people who can change the system. Waiting for a repeat delays prevention."}, {"id": 1772, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, an adult client confides that he is gay and has not told his family. His brother, visiting, asks the nurse whether there is something about his lifestyle the team should know for safety. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Keep the information confidential and invite the brother to speak with the client directly", "B": "Share the information, since family members need full details to protect the client's safety", "C": "Say there is nothing to know, since that avoids a confidential conversation", "D": "Ask the charge nurse to decide what to tell the brother for safety"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Disclosing orientation without the client's consent", "C": "Denying information instead of protecting confidentiality", "D": "Shifting a confidentiality decision to another staff member"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep the information confidential and invite the brother to speak with the client directly): Health and personal information is released only with the client's consent, and sexual orientation is private unless he chooses to share it. Sharing it for safety has no basis. Handing the decision to the charge nurse does not create consent, and saying there is nothing to know is untrue and misleading."}, {"id": 1773, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Dalton, 82, has low vision and lives in long term care. After housekeeping moved her chair and tray table during cleaning, she bumps her shin walking to the bathroom and has a small bruise. No one told her about the change. What action best prevents further injury?", "options": {"A": "Add a hip protector and a walker, and keep the new arrangement", "B": "Restore the usual layout, orient her to changes, and tell her about moves", "C": "Remind her to use the call bell and leave the new arrangement as it is now", "D": "Add bright tape to the furniture edges and keep the new arrangement"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adding equipment instead of removing the hazard", "C": "Leaving a hazard in place", "D": "Relying on contrast tape instead of restoring familiarity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Restore the usual layout, orient her to changes, and tell her about moves): Clients with low vision rely on a familiar layout, so unexpected changes cause injury, and the best response is to restore it, orient her and explain any move. Hip protectors and a walker do not remove the hazard. Bright tape helps some visual impairments but does not replace a familiar layout she can memorize. Leaving the arrangement changed keeps the hazard."}, {"id": 1774, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A nurse helps a 60 year old man who is newly blind walk from his bed to the shower room. At a doorway she grasps his upper arm and pulls him forward, and he stiffens. What is the better technique?", "options": {"A": "Offer her arm above the elbow, walk a half step ahead, and describe the route", "B": "Walk beside him, hold his elbow, and steer with gentle pressure on it", "C": "Offer her arm below the elbow and walk a step behind to watch him", "D": "Walk beside him with a hand on his shoulder, saying little to avoid distraction"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Steering by his elbow instead of letting him follow", "C": "Walking behind so he cannot follow her movement", "D": "Giving little verbal information"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Offer her arm above the elbow, walk a half step ahead, and describe the route): The sighted guide technique lets him hold her arm and feel her body movement, while spoken description gives information about the route. Steering by his elbow removes his control and increases fear. Walking behind him prevents him from sensing her movement and warning of obstacles. Walking beside with a hand on his shoulder and saying little leaves him without cues about doors and turns."}, {"id": 1775, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Quietly, a nine year old with terminal cancer asks the nurse whether she is dying. Her parents asked the team not to tell her the prognosis, and they have just left for coffee. She looks frightened and is holding her stuffed bear. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Respond honestly at her level, ask what she understands, and involve the team", "B": "Tell her she will feel better soon, to protect her hope and respect her parents' request", "C": "Change the subject, offer a toy, and ask her parents to decide what she hears", "D": "Give her complete details of the prognosis now, since children cope best with full facts"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Offers false reassurance that damages trust", "C": "Avoids her question and defers to parents", "D": "Overwhelms a child with unprepared prognosis detail"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Respond honestly at her level, ask what she understands, and involve the team): Children usually sense serious illness, so honest, age appropriate answers explore her understanding and keep trust while the team supports the parents. False reassurance damages trust. Changing the subject leaves her question unanswered. A full prognosis disclosure without preparation ignores her developmental level and the parents' role."}, {"id": 1776, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "After an unsuccessful resuscitation of a young client, the team leader says everyone is tired and the unit is busy, so the debrief should be skipped. A practical nurse who took part feels shaken and keeps replaying the event. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Discuss it with friends after the shift, since formal talk delays assignments", "B": "Request a brief debrief soon because it supports learning and wellbeing", "C": "Ask for a formal review weeks later, after emotions have settled and everyone has rested", "D": "Accept the decision and cope privately, since debriefs are optional after a death"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using informal venting instead of debrief", "C": "Delaying until feelings fade", "D": "Skipping support after a critical event"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Request a brief debrief soon because it supports learning and wellbeing): A timely debrief helps staff process the event, share what went well and identify system issues, and it protects against moral distress. Coping alone leaves unresolved distress. Delaying for weeks loses detail and support. Informal talk with friends cannot lead to process improvement."}, {"id": 1777, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Dubois's daughter tells the nurse in the hallway that her father is confused by illness and asks her to persuade him to sign the consent. He is oriented, explains his reasons clearly, and the team has assessed him as capable. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the daughter to sign the consent as next of kin, since family support helps", "B": "Ask the surgeon to explain the risks to the daughter first", "C": "Request a capacity reassessment because his daughter doubts his judgment", "D": "Support his informed decision and invite the daughter to raise concerns"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Substituting a family signature for a capable client", "B": "Giving family priority over the capable client", "C": "Treating family disagreement as lack of capacity"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C206", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dubois, 58, is a capable adult on a surgical unit who has been advised to have a bowel resection for a tumour. After discussion with the surgeon he declines surgery and prefers comfort focused treatment. His daughter wants him to have surgery, and the surgeon believes surgery is the best option. The unit is also short of beds because of an outbreak. The practical nurse caring for him works with a registered nurse and reports concerns.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Support his informed decision and invite the daughter to raise concerns): A capable adult makes his own treatment decisions, so the nurse supports his choice and helps the daughter voice concerns openly. Family cannot sign for a capable client. Asking the surgeon to brief the daughter first shifts the decision away from him. Requesting a new assessment because a relative disagrees treats disagreement as incapacity."}, {"id": 1778, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At rounds, the surgeon says to proceed with surgery as planned and asks the nurse to bring the consent form, adding that Mr. Dubois will agree once he hears the risks of waiting. He told the nurse again this morning that he refuses. What action is best?", "options": {"A": "Ask the daughter to persuade him before the consent form is brought", "B": "Tell the surgeon about his refusal and ask for a further conversation first", "C": "Bring the form and let him decide after reading it, since the physician decides treatment", "D": "Document the refusal and wait for the surgeon to ask about it"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Enlisting family to pressure a capable client", "C": "Following a physician's direction against a client's refusal", "D": "Passively documenting without informing the surgeon"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C206", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dubois, 58, is a capable adult on a surgical unit who has been advised to have a bowel resection for a tumour. After discussion with the surgeon he declines surgery and prefers comfort focused treatment. His daughter wants him to have surgery, and the surgeon believes surgery is the best option. The unit is also short of beds because of an outbreak. The practical nurse caring for him works with a registered nurse and reports concerns.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tell the surgeon about his refusal and ask for a further conversation first): Consent must be voluntary and informed, so the nurse advocates by reporting his refusal and asking for a conversation that respects it. Bringing the form under the physician's expectation applies pressure. Enlisting the daughter to persuade him adds family pressure to a capable client's decision. Documenting and waiting leaves the surgeon planning surgery the client has refused."}, {"id": 1779, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "The unit has one bed left and two clients waiting: a stable client who arrived first for a planned stay, and a client from emergency with a new serious bleed. The charge nurse asks the team how to decide. Which principle best guides fair allocation?", "options": {"A": "Allocate by arrival time, because first come, first served treats every waiting client equally.", "B": "Allocate by clinical need and urgency, using agreed criteria that are applied consistently.", "C": "Allocate by the client's expected length of stay, to free the bed sooner", "D": "Allocate by the physician's preference since physicians decide admissions"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying a fixed order of arrival", "C": "Putting bed turnover ahead of clinical need", "D": "Replacing agreed criteria with one person's preference"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C206", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dubois, 58, is a capable adult on a surgical unit who has been advised to have a bowel resection for a tumour. After discussion with the surgeon he declines surgery and prefers comfort focused treatment. His daughter wants him to have surgery, and the surgeon believes surgery is the best option. The unit is also short of beds because of an outbreak. The practical nurse caring for him works with a registered nurse and reports concerns.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Allocate by clinical need and urgency, using agreed criteria that are applied consistently.): Fair allocation of scarce resources rests on need and urgency using consistent, open criteria, which here favours the client with the new serious bleed. Arrival order ignores differences in need. Choosing by expected length of stay puts efficiency ahead of need. Deferring to one physician's preference without agreed criteria is not consistent or open."}, {"id": 1780, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Dubois's nurse feels tense and tearful after the shift, believing the team's pressure disrespects his choice, but she says nothing to anyone. She has begun dreading work and sleeps poorly. A colleague notices her distress but is unsure what to suggest. What is the best action for the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Take a few days of sick leave and avoid discussing the case", "B": "Document her disagreement with the team in the client's chart", "C": "Put her feelings aside and focus on tasks, since reflecting on them slows her work", "D": "Recognize it as moral distress, talk with a trusted colleague, and use ethics resources"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Avoiding the issue instead of seeking support", "B": "Using the client's chart for staff disagreement", "C": "Suppressing distress instead of addressing it"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C206", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dubois, 58, is a capable adult on a surgical unit who has been advised to have a bowel resection for a tumour. After discussion with the surgeon he declines surgery and prefers comfort focused treatment. His daughter wants him to have surgery, and the surgeon believes surgery is the best option. The unit is also short of beds because of an outbreak. The practical nurse caring for him works with a registered nurse and reports concerns.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Recognize it as moral distress, talk with a trusted colleague, and use ethics resources): Moral distress arises when a nurse knows the right action but feels unable to take it, and naming it and using support and ethics resources reduces harm. Suppressing it leads to burnout. Taking leave and avoiding discussion leaves the conflict unaddressed. Recording personal disagreement in the chart misuses the record for a team conflict."}, {"id": 1781, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Another nurse says she would refuse nothing that might extend life and tells Mr. Dubois he is making a mistake. He becomes quiet, avoids eye contact, and stops asking questions. The practical nurse overhears the conversation. What is the best way to respond?", "options": {"A": "Correct her colleague in front of him so he knows she was wrong", "B": "Report the colleague to the manager and delay talking with Mr. Dubois", "C": "Continue, because personal experience helps a client weigh the choice honestly", "D": "Reflect on how personal values may shape the talk, then return to his own goals"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Correcting a colleague publicly in front of the client", "B": "Delaying support for the client while escalating", "C": "Imposing personal values on a client's own decision"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C206", "cluster_text": "Mr. Dubois, 58, is a capable adult on a surgical unit who has been advised to have a bowel resection for a tumour. After discussion with the surgeon he declines surgery and prefers comfort focused treatment. His daughter wants him to have surgery, and the surgeon believes surgery is the best option. The unit is also short of beds because of an outbreak. The practical nurse caring for him works with a registered nurse and reports concerns.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reflect on how personal values may shape the talk, then return to his own goals): Values clarification helps nurses see how their beliefs may bias care so the client's values stay at the centre. Offering a personal opinion pressures him. Correcting a colleague in front of him embarrasses her and may add to his distress. Reporting first and delaying contact leaves his quiet withdrawal unaddressed."}, {"id": 1782, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "While a nurse is halfway through a dressing change on a hospital unit, a fire alarm sounds. She believes the alarm may be a drill and thinks she should finish the task first. The client is calm and mobile. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Call the switchboard to ask whether it is a drill, then decide", "B": "Finish the dressing change first, since leaving it open risks infection", "C": "Walk the client out alone and then call the fire department", "D": "Secure the client, follow the fire response plan, and begin the alarm steps"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Waiting to confirm a drill before acting", "B": "Putting a task before the alarm", "C": "Skipping the response plan and alerting steps"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Secure the client, follow the fire response plan, and begin the alarm steps): Every alarm is treated as real, so the nurse secures the client and starts the fire response steps right away. Finishing the dressing delays protection of everyone on the unit. Calling to ask about a drill wastes time. Walking one client out alone skips the shared response plan and leaves the rest of the unit unaware."}, {"id": 1783, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 45 year old client is anxious before a magnetic resonance imaging scan, and the technologist has used terms such as contrast and gradient noise. The client nods but looks confused and keeps checking the clock. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the technologist's explanation using the same terms, since consistent wording prevents confusion", "B": "Explain the test in plain words, ask her to describe it back, and invite questions", "C": "Tell her not to worry because the test is painless and most clients manage", "D": "Give a printed brochure and tell her to read it before the scan"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Repeating the technologist's jargon without checking understanding", "C": "Offering empty reassurance without explaining the test", "D": "Relying on written material alone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain the test in plain words, ask her to describe it back, and invite questions): Teach back confirms understanding, and plain words reduce anxiety about unfamiliar terms. Repeating jargon keeps the confusion. A brochure alone does not check understanding. Reassurance without information does not address her specific worries."}, {"id": 1784, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two weeks after chemotherapy, a client has an absolute neutrophil count of 0.4 times 10 to the 9 per litre. His daughter brings fresh flowers and a bowl of fruit and asks whether they are acceptable. Which response is best?", "options": {"A": "Allow both since infection risk comes mainly from visitors with colds", "B": "Allow the flowers if she keeps them at the window and washes her hands", "C": "Explain that fresh flowers and unwashed produce raise infection risk now, and suggest allowed alternatives", "D": "Allow the flowers but not the fruit because only food carries bacteria"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Overlooking environmental sources such as flowers and produce", "B": "Allowing flowers with partial precautions", "D": "Limiting risk to food only"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain that fresh flowers and unwashed produce raise infection risk now, and suggest allowed alternatives): Severe neutropenia means even environmental organisms in soil, water and unwashed produce can cause serious infection, so those items stay out while alternatives are offered. A window location does not remove the risk. Visitors matter, but the environment does too. Flowers carry organisms as well as food."}, {"id": 1785, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An adult with a cast on the forearm reports numb fingers and says the cast feels tight. The nurse notes the radial pulse is present and the fingers are slightly swollen. A coworker says a pulse means circulation is fine. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Apply ice over the cast and encourage him to wiggle his fingers hourly", "B": "Loosen the cast edges with scissors to reduce pressure and recheck sensation", "C": "Assess colour, warmth, sensation, movement and refill, keep the arm at heart level, and report promptly", "D": "Document the pulse as normal and recheck at the next scheduled check"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using ice and delaying report", "B": "Altering a cast without authority", "D": "Relying on the pulse alone"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess colour, warmth, sensation, movement and refill, keep the arm at heart level, and report promptly): Numbness and tightness point to developing neurovascular compromise or compartment syndrome, so a full assessment and prompt report are needed, with the arm kept at heart level and not raised above it because elevation above the heart lowers arterial flow because a palpable pulse can persist late. Relying on the pulse alone misses early signs. Cutting a cast is outside the nurse's role. Ice and delayed reporting risk permanent injury."}, {"id": 1786, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Jasmine asks whether one or two drinks on a weekend in her first trimester are proven safe, since she has read mixed opinions online and her friend drank lightly while pregnant. She seems unsure whom to believe. Which response by the nurse is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Alcohol harms the fetus after the first trimester, when the brain develops most", "B": "One occasional drink in the first trimester is proven safe, but daily drinking is not", "C": "No safe amount of alcohol in pregnancy is known, so abstaining is advised", "D": "Wine and beer are safe in small amounts, and only spirits risk the fetus"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Limiting harm to later pregnancy", "B": "Claiming a proven safe amount", "D": "Distinguishing drink types without evidence"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C208", "cluster_text": "Jasmine Aubrey, 20, attends a community prenatal clinic, first at 8 weeks and again at later visits. She is underweight, smokes about ten cigarettes a day, and had a few drinks most weekends before she knew she was pregnant. She is active and enjoys running. The practical nurse supports the registered nurse and midwife with counselling and teaching at each visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No safe amount of alcohol in pregnancy is known, so abstaining is advised): No threshold of alcohol use in pregnancy has been shown safe, and the fetal brain is vulnerable throughout, so abstinence is the advice and supportive resources are offered. Describing occasional drinking as proven safe is incorrect. Dividing drinks by type has no support. Harm is not limited to later pregnancy."}, {"id": 1787, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At her second visit Jasmine says she plans to cut down on smoking only in the third trimester, because the baby grows most then. She has tried to quit before and found it very stressful. What should the nurse say to her?", "options": {"A": "Cutting down in the third trimester is reasonable, since early exposure has little effect", "B": "Switching to light cigarettes removes the risk to the fetus, so quitting is optional", "C": "Smoking is less harmful than the stress of quitting, so she can continue as she is", "D": "Quitting early helps most, but stopping at any point helps; offer counselling, support"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing early exposure is harmless", "B": "Believing light cigarettes remove the fetal risk", "C": "Valuing stress over known harm"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C208", "cluster_text": "Jasmine Aubrey, 20, attends a community prenatal clinic, first at 8 weeks and again at later visits. She is underweight, smokes about ten cigarettes a day, and had a few drinks most weekends before she knew she was pregnant. She is active and enjoys running. The practical nurse supports the registered nurse and midwife with counselling and teaching at each visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Quitting early helps most, but stopping at any point helps; offer counselling, support): Smoking reduces oxygen delivery and raises risks throughout pregnancy, so early quitting is best and stopping later still helps. Waiting for the third trimester loses months of benefit. Light cigarettes deliver comparable harmful substances. Stress from quitting is manageable and does not outweigh the risks of smoking."}, {"id": 1788, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Jasmine is underweight at the start of pregnancy and worries about gaining too much. Her friend, who began at a healthy weight, was told to gain about 11 to 16 kg. How should the nurse explain Jasmine's own weight gain target?", "options": {"A": "Everyone follows the same total gain range, so Jasmine's target equals her friend's", "B": "It depends on pre pregnancy body mass index, so underweight women should gain more", "C": "Underweight women should gain less to avoid having a large baby at birth", "D": "Her target is set by the baby's size at each scan, not her body mass index"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying one target to all clients", "C": "Reversing the direction of advice", "D": "Basing the target on scan size"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C208", "cluster_text": "Jasmine Aubrey, 20, attends a community prenatal clinic, first at 8 weeks and again at later visits. She is underweight, smokes about ten cigarettes a day, and had a few drinks most weekends before she knew she was pregnant. She is active and enjoys running. The practical nurse supports the registered nurse and midwife with counselling and teaching at each visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It depends on pre pregnancy body mass index, so underweight women should gain more): Recommended gain is guided by pre pregnancy body mass index, so an underweight woman is advised to gain more to support fetal growth. A single target ignores differences in starting weight. Gaining less would add risk of a small baby. Scan size may prompt review but does not set her individual target."}, {"id": 1789, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At 24 weeks Jasmine says she felt dizzy and lightheaded halfway through a run, her heart raced, and she walked home. She has no pain or bleeding. She asks whether she must stop exercising and whether flying next month is safe. Which response is best?", "options": {"A": "Dizziness while running is normal, so she can resume her usual pace and fly", "B": "All exercise should stop now, and she should avoid flying for the rest of the pregnancy", "C": "Moderate exercise is usually safe; advise slowing down, fluids, and review of dizziness and flight plans", "D": "She may keep running but should raise her heart rate higher to improve fitness"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismissing a warning symptom", "B": "Prohibits all exercise and flying without a clinical reason", "D": "Advises higher intensity despite a warning symptom"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C208", "cluster_text": "Jasmine Aubrey, 20, attends a community prenatal clinic, first at 8 weeks and again at later visits. She is underweight, smokes about ten cigarettes a day, and had a few drinks most weekends before she knew she was pregnant. She is active and enjoys running. The practical nurse supports the registered nurse and midwife with counselling and teaching at each visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Moderate exercise is usually safe; advise slowing down, fluids, and review of dizziness and flight plans): Healthy pregnant women are generally encouraged to exercise at a moderate level, and dizziness is a prompt to slow down, hydrate and review with the midwife, who can also advise about travel. Stopping everything is unnecessary. Calling dizziness normal ignores a symptom needing review. Higher intensity increases strain without a stated benefit."}, {"id": 1790, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Medical assistance in dying is requested by a 70-year-old client in palliative care, and the practical nurse holds a personal conscientious objection. The option is lawful in Canada, and the client's pain is currently well controlled. Which response best reflects the nurse's professional obligation at the principle level?", "options": {"A": "Explain that nurses cannot take part in any way, so the client must find information independently", "B": "Share her objection with the client and suggest he choose another facility", "C": "Refer him to an outside advocacy group but leave the team uninformed", "D": "Avoid imposing personal views, and notify the team so another provider can respond and care continues"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing nurses are barred from any role in lawful assisted dying", "B": "Shifting the search for care onto the client", "C": "Withholding information from the team"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoid imposing personal views, and notify the team so another provider can respond and care continues): A conscientious objection protects the nurse from participating, but the client still has a right to information and uninterrupted care. Notifying the team lets another provider respond without delay or judgment. Sharing her objection and sending him elsewhere puts the burden of finding care on the client. Referring him outside while leaving the team uninformed breaks continuity. Saying nurses cannot take part in any way is inaccurate. Remember that an objection does not remove the duty to arrange continuity of care."}, {"id": 1791, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two hours after a forearm cast was applied, a 24-year-old tells the practical nurse about tingling in the fingers. The hand looks slightly swollen, and pain is 3 out of 10. Which assessment best identifies whether circulation and nerve function are compromised?", "options": {"A": "Check the radial pulse strength and rate in the casted arm and document the result", "B": "Measure the swelling by comparing finger circumference on both hands and elevate the arm", "C": "Compare colour, warmth, capillary refill, movement, sensation and pulse with the other hand", "D": "Ask the client to rate the pain and give the prescribed analgesic before further checks"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on a single pulse check to judge limb perfusion", "B": "Measuring swelling alone and skipping nerve and perfusion signs", "D": "Treating pain first and delaying the circulation assessment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Compare colour, warmth, capillary refill, movement, sensation and pulse with the other hand): Tingling and swelling under a new cast can signal impaired circulation or nerve compression, so a full neurovascular comparison with the other hand is needed. A pulse alone can stay present while tissue perfusion and nerve function fail. Giving an analgesic first delays recognition of a limb-threatening problem. Findings that differ from the other hand are then reported promptly to the RN or physician."}, {"id": 1792, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Fraser says, \"I'm just tired of all of this. I don't think I want to go on.\" She looks tearful while the nurse sets out her medication organizer. The daily weight is stable and her breathing is comfortable. Which response by the practical nurse is best?", "options": {"A": "Acknowledge her exhaustion and ask directly if she is thinking about ending her life", "B": "Document the comment and ask her family at the next visit whether she seems low", "C": "Reassure her that many people with heart failure feel this way and it passes with time", "D": "Suggest she focus on the positive parts of her week, then review the medication organizer"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delaying action and relying on family instead of asking the client", "C": "Offering false reassurance instead of exploring the statement", "D": "Redirecting to positives and avoiding the hard feeling"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C213", "cluster_text": "Helen Fraser is a 78-year-old widow who lives alone and receives weekly home care visits. She has heart failure, osteoarthritis in both knees, and long-standing fatigue. Her daughter lives two hours away and phones on Sundays. Over several visits the practical nurse has noticed that Mrs. Fraser eats little, stays in her recliner most days, and speaks less about her garden than she used to.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acknowledge her exhaustion and ask directly if she is thinking about ending her life): A statement of not wanting to go on needs a direct, caring question about suicidal thoughts, which does not raise risk and opens the way to safety planning. Reassurance and changing the subject close the conversation before risk is understood. Waiting for the next visit delays a possible urgent assessment. The nurse then reports to the RN or physician and follows the agency's safety process."}, {"id": 1793, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Fraser says she is exhausted by midafternoon and has stopped cooking, so she lies down most of the day. Her weight and breathing are stable. She asks what she can do to feel less tired. What advice should the practical nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Keep to her usual routine and push through the fatigue to prevent further loss of strength", "B": "Plan tasks for her best time of day and alternate short tasks with rest periods", "C": "Rest in bed through the afternoon until the fatigue passes so her heart is not strained", "D": "Complete all household tasks in the morning in one stretch to leave the afternoon free"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Encouraging her to push through fatigue without pacing", "C": "Advising prolonged bed rest, which worsens deconditioning", "D": "Grouping all tasks together, which exhausts energy reserves"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C213", "cluster_text": "Helen Fraser is a 78-year-old widow who lives alone and receives weekly home care visits. She has heart failure, osteoarthritis in both knees, and long-standing fatigue. Her daughter lives two hours away and phones on Sundays. Over several visits the practical nurse has noticed that Mrs. Fraser eats little, stays in her recliner most days, and speaks less about her garden than she used to.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Plan tasks for her best time of day and alternate short tasks with rest periods): Energy conservation means pacing, prioritizing, and sitting for tasks so that limited energy lasts through the day. Long bed rest worsens weakness and fatigue, and doing everything in one stretch depletes reserves quickly. Pushing through fatigue ignores the need for planned rest. Teach planned rest periods, not long inactivity."}, {"id": 1794, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Fraser reports that her knees feel stiff and painful when she first gets up, and that she has been staying seated to protect them. The joints are not red or warm today. Which teaching by the practical nurse best matches current arthritis care?", "options": {"A": "Rest the knees as much as possible until the stiffness resolves, then restart walking slowly", "B": "Limit movement to swollen days and sit on all other days to save the joints", "C": "Walk long distances daily to push through stiffness, since pain means the joint is loosening", "D": "Do gentle range of motion and short daily walks, resting when the joints feel sore"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Advising prolonged rest, which increases stiffness and weakness", "B": "Misunderstanding that movement helps even on days without swelling", "C": "Encouraging pain-pushing activity that can overload inflamed joints"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C213", "cluster_text": "Helen Fraser is a 78-year-old widow who lives alone and receives weekly home care visits. She has heart failure, osteoarthritis in both knees, and long-standing fatigue. Her daughter lives two hours away and phones on Sundays. Over several visits the practical nurse has noticed that Mrs. Fraser eats little, stays in her recliner most days, and speaks less about her garden than she used to.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Do gentle range of motion and short daily walks, resting when the joints feel sore): Regular gentle movement keeps joints flexible and muscles strong, and rest is used in short breaks during soreness. Prolonged rest increases stiffness and weakens the muscles that protect the joint. Pushing through pain during long walks can aggravate the joint. Limiting movement to swelling days misses the benefit of daily exercise."}, {"id": 1795, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two days later, Mrs. Fraser's right knee is swollen, warm and tender after she did extra gardening. She asks about her heating pad. The practical nurse notes the skin is intact and sensation is normal. Which advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Alternate heat and cold every few minutes and keep the knee fully straight to avoid stiffness", "B": "Apply a cold pack in a cloth for short periods and report the flare", "C": "Apply the heating pad for twenty minutes to loosen the joint and reduce the swelling", "D": "Wrap the knee tightly with a warm towel overnight so the heat can relieve the stiffness"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Alternating temperatures without a clear plan for an acute flare", "C": "Using heat on an acutely swollen warm joint", "D": "Applying prolonged heat that can increase swelling and risk burns"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C213", "cluster_text": "Helen Fraser is a 78-year-old widow who lives alone and receives weekly home care visits. She has heart failure, osteoarthritis in both knees, and long-standing fatigue. Her daughter lives two hours away and phones on Sundays. Over several visits the practical nurse has noticed that Mrs. Fraser eats little, stays in her recliner most days, and speaks less about her garden than she used to.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Apply a cold pack in a cloth for short periods and report the flare): A warm, swollen joint indicates an acute flare, and short applications of cold reduce swelling and pain. Heat suits stiff, non-inflamed joints and can increase swelling when inflammation is active. Overnight heat also risks burns, especially in an older adult. Report the flare to the RN or physician so treatment can be reviewed."}, {"id": 1796, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Chronic lung disease affects a 78-year-old client who asks whether influenza and pneumococcal vaccines are wise, because a neighbour said last year's flu shot gave her the flu. The client has no fever today. What should the practical nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Vaccines contain live virus, so a mild illness is expected, and it means protection is working", "B": "Vaccination is advised only after a hospital stay for pneumonia, so wait until that happens", "C": "Inactivated vaccines cannot cause the infection, and protection lowers serious complications", "D": "The two vaccines should be separated by several months because they weaken each other's response"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing the vaccine causes the illness it prevents", "B": "Delaying vaccination until after a complication occurs", "D": "Assuming the vaccines interfere with each other when given together"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Inactivated vaccines cannot cause the infection, and protection lowers serious complications): The injectable influenza vaccine uses inactivated virus and cannot cause influenza, although mild arm soreness or tiredness may occur for a day or two. Vaccination is most valuable before illness, particularly for chronic lung disease. Waiting for a hospital stay defeats the purpose. Provincial schedules can differ in detail, so the nurse also checks the local immunization program."}, {"id": 1797, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "After an unsuccessful resuscitation on a medical unit, a practical nurse notices two colleagues who took part in the code are quiet and avoiding conversation. The unit is now busy with admissions. What action best supports the team and safety culture?", "options": {"A": "Suggest the manager hold a brief debrief soon, covering care delivery and how staff are coping", "B": "Tell the colleagues that the outcome was not their fault and that they should move on", "C": "Wait a week to see whether colleagues raise concerns on their own, then arrange a meeting", "D": "Focus the debrief on identifying which team member made errors so they can be corrected"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Dismissing feelings with reassurance instead of structured support", "C": "Delaying the debrief until distress is visible", "D": "Turning a learning discussion into a blame exercise"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Suggest the manager hold a brief debrief soon, covering care delivery and how staff are coping): A prompt, structured debrief lets staff review the event, learn from it, and share the emotional strain. Delay lets distress build and learning fade. Telling colleagues to move on shuts down their feelings, and a blame focus damages trust and reporting. A good debrief examines systems and teamwork, not individual fault."}, {"id": 1798, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Hospital admission has left a 60-year-old blind client needing to walk to the bathroom. The practical nurse has introduced herself and explained the room layout. The hallway is clear, and the client uses no cane today. Which technique is most appropriate for guiding the client?", "options": {"A": "Offer your arm above the elbow and walk half a step ahead", "B": "Offer your arm and walk one step behind him", "C": "Place a hand on the client's shoulder and walk beside them while giving brief directions", "D": "Take his elbow and walk beside him, naming obstacles after he passes"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Walking behind so he cannot follow her movement", "C": "Guiding at the shoulder, which gives poor feedback on direction", "D": "Naming obstacles after he has passed them"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Offer your arm above the elbow and walk half a step ahead): Offering an arm lets the client follow the nurse's body movements and keep control of the pace. Walking behind him removes the cue of her movement. Holding his elbow and naming obstacles after he passes them gives warning too late. Guiding at the shoulder gives poor feedback on direction. Describe doors, turns and obstacles as they come. Orient the client to the room layout beforehand."}, {"id": 1799, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Morning medications are being prepared by a long-term care nurse for an older adult who takes an antacid, levothyroxine and an iron supplement. The pharmacy label for the antacid advises spacing from other drugs, and two other residents are waiting. How should the practical nurse proceed?", "options": {"A": "Give the antacid first and the other two drugs five minutes later as usual", "B": "Give all three together with one glass of water to save morning round time", "C": "Check the schedule and pharmacist guidance, and space the antacid from the other drugs", "D": "Skip the antacid today and let the next shift decide whether to give it"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a short interval that may not prevent binding", "B": "Giving interacting drugs together for efficiency", "D": "Omitting a medication without reporting or consulting"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check the schedule and pharmacist guidance, and space the antacid from the other drugs): Antacids can bind other drugs and reduce their absorption, so administration times must be separated using the pharmacy guidance and the facility schedule. Giving everything together saves time but can lower the effect of levothyroxine and iron. A five-minute gap is usually too short. Omitting a dose without telling anyone is a medication error risk."}, {"id": 1800, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "While preparing a morning insulin dose for a long-term care resident, a practical nurse notices that the vial label differs from the order. She catches it before giving the dose, replaces the vial, and gives the correct insulin. The resident is well. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Make no report because the resident was not harmed and the error was corrected", "B": "Document the event in the resident's chart and omit the incident report form", "C": "Discuss it informally with the pharmacy assistant and avoid involving other staff", "D": "Complete a near miss report and tell the RN so the system cause can be reviewed"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a caught error as nothing worth reporting", "B": "Recording it only in the chart and skipping the incident system", "C": "Handling it informally so the system never learns of the cause"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Complete a near miss report and tell the RN so the system cause can be reviewed): Near misses reveal weaknesses in the process and should be reported so that the cause can be corrected before harm occurs. Silence because there was no harm wastes the chance to prevent a later error. Charting an incident in the client record alone does not trigger system review. A just culture treats reporting as learning, not punishment."}, {"id": 1801, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Hard of hearing, a 79-year-old client at a clinic keeps asking the practical nurse to repeat questions. The waiting room is busy, a television is playing in the corner, and the client wears no hearing aid today. Which approach to communication is best?", "options": {"A": "Keep the busy room and speak slowly with exaggerated lip movements", "B": "Speak at a loud level directly into the client's better ear from a very close distance", "C": "Turn off the television and ask him to read her lips", "D": "Move somewhere quiet, face the client at eye level, and speak clearly at normal volume"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Exaggerating mouth movements in a noisy room", "B": "Shouting, which distorts speech and offends the client", "C": "Relying on lip reading in remaining noise"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Move somewhere quiet, face the client at eye level, and speak clearly at normal volume): Reducing background noise and facing the client lets them use lip reading and facial cues, and clear speech at normal volume carries best. Exaggerated lip movements distort speech and are hard to read, and the busy room remains. Switching off the television leaves other noise and relies on lip reading alone. Shouting into one ear distorts sounds and may feel disrespectful."}, {"id": 1802, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Anxiety about an MRI leaves a 45-year-old woman unsure what will happen. The practical nurse has already reviewed the consent form with her. She keeps nodding but asks very few questions, and the technologist is running ten minutes late. What action best supports her?", "options": {"A": "Give a detailed technical description of the scanner, then ask whether she has questions", "B": "Tell her the test is routine and quick so there is no reason to feel worried", "C": "Explain the steps in plain language and have her restate them", "D": "Hand her a brochure and let her read it quietly before the technologist arrives"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using technical terms without checking understanding", "B": "Minimizing concern instead of addressing it", "D": "Substituting a pamphlet for conversation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain the steps in plain language and have her restate them): Plain language with teach-back confirms that the client truly understands, which a nod alone does not. Technical detail can confuse an anxious person. Telling her not to worry dismisses her feelings, and a brochure alone does not allow questions. Consent requires understanding, so the nurse reports any gaps to the person obtaining consent."}, {"id": 1803, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Fear that activity causes harm has led an adult with persistent back pain to stop walking and gardening, and a home care nurse visits. The client's neurological checks are normal, and the pain has lasted over a year. Which teaching supports self-management best?", "options": {"A": "Encourage the client to push through all pain levels to regain strength quickly", "B": "Advise staying in bed or sitting until the pain ends, then resume walking gradually and carefully", "C": "Encourage gradual, paced activity with scheduled rests, and set small goals together with the client", "D": "Stress that nothing can be done and encourage acceptance of the activity limits"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pushing through pain, which can flare symptoms and reduce trust", "B": "Withholding activity, which deepens deconditioning and fear", "D": "Conveying hopelessness and ending self-management efforts"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Encourage gradual, paced activity with scheduled rests, and set small goals together with the client): Chronic pain self-management uses paced, graded activity and shared goals to rebuild confidence and function. Prolonged rest leads to weakness and more pain. Forcing activity through severe pain risks flare-ups, and telling the client nothing can be done removes hope. Report new numbness, weakness or bowel changes to the RN promptly."}, {"id": 1804, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Next month, a hospital unit faces an accreditation survey, and a coworker asks a practical nurse whether it matters to frontline staff, since administrators seem to handle it. Which statement best describes the nurse's role in accreditation and quality standards?", "options": {"A": "Frontline nurses apply the standards daily through safe practice, documentation, and speaking up", "B": "Nurses should review policies before the survey, then follow routine habits", "C": "Surveyors judge only written policies, so documentation matters more than direct client care", "D": "Only unit leaders need to know the standards, while nurses concentrate on tasks"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating standards as a pre-survey exercise", "C": "Believing policy documents matter more than actual practice", "D": "Leaving standards knowledge to leaders alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Frontline nurses apply the standards daily through safe practice, documentation, and speaking up): Accreditation standards are met through everyday care, and surveyors observe practice and speak to staff and clients. Reviewing policies before a survey and then following habits misses that standards apply daily. Leaving knowledge of the standards to unit leaders ignores that safety and quality are lived by frontline nurses. Written policy without matching practice does not satisfy surveyors."}, {"id": 1805, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A practical nurse at a clinic has a personal moral objection to a procedure scheduled for a client today. No other staff member on the shift is trained in the procedure. The client is stable and has arrived on time. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with the procedure while telling the client of the objection after it is finished", "B": "Tell the manager promptly, explain the objection, and help arrange another provider", "C": "Tell a coworker privately and trade tasks without informing the manager", "D": "Tell the client of the objection and suggest she reschedule elsewhere"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Acting against own conscience and disclosing too late", "C": "Arranging coverage informally without informing the employer", "D": "Redirecting the client instead of arranging care"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tell the manager promptly, explain the objection, and help arrange another provider): A nurse may object on conscience grounds, but the obligation is to notify the employer early and make sure the client's care continues without delay or judgment. Trading tasks informally leaves the employer unaware of a gap in coverage. Sending the client elsewhere shifts the burden to her. Proceeding against conscience is not required. Principles are national, and the regulator's standard for conscientious objection applies."}, {"id": 1806, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a community clinic, the mother of a 6-month-old plans to start cow's milk as the main drink because it is cheaper than formula. The infant is breastfed, growing well, and has begun tasting pureed vegetables. What should the practical nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Offer iron-rich solids but replace milk feeds with water between meals", "B": "Add a vitamin D drop and delay iron-rich solids until age one", "C": "Continue breast milk or formula as the main drink, adding iron-rich solids", "D": "Switch to whole cow's milk in a bottle now since the infant is growing well"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Replacing milk feeds with water", "B": "Delaying iron-rich solids despite supplementing", "D": "Starting cow's milk as a drink before the age of nine to twelve months"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue breast milk or formula as the main drink, adding iron-rich solids): Breast milk or formula remains the main drink until at least nine to twelve months, and iron-rich solids begin around six months. Cow's milk as a main drink is low in iron and can strain the infant's kidneys. Replacing milk feeds with water lowers calories and nutrients. Delaying iron-rich solids misses a window for iron and feeding skills, even with a vitamin drop."}, {"id": 1807, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A practical nurse in a long-term care home smells smoke coming from a resident's room, and the door is closed. Two residents are in the hallway. After checking that no resident is in immediate danger, what should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Move the two hallway residents to the dining room and then finish the medication round", "B": "Open the door to find the source and use an extinguisher before telling anyone", "C": "Activate the alarm and call for help, then close the door and prepare to evacuate", "D": "Wait for the alarm system to detect smoke before taking any action"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Continuing routine tasks before responding", "B": "Fighting the fire before alerting others", "D": "Waiting for automatic detection instead of acting"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Activate the alarm and call for help, then close the door and prepare to evacuate): The RACE sequence is rescue, alarm, contain, and extinguish or evacuate, so raising the alarm and closing the door come before fighting a fire. Opening the door feeds the fire with oxygen and delays alerting others. Finishing routine tasks delays the response. Staff must follow the facility's emergency plan and practise drills."}, {"id": 1808, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Unsteady on her feet, an 81-year-old shares a room on a long-term care unit. She wakes at night to use the bathroom, rarely uses the call bell, and gets up without help. Which action is most appropriate to reduce her fall risk?", "options": {"A": "Ask her to remain in bed until staff can do rounds after breakfast.", "B": "Raise all four side rails to keep her in bed during the night", "C": "Keep the room dark so that the other resident is not disturbed during the night", "D": "Keep the bed low, place the call bell in reach, and light the path"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Telling a mobile client not to get up without offering a safe option", "B": "Using full rails, which is a restraint and raises injury risk", "C": "Removing lighting, which increases tripping risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep the bed low, place the call bell in reach, and light the path): A low bed, reachable call bell and a dim path light support safe toileting and reduce falls. Raising all four rails restrains her and can cause climbing injuries. Darkness hides hazards, and instructing her to stay in bed without a plan for toileting is unrealistic. Scheduled toileting rounds are also helpful."}, {"id": 1809, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Hearing aids sit behind the ears of an 83-year-old resident in long-term care who responds poorly to questions this morning. He ate breakfast normally and is alert. The practical nurse suspects the devices are not working. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Check that each aid is on, the battery works, and the tube is free of wax", "B": "Rinse both hearing aids under running water to clear any wax before use", "C": "Replace the batteries in both aids and reassess without checking placement or wax", "D": "Switch off the aids to save battery and speak louder during care"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Cleaning electronic devices with water", "C": "Checking only the battery and skipping placement", "D": "Turning off the aids and raising voice instead"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check that each aid is on, the battery works, and the tube is free of wax): Hearing aids need regular checks of placement, battery and wax, using a soft cloth or brush made for the device. Water damages the electronics. Replacing batteries alone skips placement and wax, which are common causes. Switching the aids off and speaking louder removes the device that helps him hear. Report persistent problems so an audiologist can service the device."}, {"id": 1810, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Maya says she is active and wants to continue her daily power walks and a prenatal yoga class. She is healthy, feels well, and the baby is moving normally. What advice from the practical nurse is most appropriate at this visit?", "options": {"A": "Choose only exercises in the supine position so she can rest between movements and conserve energy", "B": "Continue current high intensity workouts and increase them to build stamina for labour", "C": "Stop all exercise now to avoid any risk of early labour or injury to the baby", "D": "Continue moderate activity, stay hydrated, avoid overheating or lying flat, stop for warning signs"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Recommending supine exercise that can reduce blood return", "B": "Encouraging intense training without regard for the pregnancy", "C": "Prohibiting exercise in an uncomplicated pregnancy"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C225", "cluster_text": "Maya Whitehorse is a 29-year-old Cree woman expecting her first baby. She attends a prenatal clinic at 24 weeks, accompanied by her aunt, and has a normal blood pressure and fetal growth. She works as a teacher, walks daily, and is curious about what to expect. The practical nurse will see her again in the third trimester and during early labour planning.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue moderate activity, stay hydrated, avoid overheating or lying flat, stop for warning signs): In an uncomplicated pregnancy, regular moderate activity is encouraged, with hydration and avoidance of overheating or prolonged supine position. Prohibiting exercise removes benefits for mood and fitness. Intense escalation increases strain, and supine exercise can compress the vena cava later in pregnancy. Bleeding, dizziness or chest pain are warning signs to stop and call."}, {"id": 1811, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Now at 34 weeks, Maya complains of heartburn at night and leg cramps. Her blood pressure is 134/86 mmHg, higher than her earlier readings. She asks about taking the antacid she uses for heartburn, which is high in sodium. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Tell her to raise her pillows and avoid fluids after supper, then recheck pressure next visit", "B": "Report the blood pressure change and ask the provider before she uses the high-sodium antacid", "C": "Advise her to take the antacid as directed since heartburn is a common pregnancy discomfort", "D": "Reassure her that the pressure is normal for late pregnancy and note the readings"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating the symptom and delaying review of a rising reading", "C": "Recommending a sodium-rich antacid without checking blood pressure", "D": "Calling a rising blood pressure normal without assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C225", "cluster_text": "Maya Whitehorse is a 29-year-old Cree woman expecting her first baby. She attends a prenatal clinic at 24 weeks, accompanied by her aunt, and has a normal blood pressure and fetal growth. She works as a teacher, walks daily, and is curious about what to expect. The practical nurse will see her again in the third trimester and during early labour planning.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the blood pressure change and ask the provider before she uses the high-sodium antacid): A blood pressure that is higher than her baseline needs to be reported to rule out hypertension of pregnancy, and a sodium-heavy antacid could add to the problem. Treating heartburn alone misses the changing reading. Calling it normal without assessment risks a missed complication. Positional measures can still be taught after the provider is informed."}, {"id": 1812, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Maya's screening test result for a chromosomal condition came back positive, and she is anxious. She asks if it means the baby definitely has the condition. The practical nurse knows that further testing is usually offered. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Explain that screening estimates chance, not certainty, and diagnostic options can be discussed", "B": "Tell her screening tests are highly accurate, so she does not need further testing or information", "C": "Suggest she wait until birth to know for sure, rather than discussing diagnostic testing options", "D": "Say the result confirms the condition and that she should plan for special care"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Overstating accuracy so follow-up appears unnecessary", "C": "Discouraging information-seeking and delaying decision making", "D": "Treating a screening result as a diagnosis"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C225", "cluster_text": "Maya Whitehorse is a 29-year-old Cree woman expecting her first baby. She attends a prenatal clinic at 24 weeks, accompanied by her aunt, and has a normal blood pressure and fetal growth. She works as a teacher, walks daily, and is curious about what to expect. The practical nurse will see her again in the third trimester and during early labour planning.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that screening estimates chance, not certainty, and diagnostic options can be discussed): Screening tests estimate probability and do not confirm a diagnosis, so follow-up diagnostic testing may be offered. Calling the result confirmed alarms her wrongly. Saying more information is unnecessary ignores her right to choose, and advising her to wait removes time-sensitive options. The nurse supports understanding, within scope, and refers questions on interpretation to the provider."}, {"id": 1813, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During labour planning, Maya asks if her aunt may smudge and drum quietly in the room during the birth. The unit policy restricts open flame. Maya is calm, the pregnancy is uncomplicated, and the charge nurse is available. Which response best reflects cultural safety?", "options": {"A": "Acknowledge the importance of her practices and work with the team on a safe way", "B": "Explain that the policy forbids it and tell her the rule cannot be discussed any further", "C": "Suggest that she leave her cultural practices out of the hospital to avoid problems", "D": "Allow open smudging in the room without telling the charge nurse to avoid conflict"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Insisting policy overrides her preferences without discussion", "C": "Discouraging cultural practices that matter to her", "D": "Ignoring safety policy by acting unilaterally"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C225", "cluster_text": "Maya Whitehorse is a 29-year-old Cree woman expecting her first baby. She attends a prenatal clinic at 24 weeks, accompanied by her aunt, and has a normal blood pressure and fetal growth. She works as a teacher, walks daily, and is curious about what to expect. The practical nurse will see her again in the third trimester and during early labour planning.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acknowledge the importance of her practices and work with the team on a safe way): Cultural safety means respecting the client's practices and problem-solving with the team, such as using a safe alternative to open flame, drumming and a quiet space. Ending the discussion with policy alone harms trust. Bypassing the charge nurse creates a fire safety risk. Telling her to leave her culture out disregards her identity and dignity."}, {"id": 1814, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Muffled hearing for several weeks brings a 62-year-old client to a clinic, and the nurse sees impacted earwax. The client says he usually uses cotton swabs at home, has no ear pain, and asks whether he should continue. What teaching is best?", "options": {"A": "Use ear candles at home, since warm suction pulls wax out", "B": "Avoid inserting anything into the ear canal, and use softening drops or request irrigation", "C": "Continue using cotton swabs gently to remove the wax from the canal each evening", "D": "Use a rolled tissue corner to remove the wax gently from the canal"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using ear candles, which burn and fail to remove wax", "C": "Using a cotton swab, which packs wax deeper", "D": "Inserting a tissue into the canal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Avoid inserting anything into the ear canal, and use softening drops or request irrigation): Cotton swabs push wax deeper and can injure the canal or eardrum. Softening drops or clinic irrigation, where ordered and the eardrum is intact, are safer. Ear candles do not remove wax and can burn the ear. Tissue corners or other objects in the canal pack wax and risk injury. Impacted wax that causes hearing loss can be treated, so leaving it in place is not advised."}, {"id": 1815, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Biopsy is planned for a 66-year-old on a medical unit, and the practical nurse is asked to have the client sign the consent form. The client says, \"I don't really know what they'll do.\" The physician has left the unit. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Tell the client the biopsy is simple and that most people sign without further questions", "B": "Ask the client to sign now and explain the procedure after the physician returns", "C": "Explain the procedure and risks in detail yourself, then witness the client's signature", "D": "Hold the form and ask the physician to explain before signing"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Downplaying the procedure to speed up consent", "B": "Obtaining a signature without explanation of risks", "C": "Providing the full consent discussion beyond the nurse's role"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the form and ask the physician to explain before signing): Informed consent requires that the person performing the procedure explains the nature, risks and alternatives, and that the client understands. Signing first reverses this order. A nurse may witness a signature but cannot replace the provider's explanation. Minimizing the procedure undermines informed choice. The nurse advocates for the client by contacting the physician."}, {"id": 1816, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ear pulling, a temperature of 38.9 C and irritability bring a 2-year-old to a clinic. The parent mentions the child recently had a cold and asks whether the child's hearing will be affected. What information should the practical nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Hearing is not affected by ear infections, so no follow-up is required", "B": "Hearing loss is permanent after an ear infection, so early planning for support is needed", "C": "Fluid behind the eardrum can temporarily reduce hearing, so follow-up care and hearing checks are important", "D": "Ear pulling alone confirms an infection and means antibiotics are needed right away"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Expecting hearing to be unaffected", "B": "Overstating permanent loss and causing needless alarm", "D": "Equating a symptom with a definite diagnosis and treatment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fluid behind the eardrum can temporarily reduce hearing, so follow-up care and hearing checks are important): Otitis media often leaves fluid behind the eardrum that muffles hearing for a time, so follow-up checks are useful. Most hearing loss from a single infection is temporary. Ear pulling can occur for other reasons, and the provider decides whether antibiotics are needed. The nurse reports the fever and symptoms to the RN or provider."}, {"id": 1817, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 38-year-old client with a known latex allergy is scheduled for surgery tomorrow morning. The perioperative nurse notices a note that powder-free latex gloves will be used by the surgical team, and the client's allergy band is in place. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Wait until the client is in the operating room and then change the gloves", "B": "Alert the surgical team so a latex-free environment is arranged before surgery", "C": "Accept that powder-free latex gloves are acceptable because they release fewer allergens", "D": "Ask the client to wear a medical alert bracelet and proceed with the plan"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying action until the client is already in the operating room", "C": "Believing powder-free latex is safe for an allergic client", "D": "Relying on an alert bracelet instead of changing the environment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Alert the surgical team so a latex-free environment is arranged before surgery): A latex allergy requires a latex-free environment, since even powder-free latex carries allergens that can trigger a reaction. A bracelet identifies the allergy but does not remove exposure. Waiting until the client is in the operating room delays preparation and increases risk. Communication before surgery lets staff stock latex-free supplies and schedule the case appropriately."}, {"id": 1818, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home care visit, the nurse finds a client who took too many tablets from the medicine cupboard but is awake and speaking. The container shows an unknown quantity, and no symptoms are present yet. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Contact the poison control centre or emergency services right away and follow the instructions given", "B": "Watch the client for several hours and call only if symptoms begin to develop", "C": "Give activated charcoal from the home first aid kit and then observe him", "D": "Call his family doctor in the morning and keep the container nearby"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Waiting for symptoms before getting help", "C": "Giving a home treatment without poison control advice", "D": "Delaying advice for an unknown ingestion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Contact the poison control centre or emergency services right away and follow the instructions given): Poison control can advise on the specific substance and amount, and early action prevents harm before symptoms appear. Waiting risks delayed treatment, since some poisons act slowly. Giving charcoal without direction can cause harm and delays expert advice. Waiting until morning leaves an unknown ingestion untreated overnight. Bring the container to the emergency department if instructed."}, {"id": 1819, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Daniel's doctor prescribed a topical corticosteroid cream for eczema on his forearms. He asks the practical nurse how to apply it at home. He mentions that he usually rubs it in hard until the skin looks shiny. What advice should the nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Apply a thin layer three times daily and continue after the rash clears", "B": "Rub a thick layer in firmly and wrap both arms in a bandage to speed absorption", "C": "Wash hands, apply a thin layer with a glove, and spread gently on affected skin", "D": "Apply a thin layer to his forearms and his son's eczema too"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Overusing a steroid beyond the prescription", "B": "Applying thickly and rubbing hard, which irritates skin", "D": "Sharing a prescription cream with another person"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C223", "cluster_text": "Daniel Moreau is a 34-year-old dental assistant who visits a family clinic with a rash on both forearms. He has a long history of eczema and recently started using new disposable gloves at work. His five-year-old son also has an eczema flare, and his father has scaly plaques on the elbows that Daniel worries are contagious. The practical nurse will assess and teach.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wash hands, apply a thin layer with a glove, and spread gently on affected skin): A thin layer on affected areas with gentle spreading limits side effects such as skin thinning. Continuing after the rash clears and extra daily applications raise the risk of thinning. Rubbing hard irritates inflamed skin, and a thick layer under a wrap raises absorption. A prescribed cream is for the client named on the label, so sharing it with his son is unsafe. Gloves or hand washing prevent unintended spread of the medicated cream to other sites."}, {"id": 1820, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Daniel's rash has worsened since he began using the new gloves at work, with redness and blisters on the hands and wrists. Symptoms ease on days off, and he has no fever or open sores. What is the most appropriate nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Advise him to switch to a different cream and keep wearing the same gloves", "B": "Report the pattern to the provider and advise avoiding suspected latex exposure while treatment is reviewed", "C": "Reassure him that eczema often worsens in winter and that it should settle on its own", "D": "Tell him to continue wearing the gloves and apply the cream more often"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing the treatment without removing the trigger", "C": "Attributing symptoms to season without assessing", "D": "Continuing the exposure while treating the skin"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C223", "cluster_text": "Daniel Moreau is a 34-year-old dental assistant who visits a family clinic with a rash on both forearms. He has a long history of eczema and recently started using new disposable gloves at work. His five-year-old son also has an eczema flare, and his father has scaly plaques on the elbows that Daniel worries are contagious. The practical nurse will assess and teach.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the pattern to the provider and advise avoiding suspected latex exposure while treatment is reviewed): Improvement on days off with worsening after new gloves suggests allergic contact dermatitis, and removing the trigger is the key step. Continued exposure keeps the rash active despite creams. Blaming winter overlooks the clear pattern. The provider confirms the cause and orders testing or alternatives. Latex-free options are usually available."}, {"id": 1821, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Daniel's 5-year-old son has itchy, dry, red patches on his elbows and knees, and Daniel asks how often to bathe him. The boy currently takes long hot baths with bubble bath each evening before bed. Which teaching is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply moisturizer only after the skin has fully dried for an hour or more", "B": "Avoid bathing for several days so the skin can recover from the water", "C": "Give a hot bath twice daily with scented soap to keep the skin clean", "D": "Use short lukewarm baths with fragrance-free cleanser, then moisturize soon after drying"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying moisturizer, losing the benefit of trapped water", "B": "Avoiding baths, which allows dryness and crusting", "C": "Recommending frequent hot baths with scented soap"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C223", "cluster_text": "Daniel Moreau is a 34-year-old dental assistant who visits a family clinic with a rash on both forearms. He has a long history of eczema and recently started using new disposable gloves at work. His five-year-old son also has an eczema flare, and his father has scaly plaques on the elbows that Daniel worries are contagious. The practical nurse will assess and teach.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use short lukewarm baths with fragrance-free cleanser, then moisturize soon after drying): Short lukewarm baths and fragrance-free products reduce irritation, and moisturizer applied soon after bathing traps water in the skin. Hot water and scent strip oils and worsen itching. Avoiding baths leaves dryness and crusting, and delayed moisturizer loses the benefit. Report signs of infection such as weeping or yellow crusts."}, {"id": 1822, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Daniel's father has scaly plaques on his elbows, and Daniel asks whether he can catch it. He also asks whether his son should stay away from his grandfather during visits this winter. The nurse knows the plaques were diagnosed years ago. What should the practical nurse teach?", "options": {"A": "Psoriasis is not contagious, and skin care and provider follow-up can help manage plaques", "B": "Psoriasis spreads through shared towels, so his father should use separate linens", "C": "Psoriasis spreads by touch, so Daniel's father should avoid physical contact with his grandson", "D": "Psoriasis is caused by dry skin, so moisturizer alone will clear the plaques"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believing psoriasis spreads through shared linens", "C": "Believing psoriasis is contagious", "D": "Treating an immune disease as simple dry skin"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C223", "cluster_text": "Daniel Moreau is a 34-year-old dental assistant who visits a family clinic with a rash on both forearms. He has a long history of eczema and recently started using new disposable gloves at work. His five-year-old son also has an eczema flare, and his father has scaly plaques on the elbows that Daniel worries are contagious. The practical nurse will assess and teach.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Psoriasis is not contagious, and skin care and provider follow-up can help manage plaques): Psoriasis is a chronic immune-mediated skin disease and cannot be spread by contact or by shared linens. Separate towels and avoiding family contact are unnecessary and isolating. It is not caused by dry skin, and moisturizer alone does not clear plaques. The condition is managed over time, with moisturizing, medication and review by the provider."}, {"id": 1823, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Potassium of 6.4 mmol/L appears on the laboratory result for a 70-year-old on a medical unit, and the client asks about the results. The practical nurse is unsure of the usual plan and the RN is in a meeting. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Report the result promptly to the RN or physician first", "B": "Tell the client the result and explain what it might mean, then notify the RN", "C": "Record the value in the chart and wait for the provider to review it later", "D": "Recheck the sample at the next scheduled draw time and document the result"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Telling the client the result before reporting", "C": "Documenting without reporting a critical value", "D": "Delaying action by waiting for the next scheduled draw"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the result promptly to the RN or physician first): A potassium of 6.4 mmol/L is a critical value that needs prompt reporting, since it can cause dangerous heart rhythm changes. Interpreting results to the client is not the first step and belongs with the provider. Waiting for a later draw or later review delays treatment. Reporting first allows timely orders and monitoring."}, {"id": 1824, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A practical nurse visits a 70-year-old on a home oxygen concentrator. The client says he sometimes smokes outdoors while still wearing the tubing, because he stays far away from the tank. His breathing is stable today. What teaching is most important?", "options": {"A": "Switch to a lower oxygen flow rate while smoking to reduce the fire risk", "B": "Use a cigarette only if a window is open and the tubing is off the face", "C": "Smoking away from the tank is acceptable since the tank is the only hazard", "D": "Do not smoke while using oxygen, remove tubing near flame, and keep ignition sources away"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reducing flow rate instead of stopping the ignition source", "B": "Allowing smoking with ventilation as a compromise", "C": "Believing distance from the tank prevents fire"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Do not smoke while using oxygen, remove tubing near flame, and keep ignition sources away): Oxygen enriches clothing, hair and tubing, so smoking while connected can cause serious burns even away from the tank. Lowering the flow does not remove the risk. Open windows do not protect against fire on the tubing or clothing. Offer support to quit and post no-smoking signs."}, {"id": 1825, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a practical nurse administered a dose of medication that was twice the ordered amount. The client is currently stable and has no complaints. The charge nurse is occupied with an admission. What is the nurse's most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Ask a colleague to disclose the error to the client so it sounds less serious", "B": "Document the error in the client chart only and monitor the client quietly", "C": "Wait for the charge nurse to finish and then ask what should be reported", "D": "Assess the client, tell the RN and provider immediately, and complete the incident report"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delegating disclosure to avoid personal responsibility", "B": "Documenting only in the chart and not reporting", "C": "Delaying reporting while a colleague is busy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess the client, tell the RN and provider immediately, and complete the incident report): The first duties are to assess and protect the client, report immediately so treatment can start, and use the incident system to learn from the event. Charting alone hides the event from the system. Waiting delays any antidote or monitoring. Disclosure is the responsibility of the nurse and team, and cannot be handed to a colleague to soften it."}, {"id": 1826, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A home visit finds a spouse caring for a partner with heart failure. The spouse appears worn out, sleeps poorly and skips meals. She declines help, saying she should manage on her own, but she looks close to tears. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Suggest her adult children should take over so she is free to rest", "B": "Focus the visit on the client's care and avoid commenting on the spouse's exhaustion", "C": "Acknowledge her effort, assess her strain, and explore respite options with her", "D": "Tell her that she must accept respite or the client will need admission"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assigning responsibility to others without consulting her", "B": "Ignoring the caregiver's needs", "D": "Threatening consequences to force acceptance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acknowledge her effort, assess her strain, and explore respite options with her): Caregiver strain affects both the caregiver and the client, so assessing and offering respite or community resources is part of care. Ignoring it overlooks a safety risk. Forcing acceptance through threat disrespects her autonomy, and assigning the role to children without discussion ignores her wishes. Share concerns with the RN so supports can be arranged."}, {"id": 1827, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A practical nurse in home care says, \"My employer has liability insurance, so I don't need anything else.\" A colleague who has worked in several settings disagrees and says the employer policy may leave gaps. Which statement best describes professional liability protection?", "options": {"A": "Liability protection is needed mainly by nurses working in independent practice", "B": "Employer coverage may not fully protect the nurse, so personal professional liability protection is advised", "C": "Employer coverage fully protects the nurse, including in regulatory complaints", "D": "Protection depends on the nurse following employer policy closely"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming independent nurses are the main group needing protection", "C": "Believing employer coverage fully replaces personal protection", "D": "Thinking policy compliance guarantees cover"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Employer coverage may not fully protect the nurse, so personal professional liability protection is advised): Employer coverage generally protects the employer and may not cover regulatory proceedings or all circumstances, so personal protection is recommended. Employed nurses can still face complaints and legal action. Following policy supports defensible practice but does not guarantee coverage. Nurses check regulatory body expectations and their own coverage terms."}, {"id": 1828, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A home care nurse arrives to find a 70-year-old on oxygen with a lit candle on the bedside table, two metres from the concentrator. The client lives alone and says the candle is a comfort at night. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Place the candle on a higher shelf, where the flame sits above the tubing.", "B": "Allow the candle if it stays at least two metres from the concentrator", "C": "Explain the oxygen fire risk and ask to remove open flames, offering a battery light", "D": "Turn the oxygen off while the candle burns and restart it afterward"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Moving the flame without resolving the hazard", "B": "Believing distance makes an open flame acceptable", "D": "Switching oxygen off and creating a hypoxia risk"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain the oxygen fire risk and ask to remove open flames, offering a battery light): Open flames near oxygen create a fire hazard because oxygen-enriched air spreads quickly. A distance of two metres is not a safe margin. Turning oxygen off puts the client at risk of low oxygen, and moving the candle does not remove the hazard. A battery candle gives comfort without flame."}, {"id": 1829, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An adult on a medical unit received an antibiotic two hours later than scheduled because of a delayed pharmacy delivery. The next dose is due in four hours. A practical nurse is unsure what to do. What action is best?", "options": {"A": "Give the late dose and keep the usual schedule without recording the delay", "B": "Give a double dose at the next time to make up for the missed amount", "C": "Skip the next dose to keep the daily total the same and avoid overdose", "D": "Check the late-dose policy, report the delay to the RN, and follow provider direction"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving the dose and leaving the delay undocumented", "B": "Doubling the next dose to catch up", "C": "Omitting a dose without direction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Check the late-dose policy, report the delay to the RN, and follow provider direction): Late doses are handled by the facility policy and provider instruction, and the delay is reported and documented. Doubling a dose risks toxicity, and skipping a dose reduces the antibiotic effect. Omitting documentation conceals the event and prevents system learning. The pharmacy or RN can help adjust timing."}, {"id": 1830, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A practical nurse in a surgical unit is asked by a surgeon to remove dead tissue from a pressure injury with a scalpel at the bedside. The nurse has no additional training in this skill and the surgeon says it is quick. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Decline because sharp debridement exceeds current competence, and tell the RN so a qualified provider acts", "B": "Perform it only if the surgeon stays at the bedside and accepts responsibility for the outcome", "C": "Complete the debridement after reviewing the procedure steps in the unit's wound care policy first", "D": "Perform the procedure since the surgeon is present and supervising"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treating the surgeon's acceptance of responsibility as creating competence", "C": "Believing a policy review replaces trained skill", "D": "Performing a skill beyond personal competence"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline because sharp debridement exceeds current competence, and tell the RN so a qualified provider acts): Nurses practise within competence, education and the regulator's standards, and sharp debridement generally needs specific authorization and training. Supervision by the surgeon does not create competence or move accountability. Reading a policy does not replace trained skill. Declining and escalating protects the client."}, {"id": 1831, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A practical nurse receives a letter from the regulatory body about a complaint filed by a client's family. The nurse is upset and thinks the complaint is unfair. The hearing may not be scheduled for months. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Ask a senior colleague to review the complaint and draft the reply on her behalf", "B": "Wait until a hearing is scheduled and respond at that time", "C": "Send the regulator a written request to dismiss the complaint before any investigation begins", "D": "Respond within the stated timelines, cooperate with the regulator, and consider contacting the liability protection provider"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delegating a personal regulatory response to a colleague", "B": "Ignoring the complaint until a hearing", "C": "Seeking early dismissal instead of responding to the process"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Respond within the stated timelines, cooperate with the regulator, and consider contacting the liability protection provider): Nurses must respond to regulator requests within the stated timelines and cooperate, and seeking advice from the liability provider is sensible. Waiting for a hearing risks sanction for non-cooperation. A request to dismiss does not replace a response, and having a colleague answer avoids her own accountability. The process lets the nurse present her account."}, {"id": 1832, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "During a community respiratory outbreak, a clinic nurse sees clients with cough and fever. A coworker says that masks and gloves are needed only for confirmed cases, since supplies are limited. The nurse has not yet seen any test results. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Wear full PPE for every client, including those with no symptoms, to be safe", "B": "Use point-of-care risk assessment and the PPE required for suspected cases", "C": "Use PPE only when the client asks for it or appears seriously ill with breathing difficulty", "D": "Agree with the coworker and wear PPE only after laboratory confirmation of the infection"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using maximum PPE for all clients without assessment", "C": "Basing PPE use on the client's request", "D": "Limiting PPE to confirmed cases"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use point-of-care risk assessment and the PPE required for suspected cases): PPE is chosen using point-of-care risk assessment and the facility's infection control guidance, covering suspected cases before confirmation. Waiting for confirmation exposes staff and other clients. Using full PPE for everyone wastes supplies without need. A client's request is not a safe basis for choosing protection."}, {"id": 1833, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Reyes tells the practical nurse that his male partner of thirty years is his emergency contact, but his family does not know. His sister arrives and asks who the visitor is and why he was listed. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Explain that the partner is a close friend and avoid confirming the relationship", "B": "Tell the sister about the partner so the family has full information for safety", "C": "Protect confidentiality, state that client information is private, and ask how he wishes to share it", "D": "Ask the sister to speak with the charge nurse about the visitor list"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Misleading the sister instead of protecting confidentiality honestly", "B": "Sharing personal information with family for safety", "D": "Redirecting the sister without consulting the client first"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C227", "cluster_text": "Alan Reyes is a 62-year-old man admitted to a medical unit through the emergency department with pneumonia. He was unhoused for two years before admission and has a hearing impairment. His partner of thirty years visits daily, and his family is not aware of the relationship. Mr. Reyes is a practising Catholic and is deciding about a bronchoscopy. The practical nurse is assigned to his care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Protect confidentiality, state that client information is private, and ask how he wishes to share it): Sexual orientation is private health information, and the client decides who knows it. Telling the sister breaches confidentiality, and describing the partner as a friend misleads her without respecting his wishes. Referring her on without speaking to him leaves him unaware and unsupported. Checking his consent preferences respects his autonomy and dignity."}, {"id": 1834, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During morning care, Mr. Reyes turns his head toward the nurse's voice, misses questions, and asks to see her mouth. The nurse is talking quickly while moving about the room, and the television is on. Which approach best supports communication with him?", "options": {"A": "Use short written notes and avoid speaking to him, so spoken words cannot cause confusion", "B": "Stand beside his bed and speak toward his better ear while completing the morning tasks", "C": "Speak in a raised slow voice from across the room so he hears everything", "D": "Face him in good light, reduce noise, speak clearly and slowly, and confirm understanding"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using writing only and removing spoken contact", "B": "Speaking from the side without face visibility or reduced noise", "C": "Raising volume from a distance without visible lip cues"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C227", "cluster_text": "Alan Reyes is a 62-year-old man admitted to a medical unit through the emergency department with pneumonia. He was unhoused for two years before admission and has a hearing impairment. His partner of thirty years visits daily, and his family is not aware of the relationship. Mr. Reyes is a practising Catholic and is deciding about a bronchoscopy. The practical nurse is assigned to his care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Face him in good light, reduce noise, speak clearly and slowly, and confirm understanding): Facing him in light supports lip reading, and slower clear speech with less noise improves understanding. Speaking from the side or from across the room removes the visual cues he relies on. Written notes help as a supplement but cannot replace direct dialogue."}, {"id": 1835, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Reyes asks the nurse whether she would pray with him before deciding about a bronchoscopy, and says his faith guides his choices. The nurse does not share his beliefs, and the bronchoscopy is scheduled for tomorrow. Which response best respects his needs?", "options": {"A": "Pray with him to build rapport and show that you share his values", "B": "Tell him that decisions are best made using medical advice, not faith", "C": "Offer to arrange a visit from spiritual care and respect his beliefs without sharing personal views", "D": "Say that praying is not part of your role and change the subject"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Taking part in prayer to build rapport", "B": "Dismissing his spiritual values in decision making", "D": "Closing the topic without offering any alternative"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C227", "cluster_text": "Alan Reyes is a 62-year-old man admitted to a medical unit through the emergency department with pneumonia. He was unhoused for two years before admission and has a hearing impairment. His partner of thirty years visits daily, and his family is not aware of the relationship. Mr. Reyes is a practising Catholic and is deciding about a bronchoscopy. The practical nurse is assigned to his care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Offer to arrange a visit from spiritual care and respect his beliefs without sharing personal views): Spiritual care supports the client's coping and decisions, and referral to a chaplain or spiritual care provider respects his beliefs while maintaining professional boundaries. Praying to build rapport blurs professional roles. Minimizing faith disrespects his values. Changing the subject without alternatives leaves a need unmet. The decision on the procedure remains his."}, {"id": 1836, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A note from the emergency department states that Mr. Reyes was \"drug seeking\" when he asked for pain relief for rib pain. He now reports pain of 8 out of 10 on the unit. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Offer only non-drug measures because of his history and review his pain tomorrow", "B": "Document that he is seeking drugs so other staff are aware of the concern", "C": "Withhold pain medication until the physician confirms that he is not drug seeking", "D": "Assess his pain fully, treat it per orders, and avoid assumptions based on the ED label"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Limiting treatment because of housing and history", "B": "Labelling the client in the record without assessment", "C": "Assuming the client is seeking drugs"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C227", "cluster_text": "Alan Reyes is a 62-year-old man admitted to a medical unit through the emergency department with pneumonia. He was unhoused for two years before admission and has a hearing impairment. His partner of thirty years visits daily, and his family is not aware of the relationship. Mr. Reyes is a practising Catholic and is deciding about a bronchoscopy. The practical nurse is assigned to his care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess his pain fully, treat it per orders, and avoid assumptions based on the ED label): Pain is what the client reports, and a full assessment, timely treatment and clear documentation of findings are the standard of care. Stigma attached to a drug seeking label leads to undertreated pain. Withholding or limiting treatment without assessment is unfair and unsafe. Labels in the chart bias future care. Report uncontrolled pain to the RN or provider."}, {"id": 1837, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 62-year-old client who has taken lorazepam nightly for years tells a community nurse she plans to stop it suddenly because she dislikes dependence. She has no seizure history, and she says she feels well. What should the practical nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Do not stop abruptly, and ask the prescriber about a gradual reduction because of seizure risk", "B": "Stop the tablets immediately since she has no history of seizures", "C": "Cut each tablet in half for a week, then stop without telling the prescriber", "D": "Replace lorazepam with an over-the-counter sleep aid to avoid dependence on the prescribed drug"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Advising abrupt stopping despite withdrawal seizure risk", "C": "Making a self-directed taper without prescriber supervision", "D": "Substituting an unreviewed sedating product for a prescribed drug"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Do not stop abruptly, and ask the prescriber about a gradual reduction because of seizure risk): Abrupt benzodiazepine cessation after long-term use can cause withdrawal including seizures, so tapering is supervised by the prescriber. Having no seizure history does not remove the risk. A self-made taper or an over-the-counter substitute skips prescriber guidance and can still trigger withdrawal. The nurse supports her goal and refers her."}, {"id": 1838, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Dubois has an order for ondansetron to treat nausea, and he now reports a mild headache. His admission ECG shows a prolonged QT interval, and his latest potassium level is normal. The nurse prepares the dose. What action is best?", "options": {"A": "Hold the dose, report the prolonged QT and headache to the RN or provider", "B": "Give half the dose and reassess his pulse and blood pressure after the dose", "C": "Give the dose since headache is a common effect and nausea needs treatment", "D": "Give the dose with food so the headache is less likely to worsen"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Altering the dose without an order", "C": "Assuming the drug has no serious adverse effects", "D": "Using food as a fix for a cardiac risk"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C219", "cluster_text": "Peter Dubois is a 71-year-old man on a medical unit with cirrhosis from past hepatitis, gastroparesis causing nausea, and osteoarthritis in both hands. His admission ECG showed a prolonged QT interval. He takes several medications, and he is hoping to go home next week, and his wife visits each evening. The practical nurse is caring for him and will teach him before discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold the dose, report the prolonged QT and headache to the RN or provider): Ondansetron can prolong the QT interval, and a documented prolonged QT is a reason to check before giving it. A headache is a common effect, but the cardiac risk is the priority. Changing the dose without an order is outside scope, and food does not alter the cardiac effect. Reporting allows the provider to choose a safer drug."}, {"id": 1839, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two days after metoclopramide was started for gastroparesis, Mr. Dubois is restless, cannot sit still, and says he feels jittery. The nurse notices his legs moving constantly. His vital signs and blood glucose are within his usual ranges. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "This is likely anxiety about going home and can be managed with reassurance and quiet", "B": "This is an expected effect that settles on its own and needs no action or reporting", "C": "This is a sign of advancing liver failure and requires immediate comfort measures only", "D": "These may be movement side effects of metoclopramide, so report them to the provider"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismissing restlessness as anxiety", "B": "Assuming the effect is harmless and needs no report", "C": "Attributing symptoms to liver failure without assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C219", "cluster_text": "Peter Dubois is a 71-year-old man on a medical unit with cirrhosis from past hepatitis, gastroparesis causing nausea, and osteoarthritis in both hands. His admission ECG showed a prolonged QT interval. He takes several medications, and he is hoping to go home next week, and his wife visits each evening. The practical nurse is caring for him and will teach him before discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (These may be movement side effects of metoclopramide, so report them to the provider): Metoclopramide can cause extrapyramidal effects such as akathisia, with restlessness and an urge to move, especially in older adults. Calling it anxiety delays treatment, and treating it as normal ignores a reportable drug effect. Liver failure signs differ and need separate assessment. The provider may stop or change the drug."}, {"id": 1840, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before discharge, Mr. Dubois says he takes ibuprofen on an empty stomach for hand arthritis pain and plans to continue after going home. He has cirrhosis and no history of ulcers. What is the most appropriate teaching by the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Continue taking it, but eat first, since an empty stomach is the main concern", "B": "Take ibuprofen with an antacid, since protecting the stomach lowers the bleeding risk", "C": "Continue ibuprofen at a lower dose since a small amount cannot strain the liver", "D": "Stop ibuprofen until the prescriber approves it, since it raises bleeding and ulcer risk"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing food alone makes NSAIDs safe", "B": "Believing stomach protection removes NSAID bleeding risk in cirrhosis", "C": "Assuming a lower dose makes an NSAID safe in cirrhosis"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C219", "cluster_text": "Peter Dubois is a 71-year-old man on a medical unit with cirrhosis from past hepatitis, gastroparesis causing nausea, and osteoarthritis in both hands. His admission ECG showed a prolonged QT interval. He takes several medications, and he is hoping to go home next week, and his wife visits each evening. The practical nurse is caring for him and will teach him before discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop ibuprofen until the prescriber approves it, since it raises bleeding and ulcer risk): NSAIDs irritate the stomach lining and impair clotting, and cirrhosis already increases bleeding risk, so he should stop ibuprofen until his prescriber reviews options. Food or an antacid reduces irritation but does not remove the bleeding risk. A lower dose still carries that risk. Report black stools or vomiting blood at once."}, {"id": 1841, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Dubois is receiving lactulose for liver disease. This morning he has had five loose stools and feels weak, and his confusion is no better than yesterday, and his vital signs are stable. What does the practical nurse understand about the goal of therapy?", "options": {"A": "The goal is as many loose stools as possible to clear the ammonia from the blood", "B": "The goal is no bowel movements, so the dose should be held now", "C": "Two to three soft stools daily is the goal, so report excess stools and confusion", "D": "The goal is to treat constipation only, so stools can be ignored"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Aiming for maximum diarrhea", "B": "Aiming for no stools", "D": "Misunderstanding lactulose as only a laxative"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C219", "cluster_text": "Peter Dubois is a 71-year-old man on a medical unit with cirrhosis from past hepatitis, gastroparesis causing nausea, and osteoarthritis in both hands. His admission ECG showed a prolonged QT interval. He takes several medications, and he is hoping to go home next week, and his wife visits each evening. The practical nurse is caring for him and will teach him before discharge.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Two to three soft stools daily is the goal, so report excess stools and confusion): Lactulose lowers ammonia by increasing stool output, and the target is usually two to three soft stools daily. Five loose stools risk dehydration and electrolyte loss, so the dose may need adjusting, and ongoing confusion must be reported. Holding the drug without direction can raise ammonia. Monitor fluid status and mental state."}, {"id": 1842, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Jordan, 16, asks the practical nurse why the medication history cannot simply be shared with the parents on request, since Jordan is a minor. The nurse has read the team note stating that Jordan understands the treatment and its consequences. Which principle best explains how the nurse should respond about sharing this information?", "options": {"A": "A minor who understands the care can decide and control who receives personal health information.", "B": "The practical nurse decides what to share with parents based on how sensitive the information seems.", "C": "Confidentiality applies only after age eighteen, so the nurse should follow the parents' wishes.", "D": "Parents are entitled to the chart because they are legally responsible for a minor child."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Replaces the client's decision with the nurse's own judgment of sensitivity", "C": "Ties privacy rights to a fixed age instead of understanding", "D": "Treats legal guardianship as automatic access to the record"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C228", "cluster_text": "Jordan, 16, is admitted to a medical unit for a kidney infection. During the admission interview, Jordan privately tells the practical nurse about starting oral contraception three months ago and asks that the parents, who are visiting, not be told. The team has judged Jordan able to understand the treatment plan and its consequences. Jordan's mother and father are waiting in the room.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A minor who understands the care can decide and control who receives personal health information.): Jordan has been judged able to understand the treatment and its consequences, so Jordan holds the decision about who receives the medication history. Parental responsibility is the tempting idea, but it does not give automatic access once a minor is capable. Basing privacy on age alone ignores capacity, and the nurse's own sense of sensitivity is not the standard. Capacity, not age, governs consent and disclosure."}, {"id": 1843, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "While riding a crowded elevator, a colleague starts describing Jordan's contraception history and kidney infection to another nurse. A visitor wearing a unit badge stands beside them. The practical nurse has worked with this colleague for two years and feels uncomfortable speaking up. What is the best action by the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Quietly ask the colleague to pause the conversation and continue it later in a private area.", "B": "Join the discussion briefly to add the details the other nurse needs for Jordan's care.", "C": "Tell the visitor the conversation is confidential and ask the visitor to forget what was heard.", "D": "Say nothing in the elevator and raise the concern with the manager after the shift."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats a public conversation as a legitimate care discussion", "C": "Tries to repair the breach through the visitor instead of stopping it", "D": "Delays acting while private information is already being overheard"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C228", "cluster_text": "Jordan, 16, is admitted to a medical unit for a kidney infection. During the admission interview, Jordan privately tells the practical nurse about starting oral contraception three months ago and asks that the parents, who are visiting, not be told. The team has judged Jordan able to understand the treatment plan and its consequences. Jordan's mother and father are waiting in the room.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Quietly ask the colleague to pause the conversation and continue it later in a private area.): Stopping the conversation immediately limits further disclosure, and a quiet request keeps the working relationship intact. Waiting until the end of shift lets the breach continue while a visitor is listening. Joining in adds more private details to a public space, and asking the visitor to forget does not undo what was heard. Discuss client information only where it cannot be overheard."}, {"id": 1844, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Jordan's father insists on staying at the bedside overnight and demands that the nurse explain every medication, while Jordan asks for time alone. Jordan has been judged capable of making care decisions. The mother says she will take the question to the physician in the morning. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Ask the physician to decide whether Jordan or the parents should control visiting and information.", "B": "Ask Jordan privately what may be shared and who stays, then explain those wishes to parents.", "C": "Explain each medication to the father because a parent of a minor must be fully informed.", "D": "Agree to the father's request to stay, since family presence supports recovery for young clients."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Passes a nursing responsibility to the physician unnecessarily", "C": "Assumes a parent's role overrides a capable minor's privacy", "D": "Chooses the family's preference over the client's expressed wishes"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C228", "cluster_text": "Jordan, 16, is admitted to a medical unit for a kidney infection. During the admission interview, Jordan privately tells the practical nurse about starting oral contraception three months ago and asks that the parents, who are visiting, not be told. The team has judged Jordan able to understand the treatment plan and its consequences. Jordan's mother and father are waiting in the room.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask Jordan privately what may be shared and who stays, then explain those wishes to parents.): Jordan is the decision-maker, so the nurse starts by learning what Jordan wants shared and who may stay, then communicates this respectfully to the parents. Giving the father every detail disregards Jordan's decision, and handing the question to the physician delays something the nurse can handle. Siding with the family because presence helps recovery overrides the client's stated wish. Put the client's choices first, then support the family."}, {"id": 1845, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At shift change the practical nurse hands over Jordan's care to the oncoming nurse. Jordan's temperature has dropped, antibiotics are infusing, and Jordan has asked that the contraception history stay private from visitors. Using SBAR, which statement should the nurse give as the recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Continue to monitor temperature and urine output, and keep Jordan's privacy request in place.", "B": "Jordan's temperature was 38.6 on admission and 37.4 two hours after antibiotics started.", "C": "Jordan is a 16-year-old admitted with a kidney infection and started antibiotics this afternoon.", "D": "Jordan appears more comfortable and the infection seems to be improving overall."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Offers assessment data instead of a requested action", "C": "Gives situation and background where a recommendation is needed", "D": "Offers an impression without stating what should be done next"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C228", "cluster_text": "Jordan, 16, is admitted to a medical unit for a kidney infection. During the admission interview, Jordan privately tells the practical nurse about starting oral contraception three months ago and asks that the parents, who are visiting, not be told. The team has judged Jordan able to understand the treatment plan and its consequences. Jordan's mother and father are waiting in the room.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continue to monitor temperature and urine output, and keep Jordan's privacy request in place.): The recommendation tells the oncoming nurse what to do next, here monitoring and keeping the privacy request. The first alternative is the situation and background, and the temperature trend is assessment data. The statement about improvement is an impression with no action attached. A handover that ends without a recommendation leaves the next shift guessing."}, {"id": 1846, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Walter, 62, felt faint and developed chest tightness only while climbing stairs, and he is now pale and sweating. A student asks the practical nurse why this should not be treated as simple dehydration. Which explanation best fits Walter's presentation?", "options": {"A": "Chest tightness with fainting is most often caused by anxiety, so reassurance and monitoring are enough.", "B": "Pallor and sweating after stairs show normal exertion, and fainting is a common vasovagal response.", "C": "Exertional fainting with chest symptoms points toward a cardiac cause, such as an arrhythmia.", "D": "Fainting follows poor fluid intake, so oral fluids and rest should be tried first."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Attributes chest symptoms to anxiety before ruling out cardiac causes", "B": "Dismisses red-flag signs as ordinary exertion or vasovagal", "D": "Assumes any faint is caused by low fluid intake"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C224", "cluster_text": "Walter, 62, is visiting the cardiac unit and climbs two flights of stairs. He says he feels faint with some chest tightness, sits on a bench in the hallway, turns pale and slumps sideways. A practical nurse on the unit is the first staff member to reach him. The nursing station, where cardiac arrest equipment is kept, is about thirty metres away. The hallway is quiet.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Exertional fainting with chest symptoms points toward a cardiac cause, such as an arrhythmia.): Fainting triggered by exertion along with chest tightness suggests the heart cannot meet demand or is in a dangerous rhythm. Treating it as dehydration or a simple faint delays assessment of a possible cardiac event. Anxiety is a diagnosis of exclusion once cardiac causes are addressed. Exertional syncope is a red flag until proven otherwise."}, {"id": 1847, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Walter does not respond when the practical nurse shouts and taps his shoulders, and his chest is not rising normally. The nurse checks for a carotid pulse for up to ten seconds and feels none. The cardiac arrest equipment is thirty metres away. What should the nurse do next?", "options": {"A": "Move Walter onto a stretcher before starting compressions so the chest is firm.", "B": "Shout for help and an AED to be brought, then start compressions at once.", "C": "Give two rescue breaths before compressions because the cause may be a blocked airway.", "D": "Run to the nursing station for the equipment first, then return to start compressions."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays compressions to reposition the client", "C": "Starts with breaths for a sudden collapse likely cardiac", "D": "Leaves the client to fetch equipment before starting compressions"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C224", "cluster_text": "Walter, 62, is visiting the cardiac unit and climbs two flights of stairs. He says he feels faint with some chest tightness, sits on a bench in the hallway, turns pale and slumps sideways. A practical nurse on the unit is the first staff member to reach him. The nursing station, where cardiac arrest equipment is kept, is about thirty metres away. The hallway is quiet.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Shout for help and an AED to be brought, then start compressions at once.): No response, no normal breathing and no pulse mean cardiac arrest, so help and an AED are summoned while compressions begin without delay. Leaving to fetch equipment removes the only rescuer and stops circulation. Opening with breaths suits some arrests but delays compressions in a sudden cardiac collapse. A firm surface matters, but compressions on the floor are better than waiting."}, {"id": 1848, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two staff arrive and one begins compressions on Walter, who lies on the hallway floor. The practical nurse coaches the rescuer, who is leaning on the chest between compressions and pressing about three centimetres deep. Which instruction describes the technique the rescuer should use?", "options": {"A": "Push deeply at a slow, steady rhythm of about 60 per minute to avoid rib injury.", "B": "Push about four centimetres at 80 to 100 per minute, resting hands lightly between pushes.", "C": "Push hard at least five centimetres, at 100 to 120 per minute, allowing full chest recoil.", "D": "Push five centimetres at 120 to 140 per minute, pausing every thirty seconds for pulse checks."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Slows the rate to avoid injury at the cost of circulation", "B": "Uses a shallow depth and slow rate with weight kept on the chest", "D": "Uses a rate that is too fast with frequent pauses"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C224", "cluster_text": "Walter, 62, is visiting the cardiac unit and climbs two flights of stairs. He says he feels faint with some chest tightness, sits on a bench in the hallway, turns pale and slumps sideways. A practical nurse on the unit is the first staff member to reach him. The nursing station, where cardiac arrest equipment is kept, is about thirty metres away. The hallway is quiet.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Push hard at least five centimetres, at 100 to 120 per minute, allowing full chest recoil.): Adult compressions need adequate depth, a rate of 100 to 120 per minute, and full recoil so the heart refills. Resting weight on the chest between pushes prevents recoil and reduces blood flow. A faster rate or frequent pulse checks cut effective compressions. A very slow rate does not circulate enough blood."}, {"id": 1849, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "With compressions continuing, an AED arrives. The rescuer wants to complete the full two-minute cycle before applying the pads, saying stopping now would waste effort. Walter's chest is bare and dry, and the device is switched on. What should the practical nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Finish the current cycle of compressions first, then apply the pads and let the device analyze.", "B": "Apply the pads after two rescue breaths so oxygen reaches the heart before the shock.", "C": "Stop compressions and wait for the device to analyze before attaching the pads.", "D": "Apply the pads at once and follow the voice prompts, pausing compressions only for analysis."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays pad placement to finish a compression cycle", "B": "Adds rescue breaths before a possible shock", "C": "Stops compressions earlier than needed before pads are on"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C224", "cluster_text": "Walter, 62, is visiting the cardiac unit and climbs two flights of stairs. He says he feels faint with some chest tightness, sits on a bench in the hallway, turns pale and slumps sideways. A practical nurse on the unit is the first staff member to reach him. The nursing station, where cardiac arrest equipment is kept, is about thirty metres away. The hallway is quiet.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Apply the pads at once and follow the voice prompts, pausing compressions only for analysis.): Early defibrillation improves survival, so pads go on immediately while another rescuer keeps compressing and compressions stop only when the device analyzes. Finishing the cycle first delays a possible shock. Adding breaths first also delays it, and stopping compressions before the pads are on wastes perfusion time. Keep compressions going until the device says otherwise."}, {"id": 1850, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Asking about a publicly funded mammogram, a woman of 45 tells a practical nurse in a community clinic that a friend in another province started at 50. The clinic's wall poster is out of date. The nurse wants to give accurate information. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Tell her the national start age is fifty, so she should return when she turns fifty.", "B": "Explain that ages differ by province and refer her to her provincial screening program.", "C": "Book her a screening mammogram now, because earlier screening reduces risk for a woman her age.", "D": "Tell her the nurse cannot discuss screening eligibility and that she must ask her physician."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "States one age as a national rule", "C": "Books a test without checking program eligibility", "D": "Refuses to give information that is within nursing health teaching"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain that ages differ by province and refer her to her provincial screening program.): Screening programs are provincial, so the accurate answer acknowledges the differences and directs her to the current program criteria. A single national age misleads her, and booking without checking eligibility ignores program rules. Telling her to ask only the physician withholds teaching that is within nursing scope. Direct clients to current, jurisdiction-specific sources."}, {"id": 1851, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Lalonde, 58, asks for his as-needed acetaminophen 650 mg, ordered every four hours. He points to his abdomen and says, \"It's bad.\" His last dose was six hours ago, and the practical nurse has not yet assessed him today. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Hold the medication and tell Mr. Lalonde to wait until the next scheduled dose.", "B": "Assess the pain's location, onset, intensity and character before deciding on the medication.", "C": "Give the acetaminophen now, since the four-hour interval has passed and the standing order allows it.", "D": "Check with the pharmacy whether a stronger analgesic is available for abdominal pain."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Refuses a due medication without any assessment", "C": "Gives an as-needed drug without assessing the complaint", "D": "Turns to a stronger drug before assessing the pain"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assess the pain's location, onset, intensity and character before deciding on the medication.): An as-needed order is a permission to give when assessment shows the need, and new abdominal pain could signal a problem that must be reported. Giving the dose right away skips that assessment. Holding the dose without assessing leaves pain unexamined, and seeking a stronger drug jumps ahead of the data. Assess, then act."}, {"id": 1852, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "In a long-term care home, a resident has a stage 3 pressure injury. The practical nurse prefers an older dressing method that has healed wounds on the unit, but the home's wound care guideline, based on current evidence, recommends a different moist dressing product. The manager asks about the plan. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Follow the home's evidence-based guideline and discuss any concerns with the wound care nurse.", "B": "Wait for the physician to order a specific dressing before changing anything about wound care.", "C": "Use the familiar dressing and document the reason for not following the guideline", "D": "Apply the guideline to new residents and keep the older method for current ones"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Defers a nursing wound-care decision to the physician unnecessarily", "C": "Documenting a deviation instead of following the guideline", "D": "Applying a guideline selectively by resident"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Follow the home's evidence-based guideline and discuss any concerns with the wound care nurse.): Evidence-based guidelines represent current best practice, so the nurse follows them and raises concerns through the wound care nurse. Preference or a note of the reason does not override the guideline, and applying it to only some residents creates unequal care. Waiting for a physician order is not required for dressings covered by the home's guideline. Use the guideline and speak up through the right channel."}, {"id": 1853, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two hours after surgery, a 64-year-old client receiving a morphine infusion is difficult to wake. Respirations are 8 per minute and shallow, oxygen saturation is 89 percent, and blood pressure is stable. The practical nurse is alone at the bedside. What is the priority action?", "options": {"A": "Position the client flat to improve circulation and continue the infusion at the same rate.", "B": "Document the findings and inform the physician at the next scheduled assessment time.", "C": "Recheck the respiratory rate in fifteen minutes since blood pressure remains stable.", "D": "Stimulate the client, pause the opioid infusion, call for help, and apply oxygen."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Continues the opioid and worsens breathing with positioning", "B": "Delays reporting a respiratory emergency to a later time", "C": "Waits for blood pressure to change before acting"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stimulate the client, pause the opioid infusion, call for help, and apply oxygen.): Slow shallow breathing, low saturation and difficulty waking suggest opioid-induced respiratory depression, an emergency that needs immediate stimulation, oxygen and help. Waiting fifteen minutes or documenting for later lets hypoxia worsen. Lying flat and continuing the infusion adds to the danger. Respiratory status guides urgency more than blood pressure does."}, {"id": 1854, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At the start of the shift, a new client, 70, arrives on the medical unit while the practical nurse also has a call bell ringing and a dressing change due. The admission form is blank, and the client looks short of breath. Which approach best uses the nursing process to set priorities?", "options": {"A": "Assess the client's breathing and vital signs first, then plan and act on findings.", "B": "Begin oxygen and a position change right away, then assess the client's condition afterward.", "C": "Complete the admission form first so the plan of care reflects an accurate history.", "D": "Answer the call bell and start the dressing change before any assessment of the new client"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Implements treatment before assessing the problem", "C": "Puts paperwork ahead of an urgent physical need", "D": "Handles tasks first without assessing the new client"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Assess the client's breathing and vital signs first, then plan and act on findings.): The nursing process begins with assessment, and a breathless new client has the most urgent need, so breathing and vital signs come first. Paperwork can wait, and starting other tasks first ignores the highest risk. Acting before assessing risks treating the wrong problem. Assess first, then plan and implement."}, {"id": 1855, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ten minutes after eating a cookie containing peanuts, a 7-year-old at school develops hives, lip swelling and wheezing. The school has a prescribed epinephrine auto-injector in the office, and the school nurse is the only health professional present. The child's mother has been called. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Sit the child upright with a cool cloth on the lips while waiting for the mother.", "B": "Call the mother for permission to use the auto-injector before giving any medication.", "C": "Give the oral antihistamine from the child's kit and watch for improvement for ten minutes.", "D": "Give the epinephrine auto-injector into the outer thigh and call emergency services."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses comfort measures while anaphylaxis progresses", "B": "Delays treatment to get permission already covered by the prescription", "C": "Chooses an antihistamine ahead of epinephrine"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give the epinephrine auto-injector into the outer thigh and call emergency services.): Hives, swelling and wheezing after a known allergen indicate anaphylaxis, where epinephrine is the first treatment and emergency services are called. An antihistamine is slow and does not treat airway or circulation effects. Comfort measures and waiting for permission both waste minutes. Epinephrine first, then call for help."}, {"id": 1856, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A home care nurse visits the family of a 72-year-old man with ALS who now needs a feeding tube. His daughter says she cries in the car because her father tells her not to cry in front of him. She asks whether she should hide her tears. How should the practical nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Advise her to keep crying outside the home until her father accepts the diagnosis.", "B": "Tell her that crying this often may mean she needs a mental health assessment soon.", "C": "Acknowledge her grief as normal and support sharing her feelings with her father.", "D": "Suggest she keep her feelings private at home so her father stays calm and hopeful."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Postpones grief until the client changes his view", "B": "Labels normal anticipatory grief as a disorder", "D": "Encourages hiding grief from the dying client"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acknowledge her grief as normal and support sharing her feelings with her father.): Anticipatory grief is a normal response, and honest sharing of feelings can strengthen the bond between daughter and father. Hiding tears or crying elsewhere teaches both that grief cannot be spoken. Suggesting a mental health referral for this level of crying pathologizes a normal reaction. Support open expression and monitor coping."}, {"id": 1857, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Confused and restless at night, a 77-year-old on a medical unit repeatedly tries to climb over the raised side rails of his bed. The practical nurse knows the unit's restraint policy, and the bed has four full rails up. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Lower the rails and bed, and try a less restrictive measure such as closer observation.", "B": "Raise the rails and ask the family to stay and hold the client's arms.", "C": "Apply a soft wrist restraint now and obtain the order from the physician afterward.", "D": "Keep all four rails raised since they prevent falls and protect confused clients overnight."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Substitutes a family member for a physical restraint", "C": "Applies a restraint before an order and alternatives", "D": "Uses full rails as a routine safety device"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lower the rails and bed, and try a less restrictive measure such as closer observation.): Full rails can act as a restraint and increase injury when a confused client climbs over them. A low bed and closer observation are less restrictive options that should be tried first. Applying a wrist restraint without an order and alternatives breaks policy, and asking family to hold the client's arms is a form of restraint. Choose the least restrictive effective measure."}, {"id": 1858, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At a unit journal club, a colleague proposes changing the catheter care routine because one small study of 24 clients, funded by a product manufacturer, showed fewer infections. The practical nurse is asked for an opinion. Which response best reflects appraising a source of evidence?", "options": {"A": "Reject the study because manufacturer funding makes the findings invalid.", "B": "Ask the manufacturer for its internal data and adopt the change if it agrees", "C": "Look for additional high-quality research and guidelines before recommending any change to practice.", "D": "Support the change since an infection reduction in any study justifies updating the routine"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismisses a study only for its funding source", "B": "Relying on the sponsor's own data to verify its product", "D": "Treating any positive study result as sufficient evidence"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Look for additional high-quality research and guidelines before recommending any change to practice.): A single small study with possible funding bias is not enough to change practice, so the nurse looks for more and better evidence and guidelines. Accepting the result ignores sample size and bias, and asking the sponsor to confirm its own data adds no independent evidence. Rejecting it solely for funding is also too simple, since funding raises caution but does not invalidate findings."}, {"id": 1859, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Depression and heavy evening drinking bring a 33-year-old to a community clinic. The client asks the practical nurse which problem should be dealt with first, assuming treatment for one must wait until the other is under control. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Suggest separate programs for each condition, starting with whichever the client chooses.", "B": "Explain that depression is treated first because drinking usually stops once mood improves.", "C": "Explain that alcohol use is treated first because depression cannot be assessed while drinking.", "D": "Explain that care for both conditions is best provided together by an integrated team."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Separates care into unconnected programs", "B": "Assumes substance use resolves when mood improves", "C": "Sequences treatment by delaying mental health care"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that care for both conditions is best provided together by an integrated team.): Concurrent depression and substance use interact, so integrated care addresses both at the same time. Treating alcohol first or depression first assumes one must be resolved before the other, which delays help. Separate programs risk fragmented care. Offer integrated, team-based treatment."}, {"id": 1860, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad's daughter privately asks the practical nurse not to tell her father the prognosis, saying he would lose hope. Mr. Haddad asks the nurse directly what the doctors expect ahead. He has said he wants to make his own plans. What is the best response by the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Support his right to information by arranging a physician conversation about his prognosis.", "B": "Tell him the prognosis yourself in detail because he asked the nurse directly.", "C": "Honour the daughter's request and gently change the subject to protect him from further distress.", "D": "Say nothing to either person and record the conflict in the chart for the team."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Gives a prognosis that belongs to the physician's role", "C": "Follows the family's wish over the client's right to know", "D": "Avoids the issue and delays the client's access to information"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C218", "cluster_text": "Mr. Haddad, 68, has advanced pancreatic cancer and is cared for on a medical unit with a palliative approach. He has a history of depression treated for several years. He is alert and speaks clearly about his wishes. His adult children visit daily, and several care decisions are discussed during the week. The practical nurse works within regulatory standards and consults the team.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Support his right to information by arranging a physician conversation about his prognosis.): A capable client has the right to information about his own condition, and prognosis discussions are led by the physician, so the nurse arranges that conversation. Honouring the daughter's request overrides his right. Giving a detailed prognosis goes beyond the nurse's role, and recording the conflict without acting leaves his question unanswered. Facilitate the right conversation with the right person."}, {"id": 1861, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad has severe pain and breathlessness, and the physician has ordered titrated morphine for comfort. His son asks the practical nurse whether the morphine is being given to hasten his father's death. The nurse has just completed a pain and respiratory assessment. Which explanation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Morphine at this stage shortens life, and the team accepts that as the purpose of care.", "B": "The intent is relieving suffering, with doses adjusted to symptoms, not ending life.", "C": "The family sets the doses once comfort becomes the goal of care", "D": "Morphine is held until breathing slows so the team can monitor the effect"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confuses symptom relief with intentionally hastening death", "C": "Giving the family authority over dosing", "D": "Withholding symptom relief until respiratory depression appears"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C218", "cluster_text": "Mr. Haddad, 68, has advanced pancreatic cancer and is cared for on a medical unit with a palliative approach. He has a history of depression treated for several years. He is alert and speaks clearly about his wishes. His adult children visit daily, and several care decisions are discussed during the week. The practical nurse works within regulatory standards and consults the team.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The intent is relieving suffering, with doses adjusted to symptoms, not ending life.): Palliative opioids are titrated to symptoms, and the intent is comfort rather than ending life. Saying the purpose is to shorten life misstates the ethical basis. Doses are ordered by the prescriber and adjusted to symptoms, not set by the family, and holding relief until breathing slows leaves suffering untreated. Explain intent and monitoring clearly to the family."}, {"id": 1862, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad's chart holds a genetic test result suggesting a hereditary cancer syndrome. His daughter, 41, asks the practical nurse to confirm it so she can arrange her own screening. He has not given permission to share results with relatives. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Tell her the result is hereditary but withhold the specific syndrome name.", "B": "Explain that results need his consent, and offer to help him decide how to tell relatives.", "C": "Advise her to request his genetic report directly from the laboratory that tested him.", "D": "Confirm the result because she shares the genetic risk and has a right to arrange screening."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives partial disclosure that still breaches confidentiality", "C": "Directs her to bypass the client to obtain his results", "D": "Discloses the result because a relative shares the risk"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C218", "cluster_text": "Mr. Haddad, 68, has advanced pancreatic cancer and is cared for on a medical unit with a palliative approach. He has a history of depression treated for several years. He is alert and speaks clearly about his wishes. His adult children visit daily, and several care decisions are discussed during the week. The practical nurse works within regulatory standards and consults the team.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain that results need his consent, and offer to help him decide how to tell relatives.): Genetic results belong to the client, so sharing needs his consent, and the nurse can support him in deciding how to tell relatives. Confirming the result or giving partial details both disclose without permission. Sending the daughter to the laboratory sidesteps his consent. Protect confidentiality and support his decision."}, {"id": 1863, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A research nurse invites Mr. Haddad to join a clinical trial of a new pain therapy. He asks the practical nurse whether he must agree to keep receiving his current palliative care. The form states participation is voluntary, and it has not been signed. What should the nurse tell him?", "options": {"A": "Participation is voluntary, he can withdraw at any point, and his usual care continues.", "B": "He should talk with his children first, because the trial affects the whole family", "C": "Joining is voluntary, but he must decide today so the research nurse can plan", "D": "He may withdraw later, but declining now means his palliative care team would change."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Implying the family decides for a capable client", "C": "Pressuring a rushed decision about research participation", "D": "Suggests declining changes the care team"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C218", "cluster_text": "Mr. Haddad, 68, has advanced pancreatic cancer and is cared for on a medical unit with a palliative approach. He has a history of depression treated for several years. He is alert and speaks clearly about his wishes. His adult children visit daily, and several care decisions are discussed during the week. The practical nurse works within regulatory standards and consults the team.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Participation is voluntary, he can withdraw at any point, and his usual care continues.): Informed consent to research is voluntary, can be withdrawn, and does not affect the standard care a client receives. Pressing him to decide today or sending the decision to his family undermines his right to choose when he is capable. Suggesting that declining changes his care team misrepresents this. Reassure him of his freedom to choose."}, {"id": 1864, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad, who has a history of depression, says he wants to stop a further round of chemotherapy. A colleague says his depression history means he cannot decide for himself and suggests calling his children for the decision. He explains his reasons clearly. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Ask his children to decide because mental illness makes his own choices unreliable.", "B": "Presume he has capacity and report his decision to the physician for discussion.", "C": "Accept his decision without telling the physician since he has clearly stated his reasons.", "D": "Delay his decision until the physician confirms that the depression is well controlled."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes mental illness removes decision-making ability", "C": "Omits reporting a major treatment decision to the team", "D": "Makes capacity depend on symptom control"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C218", "cluster_text": "Mr. Haddad, 68, has advanced pancreatic cancer and is cared for on a medical unit with a palliative approach. He has a history of depression treated for several years. He is alert and speaks clearly about his wishes. His adult children visit daily, and several care decisions are discussed during the week. The practical nurse works within regulatory standards and consults the team.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Presume he has capacity and report his decision to the physician for discussion.): Adults are presumed capable, and a mental health history alone does not remove capacity, especially when he gives clear reasons. The nurse reports his decision so the physician can discuss it and assess capacity if real doubts arise. Delaying or involving children treats illness as incapacity. Accepting without reporting leaves the team uninformed."}, {"id": 1865, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Petrov's forearms show thin, fragile skin with several new bruises and a small tear from adhesive tape. He says the prednisone makes his skin paper thin. The practical nurse is planning skin care. Which action best protects his skin and responds to the problem?", "options": {"A": "Use gentle handling, avoid adhesive tape, and report new skin tears to the physician.", "B": "Apply firm tape over the tear to hold the edges together and prevent more bruising.", "C": "Ask the physician to stop the prednisone because these skin changes show it is harming him.", "D": "Massage the bruised areas twice daily to speed the breakdown of the bruises."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Uses adhesive on already fragile skin", "C": "Proposes stopping a steroid abruptly because of a side effect", "D": "Adds friction to bruised, thin skin"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C220", "cluster_text": "Mr. Petrov, 61, takes long-term prednisone for polymyalgia and was recently diagnosed with small-cell lung cancer. He is admitted overnight to a medical unit with weakness and confusion. His face is rounded, his skin is thin with bruising, and his serum sodium is 124 mmol/L. His urine is concentrated. The physician suspects the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone. His family is at home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use gentle handling, avoid adhesive tape, and report new skin tears to the physician.): Long-term corticosteroids thin the skin, so gentle handling and avoiding adhesive protect it, and new tears are reported. Taping the tear adds more adhesive injury. Stopping prednisone abruptly can cause adrenal crisis, and any change belongs to the physician. Massage adds shear to fragile tissue."}, {"id": 1866, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Petrov, whose weakness has improved, tells the practical nurse he will skip his prednisone because he feels well and the face swelling bothers him. He has taken it daily for three years. The nurse sees the plan as risky. What teaching is most important?", "options": {"A": "Long-term steroids suppress his own cortisol, so stopping suddenly can trigger a dangerous crisis.", "B": "Skipping doses on well days is acceptable if he restarts the next morning.", "C": "Stopping is acceptable once the facial swelling resolves, since swelling shows the dose is excessive.", "D": "Prednisone should be taken only when weakness returns, because daily dosing weakens the immune system."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats missed doses on well days as harmless", "C": "Links stopping to cosmetic improvement instead of adrenal function", "D": "Misunderstands steroid dosing as symptom-driven"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C220", "cluster_text": "Mr. Petrov, 61, takes long-term prednisone for polymyalgia and was recently diagnosed with small-cell lung cancer. He is admitted overnight to a medical unit with weakness and confusion. His face is rounded, his skin is thin with bruising, and his serum sodium is 124 mmol/L. His urine is concentrated. The physician suspects the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone. His family is at home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Long-term steroids suppress his own cortisol, so stopping suddenly can trigger a dangerous crisis.): Years of prednisone suppress the adrenal glands, so abrupt withdrawal can cause adrenal crisis with low blood pressure and collapse. Skipping doses or stopping when the swelling improves ignores that suppression. Taking it only when weak turns a regular regimen into an irregular one. Any change must be a gradual taper directed by the prescriber."}, {"id": 1867, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Petrov's serum sodium falls to 120 mmol/L, he is more confused, and urine output is low and concentrated. A colleague suggests encouraging extra oral fluids to improve his weakness. The practical nurse recognizes the pattern of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Encourage fluids as suggested and recheck the sodium level with the next scheduled blood work today.", "B": "Give a dose of the as-needed diuretic and observe urine output for several hours.", "C": "Offer salty snacks and broth to raise sodium while continuing free oral fluids.", "D": "Hold the extra fluids, report the sodium and confusion promptly, and anticipate fluid restriction."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Increases fluids in water-retaining hyponatremia", "B": "Gives a drug without an order or a report", "C": "Offers sodium while continuing excess free water"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C220", "cluster_text": "Mr. Petrov, 61, takes long-term prednisone for polymyalgia and was recently diagnosed with small-cell lung cancer. He is admitted overnight to a medical unit with weakness and confusion. His face is rounded, his skin is thin with bruising, and his serum sodium is 124 mmol/L. His urine is concentrated. The physician suspects the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone. His family is at home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the extra fluids, report the sodium and confusion promptly, and anticipate fluid restriction.): In this syndrome the body retains water and dilutes sodium, so extra fluids worsen confusion and the finding must be reported urgently. Continuing fluids or adding salty foods does not fix excess free water. Giving an as-needed diuretic without a report skips the physician's decision. Restriction, not encouragement, is the usual treatment."}, {"id": 1868, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Diabetes insipidus is on a student's mind after seeing a client with head injury who passed large volumes of pale urine and complained of intense thirst. The student asks the practical nurse how it differs from Mr. Petrov's inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion. Which statement best explains the difference?", "options": {"A": "Both conditions cause dilute urine, and the difference is only the client's blood glucose level.", "B": "Diabetes insipidus involves excess hormone, causing concentrated urine, thirst and low sodium like Mr. Petrov.", "C": "Diabetes insipidus is treated with fluid restriction, which also corrects Mr. Petrov's low sodium.", "D": "Diabetes insipidus lacks hormone action, causing dilute urine, thirst and high sodium, unlike retention."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confuses the condition with diabetes mellitus", "B": "Reverses hormone excess and deficiency", "C": "Applies fluid restriction to the wrong condition"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C220", "cluster_text": "Mr. Petrov, 61, takes long-term prednisone for polymyalgia and was recently diagnosed with small-cell lung cancer. He is admitted overnight to a medical unit with weakness and confusion. His face is rounded, his skin is thin with bruising, and his serum sodium is 124 mmol/L. His urine is concentrated. The physician suspects the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone. His family is at home.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Diabetes insipidus lacks hormone action, causing dilute urine, thirst and high sodium, unlike retention.): Diabetes insipidus is a lack of antidiuretic hormone effect, so the body loses water, giving large dilute urine volumes, thirst and a high sodium. Mr. Petrov has the opposite, water retention with low sodium. Blood glucose does not separate the two, and restricting fluids would be dangerous in diabetes insipidus."}, {"id": 1869, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At 0900 Ms. Greene's blood pressure is 96/58 mmHg with a pulse of 108. She rates incision pain 4 out of 10, reports mild nausea, and has a small amount of pink drainage on the dressing. Her urine output is low. Which finding is the priority for the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Blood pressure well below her usual with a rising pulse and low urine output.", "B": "Mild nausea that makes her prefer a lighter breakfast this morning.", "C": "Incision pain of 4 out of 10 that needs an as-needed analgesic soon.", "D": "Pink drainage on the dressing that is expected after bowel surgery."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Prioritizes a mild symptom over signs of possible shock", "C": "Focuses on pain, an expected finding, over instability", "D": "Treats an expected drainage finding as the main concern"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C222", "cluster_text": "Ms. Greene, 66, is on postoperative day one after a bowel resection on a surgical unit. Her usual blood pressure is 130/80 mmHg. The practical nurse is assigned to her for the day shift and has the surgeon's orders, which call for vital signs every four hours and a call for systolic pressure below 90 mmHg. She is alert, with an abdominal dressing in place.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Blood pressure well below her usual with a rising pulse and low urine output.): A falling pressure with a rising pulse and low urine output is unexpected and suggests bleeding or volume loss, so it takes priority. Moderate incision pain, a little pink drainage and mild nausea are common after bowel surgery and can be addressed after the unstable findings are reported. Treating all abnormal findings as equal delays the urgent one."}, {"id": 1870, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two hours later Ms. Greene's respiratory rate has risen from 22 to 28 per minute, and she is restless with an oxygen saturation of 92 percent. Her blood pressure is 98/60 mmHg, above the call limit in the orders. A colleague suggests waiting for the next scheduled vital signs. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Wait for the blood pressure to fall below the ordered 90 before calling the surgeon today.", "B": "Report the respiratory change now, since early deterioration often precedes low blood pressure.", "C": "Reassure her that restlessness is expected after surgery and encourage deep breathing exercises.", "D": "Increase oxygen to ease the breathing and recheck at the next scheduled vital signs."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses blood pressure alone as the trigger to act", "C": "Dismisses restlessness as normal postoperative behaviour", "D": "Treats the number without reporting a worsening trend"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C222", "cluster_text": "Ms. Greene, 66, is on postoperative day one after a bowel resection on a surgical unit. Her usual blood pressure is 130/80 mmHg. The practical nurse is assigned to her for the day shift and has the surgeon's orders, which call for vital signs every four hours and a call for systolic pressure below 90 mmHg. She is alert, with an abdominal dressing in place.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the respiratory change now, since early deterioration often precedes low blood pressure.): A rising respiratory rate, restlessness and falling saturation are early signs of deterioration that often precede a drop in blood pressure, so reporting now is correct. Waiting for the ordered pressure limit lets her worsen. Raising oxygen and waiting treats a number but not the trend, and calling restlessness normal misses a warning sign."}, {"id": 1871, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The practical nurse phones the surgeon about Ms. Greene. The nurse has stated her name, location and current vital signs. The surgeon sounds rushed and says, \"Just tell me what you need.\" The nurse has reviewed the chart and knows her surgical history. Which statement should end the SBAR report?", "options": {"A": "She had a bowel resection yesterday and has had a steady decline in her urine output.", "B": "I am concerned she is worsening; please assess her within fifteen minutes.", "C": "She seems anxious and uncomfortable, and I think she may be developing a chest infection.", "D": "Her respiratory rate has risen since this morning and her oxygen saturation is now 92 percent."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives background where a recommendation is expected", "C": "Offers a diagnosis outside nursing scope instead of a request", "D": "Restates assessment data without a request"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C222", "cluster_text": "Ms. Greene, 66, is on postoperative day one after a bowel resection on a surgical unit. Her usual blood pressure is 130/80 mmHg. The practical nurse is assigned to her for the day shift and has the surgeon's orders, which call for vital signs every four hours and a call for systolic pressure below 90 mmHg. She is alert, with an abdominal dressing in place.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (I am concerned she is worsening; please assess her within fifteen minutes.): The recommendation states what the nurse wants done and when, here an assessment within fifteen minutes. Surgical history is background, the vital sign trend is assessment, and naming a chest infection is a diagnosis outside the practical nurse's scope. Close an SBAR call with a clear request."}, {"id": 1872, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Later Ms. Greene pulls at her oxygen tubing and shouts at the staff. The practical nurse needs to document the event, and a colleague suggests writing that she was uncooperative and aggressive. The nurse also recorded a respiratory rate of 28. Which entry best follows objective documentation standards?", "options": {"A": "Client appears confused from the surgery and is likely delirious with poor judgment.", "B": "Client was uncooperative and aggressive, refusing oxygen for no reason at 1130 during morning rounds.", "C": "At 1130 client pulled off oxygen tubing and said loudly, \"Leave me alone,\" respirations 28.", "D": "Client became very upset after the earlier phone call and acted out toward staff."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Records a diagnosis the nurse cannot confirm", "B": "Uses opinion words instead of observed behaviour", "D": "Records a guessed cause and judgmental wording"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C222", "cluster_text": "Ms. Greene, 66, is on postoperative day one after a bowel resection on a surgical unit. Her usual blood pressure is 130/80 mmHg. The practical nurse is assigned to her for the day shift and has the surgeon's orders, which call for vital signs every four hours and a call for systolic pressure below 90 mmHg. She is alert, with an abdominal dressing in place.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (At 1130 client pulled off oxygen tubing and said loudly, \"Leave me alone,\" respirations 28.): Objective charting records what was seen and heard, with times and measurements, such as the exact words and respiratory rate. Labels like uncooperative or aggressive are opinions, and delirium is a diagnosis. Guessing a cause also adds interpretation that the chart cannot support. Write facts, not conclusions."}, {"id": 1873, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Dubois, 69, with COPD on a medical unit, says he is short of breath and cannot rest while lying flat. His respiratory rate is 24, and oxygen is running at 2 litres per minute. The practical nurse wants to improve his comfort. Which position is best?", "options": {"A": "Place him in the semi-Fowler's position at thirty degrees with his legs elevated on pillows.", "B": "Place him in a side-lying position with the head of the bed raised just twenty degrees.", "C": "Sit him upright in high Fowler's position, leaning slightly forward over a supported table.", "D": "Lay him flat with one pillow and the bed lowered so his diaphragm can rest."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adds leg elevation that does not ease breathing effort", "B": "Raises the head too little for effective lung expansion", "D": "Lies flat, which compresses the diaphragm in dyspnea"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sit him upright in high Fowler's position, leaning slightly forward over a supported table.): Sitting upright and leaning forward lets the diaphragm drop and the accessory muscles work, which eases dyspnea in COPD. Lying flat pushes abdominal contents against the diaphragm, and twenty or thirty degrees is not enough elevation. Leg elevation does not help breathing effort. Positioning for dyspnea means high, forward and supported."}, {"id": 1874, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A long-term care resident, 84, who has diabetes and took her morning insulin, becomes suddenly confused and sweaty before lunch. Her speech is slurred, and a colleague wonders whether she is having a stroke. The practical nurse has a glucometer at the medication cart. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Check her capillary blood glucose right away, since low glucose can mimic stroke.", "B": "Call emergency services for a suspected stroke and document the time symptoms began.", "C": "Record her vital signs and wait for the lunchtime glucose check before acting.", "D": "Offer a meal and fluids and reassess her speech after she finishes eating."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assumes stroke without excluding a glucose cause", "C": "Waits for a scheduled check during a possible emergency", "D": "Feeds a possibly unsafe confused client before checking glucose"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check her capillary blood glucose right away, since low glucose can mimic stroke.): Sweating, confusion and slurred speech after insulin fit hypoglycemia, which can look like a stroke and is quickly checked and treated. Calling for stroke first delays a simple test. Offering food to a confused resident risks aspiration without confirming the cause, and waiting for the lunchtime check leaves her untreated. Always rule out glucose in sudden confusion."}, {"id": 1875, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Preparing for a sterile dressing change, a practical nurse has opened the glove package onto a clean field and performed hand hygiene. She is about to pick up the first glove, and a colleague asks which part she may touch with a bare hand. Which technique is correct?", "options": {"A": "Touch the outside of the glove with a bare thumb to fit it before donning.", "B": "Grasp the first glove only by the inside of its folded cuff with the bare hand.", "C": "Grasp the first glove by the outer edge of the cuff to avoid touching the inside.", "D": "Pinch the fingers of the glove to lift it, then fold the cuff back."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Contaminates the glove surface while adjusting fit", "C": "Touches the outside of the glove with bare hands", "D": "Handles the sterile fingers with bare skin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Grasp the first glove only by the inside of its folded cuff with the bare hand.): The inside of the cuff is the only part that touches skin, so it is the only part held by the bare hand, keeping the outer surface sterile. Touching the outer edge, the fingers or the outside of the glove contaminates the sterile surface. The second glove is then picked up by its outside using the gloved hand. Bare hands stay on the inside only."}, {"id": 1876, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Tran, 67, comes to a community clinic six weeks after her husband's death. She cries daily, sleeps poorly, has a reduced appetite and sometimes thinks she hears him in the house. She still cares for her cat and attends church weekly. What should the practical nurse conclude?", "options": {"A": "These findings suggest a depressive disorder, so refer her urgently for psychiatric assessment.", "B": "These are common grief responses, so offer support and monitor for lasting or worsening symptoms.", "C": "Hearing him in the house signals psychosis, so the nurse should report this immediately.", "D": "Intense sadness at six weeks shows complicated grief, so a grief counselling referral is required."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Labels normal grief as a depressive disorder", "C": "Treats a common grief experience as psychosis", "D": "Calls grief complicated too early"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (These are common grief responses, so offer support and monitor for lasting or worsening symptoms.): Crying, poor sleep, appetite change and brief sensory experiences of the deceased are common early in bereavement, and she is still functioning in daily roles. Urgent psychiatric referral, a psychosis label or a diagnosis of complicated grief at six weeks all overcall a normal course. Offer support and watch for persistent or worsening impairment."}, {"id": 1877, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In a long-term care home, a practical nurse delegates repositioning every two hours to a personal support worker for an 88-year-old at risk of pressure injury. Later the worker reports that the resident's heel looks red. The nurse wonders about responsibility. Which statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Accountability transfers to the worker once the task is accepted, so the worker assesses the heel.", "B": "The delegating nurse is accountable only if the worker lacks the training for the task", "C": "The nurse remains accountable for outcomes and must assess the heel after delegating the task.", "D": "The nurse and worker share accountability equally, so the worker decides whether to assess"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believes accountability moves entirely to the worker", "B": "Limiting accountability to the worker's training adequacy", "D": "Believing shared accountability ends the nurse's follow-up duty"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The nurse remains accountable for outcomes and must assess the heel after delegating the task.): Delegating a task does not delegate accountability for the client's outcome, so the nurse assesses the red heel and follows up. Treating accountability as moved to the worker, shared equally, or limited to training ignores continuing responsibility for monitoring. The manager shares organizational duties but does not replace the delegating nurse's accountability. Delegate the task, keep the oversight."}, {"id": 1878, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "During a home visit to an adult with a stage 3 pressure injury, the wound care nurse leaves instructions for the practical nurse, Priya, to perform sharp debridement of loose slough with a scalpel. Priya has not learned this technique, and no order or policy mentions it. What should Priya do?", "options": {"A": "Decline the task, explain her competence limit, and ask for a qualified provider.", "B": "Watch the wound care nurse once and then perform debridement independently at later visits.", "C": "Perform it with the wound care nurse guiding her by phone", "D": "Complete a self-directed online module the same day, then perform the debridement"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relies on a single observation for independent practice", "C": "Treating phone guidance as a substitute for competence", "D": "Assuming brief self-study creates authorization"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C217", "cluster_text": "Priya, a practical nurse, joins a community health centre that provides home care, an immunization clinic and postpartum visits. Her employer has given her the regulatory body's scope of practice standards and the centre's policies. Her manager explains that assignments depend on each client's needs and on her competence, and that she should speak up when a task is beyond her competence.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline the task, explain her competence limit, and ask for a qualified provider.): Practical nurses perform only skills they have been educated for and are authorized to do, so Priya declines, states her limit and asks for a qualified provider. An instruction or phone guidance from a colleague does not create competence. One observation or a same-day online module is not adequate preparation. Scope depends on competence and authorization together."}, {"id": 1879, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A client at the immunization clinic asks Priya for a routine tetanus booster. Priya knows the vaccine is routine, but she cannot find the signed medical directive or an individual order in the clinic folder. The vaccine is drawn up and the client is waiting. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the client to sign a form accepting responsibility and then give the vaccine.", "B": "Give the booster and write the order afterward once the physician arrives.", "C": "Give the booster because it is routine and the client has requested it.", "D": "Locate the directive or obtain an order, and complete screening before giving the vaccine."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Shifts responsibility to the client with a signed form", "B": "Gives the drug first and seeks authorization afterward", "C": "Treats a routine vaccine as needing no authorization"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C217", "cluster_text": "Priya, a practical nurse, joins a community health centre that provides home care, an immunization clinic and postpartum visits. Her employer has given her the regulatory body's scope of practice standards and the centre's policies. Her manager explains that assignments depend on each client's needs and on her competence, and that she should speak up when a task is beyond her competence.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Locate the directive or obtain an order, and complete screening before giving the vaccine.): Administering a vaccine needs a valid order or directive and a screening for contraindications, so Priya confirms authority first. Routine status does not remove that requirement. Giving the vaccine and obtaining the order afterward reverses the order of safe practice, and a client's signature cannot replace authorization. Check authority, screen, then give."}, {"id": 1880, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "An adult client at the centre asks Priya for a copy of her health record, saying she wants to take it to another clinic. A colleague says the records belong to the nurse who wrote them. The centre's policy describes a request process. Which response reflects accurate principles?", "options": {"A": "Records can be released only to the physician, who then decides what the client sees.", "B": "The facility keeps the record, and the client has a right of access through its process.", "C": "The client owns the paper chart and may remove it from the centre today.", "D": "The nurse who wrote the entries owns them and may release them at her discretion."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Blocks client access by routing it only through the physician", "C": "Thinks the client may take the original chart", "D": "Believes an individual nurse owns entries she charted"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C217", "cluster_text": "Priya, a practical nurse, joins a community health centre that provides home care, an immunization clinic and postpartum visits. Her employer has given her the regulatory body's scope of practice standards and the centre's policies. Her manager explains that assignments depend on each client's needs and on her competence, and that she should speak up when a task is beyond her competence.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The facility keeps the record, and the client has a right of access through its process.): Health records are held by the facility, and clients have a right to access their information through the organization's request process. Individual nurses do not own the entries they chart. The original chart stays with the facility, and clients do not need the physician to approve access. Follow the policy and support the request."}, {"id": 1881, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Fever and a sore throat bring a 6-year-old boy to the centre with his mother. Priya has experience mainly with adults and is the only nurse in the room, though her manager is on site. A colleague claims practical nurses cannot care for children in any setting. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Decline to see the child because practical nurses cannot assess children in community settings.", "B": "Give acetaminophen by adult dose and send the family home without further assessment", "C": "Assess him alone and discharge with advice, since fever and sore throat are routine", "D": "Care within her competence and policy, consult the manager or RN, and refer if needed."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Misreads scope as barring all paediatric care", "B": "Using an adult dose and skipping consultation", "C": "Treating paediatric fever as routine without consulting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C217", "cluster_text": "Priya, a practical nurse, joins a community health centre that provides home care, an immunization clinic and postpartum visits. Her employer has given her the regulatory body's scope of practice standards and the centre's policies. Her manager explains that assignments depend on each client's needs and on her competence, and that she should speak up when a task is beyond her competence.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Care within her competence and policy, consult the manager or RN, and refer if needed.): Scope of practice depends on individual competence, employer policy and client needs rather than a blanket age exclusion, so Priya works within what she can do and consults. Refusing outright misunderstands scope. Giving medication by an adult dose or discharging without consultation ignores child differences and her limited paediatric experience. Consult and refer when the need exceeds competence."}, {"id": 1882, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a postpartum home visit, Priya finds a new mother who has soaked two pads in one hour, feels dizzy and has a pulse of 112. A colleague says practical nurses have no role in maternal-newborn care. What should Priya do first?", "options": {"A": "Leave the case to the maternal-newborn nurse because the task falls outside practical nursing care.", "B": "Reassure her that heavy lochia is normal and review the next visit plan.", "C": "Stay with her, call emergency services and report the bleeding and findings urgently.", "D": "Massage the abdomen, then reassess the bleeding after thirty minutes before reporting."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withdraws from an emergency by misreading scope", "B": "Dismisses signs of hemorrhage as normal", "D": "Delays reporting while trying one intervention"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C217", "cluster_text": "Priya, a practical nurse, joins a community health centre that provides home care, an immunization clinic and postpartum visits. Her employer has given her the regulatory body's scope of practice standards and the centre's policies. Her manager explains that assignments depend on each client's needs and on her competence, and that she should speak up when a task is beyond her competence.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stay with her, call emergency services and report the bleeding and findings urgently.): Heavy bleeding with dizziness and a rapid pulse suggests postpartum hemorrhage, an emergency in which any nurse stays, calls for help and reports. Maternal-newborn care is within practical nursing scope when competent, and leaving is unsafe. Delaying a report to try massage lets the bleeding continue. Calling heavy lochia normal misses shock signs."}, {"id": 1883, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An 80-year-old in hospice tells the practical nurse, \"I am afraid of dying.\" His daughter is not present, and the nurse has twenty minutes before the next task. The nurse feels uneasy and wants to say something comforting. Which response best shows therapeutic presence?", "options": {"A": "Reassure him that dying is peaceful and that fear is not necessary.", "B": "Sit beside him, acknowledge his fear, and invite him to say more about it.", "C": "Tell him he is looking much better today and that many clients feel this way.", "D": "Change the subject to his grandchildren to lift his spirits and reduce his fear."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives premature reassurance that dismisses his feelings", "C": "Offers false cheer instead of listening", "D": "Avoids the fear by redirecting the topic"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sit beside him, acknowledge his fear, and invite him to say more about it.): Therapeutic presence means staying with the client, naming his fear and inviting him to share, which respects his experience. False improvement claims, changing the subject and blanket reassurance all shut the conversation down. The nurse does not need to fix the fear to be helpful. Listen first and let him lead."}, {"id": 1884, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A hospital's patient advisory council asks the unit practical nurse whether clients and families can help review falls on the unit. A colleague says quality work is for staff only because clients lack the knowledge. The nurse has seen feedback cards mention call-bell delays. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Include clients and families, since their experiences reveal problems staff may miss.", "B": "Invite families to join only after the unit has finished analyzing the falls data.", "C": "Include family members only for presentations, and keep falls data review to staff", "D": "Involve clients only if the manager first confirms they have patient safety training"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Involves clients too late to shape the analysis", "C": "Offering token involvement without real participation", "D": "Requiring formal training before client participation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Include clients and families, since their experiences reveal problems staff may miss.): Clients and families bring first-hand experience of delays and risks, and involving them early improves the quality work. Treating quality as staff-only or requiring formal training first misses that insight. Joining after analysis or only for presentations limits their influence. Partnership means inclusion from the start."}, {"id": 1885, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a community advice line, a nurse speaks with a caller reporting a child's fever of 39.5 C and a rash. The call lasts eight minutes, and the caller hangs up. A colleague says no record is required because no visit occurred. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Record the call only if the caller later returns or a complaint is received.", "B": "Document the caller's information, the advice given, the referral and the time of the call.", "C": "Record the call only if the nurse gave a referral to emergency services", "D": "Log the call on the unit's phone sheet with the time and caller's concern"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Documents only when a problem arises later", "C": "Documenting only when a referral is made", "D": "Using an informal log that omits advice and referral"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Document the caller's information, the advice given, the referral and the time of the call.): Telephone advice is nursing care, so the information received, advice given, referral and time are recorded in the official record. Charting only after a complaint or only when a referral is made leaves the advice unsupported, and an informal phone sheet omits the advice and referral. If it was not documented, it cannot be shown to have been done."}, {"id": 1886, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a client's home, a personal support worker asks the practical nurse whether she may give the client's daily oral pills from a blister pack while the nurse is away. The agency's policy allows delegation only with a documented process and training. The pills include warfarin. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Delegate only the pills other than warfarin and give the warfarin herself later.", "B": "Permit it once the client says she is comfortable with the worker giving them.", "C": "Agree because oral medications are low risk and the worker watches the client swallow each pill.", "D": "Follow the agency's delegation policy and assess whether the task, client and worker qualify."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Chooses by drug rather than applying the policy", "B": "Uses client comfort in place of a delegation process", "C": "Assumes oral routes are safe to delegate"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Follow the agency's delegation policy and assess whether the task, client and worker qualify.): Delegation depends on the policy, the client's stability, the worker's training and the task, not on the route of administration. Calling oral drugs low risk ignores high-alert drugs like warfarin. Splitting by drug skips the process, and client comfort does not authorize delegation. Apply the agency's criteria before delegating."}, {"id": 1887, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 37-year-old on an inpatient mental health unit is going home tomorrow after a depressive episode. The practical nurse is reviewing discharge teaching with him. He has his medication list and crisis phone numbers. His community follow-up appointment has not been booked. Which discharge plan element is most important to complete?", "options": {"A": "Give him a written copy of the unit's daily schedule to maintain his routine at home.", "B": "Provide the crisis numbers again and advise him to call if symptoms return.", "C": "Book the follow-up appointment with his community provider and confirm he knows when and where.", "D": "Review the diagnosis in detail so he understands the cause of the depressive episode."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offers a routine aid instead of arranging follow-up", "B": "Repeats information already given without arranging follow-up", "D": "Spends discharge time on explanation rather than continuity of care"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Book the follow-up appointment with his community provider and confirm he knows when and where.): Early follow-up after discharge is a key protection against relapse, so a booked appointment he understands is the missing element. A unit schedule and a diagnosis review are helpful extras but do not secure continuity. Repeating the crisis numbers duplicates what he already has. Close the gap in the discharge plan."}, {"id": 1888, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Preparing for an annual competence review at a community clinic, a practical nurse hears a colleague say a learning portfolio is needed only if a complaint is made. The nurse has attended two workshops and reflected on a medication error. What is the purpose of a personal learning portfolio?", "options": {"A": "It is a disciplinary file that the regulator reviews after a complaint is filed.", "B": "It records self-assessment, learning goals and activities to show ongoing competence.", "C": "It is a certificate issued by the regulator after completing education hours", "D": "It lists procedures performed so the employer can assign duties"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Mistakes a development tool for a disciplinary record", "C": "Confusing a portfolio with a regulator credential", "D": "Reducing it to a procedure log for employers"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It records self-assessment, learning goals and activities to show ongoing competence.): A learning portfolio documents the nurse's own self-assessment, goals and learning, including reflection on practice events, and is used to demonstrate ongoing competence. It is not triggered by complaints, and it is not a disciplinary file, a credential from the regulator, or a procedure log for the employer. Keep it current every year rather than only when asked."}, {"id": 1889, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the long-term care home, Dana finds a resident's yoghurt and leftover soup in the staff room refrigerator beside staff lunches. The resident's family brought them in that morning. The refrigerator has no temperature log, and no label shows a date. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Discard only the soup because yoghurt is a commercial product and stays safe.", "B": "Move the food to the resident refrigerator with a temperature log.", "C": "Leave the food where it is and ask staff to keep their own items separate.", "D": "Warm the soup in the microwave before serving and keep it at room temperature."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Discards by guesswork about risk", "C": "Leaves resident food in an unmonitored shared refrigerator", "D": "Holds food at room temperature after reheating"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C226", "cluster_text": "Dana, a practical nurse, works at a health campus that includes a long-term care home, a medical unit and a small newborn nursery. The infection prevention and control team has circulated reminders about routine practices and reporting. Dana moves between areas during the shift and is expected to follow facility policy and report concerns promptly. Hand hygiene supplies are stocked in every area.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Move the food to the resident refrigerator with a temperature log.): Resident food belongs in a monitored refrigerator with labels so temperature and dating can be checked. Leaving it in an unmonitored staff fridge risks spoilage and cross-contamination. Discarding by guess or leaving warmed soup at room temperature ignores safe holding temperatures. Store, label and monitor."}, {"id": 1890, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The hospital water system has an alert after two clients on the medical unit developed pneumonia within a week. Dana notes both used the same shower room. A colleague says pneumonia is common and the cluster can be ignored. What should Dana do?", "options": {"A": "Move the two clients to another unit and keep using the shower room for others.", "B": "Document the cases in the chart and wait for the physician to raise concerns.", "C": "Report the cluster to infection prevention and control and follow instructions about water use.", "D": "Advise clients to use only bottled water for drinking and continue showers as usual."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Moves cases but leaves the possible source in use", "B": "Waits for others to report an outbreak signal", "D": "Limits drinking water while ignoring aerosol exposure from showers"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C226", "cluster_text": "Dana, a practical nurse, works at a health campus that includes a long-term care home, a medical unit and a small newborn nursery. The infection prevention and control team has circulated reminders about routine practices and reporting. Dana moves between areas during the shift and is expected to follow facility policy and report concerns promptly. Hand hygiene supplies are stocked in every area.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the cluster to infection prevention and control and follow instructions about water use.): Two pneumonia cases linked to a shared water source during a water alert may signal Legionella, so the nurse reports the cluster and follows water-use instructions. Charting alone, moving clients while the shower stays open, or switching only drinking water leaves the source active. Clusters are reported, not assumed to be coincidence."}, {"id": 1891, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A resident with known infectious diarrhea dies in the care home. Dana prepares the body while the family waits to visit, and the unit has posted an outbreak reminder. A student says routine precautions can stop because the resident has died. Which approach is correct?", "options": {"A": "Continue appropriate precautions, including gloves and gown, and follow the facility's procedure.", "B": "Seal the body in a bag at once and prevent the family from seeing the resident.", "C": "Stop isolation precautions because infectious organisms cannot survive for long after the death occurs.", "D": "Use only gloves since the body poses no splash risk to the nurse."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Overreacts by barring family without a policy reason", "C": "Believes organisms die when the client dies", "D": "Understates the exposure risk during care"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C226", "cluster_text": "Dana, a practical nurse, works at a health campus that includes a long-term care home, a medical unit and a small newborn nursery. The infection prevention and control team has circulated reminders about routine practices and reporting. Dana moves between areas during the shift and is expected to follow facility policy and report concerns promptly. Hand hygiene supplies are stocked in every area.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continue appropriate precautions, including gloves and gown, and follow the facility's procedure.): Organisms can remain infectious after death, so the same precautions apply during after-death care. Stopping them, using gloves alone or sealing the body and barring the family are not supported. Family viewing is usually possible with guidance. Follow the facility procedure and standard practices."}, {"id": 1892, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the newborn nursery, a nurse reports a small cold sore on her lip that she says is healing. She is assigned to feed and hold infants today. Dana is the charge practical nurse, and the nursery has three newborns under one week old. What should Dana do?", "options": {"A": "Allow her to continue if she washes her hands before and after touching each infant", "B": "Reassign her away from direct infant contact and report to infection control", "C": "Permit her to continue if she limits feeding to bottle-fed infants.", "D": "Let her work with older infants only, since newborns are most at risk."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relies on hand hygiene alone against herpes virus transmission", "C": "Limits only one type of contact and keeps exposure possible", "D": "Splits risk by age while keeping infant contact"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C226", "cluster_text": "Dana, a practical nurse, works at a health campus that includes a long-term care home, a medical unit and a small newborn nursery. The infection prevention and control team has circulated reminders about routine practices and reporting. Dana moves between areas during the shift and is expected to follow facility policy and report concerns promptly. Hand hygiene supplies are stocked in every area.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reassign her away from direct infant contact and report to infection control): Herpes simplex from a cold sore can cause severe infection in newborns, so reassigning her away from infant contact and reporting is the safe course. Hand washing alone does not prevent transmission from a lesion, and restricting to older infants or bottle feeding still allows contact. Protect the most vulnerable first."}, {"id": 1893, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During morning checks at a community clinic, the practical nurse finds the vaccine refrigerator reading 11.2 C, and its door was slightly ajar. The log showed 4 C yesterday afternoon. Two clients are waiting for scheduled immunizations, and the supervisor is in a meeting down the hall. What is the best action now?", "options": {"A": "Close the door, wait one hour for the temperature to recover, then resume giving the vaccines", "B": "Give the scheduled immunizations as planned and record the high temperature reading in the log afterward", "C": "Discard every vaccine in the refrigerator right away and document the number of doses thrown out", "D": "Label the vaccines do not use and report the temperature excursion to the supervisor"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming a brief recovery restores vaccine integrity", "B": "Using product of unknown potency and documenting later", "C": "Destroying stock before the supervisor or manufacturer assesses viability"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Label the vaccines do not use and report the temperature excursion to the supervisor): A reading of 11.2 C is far outside the cold chain range, so the vaccines must be set aside and the excursion reported so the supervisor can contact the manufacturer or public health about viability. Giving doses now risks administering a weakened product, and documenting afterward does not undo that. Waiting for the temperature to recover does not restore potency already affected. Discarding stock too early wastes vaccine that may still be usable after review."}, {"id": 1894, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Volkov tells the nurse two weeks after starting his antidepressant, 'This pill is not helping, so I want to stop.' His mood is unchanged, he is eating poorly, and he has no thoughts of self-harm. He asks what to expect from the medication. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Support his decision to stop, since two weeks without any change shows the drug is ineffective", "B": "Tell him the antidepressant should work within a day or two, so something else is wrong", "C": "Suggest he double the daily dose for several days to see whether his mood improves faster", "D": "Explain that benefit often takes several weeks, and report his concerns to the prescriber"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Judging drug failure before a full trial period", "B": "Believing antidepressants act within days", "C": "Self-adjusting a dose without an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C221", "cluster_text": "Dmitri Volkov, 74, lives in a seniors' residence and receives community nursing visits. His history includes hypertension, overactive bladder treated with oxybutynin, and bone pain from metastatic prostate cancer treated with sustained-release morphine. His physician started an antidepressant two weeks ago for low mood. A practical nurse visits weekly, reports to the RN case manager, and reviews his medications at each visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that benefit often takes several weeks, and report his concerns to the prescriber): Antidepressants usually need about two to six weeks to reach full effect, so two weeks without change is not evidence of failure, and the prescriber should hear his concerns. Stopping early would end a trial that has barely begun. Doubling the dose is outside the nurse's role and unsafe. Claiming a day or two is wrong and would set him up for more disappointment."}, {"id": 1895, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Over three days Mr. Volkov develops a very dry mouth, new constipation, and mild confusion at supper. His vital signs are stable and his pain is controlled. The nurse reviews his medications, which include oxybutynin for bladder urgency. Which drug action best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Morphine blocks acetylcholine receptors in the gut and brain, which explains all three findings together", "B": "Reduced fluid intake from low mood explains the dry mouth, constipation, and confusion", "C": "Oxybutynin blocks acetylcholine, which dries secretions, slows the bowel, and clouds thinking", "D": "The antidepressant raises acetylcholine levels, causing dry mouth and confusion as early expected effects"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Attributing an anticholinergic mechanism to an opioid", "B": "Choosing a plausible non-drug cause over the drug effect", "D": "Reversing the direction of cholinergic action"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C221", "cluster_text": "Dmitri Volkov, 74, lives in a seniors' residence and receives community nursing visits. His history includes hypertension, overactive bladder treated with oxybutynin, and bone pain from metastatic prostate cancer treated with sustained-release morphine. His physician started an antidepressant two weeks ago for low mood. A practical nurse visits weekly, reports to the RN case manager, and reviews his medications at each visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Oxybutynin blocks acetylcholine, which dries secretions, slows the bowel, and clouds thinking): Oxybutynin is an anticholinergic, and blocking acetylcholine produces dry mouth, slowed bowel activity, and confusion, which older adults tolerate poorly. Morphine does constipate, but through opioid receptors rather than acetylcholine blockade, and it does not explain the dry mouth. Raised acetylcholine would cause the opposite picture, with more secretions. Low fluid intake is possible, but it would not be the best explanation given the drug on his list."}, {"id": 1896, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Volkov's bone pain rose from 4 to 7 out of 10 over three weeks, though his morphine dose has stayed the same, and he now asks for higher doses. His daughter worries he is becoming addicted. He has new back tenderness. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's report?", "options": {"A": "Needing more medication shows addiction, so the nurse should withhold the next dose until reassessed", "B": "Tolerance or disease progression may explain the pain, so report it for prescriber reassessment", "C": "The same dose should continue since tolerance does not develop with opioids taken for cancer pain", "D": "The pain is likely psychological from his low mood, so reassurance is the best response"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Equating dose escalation with addiction", "C": "Denying that opioid tolerance occurs", "D": "Attributing physical pain to mood without assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C221", "cluster_text": "Dmitri Volkov, 74, lives in a seniors' residence and receives community nursing visits. His history includes hypertension, overactive bladder treated with oxybutynin, and bone pain from metastatic prostate cancer treated with sustained-release morphine. His physician started an antidepressant two weeks ago for low mood. A practical nurse visits weekly, reports to the RN case manager, and reviews his medications at each visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tolerance or disease progression may explain the pain, so report it for prescriber reassessment): Rising pain on a stable opioid dose can reflect physical tolerance or progression of the cancer, and the new back tenderness makes progression a real possibility, so the prescriber needs the findings. Tolerance is a normal physical response and is not addiction. Withholding doses leaves pain untreated. Calling the pain psychological skips the assessment, and tolerance does occur with long-term opioid use."}, {"id": 1897, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Volkov plans to buy a cold remedy containing pseudoephedrine for nasal congestion. His blood pressure today is 168/96 mmHg, and he takes an antihypertensive. He asks the nurse whether the product is fine to use. What should the nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Take it only at bedtime so the blood pressure effect is lower overnight", "B": "Use it at the lowest labelled dose, since short courses do not noticeably affect blood pressure", "C": "Avoid it because it can raise blood pressure, and ask the pharmacist about safer options", "D": "Use the product but check blood pressure daily and stop only if headaches develop"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing timing removes the pressor effect", "B": "Assuming low doses are harmless in hypertension", "D": "Waiting for symptoms before stopping a risky drug"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C221", "cluster_text": "Dmitri Volkov, 74, lives in a seniors' residence and receives community nursing visits. His history includes hypertension, overactive bladder treated with oxybutynin, and bone pain from metastatic prostate cancer treated with sustained-release morphine. His physician started an antidepressant two weeks ago for low mood. A practical nurse visits weekly, reports to the RN case manager, and reviews his medications at each visit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Avoid it because it can raise blood pressure, and ask the pharmacist about safer options): Pseudoephedrine constricts blood vessels and can push an already elevated pressure higher, so he should avoid it and ask the pharmacist about alternatives. Even short courses at low doses can raise blood pressure. Taking it at night does not cancel the effect. Monitoring and stopping only when headaches appear accepts avoidable risk, and headache may come late or not at all."}, {"id": 1898, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Three months after abdominal surgery, a 36-year-old returns with a firm, raised, pink scar that is itchy but not warm, draining, or painful. She is afraid the incision is infected. The scar stays within the original incision line. Which understanding should the nurse share?", "options": {"A": "Itching and redness mean the wound is healing poorly, so dressings should be changed daily", "B": "This is a keloid, which grows beyond the incision margins, so expect it to enlarge", "C": "Raised scar tissue signals a deep infection, so she needs antibiotics and wound cultures soon", "D": "Likely hypertrophic scarring from excess collagen, so report it and discuss scar treatment"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating itching as a sign of poor healing", "B": "Calling a scar keloid despite staying within the incision line", "C": "Confusing scar tissue with infection"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Likely hypertrophic scarring from excess collagen, so report it and discuss scar treatment): A raised, itchy scar that stays within the incision line and lacks warmth or drainage fits hypertrophic scarring, which is excess collagen and not infection. The nurse should reassure her and report it so options such as silicone or pressure can be considered. A keloid extends beyond the original wound edges, so the boundary detail separates the two. Infection would bring warmth, drainage, or fever, and daily dressings would not treat a scar."}, {"id": 1899, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "'We always do it this way,' says a peer when a hospital practical nurse is asked to perform a procedure she has not done recently. The unit has a written policy for the procedure, and the RN is on break for twenty minutes. What is the best way to decide how to proceed?", "options": {"A": "Perform it from the written policy alone and ask the RN to check afterward", "B": "Ask the peer to talk her through the steps and then proceed", "C": "Review the current policy and own competence, and consult the RN before performing it", "D": "Wait for the next shift and ask the incoming nurse which approach the unit prefers"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Acting before checking competence and consulting the RN", "B": "Relying on peer habit instead of policy and the RN", "D": "Deferring to a colleague instead of the policy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Review the current policy and own competence, and consult the RN before performing it): Decisions should rest on current policy and evidence plus an honest check of her own competence, with the RN consulted before she acts. Peer habit may be outdated and is not a safe authority, and a peer walk-through does not replace the policy. Acting from the policy alone and checking afterward reverses the safe order. Waiting a full shift to ask another nurse still avoids the policy and may delay care."}, {"id": 1900, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Lip swelling and hives have appeared on a 7-year-old who just ate lunch from a friend's tray, and the school nurse is called. He has a prescribed epinephrine auto-injector in his backpack and says his throat feels tight. He is still breathing quietly. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Call his parents for permission to use the auto-injector, then administer it if they agree", "B": "Give an oral antihistamine and watch him for fifteen minutes before deciding about epinephrine", "C": "Administer the epinephrine auto-injector into the outer thigh and call emergency services", "D": "Place him upright, apply oxygen, and wait for symptoms to worsen before using the auto-injector"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Seeking consent while anaphylaxis progresses", "B": "Choosing a slow drug for a rapid airway threat", "D": "Delaying epinephrine until deterioration"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Administer the epinephrine auto-injector into the outer thigh and call emergency services): Hives, swelling, and a tight throat after a likely allergen exposure indicate anaphylaxis, and prompt intramuscular epinephrine in the outer thigh is the first-line treatment, followed by emergency services. Antihistamines act too slowly to protect the airway. Waiting for worsening wastes the window in which epinephrine works best. His prescription already covers use, so calling parents first delays lifesaving treatment."}, {"id": 1901, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Reflux has kept a 58-year-old on omeprazole daily for four years, and at the clinic he asks to continue it indefinitely, saying it is harmless. The nurse practitioner asked the nurse to teach about long-term use during this visit. What should the nurse include?", "options": {"A": "Long-term use mainly raises infection risk, so he should take a probiotic daily", "B": "Long-term use mainly harms the kidneys, so periodic urine tests are the only monitoring needed", "C": "Long-term use can affect bone, magnesium, and B12, so the prescriber should review need", "D": "Long-term use can cause low calcium, so he should start a calcium supplement himself"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Naming one risk and adding an unordered remedy", "B": "Naming one organ risk and limiting monitoring", "D": "Self-starting a supplement instead of seeking prescriber review"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C234", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse floats between a community clinic and the adjoining emergency department during one long shift. A nurse practitioner covers both areas and expects the nurse to check medications against safe-use principles, teach clients, and report concerns before giving drugs. The shift includes clients with reflux, nausea, epilepsy, and bone loss, and each client needs teaching or a safety check.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Long-term use can affect bone, magnesium, and B12, so the prescriber should review need): Prolonged proton pump inhibitor therapy is linked to lower magnesium and B12, reduced calcium absorption with fracture risk, and higher infection risk, so the prescriber should periodically confirm it is still needed. Naming one risk and adding a probiotic or a self-started supplement does not replace that review. Monitoring through urine tests alone misses several concerns."}, {"id": 1902, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the emergency department a 66-year-old with heart failure and nausea has intravenous ondansetron ordered. His potassium is 3.2 mmol/L, and he takes a diuretic. The ECG tracing on the monitor shows a prolonged QT interval. What should the nurse do before giving it?", "options": {"A": "Replace the ordered drug with an oral antihistamine the nurse selects from unit stock", "B": "Hold the dose and report the prolonged QT and low potassium to the physician", "C": "Give the dose slowly over five minutes since nausea relief is the immediate priority", "D": "Give the dose and repeat the ECG after the infusion to check for any changes"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Substituting a drug without an order", "C": "Giving a QT-prolonging drug despite risk factors", "D": "Checking for harm only after exposure to a QT-prolonging drug"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C234", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse floats between a community clinic and the adjoining emergency department during one long shift. A nurse practitioner covers both areas and expects the nurse to check medications against safe-use principles, teach clients, and report concerns before giving drugs. The shift includes clients with reflux, nausea, epilepsy, and bone loss, and each client needs teaching or a safety check.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold the dose and report the prolonged QT and low potassium to the physician): Ondansetron can prolong the QT interval, and low potassium plus heart disease raise the risk of a dangerous rhythm, so the physician must review the order before it is given. Slowing the infusion does not remove the risk. Checking the ECG afterward reacts to harm that could be prevented. Choosing another drug is a prescribing decision outside the nurse's role."}, {"id": 1903, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Seizures are well controlled and she has had no side effects, says a 23-year-old at the clinic who takes carbamazepine for epilepsy and asks about her new combined oral contraceptive. The nurse practitioner wants medication teaching before she leaves. What should the nurse teach about the two medications?", "options": {"A": "Carbamazepine can make the contraceptive less effective, so report it and discuss other options", "B": "Taking the pill at the same time daily keeps contraception reliable with carbamazepine", "C": "Add a condom only for the first month, then rely on the pill alone", "D": "Carbamazepine levels rise with contraceptives, so she should watch for drowsiness, dizziness, and double vision"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believing consistent timing overcomes enzyme induction", "C": "Assuming a short backup period resolves the interaction", "D": "Reversing which drug's level the interaction changes"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C234", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse floats between a community clinic and the adjoining emergency department during one long shift. A nurse practitioner covers both areas and expects the nurse to check medications against safe-use principles, teach clients, and report concerns before giving drugs. The shift includes clients with reflux, nausea, epilepsy, and bone loss, and each client needs teaching or a safety check.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Carbamazepine can make the contraceptive less effective, so report it and discuss other options): Carbamazepine induces liver enzymes that break down hormones faster, so a standard contraceptive may fail, and the prescriber should adjust the method. Consistent pill timing or a brief condom backup does not overcome that effect. The interaction lowers hormone levels rather than raising carbamazepine levels. Report the interaction rather than adjusting doses herself."}, {"id": 1904, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Starting an oral bisphosphonate for osteoporosis, a 71-year-old at the clinic asks how to take it. She usually has breakfast in bed and a nap after. The nurse practitioner wants the nurse to teach the correct administration. What should the nurse say?", "options": {"A": "Take it with a full glass of water, then lie down to avoid dizziness", "B": "Take it with a sip of water after a snack and stay upright", "C": "Take it with orange juice after lunch and rest in a recliner for an hour", "D": "Take it first with a full glass of water, stay upright, and wait before eating"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Lying down after the dose raises esophageal injury risk", "B": "Taking food and too little water reduces absorption and clearance", "C": "Using milk, which binds the drug, and lying down"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C234", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse floats between a community clinic and the adjoining emergency department during one long shift. A nurse practitioner covers both areas and expects the nurse to check medications against safe-use principles, teach clients, and report concerns before giving drugs. The shift includes clients with reflux, nausea, epilepsy, and bone loss, and each client needs teaching or a safety check.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Take it first with a full glass of water, stay upright, and wait before eating): Oral bisphosphonates can injure the esophagus and are poorly absorbed with food or calcium, so she should take them on an empty stomach with a full glass of plain water, stay upright for at least thirty minutes, and wait before eating. Lying down after a dose raises the risk of reflux and esophagitis, even with enough water. A snack and a small sip cancel both safeguards. Juice and a meal timing also reduce absorption."}, {"id": 1905, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Realizing she missed a scheduled set of vital signs on a client who turned out to be stable, a practical nurse on a medical unit tells her manager. No harm occurred and no one noticed. The manager invites her to set learning goals afterward. What is the purpose of a learning goal here?", "options": {"A": "To satisfy a requirement that applies after a client has been harmed", "B": "To give the manager a documented reason to reduce her future patient assignments", "C": "To close the gap in practice behind the omission and lower the chance it recurs", "D": "To confirm the missed vital signs were reported on time to the regulatory body"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing goals apply only after harm", "B": "Treating a learning goal as a restriction tool", "D": "Confusing a learning goal with a regulatory report"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (To close the gap in practice behind the omission and lower the chance it recurs): A near miss exposes a weakness in practice or in the system, and a learning goal turns that information into improvement, such as a reminder routine or a workload check. It is a growth tool and not a disciplinary record, a restriction on assignments, or a regulatory report. Reflective goals are useful whether or not harm occurred. A goal tied to harm alone misses the lesson the near miss offers."}, {"id": 1906, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Tearful and hopeless since a job loss, a 31-year-old tells a practical nurse in a community clinic that she may not want to go on. The mental health nurse is out for the day. What is the best action within the practical nurse's role?", "options": {"A": "Give her a crisis line number and let her leave, as she is not in danger", "B": "Book her with the mental health nurse for next week since crisis care is their role", "C": "Stay with her, ask directly about suicidal thoughts, and involve the RN or NP now", "D": "Reassure her that job loss is temporary and ask her to return if feelings persist"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming safety without asking", "B": "Deferring a crisis to a specialist", "D": "Offering reassurance instead of assessing risk"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stay with her, ask directly about suicidal thoughts, and involve the RN or NP now): Crisis response is part of practical nursing, and a hint of suicidal thinking calls for staying with the client, asking directly about intent, and bringing in the RN or NP at once. Waiting a week leaves a possibly high-risk client alone. Safety cannot be assumed without asking. Reassurance alone does not assess risk and may close the conversation."}, {"id": 1907, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a clinic a practical nurse prepares an injectable for a 45-year-old from a multi-dose vial. The label shows it was opened 35 days ago, with a 28-day discard date written by a colleague, and the manufacturer's expiry is next year. The client is waiting. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Use the vial after checking that the solution is clear and free of particles", "B": "Use the vial and write a new discard date seven days from today", "C": "Use the vial since the manufacturer's expiry date is still next year", "D": "Discard the vial per policy and use a new vial with a clear opened date"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Judging vial safety by appearance", "B": "Extending the beyond-use date after it has passed", "C": "Ignoring the beyond-use date after opening"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discard the vial per policy and use a new vial with a clear opened date): Once opened, a multi-dose vial has a shorter beyond-use date, and this one is past it, so the contents may be contaminated or less potent and must be discarded. The printed expiry applies to an unopened vial. A clear appearance does not show sterility, and writing a new date does not restore a vial past its limit. Using it exposes the client to avoidable risk."}, {"id": 1908, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Bruises on her upper arms and visible anxiety catch the nurse's attention at a prenatal visit with a 27-year-old. Her partner insists on staying in the room and answers questions for her. The nurse needs to screen for intimate partner violence. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Hand her a violence resource pamphlet openly and watch how her partner reacts", "B": "Ask her gently about the bruises in front of her partner, using open questions", "C": "Document the bruises and skip screening until she volunteers information about the injuries", "D": "Find a reason to speak with her alone, then ask screening questions privately"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offering resources openly in front of a possible abuser", "B": "Asking in front of the partner despite a kind approach", "C": "Waiting for disclosure instead of asking"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Find a reason to speak with her alone, then ask screening questions privately): Screening must be private so she can answer safely, and routine reasons such as providing a urine sample can separate her from her partner without alarming him. Asking, even kindly, in front of him can raise her risk and produce unreliable answers, and handing her a pamphlet openly may expose her. Waiting for her to volunteer information leaves many people unreached."}, {"id": 1909, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Knee pain from osteoarthritis leads a 79-year-old with chronic kidney disease to ask for ibuprofen. Her eGFR is low, and she takes a diuretic and an ACE inhibitor for blood pressure. Her pain is 6 out of 10. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Give acetaminophen and ibuprofen together so the lower ibuprofen dose spares her kidneys", "B": "Give ibuprofen with extra fluids encouraged so the kidneys stay well flushed", "C": "Give the lowest ibuprofen dose with food and water since that protects the kidneys from harm", "D": "Hold the ibuprofen and report the request so the prescriber can review safer options"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Combining analgesics without an order and assuming safety", "B": "Believing fluids prevent NSAID kidney injury", "C": "Believing food protects the kidneys from an NSAID"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the ibuprofen and report the request so the prescriber can review safer options): NSAIDs reduce kidney blood flow, and combined with an ACE inhibitor and a diuretic in a client with kidney disease the risk of kidney injury is high, so the prescriber should choose another analgesic plan. Extra fluids or a lower dose do not remove that risk, and food protects the stomach, not the kidneys. Combining analgesics without an order adds its own risk."}, {"id": 1910, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At his visit, Daniel's care plan, written by the team, lists only symptoms to monitor and relapse warning signs. He has missed two appointments and says, 'All you people see is my diagnosis.' The nurse recognizes the plan may be driving his disengagement. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Add more frequent symptom checks to the plan so relapse is detected earlier", "B": "Continue the current plan unchanged because relapse prevention is the first priority for the team", "C": "Revise the plan with him to include his strengths and goals, like returning to carpentry", "D": "Remind him that missed appointments raise the risk of hospital readmission"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Intensifying a deficit-focused approach", "B": "Ignoring the cause of disengagement", "D": "Using warnings instead of engagement"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C237", "cluster_text": "Daniel Fox, 40, is a member of a First Nation and lives in a rented apartment. He was hospitalized for three weeks with a manic episode and was discharged one month ago on lithium. He attends a community mental health clinic where a practical nurse sees him every two weeks. He says services have let him down before, and he misses his carpentry work.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Revise the plan with him to include his strengths and goals, like returning to carpentry): The recovery model builds on strengths, hopes, and personal goals, so adding his wish to return to work responds to his feeling reduced to a diagnosis and can restore engagement. More symptom checks would deepen that feeling. Warning him about readmission can feel punitive. Leaving the plan unchanged protects the plan, not the relationship it depends on."}, {"id": 1911, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Daniel tells the nurse he stopped taking lithium five days ago because his hands shake and he cannot hold his tools. He expects to be told off. His speech is calm, and he is sleeping well. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Explain the serious consequences of stopping lithium until he agrees to restart it today", "B": "Tell him to restart his usual dose tonight and watch the tremor", "C": "Ask about the tremor without judgment, then report it and the stopped doses", "D": "Suggest a calming herbal tea and review lithium at his next appointment"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Lecturing instead of listening", "B": "Restarting a stopped drug without prescriber review", "D": "Offering a folk remedy and delaying the report"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C237", "cluster_text": "Daniel Fox, 40, is a member of a First Nation and lives in a rented apartment. He was hospitalized for three weeks with a manic episode and was discharged one month ago on lithium. He attends a community mental health clinic where a practical nurse sees him every two weeks. He says services have let him down before, and he misses his carpentry work.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask about the tremor without judgment, then report it and the stopped doses): Side effects such as tremor are a common reason for stopping, and a nonjudgmental conversation shows why he stopped while the prescriber can adjust the plan, so both the effect and the missed doses must be reported. A lecture repeats the experience that made him distrust services. Restarting his usual dose without review risks toxicity, and an herbal tea with a delayed review leaves him unprotected against relapse."}, {"id": 1912, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Daniel, who has not yet spoken to anyone with a similar experience since leaving hospital, asks why he should attend a peer support group when he already sees clinic staff regularly. He seems open but unsure. How should the nurse explain peer support?", "options": {"A": "Peer groups replace clinic visits once a client feels stable enough to attend regularly", "B": "Peers share lived experience, hope, and coping ideas that complement his clinic care", "C": "Peer groups work best when contacts outside the group are limited to health professionals", "D": "Peer groups are led by clinicians who teach symptom control and medication management skills"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating peer support as a substitute for clinic care", "C": "Restricting social contacts to health professionals", "D": "Confusing peer groups with clinician-led education"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C237", "cluster_text": "Daniel Fox, 40, is a member of a First Nation and lives in a rented apartment. He was hospitalized for three weeks with a manic episode and was discharged one month ago on lithium. He attends a community mental health clinic where a practical nurse sees him every two weeks. He says services have let him down before, and he misses his carpentry work.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Peers share lived experience, hope, and coping ideas that complement his clinic care): Peer support offers understanding from people with lived experience, hope, and everyday strategies, and it adds to professional care instead of replacing it. Clients remain connected to the clinic while attending. Groups are generally led or shaped by peers rather than clinicians. Limiting outside contacts to professionals reduces the community connections that support recovery."}, {"id": 1913, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Daniel mentions that an Elder in his community supported him before and that he would like cultural ceremony included in his recovery. The nurse is unsure how to respond. His mood is stable, and the team has a standard discharge education package. Which response best reflects cultural safety?", "options": {"A": "Give the standard education package and note his interest in the chart for the team", "B": "Offer to arrange a hospital chaplain visit as the available spiritual support option", "C": "Ask what supports matter to him and help link him with the Elder and community resources", "D": "Encourage him to see the Elder and leave ceremony out of the plan"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Responding with a generic tool", "B": "Substituting a generic service for his chosen support", "D": "Separating culture from the care plan"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C237", "cluster_text": "Daniel Fox, 40, is a member of a First Nation and lives in a rented apartment. He was hospitalized for three weeks with a manic episode and was discharged one month ago on lithium. He attends a community mental health clinic where a practical nurse sees him every two weeks. He says services have let him down before, and he misses his carpentry work.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask what supports matter to him and help link him with the Elder and community resources): Cultural safety means the client defines what feels safe and the nurse builds a respectful relationship, so asking what supports he wants and helping connect him with the Elder honours that. Offering a generic chaplain visit substitutes the nurse's idea for his choice. Keeping ceremony outside the plan tells him his identity does not belong in his care. A standard package ignores what he said."}, {"id": 1914, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On day one after abdominal surgery, a 54-year-old with a midline incision lies flat on his back with pain of 7 out of 10. He is hesitant to move and coughs weakly. The nurse is planning positioning for comfort. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Lay him flat with a pillow under the head only and limit turning until tomorrow", "B": "Raise the head slightly, bend his knees, and support the incision with a pillow", "C": "Raise the head fully with legs straight, leaving the incision unsupported", "D": "Keep the bed flat and encourage deep coughing without splinting the incision"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Restricting turning, which raises complication risk", "C": "Straight legs and no splinting increase incision tension", "D": "Coughing without splinting increases pain and strain"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C232", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works a day shift on a combined surgical and palliative unit and shares the assignment with an unregulated care provider. The care plans emphasize comfort, dignity, privacy, and skin protection. Before morning care the nurse reviews the plans for four clients whose comfort needs differ, and decides what to do personally and what to delegate. The RN is available for consultation.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Raise the head slightly, bend his knees, and support the incision with a pillow): A slightly raised head and bent knees relax the abdominal muscles, and a pillow splint reduces pain with coughing and turning, which also helps lung expansion and circulation. Straight legs or an unsupported incision keep tension on the wound. Staying flat and limiting turns raises the risk of pneumonia and clots, and coughing without a splint adds strain."}, {"id": 1915, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Dry, cracked lips and a coated tongue are noted on a 76-year-old in the final days of life. She cannot eat or drink and sleeps most of the day. Her daughter asks the nurse to stop mouth care because she isn't eating anyway. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Offer small sips of fluid by cup every hour so her mouth stays moist", "B": "Stop routine mouth care and apply lip balm only when the client appears distressed", "C": "Continue gentle mouth and lip care often, explaining it promotes her comfort", "D": "Reduce mouth care to once daily to avoid disturbing her sleep during final days"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offering fluids to a client who cannot swallow safely", "B": "Equating no oral intake with no need for mouth care", "D": "Under-providing comfort care by cutting mouth care to once daily"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C232", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works a day shift on a combined surgical and palliative unit and shares the assignment with an unregulated care provider. The care plans emphasize comfort, dignity, privacy, and skin protection. Before morning care the nurse reviews the plans for four clients whose comfort needs differ, and decides what to do personally and what to delegate. The RN is available for consultation.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue gentle mouth and lip care often, explaining it promotes her comfort): Mouth dryness causes distress even when intake stops, and frequent gentle care with swabs and moisturizer is a core comfort measure that the nurse can explain to the family. Waiting for obvious distress delays relief for a client who cannot speak. Cup sips raise the risk of aspiration in a drowsy client. Once-daily care is too little to keep the mouth comfortable."}, {"id": 1916, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Cultural beliefs about modesty lead a 58-year-old woman to ask that only female caregivers provide her bathing and personal care. The unit is busy and a male care provider is assigned to her today. She seems uncomfortable. Which principle guides the nurse's response?", "options": {"A": "Let the male provider bathe her with extra draping for privacy", "B": "Respect cultural preferences for modesty where possible and adjust the assignment", "C": "Document the request and review it at the next care conference", "D": "Allow the male provider to proceed after explaining that every staff member is trained"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using draping instead of honouring the request", "C": "Deferring a time-sensitive request to a later meeting", "D": "Overriding the request with reassurance"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C232", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works a day shift on a combined surgical and palliative unit and shares the assignment with an unregulated care provider. The care plans emphasize comfort, dignity, privacy, and skin protection. Before morning care the nurse reviews the plans for four clients whose comfort needs differ, and decides what to do personally and what to delegate. The RN is available for consultation.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Respect cultural preferences for modesty where possible and adjust the assignment): Dignity and cultural safety call for honouring reasonable requests about who provides intimate care, and a small change in assignment usually solves it. Extra draping does not replace her stated request, and postponing it to a care conference leaves her uncomfortable today. Telling her staff are trained does not address her stated need."}, {"id": 1917, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "New redness, warmth, and a raised rash on the lower back of an 84-year-old resident are found during morning care. The care provider asks whether to apply the usual barrier cream and finish the bath. The RN is at the nurses' station. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Assess the rash personally, direct the provider's care, and report it to the RN", "B": "Ask the care provider to describe the rash and assign the skin care to that person", "C": "Delay assessment until the end of the bath and have the care provider chart it", "D": "Have the care provider apply barrier cream and monitor the rash for changes by shift end"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delegating an assessment to the care provider", "C": "Delaying assessment of a new finding", "D": "Delegating treatment of an unassessed problem"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C232", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works a day shift on a combined surgical and palliative unit and shares the assignment with an unregulated care provider. The care plans emphasize comfort, dignity, privacy, and skin protection. Before morning care the nurse reviews the plans for four clients whose comfort needs differ, and decides what to do personally and what to delegate. The RN is available for consultation.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Assess the rash personally, direct the provider's care, and report it to the RN): Assessing a new rash requires nursing judgment and cannot be delegated, so the nurse examines the skin, tells the care provider what care to give, and reports to the RN. Letting the provider describe it substitutes their observation for an assessment. Applying cream without assessment may hide or worsen a developing infection. Waiting until the bath ends delays recognizing a change in condition."}, {"id": 1918, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Newly started on warfarin after a hospital stay, a 66-year-old in home care received a leaflet at discharge and says he read most of it. He lives alone, and this is the nurse's first visit. How should the practical nurse teach him about the medication?", "options": {"A": "Hand him a second leaflet and invite questions by phone", "B": "Use teach-back to confirm understanding of dosing, bleeding signs, and blood tests", "C": "Teach only the dosing schedule today and cover bleeding signs at the next monthly visit", "D": "Ask if he understood the leaflet and accept his yes as confirmation"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a handout as a replacement for teaching", "C": "Delaying safety critical content to a later monthly visit", "D": "Accepting a yes answer instead of using teach-back"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use teach-back to confirm understanding of dosing, bleeding signs, and blood tests): Teaching is effective when the client can restate key points, and teach-back checks dosing, bleeding precautions, consistent vitamin K intake, and INR monitoring, all vital for someone living alone. A second leaflet or a yes answer does not confirm understanding. Delaying bleeding signs leaves a high risk drug with unsafe knowledge."}, {"id": 1919, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Visits from a registered therapy dog are enjoyed by an 82-year-old long-term care resident. She has a small open pressure injury on her heel and holds the dog on her lap during today's visit. The handler is present. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Cover the wound, support the visit, and have both perform hand hygiene afterward", "B": "Cancel the visit since animals cannot be around residents who have any open wound", "C": "Allow the visit and have only the handler perform hand hygiene afterward", "D": "Allow the visit and change the heel dressing the next day"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Removing a valued therapy for a covered, low-risk wound", "C": "Leaving out the resident's own hand hygiene", "D": "Delaying wound protection until after the contact"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cover the wound, support the visit, and have both perform hand hygiene afterward): A covered wound and consistent hand hygiene by both people lower the infection risk enough to let a meaningful visit continue. Hand hygiene by the handler alone leaves the resident's hands, which touch the fur, unaddressed. Changing the dressing the next day leaves the wound exposed during contact. Cancelling removes comfort when a safer solution exists."}, {"id": 1920, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At a remote nursing station, a 52-year-old First Nations man with worsening shortness of breath asks to avoid leaving his community, as the nearest hospital is 400 km away by air. The RN is on call by phone. What best reflects the practical nurse's role?", "options": {"A": "Treat his breathing with oxygen and decide about transfer after the shift ends", "B": "Assess and monitor him, consult the on-call RN or physician, and include him in planning", "C": "Arrange the medical transfer without discussing it, since transport is a clinical decision", "D": "Respect his wish by treating him locally and skipping a call to the RN"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Deferring the consultation about transfer", "C": "Excluding the client from decisions about his care", "D": "Letting preference override the need to consult"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assess and monitor him, consult the on-call RN or physician, and include him in planning): In remote care the nurse assesses, treats within scope, consults the on-call RN or physician, and involves the client so decisions reflect his needs and the distance from home. Treating locally without consultation, or deferring the transfer question, risks serious deterioration far from a hospital. A transfer arranged without him disregards consent and cultural safety."}, {"id": 1921, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Her son worries that practical nurses cannot care for dying clients, as home care visits are to continue for a 78-year-old with stable heart failure who has chosen comfort-focused care at the end of life. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Only registered nurses may care for dying clients, so a transfer of the file is required", "B": "Practical nurses may provide comfort care but must avoid assessing or monitoring her condition", "C": "Practical nurses provide palliative care within competence and consult the RN as needs grow", "D": "Practical nurses may care for her only until symptoms change, then must withdraw from care"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing end-of-life care is restricted to RNs", "B": "Separating comfort care from assessment", "D": "Believing scope ends when symptoms change"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Practical nurses provide palliative care within competence and consult the RN as needs grow): Palliative care falls within practical nursing scope when the nurse has the competence and consults the RN or NP as complexity grows. A blanket restriction to RNs is incorrect. Changing symptoms call for consultation and collaboration, not withdrawal. Assessment and monitoring are part of safe comfort care, since symptoms must be recognized to be relieved."}, {"id": 1922, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Tuesday and Wednesday compartments are still full on Friday in a 77-year-old's weekly pill organizer, which the community nurse finds on a visit. She says she forgets the midday tablets and has trouble reading small labels. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the prescriber to raise how often she takes the tablets to cover more hours", "B": "Tell her to take the missed tablets together at bedtime to catch up on her doses", "C": "Explore her barriers and ask the prescriber to simplify doses and enlarge labels", "D": "Move all tablets to one daily compartment so she remembers one time to take them"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Making a regimen more complex", "B": "Doubling up missed doses", "D": "Combining doses without an order"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explore her barriers and ask the prescriber to simplify doses and enlarge labels): Missed midday doses and small print are modifiable barriers, so the nurse should explore them with her and ask the prescriber to simplify timing, with labels she can read. More frequent dosing makes adherence harder. Merging doses into one time changes the regimen without an order and could be unsafe. Catching up by taking missed tablets together risks overdose."}, {"id": 1923, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Raj Patel, 72, has chest pain and looks pale and sweaty on the ward. The nurse is about to call the physician and wants to open the SBAR report well. She has been with him five minutes and has his chart nearby. Which opening best states the situation?", "options": {"A": "Identify self, unit, and client, then state the concern: new chest pain ten minutes ago", "B": "Begin with his admission for pneumonia and heart history, and mention the chest pain afterward", "C": "State all recent vital signs first, then explain why she is calling", "D": "Request an urgent review and name the chest pain after the physician agrees"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Leading with background before stating the concern", "C": "Giving assessment data before the situation", "D": "Requesting action before stating the concern"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C236", "cluster_text": "Raj Patel, 72, was admitted to a medical ward two days ago with pneumonia and has a history of heart disease. At 14:10 a practical nurse finds him pale and sweaty, reporting chest pain. The nurse must report to the RN and the on-call physician, using SBAR. The nurse has a full set of recent vital signs and the chart.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Identify self, unit, and client, then state the concern: new chest pain ten minutes ago): The situation statement names who is calling, the client, and the problem right away, so the listener knows why the call matters and what is urgent. Starting with history or vital sign data delays the concern. Requesting a review before naming the problem gives the physician nothing to judge. A long account is not needed first."}, {"id": 1924, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the call, the physician asks how sick Mr. Patel is. The nurse's findings: blood pressure 92/54 mmHg, heart rate 118, respiratory rate 26, oxygen saturation 89 percent on room air, and temperature 37.8 C. He ate half his lunch. Which assessment statement is best for the SBAR?", "options": {"A": "He has had pneumonia two days and ate half his lunch; vitals are charted", "B": "He is hypotensive and tachycardic from sepsis, so he needs antibiotics and fluids now", "C": "His vital signs are quite abnormal and he appears much worse than he did earlier today", "D": "BP 92/54, pulse 118, respirations 26, saturation 89 percent on room air, and worsening"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Directing the physician to the chart instead of reporting", "B": "Making a medical diagnosis and requests outside scope", "C": "Using adjectives instead of measurements"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C236", "cluster_text": "Raj Patel, 72, was admitted to a medical ward two days ago with pneumonia and has a history of heart disease. At 14:10 a practical nurse finds him pale and sweaty, reporting chest pain. The nurse must report to the RN and the on-call physician, using SBAR. The nurse has a full set of recent vital signs and the chart.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (BP 92/54, pulse 118, respirations 26, saturation 89 percent on room air, and worsening): A good assessment gives current numbers and a clear sense of the trend so the physician can judge urgency. Words such as quite abnormal leave room for misunderstanding. Naming sepsis and ordering treatment goes beyond the nurse's scope, which is to report findings. Pointing to the chart wastes time in an urgent call, and his lunch intake adds little."}, {"id": 1925, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Patel remains pale, with chest pain at 7 out of 10, after the nurse has given the situation and assessment, and the physician is quiet on the line. The nurse wants him seen quickly. Which recommendation best completes the SBAR?", "options": {"A": "End the call by saying the nurse will keep monitoring and call back if he worsens", "B": "Ask the physician whether they agree that the nurse is right to be concerned", "C": "Say the nurse is open to any orders that the physician thinks are suitable", "D": "Ask the physician to assess him urgently and say when the review is expected"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ending without a request", "B": "Seeking agreement instead of action", "C": "Leaving the request vague"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C236", "cluster_text": "Raj Patel, 72, was admitted to a medical ward two days ago with pneumonia and has a history of heart disease. At 14:10 a practical nurse finds him pale and sweaty, reporting chest pain. The nurse must report to the RN and the on-call physician, using SBAR. The nurse has a full set of recent vital signs and the chart.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask the physician to assess him urgently and say when the review is expected): The recommendation states what the nurse wants, here an urgent in-person review, and when, so the physician knows the expected action and timeframe. Asking whether the physician agrees gives no direction. Saying the nurse will monitor and call back asks for nothing. Offering openness to any order leaves the physician unsure how urgent the need is."}, {"id": 1926, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Over the phone, the physician gives a verbal order: 'Oxygen 4 litres by nasal prongs and a 12-lead ECG now.' The nurse is alone at the desk, and the RN is with another client. Which action completes the safe handling of this verbal order?", "options": {"A": "Read back the oxygen order and note the ECG for later", "B": "Act on the order immediately and chart it after the RN is free to cosign", "C": "Ask for the order to be faxed and wait before starting", "D": "Write the order down, read it back, and get confirmation before acting"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reading back an incomplete order", "B": "Acting before the order is verified or cosigned", "C": "Delaying urgent care to wait for a written order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C236", "cluster_text": "Raj Patel, 72, was admitted to a medical ward two days ago with pneumonia and has a history of heart disease. At 14:10 a practical nurse finds him pale and sweaty, reporting chest pain. The nurse must report to the RN and the on-call physician, using SBAR. The nurse has a full set of recent vital signs and the chart.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Write the order down, read it back, and get confirmation before acting): A written, read-back order with confirmation prevents mishearing of doses, routes, and tests, and it lets the nurse act safely without waiting for the RN. Acting first and cosigning later skips the safety check. Reading back only the oxygen order leaves the ECG unconfirmed. Waiting for a faxed order postpones care for a client with chest pain."}, {"id": 1927, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Purulent drainage covers the surface of an infected leg wound, and the bed is red with slough at the edges, as a practical nurse prepares a culture for an adult. The order is for culture and sensitivity. How should the specimen be collected?", "options": {"A": "Cleanse with saline, then rotate the swab over healthy tissue in the wound bed", "B": "Swab the dried crust at the wound edge and send it dry in a sterile bag", "C": "Cleanse with antiseptic, then swab the surface drainage without touching the wound bed", "D": "Swab the pus on the surface before cleansing so the sample reflects the drainage"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Sampling dead tissue and sending it dry", "C": "Using antiseptic that can kill organisms and skipping the tissue", "D": "Sampling surface contaminants instead of infecting organisms"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cleanse with saline, then rotate the swab over healthy tissue in the wound bed): Cleansing with saline removes surface contaminants, and rotating the swab over viable tissue in the wound bed captures the organisms causing infection. Surface pus mostly contains colonizers. Dried crust at the edge is poor material and must go in transport medium. Antiseptic can reduce growth in the sample, and surface drainage does not show what is invading the tissue."}, {"id": 1928, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two days of vomiting have left an 83-year-old in long-term care newly drowsy and confused. Lab sodium is 124 mmol/L, and staff think dementia is progressing. The nurse reads the result, and the vital signs are stable. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Document the confusion as dementia progression and reassess at the next routine review", "B": "Report the low sodium and acute change to the RN or physician, and monitor neurological status", "C": "Encourage rest in bed and recheck vital signs again in four hours", "D": "Give his usual antiemetic and wait to see whether the confusion clears"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Anchoring on dementia for a sudden change", "C": "Monitoring without reporting an acute change", "D": "Treating the vomiting and waiting instead of escalating"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the low sodium and acute change to the RN or physician, and monitor neurological status): A sodium of 124 mmol/L with sudden drowsiness and confusion is an acute change that needs prompt medical review and neurological monitoring, not attribution to dementia. Dementia progresses gradually. Resting and rechecking vital signs, or treating the vomiting and waiting, delays assessment of a possible emergency. Report first and monitor closely."}, {"id": 1929, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Her family is not told when she will return or who will accompany her when an Elder in a remote community must be flown out for treatment. Her grandson asks the practical nurse why the family was not involved in the decision. What is the best nursing response?", "options": {"A": "Acknowledge the concern, include the family, and advocate for clear updates and escorts", "B": "Advise the family to contact the transport service themselves for details of the return", "C": "Apologize for the missed update and assure them the Elder is doing well", "D": "Say the transfer policy limits updates to the Elder, not her family"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Passing responsibility to the family", "C": "Offering reassurance without any action", "D": "Citing policy to exclude the family"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acknowledge the concern, include the family, and advocate for clear updates and escorts): Medical transfer affects the family, community, and cultural connections, so safe care includes acknowledging the concern, involving the family, and advocating for escorts and clear updates. Apologizing and reassuring without acting leaves the problem in place, and citing a policy that limits updates to the Elder shuts the family out. Sending them to the transport service leaves them unsupported."}, {"id": 1930, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Withdrawn, eating little, and refusing therapy, a 49-year-old Inuk man is recovering from surgery at a southern hospital. He has not spoken with family in three weeks, and country food is unavailable. His vital signs are stable. What interpretation best guides the nurse's next step?", "options": {"A": "Recovery depends on medical factors, so continue standard care and recheck vital signs hourly", "B": "Separation from family, land, and food may affect recovery, so explore it and arrange supports", "C": "Low appetite signals a surgical complication, so he needs immediate laboratory testing", "D": "Withdrawal suggests depression, so request an antidepressant order before exploring his situation"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ignoring social and cultural factors in recovery", "C": "Assuming a complication without data", "D": "Jumping to a diagnosis before exploring his context"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Separation from family, land, and food may affect recovery, so explore it and arrange supports): For many Inuit clients, family, land, and country food are central to wellness, and separation can show up as withdrawal and poor intake, so the nurse should ask, listen, and arrange contact and food supports. Assuming depression or only medical causes skips that context. Stable vital signs make a complication less likely as the main driver. Isolation does affect recovery."}, {"id": 1931, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Found sitting on the floor beside her bed at 03:00, with no one having seen her fall, a long-term care resident aged 84 reports a sore hip. No injuries are visible. The nurse records the event. Which documentation approach is correct?", "options": {"A": "Write that she slipped from the bed since this is the most likely cause", "B": "Chart that the fall was unavoidable so no one is blamed for the event", "C": "Record what was found and what she said, without guessing how she fell", "D": "Note only that a fall occurred and leave the details to the incident report"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adding an assumed mechanism", "B": "Adding a judgment about blame", "D": "Under-documenting in the chart"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C229", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a small rural health centre with a few acute care beds, an attached long-term care wing, and an outpatient clinic. One registered nurse is on each shift, and the centre has written policies on incident reporting, consent, and competence. Over one week the nurse faces several legal, documentation, and accountability situations involving different clients, and follows the regulatory body's standards of practice.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Record what was found and what she said, without guessing how she fell): Charting should contain objective findings and the client's own words, such as where she was found and her hip pain, without speculation about how it happened. Writing that she slipped records an assumption as fact. Leaving details out of the chart weakens the legal record because the incident report is separate. Calling the fall unavoidable is an opinion and not an observation."}, {"id": 1932, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Found on the floor beside the bathroom, a 75-year-old on the acute care beds denies pain but cannot say what happened. When the nurse writes the chart note, a coworker suggests adding that he probably tripped over his slippers. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Chart only observed facts and his statements, and leave out assumptions about the cause", "B": "Add that he probably tripped, which helps the team plan the fall prevention steps", "C": "Chart the fall as a trip once the incident report names the likely cause", "D": "Write only that he was found on the floor and skip his statements"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Recording a guessed cause as if it were fact", "C": "Charting a cause borrowed from a report", "D": "Omitting his statements and the assessment"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C229", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a small rural health centre with a few acute care beds, an attached long-term care wing, and an outpatient clinic. One registered nurse is on each shift, and the centre has written policies on incident reporting, consent, and competence. Over one week the nurse faces several legal, documentation, and accountability situations involving different clients, and follows the regulatory body's standards of practice.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chart only observed facts and his statements, and leave out assumptions about the cause): Documentation must reflect what the nurse saw and heard, so the note records where he was found, his statement, and the assessment, not a guess that could mislead later readers or be used in a legal review. Adding a probable cause turns speculation into a record, and borrowing a cause from an incident report does the same. Leaving out his statements makes the note incomplete."}, {"id": 1933, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Both a nursing student and the wound care team ask to observe a dressing change for a 60-year-old with a draining wound on the surgical ward, and the team also wants photographs for teaching. The client signed a general admission form on arrival. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Photograph only the wound without the face, so no separate consent is needed", "B": "Proceed with both because the admission form covers student observation and photographs", "C": "Ask the client for specific consent for the student and photographs first", "D": "Obtain consent from a family member, since the client is recovering from surgery"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing a non-identifiable photo needs no consent", "B": "Assuming general admission consent covers everything", "D": "Substituting family consent for a capable client's"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C229", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a small rural health centre with a few acute care beds, an attached long-term care wing, and an outpatient clinic. One registered nurse is on each shift, and the centre has written policies on incident reporting, consent, and competence. Over one week the nurse faces several legal, documentation, and accountability situations involving different clients, and follows the regulatory body's standards of practice.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask the client for specific consent for the student and photographs first): Consent must be specific and voluntary for each extra purpose, and a capable client decides for himself, so the nurse asks him about the student and the photographs and respects a refusal. A general admission form does not cover teaching or photography. Wound photos can still identify a person and remain part of his record. A family member cannot consent when the client is capable."}, {"id": 1934, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Fear of upsetting her oncologist stops a 55-year-old in the oncology clinic from asking about a second opinion on her treatment plan, so she asks the nurse whether she is allowed. She seems anxious and has not signed consent for the next cycle. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Tell the oncologist about her plan first so the physician can decide whether to allow it", "B": "Explain that second opinions are available through a physician referral", "C": "Support her right to seek one and help her contact the team for records and information", "D": "Encourage her to continue with the current plan since another opinion delays treatment"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Seeking permission on her behalf", "B": "Misstating how clients may obtain a second opinion", "D": "Discouraging the client from seeking another opinion"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C229", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a small rural health centre with a few acute care beds, an attached long-term care wing, and an outpatient clinic. One registered nurse is on each shift, and the centre has written policies on incident reporting, consent, and competence. Over one week the nurse faces several legal, documentation, and accountability situations involving different clients, and follows the regulatory body's standards of practice.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Support her right to seek one and help her contact the team for records and information): Clients have the right to information and to seek another opinion, and the nurse supports that choice and helps her obtain what she needs, such as records. Discouraging her undermines autonomy. Reporting her plan to the oncologist for approval breaches her confidence and implies permission is needed. Clients can ask for records and contact another provider directly, so referral is not a barrier."}, {"id": 1935, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Negative pressure dressing is needed for a 38-year-old client at the outpatient clinic, and the nurse has seen it demonstrated once but never performed it. The only other nurse is away, and the wound is not an emergency. What principle applies?", "options": {"A": "The nurse may perform it if the employer's policy lists the dressing among clinic supplies", "B": "A nurse must be competent before performing a new skill and should seek training first", "C": "Competence is assumed for any skill within the nurse's legislated scope of practice", "D": "One observed demonstration is enough to qualify a nurse to perform the skill independently"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating availability of supplies as authorization", "C": "Assuming scope means competence", "D": "Equating watching with competence"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C229", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works in a small rural health centre with a few acute care beds, an attached long-term care wing, and an outpatient clinic. One registered nurse is on each shift, and the centre has written policies on incident reporting, consent, and competence. Over one week the nurse faces several legal, documentation, and accountability situations involving different clients, and follows the regulatory body's standards of practice.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A nurse must be competent before performing a new skill and should seek training first): Practical nurses are accountable for performing only what they are competent to do, so a new skill needs education, practice, and supervision before independent use. Watching once does not build competence. Supply availability does not authorize a nurse. Scope describes what the profession may do, while competence is what the individual nurse can safely do."}, {"id": 1936, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During bedside handover, a practical nurse in acute care begins to report a 74-year-old's new cancer diagnosis, which the client has not yet discussed with his family. His two adult sons are visiting at the bedside. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the sons to leave and finish the handover without asking the client's wishes", "B": "Lower the voice and continue the full handover, assuming the sons already suspect it", "C": "Ask the client how he wants to proceed and move sensitive details to a private discussion", "D": "Skip the diagnosis in handover and tell the oncoming nurse to disclose it later"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Excluding family without the client's say", "B": "Assuming family suspicion permits disclosure", "D": "Omitting critical information from handover"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask the client how he wants to proceed and move sensitive details to a private discussion): Bedside handover supports involvement, but confidentiality and the client's wishes come first, so the nurse asks him what may be shared and moves sensitive content to a private setting. Reading out the diagnosis, even quietly or assuming the sons suspect it, breaches confidentiality. Sending the sons away decides for him. Leaving the diagnosis out of handover creates a safety gap for the oncoming nurse."}, {"id": 1937, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Documentation for two clients is not complete, and a 24-year-old new practical nurse on a surgical unit asks her mentor to chart for her, saying she is overwhelmed. The mentor knows the client details well. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Dictate the entries to her from memory and let her sign without reviewing them", "B": "Chart the entries for her under her name since the mentor knows the details", "C": "Tell her to chart both clients with the same standard entry to save time", "D": "Help her prioritize and complete her own charting, offering coaching and checking it for accuracy"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Signing entries she did not verify herself", "B": "Documenting for another nurse under her name", "C": "Copying one standard entry for different clients"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Help her prioritize and complete her own charting, offering coaching and checking it for accuracy): Each nurse is accountable for documenting her own care, and mentoring means coaching her to prioritize and complete it while the mentor stays within her own scope. Entering notes for her under her name is falsification, and signing entries she did not verify or copying one standard entry produces inaccurate records. Coaching keeps the record timely and truthful."}, {"id": 1938, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Blood pressure measurement is needed for a 6-year-old in a clinic. The adult cuff wraps past the upper arm twice, and the child cuff bladder covers about 40 percent of the arm circumference in width and about 80 percent in length. His mother asks which to use. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Use the child cuff because a properly sized bladder gives accurate readings, then report abnormal values", "B": "Use the adult cuff folded over so it is snug, since fit matters more than size", "C": "Use a smaller infant cuff because the child cuff looks too large for him", "D": "Use the adult cuff and subtract ten mmHg from the reading"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Improvising with an oversized cuff", "C": "Choosing a cuff by appearance instead of measured fit", "D": "Applying a guessed correction to a wrong cuff size"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use the child cuff because a properly sized bladder gives accurate readings, then report abnormal values): A bladder about 40 percent of the arm circumference in width and about 80 percent in length is the recommended fit, and an oversized cuff underestimates pressure while an undersized one overestimates it. Folding an adult cuff or subtracting a guessed value does not give a correct reading, and a smaller infant cuff overestimates pressure. Cuffs work in young children when sized properly."}, {"id": 1939, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Crackles at both lung bases, a rise in respiratory rate from 18 to 26, and swollen ankles develop in an 81-year-old on a medical ward receiving IV fluids at 150 mL/h. The infusion is running faster than ordered. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Restore the ordered rate, sit him upright, and report the findings to the RN", "B": "Give a diuretic from unit stock immediately and inform the RN afterward", "C": "Slow the infusion to the ordered rate and recheck him at the next routine round", "D": "Stop the infusion, lay him flat, and encourage him to take deep breaths"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Giving a drug without an order", "C": "Slowing the fluid but not escalating", "D": "Lying a breathless client flat"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Restore the ordered rate, sit him upright, and report the findings to the RN): Bibasilar crackles, a faster respiratory rate, and ankle swelling suggest fluid overload, so the nurse corrects the rate, positions him upright to ease breathing, and reports promptly. Slowing the rate and waiting for a routine round misses an unfolding problem. Lying him flat worsens breathlessness. A diuretic needs an order and cannot be taken from stock."}, {"id": 1940, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Staff routinely skip the independent second-nurse check of insulin on her unit because 'no one has ever made an error,' a practical nurse notices. A new graduate asks whether this is fine. What best explains why the practice is a safety concern?", "options": {"A": "The practice is a concern because inspectors sometimes audit the insulin log", "B": "Skipped safeguards erode safety, and no harm so far does not show the practice is safe", "C": "The check matters for new graduates because experienced nurses seldom make insulin errors", "D": "Shortcuts are acceptable when no incident has occurred, but should be noted as exceptions"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Seeing the issue as audit compliance only", "C": "Assuming experience removes error risk", "D": "Treating good outcomes as proof of safety"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Skipped safeguards erode safety, and no harm so far does not show the practice is safe): Normalization of deviance means unsafe habits feel acceptable because nothing bad has happened yet, while the safeguard that protects clients from a high-alert drug is steadily lost. Calling shortcuts acceptable exceptions repeats that thinking. Experienced nurses also make errors, so the check is not just for newcomers. The reason for the check is client safety, not inspection."}, {"id": 1941, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Years ago Walter became ill after a flu shot, and he sees no reason to repeat it, though he has diabetes. His blood glucose is well controlled and he feels well today. He asks the nurse whether he really needs it. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Accept his decision and document the refusal, since he feels well and his glucose is stable", "B": "Tell him vaccines are unnecessary for people whose blood glucose is well controlled with treatment", "C": "Explore his concern respectfully and explain how yearly influenza and pneumococcal shots help him", "D": "Tell him vaccination is required for diabetes care and book the injection without further discussion"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepting refusal without informing him", "B": "Giving incorrect advice about vaccine need", "D": "Pressuring him without respecting choice"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C238", "cluster_text": "Walter Desjardins, 67, is a Metis man with type 2 diabetes who lives in a small town. He visits the community health centre where a practical nurse provides teaching under the nurse practitioner. He uses traditional herbal teas, worries about falling on icy steps, and has not had vaccines in years. He says earlier clinic visits felt rushed and judgmental.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explore his concern respectfully and explain how yearly influenza and pneumococcal shots help him): People with diabetes have a higher risk of serious complications from influenza and pneumococcal infection, so respectful discussion of his past experience and the benefits gives him what he needs to decide. Documenting a refusal without teaching misses the chance to inform. Telling him vaccines are unnecessary is wrong. Pressing him as if required ignores his right to choose."}, {"id": 1942, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Falling on icy steps worries Walter, and he has begun staying indoors to avoid it. He walks with a slight limp and has numb toes from neuropathy. He asks the nurse what he can do for his health this winter. What advice best addresses his concern?", "options": {"A": "Suggest rest for several weeks until the numbness resolves before any exercise", "B": "Advise him to buy slip resistant boots and limit outings to necessary trips", "C": "Encourage regular safe activity, such as balance and strength exercises, and review footwear and step safety", "D": "Recommend a daily long outdoor walk to build confidence, even when the steps are icy"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying activity until neuropathy resolves", "B": "Using footwear alone while reducing activity", "D": "Encouraging activity without addressing icy step hazards"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C238", "cluster_text": "Walter Desjardins, 67, is a Metis man with type 2 diabetes who lives in a small town. He visits the community health centre where a practical nurse provides teaching under the nurse practitioner. He uses traditional herbal teas, worries about falling on icy steps, and has not had vaccines in years. He says earlier clinic visits felt rushed and judgmental.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Encourage regular safe activity, such as balance and strength exercises, and review footwear and step safety): Staying active with balance and strength training reduces fall risk, and attention to footwear, steps, and winter safety lowers hazards in his daily life. Good boots alone with fewer outings still reduce strength and balance, and walking outdoors on icy steps ignores the hazard. Neuropathy may not resolve, so waiting for it wastes time."}, {"id": 1943, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Walter says he drinks a herbal tea his aunt prepares for blood sugar and has not told his physician for fear of criticism. He takes metformin and a sulfonylurea, and the nurse recognizes possible interactions. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Accept the practice and avoid recording it so he does not feel monitored", "B": "Suggest he reduce his diabetes medication when drinking the tea to prevent low sugar", "C": "Tell him to stop the tea at once since traditional remedies interfere with medicine", "D": "Ask about the tea without judgment, record it, and tell the prescriber"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Omitting safety-relevant information from the record", "B": "Self-adjusting diabetes medication without an order", "C": "Dismissing a traditional practice and ending disclosure"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C238", "cluster_text": "Walter Desjardins, 67, is a Metis man with type 2 diabetes who lives in a small town. He visits the community health centre where a practical nurse provides teaching under the nurse practitioner. He uses traditional herbal teas, worries about falling on icy steps, and has not had vaccines in years. He says earlier clinic visits felt rushed and judgmental.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask about the tea without judgment, record it, and tell the prescriber): Respectful curiosity keeps trust, and recording the tea and sharing it with the prescriber lets the team check for interactions and advise safely. Ordering him to stop dismisses his culture and may end disclosure. Leaving it out of the record hides a possible hazard. Changing diabetes drug doses without an order is unsafe and outside nursing scope."}, {"id": 1944, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two foot-care appointments have been missed by Walter. A colleague says he is simply noncompliant. The nurse knows he has described earlier visits as rushed, and his risk of foot ulcers is high because of neuropathy. Which understanding best guides the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Missed visits reflect travel barriers, so a ride voucher is enough", "B": "Missed visits are best handled by telling him the consequences of poor foot care", "C": "Missed visits suggest a scheduling issue, so assign him a shorter appointment slot", "D": "Missed visits may reflect past mistrust, so build trust and offer flexibility first"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming one barrier without asking him", "B": "Relying on warnings about consequences instead of causes", "C": "Shortening visits, which repeats rushed care"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C238", "cluster_text": "Walter Desjardins, 67, is a Metis man with type 2 diabetes who lives in a small town. He visits the community health centre where a practical nurse provides teaching under the nurse practitioner. He uses traditional herbal teas, worries about falling on icy steps, and has not had vaccines in years. He says earlier clinic visits felt rushed and judgmental.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Missed visits may reflect past mistrust, so build trust and offer flexibility first): Barriers such as past rushed or judgmental care, travel, or cost can explain missed visits, so building trust and offering flexible times responds to the cause. A ride voucher assumes one barrier without asking him. A shorter appointment slot repeats the rushed care he described, and warnings about consequences repeat an approach he already found unwelcoming."}, {"id": 1945, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Dubois, 84, lives in long-term care and has vomited twice since breakfast. This afternoon he is newly confused and drowsy, although his family says he was oriented yesterday. His sodium is 124 mmol/L and his blood pressure is 118/70 mmHg. Which action should the practical nurse take first?", "options": {"A": "Offer salty broth and fluids by mouth and recheck his confusion after supper", "B": "Move him near the nursing station and reassess his orientation at shift change", "C": "Report the new confusion and low sodium to the RN right away and monitor him closely", "D": "Document the confusion as expected dementia progression and reassess him at the next scheduled check"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Self-treating low sodium and delaying the report", "B": "Observing without reporting an acute change", "D": "Attributing acute confusion to dementia instead of an electrolyte cause"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the new confusion and low sodium to the RN right away and monitor him closely): His sudden confusion with vomiting and a sodium of 124 mmol/L signals a possible acute electrolyte emergency, so the RN or physician must hear about it promptly while the nurse watches his neurological status. Labeling the change as dementia progression delays treatment of a reversible cause. Salty broth or watching him until the next shift postpones medical review. Any acute change from baseline should be reported, not explained away."}, {"id": 1946, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before a school physical, a 6-year-old girl needs her blood pressure taken. The only cuffs on the clinic shelf are adult regular and adult large sizes, and her upper arm is thin and short. She is sitting quietly with her arm supported. What is the most appropriate action for the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Use the adult regular cuff and subtract a few mmHg from the reading obtained", "B": "Use the adult regular cuff on her thigh since the limb is larger", "C": "Obtain a pediatric cuff sized to cover two thirds of her upper arm", "D": "Use the adult cuff and average three readings to improve accuracy"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Trying to correct a wrong cuff size with a guessed adjustment", "B": "Moving the cuff to an unsuitable site", "D": "Repeating readings cannot fix a wrong bladder size"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Obtain a pediatric cuff sized to cover two thirds of her upper arm): A cuff that is too big or too small gives a false reading, so the bladder must match the child's arm, covering roughly two thirds of the upper arm length. Subtracting a few mmHg is a guess that cannot fix an oversized bladder. Averaging readings or moving to the thigh does not correct a wrong size or standard site. Obtain the right size before measuring."}, {"id": 1947, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Tran, 81, has normal saline infusing that was ordered at 125 mL/h but is now running near 300 mL/h. Over the last hour she has developed a cough, crackles at both lung bases and a respiratory rate of 26. Her husband is visiting. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Slow the infusion to the ordered rate and recheck her breathing at the next hourly rounds", "B": "Document the crackles and respiratory rate, then let the faster infusion finish first", "C": "Lay her flat to calm her breathing and continue monitoring the infusion rate", "D": "Sit her upright, slow the infusion to the ordered rate, and report to the RN now"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Correcting the rate but delaying the report of respiratory distress", "B": "Documenting findings without acting on or reporting them", "C": "Positioning flat, which worsens breathing in fluid overload"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sit her upright, slow the infusion to the ordered rate, and report to the RN now): Cough, bilateral crackles and a rapid respiratory rate after a fast infusion suggest fluid overload, so she needs upright positioning, the rate returned to the order and an immediate report. Slowing the infusion alone but waiting until the next rounds leaves a deteriorating client unreported. Lying her flat makes breathing harder. Always report new respiratory findings promptly."}, {"id": 1948, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "While charting Mr. Okafor's behaviour, the practical nurse considers several ways to describe the last hour. He paced the hallway for about ten minutes, shouted at staff twice, and declined his evening medication. Which entry best reflects accurate, objective documentation of his behaviour?", "options": {"A": "Behaved inappropriately for most of the evening and was difficult for staff to manage", "B": "Paced hallway 10 minutes, shouted 'leave me alone' twice, pulled oxygen tubing, declined medication", "C": "Appeared angry and manipulative, probably seeking attention because he dislikes the unit routine and rules", "D": "Uncooperative and aggressive this evening, refusing care despite repeated reasonable attempts by the nursing staff"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using vague opinion words that describe no observable action", "C": "Recording a guess about motive rather than facts", "D": "Using judgmental labels such as uncooperative instead of observed behaviour"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C231", "cluster_text": "Mr. Peter Okafor, 46, was admitted to a medical unit two days ago with pneumonia. Tonight he is pacing, raising his voice at staff and pulling at his oxygen tubing. His respiratory rate is 24 and his oxygen saturation is 91 percent on 2 L/min. The practical nurse is caring for him under RN supervision and must document her assessments carefully this shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Paced hallway 10 minutes, shouted 'leave me alone' twice, pulled oxygen tubing, declined medication): Objective documentation records what the nurse saw and heard, including measurable details and the client's own words. Labels such as uncooperative or aggressive, and guesses about motive, are opinions that can bias later readers. Vague phrases like behaved inappropriately describe nothing observable. Chart facts, not conclusions."}, {"id": 1949, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An hour later, the nurse realizes she entered Mr. Okafor's 2200 respiratory rate as 14 in the electronic record when she counted 24. No one has acted on the entry. The record has already been saved. What is the correct way to fix this?", "options": {"A": "Overwrite the number with the correct rate and initial it without noting the time", "B": "Tell the physician about the error and leave the entry unchanged", "C": "Strike out the old rate and enter the correct one", "D": "Add a signed, dated correction per policy, keeping the original visible"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Overwriting the entry without dating it, hiding the original", "B": "Reporting the error without correcting the record", "C": "Correcting without a signature, date, and time"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C231", "cluster_text": "Mr. Peter Okafor, 46, was admitted to a medical unit two days ago with pneumonia. Tonight he is pacing, raising his voice at staff and pulling at his oxygen tubing. His respiratory rate is 24 and his oxygen saturation is 91 percent on 2 L/min. The practical nurse is caring for him under RN supervision and must document her assessments carefully this shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Add a signed, dated correction per policy, keeping the original visible): A correction must keep the original entry readable and show who changed it and when, following agency policy. Striking out or overwriting without a signed, dated addendum hides who corrected the entry and when. A verbal mention to the physician leaves the chart wrong for everyone who reads it. Correct the record transparently."}, {"id": 1950, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Later, Mr. Okafor is calmer, but the practical nurse finds new crackles in both lungs, a heart rate of 118 and new ankle swelling. No order covers these findings. She is also due to begin a scheduled dressing change for another client. What should she do next?", "options": {"A": "Report the findings to the RN promptly, then keep monitoring him while the RN assesses", "B": "Interpret the findings as fluid overload and start a fluid restriction to treat it", "C": "Record the findings and recheck them at the next routine vital sign time", "D": "Complete the other client's dressing change first, then report the findings within the hour"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Making a diagnosis and starting treatment outside practical nurse scope", "C": "Charting new findings without reporting or acting", "D": "Delaying a report for an unrelated routine task"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C231", "cluster_text": "Mr. Peter Okafor, 46, was admitted to a medical unit two days ago with pneumonia. Tonight he is pacing, raising his voice at staff and pulling at his oxygen tubing. His respiratory rate is 24 and his oxygen saturation is 91 percent on 2 L/min. The practical nurse is caring for him under RN supervision and must document her assessments carefully this shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the findings to the RN promptly, then keep monitoring him while the RN assesses): New findings in a client's condition that fall outside her order and her scope need to reach the RN right away, with monitoring continuing in the meantime. A routine dressing can wait, but a possible decline cannot. Naming the cause and starting a restriction is diagnosing and treating beyond her scope. Report first, then document."}, {"id": 1951, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor's wife wants to learn to check his blood pressure at home before discharge. The nurse shows her the cuff, explains each step and answers her questions. What should the nurse do next to confirm she can perform the skill safely?", "options": {"A": "Give her a printed instruction sheet and ask her to read it before discharge", "B": "Demonstrate the skill a second time and have her watch a short instructional video", "C": "Ask whether she has any further questions, then record that teaching was completed", "D": "Have her perform the measurement while the nurse watches, then give feedback"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on written material without checking performance", "B": "Repeating passive demonstration instead of a return demonstration", "C": "Treating a lack of questions as proof of learning"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C231", "cluster_text": "Mr. Peter Okafor, 46, was admitted to a medical unit two days ago with pneumonia. Tonight he is pacing, raising his voice at staff and pulling at his oxygen tubing. His respiratory rate is 24 and his oxygen saturation is 91 percent on 2 L/min. The practical nurse is caring for him under RN supervision and must document her assessments carefully this shift.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Have her perform the measurement while the nurse watches, then give feedback): A return demonstration shows whether she can actually do the skill and lets the nurse correct errors right away. A handout or video offers information but not proof of ability. Having no questions does not show competence. Teaching a skill is complete only once the learner shows it back."}, {"id": 1952, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Wilson, 72, lives in long-term care and has pitting edema in both lower legs that leaves a 2 mm depression after pressing. The skin over her shins is thin and shiny. She asks the nurse to massage her legs to help the swelling. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Apply a heating pad to her shins to improve circulation and reduce swelling", "B": "Apply tight compression stockings over the shiny skin to push the fluid upward", "C": "Dangle her legs over the bed edge for an hour to encourage circulation", "D": "Elevate her legs when seated, handle the skin gently, and report the edema to the RN"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying heat to fragile edematous skin", "B": "Applies compression without an order or circulation check", "C": "Dependent positioning increases leg edema"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Elevate her legs when seated, handle the skin gently, and report the edema to the RN): Swollen, thin and shiny skin tears and breaks down easily, so the nurse elevates the legs, handles skin gently and reports the finding for assessment. Heat or dependent positioning can worsen swelling and damage fragile tissue. Compression stockings need an order and a circulation check first. Protect the skin and report."}, {"id": 1953, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Alvarez, 76, has osteoarthritis in both knees and chronic kidney disease with an eGFR of 38 mL/min. He asks the practical nurse whether he can buy ibuprofen at the pharmacy for daily knee pain, since a neighbour recommended it. Which response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Suggest drinking extra water with ibuprofen to protect his kidneys", "B": "Advise him to ask his prescriber first, since ibuprofen can harm his kidneys", "C": "Advise a seven day ibuprofen course, then recheck his kidney function", "D": "Recommend he use it only on the days when his knee pain is at its worst"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing extra fluids prevent NSAID kidney injury", "C": "Assuming a short course is safe with reduced eGFR", "D": "Assuming occasional use removes the renal risk"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise him to ask his prescriber first, since ibuprofen can harm his kidneys): NSAIDs reduce blood flow to the kidneys, so with an eGFR of 38 mL/min even short use carries risk and his prescriber should choose the pain plan. Extra water does not protect the kidneys, and a one week course or use on bad days still exposes him to harm. Direct clients with kidney disease to the prescriber before any NSAID."}, {"id": 1954, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lee, 58, has a draining, foul-smelling wound on her lower leg, and the prescriber orders a wound culture. The nurse sees thick yellow drainage on the surface and dried crust at the edges. Which technique gives the most reliable culture result?", "options": {"A": "Cleanse with antiseptic, then swab the wound bed to remove surface organisms", "B": "Swab the wound edge in a zigzag across all the skin around it", "C": "Soak the swab in the surface pus because it holds the most organisms", "D": "Rinse with saline first, then rotate the swab over healthy tissue in the wound bed"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Antiseptic residue can suppress growth in the sample", "B": "Sampling surrounding skin flora instead of wound tissue", "C": "Sampling surface exudate that grows contaminants"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Rinse with saline first, then rotate the swab over healthy tissue in the wound bed): Surface pus carries colonizing organisms, so the wound is rinsed with saline and the swab is rotated over viable tissue in the base to capture the organisms causing infection. Antiseptic residue can suppress growth, and skin around the wound adds normal flora that mislead treatment. Surface pus grows contaminants. A poor sample can lead to the wrong antibiotic. Culture the tissue, not the surface."}, {"id": 1955, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a busy medical unit, several colleagues have started skipping the independent double check of high-alert medications whenever the unit is short staffed. No client harm has occurred in months, so the shortcut is now accepted as normal by most staff. Which concept best describes this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Adaptive practice, where staff accept shortcuts as normal because they save time and cause no harm", "B": "Normalization of deviance, where repeated unsafe shortcuts without harm start to feel acceptable", "C": "Situational awareness, where staff track events around them to anticipate problems", "D": "Just culture, where staff are held fairly accountable for choices that put clients at risk"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Mistaking a risky shortcut for a safe workflow improvement", "C": "Confusing the pattern with attentiveness to the environment", "D": "Confusing the pattern with fair accountability principles"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Normalization of deviance, where repeated unsafe shortcuts without harm start to feel acceptable): When unsafe shortcuts go unpunished and seem harmless, staff gradually accept them as normal, which is normalization of deviance. Calling them adaptive ignores that the safeguard exists for the day harm does happen. Just culture and situational awareness describe accountability and attentiveness, not the drift of standards. Absence of harm does not prove a practice is safe."}, {"id": 1956, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Kaur, 34, has an acute back spasm and receives her first dose of cyclobenzaprine at the clinic. She says she will drive herself to work and wants to go straight to her shift. She takes no other medications. What is the best advice from the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Advise that drowsiness fades after thirty minutes, so she can drive once it passes", "B": "Warn that drowsiness is likely, so she should not drive until she knows her response", "C": "Tell her drowsiness usually appears only with higher doses, so driving is fine right after one", "D": "Suggest she drive carefully and pull over if she feels sleepy or dizzy"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming sedation lasts only a short window", "C": "Assuming sedation depends only on dose size", "D": "Relying on self-monitoring while driving"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Warn that drowsiness is likely, so she should not drive until she knows her response): Muscle relaxants commonly cause drowsiness and dizziness, even with the first dose, so she needs to avoid driving until she knows how the drug affects her. Sedation does not clear in half an hour, and relying on pulling over when sleepy still puts her and others at risk. Drowsiness is not limited to higher doses. Teach safety precautions before the client leaves."}, {"id": 1957, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 26-year-old woman at 20 weeks gestation attends a prenatal visit with her partner, who answers most questions for her. The nurse notices bruising on her upper arm and that she seems anxious. How should the nurse screen for intimate partner violence?", "options": {"A": "Find a reason to see her alone, then ask direct private questions about safety", "B": "Ask the partner to leave only if she signals she is unsafe", "C": "Ask the partner how the bruises happened, then confirm with her", "D": "Wait until she raises the topic herself at a later visit, respecting her privacy"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Waiting for a signal she cannot safely give", "C": "Interviewing the partner and raising her risk", "D": "Waiting for disclosure instead of screening routinely"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Find a reason to see her alone, then ask direct private questions about safety): Screening for intimate partner violence must happen privately, because asking in front of a possible abuser can put her at greater risk and prevents honest answers. Waiting for a signal she cannot safely give, or asking the partner to explain the bruises, can increase danger. Waiting for her to raise it leaves a client with warning signs unscreened. Create a private moment and ask directly and kindly."}, {"id": 1958, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Hassan, 50, has burning on urination, and the nurse collects a clean-catch urine specimen at 1630 for culture. The courier will not collect specimens until 0900 tomorrow. A colleague suggests leaving the labelled container on the counter, where the room is about 22 C. What best protects the accuracy of the result?", "options": {"A": "Keep it at room temperature in a dark cupboard, since light is the main threat", "B": "Add a preservative tablet from the supply room to the container without checking lab policy", "C": "Place it in the staff room freezer to keep the bacteria from changing overnight", "D": "Refrigerate the sealed, labelled specimen at the clinic until the courier picks it up"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing light rather than temperature changes the specimen", "B": "Adding a preservative the lab did not specify", "C": "Freezing, which damages organisms and alters the culture"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C235", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works at a community clinic and, on other days, at a long-term care home. This week she collects urine and stool specimens, handles blood-soaked dressings and works with unregulated care providers who perform fingerstick glucose tests. She follows agency policy and reports to the RN throughout. She also keeps accurate records of every task she performs or delegates.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refrigerate the sealed, labelled specimen at the clinic until the courier picks it up): Bacteria multiply quickly in urine at room temperature, which can make the colony count falsely high, so the sealed labelled specimen should be refrigerated until transport. Darkness does not stop growth. A preservative is used when the lab supplies it for that test, and freezing damages organisms. Keep specimens cool and move them promptly."}, {"id": 1959, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Later that week, a second client at the clinic, 35 years old, arrives with a dressing soaked in bloody drainage. His infection history is unknown, and he says he feels fine. The nurse prepares to remove the dressing. Which approach follows standard precautions?", "options": {"A": "Wear gloves, then reuse them on the next client after wiping them", "B": "Wear gloves, and clean hands only if they look soiled after removal", "C": "Skip gloves because he says he feels well and shows no visible signs of infection", "D": "Wear gloves and needed protective equipment for all body fluids"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reusing gloves between clients", "B": "Skipping hand hygiene unless hands look dirty", "C": "Judging infection risk by how the client looks"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C235", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works at a community clinic and, on other days, at a long-term care home. This week she collects urine and stool specimens, handles blood-soaked dressings and works with unregulated care providers who perform fingerstick glucose tests. She follows agency policy and reports to the RN throughout. She also keeps accurate records of every task she performs or delegates.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wear gloves and needed protective equipment for all body fluids): Standard precautions treat all blood and body fluids as potentially infectious, so gloves and other needed equipment are worn for the dressing. Reusing gloves or cleaning hands only when they look soiled spreads organisms. Many infected clients look well and have unknown status. Protect yourself with every client, every time."}, {"id": 1960, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After a stool specimen for culture is collected from Mr. Chen, 60, the practical nurse completes the chart. The lab requisition is already filled out and the specimen is labelled and bagged. He tolerated the collection well. Which chart entry shows the most complete documentation of the collection?", "options": {"A": "Specimen type, collection date, time and method, client response, and time sent to the lab", "B": "Stool specimen collected and sent to lab, with the requisition number and time of collection", "C": "Specimen type, collection date and time, and the name of the nurse who bagged it", "D": "Collection method, client tolerance, and the lab requisition number, without the time sent"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Omitting method and client response from the entry", "C": "Recording who bagged it instead of how it was collected", "D": "Leaving out when the specimen left for the lab"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C235", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works at a community clinic and, on other days, at a long-term care home. This week she collects urine and stool specimens, handles blood-soaked dressings and works with unregulated care providers who perform fingerstick glucose tests. She follows agency policy and reports to the RN throughout. She also keeps accurate records of every task she performs or delegates.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Specimen type, collection date, time and method, client response, and time sent to the lab): Complete documentation shows what was collected, when and how, how the client tolerated it, and when it left for the lab, so anyone can trace the specimen. Entries that omit the method, the client response or the time sent leave gaps in that trail. Chart enough that another nurse could follow the specimen's path."}, {"id": 1961, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the long-term care home, an 80-year-old resident's fingerstick glucose is checked by a trained care aide before lunch under agency policy. The result is 3.1 mmol/L and the resident seems sleepy. Who is responsible for interpreting this result and acting on it?", "options": {"A": "The registered nurse alone, since practical nurses cannot respond to an abnormal glucose result", "B": "The practical nurse, who assesses the resident, treats per protocol and reports to the RN", "C": "The physician, who must be called before any treatment is given to the resident", "D": "The care aide, because performing the test includes deciding what the result means for the resident"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing practical nurses cannot act on abnormal results", "C": "Delaying hypoglycemia treatment until a physician orders it", "D": "Delegating interpretation, which stays with the nurse"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C235", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse works at a community clinic and, on other days, at a long-term care home. This week she collects urine and stool specimens, handles blood-soaked dressings and works with unregulated care providers who perform fingerstick glucose tests. She follows agency policy and reports to the RN throughout. She also keeps accurate records of every task she performs or delegates.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The practical nurse, who assesses the resident, treats per protocol and reports to the RN): A test may be delegated, but interpreting the result and responding stay with the nurse, who assesses the sleepy resident with a low glucose, follows protocol and reports to the RN. The care aide performs a task, not clinical judgment. Practical nurses assess and treat within protocol rather than leaving it to the RN alone, and waiting for a physician order delays treatment of hypoglycemia. Tasks can be delegated, accountability cannot."}, {"id": 1962, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Ms. Brandt, 38, has schizophrenia and is admitted for medication adjustment. When offered a long-acting injectable, she explains in her own words that it lasts a month and may cause weight gain, then declines it, preferring her tablets. She is calm and consistent. How should the nurse interpret her refusal?", "options": {"A": "As an informed decision by someone who understands, to respect and document", "B": "As a symptom of her illness, so the injection can proceed with the RN's agreement", "C": "As a refusal that needs a second opinion before any further offer is made to her", "D": "As evidence she lacks capacity, so her substitute decision-maker should consent instead"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assuming mental illness removes decision-making ability", "C": "Questioning a capable client's refusal through extra review", "D": "Presuming incapacity without assessing understanding"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C230", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse rotates across a mental health unit, a community home for adults with disabilities and a medical unit during one week. At each placement she meets adult clients whose treatment decisions raise questions about capacity, consent and respect for beliefs. She works under RN supervision, follows regulatory standards, and documents each conversation carefully with the client's own words.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (As an informed decision by someone who understands, to respect and document): Capacity depends on understanding the information and appreciating the consequences, not on a diagnosis, and she shows both by explaining the treatment and choosing her tablets. Treating refusal as a symptom or seeking a substitute overrides a capable adult. A second opinion is not needed to honor her choice. Presume capacity and respect it."}, {"id": 1963, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Bell, 29, has an intellectual disability and lives in a supported home with no court-appointed substitute for health decisions. A visiting nurse offers a flu vaccine. After a plain-language explanation, he repeats the purpose back and says he wants the vaccine. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Delay the vaccine until a family member signs because disability limits consent", "B": "Accept his consent once he shows he understands the purpose and risks", "C": "Ask the staff supervisor to consent for him since he cannot sign legal forms", "D": "Give the vaccine without discussion because it is routine and carries low risk"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming disability means someone else must decide", "C": "Letting a staff member decide for a capable adult", "D": "Skipping consent for a routine procedure"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C230", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse rotates across a mental health unit, a community home for adults with disabilities and a medical unit during one week. At each placement she meets adult clients whose treatment decisions raise questions about capacity, consent and respect for beliefs. She works under RN supervision, follows regulatory standards, and documents each conversation carefully with the client's own words.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Accept his consent once he shows he understands the purpose and risks): Capacity is decision-specific, and Mr. Bell shows understanding after a plain-language explanation, so he can give his own consent. Requiring family or staff consent treats his disability as incapacity. Skipping discussion removes his right to be informed. Adapt communication so clients can decide for themselves."}, {"id": 1964, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mrs. Dhillon, 67, a capable adult, is admitted with pneumonia. She wants to keep a small religious cord tied on her wrist and asks that her daughter stay overnight to pray. The wrist is where the nurse planned to place an IV. The unit has no rule against either request. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Accommodate the cord and visit, and choose a different IV site", "B": "Ask her to remove the cord and store it with her belongings", "C": "Allow the cord but limit prayer visits to daytime so night care stays routine", "D": "Keep the cord and tape over it at the planned wrist"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Asking her to give up a belief when a safe alternative exists", "C": "Restricting a practice for staff convenience", "D": "Ignoring that another IV site would protect both safety and beliefs"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C230", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse rotates across a mental health unit, a community home for adults with disabilities and a medical unit during one week. At each placement she meets adult clients whose treatment decisions raise questions about capacity, consent and respect for beliefs. She works under RN supervision, follows regulatory standards, and documents each conversation carefully with the client's own words.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Accommodate the cord and visit, and choose a different IV site): Respecting a capable client's beliefs is part of safe care, and here a different IV site meets clinical needs without removing the cord. Asking her to remove it, or taping over it at the planned site, gives up her wishes or comfort when a simple alternative exists. Restricting prayer to daytime serves routine, not her needs. Look for an option that protects both safety and beliefs."}, {"id": 1965, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Santos, 52, a capable adult and Jehovah's Witness, has anemia with a hemoglobin of 68 g/L. The physician orders a transfusion. He clearly states he refuses blood products and signs a refusal form after the risks are explained. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Respect his informed refusal, document it, report to the RN, and continue other care", "B": "Ask his wife to override his refusal because the illness could affect his judgment", "C": "Encourage him to reconsider by describing the consequences until he agrees", "D": "Give the transfusion anyway because his hemoglobin level places his life at immediate risk"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Letting a family member overrule the client", "C": "Pressuring the client until he changes his decision", "D": "Overriding a capable adult's refusal for safety"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C230", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse rotates across a mental health unit, a community home for adults with disabilities and a medical unit during one week. At each placement she meets adult clients whose treatment decisions raise questions about capacity, consent and respect for beliefs. She works under RN supervision, follows regulatory standards, and documents each conversation carefully with the client's own words.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Respect his informed refusal, document it, report to the RN, and continue other care): A capable adult may refuse treatment, including blood products, even when refusal is risky, so the nurse honors the informed refusal, documents it and informs the RN for alternatives. Transfusing against his will is assault. His wife cannot override a capable client, and repeated pressure is coercive. Informed refusal is part of consent."}, {"id": 1966, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Kowal, 71, signed a surgical consent form yesterday. Today, while the nurse prepares him for theatre, he asks what exactly the surgeon will be taking out and says the form was handed to him with other papers. The signature is on the form. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Reassure him the surgeon is skilled and proceed because the form is complete", "B": "Pause preparation and notify the RN so the surgeon can clarify before he proceeds", "C": "Continue preparation because his signature proves he was informed during yesterday's consent discussion", "D": "Explain the surgical procedure herself in detail, since the form lists the operation name"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using reassurance in place of informed consent", "C": "Treating a signature as proof of understanding", "D": "Explaining surgery herself, which belongs to the surgeon"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C230", "cluster_text": "A practical nurse rotates across a mental health unit, a community home for adults with disabilities and a medical unit during one week. At each placement she meets adult clients whose treatment decisions raise questions about capacity, consent and respect for beliefs. She works under RN supervision, follows regulatory standards, and documents each conversation carefully with the client's own words.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pause preparation and notify the RN so the surgeon can clarify before he proceeds): A signature records consent but does not prove understanding, and his question shows he lacks key information, so the process must pause and the surgeon must explain. Proceeding because the form is complete ignores that. Explaining the operation herself exceeds her role. Consent is a process, not a piece of paper."}, {"id": 1967, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Aqpik, 44, an Inuit man, attends a clinic follow-up after heart surgery in a southern city, far from his home community. He is low in mood, eats little and says he misses hunting and family. He has few visitors. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Assess his mood with a depression screening tool and refer to the RN", "B": "Refer him for antidepressants right away since low mood after surgery is expected", "C": "Suggest he join a local support group for heart patients in the southern city", "D": "Explore his ties to family and land and help arrange contact and traditional foods"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Screening mood without exploring cultural and family causes", "B": "Moving to medication without exploring causes", "C": "Offering a generic group that ignores his own community ties"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explore his ties to family and land and help arrange contact and traditional foods): Connection to land, family and traditional food is central to wellbeing for many Inuit, and separation can slow recovery, so the nurse explores it and helps restore contact. Screening mood alone and a general support group do not address what he says he misses. Referring for antidepressants is beyond her role and skips assessment. Culturally safe care supports identity and connection."}, {"id": 1968, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Hana, 29, delivered a stillborn daughter at 38 weeks this morning. Her partner sits quietly while she stares at the wall. A colleague suggests telling them they are young and can try again soon. Which nursing response best supports the parents?", "options": {"A": "Say that having another pregnancy soon may help ease the pain of this loss", "B": "Acknowledge their loss, use the baby's name if shared, and offer time with her", "C": "Encourage them to stay busy with discharge teaching to distract them from sadness", "D": "Explain that many pregnancies end this way and most causes are often not found afterward"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Minimizing the loss by pointing to a future pregnancy", "C": "Distracting grieving parents with tasks", "D": "Offering statistics instead of empathy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acknowledge their loss, use the baby's name if shared, and offer time with her): Parents grieving a stillbirth need their loss acknowledged, their baby recognized and time to hold and see her if they wish. Talking about another pregnancy dismisses this baby. Busy tasks avoid their grief, and statistics, though true, do not comfort. Presence and validation come before information."}, {"id": 1969, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Dani, a practical nurse on a medical unit, is told to apply a wound care product she has never used before on an older client. She feels unsure about the technique and the frequency of changes. Which action is the best first step for making a safe decision?", "options": {"A": "Ask the nearest peer how she usually applies it and follow that approach", "B": "Look up the product online and apply it using the first video she finds", "C": "Check the agency policy and product guidelines, then consult the RN if unsure", "D": "Ask the care aide who has used it before to apply the dressing instead"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on a colleague's habit instead of written guidance", "B": "Using an unvetted online source instead of agency policy", "D": "Handing a nursing task to a care aide outside that scope"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C239", "cluster_text": "Dani, a practical nurse, works a day shift on a medical unit with an RN, a new graduate practical nurse and a care aide. During the morning the team receives an unfamiliar product order, plans a discharge, holds a safety huddle and gives shift report. She must decide how to use evidence, policies and team resources throughout the shift. She wants every decision to be safe and well supported today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check the agency policy and product guidelines, then consult the RN if unsure): Unfamiliar tasks should be guided by current policy and manufacturer guidelines, with the RN consulted for any gaps, so practice rests on evidence rather than habit. A peer's way may be outdated or wrong. An online video may not match agency policy, and handing the dressing to a care aide moves a nursing task to someone outside that scope. Look it up, then ask."}, {"id": 1970, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Kerr, 78, who lives alone, is being discharged home after a fall. The team has drafted mobility and bathing goals without asking him. He says he prefers evening showers and does not want a walker. What should Dani do?", "options": {"A": "Keep the plan as written because the team's goals reflect clinical judgment about safety", "B": "Bring his preferences to the team and revise the plan with him and his family", "C": "Schedule the evening showers he wants, but keep the walker as a discharge condition", "D": "Ask his family to persuade him to accept the walker before the team meets"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Planning without involving the client", "C": "Granting one preference while imposing another goal", "D": "Using family pressure instead of negotiating with the client"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C239", "cluster_text": "Dani, a practical nurse, works a day shift on a medical unit with an RN, a new graduate practical nurse and a care aide. During the morning the team receives an unfamiliar product order, plans a discharge, holds a safety huddle and gives shift report. She must decide how to use evidence, policies and team resources throughout the shift. She wants every decision to be safe and well supported today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bring his preferences to the team and revise the plan with him and his family): Care plans work best when the client helps create them, so Dani brings his preferences back to the team and revises the plan with him and his family, looking for safe alternatives to a walker. Keeping the plan unchanged ignores him. Granting only the shower time while insisting on the walker, or asking family to persuade him, still sets goals without him. Plan with clients, not for them."}, {"id": 1971, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A 10 minute safety huddle is called for 1000, but Dani's unit is very busy with three clients awaiting medication and one admission. A senior colleague says huddles can be skipped on days like this. Dani's client Mrs. Ito has a rising pain score. Which decision is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Attend a short huddle, share Mrs. Ito's pain status, and hear unit priorities first", "B": "Send the care aide in her place and ask for notes afterward", "C": "Attend the huddle, listen to priorities, and report Mrs. Ito's pain after her next reassessment", "D": "Follow the senior colleague's advice and skip it, then update the team at report"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delegating attendance to someone without clinical judgment", "C": "Delaying report of a rising pain score", "D": "Skipping a huddle on advice of a colleague when risk is rising"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C239", "cluster_text": "Dani, a practical nurse, works a day shift on a medical unit with an RN, a new graduate practical nurse and a care aide. During the morning the team receives an unfamiliar product order, plans a discharge, holds a safety huddle and gives shift report. She must decide how to use evidence, policies and team resources throughout the shift. She wants every decision to be safe and well supported today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Attend a short huddle, share Mrs. Ito's pain status, and hear unit priorities first): Huddles are most useful when the unit is busy because they surface risks and set priorities, so a brief attendance with a report on Mrs. Ito's pain serves her and the team. Skipping or sending a care aide loses clinical input. Holding back her rising pain until a later reassessment delays action. Brief, focused communication saves time."}, {"id": 1972, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At 1500 Dani gives report on Mr. Park, 66, to the oncoming practical nurse. She has completed his dressing, bath and meals. However, his pain has increased this afternoon and his urine output has been low for two hours. What should her report include?", "options": {"A": "Current condition and changes such as pain and low urine output, without tasks done or follow-up", "B": "Tasks done and pending follow-up, with his pain and urine output left for the chart", "C": "A written note of tasks done and changes left at the desk, with no verbal handover", "D": "Tasks done, current condition, changes such as pain and low urine output, and pending follow-up"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dropping completed care and follow-up from handover", "B": "Leaving concerning changes to the chart", "C": "Replacing verbal handover with a note"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C239", "cluster_text": "Dani, a practical nurse, works a day shift on a medical unit with an RN, a new graduate practical nurse and a care aide. During the morning the team receives an unfamiliar product order, plans a discharge, holds a safety huddle and gives shift report. She must decide how to use evidence, policies and team resources throughout the shift. She wants every decision to be safe and well supported today.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tasks done, current condition, changes such as pain and low urine output, and pending follow-up): A good handover gives continuity: what was done, how the client is now, what has changed and what still needs doing, so the new nurse can act on his rising pain and low urine output. Leaving out completed care and pending items causes gaps, and pushing the changes into the chart or a note hides concerns the oncoming nurse must hear. Do not depend on the oncoming nurse to find problems."}, {"id": 1973, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A therapy dog visits a long-term care home weekly. Mr. Reyes, 83, who has a chronic leg wound and takes prednisone, pets the dog and then reaches for the cookie on his tray. The nurse notices no hand hygiene was done. What action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Allow him to eat the cookie because the dog was healthy and groomed that morning", "B": "Let him eat the cookie and ask him to wash his hands after the snack", "C": "Ask the dog's handler to confirm the dog's vaccinations before he eats the cookie", "D": "Help him clean his hands, replace the cookie, and follow the policy for animal contact"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Skipping hand hygiene after animal contact", "B": "Delaying hand hygiene until after eating", "C": "Checking the animal instead of cleaning his hands"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Help him clean his hands, replace the cookie, and follow the policy for animal contact): Hands that touched an animal can carry organisms, and because his steroid use and wound raise infection risk, he needs hand hygiene before eating and a fresh cookie. A well-groomed dog does not remove that need. Eating first and washing later, or checking the dog's vaccinations, leaves his hands unclean at the moment of risk. Animal visits can continue when controls work."}, {"id": 1974, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Fraser, 79, has a venous leg ulcer. At today's dressing change the practical nurse is asked to record measurements. The previous note reads 'length 3 cm'. The ulcer now looks larger with deeper edges and pink tissue at the base. Which set of measurements gives the most useful assessment of healing?", "options": {"A": "Length alone, measured at the same site with the same ruler each visit", "B": "Length, width, depth, tissue type and drainage, compared with earlier measurements", "C": "Length and width alone, since depth is difficult to measure on a venous ulcer", "D": "Surface area estimated from a photograph, without measuring depth or describing the wound bed"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Measuring one dimension of a three dimensional wound", "C": "Omitting depth, which shows tissue loss", "D": "Replacing assessment with a photograph estimate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Length, width, depth, tissue type and drainage, compared with earlier measurements): Healing is judged by changes in size in all dimensions plus the tissue in the base and the drainage, compared with earlier findings. Length alone misses width and depth, and skipping depth hides tissue loss. A photograph cannot replace direct assessment. Describe the whole wound each time."}, {"id": 1975, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The patient advisor, Mr. Boyd, whose wife was recently a client, offers to review the hand hygiene posters and share how families see staff practice. A team member says clients cannot add much to quality work. Lena is asked for her view. What should she say?", "options": {"A": "Clients and families bring a valuable view of care, so include their input", "B": "Clients can review posters, but quality measures should come from staff expertise", "C": "Include client views after the audit ends, so they do not bias results", "D": "Clients can join if the manager approves, since quality work belongs to staff"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Limiting client input to communication materials", "C": "Involving clients late rather than in planning", "D": "Making client involvement depend on approval"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C240", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, a quality improvement team of nurses, a physician, a manager and a patient advisor is working to improve hand hygiene compliance. A practical nurse, Lena, joins the team. The unit will run a two week audit, discuss results at a staff meeting and invite client feedback on how the changes feel. Each member brings a different view of everyday care on the unit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Clients and families bring a valuable view of care, so include their input): People who receive care notice things staff miss, such as whether hands are cleaned before contact, so including clients and families strengthens quality work. Limiting them to posters, to a later stage, or to staff-approved roles keeps them outside decisions. Partnering with clients improves relevance."}, {"id": 1976, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Lena is asked to join the hand hygiene audit team. The charge nurse says her role should only be counting observations on audit sheets. Lena believes practical nurses can contribute more. Which description best fits the role of a practical nurse on a quality improvement team?", "options": {"A": "Contribute front-line insight, join data collection and discussion, and help carry out changes", "B": "Observe and report on colleagues' practice to the manager, leaving team discussion to the RN", "C": "Collect observation data only, since analysis and planning belong to the RN and the unit manager", "D": "Advise the team on changes but avoid data collection, which belongs to the quality department"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Acting as a monitor of peers rather than a team member", "C": "Limiting the role to data counting", "D": "Excluding herself from data collection within her role"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C240", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, a quality improvement team of nurses, a physician, a manager and a patient advisor is working to improve hand hygiene compliance. A practical nurse, Lena, joins the team. The unit will run a two week audit, discuss results at a staff meeting and invite client feedback on how the changes feel. Each member brings a different view of everyday care on the unit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Contribute front-line insight, join data collection and discussion, and help carry out changes): Practical nurses provide front-line knowledge, collect data, join discussions and help put changes into practice within the team. Limiting them to counting or to advice alone wastes their insight, and acting as an observer for the manager shifts the role to monitoring peers. Quality work is shared work."}, {"id": 1977, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During the audit week, Lena is assigned to observe hand hygiene before and after client contact. A colleague suggests telling staff the audit days so they can prepare and results look good. The unit wants accurate baseline data. What should Lena do?", "options": {"A": "Observe using the agreed tool and method without announcing days, to capture routine practice", "B": "Record only the opportunities she happens to notice, estimating the remaining visits from memory afterward", "C": "Observe only during busy morning shifts, when hand hygiene opportunities are most frequent", "D": "Use her own checklist instead of the agreed tool, since it is faster to complete"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Estimating observations from memory instead of recording them", "C": "Sampling one shift instead of routine practice", "D": "Changing the measurement tool so counts cannot be compared"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C240", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, a quality improvement team of nurses, a physician, a manager and a patient advisor is working to improve hand hygiene compliance. A practical nurse, Lena, joins the team. The unit will run a two week audit, discuss results at a staff meeting and invite client feedback on how the changes feel. Each member brings a different view of everyday care on the unit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Observe using the agreed tool and method without announcing days, to capture routine practice): Accurate baseline data require observing everyday practice with a consistent tool at varied times, so announcing days or sampling one shift distorts the picture. Using a personal checklist makes counts impossible to compare, and estimating visits from memory adds error. Honest data let the team target real gaps. Audit what happens, not what is staged."}, {"id": 1978, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The two week audit shows a hand hygiene compliance rate of 68 percent. The manager suggests that the numbers go only to the unit managers. Lena believes frontline staff who deliver care should also see the results. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Wait for next quarter's audit to share, so the results can be averaged with new data", "B": "Share the results with all unit staff in a respectful, non-punitive way and invite their ideas", "C": "Share results with the unit's senior nurses, who will pass the key points to staff", "D": "Post the unit's rate on the staff room wall without discussion or any request for ideas"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying feedback until it loses value", "C": "Filtering results through intermediaries", "D": "Sharing a number without engaging staff"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C240", "cluster_text": "On a medical unit, a quality improvement team of nurses, a physician, a manager and a patient advisor is working to improve hand hygiene compliance. A practical nurse, Lena, joins the team. The unit will run a two week audit, discuss results at a staff meeting and invite client feedback on how the changes feel. Each member brings a different view of everyday care on the unit.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Share the results with all unit staff in a respectful, non-punitive way and invite their ideas): Sharing audit results with the whole team, framed as learning rather than blame, lets the people who provide care help improve it. Passing results through senior nurses or posting a number without discussion limits engagement, and waiting a quarter prevents timely action. Transparent feedback drives change."}, {"id": 1979, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A practical nurse is giving prescribed antibiotic ear drops to a 2-year-old with an ear infection. The child sits on his mother's lap and is fidgety, and the drops are at room temperature. Which technique is correct for this child's age?", "options": {"A": "Pull the ear up and back, instill the drops, and keep him lying on his side", "B": "Pull the ear down and back, instill the drops, and keep him lying on his side", "C": "Insert the dropper tip deep into the canal, instill the drops, and keep him lying down", "D": "Pull the ear down and back, instill the drops, then have him sit up right away"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using the adult pinna direction on a young child", "C": "Placing the dropper deep in the canal, risking injury", "D": "Letting the child sit up and lose the medication"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pull the ear down and back, instill the drops, and keep him lying on his side): In young children the canal slopes upward, so the ear is pulled down and back to straighten it, and lying on the side lets the drops reach the canal. The adult direction does not straighten a child's canal. Sitting up wastes the dose, and a deep dropper tip can injure the eardrum. Match technique to the child's age."}, {"id": 1980, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Kyle, 22, attends a community clinic. Over three weeks he has been drinking several litres of water daily, urinating often at night and has lost 5 kg without trying. He feels tired. His capillary glucose is 18.2 mmol/L. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Explain that these symptoms mean diabetes and begin teaching about insulin injections and diet changes", "B": "Teach him to lower his carbohydrate intake and recheck his glucose at home in one week", "C": "Tell him the symptoms are likely stress related and advise rest until his next appointment", "D": "Report the symptoms and glucose result to the RN or physician promptly for assessment and orders"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Diagnosing and teaching treatment outside her scope", "B": "Starting self-management advice and delaying assessment", "C": "Attributing physical symptoms to stress"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report the symptoms and glucose result to the RN or physician promptly for assessment and orders): Excessive thirst, urination, weight loss and a glucose of 18.2 mmol/L point to possible new diabetes needing prompt assessment by a provider, so the nurse reports at once. Waiting a week or attributing symptoms to stress risks ketoacidosis. Diet advice alone skips assessment, and announcing a diagnosis and starting insulin teaching exceeds her scope. Report significant findings promptly."}, {"id": 1981, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Liu, 65, visits the clinic reporting spinning dizziness when he turns over in bed or looks up. He lives alone and has stairs at home, and his blood pressure is normal. The nurse is giving fall prevention teaching. Which statement should be included?", "options": {"A": "Hold the handrail on the stairs and climb quickly to limit time spent dizzy", "B": "Avoid looking up and keep the room dark at night to prevent the spinning", "C": "Move slowly, sit down when dizzy, and keep walkways clear and well lit", "D": "Drink extra fluids and add salt to his meals to raise his blood pressure"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Rushing on stairs while dizzy", "B": "Using darkness, which raises fall risk", "D": "Treating normal blood pressure as the cause of vertigo"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Move slowly, sit down when dizzy, and keep walkways clear and well lit): Slow position changes, sitting at the first sign of dizziness and a safe, lit home reduce falls in someone with positional vertigo. A dark room raises the chance of a fall, and rushing on stairs adds risk when the spinning comes without warning. Extra salt does not help because his blood pressure is normal. Teach practical precautions that fit his daily life."}, {"id": 1982, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Novak, 82, takes seven pills daily and uses a weekly pill organizer. At a community visit the nurse finds Thursday and Friday still full. She says the small pills are hard to see and she forgets the noon dose. Which action best addresses the barrier?", "options": {"A": "Move all her pills to a larger bottle so she can see them more easily", "B": "Teach her to set a phone alarm for noon and leave the schedule as it is", "C": "Ask her daughter to take charge of all medications without discussing it with Mrs. Novak", "D": "Assess vision, memory and routine, then ask the RN or pharmacist about simplifying the schedule"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Removing the organizer's safeguards instead of treating the barrier", "B": "Offering a reminder without simplifying a complex schedule", "C": "Taking over medications without involving the client"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess vision, memory and routine, then ask the RN or pharmacist about simplifying the schedule): Missed doses here come from poor vision, forgetfulness and a complex schedule, so the nurse assesses those barriers and involves the RN or pharmacist to simplify regimen, packaging and timing. Moving pills to a large bottle removes the organizer's safeguards, and an alarm alone leaves the vision problem and the complex schedule. Removing her control disrespects her. Address the cause of non-adherence."}, {"id": 1983, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Bauer, 45, steps on a rusty nail while gardening and presents to the clinic with a deep puncture wound on his foot. He cannot remember his last tetanus shot. The wound is bleeding slightly and does not look infected. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Give a tetanus booster right away without checking, since extra doses are low risk", "B": "Clean the wound and reassure him tetanus is rare because the wound looks clean", "C": "Close the puncture with adhesive strips so that bacteria cannot easily enter the wound", "D": "Clean the wound and obtain his immunization history so the prescriber can decide on tetanus prophylaxis"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving a vaccine without an order or history", "B": "Judging tetanus risk by how the wound looks", "C": "Closing a deep puncture, which traps bacteria"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Clean the wound and obtain his immunization history so the prescriber can decide on tetanus prophylaxis): Deep puncture wounds contaminated with soil carry a tetanus risk even when they look clean, so the nurse cleans the wound and finds out his immunization status for the prescriber's decision. Reassurance based on appearance is unsafe. Giving a booster needs an order and a history. Sealing a puncture traps anaerobic bacteria."}, {"id": 1984, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Priya, 30, cooked a large pot of chili for her family and asks how to store the leftovers. She plans to leave the pot on the stove until it cools before putting it in the fridge, about three hours. What advice should the nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Leave it on the stove until the pot cools, then refrigerate it in the same pot", "B": "Leave it in the switched off oven until it reaches room temperature, then refrigerate", "C": "Divide it into shallow containers and refrigerate within two hours instead of waiting", "D": "Refrigerate it in the same deep pot, uncovered, within four hours of cooking"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Cooling slowly in the temperature danger zone", "B": "Cooling slowly in the temperature danger zone", "D": "Using a four hour limit and a deep pot that cools slowly"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Divide it into shallow containers and refrigerate within two hours instead of waiting): Food should be refrigerated within two hours, and dividing it into shallow containers lets it cool quickly through the temperature range where bacteria multiply. A large pot cools slowly in the middle, whether on the stove, in an oven or in the fridge. Waiting four hours is too long. Chill leftovers quickly."}, {"id": 1985, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a community clinic, a reusable speculum has been washed with soap and water and rinsed. The nurse wonders whether it can be used on the next client. It contacts mucous membranes but not sterile tissue. Which statement correctly describes what is needed?", "options": {"A": "It needs sterilization in an autoclave, because disinfection cannot make mucosal devices safe", "B": "Cleaning with soap and water is equal to disinfection, so it may be reused now", "C": "Cleaning removes soil only; it still needs high-level disinfection before reuse on mucous membranes", "D": "Cleaning followed by a quick alcohol wipe gives the same level of safety as disinfection"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Thinking only sterilization is acceptable for mucosal devices", "B": "Treating cleaning as equal to disinfection", "D": "Believing an alcohol wipe replaces high-level disinfection"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cleaning removes soil only; it still needs high-level disinfection before reuse on mucous membranes): Cleaning removes visible soil but does not kill enough organisms, and a device touching mucous membranes needs at least high-level disinfection before reuse. A quick alcohol wipe does not reach that level. Insisting on sterilization alone is incorrect because high-level disinfection is accepted for this category. Clean first, then disinfect."}, {"id": 1986, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 12-month-old girl was at the 50th percentile for weight at 6 months. Today at her clinic visit she is at the 15th percentile, still above the third. Her mother says she eats well. The nurse reviews the growth chart. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Report the downward percentile crossing to the RN or provider and gather a feeding history", "B": "Advise her mother to offer extra milk after meals and review growth at the next visit", "C": "Document the measurements and reassure her mother since the weight is above the third percentile", "D": "Report only if her length is also below the third percentile for her age"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Advising feeding changes before assessment", "C": "Using only the lowest percentile cutoff as a concern", "D": "Waiting for a second measure to cross a cutoff"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the downward percentile crossing to the RN or provider and gather a feeding history): The trend matters more than a single percentile, and crossing downward from the 50th to the 15th warrants report and a feeding history even though she is above the third. Reassurance based on the cutoff misses the pattern. Waiting for length to fall as well, or adding feeds before assessment, delays the cause being found."}, {"id": 1987, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At a home visit, Mr. Gill's daughter asks whether a practical nurse can look after a dying person. Mr. Gill has end-stage lung cancer and a palliative plan written by the team. The nurse is competent in comfort care and has RN support. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Only RNs may care for dying clients, so the practical nurse should withdraw from this care.", "B": "Practical nurses may provide palliative care within their competence and consult the RN.", "C": "Care of dying clients belongs to unregulated providers because it needs no clinical judgment", "D": "Practical nurses can lead dying care independently once a plan exists, without RN involvement."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing practical nurses cannot care for dying clients", "C": "Underestimating the clinical judgment end of life care needs", "D": "Practicing independently beyond competence on complex needs"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Practical nurses may provide palliative care within their competence and consult the RN.): Practical nurses can provide palliative and end of life care within their individual competence and consult the RN when needs are complex or unpredictable. Withdrawing leaves the family unsupported. Leading care without RN involvement ignores complexity, and handing it to unregulated providers ignores the judgment symptom management needs. Know your competence and when to consult."}, {"id": 1988, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A new father, 28, tells the nurse at a clinic that he is cooking chicken breasts for his family tonight and plans to check that the meat is no longer pink inside. He has a digital food thermometer in a drawer. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Cutting into the thickest piece and checking for firm texture is accurate enough", "B": "Colour is a reliable guide, so cooking until the juices run clear is safe enough", "C": "Use the thermometer, since poultry is safe at 74 C in the thickest part", "D": "Cook to 63 C in the thickest part, which is the safe temperature for poultry breasts"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on texture instead of a thermometer", "B": "Judging doneness by colour alone", "D": "Using a temperature that is for other foods"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use the thermometer, since poultry is safe at 74 C in the thickest part): Colour and texture do not reliably show that harmful bacteria have been killed, while an internal temperature of 74 C in the thickest part does for poultry pieces. The 63 C figure applies to other foods, not poultry. Encourage a thermometer for every cook. Measure, do not guess."}, {"id": 1989, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At shift change on an acute care unit, the outgoing nurse gives bedside handover for Mr. Ahmed, 74, whose roommate's family is visiting. She begins discussing his new HIV test result and his wife's recent illness. He appears uneasy. What should the oncoming practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Continue the report but leave out Mr. Ahmed's name and use his bed number instead", "B": "Politely redirect sensitive details to a private place and keep bedside talk to non-sensitive information", "C": "Skip bedside handover and give the whole report at the nursing station instead", "D": "Draw the curtain around the bed and continue the full report in a low voice"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believing removing a name protects privacy", "C": "Dropping client involvement in handover entirely", "D": "Treating a curtain as a privacy barrier"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Politely redirect sensitive details to a private place and keep bedside talk to non-sensitive information): Bedside handover supports client involvement, but sensitive information must stay private, so the nurse redirects those details to a private setting and keeps the rest at the bedside. Using a bed number or drawing a curtain does not stop roommates and visitors from hearing. Moving the whole report loses the client's voice."}, {"id": 1990, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mrs. Perron, 70, is in a hospice with advanced cancer and asks the practical nurse about medical assistance in dying and how to request it. The nurse feels uncertain about her own role and about the unit's process. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Listen respectfully, document her request, and tell the RN or team so the process starts", "B": "Tell her requests go through her family physician and refer her there after discharge", "C": "Give her a printed federal information sheet and leave the next steps to her", "D": "Assess her eligibility herself using the federal criteria and then tell her the result today"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Redirecting the client away from the team caring for her", "C": "Handing over a leaflet instead of routing the request", "D": "Assessing eligibility, which belongs to authorized assessors"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Listen respectfully, document her request, and tell the RN or team so the process starts): A client's request deserves a respectful hearing and accurate routing, so the nurse documents it and informs the RN or team who can start the formal assessment. Redirecting her elsewhere or handing over a leaflet leaves her without a route, and eligibility is assessed by designated clinicians, not the practical nurse. Nurses are not required to provide the procedure and may follow conscientious objection policy. Listen, document, refer."}, {"id": 1991, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 40-year-old woman visits a travel clinic before a two week trip to a region with limited water safety. She says she plans to enjoy local salads, fresh fruit and drinks with ice, and to brush her teeth with tap water. Which teaching point is most important?", "options": {"A": "Salads are safe if they are washed in local tap water before serving", "B": "Ice is safe if the drink is carbonated, since the fizz kills bacteria", "C": "Carry an antibiotic and take it daily to prevent illness during the trip", "D": "Drink bottled or boiled water, avoid ice, and eat only thoroughly cooked or peelable foods"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Trusting washing in unsafe tap water", "B": "Believing carbonation makes ice safe", "C": "Using routine antibiotics instead of food and water precautions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Drink bottled or boiled water, avoid ice, and eat only thoroughly cooked or peelable foods): Traveller's diarrhea comes from contaminated water and food, so safe water, no ice and cooked or peelable foods are the key precautions. Washing in local tap water does not make produce safe, and fizz does not make ice safe. Daily antibiotics are not routine prevention. Teach 'boil it, cook it, peel it'."}, {"id": 1992, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Lopez, 59, is a participant in a clinical trial on a unit where the practical nurse works. He tells her he is thinking of withdrawing because of nausea and asks whether he qualifies for another study group. She is unsure of her role. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Advise him to stop the study drug now, then inform the investigator next week", "B": "Ask his physician outside the study to decide whether he should stay in the trial", "C": "Report his concerns and request to the research nurse or investigator, since they decide eligibility", "D": "Tell him he qualifies for the other group because nausea is a common reason to switch"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing study treatment and delaying report", "B": "Routing a study question to someone outside the research team", "D": "Deciding eligibility herself instead of referring"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report his concerns and request to the research nurse or investigator, since they decide eligibility): Participants may withdraw at any time, and the research team, not the practical nurse, decides eligibility and group placement, so she reports his concerns and request to them. Telling him he qualifies oversteps her role. Advising him to stop the study drug herself, or routing the question to a physician outside the study, leaves the investigator uninformed. Support the participant and route questions to the investigator."}, {"id": 1993, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mrs. Ouellette, 86, a long-term care resident, has new confusion since this morning. The practical nurse plans to phone the physician but has not yet checked vital signs, intake or her medication list. Which preparation best supports an effective call?", "options": {"A": "Collect current vital signs, medication list and onset details, then report using a structured format", "B": "Phone the physician now with the confusion and add the vital signs as they are taken", "C": "Wait for the next scheduled vital signs before calling so information is complete", "D": "Document the confusion and review it with the RN at the end of shift"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Calling before gathering key data", "C": "Delaying a call about an acute change", "D": "Delaying a report of an acute change"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Collect current vital signs, medication list and onset details, then report using a structured format): An acute change calls for a prompt call that carries current vital signs, medications and when the confusion began, ideally in a structured format such as SBAR. Calling first and gathering data afterward forces the physician to wait or ask for everything. Waiting for scheduled checks or leaving it until end of shift delays care."}, {"id": 1994, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a busy medical night shift, a practical nurse is assigned 20 clients with no other staff to help. She worries she cannot safely meet their needs. A colleague says accepting the assignment shows teamwork and that complaints cause conflict. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Complain to other staff and clients about the staffing level and wait for it to improve", "B": "Refuse to start work and leave the unit until the manager arrives", "C": "Raise the concern with the charge nurse or manager promptly and complete the required report form", "D": "Accept the assignment silently and work faster to protect the team's reputation"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Voicing concerns to the wrong people", "B": "Abandoning clients by leaving the unit", "D": "Staying silent about an unsafe assignment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raise the concern with the charge nurse or manager promptly and complete the required report form): Unsafe staffing is a client safety risk, so the nurse reports it through the proper channels right away so assignments can change, and files the required report. Silent acceptance hides the risk. Walking off the unit is abandonment, and complaining to staff or clients does not trigger a solution. Speak up to the people who can fix it."}, {"id": 1995, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, the practical nurse weighs a 9-month-old whose weight has fallen from the 50th percentile to below the 5th over three months. The baby feeds eagerly, and the mother says she plans to mix the formula stronger to add calories. Which response by the nurse is best?", "options": {"A": "Keep the prepared strength, and report the growth drop so the team can assess the feeding.", "B": "Suggest adding cereal to the bottle and reweighing the baby at the next routine visit.", "C": "Agree to the stronger mixture because extra calories will help the baby gain weight quickly.", "D": "Reassure the mother that growth slows normally by nine months and no follow up is needed."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Changing feeds without a prescriber or dietitian plan and delaying reassessment.", "C": "Concentrating formula without direction risks kidney strain and does not address the cause of poor growth.", "D": "Normalizing a crossing of growth percentiles and skipping referral."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep the prepared strength, and report the growth drop so the team can assess the feeding.): The baby has crossed major percentile lines, which signals possible failure to thrive that needs team assessment of intake, absorption and home factors. Stronger formula is unsafe because it changes the intended concentration and can harm the infant, so the mother needs clear direction. Adding cereal to the bottle and waiting also changes feeding without a plan and delays care. Report a falling growth trend promptly and let the team set the feeding plan."}, {"id": 1996, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Late one night Mr. Haddad grips the nurse's hand and says he does not want to die alone. His pain is rated 2 out of 10 and his vital signs are stable. The practical nurse has several routine tasks waiting, but the unit is quiet. Which approach best supports him now?", "options": {"A": "Sit with him, listen without hurrying, and let him express his fears and wishes.", "B": "Finish the waiting tasks first, then return to talk once the rounds are complete.", "C": "Call his daughter to come and sit with him, and continue with the waiting tasks.", "D": "Ask the spiritual care provider to visit in the morning and continue the routine tasks."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Putting routine tasks ahead of a client's urgent emotional need.", "C": "Handing his need for company to an exhausted relative", "D": "Postponing support until the next day"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C233", "cluster_text": "Mr. Ibrahim Haddad, 80, is in a hospice unit with advanced heart failure. He tells the practical nurse he is afraid of dying, and his two young grandchildren plan to visit this weekend. His daughter has slept in his room for a week and is exhausted. He has also said he keeps wondering what his life has meant. The team includes an RN, a physician, a social worker, and a spiritual care provider.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sit with him, listen without hurrying, and let him express his fears and wishes.): Mr. Haddad has voiced a fear, and his stable vital signs and low pain mean presence and listening are the nursing priority. Finishing tasks first, calling his daughter in, or waiting for a morning visit leaves him alone at the moment he asked for company. Quiet, unhurried presence is itself a nursing intervention in end-of-life care."}, {"id": 1997, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad's daughter asks whether his grandchildren, aged 5 and 8, should stay away because his appearance may frighten them. He is alert and says he wants to see them. His breathing is slightly labored but comfortable with oxygen. Which nursing response best reflects family-centred palliative care?", "options": {"A": "Recommend a video call instead so the children avoid seeing his physical changes.", "B": "Allow a visit but ask the children to stay brief and avoid touching the bed.", "C": "Prepare the children with honest, simple explanations, then support the visit.", "D": "Ask the daughter to decide alone, since she knows the children best."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Excluding children from the room despite the client's wish, assuming protection is kinder.", "B": "Adding restrictions that make the visit feel frightening rather than preparing the children.", "D": "Overriding the client's own wishes by handing the decision to a relative."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C233", "cluster_text": "Mr. Ibrahim Haddad, 80, is in a hospice unit with advanced heart failure. He tells the practical nurse he is afraid of dying, and his two young grandchildren plan to visit this weekend. His daughter has slept in his room for a week and is exhausted. He has also said he keeps wondering what his life has meant. The team includes an RN, a physician, a social worker, and a spiritual care provider.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Prepare the children with honest, simple explanations, then support the visit.): Mr. Haddad is alert and wants the visit, so his wish guides the plan. Children usually cope better when they are told truthfully what they will see and are supported during the visit. A video call and strict rules both keep them at a distance, and letting the daughter decide alone ignores the client's voice. Include children with preparation and support when the dying person wants them there."}, {"id": 1998, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad's daughter has slept in his room for seven days, skips meals, and snaps at staff when asked to rest. She says leaving would feel like abandoning him. Her father is comfortable and sleeping. Which nursing action best addresses her situation?", "options": {"A": "Acknowledge her commitment, ask about her needs, and arrange respite with the social worker.", "B": "Praise her devotion and encourage her to keep staying since he gains comfort from her presence.", "C": "Assess her blood pressure and blood glucose in the room, then let her decide about rest.", "D": "Offer her a cot and a meal tray, and let her continue staying as she chooses."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Praising endurance without offering support leaves caregiver fatigue untreated.", "C": "Treating a family member as a client outside scope", "D": "Meeting comfort needs without exploring her situation or arranging respite"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C233", "cluster_text": "Mr. Ibrahim Haddad, 80, is in a hospice unit with advanced heart failure. He tells the practical nurse he is afraid of dying, and his two young grandchildren plan to visit this weekend. His daughter has slept in his room for a week and is exhausted. He has also said he keeps wondering what his life has meant. The team includes an RN, a physician, a social worker, and a spiritual care provider.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acknowledge her commitment, ask about her needs, and arrange respite with the social worker.): Seven days without rest, missed meals and irritability are signs of caregiver fatigue that can harm her and her ability to support her father. Acknowledging her devotion while assessing her needs and arranging respite with the social worker gives practical help. Praise alone changes nothing, and a cot or meal tray without exploring her needs leaves the exhaustion in place. A practical nurse does not assess a family member as a client."}, {"id": 1999, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad says he does not follow any religion, yet he keeps asking what his life has meant and whether he did enough. The nurse recognizes possible spiritual distress. Which statement about spiritual needs best guides the practical nurse's next step?", "options": {"A": "Spiritual needs apply to clients who follow a faith, so a referral is unnecessary here.", "B": "Spiritual needs include meaning and connection, so a spiritual care referral is appropriate.", "C": "Spiritual needs are met when the nurse prays with the client at the bedside.", "D": "Spiritual distress is a mental illness, so a psychiatric consultation should come first."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming only religious clients have spiritual needs and missing his distress.", "C": "Imposing the nurse's own practice on the client", "D": "Treating a search for meaning as a psychiatric disorder"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C233", "cluster_text": "Mr. Ibrahim Haddad, 80, is in a hospice unit with advanced heart failure. He tells the practical nurse he is afraid of dying, and his two young grandchildren plan to visit this weekend. His daughter has slept in his room for a week and is exhausted. He has also said he keeps wondering what his life has meant. The team includes an RN, a physician, a social worker, and a spiritual care provider.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Spiritual needs include meaning and connection, so a spiritual care referral is appropriate.): Spiritual needs go beyond religion and include meaning, purpose and connection, which is exactly what Mr. Haddad is questioning. Offering a referral to the spiritual care provider respects that need. Claiming only believers have spiritual needs overlooks his distress, praying with him imposes the nurse's practice, and treating his search for meaning as mental illness bypasses spiritual care. Offer spiritual care to every dying client, whether or not they are religious."}, {"id": 2000, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 55-year-old client in the clinic reports episodes of vertigo lasting hours, ringing in the left ear, and a feeling of fullness. The physician diagnoses Meniere disease and asks the practical nurse to teach about home care. Which dietary teaching is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Drink extra caffeinated beverages to improve circulation to the ear.", "B": "Eat larger meals less often to steady body fluid levels.", "C": "Drink three litres of water daily to flush inner ear fluid.", "D": "Limit sodium to lower inner ear fluid pressure."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a stimulant that may worsen tinnitus and vertigo.", "B": "Believing meal size controls inner ear fluid", "C": "Believing extra water drains inner ear fluid"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Limit sodium to lower inner ear fluid pressure.): Meniere disease involves excess fluid pressure in the inner ear, so a lower sodium intake is the standard dietary teaching. Forcing extra fluids does not drain the ear, and large infrequent meals do not steady fluid levels. Caffeine can aggravate tinnitus rather than relieve it. Reinforce sodium limits, along with safety teaching during attacks."}, {"id": 2001, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A 48-year-old is in a day surgery unit awaiting an endoscopic procedure. The practical nurse is part of the team, and the surgeon says the time-out is a surgeon task, so the team can skip it today. The schedule is running late. Which action by the nurse is best?", "options": {"A": "Join the pause and confirm client identity, procedure, and site aloud with the team.", "B": "Verify the consent form later in recovery once the client is awake.", "C": "Defer to the surgeon because the time-out belongs to the physician's responsibilities.", "D": "Complete the identity check silently at the bedside and let the team proceed."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Postponing verification until after the procedure, when errors cannot be prevented.", "C": "Treating a team safety step as a single professional's duty.", "D": "Replacing a shared verbal check with a private check."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Join the pause and confirm client identity, procedure, and site aloud with the team.): A pre-procedure time-out is a shared team safety step in which every member confirms the client, the procedure and the site aloud. A private check at the bedside does not give the team the same chance to catch a discrepancy. Leaving it to the surgeon ignores each member's accountability, and verifying after the procedure is too late. Speak up and take part in the time-out even when the schedule is tight."}, {"id": 2002, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At shift change, a practical nurse handing over six clients uses unit slang and unapproved abbreviations because colleagues usually understand them. A newly hired nurse looks confused and nods anyway. The unit is short staffed that evening. Which practice best supports safe handover?", "options": {"A": "Use approved terms in plain words, and check that the receiving nurse understands each key point.", "B": "Limit handover to medications and vital signs and leave the rest for the chart.", "C": "Give written notes only and skip verbal discussion to avoid confusion over terms and abbreviations.", "D": "Keep using familiar unit slang since most staff know it and handover saves time."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Omitting clinical information that affects safety.", "C": "Removing the chance for questions by dropping verbal handover.", "D": "Assuming slang is clear to everyone, including new staff."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use approved terms in plain words, and check that the receiving nurse understands each key point.): Unapproved abbreviations and slang can be misread, and the new nurse's nod does not prove understanding. Approved terms in plain words, with a check for understanding, give a safer transfer of information. Dropping the verbal report removes the chance to ask questions, and covering only medications and vital signs leaves out information needed for safe care. Confirm understanding at every handover."}, {"id": 2003, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a community setting, the practical nurse is teaching an older adult who is starting oral alendronate for osteoporosis. The client usually eats breakfast at 7 a.m. and likes to rest in a recliner after morning pills. Which instruction is best?", "options": {"A": "Take it with milk or calcium-fortified juice to support bone building.", "B": "Take it at bedtime with a snack so it absorbs during sleep.", "C": "Take it with breakfast to protect the stomach and lie down afterward.", "D": "Take it on waking with water and stay upright for 30 minutes."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Combining it with calcium, which blocks absorption.", "B": "Taking it at night and lying down soon after the dose.", "C": "Taking it with food and reclining, which reduces absorption and raises esophageal irritation."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Take it on waking with water and stay upright for 30 minutes.): Oral bisphosphonates must be taken on an empty stomach with plain water and followed by staying upright, which lowers the risk of esophageal irritation. Food, milk and calcium-fortified juice all reduce absorption. Taking it at bedtime means lying down soon after the dose. Teach this client to keep upright, even though he likes to rest after morning pills."}, {"id": 2004, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a practical nurse sees a coworker give an oral medication without checking the client's two identifiers. The colleague is senior and busy, and nothing went wrong this time. The nurse is unsure how to respond. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Mention the concern informally at the next staff gathering when tensions are lower.", "B": "Raise the omission with the colleague privately and promptly, reporting per policy if it continues.", "C": "Tell other staff about the omission so peer pressure corrects the habit.", "D": "Say nothing since no harm occurred and the colleague has more experience."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying feedback until it loses its link to the event.", "C": "Discussing a colleague's practice with the wrong audience.", "D": "Treating a near miss as unimportant because no harm occurred."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Raise the omission with the colleague privately and promptly, reporting per policy if it continues.): Skipping identifier checks is a safety risk even when no harm follows, and prompt, private feedback fits a safety culture. Waiting for a social occasion weakens the message, and silence leaves the risk in place. Talking to other staff about the colleague is gossip and does not fix the practice. Escalate through the policy channel if the behaviour continues."}, {"id": 2005, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On an evening shift in long-term care, a practical nurse is assigned the charge role. A care aide asks whether the nurse is now accountable for every task done by all staff. She is new to leading shifts. Which response is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Everyone stays accountable for own actions, and the charge role adds duties such as assigning work.", "B": "Only the unit manager holds accountability, so a charge nurse need not review assignments.", "C": "No, the charge role is only a title and carries no extra duties beyond routine care.", "D": "Yes, charge accountability replaces individual accountability so the nurse answers for every task performed."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assigning all accountability upward to management.", "C": "Underestimating the leadership duties of the role.", "D": "Believing the charge role transfers everyone's accountability to one nurse."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Everyone stays accountable for own actions, and the charge role adds duties such as assigning work.): Each regulated and unregulated team member remains accountable for personal practice, so the charge role does not absorb everyone's accountability. The role does add leadership duties such as assigning work fairly and supporting colleagues. Calling it a title only denies those duties, and placing all accountability with the manager ignores personal responsibility. Know both the personal and the added duties of a charge role."}, {"id": 2006, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Working with a care aide, the practical nurse delegates blood pressure and pulse measurement for four stable clients. The aide reports the numbers near the end of the shift. All four clients were admitted two days ago. Which responsibility stays with the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Ask the aide to teach the clients what their readings mean.", "B": "Interpret the results against baselines and act on abnormal values.", "C": "Ask the aide to flag values outside the printed ranges.", "D": "Review only the readings from clients whose condition changed during the shift."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Handing client teaching to someone outside that scope.", "C": "Letting the aide screen results without nursing judgment", "D": "Reviewing a sample instead of every delegated result"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Interpret the results against baselines and act on abnormal values.): Measuring can be delegated for stable clients, but interpreting results against baselines and acting on them requires nursing judgment and stays with the nurse. Having the aide screen for abnormal values or reviewing only some readings leaves delegated results unchecked. Teaching about readings is also beyond the aide's role. Delegate the task, not the judgment."}, {"id": 2007, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In the emergency department, a 28-year-old has a positive rapid screening test for a viral infection. The report states that results need laboratory confirmation. The client wants to tell relatives the result is certain. What should the practical nurse explain?", "options": {"A": "A positive rapid result is final, so confirmation is only needed when it is negative.", "B": "A second rapid test on the same sample is enough to confirm the result.", "C": "A positive screen is probably accurate, so confirmation can wait until he is treated.", "D": "Rapid screens can be wrong, so laboratory confirmation is needed first."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treating a rapid screen as a final diagnosis.", "B": "Treating a repeat screen as confirmation", "C": "Delaying confirmation until after treatment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Rapid screens can be wrong, so laboratory confirmation is needed first.): Rapid tests are screening tools that can produce false positives or false negatives, so the report calls for laboratory confirmation. A positive result is not final. Repeating the same rapid test adds little, and treatment or sharing the result should not wait on a probable result. Explain the limit of the test before the client shares the result as certain."}, {"id": 2008, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In long-term care, an 84-year-old needs a bath, and the practical nurse notices a new red rash on the back. A care aide offers to bathe the client and also check the skin. The client is on no new medications. What is the best plan?", "options": {"A": "Hold the bath until the rash is gone to avoid worsening the irritation.", "B": "Bathe the client personally and leave the rash unreported since it looks minor.", "C": "Delegate the bath, but assess the rash personally and report the findings to the RN.", "D": "Delegate both the bath and the skin check, then review the aide's description later."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withholding hygiene care instead of assessing the cause.", "B": "Failing to report a new skin finding.", "D": "Delegating the skin assessment, which needs nursing judgment."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Delegate the bath, but assess the rash personally and report the findings to the RN.): A routine bath is within the aide's role, but assessing a new rash requires nursing judgment and cannot be delegated. Delegating both tasks hands over assessment, and withholding the bath neglects hygiene without finding the cause. Leaving a new rash unreported delays care for a possible allergy or infection. Assess and report new findings yourself."}, {"id": 2009, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a rehabilitation unit, an adult wants to manage personal inhalers in preparation for discharge. The nurse observes adequate technique. The client asks to keep the inhalers at the bedside. She has used inhalers at home for years. Which action best balances independence with safety?", "options": {"A": "Allow bedside storage and ask the client to tell staff if doses are missed.", "B": "Leave the inhalers in the bedside drawer without further checks since technique looked correct.", "C": "Follow facility policy, assess technique and understanding, and document ongoing checks.", "D": "Keep the inhalers at the nurses' station and give each dose to preserve safety."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on self-report alone.", "B": "Granting independence with no oversight.", "D": "Removing independence that the client has shown the skill to hold."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Follow facility policy, assess technique and understanding, and document ongoing checks.): Self-administration prepares the client for discharge, and good technique supports it, but oversight is still needed. Following policy, assessing understanding and documenting checks keeps the client safe while building independence. Leaving inhalers with no checks is unmonitored, and holding them at the station removes a skill the client has shown. Self-report alone may miss errors, so checks are documented."}, {"id": 2010, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home care visit, the practical nurse must prepare a reusable bedside commode for the next use. Visible stool soils the seat and rim. The client has no known infection. The client lives alone and the home care visit is brief today. Which step should come first?", "options": {"A": "Spray disinfectant directly over the soil so it kills microorganisms underneath.", "B": "Wipe the commode with a dry cloth and leave it to air dry.", "C": "Clean visible soil with detergent and water, then apply disinfectant following product directions.", "D": "Apply two layers of disinfectant and wipe it off after thirty seconds."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying disinfectant over visible soil, which reduces its action.", "B": "Skipping disinfection after wiping.", "D": "Misusing contact time and product directions."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Clean visible soil with detergent and water, then apply disinfectant following product directions.): Organic soil blocks disinfectants, so surfaces are cleaned with detergent and water first and then disinfected as directed. Spraying over soil leaves microorganisms protected. A dry wipe does not disinfect, and extra layers wiped off early ignore the required contact time. Clean before you disinfect."}, {"id": 2011, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Ms. Rahimi's sister stops the practical nurse at the desk, upset, and asks whether the diagnosis is serious since she is the closest relative. Ms. Rahimi is awake in her room down the hall. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Say that sharing needs her permission, and tell Ms. Rahimi.", "B": "Report the sister's request to the manager and leave the matter to her.", "C": "Share only that the diagnosis is manageable, since general reassurance does not breach privacy.", "D": "Direct the sister to the physician, who may share details with close relatives."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Escalating without speaking to the client.", "C": "Giving partial information about a diagnosis without consent.", "D": "Assuming physicians may disclose to relatives without the client's permission."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C241", "cluster_text": "Ms. Nadia Rahimi, 46, is a capable adult admitted to a medical unit with newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes. She asked the practical nurse not to share her diagnosis with her sister, who visits daily and has been asking staff for updates. The nurse is preparing glucose checks, insulin teaching, and documentation for the day. Ms. Rahimi also asked to speak with the team about her options.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Say that sharing needs her permission, and tell Ms. Rahimi.): Ms. Rahimi is a capable adult, so her health information is shared only with her permission, even with a close relative. Telling the sister even a general reassurance reveals that something is being discussed. Sending her to the physician does not change the client's wishes, and passing it to the manager skips the person who can decide. Go to the client first."}, {"id": 2012, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Ms. Rahimi tells the practical nurse she is thinking of declining the insulin teaching because she feels overwhelmed. The unit is busy, and the nurse wonders what to do before involving other team members. She is otherwise coping well today. Which step should come first?", "options": {"A": "Document the refusal and move on to the next client on the assignment.", "B": "Talk with Ms. Rahimi to explore her concerns and preferences.", "C": "Report the refusal to the physician before speaking with Ms. Rahimi about it.", "D": "Tell her sister so she can help persuade Ms. Rahimi to learn insulin."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Recording a refusal without exploring the reason.", "C": "Going straight to reporting without a conversation with the client.", "D": "Breaching confidentiality by involving the sister."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C241", "cluster_text": "Ms. Nadia Rahimi, 46, is a capable adult admitted to a medical unit with newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes. She asked the practical nurse not to share her diagnosis with her sister, who visits daily and has been asking staff for updates. The nurse is preparing glucose checks, insulin teaching, and documentation for the day. Ms. Rahimi also asked to speak with the team about her options.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Talk with Ms. Rahimi to explore her concerns and preferences.): A client's concern should be explored with her directly before a refusal is reported or recorded, because the reason may be fixable. Reporting first skips the person most affected. Documenting and moving on leaves the barrier unaddressed, and telling the sister discloses her health information without her permission. Start with a conversation."}, {"id": 2013, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Ms. Rahimi holds out her finger when the nurse arrives for a glucose check. Later the nurse plans to begin insulin self-injection teaching, and Ms. Rahimi is quiet and looking away. She is otherwise cooperative with care. Which statement about consent is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Implied consent for the glucose check also covers insulin teaching during the same shift.", "B": "Consent is unnecessary for teaching because education is not a treatment procedure.", "C": "Her offered finger shows implied consent for the check, but teaching needs her agreement.", "D": "Written consent is required before every glucose check on each new shift."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stretching implied consent from one task to a different one.", "B": "Treating teaching as exempt from client agreement.", "D": "Demanding written consent for a routine low-risk check."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C241", "cluster_text": "Ms. Nadia Rahimi, 46, is a capable adult admitted to a medical unit with newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes. She asked the practical nurse not to share her diagnosis with her sister, who visits daily and has been asking staff for updates. The nurse is preparing glucose checks, insulin teaching, and documentation for the day. Ms. Rahimi also asked to speak with the team about her options.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Her offered finger shows implied consent for the check, but teaching needs her agreement.): Holding out a finger signals implied consent for that specific check. Insulin teaching is a different activity, and her silence and averted gaze mean the nurse should seek her agreement before starting. Written consent for each routine check is not required, and teaching still needs the client's participation. Consent applies to each activity, not to the whole shift."}, {"id": 2014, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Ms. Rahimi later needs a minor procedure. After opioid analgesia for severe pain she appears drowsy when the nurse brings the consent form for her signature. The physician is waiting. Her pain was rated 8 out of 10 an hour ago. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Proceed now and obtain written consent once she has fully woken.", "B": "Consent given while drowsy or in severe pain may be invalid, so reassess first.", "C": "Ask her sister to sign on her behalf since Ms. Rahimi is drowsy.", "D": "Her signature is valid because she was capable earlier today and did not object."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Acting first and obtaining consent afterward.", "C": "Seeking a substitute signature while the client may regain capacity.", "D": "Assuming earlier capacity carries over to the current moment."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C241", "cluster_text": "Ms. Nadia Rahimi, 46, is a capable adult admitted to a medical unit with newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes. She asked the practical nurse not to share her diagnosis with her sister, who visits daily and has been asking staff for updates. The nurse is preparing glucose checks, insulin teaching, and documentation for the day. Ms. Rahimi also asked to speak with the team about her options.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Consent given while drowsy or in severe pain may be invalid, so reassess first.): Consent depends on understanding at the time it is given, and sedation or severe pain can limit that. Reassessing her readiness protects both her rights and the team. Earlier capacity does not carry forward, her sister has no authority while Ms. Rahimi may regain capacity, and consent after the procedure is not consent. Delay for reassessment instead of rushing."}, {"id": 2015, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In the evening Ms. Rahimi declines her routine bed bath, saying she is tired and wants to sleep. Her glucose is stable and her skin is intact. She has been cooperative with all other care today and ate well. How should the practical nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Respect her choice, offer to return later, and document the refusal.", "B": "Proceed with the bath since routine hygiene care does not require her agreement.", "C": "Persuade her to accept it, since skipping breaks the unit routine.", "D": "Respect her choice but skip documenting it, since hygiene refusals are routine."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believing routine care needs no consent.", "C": "Pressing a capable adult to accept care for routine's sake", "D": "Leaving a refusal out of the record"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C241", "cluster_text": "Ms. Nadia Rahimi, 46, is a capable adult admitted to a medical unit with newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes. She asked the practical nurse not to share her diagnosis with her sister, who visits daily and has been asking staff for updates. The nurse is preparing glucose checks, insulin teaching, and documentation for the day. Ms. Rahimi also asked to speak with the team about her options.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Respect her choice, offer to return later, and document the refusal.): A capable adult may decline any care, including routine hygiene, and her stable condition means waiting carries little risk. Respecting the choice, offering another time and documenting it protects her rights. Bathing her anyway or pressing her to accept for the sake of routine removes her choice, and leaving the refusal unrecorded hides information the team needs. Routine care still needs her agreement."}, {"id": 2016, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Despite being unable to report pain, an 85-year-old with advanced dementia grimaces and guards her left hip during repositioning. Her family says she has been calm and never complains. The practical nurse hears no verbal complaint. Which interpretation and action is best?", "options": {"A": "Use a behavioural pain tool and report the grimacing.", "B": "Attribute the grimacing to her dementia and add orientation measures at each turn.", "C": "Ask her family to rate her pain, since they know her best.", "D": "Rely on her vital signs, since normal readings confirm no pain."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Blaming behaviour on dementia and missing pain.", "C": "Replacing direct observation with a proxy report", "D": "Treating normal vital signs as proof of no pain"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a behavioural pain tool and report the grimacing.): Clients with dementia often express pain through facial expression and guarding, so a behavioural pain tool applied during movement gives objective data. Grimacing with hip guarding should be treated as possible pain and reported. Relying on family ratings or on vital signs alone can miss pain, since normal readings do not exclude it. Orientation measures do not address pain. Observe behaviour when words are not available."}, {"id": 2017, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 55-year-old on a medical unit receives mealtime insulin and says he feels well. The order says to check glucose before meals and at bedtime, and a colleague suggests testing only if he feels unwell. He has no history of hypoglycemia. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Test only when symptoms of high or low glucose develop, since he feels well.", "B": "Test before breakfast only and estimate the other values from that single morning result.", "C": "Check glucose before meals and at bedtime as ordered, whether or not he feels well.", "D": "Test after each meal instead, since peak values show the insulin effect best."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relying on symptoms, which may be absent when glucose is abnormal.", "B": "Estimating values instead of measuring them.", "D": "Replacing the ordered timing with a different schedule."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check glucose before meals and at bedtime as ordered, whether or not he feels well.): Glucose checks before meals and at bedtime guide mealtime insulin dosing and catch unrecognized lows or highs, and symptoms are an unreliable guide. Following the ordered times is the safe practice. Testing only with symptoms misses silent changes, and post-meal testing does not match the order. Estimating values from one test is not measurement. Check at the ordered times."}, {"id": 2018, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On a busy unit, a practical nurse disagrees with a colleague about who should complete a delayed dressing, and tension rises at the desk while clients are nearby. The nurse is tempted to avoid the colleague and hope it settles. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Avoid the colleague and complete the dressing alone so the dispute fades.", "B": "Raise the disagreement at the desk so other staff can weigh in.", "C": "Speak privately with the colleague, focus on client needs, and involve the charge nurse if unresolved.", "D": "Report the colleague to the manager before any discussion takes place."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Avoiding conflict and hoping it resolves.", "B": "Airing a dispute publicly in front of clients and staff.", "D": "Escalating before trying direct, respectful communication."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Speak privately with the colleague, focus on client needs, and involve the charge nurse if unresolved.): Conflict handled directly and privately, with attention on client needs, is the usual first step in team communication. Avoidance leaves the problem and the delayed dressing in place. Public debate escalates tension in front of clients, and reporting before talking skips the step that often resolves it. Involve the charge nurse if direct discussion does not work."}, {"id": 2019, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Thompson says Mia refuses the potty, and he wants to start training this weekend because daycare expects it. She has hard stools and cries when passing them. Her father has read about training online. What advice should the practical nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Wait until age four, because earlier training usually causes constipation.", "B": "Begin training this weekend and use sitting sessions after meals to encourage stools.", "C": "Give a daily laxative from the pharmacy and train this weekend.", "D": "Treat the constipation, then train once she is ready."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applying a fixed age rule instead of readiness signs", "B": "Starting training before readiness and before treating painful stools.", "C": "Starting a laxative without assessment or an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C244", "cluster_text": "The Thompson family visits a community clinic. Mia, 3, has had hard stools for two weeks and her father wants to start toilet training. Her brother Noah, 1, has dry itchy skin patches, and her brother Ethan, 7, brought home a school note about head lice. Mia's daycare has reported a hand, foot and mouth outbreak. The practical nurse works under the direction of the nurse practitioner.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat the constipation, then train once she is ready.): Painful hard stools can make a child withhold, so constipation should be treated before training starts. Readiness signs such as staying dry for longer periods and showing interest guide timing, not daycare expectations or a fixed age. Starting this weekend or giving a laxative without assessment adds pressure, and rigid age rules ignore her readiness. Treat first, then train when she is ready."}, {"id": 2020, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Noah, 1, has dry, itchy, red patches on his cheeks and limbs. His father usually gives him a long hot bath at bedtime because he thinks the warmth soothes the itch. Noah has no fever or oozing. Which skin care teaching is best?", "options": {"A": "Use short lukewarm baths, pat dry, and apply fragrance-free moisturizer.", "B": "Continue long hot baths since warmth relaxes him and relieves itching quickly.", "C": "Use a mild scented baby wash daily and rinse with warm water.", "D": "Skip moisturizer on red patches and use it just on dry areas."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using hot water, which dries and inflames eczema.", "C": "Using a scented product and warmer water on eczema", "D": "Leaving inflamed skin without moisturizer"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C244", "cluster_text": "The Thompson family visits a community clinic. Mia, 3, has had hard stools for two weeks and her father wants to start toilet training. Her brother Noah, 1, has dry itchy skin patches, and her brother Ethan, 7, brought home a school note about head lice. Mia's daycare has reported a hand, foot and mouth outbreak. The practical nurse works under the direction of the nurse practitioner.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use short lukewarm baths, pat dry, and apply fragrance-free moisturizer.): Eczema skin loses moisture easily, so short lukewarm baths followed by prompt moisturizer lock in water. Hot baths and scented wash both irritate, and leaving red patches without moisturizer lets them dry further. Skin care for an infant should be gentle and consistent. Pat dry, moisturize while the skin is damp, and avoid heat and fragrance."}, {"id": 2021, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ethan, 7, scratches his scalp, and the school note says live lice were seen. His father asks whether Ethan must stay home for several days and whether the whole house needs spraying. Ethan is otherwise well and playful. What is the best guidance?", "options": {"A": "Wait for a physician to confirm with a scalp culture before any treatment.", "B": "Keep him home until every nit is removed, which may take many days.", "C": "Treat his hair as directed, check contacts, and wash linens and hats.", "D": "Spray the whole house with insecticide and replace all mattresses."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delaying treatment for a test that is not needed.", "B": "Excluding a child until all nits are gone, an outdated rule.", "D": "Using insecticides on the home, which is unnecessary and unsafe."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C244", "cluster_text": "The Thompson family visits a community clinic. Mia, 3, has had hard stools for two weeks and her father wants to start toilet training. Her brother Noah, 1, has dry itchy skin patches, and her brother Ethan, 7, brought home a school note about head lice. Mia's daycare has reported a hand, foot and mouth outbreak. The practical nurse works under the direction of the nurse practitioner.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat his hair as directed, check contacts, and wash linens and hats.): Head lice are treated with an approved product used as directed, with checks of close contacts and washing of recently used personal items. Children generally do not need long exclusion, and nit removal is not a condition for return. Household spraying is unsafe and unnecessary, and no scalp culture is needed to treat live lice. Teach direct, practical steps."}, {"id": 2022, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mia's daycare reports an outbreak of hand, foot and mouth disease. Mr. Thompson asks about preventing spread at home and when Mia may return if she has no fever or blisters. Mia has no fever today. Which infection control advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Rely on hand sanitizer alone, since soap and water add little protection.", "B": "Keep Mia home until every blister has fully healed, even after she is well.", "C": "Reinforce handwashing and cleaning, and follow daycare and public health return guidance.", "D": "Exclude her for ten days after the outbreak report even if she stays well."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Replacing handwashing with sanitizer only.", "B": "Applying a rigid exclusion rule not required by public health", "D": "Using a fixed exclusion for a well child"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C244", "cluster_text": "The Thompson family visits a community clinic. Mia, 3, has had hard stools for two weeks and her father wants to start toilet training. Her brother Noah, 1, has dry itchy skin patches, and her brother Ethan, 7, brought home a school note about head lice. Mia's daycare has reported a hand, foot and mouth outbreak. The practical nurse works under the direction of the nurse practitioner.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reinforce handwashing and cleaning, and follow daycare and public health return guidance.): The virus spreads through stool, saliva and surfaces, and children can shed it without blisters, so hygiene and cleaning matter most. Return follows daycare and public health guidance, not exclusion until every blister heals or for a fixed number of days. Sanitizer alone is less effective on soiled hands than soap and water. Support handwashing and cleaning while using local guidance for return."}, {"id": 2023, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Within a community clinic, a practical nurse is caring for an adult who identifies as Inuit. A colleague suggests using the same ceremonies and communication practices that worked for a First Nations client last week. Which approach best reflects cultural safety?", "options": {"A": "Rely on general online descriptions of Inuit culture to guide care decisions.", "B": "Ask a First Nations colleague which practices to use, then apply them to this client.", "C": "Follow the client's religion as listed on the registration form when planning care.", "D": "Ask the client about personal and community values and preferences, and plan care together."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Replacing the client's voice with generic information.", "B": "Using another group's practices through a colleague instead of the client", "C": "Taking a form entry as the client's cultural preference"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask the client about personal and community values and preferences, and plan care together.): First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples have distinct cultures and values, so assumptions from one group do not fit another. Cultural safety is built by asking the client what matters and planning together. Borrowing practices from another group, or reading a registration form, replaces the client's voice, and general descriptions can mislead. Let the client define the cultural care they want."}, {"id": 2024, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On day 4 postpartum, during a home visit, Kayla reports a severe headache and blurred vision. Her blood pressure is 156/102 mmHg and the baby is feeding well. Kayla says her pregnancy is over so pressure no longer matters. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Recognize possible postpartum preeclampsia and arrange urgent medical assessment right away.", "B": "Give acetaminophen for the headache and reassure her that headaches are common after delivery.", "C": "Reassure her that preeclampsia resolves at birth, and suggest limiting screen time.", "D": "Advise rest and fluids at home, and recheck her blood pressure at the next scheduled visit."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Masking a warning symptom with analgesia.", "C": "Believing preeclampsia always ends at delivery.", "D": "Treating warning signs as fatigue and delaying reassessment."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C249", "cluster_text": "Kayla Dubois, 29, has been followed through pregnancy and the early postpartum period by a community clinic team. The practical nurse is reviewing her records and visits from several time points, including a mid-pregnancy clinic visit, a late-pregnancy visit, and a home visit after birth. Each question describes the visit named in its stem. The nurse practitioner provides orders, and the RN is available for consultation.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Recognize possible postpartum preeclampsia and arrange urgent medical assessment right away.): A severe headache, blurred vision and a pressure of 156/102 mmHg after birth suggest postpartum preeclampsia, which can still progress to seizures. Urgent reporting and medical assessment are required. Rest, analgesia and reassurance all delay care for a dangerous condition. Preeclampsia can appear or persist after delivery."}, {"id": 2025, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a prenatal visit, Kayla's hemoglobin is 98 g/L and she reports fatigue. The physician orders oral iron. She asks how to take it and mentions that she drinks tea with every meal. She is also taking a daily prenatal vitamin. Which teaching is best?", "options": {"A": "Take iron with a glass of milk to reduce constipation and improve uptake.", "B": "Take iron with antacids to prevent nausea and increase how much is absorbed.", "C": "Take iron with vitamin C-rich juice, and keep tea two hours away from the dose.", "D": "Take iron with tea, since tannins help absorption and reduce stomach upset for most people."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Combining iron with calcium, which lowers absorption.", "B": "Combining iron with antacids, which blocks absorption.", "D": "Believing tea improves iron absorption."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C249", "cluster_text": "Kayla Dubois, 29, has been followed through pregnancy and the early postpartum period by a community clinic team. The practical nurse is reviewing her records and visits from several time points, including a mid-pregnancy clinic visit, a late-pregnancy visit, and a home visit after birth. Each question describes the visit named in its stem. The nurse practitioner provides orders, and the RN is available for consultation.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Take iron with vitamin C-rich juice, and keep tea two hours away from the dose.): Vitamin C improves iron absorption, while tannins in tea, calcium in milk and antacids all reduce it. Spacing tea away from the dose protects absorption, and Kayla's low hemoglobin makes this important. Her habit of drinking tea with meals is the detail that needs direct teaching. Pair iron with vitamin C and separate it from inhibitors."}, {"id": 2026, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At 27 weeks, Kayla reports burning on urination and urinary frequency. She is afebrile and otherwise feels well. A colleague suggests waiting until a fever appears. She has no back pain and is eating and sleeping normally. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Advise cranberry juice and recheck her symptoms at the next prenatal visit.", "B": "Report the symptoms promptly so urine is tested and treatment arranged for the infection risk.", "C": "Suggest she limit fluids to reduce how often she needs to void.", "D": "Wait for fever before reporting, since afebrile urinary symptoms usually settle with fluids."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Using a home remedy in place of testing.", "C": "Cutting fluids, which concentrates urine and worsens irritation.", "D": "Waiting for fever, which can mean a kidney infection has developed."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C249", "cluster_text": "Kayla Dubois, 29, has been followed through pregnancy and the early postpartum period by a community clinic team. The practical nurse is reviewing her records and visits from several time points, including a mid-pregnancy clinic visit, a late-pregnancy visit, and a home visit after birth. Each question describes the visit named in its stem. The nurse practitioner provides orders, and the RN is available for consultation.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the symptoms promptly so urine is tested and treatment arranged for the infection risk.): Urinary symptoms in pregnancy may signal a urinary tract infection, which can ascend to the kidneys and raise the risk of preterm labour. Testing and treatment should not wait for fever. Cranberry juice does not replace testing, and limiting fluids makes irritation worse. Report symptoms the same day."}, {"id": 2027, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At 37 weeks, Kayla's fundal height is 37 cm. The nurse feels a firm round part under her ribs and hears fetal heart tones best above the umbilicus. Kayla asks whether the head is down. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Findings suggest a normal presentation, so no further review is needed before labour.", "B": "Findings suggest breech presentation, so report them for ultrasound confirmation.", "C": "Findings suggest the baby has dropped into the pelvis, so labour will start soon.", "D": "Heart tones confirm the head is down, since the heartbeat is easily heard."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismissing abnormal findings near term.", "C": "Misreading engagement as the cause of a high round part.", "D": "Assuming the head is down because the heartbeat is easy to hear."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C249", "cluster_text": "Kayla Dubois, 29, has been followed through pregnancy and the early postpartum period by a community clinic team. The practical nurse is reviewing her records and visits from several time points, including a mid-pregnancy clinic visit, a late-pregnancy visit, and a home visit after birth. Each question describes the visit named in its stem. The nurse practitioner provides orders, and the RN is available for consultation.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Findings suggest breech presentation, so report them for ultrasound confirmation.): A firm round part under the ribs and fetal heart tones heard above the umbilicus point to the head sitting at the fundus, which suggests breech. Heart tone location is a clue to position, not proof the head is down. Engagement would place the head low, not near the ribs. Report the finding so ultrasound can confirm before labour."}, {"id": 2028, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A postmenopausal woman started weekly alendronate and now reports burning pain behind the breastbone after the dose, which she took after breakfast and followed with a nap. She asks whether to continue as usual. What is the nurse's best action?", "options": {"A": "Advise her to continue unchanged, since indigestion is expected and will pass.", "B": "Report the symptom promptly, and reinforce fasting dosing with water and staying upright afterward.", "C": "Advise her to take the next dose with milk and lie down to ease the burning.", "D": "Tell her to split the weekly tablet in half and take the halves on separate days."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismissing possible esophageal irritation.", "C": "Taking the dose with food and lying down", "D": "Changing the prescribed dose without an order"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the symptom promptly, and reinforce fasting dosing with water and staying upright afterward.): Burning pain behind the breastbone after a bisphosphonate suggests esophageal irritation, which is made more likely by taking it with food and lying down. Reporting the symptom and correcting the technique addresses both the cause and the risk. Continuing unchanged risks injury, and taking it with milk while lying down repeats the same errors. Splitting the tablet changes the prescribed dose without an order."}, {"id": 2029, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Describing burning, shooting pain in both feet that is worse at night, a 60-year-old with long-standing diabetes gets little relief from acetaminophen. Light contact from the bedsheets hurts. He walks daily and checks his feet carefully. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Pain is from poor circulation, so advise more walking and warmer socks at night.", "B": "Pain is musculoskeletal, so more frequent acetaminophen and rest should control it.", "C": "Features suggest neuropathic pain, so report them and expect nerve-targeted therapy.", "D": "Pain is caused by arthritis, so advise warm soaks and gentle joint exercises."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Attributing nerve pain to circulation problems", "B": "Expecting simple analgesics to relieve nerve pain.", "D": "Mistaking nerve pain for arthritis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Features suggest neuropathic pain, so report them and expect nerve-targeted therapy.): Burning, shooting pain that is worse at night and triggered by light touch is typical of diabetic neuropathic pain, which responds poorly to simple analgesics. Reporting the pattern leads to assessment for nerve-targeted treatment. Musculoskeletal pain would not behave this way, poor circulation would not cause pain from light touch, and arthritis does not produce this nerve pattern."}, {"id": 2030, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, a practical nurse holds a scheduled antihypertensive because the client's pressure is 88/50 mmHg and the client is dizzy. A colleague says nothing needs charting since the drug was not given. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Give the dose once the client lies down and note only that it was late.", "B": "Hold the dose, report it verbally, and document only if the prescriber changes the order.", "C": "Withhold it, report to the prescriber, and document the hold with the reason.", "D": "Give half the dose and document the reduced amount with the blood pressure reading."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Giving a dose to a hypotensive client and understating the delay.", "B": "Documenting a held dose only when orders change", "D": "Altering the ordered dose without authority"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Withhold it, report to the prescriber, and document the hold with the reason.): A pressure of 88/50 mmHg with dizziness makes giving an antihypertensive unsafe, so the nurse holds it, reports to the prescriber and documents the dose held with the reason and findings. Recording a hold only if orders change leaves gaps in the record, and giving half a dose alters the order without authority. Giving it after lying down still risks harm. Record held doses."}, {"id": 2031, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Several negative comments about slow call bell response appear in a family survey received by a long-term care home. A practical nurse says the comments come from a few unhappy families and can be set aside. The survey response rate was high. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Set the comments aside, since a few families do not represent the home.", "B": "Ask management to remove negative comments from the report shared with all staff members.", "C": "Reply to the families with reasons why call bells are answered slowly.", "D": "Treat the comments as quality information, share them, and join in improvement planning."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismissing client and family feedback.", "B": "Hiding unfavourable information from the team.", "C": "Defending current practice instead of learning from it."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat the comments as quality information, share them, and join in improvement planning.): Family feedback is a source of quality information, and repeated comments about call bell response point to a possible safety and comfort gap. Sharing them with the team and joining improvement planning fits a safety culture. Setting comments aside or defending the home ignores the signal, and hiding them from staff prevents learning. Use feedback to improve care."}, {"id": 2032, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 70-year-old on a medical unit has a new open area at the coccyx found during morning care. The nurse is tempted to apply a dressing and carry on without telling anyone because it looks small. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Massage the area with lotion to improve circulation before applying a dressing.", "B": "Assess and describe the area, relieve pressure, and report it to the RN.", "C": "Document it at end of shift together with other routine notes.", "D": "Apply a dressing and skip reporting since a small open area heals on its own."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Massaging a damaged area, which causes more injury.", "C": "Delaying documentation and reporting.", "D": "Treating without reporting a new skin finding."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assess and describe the area, relieve pressure, and report it to the RN.): A new open area over a pressure point needs prompt assessment, pressure relief and a report so the team can classify it and plan care. Treating without reporting leaves out the RN and any orders. Massage damages tissue, and delaying notes until the end of the shift delays treatment. Report new skin breakdown promptly."}, {"id": 2033, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "On the acute care unit, the physician gives the practical nurse a telephone order for Ms. Park's heart failure medication while the unit is noisy. The nurse writes it down and the physician says goodbye. What should the nurse do before acting on the order?", "options": {"A": "Read the whole order back and wait for confirmation.", "B": "Have a colleague confirm what she heard, then act.", "C": "Write it carefully, then clarify with the physician only if it seems unusual.", "D": "Read back the drug and dose, leaving the rest to pharmacy."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Using a colleague's memory instead of prescriber confirmation", "C": "Skipping read-back unless the order appears unusual.", "D": "Reading back only part of the order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C251", "cluster_text": "Ms. Helen Park, 58, has type 2 diabetes and heart failure and is cared for by a practical nurse who works across acute care, a home care program, and a community clinic. The nurse communicates with physicians, team members, and Ms. Park throughout. Each question describes a different setting or moment. Ms. Park speaks English as a second language and values being included in decisions about her care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Read the whole order back and wait for confirmation.): A verbal or telephone order is verified by reading it back in full and waiting for confirmation, which catches mishearing in noisy settings. A colleague's memory is not confirmation from the prescriber, and reading back only the drug and dose leaves route and frequency unchecked. Clarifying only when it looks unusual misses errors that look ordinary. Read back every element."}, {"id": 2034, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Ms. Park's heart stops in the emergency department, and the team starts resuscitation. The team leader says, 'Draw up one milligram of epinephrine.' The practical nurse prepares it and hands it over without speaking. The resuscitation room is crowded and loud. Which communication step was missed?", "options": {"A": "Waiting for another team member to restate the order in writing.", "B": "Asking the leader to confirm the order twice before touching the medication.", "C": "Repeating the order aloud and announcing when the dose was given.", "D": "Announcing the dose after it was given, without repeating the order."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adding a written step that closed-loop communication does not require.", "B": "Demanding repeated confirmation that delays care", "D": "Reporting completion without first repeating the order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C251", "cluster_text": "Ms. Helen Park, 58, has type 2 diabetes and heart failure and is cared for by a practical nurse who works across acute care, a home care program, and a community clinic. The nurse communicates with physicians, team members, and Ms. Park throughout. Each question describes a different setting or moment. Ms. Park speaks English as a second language and values being included in decisions about her care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeating the order aloud and announcing when the dose was given.): Closed-loop communication means the receiver repeats the order and then reports when the task is complete, so the leader knows the dose was given. Silent action leaves the leader uncertain. Announcing completion without first repeating the order skips half the loop, and demanding repeated confirmation or a written order slows resuscitation. Say it back and say when it is done."}, {"id": 2035, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "During a home visit, Ms. Park declines teaching about her fluid restriction, saying she already knows it. The nurse offers a written handout, which she takes. She lives with her husband, who is supportive. How should the nurse document the visit?", "options": {"A": "Record what was offered, her response and reason, and follow-up plans.", "B": "Record that teaching was offered and declined, without noting her reason or the handout.", "C": "Record teaching as given and understood, since she says she knows it.", "D": "Record the refusal but leave the follow-up plan for next visit."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Leaving out her reason and the material provided", "C": "Charting understanding that was not assessed", "D": "Omitting the follow-up plan"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C251", "cluster_text": "Ms. Helen Park, 58, has type 2 diabetes and heart failure and is cared for by a practical nurse who works across acute care, a home care program, and a community clinic. The nurse communicates with physicians, team members, and Ms. Park throughout. Each question describes a different setting or moment. Ms. Park speaks English as a second language and values being included in decisions about her care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Record what was offered, her response and reason, and follow-up plans.): Documentation should be accurate and complete, showing what was offered, how the client responded and why, and what follow-up is planned. Leaving out her reason or the plan makes the entry incomplete, and charting teaching as understood only because she said she knew it records a result that was not assessed. Record the client's response in the chart."}, {"id": 2036, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At a clinic visit, Ms. Park declines diet counselling after the dietitian's advice. She says it conflicts with meals her family cooks. Her blood pressure is 128/80 mmHg. She attends with her adult daughter today. What is the best nursing response?", "options": {"A": "Accept the refusal and close the session without further discussion or documentation.", "B": "Arrange a stricter plan with the dietitian and schedule a follow-up visit.", "C": "Explore her reasons, respect her decision, and revisit options that fit her meals.", "D": "Give her a printed diet sheet and ask her to follow it before her next visit."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ending the conversation and omitting the record.", "B": "Escalating a plan she has said does not fit her home", "D": "Repeating the advice in writing without exploring barriers"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C251", "cluster_text": "Ms. Helen Park, 58, has type 2 diabetes and heart failure and is cared for by a practical nurse who works across acute care, a home care program, and a community clinic. The nurse communicates with physicians, team members, and Ms. Park throughout. Each question describes a different setting or moment. Ms. Park speaks English as a second language and values being included in decisions about her care.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explore her reasons, respect her decision, and revisit options that fit her meals.): A capable client may decline teaching, and exploring her reasons reveals barriers such as family meals that a plan can address. Pressing for a stricter plan or handing over a printed sheet repeats advice she has already said does not fit. Closing without discussion or documentation loses information the team needs. Respect the choice and keep the door open to a workable plan."}, {"id": 2037, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During dressing changes, an adult with a partial-thickness burn on the forearm rates pain 7 out of 10. The nurse has an order for an oral analgesic as needed. The next dressing change is in an hour. When should the practical nurse give it?", "options": {"A": "Hold analgesia and distract the client with conversation during the dressing change.", "B": "Give it ahead of the dressing change, timed to onset, and reassess comfort.", "C": "Give it only if the client asks twice, to limit exposure to the drug.", "D": "Give it after the dressing change, once the client reports pain afterward."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Substituting distraction for ordered analgesia.", "C": "Withholding ordered medication for no clinical reason.", "D": "Giving pain relief after the painful procedure."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give it ahead of the dressing change, timed to onset, and reassess comfort.): Pain from dressing changes is predictable, so ordered analgesia should be given early enough to take effect before the procedure begins. Waiting until afterward lets the client suffer through it. Distraction can help but does not replace an available order, and requiring repeated requests is unjustified. Time the dose to the drug's onset, then reassess."}, {"id": 2038, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a ward, an adult reports chest tightness and looks pale. The practical nurse measures a blood pressure of 90/58 mmHg and considers repeating it several times to be sure. The client's usual pressure is 130/80 mmHg. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Stay with the client and report findings to the RN immediately.", "B": "Document the findings and reassess at the next scheduled vital sign check.", "C": "Retake the blood pressure twice more before reporting to confirm the reading.", "D": "Give a prescribed as-needed analgesic and observe whether the chest tightness eases."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Postponing action on a serious new finding.", "C": "Delaying a report to confirm numbers.", "D": "Treating possible cardiac pain with analgesia alone."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stay with the client and report findings to the RN immediately.): Chest tightness, pallor and a pressure well below baseline are possible signs of a serious cardiac or circulatory event that needs immediate reporting. Repeated readings delay care, and waiting for the next scheduled check leaves the client unassessed. Analgesia alone does not address a possible cardiac cause. Stay, report and act on the emergency plan."}, {"id": 2039, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In clinic, an adult has an acute gout flare in the right great toe. The prescriber orders allopurinol to start today with no other medication. The client asks whether it will stop the pain. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Clarify the order, since allopurinol started in a flare without cover can worsen it", "B": "Give the allopurinol as ordered and ask the prescriber to review it at his next visit", "C": "Teach that allopurinol works best taken during flares, so he should stop it afterward", "D": "Hold all gout medications until repeat blood tests show his uric acid is back to normal"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Giving a questionable order and delaying clarification", "C": "Misstating allopurinol as a flare-only drug", "D": "Withholding all medication instead of clarifying."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Clarify the order, since allopurinol started in a flare without cover can worsen it): Allopurinol lowers uric acid over time and does not treat acute pain, and starting it during a flare without anti-inflammatory cover can worsen the attack. Clarifying the order with the prescriber is the safe step. Giving it first and reviewing later exposes him to a worse flare, and teaching that it is a flare-only drug misstates how it works. Holding everything also leaves the flare untreated."}, {"id": 2040, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "At a rural clinic, a 38-year-old client needs a type of wound dressing that Tomasz has seen demonstrated once but never performed. No colleague is available and the client is waiting. The wound is clean and the client is stable. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Complete the dressing and document that it was done under supervision from a colleague.", "B": "Perform it, since watching one demonstration meets the standard of competence for practice.", "C": "Decline until competent, and arrange supervision or a skilled colleague to do it.", "D": "Perform it and ask the client to report any pain, swelling or drainage afterward."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Charting supervision that did not occur.", "B": "Treating observation as competence.", "D": "Placing monitoring on the client."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C242", "cluster_text": "Tomasz Kowalski, a practical nurse, rotates through a rural clinic, a surgical ward, a medical unit, a long-term care home, and a hospital unit over several weeks. Each question describes one situation from this rotation. His employer expects him to follow regulatory body standards, practise within his scope, document accurately, and protect client privacy. He wants to meet his legal duties in each setting.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline until competent, and arrange supervision or a skilled colleague to do it.): A nurse must have the knowledge, skill and judgment for a procedure before performing it, and one demonstration does not provide that. Arranging supervision or a skilled colleague protects the client. Asking the client to watch for problems shifts responsibility, and documenting supervision that did not happen is false. Practise only within demonstrated competence."}, {"id": 2041, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On the surgical ward, a 66-year-old signed consent for a laparoscopic procedure, but the surgeon now plans an open approach after new imaging. The client has not been told. The surgery is scheduled for tomorrow morning at seven. What should Tomasz do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the surgeon to add a note to the consent form, without involving the client", "B": "Proceed with preparation, because the consent form lists open surgery as a possible conversion", "C": "Explain the new approach to the client and obtain his signature.", "D": "Tell the surgeon the client must be informed and consent again before the change proceeds."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changing the consent record without the client", "B": "Treating a general conversion clause as informed consent", "C": "Obtaining consent for a procedure the nurse cannot explain."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C242", "cluster_text": "Tomasz Kowalski, a practical nurse, rotates through a rural clinic, a surgical ward, a medical unit, a long-term care home, and a hospital unit over several weeks. Each question describes one situation from this rotation. His employer expects him to follow regulatory body standards, practise within his scope, document accurately, and protect client privacy. He wants to meet his legal duties in each setting.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tell the surgeon the client must be informed and consent again before the change proceeds.): Consent applies to the specific procedure explained, so a major change in plan needs new information and new consent from the client. The surgeon is responsible for explaining the procedure, so Tomasz brings the gap to the surgeon. A note added to the form without the client, or a general conversion clause, does not replace informed consent, and nurses do not explain surgical plans in the surgeon's place."}, {"id": 2042, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A 74-year-old on a medical unit refuses transfer to another facility. The RN and physician judge him capable. Later that day he says he will agree to the transfer. He has no history of confusion. How should the entry be made?", "options": {"A": "Document the capacity assessment, the refusal, and the later decision.", "B": "Record the refusal and the final agreement, without noting how capacity was judged.", "C": "Document the capacity assessment and the final agreement, leaving out the earlier refusal.", "D": "Record the transfer details, since the RN and physician judged capacity."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Omitting the capacity basis from the record", "C": "Omitting the refusal from the record", "D": "Leaving the capacity decision undocumented"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C242", "cluster_text": "Tomasz Kowalski, a practical nurse, rotates through a rural clinic, a surgical ward, a medical unit, a long-term care home, and a hospital unit over several weeks. Each question describes one situation from this rotation. His employer expects him to follow regulatory body standards, practise within his scope, document accurately, and protect client privacy. He wants to meet his legal duties in each setting.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Document the capacity assessment, the refusal, and the later decision.): A complete record shows how the capacity was judged, what information he received, his refusal and his later decision, each with times. Omitting the capacity basis or the refusal removes the evidence that his choice was informed. Recording only transfer details leaves the capacity decision undocumented, which is a required entry. Document capacity decisions fully."}, {"id": 2043, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In long-term care, a manager asks Tomasz to change yesterday's medication entry so the chart shows a dose given on time. The dose was given late. Tomasz wants to avoid conflict. The manager is respected and persuasive. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Alter the time with a single line and initial it, as the manager asked.", "B": "Leave the entry unchanged and mention the late dose only at verbal handover.", "C": "Agree to the change and keep a private note of the manager's request.", "D": "Decline to alter it, add a dated late entry explaining what happened, and report per policy."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Falsifying a time while using correct correction format", "B": "Keeping the late dose out of the written record", "C": "Keeping evidence outside the legal record"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C242", "cluster_text": "Tomasz Kowalski, a practical nurse, rotates through a rural clinic, a surgical ward, a medical unit, a long-term care home, and a hospital unit over several weeks. Each question describes one situation from this rotation. His employer expects him to follow regulatory body standards, practise within his scope, document accurately, and protect client privacy. He wants to meet his legal duties in each setting.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline to alter it, add a dated late entry explaining what happened, and report per policy.): Records must be accurate, and a late dose is documented as late through a dated late entry, not by changing the original. Altering the time, even with a neat single line and initials, is still falsification. Leaving the chart unchanged and mentioning it verbally, or keeping a private note, leaves no accurate record. Report the request through the proper channel."}, {"id": 2044, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A lawyer phones the hospital unit asking Tomasz to send a 52-year-old client's complete chart today, saying a subpoena is on the way. The client has not consented. The lawyer sounds confident and says the matter is urgent today. What should Tomasz do?", "options": {"A": "Fax the full chart to the lawyer directly to meet the deadline.", "B": "Tell the lawyer all charts are confidential and refuse to speak with lawyers.", "C": "Release only the last day's notes since a partial record needs no consent.", "D": "Refer the request to the manager or privacy office to verify the legal order."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Releasing the whole chart without verifying authority.", "B": "Refusing communication rather than routing the request.", "C": "Disclosing part of a record without consent."}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C242", "cluster_text": "Tomasz Kowalski, a practical nurse, rotates through a rural clinic, a surgical ward, a medical unit, a long-term care home, and a hospital unit over several weeks. Each question describes one situation from this rotation. His employer expects him to follow regulatory body standards, practise within his scope, document accurately, and protect client privacy. He wants to meet his legal duties in each setting.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refer the request to the manager or privacy office to verify the legal order.): Records are released only with consent or a valid legal order, and verification belongs with the manager or privacy office. A phone call is not a subpoena, so releasing the full chart or part of it breaches privacy. A blanket refusal to speak with lawyers is unhelpful. Route the request and verify first."}, {"id": 2045, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Lavoie, 67, is two days after a hip replacement and receives hydromorphone every four hours. At the 1400 check his respiratory rate is 12, down from 16, and he dozes mid-sentence but wakes to voice. His pain is 3 out of 10. Which action best follows this change?", "options": {"A": "Hold the next dose, monitor sedation and breathing, and tell the RN now.", "B": "Wake him fully and coach deep breathing, then give the dose once he is alert.", "C": "Give the scheduled dose, since his pain is controlled and he still wakes to voice.", "D": "Record the sleepiness as expected after surgery and recheck at the next scheduled interval."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats stimulation as a fix while still giving the opioid", "C": "Ignores a falling respiratory rate and rising sedation as a warning trend", "D": "Normalizes progressive drowsiness as routine after surgery"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold the next dose, monitor sedation and breathing, and tell the RN now.): His respiratory rate has fallen and his alertness is slipping, which are early signs of opioid-induced respiratory depression, so the dose is held and the RN is told. Giving the scheduled dose only because his pain is low adds drug to a client already becoming sedated. Waking him and coaching breaths does not remove the opioid effect, and the drug would still peak later. Report a downward trend in alertness before the next dose."}, {"id": 2046, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Chan, 30, visits a clinic with fatigue and heavy menstrual periods lasting eight days. Her hemoglobin is 112 g/L, only slightly below range, but her ferritin is low. She asks what the ferritin result means and mentions her mother has thyroid disease. Which explanation should the practical nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Her ferritin is low because her thyroid is underactive, so iron is not the issue.", "B": "Her iron stores are depleted, which can occur before hemoglobin leaves the normal range.", "C": "Her iron stores are normal, because her hemoglobin is slightly reduced for her age and sex", "D": "Her ferritin shows an inflammation marker, which means iron stores are probably adequate."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Linking low ferritin to family thyroid history", "C": "Treats near-normal hemoglobin as proof of normal iron stores", "D": "Mistaking ferritin for an inflammation marker that lowers when inflamed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Her iron stores are depleted, which can occur before hemoglobin leaves the normal range.): Ferritin reflects stored iron, and it falls first when blood loss exceeds intake, so Ms. Chan's stores are depleted even though her hemoglobin is near range. Reading the mild hemoglobin as reassurance misses that stores drain before anemia appears. Her mother's thyroid disease does not explain low ferritin, and inflammation raises ferritin rather than lowering it. Low ferritin means low iron stores until proven otherwise."}, {"id": 2047, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Bianchi, 58, has taken prednisone daily for two years for severe COPD and asks the nurse what long-term effects to watch for. He is proud that he still works outdoors and bruises easily on his forearms. Which statement about prednisone's effects on his bones and skin is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Bone thins and fractures more easily, and skin bruises and heals slowly.", "B": "Bone density is preserved when taken with calcium, and skin thickens from the medication.", "C": "Bone is unaffected, but the skin thins and bruises because of reduced circulation.", "D": "Bone thins only at high doses, with no change to skin healing or bruising."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Believing calcium cancels steroid bone loss and steroids thicken skin", "C": "Believing steroids spare bone and thin skin through circulation", "D": "Assumes bone harm needs high dose only and skin is unaffected"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bone thins and fractures more easily, and skin bruises and heals slowly.): Long-term corticosteroids reduce bone formation and collagen, which explains Mr. Bianchi's easy bruising and his raised fracture risk. Steroids do not strengthen bone or thicken skin, and bone is affected as well as skin. Thinning also occurs at moderate doses over time, so dose alone does not protect him. Report new bone pain or skin breakdown and review fall and calcium measures with the RN."}, {"id": 2048, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Haddad, 44, arrives at a clinic with sudden weakness on the left side of her face. She cannot close her left eyelid fully, her eye waters, and she cannot raise her left eyebrow. Her arms and legs are strong. Which nursing action best protects her from harm?", "options": {"A": "Leave the eye uncovered so air can reach the surface and keep it comfortable.", "B": "Apply prescribed lubricant to the eye and protect it with a patch while she sleeps.", "C": "Teach forceful grimacing exercises to restore facial muscle strength early.", "D": "Apply warm compresses to the cheek and reassure her that the weakness resolves quickly."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believes air exposure helps a lid that will not close", "C": "Starts vigorous exercise that does not protect the eye", "D": "Treats the facial droop but not the exposed cornea"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Apply prescribed lubricant to the eye and protect it with a patch while she sleeps.): The affected lid does not close, so the cornea dries and can be scratched, and lubricant with a night patch protects it. Warm compresses and reassurance do nothing for corneal exposure. Leaving the eye open worsens drying. Forceful exercises are not the first priority and can strain muscles. Protect the eye first in any one-sided facial weakness."}, {"id": 2049, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On admission to a long-term care home, the practical nurse checks Mr. Osei, 81, from head to toe and finds intact skin except a dry, red area over his coccyx. Another nurse says charting only abnormal findings saves time. Which documentation is most appropriate for the admission skin assessment?", "options": {"A": "Note the skin as normal on admission and reassess only when staff notice a change.", "B": "Chart only areas with abnormal findings so the record stays brief, quick and focused.", "C": "Report the red coccyx area verbally to the RN and enter a note after the shift.", "D": "Record intact areas as well as findings, with location, size and description."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats a baseline as a one-time normal statement", "B": "Believes only abnormal skin needs recording", "C": "Relies on verbal report instead of written documentation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Record intact areas as well as findings, with location, size and description.): A complete baseline, including intact skin, lets staff later tell what changed and when, and the coccyx finding needs location, size and description. Charting only abnormalities leaves no record that other areas were checked. A single normal note with no reassessment plan misses early pressure injury. Verbal reports do not replace a timely written entry. Document what you assessed, not just what you found."}, {"id": 2050, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Rossi, 79, lives in a long-term care home and sleeps poorly. Last night she woke at 0200, was repositioned, drank warm milk, and slept by 0300. Her bath needed partial assistance. The nurse is writing the shift note. Which entry best documents hygiene and sleep care?", "options": {"A": "Awake at 0200, repositioned, given warm milk, asleep by 0300; partial assist for bathing.", "B": "Routine hygiene and night care given, resident comfortable, no concerns noted at this time.", "C": "Slept poorly again, likely because of age, and no further action was taken during the shift.", "D": "Hygiene done and sleep aid requested from the pharmacist for nightly use, as needed."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Writes vague routine care with no details", "C": "Attributes poor sleep to age with no assessment", "D": "Records an unsupported request for a sleep aid"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Awake at 0200, repositioned, given warm milk, asleep by 0300; partial assist for bathing.): The best entry names what happened, what the nurse did, the response and the level of assistance, so the team can see patterns and plan care. A generic entry says nothing others can act on. Blaming age ends assessment of causes such as pain or noise. A sleep aid was never ordered or discussed. Specific, factual notes make the plan of care testable."}, {"id": 2051, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Alvarez, 36, finishes a series of clinic visits for wound care. The next day he sends the practical nurse a friend request on a social media site, with a friendly message thanking her. She uses the same site with her family. What is the most professional response?", "options": {"A": "Decline politely, explain professional boundaries, and use the clinic's approved channels.", "B": "Ignore the request and say nothing, then avoid him at his next scheduled visit.", "C": "Accept the request but keep her page private and avoid posting about her work or clients", "D": "Accept once he is discharged from the clinic, since the nurse-client relationship has then ended"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Avoids the issue without explaining or documenting it", "C": "Treats privacy settings as enough to protect boundaries", "D": "Believes discharge ends all boundary duties"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline politely, explain professional boundaries, and use the clinic's approved channels.): A friend request blurs the professional relationship, so a polite explanation with approved communication channels protects both of them. Private settings still create a personal connection. Silence leaves him confused and the behaviour unaddressed. Discharge does not erase the power imbalance in the relationship. Keep the relationship professional and explain why."}, {"id": 2052, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A family member of Mr. Singh, 86, a long-term care resident, offers the practical nurse a fifty-dollar gift card at the end of a difficult month, and also a box of chocolates for the whole unit. The home has a written gift policy. Which response is best?", "options": {"A": "Accept it privately and report it to the manager at the end of the month.", "B": "Thank them, decline the gift card, and follow facility policy on shared unit gifts.", "C": "Accept the gift card because its value is small and refusing may hurt feelings.", "D": "Decline everything, including the chocolates, without explaining the reason to the family."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepts first and reports later", "C": "Uses a low dollar value to justify personal benefit", "D": "Over-refuses and offends without explaining"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Thank them, decline the gift card, and follow facility policy on shared unit gifts.): A personal gift card can create an obligation or a perception of favouritism, so it is declined kindly, while the shared unit gift is handled under the policy. Small value does not remove the conflict. Secret acceptance and late reporting defeat transparency. Refusing without explanation can damage the relationship. Decline personal benefit and follow the written policy."}, {"id": 2053, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The cardiac monitor shows peaked T waves and a widening QRS complex. Mr. Okafor says his legs feel heavy and he cannot stand. The physician has ordered a repeat potassium level and an electrocardiogram. How should the practical nurse interpret the monitor changes in light of his potassium of 7.2 mmol/L?", "options": {"A": "They indicate low potassium, since cardiac irritability usually follows a fall in serum potassium.", "B": "They suggest a monitoring artifact from his movement, so the leads should be rechecked first.", "C": "They fit high potassium affecting the heart and need immediate report to the RN and physician.", "D": "They reflect an expected effect of missed dialysis and can be recorded for later review."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reverses the direction of the potassium disturbance", "B": "Dismisses dangerous changes as artifact", "D": "Treats missed dialysis effects as expected and harmless"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C247", "cluster_text": "Mr. Okafor, 54, receives hemodialysis three times weekly and missed his last session. He arrives in the emergency department with muscle weakness and palpitations. His blood pressure is 148/88, pulse 52 and irregular, respirations 18, and oxygen saturation 96%. His serum potassium is 7.2 mmol/L. You are the practical nurse caring for him with the RN and physician. He is alert and oriented.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (They fit high potassium affecting the heart and need immediate report to the RN and physician.): Peaked T waves and a widening QRS in a client with potassium 7.2 mmol/L point to hyperkalemia threatening his heart rhythm, so the team is told right away. Rechecking leads first delays care. Calling the changes expected ignores the arrhythmia risk. Low potassium produces different changes. Match ECG changes to the lab value and report urgently."}, {"id": 2054, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Okafor, anxious and tired, asks why missing one dialysis session made him weak and why his heart feels odd. He also says he thought only salt mattered. The practical nurse wants to explain simply and accurately. Which explanation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Missed dialysis lowers sodium, which is the electrolyte that controls muscle contraction and heartbeat.", "B": "Weakness comes from low blood sugar after the missed session, which also disturbs the heartbeat.", "C": "His kidneys cannot excrete potassium, so missed dialysis lets it build up in the blood.", "D": "Missed dialysis let calcium rise, and high calcium is what weakens muscle and disturbs heartbeat."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Substitutes sodium for potassium as the cause", "B": "Blames low blood sugar instead of potassium retention", "D": "Blames raised calcium instead of potassium retention"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C247", "cluster_text": "Mr. Okafor, 54, receives hemodialysis three times weekly and missed his last session. He arrives in the emergency department with muscle weakness and palpitations. His blood pressure is 148/88, pulse 52 and irregular, respirations 18, and oxygen saturation 96%. His serum potassium is 7.2 mmol/L. You are the practical nurse caring for him with the RN and physician. He is alert and oriented.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (His kidneys cannot excrete potassium, so missed dialysis lets it build up in the blood.): Failing kidneys cannot remove potassium, and dialysis does that job, so a missed session lets potassium rise and disturb muscle and heart function. Sodium matters in his diet but does not explain this weakness. Blood sugar and calcium are not the cause here, because his potassium is 7.2 mmol/L. Dialysis removes potassium rather than adding it."}, {"id": 2055, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Orders for Mr. Okafor include IV calcium gluconate, then regular insulin with dextrose. He asks what the calcium will do, since the team says his potassium will still be high afterward. The practical nurse must explain the drug's role correctly. Which explanation of calcium gluconate is correct?", "options": {"A": "It pulls potassium into the cells, which lowers the serum level within minutes.", "B": "It protects heart cell membranes but does not lower the potassium level.", "C": "It binds potassium in the bowel so that it leaves the body in stool within hours.", "D": "It increases urine output so that the kidneys excrete extra potassium quickly."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Credits calcium with insulin's shifting action", "C": "Confuses calcium with a bowel potassium binder", "D": "Assumes his kidneys can still excrete potassium"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C247", "cluster_text": "Mr. Okafor, 54, receives hemodialysis three times weekly and missed his last session. He arrives in the emergency department with muscle weakness and palpitations. His blood pressure is 148/88, pulse 52 and irregular, respirations 18, and oxygen saturation 96%. His serum potassium is 7.2 mmol/L. You are the practical nurse caring for him with the RN and physician. He is alert and oriented.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It protects heart cell membranes but does not lower the potassium level.): Calcium protects the heart muscle membrane from the effect of high potassium but leaves the level unchanged, so other treatments and dialysis are still needed. Shifting potassium into cells is the action of insulin with dextrose. Binding in the bowel describes a different drug. Increasing urine output would not help a dialysis-dependent client. Calcium protects the heart, it does not lower potassium."}, {"id": 2056, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Later, Mr. Okafor's potassium is 5.4 mmol/L and he is waiting for emergency dialysis. His tray holds a banana, orange juice, white rice with chicken, and tomato soup. He asks which food is safest to eat now while his level is still high. What should the practical nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "Tomato soup, since cooked vegetables lose most of their potassium.", "B": "White rice with a small portion of chicken, because it is lower in potassium.", "C": "Orange juice, since citrus acidity helps the body excrete extra potassium before dialysis.", "D": "A banana, since its potassium is bound to fibre and absorbs slowly before dialysis."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes cooking removes potassium from tomato", "C": "Believes acidity helps the body excrete potassium", "D": "Thinks fibre keeps banana potassium from being absorbed"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C247", "cluster_text": "Mr. Okafor, 54, receives hemodialysis three times weekly and missed his last session. He arrives in the emergency department with muscle weakness and palpitations. His blood pressure is 148/88, pulse 52 and irregular, respirations 18, and oxygen saturation 96%. His serum potassium is 7.2 mmol/L. You are the practical nurse caring for him with the RN and physician. He is alert and oriented.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (White rice with a small portion of chicken, because it is lower in potassium.): White rice and chicken are lower in potassium than the other three items, so they are the safer choice until dialysis. Bananas, orange juice and tomato products are all high in potassium. Citrus acidity does not help a client whose kidneys cannot excrete potassium, and fibre does not block its absorption. Cooking does not remove most of it."}, {"id": 2057, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A kitchen support worker, Mr. Dang, tells you he had diarrhea two days ago, none since this morning, and feels fine. He wears gloves when serving trays and wants to work today. Residents on the wing are ill. Which interpretation of his situation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "He may work in the dish room only, because no food contact occurs there.", "B": "Gloves protect residents adequately, so he can serve trays if he washes his hands afterward.", "C": "He may still shed virus, so he stays off food handling until cleared by policy.", "D": "He is recovered since symptoms stopped, so he may work once he feels well."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes dish room work carries no risk", "B": "Relies on gloves instead of exclusion", "D": "Equates feeling well with no longer being infectious"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C246", "cluster_text": "A long-term care home has six residents on one wing with sudden vomiting and watery diarrhea over 24 hours. Several staff members have similar symptoms. The infection control nurse suspects norovirus, but stool results will take three days. You are the practical nurse on the day shift caring for residents on that wing, and the RN is in charge. The home has outbreak precautions in its policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (He may still shed virus, so he stays off food handling until cleared by policy.): People with norovirus can shed it for days after diarrhea stops, so Mr. Dang stays out of food handling until the policy clears him, and gloves do not change that. Hand washing after glove use helps but cannot offset infectious shedding. Feeling well is not proof he is no longer contagious. The dish room still involves shared surfaces and utensils. Exclude food handlers until the policy period ends."}, {"id": 2058, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Three more residents become ill. Stool results have not returned. Several ill residents share rooms with well residents, and the wing has contact precaution signs but no plan for separating them. The RN asks for suggestions on what to do first. What is the best action now?", "options": {"A": "Cohort ill residents but delay visitor limits until stool results return.", "B": "Start outbreak precautions now and separate ill residents from well ones.", "C": "Wait for stool results so that the correct precautions are chosen for the pathogen.", "D": "Keep contact signs up and clean twice daily without separating residents."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays visitor restriction while awaiting lab results", "C": "Delays control measures for confirmation", "D": "Relies on signs and cleaning without separating residents"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C246", "cluster_text": "A long-term care home has six residents on one wing with sudden vomiting and watery diarrhea over 24 hours. Several staff members have similar symptoms. The infection control nurse suspects norovirus, but stool results will take three days. You are the practical nurse on the day shift caring for residents on that wing, and the RN is in charge. The home has outbreak precautions in its policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Start outbreak precautions now and separate ill residents from well ones.): Suspected norovirus spreads fast, so ill residents are separated and precautions begin before results arrive. Waiting for stool results costs days of spread. Signs and extra cleaning do not stop spread between roommates. Grouping ill residents but delaying visitor limits leaves exposure open. Act on the clinical picture and confirm later."}, {"id": 2059, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Reyes, 84, takes warfarin and has vomited three times since breakfast. Her roommate has norovirus. Her latest emesis looks like dark coffee grounds. Her pulse is 104 and her blood pressure is 98/60, down from 126/74 yesterday. What is the best conclusion?", "options": {"A": "Norovirus explains the vomiting, so continue fluids and recheck her vital signs in four hours.", "B": "She is dehydrated from the outbreak, so encourage oral fluids and record her intake.", "C": "Possible upper GI bleeding, so report to the RN immediately and monitor her vital signs.", "D": "Dark vomit is common with repeated retching, so offer an antiemetic and rest."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Anchors on the outbreak and misses bleeding", "B": "Attributes unstable vitals to dehydration alone", "D": "Normalizes dark emesis in a client on an anticoagulant"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C246", "cluster_text": "A long-term care home has six residents on one wing with sudden vomiting and watery diarrhea over 24 hours. Several staff members have similar symptoms. The infection control nurse suspects norovirus, but stool results will take three days. You are the practical nurse on the day shift caring for residents on that wing, and the RN is in charge. The home has outbreak precautions in its policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Possible upper GI bleeding, so report to the RN immediately and monitor her vital signs.): Coffee-ground emesis with a rising pulse and falling pressure in a client on warfarin suggests upper gastrointestinal bleeding, which needs urgent RN assessment, even in the middle of an outbreak. Assuming norovirus delays care. Antiemetics and fluids do not address bleeding. Dehydration alone would not explain the emesis colour. Look past the outbreak when findings differ."}, {"id": 2060, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the end of your shift you feel mild nausea. A colleague suggests you not tell anyone so the wing keeps enough staff, and you feel well enough to continue. The home has an outbreak staffing policy. What is the appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Continue working with a mask on and extra handwashing, since you feel well.", "B": "Wait until vomiting or diarrhea begins before telling anyone, as nausea alone is unconfirmed.", "C": "Work only with residents who are already ill, since they are infected already.", "D": "Report symptoms to the RN and step away from care per policy."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relies on mask and handwashing despite symptoms", "B": "Delays reporting until symptoms are confirmed", "C": "Cohorts ill staff with ill residents"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C246", "cluster_text": "A long-term care home has six residents on one wing with sudden vomiting and watery diarrhea over 24 hours. Several staff members have similar symptoms. The infection control nurse suspects norovirus, but stool results will take three days. You are the practical nurse on the day shift caring for residents on that wing, and the RN is in charge. The home has outbreak precautions in its policy.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report symptoms to the RN and step away from care per policy.): Early symptoms of norovirus in a worker are reported at once because staff can spread it before becoming fully ill, and the outbreak policy sets the exclusion period. Masks and handwashing reduce risk but do not make an ill worker safe. Residents can be infected with more strains or other organisms. Waiting leaves residents exposed. Staff report symptoms and stay away."}, {"id": 2061, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Hill rings again for water and a pillow change while Ms. Tran's meal tray is cooling and she is at risk of aspiration if rushed. Mr. Hill is not in distress and has rung six times this hour. How should the nurse use her time ethically?", "options": {"A": "Answer Mr. Hill first, since a client who rings six times may be hiding distress.", "B": "Finish Ms. Tran's meal assistance before answering any other client's call light.", "C": "Ask the UCP to finish Ms. Tran's feeding so she can answer every call light promptly.", "D": "Prioritize by need and risk, reassure Mr. Hill briefly, and arrange help for his minor requests."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats call frequency as proof of clinical urgency", "B": "Ignores other clients' needs during one task", "C": "Delegates a high aspiration risk task unsafely"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C252", "cluster_text": "On a busy medical unit you are the practical nurse working with an RN and an unregulated care provider (UCP). Your five clients include Mr. Hill, who rings often with minor requests, Ms. Tran, who is post stroke and needs a meal assist, and Mr. Ivanov, who is stable and recovering from pneumonia. The unit has full capacity today and is short one staff member.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Prioritize by need and risk, reassure Mr. Hill briefly, and arrange help for his minor requests.): Fair use of time follows need and risk, so Ms. Tran's safe feeding comes first while Mr. Hill gets a brief response and help for small tasks. Frequent ringing does not equal greater need, although a quick check confirms he is stable. Handing an aspiration risk feeding to a UCP removes nursing judgment from the riskiest task. Blocking all other calls ignores that another client may be unwell."}, {"id": 2062, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "You asked the UCP to measure Ms. Tran's weight and vital signs at 1000. At 1100 the physician asks who took the values, and you cannot recall. The chart shows numbers without a name. What should you have done when you delegated this task?", "options": {"A": "Note only abnormal values so the chart stays concise and easier for the team to read.", "B": "Document the task, who performed it, and the results reported back.", "C": "Chart the values and name the unit team, not the individual.", "D": "Document the values only after the physician asks who took them."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Omits routine values from the record", "C": "Names a team instead of the individual accountable", "D": "Delays charting until questioned about it"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C252", "cluster_text": "On a busy medical unit you are the practical nurse working with an RN and an unregulated care provider (UCP). Your five clients include Mr. Hill, who rings often with minor requests, Ms. Tran, who is post stroke and needs a meal assist, and Mr. Ivanov, who is stable and recovering from pneumonia. The unit has full capacity today and is short one staff member.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Document the task, who performed it, and the results reported back.): Delegation charting names the task, who did it and what was reported, so accountability can be traced when the physician asks. Naming only the unit hides who is responsible. Charting after a question is asked risks forgotten detail and gaps. Charting only abnormal values leaves gaps. A clear record answers who did what and when."}, {"id": 2063, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The UCP reports that Mr. Ivanov seems more tired and is breathing faster after his walk, but wrote that his vital signs were fine. You are about to chart the task as completed. Which action best evaluates the outcome of this delegated task?", "options": {"A": "Chart the report and ask the UCP to watch his breathing.", "B": "Reassess Mr. Ivanov yourself, including respirations and oxygen saturation, before accepting the report.", "C": "Ask the UCP to repeat the vital signs in an hour and document the average.", "D": "Accept the report, since UCPs are trained to judge normal vital signs."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Keeps the contradicting report and delegates more monitoring", "C": "Delays reassessment of a possible change", "D": "Overstates the UCP's role in judging vital signs"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C252", "cluster_text": "On a busy medical unit you are the practical nurse working with an RN and an unregulated care provider (UCP). Your five clients include Mr. Hill, who rings often with minor requests, Ms. Tran, who is post stroke and needs a meal assist, and Mr. Ivanov, who is stable and recovering from pneumonia. The unit has full capacity today and is short one staff member.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reassess Mr. Ivanov yourself, including respirations and oxygen saturation, before accepting the report.): The nurse stays accountable for evaluating delegated work, and tiredness with faster breathing after his walk may signal a change that needs her own assessment. Accepting the report because UCPs judge vital signs overstates their role. Asking the UCP to watch him leaves the assessment undone. Waiting an hour and averaging hides a trend. Verify a worrying report yourself."}, {"id": 2064, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Ivanov's oxygen saturation has fallen to 88% on his usual oxygen, his pulse is 118, and he is using accessory muscles to breathe. The RN is in another room. Which action best reflects practical nurse scope and safe handoff?", "options": {"A": "Stay with him, apply ordered measures, and call the RN urgently for assessment and direction.", "B": "Apply ordered oxygen, then wait to see if saturation improves before calling the RN.", "C": "Increase his oxygen flow above the ordered rate until saturation improves, then report.", "D": "Continue adjusting oxygen and positioning on your own, since you know his history well."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delays escalation while waiting for improvement", "C": "Exceeds the oxygen order without direction", "D": "Manages beyond competence without help"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C252", "cluster_text": "On a busy medical unit you are the practical nurse working with an RN and an unregulated care provider (UCP). Your five clients include Mr. Hill, who rings often with minor requests, Ms. Tran, who is post stroke and needs a meal assist, and Mr. Ivanov, who is stable and recovering from pneumonia. The unit has full capacity today and is short one staff member.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stay with him, apply ordered measures, and call the RN urgently for assessment and direction.): A client who is declining needs an urgent RN assessment, and the nurse stays, uses ordered measures and calls for help. Waiting to see if saturation improves delays advanced care. Raising oxygen above the order is outside scope. Managing alone delays direction. Hand off early when care is beyond scope."}, {"id": 2065, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "In a community clinic, a supplement company representative offers Ms. Reid, a nurse, a gift card for each client she enrols in a product program. Ms. Park, 52, asks the nurse about vitamins that day. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Recommend the supplement quietly only to clients who ask, to avoid appearing biased.", "B": "Decline the benefit, give unbiased information, and tell the manager.", "C": "Give unbiased advice and accept the card privately afterward.", "D": "Accept the card and report it at the next meeting."}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Hides the benefit while still recommending", "C": "Accepts the benefit after the teaching to hide the link", "D": "Accepts the benefit and discloses it late"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline the benefit, give unbiased information, and tell the manager.): Personal benefit tied to a recommendation is a conflict of interest, so she declines it, gives unbiased information and informs the manager. Accepting the card after the visit does not remove the conflict. Late reporting does not undo it. Quiet recommendations keep the conflict hidden. Put the client's interest first."}, {"id": 2066, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a ward, a physician gives an urgent verbal order for IV furosemide 40 mg for a client in respiratory distress while the RN is busy. The nurse hears the order clearly in a noisy hallway. Policy permits verbal orders in urgent situations. What is the best way to proceed?", "options": {"A": "Give the drug immediately since urgency means the order cannot be delayed by repeating.", "B": "Write down the order, read it back, and have the physician confirm before giving it.", "C": "Write it down and ask the RN to check it after the drug has been given.", "D": "Repeat only the dose to the physician, since drug names are already known."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Skips verification because of urgency", "C": "Verifies only after the drug is given", "D": "Reads back only part of the order"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Write down the order, read it back, and have the physician confirm before giving it.): Read back confirms drug, dose and route, which catches mishearing in a noisy hallway, and it takes seconds even in an urgent situation. Giving the drug first removes the safety check. Verification after giving is too late. Reading back only the dose misses a sound-alike drug error. Always read back a full verbal order."}, {"id": 2067, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A hospital unit lowered falls for three months after starting hourly rounding. The project lead leaves this week and the manager says success will carry itself. The team asks what to do so the improvement lasts. Which plan is best?", "options": {"A": "Replace hourly rounding with monthly audits once the fall rate stays low.", "B": "Rely on staff memory of the project, since success makes habits persist without prompts.", "C": "Keep rounding but review the fall data only if rates rise again.", "D": "Keep tracking falls, share results, and name someone to own the routine."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Replaces the proven routine with lighter monitoring", "B": "Trusts habit without reminders or ownership", "C": "Reviews data only after a decline appears"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep tracking falls, share results, and name someone to own the routine.): Gains fade without ownership, so a named person, continued data and shared results keep the routine alive. Replacing rounding with audits drops the proven routine. Reviewing data only after rates rise means harm has already happened. Relying on memory fails as staff change. Sustain change with monitoring and ownership."}, {"id": 2068, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A client's pain has been 9 out of 10 for two hours despite ordered analgesia, and his heart rate is climbing. The physician, called twice, says to wait. The nurse feels uneasy challenging the physician and wonders about being polite. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Reposition him and apply a cold pack, then reassess pain in thirty minutes.", "B": "Ask the physician a third time to review the order before involving others.", "C": "Report to the charge nurse and follow the chain of command to get him reassessed.", "D": "Document the physician's response carefully and add it to the handover report."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adds comfort measures but delays escalation", "B": "Repeats the same request without using the chain of command", "D": "Documents the response without escalating the concern"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report to the charge nurse and follow the chain of command to get him reassessed.): Uncontrolled pain with a rising heart rate is a change that needs action, and the chain of command exists for unresolved concerns. Comfort measures alone delay reassessment. Asking the same physician again skips the next level of authority. Documentation does not treat the client. Escalate when a concern remains."}, {"id": 2069, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a dressing change, a stage 3 sacral pressure injury has a red granulating bed, slightly more clear yellow drainage than last week, and size reduced by 1 cm. There is no odour, warmth, or surrounding redness. How should the nurse interpret these findings?", "options": {"A": "Healing is progressing, so continue the plan and record measurements.", "B": "Healing has stalled, so apply a drying dressing daily.", "C": "Infection is developing because drainage increased, so report and request a culture.", "D": "Red granulation means deepening, so restage to stage 4."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Reads increased drainage as stalled healing", "C": "Reads increased drainage as infection alone", "D": "Restages a healing wound upward from its appearance"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Healing is progressing, so continue the plan and record measurements.): A shrinking wound with red granulation tissue and clear drainage and no signs of infection shows healing, and drainage often increases as tissue forms. Infection would add odour, warmth, pus or spreading redness. A drying dressing is wrong for a healing moist wound bed. Pressure injuries are not restaged upward when they heal. Look at the whole wound, not drainage alone."}, {"id": 2070, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A nurse in a clinic finds a used needle in the regular waste bin after a procedure. A colleague suggests recapping needles in future to keep the sharps bin tidy. A puncture-resistant sharps container is nearby. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Wrap the needle in paper and put it in the sharps container beside the bin.", "B": "Recap the needle with one hand and place it into the sharps container.", "C": "Leave it and tell the cleaning staff to take care when handling the waste.", "D": "Retrieve it with safe technique, place it in the sharps container, and report the incident."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Handles a needle by hand through paper", "B": "Recaps a used needle", "C": "Passes the hazard to others"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Retrieve it with safe technique, place it in the sharps container, and report the incident.): A needle in regular waste is a hazard, so it is removed safely, placed in a sharps container and reported so the cause is fixed. Recapping is a common cause of needlestick injuries. Wrapping by hand still risks a stick. Warning cleaners leaves the hazard in place. Dispose of sharps unrecapped at point of use."}, {"id": 2071, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Ito, 70, becomes pale and sweaty and says her chest is tight. Her blood pressure is 84/50 and her pulse is 120. You are alone with her, and the call bell is within reach. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Apply oxygen and recheck her vital signs in five minutes before calling anyone.", "B": "Step into the hall to alert the RN, then return in a minute.", "C": "Lay her flat, then leave briefly to bring the emergency cart to the bedside.", "D": "Stay at the bedside, call for help using the call bell, and begin ordered emergency measures."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays calling for help to confirm a trend", "B": "Leaves briefly to call", "C": "Leaves an unstable client to fetch equipment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stay at the bedside, call for help using the call bell, and begin ordered emergency measures.): Her findings suggest a serious cardiac or circulatory problem, so the nurse stays with her and summons help without leaving. Leaving to fetch equipment abandons the client. Stepping out, even briefly, removes monitoring. Waiting five minutes before calling delays care. Bring help to the bedside."}, {"id": 2072, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Jordan, 16, comes to a community mental health clinic alone, reporting low mood and poor sleep for two months. He says he has had bad experiences with services and gives short answers. The nurse wants to start well. Which approach best supports building trust with him?", "options": {"A": "Use a brief screening tool for depression first, then discuss his concerns afterward.", "B": "Build the relationship first, listen without judgment, and ask what matters to him.", "C": "Ask his parents to join so the history is complete and consistent.", "D": "Explain that the clinic has strict rules and expects honesty before treatment starts."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Puts a screening tool before relationship building", "C": "Involves parents against his stated wish for privacy", "D": "Leads with rules and expectations"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C245", "cluster_text": "Jordan, 16, who is Cree, comes to a community mental health clinic alone. He reports low mood, poor sleep and loss of interest for two months. He says he has had bad experiences with services and is reluctant to talk. You are the practical nurse in the clinic, and the RN is available for consultation. He is accompanied by no one and sits near the door.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Build the relationship first, listen without judgment, and ask what matters to him.): Trust comes first, especially after past harm from services, and asking what matters to him respects his culture and identity. A screening tool first can feel like interrogation. Inviting his parents without his agreement can break trust. Opening with rules repeats what he may have felt before. Relationship before assessment."}, {"id": 2073, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Jordan says he wants counselling but does not want his parents told. He understands the options, reasons clearly, and denies thoughts of self-harm. The receptionist insists parental consent is always required. He has already attended two sessions with the clinic counsellor. Which statement best guides the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Share the information with parents because they hold legal responsibility for his care decisions.", "B": "Obtain parental consent first, since anyone under eighteen requires a parent's approval for care.", "C": "Delay counselling until a parent can attend, then offer him a private visit later.", "D": "Assess his capacity to consent, since a youth who understands can often consent under standards."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Discloses to parents without consent or risk of harm", "B": "Assumes age alone decides consent to care", "C": "Delays needed care until a parent can attend"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C245", "cluster_text": "Jordan, 16, who is Cree, comes to a community mental health clinic alone. He reports low mood, poor sleep and loss of interest for two months. He says he has had bad experiences with services and is reluctant to talk. You are the practical nurse in the clinic, and the RN is available for consultation. He is accompanied by no one and sits near the door.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess his capacity to consent, since a youth who understands can often consent under standards.): Consent depends on capacity to understand and decide, not only on age, so the nurse assesses capacity and follows regulatory standards and clinic policy. A blanket parental-consent rule ignores that. Delay could lose his engagement. Disclosing without consent breaks confidentiality unless there is a risk of harm. Assess capacity, then act."}, {"id": 2074, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Later, Jordan's uncle, 60, arrives with visual hallucinations that started yesterday. He is drowsy, inattentive, unsure of the day, and worse at night. The RN asks which finding best distinguishes delirium from a primary psychotic illness. What is the answer?", "options": {"A": "Visual hallucinations, which are not seen in primary psychosis.", "B": "Symptoms worse at night, which points to psychotic illness.", "C": "Fixed false beliefs about being followed that he describes calmly and consistently.", "D": "Acute onset with fluctuating attention and level of alertness."}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats visual hallucinations as absent in psychosis", "B": "Treats nighttime worsening as a sign of psychosis", "C": "Picks fixed delusions typical of primary psychosis"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C245", "cluster_text": "Jordan, 16, who is Cree, comes to a community mental health clinic alone. He reports low mood, poor sleep and loss of interest for two months. He says he has had bad experiences with services and is reluctant to talk. You are the practical nurse in the clinic, and the RN is available for consultation. He is accompanied by no one and sits near the door.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acute onset with fluctuating attention and level of alertness.): Delirium begins suddenly and causes inattention and a fluctuating level of consciousness, which is what separates it from primary psychosis. Visual hallucinations occur in both conditions. Night worsening is typical of delirium, not psychosis. Fixed, calm delusions suggest psychosis. Look for acute fluctuation and inattention."}, {"id": 2075, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After a difficult week the nurse finds herself thinking about Jordan at home, researching his family, and planning to visit him unannounced. She feels responsible for fixing his difficulties. She has not told her supervisor how she feels and has skipped her breaks. How should this be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "This reflects advocacy, so she should keep going and document her extra visits.", "B": "This shows strong commitment, so continue the visits and extra research as part of caring.", "C": "She should take extra time off and return without discussing the visits.", "D": "These are signs of overinvolvement; she should seek supervision and use debriefing to restore boundaries."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Calls overinvolvement advocacy and documents it", "B": "Mistakes overinvolvement for dedication", "C": "Withdraws without seeking supervision or debriefing"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C245", "cluster_text": "Jordan, 16, who is Cree, comes to a community mental health clinic alone. He reports low mood, poor sleep and loss of interest for two months. He says he has had bad experiences with services and is reluctant to talk. You are the practical nurse in the clinic, and the RN is available for consultation. He is accompanied by no one and sits near the door.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (These are signs of overinvolvement; she should seek supervision and use debriefing to restore boundaries.): Taking a client's problems home and acting outside the role signals overinvolvement, which harms both nurse and client, and supervision restores boundaries. Calling it advocacy ignores the risk. Time off without discussion avoids the lesson. Commitment alone does not justify unannounced visits. Seek support early."}, {"id": 2076, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On postoperative day 1, an adult with an abdominal drain has sudden bright red output of 150 mL in 30 minutes, previously 20 mL per shift. Blood pressure is 100/62, down from 124/78, and pulse is 112. The dressing is dry. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Apply pressure over the drain site and reassess his output at the next hour.", "B": "Stay with the client, check vital signs, and report to the RN and surgeon now.", "C": "Clamp the drain to slow output, then call the RN if bleeding continues.", "D": "Strip the drain tubing to clear possible clots and compare the drainage colour again."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats the drain site instead of the client", "C": "Clamps the drain without an order and delays reporting", "D": "Strips tubing without an order"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stay with the client, check vital signs, and report to the RN and surgeon now.): Sudden bright red output with falling blood pressure and rising pulse suggests hemorrhage, so the nurse stays, checks vital signs and calls for urgent help. Pressure on the drain site and waiting an hour delays care. Clamping a drain without direction can hide ongoing bleeding. Stripping tubing can damage tissue. Treat the client, not the drain."}, {"id": 2077, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A nurse overhears a colleague make demeaning remarks about an Indigenous client during handover. No one else heard it, and the nurse worries she cannot prove it. She wants to act safely. The remarks concerned the client's culture and were made at the nursing station. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Confront the colleague privately, then decide whether to report formally.", "B": "Wait until another witness can confirm the remarks before raising the concern formally.", "C": "Discuss it informally with the manager but avoid naming the colleague.", "D": "Report through the workplace process, describing what she heard and saw."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Handles the conduct informally before reporting", "B": "Demands proof before reporting", "C": "Reports vaguely without identifying the person"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report through the workplace process, describing what she heard and saw.): Reports of racism are based on what the nurse observed, and the workplace process exists to assess it, so proof beyond doubt is not required. Confronting alone first can delay a report. An unnamed informal comment cannot be investigated. Waiting for a witness may mean no report. Report honestly and factually."}, {"id": 2078, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Gagnon's ankles are swollen and he asks to keep his legs elevated high on pillows all day. He becomes short of breath lying flat and sleeps in a recliner. His lungs have a few crackles. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Keep his legs flat while lying supine, since this is safest for edema.", "B": "Elevate legs high above heart level for several hours, since swelling is the main concern.", "C": "Sit upright with legs dependent all day, since gravity drains fluid from the lungs.", "D": "Elevate legs only as tolerated, keep his head raised, and stop if breathing worsens."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Lies a heart failure client flat", "B": "Ignores orthopnea when elevating legs", "C": "Chooses dependent legs to treat lung fluid"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C243", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gagnon, 75, lives at home and receives visits from the community nursing team. He has heart failure and venous insufficiency with mild ankle swelling, and takes a statin and spironolactone, a potassium-sparing diuretic. You are the practical nurse completing his weekly visit, and the RN supervises his plan of care. He lives with his wife, who helps with his daily care and meals.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Elevate legs only as tolerated, keep his head raised, and stop if breathing worsens.): Leg elevation can raise fluid return to the heart, so with heart failure and orthopnea, he elevates legs only as tolerated with his head raised. Elevating high for hours could worsen his breathing. Lying flat worsens orthopnea. Keeping legs dependent all day worsens edema. Balance edema care with breathing."}, {"id": 2079, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Gagnon says he puts on his compression stockings after he has been up and about for the morning, when his ankles are already swollen, and feels they are tight. He has worn them daily for three weeks and his skin looks intact. What advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply them before getting out of bed, when swelling is lowest, and check the skin daily.", "B": "Apply them after a morning walk, when circulation is strongest, and check the skin weekly.", "C": "Apply them in bed but skip days when his ankles feel tight.", "D": "Applying them after swelling develops is acceptable because they then compress the fluid."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Applies stockings after swelling has built up", "C": "Skips wear when swelling makes them feel tight", "D": "Applies stockings after edema has formed"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C243", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gagnon, 75, lives at home and receives visits from the community nursing team. He has heart failure and venous insufficiency with mild ankle swelling, and takes a statin and spironolactone, a potassium-sparing diuretic. You are the practical nurse completing his weekly visit, and the RN supervises his plan of care. He lives with his wife, who helps with his daily care and meals.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Apply them before getting out of bed, when swelling is lowest, and check the skin daily.): Stockings work best applied before standing, when the legs are least swollen, and daily skin checks catch pressure damage. Applying after a walk fits poorly and checking weekly misses skin damage. Skipping days on tight ankles removes the benefit when swelling is worst. Folded tops create a tight band. Put them on first thing, in bed."}, {"id": 2080, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Gagnon takes atorvastatin and reports aching in both thighs for a week. He believes it is from yardwork and wants to continue. His urine is darker than usual. He has no fever, and his last blood work was normal six months ago. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Report the muscle pain and dark urine to the RN, since statins can harm muscle.", "B": "Advise increased water intake to clear the dark urine and continue the statin.", "C": "Tell him statin aches are common, and ask him to take the dose with food.", "D": "Advise rest and stretching, and recheck the thighs at the next weekly visit."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats dark urine as simple dehydration", "C": "Dismisses statin muscle pain as a common effect", "D": "Attributes symptoms to activity"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C243", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gagnon, 75, lives at home and receives visits from the community nursing team. He has heart failure and venous insufficiency with mild ankle swelling, and takes a statin and spironolactone, a potassium-sparing diuretic. You are the practical nurse completing his weekly visit, and the RN supervises his plan of care. He lives with his wife, who helps with his daily care and meals.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report the muscle pain and dark urine to the RN, since statins can harm muscle.): Muscle pain with dark urine on a statin may signal muscle breakdown, which needs RN and prescriber review. Waiting a week delays care. Extra water and continuing the statin could allow harm to progress. Taking the dose with food does not make muscle symptoms safe. Report unexplained muscle symptoms on statins."}, {"id": 2081, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Gagnon asks if he can use a potassium-based salt substitute to cut sodium, since he takes spironolactone. His last potassium was high-normal. He says the label calls it a healthy choice. He is also eating more vegetables and fruit lately. What should the nurse say?", "options": {"A": "It lowers sodium and potassium together, so it is safe alongside spironolactone.", "B": "It can raise potassium to dangerous levels with spironolactone, so check with the RN.", "C": "It is safe if he uses small amounts spread across the day, with meals and water.", "D": "It is safe because spironolactone blocks absorption of the potassium in the substitute."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believes the substitute lowers potassium", "C": "Assuming small amounts remove the potassium risk", "D": "Invents an absorption block by spironolactone"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C243", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gagnon, 75, lives at home and receives visits from the community nursing team. He has heart failure and venous insufficiency with mild ankle swelling, and takes a statin and spironolactone, a potassium-sparing diuretic. You are the practical nurse completing his weekly visit, and the RN supervises his plan of care. He lives with his wife, who helps with his daily care and meals.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It can raise potassium to dangerous levels with spironolactone, so check with the RN.): Spironolactone holds potassium in the body, and potassium salt substitutes add more, so high levels and arrhythmias are possible. Marketing claims do not account for his drug. Small amounts can still add up. The drug does not block absorption, and the substitute raises potassium rather than lowering it. Check before adding potassium."}, {"id": 2082, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At 2100, a home care client, 46, three months after kidney transplant, calls with a temperature of 38.1 C, chills and a mild sore throat. He takes tacrolimus and wants to wait for his clinic visit in the morning. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Suggest acetaminophen and fluids and recheck his temperature in the morning at the clinic.", "B": "Advise assessment today through the RN or transplant team, as fever may be infection.", "C": "Recommend rest and a warm bath since a low fever often signals a simple cold.", "D": "Advise him to hold the tacrolimus until the fever settles to help his body fight infection."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Masks the fever and delays assessment", "C": "Attributes fever to a simple cold", "D": "Stops an anti-rejection drug without direction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise assessment today through the RN or transplant team, as fever may be infection.): Immunosuppressed clients may show few signs of infection, so a fever needs same-day assessment. Treating the fever and waiting hides a serious infection. Holding tacrolimus risks rejection and is not the nurse's decision. A cold is only one possibility. Fever in the immunosuppressed is urgent."}, {"id": 2083, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Iqbal, 38, is waiting for an endoscopy and looks anxious. Her pulse is 112, respirations 22 and blood pressure 150/92, with a usual pulse near 76. She also reports weight loss and heat intolerance over two months. What is the best nursing approach?", "options": {"A": "Reassure her, reassess vital signs once calm, and report persistent changes with other symptoms.", "B": "Ask her to breathe slowly for several minutes, then record only the final reading taken.", "C": "Delay the procedure automatically and request a sedative order to lower her heart rate quickly.", "D": "Document anxiety as the cause and proceed, since the changes match her nervousness before the test."}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Records only the improved reading", "C": "Delays and sedates without assessment", "D": "Attributes findings to anxiety alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reassure her, reassess vital signs once calm, and report persistent changes with other symptoms.): Anxiety can raise vital signs, but weight loss and heat intolerance suggest another cause such as thyroid disease, so reassess after calming and report persistent findings. Calling it anxiety alone misses a possible problem. Delay and sedation are not the nurse's decision. Recording only the final value hides the first. Report the full picture."}, {"id": 2084, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lemieux grimaces when turned, moans, and guards her hip, but her pulse and blood pressure are unchanged. She speaks mainly Italian and cannot rate pain with a number. The care team notes she has a hip contracture from an old fall. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Use a behavioural pain scale, treat as ordered, and report to the RN for review.", "B": "Wait until she cries out or her vital signs rise before giving analgesia.", "C": "Rely on stable vital signs, which show her pain is controlled.", "D": "Ask her family to rate her pain numerically, since they know her daily habits best."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Delays analgesia until crisis", "C": "Uses vital signs as the only pain measure", "D": "Substitutes family rating for observation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C250", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Lemieux, 91, lives in long-term care and speaks mainly Italian. She has advanced dementia and heart failure and has recently begun palliative care. Her family reports reduced intake and restlessness. You are the practical nurse, and the RN leads the palliative plan of care. Her goals of care were reviewed with the family last week and focus on comfort. She cannot say how she feels in English.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a behavioural pain scale, treat as ordered, and report to the RN for review.): People who cannot self report pain because of language or cognitive barriers show pain through behaviour, so a validated behavioural scale guides treatment and RN review. Vital signs often stay normal with chronic pain. Family can help but should not replace observation. Waiting for a crisis allows suffering. Observe behaviour when words are not available."}, {"id": 2085, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In her last days, Mrs. Lemieux is restless and picks at her sheets. Pain and bowel concerns have been assessed and treated. Her family asks what else can be done. Her blood pressure is low and she is no longer eating or drinking much. Which non-drug measure is best?", "options": {"A": "Reduce noise, use gentle touch and familiar music, and reposition her slowly to maintain comfort.", "B": "Keep the room lit and talk to her often to keep her oriented.", "C": "Offer sips of fluid every ten minutes to correct dehydration and ease restlessness.", "D": "Encourage the family to speak loudly and ask her questions to keep her responsive."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Uses stimulation and light that increase agitation", "C": "Pushes fluids on a client who cannot swallow safely", "D": "Overstimulates her with loud speech and questions"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C250", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Lemieux, 91, lives in long-term care and speaks mainly Italian. She has advanced dementia and heart failure and has recently begun palliative care. Her family reports reduced intake and restlessness. You are the practical nurse, and the RN leads the palliative plan of care. Her goals of care were reviewed with the family last week and focus on comfort. She cannot say how she feels in English.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduce noise, use gentle touch and familiar music, and reposition her slowly to maintain comfort.): Quiet surroundings, touch, familiar music and gentle repositioning calm restlessness without side effects. Constant light and talking add to agitation. Pushing sips risks choking and does not settle restlessness. Loud questions overwhelm her. Comfort at the end of life is calm and familiar."}, {"id": 2086, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Lemieux's family asks about starting IV fluids because her intake has fallen. The team's goals are comfort, and swallowing is poor. They want to know what is accurate. She has dry mucous membranes and takes only sips of water with help. Which explanation is correct?", "options": {"A": "Fluids are given only by mouth in palliative care, so nothing else is offered.", "B": "IV fluids should be started because dry mucous membranes mean she is in distress.", "C": "Fluids are withheld in palliative care, since fluid given causes overload and distress.", "D": "Subcutaneous fluids are an option if they match her goals of care and the team agrees."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Wrongly limits fluids to the oral route", "B": "Treats dry membranes as proof of distress needing IV fluids", "C": "Believes palliative care bans fluids"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C250", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Lemieux, 91, lives in long-term care and speaks mainly Italian. She has advanced dementia and heart failure and has recently begun palliative care. Her family reports reduced intake and restlessness. You are the practical nurse, and the RN leads the palliative plan of care. Her goals of care were reviewed with the family last week and focus on comfort. She cannot say how she feels in English.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Subcutaneous fluids are an option if they match her goals of care and the team agrees.): Hydration at end of life is an individual decision tied to comfort and goals, and subcutaneous routes are less invasive. Routine IV fluids can add burden. Dry mucous membranes are eased by mouth care. Palliative care does not limit fluids to the oral route or forbid them. The team advises and shares the decision."}, {"id": 2087, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before her decline, Mrs. Lemieux had weight loss, slow gait and exhaustion over months. A student asks the nurse how frailty differs from a single disease. Her daughter notes she once walked daily but now sits most of the day. Which description of frailty is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "A mental health condition in older adults that causes withdrawal and low energy.", "B": "A single chronic disease that causes weight loss and weakness and is treated with specific drugs.", "C": "A state of reduced reserve across several body systems that raises the risk of sudden decline.", "D": "A mild loss of fitness in older adults that reverses with exercise alone."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Confuses frailty with depression", "B": "Describes one disease with one treatment", "D": "Treats frailty as simple deconditioning"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C250", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Lemieux, 91, lives in long-term care and speaks mainly Italian. She has advanced dementia and heart failure and has recently begun palliative care. Her family reports reduced intake and restlessness. You are the practical nurse, and the RN leads the palliative plan of care. Her goals of care were reviewed with the family last week and focus on comfort. She cannot say how she feels in English.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A state of reduced reserve across several body systems that raises the risk of sudden decline.): Frailty is a syndrome of lost reserve across systems, so small stressors can cause big decline, and care planning focuses on the whole person. A single disease has a different pattern. Frailty is not a mental health condition and is not fixed by exercise alone. It is not normal ageing and needs assessment."}, {"id": 2088, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Costa, 33, is scheduled for knee arthroscopy tomorrow. She says she is terrified of anesthesia and not waking, and becomes tearful. Her mother accompanies her. She has no previous history of surgery and has not eaten since the morning. What is the best response from the nurse?", "options": {"A": "Reassure her that serious anesthetic problems are very rare and move on.", "B": "Suggest she cancel and reschedule if she cannot feel calmer by tomorrow morning.", "C": "Acknowledge her fear, ask what worries her most, and offer accurate information about what to expect.", "D": "Arrange for her mother to explain the anesthetic process so she hears it calmly."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offers reassurance that closes the conversation", "B": "Encourages cancellation as a solution", "D": "Hands the explanation to a family member"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acknowledge her fear, ask what worries her most, and offer accurate information about what to expect.): Acknowledging the fear and exploring it lets her voice specific concerns, and accurate information reduces uncertainty. Quick reassurance dismisses her feeling. Asking her mother to explain leaves the nurse's role undone and may spread wrong details. Suggesting cancellation puts pressure on her. Listen, explore, inform."}, {"id": 2089, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "After a visit, a nurse realizes she addressed an Indigenous client's grandmother by first name and asked why the family 'still' uses a healer. The client looked uncomfortable but said nothing. The nurse worries mentioning it may damage trust. What is best?", "options": {"A": "Write an apology note and consider it closed.", "B": "Ask a colleague from the community to speak with the family on her behalf.", "C": "Read policy and avoid raising it with the family.", "D": "Reflect on her assumptions, acknowledge the misstep, and learn."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offers a one-time apology without reflection", "B": "Delegates repair to someone else", "C": "Studies policy but avoids honest acknowledgement"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reflect on her assumptions, acknowledge the misstep, and learn.): Cultural safety needs self-reflection and honest acknowledgement, and the client is the one who judges whether care felt safe. A note that closes the matter skips reflection. Reading policy without acknowledging the misstep leaves the harm. Using a colleague hands off her responsibility. Reflect, acknowledge and learn."}, {"id": 2090, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Tran, 49, tells a nurse that her remark about his culture was hurtful. She replies that she meant well. He turns away and stops answering questions. The conversation took place in front of another client waiting nearby. Which response best repairs trust?", "options": {"A": "Apologize in front of the waiting client so everyone sees she is accountable.", "B": "Apologize briefly while explaining how the remark was misunderstood by many in similar situations.", "C": "Apologize briefly, then continue the assessment so his care is not delayed.", "D": "Offer a sincere apology for the impact, listen to his concerns, and change the behaviour."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Apologizes publicly and breaches his privacy", "B": "Apologizes while blaming the listener", "C": "Apologizes but moves on without listening"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Offer a sincere apology for the impact, listen to his concerns, and change the behaviour.): Repair begins with a sincere apology for the impact and listening to his concerns, followed by changed behaviour. A public apology exposes him in front of another client. A brief apology followed by moving on gives him no chance to be heard. An apology that explains how others misread it shifts blame. Own the impact, not the intent."}, {"id": 2091, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During a home visit, a client, 72, has a new 3 cm blistered red area on her heel. Her family offers an antibiotic cream they had at home and asks the nurse to put it on. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Advise the family to watch for changes and call if the area grows larger.", "B": "Cleanse and apply a hydrocolloid dressing based on the nurse's own judgment of the wound.", "C": "Assess and document the area, then report it to the RN before applying any product.", "D": "Apply the family's cream and cover it, then report at the next scheduled visit."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays reporting a new skin change", "B": "Treats independently outside the plan", "D": "Applies an unordered product"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess and document the area, then report it to the RN before applying any product.): A new blistered area on the heel may be an early pressure injury, so it is assessed, documented and reported to the RN, who guides treatment. Using family cream without direction is outside scope. Choosing a dressing alone bypasses the plan. Waiting for it to grow delays care. Report new wounds before treating."}, {"id": 2092, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "A nurse licensed for four years feels competent and says she has finished with education. Last week she made a near-miss insulin error. Her regulatory body requires an annual learning plan. She has no recent education recorded and her renewal is due in three months. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Complete an insulin safety course now and consider her learning plan finished.", "B": "Ask a peer to review her charts yearly and rely on that feedback.", "C": "Reflect on the near miss, set learning goals, and update her professional learning plan.", "D": "Complete the required hours at the end of the cycle with any available course."}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats one course as ending continuing learning", "B": "Relies on peer review instead of a learning plan", "D": "Chooses courses for hours only"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reflect on the near miss, set learning goals, and update her professional learning plan.): Competence is maintained through reflection and learning, and a near miss shows a gap worth addressing in her plan. One course does not complete a plan that must be updated each year. Peer feedback helps but does not replace her own goals. Last minute courses may not match her gaps. Learning continues after licensure."}, {"id": 2093, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a ward, a nurse sprays disinfectant on a bed rail after a client with MRSA is discharged, then immediately wipes it dry with a paper towel. The product label says the surface must stay wet for three minutes. Which statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Contact time applies, but wiping within one minute is enough for MRSA surfaces.", "B": "Contact time was met, since the surface was wet when the towel touched it.", "C": "Contact time was not met, so reapply and keep the surface wet for the labelled time.", "D": "The step was correct, since physical wiping removes organisms and makes contact time unnecessary."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Invents a shorter contact time for MRSA", "B": "Counts the moment of wetting as the full contact time", "D": "Replaces contact time with wiping"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Contact time was not met, so reapply and keep the surface wet for the labelled time.): Disinfectants kill organisms only when they stay wet on the surface for the labelled time, and wiping dry immediately cut that short. Wiping within a minute does not meet a three minute label. Being wet at first touch is not the same as staying wet. Contact time applies to routine surfaces too. Follow the label contact time."}, {"id": 2094, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, an order reads ondansetron 4 mg every eight hours as needed, with no route stated. The client is nauseated but awake and has both IV and oral forms available on the unit. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Choose the form on hand and record the route used on the medication record afterward.", "B": "Give the IV form since nausea makes oral absorption unreliable and it acts faster.", "C": "Give the oral form because the client is awake and it is lower risk than injection.", "D": "Clarify the route with the prescriber first, since the order is incomplete."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses convenience and documents after", "B": "Selects the faster route by assumption", "C": "Chooses a route without an order"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Clarify the route with the prescriber first, since the order is incomplete.): An order without a route is incomplete, and choosing a route is a prescriber's decision, so the nurse clarifies before giving. Picking oral because it is safer assumes the order. Picking IV for speed does the same. Convenience is not a basis for route. Clarify incomplete orders."}, {"id": 2095, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a home visit, a nurse finds a 66-year-old man with a new tracheostomy. His daughter rinses the suction catheter in a cup of tap water kept on the table and reuses it through the day. His secretions are thick but clear and his temperature is normal. How should the nurse interpret this practice?", "options": {"A": "Normal temperature and clear secretions show infection is absent, so the routine continues", "B": "Boiled and cooled tap water makes the rinse safe, so the cup needs refilling daily", "C": "Reuse is acceptable if the catheter is rinsed in fresh tap water after each pass", "D": "Tap water and reuse can carry bacteria into the airway, so a fresh catheter is needed"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reads a normal temperature as proof of safe technique", "B": "Believes boiling tap water makes a reused rinse sterile", "C": "Believes fresh tap water rinsing makes reuse safe"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tap water and reuse can carry bacteria into the airway, so a fresh catheter is needed): A reused catheter and a cup of tap water bring organisms straight into the lower airway, so the daughter's routine needs to change even though the man looks well today. Normal temperature and clear secretions do not show the technique is safe, because infection may take days to appear. Fresh tap water is still not sterile. Boiled water left in an open cup is contaminated again."}, {"id": 2096, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the visit, Mr. Delorme says he skipped his last two appointments because staff at another clinic spoke to him as though he did not care about his health. He asks whether this clinic will do the same. What understanding should guide the nurse's response to this missed care?", "options": {"A": "His complaint concerns another clinic, so the nurse offers to forward it to that clinic", "B": "Missed visits show low motivation, so the nurse sets a firm attendance agreement before more teaching", "C": "Past disrespect can erode trust, so the nurse acknowledges it and builds a respectful relationship", "D": "Missed visits risk discharge, so the nurse explains clinic policy on attendance and moves on"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats the concern as an administrative complaint only", "B": "Blames the client's motivation instead of past experiences", "D": "Responds with policy instead of trust building"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C248", "cluster_text": "Mr. Raymond Delorme, 58, is Métis and has type 2 diabetes and heart failure. He attends a community health clinic where a practical nurse sees him monthly. His physician has just started bedtime insulin. He relies on a food bank for most groceries, lives forty minutes from the clinic, and says earlier clinics wrote him off when he missed visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Past disrespect can erode trust, so the nurse acknowledges it and builds a respectful relationship): Mr. Delorme links his missed visits to disrespectful treatment, so the nurse acknowledges that experience and rebuilds trust instead of treating nonadherence as a personal failing. A firm attendance agreement would repeat the pattern that pushed him away. Forwarding the complaint or reciting policy ignores the worry he voiced. Respect is the base for engagement."}, {"id": 2097, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Delorme explains that groceries from the food bank are mostly canned soup, pasta and bread, and that he cannot afford the fresh foods his earlier dietitian listed. He is starting insulin and worries about low blood sugar. Which action best addresses his food security alongside his diabetes teaching?", "options": {"A": "Build teaching around the foods he receives and refer him to food programs", "B": "Teach him to halve insulin doses on days when food runs short", "C": "Give him the dietitian's original list of fresh foods and ask him to follow it closely", "D": "Teach low sugar treatment with candy and leave meal planning to the dietitian"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Advises unsafe dose changes without an order", "C": "Repeats an unaffordable diet plan he already cannot follow", "D": "Teaches only low sugar rescue and ignores food access"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C248", "cluster_text": "Mr. Raymond Delorme, 58, is Métis and has type 2 diabetes and heart failure. He attends a community health clinic where a practical nurse sees him monthly. His physician has just started bedtime insulin. He relies on a food bank for most groceries, lives forty minutes from the clinic, and says earlier clinics wrote him off when he missed visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Build teaching around the foods he receives and refer him to food programs): Teaching built around the foods Mr. Delorme actually receives makes the plan achievable, and a referral addresses the food insecurity behind it. Repeating the dietitian's fresh food list sets him up to fail because he cannot afford it. Halving insulin is outside the nurse's authority and risks high glucose. Teaching only low sugar rescue leaves food security unaddressed."}, {"id": 2098, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At today's visit the nurse notes Mr. Delorme's weight is 3.2 kg above his clinic weight two weeks ago, with new ankle swelling he describes as tight shoes. Which documentation of these observations best supports the care team's next decision about his heart failure?", "options": {"A": "Weight gain and swelling present, which is expected in heart failure and needs no further comment", "B": "Weight up 3.2 kg in two weeks, new ankle swelling, client reports tight shoes, physician informed", "C": "Client appears worse than last visit with fluid problems, heart failure likely worsening, physician to review", "D": "Fluid retention due to missed medication doses, client counselled about adherence at this visit"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes the finding is normal and leaves it unreported", "C": "Writes vague opinions instead of measured findings", "D": "Records an unverified cause as if it were fact"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C248", "cluster_text": "Mr. Raymond Delorme, 58, is Métis and has type 2 diabetes and heart failure. He attends a community health clinic where a practical nurse sees him monthly. His physician has just started bedtime insulin. He relies on a food bank for most groceries, lives forty minutes from the clinic, and says earlier clinics wrote him off when he missed visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Weight up 3.2 kg in two weeks, new ankle swelling, client reports tight shoes, physician informed): Measured values, a time frame and the client's own words give the physician what is needed to judge fluid change. Entries that say a client seems worse or name a likely cause are opinions and cannot be compared at the next visit. Calling the finding expected leaves a possible deterioration unreported. Record what you measure and observe, then report it."}, {"id": 2099, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The nurse teaches Mr. Delorme to inject bedtime insulin using a pen. He demonstrates the steps correctly but cannot say what he would do if his sugar felt low. Which entry best records the outcome of this teaching session for the next nurse who sees him?", "options": {"A": "Insulin teaching provided at the clinic visit today, with written instructions given to the client", "B": "Pen injection correct; could not state low sugar actions; repeat hypoglycemia teaching", "C": "Client is a poor learner who forgot low sugar information and may need family present", "D": "Insulin pen teaching completed; client understands the injection process and what to do for low sugar"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Records only that teaching occurred, not what he learned", "C": "Labels the client instead of describing the observed gap", "D": "Records success that the client did not show"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C248", "cluster_text": "Mr. Raymond Delorme, 58, is Métis and has type 2 diabetes and heart failure. He attends a community health clinic where a practical nurse sees him monthly. His physician has just started bedtime insulin. He relies on a food bank for most groceries, lives forty minutes from the clinic, and says earlier clinics wrote him off when he missed visits.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pen injection correct; could not state low sugar actions; repeat hypoglycemia teaching): A useful teaching entry says what the client could and could not do, so the next nurse knows what to repeat. Mr. Delorme showed the injection technique but not the low sugar response, and the entry says so. Noting only that teaching was given hides the gap, and claiming full understanding is false. Judgments such as poor learner describe the person, not the outcome."}, {"id": 2100, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Over several weeks on her medical unit, Ines, a practical nurse, has grown cynical and dreads each shift. She feels guilty that short staffing forces her to rush care she believes is unsafe. A coworker tells her to push through because everyone is stretched. What should Ines do first?", "options": {"A": "Take sick leave without explanation and hope staffing improves while she is away", "B": "Ask for a transfer to a quieter unit without telling anyone why she wants to leave", "C": "Talk with coworkers about the staffing problem and wait for the manager to notice", "D": "Seek support from her manager or an assistance program and report the staffing concern"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Avoids the problem without reporting the cause", "B": "Avoids the problem by leaving without raising the cause", "C": "Vents informally without escalating the concern"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Seek support from her manager or an assistance program and report the staffing concern): Ines describes burnout and moral distress tied to unsafe staffing, so she needs support and the staffing concern needs to be raised through the proper channels. Leaving without explanation avoids the cause. Talking only with coworkers does not escalate it. Quietly transferring protects her but leaves clients exposed. Pushing through lets distress grow."}, {"id": 2101, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "New on the medication record of an 82-year-old long-term care resident is a tablet with no reason for the drug listed, and she does not know why she takes it. The morning medication pass is running twenty minutes late and the order is in hand. What should the nurse do before giving it?", "options": {"A": "Ask the resident's daughter what the tablet is for and give it if she agrees", "B": "Look the drug up in a reference book and give it if the usual dose matches", "C": "Give the tablet because a valid order is present on the medication record this morning", "D": "Confirm the indication with the prescriber or pharmacist before giving the tablet"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relies on a family member instead of the prescriber", "B": "Substitutes a reference check for confirming the intent of the order", "C": "Treats an order as enough without knowing the reason"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Confirm the indication with the prescriber or pharmacist before giving the tablet): The nurse needs to know the right reason for a drug, and an order with no indication calls for clarification before administration. A valid order alone does not remove that duty. A reference book gives usual uses but cannot confirm why this prescriber ordered it for this resident. A family member cannot confirm the prescriber's intent, and being late does not change the steps."}, {"id": 2102, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Realizing she gave a 69-year-old client a tablet prescribed for his roommate, an acute care nurse checks him. He feels well, his vital signs are unchanged, and the drug is not known to cause harm in a single dose. The charge nurse is at lunch. What should the nurse do next?", "options": {"A": "Wait until the charge nurse returns from lunch since the client is stable now", "B": "Assess him, tell the prescriber and manager, and file an incident report", "C": "Tell the client about the mix up and chart it before telling anyone else", "D": "Monitor him closely for symptoms and report the error only if his condition changes"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays reporting because the client looks stable", "C": "Discloses to the client but skips the prescriber and manager", "D": "Reports only when harm appears"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assess him, tell the prescriber and manager, and file an incident report): A medication error is reported promptly whether or not the client shows symptoms, because the prescriber may need to change monitoring or treatment. Assessment comes first, followed by notifying the prescriber and manager and completing an incident report. Waiting for symptoms or for the charge nurse's return delays a decision that belongs to others. Disclosure to the client matters, but it follows and does not replace reporting."}, {"id": 2103, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Two days after his wife died suddenly, a 38-year-old man comes to a community mental health walk-in clinic. He is tearful, cannot sleep, and says he cannot think clearly about funeral arrangements. Which statement best describes the purpose of crisis intervention for this client?", "options": {"A": "A screening step that decides whether he needs hospital admission for his grief", "B": "A waiting period until his emotions settle so that he can plan clearly", "C": "Grief counselling that follows a set stage model until his acute symptoms resolve", "D": "Short term support that restores coping and safety by focusing on the present problem"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reduces crisis intervention to admission screening", "B": "Believes help should be delayed until the client is calm", "C": "Confuses crisis intervention with long grief counselling"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Short term support that restores coping and safety by focusing on the present problem): Crisis intervention is brief, immediate and focused on the current problem. This man needs help now because he cannot sleep or plan the funeral. Admission screening is only one part of an assessment. A stage-based counselling course is longer than crisis care. Waiting until he is calm withholds help when it matters most."}, {"id": 2104, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Taking vital signs at 14:00, a medical unit nurse finds an 80-year-old man's early warning score has risen from 2 to 5 since morning, with new confusion and a respiratory rate of 24. He says he is just tired. The next routine check is at 16:00. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Give oxygen by mask and wait to see if his respiratory rate improves before calling anyone", "B": "Ask the client's family whether the confusion is usual before deciding on any action", "C": "Recheck vital signs at the scheduled 16:00 time since the client reports only tiredness", "D": "Reassess now and escalate to the RN and physician per the early warning protocol"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats first and delays escalation", "B": "Seeks family input instead of acting on objective changes", "C": "Waits for the routine check despite a rising score"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reassess now and escalate to the RN and physician per the early warning protocol): A score that has risen from 2 to 5 with new confusion signals deterioration, and the protocol calls for immediate reassessment and escalation. The client's claim of tiredness does not outweigh objective findings. Waiting for the 16:00 check loses time. Oxygen may be needed, but it does not replace calling the RN and physician, and family input can follow without delaying escalation."}, {"id": 2105, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At a hospital unit meeting, a practical nurse mentions that several First Nations clients have left against advice after being told that smudging or drumming is not allowed near the unit. Colleagues say rules are set by management. What is the nurse's best interpretation of her role?", "options": {"A": "Departures are individual decisions, but she should document each one in the chart", "B": "Bring client feedback to leaders and propose a change toward culturally safe policy", "C": "She should direct clients to use the hospital chapel instead of the unit", "D": "Policy change belongs to managers, so she should follow the rule and explain it to clients"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Records departures without reviewing the unit's practice", "C": "Redirects clients rather than changing the barrier", "D": "Assumes advocacy is only a manager's job"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bring client feedback to leaders and propose a change toward culturally safe policy): Advocacy for culturally safe care is part of every nurse's role, and a pattern of clients leaving is a signal the unit's practice needs review. Bringing the feedback to leaders and proposing a change works through the proper channel. Documenting departures does not change policy. Sending clients to the chapel leaves the barrier in place. Explaining the rule does not address why clients leave."}, {"id": 2106, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At a community mental health clinic, a 34-year-old man asks his nurse where she lives, whether she is married, and whether she has ever felt depressed. He says her sharing would help him trust her. His appointment has fifteen minutes left. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Share her marital status and city, but decline questions about her mental health", "B": "Redirect kindly to his concerns and share personal details only when it serves his care", "C": "Share a brief personal story of her own depression to build the trust he describes", "D": "Refuse to discuss it and tell him questions about the nurse are inappropriate in therapy"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Shares personal details that do not serve his care", "C": "Discloses her own health history to build rapport", "D": "Responds in a cold, shaming way that damages trust"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Redirect kindly to his concerns and share personal details only when it serves his care): The relationship exists for the client's needs, so personal disclosure is limited to what helps his care. Kindly redirecting keeps the focus on him and respects his wish for connection. Sharing marital status and city shifts attention to the nurse. Telling a depression story blurs boundaries. A curt refusal shames him."}, {"id": 2107, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Maintenance IV fluids are ordered overnight for an older adult on a medical unit who has heart failure and poor oral intake. The client has bilateral crackles and 2+ ankle edema. Which risk should the nurse recognize as the main concern with this order?", "options": {"A": "Hyponatremia, because dilute maintenance fluids lower sodium in a failing heart", "B": "Phlebitis, because overnight infusions inflame the vein of an older adult", "C": "Fluid overload, worsening congestion in the lungs of a failing heart", "D": "Dehydration, because the heart cannot move fluid to the tissues that need it"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Names a possible but secondary electrolyte effect", "B": "Names a real infusion risk that is not the main concern", "D": "Reverses the problem by predicting fluid depletion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fluid overload, worsening congestion in the lungs of a failing heart): A client with heart failure, crackles and edema is already holding extra fluid, so added IV volume can worsen pulmonary congestion. The nurse should question the order and report. Sodium dilution and phlebitis are possible but are not the main danger. Dehydration is not the issue when edema and crackles are present."}, {"id": 2108, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "After six years in long-term care, a resident offers his nurse a gift card worth about $100 and a handmade quilt to thank her for her kindness. He says it is a gift, not a bribe. How should the nurse plan her response using the team and employer policy?", "options": {"A": "Accept the quilt and decline the gift card, then tell the manager later", "B": "Accept both items but donate the gift card to the unit fund", "C": "Decline the gift card kindly, explain the policy, and discuss the quilt with her manager", "D": "Decline both items without explanation and ask the team to avoid assigning her to him"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Accepts a gift before consulting policy", "B": "Accepts gifts and reassigns the value instead of declining", "D": "Rejects him abruptly and withdraws care"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline the gift card kindly, explain the policy, and discuss the quilt with her manager): Accepting gifts of value can blur professional boundaries, so the nurse follows employer policy and involves her manager. A kind explanation preserves the relationship while declining the gift card. Accepting the quilt first and telling the manager later skips policy. Donating the card still means accepting it. Refusing with no explanation and avoiding him harms the bond."}, {"id": 2109, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "During the visit, Mrs. Hartnett says she has a mild runny nose and no fever, and asks whether she should still get the influenza vaccine her pharmacist offered this afternoon. She feels otherwise well and her temperature today is 36.8 C. What advice should the nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Have her take acetaminophen first so any reaction can be separated from cold symptoms", "B": "Skip the vaccine this season because a mild cold means her immune system is already active", "C": "A mild illness without fever need not delay vaccination, so she can proceed as planned", "D": "Postpone the vaccine until her nose has been clear for two weeks to avoid weak response"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Adds an unnecessary premedication", "B": "Believes a cold replaces vaccine protection", "D": "Delays for a mild illness without reason"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C255", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Joan Hartnett, 74, lives alone and receives home care after a hip replacement. Her medication list includes cyclobenzaprine for back spasm, a topical steroid cream she uses on eczema, and leftover oxycodone tablets from surgery. A practical nurse visits weekly to review her medications and teach, and Mrs. Hartnett keeps all of them in a kitchen drawer in a pill organizer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A mild illness without fever need not delay vaccination, so she can proceed as planned): Mild illness without fever is generally not a reason to delay vaccines, which are biologic drugs. Mrs. Hartnett has no fever and no contraindication. Waiting two weeks leaves her unprotected, and a cold gives no influenza immunity. Premedication with acetaminophen is not routine."}, {"id": 2110, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the next visit Mrs. Hartnett has been drowsy and reports nearly falling when she stood to answer the door. She takes cyclobenzaprine at bedtime and again in the afternoon, and says she plans to drive to her sister's tomorrow. Which understanding should guide the nurse?", "options": {"A": "It causes drowsiness, so she should not drive until reviewed", "B": "Drowsiness likely reflects low blood pressure, so driving is safe", "C": "Drowsiness fades after the first week of therapy, so driving is safe in the evening", "D": "Moving the second dose earlier prevents sedation before she drives"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Blames low blood pressure and ignores the drug", "C": "Assumes sedation always resolves quickly", "D": "Believes dose timing removes sedation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C255", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Joan Hartnett, 74, lives alone and receives home care after a hip replacement. Her medication list includes cyclobenzaprine for back spasm, a topical steroid cream she uses on eczema, and leftover oxycodone tablets from surgery. A practical nurse visits weekly to review her medications and teach, and Mrs. Hartnett keeps all of them in a kitchen drawer in a pill organizer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It causes drowsiness, so she should not drive until reviewed): Cyclobenzaprine is a sedating muscle relaxant, and in an older adult it raises the risk of dizziness, falls and unsafe driving. She should not drive until the prescriber reviews the dose, and the nurse reports the near fall. Changing dose times or waiting for the first week to pass does not remove the sedative effect, and blaming blood pressure ignores the drug."}, {"id": 2111, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The nurse notices thin, shiny, red skin with small visible vessels on Mrs. Hartnett's cheeks. She says she has applied her strongest steroid cream to her face twice daily for three months because it clears the eczema fastest. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Switch her to a moisturizer without telling the prescriber, since the steroid is no longer needed", "B": "Report the thinning to the prescriber and avoid the potent cream on her face until reviewed", "C": "Continue the cream because thin red skin is the expected sign of eczema clearing", "D": "Reduce the cream to once daily and apply a thicker layer to protect the thinned skin"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Changes therapy without informing the prescriber", "C": "Misreads steroid skin damage as healing", "D": "Changes frequency and amount without an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C255", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Joan Hartnett, 74, lives alone and receives home care after a hip replacement. Her medication list includes cyclobenzaprine for back spasm, a topical steroid cream she uses on eczema, and leftover oxycodone tablets from surgery. A practical nurse visits weekly to review her medications and teach, and Mrs. Hartnett keeps all of them in a kitchen drawer in a pill organizer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the thinning to the prescriber and avoid the potent cream on her face until reviewed): Long use of a potent topical steroid on thin facial skin can cause atrophy and visible vessels, which is what the nurse sees. Nurses report the adverse effect and have the prescriber choose a safer product. Changing frequency or thickness without an order is outside the nurse's role, and thin red skin is damage, not healing. Switching products without telling the prescriber leaves the eczema untreated."}, {"id": 2112, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Hartnett still has eleven oxycodone tablets from surgery and says her pain is now mild. Her neighbour has a bad back and asked for them, and she wonders whether to flush them or keep them in case her hip hurts again. What does this situation call for?", "options": {"A": "Return the unused tablets to a pharmacy take-back program and do not share them", "B": "Flush the tablets down the toilet so that no one else can reach them", "C": "Keep the tablets in a locked drawer for future hip pain and refuse the neighbour", "D": "Give the neighbour only two tablets after checking that his pain resembles hers"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Flushes drugs, harming the water supply", "C": "Stores leftover opioids that may be misused later", "D": "Shares a prescription drug with another person"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C255", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Joan Hartnett, 74, lives alone and receives home care after a hip replacement. Her medication list includes cyclobenzaprine for back spasm, a topical steroid cream she uses on eczema, and leftover oxycodone tablets from surgery. A practical nurse visits weekly to review her medications and teach, and Mrs. Hartnett keeps all of them in a kitchen drawer in a pill organizer.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Return the unused tablets to a pharmacy take-back program and do not share them): Leftover opioids should be returned to a pharmacy take-back program, which keeps them away from misuse and from the environment. Keeping them for possible future pain leaves a risk in the home. Flushing is not the recommended route for most drugs, and giving tablets to the neighbour is sharing a prescribed controlled drug, which is unsafe and unlawful."}, {"id": 2113, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a combined maternity unit, a new graduate practical nurse is asked by a colleague to review a labouring client's fetal heart strip and decide whether the physician must be called for delivery. The strip shows decelerations she is unsure about. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Report her observations to the RN and leave interpretation and delivery decisions to them", "B": "Review the strip with another practical nurse and call the physician if both agree", "C": "Interpret the strip using the unit's classification chart and call the physician if it appears abnormal", "D": "Place the client on her left side, then decide whether the strip needs escalation"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Relies on peer interpretation within the same scope", "C": "Takes on interpretation and decisions beyond her scope", "D": "Delays escalation while deciding alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report her observations to the RN and leave interpretation and delivery decisions to them): Interpreting fetal heart patterns and deciding on delivery belong to the RN or physician, and a new practical nurse works within her competence. Describing what she sees and getting help is the right action when she is unsure. Peer agreement does not change the scope boundary. Deciding alone after repositioning delays escalation. Using a chart to decide overlooks scope."}, {"id": 2114, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On the surgical ward, a 60-year-old man awaits left knee arthroscopy. The nurse preparing him sees that the consent form lists the left knee, but no side marking is visible on the leg. The client says he is sure which knee it is. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Verify the consent and chart, then report the missing mark before he leaves the unit", "B": "Rely on the client's certainty and send him to the operating room as scheduled", "C": "Mark the left knee herself using the consent form and the client's confirmation of the side", "D": "Document the missing mark and let the operating room team check it on arrival"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Depends on memory of the client alone", "C": "Marks the site herself outside the surgeon's role", "D": "Passes the problem on to a later team"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Verify the consent and chart, then report the missing mark before he leaves the unit): Site marking is a safety step done by the surgeon, and its absence must be reported before the client leaves the unit. Verifying the consent and chart confirms the side and gives the team accurate information. Marking the knee herself or relying on the client's certainty skips the defense meant to catch errors, and leaving the check to the operating room delays a fix that is easiest now."}, {"id": 2115, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Living at home, a 42-year-old wheelchair user asks the visiting nurse how often he needs to shift his weight. He sits most of the day and has a reddened area over his tailbone that blanches when pressed. How should the nurse interpret the redness and teach him?", "options": {"A": "Apply barrier cream and a dressing over the area, and keep sitting normally", "B": "Blanching redness is normal after long sitting, so he can shift weight once each day", "C": "Blanchable redness is stage 1 injury, so he must stay off it until it fades", "D": "Skin is at risk, so teach frequent weight shifts, daily skin checks and cushion use"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats at-risk skin as a wound while allowing continued pressure", "B": "Treats a warning sign as normal and under-teaches", "C": "Misstages blanching redness as a stage 1 injury"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Skin is at risk, so teach frequent weight shifts, daily skin checks and cushion use): Redness that blanches over a bony area signals pressure and risk of injury, so prevention teaching starts now. Frequent weight shifts, daily skin checks and a pressure relieving cushion reduce that risk. A dressing does not relieve pressure. Blanching redness is not a stage 1 injury, which does not blanch. Shifting weight once a day is too seldom."}, {"id": 2116, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At a community clinic, a 70-year-old woman says her friend told her vaccines stop working after 65 and that she does not need any more. She is healthy, walks daily, and has had no immunizations since her forties. How should the nurse interpret this belief?", "options": {"A": "Partly true, since most vaccines need boosting only once before age sixty", "B": "It is partly true, so only an annual influenza vaccine is worth discussing with her", "C": "A misconception, since older adults stay at higher risk and need review", "D": "Accurate for newer vaccines, so only the older vaccines need review"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Invents an age limit for vaccine boosting", "B": "Narrows the discussion to one vaccine without assessing others", "D": "Treats vaccine review as limited to older products"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A misconception, since older adults stay at higher risk and need review): Older adults often respond well to vaccines and face higher risk from infections such as influenza, pneumonia and shingles. The nurse corrects the belief and reviews which immunizations she is due for. Saying vaccines stop working or have an age limit overstates age related decline. Limiting talk to influenza misses other recommended vaccines."}, {"id": 2117, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Chu, 81, receives continuous enteral feeding through a gastrostomy tube. At 10:00 the bag holds about 150 mL of formula hung at 04:00, and a new can sits on the cart. Which action is most consistent with infection prevention?", "options": {"A": "Keep the existing formula hanging until empty, then rinse the bag with warm water before refilling", "B": "Pour new formula on top of the remaining volume to avoid waste and keep feeding going", "C": "Wipe the top of the bag with an alcohol swab and add fresh formula", "D": "Discard the old formula and use clean equipment, adding only a limited volume of fresh formula"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaves old formula hanging too long and rinses a contaminated bag", "B": "Tops up old formula, which keeps contaminated product in use", "C": "Uses a swab as if it makes the old formula safe"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C262", "cluster_text": "Priya, a practical nurse, is floating to a combined medical and pediatric unit for the day. She is assigned an adult with a feeding tube, an older adult with a diabetic foot ulcer, and a child admitted with RSV. A hand hygiene audit posted on the unit that morning showed low compliance, and the charge nurse asks her to follow policy and speak up about any concern.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discard the old formula and use clean equipment, adding only a limited volume of fresh formula): Formula that has hung for hours can grow bacteria, and adding fresh product to it contaminates the new supply. Discarding the old formula and hanging a limited volume in clean equipment limits that risk. Pouring new formula on old, or waiting until the bag is empty, keeps aged product in use. Swabbing the bag opening does not make the remaining formula safe."}, {"id": 2118, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Later, Priya meets Mrs. Ellis, 77, who has a diabetic foot ulcer. Mrs. Ellis says she soaks the foot each evening in the shared bathroom basin, and today the wound edge is red with new cloudy drainage and an odour. What action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply an antibiotic ointment from the unit supply and recheck the wound tomorrow morning", "B": "Clean the basin with disinfectant and let her continue soaking since the foot needs cleansing", "C": "Report the infection signs to the RN or physician and stop the basin soaks", "D": "Cover the wound with a waterproof dressing and reassess at the next routine visit"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats without an order and delays assessment", "B": "Fixes the basin but ignores the infection signs", "D": "Covers the problem and postpones escalation"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C262", "cluster_text": "Priya, a practical nurse, is floating to a combined medical and pediatric unit for the day. She is assigned an adult with a feeding tube, an older adult with a diabetic foot ulcer, and a child admitted with RSV. A hand hygiene audit posted on the unit that morning showed low compliance, and the charge nurse asks her to follow policy and speak up about any concern.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the infection signs to the RN or physician and stop the basin soaks): Redness, cloudy drainage and odour suggest wound infection in a client with diabetes, which needs prompt report to the RN or physician. Soaking in a shared basin adds exposure and softens skin, so it should stop. Disinfecting the basin does not address the infection. Ointment without an order is outside scope, and a waterproof dressing with delayed reassessment postpones needed treatment."}, {"id": 2119, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Priya finds a child with RSV in a single room. The door sign says droplet precautions only, and the child's wet cough soils the bed rails and toys. The mother hugs the child often and helps with feeds. What isolation approach should Priya follow?", "options": {"A": "Keep droplet precautions in place, since the child's cough spreads respiratory droplets", "B": "Use contact and droplet precautions together, with gown, gloves and mask", "C": "Use airborne precautions with a fit tested respirator and a negative pressure room", "D": "Use contact precautions, because the soiled rails and toys are the main source"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applies only one transmission route", "C": "Chooses airborne measures for a droplet and contact organism", "D": "Overlooks respiratory droplets and applies contact measures alone"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C262", "cluster_text": "Priya, a practical nurse, is floating to a combined medical and pediatric unit for the day. She is assigned an adult with a feeding tube, an older adult with a diabetic foot ulcer, and a child admitted with RSV. A hand hygiene audit posted on the unit that morning showed low compliance, and the charge nurse asks her to follow policy and speak up about any concern.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use contact and droplet precautions together, with gown, gloves and mask): RSV spreads by respiratory droplets and by contact with contaminated surfaces, so both sets of precautions apply. Gloves and gown guard against contact, and a mask with eye protection guards against droplets when close to the child. A droplet sign alone misses the soiled rails and toys. Airborne precautions are not needed for RSV, and contact measures alone leave her face exposed."}, {"id": 2120, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the end of the day the unit manager reviews the audit with staff. Compliance is low, and the only alcohol hand rub dispensers are at the nursing station. Priya is asked how the unit should respond. Which suggestion best supports hand hygiene at the point of care?", "options": {"A": "Place hand rub at the point of care, such as at the bedside or room entrance", "B": "Replace hand rub with extra sinks in the hallway so staff can wash more often", "C": "Keep dispensers at the nursing station and remind staff to walk there before each client contact", "D": "Post more reminder signs near the nursing station and monitor compliance weekly"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Swaps a faster method for a slower one", "C": "Keeps product away from where care happens", "D": "Relies on reminders instead of changing access"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C262", "cluster_text": "Priya, a practical nurse, is floating to a combined medical and pediatric unit for the day. She is assigned an adult with a feeding tube, an older adult with a diabetic foot ulcer, and a child admitted with RSV. A hand hygiene audit posted on the unit that morning showed low compliance, and the charge nurse asks her to follow policy and speak up about any concern.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Place hand rub at the point of care, such as at the bedside or room entrance): Hand hygiene improves when product is within reach at the point of care, because convenience drives use. Bedside or doorway dispensers remove the walk to the nursing station. Asking staff to walk back keeps the barrier. Hallway sinks are slower than hand rub, and signs at the station do not help where care is given."}, {"id": 2121, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Before report, Dana realizes she has skipped lunch for two days and now feels shaky, irritable and slow to concentrate. A colleague jokes that leaders do not take breaks. Which approach to her own wellbeing best supports safe leadership today?", "options": {"A": "Push through the shift and take time to rest after the clients are all settled", "B": "Eat a snack at the desk while reviewing charts and staying available", "C": "Ask a colleague to cover her break, then skip delegating until she returns", "D": "Schedule her meal break and delegate within scope so she can keep leading safely"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays self care until the shift ends", "B": "Eats while working and stays on call", "C": "Pauses delegation and leaves the team without direction"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C263", "cluster_text": "Dana, a practical nurse on a busy medical unit, has been asked to act as team leader for the day shift. She has worked three twelve hour shifts, slept poorly, and leads a team of one registered nurse and one health care aide. Several clients need care at once, and the manager plans to hold a quality meeting later in the day.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Schedule her meal break and delegate within scope so she can keep leading safely): Leaders who are hungry and tired make more errors, so self care is part of safe practice and not selfish. Scheduling a break and delegating within each team member's scope lets Dana stay alert. Pushing on delays the risk to later in the shift. A desk snack gives no real break. Pausing delegation leaves the team without direction."}, {"id": 2122, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mid morning, Dana must reassess a client whose oxygen saturation fell to 89 percent, give a scheduled medication due in thirty minutes, and return a family member's phone call. The aide reports that another client needs help to the washroom. How should Dana prioritize?", "options": {"A": "Take the family call first to prevent complaints, then assess the client and give the medication", "B": "Give the scheduled medication first because it has a time limit, then assess the oxygen level", "C": "Assess the client with low oxygen first, then delegate the washroom help and give the medication", "D": "Complete tasks in the order they were requested, starting with the family member's call"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Puts customer relations ahead of a possible airway problem", "B": "Lets a scheduled task outrank an urgent change", "D": "Follows request order instead of urgency"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C263", "cluster_text": "Dana, a practical nurse on a busy medical unit, has been asked to act as team leader for the day shift. She has worked three twelve hour shifts, slept poorly, and leads a team of one registered nurse and one health care aide. Several clients need care at once, and the manager plans to hold a quality meeting later in the day.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Assess the client with low oxygen first, then delegate the washroom help and give the medication): A falling oxygen saturation is a possible threat to breathing and takes priority over scheduled or social tasks. Dana assesses that client, asks the aide to help with the washroom, and gives the medication within its window. The medication can wait a short time, and the family call can follow later. Working in the order requested ignores urgency."}, {"id": 2123, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "The RN on Dana's team tells her to hold a client's blood pressure medication, saying the pressure is low. The order says to give it unless systolic pressure is below 100, and the client's reading is 108. The RN says Dana should just do it because the RN has more education. How should Dana interpret her accountability?", "options": {"A": "She asks the other practical nurse on the unit which instruction she should follow", "B": "She gives the medication as ordered and tells the RN afterward that she disagrees", "C": "The RN carries responsibility for the decision, so Dana holds the medication as instructed", "D": "She is accountable for her own practice, so she clarifies the conflicting instruction first"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Seeks a peer ruling instead of the prescriber", "B": "Acts first and raises the conflict after the fact", "C": "Believes a higher educated colleague takes over accountability"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C263", "cluster_text": "Dana, a practical nurse on a busy medical unit, has been asked to act as team leader for the day shift. She has worked three twelve hour shifts, slept poorly, and leads a team of one registered nurse and one health care aide. Several clients need care at once, and the manager plans to hold a quality meeting later in the day.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She is accountable for her own practice, so she clarifies the conflicting instruction first): Every nurse is accountable for her own actions, even when following a colleague's instruction. The order and the reading conflict, so Dana should clarify with the RN and the prescriber before holding or giving the dose. Education level does not transfer accountability. A peer cannot settle the conflict, and telling the RN afterward comes too late."}, {"id": 2124, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At the quality meeting, Dana proposes bedside hand hygiene reminders. She has audit numbers showing 62 percent compliance and one client's story of an infection that extended her stay. A colleague says only the numbers matter. Which approach should Dana use?", "options": {"A": "Present the audit data and the client story together to show both scale and human impact", "B": "Show the audit data with staff quotes about workload so the numbers feel relevant", "C": "Ask the manager to present the proposal so the numbers carry more authority", "D": "Share the client story with a call for volunteers and add audit numbers later"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Substitutes staff opinion for the client story", "C": "Hands off her own proposal", "D": "Leads with emotion and delays the data"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C263", "cluster_text": "Dana, a practical nurse on a busy medical unit, has been asked to act as team leader for the day shift. She has worked three twelve hour shifts, slept poorly, and leads a team of one registered nurse and one health care aide. Several clients need care at once, and the manager plans to hold a quality meeting later in the day.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Present the audit data and the client story together to show both scale and human impact): Data show how big a problem is, and a story shows why it matters, so together they make a stronger case for change. Dana can present both and propose a clear action. Staff quotes about workload do not show client harm. Leading with a story and delaying numbers lacks scale. Passing the proposal to the manager gives up her role."}, {"id": 2125, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On an acute care unit, a nurse is told to keep every client's urinary catheter until discharge, as the unit has always done. A recent hospital guideline says to remove catheters as soon as they are no longer needed. The client is mobile and no longer needs strict output monitoring. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Leave it in and mention the guideline at the next staff meeting", "B": "Raise the guideline with the prescriber and RN and ask whether the catheter can be removed", "C": "Remove the catheter herself since the guideline says to remove it when unneeded", "D": "Follow the unit habit because experienced nurses know the practice works well in this setting"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Postpones action despite ongoing infection risk", "C": "Acts without an order", "D": "Defers to habit over current evidence"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Raise the guideline with the prescriber and RN and ask whether the catheter can be removed): A catheter that is no longer needed raises infection risk, and current guidelines favour early removal. The nurse should bring the guideline to the prescriber and RN and ask for removal to be ordered. Tradition is not a reason to keep a device. Removing it without an order bypasses the team, and waiting for a meeting leaves the risk in place."}, {"id": 2126, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the ward, a 70-year-old man collapses in the hallway without a pulse. Five staff arrive, including Lena, and all crowd around his chest. No one has called the code team or retrieved the defibrillator. What is the best action for the team?", "options": {"A": "Have one nurse start compressions while the rest observe and record the time", "B": "Wait for the most senior nurse to give instructions before anyone acts", "C": "Let everyone take turns doing compressions until the code team arrives with the equipment", "D": "Assign roles quickly so compressions, airway, equipment and the call for help are each covered"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaves most staff idle while key tasks are missed", "B": "Delays action until someone takes charge", "C": "Gives everyone the same task and leaves needs uncovered"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C259", "cluster_text": "Lena is a practical nurse who works on a surgical ward and also makes weekend home care visits. This week she is orienting to a new shift on the ward, and the manager has asked her to review the unit's emergency routines. A code team is available for the ward, and on weekends she sometimes sees a client who lives alone.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assign roles quickly so compressions, airway, equipment and the call for help are each covered): In a code, clear roles prevent duplication and gaps, so compressions, airway, equipment and the call for help each need an owner. Five people on compressions leaves no one calling or fetching the defibrillator. Waiting for the senior nurse wastes time when a pulseless client needs compressions now, and observers cannot cover the other tasks."}, {"id": 2127, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After the arrest, Lena is asked to complete her record of the event. Another nurse suggests writing that the ward was understaffed and that a colleague was slow to respond. The event lasted twenty minutes and several staff acted. What should Lena include in her entry?", "options": {"A": "Her view of why the response was delayed, with staffing levels, for managers to act on", "B": "A short note that the code was called and the client was transferred, nothing further", "C": "Only her own actions, leaving out what other team members did or said", "D": "A factual, timed account of what she saw, did and heard, without opinions or blame"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Mixes opinion and blame into the clinical record", "B": "Gives too little detail to reconstruct the event", "C": "Omits observations that others need for the full picture"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C259", "cluster_text": "Lena is a practical nurse who works on a surgical ward and also makes weekend home care visits. This week she is orienting to a new shift on the ward, and the manager has asked her to review the unit's emergency routines. A code team is available for the ward, and on weekends she sometimes sees a client who lives alone.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A factual, timed account of what she saw, did and heard, without opinions or blame): Documentation after an event should be objective, timed and factual, covering what the nurse saw, did and heard. Opinions about staffing or a colleague belong in an incident report or discussion with the manager, not the clinical record. A very short note leaves out facts needed later, and describing only her own actions leaves gaps in the account."}, {"id": 2128, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At the start of a shift on an unfamiliar unit, Lena is told that a client in room 6 has a changing level of consciousness. She does not know where the oxygen, suction or emergency cart are kept, and the unit is short staffed. How should she interpret this gap?", "options": {"A": "A task to complete after she finishes her first assessments and medication round", "B": "A gap the charge nurse will address if the client's condition worsens", "C": "A detail she can pick up during an event when staff point out equipment", "D": "A safety risk to close now by locating equipment and asking for a brief orientation"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Postpones orientation behind other tasks", "B": "Leaves the gap to someone else until an emergency", "C": "Postpones learning until an emergency happens"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C259", "cluster_text": "Lena is a practical nurse who works on a surgical ward and also makes weekend home care visits. This week she is orienting to a new shift on the ward, and the manager has asked her to review the unit's emergency routines. A code team is available for the ward, and on weekends she sometimes sees a client who lives alone.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A safety risk to close now by locating equipment and asking for a brief orientation): Not knowing where emergency equipment is kept is a safety risk, especially with a client whose consciousness is changing. Lena should find the oxygen, suction and cart now and ask for a brief orientation. Postponing it or leaving it to the charge nurse leaves the gap open. Learning during an event costs minutes."}, {"id": 2129, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a weekend home visit, Lena finds a client who lives alone slumped in a chair, pale and hard to wake, with slow shallow breathing. No one else is in the home. She wonders whether to take a full set of vital signs first. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Take a full set of vital signs, then call 9-1-1 if the readings are abnormal", "B": "Call 9-1-1 right away, then stay with the client and give what care she can", "C": "Place the client in a recovery position and ask a neighbour to call", "D": "Call the client's physician's office and wait for instructions before calling anyone else"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays help while gathering data", "C": "Leaves the call to someone else", "D": "Calls a non emergency contact for an emergency"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C259", "cluster_text": "Lena is a practical nurse who works on a surgical ward and also makes weekend home care visits. This week she is orienting to a new shift on the ward, and the manager has asked her to review the unit's emergency routines. A code team is available for the ward, and on weekends she sometimes sees a client who lives alone.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Call 9-1-1 right away, then stay with the client and give what care she can): A client who is hard to wake with slow shallow breathing is in an emergency, and an alone nurse calls 9-1-1 early. She can then stay, assess airway and breathing, and give care within her scope. Taking vital signs first delays help. A physician's office cannot respond to an emergency, and sending a neighbour to call leaves the client without the nurse's attention."}, {"id": 2130, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Abara asks the nurse to change the pouch for her at each visit because she is afraid to look at the stoma. The nurse has shown her the steps once and she cried. How should the nurse proceed with teaching her appliance care?", "options": {"A": "Show her the full routine again and then watch her do all the steps at once", "B": "Change the pouch at every visit for now and teach only when she asks to learn", "C": "Give her the manufacturer instructions and a phone number and review them next week", "D": "Teach in small steps, have her demonstrate each one, and confirm understanding with teach back"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Overloads her fearful learner with the whole routine", "B": "Takes over care and delays independence", "C": "Hands over written material without checking skill"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C256", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Abara, 72, was discharged home last week with a new colostomy after bowel surgery. She lives alone and is visited by a practical nurse from home care. She has lost weight, eats little, and has an upper endoscopy and a colonoscopy planned. Her daughter visits on weekends, and Mrs. Abara says she is frightened of managing the bag and anxious about leaving the house.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Teach in small steps, have her demonstrate each one, and confirm understanding with teach back): A fearful learner does best with short steps, hands on practice and teach back that confirms she can do each one. This builds confidence and independence. Changing the pouch every time keeps her dependent. Written instructions alone do not show whether she can perform the skill, and demonstrating the whole routine at once is likely to overwhelm her."}, {"id": 2131, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Abara has lost 5 kg in six weeks, her clothes hang loose, and she eats only a few bites because the smell of food makes her queasy. The nurse could offer oral nutrition supplements from the cupboard. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Tell her the weight loss is expected after surgery and recheck her weight next visit", "B": "Give the supplements twice daily and weigh her again in a month to see the effect", "C": "Complete a malnutrition screening, report the weight loss, and request a dietitian referral", "D": "Encourage larger meals with high fat foods and record her intake for the daughter"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Dismisses significant weight loss as expected", "B": "Starts supplements without assessment or referral", "D": "Pushes a food plan that worsens nausea"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C256", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Abara, 72, was discharged home last week with a new colostomy after bowel surgery. She lives alone and is visited by a practical nurse from home care. She has lost weight, eats little, and has an upper endoscopy and a colonoscopy planned. Her daughter visits on weekends, and Mrs. Abara says she is frightened of managing the bag and anxious about leaving the house.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Complete a malnutrition screening, report the weight loss, and request a dietitian referral): A loss of 5 kg in six weeks is significant and calls for screening, reporting and a dietitian referral so the cause and plan are properly assessed. Starting supplements without that step may miss a treatable cause. High fat meals can worsen nausea, and calling the loss normal delays care in a client who is already eating little."}, {"id": 2132, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Abara has started bowel preparation for her colonoscopy through the stoma. She has cramps and mild nausea and wants to stop drinking the solution. She is 72, takes a diuretic, and has had little intake this week. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Let her stop the solution once her output looks clear", "B": "Stop the preparation and withhold fluids until the cramping settles", "C": "Encourage slower sips of clear fluids and report ongoing vomiting", "D": "Give an antiemetic from unit stock and continue the preparation"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Stops preparation based on appearance without direction", "B": "Withholds fluids from a client at risk of dehydration", "D": "Gives a medication without an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C256", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Abara, 72, was discharged home last week with a new colostomy after bowel surgery. She lives alone and is visited by a practical nurse from home care. She has lost weight, eats little, and has an upper endoscopy and a colonoscopy planned. Her daughter visits on weekends, and Mrs. Abara says she is frightened of managing the bag and anxious about leaving the house.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Encourage slower sips of clear fluids and report ongoing vomiting): Mrs. Abara is older, on a diuretic and has poor intake, so dehydration is a real risk during bowel preparation. Slower sips and clear fluids help her complete the preparation, and the nurse reports persistent cramping or vomiting. Stopping based on appearance may leave the bowel unprepared. An antiemetic from stock needs an order. Withholding fluids adds to dehydration."}, {"id": 2133, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "After her upper endoscopy with throat spray, Mrs. Abara is awake, talking and hungry. She says her throat feels numb but she can swallow saliva. The nurse is considering offering the tea she asked for. What is the safest approach?", "options": {"A": "Offer the tea now because she is awake and can swallow her own saliva", "B": "Withhold oral intake until the gag reflex has returned, then start with small sips of water", "C": "Offer a soft meal rather than liquids, because food stimulates the gag reflex to return", "D": "Offer ice chips first since cold liquid is safer than warm drinks for a numb throat"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Equates alertness with a safe swallow", "C": "Uses food to test a swallow that is not yet protected", "D": "Believes cold items are safe while the throat is numb"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C256", "cluster_text": "Mrs. Abara, 72, was discharged home last week with a new colostomy after bowel surgery. She lives alone and is visited by a practical nurse from home care. She has lost weight, eats little, and has an upper endoscopy and a colonoscopy planned. Her daughter visits on weekends, and Mrs. Abara says she is frightened of managing the bag and anxious about leaving the house.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Withhold oral intake until the gag reflex has returned, then start with small sips of water): Throat spray numbs the protective gag reflex, so oral intake waits until the reflex returns to prevent aspiration. After that, small sips of water are a safe first trial. Being awake and managing saliva does not prove the swallow is protected. Ice chips carry the same aspiration risk, and food is a poor test of a numb throat."}, {"id": 2134, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Gerald reports queasiness for several hours after his chemotherapy infusion and looks away when the lunch tray arrives, saying the smell of the food is making it worse. He asks whether anything besides pills might help. Which comfort measure should the nurse offer?", "options": {"A": "Encourage him to eat favourite foods now so he builds strength before nausea returns", "B": "Offer small cool meals, fresh air, and mouth care before and after each meal he eats", "C": "Offer small warm meals with strong flavours so the tastes mask his nausea", "D": "Limit all fluids with meals and keep him lying flat after eating"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Pushes food during active nausea", "C": "Uses warm, strongly flavoured food that worsens queasiness", "D": "Advises lying flat and limiting fluids, which worsens nausea"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C257", "cluster_text": "Gerald, 67, is on a medical unit receiving chemotherapy and has depression that has worsened this week. His daughter says he is tired of everything. The practical nurse is assigned his care for the week. He has dry skin, feels chilled at night, and has said he would rather not bathe. Comfort and respect are the nursing priorities, and he has been sleeping poorly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Offer small cool meals, fresh air, and mouth care before and after each meal he eats): Nausea after chemotherapy eases with small, cool or room temperature meals, fresh air and mouth care, which reduce strong smells and tastes. Pushing favourite foods during nausea risks a food aversion. Warm strongly flavoured food makes nausea worse. Lying flat after eating raises the risk of reflux and vomiting."}, {"id": 2135, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Gerald scratches his forearms and back at night, and his skin is dry with fine scratch marks. He likes long hot baths because they warm him. There is no rash and his skin is intact. What is the best nursing action?", "options": {"A": "Use lukewarm baths, a scented moisturizer after bathing, and trim his nails weekly", "B": "Switch to daily hot showers and apply an unscented lotion once a week", "C": "Give him a sedating antihistamine from the ward stock at bedtime to settle his night itching", "D": "Use lukewarm baths, fragrance free moisturizer after bathing, and keep his nails short"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses a scented product that irritates dry skin", "B": "Keeps hot water and applies moisturizer too seldom", "C": "Gives a drug without an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C257", "cluster_text": "Gerald, 67, is on a medical unit receiving chemotherapy and has depression that has worsened this week. His daughter says he is tired of everything. The practical nurse is assigned his care for the week. He has dry skin, feels chilled at night, and has said he would rather not bathe. Comfort and respect are the nursing priorities, and he has been sleeping poorly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use lukewarm baths, fragrance free moisturizer after bathing, and keep his nails short): Dry itchy skin improves with lukewarm water, a fragrance free moisturizer applied after bathing and short nails to limit damage from scratching. A scented product irritates fragile skin. Hot showers and weekly lotion are too drying and too seldom. Giving an antihistamine from ward stock needs an order and falls outside scope."}, {"id": 2136, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Gerald says he feels chilled at night even under two blankets and asks for a heating pad to place on his feet. His skin is dry and fragile from chemotherapy, and he has reduced feeling in his feet. Which action best meets his need?", "options": {"A": "Use an electric blanket on its lowest setting and check his skin later", "B": "Place the heating pad on a low setting directly over his chilled feet and lower legs", "C": "Offer a hot drink and warm socks, plus a heated gel pack at ankles", "D": "Add warm layers, adjust the room temperature, and keep direct heat off his skin"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Applies continuous direct heat to skin with reduced feeling", "B": "Applies direct heat to numb, fragile skin", "C": "Adds a heated pack against skin that cannot feel burns"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C257", "cluster_text": "Gerald, 67, is on a medical unit receiving chemotherapy and has depression that has worsened this week. His daughter says he is tired of everything. The practical nurse is assigned his care for the week. He has dry skin, feels chilled at night, and has said he would rather not bathe. Comfort and respect are the nursing priorities, and he has been sleeping poorly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Add warm layers, adjust the room temperature, and keep direct heat off his skin): Gerald has reduced sensation and fragile skin, so direct heat could burn him without his noticing. Added layers, blankets and a comfortable room temperature meet his need safely. An electric blanket or heated pack applies direct heat, even on low. A heating pad on low is not safe for numb feet."}, {"id": 2137, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "This morning Gerald says he does not want to bathe and turns toward the wall. He has refused hygiene care for two days, eats little, and says nothing matters. The nurse is concerned about his mood. How should she interpret the refusal and respond?", "options": {"A": "It is a personal choice, so document the refusal and offer a bath with his daughter.", "B": "It is expected during chemotherapy, so a bed bath done quickly while he rests is best", "C": "It may signal depression, so respect the refusal, offer choices and notify the RN", "D": "It is a behavioural problem, so explain the unit expectations and insist he bathe today"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Records the refusal without noticing the pattern", "B": "Overrides his refusal and normalizes the change", "D": "Treats a mood sign as defiance"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C257", "cluster_text": "Gerald, 67, is on a medical unit receiving chemotherapy and has depression that has worsened this week. His daughter says he is tired of everything. The practical nurse is assigned his care for the week. He has dry skin, feels chilled at night, and has said he would rather not bathe. Comfort and respect are the nursing priorities, and he has been sleeping poorly.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It may signal depression, so respect the refusal, offer choices and notify the RN): Refusing hygiene for two days with poor intake and hopeless statements may signal worsening depression, so the nurse respects his refusal, offers choices and reports to the RN. Insisting on bathing violates his right to refuse and ignores the cause. Charting the refusal alone misses the pattern, and bathing him while he rests overrides his choice."}, {"id": 2138, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Standing at a station, a nurse overhears a colleague say to a First Nations client's daughter, You people always have so many relatives here. The daughter looks away and stops asking questions. The colleague laughs and says she meant it kindly. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Apologize on behalf of the unit to the daughter and avoid involving the colleague", "B": "Speak to the colleague privately later, but do not involve the manager or daughter", "C": "Explain to the daughter that the comment was unprofessional, then end the matter", "D": "Check in with the daughter and address the comment with the colleague and manager"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Apologizes but leaves the behaviour unaddressed", "B": "Handles it privately and leaves the daughter unsupported", "C": "Comments to the daughter but does not address the colleague"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Check in with the daughter and address the comment with the colleague and manager): A remark that stereotypes a family is a microaggression, and its impact matters more than its intent. The daughter stopped asking questions, which shows harm to trust. Checking in with her and addressing the comment with the colleague and manager supports cultural safety. Speaking only privately, or only to the daughter, leaves the pattern in place. Apologizing for the unit without follow through is not enough."}, {"id": 2139, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Agitation develops in a 78-year-old woman on day 1 after hip surgery. She pulls at her IV and oxygen tubing and calls the nurse by her late husband's name. Her temperature is 37.9 C and she was oriented yesterday. How should the nurse interpret this change and respond?", "options": {"A": "Expected post anesthesia confusion, so reassure her and recheck her orientation at the end of shift", "B": "Sundowning from dementia, so dim the lights, orient her gently, and reassess tomorrow morning", "C": "Fever related confusion, so give acetaminophen and monitor for a change before notifying anyone", "D": "Likely delirium, so report it, check for causes like pain, and use non restraint safety measures"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Normalizes an acute postoperative change and delays reporting", "B": "Attributes sudden confusion to dementia without any history of it", "C": "Treats the temperature and delays reporting"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Likely delirium, so report it, check for causes like pain, and use non restraint safety measures): A sudden change in orientation and attention the day after surgery points to postoperative delirium, which needs prompt reporting and a search for causes such as pain, retention or low oxygen. Safety measures that avoid restraints protect her lines without increasing agitation. Calling it expected anesthesia effect or sundowning ignores the acute onset, and treating only the low grade temperature delays escalation."}, {"id": 2140, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Alarm rises when a nurse finds an irregular pulse of 84 in a 74-year-old man admitted for pneumonia. His chart lists chronic atrial fibrillation, and his rate was 80 irregular yesterday. He feels fine, his blood pressure is stable, and his oxygen saturation is 95 percent. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "It matches his baseline, so record it and continue monitoring for changes in rate or symptoms", "B": "It signals a worsening rhythm, so recheck in an hour and call if it persists", "C": "It is acceptable since he feels well, so reduce pulse checks to once daily", "D": "It is likely caused by the pneumonia, so request medication to control the rate"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats a known rhythm as worsening and delays the call", "C": "Reduces monitoring during an acute illness without an order", "D": "Blames a cause without evidence of change"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It matches his baseline, so record it and continue monitoring for changes in rate or symptoms): His irregular pulse of 84 matches the chronic atrial fibrillation in his chart and yesterday's reading, and his other findings are stable. Comparing a finding with baseline prevents false alarms, and the nurse records it and keeps monitoring. Reducing pulse checks removes monitoring during acute illness, and calling it a worsening rhythm is not supported by his baseline. Requesting rate control without a change in status is premature."}, {"id": 2141, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "An 82-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease is due for her morning spironolactone. Her potassium result this morning is 5.4 mmol/L, her creatinine is elevated, and she says she feels weak and tired. What should the nurse understand before giving the dose?", "options": {"A": "Spironolactone spares potassium and kidney disease adds to this, so hold the dose and report", "B": "Her weakness is likely from anemia, so give the dose and recheck potassium next week", "C": "A potassium of 5.4 is within the usual range, so only a repeat sample is needed", "D": "Potassium of 5.4 is mildly high, so give the dose with extra fluids to dilute it"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Blames weakness on another cause and ignores the raised potassium", "C": "Misreads an elevated value as normal", "D": "Believes extra fluids can offset a potassium sparing drug"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Spironolactone spares potassium and kidney disease adds to this, so hold the dose and report): Spironolactone is a potassium sparing diuretic, and reduced kidney function makes potassium rise further. A value of 5.4 mmol/L is above the usual range, and her weakness adds concern, so the nurse holds the dose and reports. Giving the dose with extra fluids or blaming anemia leaves the raised potassium unaddressed. A repeat sample alone leaves the next dose unchecked."}, {"id": 2142, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "After a long resuscitation in the emergency department, a young nurse is crying in the break room and says she cannot stop thinking about the dead client's family. A colleague suggests telling the whole unit the details to comfort her. How should the nurse support her?", "options": {"A": "Tell her to go home and avoid thinking about the case until she feels ready", "B": "Listen, direct her to debriefing, and keep the details confidential", "C": "Encourage her to talk with the family at the bedside so both can grieve together", "D": "Reassure her that strong reactions fade quickly and return her to the assignment to stay busy"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Sends her home and dismisses her distress without support", "C": "Brings the family into a peer support need and risks sharing details", "D": "Minimizes her distress and skips formal support"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Listen, direct her to debriefing, and keep the details confidential): A peer in distress needs listening and access to formal support such as debriefing or an employee program. Client information stays confidential, so details are shared only with those who need them. Talking with the family at the bedside mixes peer support with client information, and returning her to work to stay busy minimizes real distress. Sending her home to avoid the case leaves her unsupported."}, {"id": 2143, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Preparing a 71-year-old man for transfer to home care, a hospital nurse reviews his chart. His warfarin dose was reduced and his diuretic was stopped this admission. The family says the pharmacy will sort it out. What should the nurse include in the handover?", "options": {"A": "A current medication list only, since home care will compare it with the community pharmacy record", "B": "A reconciled medication list highlighting the dose change and stopped drug, with reasons given", "C": "The full hospital chart copied for home care to review for any medication changes", "D": "A verbal note that medicines changed, with details to be confirmed by the family"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Omits the changes and assumes the new team will find them", "C": "Hands over unsorted records instead of a clear summary", "D": "Relies on family memory for critical changes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A reconciled medication list highlighting the dose change and stopped drug, with reasons given): Changes in a high risk drug like warfarin need to be named clearly, with the reason, so the receiving team does not continue the old plan. A reconciled list that highlights what changed and why prevents errors across settings. A current list alone hides the changes. Family memory is unreliable for dose details, and an unsorted chart leaves home care to search for them."}, {"id": 2144, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At a community mental health program, a practical nurse is meeting a 29-year-old woman for the first time. The woman wants to discuss her daily routine and her trouble with sleep. The nurse feels pressed to start problem solving immediately. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Explain the nurse's role, purpose and confidentiality first", "B": "Begin working on her sleep goals now, since she is ready and eager to talk", "C": "Assess her sleep pattern with a standard questionnaire first", "D": "Offer advice about sleep habits to build rapport quickly"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Skips orientation and jumps to the working phase", "C": "Starts assessment before setting the terms of the relationship", "D": "Gives advice before the relationship is oriented"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain the nurse's role, purpose and confidentiality first): The orientation phase comes first, and it covers the nurse's role, the purpose of meeting, confidentiality limits and the time frame. Doing this builds trust and safety before problem solving. Starting sleep goals right away skips the structure the relationship needs. Assessing with a questionnaire or giving sleep advice first also reverses the usual sequence of phases."}, {"id": 2145, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Ms. Pelletier, 76, lives with her daughter and has several bruises on both upper arms in different stages of healing. The practical nurse notices them during a routine home visit while the daughter waits in the next room. How should the nurse document the finding in the client's chart?", "options": {"A": "Chart that the bruising shows elder abuse by the daughter so the whole team acts promptly", "B": "Record measured bruise sites and sizes with the client's own words, naming no cause", "C": "Record that the client fell, using the daughter's explanation as the stated cause of injury", "D": "Leave bruising out of the chart until the physician confirms the cause of each new injury"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Draws a conclusion about the abuser instead of recording objective observations", "C": "Accepts a caregiver's account as fact and leaves out the client's own words", "D": "Delays documentation, so evidence is lost and reporting is held up"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Record measured bruise sites and sizes with the client's own words, naming no cause): Objective charting of measured findings and the client's own words protects her and keeps the record accurate for those who investigate. Naming the daughter as the abuser is a conclusion the nurse cannot prove. Delaying the entry or adopting the daughter's story loses or distorts the evidence. Chart what you see and hear, then report concerns."}, {"id": 2146, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Two days after a below-knee amputation, Mr. Tessier, 62, turns his head away whenever the dressing is changed and says the leg is just fine. The team is planning discharge teaching on stump care. What approach should the practical nurse use when contributing to the teaching plan?", "options": {"A": "Wait until discharge day, then teach stump care in one session with a demonstration", "B": "Give written stump care instructions and ask his family to reinforce the steps at home", "C": "Acknowledge his feelings, offer him control over when he looks, and pace teaching as he adjusts", "D": "Show a photo of a healed stump to encourage acceptance, then teach all steps at once"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Leaves teaching too late to match his readiness", "B": "Relies on handouts and family without addressing his readiness to learn", "D": "Forces confrontation with the loss and overloads him with steps"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acknowledge his feelings, offer him control over when he looks, and pace teaching as he adjusts): Denial after an amputation is a coping response, so the nurse acknowledges feelings and gives him choices to rebuild a sense of control. Forcing the issue with photos and a single large session usually deepens avoidance. Waiting until discharge day leaves a safety gap, and handouts alone ignore his readiness. Teach in small steps matched to his readiness."}, {"id": 2147, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Alvarez, 58, is six hours past a laparotomy and has a continuous epidural infusion. She says her feet feel heavy and she cannot lift her right leg off the bed. Her blood pressure and pain score are acceptable. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Reposition her onto the left side and elevate the legs to improve circulation to the feet", "B": "Turn the infusion pump off and wait for leg strength to return before calling the RN", "C": "Record leg heaviness as an expected epidural effect and recheck at the next scheduled round", "D": "Assess motor and sensory level, then report new leg weakness to the RN or anesthesia"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes a circulation problem and ignores possible spinal cord compression", "B": "Changes the infusion without an order, which is outside practical nurse scope", "C": "Treats a new motor block as normal and delays reporting a possible complication"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess motor and sensory level, then report new leg weakness to the RN or anesthesia): New weakness in one leg is not an expected epidural effect and may signal a hematoma or an excessive block, so the nurse quantifies the deficit and reports at once. Waiting for the next round delays time-sensitive treatment, stopping the pump independently exceeds her scope, and repositioning treats the wrong problem. Report any change in motor function quickly."}, {"id": 2148, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Outside the hospital, another neighbour stops Priya in the parking lot and asks whether Mr. Osei is on the palliative unit and how his illness is expected to progress. She says she wants to organize meals for the street. How should Priya respond?", "options": {"A": "Share nothing, suggest she ask Mr. Osei directly, and offer to pass on her kind wishes", "B": "Say that confidentiality rules prevent a reply and walk away without offering another option", "C": "Give general information that he is stable so the neighbour can plan meal deliveries", "D": "Confirm he is on the unit but politely decline to discuss his prognosis or treatment"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Protects privacy but ignores a respectful way to connect the neighbour with him", "C": "Releases partial details without his permission because the intent seems kind", "D": "Confirms his presence, which is itself protected health information"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C253", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gerald Osei, 58, has advanced lung cancer and is admitted to a hospital palliative care unit for symptom control. He is alert, understands his illness and makes his own decisions. Priya, a practical nurse on the unit, recognizes him as a neighbour from her street. He shares a two-bed room, and his daughter Abena visits every day after work.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Share nothing, suggest she ask Mr. Osei directly, and offer to pass on her kind wishes): Even confirming that a person is a client discloses private health information, and good intentions do not create permission. Priya can honour confidentiality while still being kind by directing the neighbour to Mr. Osei and offering to pass on the message. Sharing his status or stability breaches privacy, and a curt refusal misses the chance to respect his wishes."}, {"id": 2149, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "On day three the physician changes Mr. Osei's plan, adding a subcutaneous pump for pain control and reducing his oral doses. The night shift did not mention it to him, and he asks Priya why his pills were removed. What is the practical nurse's best response?", "options": {"A": "Explain that the pump gives steadier relief and say the physician will review pills later", "B": "Acknowledge the change, check with the RN, and ask the physician to explain", "C": "Wait until his daughter visits and explain the change to her so she can inform him", "D": "Document his question and mention the change to the day RN at end of shift"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Gives a partial explanation without verifying the reason for the change", "C": "Passes information through family instead of the capable client himself", "D": "Delays an answer he is entitled to receive now"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C253", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gerald Osei, 58, has advanced lung cancer and is admitted to a hospital palliative care unit for symptom control. He is alert, understands his illness and makes his own decisions. Priya, a practical nurse on the unit, recognizes him as a neighbour from her street. He shares a two-bed room, and his daughter Abena visits every day after work.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acknowledge the change, check with the RN, and ask the physician to explain): A capable client has a right to know about changes to his care, so Priya acknowledges the change and makes sure the physician explains the reasons and options. Giving a partial explanation guesses at the reasons. Informing the daughter first bypasses him, and waiting until the end of shift delays information he asked for now. Information flows to the client first."}, {"id": 2150, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "That evening Mr. Osei whispers to Priya that he has thought about ending his life and begs her not to tell the team, saying the chaplain already knows enough. He appears calm and has asked about his stored medications at home. What is Priya's best action?", "options": {"A": "Ask the chaplain to talk with him and report only if the chaplain finds risk", "B": "Explain she cannot keep this secret, stay nearby, and report to the RN right away", "C": "Reassure him that feelings like these are common at end of life and let him rest", "D": "Promise privacy, chart his wishes, and revisit the topic at the next shift change"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Hands the assessment of risk to a non-nursing role and delays reporting", "C": "Normalizes the statement and leaves a possible suicide risk unassessed", "D": "Agrees to secrecy that conflicts with the duty to protect safety"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C253", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gerald Osei, 58, has advanced lung cancer and is admitted to a hospital palliative care unit for symptom control. He is alert, understands his illness and makes his own decisions. Priya, a practical nurse on the unit, recognizes him as a neighbour from her street. He shares a two-bed room, and his daughter Abena visits every day after work.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain she cannot keep this secret, stay nearby, and report to the RN right away): A statement about ending one's life with a means in mind is a safety concern that overrides a request for secrecy, so Priya is honest that she must share it and stays with him until the RN assesses. Promising privacy sets up a broken trust and a delayed response. The chaplain cannot replace a nursing risk assessment, and simple reassurance ignores the risk. Secrecy cannot be promised when safety is at stake."}, {"id": 2151, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Dr. Hall plans to discuss palliative sedation with Mr. Osei, who is alert and understands his illness. His daughter Abena asks Priya to sign the consent form for him so he is not burdened by the decision. What should Priya tell Abena?", "options": {"A": "Mr. Osei decides while capable, so the physician will seek his consent directly", "B": "Priya should collect the signature herself, since consent is a nursing task before sedation", "C": "Abena may sign as next of kin, because family decide when a client is very ill", "D": "The ethics committee must approve the consent before Abena or Mr. Osei can proceed"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Assigns consent to the nurse instead of the treating physician", "C": "Substitutes the family for a client who still has capacity", "D": "Invents a committee approval step for a capable client's consent"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C253", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gerald Osei, 58, has advanced lung cancer and is admitted to a hospital palliative care unit for symptom control. He is alert, understands his illness and makes his own decisions. Priya, a practical nurse on the unit, recognizes him as a neighbour from her street. He shares a two-bed room, and his daughter Abena visits every day after work.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mr. Osei decides while capable, so the physician will seek his consent directly): Capacity, not illness severity, decides who consents, and Mr. Osei understands his situation, so the decision and the consent conversation are his. Family become substitute decision makers only if he loses capacity. The physician who proposes the treatment obtains consent, so the nurse does not collect it, and no committee approval is needed for a capable adult. Capable clients consent for themselves."}, {"id": 2152, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Osei shares a double room, and the roommate's visitors are present during morning bathing. Embarrassed, Mr. Osei asks Priya, the practical nurse, to hurry through his personal care so the visitors do not see him. How should Priya protect his dignity while planning his personal care with the team?", "options": {"A": "Delegate bathing to the UCP and ask her to complete it as rapidly as possible", "B": "Postpone the bath to the evening shift so the roommate's visitors do not see anything", "C": "Ask visitors to step out, draw the curtain, and expose only the area being washed", "D": "Bathe him quickly with the curtain open since the visitors are unlikely to look over"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Passes the task along without any plan for protecting his privacy", "B": "Delays hygiene care instead of using simple privacy measures available now", "D": "Treats privacy as optional and rushes care at the cost of dignity"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C253", "cluster_text": "Mr. Gerald Osei, 58, has advanced lung cancer and is admitted to a hospital palliative care unit for symptom control. He is alert, understands his illness and makes his own decisions. Priya, a practical nurse on the unit, recognizes him as a neighbour from her street. He shares a two-bed room, and his daughter Abena visits every day after work.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask visitors to step out, draw the curtain, and expose only the area being washed): Privacy during personal care is a client right, and simple measures such as asking visitors to leave, drawing the curtain and exposing only what is being washed protect it without delaying care. Rushing with the curtain open ignores the problem. Postponing the bath leaves him unwashed, and delegating without a privacy plan only moves the problem. Dignity measures belong in every care plan."}, {"id": 2153, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Jasmine, 28, has a personality disorder and tells her practical nurse that she trusts only him while the evening nurse is cruel. Two other staff members report hearing similar comparisons about them. What should the practical nurse do about this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Accept her trust and give her extra time as the one nurse she feels safe with", "B": "Report the pattern to the team and agree on one approach for all staff", "C": "Transfer her care to him alone so one nurse builds a consistent relationship", "D": "Tell her the evening nurse was wrong and ask her to speak to the manager"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Reinforces splitting by treating one nurse as special and others as bad", "C": "Gives one nurse special status and feeds the splitting", "D": "Validates the split by agreeing the other nurse is bad"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the pattern to the team and agree on one approach for all staff): Splitting divides staff into good and bad, and the most effective response is a consistent team approach with shared limits. Giving one nurse special status or care of her alone feeds the pattern. Agreeing that a colleague was wrong validates the split and sets staff against each other. Consistency across staff is the main intervention."}, {"id": 2154, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Ibrahim, 81, lives in a retirement home and has an oral temperature of 37.6 C, below the usual fever cut-off. He is more confused than yesterday, ate half his breakfast and has a new cough. His usual temperature runs near 36.2 C. How should the practical nurse interpret this finding?", "options": {"A": "As a possible early infection given the baseline change and confusion, needing RN report", "B": "As an expected finding of aging that needs no report if his vital signs stay stable", "C": "As a normal temperature because it is under the standard fever cut-off, so record only", "D": "As a measurement error from the oral route that should be repeated rectally before reporting"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Dismisses the finding as aging despite new symptoms", "C": "Ignores the individual baseline and compares only with a general cut-off", "D": "Blames technique and delays reporting while using an unnecessary route"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (As a possible early infection given the baseline change and confusion, needing RN report): Older adults often mount a blunted fever, so a rise of 1.4 C above his own baseline together with new confusion, cough and reduced intake suggests early infection and must be reported. A reading under 38 C is not automatically normal for him. Calling it aging or an oral error dismisses the whole picture. Interpret one finding against the whole client."}, {"id": 2155, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Marcus, 34, has type 1 diabetes and wears a continuous glucose monitor reading 4.2 mmol/L with a downward arrow. A community practical nurse visits and finds him shaky, sweaty and confused, with his fingerstick meter on the table. What should the nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Call his endocrinologist for advice before starting any testing or treatment", "B": "Recheck the sensor in 30 minutes and record the trend before taking any other action", "C": "Check a fingerstick glucose now and treat for hypoglycemia per protocol if low", "D": "Trust the sensor trend and have him lie down and rest for fifteen minutes"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Delays testing and treatment for a symptomatic client", "B": "Delays confirmation and treatment while symptoms are present", "D": "Relies on a lagging sensor while he is symptomatic"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check a fingerstick glucose now and treat for hypoglycemia per protocol if low): When symptoms do not match a sensor reading, or glucose is trending down, a fingerstick confirms the true value and treatment follows protocol. The sensor lags blood glucose and can differ from it. Calling for advice first or resting while symptoms continue delays care, and giving insulin to a symptomatic client with a falling reading is unsafe. Trust symptoms and confirm with a fingerstick."}, {"id": 2156, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Brodeur, 49, visits a primary care clinic worried about weight gain and says he weighs about 90 kg. His clothes feel tight, but he declines to step on the scale, saying the number does not matter. The practical nurse is taking his vital signs. What should the nurse do to obtain a usable BMI?", "options": {"A": "Record his reported weight as stated and measure his waist circumference instead", "B": "Measure weight on the scale and record BMI using the height from his old chart", "C": "Weigh him once without discussion, then record the BMI even if he objects", "D": "Explain why accuracy matters, then measure his height and weight consistently"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses a self reported weight that is often inaccurate", "B": "Uses an outdated height, which makes the BMI unreliable", "C": "Ignores his objection and skips explaining the purpose"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain why accuracy matters, then measure his height and weight consistently): An accurate BMI needs measured height and weight taken the same way each time, and explaining why accuracy helps his own plan respects his right to decide while encouraging cooperation. Self reported values are unreliable, and proceeding over his objection without explanation disrespects his choice. An old chart height may be wrong. Measure rather than guess."}, {"id": 2157, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "While covering a shift, Amara watches a colleague dress Mr. Singh's ulcer without cleaning her hands and reuse a contaminated scissors. The colleague is often praised by management, and Amara fears speaking up could harm her own job. What should Amara do?", "options": {"A": "Address the risk, then report it through the proper channel with her legal protection", "B": "Say nothing, since the colleague has a strong record and management would not agree", "C": "Wait until her yearly performance review and then raise concerns in writing with management", "D": "Speak privately to the colleague only and take no further action unless it happens again"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Stays silent because of fear and the colleague's reputation", "C": "Delays reporting a current infection risk to a distant review", "D": "Handles an ongoing risk informally without reporting it"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C254", "cluster_text": "Amara, a practical nurse with a community nursing agency, visits Mr. Singh, 70, who has newly diagnosed hypertension and a healing leg ulcer. His daughter Meera helps at home. Amara works with an RN case manager and follows both agency policies and her regulatory body's standards. The agency also employs other practical nurses and unregulated care providers who visit him.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Address the risk, then report it through the proper channel with her legal protection): Nurses have a professional duty to protect clients and to report unsafe practice, and reports made in good faith are protected, so the risk is addressed now and reported through the proper channel. A private talk alone leaves the system unaware. Silence because of the colleague's reputation, or waiting for a review, leaves Mr. Singh exposed. Duty to the client outweighs personal worry."}, {"id": 2158, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Dr. Moreau phones in an order for a specialized debridement technique that Amara has never been taught and the agency has not approved her to perform. He says he will take responsibility. Meera is pleased it will be done today. What is Amara's best action?", "options": {"A": "Perform it after reading the agency manual, since the order transfers responsibility to the physician", "B": "Perform it carefully once while the physician stays on the phone to guide each step", "C": "Ask Meera to hold the supplies and watch so the procedure is witnessed and safe", "D": "Decline the task, state her competence limits, and ask the RN case manager to arrange it"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Believes an order shifts accountability away from the nurse", "B": "Attempts a procedure beyond her competence under remote guidance", "C": "Uses a lay witness as a substitute for competence"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C254", "cluster_text": "Amara, a practical nurse with a community nursing agency, visits Mr. Singh, 70, who has newly diagnosed hypertension and a healing leg ulcer. His daughter Meera helps at home. Amara works with an RN case manager and follows both agency policies and her regulatory body's standards. The agency also employs other practical nurses and unregulated care providers who visit him.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline the task, state her competence limits, and ask the RN case manager to arrange it): An order does not make a nurse competent or authorized, and each nurse is accountable for practising only within her own education and employer approval. Amara declines, states her limits and enlists the RN to find a qualified person. Phone guidance, reading a manual or a lay witness cannot supply competence. Competence plus authority are both required."}, {"id": 2159, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Agency policy states that practical nurses may not pack deep wounds, although regulatory standards allow it for competent practical nurses. Mr. Singh's ulcer now needs packing, and Amara is competent. The RN case manager is reachable by phone. What should Amara do?", "options": {"A": "Follow the stricter agency policy and ask the RN case manager to arrange the packing", "B": "Pack the wound because regulatory standards permit it and they outrank agency policy", "C": "Ignore the policy this once because the wound will worsen before the RN arrives", "D": "Pack the wound and report afterward, since her competence makes the policy unenforceable for her"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats scope law and employer policy as interchangeable and ignores the stricter one", "C": "Breaks policy for convenience although a safe route exists", "D": "Assumes personal competence overrides an employer rule"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C254", "cluster_text": "Amara, a practical nurse with a community nursing agency, visits Mr. Singh, 70, who has newly diagnosed hypertension and a healing leg ulcer. His daughter Meera helps at home. Amara works with an RN case manager and follows both agency policies and her regulatory body's standards. The agency also employs other practical nurses and unregulated care providers who visit him.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Follow the stricter agency policy and ask the RN case manager to arrange the packing): Policy and law are different, and an employer may set limits narrower than legal scope that the nurse must follow while employed. Amara follows the policy and uses the RN to arrange packing, and she can raise the policy for review through proper channels. Claiming that standards outrank policy, or that competence cancels it, misreads the hierarchy. Follow the stricter limit."}, {"id": 2160, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Meera asks Amara to increase her father's blood pressure pill from one to two tablets on days his readings are high, saying the family pharmacist agrees. The prescription says once daily, and Amara has no new order. How should Amara respond to Meera?", "options": {"A": "Suggest Meera give the extra tablet on high days and tell the physician later", "B": "Allow Meera to adjust the dose herself while Amara documents what the family decided", "C": "Decline to change the dose, explain the order, and report the readings to the RN", "D": "Agree because the family pharmacist supports the plan and the extra tablet is a low dose"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Advises a dose change without an order and delays notifying the prescriber", "B": "Delegates dose changes to a lay caregiver", "D": "Lets a family request override the prescription"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C254", "cluster_text": "Amara, a practical nurse with a community nursing agency, visits Mr. Singh, 70, who has newly diagnosed hypertension and a healing leg ulcer. His daughter Meera helps at home. Amara works with an RN case manager and follows both agency policies and her regulatory body's standards. The agency also employs other practical nurses and unregulated care providers who visit him.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline to change the dose, explain the order, and report the readings to the RN): Only the prescriber can change a dose, and a family request does not create authority for the nurse or caregiver to change it. Amara explains the order and reports the high readings so the prescriber can decide. Agreeing, letting Meera adjust, or suggesting extra tablets all alter therapy without an order. Readings go to the prescriber, not into the dose."}, {"id": 2161, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "Mr. Singh's blood pressure is 158/94 on repeat readings, and his new hypertension diagnosis worries him. He asks Amara how to lower it, and she wonders whether she needs an RN order before she begins any teaching. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Give a printed pamphlet and avoid questions about his blood pressure to prevent overstepping", "B": "Limit instruction to medication only, leaving diet and activity advice to the physician or dietitian", "C": "Wait for an RN order before teaching, because teaching about a new diagnosis needs authorization", "D": "Teach about salt, activity, home monitoring and medication, as health teaching is within scope"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Avoids client questions out of fear of exceeding scope", "B": "Narrows teaching unnecessarily and leaves him without lifestyle guidance", "C": "Believes health teaching requires an order"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C254", "cluster_text": "Amara, a practical nurse with a community nursing agency, visits Mr. Singh, 70, who has newly diagnosed hypertension and a healing leg ulcer. His daughter Meera helps at home. Amara works with an RN case manager and follows both agency policies and her regulatory body's standards. The agency also employs other practical nurses and unregulated care providers who visit him.", "cluster_size": 5, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Teach about salt, activity, home monitoring and medication, as health teaching is within scope): Health teaching is part of practical nursing, and Amara can teach about lifestyle, home monitoring and medications within her competence without an order, while reporting readings and referring questions beyond her knowledge. Waiting for permission, restricting the content or avoiding questions leaves the client without help. Teach what you know and refer the rest."}, {"id": 2162, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A 45-year-old coworker, Dev, is admitted to the small hospital where the practical nurse works, with a new cancer diagnosis. Colleagues on other units ask the nurse how he is doing, saying they want to support him. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Give general updates such as stable condition because that is not detailed information", "B": "Share information only with colleagues caring for him, and suggest others ask Dev", "C": "Tell the team the diagnosis so they can send supportive cards and visit him", "D": "Discuss him only at the nurses' station where visitors cannot hear"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats any status update as harmless when it is still private information", "C": "Discloses a diagnosis without consent because the motive is supportive", "D": "Believes location rather than need to know makes a discussion acceptable"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Share information only with colleagues caring for him, and suggest others ask Dev): Being a colleague gives no right of access, and only staff directly involved in his care may see his information. Dev can decide what to share with friends at work. Even general updates disclose private details, and discussing him at the station still exposes him to people with no need to know. Access depends on role in care."}, {"id": 2163, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "At 0340 Mr. Lavoie wakes and says he has pressure in the centre of his chest that began twenty minutes ago and spreads to his left jaw. He rates it 6 out of 10. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Give his prn acetaminophen for the pain and reassess its effect in thirty minutes", "B": "Take his vital signs once and notify the RN only if his pressure drops", "C": "Stay with him, assess the pain and vital signs quickly, and notify the RN", "D": "Document the complaint and reassess at the next routine vital signs round in the morning"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats possible cardiac pain as routine pain and delays escalation", "B": "Sets a late reporting trigger for a possible cardiac event", "D": "Delays response to a possible cardiac event until routine checks"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C258", "cluster_text": "Mr. Henri Lavoie, 73, is admitted to a medical unit with heart failure and atrial fibrillation. He takes furosemide, digoxin and metoprolol. He lives with his wife, is alert and manages his own affairs. His baseline blood pressure is about 128/76. A practical nurse cares for him over several days, working with an RN, and his condition changes along the way.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stay with him, assess the pain and vital signs quickly, and notify the RN): New chest pressure with jaw radiation could be an acute coronary event, so the nurse stays, gathers a focused history and vital signs, and calls the RN right away. Giving analgesic and waiting treats it as routine pain. Waiting for a blood pressure drop before reporting delays time sensitive care, and waiting for morning rounds is slower still. Assess quickly and escalate."}, {"id": 2164, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Lavoie's chest discomfort has settled. During the morning check, the practical nurse counts a radial pulse of 76 per minute and irregular, then listens to the apical pulse for a full minute and counts 110. What does this difference indicate and require?", "options": {"A": "A deficit of 34, suggesting ineffective beats, which should be reported to the RN", "B": "A deficit of 34, so hold the next digoxin dose and report afterward", "C": "A deficit of 34, so recount the radial pulse for one minute and chart both", "D": "A pulse deficit of 34 that is expected with digoxin and needs no report"}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Holds a medication without an order before reporting", "C": "Records both rates but skips reporting a real finding", "D": "Attributes the deficit to a drug effect and skips reporting"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C258", "cluster_text": "Mr. Henri Lavoie, 73, is admitted to a medical unit with heart failure and atrial fibrillation. He takes furosemide, digoxin and metoprolol. He lives with his wife, is alert and manages his own affairs. His baseline blood pressure is about 128/76. A practical nurse cares for him over several days, working with an RN, and his condition changes along the way.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A deficit of 34, suggesting ineffective beats, which should be reported to the RN): An apical rate of 110 against a radial rate of 76 gives a pulse deficit of 34, meaning some beats do not generate a palpable pulse, which is common in atrial fibrillation and signals reduced cardiac output. The nurse reports it. Holding digoxin without an order is outside her authority, and charting both rates without reporting leaves the problem unaddressed. Compare apical and radial together."}, {"id": 2165, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Later, the monitor alarms with a new fast irregular rhythm at 150, and Mr. Lavoie reports lightheadedness. His blood pressure is 92/54, down from his usual 128/76. The RN is with another client. What is the best action for the practical nurse?", "options": {"A": "Lay him flat, raise his legs, and recheck his pressure in ten minutes before calling", "B": "Give an extra dose of his metoprolol to slow the rate while waiting for the RN", "C": "Apply oxygen and recheck his vital signs in fifteen minutes before notifying the RN", "D": "Stay with him, check airway, breathing and circulation, and call the RN urgently"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Positions him but delays calling for an unstable client", "B": "Adjusts medication independently for an unstable client", "C": "Delays reporting while an unstable client may be deteriorating"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C258", "cluster_text": "Mr. Henri Lavoie, 73, is admitted to a medical unit with heart failure and atrial fibrillation. He takes furosemide, digoxin and metoprolol. He lives with his wife, is alert and manages his own affairs. His baseline blood pressure is about 128/76. A practical nurse cares for him over several days, working with an RN, and his condition changes along the way.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stay with him, check airway, breathing and circulation, and call the RN urgently): A fast new rhythm with a fall in blood pressure and lightheadedness means he is unstable, so the nurse stays, checks the basics and gets help immediately. Giving extra metoprolol is outside her authority and could worsen hypotension. Repositioning or applying oxygen and then waiting to call delays treatment. Report an unstable client at once."}, {"id": 2166, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Before discharge, the practical nurse teaches Mr. Lavoie and his wife what to do if his chest pressure returns. His wife says she can drive him to the hospital faster than waiting for an ambulance. Which point should the nurse emphasize?", "options": {"A": "Have his wife drive while he rests, calling ahead only if he becomes pale or faint", "B": "Wait twenty minutes for the symptoms to ease, then phone the family doctor for advice", "C": "Call emergency services right away, since paramedics begin treatment on the way", "D": "Drive him to the closest emergency department, since leaving earlier saves heart muscle"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Avoids emergency services unless obvious signs of collapse appear", "B": "Waits and calls the wrong service, delaying care", "D": "Believes private transport is faster, losing early treatment and monitoring"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C258", "cluster_text": "Mr. Henri Lavoie, 73, is admitted to a medical unit with heart failure and atrial fibrillation. He takes furosemide, digoxin and metoprolol. He lives with his wife, is alert and manages his own affairs. His baseline blood pressure is about 128/76. A practical nurse cares for him over several days, working with an RN, and his condition changes along the way.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Call emergency services right away, since paramedics begin treatment on the way): Emergency services provide monitoring, early treatment and a defibrillator if he arrests, so calling them is safer than driving even if the trip looks short. Driving himself or having his wife drive gives up those advantages. Waiting to see whether symptoms ease or calling the family doctor wastes time. Teach to call first for suspected heart attack."}, {"id": 2167, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Chen, 33, tells the practical nurse at a community clinic that she is declining the influenza vaccine today because she heard it causes illness. She has no allergy or fever, and the dose is already drawn. How should the nurse respond?", "options": {"A": "Document refusal and end the conversation to avoid pressuring her about the decision", "B": "Give accurate information, answer concerns, and respect her decision", "C": "Offer to give half the vaccine dose as a compromise she may accept", "D": "Explain that most people tolerate it well and give it since the dose is drawn"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Ends the conversation without offering information", "C": "Offers an unordered partial dose as pressure toward consent", "D": "Overrides her refusal because the dose is already prepared"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give accurate information, answer concerns, and respect her decision): A capable adult has the right to refuse treatment, and the nurse supports an informed choice by correcting misinformation respectfully. Offering a partial dose or giving the vaccine because it is drawn overrides her decision. Closing the conversation without information misses an educational opportunity. Informed refusal is respected and documented."}, {"id": 2168, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Jordan privately asks for a urine pregnancy test and a drug screen, and asks the nurse not to tell her parent. She understands the tests and what the results could mean. What should the practical nurse do before collecting the specimens?", "options": {"A": "Collect both specimens and tell her parent the results only if they are positive", "B": "Refuse testing until her parent arrives and provides written consent for each test", "C": "Explain the tests, get Jordan's own consent, and keep results private within safety limits", "D": "Collect the pregnancy test now and defer the drug screen until the nurse practitioner reviews it"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Breaches confidentiality for a capable adolescent without her consent", "B": "Makes parental consent a barrier despite her demonstrated understanding", "D": "Delays part of testing and skips consent for the collected test"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C260", "cluster_text": "Jordan, 16, attends a community clinic alone, reporting aching bones and joint stiffness for three weeks. She lives with a parent, attends school and plays hockey. The practical nurse works with a nurse practitioner who orders blood and urine tests. Over the following days, Jordan has more tests and follow-up teaching, and the practical nurse supports her through each step.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain the tests, get Jordan's own consent, and keep results private within safety limits): A capable adolescent who understands the tests and consequences can consent for herself, and her results are protected like any adult's, with limits only where safety requires disclosure. The nurse explains the tests and confirms her consent. Reporting to the parent or insisting on parental consent breaches privacy, and collecting a specimen without explanation ignores consent. Capacity, not age alone, guides consent."}, {"id": 2169, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Jordan's results return with an ESR of 48 mm/h and a CRP of 32 mg/L, both raised. She asks the practical nurse whether this confirms arthritis, and the nurse practitioner has not yet interpreted the results with her. What should the practical nurse say?", "options": {"A": "These show inflammation somewhere in the body, and the nurse practitioner will explain what it means", "B": "They point to a bone problem, so she should avoid hockey until further notice", "C": "They are borderline, so repeat them in a month before discussing anything with her", "D": "They confirm an inflammatory arthritis, so she should begin joint protection exercises now"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Names a cause and gives activity advice without a diagnosis", "C": "Dismisses raised values and withholds an honest explanation", "D": "Treats nonspecific markers as a diagnosis"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C260", "cluster_text": "Jordan, 16, attends a community clinic alone, reporting aching bones and joint stiffness for three weeks. She lives with a parent, attends school and plays hockey. The practical nurse works with a nurse practitioner who orders blood and urine tests. Over the following days, Jordan has more tests and follow-up teaching, and the practical nurse supports her through each step.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (These show inflammation somewhere in the body, and the nurse practitioner will explain what it means): ESR and CRP rise with many conditions, so a raised value shows inflammation without naming its cause, and diagnosis belongs to the nurse practitioner. The nurse explains this honestly and avoids guessing. Calling it arthritis, a bone problem or borderline gives information she cannot support. Inflammatory markers are not diagnoses."}, {"id": 2170, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Three days later Jordan has tingling around her mouth and muscle twitching in her hands. Her serum calcium is 1.9 mmol/L, below the normal range, and a nurse notes a positive Chvostek sign. What should the practical nurse do first?", "options": {"A": "Report promptly to the RN or nurse practitioner and monitor for tetany and airway changes", "B": "Have her breathe into a paper bag for relief and recheck symptoms in thirty minutes", "C": "Draw a repeat calcium level and report the result when it returns", "D": "Reassure her that tingling is anxiety, encourage slow breathing, and recheck calcium in a week"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Treats calcium symptoms as hyperventilation and delays reporting", "C": "Delays reporting a symptomatic low calcium until a repeat result", "D": "Dismisses symptoms of low calcium as anxiety"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C260", "cluster_text": "Jordan, 16, attends a community clinic alone, reporting aching bones and joint stiffness for three weeks. She lives with a parent, attends school and plays hockey. The practical nurse works with a nurse practitioner who orders blood and urine tests. Over the following days, Jordan has more tests and follow-up teaching, and the practical nurse supports her through each step.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report promptly to the RN or nurse practitioner and monitor for tetany and airway changes): Tingling, twitching and a positive Chvostek sign with a low calcium indicate hypocalcemia that can progress to tetany or laryngospasm, so the nurse reports promptly and watches the airway. Breathing into a bag treats the wrong problem. Waiting for a repeat level delays escalation, and calling it anxiety misses a true deficit. Symptomatic electrolyte changes need prompt reporting."}, {"id": 2171, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "The team orders a bone scan for Jordan to look for the cause of her bone pain. She is anxious about radiation and asks whether she will be a danger to her friends and family afterward. How should the practical nurse teach her about the test?", "options": {"A": "Explain the small dose, advise fluids and voiding, and answer her questions", "B": "Advise her to stay away from friends and family for a week after the scan", "C": "Tell her to delay the scan until she has spoken with a radiation specialist", "D": "Advise her to hold her urine for several hours so the tracer is absorbed"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Gives exaggerated isolation advice that increases fear", "C": "Postpones an ordered test and ignores her need for teaching now", "D": "Gives wrong advice since fluids and frequent voiding clear the tracer"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C260", "cluster_text": "Jordan, 16, attends a community clinic alone, reporting aching bones and joint stiffness for three weeks. She lives with a parent, attends school and plays hockey. The practical nurse works with a nurse practitioner who orders blood and urine tests. Over the following days, Jordan has more tests and follow-up teaching, and the practical nurse supports her through each step.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain the small dose, advise fluids and voiding, and answer her questions): Teaching should cover the small dose, why fluids and frequent voiding help clear the tracer, and honest answers to her fears, since the exposure is low and brief precautions are simple. A week of isolation is unnecessary. Delaying the scan leaves her anxious and untaught, and holding urine keeps the tracer in the body longer. Honest, specific teaching eases anxiety."}, {"id": 2172, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Wong brings her 2-year-old son to a well-child visit and asks how much fluoride toothpaste to use when brushing his teeth. She currently uses a large strip and he swallows most of it. What should the practical nurse advise?", "options": {"A": "No toothpaste until he can spit, using only water until he is three years old", "B": "A full strip of toothpaste covering the bristles so the fluoride protects every surface", "C": "A smear about the size of a grain of rice, with a parent brushing twice daily", "D": "A pea-sized amount on a soft brush, with the toddler brushing independently each morning"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Withholds fluoride that protects young teeth", "B": "Overuses toothpaste, risking fluorosis", "D": "Uses the amount recommended for older children and lets him brush alone"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A smear about the size of a grain of rice, with a parent brushing twice daily): For toddlers a rice-grain smear gives the benefit of fluoride while limiting swallowed amounts, and a parent should do the brushing. A pea-sized amount suits older children. A full strip increases the risk of fluorosis, and using no toothpaste loses protection. Match the amount to age."}, {"id": 2173, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "The laboratory phones the unit about a critical serum potassium of 6.8 mmol/L for Mr. Dubois, 67, who takes an ACE inhibitor and has kidney disease. The practical nurse answers the call. What should the nurse do while receiving this result?", "options": {"A": "Record the value, read it back to confirm, and report it to the RN at once", "B": "Hold his ACE inhibitor dose first and report the result when the RN returns from break", "C": "Write the number down, then enter it in the chart before reporting at the next round", "D": "Ask the laboratory to fax the result and report it once the printout arrives"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Changes medication without authority and delays escalation", "C": "Delays reporting a critical value", "D": "Waits for paperwork instead of reporting immediately"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Record the value, read it back to confirm, and report it to the RN at once): Critical results need an accurate record, a read-back to avoid transcription errors, and immediate communication to the RN because severe hyperkalemia can cause dangerous arrhythmias. Charting first or waiting for a fax delays care. Holding a drug is a decision for the RN or prescriber. Read back, then report."}, {"id": 2174, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Larocque, 79, wears hearing aids but left them at home. At the clinic she answers questions oddly and nods often. The practical nurse needs an accurate health history, and her niece offers to speak for her. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Speak louder, stand beside her chair, and let the niece answer the questions for her", "B": "Use short written yes or no questions only, and skip open-ended interview questions", "C": "Reduce noise, face her, speak slowly in a lower pitch, and ask her to restate answers", "D": "Ask the niece to complete the interview and confirm the answers with Mrs. Larocque later"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Shouts and stands out of sight, which distorts speech and blocks lip reading", "B": "Limits assessment to closed questions and loses detail", "D": "Replaces the client's voice with a relative's"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduce noise, face her, speak slowly in a lower pitch, and ask her to restate answers): Facing the client, reducing background noise and speaking slowly in a lower pitch help most, and asking her to restate answers confirms understanding. Shouting raises pitch and distorts words, and standing beside her removes visual cues. Written yes or no questions limit history, and relying on the niece sidelines the client. Communicate with her first."}, {"id": 2175, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Fraser, 55, has hypertension and drinks a glass of red wine every evening because a friend told him it protects the heart. He asks the clinic practical nurse what the safe limits are for alcohol. What should the nurse tell him?", "options": {"A": "One drink daily protects the heart, so he may continue while monitoring blood pressure", "B": "Less alcohol is better for blood pressure and heart health, and no amount is considered protective", "C": "Alcohol affects blood pressure only when drinking more than four drinks in one sitting", "D": "Wine is safer than spirits, so switching to red wine only is the best option"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Repeats the myth that wine protects the heart", "C": "Thinks only heavy episodes raise blood pressure", "D": "Believes the type of alcohol changes the risk"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Less alcohol is better for blood pressure and heart health, and no amount is considered protective): Current guidance states that less is better and that no amount of alcohol has proven protective benefit, and alcohol can raise blood pressure. The nurse counsels reduction. Claiming wine protects the heart, that type matters or that only binges count repeats common myths. Teach that lower intake means lower risk."}, {"id": 2176, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Kowalski, 76, has stable heart failure with no chest pain or weight gain. At rehabilitation she says her daughter told her to stay in bed because she tires easily, and she now struggles on stairs. What activity advice fits her situation?", "options": {"A": "Gradual, supervised activity with rest breaks, adjusted to symptoms", "B": "Short walks at home when she feels energetic, without monitoring of her symptoms", "C": "Rest in bed until her fatigue resolves, with short transfers needed for bathing", "D": "Moderate resistance training with heavy weights twice weekly to rebuild leg strength quickly"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Removes supervision and symptom monitoring that make activity safe", "C": "Prescribes bed rest, which causes deconditioning", "D": "Recommends heavy resistance work that stable heart failure may not tolerate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gradual, supervised activity with rest breaks, adjusted to symptoms): Stable heart failure benefits from progressive, supervised, low intensity exercise with rest breaks, because inactivity worsens weakness and function. Bed rest causes deconditioning, heavy resistance training risks decompensation, and unmonitored activity lacks safety checks. Advise graded activity rather than none or too much."}, {"id": 2177, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Haddad, 62, receives hemodialysis three times weekly. Today his pre-dialysis weight is 74.8 kg, and his post-dialysis weight from the last session was 72.0 kg. The practical nurse is judging how much fluid he gained. Which comparison is most useful?", "options": {"A": "Today's pre-dialysis weight against his dry weight set at the clinic last year", "B": "Today's pre-dialysis weight against the average of his last three pre-dialysis weights recorded", "C": "Today's pre-dialysis weight against his last post-dialysis weight, showing fluid gained between sessions", "D": "Today's pre-dialysis weight against his post-dialysis weight from two sessions ago"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses an outdated target weight instead of the last session", "B": "Averages earlier gains and loses the interdialytic gain", "D": "Uses a weight from the wrong session and overstates the gain"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Today's pre-dialysis weight against his last post-dialysis weight, showing fluid gained between sessions): The gain between the last post-dialysis weight and today's pre-dialysis weight, here 2.8 kg, reflects fluid accumulated between sessions. An old target weight, an earlier session or an average cannot show it. Compare like with like, measured the same way."}, {"id": 2178, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "During a clinical placement, a practical nurse sees a 23-year-old nursing student give a medication without checking the client's identification band. The client's name differs from the label on the dose. The client is unharmed. What should the practical nurse do now?", "options": {"A": "Advise the student privately to be careful and take no further action if no harm occurred", "B": "Ignore it because students are still learning and the supervising instructor will notice errors", "C": "Wait to see whether the student repeats the error before documenting a formal concern", "D": "Report to the instructor or charge nurse, because unsafe practice is reportable even without harm"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats lack of harm as a reason not to report", "B": "Assumes someone else will catch the error", "C": "Delays reporting until a pattern emerges"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report to the instructor or charge nurse, because unsafe practice is reportable even without harm): Giving a drug to a client whose identity was not checked, and whose name did not match the label, is an unsafe practice and a near miss, and nurses must report it so the client and the student are protected. A private word alone leaves the system unaware. Assuming the instructor will notice or waiting for repetition is unsafe. Report errors even without harm."}, {"id": 2179, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Ahmadi, 68, has lost 4 cm of height over ten years and has never had a fracture. She asks the practical nurse whether bone density testing is only needed after she breaks a bone. What should the nurse explain?", "options": {"A": "Testing is advised after age seventy five, so she should wait several more years", "B": "Testing is advised based on risk factors like age and height loss, before any fracture", "C": "Testing is needed if a parent had a hip fracture, which she does not report", "D": "Testing is only worthwhile after a fracture shows that the bones have already weakened"}, "level": "recall", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Sets a late age cutoff and ignores her current risk factors", "C": "Limits testing to family history and ignores height loss", "D": "Believes screening waits for a fracture"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Testing is advised based on risk factors like age and height loss, before any fracture): Osteoporosis often causes no symptoms until a fracture, so screening is recommended according to risk, and height loss is a warning sign. Waiting for a break or a later age misses the chance for prevention. Family history is one risk factor among several, and treatment can reduce fracture risk. Screen on risk, not on injury."}, {"id": 2180, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On the postnatal unit, a mother asks the practical nurse how to give her 2-day-old baby his first bath. His umbilical cord stump is still attached, and his temperature is 36.8 C. She plans to use a baby tub tonight. What teaching should the nurse give?", "options": {"A": "Bathe him in a tub with mild soap daily and cover the cord with wet cloth", "B": "Give a sponge bath, uncover one area at a time, and keep the cord dry", "C": "Place him in a warm tub up to his shoulders and wipe the cord with water", "D": "Wait for the cord to fall off before any washing and clean only the diaper area"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Keeps the cord moist, which delays drying", "C": "Immerses the baby before cord separation, risking infection and chilling", "D": "Delays all hygiene and risks skin problems"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give a sponge bath, uncover one area at a time, and keep the cord dry): Until the cord separates, sponge bathing keeps the stump dry, and uncovering one area at a time limits heat loss. Tub baths and wet coverings keep the cord moist and slow healing. Skipping washing entirely leaves skin folds unclean. Keep the cord dry and the baby warm."}, {"id": 2181, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Beaulieu, 66, has rheumatoid arthritis with weak, painful hand grip. She struggles with small buttons and zippers on her clothing, and mornings are worst. The practical nurse and occupational therapist are planning dressing aids. Which choice best supports her independence?", "options": {"A": "Have her wait until afternoon when her hands loosen, then dress herself with small buttons", "B": "Use adapted aids such as a button hook and zipper pull, plus clothing with larger fasteners", "C": "Keep her usual clothing with buttons and zippers and have staff dress her each morning", "D": "Use a long handled reacher to fasten her buttons and zippers from a distance"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Relies on timing alone and keeps fasteners her grip cannot manage", "C": "Takes over a task she can do with help, reducing independence", "D": "Chooses a device that does not fit the task of fastening"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use adapted aids such as a button hook and zipper pull, plus clothing with larger fasteners): Adaptive aids and easier fasteners let her dress herself, preserve independence and spare painful joints. Having staff dress her removes her control, and waiting for better hands in the afternoon still leaves small buttons she cannot manage. A reacher does not fasten buttons or zippers. Adapt the task rather than the client."}, {"id": 2182, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "An Indigenous elder is dying on a hospital unit, and about fifteen family members want to gather in his room for a ceremony that includes smudging. The visiting policy allows two visitors at a time. The practical nurse is helping the team plan. What best supports culturally safe care?", "options": {"A": "Work with the team, an Indigenous liaison and the family to arrange a suitable space", "B": "Suggest the family hold the gathering in the hospital cafeteria or chapel on another day", "C": "Allow the ceremony in his room immediately and notify the unit manager afterward", "D": "Explain the two-visitor policy and offer a video call for the remaining relatives to join"}, "level": "application", "approach": "guideline_mdt_prognosis", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Moves an end-of-life ceremony away from the dying person", "C": "Bypasses safety processes such as fire and ventilation checks", "D": "Applies the rule rigidly without exploring options"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Work with the team, an Indigenous liaison and the family to arrange a suitable space): Cultural safety means collaborating with the family and liaison to meet important needs within safety limits, such as using a suitable room and handling smoke detectors. Quoting the visitor limit ignores the ceremony's importance. Acting without notice risks safety, and relocating the event denies him the presence of family. Problem solve with the family."}, {"id": 2183, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A new graduate practical nurse on a pediatric unit finds a 4-year-old with a respiratory rate of 48, grunting, and an oxygen saturation of 89 percent. The charge RN is on a break, and the nurse is unsure of her role. What should she do?", "options": {"A": "Document the findings and continue the routine rounds until the charge RN returns", "B": "Manage the child alone using her assessment skills, since pediatric care is within her scope", "C": "Stay with the child, apply oxygen per protocol, and call the RN or rapid response now", "D": "Begin the child's prescribed nebulizer, reassess in thirty minutes, and tell the RN later"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Documents without acting on a deteriorating child", "B": "Assumes scope allows independent management of an unstable child", "D": "Delays reporting while treating an unstable child alone"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stay with the child, apply oxygen per protocol, and call the RN or rapid response now): A grunting child with a low saturation and fast breathing is unstable, so the nurse stays, applies oxygen per protocol and calls for help immediately. Managing independently or delaying reports exceeds her role in an unstable situation. Documenting and continuing rounds leaves the child at risk. Unstable children need immediate escalation."}, {"id": 2184, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In a pediatric clinic, a mother asks to give her 2-year-old the prescribed amoxicillin dose from the oral syringe herself. The practical nurse has checked the order and drawn up 5 mL of the suspension. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Decline her request because parents may not be involved in medication administration in clinics", "B": "Let her give it with the syringe plunged quickly to the back of the throat", "C": "Mix the dose into a full bottle of milk so the child drinks it", "D": "Involve the parent by teaching the technique, then supervise her giving it slowly into the cheek"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Excludes the parent unnecessarily", "B": "Teaches a technique that risks choking and aspiration", "C": "Risks an incomplete dose if the child does not finish the milk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Involve the parent by teaching the technique, then supervise her giving it slowly into the cheek): Involving parents builds skills they will use at home, and teaching then supervising keeps the dose safe, with the syringe aimed at the cheek to prevent choking. Refusing excludes her without reason. Squirting toward the throat risks choking, and hiding the dose in a full bottle risks an incomplete dose. Teach, observe, then document."}, {"id": 2185, "domain": "professional_ethical_and_legal_practice", "question": "A 91-year-old resident in long-term care sustains a hip injury during a transfer by an unregulated care provider to whom a practical nurse delegated. The nurse says the care provider alone is accountable. Which statement about accountability for this injury is correct?", "options": {"A": "The nurse answers for delegating and supervising, and the provider for their actions", "B": "Only the care provider is accountable since the provider physically carried out the transfer", "C": "No one is accountable if the nurse gave verbal delegation that followed facility policy", "D": "Only the facility is accountable because it employs both staff and set the policy"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Ignores the nurse's accountability for delegation decisions", "C": "Believes policy compliance removes accountability", "D": "Shifts all accountability to the employer"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The nurse answers for delegating and supervising, and the provider for their actions): Delegation does not transfer accountability, so the nurse answers for choosing the right task, person and supervision, while the provider answers for performing the task. Blaming only the provider or the facility misstates the shared responsibility, and following policy does not erase it. Accountability stays with the delegator."}, {"id": 2186, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mr. Gagnon, 88, in long-term care has dysphagia and a new order for an extended-release metformin tablet. The pharmacy label says do not crush, and staff usually crush all tablets in applesauce. What should the practical nurse do about this order?", "options": {"A": "Omit the tablet and document that he cannot swallow it until the next rounds", "B": "Give the extended-release tablet whole with thickened fluids and watch for choking closely", "C": "Crush the tablet in applesauce since that is the usual way medications are given", "D": "Hold the tablet and ask the pharmacist about a swallowable form"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Omits a medication without consulting anyone", "B": "Gives a tablet that he may not swallow safely", "C": "Crushes an extended-release product and risks a dose dump"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the tablet and ask the pharmacist about a swallowable form): Crushing extended-release tablets destroys the release mechanism and may cause a toxic burst, and giving it whole to a client with dysphagia risks choking, so the nurse holds it and consults for a suitable form. Following routine habit is unsafe. Omitting the drug without telling anyone leaves a gap in therapy. Consult before altering a dose form."}, {"id": 2187, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "On a medical unit, an infusion pump for Mr. Pham, 54, sounds an occlusion alarm during an intravenous antibiotic. The practical nurse sees the line kinked beneath his arm and the site slightly swollen and cool. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Raise his arm on a pillow and leave the infusion running while monitoring the site", "B": "Flush the line with saline to clear the occlusion and then resume the infusion", "C": "Stop the infusion, assess the site, and report the suspected infiltration to the RN", "D": "Silence the alarm, straighten the line, and restart the infusion at the same rate"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Continues the infusion while the site worsens", "B": "Flushes into a possibly infiltrated site", "D": "Overrides an alarm and restarts despite signs of infiltration"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the infusion, assess the site, and report the suspected infiltration to the RN): A swollen, cool site suggests the catheter is no longer in the vein, so the infusion must stop and the site must be assessed and reported. The kink may explain the alarm but does not explain the site changes. Restarting or flushing pushes fluid into tissue. Stop first when infiltration is suspected."}, {"id": 2188, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 3-year-old, Eli, is two hours after surgery and whimpers when moved, though he stops when a cartoon plays. His FLACC score is 6, and his mother says he is just distracted. The practical nurse has a prescribed acetaminophen dose available. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Continue distraction and withhold analgesia, since his cries stop whenever the cartoon plays", "B": "Wait for him to ask for pain medication using words before giving any analgesic", "C": "Give the prescribed analgesic and add holding and distraction", "D": "Give distraction first and reassess in two hours before considering any medication"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Treats distraction as proof of no pain", "B": "Requires verbal self-report from a toddler", "D": "Delays analgesia for a moderate pain score"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give the prescribed analgesic and add holding and distraction): A FLACC score of 6 indicates moderate pain, and distraction masks pain without treating it, so he needs the prescribed analgesic along with comfort measures. Withholding it or waiting for him to ask fails a child who cannot report. A two-hour delay prolongs distress. Combine medication with non-drug comfort."}, {"id": 2189, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 35-week infant weighing 2.3 kg is stable in the nursery with a temperature of 36.7 C. His mother asks to hold him skin to skin, but a colleague suggests waiting until he reaches 2.5 kg. What should the practical nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Support skin-to-skin contact now while monitoring his temperature and breathing", "B": "Offer clothed holding in a blanket instead, since skin contact lowers an infant's temperature", "C": "Allow short five-minute holds twice daily until the physician writes a specific order", "D": "Wait until he reaches the higher weight, since contact before then tires him"}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Misbelieves skin contact cools the infant", "C": "Restricts contact without a clinical reason", "D": "Applies a weight rule that is not required for stable infants"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Support skin-to-skin contact now while monitoring his temperature and breathing): Skin-to-skin contact helps stable late preterm infants regulate temperature, breathing and feeding, and the nurse monitors him during contact. A weight threshold, fixed brief holds or clothed holding add restrictions without evidence. Support kangaroo care while watching the infant."}, {"id": 2190, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Ms. Reyes, 70, arrives in the recovery area shivering after abdominal surgery, with a temperature of 35.2 C. A colleague suggests removing her blankets to keep the field sterile. The practical nurse is helping with her care. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Offer a warm drink and recheck her temperature in two hours", "B": "Apply warmed blankets or forced-air warming and recheck her temperature regularly", "C": "Remove the blankets so the incision stays sterile and give her warmed intravenous fluids", "D": "Increase the room temperature by two degrees and recheck her temperature after one hour"}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Offers oral intake to a postoperative client and delays active warming", "C": "Confuses a covered incision with the sterile field", "D": "Relies on room temperature and leaves heat loss largely unaddressed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Apply warmed blankets or forced-air warming and recheck her temperature regularly): Hypothermia after surgery increases bleeding, infection and cardiac stress, and warm blankets or forced air warming restore temperature without touching the covered dressing. Removing blankets or relying on a warm drink or a warmer room is too slow. Warm the client actively and recheck."}, {"id": 2191, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Mrs. Dupuis, 85, went home with four new medications and a changed warfarin dose after verbal teaching only. She lives alone with poor vision. Two days later her son finds her confused and bruised after taking double doses. Which gap in transition care most likely caused this?", "options": {"A": "Her warfarin teaching should have come from the pharmacist, so nursing teaching was unnecessary", "B": "Her son should have been given the medication list but she needed no teaching", "C": "Teaching was not reinforced with large-print instructions, a medication list and a follow-up check", "D": "The warfarin dose should have been left unchanged until the clinic visit the following month"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Blames the wrong discipline instead of the teaching process", "B": "Excludes the client from teaching and ignores her poor vision", "D": "Blames the dose change instead of the teaching method"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Teaching was not reinforced with large-print instructions, a medication list and a follow-up check): Older adults with poor vision need teaching in several forms, such as large print, a clear list and a follow up call, so verbal instructions alone left a predictable gap. Leaving her out of teaching, blaming another discipline or blaming the dose change misreads the cause. Strong transitions use repeated, multiple format teaching."}, {"id": 2192, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At 1000, Mr. Roy, 47, has not voided for eight hours after surgery and rates lower abdominal discomfort at 5 out of 10. His abdomen is slightly distended above the pubic bone. Dana's schedule shows a voiding check at 1200. What should Dana do?", "options": {"A": "Apply a warm pack to his lower abdomen and wait for spontaneous voiding later", "B": "Insert a urinary catheter immediately to relieve the discomfort and measure residual urine", "C": "Offer extra oral fluids and recheck him at the scheduled 1200 voiding check as planned", "D": "Assess his bladder now, help him try to void, and report to the RN if unable"}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Uses a comfort measure alone and waits", "B": "Catheterizes without an order", "C": "Waits for a routine check while a client is in discomfort with distension"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C264", "cluster_text": "Dana, a practical nurse on a surgical unit, shares care of four clients with an RN and an unregulated care provider (UCP) named Sam during a day shift. Mr. Roy is on day one after a hernia repair, Mr. Singh needs a first-dose intravenous antibiotic, Mr. Costa is on contact precautions for Clostridioides difficile, and Ms. Park is on day five after bowel surgery. The RN is also the charge nurse.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess his bladder now, help him try to void, and report to the RN if unable): Distension, discomfort and no voiding suggest retention, so Dana acts now by assessing, helping him void and informing the RN if that fails. Waiting for the routine check leaves him uncomfortable and risks bladder overdistension. Heat alone is passive, and catheterization needs an order. Respond to retention without delay."}, {"id": 2193, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Singh, 62, is receiving his first dose of intravenous cefazolin when he reports a tight throat and itching. His lips are swelling and he has an audible wheeze. Dana is in his room with the call bell. What should Dana do first?", "options": {"A": "Stop the infusion, flush the line with saline, and reassess his symptoms in ten minutes", "B": "Stop the infusion, keep the line open, call for emergency help, and stay with him", "C": "Give diphenhydramine from the medication cart and then reassess his breathing in fifteen minutes", "D": "Stop the infusion, remove the intravenous catheter, and wait for the RN to arrive"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Flushing pushes more drug into him and delays emergency help", "C": "Gives a medication without an order and delays emergency help", "D": "Removes needed access and delays calling for emergency help"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C264", "cluster_text": "Dana, a practical nurse on a surgical unit, shares care of four clients with an RN and an unregulated care provider (UCP) named Sam during a day shift. Mr. Roy is on day one after a hernia repair, Mr. Singh needs a first-dose intravenous antibiotic, Mr. Costa is on contact precautions for Clostridioides difficile, and Ms. Park is on day five after bowel surgery. The RN is also the charge nurse.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop the infusion, keep the line open, call for emergency help, and stay with him): Swelling, wheeze and throat tightness after a new antibiotic indicate anaphylaxis, so she stops the drug, keeps the line, summons help and remains at the bedside. Flushing the line pushes remaining drug in, and removing the catheter loses access for emergency drugs. Giving an unordered drug wastes minutes that threaten his airway. Act immediately in anaphylaxis."}, {"id": 2194, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "Mr. Costa, 78, is on contact precautions for C. difficile and needs a bed bath. Dana wants to delegate it to Sam, the UCP, who has not cared for a client on precautions before. What should Dana do before delegating?", "options": {"A": "Teach Sam the gown, glove and soap-and-water hand hygiene steps, then check his work afterward", "B": "Teach Sam the gown and glove steps, and let him choose sanitizer or soap", "C": "Delegate the bath and tell Sam to use hand sanitizer between care tasks in the room", "D": "Have Sam wear gloves and an apron, then use sanitizer after leaving the room"}, "level": "application", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Leaves the hand hygiene method to the delegate for spore forming organisms", "C": "Gives the wrong hand hygiene method for spore-forming organisms", "D": "Uses sanitizer, which does not remove C. difficile spores"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C264", "cluster_text": "Dana, a practical nurse on a surgical unit, shares care of four clients with an RN and an unregulated care provider (UCP) named Sam during a day shift. Mr. Roy is on day one after a hernia repair, Mr. Singh needs a first-dose intravenous antibiotic, Mr. Costa is on contact precautions for Clostridioides difficile, and Ms. Park is on day five after bowel surgery. The RN is also the charge nurse.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Teach Sam the gown, glove and soap-and-water hand hygiene steps, then check his work afterward): Delegation requires confirming that the person can do the task safely, and C. difficile spores need gown, gloves and soap and water hand washing because sanitizer is ineffective. Dana teaches and then checks the work. Letting Sam choose sanitizer or relying on gloves and an apron with sanitizer leaves spores on the hands. Confirm competence before delegating."}, {"id": 2195, "domain": "collaborative_practice", "question": "At 1400, Ms. Park, 55, says she felt something give while coughing, and her abdominal dressing is soaked with pink fluid. Dana sees loops of bowel through the incision. A routine dressing change is also due for Mr. Roy. What should Dana do first?", "options": {"A": "Push the bowel loops gently back inside and apply a dry dressing before calling", "B": "Stay with her, cover the wound with sterile saline-moistened gauze, and call the RN", "C": "Reinforce the dressing with dry gauze and apply an abdominal binder snugly.", "D": "Finish the routine dressing change for Mr. Roy first, then assess Ms. Park's incision"}, "level": "application", "approach": "safety_governance_team", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Handles exposed bowel and applies a dry dressing that sticks", "C": "Covers exposed bowel with dry gauze and a binder", "D": "Puts a routine task ahead of a surgical emergency"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C264", "cluster_text": "Dana, a practical nurse on a surgical unit, shares care of four clients with an RN and an unregulated care provider (UCP) named Sam during a day shift. Mr. Roy is on day one after a hernia repair, Mr. Singh needs a first-dose intravenous antibiotic, Mr. Costa is on contact precautions for Clostridioides difficile, and Ms. Park is on day five after bowel surgery. The RN is also the charge nurse.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stay with her, cover the wound with sterile saline-moistened gauze, and call the RN): Exposed bowel after coughing is an evisceration, a surgical emergency, so Dana stays, covers the loops with sterile moist gauze, and calls the RN at once. A routine dressing change can wait. Pushing bowel back or using dry gauze damages tissue, and a binder over exposed bowel adds pressure. Emergencies take priority over routine care."}, {"id": 2196, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Gaetan Roy, 74, tells the clinic nurse that his memory is gone and he sees no point in trying. His wife says he dropped cards and gardening four months ago, sleeps poorly, and answers \"I don't know\" to simple questions, yet he recalls yesterday's hockey score. Which interpretation best guides what the nurse reports to the clinic provider?", "options": {"A": "Gradual forgetting at his age points to early dementia, so report it that way.", "B": "Flat mood and weak recall effort suggest possible depression, so report mood changes.", "C": "Poor memory and sleep are expected aging changes, so document and rebook for next year.", "D": "Lost interest suggests a medication side effect, so advise him to stop his newest tablet."}, "level": "application", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assuming memory complaints in an older adult are irreversible dementia without considering depression", "C": "Dismissing treatable symptoms as normal aging and delaying assessment", "D": "Practical nurse advising a medication change outside scope based on a guess"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Flat mood and weak recall effort suggest possible depression, so report mood changes.): The scenario shows low mood, loss of interest, poor sleep and inconsistent recall, a pattern that fits depression, which is treatable and carries a suicide risk, so mood and safety need assessment by the provider. Labelling this as dementia would anchor on age and could leave a treatable illness untreated. Calling the changes normal aging delays care for a client who has lost interest in daily life. Remember that depression in older adults can look like memory loss and must be ruled out."}, {"id": 2197, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "Marcus, 36, returned to the ward ninety minutes ago after a lumbar puncture, and the order reads flat bed rest for four hours. He asks to sit up for lunch, saying his back is sore but his head feels fine. His dressing is dry and his blood pressure is 118 over 76. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Call the prescriber to cancel the order because he has no headache.", "B": "Let him sit up to eat his lunch, then lower the bed flat afterward", "C": "Keep him flat as ordered, help him eat lying down, and encourage fluids.", "D": "Keep him flat as ordered and ask him to avoid turning side to side."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "operative_decision", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Seeks to cancel a positioning order because he feels well", "B": "Allowing a temporary upright position that still risks a spinal headache from fluid leak", "D": "Adds an unneeded movement restriction while missing the point of flat rest"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C261", "cluster_text": "Carmen is a practical nurse who floats across three areas of a community hospital during one day shift: the medical ward, the adult intensive care unit and the pediatric outpatient clinic. Each assignment brings a client with a neurological or sensory concern. She works within her scope, follows orders and facility policy, and reports changes to the registered nurse or prescriber. The questions follow the order of her morning.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep him flat as ordered, help him eat lying down, and encourage fluids.): He is only ninety minutes into a four hour flat rest order, and staying flat lowers the chance of a post puncture headache from cerebrospinal fluid leaking at the needle site. Sitting him up for lunch, even briefly, breaks the order and risks that headache despite a dry dressing and normal pressure. Asking to cancel the order because he feels well or restricting turning does not address the leak risk. Follow the positioning order exactly unless the RN or prescriber changes it."}, {"id": 2198, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "In intensive care, a 52-year-old man is unresponsive after a brain bleed. The nurse finds red bruising over his breastbone after two colleagues repeatedly ground their knuckles there to test him, and a fresh neurological check is due. Which action should the nurse take now?", "options": {"A": "Skip the painful stimulus and record him as unresponsive until the physician reorders.", "B": "Press a pen firmly across each fingernail bed repeatedly until he responds.", "C": "Stop sternal rubbing, report the bruising to the RN, and use a trapezius squeeze.", "D": "Continue sternal rubbing with lighter pressure so the bruise does not enlarge."}, "level": "application", "approach": "complication_troubleshooting", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Omitting the scheduled neurological check, which hides any change in condition", "B": "Using repeated peripheral pressure that can injure tissue and give a reflex rather than a true response", "D": "Repeating the same injurious stimulus on already bruised skin"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C261", "cluster_text": "Carmen is a practical nurse who floats across three areas of a community hospital during one day shift: the medical ward, the adult intensive care unit and the pediatric outpatient clinic. Each assignment brings a client with a neurological or sensory concern. She works within her scope, follows orders and facility policy, and reports changes to the registered nurse or prescriber. The questions follow the order of her morning.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop sternal rubbing, report the bruising to the RN, and use a trapezius squeeze.): Repeated sternal rubs have already injured his skin, so the nurse stops that method, reports the bruising so it is assessed and documented, and chooses a less damaging central stimulus such as a trapezius squeeze following facility policy. Lighter rubbing on the same bruise still causes harm. Hard repeated fingernail pressure risks injury and may only trigger a spinal reflex. Neurological checks are done at the ordered interval, never skipped."}, {"id": 2199, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A mother brings her 4-year-old daughter to the clinic after the girl pushed a plastic bead into her right ear canal an hour ago. The child is calm, hears normally and has no drainage. The mother asks why the nurse will not pull the bead out with tweezers from her sewing kit. What is the best explanation?", "options": {"A": "Have her tilt her head and shake it, which usually dislodges small objects.", "B": "A cotton swab is safer than tweezers because its soft tip pushes objects out.", "C": "A plastic bead can only be removed by flushing the ear with water.", "D": "Tweezers can push a smooth object deeper and injure the canal or eardrum."}, "level": "application", "approach": "knowledge_concept", "distractor_mistakes": {"A": "Assumes shaking the head can remove a lodged object safely", "B": "Believes a swab removes objects when it pushes them deeper", "C": "Believing every foreign body can be flushed out, ignoring the risk of pushing it deeper"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C261", "cluster_text": "Carmen is a practical nurse who floats across three areas of a community hospital during one day shift: the medical ward, the adult intensive care unit and the pediatric outpatient clinic. Each assignment brings a client with a neurological or sensory concern. She works within her scope, follows orders and facility policy, and reports changes to the registered nurse or prescriber. The questions follow the order of her morning.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tweezers can push a smooth object deeper and injure the canal or eardrum.): The canal is narrow and the eardrum lies close behind the bead, so grasping a smooth round object with tweezers tends to push it deeper and can tear the canal lining or damage the eardrum. A cotton swab can push it deeper too, and shaking the head does not reliably free a wedged object. Flushing is not the standard plan for this object either. The nurse keeps the child calm and arranges removal by the prescriber."}, {"id": 2200, "domain": "foundations_of_practice", "question": "A 70-year-old resident of long-term care dropped her spoon at 1400, and her left hand now feels weak and clumsy. She was chatting and eating normally at 1230. Her blood pressure is 168 over 92 and her blood glucose is 6.2 mmol/L. What should the nurse do?", "options": {"A": "Treat it as a suspected stroke, note 1230 as last seen well, and call emergency help.", "B": "Have her lie down in bed and monitor her blood pressure every thirty minutes.", "C": "Offer juice and a snack, then recheck her hand strength after she rests.", "D": "Give her aspirin from the stock supply and call the physician after one hour."}, "level": "diagnose", "approach": "interpretation_application", "distractor_mistakes": {"B": "Monitors only blood pressure and delays emergency assessment", "C": "Treating new weakness as low blood sugar even though the glucose is normal", "D": "Gives a medication without an order and delays the call"}, "cluster_id": "CPNRE-C261", "cluster_text": "Carmen is a practical nurse who floats across three areas of a community hospital during one day shift: the medical ward, the adult intensive care unit and the pediatric outpatient clinic. Each assignment brings a client with a neurological or sensory concern. She works within her scope, follows orders and facility policy, and reports changes to the registered nurse or prescriber. The questions follow the order of her morning.", "cluster_size": 4, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Treat it as a suspected stroke, note 1230 as last seen well, and call emergency help.): New one sided weakness with a known last seen well time of 1230 is a stroke warning, and treatment depends on how long ago symptoms began, so emergency help is called now and the time is noted. A glucose of 6.2 mmol/L rules out low blood sugar, so juice and a snack would only delay care and risk choking. Lying her down to monitor, or giving aspirin without an order before calling, wastes the time window. Report new neurological change immediately and do not wait."}];
